{"format":"care-public-library/1","exported":"2026-10-05","works":[{"id":"WORK_KANT_PURE_REASON","slug":"kant","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","author":"Immanuel Kant","edition":"J. M. D. Meiklejohn translation · Gutenberg eText #4280","year":"1781 / 1787","description":"What can we know—and what gives a claim the right to call itself knowledge?","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-73b8664b61f6f07f-e4b1ac743ae9ce7e.png","source_url":"","source_notices":{},"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","sources":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IDBP","text":"## Introduction\n\n### I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge\n\nThat all our knowledge begins with experience there can be no doubt. For how is it possible that the faculty of cognition should be awakened into exercise otherwise than by means of objects which affect our senses, and partly of themselves produce representations, partly rouse our powers of understanding into activity, to compare to connect, or to separate these, and so to convert the raw material of our sensuous impressions into a knowledge of objects, which is called experience? In respect of time, therefore, no knowledge of ours is antecedent to experience, but begins with it.\n\nBut, though all our knowledge begins with experience, it by no means follows that all arises out of experience. For, on the contrary, it is quite possible that our empirical knowledge is a compound of that which we receive through impressions, and that which the faculty of cognition supplies from itself (sensuous impressions giving merely the occasion), an addition which we cannot distinguish from the original element given by sense, till long practice has made us attentive to, and skilful in separating it. It is, therefore, a question which requires close investigation, and not to be answered at first sight, whether there exists a knowledge altogether independent of experience, and even of all sensuous impressions? Knowledge of this kind is called à priori, in contradistinction to empirical knowledge, which has its sources à posteriori, that is, in experience.\n\nBut the expression, “à priori,” is not as yet definite enough adequately to indicate the whole meaning of the question above started. For, in speaking of knowledge which has its sources in experience, we are wont to say, that this or that may be known à priori, because we do not derive this knowledge immediately from experience, but from a general rule, which, however, we have itself borrowed from experience. Thus, if a man undermined his house, we say, “he might know à priori that it would have fallen;” that is, he needed not to have waited for the experience that it did actually fall. But still, à priori, he could not know even this much. For, that bodies are heavy, and, consequently, that they fall when their supports are taken away, must have been known to him previously, by means of experience.\n\nBy the term “knowledge à priori,” therefore, we shall in the sequel understand, not such as is independent of this or that kind of experience, but such as is absolutely so of all experience. Opposed to this is empirical knowledge, or that which is possible only à posteriori, that is, through experience. Knowledge à priori is either pure or impure. Pure knowledge à priori is that with which no empirical element is mixed up. For example, the proposition, “Every change has a cause,” is a proposition à priori, but impure, because change is a conception which can only be derived from experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","text":"## Introduction\n\n### I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge\n\nThat all our knowledge begins with experience there can be no doubt. For how is it possible that the faculty of cognition should be awakened into exercise otherwise than by means of objects which affect our senses, and partly of themselves produce representations, partly rouse our powers of understanding into activity, to compare to connect, or to separate these, and so to convert the raw material of our sensuous impressions into a knowledge of objects, which is called experience? In respect of time, therefore, no knowledge of ours is antecedent to experience, but begins with it.\n\nBut, though all our knowledge begins with experience, it by no means follows that all arises out of experience. For, on the contrary, it is quite possible that our empirical knowledge is a compound of that which we receive through impressions, and that which the faculty of cognition supplies from itself (sensuous impressions giving merely the occasion), an addition which we cannot distinguish from the original element given by sense, till long practice has made us attentive to, and skilful in separating it. It is, therefore, a question which requires close investigation, and not to be answered at first sight, whether there exists a knowledge altogether independent of experience, and even of all sensuous impressions? Knowledge of this kind is called à priori, in contradistinction to empirical knowledge, which has its sources à posteriori, that is, in experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","text":"But the expression, “à priori,” is not as yet definite enough adequately to indicate the whole meaning of the question above started. For, in speaking of knowledge which has its sources in experience, we are wont to say, that this or that may be known à priori, because we do not derive this knowledge immediately from experience, but from a general rule, which, however, we have itself borrowed from experience. Thus, if a man undermined his house, we say, “he might know à priori that it would have fallen;” that is, he needed not to have waited for the experience that it did actually fall. But still, à priori, he could not know even this much. For, that bodies are heavy, and, consequently, that they fall when their supports are taken away, must have been known to him previously, by means of experience.\n\nBy the term “knowledge à priori,” therefore, we shall in the sequel understand, not such as is independent of this or that kind of experience, but such as is absolutely so of all experience. Opposed to this is empirical knowledge, or that which is possible only à posteriori, that is, through experience. Knowledge à priori is either pure or impure. Pure knowledge à priori is that with which no empirical element is mixed up. For example, the proposition, “Every change has a cause,” is a proposition à priori, but impure, because change is a conception which can only be derived from experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fd5669aba309e950413a9d61528ae8a0082bfa460a74b2f917dd8c7ef3be9d57","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IHIE","text":"### II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.\n\nThe question now is as to a criterion, by which we may securely distinguish a pure from an empirical cognition. Experience no doubt teaches us that this or that object is constituted in such and such a manner, but not that it could not possibly exist otherwise. Now, in the first place, if we have a proposition which contains the idea of necessity in its very conception, it is priori. If, moreover, it is not derived from any other proposition, unless from one equally involving the idea of necessity, it is absolutely priori. Secondly, an empirical judgement never exhibits strict and absolute, but only assumed and comparative universality (by induction); therefore, the most we can say is—so far as we have hitherto observed, there is no exception to this or that rule. If, on the other hand, a judgement carries with it strict and absolute universality, that is, admits of no possible exception, it is not derived from experience, but is valid absolutely à priori.\n\nEmpirical universality is, therefore, only an arbitrary extension of validity, from that which may be predicated of a proposition valid in most cases, to that which is asserted of a proposition which holds good in all; as, for example, in the affirmation, “All bodies are heavy.” When, on the contrary, strict universality characterizes a judgement, it necessarily indicates another peculiar source of knowledge, namely, a faculty of cognition à priori. Necessity and strict universality, therefore, are infallible tests for distinguishing pure from empirical knowledge, and are inseparably connected with each other. But as in the use of these criteria the empirical limitation is sometimes more easily detected than the contingency of the judgement, or the unlimited universality which we attach to a judgement is often a more convincing proof than its necessity, it may be advisable to use the criteria separately, each being by itself infallible.\n\nNow, that in the sphere of human cognition we have judgements which are necessary, and in the strictest sense universal, consequently pure à priori, it will be an easy matter to show. If we desire an example from the sciences, we need only take any proposition in mathematics. If we cast our eyes upon the commonest operations of the understanding, the proposition, “Every change must have a cause,” will amply serve our purpose. In the latter case, indeed, the conception of a cause so plainly involves the conception of a necessity of connection with an effect, and of a strict universality of the law, that the very notion of a cause would entirely disappear, were we to derive it, like Hume, from a frequent association of what happens with that which precedes; and the habit thence originating of connecting representations—the necessity inherent in the judgement being therefore merely subjective. Besides, without seeking for such examples of principles existing à priori in cognition, we might easily show that such principles are the indispensable basis of the possibility of experience itself, and consequently prove their existence à priori. For whence could our experience itself acquire certainty, if all the rules on which it depends were themselves empirical, and consequently fortuitous? No one, therefore, can admit the validity of the use of such rules as first principles. But, for the present, we may content ourselves with having established the fact, that we do possess and exercise a faculty of pure à priori cognition; and, secondly, with having pointed out the proper tests of such cognition, namely, universality and necessity.\n\nNot only in judgements, however, but even in conceptions, is an à priori origin manifest. For example, if we take away by degrees from our conceptions of a body all that can be referred to mere sensuous experience—colour, hardness or softness, weight, even impenetrability—the body will then vanish; but the space which it occupied still remains, and this it is utterly impossible to annihilate in thought. Again, if we take away, in like manner, from our empirical conception of any object, corporeal or incorporeal, all properties which mere experience has taught us to connect with it, still we cannot think away those through which we cogitate it as substance, or adhering to substance, although our conception of substance is more determined than that of an object. Compelled, therefore, by that necessity with which the conception of substance forces itself upon us, we must confess that it has its seat in our faculty of cognition à priori.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","text":"### II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.\n\nThe question now is as to a criterion, by which we may securely distinguish a pure from an empirical cognition. Experience no doubt teaches us that this or that object is constituted in such and such a manner, but not that it could not possibly exist otherwise. Now, in the first place, if we have a proposition which contains the idea of necessity in its very conception, it is priori. If, moreover, it is not derived from any other proposition, unless from one equally involving the idea of necessity, it is absolutely priori. Secondly, an empirical judgement never exhibits strict and absolute, but only assumed and comparative universality (by induction); therefore, the most we can say is—so far as we have hitherto observed, there is no exception to this or that rule. If, on the other hand, a judgement carries with it strict and absolute universality, that is, admits of no possible exception, it is not derived from experience, but is valid absolutely à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","text":"Empirical universality is, therefore, only an arbitrary extension of validity, from that which may be predicated of a proposition valid in most cases, to that which is asserted of a proposition which holds good in all; as, for example, in the affirmation, “All bodies are heavy.” When, on the contrary, strict universality characterizes a judgement, it necessarily indicates another peculiar source of knowledge, namely, a faculty of cognition à priori. Necessity and strict universality, therefore, are infallible tests for distinguishing pure from empirical knowledge, and are inseparably connected with each other. But as in the use of these criteria the empirical limitation is sometimes more easily detected than the contingency of the judgement, or the unlimited universality which we attach to a judgement is often a more convincing proof than its necessity, it may be advisable to use the criteria separately, each being by itself infallible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","text":"Now, that in the sphere of human cognition we have judgements which are necessary, and in the strictest sense universal, consequently pure à priori, it will be an easy matter to show. If we desire an example from the sciences, we need only take any proposition in mathematics. If we cast our eyes upon the commonest operations of the understanding, the proposition, “Every change must have a cause,” will amply serve our purpose. In the latter case, indeed, the conception of a cause so plainly involves the conception of a necessity of connection with an effect, and of a strict universality of the law, that the very notion of a cause would entirely disappear, were we to derive it, like Hume, from a frequent association of what happens with that which precedes; and the habit thence originating of connecting representations—the necessity inherent in the judgement being therefore merely subjective. Besides, without seeking for such examples of principles existing à priori in cognition, we might easily show that such principles are the indispensable basis of the possibility of experience itself, and consequently prove their existence à priori. For whence could our experience itself acquire certainty, if all the rules on which it depends were themselves empirical, and consequently fortuitous? No one, therefore, can admit the validity of the use of such rules as first principles. But, for the present, we may content ourselves with having established the fact, that we do possess and exercise a faculty of pure à priori cognition; and, secondly, with having pointed out the proper tests of such cognition, namely, universality and necessity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","text":"Not only in judgements, however, but even in conceptions, is an à priori origin manifest. For example, if we take away by degrees from our conceptions of a body all that can be referred to mere sensuous experience—colour, hardness or softness, weight, even impenetrability—the body will then vanish; but the space which it occupied still remains, and this it is utterly impossible to annihilate in thought. Again, if we take away, in like manner, from our empirical conception of any object, corporeal or incorporeal, all properties which mere experience has taught us to connect with it, still we cannot think away those through which we cogitate it as substance, or adhering to substance, although our conception of substance is more determined than that of an object. Compelled, therefore, by that necessity with which the conception of substance forces itself upon us, we must confess that it has its seat in our faculty of cognition à priori.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"246eab62a08dcabf4d800525956e68dfbbb835d624e64dcb0ee0a6b36941a3c2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IPSN","text":"### III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”\n\nOf far more importance than all that has been above said, is the consideration that certain of our cognitions rise completely above the sphere of all possible experience, and by means of conceptions, to which there exists in the whole extent of experience no corresponding object, seem to extend the range of our judgements beyond its bounds. And just in this transcendental or supersensible sphere, where experience affords us neither instruction nor guidance, lie the investigations of reason, which, on account of their importance, we consider far preferable to, and as having a far more elevated aim than, all that the understanding can achieve within the sphere of sensuous phenomena. So high a value do we set upon these investigations, that even at the risk of error, we persist in following them out, and permit neither doubt nor disregard nor indifference to restrain us from the pursuit. These unavoidable problems of mere pure reason are God, freedom (of will), and immortality. The science which, with all its preliminaries, has for its especial object the solution of these problems is named metaphysics—a science which is at the very outset dogmatical, that is, it confidently takes upon itself the execution of this task without any previous investigation of the ability or inability of reason for such an undertaking.\n\nNow the safe ground of experience being thus abandoned, it seems nevertheless natural that we should hesitate to erect a building with the cognitions we possess, without knowing whence they come, and on the strength of principles, the origin of which is undiscovered. Instead of thus trying to build without a foundation, it is rather to be expected that we should long ago have put the question, how the understanding can arrive at these à priori cognitions, and what is the extent, validity, and worth which they may possess? We say, “This is natural enough,” meaning by the word natural, that which is consistent with a just and reasonable way of thinking; but if we understand by the term, that which usually happens, nothing indeed could be more natural and more comprehensible than that this investigation should be left long unattempted. For one part of our pure knowledge, the science of mathematics, has been long firmly established, and thus leads us to form flattering expectations with regard to others, though these may be of quite a different nature. Besides, when we get beyond the bounds of experience, we are of course safe from opposition in that quarter; and the charm of widening the range of our knowledge is so great that, unless we are brought to a standstill by some evident contradiction, we hurry on undoubtingly in our course. This, however, may be avoided, if we are sufficiently cautious in the construction of our fictions, which are not the less fictions on that account.\n\nMathematical science affords us a brilliant example, how far, independently of all experience, we may carry our à priori knowledge. It is true that the mathematician occupies himself with objects and cognitions only in so far as they can be represented by means of intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the said intuition can itself be given à priori, and therefore is hardly to be distinguished from a mere pure conception. Deceived by such a proof of the power of reason, we can perceive no limits to the extension of our knowledge. The light dove cleaving in free flight the thin air, whose resistance it feels, might imagine that her movements would be far more free and rapid in airless space. Just in the same way did Plato, abandoning the world of sense because of the narrow limits it sets to the understanding, venture upon the wings of ideas beyond it, into the void space of pure intellect. He did not reflect that he made no real progress by all his efforts; for he met with no resistance which might serve him for a support, as it were, whereon to rest, and on which he might apply his powers, in order to let the intellect acquire momentum for its progress. It is, indeed, the common fate of human reason in speculation, to finish the imposing edifice of thought as rapidly as possible, and then for the first time to begin to examine whether the foundation is a solid one or no. Arrived at this point, all sorts of excuses are sought after, in order to console us for its want of stability, or rather, indeed, to enable Us to dispense altogether with so late and dangerous an investigation. But what frees us during the process of building from all apprehension or suspicion, and flatters us into the belief of its solidity, is this. A great part, perhaps the greatest part, of the business of our reason consists in the analysation of the conceptions which we already possess of objects. By this means we gain a multitude of cognitions, which although really nothing more than elucidations or explanations of that which (though in a confused manner) was already thought in our conceptions, are, at least in respect of their form, prized as new introspections; whilst, so far as regards their matter or content, we have really made no addition to our conceptions, but only disinvolved them. But as this process does furnish a real priori knowledge, which has a sure progress and useful results, reason, deceived by this, slips in, without being itself aware of it, assertions of a quite different kind; in which, to given conceptions it adds others, à priori indeed, but entirely foreign to them, without our knowing how it arrives at these, and, indeed, without such a question ever suggesting itself. I shall therefore at once proceed to examine the difference between these two modes of knowledge.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","text":"### III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”\n\nOf far more importance than all that has been above said, is the consideration that certain of our cognitions rise completely above the sphere of all possible experience, and by means of conceptions, to which there exists in the whole extent of experience no corresponding object, seem to extend the range of our judgements beyond its bounds. And just in this transcendental or supersensible sphere, where experience affords us neither instruction nor guidance, lie the investigations of reason, which, on account of their importance, we consider far preferable to, and as having a far more elevated aim than, all that the understanding can achieve within the sphere of sensuous phenomena. So high a value do we set upon these investigations, that even at the risk of error, we persist in following them out, and permit neither doubt nor disregard nor indifference to restrain us from the pursuit. These unavoidable problems of mere pure reason are God, freedom (of will), and immortality. The science which, with all its preliminaries, has for its especial object the solution of these problems is named metaphysics—a science which is at the very outset dogmatical, that is, it confidently takes upon itself the execution of this task without any previous investigation of the ability or inability of reason for such an undertaking.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","text":"Now the safe ground of experience being thus abandoned, it seems nevertheless natural that we should hesitate to erect a building with the cognitions we possess, without knowing whence they come, and on the strength of principles, the origin of which is undiscovered. Instead of thus trying to build without a foundation, it is rather to be expected that we should long ago have put the question, how the understanding can arrive at these à priori cognitions, and what is the extent, validity, and worth which they may possess? We say, “This is natural enough,” meaning by the word natural, that which is consistent with a just and reasonable way of thinking; but if we understand by the term, that which usually happens, nothing indeed could be more natural and more comprehensible than that this investigation should be left long unattempted. For one part of our pure knowledge, the science of mathematics, has been long firmly established, and thus leads us to form flattering expectations with regard to others, though these may be of quite a different nature. Besides, when we get beyond the bounds of experience, we are of course safe from opposition in that quarter; and the charm of widening the range of our knowledge is so great that, unless we are brought to a standstill by some evident contradiction, we hurry on undoubtingly in our course. This, however, may be avoided, if we are sufficiently cautious in the construction of our fictions, which are not the less fictions on that account.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","text":"Mathematical science affords us a brilliant example, how far, independently of all experience, we may carry our à priori knowledge. It is true that the mathematician occupies himself with objects and cognitions only in so far as they can be represented by means of intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the said intuition can itself be given à priori, and therefore is hardly to be distinguished from a mere pure conception. Deceived by such a proof of the power of reason, we can perceive no limits to the extension of our knowledge. The light dove cleaving in free flight the thin air, whose resistance it feels, might imagine that her movements would be far more free and rapid in airless space. Just in the same way did Plato, abandoning the world of sense because of the narrow limits it sets to the understanding, venture upon the wings of ideas beyond it, into the void space of pure intellect. He did not reflect that he made no real progress by all his efforts; for he met with no resistance which might serve him for a support, as it were, whereon to rest, and on which he might apply his powers, in order to let the intellect acquire momentum for its progress. It is, indeed, the common fate of human reason in speculation, to finish the imposing edifice of thought as rapidly as possible, and then for the first time to begin to examine whether the foundation is a solid one or no. Arrived at this point, all sorts of excuses are sought after, in order to console us for its want of stability, or rather, indeed, to enable Us to dispense altogether with so late and dangerous an investigation. But what frees us during the process of building from all apprehension or suspicion, and flatters us into the belief of its solidity, is this. A great part, perhaps the greatest part, of the business of our reason consists in the analysation of the conceptions which we already possess of objects. By this means we gain a multitude of cognitions, which although really nothing more than elucidations or explanations of that which (though in a confused manner) was already thought in our conceptions, are, at least in respect of their form, prized as new introspections; whilst, so far as regards their matter or content, we have really made no addition to our conceptions, but only disinvolved them. But as this process does furnish a real priori knowledge, which has a sure progress and useful results, reason, deceived by this, slips in, without being itself aware of it, assertions of a quite different kind; in which, to given conceptions it adds others, à priori indeed, but entirely foreign to them, without our knowing how it arrives at these, and, indeed, without such a question ever suggesting itself. I shall therefore at once proceed to examine the difference between these two modes of knowledge.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a3a558a7b642c2ca425c9f1d7b07f6ef5f3690124102bb9b5997e8a6df9f620e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IDBA","text":"### IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.\n\nIn all judgements wherein the relation of a subject to the predicate is cogitated (I mention affirmative judgements only here; the application to negative will be very easy), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A, as somewhat which is contained (though covertly) in the conception A; or the predicate B lies completely out of the conception A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first instance, I term the judgement analytical, in the second, synthetical. Analytical judgements (affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is cogitated through identity; those in which this connection is cogitated without identity, are called synthetical judgements. The former may be called explicative, the latter augmentative judgements; because the former add in the predicate nothing to the conception of the subject, but only analyse it into its constituent conceptions, which were thought already in the subject, although in a confused manner; the latter add to our conceptions of the subject a predicate which was not contained in it, and which no analysis could ever have discovered therein. For example, when I say, “All bodies are extended,” this is an analytical judgement. For I need not go beyond the conception of body in order to find extension connected with it, but merely analyse the conception, that is, become conscious of the manifold properties which I think in that conception, in order to discover this predicate in it: it is therefore an analytical judgement. On the other hand, when I say, “All bodies are heavy,” the predicate is something totally different from that which I think in the mere conception of a body. By the addition of such a predicate, therefore, it becomes a synthetical judgement.\n\nJudgements of experience, as such, are always synthetical. For it would be absurd to think of grounding an analytical judgement on experience, because in forming such a judgement I need not go out of the sphere of my conceptions, and therefore recourse to the testimony of experience is quite unnecessary. That “bodies are extended” is not an empirical judgement, but a proposition which stands firm à priori. For before addressing myself to experience, I already have in my conception all the requisite conditions for the judgement, and I have only to extract the predicate from the conception, according to the principle of contradiction, and thereby at the same time become conscious of the necessity of the judgement, a necessity which I could never learn from experience. On the other hand, though at first I do not at all include the predicate of weight in my conception of body in general, that conception still indicates an object of experience, a part of the totality of experience, to which I can still add other parts; and this I do when I recognize by observation that bodies are heavy. I can cognize beforehand by analysis the conception of body through the characteristics of extension, impenetrability, shape, etc., all which are cogitated in this conception. But now I extend my knowledge, and looking back on experience from which I had derived this conception of body, I find weight at all times connected with the above characteristics, and therefore I synthetically add to my conceptions this as a predicate, and say, “All bodies are heavy.” Thus it is experience upon which rests the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight with the conception of body, because both conceptions, although the one is not contained in the other, still belong to one another (only contingently, however), as parts of a whole, namely, of experience, which is itself a synthesis of intuitions.\n\nBut to synthetical judgements à priori, such aid is entirely wanting. If I go out of and beyond the conception A, in order to recognize another B as connected with it, what foundation have I to rest on, whereby to render the synthesis possible? I have here no longer the advantage of looking out in the sphere of experience for what I want. Let us take, for example, the proposition, “Everything that happens has a cause.” In the conception of “something that happens,” I indeed think an existence which a certain time antecedes, and from this I can derive analytical judgements. But the conception of a cause lies quite out of the above conception, and indicates something entirely different from “that which happens,” and is consequently not contained in that conception. How then am I able to assert concerning the general conception—“that which happens”—something entirely different from that conception, and to recognize the conception of cause although not contained in it, yet as belonging to it, and even necessarily? what is here the unknown = X, upon which the understanding rests when it believes it has found, out of the conception A a foreign predicate B, which it nevertheless considers to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle adduced annexes the two representations, cause and effect, to the representation existence, not only with universality, which experience cannot give, but also with the expression of necessity, therefore completely à priori and from pure conceptions. Upon such synthetical, that is augmentative propositions, depends the whole aim of our speculative knowledge à priori; for although analytical judgements are indeed highly important and necessary, they are so, only to arrive at that clearness of conceptions which is requisite for a sure and extended synthesis, and this alone is a real acquisition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","text":"### IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.\n\nIn all judgements wherein the relation of a subject to the predicate is cogitated (I mention affirmative judgements only here; the application to negative will be very easy), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A, as somewhat which is contained (though covertly) in the conception A; or the predicate B lies completely out of the conception A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first instance, I term the judgement analytical, in the second, synthetical. Analytical judgements (affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is cogitated through identity; those in which this connection is cogitated without identity, are called synthetical judgements. The former may be called explicative, the latter augmentative judgements; because the former add in the predicate nothing to the conception of the subject, but only analyse it into its constituent conceptions, which were thought already in the subject, although in a confused manner; the latter add to our conceptions of the subject a predicate which was not contained in it, and which no analysis could ever have discovered therein. For example, when I say, “All bodies are extended,” this is an analytical judgement. For I need not go beyond the conception of body in order to find extension connected with it, but merely analyse the conception, that is, become conscious of the manifold properties which I think in that conception, in order to discover this predicate in it: it is therefore an analytical judgement. On the other hand, when I say, “All bodies are heavy,” the predicate is something totally different from that which I think in the mere conception of a body. By the addition of such a predicate, therefore, it becomes a synthetical judgement.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","text":"Judgements of experience, as such, are always synthetical. For it would be absurd to think of grounding an analytical judgement on experience, because in forming such a judgement I need not go out of the sphere of my conceptions, and therefore recourse to the testimony of experience is quite unnecessary. That “bodies are extended” is not an empirical judgement, but a proposition which stands firm à priori. For before addressing myself to experience, I already have in my conception all the requisite conditions for the judgement, and I have only to extract the predicate from the conception, according to the principle of contradiction, and thereby at the same time become conscious of the necessity of the judgement, a necessity which I could never learn from experience. On the other hand, though at first I do not at all include the predicate of weight in my conception of body in general, that conception still indicates an object of experience, a part of the totality of experience, to which I can still add other parts; and this I do when I recognize by observation that bodies are heavy. I can cognize beforehand by analysis the conception of body through the characteristics of extension, impenetrability, shape, etc., all which are cogitated in this conception. But now I extend my knowledge, and looking back on experience from which I had derived this conception of body, I find weight at all times connected with the above characteristics, and therefore I synthetically add to my conceptions this as a predicate, and say, “All bodies are heavy.” Thus it is experience upon which rests the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight with the conception of body, because both conceptions, although the one is not contained in the other, still belong to one another (only contingently, however), as parts of a whole, namely, of experience, which is itself a synthesis of intuitions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","text":"But to synthetical judgements à priori, such aid is entirely wanting. If I go out of and beyond the conception A, in order to recognize another B as connected with it, what foundation have I to rest on, whereby to render the synthesis possible? I have here no longer the advantage of looking out in the sphere of experience for what I want. Let us take, for example, the proposition, “Everything that happens has a cause.” In the conception of “something that happens,” I indeed think an existence which a certain time antecedes, and from this I can derive analytical judgements. But the conception of a cause lies quite out of the above conception, and indicates something entirely different from “that which happens,” and is consequently not contained in that conception. How then am I able to assert concerning the general conception—“that which happens”—something entirely different from that conception, and to recognize the conception of cause although not contained in it, yet as belonging to it, and even necessarily? what is here the unknown = X, upon which the understanding rests when it believes it has found, out of the conception A a foreign predicate B, which it nevertheless considers to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle adduced annexes the two representations, cause and effect, to the representation existence, not only with universality, which experience cannot give, but also with the expression of necessity, therefore completely à priori and from pure conceptions. Upon such synthetical, that is augmentative propositions, depends the whole aim of our speculative knowledge à priori; for although analytical judgements are indeed highly important and necessary, they are so, only to arrive at that clearness of conceptions which is requisite for a sure and extended synthesis, and this alone is a real acquisition.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ed338e8ab27dd14dd60d70fdf71c9576eb54ac70aaaf38949db36cd6c330ce59","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_VATS","text":"### V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.\n\n1. Mathematical judgements are always synthetical. Hitherto this fact, though incontestably true and very important in its consequences, seems to have escaped the analysts of the human mind, nay, to be in complete opposition to all their conjectures. For as it was found that mathematical conclusions all proceed according to the principle of contradiction (which the nature of every apodeictic certainty requires), people became persuaded that the fundamental principles of the science also were recognized and admitted in the same way. But the notion is fallacious; for although a synthetical proposition can certainly be discerned by means of the principle of contradiction, this is possible only when another synthetical proposition precedes, from which the latter is deduced, but never of itself.\n\nBefore all, be it observed, that proper mathematical propositions are always judgements à priori, and not empirical, because they carry along with them the conception of necessity, which cannot be given by experience. If this be demurred to, it matters not; I will then limit my assertion to pure mathematics, the very conception of which implies that it consists of knowledge altogether non-empirical and à priori.\n\nWe might, indeed at first suppose that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is a merely analytical proposition, following (according to the principle of contradiction) from the conception of a sum of seven and five. But if we regard it more narrowly, we find that our conception of the sum of seven and five contains nothing more than the uniting of both sums into one, whereby it cannot at all be cogitated what this single number is which embraces both. The conception of twelve is by no means obtained by merely cogitating the union of seven and five; and we may analyse our conception of such a possible sum as long as we will, still we shall never discover in it the notion of twelve. We must go beyond these conceptions, and have recourse to an intuition which corresponds to one of the two—our five fingers, for example, or like Segner in his Arithmetic five points, and so by degrees, add the units contained in the five given in the intuition, to the conception of seven. For I first take the number 7, and, for the conception of 5 calling in the aid of the fingers of my hand as objects of intuition, I add the units, which I before took together to make up the number 5, gradually now by means of the material image my hand, to the number 7, and by this process, I at length see the number 12 arise. That 7 should be added to 5, I have certainly cogitated in my conception of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum was equal to 12. Arithmetical propositions are therefore always synthetical, of which we may become more clearly convinced by trying large numbers. For it will thus become quite evident that, turn and twist our conceptions as we may, it is impossible, without having recourse to intuition, to arrive at the sum total or product by means of the mere analysis of our conceptions. Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytical. “A straight line between two points is the shortest,” is a synthetical proposition. For my conception of straight contains no notion of quantity, but is merely qualitative. The conception of the shortest is therefore fore wholly an addition, and by no analysis can it be extracted from our conception of a straight line. Intuition must therefore here lend its aid, by means of which, and thus only, our synthesis is possible.\n\nSome few principles preposited by geometricians are, indeed, really analytical, and depend on the principle of contradiction. They serve, however, like identical propositions, as links in the chain of method, not as principles—for example, a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a+b) —> a, the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these principles themselves, though they derive their validity from pure conceptions, are only admitted in mathematics because they can be presented in intuition. What causes us here commonly to believe that the predicate of such apodeictic judgements is already contained in our conception, and that the judgement is therefore analytical, is merely the equivocal nature of the expression. We must join in thought a certain predicate to a given conception, and this necessity cleaves already to the conception. But the question is, not what we must join in thought to the given conception, but what we really think therein, though only obscurely, and then it becomes manifest that the predicate pertains to these conceptions, necessarily indeed, yet not as thought in the conception itself, but by virtue of an intuition, which must be added to the conception.\n\n2. The science of natural philosophy (physics) contains in itself synthetical judgements à priori, as principles. I shall adduce two propositions. For instance, the proposition, “In all changes of the material world, the quantity of matter remains unchanged”; or, that, “In all communication of motion, action and reaction must always be equal.” In both of these, not only is the necessity, and therefore their origin à priori clear, but also that they are synthetical propositions. For in the conception of matter, I do not cogitate its permanency, but merely its presence in space, which it fills. I therefore really go out of and beyond the conception of matter, in order to think on to it something à priori, which I did not think in it. The proposition is therefore not analytical, but synthetical, and nevertheless conceived à priori; and so it is with regard to the other propositions of the pure part of natural philosophy.\n\n3. As to metaphysics, even if we look upon it merely as an attempted science, yet, from the nature of human reason, an indispensable one, we find that it must contain synthetical propositions à priori. It is not merely the duty of metaphysics to dissect, and thereby analytically to illustrate the conceptions which we form à priori of things; but we seek to widen the range of our à priori knowledge. For this purpose, we must avail ourselves of such principles as add something to the original conception—something not identical with, nor contained in it, and by means of synthetical judgements à priori, leave far behind us the limits of experience; for example, in the proposition, “the world must have a beginning,” and such like. Thus metaphysics, according to the proper aim of the science, consists merely of synthetical propositions à priori.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","text":"### V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.\n\n1. Mathematical judgements are always synthetical. Hitherto this fact, though incontestably true and very important in its consequences, seems to have escaped the analysts of the human mind, nay, to be in complete opposition to all their conjectures. For as it was found that mathematical conclusions all proceed according to the principle of contradiction (which the nature of every apodeictic certainty requires), people became persuaded that the fundamental principles of the science also were recognized and admitted in the same way. But the notion is fallacious; for although a synthetical proposition can certainly be discerned by means of the principle of contradiction, this is possible only when another synthetical proposition precedes, from which the latter is deduced, but never of itself.\n\nBefore all, be it observed, that proper mathematical propositions are always judgements à priori, and not empirical, because they carry along with them the conception of necessity, which cannot be given by experience. If this be demurred to, it matters not; I will then limit my assertion to pure mathematics, the very conception of which implies that it consists of knowledge altogether non-empirical and à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","text":"We might, indeed at first suppose that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is a merely analytical proposition, following (according to the principle of contradiction) from the conception of a sum of seven and five. But if we regard it more narrowly, we find that our conception of the sum of seven and five contains nothing more than the uniting of both sums into one, whereby it cannot at all be cogitated what this single number is which embraces both. The conception of twelve is by no means obtained by merely cogitating the union of seven and five; and we may analyse our conception of such a possible sum as long as we will, still we shall never discover in it the notion of twelve. We must go beyond these conceptions, and have recourse to an intuition which corresponds to one of the two—our five fingers, for example, or like Segner in his Arithmetic five points, and so by degrees, add the units contained in the five given in the intuition, to the conception of seven. For I first take the number 7, and, for the conception of 5 calling in the aid of the fingers of my hand as objects of intuition, I add the units, which I before took together to make up the number 5, gradually now by means of the material image my hand, to the number 7, and by this process, I at length see the number 12 arise. That 7 should be added to 5, I have certainly cogitated in my conception of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum was equal to 12. Arithmetical propositions are therefore always synthetical, of which we may become more clearly convinced by trying large numbers. For it will thus become quite evident that, turn and twist our conceptions as we may, it is impossible, without having recourse to intuition, to arrive at the sum total or product by means of the mere analysis of our conceptions. Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytical. “A straight line between two points is the shortest,” is a synthetical proposition. For my conception of straight contains no notion of quantity, but is merely qualitative. The conception of the shortest is therefore fore wholly an addition, and by no analysis can it be extracted from our conception of a straight line. Intuition must therefore here lend its aid, by means of which, and thus only, our synthesis is possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0003","text":"Some few principles preposited by geometricians are, indeed, really analytical, and depend on the principle of contradiction. They serve, however, like identical propositions, as links in the chain of method, not as principles—for example, a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a+b) —> a, the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these principles themselves, though they derive their validity from pure conceptions, are only admitted in mathematics because they can be presented in intuition. What causes us here commonly to believe that the predicate of such apodeictic judgements is already contained in our conception, and that the judgement is therefore analytical, is merely the equivocal nature of the expression. We must join in thought a certain predicate to a given conception, and this necessity cleaves already to the conception. But the question is, not what we must join in thought to the given conception, but what we really think therein, though only obscurely, and then it becomes manifest that the predicate pertains to these conceptions, necessarily indeed, yet not as thought in the conception itself, but by virtue of an intuition, which must be added to the conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","text":"2. The science of natural philosophy (physics) contains in itself synthetical judgements à priori, as principles. I shall adduce two propositions. For instance, the proposition, “In all changes of the material world, the quantity of matter remains unchanged”; or, that, “In all communication of motion, action and reaction must always be equal.” In both of these, not only is the necessity, and therefore their origin à priori clear, but also that they are synthetical propositions. For in the conception of matter, I do not cogitate its permanency, but merely its presence in space, which it fills. I therefore really go out of and beyond the conception of matter, in order to think on to it something à priori, which I did not think in it. The proposition is therefore not analytical, but synthetical, and nevertheless conceived à priori; and so it is with regard to the other propositions of the pure part of natural philosophy.\n\n3. As to metaphysics, even if we look upon it merely as an attempted science, yet, from the nature of human reason, an indispensable one, we find that it must contain synthetical propositions à priori. It is not merely the duty of metaphysics to dissect, and thereby analytically to illustrate the conceptions which we form à priori of things; but we seek to widen the range of our à priori knowledge. For this purpose, we must avail ourselves of such principles as add something to the original conception—something not identical with, nor contained in it, and by means of synthetical judgements à priori, leave far behind us the limits of experience; for example, in the proposition, “the world must have a beginning,” and such like. Thus metaphysics, according to the proper aim of the science, consists merely of synthetical propositions à priori.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"248a2b7aa1eb6c0f0c47d90226b98c736451a976ee57bcca7322add9ac66cb5e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_VUPP","text":"### VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.\n\nIt is extremely advantageous to be able to bring a number of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For in this manner, we not only facilitate our own labour, inasmuch as we define it clearly to ourselves, but also render it more easy for others to decide whether we have done justice to our undertaking. The proper problem of pure reason, then, is contained in the question: “How are synthetical judgements à priori possible?”\n\nThat metaphysical science has hitherto remained in so vacillating a state of uncertainty and contradiction, is only to be attributed to the fact that this great problem, and perhaps even the difference between analytical and synthetical judgements, did not sooner suggest itself to philosophers. Upon the solution of this problem, or upon sufficient proof of the impossibility of synthetical knowledge à priori, depends the existence or downfall of the science of metaphysics. Among philosophers, David Hume came the nearest of all to this problem; yet it never acquired in his mind sufficient precision, nor did he regard the question in its universality. On the contrary, he stopped short at the synthetical proposition of the connection of an effect with its cause (principium causalitatis), insisting that such proposition à priori was impossible. According to his conclusions, then, all that we term metaphysical science is a mere delusion, arising from the fancied insight of reason into that which is in truth borrowed from experience, and to which habit has given the appearance of necessity. Against this assertion, destructive to all pure philosophy, he would have been guarded, had he had our problem before his eyes in its universality. For he would then have perceived that, according to his own argument, there likewise could not be any pure mathematical science, which assuredly cannot exist without synthetical propositions à priori—an absurdity from which his good understanding must have saved him.\n\nIn the solution of the above problem is at the same time comprehended the possibility of the use of pure reason in the foundation and construction of all sciences which contain theoretical knowledge à priori of objects, that is to say, the answer to the following questions:\n\nHow is pure mathematical science possible?\n\nHow is pure natural science possible?\n\nRespecting these sciences, as they do certainly exist, it may with propriety be asked, how they are possible?—for that they must be possible is shown by the fact of their really existing.[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-9) But as to metaphysics, the miserable progress it has hitherto made, and the fact that of no one system yet brought forward, far as regards its true aim, can it be said that this science really exists, leaves any one at liberty to doubt with reason the very possibility of its existence.\n\n[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-9) As to the existence of pure natural science, or physics, perhaps many may still express doubts. But we have only to look at the different propositions which are commonly treated of at the commencement of proper (empirical) physical science—those, for example, relating to the permanence of the same quantity of matter, the vis inertiae, the equality of action and reaction, etc.—to be soon convinced that they form a science of pure physics (physica pura, or rationalis), which well deserves to be separately exposed as a special science, in its whole extent, whether that be great or confined.\n\nYet, in a certain sense, this kind of knowledge must unquestionably be looked upon as given; in other words, metaphysics must be considered as really existing, if not as a science, nevertheless as a natural disposition of the human mind (metaphysica naturalis). For human reason, without any instigations imputable to the mere vanity of great knowledge, unceasingly progresses, urged on by its own feeling of need, towards such questions as cannot be answered by any empirical application of reason, or principles derived therefrom; and so there has ever really existed in every man some system of metaphysics. It will always exist, so soon as reason awakes to the exercise of its power of speculation. And now the question arises: “How is metaphysics, as a natural disposition, possible?” In other words, how, from the nature of universal human reason, do those questions arise which pure reason proposes to itself, and which it is impelled by its own feeling of need to answer as well as it can?\n\nBut as in all the attempts hitherto made to answer the questions which reason is prompted by its very nature to propose to itself, for example, whether the world had a beginning, or has existed from eternity, it has always met with unavoidable contradictions, we must not rest satisfied with the mere natural disposition of the mind to metaphysics, that is, with the existence of the faculty of pure reason, whence, indeed, some sort of metaphysical system always arises; but it must be possible to arrive at certainty in regard to the question whether we know or do not know the things of which metaphysics treats. We must be able to arrive at a decision on the subjects of its questions, or on the ability or inability of reason to form any judgement respecting them; and therefore either to extend with confidence the bounds of our pure reason, or to set strictly defined and safe limits to its action. This last question, which arises out of the above universal problem, would properly run thus: “How is metaphysics possible as a science?”\n\nThus, the critique of reason leads at last, naturally and necessarily, to science; and, on the other hand, the dogmatical use of reason without criticism leads to groundless assertions, against which others equally specious can always be set, thus ending unavoidably in scepticism.\n\nBesides, this science cannot be of great and formidable prolixity, because it has not to do with objects of reason, the variety of which is inexhaustible, but merely with Reason herself and her problems; problems which arise out of her own bosom, and are not proposed to her by the nature of outward things, but by her own nature. And when once Reason has previously become able completely to understand her own power in regard to objects which she meets with in experience, it will be easy to determine securely the extent and limits of her attempted application to objects beyond the confines of experience.\n\nWe may and must, therefore, regard the attempts hitherto made to establish metaphysical science dogmatically as non-existent. For what of analysis, that is, mere dissection of conceptions, is contained in one or other, is not the aim of, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, which has for its object the extension, by means of synthesis, of our à priori knowledge. And for this purpose, mere analysis is of course useless, because it only shows what is contained in these conceptions, but not how we arrive, à priori, at them; and this it is her duty to show, in order to be able afterwards to determine their valid use in regard to all objects of experience, to all knowledge in general. But little self-denial, indeed, is needed to give up these pretensions, seeing the undeniable, and in the dogmatic mode of procedure, inevitable contradictions of Reason with herself, have long since ruined the reputation of every system of metaphysics that has appeared up to this time. It will require more firmness to remain undeterred by difficulty from within, and opposition from without, from endeavouring, by a method quite opposed to all those hitherto followed, to further the growth and fruitfulness of a science indispensable to human reason—a science from which every branch it has borne may be cut away, but whose roots remain indestructible.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","text":"### VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.\n\nIt is extremely advantageous to be able to bring a number of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For in this manner, we not only facilitate our own labour, inasmuch as we define it clearly to ourselves, but also render it more easy for others to decide whether we have done justice to our undertaking. The proper problem of pure reason, then, is contained in the question: “How are synthetical judgements à priori possible?”\n\nThat metaphysical science has hitherto remained in so vacillating a state of uncertainty and contradiction, is only to be attributed to the fact that this great problem, and perhaps even the difference between analytical and synthetical judgements, did not sooner suggest itself to philosophers. Upon the solution of this problem, or upon sufficient proof of the impossibility of synthetical knowledge à priori, depends the existence or downfall of the science of metaphysics. Among philosophers, David Hume came the nearest of all to this problem; yet it never acquired in his mind sufficient precision, nor did he regard the question in its universality. On the contrary, he stopped short at the synthetical proposition of the connection of an effect with its cause (principium causalitatis), insisting that such proposition à priori was impossible. According to his conclusions, then, all that we term metaphysical science is a mere delusion, arising from the fancied insight of reason into that which is in truth borrowed from experience, and to which habit has given the appearance of necessity. Against this assertion, destructive to all pure philosophy, he would have been guarded, had he had our problem before his eyes in its universality. For he would then have perceived that, according to his own argument, there likewise could not be any pure mathematical science, which assuredly cannot exist without synthetical propositions à priori—an absurdity from which his good understanding must have saved him.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","text":"In the solution of the above problem is at the same time comprehended the possibility of the use of pure reason in the foundation and construction of all sciences which contain theoretical knowledge à priori of objects, that is to say, the answer to the following questions:\n\nHow is pure mathematical science possible?\n\nHow is pure natural science possible?\n\nRespecting these sciences, as they do certainly exist, it may with propriety be asked, how they are possible?—for that they must be possible is shown by the fact of their really existing.[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-9) But as to metaphysics, the miserable progress it has hitherto made, and the fact that of no one system yet brought forward, far as regards its true aim, can it be said that this science really exists, leaves any one at liberty to doubt with reason the very possibility of its existence.\n\n[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-9) As to the existence of pure natural science, or physics, perhaps many may still express doubts. But we have only to look at the different propositions which are commonly treated of at the commencement of proper (empirical) physical science—those, for example, relating to the permanence of the same quantity of matter, the vis inertiae, the equality of action and reaction, etc.—to be soon convinced that they form a science of pure physics (physica pura, or rationalis), which well deserves to be separately exposed as a special science, in its whole extent, whether that be great or confined.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","text":"Yet, in a certain sense, this kind of knowledge must unquestionably be looked upon as given; in other words, metaphysics must be considered as really existing, if not as a science, nevertheless as a natural disposition of the human mind (metaphysica naturalis). For human reason, without any instigations imputable to the mere vanity of great knowledge, unceasingly progresses, urged on by its own feeling of need, towards such questions as cannot be answered by any empirical application of reason, or principles derived therefrom; and so there has ever really existed in every man some system of metaphysics. It will always exist, so soon as reason awakes to the exercise of its power of speculation. And now the question arises: “How is metaphysics, as a natural disposition, possible?” In other words, how, from the nature of universal human reason, do those questions arise which pure reason proposes to itself, and which it is impelled by its own feeling of need to answer as well as it can?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","text":"But as in all the attempts hitherto made to answer the questions which reason is prompted by its very nature to propose to itself, for example, whether the world had a beginning, or has existed from eternity, it has always met with unavoidable contradictions, we must not rest satisfied with the mere natural disposition of the mind to metaphysics, that is, with the existence of the faculty of pure reason, whence, indeed, some sort of metaphysical system always arises; but it must be possible to arrive at certainty in regard to the question whether we know or do not know the things of which metaphysics treats. We must be able to arrive at a decision on the subjects of its questions, or on the ability or inability of reason to form any judgement respecting them; and therefore either to extend with confidence the bounds of our pure reason, or to set strictly defined and safe limits to its action. This last question, which arises out of the above universal problem, would properly run thus: “How is metaphysics possible as a science?”\n\nThus, the critique of reason leads at last, naturally and necessarily, to science; and, on the other hand, the dogmatical use of reason without criticism leads to groundless assertions, against which others equally specious can always be set, thus ending unavoidably in scepticism.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","text":"Besides, this science cannot be of great and formidable prolixity, because it has not to do with objects of reason, the variety of which is inexhaustible, but merely with Reason herself and her problems; problems which arise out of her own bosom, and are not proposed to her by the nature of outward things, but by her own nature. And when once Reason has previously become able completely to understand her own power in regard to objects which she meets with in experience, it will be easy to determine securely the extent and limits of her attempted application to objects beyond the confines of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","text":"We may and must, therefore, regard the attempts hitherto made to establish metaphysical science dogmatically as non-existent. For what of analysis, that is, mere dissection of conceptions, is contained in one or other, is not the aim of, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, which has for its object the extension, by means of synthesis, of our à priori knowledge. And for this purpose, mere analysis is of course useless, because it only shows what is contained in these conceptions, but not how we arrive, à priori, at them; and this it is her duty to show, in order to be able afterwards to determine their valid use in regard to all objects of experience, to all knowledge in general. But little self-denial, indeed, is needed to give up these pretensions, seeing the undeniable, and in the dogmatic mode of procedure, inevitable contradictions of Reason with herself, have long since ruined the reputation of every system of metaphysics that has appeared up to this time. It will require more firmness to remain undeterred by difficulty from within, and opposition from without, from endeavouring, by a method quite opposed to all those hitherto followed, to further the growth and fruitfulness of a science indispensable to human reason—a science from which every branch it has borne may be cut away, but whose roots remain indestructible.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"19219f9a535e1eb43e946ed168b00daa983e59dc42b83721c5cef681df341863","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_VIDP","text":"### VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.\n\nFrom all that has been said, there results the idea of a particular science, which may be called the Critique of Pure Reason. For reason is the faculty which furnishes us with the principles of knowledge à priori. Hence, pure reason is the faculty which contains the principles of cognizing anything absolutely à priori. An organon of pure reason would be a compendium of those principles according to which alone all pure cognitions à priori can be obtained. The completely extended application of such an organon would afford us a system of pure reason. As this, however, is demanding a great deal, and it is yet doubtful whether any extension of our knowledge be here possible, or, if so, in what cases; we can regard a science of the mere criticism of pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propædeutic to a system of pure reason. Such a science must not be called a doctrine, but only a critique of pure reason; and its use, in regard to speculation, would be only negative, not to enlarge the bounds of, but to purify, our reason, and to shield it against error—which alone is no little gain. I apply the term transcendental to all knowledge which is not so much occupied with objects as with the mode of our cognition of these objects, so far as this mode of cognition is possible à priori. A system of such conceptions would be called transcendental philosophy. But this, again, is still beyond the bounds of our present essay. For as such a science must contain a complete exposition not only of our synthetical à priori, but of our analytical à priori knowledge, it is of too wide a range for our present purpose, because we do not require to carry our analysis any farther than is necessary to understand, in their full extent, the principles of synthesis à priori, with which alone we have to do. This investigation, which we cannot properly call a doctrine, but only a transcendental critique, because it aims not at the enlargement, but at the correction and guidance, of our knowledge, and is to serve as a touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge à priori, is the sole object of our present essay. Such a critique is consequently, as far as possible, a preparation for an organon; and if this new organon should be found to fail, at least for a canon of pure reason, according to which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it extend or limit the bounds of that reason, might one day be set forth both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, nay, that such a system is not of so great extent as to preclude the hope of its ever being completed, is evident. For we have not here to do with the nature of outward objects, which is infinite, but solely with the mind, which judges of the nature of objects, and, again, with the mind only in respect of its cognition à priori. And the object of our investigations, as it is not to be sought without, but, altogether within, ourselves, cannot remain concealed, and in all probability is limited enough to be completely surveyed and fairly estimated, according to its worth or worthlessness. Still less let the reader here expect a critique of books and systems of pure reason; our present object is exclusively a critique of the faculty of pure reason itself. Only when we make this critique our foundation, do we possess a pure touchstone for estimating the philosophical value of ancient and modern writings on this subject; and without this criterion, the incompetent historian or judge decides upon and corrects the groundless assertions of others with his own, which have themselves just as little foundation.\n\nTranscendental philosophy is the idea of a science, for which the Critique of Pure Reason must sketch the whole plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with a full guarantee for the validity and stability of all the parts which enter into the building. It is the system of all the principles of pure reason. If this Critique itself does not assume the title of transcendental philosophy, it is only because, to be a complete system, it ought to contain a full analysis of all human knowledge à priori. Our critique must, indeed, lay before us a complete enumeration of all the radical conceptions which constitute the said pure knowledge. But from the complete analysis of these conceptions themselves, as also from a complete investigation of those derived from them, it abstains with reason; partly because it would be deviating from the end in view to occupy itself with this analysis, since this process is not attended with the difficulty and insecurity to be found in the synthesis, to which our critique is entirely devoted, and partly because it would be inconsistent with the unity of our plan to burden this essay with the vindication of the completeness of such an analysis and deduction, with which, after all, we have at present nothing to do. This completeness of the analysis of these radical conceptions, as well as of the deduction from the conceptions à priori which may be given by the analysis, we can, however, easily attain, provided only that we are in possession of all these radical conceptions, which are to serve as principles of the synthesis, and that in respect of this main purpose nothing is wanting.\n\nTo the Critique of Pure Reason, therefore, belongs all that constitutes transcendental philosophy; and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but still not the science itself; because it only proceeds so far with the analysis as is necessary to the power of judging completely of our synthetical knowledge à priori.\n\nThe principal thing we must attend to, in the division of the parts of a science like this, is that no conceptions must enter it which contain aught empirical; in other words, that the knowledge à priori must be completely pure. Hence, although the highest principles and fundamental conceptions of morality are certainly cognitions à priori, yet they do not belong to transcendental philosophy; because, though they certainly do not lay the conceptions of pain, pleasure, desires, inclinations, etc. (which are all of empirical origin), at the foundation of its precepts, yet still into the conception of duty—as an obstacle to be overcome, or as an incitement which should not be made into a motive—these empirical conceptions must necessarily enter, in the construction of a system of pure morality. Transcendental philosophy is consequently a philosophy of the pure and merely speculative reason. For all that is practical, so far as it contains motives, relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of cognition.\n\nIf we wish to divide this science from the universal point of view of a science in general, it ought to comprehend, first, a Doctrine of the Elements, and, secondly, a Doctrine of the Method of pure reason. Each of these main divisions will have its subdivisions, the separate reasons for which we cannot here particularize. Only so much seems necessary, by way of introduction of premonition, that there are two sources of human knowledge (which probably spring from a common, but to us unknown root), namely, sense and understanding. By the former, objects are given to us; by the latter, thought. So far as the faculty of sense may contain representations à priori, which form the conditions under which objects are given, in so far it belongs to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense must form the first part of our science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given must precede those under which they are thought.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","text":"### VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.\n\nFrom all that has been said, there results the idea of a particular science, which may be called the Critique of Pure Reason. For reason is the faculty which furnishes us with the principles of knowledge à priori. Hence, pure reason is the faculty which contains the principles of cognizing anything absolutely à priori. An organon of pure reason would be a compendium of those principles according to which alone all pure cognitions à priori can be obtained. The completely extended application of such an organon would afford us a system of pure reason. As this, however, is demanding a great deal, and it is yet doubtful whether any extension of our knowledge be here possible, or, if so, in what cases; we can regard a science of the mere criticism of pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propædeutic to a system of pure reason. Such a science must not be called a doctrine, but only a critique of pure reason; and its use, in regard to speculation, would be only negative, not to enlarge the bounds of, but to purify, our reason, and to shield it against error—which alone is no little gain. I apply the term transcendental to all knowledge which is not so much occupied with objects as with the mode of our cognition of these objects, so far as this mode of cognition is possible à priori. A system of such conceptions would be called transcendental philosophy. But this, again, is still beyond the bounds of our present essay.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","text":"For as such a science must contain a complete exposition not only of our synthetical à priori, but of our analytical à priori knowledge, it is of too wide a range for our present purpose, because we do not require to carry our analysis any farther than is necessary to understand, in their full extent, the principles of synthesis à priori, with which alone we have to do. This investigation, which we cannot properly call a doctrine, but only a transcendental critique, because it aims not at the enlargement, but at the correction and guidance, of our knowledge, and is to serve as a touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge à priori, is the sole object of our present essay. Such a critique is consequently, as far as possible, a preparation for an organon; and if this new organon should be found to fail, at least for a canon of pure reason, according to which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it extend or limit the bounds of that reason, might one day be set forth both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, nay, that such a system is not of so great extent as to preclude the hope of its ever being completed, is evident. For we have not here to do with the nature of outward objects, which is infinite, but solely with the mind, which judges of the nature of objects, and, again, with the mind only in respect of its cognition à priori. And the object of our investigations, as it is not to be sought without, but, altogether within, ourselves, cannot remain concealed, and in all probability is limited enough to be completely surveyed and fairly estimated, according to its worth or worthlessness.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","text":"Still less let the reader here expect a critique of books and systems of pure reason; our present object is exclusively a critique of the faculty of pure reason itself. Only when we make this critique our foundation, do we possess a pure touchstone for estimating the philosophical value of ancient and modern writings on this subject; and without this criterion, the incompetent historian or judge decides upon and corrects the groundless assertions of others with his own, which have themselves just as little foundation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","text":"Transcendental philosophy is the idea of a science, for which the Critique of Pure Reason must sketch the whole plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with a full guarantee for the validity and stability of all the parts which enter into the building. It is the system of all the principles of pure reason. If this Critique itself does not assume the title of transcendental philosophy, it is only because, to be a complete system, it ought to contain a full analysis of all human knowledge à priori. Our critique must, indeed, lay before us a complete enumeration of all the radical conceptions which constitute the said pure knowledge. But from the complete analysis of these conceptions themselves, as also from a complete investigation of those derived from them, it abstains with reason; partly because it would be deviating from the end in view to occupy itself with this analysis, since this process is not attended with the difficulty and insecurity to be found in the synthesis, to which our critique is entirely devoted, and partly because it would be inconsistent with the unity of our plan to burden this essay with the vindication of the completeness of such an analysis and deduction, with which, after all, we have at present nothing to do. This completeness of the analysis of these radical conceptions, as well as of the deduction from the conceptions à priori which may be given by the analysis, we can, however, easily attain, provided only that we are in possession of all these radical conceptions, which are to serve as principles of the synthesis, and that in respect of this main purpose nothing is wanting.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","text":"To the Critique of Pure Reason, therefore, belongs all that constitutes transcendental philosophy; and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but still not the science itself; because it only proceeds so far with the analysis as is necessary to the power of judging completely of our synthetical knowledge à priori.\n\nThe principal thing we must attend to, in the division of the parts of a science like this, is that no conceptions must enter it which contain aught empirical; in other words, that the knowledge à priori must be completely pure. Hence, although the highest principles and fundamental conceptions of morality are certainly cognitions à priori, yet they do not belong to transcendental philosophy; because, though they certainly do not lay the conceptions of pain, pleasure, desires, inclinations, etc. (which are all of empirical origin), at the foundation of its precepts, yet still into the conception of duty—as an obstacle to be overcome, or as an incitement which should not be made into a motive—these empirical conceptions must necessarily enter, in the construction of a system of pure morality. Transcendental philosophy is consequently a philosophy of the pure and merely speculative reason. For all that is practical, so far as it contains motives, relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","text":"If we wish to divide this science from the universal point of view of a science in general, it ought to comprehend, first, a Doctrine of the Elements, and, secondly, a Doctrine of the Method of pure reason. Each of these main divisions will have its subdivisions, the separate reasons for which we cannot here particularize. Only so much seems necessary, by way of introduction of premonition, that there are two sources of human knowledge (which probably spring from a common, but to us unknown root), namely, sense and understanding. By the former, objects are given to us; by the latter, thought. So far as the faculty of sense may contain representations à priori, which form the conditions under which objects are given, in so far it belongs to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense must form the first part of our science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given must precede those under which they are thought.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"78be5cf4ffc44a178346df21ce30ef78bd52610218e2b112459e8e3986ca4304","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT","title":"§ I. Introductory.","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IINT","text":"## I. TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF ELEMENTS.\n\n### FIRST PART. TRANSCENDENTAL ÆSTHETIC.\n\n### § I. Introductory.\n\nIn whatsoever mode, or by whatsoever means, our knowledge may relate to objects, it is at least quite clear that the only manner in which it immediately relates to them is by means of an intuition. To this as the indispensable groundwork, all thought points. But an intuition can take place only in so far as the object is given to us. This, again, is only possible, to man at least, on condition that the object affect the mind in a certain manner. The capacity for receiving representations (receptivity) through the mode in which we are affected by objects, objects, is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone furnishes us with intuitions; by the understanding they are thought, and from it arise conceptions. But an thought must directly, or indirectly, by means of certain signs, relate ultimately to intuitions; consequently, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.\n\nThe effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by the said object, is sensation. That sort of intuition which relates to an object by means of sensation is called an empirical intuition. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called phenomenon. That which in the phenomenon corresponds to the sensation, I term its matter; but that which effects that the content of the phenomenon can be arranged under certain relations, I call its form. But that in which our sensations are merely arranged, and by which they are susceptible of assuming a certain form, cannot be itself sensation. It is, then, the matter of all phenomena that is given to us à posteriori; the form must lie ready à priori for them in the mind, and consequently can be regarded separately from all sensation.\n\nI call all representations pure, in the transcendental meaning of the word, wherein nothing is met with that belongs to sensation. And accordingly we find existing in the mind à priori, the pure form of sensuous intuitions in general, in which all the manifold content of the phenomenal world is arranged and viewed under certain relations. This pure form of sensibility I shall call pure intuition. Thus, if I take away from our representation of a body all that the understanding thinks as belonging to it, as substance, force, divisibility, etc., and also whatever belongs to sensation, as impenetrability, hardness, colour, etc.; yet there is still something left us from this empirical intuition, namely, extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which exists à priori in the mind, as a mere form of sensibility, and without any real object of the senses or any sensation.\n\nThe science of all the principles of sensibility à priori, I call transcendental æsthetic.[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-10) There must, then, be such a science forming the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contradistinction to that part which contains the principles of pure thought, and which is called transcendental logic.\n\n[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-10) The Germans are the only people who at present use this word to indicate what others call the critique of taste. At the foundation of this term lies the disappointed hope, which the eminent analyst, Baumgarten, conceived, of subjecting the criticism of the beautiful to principles of reason, and so of elevating its rules into a science. But his endeavours were vain. For the said rules or criteria are, in respect to their chief sources, merely empirical, consequently never can serve as determinate laws à priori, by which our judgement in matters of taste is to be directed. It is rather our judgement which forms the proper test as to the correctness of the principles. On this account it is advisable to give up the use of the term as designating the critique of taste, and to apply it solely to that doctrine, which is true science—the science of the laws of sensibility—and thus come nearer to the language and the sense of the ancients in their well-known division of the objects of cognition into aiotheta kai noeta, or to share it with speculative philosophy, and employ it partly in a transcendental, partly in a psychological signification.\n\nIn the science of transcendental æsthetic accordingly, we shall first isolate sensibility or the sensuous faculty, by separating from it all that is annexed to its perceptions by the conceptions of understanding, so that nothing be left but empirical intuition. In the next place we shall take away from this intuition all that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain but pure intuition, and the mere form of phenomena, which is all that the sensibility can afford à priori. From this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensuous intuition, as principles of knowledge à priori, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we shall now proceed.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","text":"## I. TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF ELEMENTS.\n\n### FIRST PART. TRANSCENDENTAL ÆSTHETIC.\n\n### § I. Introductory.\n\nIn whatsoever mode, or by whatsoever means, our knowledge may relate to objects, it is at least quite clear that the only manner in which it immediately relates to them is by means of an intuition. To this as the indispensable groundwork, all thought points. But an intuition can take place only in so far as the object is given to us. This, again, is only possible, to man at least, on condition that the object affect the mind in a certain manner. The capacity for receiving representations (receptivity) through the mode in which we are affected by objects, objects, is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone furnishes us with intuitions; by the understanding they are thought, and from it arise conceptions. But an thought must directly, or indirectly, by means of certain signs, relate ultimately to intuitions; consequently, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","text":"The effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by the said object, is sensation. That sort of intuition which relates to an object by means of sensation is called an empirical intuition. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called phenomenon. That which in the phenomenon corresponds to the sensation, I term its matter; but that which effects that the content of the phenomenon can be arranged under certain relations, I call its form. But that in which our sensations are merely arranged, and by which they are susceptible of assuming a certain form, cannot be itself sensation. It is, then, the matter of all phenomena that is given to us à posteriori; the form must lie ready à priori for them in the mind, and consequently can be regarded separately from all sensation.\n\nI call all representations pure, in the transcendental meaning of the word, wherein nothing is met with that belongs to sensation. And accordingly we find existing in the mind à priori, the pure form of sensuous intuitions in general, in which all the manifold content of the phenomenal world is arranged and viewed under certain relations. This pure form of sensibility I shall call pure intuition. Thus, if I take away from our representation of a body all that the understanding thinks as belonging to it, as substance, force, divisibility, etc., and also whatever belongs to sensation, as impenetrability, hardness, colour, etc.; yet there is still something left us from this empirical intuition, namely, extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which exists à priori in the mind, as a mere form of sensibility, and without any real object of the senses or any sensation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","text":"The science of all the principles of sensibility à priori, I call transcendental æsthetic.[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-10) There must, then, be such a science forming the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contradistinction to that part which contains the principles of pure thought, and which is called transcendental logic.\n\n[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-10) The Germans are the only people who at present use this word to indicate what others call the critique of taste. At the foundation of this term lies the disappointed hope, which the eminent analyst, Baumgarten, conceived, of subjecting the criticism of the beautiful to principles of reason, and so of elevating its rules into a science. But his endeavours were vain. For the said rules or criteria are, in respect to their chief sources, merely empirical, consequently never can serve as determinate laws à priori, by which our judgement in matters of taste is to be directed. It is rather our judgement which forms the proper test as to the correctness of the principles. On this account it is advisable to give up the use of the term as designating the critique of taste, and to apply it solely to that doctrine, which is true science—the science of the laws of sensibility—and thus come nearer to the language and the sense of the ancients in their well-known division of the objects of cognition into aiotheta kai noeta, or to share it with speculative philosophy, and employ it partly in a transcendental, partly in a psychological signification.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","text":"In the science of transcendental æsthetic accordingly, we shall first isolate sensibility or the sensuous faculty, by separating from it all that is annexed to its perceptions by the conceptions of understanding, so that nothing be left but empirical intuition. In the next place we shall take away from this intuition all that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain but pure intuition, and the mere form of phenomena, which is all that the sensibility can afford à priori. From this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensuous intuition, as principles of knowledge à priori, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we shall now proceed.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3a8ddc8d60ea99e3c80f6aa2a979988b50bff6cd8bf0e9f783bd4a4f989fa05b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ I. Introductory.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_2MET","text":"### SECTION I. Of Space.\n\n### § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\nBy means of the external sense (a property of the mind), we represent to ourselves objects as without us, and these all in space. Herein alone are their shape, dimensions, and relations to each other determined or determinable. The internal sense, by means of which the mind contemplates itself or its internal state, gives, indeed, no intuition of the soul as an object; yet there is nevertheless a determinate form, under which alone the contemplation of our internal state is possible, so that all which relates to the inward determinations of the mind is represented in relations of time. Of time we cannot have any external intuition, any more than we can have an internal intuition of space. What then are time and space? Are they real existences? Or, are they merely relations or determinations of things, such, however, as would equally belong to these things in themselves, though they should never become objects of intuition; or, are they such as belong only to the form of intuition, and consequently to the subjective constitution of the mind, without which these predicates of time and space could not be attached to any object? In order to become informed on these points, we shall first give an exposition of the conception of space. By exposition, I mean the clear, though not detailed, representation of that which belongs to a conception; and an exposition is metaphysical when it contains that which represents the conception as given à priori.\n\n1. Space is not a conception which has been derived from outward experiences. For, in order that certain sensations may relate to something without me (that is, to something which occupies a different part of space from that in which I am); in like manner, in order that I may represent them not merely as without, of, and near to each other, but also in separate places, the representation of space must already exist as a foundation. Consequently, the representation of space cannot be borrowed from the relations of external phenomena through experience; but, on the contrary, this external experience is itself only possible through the said antecedent representation.\n\n2. Space then is a necessary representation à priori, which serves for the foundation of all external intuitions. We never can imagine or make a representation to ourselves of the non-existence of space, though we may easily enough think that no objects are found in it. It must, therefore, be considered as the condition of the possibility of phenomena, and by no means as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation à priori, which necessarily supplies the basis for external phenomena.\n\n3. Space is no discursive, or as we say, general conception of the relations of things, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can only represent to ourselves one space, and, when we talk of divers spaces, we mean only parts of one and the same space. Moreover, these parts cannot antecede this one all-embracing space, as the component parts from which the aggregate can be made up, but can be cogitated only as existing in it. Space is essentially one, and multiplicity in it, consequently the general notion of spaces, of this or that space, depends solely upon limitations. Hence it follows that an à priori intuition (which is not empirical) lies at the root of all our conceptions of space. Thus, moreover, the principles of geometry—for example, that “in a triangle, two sides together are greater than the third,” are never deduced from general conceptions of line and triangle, but from intuition, and this à priori, with apodeictic certainty.\n\n4. Space is represented as an infinite given quantity. Now every conception must indeed be considered as a representation which is contained in an infinite multitude of different possible representations, which, therefore, comprises these under itself; but no conception, as such, can be so conceived, as if it contained within itself an infinite multitude of representations. Nevertheless, space is so conceived of, for all parts of space are equally capable of being produced to infinity. Consequently, the original representation of space is an intuition à priori, and not a conception.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","text":"### SECTION I. Of Space.\n\n### § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\nBy means of the external sense (a property of the mind), we represent to ourselves objects as without us, and these all in space. Herein alone are their shape, dimensions, and relations to each other determined or determinable. The internal sense, by means of which the mind contemplates itself or its internal state, gives, indeed, no intuition of the soul as an object; yet there is nevertheless a determinate form, under which alone the contemplation of our internal state is possible, so that all which relates to the inward determinations of the mind is represented in relations of time. Of time we cannot have any external intuition, any more than we can have an internal intuition of space. What then are time and space? Are they real existences? Or, are they merely relations or determinations of things, such, however, as would equally belong to these things in themselves, though they should never become objects of intuition; or, are they such as belong only to the form of intuition, and consequently to the subjective constitution of the mind, without which these predicates of time and space could not be attached to any object? In order to become informed on these points, we shall first give an exposition of the conception of space. By exposition, I mean the clear, though not detailed, representation of that which belongs to a conception; and an exposition is metaphysical when it contains that which represents the conception as given à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","text":"1. Space is not a conception which has been derived from outward experiences. For, in order that certain sensations may relate to something without me (that is, to something which occupies a different part of space from that in which I am); in like manner, in order that I may represent them not merely as without, of, and near to each other, but also in separate places, the representation of space must already exist as a foundation. Consequently, the representation of space cannot be borrowed from the relations of external phenomena through experience; but, on the contrary, this external experience is itself only possible through the said antecedent representation.\n\n2. Space then is a necessary representation à priori, which serves for the foundation of all external intuitions. We never can imagine or make a representation to ourselves of the non-existence of space, though we may easily enough think that no objects are found in it. It must, therefore, be considered as the condition of the possibility of phenomena, and by no means as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation à priori, which necessarily supplies the basis for external phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","text":"3. Space is no discursive, or as we say, general conception of the relations of things, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can only represent to ourselves one space, and, when we talk of divers spaces, we mean only parts of one and the same space. Moreover, these parts cannot antecede this one all-embracing space, as the component parts from which the aggregate can be made up, but can be cogitated only as existing in it. Space is essentially one, and multiplicity in it, consequently the general notion of spaces, of this or that space, depends solely upon limitations. Hence it follows that an à priori intuition (which is not empirical) lies at the root of all our conceptions of space. Thus, moreover, the principles of geometry—for example, that “in a triangle, two sides together are greater than the third,” are never deduced from general conceptions of line and triangle, but from intuition, and this à priori, with apodeictic certainty.\n\n4. Space is represented as an infinite given quantity. Now every conception must indeed be considered as a representation which is contained in an infinite multitude of different possible representations, which, therefore, comprises these under itself; but no conception, as such, can be so conceived, as if it contained within itself an infinite multitude of representations. Nevertheless, space is so conceived of, for all parts of space are equally capable of being produced to infinity. Consequently, the original representation of space is an intuition à priori, and not a conception.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"813671d135a25f602bb5418657e2fbad52c65644fa5cfcd8088d787f1bfb7f63","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_3TEC","text":"### § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.\n\nBy a transcendental exposition, I mean the explanation of a conception, as a principle, whence can be discerned the possibility of other synthetical à priori cognitions. For this purpose, it is requisite, firstly, that such cognitions do really flow from the given conception; and, secondly, that the said cognitions are only possible under the presupposition of a given mode of explaining this conception.\n\nGeometry is a science which determines the properties of space synthetically, and yet à priori. What, then, must be our representation of space, in order that such a cognition of it may be possible? It must be originally intuition, for from a mere conception, no propositions can be deduced which go out beyond the conception, and yet this happens in geometry. (Introd. V.) But this intuition must be found in the mind à priori, that is, before any perception of objects, consequently must be pure, not empirical, intuition. For geometrical principles are always apodeictic, that is, united with the consciousness of their necessity, as: “Space has only three dimensions.” But propositions of this kind cannot be empirical judgements, nor conclusions from them. (Introd. II.) Now, how can an external intuition anterior to objects themselves, and in which our conception of objects can be determined à priori, exist in the human mind? Obviously not otherwise than in so far as it has its seat in the subject only, as the formal capacity of the subject’s being affected by objects, and thereby of obtaining immediate representation, that is, intuition; consequently, only as the form of the external sense in general.\n\nThus it is only by means of our explanation that the possibility of geometry, as a synthetical science à priori, becomes comprehensible. Every mode of explanation which does not show us this possibility, although in appearance it may be similar to ours, can with the utmost certainty be distinguished from it by these marks.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","text":"### § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.\n\nBy a transcendental exposition, I mean the explanation of a conception, as a principle, whence can be discerned the possibility of other synthetical à priori cognitions. For this purpose, it is requisite, firstly, that such cognitions do really flow from the given conception; and, secondly, that the said cognitions are only possible under the presupposition of a given mode of explaining this conception.\n\nGeometry is a science which determines the properties of space synthetically, and yet à priori. What, then, must be our representation of space, in order that such a cognition of it may be possible? It must be originally intuition, for from a mere conception, no propositions can be deduced which go out beyond the conception, and yet this happens in geometry. (Introd. V.) But this intuition must be found in the mind à priori, that is, before any perception of objects, consequently must be pure, not empirical, intuition. For geometrical principles are always apodeictic, that is, united with the consciousness of their necessity, as: “Space has only three dimensions.” But propositions of this kind cannot be empirical judgements, nor conclusions from them. (Introd. II.) Now, how can an external intuition anterior to objects themselves, and in which our conception of objects can be determined à priori, exist in the human mind? Obviously not otherwise than in so far as it has its seat in the subject only, as the formal capacity of the subject’s being affected by objects, and thereby of obtaining immediate representation, that is, intuition; consequently, only as the form of the external sense in general.\n\nThus it is only by means of our explanation that the possibility of geometry, as a synthetical science à priori, becomes comprehensible. Every mode of explanation which does not show us this possibility, although in appearance it may be similar to ours, can with the utmost certainty be distinguished from it by these marks.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"35e4f00a8947d50bddb9a3fdaf526208b6848b273c440d522f345b781a7a7bec","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_4CFC","text":"### § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.\n\n(a) Space does not represent any property of objects as things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relations to each other; in other words, space does not represent to us any determination of objects such as attaches to the objects themselves, and would remain, even though all subjective conditions of the intuition were abstracted. For neither absolute nor relative determinations of objects can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore not à priori.\n\n(b) Space is nothing else than the form of all phenomena of the external sense, that is, the subjective condition of the sensibility, under which alone external intuition is possible. Now, because the receptivity or capacity of the subject to be affected by objects necessarily antecedes all intuitions of these objects, it is easily understood how the form of all phenomena can be given in the mind previous to all actual perceptions, therefore à priori, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of the relations of these objects prior to all experience.\n\nIt is therefore from the human point of view only that we can speak of space, extended objects, etc. If we depart from the subjective condition, under which alone we can obtain external intuition, or, in other words, by means of which we are affected by objects, the representation of space has no meaning whatsoever. This predicate is only applicable to things in so far as they appear to us, that is, are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects can be intuited as existing without us, and when abstraction of these objects is made, is a pure intuition, to which we give the name of space. It is clear that we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of the possibility of their existence as far as they are phenomena. And so we may correctly say that space contains all which can appear to us externally, but not all things considered as things in themselves, be they intuited or not, or by whatsoever subject one will. As to the intuitions of other thinking beings, we cannot judge whether they are or are not bound by the same conditions which limit our own intuition, and which for us are universally valid. If we join the limitation of a judgement to the conception of the subject, then the judgement will possess unconditioned validity. For example, the proposition, “All objects are beside each other in space,” is valid only under the limitation that these things are taken as objects of our sensuous intuition. But if I join the condition to the conception and say, “All things, as external phenomena, are beside each other in space,” then the rule is valid universally, and without any limitation. Our expositions, consequently, teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space in regard of all which can be presented to us externally as object, and at the same time also the ideality of space in regard to objects when they are considered by means of reason as things in themselves, that is, without reference to the constitution of our sensibility. We maintain, therefore, the empirical reality of space in regard to all possible external experience, although we must admit its transcendental ideality; in other words, that it is nothing, so soon as we withdraw the condition upon which the possibility of all experience depends and look upon space as something that belongs to things in themselves.\n\nBut, with the exception of space, there is no representation, subjective and referring to something external to us, which could be called objective à priori. For there are no other subjective representations from which we can deduce synthetical propositions à priori, as we can from the intuition of space. (See § 3.) Therefore, to speak accurately, no ideality whatever belongs to these, although they agree in this respect with the representation of space, that they belong merely to the subjective nature of the mode of sensuous perception; such a mode, for example, as that of sight, of hearing, and of feeling, by means of the sensations of colour, sound, and heat, but which, because they are only sensations and not intuitions, do not of themselves give us the cognition of any object, least of all, an à priori cognition. My purpose, in the above remark, is merely this: to guard any one against illustrating the asserted ideality of space by examples quite insufficient, for example, by colour, taste, etc.; for these must be contemplated not as properties of things, but only as changes in the subject, changes which may be different in different men. For, in such a case, that which is originally a mere phenomenon, a rose, for example, is taken by the empirical understanding for a thing in itself, though to every different eye, in respect of its colour, it may appear different. On the contrary, the transcendental conception of phenomena in space is a critical admonition, that, in general, nothing which is intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form which belongs as a property to things; but that objects are quite unknown to us in themselves, and what we call outward objects, are nothing else but mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose real correlate, the thing in itself, is not known by means of these representations, nor ever can be, but respecting which, in experience, no inquiry is ever made.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","text":"### § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.\n\n(a) Space does not represent any property of objects as things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relations to each other; in other words, space does not represent to us any determination of objects such as attaches to the objects themselves, and would remain, even though all subjective conditions of the intuition were abstracted. For neither absolute nor relative determinations of objects can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore not à priori.\n\n(b) Space is nothing else than the form of all phenomena of the external sense, that is, the subjective condition of the sensibility, under which alone external intuition is possible. Now, because the receptivity or capacity of the subject to be affected by objects necessarily antecedes all intuitions of these objects, it is easily understood how the form of all phenomena can be given in the mind previous to all actual perceptions, therefore à priori, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of the relations of these objects prior to all experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","text":"It is therefore from the human point of view only that we can speak of space, extended objects, etc. If we depart from the subjective condition, under which alone we can obtain external intuition, or, in other words, by means of which we are affected by objects, the representation of space has no meaning whatsoever. This predicate is only applicable to things in so far as they appear to us, that is, are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects can be intuited as existing without us, and when abstraction of these objects is made, is a pure intuition, to which we give the name of space. It is clear that we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of the possibility of their existence as far as they are phenomena. And so we may correctly say that space contains all which can appear to us externally, but not all things considered as things in themselves, be they intuited or not, or by whatsoever subject one will. As to the intuitions of other thinking beings, we cannot judge whether they are or are not bound by the same conditions which limit our own intuition, and which for us are universally valid. If we join the limitation of a judgement to the conception of the subject, then the judgement will possess unconditioned validity. For example, the proposition, “All objects are beside each other in space,” is valid only under the limitation that these things are taken as objects of our sensuous intuition. But if I join the condition to the conception and say, “All things, as external phenomena, are beside each other in space,” then the rule is valid universally, and without any limitation. Our expositions, consequently, teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space in regard of all which can be presented to us externally as object, and at the same time also the ideality of space in regard to objects when they are considered by means of reason as things in themselves, that is, without reference to the constitution of our sensibility. We maintain, therefore, the empirical reality of space in regard to all possible external experience, although we must admit its transcendental ideality; in other words, that it is nothing, so soon as we withdraw the condition upon which the possibility of all experience depends and look upon space as something that belongs to things in themselves.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","text":"But, with the exception of space, there is no representation, subjective and referring to something external to us, which could be called objective à priori. For there are no other subjective representations from which we can deduce synthetical propositions à priori, as we can from the intuition of space. (See § 3.) Therefore, to speak accurately, no ideality whatever belongs to these, although they agree in this respect with the representation of space, that they belong merely to the subjective nature of the mode of sensuous perception; such a mode, for example, as that of sight, of hearing, and of feeling, by means of the sensations of colour, sound, and heat, but which, because they are only sensations and not intuitions, do not of themselves give us the cognition of any object, least of all, an à priori cognition. My purpose, in the above remark, is merely this: to guard any one against illustrating the asserted ideality of space by examples quite insufficient, for example, by colour, taste, etc.; for these must be contemplated not as properties of things, but only as changes in the subject, changes which may be different in different men. For, in such a case, that which is originally a mere phenomenon, a rose, for example, is taken by the empirical understanding for a thing in itself, though to every different eye, in respect of its colour, it may appear different. On the contrary, the transcendental conception of phenomena in space is a critical admonition, that, in general, nothing which is intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form which belongs as a property to things; but that objects are quite unknown to us in themselves, and what we call outward objects, are nothing else but mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose real correlate, the thing in itself, is not known by means of these representations, nor ever can be, but respecting which, in experience, no inquiry is ever made.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4cb64eb032ebc7ba01a66504f8755892d1a140baee5ad898cf6d0422ef0d460f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_5MET","text":"### SECTION II. Of Time.\n\n### § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\n1. Time is not an empirical conception. For neither coexistence nor succession would be perceived by us, if the representation of time did not exist as a foundation à priori. Without this presupposition we could not represent to ourselves that things exist together at one and the same time, or at different times, that is, contemporaneously, or in succession.\n\n2. Time is a necessary representation, lying at the foundation of all our intuitions. With regard to phenomena in general, we cannot think away time from them, and represent them to ourselves as out of and unconnected with time, but we can quite well represent to ourselves time void of phenomena. Time is therefore given à priori. In it alone is all reality of phenomena possible. These may all be annihilated in thought, but time itself, as the universal condition of their possibility, cannot be so annulled.\n\n3. On this necessity à priori is also founded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general, such as: “Time has only one dimension,” “Different times are not coexistent but successive” (as different spaces are not successive but coexistent). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for it would give neither strict universality, nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say, “so common experience teaches us,” but not “it must be so.” They are valid as rules, through which, in general, experience is possible; and they instruct us respecting experience, and not by means of it.\n\n4. Time is not a discursive, or as it is called, general conception, but a pure form of the sensuous intuition. Different times are merely parts of one and the same time. But the representation which can only be given by a single object is an intuition. Besides, the proposition that different times cannot be coexistent could not be derived from a general conception. For this proposition is synthetical, and therefore cannot spring out of conceptions alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.\n\n5. The infinity of time signifies nothing more than that every determined quantity of time is possible only through limitations of one time lying at the foundation. Consequently, the original representation, time, must be given as unlimited. But as the determinate representation of the parts of time and of every quantity of an object can only be obtained by limitation, the complete representation of time must not be furnished by means of conceptions, for these contain only partial representations. Conceptions, on the contrary, must have immediate intuition for their basis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","text":"### SECTION II. Of Time.\n\n### § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\n1. Time is not an empirical conception. For neither coexistence nor succession would be perceived by us, if the representation of time did not exist as a foundation à priori. Without this presupposition we could not represent to ourselves that things exist together at one and the same time, or at different times, that is, contemporaneously, or in succession.\n\n2. Time is a necessary representation, lying at the foundation of all our intuitions. With regard to phenomena in general, we cannot think away time from them, and represent them to ourselves as out of and unconnected with time, but we can quite well represent to ourselves time void of phenomena. Time is therefore given à priori. In it alone is all reality of phenomena possible. These may all be annihilated in thought, but time itself, as the universal condition of their possibility, cannot be so annulled.\n\n3. On this necessity à priori is also founded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general, such as: “Time has only one dimension,” “Different times are not coexistent but successive” (as different spaces are not successive but coexistent). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for it would give neither strict universality, nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say, “so common experience teaches us,” but not “it must be so.” They are valid as rules, through which, in general, experience is possible; and they instruct us respecting experience, and not by means of it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","text":"4. Time is not a discursive, or as it is called, general conception, but a pure form of the sensuous intuition. Different times are merely parts of one and the same time. But the representation which can only be given by a single object is an intuition. Besides, the proposition that different times cannot be coexistent could not be derived from a general conception. For this proposition is synthetical, and therefore cannot spring out of conceptions alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.\n\n5. The infinity of time signifies nothing more than that every determined quantity of time is possible only through limitations of one time lying at the foundation. Consequently, the original representation, time, must be given as unlimited. But as the determinate representation of the parts of time and of every quantity of an object can only be obtained by limitation, the complete representation of time must not be furnished by means of conceptions, for these contain only partial representations. Conceptions, on the contrary, must have immediate intuition for their basis.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0ebc0f16c945ebf62b6b672c3beb24c274142839c1b69c395dbe39f5be78543a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_6TEC","text":"### § 6 Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.\n\nI may here refer to what is said above (§ 5, 3), where, for or sake of brevity, I have placed under the head of metaphysical exposition, that which is properly transcendental. Here I shall add that the conception of change, and with it the conception of motion, as change of place, is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an intuition (internal) à priori, no conception, of whatever kind, could render comprehensible the possibility of change, in other words, of a conjunction of contradictorily opposed predicates in one and the same object, for example, the presence of a thing in a place and the non-presence of the same thing in the same place. It is only in time that it is possible to meet with two contradictorily opposed determinations in one thing, that is, after each other. Thus our conception of time explains the possibility of so much synthetical knowledge à priori, as is exhibited in the general doctrine of motion, which is not a little fruitful.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","text":"### § 6 Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.\n\nI may here refer to what is said above (§ 5, 3), where, for or sake of brevity, I have placed under the head of metaphysical exposition, that which is properly transcendental. Here I shall add that the conception of change, and with it the conception of motion, as change of place, is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an intuition (internal) à priori, no conception, of whatever kind, could render comprehensible the possibility of change, in other words, of a conjunction of contradictorily opposed predicates in one and the same object, for example, the presence of a thing in a place and the non-presence of the same thing in the same place. It is only in time that it is possible to meet with two contradictorily opposed determinations in one thing, that is, after each other. Thus our conception of time explains the possibility of so much synthetical knowledge à priori, as is exhibited in the general doctrine of motion, which is not a little fruitful.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"61d70a97c531b00e9b4867491fddaba0ebbc06b74e65a218e069b63fc2759542","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_7CAC","text":"### § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.\n\n(a) Time is not something which subsists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and therefore remains, when abstraction is made of the subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the former case, it would be something real, yet without presenting to any power of perception any real object. In the latter case, as an order or determination inherent in things themselves, it could not be antecedent to things, as their condition, nor discerned or intuited by means of synthetical propositions à priori. But all this is quite possible when we regard time as merely the subjective condition under which all our intuitions take place. For in that case, this form of the inward intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and consequently à priori.\n\n(b) Time is nothing else than the form of the internal sense, that is, of the intuitions of self and of our internal state. For time cannot be any determination of outward phenomena. It has to do neither with shape nor position; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our internal state. And precisely because this internal intuition presents to us no shape or form, we endeavour to supply this want by analogies, and represent the course of time by a line progressing to infinity, the content of which constitutes a series which is only of one dimension; and we conclude from the properties of this line as to all the properties of time, with this single exception, that the parts of the line are coexistent, whilst those of time are successive. From this it is clear also that the representation of time is itself an intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an external intuition.\n\n(c) Time is the formal condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of external intuition, is limited as a condition à priori to external phenomena alone. On the other hand, because all representations, whether they have or have not external things for their objects, still in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to our internal state; and because this internal state is subject to the formal condition of the internal intuition, that is, to time—time is a condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever—the immediate condition of all internal, and thereby the mediate condition of all external phenomena. If I can say à priori, “All outward phenomena are in space, and determined à priori according to the relations of space,” I can also, from the principle of the internal sense, affirm universally, “All phenomena in general, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time and stand necessarily in relations of time.”\n\nIf we abstract our internal intuition of ourselves and all external intuitions, possible only by virtue of this internal intuition and presented to us by our faculty of representation, and consequently take objects as they are in themselves, then time is nothing. It is only of objective validity in regard to phenomena, because these are things which we regard as objects of our senses. It no longer objective we, make abstraction of the sensuousness of our intuition, in other words, of that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensuous, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, independently of the mind or subject, is nothing. Nevertheless, in respect of all phenomena, consequently of all things which come within the sphere of our experience, it is necessarily objective. We cannot say, “All things are in time,” because in this conception of things in general, we abstract and make no mention of any sort of intuition of things. But this is the proper condition under which time belongs to our representation of objects. If we add the condition to the conception, and say, “All things, as phenomena, that is, objects of sensuous intuition, are in time,” then the proposition has its sound objective validity and universality à priori.\n\nWhat we have now set forth teaches, therefore, the empirical reality of time; that is, its objective validity in reference to all objects which can ever be presented to our senses. And as our intuition is always sensuous, no object ever can be presented to us in experience, which does not come under the conditions of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality; that is, we deny that it, without having regard to the form of our sensuous intuition, absolutely inheres in things as a condition or property. Such properties as belong to objects as things in themselves never can be presented to us through the medium of the senses. Herein consists, therefore, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which, if we abstract the subjective conditions of sensuous intuition, it is nothing, and cannot be reckoned as subsisting or inhering in objects as things in themselves, independently of its relation to our intuition. This ideality, like that of space, is not to be proved or illustrated by fallacious analogies with sensations, for this reason—that in such arguments or illustrations, we make the presupposition that the phenomenon, in which such and such predicates inhere, has objective reality, while in this case we can only find such an objective reality as is itself empirical, that is, regards the object as a mere phenomenon. In reference to this subject, see the remark in Section I (§ 4)","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0001","text":"### § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0002","text":"(a) Time is not something which subsists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and therefore remains, when abstraction is made of the subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the former case, it would be something real, yet without presenting to any power of perception any real object. In the latter case, as an order or determination inherent in things themselves, it could not be antecedent to things, as their condition, nor discerned or intuited by means of synthetical propositions à priori. But all this is quite possible when we regard time as merely the subjective condition under which all our intuitions take place. For in that case, this form of the inward intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and consequently à priori.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0003","text":"(b) Time is nothing else than the form of the internal sense, that is, of the intuitions of self and of our internal state. For time cannot be any determination of outward phenomena. It has to do neither with shape nor position; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our internal state. And precisely because this internal intuition presents to us no shape or form, we endeavour to supply this want by analogies, and represent the course of time by a line progressing to infinity, the content of which constitutes a series which is only of one dimension; and we conclude from the properties of this line as to all the properties of time, with this single exception, that the parts of the line are coexistent, whilst those of time are successive. From this it is clear also that the representation of time is itself an intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an external intuition.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0004","text":"(c) Time is the formal condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of external intuition, is limited as a condition à priori to external phenomena alone. On the other hand, because all representations, whether they have or have not external things for their objects, still in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to our internal state; and because this internal state is subject to the formal condition of the internal intuition, that is, to time—time is a condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever—the immediate condition of all internal, and thereby the mediate condition of all external phenomena. If I can say à priori, “All outward phenomena are in space, and determined à priori according to the relations of space,” I can also, from the principle of the internal sense, affirm universally, “All phenomena in general, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time and stand necessarily in relations of time.”","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0005","text":"If we abstract our internal intuition of ourselves and all external intuitions, possible only by virtue of this internal intuition and presented to us by our faculty of representation, and consequently take objects as they are in themselves, then time is nothing. It is only of objective validity in regard to phenomena, because these are things which we regard as objects of our senses. It no longer objective we, make abstraction of the sensuousness of our intuition, in other words, of that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensuous, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, independently of the mind or subject, is nothing. Nevertheless, in respect of all phenomena, consequently of all things which come within the sphere of our experience, it is necessarily objective. We cannot say, “All things are in time,” because in this conception of things in general, we abstract and make no mention of any sort of intuition of things. But this is the proper condition under which time belongs to our representation of objects. If we add the condition to the conception, and say, “All things, as phenomena, that is, objects of sensuous intuition, are in time,” then the proposition has its sound objective validity and universality à priori.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_7CAC:DISPLAY-0006","text":"What we have now set forth teaches, therefore, the empirical reality of time; that is, its objective validity in reference to all objects which can ever be presented to our senses. And as our intuition is always sensuous, no object ever can be presented to us in experience, which does not come under the conditions of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality; that is, we deny that it, without having regard to the form of our sensuous intuition, absolutely inheres in things as a condition or property. Such properties as belong to objects as things in themselves never can be presented to us through the medium of the senses. Herein consists, therefore, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which, if we abstract the subjective conditions of sensuous intuition, it is nothing, and cannot be reckoned as subsisting or inhering in objects as things in themselves, independently of its relation to our intuition. This ideality, like that of space, is not to be proved or illustrated by fallacious analogies with sensations, for this reason—that in such arguments or illustrations, we make the presupposition that the phenomenon, in which such and such predicates inhere, has objective reality, while in this case we can only find such an objective reality as is itself empirical, that is, regards the object as a mere phenomenon. In reference to this subject, see the remark in Section I (§ 4)","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"f22890f5e4a3edf8b5c520d7c1b91e99859483055e7159e490bf9ebda36ea775","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"original_title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_8ELU","text":"### § 8. Elucidation.\n\nAgainst this theory, which grants empirical reality to time, but denies to it absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard from intelligent men an objection so unanimously urged that I conclude that it must naturally present itself to every reader to whom these considerations are novel. It runs thus: “Changes are real” (this the continual change in our own representations demonstrates, even though the existence of all external phenomena, together with their changes, is denied). Now, changes are only possible in time, and therefore time must be something real. But there is no difficulty in answering this. I grant the whole argument. Time, no doubt, is something real, that is, it is the real form of our internal intuition. It therefore has subjective reality, in reference to our internal experience, that is, I have really the representation of time and of my determinations therein. Time, therefore, is not to be regarded as an object, but as the mode of representation of myself as an object. But if I could intuite myself, or be intuited by another being, without this condition of sensibility, then those very determinations which we now represent to ourselves as changes, would present to us a knowledge in which the representation of time, and consequently of change, would not appear. The empirical reality of time, therefore, remains, as the condition of all our experience. But absolute reality, according to what has been said above, cannot be granted it. Time is nothing but the form of our internal intuition.[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-11) If we take away from it the special condition of our sensibility, the conception of time also vanishes; and it inheres not in the objects themselves, but solely in the subject (or mind) which intuites them.\n\n[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-11) I can indeed say “my representations follow one another, or are successive”; but this means only that we are conscious of them as in a succession, that is, according to the form of the internal sense. Time, therefore, is not a thing in itself, nor is it any objective determination pertaining to, or inherent in things.\n\nBut the reason why this objection is so unanimously brought against our doctrine of time, and that too by disputants who cannot start any intelligible arguments against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this—they have no hope of demonstrating apodeictically the absolute reality of space, because the doctrine of idealism is against them, according to which the reality of external objects is not capable of any strict proof. On the other hand, the reality of the object of our internal sense (that is, myself and my internal state) is clear immediately through consciousness. The former—external objects in space—might be a mere delusion, but the latter—the object of my internal perception—is undeniably real. They do not, however, reflect that both, without question of their reality as representations, belong only to the genus phenomenon, which has always two aspects, the one, the object considered as a thing in itself, without regard to the mode of intuiting it, and the nature of which remains for this very reason problematical, the other, the form of our intuition of the object, which must be sought not in the object as a thing in itself, but in the subject to which it appears—which form of intuition nevertheless belongs really and necessarily to the phenomenal object.\n\nTime and space are, therefore, two sources of knowledge, from which, à priori, various synthetical cognitions can be drawn. Of this we find a striking example in the cognitions of space and its relations, which form the foundation of pure mathematics. They are the two pure forms of all intuitions, and thereby make synthetical propositions à priori possible. But these sources of knowledge being merely conditions of our sensibility, do therefore, and as such, strictly determine their own range and purpose, in that they do not and cannot present objects as things in themselves, but are applicable to them solely in so far as they are considered as sensuous phenomena. The sphere of phenomena is the only sphere of their validity, and if we venture out of this, no further objective use can be made of them. For the rest, this formal reality of time and space leaves the validity of our empirical knowledge unshaken; for our certainty in that respect is equally firm, whether these forms necessarily inhere in the things themselves, or only in our intuitions of them. On the other hand, those who maintain the absolute reality of time and space, whether as essentially subsisting, or only inhering, as modifications, in things, must find themselves at utter variance with the principles of experience itself. For, if they decide for the first view, and make space and time into substances, this being the side taken by mathematical natural philosophers, they must admit two self-subsisting nonentities, infinite and eternal, which exist (yet without there being anything real) for the purpose of containing in themselves everything that is real. If they adopt the second view of inherence, which is preferred by some metaphysical natural philosophers, and regard space and time as relations (contiguity in space or succession in time), abstracted from experience, though represented confusedly in this state of separation, they find themselves in that case necessitated to deny the validity of mathematical doctrines à priori in reference to real things (for example, in space)—at all events their apodeictic certainty. For such certainty cannot be found in an à posteriori proposition; and the conceptions à priori of space and time are, according to this opinion, mere creations of the imagination, having their source really in experience, inasmuch as, out of relations abstracted from experience, imagination has made up something which contains, indeed, general statements of these relations, yet of which no application can be made without the restrictions attached thereto by nature. The former of these parties gains this advantage, that they keep the sphere of phenomena free for mathematical science. On the other hand, these very conditions (space and time) embarrass them greatly, when the understanding endeavours to pass the limits of that sphere. The latter has, indeed, this advantage, that the representations of space and time do not come in their way when they wish to judge of objects, not as phenomena, but merely in their relation to the understanding. Devoid, however, of a true and objectively valid à priori intuition, they can neither furnish any basis for the possibility of mathematical cognitions à priori, nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary accordance with those of mathematics. In our theory of the true nature of these two original forms of the sensibility, both difficulties are surmounted.\n\nIn conclusion, that transcendental æsthetic cannot contain any more than these two elements—space and time, is sufficiently obvious from the fact that all other conceptions appertaining to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites in itself both elements, presuppose something empirical. Motion, for example, presupposes the perception of something movable. But space considered in itself contains nothing movable, consequently motion must be something which is found in space only through experience—in other words, an empirical datum. In like manner, transcendental æsthetic cannot number the conception of change among its data à priori; for time itself does not change, but only something which is in time. To acquire the conception of change, therefore, the perception of some existing object and of the succession of its determinations, in one word, experience, is necessary.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","text":"### § 8. Elucidation.\n\nAgainst this theory, which grants empirical reality to time, but denies to it absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard from intelligent men an objection so unanimously urged that I conclude that it must naturally present itself to every reader to whom these considerations are novel. It runs thus: “Changes are real” (this the continual change in our own representations demonstrates, even though the existence of all external phenomena, together with their changes, is denied). Now, changes are only possible in time, and therefore time must be something real. But there is no difficulty in answering this. I grant the whole argument. Time, no doubt, is something real, that is, it is the real form of our internal intuition. It therefore has subjective reality, in reference to our internal experience, that is, I have really the representation of time and of my determinations therein. Time, therefore, is not to be regarded as an object, but as the mode of representation of myself as an object. But if I could intuite myself, or be intuited by another being, without this condition of sensibility, then those very determinations which we now represent to ourselves as changes, would present to us a knowledge in which the representation of time, and consequently of change, would not appear. The empirical reality of time, therefore, remains, as the condition of all our experience. But absolute reality, according to what has been said above, cannot be granted it. Time is nothing but the form of our internal intuition.[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-11) If we take away from it the special condition of our sensibility, the conception of time also vanishes; and it inheres not in the objects themselves, but solely in the subject (or mind) which intuites them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","text":"[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-11) I can indeed say “my representations follow one another, or are successive”; but this means only that we are conscious of them as in a succession, that is, according to the form of the internal sense. Time, therefore, is not a thing in itself, nor is it any objective determination pertaining to, or inherent in things.\n\nBut the reason why this objection is so unanimously brought against our doctrine of time, and that too by disputants who cannot start any intelligible arguments against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this—they have no hope of demonstrating apodeictically the absolute reality of space, because the doctrine of idealism is against them, according to which the reality of external objects is not capable of any strict proof. On the other hand, the reality of the object of our internal sense (that is, myself and my internal state) is clear immediately through consciousness. The former—external objects in space—might be a mere delusion, but the latter—the object of my internal perception—is undeniably real. They do not, however, reflect that both, without question of their reality as representations, belong only to the genus phenomenon, which has always two aspects, the one, the object considered as a thing in itself, without regard to the mode of intuiting it, and the nature of which remains for this very reason problematical, the other, the form of our intuition of the object, which must be sought not in the object as a thing in itself, but in the subject to which it appears—which form of intuition nevertheless belongs really and necessarily to the phenomenal object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","text":"Time and space are, therefore, two sources of knowledge, from which, à priori, various synthetical cognitions can be drawn. Of this we find a striking example in the cognitions of space and its relations, which form the foundation of pure mathematics. They are the two pure forms of all intuitions, and thereby make synthetical propositions à priori possible. But these sources of knowledge being merely conditions of our sensibility, do therefore, and as such, strictly determine their own range and purpose, in that they do not and cannot present objects as things in themselves, but are applicable to them solely in so far as they are considered as sensuous phenomena. The sphere of phenomena is the only sphere of their validity, and if we venture out of this, no further objective use can be made of them. For the rest, this formal reality of time and space leaves the validity of our empirical knowledge unshaken; for our certainty in that respect is equally firm, whether these forms necessarily inhere in the things themselves, or only in our intuitions of them. On the other hand, those who maintain the absolute reality of time and space, whether as essentially subsisting, or only inhering, as modifications, in things, must find themselves at utter variance with the principles of experience itself. For, if they decide for the first view, and make space and time into substances, this being the side taken by mathematical natural philosophers, they must admit two self-subsisting nonentities, infinite and eternal, which exist (yet without there being anything real) for the purpose of containing in themselves everything that is real. If they adopt the second view of inherence, which is preferred by some metaphysical natural philosophers, and regard space and time as relations (contiguity in space or succession in time), abstracted from experience, though represented confusedly in this state of separation, they find themselves in that case necessitated to deny the validity of mathematical doctrines à priori in reference to real things (for example, in space)—at all events their apodeictic certainty. For such certainty cannot be found in an à posteriori proposition; and the conceptions à priori of space and time are, according to this opinion, mere creations of the imagination, having their source really in experience, inasmuch as, out of relations abstracted from experience, imagination has made up something which contains, indeed, general statements of these relations, yet of which no application can be made without the restrictions attached thereto by nature. The former of these parties gains this advantage, that they keep the sphere of phenomena free for mathematical science. On the other hand, these very conditions (space and time) embarrass them greatly, when the understanding endeavours to pass the limits of that sphere. The latter has, indeed, this advantage, that the representations of space and time do not come in their way when they wish to judge of objects, not as phenomena, but merely in their relation to the understanding. Devoid, however, of a true and objectively valid à priori intuition, they can neither furnish any basis for the possibility of mathematical cognitions à priori, nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary accordance with those of mathematics. In our theory of the true nature of these two original forms of the sensibility, both difficulties are surmounted.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","text":"In conclusion, that transcendental æsthetic cannot contain any more than these two elements—space and time, is sufficiently obvious from the fact that all other conceptions appertaining to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites in itself both elements, presuppose something empirical. Motion, for example, presupposes the perception of something movable. But space considered in itself contains nothing movable, consequently motion must be something which is found in space only through experience—in other words, an empirical datum. In like manner, transcendental æsthetic cannot number the conception of change among its data à priori; for time itself does not change, but only something which is in time. To acquire the conception of change, therefore, the perception of some existing object and of the succession of its determinations, in one word, experience, is necessary.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"89abecbac410cc879bf7f6a57dbe17e00e7641d8487e0a6544320d89bc6a3c1d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_9GRT","text":"### § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nI. In order to prevent any misunderstanding, it will be requisite, in the first place, to recapitulate, as clearly as possible, what our opinion is with respect to the fundamental nature of our sensuous cognition in general. We have intended, then, to say that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of phenomena; that the things which we intuite, are not in themselves the same as our representations of them in intuition, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us; and that if we take away the subject, or even only the subjective constitution of our senses in general, then not only the nature and relations of objects in space and time, but even space and time themselves disappear; and that these, as phenomena, cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What may be the nature of objects considered as things in themselves and without reference to the receptivity of our sensibility is quite unknown to us. We know nothing more than our mode of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, and which, though not of necessity pertaining to every animated being, is so to the whole human race. With this alone we have to do. Space and time are the pure forms thereof; sensation the matter. The former alone can we cognize à priori, that is, antecedent to all actual perception; and for this reason such cognition is called pure intuition. The latter is that in our cognition which is called cognition à posteriori, that is, empirical intuition. The former appertain absolutely and necessarily to our sensibility, of whatsoever kind our sensations may be; the latter may be of very diversified character. Supposing that we should carry our empirical intuition even to the very highest degree of clearness, we should not thereby advance one step nearer to a knowledge of the constitution of objects as things in themselves. For we could only, at best, arrive at a complete cognition of our own mode of intuition, that is of our sensibility, and this always under the conditions originally attaching to the subject, namely, the conditions of space and time; while the question: “What are objects considered as things in themselves?” remains unanswerable even after the most thorough examination of the phenomenal world.\n\nTo say, then, that all our sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things containing exclusively that which belongs to them as things in themselves, and this under an accumulation of characteristic marks and partial representations which we cannot distinguish in consciousness, is a falsification of the conception of sensibility and phenomenization, which renders our whole doctrine thereof empty and useless. The difference between a confused and a clear representation is merely logical and has nothing to do with content. No doubt the conception of right, as employed by a sound understanding, contains all that the most subtle investigation could unfold from it, although, in the ordinary practical use of the word, we are not conscious of the manifold representations comprised in the conception. But we cannot for this reason assert that the ordinary conception is a sensuous one, containing a mere phenomenon, for right cannot appear as a phenomenon; but the conception of it lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral property) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. On the other hand, the representation in intuition of a body contains nothing which could belong to an object considered as a thing in itself, but merely the phenomenon or appearance of something, and the mode in which we are affected by that appearance; and this receptivity of our faculty of cognition is called sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from the cognition of an object in itself, even though we should examine the content of the phenomenon to the very bottom.\n\nIt must be admitted that the Leibnitz-Wolfian philosophy has assigned an entirely erroneous point of view to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions, inasmuch as it regards the distinction between the sensuous and the intellectual as merely logical, whereas it is plainly transcendental, and concerns not merely the clearness or obscurity, but the content and origin of both. For the faculty of sensibility not only does not present us with an indistinct and confused cognition of objects as things in themselves, but, in fact, gives us no knowledge of these at all. On the contrary, so soon as we abstract in thought our own subjective nature, the object represented, with the properties ascribed to it by sensuous intuition, entirely disappears, because it was only this subjective nature that determined the form of the object as a phenomenon.\n\nIn phenomena, we commonly, indeed, distinguish that which essentially belongs to the intuition of them, and is valid for the sensuous faculty of every human being, from that which belongs to the same intuition accidentally, as valid not for the sensuous faculty in general, but for a particular state or organization of this or that sense. Accordingly, we are accustomed to say that the former is a cognition which represents the object itself, whilst the latter presents only a particular appearance or phenomenon thereof. This distinction, however, is only empirical. If we stop here (as is usual), and do not regard the empirical intuition as itself a mere phenomenon (as we ought to do), in which nothing that can appertain to a thing in itself is to be found, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we believe that we cognize objects as things in themselves, although in the whole range of the sensuous world, investigate the nature of its objects as profoundly as we may, we have to do with nothing but phenomena. Thus, we call the rainbow a mere appearance of phenomenon in a sunny shower, and the rain, the reality or thing in itself; and this is right enough, if we understand the latter conception in a merely physical sense, that is, as that which in universal experience, and under whatever conditions of sensuous perception, is known in intuition to be so and so determined, and not otherwise. But if we consider this empirical datum generally, and inquire, without reference to its accordance with all our senses, whether there can be discovered in it aught which represents an object as a thing in itself (the raindrops of course are not such, for they are, as phenomena, empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental; and not only are the raindrops mere phenomena, but even their circular form, nay, the space itself through which they fall, is nothing in itself, but both are mere modifications or fundamental dispositions of our sensuous intuition, whilst the transcendental object remains for us utterly unknown.\n\nThe second important concern of our æsthetic is that it does not obtain favour merely as a plausible hypothesis, but possess as undoubted a character of certainty as can be demanded of any theory which is to serve for an organon. In order fully to convince the reader of this certainty, we shall select a case which will serve to make its validity apparent, and also to illustrate what has been said in § 3.\n\nSuppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and conditions of the—possibility of objects as things in themselves. In the first place, it is evident that both present us, with very many apodeictic and synthetic propositions à priori, but especially space—and for this reason we shall prefer it for investigation at present. As the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically à priori, and with apodeictic certainty, I inquire: Whence do you obtain propositions of this kind, and on what basis does the understanding rest, in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths?\n\nThere is no other way than through intuitions or conceptions, as such; and these are given either à priori or à posteriori. The latter, namely, empirical conceptions, together with the empirical intuition on which they are founded, cannot afford any synthetical proposition, except such as is itself also empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. But an empirical proposition cannot possess the qualities of necessity and absolute universality, which, nevertheless, are the characteristics of all geometrical propositions. As to the first and only means to arrive at such cognitions, namely, through mere conceptions or intuitions à priori, it is quite clear that from mere conceptions no synthetical cognitions, but only analytical ones, can be obtained. Take, for example, the proposition: “Two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and with these alone no figure is possible,” and try to deduce it from the conception of a straight line and the number two; or take the proposition: “It is possible to construct a figure with three straight lines,” and endeavour, in like manner, to deduce it from the mere conception of a straight line and the number three. All your endeavours are in vain, and you find yourself forced to have recourse to intuition, as, in fact, geometry always does. You therefore give yourself an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it a pure à priori, or is it an empirical intuition? If the latter, then neither an universally valid, much less an apodeictic proposition can arise from it, for experience never can give us any such proposition. You must, therefore, give yourself an object à priori in intuition, and upon that ground your synthetical proposition. Now if there did not exist within you a faculty of intuition à priori; if this subjective condition were not in respect to its form also the universal condition à priori under which alone the object of this external intuition is itself possible; if the object (that is, the triangle) were something in itself, without relation to you the subject; how could you affirm that that which lies necessarily in your subjective conditions in order to construct a triangle, must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For to your conceptions of three lines, you could not add anything new (that is, the figure); which, therefore, must necessarily be found in the object, because the object is given before your cognition, and not by means of it. If, therefore, space (and time also) were not a mere form of your intuition, which contains conditions à priori, under which alone things can become external objects for you, and without which subjective conditions the objects are in themselves nothing, you could not construct any synthetical proposition whatsoever regarding external objects. It is therefore not merely possible or probable, but indubitably certain, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all our external and internal experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuitions, in relation to which all objects are therefore mere phenomena, and not things in themselves, presented to us in this particular manner. And for this reason, in respect to the form of phenomena, much may be said à priori, whilst of the thing in itself, which may lie at the foundation of these phenomena, it is impossible to say anything.\n\nII. In confirmation of this theory of the ideality of the external as well as internal sense, consequently of all objects of sense, as mere phenomena, we may especially remark that all in our cognition that belongs to intuition contains nothing more than mere relations. (The feelings of pain and pleasure, and the will, which are not cognitions, are excepted.) The relations, to wit, of place in an intuition (extension), change of place (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). That, however, which is present in this or that place, or any operation going on, or result taking place in the things themselves, with the exception of change of place, is not given to us by intuition. Now by means of mere relations, a thing cannot be known in itself; and it may therefore be fairly concluded, that, as through the external sense nothing but mere representations of relations are given us, the said external sense in its representation can contain only the relation of the object to the subject, but not the essential nature of the object as a thing in itself.\n\nThe same is the case with the internal intuition, not only because, in the internal intuition, the representation of the external senses constitutes the material with which the mind is occupied; but because time, in which we place, and which itself antecedes the consciousness of, these representations in experience, and which, as the formal condition of the mode according to which objects are placed in the mind, lies at the foundation of them, contains relations of the successive, the coexistent, and of that which always must be coexistent with succession, the permanent. Now that which, as representation, can antecede every exercise of thought (of an object), is intuition; and when it contains nothing but relations, it is the form of the intuition, which, as it presents us with no representation, except in so far as something is placed in the mind, can be nothing else than the mode in which the mind is affected by its own activity, to wit—its presenting to itself representations, consequently the mode in which the mind is affected by itself; that is, it can be nothing but an internal sense in respect to its form. Everything that is represented through the medium of sense is so far phenomenal; consequently, we must either refuse altogether to admit an internal sense, or the subject, which is the object of that sense, could only be represented by it as phenomenon, and not as it would judge of itself, if its intuition were pure spontaneous activity, that is, were intellectual. The difficulty here lies wholly in the question: How can the subject have an internal intuition of itself? But this difficulty is common to every theory. The consciousness of self (apperception) is the simple representation of the “ego”; and if by means of that representation alone, all the manifold representations in the subject were spontaneously given, then our internal intuition would be intellectual. This consciousness in man requires an internal perception of the manifold representations which are previously given in the subject; and the manner in which these representations are given in the mind without spontaneity, must, on account of this difference (the want of spontaneity), be called sensibility. If the faculty of self-consciousness is to apprehend what lies in the mind, it must all act that and can in this way alone produce an intuition of self. But the form of this intuition, which lies in the original constitution of the mind, determines, in the representation of time, the manner in which the manifold representations are to combine themselves in the mind; since the subject intuites itself, not as it would represent itself immediately and spontaneously, but according to the manner in which the mind is internally affected, consequently, as it appears, and not as it is.\n\nIII. When we say that the intuition of external objects, and also the self-intuition of the subject, represent both, objects and subject, in space and time, as they affect our senses, that is, as they appear—this is by no means equivalent to asserting that these objects are mere illusory appearances. For when we speak of things as phenomena, the objects, nay, even the properties which we ascribe to them, are looked upon as really given; only that, in so far as this or that property depends upon the mode of intuition of the subject, in the relation of the given object to the subject, the object as phenomenon is to be distinguished from the object as a thing in itself. Thus I do not say that bodies seem or appear to be external to me, or that my soul seems merely to be given in my self-consciousness, although I maintain that the properties of space and time, in conformity to which I set both, as the condition of their existence, abide in my mode of intuition, and not in the objects in themselves. It would be my own fault, if out of that which I should reckon as phenomenon, I made mere illusory appearance.[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-12) But this will not happen, because of our principle of the ideality of all sensuous intuitions. On the contrary, if we ascribe objective reality to these forms of representation, it becomes impossible to avoid changing everything into mere appearance. For if we regard space and time as properties, which must be found in objects as things in themselves, as sine quibus non of the possibility of their existence, and reflect on the absurdities in which we then find ourselves involved, inasmuch as we are compelled to admit the existence of two infinite things, which are nevertheless not substances, nor anything really inhering in substances, nay, to admit that they are the necessary conditions of the existence of all things, and moreover, that they must continue to exist, although all existing things were annihilated—we cannot blame the good Berkeley for degrading bodies to mere illusory appearances. Nay, even our own existence, which would in this case depend upon the self-existent reality of such a mere nonentity as time, would necessarily be changed with it into mere appearance—an absurdity which no one has as yet been guilty of.\n\n[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-12) The predicates of the phenomenon can be affixed to the object itself in relation to our sensuous faculty; for example, the red colour or the perfume to the rose. But (illusory) appearance never can be attributed as a predicate to an object, for this very reason, that it attributes to this object in itself that which belongs to it only in relation to our sensuous faculty, or to the subject in general, e.g., the two handles which were formerly ascribed to Saturn. That which is never to be found in the object itself, but always in the relation of the object to the subject, and which moreover is inseparable from our representation of the object, we denominate phenomenon. Thus the predicates of space and time are rightly attributed to objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. On the contrary, if I ascribe redness of the rose as a thing in itself, or to Saturn his handles, or extension to all external objects, considered as things in themselves, without regarding the determinate relation of these objects to the subject, and without limiting my judgement to that relation—then, and then only, arises illusion.\n\nIV. In natural theology, where we think of an object—God—which never can be an object of intuition to us, and even to himself can never be an object of sensuous intuition, we carefully avoid attributing to his intuition the conditions of space and time—and intuition all his cognition must be, and not thought, which always includes limitation. But with what right can we do this if we make them forms of objects as things in themselves, and such, moreover, as would continue to exist as à priori conditions of the existence of things, even though the things themselves were annihilated? For as conditions of all existence in general, space and time must be conditions of the existence of the Supreme Being also. But if we do not thus make them objective forms of all things, there is no other way left than to make them subjective forms of our mode of intuition—external and internal; which is called sensuous, because it is not primitive, that is, is not such as gives in itself the existence of the object of the intuition (a mode of intuition which, so far as we can judge, can belong only to the Creator), but is dependent on the existence of the object, is possible, therefore, only on condition that the representative faculty of the subject is affected by the object.\n\nIt is, moreover, not necessary that we should limit the mode of intuition in space and time to the sensuous faculty of man. It may well be that all finite thinking beings must necessarily in this respect agree with man (though as to this we cannot decide), but sensibility does not on account of this universality cease to be sensibility, for this very reason, that it is a deduced (intuitus derivativus), and not an original (intuitus originarius), consequently not an intellectual intuition, and this intuition, as such, for reasons above mentioned, seems to belong solely to the Supreme Being, but never to a being dependent, quoad its existence, as well as its intuition (which its existence determines and limits relatively to given objects). This latter remark, however, must be taken only as an illustration, and not as any proof of the truth of our æsthetical theory.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","text":"### § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nI. In order to prevent any misunderstanding, it will be requisite, in the first place, to recapitulate, as clearly as possible, what our opinion is with respect to the fundamental nature of our sensuous cognition in general. We have intended, then, to say that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of phenomena; that the things which we intuite, are not in themselves the same as our representations of them in intuition, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us; and that if we take away the subject, or even only the subjective constitution of our senses in general, then not only the nature and relations of objects in space and time, but even space and time themselves disappear; and that these, as phenomena, cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What may be the nature of objects considered as things in themselves and without reference to the receptivity of our sensibility is quite unknown to us. We know nothing more than our mode of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, and which, though not of necessity pertaining to every animated being, is so to the whole human race. With this alone we have to do. Space and time are the pure forms thereof; sensation the matter. The former alone can we cognize à priori, that is, antecedent to all actual perception; and for this reason such cognition is called pure intuition. The latter is that in our cognition which is called cognition à posteriori, that is, empirical intuition. The former appertain absolutely and necessarily to our sensibility, of whatsoever kind our sensations may be; the latter may be of very diversified character. Supposing that we should carry our empirical intuition even to the very highest degree of clearness, we should not thereby advance one step nearer to a knowledge of the constitution of objects as things in themselves. For we could only, at best, arrive at a complete cognition of our own mode of intuition, that is of our sensibility, and this always under the conditions originally attaching to the subject, namely, the conditions of space and time; while the question: “What are objects considered as things in themselves?” remains unanswerable even after the most thorough examination of the phenomenal world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","text":"To say, then, that all our sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things containing exclusively that which belongs to them as things in themselves, and this under an accumulation of characteristic marks and partial representations which we cannot distinguish in consciousness, is a falsification of the conception of sensibility and phenomenization, which renders our whole doctrine thereof empty and useless. The difference between a confused and a clear representation is merely logical and has nothing to do with content. No doubt the conception of right, as employed by a sound understanding, contains all that the most subtle investigation could unfold from it, although, in the ordinary practical use of the word, we are not conscious of the manifold representations comprised in the conception. But we cannot for this reason assert that the ordinary conception is a sensuous one, containing a mere phenomenon, for right cannot appear as a phenomenon; but the conception of it lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral property) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. On the other hand, the representation in intuition of a body contains nothing which could belong to an object considered as a thing in itself, but merely the phenomenon or appearance of something, and the mode in which we are affected by that appearance; and this receptivity of our faculty of cognition is called sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from the cognition of an object in itself, even though we should examine the content of the phenomenon to the very bottom.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","text":"It must be admitted that the Leibnitz-Wolfian philosophy has assigned an entirely erroneous point of view to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions, inasmuch as it regards the distinction between the sensuous and the intellectual as merely logical, whereas it is plainly transcendental, and concerns not merely the clearness or obscurity, but the content and origin of both. For the faculty of sensibility not only does not present us with an indistinct and confused cognition of objects as things in themselves, but, in fact, gives us no knowledge of these at all. On the contrary, so soon as we abstract in thought our own subjective nature, the object represented, with the properties ascribed to it by sensuous intuition, entirely disappears, because it was only this subjective nature that determined the form of the object as a phenomenon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","text":"In phenomena, we commonly, indeed, distinguish that which essentially belongs to the intuition of them, and is valid for the sensuous faculty of every human being, from that which belongs to the same intuition accidentally, as valid not for the sensuous faculty in general, but for a particular state or organization of this or that sense. Accordingly, we are accustomed to say that the former is a cognition which represents the object itself, whilst the latter presents only a particular appearance or phenomenon thereof. This distinction, however, is only empirical. If we stop here (as is usual), and do not regard the empirical intuition as itself a mere phenomenon (as we ought to do), in which nothing that can appertain to a thing in itself is to be found, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we believe that we cognize objects as things in themselves, although in the whole range of the sensuous world, investigate the nature of its objects as profoundly as we may, we have to do with nothing but phenomena. Thus, we call the rainbow a mere appearance of phenomenon in a sunny shower, and the rain, the reality or thing in itself; and this is right enough, if we understand the latter conception in a merely physical sense, that is, as that which in universal experience, and under whatever conditions of sensuous perception, is known in intuition to be so and so determined, and not otherwise. But if we consider this empirical datum generally, and inquire, without reference to its accordance with all our senses, whether there can be discovered in it aught which represents an object as a thing in itself (the raindrops of course are not such, for they are, as phenomena, empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental; and not only are the raindrops mere phenomena, but even their circular form, nay, the space itself through which they fall, is nothing in itself, but both are mere modifications or fundamental dispositions of our sensuous intuition, whilst the transcendental object remains for us utterly unknown.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","text":"The second important concern of our æsthetic is that it does not obtain favour merely as a plausible hypothesis, but possess as undoubted a character of certainty as can be demanded of any theory which is to serve for an organon. In order fully to convince the reader of this certainty, we shall select a case which will serve to make its validity apparent, and also to illustrate what has been said in § 3.\n\nSuppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and conditions of the—possibility of objects as things in themselves. In the first place, it is evident that both present us, with very many apodeictic and synthetic propositions à priori, but especially space—and for this reason we shall prefer it for investigation at present. As the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically à priori, and with apodeictic certainty, I inquire: Whence do you obtain propositions of this kind, and on what basis does the understanding rest, in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","text":"There is no other way than through intuitions or conceptions, as such; and these are given either à priori or à posteriori. The latter, namely, empirical conceptions, together with the empirical intuition on which they are founded, cannot afford any synthetical proposition, except such as is itself also empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. But an empirical proposition cannot possess the qualities of necessity and absolute universality, which, nevertheless, are the characteristics of all geometrical propositions. As to the first and only means to arrive at such cognitions, namely, through mere conceptions or intuitions à priori, it is quite clear that from mere conceptions no synthetical cognitions, but only analytical ones, can be obtained. Take, for example, the proposition: “Two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and with these alone no figure is possible,” and try to deduce it from the conception of a straight line and the number two; or take the proposition: “It is possible to construct a figure with three straight lines,” and endeavour, in like manner, to deduce it from the mere conception of a straight line and the number three. All your endeavours are in vain, and you find yourself forced to have recourse to intuition, as, in fact, geometry always does. You therefore give yourself an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it a pure à priori, or is it an empirical intuition? If the latter, then neither an universally valid, much less an apodeictic proposition can arise from it, for experience never can give us any such proposition. You must, therefore, give yourself an object à priori in intuition, and upon that ground your synthetical proposition. Now if there did not exist within you a faculty of intuition à priori; if this subjective condition were not in respect to its form also the universal condition à priori under which alone the object of this external intuition is itself possible; if the object (that is, the triangle) were something in itself, without relation to you the subject; how could you affirm that that which lies necessarily in your subjective conditions in order to construct a triangle, must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For to your conceptions of three lines, you could not add anything new (that is, the figure); which, therefore, must necessarily be found in the object, because the object is given before your cognition, and not by means of it. If, therefore, space (and time also) were not a mere form of your intuition, which contains conditions à priori, under which alone things can become external objects for you, and without which subjective conditions the objects are in themselves nothing, you could not construct any synthetical proposition whatsoever regarding external objects. It is therefore not merely possible or probable, but indubitably certain, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all our external and internal experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuitions, in relation to which all objects are therefore mere phenomena, and not things in themselves, presented to us in this particular manner. And for this reason, in respect to the form of phenomena, much may be said à priori, whilst of the thing in itself, which may lie at the foundation of these phenomena, it is impossible to say anything.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","text":"II. In confirmation of this theory of the ideality of the external as well as internal sense, consequently of all objects of sense, as mere phenomena, we may especially remark that all in our cognition that belongs to intuition contains nothing more than mere relations. (The feelings of pain and pleasure, and the will, which are not cognitions, are excepted.) The relations, to wit, of place in an intuition (extension), change of place (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). That, however, which is present in this or that place, or any operation going on, or result taking place in the things themselves, with the exception of change of place, is not given to us by intuition. Now by means of mere relations, a thing cannot be known in itself; and it may therefore be fairly concluded, that, as through the external sense nothing but mere representations of relations are given us, the said external sense in its representation can contain only the relation of the object to the subject, but not the essential nature of the object as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","text":"The same is the case with the internal intuition, not only because, in the internal intuition, the representation of the external senses constitutes the material with which the mind is occupied; but because time, in which we place, and which itself antecedes the consciousness of, these representations in experience, and which, as the formal condition of the mode according to which objects are placed in the mind, lies at the foundation of them, contains relations of the successive, the coexistent, and of that which always must be coexistent with succession, the permanent. Now that which, as representation, can antecede every exercise of thought (of an object), is intuition; and when it contains nothing but relations, it is the form of the intuition, which, as it presents us with no representation, except in so far as something is placed in the mind, can be nothing else than the mode in which the mind is affected by its own activity, to wit—its presenting to itself representations, consequently the mode in which the mind is affected by itself; that is, it can be nothing but an internal sense in respect to its form. Everything that is represented through the medium of sense is so far phenomenal; consequently, we must either refuse altogether to admit an internal sense, or the subject, which is the object of that sense, could only be represented by it as phenomenon, and not as it would judge of itself, if its intuition were pure spontaneous activity, that is, were intellectual. The difficulty here lies wholly in the question: How can the subject have an internal intuition of itself? But this difficulty is common to every theory. The consciousness of self (apperception) is the simple representation of the “ego”; and if by means of that representation alone, all the manifold representations in the subject were spontaneously given, then our internal intuition would be intellectual. This consciousness in man requires an internal perception of the manifold representations which are previously given in the subject; and the manner in which these representations are given in the mind without spontaneity, must, on account of this difference (the want of spontaneity), be called sensibility. If the faculty of self-consciousness is to apprehend what lies in the mind, it must all act that and can in this way alone produce an intuition of self. But the form of this intuition, which lies in the original constitution of the mind, determines, in the representation of time, the manner in which the manifold representations are to combine themselves in the mind; since the subject intuites itself, not as it would represent itself immediately and spontaneously, but according to the manner in which the mind is internally affected, consequently, as it appears, and not as it is.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","text":"III. When we say that the intuition of external objects, and also the self-intuition of the subject, represent both, objects and subject, in space and time, as they affect our senses, that is, as they appear—this is by no means equivalent to asserting that these objects are mere illusory appearances. For when we speak of things as phenomena, the objects, nay, even the properties which we ascribe to them, are looked upon as really given; only that, in so far as this or that property depends upon the mode of intuition of the subject, in the relation of the given object to the subject, the object as phenomenon is to be distinguished from the object as a thing in itself. Thus I do not say that bodies seem or appear to be external to me, or that my soul seems merely to be given in my self-consciousness, although I maintain that the properties of space and time, in conformity to which I set both, as the condition of their existence, abide in my mode of intuition, and not in the objects in themselves. It would be my own fault, if out of that which I should reckon as phenomenon, I made mere illusory appearance.[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-12) But this will not happen, because of our principle of the ideality of all sensuous intuitions. On the contrary, if we ascribe objective reality to these forms of representation, it becomes impossible to avoid changing everything into mere appearance. For if we regard space and time as properties, which must be found in objects as things in themselves, as sine quibus non of the possibility of their existence, and reflect on the absurdities in which we then find ourselves involved, inasmuch as we are compelled to admit the existence of two infinite things, which are nevertheless not substances, nor anything really inhering in substances, nay, to admit that they are the necessary conditions of the existence of all things, and moreover, that they must continue to exist, although all existing things were annihilated—we cannot blame the good Berkeley for degrading bodies to mere illusory appearances. Nay, even our own existence, which would in this case depend upon the self-existent reality of such a mere nonentity as time, would necessarily be changed with it into mere appearance—an absurdity which no one has as yet been guilty of.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","text":"[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-12) The predicates of the phenomenon can be affixed to the object itself in relation to our sensuous faculty; for example, the red colour or the perfume to the rose. But (illusory) appearance never can be attributed as a predicate to an object, for this very reason, that it attributes to this object in itself that which belongs to it only in relation to our sensuous faculty, or to the subject in general, e.g., the two handles which were formerly ascribed to Saturn. That which is never to be found in the object itself, but always in the relation of the object to the subject, and which moreover is inseparable from our representation of the object, we denominate phenomenon. Thus the predicates of space and time are rightly attributed to objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. On the contrary, if I ascribe redness of the rose as a thing in itself, or to Saturn his handles, or extension to all external objects, considered as things in themselves, without regarding the determinate relation of these objects to the subject, and without limiting my judgement to that relation—then, and then only, arises illusion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","text":"IV. In natural theology, where we think of an object—God—which never can be an object of intuition to us, and even to himself can never be an object of sensuous intuition, we carefully avoid attributing to his intuition the conditions of space and time—and intuition all his cognition must be, and not thought, which always includes limitation. But with what right can we do this if we make them forms of objects as things in themselves, and such, moreover, as would continue to exist as à priori conditions of the existence of things, even though the things themselves were annihilated? For as conditions of all existence in general, space and time must be conditions of the existence of the Supreme Being also. But if we do not thus make them objective forms of all things, there is no other way left than to make them subjective forms of our mode of intuition—external and internal; which is called sensuous, because it is not primitive, that is, is not such as gives in itself the existence of the object of the intuition (a mode of intuition which, so far as we can judge, can belong only to the Creator), but is dependent on the existence of the object, is possible, therefore, only on condition that the representative faculty of the subject is affected by the object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","text":"It is, moreover, not necessary that we should limit the mode of intuition in space and time to the sensuous faculty of man. It may well be that all finite thinking beings must necessarily in this respect agree with man (though as to this we cannot decide), but sensibility does not on account of this universality cease to be sensibility, for this very reason, that it is a deduced (intuitus derivativus), and not an original (intuitus originarius), consequently not an intellectual intuition, and this intuition, as such, for reasons above mentioned, seems to belong solely to the Supreme Being, but never to a being dependent, quoad its existence, as well as its intuition (which its existence determines and limits relatively to given objects). This latter remark, however, must be taken only as an illustration, and not as any proof of the truth of our æsthetical theory.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ce7ead8ccac3badf7d6a2a33ad42fc5779c1ed4709c2797c57f3fca1b62c5495","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_1CTS","text":"### § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nWe have now completely before us one part of the solution of the grand general problem of transcendental philosophy, namely, the question: “How are synthetical propositions à priori possible?” That is to say, we have shown that we are in possession of pure à priori intuitions, namely, space and time, in which we find, when in a judgement à priori we pass out beyond the given conception, something which is not discoverable in that conception, but is certainly found à priori in the intuition which corresponds to the conception, and can be united synthetically with it. But the judgements which these pure intuitions enable us to make, never reach farther than to objects of the senses, and are valid only for objects of possible experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","text":"### § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nWe have now completely before us one part of the solution of the grand general problem of transcendental philosophy, namely, the question: “How are synthetical propositions à priori possible?” That is to say, we have shown that we are in possession of pure à priori intuitions, namely, space and time, in which we find, when in a judgement à priori we pass out beyond the given conception, something which is not discoverable in that conception, but is certainly found à priori in the intuition which corresponds to the conception, and can be united synthetically with it. But the judgements which these pure intuitions enable us to make, never reach farther than to objects of the senses, and are valid only for objects of possible experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"981feb5395a71ac211d1a07298ffd4d3a84d5abad674eb37aaad79ee5b4ee598","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ILG0","text":"# Transcendental Logic\n\n### Second Part—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### INTRODUCTION. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.\n\n### I. Of Logic in General.\n\nOur knowledge springs from two main sources in the mind, first of which is the faculty or power of receiving representations (receptivity for impressions); the second is the power of cognizing by means of these representations (spontaneity in the production of conceptions). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second, it is, in relation to the representation (which is a mere determination of the mind), thought. Intuition and conceptions constitute, therefore, the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither conceptions without an intuition in some way corresponding to them, nor intuition without conceptions, can afford us a cognition. Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical, when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; and pure, when no sensation is mixed with the representation. Sensations we may call the matter of sensuous cognition. Pure intuition consequently contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure conception only the form of the thought of an object. Only pure intuitions and pure conceptions are possible à priori; the empirical only à posteriori.\n\nWe apply the term sensibility to the receptivity of the mind for impressions, in so far as it is in some way affected; and, on the other hand, we call the faculty of spontaneously producing representations, or the spontaneity of cognition, understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition with us never can be other than sensuous, that is, it contains only the mode in which we are affected by objects. On the other hand, the faculty of thinking the object of sensuous intuition is the understanding. Neither of these faculties has a preference over the other. Without the sensuous faculty no object would be given to us, and without the understanding no object would be thought. Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind. Hence it is as necessary for the mind to make its conceptions sensuous (that is, to join to them the object in intuition), as to make its intuitions intelligible (that is, to bring them under conceptions). Neither of these faculties can exchange its proper function. Understanding cannot intuite, and the sensuous faculty cannot think. In no other way than from the united operation of both, can knowledge arise. But no one ought, on this account, to overlook the difference of the elements contributed by each; we have rather great reason carefully to separate and distinguish them. We therefore distinguish the science of the laws of sensibility, that is, æsthetic, from the science of the laws of the understanding, that is, logic.\n\nNow, logic in its turn may be considered as twofold—namely, as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary laws of thought, without which no use whatsoever of the understanding is possible, and gives laws therefore to the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects on which it may be employed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the laws of correct thinking upon a particular class of objects. The former may be called elemental logic—the latter, the organon of this or that particular science. The latter is for the most part employed in the schools, as a propædeutic to the sciences, although, indeed, according to the course of human reason, it is the last thing we arrive at, when the science has been already matured, and needs only the finishing touches towards its correction and completion; for our knowledge of the objects of our attempted science must be tolerably extensive and complete before we can indicate the laws by which a science of these objects can be established.\n\nGeneral logic is again either pure or applied. In the former, we abstract all the empirical conditions under which the understanding is exercised; for example, the influence of the senses, the play of the fantasy or imagination, the laws of the memory, the force of habit, of inclination, etc., consequently also, the sources of prejudice—in a word, we abstract all causes from which particular cognitions arise, because these causes regard the understanding under certain circumstances of its application, and, to the knowledge of them experience is required. Pure general logic has to do, therefore, merely with pure à priori principles, and is a canon of understanding and reason, but only in respect of the formal part of their use, be the content what it may, empirical or transcendental. General logic is called applied, when it is directed to the laws of the use of the understanding, under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has therefore empirical principles, although, at the same time, it is in so far general, that it applies to the exercise of the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects. On this account, moreover, it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of a particular science, but merely a cathartic of the human understanding.\n\nIn general logic, therefore, that part which constitutes pure logic must be carefully distinguished from that which constitutes applied (though still general) logic. The former alone is properly science, although short and dry, as the methodical exposition of an elemental doctrine of the understanding ought to be. In this, therefore, logicians must always bear in mind two rules:\n\n1. As general logic, it makes abstraction of all content of the cognition of the understanding, and of the difference of objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.\n\n2. As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and consequently draws nothing (contrary to the common persuasion) from psychology, which therefore has no influence on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely à priori.\n\nWhat I called applied logic (contrary to the common acceptation of this term, according to which it should contain certain exercises for the scholar, for which pure logic gives the rules), is a representation of the understanding, and of the rules of its necessary employment in concreto, that is to say, under the accidental conditions of the subject, which may either hinder or promote this employment, and which are all given only empirically. Thus applied logic treats of attention, its impediments and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, hesitation, conviction, etc., and to it is related pure general logic in the same way that pure morality, which contains only the necessary moral laws of a free will, is related to practical ethics, which considers these laws under all the impediments of feelings, inclinations, and passions to which men are more or less subjected, and which never can furnish us with a true and demonstrated science, because it, as well as applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","text":"# Transcendental Logic\n\n### Second Part—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### INTRODUCTION. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.\n\n### I. Of Logic in General.\n\nOur knowledge springs from two main sources in the mind, first of which is the faculty or power of receiving representations (receptivity for impressions); the second is the power of cognizing by means of these representations (spontaneity in the production of conceptions). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second, it is, in relation to the representation (which is a mere determination of the mind), thought. Intuition and conceptions constitute, therefore, the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither conceptions without an intuition in some way corresponding to them, nor intuition without conceptions, can afford us a cognition. Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical, when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; and pure, when no sensation is mixed with the representation. Sensations we may call the matter of sensuous cognition. Pure intuition consequently contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure conception only the form of the thought of an object. Only pure intuitions and pure conceptions are possible à priori; the empirical only à posteriori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","text":"We apply the term sensibility to the receptivity of the mind for impressions, in so far as it is in some way affected; and, on the other hand, we call the faculty of spontaneously producing representations, or the spontaneity of cognition, understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition with us never can be other than sensuous, that is, it contains only the mode in which we are affected by objects. On the other hand, the faculty of thinking the object of sensuous intuition is the understanding. Neither of these faculties has a preference over the other. Without the sensuous faculty no object would be given to us, and without the understanding no object would be thought. Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind. Hence it is as necessary for the mind to make its conceptions sensuous (that is, to join to them the object in intuition), as to make its intuitions intelligible (that is, to bring them under conceptions). Neither of these faculties can exchange its proper function. Understanding cannot intuite, and the sensuous faculty cannot think. In no other way than from the united operation of both, can knowledge arise. But no one ought, on this account, to overlook the difference of the elements contributed by each; we have rather great reason carefully to separate and distinguish them. We therefore distinguish the science of the laws of sensibility, that is, æsthetic, from the science of the laws of the understanding, that is, logic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","text":"Now, logic in its turn may be considered as twofold—namely, as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary laws of thought, without which no use whatsoever of the understanding is possible, and gives laws therefore to the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects on which it may be employed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the laws of correct thinking upon a particular class of objects. The former may be called elemental logic—the latter, the organon of this or that particular science. The latter is for the most part employed in the schools, as a propædeutic to the sciences, although, indeed, according to the course of human reason, it is the last thing we arrive at, when the science has been already matured, and needs only the finishing touches towards its correction and completion; for our knowledge of the objects of our attempted science must be tolerably extensive and complete before we can indicate the laws by which a science of these objects can be established.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","text":"General logic is again either pure or applied. In the former, we abstract all the empirical conditions under which the understanding is exercised; for example, the influence of the senses, the play of the fantasy or imagination, the laws of the memory, the force of habit, of inclination, etc., consequently also, the sources of prejudice—in a word, we abstract all causes from which particular cognitions arise, because these causes regard the understanding under certain circumstances of its application, and, to the knowledge of them experience is required. Pure general logic has to do, therefore, merely with pure à priori principles, and is a canon of understanding and reason, but only in respect of the formal part of their use, be the content what it may, empirical or transcendental. General logic is called applied, when it is directed to the laws of the use of the understanding, under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has therefore empirical principles, although, at the same time, it is in so far general, that it applies to the exercise of the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects. On this account, moreover, it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of a particular science, but merely a cathartic of the human understanding.\n\nIn general logic, therefore, that part which constitutes pure logic must be carefully distinguished from that which constitutes applied (though still general) logic. The former alone is properly science, although short and dry, as the methodical exposition of an elemental doctrine of the understanding ought to be. In this, therefore, logicians must always bear in mind two rules:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","text":"1. As general logic, it makes abstraction of all content of the cognition of the understanding, and of the difference of objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.\n\n2. As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and consequently draws nothing (contrary to the common persuasion) from psychology, which therefore has no influence on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely à priori.\n\nWhat I called applied logic (contrary to the common acceptation of this term, according to which it should contain certain exercises for the scholar, for which pure logic gives the rules), is a representation of the understanding, and of the rules of its necessary employment in concreto, that is to say, under the accidental conditions of the subject, which may either hinder or promote this employment, and which are all given only empirically. Thus applied logic treats of attention, its impediments and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, hesitation, conviction, etc., and to it is related pure general logic in the same way that pure morality, which contains only the necessary moral laws of a free will, is related to practical ethics, which considers these laws under all the impediments of feelings, inclinations, and passions to which men are more or less subjected, and which never can furnish us with a true and demonstrated science, because it, as well as applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"007f27531f5e13ae9b5618996295c657486539cfc87e28a65bdae0245178b0ff","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"I. Of Logic in General.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ITL0","text":"### II. Of Transcendental Logic.\n\nGeneral logic, as we have seen, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, that is, of all relation of cognition to its object, and regards only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to each other, that is, the form of thought in general. But as we have both pure and empirical intuitions (as transcendental æsthetic proves), in like manner a distinction might be drawn between pure and empirical thought (of objects). In this case, there would exist a kind of logic, in which we should not make abstraction of all content of cognition; for or logic which should comprise merely the laws of pure thought (of an object), would of course exclude all those cognitions which were of empirical content. This kind of logic would also examine the origin of our cognitions of objects, so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects themselves; while, on the contrary, general logic has nothing to do with the origin of our cognitions, but contemplates our representations, be they given primitively à priori in ourselves, or be they only of empirical origin, solely according to the laws which the understanding observes in employing them in the process of thought, in relation to each other. Consequently, general logic treats of the form of the understanding only, which can be applied to representations, from whatever source they may have arisen.\n\nAnd here I shall make a remark, which the reader must bear well in mind in the course of the following considerations, to wit, that not every cognition à priori, but only those through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or conceptions) are applied or are possible only à priori; that is to say, the à priori possibility of cognition and the à priori use of it are transcendental. Therefore neither is space, nor any à priori geometrical determination of space, a transcendental Representation, but only the knowledge that such a representation is not of empirical origin, and the possibility of its relating to objects of experience, although itself à priori, can be called transcendental. So also, the application of space to objects in general would be transcendental; but if it be limited to objects of sense it is empirical. Thus, the distinction of the transcendental and empirical belongs only to the critique of cognitions, and does not concern the relation of these to their object.\n\nAccordingly, in the expectation that there may perhaps be conceptions which relate à priori to objects, not as pure or sensuous intuitions, but merely as acts of pure thought (which are therefore conceptions, but neither of empirical nor æsthetical origin)—in this expectation, I say, we form to ourselves, by anticipation, the idea of a science of pure understanding and rational cognition, by means of which we may cogitate objects entirely à priori. A science of this kind, which should determine the origin, the extent, and the objective validity of such cognitions, must be called transcendental logic, because it has not, like general logic, to do with the laws of understanding and reason in relation to empirical as well as pure rational cognitions without distinction, but concerns itself with these only in an à priori relation to objects.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","text":"### II. Of Transcendental Logic.\n\nGeneral logic, as we have seen, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, that is, of all relation of cognition to its object, and regards only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to each other, that is, the form of thought in general. But as we have both pure and empirical intuitions (as transcendental æsthetic proves), in like manner a distinction might be drawn between pure and empirical thought (of objects). In this case, there would exist a kind of logic, in which we should not make abstraction of all content of cognition; for or logic which should comprise merely the laws of pure thought (of an object), would of course exclude all those cognitions which were of empirical content. This kind of logic would also examine the origin of our cognitions of objects, so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects themselves; while, on the contrary, general logic has nothing to do with the origin of our cognitions, but contemplates our representations, be they given primitively à priori in ourselves, or be they only of empirical origin, solely according to the laws which the understanding observes in employing them in the process of thought, in relation to each other. Consequently, general logic treats of the form of the understanding only, which can be applied to representations, from whatever source they may have arisen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","text":"And here I shall make a remark, which the reader must bear well in mind in the course of the following considerations, to wit, that not every cognition à priori, but only those through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or conceptions) are applied or are possible only à priori; that is to say, the à priori possibility of cognition and the à priori use of it are transcendental. Therefore neither is space, nor any à priori geometrical determination of space, a transcendental Representation, but only the knowledge that such a representation is not of empirical origin, and the possibility of its relating to objects of experience, although itself à priori, can be called transcendental. So also, the application of space to objects in general would be transcendental; but if it be limited to objects of sense it is empirical. Thus, the distinction of the transcendental and empirical belongs only to the critique of cognitions, and does not concern the relation of these to their object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","text":"Accordingly, in the expectation that there may perhaps be conceptions which relate à priori to objects, not as pure or sensuous intuitions, but merely as acts of pure thought (which are therefore conceptions, but neither of empirical nor æsthetical origin)—in this expectation, I say, we form to ourselves, by anticipation, the idea of a science of pure understanding and rational cognition, by means of which we may cogitate objects entirely à priori. A science of this kind, which should determine the origin, the extent, and the objective validity of such cognitions, must be called transcendental logic, because it has not, like general logic, to do with the laws of understanding and reason in relation to empirical as well as pure rational cognitions without distinction, but concerns itself with these only in an à priori relation to objects.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ae5dd74dd038bd5e71c0a06d24d48fbe1310809d1fcecc84758da603c2ba709f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IDGL","text":"### III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nThe old question with which people sought to push logicians into a corner, so that they must either have recourse to pitiful sophisms or confess their ignorance, and consequently the vanity of their whole art, is this: “What is truth?” The definition of the word truth, to wit, “the accordance of the cognition with its object,” is presupposed in the question; but we desire to be told, in the answer to it, what is the universal and secure criterion of the truth of every cognition.\n\nTo know what questions we may reasonably propose is in itself a strong evidence of sagacity and intelligence. For if a question be in itself absurd and unsusceptible of a rational answer, it is attended with the danger—not to mention the shame that falls upon the person who proposes it—of seducing the unguarded listener into making absurd answers, and we are presented with the ridiculous spectacle of one (as the ancients said) “milking the he-goat, and the other holding a sieve.”\n\nIf truth consists in the accordance of a cognition with its object, this object must be, ipso facto, distinguished from all others; for a cognition is false if it does not accord with the object to which it relates, although it contains something which may be affirmed of other objects. Now an universal criterion of truth would be that which is valid for all cognitions, without distinction of their objects. But it is evident that since, in the case of such a criterion, we make abstraction of all the content of a cognition (that is, of all relation to its object), and truth relates precisely to this content, it must be utterly absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of cognition; and that, accordingly, a sufficient, and at the same time universal, test of truth cannot possibly be found. As we have already termed the content of a cognition its matter, we shall say: “Of the truth of our cognitions in respect of their matter, no universal test can be demanded, because such a demand is self-contradictory.”\n\nOn the other hand, with regard to our cognition in respect of its mere form (excluding all content), it is equally manifest that logic, in so far as it exhibits the universal and necessary laws of the understanding, must in these very laws present us with criteria of truth. Whatever contradicts these rules is false, because thereby the understanding is made to contradict its own universal laws of thought; that is, to contradict itself. These criteria, however, apply solely to the form of truth, that is, of thought in general, and in so far they are perfectly accurate, yet not sufficient. For although a cognition may be perfectly accurate as to logical form, that is, not self-contradictory, it is notwithstanding quite possible that it may not stand in agreement with its object. Consequently, the merely logical criterion of truth, namely, the accordance of a cognition with the universal and formal laws of understanding and reason, is nothing more than the conditio sine qua non, or negative condition of all truth. Farther than this logic cannot go, and the error which depends not on the form, but on the content of the cognition, it has no test to discover.\n\nGeneral logic, then, resolves the whole formal business of understanding and reason into its elements, and exhibits them as principles of all logical judging of our cognitions. This part of logic may, therefore, be called analytic, and is at least the negative test of truth, because all cognitions must first of an be estimated and tried according to these laws before we proceed to investigate them in respect of their content, in order to discover whether they contain positive truth in regard to their object. Because, however, the mere form of a cognition, accurately as it may accord with logical laws, is insufficient to supply us with material (objective) truth, no one, by means of logic alone, can venture to predicate anything of or decide concerning objects, unless he has obtained, independently of logic, well-grounded information about them, in order afterwards to examine, according to logical laws, into the use and connection, in a cohering whole, of that information, or, what is still better, merely to test it by them. Notwithstanding, there lies so seductive a charm in the possession of a specious art like this—an art which gives to all our cognitions the form of the understanding, although with respect to the content thereof we may be sadly deficient—that general logic, which is merely a canon of judgement, has been employed as an organon for the actual production, or rather for the semblance of production, of objective assertions, and has thus been grossly misapplied. Now general logic, in its assumed character of organon, is called dialectic.\n\nDifferent as are the significations in which the ancients used this term for a science or an art, we may safely infer, from their actual employment of it, that with them it was nothing else than a logic of illusion—a sophistical art for giving ignorance, nay, even intentional sophistries, the colouring of truth, in which the thoroughness of procedure which logic requires was imitated, and their topic employed to cloak the empty pretensions. Now it may be taken as a safe and useful warning, that general logic, considered as an organon, must always be a logic of illusion, that is, be dialectical, for, as it teaches us nothing whatever respecting the content of our cognitions, but merely the formal conditions of their accordance with the understanding, which do not relate to and are quite indifferent in respect of objects, any attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon) in order to extend and enlarge the range of our knowledge must end in mere prating; any one being able to maintain or oppose, with some appearance of truth, any single assertion whatever.\n\nSuch instruction is quite unbecoming the dignity of philosophy. For these reasons we have chosen to denominate this part of logic dialectic, in the sense of a critique of dialectical illusion, and we wish the term to be so understood in this place.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","text":"### III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nThe old question with which people sought to push logicians into a corner, so that they must either have recourse to pitiful sophisms or confess their ignorance, and consequently the vanity of their whole art, is this: “What is truth?” The definition of the word truth, to wit, “the accordance of the cognition with its object,” is presupposed in the question; but we desire to be told, in the answer to it, what is the universal and secure criterion of the truth of every cognition.\n\nTo know what questions we may reasonably propose is in itself a strong evidence of sagacity and intelligence. For if a question be in itself absurd and unsusceptible of a rational answer, it is attended with the danger—not to mention the shame that falls upon the person who proposes it—of seducing the unguarded listener into making absurd answers, and we are presented with the ridiculous spectacle of one (as the ancients said) “milking the he-goat, and the other holding a sieve.”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","text":"If truth consists in the accordance of a cognition with its object, this object must be, ipso facto, distinguished from all others; for a cognition is false if it does not accord with the object to which it relates, although it contains something which may be affirmed of other objects. Now an universal criterion of truth would be that which is valid for all cognitions, without distinction of their objects. But it is evident that since, in the case of such a criterion, we make abstraction of all the content of a cognition (that is, of all relation to its object), and truth relates precisely to this content, it must be utterly absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of cognition; and that, accordingly, a sufficient, and at the same time universal, test of truth cannot possibly be found. As we have already termed the content of a cognition its matter, we shall say: “Of the truth of our cognitions in respect of their matter, no universal test can be demanded, because such a demand is self-contradictory.”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","text":"On the other hand, with regard to our cognition in respect of its mere form (excluding all content), it is equally manifest that logic, in so far as it exhibits the universal and necessary laws of the understanding, must in these very laws present us with criteria of truth. Whatever contradicts these rules is false, because thereby the understanding is made to contradict its own universal laws of thought; that is, to contradict itself. These criteria, however, apply solely to the form of truth, that is, of thought in general, and in so far they are perfectly accurate, yet not sufficient. For although a cognition may be perfectly accurate as to logical form, that is, not self-contradictory, it is notwithstanding quite possible that it may not stand in agreement with its object. Consequently, the merely logical criterion of truth, namely, the accordance of a cognition with the universal and formal laws of understanding and reason, is nothing more than the conditio sine qua non, or negative condition of all truth. Farther than this logic cannot go, and the error which depends not on the form, but on the content of the cognition, it has no test to discover.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","text":"General logic, then, resolves the whole formal business of understanding and reason into its elements, and exhibits them as principles of all logical judging of our cognitions. This part of logic may, therefore, be called analytic, and is at least the negative test of truth, because all cognitions must first of an be estimated and tried according to these laws before we proceed to investigate them in respect of their content, in order to discover whether they contain positive truth in regard to their object. Because, however, the mere form of a cognition, accurately as it may accord with logical laws, is insufficient to supply us with material (objective) truth, no one, by means of logic alone, can venture to predicate anything of or decide concerning objects, unless he has obtained, independently of logic, well-grounded information about them, in order afterwards to examine, according to logical laws, into the use and connection, in a cohering whole, of that information, or, what is still better, merely to test it by them. Notwithstanding, there lies so seductive a charm in the possession of a specious art like this—an art which gives to all our cognitions the form of the understanding, although with respect to the content thereof we may be sadly deficient—that general logic, which is merely a canon of judgement, has been employed as an organon for the actual production, or rather for the semblance of production, of objective assertions, and has thus been grossly misapplied. Now general logic, in its assumed character of organon, is called dialectic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","text":"Different as are the significations in which the ancients used this term for a science or an art, we may safely infer, from their actual employment of it, that with them it was nothing else than a logic of illusion—a sophistical art for giving ignorance, nay, even intentional sophistries, the colouring of truth, in which the thoroughness of procedure which logic requires was imitated, and their topic employed to cloak the empty pretensions. Now it may be taken as a safe and useful warning, that general logic, considered as an organon, must always be a logic of illusion, that is, be dialectical, for, as it teaches us nothing whatever respecting the content of our cognitions, but merely the formal conditions of their accordance with the understanding, which do not relate to and are quite indifferent in respect of objects, any attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon) in order to extend and enlarge the range of our knowledge must end in mere prating; any one being able to maintain or oppose, with some appearance of truth, any single assertion whatever.\n\nSuch instruction is quite unbecoming the dignity of philosophy. For these reasons we have chosen to denominate this part of logic dialectic, in the sense of a critique of dialectical illusion, and we wish the term to be so understood in this place.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"53d51769011894c47d9a85c0a0726c450b3b079a048448890254968ce74d0706","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IDTL","text":"### IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nIn transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as in transcendental æsthetic the sensibility) and select from our cognition merely that part of thought which has its origin in the understanding alone. The exercise of this pure cognition, however, depends upon this as its condition, that objects to which it may be applied be given to us in intuition, for without intuition the whole of our cognition is without objects, and is therefore quite void. That part of transcendental logic, then, which treats of the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and of the principles without which no object at all can be thought, is transcendental analytic, and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it, without losing at the same time all content, that is, losing all reference to an object, and therefore all truth. But because we are very easily seduced into employing these pure cognitions and principles of the understanding by themselves, and that even beyond the boundaries of experience, which yet is the only source whence we can obtain matter (objects) on which those pure conceptions may be employed—understanding runs the risk of making, by means of empty sophisms, a material and objective use of the mere formal principles of the pure understanding, and of passing judgements on objects without distinction—objects which are not given to us, nay, perhaps cannot be given to us in any way. Now, as it ought properly to be only a canon for judging of the empirical use of the understanding, this kind of logic is misused when we seek to employ it as an organon of the universal and unlimited exercise of the understanding, and attempt with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, affirm, and determine respecting objects in general. In this case the exercise of the pure understanding becomes dialectical. The second part of our transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of dialectical illusion, and this critique we shall term transcendental dialectic—not meaning it as an art of producing dogmatically such illusion (an art which is unfortunately too current among the practitioners of metaphysical juggling), but as a critique of understanding and reason in regard to their hyperphysical use. This critique will expose the groundless nature of the pretensions of these two faculties, and invalidate their claims to the discovery and enlargement of our cognitions merely by means of transcendental principles, and show that the proper employment of these faculties is to test the judgements made by the pure understanding, and to guard it from sophistical delusion.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","text":"### IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nIn transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as in transcendental æsthetic the sensibility) and select from our cognition merely that part of thought which has its origin in the understanding alone. The exercise of this pure cognition, however, depends upon this as its condition, that objects to which it may be applied be given to us in intuition, for without intuition the whole of our cognition is without objects, and is therefore quite void. That part of transcendental logic, then, which treats of the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and of the principles without which no object at all can be thought, is transcendental analytic, and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it, without losing at the same time all content, that is, losing all reference to an object, and therefore all truth. But because we are very easily seduced into employing these pure cognitions and principles of the understanding by themselves, and that even beyond the boundaries of experience, which yet is the only source whence we can obtain matter (objects) on which those pure conceptions may be employed—understanding runs the risk of making, by means of empty sophisms, a material and objective use of the mere formal principles of the pure understanding, and of passing judgements on objects without distinction—objects which are not given to us, nay, perhaps cannot be given to us in any way. Now, as it ought properly to be only a canon for judging of the empirical use of the understanding, this kind of logic is misused when we seek to employ it as an organon of the universal and unlimited exercise of the understanding, and attempt with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, affirm, and determine respecting objects in general. In this case the exercise of the pure understanding becomes dialectical. The second part of our transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of dialectical illusion, and this critique we shall term transcendental dialectic—not meaning it as an art of producing dogmatically such illusion (an art which is unfortunately too current among the practitioners of metaphysical juggling), but as a critique of understanding and reason in regard to their hyperphysical use. This critique will expose the groundless nature of the pretensions of these two faculties, and invalidate their claims to the discovery and enlargement of our cognitions merely by means of transcendental principles, and show that the proper employment of these faculties is to test the judgements made by the pure understanding, and to guard it from sophistical delusion.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"685d70282aa91801762cfcfb45078e283cd7a9a633d2e48f81f07a7f9bda3587","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL","title":"FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1","order":22,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_FDTL","text":"### FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1\n\nTranscendental analytic is the dissection of the whole of our à priori knowledge into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. In order to effect our purpose, it is necessary: (1) That the conceptions be pure and not empirical; (2) That they belong not to intuition and sensibility, but to thought and understanding; (3) That they be elementary conceptions, and as such, quite different from deduced or compound conceptions; (4) That our table of these elementary conceptions be complete, and fill up the whole sphere of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be accepted with confidence on the guarantee of a mere estimate of its existence in an aggregate formed only by means of repeated experiments and attempts. The completeness which we require is possible only by means of an idea of the totality of the à priori cognition of the understanding, and through the thereby determined division of the conceptions which form the said whole; consequently, only by means of their connection in a system. Pure understanding distinguishes itself not merely from everything empirical, but also completely from all sensibility. It is a unity self-subsistent, self-sufficient, and not to be enlarged by any additions from without. Hence the sum of its cognition constitutes a system to be determined by and comprised under an idea; and the completeness and articulation of this system can at the same time serve as a test of the correctness and genuineness of all the parts of cognition that belong to it. The whole of this part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which the one contains the conceptions, and the other the principles of pure understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","text":"### FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1\n\nTranscendental analytic is the dissection of the whole of our à priori knowledge into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. In order to effect our purpose, it is necessary: (1) That the conceptions be pure and not empirical; (2) That they belong not to intuition and sensibility, but to thought and understanding; (3) That they be elementary conceptions, and as such, quite different from deduced or compound conceptions; (4) That our table of these elementary conceptions be complete, and fill up the whole sphere of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be accepted with confidence on the guarantee of a mere estimate of its existence in an aggregate formed only by means of repeated experiments and attempts. The completeness which we require is possible only by means of an idea of the totality of the à priori cognition of the understanding, and through the thereby determined division of the conceptions which form the said whole; consequently, only by means of their connection in a system. Pure understanding distinguishes itself not merely from everything empirical, but also completely from all sensibility. It is a unity self-subsistent, self-sufficient, and not to be enlarged by any additions from without. Hence the sum of its cognition constitutes a system to be determined by and comprised under an idea; and the completeness and articulation of this system can at the same time serve as a test of the correctness and genuineness of all the parts of cognition that belong to it. The whole of this part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which the one contains the conceptions, and the other the principles of pure understanding.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"52f8d30f77c35337fb54ead4d2a5802e519888d55e99530b8ba2e61ead568d3e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","order":23,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_BIAC","text":"### BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2\n\nBy the term Analytic of Conceptions, I do not understand the analysis of these, or the usual process in philosophical investigations of dissecting the conceptions which present themselves, according to their content, and so making them clear; but I mean the hitherto little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself, in order to investigate the possibility of conceptions à priori, by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analysing the pure use of this faculty. For this is the proper duty of a transcendental philosophy; what remains is the logical treatment of the conceptions in philosophy in general. We shall therefore follow up the pure conceptions even to their germs and beginnings in the human understanding, in which they lie, until they are developed on occasions presented by experience, and, freed by the same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, are set forth in their unalloyed purity.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","text":"### BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2\n\nBy the term Analytic of Conceptions, I do not understand the analysis of these, or the usual process in philosophical investigations of dissecting the conceptions which present themselves, according to their content, and so making them clear; but I mean the hitherto little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself, in order to investigate the possibility of conceptions à priori, by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analysing the pure use of this faculty. For this is the proper duty of a transcendental philosophy; what remains is the logical treatment of the conceptions in philosophy in general. We shall therefore follow up the pure conceptions even to their germs and beginnings in the human understanding, in which they lie, until they are developed on occasions presented by experience, and, freed by the same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, are set forth in their unalloyed purity.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"616d0d5522dfb8ff4f4e62eccf1a677c09bd432b9bcb9841a9af6d4bca06dde3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3","title":"Introductory § 3","order":24,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_INT3","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Introductory § 3\n\nWhen we call into play a faculty of cognition, different conceptions manifest themselves according to the different circumstances, and make known this faculty, and assemble themselves into a more or less extensive collection, according to the time or penetration that has been applied to the consideration of them. Where this process, conducted as it is mechanically, so to speak, will end, cannot be determined with certainty. Besides, the conceptions which we discover in this haphazard manner present themselves by no means in order and systematic unity, but are at last coupled together only according to resemblances to each other, and arranged in series, according to the quantity of their content, from the simpler to the more complex—series which are anything but systematic, though not altogether without a certain kind of method in their construction.\n\nTranscendental philosophy has the advantage, and moreover the duty, of searching for its conceptions according to a principle; because these conceptions spring pure and unmixed out of the understanding as an absolute unity, and therefore must be connected with each other according to one conception or idea. A connection of this kind, however, furnishes us with a ready prepared rule, by which its proper place may be assigned to every pure conception of the understanding, and the completeness of the system of all be determined à priori—both which would otherwise have been dependent on mere choice or chance.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Introductory § 3\n\nWhen we call into play a faculty of cognition, different conceptions manifest themselves according to the different circumstances, and make known this faculty, and assemble themselves into a more or less extensive collection, according to the time or penetration that has been applied to the consideration of them. Where this process, conducted as it is mechanically, so to speak, will end, cannot be determined with certainty. Besides, the conceptions which we discover in this haphazard manner present themselves by no means in order and systematic unity, but are at last coupled together only according to resemblances to each other, and arranged in series, according to the quantity of their content, from the simpler to the more complex—series which are anything but systematic, though not altogether without a certain kind of method in their construction.\n\nTranscendental philosophy has the advantage, and moreover the duty, of searching for its conceptions according to a principle; because these conceptions spring pure and unmixed out of the understanding as an absolute unity, and therefore must be connected with each other according to one conception or idea. A connection of this kind, however, furnishes us with a ready prepared rule, by which its proper place may be assigned to every pure conception of the understanding, and the completeness of the system of all be determined à priori—both which would otherwise have been dependent on mere choice or chance.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e4c1bf9d0ba2abc5e0f19eb301014dace30f48efd2935725f4bf8fd73d8095c2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Introductory § 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","order":25,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SILU","text":"### Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4\n\nThe understanding was defined above only negatively, as a non-sensuous faculty of cognition. Now, independently of sensibility, we cannot possibly have any intuition; consequently, the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of cognition, except through conceptions; consequently, the cognition of every, at least of every human, understanding is a cognition through conceptions—not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensuous, depend on affections; conceptions, therefore, upon functions. By the word function I understand the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation. Conceptions, then, are based on the spontaneity of thought, as sensuous intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Now, the understanding cannot make any other use of these conceptions than to judge by means of them. As no representation, except an intuition, relates immediately to its object, a conception never relates immediately to an object, but only to some other representation thereof, be that an intuition or itself a conception. A judgement, therefore, is the mediate cognition of an object, consequently the representation of a representation of it. In every judgement there is a conception which applies to, and is valid for many other conceptions, and which among these comprehends also a given representation, this last being immediately connected with an object. For example, in the judgement—“All bodies are divisible,” our conception of divisible applies to various other conceptions; among these, however, it is here particularly applied to the conception of body, and this conception of body relates to certain phenomena which occur to us. These objects, therefore, are mediately represented by the conception of divisibility. All judgements, accordingly, are functions of unity in our representations, inasmuch as, instead of an immediate, a higher representation, which comprises this and various others, is used for our cognition of the object, and thereby many possible cognitions are collected into one. But we can reduce all acts of the understanding to judgements, so that understanding may be represented as the faculty of judging. For it is, according to what has been said above, a faculty of thought. Now thought is cognition by means of conceptions. But conceptions, as predicates of possible judgements, relate to some representation of a yet undetermined object. Thus the conception of body indicates something—for example, metal—which can be cognized by means of that conception. It is therefore a conception, for the reason alone that other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate to a possible judgement; for example: “Every metal is a body.” All the functions of the understanding therefore can be discovered, when we can completely exhibit the functions of unity in judgements. And that this may be effected very easily, the following section will show.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4\n\nThe understanding was defined above only negatively, as a non-sensuous faculty of cognition. Now, independently of sensibility, we cannot possibly have any intuition; consequently, the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of cognition, except through conceptions; consequently, the cognition of every, at least of every human, understanding is a cognition through conceptions—not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensuous, depend on affections; conceptions, therefore, upon functions. By the word function I understand the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation. Conceptions, then, are based on the spontaneity of thought, as sensuous intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Now, the understanding cannot make any other use of these conceptions than to judge by means of them. As no representation, except an intuition, relates immediately to its object, a conception never relates immediately to an object, but only to some other representation thereof, be that an intuition or itself a conception. A judgement, therefore, is the mediate cognition of an object, consequently the representation of a representation of it. In every judgement there is a conception which applies to, and is valid for many other conceptions, and which among these comprehends also a given representation, this last being immediately connected with an object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","text":"For example, in the judgement—“All bodies are divisible,” our conception of divisible applies to various other conceptions; among these, however, it is here particularly applied to the conception of body, and this conception of body relates to certain phenomena which occur to us. These objects, therefore, are mediately represented by the conception of divisibility. All judgements, accordingly, are functions of unity in our representations, inasmuch as, instead of an immediate, a higher representation, which comprises this and various others, is used for our cognition of the object, and thereby many possible cognitions are collected into one. But we can reduce all acts of the understanding to judgements, so that understanding may be represented as the faculty of judging. For it is, according to what has been said above, a faculty of thought. Now thought is cognition by means of conceptions. But conceptions, as predicates of possible judgements, relate to some representation of a yet undetermined object. Thus the conception of body indicates something—for example, metal—which can be cognized by means of that conception. It is therefore a conception, for the reason alone that other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate to a possible judgement; for example: “Every metal is a body.” All the functions of the understanding therefore can be discovered, when we can completely exhibit the functions of unity in judgements. And that this may be effected very easily, the following section will show.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4ef89e750a822653bac22f9d5c6aa85df1d361618117bfc88a3cb446ae65ecfa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","order":26,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SILF","text":"### Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5\n\nIf we abstract all the content of a judgement, and consider only the intellectual form thereof, we find that the function of thought in a judgement can be brought under four heads, of which each contains three momenta. These may be conveniently represented in the following table:\n\n                                    1\n                         _Quantity of judgements_\n                                Universal\n                                Particular\n                                Singular\n\n                      2                           3\n                    _Quality                   Relation_\n                  Affirmative                Categorical\n                  Negative                   Hypothetical\n                  Infinite                   Disjunctive\n\n                                    4\n                                 _Modality_\n                               Problematical\n                               Assertorical\n                               Apodeictical\n\nAs this division appears to differ in some, though not essential points, from the usual technique of logicians, the following observations, for the prevention of otherwise possible misunderstanding, will not be without their use.\n\n1. Logicians say, with justice, that in the use of judgements in syllogisms, singular judgements may be treated like universal ones. For, precisely because a singular judgement has no extent at all, its predicate cannot refer to a part of that which is contained in the conception of the subject and be excluded from the rest. The predicate is valid for the whole conception just as if it were a general conception, and had extent, to the whole of which the predicate applied. On the other hand, let us compare a singular with a general judgement, merely as a cognition, in regard to quantity. The singular judgement relates to the general one, as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different. Thus, if we estimate a singular judgement (_judicium singulare_) not merely according to its intrinsic validity as a judgement, but also as a cognition generally, according to its quantity in comparison with that of other cognitions, it is then entirely different from a general judgement (_judicium commune_), and in a complete table of the momenta of thought deserves a separate place—though, indeed, this would not be necessary in a logic limited merely to the consideration of the use of judgements in reference to each other.\n\n2. In like manner, in transcendental logic, infinite must be distinguished from affirmative judgements, although in general logic they are rightly enough classed under affirmative. General logic abstracts all content of the predicate (though it be negative), and only considers whether the said predicate be affirmed or denied of the subject. But transcendental logic considers also the worth or content of this logical affirmation—an affirmation by means of a merely negative predicate, and inquires how much the sum total of our cognition gains by this affirmation. For example, if I say of the soul, “It is not mortal”—by this negative judgement I should at least ward off error. Now, by the proposition, “The soul is not mortal,” I have, in respect of the logical form, really affirmed, inasmuch as I thereby place the soul in the unlimited sphere of immortal beings. Now, because of the whole sphere of possible existences, the mortal occupies one part, and the immortal the other, neither more nor less is affirmed by the proposition than that the soul is one among the infinite multitude of things which remain over, when I take away the whole mortal part. But by this proceeding we accomplish only this much, that the infinite sphere of all possible existences is in so far limited that the mortal is excluded from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining part of the extent of this sphere. But this part remains, notwithstanding this exception, infinite, and more and more parts may be taken away from the whole sphere, without in the slightest degree thereby augmenting or affirmatively determining our conception of the soul. These judgements, therefore, infinite in respect of their logical extent, are, in respect of the content of their cognition, merely limitative; and are consequently entitled to a place in our transcendental table of all the momenta of thought in judgements, because the function of the understanding exercised by them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure à priori cognition.\n\n3. All relations of thought in judgements are those (a) of the predicate to the subject; (b) of the principle to its consequence; (c) of the divided cognition and all the members of the division to each other. In the first of these three classes, we consider only two conceptions; in the second, two judgements; in the third, several judgements in relation to each other. The hypothetical proposition, “If perfect justice exists, the obstinately wicked are punished,” contains properly the relation to each other of two propositions, namely, “Perfect justice exists,” and “The obstinately wicked are punished.” Whether these propositions are in themselves true is a question not here decided. Nothing is cogitated by means of this judgement except a certain consequence. Finally, the disjunctive judgement contains a relation of two or more propositions to each other—a relation not of consequence, but of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one proposition excludes that of the other. But it contains at the same time a relation of community, in so far as all the propositions taken together fill up the sphere of the cognition. The disjunctive judgement contains, therefore, the relation of the parts of the whole sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a complemental part of the sphere of the other, each contributing to form the sum total of the divided cognition. Take, for example, the proposition, “The world exists either through blind chance, or through internal necessity, or through an external cause.” Each of these propositions embraces a part of the sphere of our possible cognition as to the existence of a world; all of them taken together, the whole sphere. To take the cognition out of one of these spheres, is equivalent to placing it in one of the others; and, on the other hand, to place it in one sphere is equivalent to taking it out of the rest. There is, therefore, in a disjunctive judgement a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude each other, yet thereby determine, as a whole, the true cognition, inasmuch as, taken together, they make up the complete content of a particular given cognition. And this is all that I find necessary, for the sake of what follows, to remark in this place.\n\n4. The modality of judgements is a quite peculiar function, with this distinguishing characteristic, that it contributes nothing to the content of a judgement (for besides quantity, quality, and relation, there is nothing more that constitutes the content of a judgement), but concerns itself only with the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematical judgements are those in which the affirmation or negation is accepted as merely possible (ad libitum). In the assertorical, we regard the proposition as real (true); in the apodeictical, we look on it as necessary.[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-13) Thus the two judgements (antecedens et consequens), the relation of which constitutes a hypothetical judgement, likewise those (the members of the division) in whose reciprocity the disjunctive consists, are only problematical. In the example above given the proposition, “There exists perfect justice,” is not stated assertorically, but as an ad libitum judgement, which someone may choose to adopt, and the consequence alone is assertorical. Hence such judgements may be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of our cognition of the truth. Thus the proposition, “The world exists only by blind chance,” is in the disjunctive judgement of problematical import only: that is to say, one may accept it for the moment, and it helps us (like the indication of the wrong road among all the roads that one can take) to find out the true proposition. The problematical proposition is, therefore, that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective); that is, it expresses a free choice to admit the validity of such a proposition—a merely arbitrary reception of it into the understanding. The assertorical speaks of logical reality or truth; as, for example, in a hypothetical syllogism, the antecedens presents itself in a problematical form in the major, in an assertorical form in the minor, and it shows that the proposition is in harmony with the laws of the understanding. The apodeictical proposition cogitates the assertorical as determined by these very laws of the understanding, consequently as affirming à priori, and in this manner it expresses logical necessity. Now because all is here gradually incorporated with the understanding—inasmuch as in the first place we judge problematically; then accept assertorically our judgement as true; lastly, affirm it as inseparably united with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictical—we may safely reckon these three functions of modality as so many momenta of thought.\n\n[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-13) Just as if thought were in the first instance a function of the understanding; in the second, of judgement; in the third, of reason. A remark which will be explained in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5\n\nIf we abstract all the content of a judgement, and consider only the intellectual form thereof, we find that the function of thought in a judgement can be brought under four heads, of which each contains three momenta. These may be conveniently represented in the following table:\n\n                                    1\n                         _Quantity of judgements_\n                                Universal\n                                Particular\n                                Singular\n\n                      2                           3\n                    _Quality                   Relation_\n                  Affirmative                Categorical\n                  Negative                   Hypothetical\n                  Infinite                   Disjunctive\n\n                                    4\n                                 _Modality_\n                               Problematical\n                               Assertorical\n                               Apodeictical\n\nAs this division appears to differ in some, though not essential points, from the usual technique of logicians, the following observations, for the prevention of otherwise possible misunderstanding, will not be without their use.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","text":"1. Logicians say, with justice, that in the use of judgements in syllogisms, singular judgements may be treated like universal ones. For, precisely because a singular judgement has no extent at all, its predicate cannot refer to a part of that which is contained in the conception of the subject and be excluded from the rest. The predicate is valid for the whole conception just as if it were a general conception, and had extent, to the whole of which the predicate applied. On the other hand, let us compare a singular with a general judgement, merely as a cognition, in regard to quantity. The singular judgement relates to the general one, as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different. Thus, if we estimate a singular judgement (_judicium singulare_) not merely according to its intrinsic validity as a judgement, but also as a cognition generally, according to its quantity in comparison with that of other cognitions, it is then entirely different from a general judgement (_judicium commune_), and in a complete table of the momenta of thought deserves a separate place—though, indeed, this would not be necessary in a logic limited merely to the consideration of the use of judgements in reference to each other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","text":"2. In like manner, in transcendental logic, infinite must be distinguished from affirmative judgements, although in general logic they are rightly enough classed under affirmative. General logic abstracts all content of the predicate (though it be negative), and only considers whether the said predicate be affirmed or denied of the subject. But transcendental logic considers also the worth or content of this logical affirmation—an affirmation by means of a merely negative predicate, and inquires how much the sum total of our cognition gains by this affirmation. For example, if I say of the soul, “It is not mortal”—by this negative judgement I should at least ward off error. Now, by the proposition, “The soul is not mortal,” I have, in respect of the logical form, really affirmed, inasmuch as I thereby place the soul in the unlimited sphere of immortal beings. Now, because of the whole sphere of possible existences, the mortal occupies one part, and the immortal the other, neither more nor less is affirmed by the proposition than that the soul is one among the infinite multitude of things which remain over, when I take away the whole mortal part. But by this proceeding we accomplish only this much, that the infinite sphere of all possible existences is in so far limited that the mortal is excluded from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining part of the extent of this sphere. But this part remains, notwithstanding this exception, infinite, and more and more parts may be taken away from the whole sphere, without in the slightest degree thereby augmenting or affirmatively determining our conception of the soul. These judgements, therefore, infinite in respect of their logical extent, are, in respect of the content of their cognition, merely limitative; and are consequently entitled to a place in our transcendental table of all the momenta of thought in judgements, because the function of the understanding exercised by them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure à priori cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","text":"3. All relations of thought in judgements are those (a) of the predicate to the subject; (b) of the principle to its consequence; (c) of the divided cognition and all the members of the division to each other. In the first of these three classes, we consider only two conceptions; in the second, two judgements; in the third, several judgements in relation to each other. The hypothetical proposition, “If perfect justice exists, the obstinately wicked are punished,” contains properly the relation to each other of two propositions, namely, “Perfect justice exists,” and “The obstinately wicked are punished.” Whether these propositions are in themselves true is a question not here decided. Nothing is cogitated by means of this judgement except a certain consequence. Finally, the disjunctive judgement contains a relation of two or more propositions to each other—a relation not of consequence, but of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one proposition excludes that of the other. But it contains at the same time a relation of community, in so far as all the propositions taken together fill up the sphere of the cognition. The disjunctive judgement contains, therefore, the relation of the parts of the whole sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a complemental part of the sphere of the other, each contributing to form the sum total of the divided cognition. Take, for example, the proposition, “The world exists either through blind chance, or through internal necessity, or through an external cause.” Each of these propositions embraces a part of the sphere of our possible cognition as to the existence of a world; all of them taken together, the whole sphere. To take the cognition out of one of these spheres, is equivalent to placing it in one of the others; and, on the other hand, to place it in one sphere is equivalent to taking it out of the rest. There is, therefore, in a disjunctive judgement a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude each other, yet thereby determine, as a whole, the true cognition, inasmuch as, taken together, they make up the complete content of a particular given cognition. And this is all that I find necessary, for the sake of what follows, to remark in this place.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","text":"4. The modality of judgements is a quite peculiar function, with this distinguishing characteristic, that it contributes nothing to the content of a judgement (for besides quantity, quality, and relation, there is nothing more that constitutes the content of a judgement), but concerns itself only with the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematical judgements are those in which the affirmation or negation is accepted as merely possible (ad libitum). In the assertorical, we regard the proposition as real (true); in the apodeictical, we look on it as necessary.[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-13) Thus the two judgements (antecedens et consequens), the relation of which constitutes a hypothetical judgement, likewise those (the members of the division) in whose reciprocity the disjunctive consists, are only problematical. In the example above given the proposition, “There exists perfect justice,” is not stated assertorically, but as an ad libitum judgement, which someone may choose to adopt, and the consequence alone is assertorical. Hence such judgements may be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of our cognition of the truth. Thus the proposition, “The world exists only by blind chance,” is in the disjunctive judgement of problematical import only: that is to say, one may accept it for the moment, and it helps us (like the indication of the wrong road among all the roads that one can take) to find out the true proposition. The problematical proposition is, therefore, that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective); that is, it expresses a free choice to admit the validity of such a proposition—a merely arbitrary reception of it into the understanding. The assertorical speaks of logical reality or truth; as, for example, in a hypothetical syllogism, the antecedens presents itself in a problematical form in the major, in an assertorical form in the minor, and it shows that the proposition is in harmony with the laws of the understanding. The apodeictical proposition cogitates the assertorical as determined by these very laws of the understanding, consequently as affirming à priori, and in this manner it expresses logical necessity. Now because all is here gradually incorporated with the understanding—inasmuch as in the first place we judge problematically; then accept assertorically our judgement as true; lastly, affirm it as inseparably united with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictical—we may safely reckon these three functions of modality as so many momenta of thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0006","text":"[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-13) Just as if thought were in the first instance a function of the understanding; in the second, of judgement; in the third, of reason. A remark which will be explained in the sequel.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7b06cf49eb113b4ef9bfb4b94e030f38c430ff78dc31801c59670fb28e1162f3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","order":27,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIPC","text":"### Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6\n\nGeneral logic, as has been repeatedly said, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, and expects to receive representations from some other quarter, in order, by means of analysis, to convert them into conceptions. On the contrary, transcendental logic has lying before it the manifold content of à priori sensibility, which transcendental æsthetic presents to it in order to give matter to the pure conceptions of the understanding, without which transcendental logic would have no content, and be therefore utterly void. Now space and time contain an infinite diversity of determinations of pure à priori intuition, but are nevertheless the condition of the mind’s receptivity, under which alone it can obtain representations of objects, and which, consequently, must always affect the conception of these objects. But the spontaneity of thought requires that this diversity be examined after a certain manner, received into the mind, and connected, in order afterwards to form a cognition out of it. This Process I call synthesis.\n\nBy the word synthesis, in its most general signification, I understand the process of joining different representations to each other and of comprehending their diversity in one cognition. This synthesis is pure when the diversity is not given empirically but à priori (as that in space and time). Our representations must be given previously to any analysis of them; and no conceptions can arise, quoad their content, analytically. But the synthesis of a diversity (be it given à priori or empirically) is the first requisite for the production of a cognition, which in its beginning, indeed, may be crude and confused, and therefore in need of analysis—still, synthesis is that by which alone the elements of our cognitions are collected and united into a certain content, consequently it is the first thing on which we must fix our attention, if we wish to investigate the origin of our knowledge.\n\nSynthesis, generally speaking, is, as we shall afterwards see, the mere operation of the imagination—a blind but indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no cognition whatever, but of the working of which we are seldom even conscious. But to reduce this synthesis to conceptions is a function of the understanding, by means of which we attain to cognition, in the proper meaning of the term.\n\nPure synthesis, represented generally, gives us the pure conception of the understanding. But by this pure synthesis, I mean that which rests upon a basis of à priori synthetical unity. Thus, our numeration (and this is more observable in large numbers) is a synthesis according to conceptions, because it takes place according to a common basis of unity (for example, the decade). By means of this conception, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.\n\nBy means of analysis different representations are brought under one conception—an operation of which general logic treats. On the other hand, the duty of transcendental logic is to reduce to conceptions, not representations, but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing which must be given to us for the sake of the à priori cognition of all objects, is the diversity of the pure intuition; the synthesis of this diversity by means of the imagination is the second; but this gives, as yet, no cognition. The conceptions which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetical unity, furnish the third requisite for the cognition of an object, and these conceptions are given by the understanding.\n\nThe same function which gives unity to the different representation in a judgement, gives also unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity we call the pure conception of the understanding. Thus, the same understanding, and by the same operations, whereby in conceptions, by means of analytical unity, it produced the logical form of a judgement, introduces, by means of the synthetical unity of the manifold in intuition, a transcendental content into its representations, on which account they are called pure conceptions of the understanding, and they apply à priori to objects, a result not within the power of general logic.\n\nIn this manner, there arise exactly so many pure conceptions of the understanding, applying à priori to objects of intuition in general, as there are logical functions in all possible judgements. For there is no other function or faculty existing in the understanding besides those enumerated in that table. These conceptions we shall, with Aristotle, call categories, our purpose being originally identical with his, notwithstanding the great difference in the execution.\n\n                     TABLE OF THE CATEGORIES\n\n                    1                         2\n\n              _Of Quantity                Of Quality_\n              Unity                      Reality\n              Plurality                  Negation\n              Totality                   Limitation\n\n                           3\n                      _Of Relation_\n   Of Inherence and Subsistence (substantia et accidens)\n   Of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)\n   Of Community (reciprocity between the agent and patient)\n\n                           4\n                     _Of Modality_\n              Possibility—Impossibility\n              Existence—Non-existence\n              Necessity—Contingence\n\nThis, then, is a catalogue of all the originally pure conceptions of the synthesis which the understanding contains à priori, and these conceptions alone entitle it to be called a pure understanding; inasmuch as only by them it can render the manifold of intuition conceivable, in other words, think an object of intuition. This division is made systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgement (which is just the same as the power of thought), and has not arisen rhapsodically from a search at haphazard after pure conceptions, respecting the full number of which we never could be certain, inasmuch as we employ induction alone in our search, without considering that in this way we can never understand wherefore precisely these conceptions, and none others, abide in the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker like Aristotle, to search for these fundamental conceptions. Destitute, however, of any guiding principle, he picked them up just as they occurred to him, and at first hunted out ten, which he called categories (predicaments). Afterwards be believed that he had discovered five others, which were added under the name of post predicaments. But his catalogue still remained defective. Besides, there are to be found among them some of the modes of pure sensibility (quando, ubi, situs, also prius, simul), and likewise an empirical conception (motus)—which can by no means belong to this genealogical register of the pure understanding. Moreover, there are deduced conceptions (actio, passio) enumerated among the original conceptions, and, of the latter, some are entirely wanting.\n\nWith regard to these, it is to be remarked, that the categories, as the true primitive conceptions of the pure understanding, have also their pure deduced conceptions, which, in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, must by no means be passed over; though in a merely critical essay we must be contented with the simple mention of the fact.\n\nLet it be allowed me to call these pure, but deduced conceptions of the understanding, the predicables of the pure understanding, in contradistinction to predicaments. If we are in possession of the original and primitive, the deduced and subsidiary conceptions can easily be added, and the genealogical tree of the understanding completely delineated. As my present aim is not to set forth a complete system, but merely the principles of one, I reserve this task for another time. It may be easily executed by any one who will refer to the ontological manuals, and subordinate to the category of causality, for example, the predicables of force, action, passion; to that of community, those of presence and resistance; to the categories of modality, those of origination, extinction, change; and so with the rest. The categories combined with the modes of pure sensibility, or with one another, afford a great number of deduced à priori conceptions; a complete enumeration of which would be a useful and not unpleasant, but in this place a perfectly dispensable, occupation.\n\nI purposely omit the definitions of the categories in this treatise. I shall analyse these conceptions only so far as is necessary for the doctrine of method, which is to form a part of this critique. In a system of pure reason, definitions of them would be with justice demanded of me, but to give them here would only bide from our view the main aim of our investigation, at the same time raising doubts and objections, the consideration of which, without injustice to our main purpose, may be very well postponed till another opportunity. Meanwhile, it ought to be sufficiently clear, from the little we have already said on this subject, that the formation of a complete vocabulary of pure conceptions, accompanied by all the requisite explanations, is not only a possible, but an easy undertaking. The compartments already exist; it is only necessary to fill them up; and a systematic topic like the present, indicates with perfect precision the proper place to which each conception belongs, while it readily points out any that have not yet been filled up.\n\n§ 7\n\nOur table of the categories suggests considerations of some importance, which may perhaps have significant results in regard to the scientific form of all rational cognitions. For, that this table is useful in the theoretical part of philosophy, nay, indispensable for the sketching of the complete plan of a science, so far as that science rests upon conceptions à priori, and for dividing it mathematically, according to fixed principles, is most manifest from the fact that it contains all the elementary conceptions of the understanding, nay, even the form of a system of these in the understanding itself, and consequently indicates all the momenta, and also the internal arrangement of a projected speculative science, as I have elsewhere shown.[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-14) Here follow some of these observations.\n\n[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-14) In the “Metaphysical Principles of Natural Science.”\n\nI. This table, which contains four classes of conceptions of the understanding, may, in the first instance, be divided into two classes, the first of which relates to objects of intuition—pure as well as empirical; the second, to the existence of these objects, either in relation to one another, or to the understanding.\n\nThe former of these classes of categories I would entitle the mathematical, and the latter the dynamical categories. The former, as we see, has no correlates; these are only to be found in the second class. This difference must have a ground in the nature of the human understanding.\n\nII. The number of the categories in each class is always the same, namely, three—a fact which also demands some consideration, because in all other cases division à priori through conceptions is necessarily dichotomy. It is to be added, that the third category in each triad always arises from the combination of the second with the first.\n\nThus totality is nothing else but plurality contemplated as unity; limitation is merely reality conjoined with negation; community is the causality of a substance, reciprocally determining, and determined by other substances; and finally, necessity is nothing but existence, which is given through the possibility itself. Let it not be supposed, however, that the third category is merely a deduced, and not a primitive conception of the pure understanding. For the conjunction of the first and second, in order to produce the third conception, requires a particular function of the understanding, which is by no means identical with those which are exercised in the first and second. Thus, the conception of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible, where the conceptions of multitude and unity exist (for example, in the representation of the infinite). Or, if I conjoin the conception of a cause with that of a substance, it does not follow that the conception of influence, that is, how one substance can be the cause of something in another substance, will be understood from that. Thus it is evident that a particular act of the understanding is here necessary; and so in the other instances.\n\nIII. With respect to one category, namely, that of community, which is found in the third class, it is not so easy as with the others to detect its accordance with the form of the disjunctive judgement which corresponds to it in the table of the logical functions.\n\nIn order to assure ourselves of this accordance, we must observe that in every disjunctive judgement, the sphere of the judgement (that is, the complex of all that is contained in it) is represented as a whole divided into parts; and, since one part cannot be contained in the other, they are cogitated as co-ordinated with, not subordinated to each other, so that they do not determine each other unilaterally, as in a linear series, but reciprocally, as in an aggregate—(if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded; and conversely).\n\nNow a like connection is cogitated in a whole of things; for one thing is not subordinated, as effect, to another as cause of its existence, but, on the contrary, is co-ordinated contemporaneously and reciprocally, as a cause in relation to the determination of the others (for example, in a body—the parts of which mutually attract and repel each other). And this is an entirely different kind of connection from that which we find in the mere relation of the cause to the effect (the principle to the consequence), for in such a connection the consequence does not in its turn determine the principle, and therefore does not constitute, with the latter, a whole—just as the Creator does not with the world make up a whole. The process of understanding by which it represents to itself the sphere of a divided conception, is employed also when we think of a thing as divisible; and in the same manner as the members of the division in the former exclude one another, and yet are connected in one sphere, so the understanding represents to itself the parts of the latter, as having—each of them—an existence (as substances), independently of the others, and yet as united in one whole.\n\n§ 8\n\nIn the transcendental philosophy of the ancients there exists one more leading division, which contains pure conceptions of the understanding, and which, although not numbered among the categories, ought, according to them, as conceptions à priori, to be valid of objects. But in this case they would augment the number of the categories; which cannot be. These are set forth in the proposition, so renowned among the schoolmen—‘_Quodlibet ens est UNUM, VERUM, BONUM_.’ Now, though the inferences from this principle were mere tautological propositions, and though it is allowed only by courtesy to retain a place in modern metaphysics, yet a thought which maintained itself for such a length of time, however empty it seems to be, deserves an investigation of its origin, and justifies the conjecture that it must be grounded in some law of the understanding, which, as is often the case, has only been erroneously interpreted. These pretended transcendental predicates are, in fact, nothing but logical requisites and criteria of all cognition of objects, and they employ, as the basis for this cognition, the categories of quantity, namely, unity, plurality, and totality. But these, which must be taken as material conditions, that is, as belonging to the possibility of things themselves, they employed merely in a formal signification, as belonging to the logical requisites of all cognition, and yet most unguardedly changed these criteria of thought into properties of objects, as things in themselves. Now, in every cognition of an object, there is unity of conception, which may be called qualitative unity, so far as by this term we understand only the unity in our connection of the manifold; for example, unity of the theme in a play, an oration, or a story. Secondly, there is truth in respect of the deductions from it. The more true deductions we have from a given conception, the more criteria of its objective reality. This we might call the qualitative plurality of characteristic marks, which belong to a conception as to a common foundation, but are not cogitated as a quantity in it. Thirdly, there is perfection—which consists in this, that the plurality falls back upon the unity of the conception, and accords completely with that conception and with no other. This we may denominate qualitative completeness. Hence it is evident that these logical criteria of the possibility of cognition are merely the three categories of quantity modified and transformed to suit an unauthorized manner of applying them. That is to say, the three categories, in which the unity in the production of the quantum must be homogeneous throughout, are transformed solely with a view to the connection of heterogeneous parts of cognition in one act of consciousness, by means of the quality of the cognition, which is the principle of that connection. Thus the criterion of the possibility of a conception (not of its object) is the definition of it, in which the unity of the conception, the truth of all that may be immediately deduced from it, and finally, the completeness of what has been thus deduced, constitute the requisites for the reproduction of the whole conception. Thus also, the criterion or test of an hypothesis is the intelligibility of the received principle of explanation, or its unity (without help from any subsidiary hypothesis)—the truth of our deductions from it (consistency with each other and with experience)—and lastly, the completeness of the principle of the explanation of these deductions, which refer to neither more nor less than what was admitted in the hypothesis, restoring analytically and à posteriori, what was cogitated synthetically and à priori. By the conceptions, therefore, of unity, truth, and perfection, we have made no addition to the transcendental table of the categories, which is complete without them. We have, on the contrary, merely employed the three categories of quantity, setting aside their application to objects of experience, as general logical laws of the consistency of cognition with itself.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6\n\nGeneral logic, as has been repeatedly said, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, and expects to receive representations from some other quarter, in order, by means of analysis, to convert them into conceptions. On the contrary, transcendental logic has lying before it the manifold content of à priori sensibility, which transcendental æsthetic presents to it in order to give matter to the pure conceptions of the understanding, without which transcendental logic would have no content, and be therefore utterly void. Now space and time contain an infinite diversity of determinations of pure à priori intuition, but are nevertheless the condition of the mind’s receptivity, under which alone it can obtain representations of objects, and which, consequently, must always affect the conception of these objects. But the spontaneity of thought requires that this diversity be examined after a certain manner, received into the mind, and connected, in order afterwards to form a cognition out of it. This Process I call synthesis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","text":"By the word synthesis, in its most general signification, I understand the process of joining different representations to each other and of comprehending their diversity in one cognition. This synthesis is pure when the diversity is not given empirically but à priori (as that in space and time). Our representations must be given previously to any analysis of them; and no conceptions can arise, quoad their content, analytically. But the synthesis of a diversity (be it given à priori or empirically) is the first requisite for the production of a cognition, which in its beginning, indeed, may be crude and confused, and therefore in need of analysis—still, synthesis is that by which alone the elements of our cognitions are collected and united into a certain content, consequently it is the first thing on which we must fix our attention, if we wish to investigate the origin of our knowledge.\n\nSynthesis, generally speaking, is, as we shall afterwards see, the mere operation of the imagination—a blind but indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no cognition whatever, but of the working of which we are seldom even conscious. But to reduce this synthesis to conceptions is a function of the understanding, by means of which we attain to cognition, in the proper meaning of the term.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","text":"Pure synthesis, represented generally, gives us the pure conception of the understanding. But by this pure synthesis, I mean that which rests upon a basis of à priori synthetical unity. Thus, our numeration (and this is more observable in large numbers) is a synthesis according to conceptions, because it takes place according to a common basis of unity (for example, the decade). By means of this conception, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.\n\nBy means of analysis different representations are brought under one conception—an operation of which general logic treats. On the other hand, the duty of transcendental logic is to reduce to conceptions, not representations, but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing which must be given to us for the sake of the à priori cognition of all objects, is the diversity of the pure intuition; the synthesis of this diversity by means of the imagination is the second; but this gives, as yet, no cognition. The conceptions which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetical unity, furnish the third requisite for the cognition of an object, and these conceptions are given by the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","text":"The same function which gives unity to the different representation in a judgement, gives also unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity we call the pure conception of the understanding. Thus, the same understanding, and by the same operations, whereby in conceptions, by means of analytical unity, it produced the logical form of a judgement, introduces, by means of the synthetical unity of the manifold in intuition, a transcendental content into its representations, on which account they are called pure conceptions of the understanding, and they apply à priori to objects, a result not within the power of general logic.\n\nIn this manner, there arise exactly so many pure conceptions of the understanding, applying à priori to objects of intuition in general, as there are logical functions in all possible judgements. For there is no other function or faculty existing in the understanding besides those enumerated in that table. These conceptions we shall, with Aristotle, call categories, our purpose being originally identical with his, notwithstanding the great difference in the execution.\n\n                     TABLE OF THE CATEGORIES\n\n                    1                         2\n\n              _Of Quantity                Of Quality_\n              Unity                      Reality\n              Plurality                  Negation\n              Totality                   Limitation\n\n                           3\n                      _Of Relation_\n   Of Inherence and Subsistence (substantia et accidens)\n   Of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)\n   Of Community (reciprocity between the agent and patient)\n\n                           4\n                     _Of Modality_\n              Possibility—Impossibility\n              Existence—Non-existence\n              Necessity—Contingence","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","text":"This, then, is a catalogue of all the originally pure conceptions of the synthesis which the understanding contains à priori, and these conceptions alone entitle it to be called a pure understanding; inasmuch as only by them it can render the manifold of intuition conceivable, in other words, think an object of intuition. This division is made systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgement (which is just the same as the power of thought), and has not arisen rhapsodically from a search at haphazard after pure conceptions, respecting the full number of which we never could be certain, inasmuch as we employ induction alone in our search, without considering that in this way we can never understand wherefore precisely these conceptions, and none others, abide in the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker like Aristotle, to search for these fundamental conceptions. Destitute, however, of any guiding principle, he picked them up just as they occurred to him, and at first hunted out ten, which he called categories (predicaments). Afterwards be believed that he had discovered five others, which were added under the name of post predicaments. But his catalogue still remained defective. Besides, there are to be found among them some of the modes of pure sensibility (quando, ubi, situs, also prius, simul), and likewise an empirical conception (motus)—which can by no means belong to this genealogical register of the pure understanding. Moreover, there are deduced conceptions (actio, passio) enumerated among the original conceptions, and, of the latter, some are entirely wanting.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","text":"With regard to these, it is to be remarked, that the categories, as the true primitive conceptions of the pure understanding, have also their pure deduced conceptions, which, in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, must by no means be passed over; though in a merely critical essay we must be contented with the simple mention of the fact.\n\nLet it be allowed me to call these pure, but deduced conceptions of the understanding, the predicables of the pure understanding, in contradistinction to predicaments. If we are in possession of the original and primitive, the deduced and subsidiary conceptions can easily be added, and the genealogical tree of the understanding completely delineated. As my present aim is not to set forth a complete system, but merely the principles of one, I reserve this task for another time. It may be easily executed by any one who will refer to the ontological manuals, and subordinate to the category of causality, for example, the predicables of force, action, passion; to that of community, those of presence and resistance; to the categories of modality, those of origination, extinction, change; and so with the rest. The categories combined with the modes of pure sensibility, or with one another, afford a great number of deduced à priori conceptions; a complete enumeration of which would be a useful and not unpleasant, but in this place a perfectly dispensable, occupation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0007","text":"I purposely omit the definitions of the categories in this treatise. I shall analyse these conceptions only so far as is necessary for the doctrine of method, which is to form a part of this critique. In a system of pure reason, definitions of them would be with justice demanded of me, but to give them here would only bide from our view the main aim of our investigation, at the same time raising doubts and objections, the consideration of which, without injustice to our main purpose, may be very well postponed till another opportunity. Meanwhile, it ought to be sufficiently clear, from the little we have already said on this subject, that the formation of a complete vocabulary of pure conceptions, accompanied by all the requisite explanations, is not only a possible, but an easy undertaking. The compartments already exist; it is only necessary to fill them up; and a systematic topic like the present, indicates with perfect precision the proper place to which each conception belongs, while it readily points out any that have not yet been filled up.\n\n§ 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","text":"Our table of the categories suggests considerations of some importance, which may perhaps have significant results in regard to the scientific form of all rational cognitions. For, that this table is useful in the theoretical part of philosophy, nay, indispensable for the sketching of the complete plan of a science, so far as that science rests upon conceptions à priori, and for dividing it mathematically, according to fixed principles, is most manifest from the fact that it contains all the elementary conceptions of the understanding, nay, even the form of a system of these in the understanding itself, and consequently indicates all the momenta, and also the internal arrangement of a projected speculative science, as I have elsewhere shown.[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-14) Here follow some of these observations.\n\n[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-14) In the “Metaphysical Principles of Natural Science.”\n\nI. This table, which contains four classes of conceptions of the understanding, may, in the first instance, be divided into two classes, the first of which relates to objects of intuition—pure as well as empirical; the second, to the existence of these objects, either in relation to one another, or to the understanding.\n\nThe former of these classes of categories I would entitle the mathematical, and the latter the dynamical categories. The former, as we see, has no correlates; these are only to be found in the second class. This difference must have a ground in the nature of the human understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","text":"II. The number of the categories in each class is always the same, namely, three—a fact which also demands some consideration, because in all other cases division à priori through conceptions is necessarily dichotomy. It is to be added, that the third category in each triad always arises from the combination of the second with the first.\n\nThus totality is nothing else but plurality contemplated as unity; limitation is merely reality conjoined with negation; community is the causality of a substance, reciprocally determining, and determined by other substances; and finally, necessity is nothing but existence, which is given through the possibility itself. Let it not be supposed, however, that the third category is merely a deduced, and not a primitive conception of the pure understanding. For the conjunction of the first and second, in order to produce the third conception, requires a particular function of the understanding, which is by no means identical with those which are exercised in the first and second. Thus, the conception of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible, where the conceptions of multitude and unity exist (for example, in the representation of the infinite). Or, if I conjoin the conception of a cause with that of a substance, it does not follow that the conception of influence, that is, how one substance can be the cause of something in another substance, will be understood from that. Thus it is evident that a particular act of the understanding is here necessary; and so in the other instances.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0010","text":"III. With respect to one category, namely, that of community, which is found in the third class, it is not so easy as with the others to detect its accordance with the form of the disjunctive judgement which corresponds to it in the table of the logical functions.\n\nIn order to assure ourselves of this accordance, we must observe that in every disjunctive judgement, the sphere of the judgement (that is, the complex of all that is contained in it) is represented as a whole divided into parts; and, since one part cannot be contained in the other, they are cogitated as co-ordinated with, not subordinated to each other, so that they do not determine each other unilaterally, as in a linear series, but reciprocally, as in an aggregate—(if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded; and conversely).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","text":"Now a like connection is cogitated in a whole of things; for one thing is not subordinated, as effect, to another as cause of its existence, but, on the contrary, is co-ordinated contemporaneously and reciprocally, as a cause in relation to the determination of the others (for example, in a body—the parts of which mutually attract and repel each other). And this is an entirely different kind of connection from that which we find in the mere relation of the cause to the effect (the principle to the consequence), for in such a connection the consequence does not in its turn determine the principle, and therefore does not constitute, with the latter, a whole—just as the Creator does not with the world make up a whole. The process of understanding by which it represents to itself the sphere of a divided conception, is employed also when we think of a thing as divisible; and in the same manner as the members of the division in the former exclude one another, and yet are connected in one sphere, so the understanding represents to itself the parts of the latter, as having—each of them—an existence (as substances), independently of the others, and yet as united in one whole.\n\n§ 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","text":"In the transcendental philosophy of the ancients there exists one more leading division, which contains pure conceptions of the understanding, and which, although not numbered among the categories, ought, according to them, as conceptions à priori, to be valid of objects. But in this case they would augment the number of the categories; which cannot be. These are set forth in the proposition, so renowned among the schoolmen—‘_Quodlibet ens est UNUM, VERUM, BONUM_.’ Now, though the inferences from this principle were mere tautological propositions, and though it is allowed only by courtesy to retain a place in modern metaphysics, yet a thought which maintained itself for such a length of time, however empty it seems to be, deserves an investigation of its origin, and justifies the conjecture that it must be grounded in some law of the understanding, which, as is often the case, has only been erroneously interpreted. These pretended transcendental predicates are, in fact, nothing but logical requisites and criteria of all cognition of objects, and they employ, as the basis for this cognition, the categories of quantity, namely, unity, plurality, and totality. But these, which must be taken as material conditions, that is, as belonging to the possibility of things themselves, they employed merely in a formal signification, as belonging to the logical requisites of all cognition, and yet most unguardedly changed these criteria of thought into properties of objects, as things in themselves. Now, in every cognition of an object, there is unity of conception, which may be called qualitative unity, so far as by this term we understand only the unity in our connection of the manifold; for example, unity of the theme in a play, an oration, or a story. Secondly, there is truth in respect of the deductions from it. The more true deductions we have from a given conception, the more criteria of its objective reality. This we might call the qualitative plurality of characteristic marks, which belong to a conception as to a common foundation, but are not cogitated as a quantity in it. Thirdly, there is perfection—which consists in this, that the plurality falls back upon the unity of the conception, and accords completely with that conception and with no other. This we may denominate qualitative completeness. Hence it is evident that these logical criteria of the possibility of cognition are merely the three categories of quantity modified and transformed to suit an unauthorized manner of applying them. That is to say, the three categories, in which the unity in the production of the quantum must be homogeneous throughout, are transformed solely with a view to the connection of heterogeneous parts of cognition in one act of consciousness, by means of the quality of the cognition, which is the principle of that connection. Thus the criterion of the possibility of a conception (not of its object) is the definition of it, in which the unity of the conception, the truth of all that may be immediately deduced from it, and finally, the completeness of what has been thus deduced, constitute the requisites for the reproduction of the whole conception. Thus also, the criterion or test of an hypothesis is the intelligibility of the received principle of explanation, or its unity (without help from any subsidiary hypothesis)—the truth of our deductions from it (consistency with each other and with experience)—and lastly, the completeness of the principle of the explanation of these deductions, which refer to neither more nor less than what was admitted in the hypothesis, restoring analytically and à posteriori, what was cogitated synthetically and à priori. By the conceptions, therefore, of unity, truth, and perfection, we have made no addition to the transcendental table of the categories, which is complete without them. We have, on the contrary, merely employed the three categories of quantity, setting aside their application to objects of experience, as general logical laws of the consistency of cognition with itself.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3a3db951869b780f7d54aba28a63deb799e1f5f7e57169cc94dc15a711cf742d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","order":28,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIPT","text":"### Chapter II. Of the Deduction of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9\n\nTeachers of jurisprudence, when speaking of rights and claims, distinguish in a cause the question of right (quid juris) from the question of fact (quid facti), and while they demand proof of both, they give to the proof of the former, which goes to establish right or claim in law, the name of deduction. Now we make use of a great number of empirical conceptions, without opposition from any one; and consider ourselves, even without any attempt at deduction, justified in attaching to them a sense, and a supposititious signification, because we have always experience at hand to demonstrate their objective reality. There exist also, however, usurped conceptions, such as fortune, fate, which circulate with almost universal indulgence, and yet are occasionally challenged by the question, “quid juris?” In such cases, we have great difficulty in discovering any deduction for these terms, inasmuch as we cannot produce any manifest ground of right, either from experience or from reason, on which the claim to employ them can be founded.\n\nAmong the many conceptions, which make up the very variegated web of human cognition, some are destined for pure use à priori, independent of all experience; and their title to be so employed always requires a deduction, inasmuch as, to justify such use of them, proofs from experience are not sufficient; but it is necessary to know how these conceptions can apply to objects without being derived from experience. I term, therefore, an examination of the manner in which conceptions can apply à priori to objects, the transcendental deduction of conceptions, and I distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the mode in which conception is obtained through experience and reflection thereon; consequently, does not concern itself with the right, but only with the fact of our obtaining conceptions in such and such a manner. We have already seen that we are in possession of two perfectly different kinds of conceptions, which nevertheless agree with each other in this, that they both apply to objects completely à priori. These are the conceptions of space and time as forms of sensibility, and the categories as pure conceptions of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of either of these classes would be labour in vain, because the distinguishing characteristic of their nature consists in this, that they apply to their objects, without having borrowed anything from experience towards the representation of them. Consequently, if a deduction of these conceptions is necessary, it must always be transcendental.\n\nMeanwhile, with respect to these conceptions, as with respect to all our cognition, we certainly may discover in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, yet the occasioning causes of their production. It will be found that the impressions of sense give the first occasion for bringing into action the whole faculty of cognition, and for the production of experience, which contains two very dissimilar elements, namely, a matter for cognition, given by the senses, and a certain form for the arrangement of this matter, arising out of the inner fountain of pure intuition and thought; and these, on occasion given by sensuous impressions, are called into exercise and produce conceptions. Such an investigation into the first efforts of our faculty of cognition to mount from particular perceptions to general conceptions is undoubtedly of great utility; and we have to thank the celebrated Locke for having first opened the way for this inquiry. But a deduction of the pure à priori conceptions of course never can be made in this way, seeing that, in regard to their future employment, which must be entirely independent of experience, they must have a far different certificate of birth to show from that of a descent from experience. This attempted physiological derivation, which cannot properly be called deduction, because it relates merely to a quaestio facti, I shall entitle an explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is therefore manifest that there can only be a transcendental deduction of these conceptions and by no means an empirical one; also, that all attempts at an empirical deduction, in regard to pure à priori conceptions, are vain, and can only be made by one who does not understand the altogether peculiar nature of these cognitions.\n\nBut although it is admitted that the only possible deduction of pure à priori cognition is a transcendental deduction, it is not, for that reason, perfectly manifest that such a deduction is absolutely necessary. We have already traced to their sources the conceptions of space and time, by means of a transcendental deduction, and we have explained and determined their objective validity à priori. Geometry, nevertheless, advances steadily and securely in the province of pure à priori cognitions, without needing to ask from philosophy any certificate as to the pure and legitimate origin of its fundamental conception of space. But the use of the conception in this science extends only to the external world of sense, the pure form of the intuition of which is space; and in this world, therefore, all geometrical cognition, because it is founded upon à priori intuition, possesses immediate evidence, and the objects of this cognition are given à priori (as regards their form) in intuition by and through the cognition itself. With the pure conceptions of understanding, on the contrary, commences the absolute necessity of seeking a transcendental deduction, not only of these conceptions themselves, but likewise of space, because, inasmuch as they make affirmations concerning objects not by means of the predicates of intuition and sensibility, but of pure thought à priori, they apply to objects without any of the conditions of sensibility. Besides, not being founded on experience, they are not presented with any object in à priori intuition upon which, antecedently to experience, they might base their synthesis. Hence results, not only doubt as to the objective validity and proper limits of their use, but that even our conception of space is rendered equivocal; inasmuch as we are very ready with the aid of the categories, to carry the use of this conception beyond the conditions of sensuous intuition—and, for this reason, we have already found a transcendental deduction of it needful. The reader, then, must be quite convinced of the absolute necessity of a transcendental deduction, before taking a single step in the field of pure reason; because otherwise he goes to work blindly, and after he has wondered about in all directions, returns to the state of utter ignorance from which he started. He ought, moreover, clearly to recognize beforehand the unavoidable difficulties in his undertaking, so that he may not afterwards complain of the obscurity in which the subject itself is deeply involved, or become too soon impatient of the obstacles in his path; because we have a choice of only two things—either at once to give up all pretensions to knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to completion.\n\nWe have been able, with very little trouble, to make it comprehensible how the conceptions of space and time, although à priori cognitions, must necessarily apply to external objects, and render a synthetical cognition of these possible, independently of all experience. For inasmuch as only by means of such pure form of sensibility an object can appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain à priori the condition of the possibility of objects as phenomena, and an à priori synthesis in these intuitions possesses objective validity.\n\nOn the other hand, the categories of the understanding do not represent the conditions under which objects are given to us in intuition; objects can consequently appear to us without necessarily connecting themselves with these, and consequently without any necessity binding on the understanding to contain à priori the conditions of these objects. Thus we find ourselves involved in a difficulty which did not present itself in the sphere of sensibility, that is to say, we cannot discover how the subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, in other words, can become conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects; for phenomena may certainly be given to us in intuition without any help from the functions of the understanding. Let us take, for example, the conception of cause, which indicates a peculiar kind of synthesis, namely, that with something, A, something entirely different, B, is connected according to a law. It is not à priori manifest why phenomena should contain anything of this kind (we are of course debarred from appealing for proof to experience, for the objective validity of this conception must be demonstrated à priori), and it hence remains doubtful à priori, whether such a conception be not quite void and without any corresponding object among phenomena. For that objects of sensuous intuition must correspond to the formal conditions of sensibility existing à priori in the mind is quite evident, from the fact that without these they could not be objects for us; but that they must also correspond to the conditions which understanding requires for the synthetical unity of thought is an assertion, the grounds for which are not so easily to be discovered. For phenomena might be so constituted as not to correspond to the conditions of the unity of thought; and all things might lie in such confusion that, for example, nothing could be met with in the sphere of phenomena to suggest a law of synthesis, and so correspond to the conception of cause and effect; so that this conception would be quite void, null, and without significance. Phenomena would nevertheless continue to present objects to our intuition; for mere intuition does not in any respect stand in need of the functions of thought.\n\nIf we thought to free ourselves from the labour of these investigations by saying: “Experience is constantly offering us examples of the relation of cause and effect in phenomena, and presents us with abundant opportunity of abstracting the conception of cause, and so at the same time of corroborating the objective validity of this conception”; we should in this case be overlooking the fact, that the conception of cause cannot arise in this way at all; that, on the contrary, it must either have an à priori basis in the understanding, or be rejected as a mere chimera. For this conception demands that something, A, should be of such a nature that something else, B, should follow from it necessarily, and according to an absolutely universal law. We may certainly collect from phenomena a law, according to which this or that usually happens, but the element of necessity is not to be found in it. Hence it is evident that to the synthesis of cause and effect belongs a dignity, which is utterly wanting in any empirical synthesis; for it is no mere mechanical synthesis, by means of addition, but a dynamical one; that is to say, the effect is not to be cogitated as merely annexed to the cause, but as posited by and through the cause, and resulting from it. The strict universality of this law never can be a characteristic of empirical laws, which obtain through induction only a comparative universality, that is, an extended range of practical application. But the pure conceptions of the understanding would entirely lose all their peculiar character, if we treated them merely as the productions of experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. Of the Deduction of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9\n\nTeachers of jurisprudence, when speaking of rights and claims, distinguish in a cause the question of right (quid juris) from the question of fact (quid facti), and while they demand proof of both, they give to the proof of the former, which goes to establish right or claim in law, the name of deduction. Now we make use of a great number of empirical conceptions, without opposition from any one; and consider ourselves, even without any attempt at deduction, justified in attaching to them a sense, and a supposititious signification, because we have always experience at hand to demonstrate their objective reality. There exist also, however, usurped conceptions, such as fortune, fate, which circulate with almost universal indulgence, and yet are occasionally challenged by the question, “quid juris?” In such cases, we have great difficulty in discovering any deduction for these terms, inasmuch as we cannot produce any manifest ground of right, either from experience or from reason, on which the claim to employ them can be founded.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","text":"Among the many conceptions, which make up the very variegated web of human cognition, some are destined for pure use à priori, independent of all experience; and their title to be so employed always requires a deduction, inasmuch as, to justify such use of them, proofs from experience are not sufficient; but it is necessary to know how these conceptions can apply to objects without being derived from experience. I term, therefore, an examination of the manner in which conceptions can apply à priori to objects, the transcendental deduction of conceptions, and I distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the mode in which conception is obtained through experience and reflection thereon; consequently, does not concern itself with the right, but only with the fact of our obtaining conceptions in such and such a manner. We have already seen that we are in possession of two perfectly different kinds of conceptions, which nevertheless agree with each other in this, that they both apply to objects completely à priori. These are the conceptions of space and time as forms of sensibility, and the categories as pure conceptions of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of either of these classes would be labour in vain, because the distinguishing characteristic of their nature consists in this, that they apply to their objects, without having borrowed anything from experience towards the representation of them. Consequently, if a deduction of these conceptions is necessary, it must always be transcendental.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","text":"Meanwhile, with respect to these conceptions, as with respect to all our cognition, we certainly may discover in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, yet the occasioning causes of their production. It will be found that the impressions of sense give the first occasion for bringing into action the whole faculty of cognition, and for the production of experience, which contains two very dissimilar elements, namely, a matter for cognition, given by the senses, and a certain form for the arrangement of this matter, arising out of the inner fountain of pure intuition and thought; and these, on occasion given by sensuous impressions, are called into exercise and produce conceptions. Such an investigation into the first efforts of our faculty of cognition to mount from particular perceptions to general conceptions is undoubtedly of great utility; and we have to thank the celebrated Locke for having first opened the way for this inquiry. But a deduction of the pure à priori conceptions of course never can be made in this way, seeing that, in regard to their future employment, which must be entirely independent of experience, they must have a far different certificate of birth to show from that of a descent from experience. This attempted physiological derivation, which cannot properly be called deduction, because it relates merely to a quaestio facti, I shall entitle an explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is therefore manifest that there can only be a transcendental deduction of these conceptions and by no means an empirical one; also, that all attempts at an empirical deduction, in regard to pure à priori conceptions, are vain, and can only be made by one who does not understand the altogether peculiar nature of these cognitions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","text":"But although it is admitted that the only possible deduction of pure à priori cognition is a transcendental deduction, it is not, for that reason, perfectly manifest that such a deduction is absolutely necessary. We have already traced to their sources the conceptions of space and time, by means of a transcendental deduction, and we have explained and determined their objective validity à priori. Geometry, nevertheless, advances steadily and securely in the province of pure à priori cognitions, without needing to ask from philosophy any certificate as to the pure and legitimate origin of its fundamental conception of space. But the use of the conception in this science extends only to the external world of sense, the pure form of the intuition of which is space; and in this world, therefore, all geometrical cognition, because it is founded upon à priori intuition, possesses immediate evidence, and the objects of this cognition are given à priori (as regards their form) in intuition by and through the cognition itself. With the pure conceptions of understanding, on the contrary, commences the absolute necessity of seeking a transcendental deduction, not only of these conceptions themselves, but likewise of space, because, inasmuch as they make affirmations concerning objects not by means of the predicates of intuition and sensibility, but of pure thought à priori, they apply to objects without any of the conditions of sensibility. Besides, not being founded on experience, they are not presented with any object in à priori intuition upon which, antecedently to experience, they might base their synthesis. Hence results, not only doubt as to the objective validity and proper limits of their use, but that even our conception of space is rendered equivocal; inasmuch as we are very ready with the aid of the categories, to carry the use of this conception beyond the conditions of sensuous intuition—and, for this reason, we have already found a transcendental deduction of it needful. The reader, then, must be quite convinced of the absolute necessity of a transcendental deduction, before taking a single step in the field of pure reason; because otherwise he goes to work blindly, and after he has wondered about in all directions, returns to the state of utter ignorance from which he started. He ought, moreover, clearly to recognize beforehand the unavoidable difficulties in his undertaking, so that he may not afterwards complain of the obscurity in which the subject itself is deeply involved, or become too soon impatient of the obstacles in his path; because we have a choice of only two things—either at once to give up all pretensions to knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to completion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","text":"We have been able, with very little trouble, to make it comprehensible how the conceptions of space and time, although à priori cognitions, must necessarily apply to external objects, and render a synthetical cognition of these possible, independently of all experience. For inasmuch as only by means of such pure form of sensibility an object can appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain à priori the condition of the possibility of objects as phenomena, and an à priori synthesis in these intuitions possesses objective validity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","text":"On the other hand, the categories of the understanding do not represent the conditions under which objects are given to us in intuition; objects can consequently appear to us without necessarily connecting themselves with these, and consequently without any necessity binding on the understanding to contain à priori the conditions of these objects. Thus we find ourselves involved in a difficulty which did not present itself in the sphere of sensibility, that is to say, we cannot discover how the subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, in other words, can become conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects; for phenomena may certainly be given to us in intuition without any help from the functions of the understanding. Let us take, for example, the conception of cause, which indicates a peculiar kind of synthesis, namely, that with something, A, something entirely different, B, is connected according to a law. It is not à priori manifest why phenomena should contain anything of this kind (we are of course debarred from appealing for proof to experience, for the objective validity of this conception must be demonstrated à priori), and it hence remains doubtful à priori, whether such a conception be not quite void and without any corresponding object among phenomena. For that objects of sensuous intuition must correspond to the formal conditions of sensibility existing à priori in the mind is quite evident, from the fact that without these they could not be objects for us; but that they must also correspond to the conditions which understanding requires for the synthetical unity of thought is an assertion, the grounds for which are not so easily to be discovered. For phenomena might be so constituted as not to correspond to the conditions of the unity of thought; and all things might lie in such confusion that, for example, nothing could be met with in the sphere of phenomena to suggest a law of synthesis, and so correspond to the conception of cause and effect; so that this conception would be quite void, null, and without significance. Phenomena would nevertheless continue to present objects to our intuition; for mere intuition does not in any respect stand in need of the functions of thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","text":"If we thought to free ourselves from the labour of these investigations by saying: “Experience is constantly offering us examples of the relation of cause and effect in phenomena, and presents us with abundant opportunity of abstracting the conception of cause, and so at the same time of corroborating the objective validity of this conception”; we should in this case be overlooking the fact, that the conception of cause cannot arise in this way at all; that, on the contrary, it must either have an à priori basis in the understanding, or be rejected as a mere chimera. For this conception demands that something, A, should be of such a nature that something else, B, should follow from it necessarily, and according to an absolutely universal law. We may certainly collect from phenomena a law, according to which this or that usually happens, but the element of necessity is not to be found in it. Hence it is evident that to the synthesis of cause and effect belongs a dignity, which is utterly wanting in any empirical synthesis; for it is no mere mechanical synthesis, by means of addition, but a dynamical one; that is to say, the effect is not to be cogitated as merely annexed to the cause, but as posited by and through the cause, and resulting from it. The strict universality of this law never can be a characteristic of empirical laws, which obtain through induction only a comparative universality, that is, an extended range of practical application. But the pure conceptions of the understanding would entirely lose all their peculiar character, if we treated them merely as the productions of experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8b3dd61c230fc247d1e4d91c1082953150d65e222c41714f53fa9680cf07eefa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","order":29,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_TTDC","text":"# Catagories\n\n### Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10\n\nThere are only two possible ways in which synthetical representation and its objects can coincide with and relate necessarily to each other, and, as it were, meet together. Either the object alone makes the representation possible, or the representation alone makes the object possible. In the former case, the relation between them is only empirical, and an à priori representation is impossible. And this is the case with phenomena, as regards that in them which is referable to mere sensation. In the latter case—although representation alone (for of its causality, by means of the will, we do not here speak) does not produce the object as to its existence, it must nevertheless be à priori determinative in regard to the object, if it is only by means of the representation that we can cognize anything as an object. Now there are only two conditions of the possibility of a cognition of objects; firstly, intuition, by means of which the object, though only as phenomenon, is given; secondly, conception, by means of which the object which corresponds to this intuition is thought. But it is evident from what has been said on æsthetic that the first condition, under which alone objects can be intuited, must in fact exist, as a formal basis for them, à priori in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility, therefore, all phenomena necessarily correspond, because it is only through it that they can be phenomena at all; that is, can be empirically intuited and given. Now the question is whether there do not exist, à priori in the mind, conceptions of understanding also, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is yet thought as object. If this question be answered in the affirmative, it follows that all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily conformable to such conceptions, since, if they are not presupposed, it is impossible that anything can be an object of experience. Now all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses through which an object is given, a conception also of an object that is given in intuition. Accordingly, conceptions of objects in general must lie as à priori conditions at the foundation of all empirical cognition; and consequently, the objective validity of the categories, as à priori conceptions, will rest upon this, that experience (as far as regards the form of thought) is possible only by their means. For in that case they apply necessarily and à priori to objects of experience, because only through them can an object of experience be thought.\n\nThe whole aim of the transcendental deduction of all à priori conceptions is to show that these conceptions are à priori conditions of the possibility of all experience. Conceptions which afford us the objective foundation of the possibility of experience are for that very reason necessary. But the analysis of the experiences in which they are met with is not deduction, but only an illustration of them, because from experience they could never derive the attribute of necessity. Without their original applicability and relation to all possible experience, in which all objects of cognition present themselves, the relation of the categories to objects, of whatever nature, would be quite incomprehensible.\n\nThe celebrated Locke, for want of due reflection on these points, and because he met with pure conceptions of the understanding in experience, sought also to deduce them from experience, and yet proceeded so inconsequently as to attempt, with their aid, to arrive it cognitions which lie far beyond the limits of all experience. David Hume perceived that, to render this possible, it was necessary that the conceptions should have an à priori origin. But as he could not explain how it was possible that conceptions which are not connected with each other in the understanding must nevertheless be thought as necessarily connected in the object—and it never occurred to him that the understanding itself might, perhaps, by means of these conceptions, be the author of the experience in which its objects were presented to it—he was forced to drive these conceptions from experience, that is, from a subjective necessity arising from repeated association of experiences erroneously considered to be objective—in one word, from habit. But he proceeded with perfect consequence and declared it to be impossible, with such conceptions and the principles arising from them, to overstep the limits of experience. The empirical derivation, however, which both of these philosophers attributed to these conceptions, cannot possibly be reconciled with the fact that we do possess scientific à priori cognitions, namely, those of pure mathematics and general physics.\n\nThe former of these two celebrated men opened a wide door to extravagance—(for if reason has once undoubted right on its side, it will not allow itself to be confined to set limits, by vague recommendations of moderation); the latter gave himself up entirely to scepticism—a natural consequence, after having discovered, as he thought, that the faculty of cognition was not trustworthy. We now intend to make a trial whether it be not possible safely to conduct reason between these two rocks, to assign her determinate limits, and yet leave open for her the entire sphere of her legitimate activity.\n\nI shall merely premise an explanation of what the categories are. They are conceptions of an object in general, by means of which its intuition is contemplated as determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement. The following will make this plain. The function of the categorical judgement is that of the relation of subject to predicate; for example, in the proposition: “All bodies are divisible.” But in regard to the merely logical use of the understanding, it still remains undetermined to which Of these two conceptions belongs the function Of subject and to which that of predicate. For we could also say: “Some divisible is a body.” But the category of substance, when the conception of a body is brought under it, determines that; and its empirical intuition in experience must be contemplated always as subject and never as mere predicate. And so with all the other categories.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","text":"# Catagories\n\n### Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10\n\nThere are only two possible ways in which synthetical representation and its objects can coincide with and relate necessarily to each other, and, as it were, meet together. Either the object alone makes the representation possible, or the representation alone makes the object possible. In the former case, the relation between them is only empirical, and an à priori representation is impossible. And this is the case with phenomena, as regards that in them which is referable to mere sensation. In the latter case—although representation alone (for of its causality, by means of the will, we do not here speak) does not produce the object as to its existence, it must nevertheless be à priori determinative in regard to the object, if it is only by means of the representation that we can cognize anything as an object. Now there are only two conditions of the possibility of a cognition of objects; firstly, intuition, by means of which the object, though only as phenomenon, is given; secondly, conception, by means of which the object which corresponds to this intuition is thought. But it is evident from what has been said on æsthetic that the first condition, under which alone objects can be intuited, must in fact exist, as a formal basis for them, à priori in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility, therefore, all phenomena necessarily correspond, because it is only through it that they can be phenomena at all; that is, can be empirically intuited and given. Now the question is whether there do not exist, à priori in the mind, conceptions of understanding also, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is yet thought as object. If this question be answered in the affirmative, it follows that all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily conformable to such conceptions, since, if they are not presupposed, it is impossible that anything can be an object of experience. Now all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses through which an object is given, a conception also of an object that is given in intuition. Accordingly, conceptions of objects in general must lie as à priori conditions at the foundation of all empirical cognition; and consequently, the objective validity of the categories, as à priori conceptions, will rest upon this, that experience (as far as regards the form of thought) is possible only by their means. For in that case they apply necessarily and à priori to objects of experience, because only through them can an object of experience be thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","text":"The whole aim of the transcendental deduction of all à priori conceptions is to show that these conceptions are à priori conditions of the possibility of all experience. Conceptions which afford us the objective foundation of the possibility of experience are for that very reason necessary. But the analysis of the experiences in which they are met with is not deduction, but only an illustration of them, because from experience they could never derive the attribute of necessity. Without their original applicability and relation to all possible experience, in which all objects of cognition present themselves, the relation of the categories to objects, of whatever nature, would be quite incomprehensible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","text":"The celebrated Locke, for want of due reflection on these points, and because he met with pure conceptions of the understanding in experience, sought also to deduce them from experience, and yet proceeded so inconsequently as to attempt, with their aid, to arrive it cognitions which lie far beyond the limits of all experience. David Hume perceived that, to render this possible, it was necessary that the conceptions should have an à priori origin. But as he could not explain how it was possible that conceptions which are not connected with each other in the understanding must nevertheless be thought as necessarily connected in the object—and it never occurred to him that the understanding itself might, perhaps, by means of these conceptions, be the author of the experience in which its objects were presented to it—he was forced to drive these conceptions from experience, that is, from a subjective necessity arising from repeated association of experiences erroneously considered to be objective—in one word, from habit. But he proceeded with perfect consequence and declared it to be impossible, with such conceptions and the principles arising from them, to overstep the limits of experience. The empirical derivation, however, which both of these philosophers attributed to these conceptions, cannot possibly be reconciled with the fact that we do possess scientific à priori cognitions, namely, those of pure mathematics and general physics.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","text":"The former of these two celebrated men opened a wide door to extravagance—(for if reason has once undoubted right on its side, it will not allow itself to be confined to set limits, by vague recommendations of moderation); the latter gave himself up entirely to scepticism—a natural consequence, after having discovered, as he thought, that the faculty of cognition was not trustworthy. We now intend to make a trial whether it be not possible safely to conduct reason between these two rocks, to assign her determinate limits, and yet leave open for her the entire sphere of her legitimate activity.\n\nI shall merely premise an explanation of what the categories are. They are conceptions of an object in general, by means of which its intuition is contemplated as determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement. The following will make this plain. The function of the categorical judgement is that of the relation of subject to predicate; for example, in the proposition: “All bodies are divisible.” But in regard to the merely logical use of the understanding, it still remains undetermined to which Of these two conceptions belongs the function Of subject and to which that of predicate. For we could also say: “Some divisible is a body.” But the category of substance, when the conception of a body is brought under it, determines that; and its empirical intuition in experience must be contemplated always as subject and never as mere predicate. And so with all the other categories.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3368d7abd6733d9ef2f66f414a5d132c77143600a70b65cee2ad3c3dad35a108","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","order":30,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_PCMR","text":"### Section II Transcendental Deduction of the pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.\n\nThe manifold content in our representations can be given in an intuition which is merely sensuous—in other words, is nothing but susceptibility; and the form of this intuition can exist à priori in our faculty of representation, without being anything else but the mode in which the subject is affected. But the conjunction (conjunctio) of a manifold in intuition never can be given us by the senses; it cannot therefore be contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition, for it is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation. And as we must, to distinguish it from sensibility, entitle this faculty understanding; so all conjunction whether conscious or unconscious, be it of the manifold in intuition, sensuous or non-sensuous, or of several conceptions—is an act of the understanding. To this act we shall give the general appellation of synthesis, thereby to indicate, at the same time, that we cannot represent anything as conjoined in the object without having previously conjoined it ourselves. Of all mental notions, that of conjunction is the only one which cannot be given through objects, but can be originated only by the subject itself, because it is an act of its purely spontaneous activity. The reader will easily enough perceive that the possibility of conjunction must be grounded in the very nature of this act, and that it must be equally valid for all conjunction, and that analysis, which appears to be its contrary, must, nevertheless, always presuppose it; for where the understanding has not previously conjoined, it cannot dissect or analyse, because only as conjoined by it, must that which is to be analysed have been given to our faculty of representation.\n\nBut the conception of conjunction includes, besides the conception of the manifold and of the synthesis of it, that of the unity of it also. Conjunction is the representation of the synthetical unity of the manifold.[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-15) This idea of unity, therefore, cannot arise out of that of conjunction; much rather does that idea, by combining itself with the representation of the manifold, render the conception of conjunction possible. This unity, which à priori precedes all conceptions of conjunction, is not the category of unity (§ 6); for all the categories are based upon logical functions of judgement, and in these functions we already have conjunction, and consequently unity of given conceptions. It is therefore evident that the category of unity presupposes conjunction. We must therefore look still higher for this unity (as qualitative, § 8), in that, namely, which contains the ground of the unity of diverse conceptions in judgements, the ground, consequently, of the possibility of the existence of the understanding, even in regard to its logical use.\n\n[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-15) Whether the representations are in themselves identical, and consequently whether one can be thought analytically by means of and through the other, is a question which we need not at present consider. Our Consciousness of the one, when we speak of the manifold, is always distinguishable from our consciousness of the other; and it is only respecting the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness that we here treat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","text":"### Section II Transcendental Deduction of the pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.\n\nThe manifold content in our representations can be given in an intuition which is merely sensuous—in other words, is nothing but susceptibility; and the form of this intuition can exist à priori in our faculty of representation, without being anything else but the mode in which the subject is affected. But the conjunction (conjunctio) of a manifold in intuition never can be given us by the senses; it cannot therefore be contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition, for it is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation. And as we must, to distinguish it from sensibility, entitle this faculty understanding; so all conjunction whether conscious or unconscious, be it of the manifold in intuition, sensuous or non-sensuous, or of several conceptions—is an act of the understanding. To this act we shall give the general appellation of synthesis, thereby to indicate, at the same time, that we cannot represent anything as conjoined in the object without having previously conjoined it ourselves. Of all mental notions, that of conjunction is the only one which cannot be given through objects, but can be originated only by the subject itself, because it is an act of its purely spontaneous activity. The reader will easily enough perceive that the possibility of conjunction must be grounded in the very nature of this act, and that it must be equally valid for all conjunction, and that analysis, which appears to be its contrary, must, nevertheless, always presuppose it; for where the understanding has not previously conjoined, it cannot dissect or analyse, because only as conjoined by it, must that which is to be analysed have been given to our faculty of representation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","text":"But the conception of conjunction includes, besides the conception of the manifold and of the synthesis of it, that of the unity of it also. Conjunction is the representation of the synthetical unity of the manifold.[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-15) This idea of unity, therefore, cannot arise out of that of conjunction; much rather does that idea, by combining itself with the representation of the manifold, render the conception of conjunction possible. This unity, which à priori precedes all conceptions of conjunction, is not the category of unity (§ 6); for all the categories are based upon logical functions of judgement, and in these functions we already have conjunction, and consequently unity of given conceptions. It is therefore evident that the category of unity presupposes conjunction. We must therefore look still higher for this unity (as qualitative, § 8), in that, namely, which contains the ground of the unity of diverse conceptions in judgements, the ground, consequently, of the possibility of the existence of the understanding, even in regard to its logical use.\n\n[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-15) Whether the representations are in themselves identical, and consequently whether one can be thought analytically by means of and through the other, is a question which we need not at present consider. Our Consciousness of the one, when we speak of the manifold, is always distinguishable from our consciousness of the other; and it is only respecting the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness that we here treat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c40f4fbe2b05f885285490eb11d0345c8fea0959f4c73f7dc0abbb14d4506089","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","order":31,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_OSUA","text":"### Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12\n\nThe “I think” must accompany all my representations, for otherwise something would be represented in me which could not be thought; in other words, the representation would either be impossible, or at least be, in relation to me, nothing. That representation which can be given previously to all thought is called intuition. All the diversity or manifold content of intuition, has, therefore, a necessary relation to the “I think,” in the subject in which this diversity is found. But this representation, “I think,” is an act of spontaneity; that is to say, it cannot be regarded as belonging to mere sensibility. I call it pure apperception, in order to distinguish it from empirical; or primitive apperception, because it is self-consciousness which, whilst it gives birth to the representation “I think,” must necessarily be capable of accompanying all our representations. It is in all acts of consciousness one and the same, and unaccompanied by it, no representation can exist for me. The unity of this apperception I call the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to indicate the possibility of à priori cognition arising from it. For the manifold representations which are given in an intuition would not all of them be my representations, if they did not all belong to one self-consciousness, that is, as my representations (even although I am not conscious of them as such), they must conform to the condition under which alone they can exist together in a common self-consciousness, because otherwise they would not all without exception belong to me. From this primitive conjunction follow many important results.\n\nFor example, this universal identity of the apperception of the manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations and is possible only by means of the consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness which accompanies different representations is in itself fragmentary and disunited, and without relation to the identity of the subject. This relation, then, does not exist because I accompany every representation with consciousness, but because I join one representation to another, and am conscious of the synthesis of them. Consequently, only because I can connect a variety of given representations in one consciousness, is it possible that I can represent to myself the identity of consciousness in these representations; in other words, the analytical unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of a synthetical unity.[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-16) The thought, “These representations given in intuition belong all of them to me,” is accordingly just the same as, “I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least so unite them”; and although this thought is not itself the consciousness of the synthesis of representations, it presupposes the possibility of it; that is to say, for the reason alone that I can comprehend the variety of my representations in one consciousness, do I call them my representations, for otherwise I must have as many-coloured and various a self as are the representations of which I am conscious. Synthetical unity of the manifold in intuitions, as given à priori, is therefore the foundation of the identity of apperception itself, which antecedes à priori all determinate thought. But the conjunction of representations into a conception is not to be found in objects themselves, nor can it be, as it were, borrowed from them and taken up into the understanding by perception, but it is on the contrary an operation of the understanding itself, which is nothing more than the faculty of conjoining à priori and of bringing the variety of given representations under the unity of apperception. This principle is the highest in all human cognition.\n\n[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-16) All general conceptions—as such—depend, for their existence, on the analytical unity of consciousness. For example, when I think of red in general, I thereby think to myself a property which (as a characteristic mark) can be discovered somewhere, or can be united with other representations; consequently, it is only by means of a forethought possible synthetical unity that I can think to myself the analytical. A representation which is cogitated as common to different representations, is regarded as belonging to such as, besides this common representation, contain something different; consequently it must be previously thought in synthetical unity with other although only possible representations, before I can think in it the analytical unity of consciousness which makes it a conceptas communis. And thus the synthetical unity of apperception is the highest point with which we must connect every operation of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it our transcendental philosophy; indeed, this faculty is the understanding itself.\n\nThis fundamental principle of the necessary unity of apperception is indeed an identical, and therefore analytical, proposition; but it nevertheless explains the necessity for a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition, without which the identity of self-consciousness would be incogitable. For the ego, as a simple representation, presents us with no manifold content; only in intuition, which is quite different from the representation ego, can it be given us, and by means of conjunction it is cogitated in one self-consciousness. An understanding, in which all the manifold should be given by means of consciousness itself, would be intuitive; our understanding can only think and must look for its intuition to sense. I am, therefore, conscious of my identical self, in relation to all the variety of representations given to me in an intuition, because I call all of them my representations. In other words, I am conscious myself of a necessary à priori synthesis of my representations, which is called the original synthetical unity of apperception, under which rank all the representations presented to me, but that only by means of a synthesis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","text":"### Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12\n\nThe “I think” must accompany all my representations, for otherwise something would be represented in me which could not be thought; in other words, the representation would either be impossible, or at least be, in relation to me, nothing. That representation which can be given previously to all thought is called intuition. All the diversity or manifold content of intuition, has, therefore, a necessary relation to the “I think,” in the subject in which this diversity is found. But this representation, “I think,” is an act of spontaneity; that is to say, it cannot be regarded as belonging to mere sensibility. I call it pure apperception, in order to distinguish it from empirical; or primitive apperception, because it is self-consciousness which, whilst it gives birth to the representation “I think,” must necessarily be capable of accompanying all our representations. It is in all acts of consciousness one and the same, and unaccompanied by it, no representation can exist for me. The unity of this apperception I call the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to indicate the possibility of à priori cognition arising from it. For the manifold representations which are given in an intuition would not all of them be my representations, if they did not all belong to one self-consciousness, that is, as my representations (even although I am not conscious of them as such), they must conform to the condition under which alone they can exist together in a common self-consciousness, because otherwise they would not all without exception belong to me. From this primitive conjunction follow many important results.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","text":"For example, this universal identity of the apperception of the manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations and is possible only by means of the consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness which accompanies different representations is in itself fragmentary and disunited, and without relation to the identity of the subject. This relation, then, does not exist because I accompany every representation with consciousness, but because I join one representation to another, and am conscious of the synthesis of them. Consequently, only because I can connect a variety of given representations in one consciousness, is it possible that I can represent to myself the identity of consciousness in these representations; in other words, the analytical unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of a synthetical unity.[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-16) The thought, “These representations given in intuition belong all of them to me,” is accordingly just the same as, “I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least so unite them”; and although this thought is not itself the consciousness of the synthesis of representations, it presupposes the possibility of it; that is to say, for the reason alone that I can comprehend the variety of my representations in one consciousness, do I call them my representations, for otherwise I must have as many-coloured and various a self as are the representations of which I am conscious. Synthetical unity of the manifold in intuitions, as given à priori, is therefore the foundation of the identity of apperception itself, which antecedes à priori all determinate thought. But the conjunction of representations into a conception is not to be found in objects themselves, nor can it be, as it were, borrowed from them and taken up into the understanding by perception, but it is on the contrary an operation of the understanding itself, which is nothing more than the faculty of conjoining à priori and of bringing the variety of given representations under the unity of apperception. This principle is the highest in all human cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","text":"[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-16) All general conceptions—as such—depend, for their existence, on the analytical unity of consciousness. For example, when I think of red in general, I thereby think to myself a property which (as a characteristic mark) can be discovered somewhere, or can be united with other representations; consequently, it is only by means of a forethought possible synthetical unity that I can think to myself the analytical. A representation which is cogitated as common to different representations, is regarded as belonging to such as, besides this common representation, contain something different; consequently it must be previously thought in synthetical unity with other although only possible representations, before I can think in it the analytical unity of consciousness which makes it a conceptas communis. And thus the synthetical unity of apperception is the highest point with which we must connect every operation of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it our transcendental philosophy; indeed, this faculty is the understanding itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","text":"This fundamental principle of the necessary unity of apperception is indeed an identical, and therefore analytical, proposition; but it nevertheless explains the necessity for a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition, without which the identity of self-consciousness would be incogitable. For the ego, as a simple representation, presents us with no manifold content; only in intuition, which is quite different from the representation ego, can it be given us, and by means of conjunction it is cogitated in one self-consciousness. An understanding, in which all the manifold should be given by means of consciousness itself, would be intuitive; our understanding can only think and must look for its intuition to sense. I am, therefore, conscious of my identical self, in relation to all the variety of representations given to me in an intuition, because I call all of them my representations. In other words, I am conscious myself of a necessary à priori synthesis of my representations, which is called the original synthetical unity of apperception, under which rank all the representations presented to me, but that only by means of a synthesis.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"88b1dbeae2227a33b096458fa905d3f8f64e14c733a79fc73b7c1d5b8a4140f2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","order":32,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_PSUA","text":"### The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13\n\nThe supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in relation to sensibility was, according to our transcendental æsthetic, that all the manifold in intuition be subject to the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the possibility of it in relation to the understanding is that all the manifold in it be subject to conditions of the originally synthetical unity or apperception.[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-17) To the former of these two principles are subject all the various representations of intuition, in so far as they are given to us; to the latter, in so far as they must be capable of conjunction in one consciousness; for without this nothing can be thought or cognized, because the given representations would not have in common the act Of the apperception “I think” and therefore could not be connected in one self-consciousness.\n\n[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-17) Space and time, and all portions thereof, are intuitions; consequently are, with a manifold for their content, single representations. (See the Transcendental Æsthetic.) Consequently, they are not pure conceptions, by means of which the same consciousness is found in a great number of representations; but, on the contrary, they are many representations contained in one, the consciousness of which is, so to speak, compounded. The unity of consciousness is nevertheless synthetical and, therefore, primitive. From this peculiar character of consciousness follow many important consequences. (See § 21.)\n\nUnderstanding is, to speak generally, the faculty Of cognitions. These consist in the determined relation of given representation to an object. But an object is that, in the conception of which the manifold in a given intuition is united. Now all union of representations requires unity of consciousness in the synthesis of them. Consequently, it is the unity of consciousness alone that constitutes the possibility of representations relating to an object, and therefore of their objective validity, and of their becoming cognitions, and consequently, the possibility of the existence of the understanding itself.\n\nThe first pure cognition of understanding, then, upon which is founded all its other exercise, and which is at the same time perfectly independent of all conditions of mere sensuous intuition, is the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of external sensuous intuition, namely, space, affords us, per se, no cognition; it merely contributes the manifold in à priori intuition to a possible cognition. But, in order to cognize something in space (for example, a line), I must draw it, and thus produce synthetically a determined conjunction of the given manifold, so that the unity of this act is at the same time the unity of consciousness (in the conception of a line), and by this means alone is an object (a determinate space) cognized. The synthetical unity of consciousness is, therefore, an objective condition of all cognition, which I do not merely require in order to cognize an object, but to which every intuition must necessarily be subject, in order to become an object for me; because in any other way, and without this synthesis, the manifold in intuition could not be united in one consciousness.\n\nThis proposition is, as already said, itself analytical, although it constitutes the synthetical unity, the condition of all thought; for it states nothing more than that all my representations in any given intuition must be subject to the condition which alone enables me to connect them, as my representation with the identical self, and so to unite them synthetically in one apperception, by means of the general expression, “I think.”\n\nBut this principle is not to be regarded as a principle for every possible understanding, but only for the understanding by means of whose pure apperception in the thought I am, no manifold content is given. The understanding or mind which contained the manifold in intuition, in and through the act itself of its own self-consciousness, in other words, an understanding by and in the representation of which the objects of the representation should at the same time exist, would not require a special act of synthesis of the manifold as the condition of the unity of its consciousness, an act of which the human understanding, which thinks only and cannot intuite, has absolute need. But this principle is the first principle of all the operations of our understanding, so that we cannot form the least conception of any other possible understanding, either of one such as should be itself intuition, or possess a sensuous intuition, but with forms different from those of space and time.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","text":"### The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13\n\nThe supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in relation to sensibility was, according to our transcendental æsthetic, that all the manifold in intuition be subject to the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the possibility of it in relation to the understanding is that all the manifold in it be subject to conditions of the originally synthetical unity or apperception.[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-17) To the former of these two principles are subject all the various representations of intuition, in so far as they are given to us; to the latter, in so far as they must be capable of conjunction in one consciousness; for without this nothing can be thought or cognized, because the given representations would not have in common the act Of the apperception “I think” and therefore could not be connected in one self-consciousness.\n\n[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-17) Space and time, and all portions thereof, are intuitions; consequently are, with a manifold for their content, single representations. (See the Transcendental Æsthetic.) Consequently, they are not pure conceptions, by means of which the same consciousness is found in a great number of representations; but, on the contrary, they are many representations contained in one, the consciousness of which is, so to speak, compounded. The unity of consciousness is nevertheless synthetical and, therefore, primitive. From this peculiar character of consciousness follow many important consequences. (See § 21.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","text":"Understanding is, to speak generally, the faculty Of cognitions. These consist in the determined relation of given representation to an object. But an object is that, in the conception of which the manifold in a given intuition is united. Now all union of representations requires unity of consciousness in the synthesis of them. Consequently, it is the unity of consciousness alone that constitutes the possibility of representations relating to an object, and therefore of their objective validity, and of their becoming cognitions, and consequently, the possibility of the existence of the understanding itself.\n\nThe first pure cognition of understanding, then, upon which is founded all its other exercise, and which is at the same time perfectly independent of all conditions of mere sensuous intuition, is the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of external sensuous intuition, namely, space, affords us, per se, no cognition; it merely contributes the manifold in à priori intuition to a possible cognition. But, in order to cognize something in space (for example, a line), I must draw it, and thus produce synthetically a determined conjunction of the given manifold, so that the unity of this act is at the same time the unity of consciousness (in the conception of a line), and by this means alone is an object (a determinate space) cognized. The synthetical unity of consciousness is, therefore, an objective condition of all cognition, which I do not merely require in order to cognize an object, but to which every intuition must necessarily be subject, in order to become an object for me; because in any other way, and without this synthesis, the manifold in intuition could not be united in one consciousness.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","text":"This proposition is, as already said, itself analytical, although it constitutes the synthetical unity, the condition of all thought; for it states nothing more than that all my representations in any given intuition must be subject to the condition which alone enables me to connect them, as my representation with the identical self, and so to unite them synthetically in one apperception, by means of the general expression, “I think.”\n\nBut this principle is not to be regarded as a principle for every possible understanding, but only for the understanding by means of whose pure apperception in the thought I am, no manifold content is given. The understanding or mind which contained the manifold in intuition, in and through the act itself of its own self-consciousness, in other words, an understanding by and in the representation of which the objects of the representation should at the same time exist, would not require a special act of synthesis of the manifold as the condition of the unity of its consciousness, an act of which the human understanding, which thinks only and cannot intuite, has absolute need. But this principle is the first principle of all the operations of our understanding, so that we cannot form the least conception of any other possible understanding, either of one such as should be itself intuition, or possess a sensuous intuition, but with forms different from those of space and time.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"be4f5020d4ff547eab4229ba69c69182a7cfdd9df4960e7cd0a027f927d182c0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","order":33,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_WOUS","text":"### What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14\n\nIt is by means of the transcendental unity of apperception that all the manifold, given in an intuition is united into a conception of the object. On this account it is called objective, and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of the internal sense, by means of which the said manifold in intuition is given empirically to be so united. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as coexistent or as successive, depends upon circumstances, or empirical conditions. Hence the empirical unity of consciousness by means of association of representations, itself relates to a phenomenal world and is wholly contingent. On the contrary, the pure form of intuition in time, merely as an intuition, which contains a given manifold, is subject to the original unity of consciousness, and that solely by means of the necessary relation of the manifold in intuition to the “I think,” consequently by means of the pure synthesis of the understanding, which lies à priori at the foundation of all empirical synthesis. The transcendental unity of apperception is alone objectively valid; the empirical which we do not consider in this essay, and which is merely a unity deduced from the former under given conditions in concreto, possesses only subjective validity. One person connects the notion conveyed in a word with one thing, another with another thing; and the unity of consciousness in that which is empirical, is, in relation to that which is given by experience, not necessarily and universally valid.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","text":"### What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14\n\nIt is by means of the transcendental unity of apperception that all the manifold, given in an intuition is united into a conception of the object. On this account it is called objective, and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of the internal sense, by means of which the said manifold in intuition is given empirically to be so united. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as coexistent or as successive, depends upon circumstances, or empirical conditions. Hence the empirical unity of consciousness by means of association of representations, itself relates to a phenomenal world and is wholly contingent. On the contrary, the pure form of intuition in time, merely as an intuition, which contains a given manifold, is subject to the original unity of consciousness, and that solely by means of the necessary relation of the manifold in intuition to the “I think,” consequently by means of the pure synthesis of the understanding, which lies à priori at the foundation of all empirical synthesis. The transcendental unity of apperception is alone objectively valid; the empirical which we do not consider in this essay, and which is merely a unity deduced from the former under given conditions in concreto, possesses only subjective validity. One person connects the notion conveyed in a word with one thing, another with another thing; and the unity of consciousness in that which is empirical, is, in relation to that which is given by experience, not necessarily and universally valid.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fe51095b02ab6ba96b1878c8d409127270329c250366485c6204c3f117c87048","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","order":34,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ","text":"### The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15\n\nI could never satisfy myself with the definition which logicians give of a judgement. It is, according to them, the representation of a relation between two conceptions. I shall not dwell here on the faultiness of this definition, in that it suits only for categorical and not for hypothetical or disjunctive judgements, these latter containing a relation not of conceptions but of judgements themselves—a blunder from which many evil results have followed.[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-18) It is more important for our present purpose to observe, that this definition does not determine in what the said relation consists.\n\n[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-18) The tedious doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing more than an artifice by surreptitiously introducing immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae) among the premises of a pure syllogism, to give rise to an appearance of more modes of drawing a conclusion than that in the first figure, the artifice would not have had much success, had not its authors succeeded in bringing categorical judgements into exclusive respect, as those to which all others must be referred—a doctrine, however, which, according to § 5, is utterly false.\n\nBut if I investigate more closely the relation of given cognitions in every judgement, and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation which is produced according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), I find that judgement is nothing but the mode of bringing given cognitions under the objective unit of apperception. This is plain from our use of the term of relation is in judgements, in order to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective unity. For this term indicates the relation of these representations to the original apperception, and also their necessary unity, even although the judgement is empirical, therefore contingent, as in the judgement: “All bodies are heavy.” I do not mean by this, that these representations do necessarily belong to each other in empirical intuition, but that by means of the necessary unity of appreciation they belong to each other in the synthesis of intuitions, that is to say, they belong to each other according to principles of the objective determination of all our representations, in so far as cognition can arise from them, these principles being all deduced from the main principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. In this way alone can there arise from this relation a judgement, that is, a relation which has objective validity, and is perfectly distinct from that relation of the very same representations which has only subjective validity—a relation, to wit, which is produced according to laws of association. According to these laws, I could only say: “When I hold in my hand or carry a body, I feel an impression of weight”; but I could not say: “It, the body, is heavy”; for this is tantamount to saying both these representations are conjoined in the object, that is, without distinction as to the condition of the subject, and do not merely stand together in my perception, however frequently the perceptive act may be repeated.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","text":"### The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15\n\nI could never satisfy myself with the definition which logicians give of a judgement. It is, according to them, the representation of a relation between two conceptions. I shall not dwell here on the faultiness of this definition, in that it suits only for categorical and not for hypothetical or disjunctive judgements, these latter containing a relation not of conceptions but of judgements themselves—a blunder from which many evil results have followed.[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-18) It is more important for our present purpose to observe, that this definition does not determine in what the said relation consists.\n\n[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-18) The tedious doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing more than an artifice by surreptitiously introducing immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae) among the premises of a pure syllogism, to give rise to an appearance of more modes of drawing a conclusion than that in the first figure, the artifice would not have had much success, had not its authors succeeded in bringing categorical judgements into exclusive respect, as those to which all others must be referred—a doctrine, however, which, according to § 5, is utterly false.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","text":"But if I investigate more closely the relation of given cognitions in every judgement, and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation which is produced according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), I find that judgement is nothing but the mode of bringing given cognitions under the objective unit of apperception. This is plain from our use of the term of relation is in judgements, in order to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective unity. For this term indicates the relation of these representations to the original apperception, and also their necessary unity, even although the judgement is empirical, therefore contingent, as in the judgement: “All bodies are heavy.” I do not mean by this, that these representations do necessarily belong to each other in empirical intuition, but that by means of the necessary unity of appreciation they belong to each other in the synthesis of intuitions, that is to say, they belong to each other according to principles of the objective determination of all our representations, in so far as cognition can arise from them, these principles being all deduced from the main principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. In this way alone can there arise from this relation a judgement, that is, a relation which has objective validity, and is perfectly distinct from that relation of the very same representations which has only subjective validity—a relation, to wit, which is produced according to laws of association. According to these laws, I could only say: “When I hold in my hand or carry a body, I feel an impression of weight”; but I could not say: “It, the body, is heavy”; for this is tantamount to saying both these representations are conjoined in the object, that is, without distinction as to the condition of the subject, and do not merely stand together in my perception, however frequently the perceptive act may be repeated.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6320996b0ef2651f303bb97c4f3d41c9f4eb0800c2c3f1e01971a3f93f214f03","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","order":35,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ASIA","text":"### All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16\n\nThe manifold content given in a sensuous intuition comes necessarily under the original synthetical unity of apperception, because thereby alone is the unity of intuition possible (§ 13). But that act of the understanding, by which the manifold content of given representations (whether intuitions or conceptions) is brought under one apperception, is the logical function of judgements (§ 15). All the manifold, therefore, in so far as it is given in one empirical intuition, is determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement, by means of which it is brought into union in one consciousness. Now the categories are nothing else than these functions of judgement so far as the manifold in a given intuition is determined in relation to them (§ 9). Consequently, the manifold in a given intuition is necessarily subject to the categories of the understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","text":"### All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16\n\nThe manifold content given in a sensuous intuition comes necessarily under the original synthetical unity of apperception, because thereby alone is the unity of intuition possible (§ 13). But that act of the understanding, by which the manifold content of given representations (whether intuitions or conceptions) is brought under one apperception, is the logical function of judgements (§ 15). All the manifold, therefore, in so far as it is given in one empirical intuition, is determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement, by means of which it is brought into union in one consciousness. Now the categories are nothing else than these functions of judgement so far as the manifold in a given intuition is determined in relation to them (§ 9). Consequently, the manifold in a given intuition is necessarily subject to the categories of the understanding.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7b3582aaad179eed331a3f033cba3ca94fa2820b534b022a95a0dfb35e8ab467","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1","title":"Observation § 17","order":36,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_OBS1","text":"### Observation § 17\n\nThe manifold in an intuition, which I call mine, is represented by means of the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness, and this takes place by means of the category.[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-19) The category indicates accordingly that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold in an intuition is subject to a pure self-consciousness à priori, in the same manner as an empirical intuition is subject to a pure sensuous intuition, which is also à priori. In the above proposition, then, lies the beginning of a deduction of the pure conceptions of the understanding. Now, as the categories have their origin in the understanding alone, independently of sensibility, I must in my deduction make abstraction of the mode in which the manifold of an empirical intuition is given, in order to fix my attention exclusively on the unity which is brought by the understanding into the intuition by means of the category. In what follows (§ 22), it will be shown, from the mode in which the empirical intuition is given in the faculty of sensibility, that the unity which belongs to it is no other than that which the category (according to § 16) imposes on the manifold in a given intuition, and thus, its à priori validity in regard to all objects of sense being established, the purpose of our deduction will be fully attained.\n\n[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-19) The proof of this rests on the represented unity of intuition, by means of which an object is given, and which always includes in itself a synthesis of the manifold to be intuited, and also the relation of this latter to unity of apperception.\n\nBut there is one thing in the above demonstration of which I could not make abstraction, namely, that the manifold to be intuited must be given previously to the synthesis of the understanding, and independently of it. How this takes place remains here undetermined. For if I cogitate an understanding which was itself intuitive (as, for example, a divine understanding which should not represent given objects, but by whose representation the objects themselves should be given or produced), the categories would possess no significance in relation to such a faculty of cognition. They are merely rules for an understanding, whose whole power consists in thought, that is, in the act of submitting the synthesis of the manifold which is presented to it in intuition from a very different quarter, to the unity of apperception; a faculty, therefore, which cognizes nothing per se, but only connects and arranges the material of cognition, the intuition, namely, which must be presented to it by means of the object. But to show reasons for this peculiar character of our understandings, that it produces unity of apperception à priori only by means of categories, and a certain kind and number thereof, is as impossible as to explain why we are endowed with precisely so many functions of judgement and no more, or why time and space are the only forms of our intuition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","text":"### Observation § 17\n\nThe manifold in an intuition, which I call mine, is represented by means of the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness, and this takes place by means of the category.[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-19) The category indicates accordingly that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold in an intuition is subject to a pure self-consciousness à priori, in the same manner as an empirical intuition is subject to a pure sensuous intuition, which is also à priori. In the above proposition, then, lies the beginning of a deduction of the pure conceptions of the understanding. Now, as the categories have their origin in the understanding alone, independently of sensibility, I must in my deduction make abstraction of the mode in which the manifold of an empirical intuition is given, in order to fix my attention exclusively on the unity which is brought by the understanding into the intuition by means of the category. In what follows (§ 22), it will be shown, from the mode in which the empirical intuition is given in the faculty of sensibility, that the unity which belongs to it is no other than that which the category (according to § 16) imposes on the manifold in a given intuition, and thus, its à priori validity in regard to all objects of sense being established, the purpose of our deduction will be fully attained.\n\n[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-19) The proof of this rests on the represented unity of intuition, by means of which an object is given, and which always includes in itself a synthesis of the manifold to be intuited, and also the relation of this latter to unity of apperception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","text":"But there is one thing in the above demonstration of which I could not make abstraction, namely, that the manifold to be intuited must be given previously to the synthesis of the understanding, and independently of it. How this takes place remains here undetermined. For if I cogitate an understanding which was itself intuitive (as, for example, a divine understanding which should not represent given objects, but by whose representation the objects themselves should be given or produced), the categories would possess no significance in relation to such a faculty of cognition. They are merely rules for an understanding, whose whole power consists in thought, that is, in the act of submitting the synthesis of the manifold which is presented to it in intuition from a very different quarter, to the unity of apperception; a faculty, therefore, which cognizes nothing per se, but only connects and arranges the material of cognition, the intuition, namely, which must be presented to it by means of the object. But to show reasons for this peculiar character of our understandings, that it produces unity of apperception à priori only by means of categories, and a certain kind and number thereof, is as impossible as to explain why we are endowed with precisely so many functions of judgement and no more, or why time and space are the only forms of our intuition.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2a21547e866cf9715c5cc634bf824fde93b0831545c65b0d0531fc5c6c85b953","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Observation § 17","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","order":37,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CAOE","text":"### In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18\n\nTo think an object and to cognize an object are by no means the same thing. In cognition there are two elements: firstly, the conception, whereby an object is cogitated (the category); and, secondly, the intuition, whereby the object is given. For supposing that to the conception a corresponding intuition could not be given, it would still be a thought as regards its form, but without any object, and no cognition of anything would be possible by means of it, inasmuch as, so far as I knew, there existed and could exist nothing to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible to us is sensuous; consequently, our thought of an object by means of a pure conception of the understanding, can become cognition for us only in so far as this conception is applied to objects of the senses. Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition—of that which is immediately represented in space and time by means of sensation as real. Through the determination of pure intuition we obtain à priori cognitions of objects, as in mathematics, but only as regards their form as phenomena; whether there can exist things which must be intuited in this form is not thereby established. All mathematical conceptions, therefore, are not per se cognition, except in so far as we presuppose that there exist things which can only be represented conformably to the form of our pure sensuous intuition. But things in space and time are given only in so far as they are perceptions (representations accompanied with sensation), therefore only by empirical representation. Consequently the pure conceptions of the understanding, even when they are applied to intuitions à priori (as in mathematics), produce cognition only in so far as these (and therefore the conceptions of the understanding by means of them) can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories do not, even by means of pure intuition afford us any cognition of things; they can only do so in so far as they can be applied to empirical intuition. That is to say, the categories serve only to render empirical cognition possible. But this is what we call experience. Consequently, in cognition, their application to objects of experience is the only legitimate use of the categories.\n\n§ 19\n\nThe foregoing proposition is of the utmost importance, for it determines the limits of the exercise of the pure conceptions of the understanding in regard to objects, just as transcendental æsthetic determined the limits of the exercise of the pure form of our sensuous intuition. Space and time, as conditions of the possibility of the presentation of objects to us, are valid no further than for objects of sense, consequently, only for experience. Beyond these limits they represent to us nothing, for they belong only to sense, and have no reality apart from it. The pure conceptions of the understanding are free from this limitation, and extend to objects of intuition in general, be the intuition like or unlike to ours, provided only it be sensuous, and not intellectual. But this extension of conceptions beyond the range of our intuition is of no advantage; for they are then mere empty conceptions of objects, as to the possibility or impossibility of the existence of which they furnish us with no means of discovery. They are mere forms of thought, without objective reality, because we have no intuition to which the synthetical unity of apperception, which alone the categories contain, could be applied, for the purpose of determining an object. Our sensuous and empirical intuition can alone give them significance and meaning.\n\nIf, then, we suppose an object of a non-sensuous intuition to be given we can in that case represent it by all those predicates which are implied in the presupposition that nothing appertaining to sensuous intuition belongs to it; for example, that it is not extended, or in space; that its duration is not time; that in it no change (the effect of the determinations in time) is to be met with, and so on. But it is no proper knowledge if I merely indicate what the intuition of the object is not, without being able to say what is contained in it, for I have not shown the possibility of an object to which my pure conception of understanding could be applicable, because I have not been able to furnish any intuition corresponding to it, but am only able to say that our intuition is not valid for it. But the most important point is this, that to a something of this kind not one category can be found applicable. Take, for example, the conception of substance, that is, something that can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate; in regard to this conception I am quite ignorant whether there can really be anything to correspond to such a determination of thought, if empirical intuition did not afford me the occasion for its application. But of this more in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","text":"### In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18\n\nTo think an object and to cognize an object are by no means the same thing. In cognition there are two elements: firstly, the conception, whereby an object is cogitated (the category); and, secondly, the intuition, whereby the object is given. For supposing that to the conception a corresponding intuition could not be given, it would still be a thought as regards its form, but without any object, and no cognition of anything would be possible by means of it, inasmuch as, so far as I knew, there existed and could exist nothing to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible to us is sensuous; consequently, our thought of an object by means of a pure conception of the understanding, can become cognition for us only in so far as this conception is applied to objects of the senses. Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition—of that which is immediately represented in space and time by means of sensation as real. Through the determination of pure intuition we obtain à priori cognitions of objects, as in mathematics, but only as regards their form as phenomena; whether there can exist things which must be intuited in this form is not thereby established. All mathematical conceptions, therefore, are not per se cognition, except in so far as we presuppose that there exist things which can only be represented conformably to the form of our pure sensuous intuition. But things in space and time are given only in so far as they are perceptions (representations accompanied with sensation), therefore only by empirical representation. Consequently the pure conceptions of the understanding, even when they are applied to intuitions à priori (as in mathematics), produce cognition only in so far as these (and therefore the conceptions of the understanding by means of them) can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories do not, even by means of pure intuition afford us any cognition of things; they can only do so in so far as they can be applied to empirical intuition. That is to say, the categories serve only to render empirical cognition possible. But this is what we call experience. Consequently, in cognition, their application to objects of experience is the only legitimate use of the categories.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","text":"§ 19\n\nThe foregoing proposition is of the utmost importance, for it determines the limits of the exercise of the pure conceptions of the understanding in regard to objects, just as transcendental æsthetic determined the limits of the exercise of the pure form of our sensuous intuition. Space and time, as conditions of the possibility of the presentation of objects to us, are valid no further than for objects of sense, consequently, only for experience. Beyond these limits they represent to us nothing, for they belong only to sense, and have no reality apart from it. The pure conceptions of the understanding are free from this limitation, and extend to objects of intuition in general, be the intuition like or unlike to ours, provided only it be sensuous, and not intellectual. But this extension of conceptions beyond the range of our intuition is of no advantage; for they are then mere empty conceptions of objects, as to the possibility or impossibility of the existence of which they furnish us with no means of discovery. They are mere forms of thought, without objective reality, because we have no intuition to which the synthetical unity of apperception, which alone the categories contain, could be applied, for the purpose of determining an object. Our sensuous and empirical intuition can alone give them significance and meaning.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","text":"If, then, we suppose an object of a non-sensuous intuition to be given we can in that case represent it by all those predicates which are implied in the presupposition that nothing appertaining to sensuous intuition belongs to it; for example, that it is not extended, or in space; that its duration is not time; that in it no change (the effect of the determinations in time) is to be met with, and so on. But it is no proper knowledge if I merely indicate what the intuition of the object is not, without being able to say what is contained in it, for I have not shown the possibility of an object to which my pure conception of understanding could be applicable, because I have not been able to furnish any intuition corresponding to it, but am only able to say that our intuition is not valid for it. But the most important point is this, that to a something of this kind not one category can be found applicable. Take, for example, the conception of substance, that is, something that can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate; in regard to this conception I am quite ignorant whether there can really be anything to correspond to such a determination of thought, if empirical intuition did not afford me the occasion for its application. But of this more in the sequel.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2a8bbe82a3d4268fb1e1e5352b7beaae2bb45bf231ddcb6dbdf45e6d12cc2550","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","order":38,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ACOS","text":"### Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20\n\nThe pure conceptions of the understanding apply to objects of intuition in general, through the understanding alone, whether the intuition be our own or some other, provided only it be sensuous, but are, for this very reason, mere forms of thought, by means of which alone no determined object can be cognized. The synthesis or conjunction of the manifold in these conceptions relates, we have said, only to the unity of apperception, and is for this reason the ground of the possibility of à priori cognition, in so far as this cognition is dependent on the understanding. This synthesis is, therefore, not merely transcendental, but also purely intellectual. But because a certain form of sensuous intuition exists in the mind à priori which rests on the receptivity of the representative faculty (sensibility), the understanding, as a spontaneity, is able to determine the internal sense by means of the diversity of given representations, conformably to the synthetical unity of apperception, and thus to cogitate the synthetical unity of the apperception of the manifold of sensuous intuition à priori, as the condition to which must necessarily be submitted all objects of human intuition. And in this manner the categories as mere forms of thought receive objective reality, that is, application to objects which are given to us in intuition, but that only as phenomena, for it is only of phenomena that we are capable of à priori intuition.\n\nThis synthesis of the manifold of sensuous intuition, which is possible and necessary à priori, may be called figurative (synthesis speciosa), in contradistinction to that which is cogitated in the mere category in regard to the manifold of an intuition in general, and is called connection or conjunction of the understanding (synthesis intellectualis). Both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves precede à priori all experience, but also because they form the basis for the possibility of other cognition à priori.\n\nBut the figurative synthesis, when it has relation only to the originally synthetical unity of apperception, that is to the transcendental unity cogitated in the categories, must, to be distinguished from the purely intellectual conjunction, be entitled the transcendental synthesis of imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object even without its presence in intuition. Now, as all our intuition is sensuous, imagination, by reason of the subjective condition under which alone it can give a corresponding intuition to the conceptions of the understanding, belongs to sensibility. But in so far as the synthesis of the imagination is an act of spontaneity, which is determinative, and not, like sense, merely determinable, and which is consequently able to determine sense à priori, according to its form, conformably to the unity of apperception, in so far is the imagination a faculty of determining sensibility à priori, and its synthesis of intuitions according to the categories must be the transcendental synthesis of the imagination. It is an operation of the understanding on sensibility, and the first application of the understanding to objects of possible intuition, and at the same time the basis for the exercise of the other functions of that faculty. As figurative, it is distinguished from the merely intellectual synthesis, which is produced by the understanding alone, without the aid of imagination. Now, in so far as imagination is spontaneity, I sometimes call it also the productive imagination, and distinguish it from the reproductive, the synthesis of which is subject entirely to empirical laws, those of association, namely, and which, therefore, contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of à priori cognition, and for this reason belongs not to transcendental philosophy, but to psychology.\n\nWe have now arrived at the proper place for explaining the paradox which must have struck every one in our exposition of the internal sense (§ 6), namely—how this sense represents us to our own consciousness, only as we appear to ourselves, not as we are in ourselves, because, to wit, we intuite ourselves only as we are inwardly affected. Now this appears to be contradictory, inasmuch as we thus stand in a passive relation to ourselves; and therefore in the systems of psychology, the internal sense is commonly held to be one with the faculty of apperception, while we, on the contrary, carefully distinguish them.\n\nThat which determines the internal sense is the understanding, and its original power of conjoining the manifold of intuition, that is, of bringing this under an apperception (upon which rests the possibility of the understanding itself). Now, as the human understanding is not in itself a faculty of intuition, and is unable to exercise such a power, in order to conjoin, as it were, the manifold of its own intuition, the synthesis of understanding is, considered per se, nothing but the unity of action, of which, as such, it is self-conscious, even apart from sensibility, by which, moreover, it is able to determine our internal sense in respect of the manifold which may be presented to it according to the form of sensuous intuition. Thus, under the name of a transcendental synthesis of imagination, the understanding exercises an activity upon the passive subject, whose faculty it is; and so we are right in saying that the internal sense is affected thereby. Apperception and its synthetical unity are by no means one and the same with the internal sense. The former, as the source of all our synthetical conjunction, applies, under the name of the categories, to the manifold of intuition in general, prior to all sensuous intuition of objects. The internal sense, on the contrary, contains merely the form of intuition, but without any synthetical conjunction of the manifold therein, and consequently does not contain any determined intuition, which is possible only through consciousness of the determination of the manifold by the transcendental act of the imagination (synthetical influence of the understanding on the internal sense), which I have named figurative synthesis.\n\nThis we can indeed always perceive in ourselves. We cannot cogitate a geometrical line without drawing it in thought, nor a circle without describing it, nor represent the three dimensions of space without drawing three lines from the same point perpendicular to one another. We cannot even cogitate time, unless, in drawing a straight line (which is to serve as the external figurative representation of time), we fix our attention on the act of the synthesis of the manifold, whereby we determine successively the internal sense, and thus attend also to the succession of this determination. Motion as an act of the subject (not as a determination of an object),[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-20) consequently the synthesis of the manifold in space, if we make abstraction of space and attend merely to the act by which we determine the internal sense according to its form, is that which produces the conception of succession. The understanding, therefore, does by no means find in the internal sense any such synthesis of the manifold, but produces it, in that it affects this sense. At the same time, how “I who think” is distinct from the “i” which intuites itself (other modes of intuition being cogitable as at least possible), and yet one and the same with this latter as the same subject; how, therefore, I am able to say: “I, as an intelligence and thinking subject, cognize myself as an object thought, so far as I am, moreover, given to myself in intuition—only, like other phenomena, not as I am in myself, and as considered by the understanding, but merely as I appear”—is a question that has in it neither more nor less difficulty than the question—“How can I be an object to myself?” or this—“How I can be an object of my own intuition and internal perceptions?” But that such must be the fact, if we admit that space is merely a pure form of the phenomena of external sense, can be clearly proved by the consideration that we cannot represent time, which is not an object of external intuition, in any other way than under the image of a line, which we draw in thought, a mode of representation without which we could not cognize the unity of its dimension, and also that we are necessitated to take our determination of periods of time, or of points of time, for all our internal perceptions from the changes which we perceive in outward things. It follows that we must arrange the determinations of the internal sense, as phenomena in time, exactly in the same manner as we arrange those of the external senses in space. And consequently, if we grant, respecting this latter, that by means of them we know objects only in so far as we are affected externally, we must also confess, with regard to the internal sense, that by means of it we intuite ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves; in other words, as regards internal intuition, we cognize our own subject only as phenomenon, and not as it is in itself.[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-21)\n\n[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-20) Motion of an object in space does not belong to a pure science, consequently not to geometry; because, that a thing is movable cannot be known à priori, but only from experience. But motion, considered as the description of a space, is a pure act of the successive synthesis of the manifold in external intuition by means of productive imagination, and belongs not only to geometry, but even to transcendental philosophy.\n\n[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-21) I do not see why so much difficulty should be found in admitting that our internal sense is affected by ourselves. Every act of attention exemplifies it. In such an act the understanding determines the internal sense by the synthetical conjunction which it cogitates, conformably to the internal intuition which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How much the mind is usually affected thereby every one will be able to perceive in himself.\n\n§ 21\n\nOn the other hand, in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold content of representations, consequently in the synthetical unity of apperception, I am conscious of myself, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that “I am.” This representation is a thought, not an intuition. Now, as in order to cognize ourselves, in addition to the act of thinking, which subjects the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, there is necessary a determinate mode of intuition, whereby this manifold is given; although my own existence is certainly not mere phenomenon (much less mere illusion), the determination of my existence[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-22) Can only take place conformably to the form of the internal sense, according to the particular mode in which the manifold which I conjoin is given in internal intuition, and I have therefore no knowledge of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of self is thus very far from a knowledge of self, in which I do not use the categories, whereby I cogitate an object, by means of the conjunction of the manifold in one apperception. In the same way as I require, for the sake of the cognition of an object distinct from myself, not only the thought of an object in general (in the category), but also an intuition by which to determine that general conception, in the same way do I require, in order to the cognition of myself, not only the consciousness of myself or the thought that I think myself, but in addition an intuition of the manifold in myself, by which to determine this thought. It is true that I exist as an intelligence which is conscious only of its faculty of conjunction or synthesis, but subjected in relation to the manifold which this intelligence has to conjoin to a limitative conjunction called the internal sense. My intelligence (that is, I) can render that conjunction or synthesis perceptible only according to the relations of time, which are quite beyond the proper sphere of the conceptions of the understanding and consequently cognize itself in respect to an intuition (which cannot possibly be intellectual, nor given by the understanding), only as it appears to itself, and not as it would cognize itself, if its intuition were intellectual.\n\n[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-22) The “I think” expresses the act of determining my own existence. My existence is thus already given by the act of consciousness; but the mode in which I must determine my existence, that is, the mode in which I must place the manifold belonging to my existence, is not thereby given. For this purpose intuition of self is required, and this intuition possesses a form given à priori, namely, time, which is sensuous, and belongs to our receptivity of the determinable. Now, as I do not possess another intuition of self which gives the determining in me (of the spontaneity of which I am conscious), prior to the act of determination, in the same manner as time gives the determinable, it is clear that I am unable to determine my own existence as that of a spontaneous being, but I am only able to represent to myself the spontaneity of my thought, that is, of my determination, and my existence remains ever determinable in a purely sensuous manner, that is to say, like the existence of a phenomenon. But it is because of this spontaneity that I call myself an intelligence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","text":"### Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20\n\nThe pure conceptions of the understanding apply to objects of intuition in general, through the understanding alone, whether the intuition be our own or some other, provided only it be sensuous, but are, for this very reason, mere forms of thought, by means of which alone no determined object can be cognized. The synthesis or conjunction of the manifold in these conceptions relates, we have said, only to the unity of apperception, and is for this reason the ground of the possibility of à priori cognition, in so far as this cognition is dependent on the understanding. This synthesis is, therefore, not merely transcendental, but also purely intellectual. But because a certain form of sensuous intuition exists in the mind à priori which rests on the receptivity of the representative faculty (sensibility), the understanding, as a spontaneity, is able to determine the internal sense by means of the diversity of given representations, conformably to the synthetical unity of apperception, and thus to cogitate the synthetical unity of the apperception of the manifold of sensuous intuition à priori, as the condition to which must necessarily be submitted all objects of human intuition. And in this manner the categories as mere forms of thought receive objective reality, that is, application to objects which are given to us in intuition, but that only as phenomena, for it is only of phenomena that we are capable of à priori intuition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0002","text":"This synthesis of the manifold of sensuous intuition, which is possible and necessary à priori, may be called figurative (synthesis speciosa), in contradistinction to that which is cogitated in the mere category in regard to the manifold of an intuition in general, and is called connection or conjunction of the understanding (synthesis intellectualis). Both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves precede à priori all experience, but also because they form the basis for the possibility of other cognition à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","text":"But the figurative synthesis, when it has relation only to the originally synthetical unity of apperception, that is to the transcendental unity cogitated in the categories, must, to be distinguished from the purely intellectual conjunction, be entitled the transcendental synthesis of imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object even without its presence in intuition. Now, as all our intuition is sensuous, imagination, by reason of the subjective condition under which alone it can give a corresponding intuition to the conceptions of the understanding, belongs to sensibility. But in so far as the synthesis of the imagination is an act of spontaneity, which is determinative, and not, like sense, merely determinable, and which is consequently able to determine sense à priori, according to its form, conformably to the unity of apperception, in so far is the imagination a faculty of determining sensibility à priori, and its synthesis of intuitions according to the categories must be the transcendental synthesis of the imagination. It is an operation of the understanding on sensibility, and the first application of the understanding to objects of possible intuition, and at the same time the basis for the exercise of the other functions of that faculty. As figurative, it is distinguished from the merely intellectual synthesis, which is produced by the understanding alone, without the aid of imagination. Now, in so far as imagination is spontaneity, I sometimes call it also the productive imagination, and distinguish it from the reproductive, the synthesis of which is subject entirely to empirical laws, those of association, namely, and which, therefore, contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of à priori cognition, and for this reason belongs not to transcendental philosophy, but to psychology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0004","text":"We have now arrived at the proper place for explaining the paradox which must have struck every one in our exposition of the internal sense (§ 6), namely—how this sense represents us to our own consciousness, only as we appear to ourselves, not as we are in ourselves, because, to wit, we intuite ourselves only as we are inwardly affected. Now this appears to be contradictory, inasmuch as we thus stand in a passive relation to ourselves; and therefore in the systems of psychology, the internal sense is commonly held to be one with the faculty of apperception, while we, on the contrary, carefully distinguish them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","text":"That which determines the internal sense is the understanding, and its original power of conjoining the manifold of intuition, that is, of bringing this under an apperception (upon which rests the possibility of the understanding itself). Now, as the human understanding is not in itself a faculty of intuition, and is unable to exercise such a power, in order to conjoin, as it were, the manifold of its own intuition, the synthesis of understanding is, considered per se, nothing but the unity of action, of which, as such, it is self-conscious, even apart from sensibility, by which, moreover, it is able to determine our internal sense in respect of the manifold which may be presented to it according to the form of sensuous intuition. Thus, under the name of a transcendental synthesis of imagination, the understanding exercises an activity upon the passive subject, whose faculty it is; and so we are right in saying that the internal sense is affected thereby. Apperception and its synthetical unity are by no means one and the same with the internal sense. The former, as the source of all our synthetical conjunction, applies, under the name of the categories, to the manifold of intuition in general, prior to all sensuous intuition of objects. The internal sense, on the contrary, contains merely the form of intuition, but without any synthetical conjunction of the manifold therein, and consequently does not contain any determined intuition, which is possible only through consciousness of the determination of the manifold by the transcendental act of the imagination (synthetical influence of the understanding on the internal sense), which I have named figurative synthesis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","text":"This we can indeed always perceive in ourselves. We cannot cogitate a geometrical line without drawing it in thought, nor a circle without describing it, nor represent the three dimensions of space without drawing three lines from the same point perpendicular to one another. We cannot even cogitate time, unless, in drawing a straight line (which is to serve as the external figurative representation of time), we fix our attention on the act of the synthesis of the manifold, whereby we determine successively the internal sense, and thus attend also to the succession of this determination. Motion as an act of the subject (not as a determination of an object),[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-20) consequently the synthesis of the manifold in space, if we make abstraction of space and attend merely to the act by which we determine the internal sense according to its form, is that which produces the conception of succession. The understanding, therefore, does by no means find in the internal sense any such synthesis of the manifold, but produces it, in that it affects this sense. At the same time, how “I who think” is distinct from the “i” which intuites itself (other modes of intuition being cogitable as at least possible), and yet one and the same with this latter as the same subject; how, therefore, I am able to say: “I, as an intelligence and thinking subject, cognize myself as an object thought, so far as I am, moreover, given to myself in intuition—only, like other phenomena, not as I am in myself, and as considered by the understanding, but merely as I appear”—is a question that has in it neither more nor less difficulty than the question—“How can I be an object to myself?” or this—“How I can be an object of my own intuition and internal perceptions?” But that such must be the fact, if we admit that space is merely a pure form of the phenomena of external sense, can be clearly proved by the consideration that we cannot represent time, which is not an object of external intuition, in any other way than under the image of a line, which we draw in thought, a mode of representation without which we could not cognize the unity of its dimension, and also that we are necessitated to take our determination of periods of time, or of points of time, for all our internal perceptions from the changes which we perceive in outward things. It follows that we must arrange the determinations of the internal sense, as phenomena in time, exactly in the same manner as we arrange those of the external senses in space. And consequently, if we grant, respecting this latter, that by means of them we know objects only in so far as we are affected externally, we must also confess, with regard to the internal sense, that by means of it we intuite ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves; in other words, as regards internal intuition, we cognize our own subject only as phenomenon, and not as it is in itself.[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-21)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0007","text":"[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-20) Motion of an object in space does not belong to a pure science, consequently not to geometry; because, that a thing is movable cannot be known à priori, but only from experience. But motion, considered as the description of a space, is a pure act of the successive synthesis of the manifold in external intuition by means of productive imagination, and belongs not only to geometry, but even to transcendental philosophy.\n\n[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-21) I do not see why so much difficulty should be found in admitting that our internal sense is affected by ourselves. Every act of attention exemplifies it. In such an act the understanding determines the internal sense by the synthetical conjunction which it cogitates, conformably to the internal intuition which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How much the mind is usually affected thereby every one will be able to perceive in himself.\n\n§ 21","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","text":"On the other hand, in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold content of representations, consequently in the synthetical unity of apperception, I am conscious of myself, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that “I am.” This representation is a thought, not an intuition. Now, as in order to cognize ourselves, in addition to the act of thinking, which subjects the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, there is necessary a determinate mode of intuition, whereby this manifold is given; although my own existence is certainly not mere phenomenon (much less mere illusion), the determination of my existence[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-22) Can only take place conformably to the form of the internal sense, according to the particular mode in which the manifold which I conjoin is given in internal intuition, and I have therefore no knowledge of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of self is thus very far from a knowledge of self, in which I do not use the categories, whereby I cogitate an object, by means of the conjunction of the manifold in one apperception. In the same way as I require, for the sake of the cognition of an object distinct from myself, not only the thought of an object in general (in the category), but also an intuition by which to determine that general conception, in the same way do I require, in order to the cognition of myself, not only the consciousness of myself or the thought that I think myself, but in addition an intuition of the manifold in myself, by which to determine this thought. It is true that I exist as an intelligence which is conscious only of its faculty of conjunction or synthesis, but subjected in relation to the manifold which this intelligence has to conjoin to a limitative conjunction called the internal sense. My intelligence (that is, I) can render that conjunction or synthesis perceptible only according to the relations of time, which are quite beyond the proper sphere of the conceptions of the understanding and consequently cognize itself in respect to an intuition (which cannot possibly be intellectual, nor given by the understanding), only as it appears to itself, and not as it would cognize itself, if its intuition were intellectual.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","text":"[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-22) The “I think” expresses the act of determining my own existence. My existence is thus already given by the act of consciousness; but the mode in which I must determine my existence, that is, the mode in which I must place the manifold belonging to my existence, is not thereby given. For this purpose intuition of self is required, and this intuition possesses a form given à priori, namely, time, which is sensuous, and belongs to our receptivity of the determinable. Now, as I do not possess another intuition of self which gives the determining in me (of the spontaneity of which I am conscious), prior to the act of determination, in the same manner as time gives the determinable, it is clear that I am unable to determine my own existence as that of a spontaneous being, but I am only able to represent to myself the spontaneity of my thought, that is, of my determination, and my existence remains ever determinable in a purely sensuous manner, that is to say, like the existence of a phenomenon. But it is because of this spontaneity that I call myself an intelligence.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ab25c680140b62917152e6fe20f905de1a45c7bce84f118af7e3183dd3ffde84","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","order":39,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_TDUP","text":"### Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22\n\nIn the metaphysical deduction, the à priori origin of categories was proved by their complete accordance with the general logical of thought; in the transcendental deduction was exhibited the possibility of the categories as à priori cognitions of objects of an intuition in general (§ 16 and 17).At present we are about to explain the possibility of cognizing, à priori, by means of the categories, all objects which can possibly be presented to our senses, not, indeed, according to the form of their intuition, but according to the laws of their conjunction or synthesis, and thus, as it were, of prescribing laws to nature and even of rendering nature possible. For if the categories were inadequate to this task, it would not be evident to us why everything that is presented to our senses must be subject to those laws which have an à priori origin in the understanding itself.\n\nI premise that by the term synthesis of apprehension I understand the combination of the manifold in an empirical intuition, whereby perception, that is, empirical consciousness of the intuition (as phenomenon), is possible.\n\nWe have à priori forms of the external and internal sensuous intuition in the representations of space and time, and to these must the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon be always comformable, because the synthesis itself can only take place according to these forms. But space and time are not merely forms of sensuous intuition, but intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), and therefore contain à priori the determination of the unity of this manifold.[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-23) (See the Transcendent Æsthetic.) Therefore is unity of the synthesis of the manifold without or within us, consequently also a conjunction to which all that is to be represented as determined in space or time must correspond, given à priori along with (not in) these intuitions, as the condition of the synthesis of all apprehension of them. But this synthetical unity can be no other than that of the conjunction of the manifold of a given intuition in general, in a primitive act of consciousness, according to the categories, but applied to our sensuous intuition. Consequently all synthesis, whereby alone is even perception possible, is subject to the categories. And, as experience is cognition by means of conjoined perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience and are therefore valid à priori for all objects of experience.\n\n[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-23) Space represented as an object (as geometry really requires it to be) contains more than the mere form of the intuition; namely, a combination of the manifold given according to the form of sensibility into a representation that can be intuited; so that the form of the intuition gives us merely the manifold, but the formal intuition gives unity of representation. In the æsthetic, I regarded this unity as belonging entirely to sensibility, for the purpose of indicating that it antecedes all conceptions, although it presupposes a synthesis which does not belong to sense, through which alone, however, all our conceptions of space and time are possible. For as by means of this unity alone (the understanding determining the sensibility) space and time are given as intuitions, it follows that the unity of this intuition à priori belongs to space and time, and not to the conception of the understanding (§ 20).\n\nWhen, then, for example, I make the empirical intuition of a house by apprehension of the manifold contained therein into a perception, the necessary unity of space and of my external sensuous intuition lies at the foundation of this act, and I, as it were, draw the form of the house conformably to this synthetical unity of the manifold in space. But this very synthetical unity remains, even when I abstract the form of space, and has its seat in the understanding, and is in fact the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition; that is to say, the category of quantity, to which the aforesaid synthesis of apprehension, that is, the perception, must be completely conformable.[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-24)\n\n[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-24) In this manner it is proved, that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be conformable to the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual, and contained à priori in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity which at one time, under the name of imagination, at another under that of understanding, produces conjunction in the manifold of intuition.\n\nTo take another example, when I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (fluidity and solidity), which, as such, stand toward each other mutually in a relation of time. But in the time, which I place as an internal intuition, at the foundation of this phenomenon, I represent to myself synthetical unity of the manifold, without which the aforesaid relation could not be given in an intuition as determined (in regard to the succession of time). Now this synthetical unity, as the à priori condition under which I conjoin the manifold of an intuition, is, if I make abstraction of the permanent form of my internal intuition (that is to say, of time), the category of cause, by means of which, when applied to my sensibility, I determine everything that occurs according to relations of time. Consequently apprehension in such an event, and the event itself, as far as regards the possibility of its perception, stands under the conception of the relation of cause and effect: and so in all other cases.\n\nCategories are conceptions which prescribe laws à priori to phenomena, consequently to nature as the complex of all phenomena (natura materialiter spectata). And now the question arises—inasmuch as these categories are not derived from nature, and do not regulate themselves according to her as their model (for in that case they would be empirical)—how it is conceivable that nature must regulate herself according to them, in other words, how the categories can determine à priori the synthesis of the manifold of nature, and yet not derive their origin from her. The following is the solution of this enigma.\n\nIt is not in the least more difficult to conceive how the laws of the phenomena of nature must harmonize with the understanding and with its à priori form—that is, its faculty of conjoining the manifold—than it is to understand how the phenomena themselves must correspond with the à priori form of our sensuous intuition. For laws do not exist in the phenomena any more than the phenomena exist as things in themselves. Laws do not exist except by relation to the subject in which the phenomena inhere, in so far as it possesses understanding, just as phenomena have no existence except by relation to the same existing subject in so far as it has senses. To things as things in themselves, conformability to law must necessarily belong independently of an understanding to cognize them. But phenomena are only representations of things which are utterly unknown in respect to what they are in themselves. But as mere representations, they stand under no law of conjunction except that which the conjoining faculty prescribes. Now that which conjoins the manifold of sensuous intuition is imagination, a mental act to which understanding contributes unity of intellectual synthesis, and sensibility, manifoldness of apprehension. Now as all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, and this empirical synthesis itself on the transcendental, consequently on the categories, it is evident that all possible perceptions, and therefore everything that can attain to empirical consciousness, that is, all phenomena of nature, must, as regards their conjunction, be subject to the categories. And nature (considered merely as nature in general) is dependent on them, as the original ground of her necessary conformability to law (as natura formaliter spectata). But the pure faculty (of the understanding) of prescribing laws à priori to phenomena by means of mere categories, is not competent to enounce other or more laws than those on which a nature in general, as a conformability to law of phenomena of space and time, depends. Particular laws, inasmuch as they concern empirically determined phenomena, cannot be entirely deduced from pure laws, although they all stand under them. Experience must be superadded in order to know these particular laws; but in regard to experience in general, and everything that can be cognized as an object thereof, these à priori laws are our only rule and guide.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","text":"### Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22\n\nIn the metaphysical deduction, the à priori origin of categories was proved by their complete accordance with the general logical of thought; in the transcendental deduction was exhibited the possibility of the categories as à priori cognitions of objects of an intuition in general (§ 16 and 17).At present we are about to explain the possibility of cognizing, à priori, by means of the categories, all objects which can possibly be presented to our senses, not, indeed, according to the form of their intuition, but according to the laws of their conjunction or synthesis, and thus, as it were, of prescribing laws to nature and even of rendering nature possible. For if the categories were inadequate to this task, it would not be evident to us why everything that is presented to our senses must be subject to those laws which have an à priori origin in the understanding itself.\n\nI premise that by the term synthesis of apprehension I understand the combination of the manifold in an empirical intuition, whereby perception, that is, empirical consciousness of the intuition (as phenomenon), is possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","text":"We have à priori forms of the external and internal sensuous intuition in the representations of space and time, and to these must the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon be always comformable, because the synthesis itself can only take place according to these forms. But space and time are not merely forms of sensuous intuition, but intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), and therefore contain à priori the determination of the unity of this manifold.[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-23) (See the Transcendent Æsthetic.) Therefore is unity of the synthesis of the manifold without or within us, consequently also a conjunction to which all that is to be represented as determined in space or time must correspond, given à priori along with (not in) these intuitions, as the condition of the synthesis of all apprehension of them. But this synthetical unity can be no other than that of the conjunction of the manifold of a given intuition in general, in a primitive act of consciousness, according to the categories, but applied to our sensuous intuition. Consequently all synthesis, whereby alone is even perception possible, is subject to the categories. And, as experience is cognition by means of conjoined perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience and are therefore valid à priori for all objects of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","text":"[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-23) Space represented as an object (as geometry really requires it to be) contains more than the mere form of the intuition; namely, a combination of the manifold given according to the form of sensibility into a representation that can be intuited; so that the form of the intuition gives us merely the manifold, but the formal intuition gives unity of representation. In the æsthetic, I regarded this unity as belonging entirely to sensibility, for the purpose of indicating that it antecedes all conceptions, although it presupposes a synthesis which does not belong to sense, through which alone, however, all our conceptions of space and time are possible. For as by means of this unity alone (the understanding determining the sensibility) space and time are given as intuitions, it follows that the unity of this intuition à priori belongs to space and time, and not to the conception of the understanding (§ 20).\n\nWhen, then, for example, I make the empirical intuition of a house by apprehension of the manifold contained therein into a perception, the necessary unity of space and of my external sensuous intuition lies at the foundation of this act, and I, as it were, draw the form of the house conformably to this synthetical unity of the manifold in space. But this very synthetical unity remains, even when I abstract the form of space, and has its seat in the understanding, and is in fact the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition; that is to say, the category of quantity, to which the aforesaid synthesis of apprehension, that is, the perception, must be completely conformable.[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-24)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","text":"[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-24) In this manner it is proved, that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be conformable to the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual, and contained à priori in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity which at one time, under the name of imagination, at another under that of understanding, produces conjunction in the manifold of intuition.\n\nTo take another example, when I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (fluidity and solidity), which, as such, stand toward each other mutually in a relation of time. But in the time, which I place as an internal intuition, at the foundation of this phenomenon, I represent to myself synthetical unity of the manifold, without which the aforesaid relation could not be given in an intuition as determined (in regard to the succession of time). Now this synthetical unity, as the à priori condition under which I conjoin the manifold of an intuition, is, if I make abstraction of the permanent form of my internal intuition (that is to say, of time), the category of cause, by means of which, when applied to my sensibility, I determine everything that occurs according to relations of time. Consequently apprehension in such an event, and the event itself, as far as regards the possibility of its perception, stands under the conception of the relation of cause and effect: and so in all other cases.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0005","text":"Categories are conceptions which prescribe laws à priori to phenomena, consequently to nature as the complex of all phenomena (natura materialiter spectata). And now the question arises—inasmuch as these categories are not derived from nature, and do not regulate themselves according to her as their model (for in that case they would be empirical)—how it is conceivable that nature must regulate herself according to them, in other words, how the categories can determine à priori the synthesis of the manifold of nature, and yet not derive their origin from her. The following is the solution of this enigma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","text":"It is not in the least more difficult to conceive how the laws of the phenomena of nature must harmonize with the understanding and with its à priori form—that is, its faculty of conjoining the manifold—than it is to understand how the phenomena themselves must correspond with the à priori form of our sensuous intuition. For laws do not exist in the phenomena any more than the phenomena exist as things in themselves. Laws do not exist except by relation to the subject in which the phenomena inhere, in so far as it possesses understanding, just as phenomena have no existence except by relation to the same existing subject in so far as it has senses. To things as things in themselves, conformability to law must necessarily belong independently of an understanding to cognize them. But phenomena are only representations of things which are utterly unknown in respect to what they are in themselves. But as mere representations, they stand under no law of conjunction except that which the conjoining faculty prescribes. Now that which conjoins the manifold of sensuous intuition is imagination, a mental act to which understanding contributes unity of intellectual synthesis, and sensibility, manifoldness of apprehension. Now as all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, and this empirical synthesis itself on the transcendental, consequently on the categories, it is evident that all possible perceptions, and therefore everything that can attain to empirical consciousness, that is, all phenomena of nature, must, as regards their conjunction, be subject to the categories. And nature (considered merely as nature in general) is dependent on them, as the original ground of her necessary conformability to law (as natura formaliter spectata). But the pure faculty (of the understanding) of prescribing laws à priori to phenomena by means of mere categories, is not competent to enounce other or more laws than those on which a nature in general, as a conformability to law of phenomena of space and time, depends. Particular laws, inasmuch as they concern empirically determined phenomena, cannot be entirely deduced from pure laws, although they all stand under them. Experience must be superadded in order to know these particular laws; but in regard to experience in general, and everything that can be cognized as an object thereof, these à priori laws are our only rule and guide.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ccb66f3c22b1ddbb1fad5d1a23a429259ecaf7b6bb252957a506044e1693557f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","order":40,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_RTDC","text":"### Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23\n\nWe cannot think any object except by means of the categories; we cannot cognize any thought except by means of intuitions corresponding to these conceptions. Now all our intuitions are sensuous, and our cognition, in so far as the object of it is given, is empirical. But empirical cognition is experience; consequently no à priori cognition is possible for us, except of objects of possible experience.[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-25)\n\n[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-25) Lest my readers should stumble at this assertion, and the conclusions that may be too rashly drawn from it, I must remind them that the categories in the act of thought are by no means limited by the conditions of our sensuous intuition, but have an unbounded sphere of action. It is only the cognition of the object of thought, the determining of the object, which requires intuition. In the absence of intuition, our thought of an object may still have true and useful consequences in regard to the exercise of reason by the subject. But as this exercise of reason is not always directed on the determination of the object, in other words, on cognition thereof, but also on the determination of the subject and its volition, I do not intend to treat of it in this place.\n\nBut this cognition, which is limited to objects of experience, is not for that reason derived entirely, from, experience, but—and this is asserted of the pure intuitions and the pure conceptions of the understanding—there are, unquestionably, elements of cognition, which exist in the mind à priori. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary harmony of experience with the conceptions of its objects can be cogitated. Either experience makes these conceptions possible, or the conceptions make experience possible. The former of these statements will not hold good with respect to the categories (nor in regard to pure sensuous intuition), for they are à priori conceptions, and therefore independent of experience. The assertion of an empirical origin would attribute to them a sort of generatio aequivoca. Consequently, nothing remains but to adopt the second alternative (which presents us with a system, as it were, of the epigenesis of pure reason), namely, that on the part of the understanding the categories do contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience. But with respect to the questions how they make experience possible, and what are the principles of the possibility thereof with which they present us in their application to phenomena, the following section on the transcendental exercise of the faculty of judgement will inform the reader.\n\nIt is quite possible that someone may propose a species of preformation-system of pure reason—a middle way between the two—to wit, that the categories are neither innate and first à priori principles of cognition, nor derived from experience, but are merely subjective aptitudes for thought implanted in us contemporaneously with our existence, which were so ordered and disposed by our Creator, that their exercise perfectly harmonizes with the laws of nature which regulate experience. Now, not to mention that with such an hypothesis it is impossible to say at what point we must stop in the employment of predetermined aptitudes, the fact that the categories would in this case entirely lose that character of necessity which is essentially involved in the very conception of them, is a conclusive objection to it. The conception of cause, for example, which expresses the necessity of an effect under a presupposed condition, would be false, if it rested only upon such an arbitrary subjective necessity of uniting certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation. I could not then say—“The effect is connected with its cause in the object (that is, necessarily),” but only, “I am so constituted that I can think this representation as so connected, and not otherwise.” Now this is just what the sceptic wants. For in this case, all our knowledge, depending on the supposed objective validity of our judgement, is nothing but mere illusion; nor would there be wanting people who would deny any such subjective necessity in respect to themselves, though they must feel it. At all events, we could not dispute with any one on that which merely depends on the manner in which his subject is organized.\n\nShort view of the above Deduction.\n\nThe foregoing deduction is an exposition of the pure conceptions of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical à priori cognition), as principles of the possibility of experience, but of experience as the determination of all phenomena in space and time in general—of experience, finally, from the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to time and space as original forms of sensibility.\n\nI consider the division by paragraphs to be necessary only up to this point, because we had to treat of the elementary conceptions. As we now proceed to the exposition of the employment of these, I shall not designate the chapters in this manner any further.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","text":"### Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23\n\nWe cannot think any object except by means of the categories; we cannot cognize any thought except by means of intuitions corresponding to these conceptions. Now all our intuitions are sensuous, and our cognition, in so far as the object of it is given, is empirical. But empirical cognition is experience; consequently no à priori cognition is possible for us, except of objects of possible experience.[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-25)\n\n[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-25) Lest my readers should stumble at this assertion, and the conclusions that may be too rashly drawn from it, I must remind them that the categories in the act of thought are by no means limited by the conditions of our sensuous intuition, but have an unbounded sphere of action. It is only the cognition of the object of thought, the determining of the object, which requires intuition. In the absence of intuition, our thought of an object may still have true and useful consequences in regard to the exercise of reason by the subject. But as this exercise of reason is not always directed on the determination of the object, in other words, on cognition thereof, but also on the determination of the subject and its volition, I do not intend to treat of it in this place.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","text":"But this cognition, which is limited to objects of experience, is not for that reason derived entirely, from, experience, but—and this is asserted of the pure intuitions and the pure conceptions of the understanding—there are, unquestionably, elements of cognition, which exist in the mind à priori. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary harmony of experience with the conceptions of its objects can be cogitated. Either experience makes these conceptions possible, or the conceptions make experience possible. The former of these statements will not hold good with respect to the categories (nor in regard to pure sensuous intuition), for they are à priori conceptions, and therefore independent of experience. The assertion of an empirical origin would attribute to them a sort of generatio aequivoca. Consequently, nothing remains but to adopt the second alternative (which presents us with a system, as it were, of the epigenesis of pure reason), namely, that on the part of the understanding the categories do contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience. But with respect to the questions how they make experience possible, and what are the principles of the possibility thereof with which they present us in their application to phenomena, the following section on the transcendental exercise of the faculty of judgement will inform the reader.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","text":"It is quite possible that someone may propose a species of preformation-system of pure reason—a middle way between the two—to wit, that the categories are neither innate and first à priori principles of cognition, nor derived from experience, but are merely subjective aptitudes for thought implanted in us contemporaneously with our existence, which were so ordered and disposed by our Creator, that their exercise perfectly harmonizes with the laws of nature which regulate experience. Now, not to mention that with such an hypothesis it is impossible to say at what point we must stop in the employment of predetermined aptitudes, the fact that the categories would in this case entirely lose that character of necessity which is essentially involved in the very conception of them, is a conclusive objection to it. The conception of cause, for example, which expresses the necessity of an effect under a presupposed condition, would be false, if it rested only upon such an arbitrary subjective necessity of uniting certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation. I could not then say—“The effect is connected with its cause in the object (that is, necessarily),” but only, “I am so constituted that I can think this representation as so connected, and not otherwise.” Now this is just what the sceptic wants. For in this case, all our knowledge, depending on the supposed objective validity of our judgement, is nothing but mere illusion; nor would there be wanting people who would deny any such subjective necessity in respect to themselves, though they must feel it. At all events, we could not dispute with any one on that which merely depends on the manner in which his subject is organized.\n\nShort view of the above Deduction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","text":"The foregoing deduction is an exposition of the pure conceptions of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical à priori cognition), as principles of the possibility of experience, but of experience as the determination of all phenomena in space and time in general—of experience, finally, from the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to time and space as original forms of sensibility.\n\nI consider the division by paragraphs to be necessary only up to this point, because we had to treat of the elementary conceptions. As we now proceed to the exposition of the employment of these, I shall not designate the chapters in this manner any further.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"33f4ba0d1f6c76086fdf4b2714dadb0e72065e6845f9eb071ea9ea298541304d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","order":41,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_BIAP","text":"# Book Two\n\n### BOOK II. Analytic of Principles\n\nGeneral logic is constructed upon a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are, understanding, judgement, and reason. This science, accordingly, treats in its analytic of conceptions, judgements, and conclusions in exact correspondence with the functions and order of those mental powers which we include generally under the generic denomination of understanding.\n\nAs this merely formal logic makes abstraction of all content of cognition, whether pure or empirical, and occupies itself with the mere form of thought (discursive cognition), it must contain in its analytic a canon for reason. For the form of reason has its law, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition about which it is employed, can be discovered à priori, by the simple analysis of the action of reason into its momenta.\n\nTranscendental logic, limited as it is to a determinate content, that of pure à priori cognitions, to wit, cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is evident that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid, and therefore does not belong to the logic of truth (that is, to analytic), but as a logic of illusion, occupies a particular department in the scholastic system under the name of transcendental dialectic.\n\nUnderstanding and judgement accordingly possess in transcendental logic a canon of objectively valid, and therefore true exercise, and are comprehended in the analytical department of that logic. But reason, in her endeavours to arrive by à priori means at some true statement concerning objects and to extend cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience, is altogether dialectic, and her illusory assertions cannot be constructed into a canon such as an analytic ought to contain.\n\nAccordingly, the analytic of principles will be merely a canon for the faculty of judgement, for the instruction of this faculty in its application to phenomena of the pure conceptions of the understanding, which contain the necessary condition for the establishment of à priori laws. On this account, although the subject of the following chapters is the especial principles of understanding, I shall make use of the term Doctrine of the faculty of judgement, in order to define more particularly my present purpose.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","text":"# Book Two\n\n### BOOK II. Analytic of Principles\n\nGeneral logic is constructed upon a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are, understanding, judgement, and reason. This science, accordingly, treats in its analytic of conceptions, judgements, and conclusions in exact correspondence with the functions and order of those mental powers which we include generally under the generic denomination of understanding.\n\nAs this merely formal logic makes abstraction of all content of cognition, whether pure or empirical, and occupies itself with the mere form of thought (discursive cognition), it must contain in its analytic a canon for reason. For the form of reason has its law, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition about which it is employed, can be discovered à priori, by the simple analysis of the action of reason into its momenta.\n\nTranscendental logic, limited as it is to a determinate content, that of pure à priori cognitions, to wit, cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is evident that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid, and therefore does not belong to the logic of truth (that is, to analytic), but as a logic of illusion, occupies a particular department in the scholastic system under the name of transcendental dialectic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","text":"Understanding and judgement accordingly possess in transcendental logic a canon of objectively valid, and therefore true exercise, and are comprehended in the analytical department of that logic. But reason, in her endeavours to arrive by à priori means at some true statement concerning objects and to extend cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience, is altogether dialectic, and her illusory assertions cannot be constructed into a canon such as an analytic ought to contain.\n\nAccordingly, the analytic of principles will be merely a canon for the faculty of judgement, for the instruction of this faculty in its application to phenomena of the pure conceptions of the understanding, which contain the necessary condition for the establishment of à priori laws. On this account, although the subject of the following chapters is the especial principles of understanding, I shall make use of the term Doctrine of the faculty of judgement, in order to define more particularly my present purpose.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"daaf5f1aae5587fcdce2dc7a22c225139851e50c7483684f95a279ca362b3727","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","order":42,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ","text":"### INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General\n\nIf understanding in general be defined as the faculty of laws or rules, the faculty of judgement may be termed the faculty of subsumption under these rules; that is, of distinguishing whether this or that does or does not stand under a given rule (casus datae legis). General logic contains no directions or precepts for the faculty of judgement, nor can it contain any such. For as it makes abstraction of all content of cognition, no duty is left for it, except that of exposing analytically the mere form of cognition in conceptions, judgements, and conclusions, and of thereby establishing formal rules for all exercise of the understanding. Now if this logic wished to give some general direction how we should subsume under these rules, that is, how we should distinguish whether this or that did or did not stand under them, this again could not be done otherwise than by means of a rule. But this rule, precisely because it is a rule, requires for itself direction from the faculty of judgement. Thus, it is evident that the understanding is capable of being instructed by rules, but that the judgement is a peculiar talent, which does not, and cannot require tuition, but only exercise. This faculty is therefore the specific quality of the so-called mother wit, the want of which no scholastic discipline can compensate.\n\nFor although education may furnish, and, as it were, engraft upon a limited understanding rules borrowed from other minds, yet the power of employing these rules correctly must belong to the pupil himself; and no rule which we can prescribe to him with this purpose is, in the absence or deficiency of this gift of nature, secure from misuse.[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-26) A physician therefore, a judge or a statesman, may have in his head many admirable pathological, juridical, or political rules, in a degree that may enable him to be a profound teacher in his particular science, and yet in the application of these rules he may very possibly blunder—either because he is wanting in natural judgement (though not in understanding) and, whilst he can comprehend the general in abstracto, cannot distinguish whether a particular case in concreto ought to rank under the former; or because his faculty of judgement has not been sufficiently exercised by examples and real practice. Indeed, the grand and only use of examples, is to sharpen the judgement. For as regards the correctness and precision of the insight of the understanding, examples are commonly injurious rather than otherwise, because, as casus in terminis they seldom adequately fulfil the conditions of the rule. Besides, they often weaken the power of our understanding to apprehend rules or laws in their universality, independently of particular circumstances of experience; and hence, accustom us to employ them more as formulae than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of the judgement, which he who is naturally deficient in that faculty cannot afford to dispense with.\n\n[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-26) Deficiency in judgement is properly that which is called stupidity; and for such a failing we know no remedy. A dull or narrow-minded person, to whom nothing is wanting but a proper degree of understanding, may be improved by tuition, even so far as to deserve the epithet of learned. But as such persons frequently labour under a deficiency in the faculty of judgement, it is not uncommon to find men extremely learned who in the application of their science betray a lamentable degree this irremediable want.\n\nBut although general logic cannot give directions to the faculty of judgement, the case is very different as regards transcendental logic, insomuch that it appears to be the especial duty of the latter to secure and direct, by means of determinate rules, the faculty of judgement in the employment of the pure understanding. For, as a doctrine, that is, as an endeavour to enlarge the sphere of the understanding in regard to pure à priori cognitions, philosophy is worse than useless, since from all the attempts hitherto made, little or no ground has been gained. But, as a critique, in order to guard against the mistakes of the faculty of judgement (lapsus judicii) in the employment of the few pure conceptions of the understanding which we possess, although its use is in this case purely negative, philosophy is called upon to apply all its acuteness and penetration.\n\nBut transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity, that besides indicating the rule, or rather the general condition for rules, which is given in the pure conception of the understanding, it can, at the same time, indicate à priori the case to which the rule must be applied. The cause of the superiority which, in this respect, transcendental philosophy possesses above all other sciences except mathematics, lies in this: it treats of conceptions which must relate à priori to their objects, whose objective validity consequently cannot be demonstrated à posteriori, and is, at the same time, under the obligation of presenting in general but sufficient tests, the conditions under which objects can be given in harmony with those conceptions; otherwise they would be mere logical forms, without content, and not pure conceptions of the understanding.\n\nOur transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement will contain two chapters. The first will treat of the sensuous condition under which alone pure conceptions of the understanding can be employed—that is, of the schematism of the pure understanding. The second will treat of those synthetical judgements which are derived à priori from pure conceptions of the understanding under those conditions, and which lie à priori at the foundation of all other cognitions, that is to say, it will treat of the principles of the pure understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","text":"### INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General\n\nIf understanding in general be defined as the faculty of laws or rules, the faculty of judgement may be termed the faculty of subsumption under these rules; that is, of distinguishing whether this or that does or does not stand under a given rule (casus datae legis). General logic contains no directions or precepts for the faculty of judgement, nor can it contain any such. For as it makes abstraction of all content of cognition, no duty is left for it, except that of exposing analytically the mere form of cognition in conceptions, judgements, and conclusions, and of thereby establishing formal rules for all exercise of the understanding. Now if this logic wished to give some general direction how we should subsume under these rules, that is, how we should distinguish whether this or that did or did not stand under them, this again could not be done otherwise than by means of a rule. But this rule, precisely because it is a rule, requires for itself direction from the faculty of judgement. Thus, it is evident that the understanding is capable of being instructed by rules, but that the judgement is a peculiar talent, which does not, and cannot require tuition, but only exercise. This faculty is therefore the specific quality of the so-called mother wit, the want of which no scholastic discipline can compensate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","text":"For although education may furnish, and, as it were, engraft upon a limited understanding rules borrowed from other minds, yet the power of employing these rules correctly must belong to the pupil himself; and no rule which we can prescribe to him with this purpose is, in the absence or deficiency of this gift of nature, secure from misuse.[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-26) A physician therefore, a judge or a statesman, may have in his head many admirable pathological, juridical, or political rules, in a degree that may enable him to be a profound teacher in his particular science, and yet in the application of these rules he may very possibly blunder—either because he is wanting in natural judgement (though not in understanding) and, whilst he can comprehend the general in abstracto, cannot distinguish whether a particular case in concreto ought to rank under the former; or because his faculty of judgement has not been sufficiently exercised by examples and real practice. Indeed, the grand and only use of examples, is to sharpen the judgement. For as regards the correctness and precision of the insight of the understanding, examples are commonly injurious rather than otherwise, because, as casus in terminis they seldom adequately fulfil the conditions of the rule. Besides, they often weaken the power of our understanding to apprehend rules or laws in their universality, independently of particular circumstances of experience; and hence, accustom us to employ them more as formulae than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of the judgement, which he who is naturally deficient in that faculty cannot afford to dispense with.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","text":"[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-26) Deficiency in judgement is properly that which is called stupidity; and for such a failing we know no remedy. A dull or narrow-minded person, to whom nothing is wanting but a proper degree of understanding, may be improved by tuition, even so far as to deserve the epithet of learned. But as such persons frequently labour under a deficiency in the faculty of judgement, it is not uncommon to find men extremely learned who in the application of their science betray a lamentable degree this irremediable want.\n\nBut although general logic cannot give directions to the faculty of judgement, the case is very different as regards transcendental logic, insomuch that it appears to be the especial duty of the latter to secure and direct, by means of determinate rules, the faculty of judgement in the employment of the pure understanding. For, as a doctrine, that is, as an endeavour to enlarge the sphere of the understanding in regard to pure à priori cognitions, philosophy is worse than useless, since from all the attempts hitherto made, little or no ground has been gained. But, as a critique, in order to guard against the mistakes of the faculty of judgement (lapsus judicii) in the employment of the few pure conceptions of the understanding which we possess, although its use is in this case purely negative, philosophy is called upon to apply all its acuteness and penetration.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","text":"But transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity, that besides indicating the rule, or rather the general condition for rules, which is given in the pure conception of the understanding, it can, at the same time, indicate à priori the case to which the rule must be applied. The cause of the superiority which, in this respect, transcendental philosophy possesses above all other sciences except mathematics, lies in this: it treats of conceptions which must relate à priori to their objects, whose objective validity consequently cannot be demonstrated à posteriori, and is, at the same time, under the obligation of presenting in general but sufficient tests, the conditions under which objects can be given in harmony with those conceptions; otherwise they would be mere logical forms, without content, and not pure conceptions of the understanding.\n\nOur transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement will contain two chapters. The first will treat of the sensuous condition under which alone pure conceptions of the understanding can be employed—that is, of the schematism of the pure understanding. The second will treat of those synthetical judgements which are derived à priori from pure conceptions of the understanding under those conditions, and which lie à priori at the foundation of all other cognitions, that is to say, it will treat of the principles of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8ce5ae07a72fba9439042421b56309aaa461031fe0a9c5b9c337691f2a6b977a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","order":43,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CISP","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF THE FACULTY OF JUDGEMENT OR, ANALYTIC OF PRINCIPLES\n\n### Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\nIn all subsumptions of an object under a conception, the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the conception; in other words, the conception must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it. For this is the meaning of the expression: “An object is contained under a conception.” Thus the empirical conception of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical conception of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is cogitated in the former is intuited in the latter.\n\nBut pure conceptions of the understanding, when compared with empirical intuitions, or even with sensuous intuitions in general, are quite heterogeneous, and never can be discovered in any intuition. How then is the subsumption of the latter under the former, and consequently the application of the categories to phenomena, possible?—For it is impossible to say, for example: “Causality can be intuited through the senses and is contained in the phenomenon.”—This natural and important question forms the real cause of the necessity of a transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement, with the purpose, to wit, of showing how pure conceptions of the understanding can be applied to phenomena. In all other sciences, where the conceptions by which the object is thought in the general are not so different and heterogeneous from those which represent the object in concreto—as it is given, it is quite unnecessary to institute any special inquiries concerning the application of the former to the latter.\n\nNow it is quite clear that there must be some third thing, which on the one side is homogeneous with the category, and with the phenomenon on the other, and so makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without any empirical content), and yet must on the one side be intellectual, on the other sensuous. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.\n\nThe conception of the understanding contains pure synthetical unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of the internal sense, consequently of the conjunction of all representations, contains à priori a manifold in the pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is so far homogeneous with the category, which constitutes the unity thereof, that it is universal and rests upon a rule à priori. On the other hand, it is so far homogeneous with the phenomenon, inasmuch as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Thus an application of the category to phenomena becomes possible, by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the conceptions of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the latter under the former.\n\nAfter what has been proved in our deduction of the categories, no one, it is to be hoped, can hesitate as to the proper decision of the question, whether the employment of these pure conceptions of the understanding ought to be merely empirical or also transcendental; in other words, whether the categories, as conditions of a possible experience, relate à priori solely to phenomena, or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, their application can be extended to objects as things in themselves. For we have there seen that conceptions are quite impossible, and utterly without signification, unless either to them, or at least to the elements of which they consist, an object be given; and that, consequently, they cannot possibly apply to objects as things in themselves without regard to the question whether and how these may be given to us; and, further, that the only manner in which objects can be given to us is by means of the modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure à priori conceptions, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, must contain à priori formal conditions of sensibility (of the internal sense, namely), which again contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the conception of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall name the schema of the conception of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall call the schematism of the pure understanding.\n\nThe schema is, in itself, always a mere product of the imagination. But, as the synthesis of imagination has for its aim no single intuition, but merely unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is clearly distinguishable from the image. Thus, if I place five points one after another.... this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which may be either five or a hundred, this thought is rather the representation of a method of representing in an image a sum (e.g., a thousand) in conformity with a conception, than the image itself, an image which I should find some little difficulty in reviewing, and comparing with the conception. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination to present its image to a conception, I call the schema of this conception.\n\nIn truth, it is not images of objects, but schemata, which lie at the foundation of our pure sensuous conceptions. No image could ever be adequate to our conception of a triangle in general. For the generalness of the conception it never could attain to, as this includes under itself all triangles, whether right-angled, acute-angled, etc., whilst the image would always be limited to a single part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere else than in thought, and it indicates a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in regard to pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of the object, ever to the empirical conception. On the contrary, the conception always relates immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition, in conformity with a certain general conception. The conception of a dog indicates a rule, according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in general, without being limited to any particular individual form which experience presents to me, or indeed to any possible image that I can represent to myself in concreto. This schematism of our understanding in regard to phenomena and their mere form, is an art, hidden in the depths of the human soul, whose true modes of action we shall only with difficulty discover and unveil. Thus much only can we say: “The image is a product of the empirical faculty of the productive imagination—the schema of sensuous conceptions (of figures in space, for example) is a product, and, as it were, a monogram of the pure imagination à priori, whereby and according to which images first become possible, which, however, can be connected with the conception only mediately by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never fully adequate to it.” On the other hand, the schema of a pure conception of the understanding is something that cannot be reduced into any image—it is nothing else than the pure synthesis expressed by the category, conformably, to a rule of unity according to conceptions. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, a product which concerns the determination of the internal sense, according to conditions of its form (time) in respect to all representations, in so far as these representations must be conjoined à priori in one conception, conformably to the unity of apperception.\n\nWithout entering upon a dry and tedious analysis of the essential requisites of transcendental schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding, we shall rather proceed at once to give an explanation of them according to the order of the categories, and in connection therewith.\n\nFor the external sense the pure image of all quantities (quantorum) is space; the pure image of all objects of sense in general, is time. But the pure schema of quantity (quantitatis) as a conception of the understanding, is number, a representation which comprehends the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous quantities). Thus, number is nothing else than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold in a homogeneous intuition, by means of my generating time itself in my apprehension of the intuition.\n\nReality, in the pure conception of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, consequently, the conception of which indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the conception of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of these two consists therefore in the difference of one and the same time, as a time filled or a time empty. Now as time is only the form of intuition, consequently of objects as phenomena, that which in objects corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (Sachheit, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or quantity by which it can fill time, that is to say, the internal sense in respect of the representation of an object, more or less, until it vanishes into nothing (= 0 = negatio). Thus there is a relation and connection between reality and negation, or rather a transition from the former to the latter, which makes every reality representable to us as a quantum; and the schema of a reality as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is exactly this continuous and uniform generation of the reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree, down to the vanishing thereof, or gradually ascend from negation to the quantity thereof.\n\nThe schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time; that is, the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time; a substratum which therefore remains, whilst all else changes. (Time passes not, but in it passes the existence of the changeable. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and permanent, corresponds that which in the phenomenon is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance, and it is only by it that the succession and coexistence of phenomena can be determined in regard to time.)\n\nThe schema of cause and of the causality of a thing is the real which, when posited, is always followed by something else. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subjected to a rule.\n\nThe schema of community (reciprocity of action and reaction), or the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one with those of the other, according to a general rule.\n\nThe schema of possibility is the accordance of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (as, for example, opposites cannot exist together at the same time in the same thing, but only after each other), and is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at any time.\n\nThe schema of reality is existence in a determined time.\n\nThe schema of necessity is the existence of an object in all time.\n\nIt is clear, from all this, that the schema of the category of quantity contains and represents the generation (synthesis) of time itself, in the successive apprehension of an object; the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation with the representation of time, or the filling up of time; the schema of relation the relation of perceptions to each other in all time (that is, according to a rule of the determination of time): and finally, the schema of modality and its categories, time itself, as the correlative of the determination of an object—whether it does belong to time, and how. The schemata, therefore, are nothing but à priori determinations of time according to rules, and these, in regard to all possible objects, following the arrangement of the categories, relate to the series in time, the content in time, the order in time, and finally, to the complex or totality in time.\n\nHence it is apparent that the schematism of the understanding, by means of the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, amounts to nothing else than the unity of the manifold of intuition in the internal sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function corresponding to the internal sense (a receptivity). Thus, the schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding are the true and only conditions whereby our understanding receives an application to objects, and consequently significance. Finally, therefore, the categories are only capable of empirical use, inasmuch as they serve merely to subject phenomena to the universal rules of synthesis, by means of an à priori necessary unity (on account of the necessary union of all consciousness in one original apperception); and so to render them susceptible of a complete connection in one experience. But within this whole of possible experience lie all our cognitions, and in the universal relation to this experience consists transcendental truth, which antecedes all empirical truth, and renders the latter possible.\n\nIt is, however, evident at first sight, that although the schemata of sensibility are the sole agents in realizing the categories, they do, nevertheless, also restrict them, that is, they limit the categories by conditions which lie beyond the sphere of understanding—namely, in sensibility. Hence the schema is properly only the phenomenon, or the sensuous conception of an object in harmony with the category. (Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon—sensatio realitas phaenomenon; constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon—aeternitas, necessitas, phaenomena, etc.) Now, if we remove a restrictive condition, we thereby amplify, it appears, the formerly limited conception. In this way, the categories in their pure signification, free from all conditions of sensibility, ought to be valid of things as they are, and not, as the schemata represent them, merely as they appear; and consequently the categories must have a significance far more extended, and wholly independent of all schemata. In truth, there does always remain to the pure conceptions of the understanding, after abstracting every sensuous condition, a value and significance, which is, however, merely logical. But in this case, no object is given them, and therefore they have no meaning sufficient to afford us a conception of an object. The notion of substance, for example, if we leave out the sensuous determination of permanence, would mean nothing more than a something which can be cogitated as subject, without the possibility of becoming a predicate to anything else. Of this representation I can make nothing, inasmuch as it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing possesses which must thus be valid as premier subject. Consequently, the categories, without schemata are merely functions of the understanding for the production of conceptions, but do not represent any object. This significance they derive from sensibility, which at the same time realizes the understanding and restricts it.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF THE FACULTY OF JUDGEMENT OR, ANALYTIC OF PRINCIPLES\n\n### Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\nIn all subsumptions of an object under a conception, the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the conception; in other words, the conception must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it. For this is the meaning of the expression: “An object is contained under a conception.” Thus the empirical conception of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical conception of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is cogitated in the former is intuited in the latter.\n\nBut pure conceptions of the understanding, when compared with empirical intuitions, or even with sensuous intuitions in general, are quite heterogeneous, and never can be discovered in any intuition. How then is the subsumption of the latter under the former, and consequently the application of the categories to phenomena, possible?—For it is impossible to say, for example: “Causality can be intuited through the senses and is contained in the phenomenon.”—This natural and important question forms the real cause of the necessity of a transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement, with the purpose, to wit, of showing how pure conceptions of the understanding can be applied to phenomena. In all other sciences, where the conceptions by which the object is thought in the general are not so different and heterogeneous from those which represent the object in concreto—as it is given, it is quite unnecessary to institute any special inquiries concerning the application of the former to the latter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","text":"Now it is quite clear that there must be some third thing, which on the one side is homogeneous with the category, and with the phenomenon on the other, and so makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without any empirical content), and yet must on the one side be intellectual, on the other sensuous. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.\n\nThe conception of the understanding contains pure synthetical unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of the internal sense, consequently of the conjunction of all representations, contains à priori a manifold in the pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is so far homogeneous with the category, which constitutes the unity thereof, that it is universal and rests upon a rule à priori. On the other hand, it is so far homogeneous with the phenomenon, inasmuch as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Thus an application of the category to phenomena becomes possible, by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the conceptions of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the latter under the former.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","text":"After what has been proved in our deduction of the categories, no one, it is to be hoped, can hesitate as to the proper decision of the question, whether the employment of these pure conceptions of the understanding ought to be merely empirical or also transcendental; in other words, whether the categories, as conditions of a possible experience, relate à priori solely to phenomena, or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, their application can be extended to objects as things in themselves. For we have there seen that conceptions are quite impossible, and utterly without signification, unless either to them, or at least to the elements of which they consist, an object be given; and that, consequently, they cannot possibly apply to objects as things in themselves without regard to the question whether and how these may be given to us; and, further, that the only manner in which objects can be given to us is by means of the modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure à priori conceptions, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, must contain à priori formal conditions of sensibility (of the internal sense, namely), which again contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the conception of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall name the schema of the conception of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall call the schematism of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0004","text":"The schema is, in itself, always a mere product of the imagination. But, as the synthesis of imagination has for its aim no single intuition, but merely unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is clearly distinguishable from the image. Thus, if I place five points one after another.... this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which may be either five or a hundred, this thought is rather the representation of a method of representing in an image a sum (e.g., a thousand) in conformity with a conception, than the image itself, an image which I should find some little difficulty in reviewing, and comparing with the conception. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination to present its image to a conception, I call the schema of this conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","text":"In truth, it is not images of objects, but schemata, which lie at the foundation of our pure sensuous conceptions. No image could ever be adequate to our conception of a triangle in general. For the generalness of the conception it never could attain to, as this includes under itself all triangles, whether right-angled, acute-angled, etc., whilst the image would always be limited to a single part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere else than in thought, and it indicates a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in regard to pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of the object, ever to the empirical conception. On the contrary, the conception always relates immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition, in conformity with a certain general conception. The conception of a dog indicates a rule, according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in general, without being limited to any particular individual form which experience presents to me, or indeed to any possible image that I can represent to myself in concreto. This schematism of our understanding in regard to phenomena and their mere form, is an art, hidden in the depths of the human soul, whose true modes of action we shall only with difficulty discover and unveil. Thus much only can we say: “The image is a product of the empirical faculty of the productive imagination—the schema of sensuous conceptions (of figures in space, for example) is a product, and, as it were, a monogram of the pure imagination à priori, whereby and according to which images first become possible, which, however, can be connected with the conception only mediately by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never fully adequate to it.” On the other hand, the schema of a pure conception of the understanding is something that cannot be reduced into any image—it is nothing else than the pure synthesis expressed by the category, conformably, to a rule of unity according to conceptions. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, a product which concerns the determination of the internal sense, according to conditions of its form (time) in respect to all representations, in so far as these representations must be conjoined à priori in one conception, conformably to the unity of apperception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0006","text":"Without entering upon a dry and tedious analysis of the essential requisites of transcendental schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding, we shall rather proceed at once to give an explanation of them according to the order of the categories, and in connection therewith.\n\nFor the external sense the pure image of all quantities (quantorum) is space; the pure image of all objects of sense in general, is time. But the pure schema of quantity (quantitatis) as a conception of the understanding, is number, a representation which comprehends the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous quantities). Thus, number is nothing else than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold in a homogeneous intuition, by means of my generating time itself in my apprehension of the intuition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0007","text":"Reality, in the pure conception of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, consequently, the conception of which indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the conception of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of these two consists therefore in the difference of one and the same time, as a time filled or a time empty. Now as time is only the form of intuition, consequently of objects as phenomena, that which in objects corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (Sachheit, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or quantity by which it can fill time, that is to say, the internal sense in respect of the representation of an object, more or less, until it vanishes into nothing (= 0 = negatio). Thus there is a relation and connection between reality and negation, or rather a transition from the former to the latter, which makes every reality representable to us as a quantum; and the schema of a reality as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is exactly this continuous and uniform generation of the reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree, down to the vanishing thereof, or gradually ascend from negation to the quantity thereof.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0008","text":"The schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time; that is, the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time; a substratum which therefore remains, whilst all else changes. (Time passes not, but in it passes the existence of the changeable. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and permanent, corresponds that which in the phenomenon is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance, and it is only by it that the succession and coexistence of phenomena can be determined in regard to time.)\n\nThe schema of cause and of the causality of a thing is the real which, when posited, is always followed by something else. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subjected to a rule.\n\nThe schema of community (reciprocity of action and reaction), or the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one with those of the other, according to a general rule.\n\nThe schema of possibility is the accordance of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (as, for example, opposites cannot exist together at the same time in the same thing, but only after each other), and is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at any time.\n\nThe schema of reality is existence in a determined time.\n\nThe schema of necessity is the existence of an object in all time.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","text":"It is clear, from all this, that the schema of the category of quantity contains and represents the generation (synthesis) of time itself, in the successive apprehension of an object; the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation with the representation of time, or the filling up of time; the schema of relation the relation of perceptions to each other in all time (that is, according to a rule of the determination of time): and finally, the schema of modality and its categories, time itself, as the correlative of the determination of an object—whether it does belong to time, and how. The schemata, therefore, are nothing but à priori determinations of time according to rules, and these, in regard to all possible objects, following the arrangement of the categories, relate to the series in time, the content in time, the order in time, and finally, to the complex or totality in time.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","text":"Hence it is apparent that the schematism of the understanding, by means of the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, amounts to nothing else than the unity of the manifold of intuition in the internal sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function corresponding to the internal sense (a receptivity). Thus, the schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding are the true and only conditions whereby our understanding receives an application to objects, and consequently significance. Finally, therefore, the categories are only capable of empirical use, inasmuch as they serve merely to subject phenomena to the universal rules of synthesis, by means of an à priori necessary unity (on account of the necessary union of all consciousness in one original apperception); and so to render them susceptible of a complete connection in one experience. But within this whole of possible experience lie all our cognitions, and in the universal relation to this experience consists transcendental truth, which antecedes all empirical truth, and renders the latter possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","text":"It is, however, evident at first sight, that although the schemata of sensibility are the sole agents in realizing the categories, they do, nevertheless, also restrict them, that is, they limit the categories by conditions which lie beyond the sphere of understanding—namely, in sensibility. Hence the schema is properly only the phenomenon, or the sensuous conception of an object in harmony with the category. (Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon—sensatio realitas phaenomenon; constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon—aeternitas, necessitas, phaenomena, etc.) Now, if we remove a restrictive condition, we thereby amplify, it appears, the formerly limited conception. In this way, the categories in their pure signification, free from all conditions of sensibility, ought to be valid of things as they are, and not, as the schemata represent them, merely as they appear; and consequently the categories must have a significance far more extended, and wholly independent of all schemata. In truth, there does always remain to the pure conceptions of the understanding, after abstracting every sensuous condition, a value and significance, which is, however, merely logical. But in this case, no object is given them, and therefore they have no meaning sufficient to afford us a conception of an object. The notion of substance, for example, if we leave out the sensuous determination of permanence, would mean nothing more than a something which can be cogitated as subject, without the possibility of becoming a predicate to anything else. Of this representation I can make nothing, inasmuch as it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing possesses which must thus be valid as premier subject. Consequently, the categories, without schemata are merely functions of the understanding for the production of conceptions, but do not represent any object. This significance they derive from sensibility, which at the same time realizes the understanding and restricts it.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d143541b3e1840dcb76b994b416ef980b3660e74e08192b0ac9ffe94a1863311","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":44,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CISA","text":"### Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nIn the foregoing chapter we have merely considered the general conditions under which alone the transcendental faculty of judgement is justified in using the pure conceptions of the understanding for synthetical judgements. Our duty at present is to exhibit in systematic connection those judgements which the understanding really produces à priori. For this purpose, our table of the categories will certainly afford us the natural and safe guidance. For it is precisely the categories whose application to possible experience must constitute all pure à priori cognition of the understanding; and the relation of which to sensibility will, on that very account, present us with a complete and systematic catalogue of all the transcendental principles of the use of the understanding.\n\nPrinciples à priori are so called, not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgements, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This peculiarity, however, does not raise them altogether above the need of a proof. For although there could be found no higher cognition, and therefore no objective proof, and although such a principle rather serves as the foundation for all cognition of the object, this by no means hinders us from drawing a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of the cognition of an object. Such a proof is necessary, moreover, because without it the principle might be liable to the imputation of being a mere gratuitous assertion.\n\nIn the second place, we shall limit our investigations to those principles which relate to the categories. For as to the principles of transcendental æsthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of things as phenomena, as also the restriction of these principles, namely, that they cannot be applied to objects as things in themselves—these, of course, do not fall within the scope of our present inquiry. In like manner, the principles of mathematical science form no part of this system, because they are all drawn from intuition, and not from the pure conception of the understanding. The possibility of these principles, however, will necessarily be considered here, inasmuch as they are synthetical judgements à priori, not indeed for the purpose of proving their accuracy and apodeictic certainty, which is unnecessary, but merely to render conceivable and deduce the possibility of such evident à priori cognitions.\n\nBut we shall have also to speak of the principle of analytical judgements, in opposition to synthetical judgements, which is the proper subject of our inquiries, because this very opposition will free the theory of the latter from all ambiguity, and place it clearly before our eyes in its true nature.\n\nSYSTEM OF THE PRINCIPLES OF THE PURE UNDERSTANDING","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nIn the foregoing chapter we have merely considered the general conditions under which alone the transcendental faculty of judgement is justified in using the pure conceptions of the understanding for synthetical judgements. Our duty at present is to exhibit in systematic connection those judgements which the understanding really produces à priori. For this purpose, our table of the categories will certainly afford us the natural and safe guidance. For it is precisely the categories whose application to possible experience must constitute all pure à priori cognition of the understanding; and the relation of which to sensibility will, on that very account, present us with a complete and systematic catalogue of all the transcendental principles of the use of the understanding.\n\nPrinciples à priori are so called, not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgements, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This peculiarity, however, does not raise them altogether above the need of a proof. For although there could be found no higher cognition, and therefore no objective proof, and although such a principle rather serves as the foundation for all cognition of the object, this by no means hinders us from drawing a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of the cognition of an object. Such a proof is necessary, moreover, because without it the principle might be liable to the imputation of being a mere gratuitous assertion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","text":"In the second place, we shall limit our investigations to those principles which relate to the categories. For as to the principles of transcendental æsthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of things as phenomena, as also the restriction of these principles, namely, that they cannot be applied to objects as things in themselves—these, of course, do not fall within the scope of our present inquiry. In like manner, the principles of mathematical science form no part of this system, because they are all drawn from intuition, and not from the pure conception of the understanding. The possibility of these principles, however, will necessarily be considered here, inasmuch as they are synthetical judgements à priori, not indeed for the purpose of proving their accuracy and apodeictic certainty, which is unnecessary, but merely to render conceivable and deduce the possibility of such evident à priori cognitions.\n\nBut we shall have also to speak of the principle of analytical judgements, in opposition to synthetical judgements, which is the proper subject of our inquiries, because this very opposition will free the theory of the latter from all ambiguity, and place it clearly before our eyes in its true nature.\n\nSYSTEM OF THE PRINCIPLES OF THE PURE UNDERSTANDING","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9beba086049a77d02d471a4bbd907e461b7df3db16bd0c328cf61ea9e1d3e189","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","order":45,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SISP","text":"### Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements\n\nWhatever may be the content of our cognition, and in whatever manner our cognition may be related to its object, the universal, although only negative conditions of all our judgements is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgements are in themselves (even without respect to the object) nothing. But although there may exist no contradiction in our judgement, it may nevertheless connect conceptions in such a manner that they do not correspond to the object, or without any grounds either à priori or à posteriori for arriving at such a judgement, and thus, without being self-contradictory, a judgement may nevertheless be either false or groundless.\n\nNow, the proposition: “No subject can have a predicate that contradicts it,” is called the principle of contradiction, and is a universal but purely negative criterion of all truth. But it belongs to logic alone, because it is valid of cognitions, merely as cognitions and without respect to their content, and declares that the contradiction entirely nullifies them. We can also, however, make a positive use of this principle, that is, not merely to banish falsehood and error (in so far as it rests upon contradiction), but also for the cognition of truth. For if the judgement is analytical, be it affirmative or negative, its truth must always be recognizable by means of the principle of contradiction. For the contrary of that which lies and is cogitated as conception in the cognition of the object will be always properly negatived, but the conception itself must always be affirmed of the object, inasmuch as the contrary thereof would be in contradiction to the object.\n\nWe must therefore hold the principle of contradiction to be the universal and fully sufficient Principle of all analytical cognition. But as a sufficient criterion of truth, it has no further utility or authority. For the fact that no cognition can be at variance with this principle without nullifying itself, constitutes this principle the sine qua non, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. As our business at present is properly with the synthetical part of our knowledge only, we shall always be on our guard not to transgress this inviolable principle; but at the same time not to expect from it any direct assistance in the establishment of the truth of any synthetical proposition.\n\nThere exists, however, a formula of this celebrated principle—a principle merely formal and entirely without content—which contains a synthesis that has been inadvertently and quite unnecessarily mixed up with it. It is this: “It is impossible for a thing to be and not to be at the same time.” Not to mention the superfluousness of the addition of the word impossible to indicate the apodeictic certainty, which ought to be self-evident from the proposition itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time, and as it were says: “A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be non-B.” But both, B as well as non-B, may quite well exist in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man can very well be at one time young, and at another not young, that is, old. Now the principle of contradiction as a merely logical proposition must not by any means limit its application merely to relations of time, and consequently a formula like the preceding is quite foreign to its true purpose. The misunderstanding arises in this way. We first of all separate a predicate of a thing from the conception of the thing, and afterwards connect with this predicate its opposite, and hence do not establish any contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been conjoined with the subject synthetically—a contradiction, moreover, which obtains only when the first and second predicate are affirmed in the same time. If I say: “A man who is ignorant is not learned,” the condition “at the same time” must be added, for he who is at one time ignorant, may at another be learned. But if I say: “No ignorant man is a learned man,” the proposition is analytical, because the characteristic ignorance is now a constituent part of the conception of the subject; and in this case the negative proposition is evident immediately from the proposition of contradiction, without the necessity of adding the condition “the same time.” This is the reason why I have altered the formula of this principle—an alteration which shows very clearly the nature of an analytical proposition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements\n\nWhatever may be the content of our cognition, and in whatever manner our cognition may be related to its object, the universal, although only negative conditions of all our judgements is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgements are in themselves (even without respect to the object) nothing. But although there may exist no contradiction in our judgement, it may nevertheless connect conceptions in such a manner that they do not correspond to the object, or without any grounds either à priori or à posteriori for arriving at such a judgement, and thus, without being self-contradictory, a judgement may nevertheless be either false or groundless.\n\nNow, the proposition: “No subject can have a predicate that contradicts it,” is called the principle of contradiction, and is a universal but purely negative criterion of all truth. But it belongs to logic alone, because it is valid of cognitions, merely as cognitions and without respect to their content, and declares that the contradiction entirely nullifies them. We can also, however, make a positive use of this principle, that is, not merely to banish falsehood and error (in so far as it rests upon contradiction), but also for the cognition of truth. For if the judgement is analytical, be it affirmative or negative, its truth must always be recognizable by means of the principle of contradiction. For the contrary of that which lies and is cogitated as conception in the cognition of the object will be always properly negatived, but the conception itself must always be affirmed of the object, inasmuch as the contrary thereof would be in contradiction to the object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","text":"We must therefore hold the principle of contradiction to be the universal and fully sufficient Principle of all analytical cognition. But as a sufficient criterion of truth, it has no further utility or authority. For the fact that no cognition can be at variance with this principle without nullifying itself, constitutes this principle the sine qua non, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. As our business at present is properly with the synthetical part of our knowledge only, we shall always be on our guard not to transgress this inviolable principle; but at the same time not to expect from it any direct assistance in the establishment of the truth of any synthetical proposition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","text":"There exists, however, a formula of this celebrated principle—a principle merely formal and entirely without content—which contains a synthesis that has been inadvertently and quite unnecessarily mixed up with it. It is this: “It is impossible for a thing to be and not to be at the same time.” Not to mention the superfluousness of the addition of the word impossible to indicate the apodeictic certainty, which ought to be self-evident from the proposition itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time, and as it were says: “A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be non-B.” But both, B as well as non-B, may quite well exist in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man can very well be at one time young, and at another not young, that is, old. Now the principle of contradiction as a merely logical proposition must not by any means limit its application merely to relations of time, and consequently a formula like the preceding is quite foreign to its true purpose. The misunderstanding arises in this way. We first of all separate a predicate of a thing from the conception of the thing, and afterwards connect with this predicate its opposite, and hence do not establish any contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been conjoined with the subject synthetically—a contradiction, moreover, which obtains only when the first and second predicate are affirmed in the same time. If I say: “A man who is ignorant is not learned,” the condition “at the same time” must be added, for he who is at one time ignorant, may at another be learned. But if I say: “No ignorant man is a learned man,” the proposition is analytical, because the characteristic ignorance is now a constituent part of the conception of the subject; and in this case the negative proposition is evident immediately from the proposition of contradiction, without the necessity of adding the condition “the same time.” This is the reason why I have altered the formula of this principle—an alteration which shows very clearly the nature of an analytical proposition.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a72df754a4e1e7f6a2d25d215083e61a4851fb72ec2e4530ebbf9f9a55f7b10a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","order":46,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_2","text":"### Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements\n\nThe explanation of the possibility of synthetical judgements is a task with which general logic has nothing to do; indeed she needs not even be acquainted with its name. But in transcendental logic it is the most important matter to be dealt with—indeed the only one, if the question is of the possibility of synthetical judgements à priori, the conditions and extent of their validity. For when this question is fully decided, it can reach its aim with perfect ease, the determination, to wit, of the extent and limits of the pure understanding.\n\nIn an analytical judgement I do not go beyond the given conception, in order to arrive at some decision respecting it. If the judgement is affirmative, I predicate of the conception only that which was already cogitated in it; if negative, I merely exclude from the conception its contrary. But in synthetical judgements, I must go beyond the given conception, in order to cogitate, in relation with it, something quite different from that which was cogitated in it, a relation which is consequently never one either of identity or contradiction, and by means of which the truth or error of the judgement cannot be discerned merely from the judgement itself.\n\nGranted, then, that we must go out beyond a given conception, in order to compare it synthetically with another, a third thing is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two conceptions can originate. Now what is this tertium quid that is to be the medium of all synthetical judgements? It is only a complex in which all our representations are contained, the internal sense to wit, and its form à priori, time.\n\nThe synthesis of our representations rests upon the imagination; their synthetical unity (which is requisite to a judgement), upon the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, is to be sought the possibility of synthetical judgements, and as all three contain the sources of à priori representations, the possibility of pure synthetical judgements also; nay, they are necessary upon these grounds, if we are to possess a knowledge of objects, which rests solely upon the synthesis of representations.\n\nIf a cognition is to have objective reality, that is, to relate to an object, and possess sense and meaning in respect to it, it is necessary that the object be given in some way or another. Without this, our conceptions are empty, and we may indeed have thought by means of them, but by such thinking we have not, in fact, cognized anything, we have merely played with representation. To give an object, if this expression be understood in the sense of “to present” the object, not mediately but immediately in intuition, means nothing else than to apply the representation of it to experience, be that experience real or only possible. Space and time themselves, pure as these conceptions are from all that is empirical, and certain as it is that they are represented fully à priori in the mind, would be completely without objective validity, and without sense and significance, if their necessary use in the objects of experience were not shown. Nay, the representation of them is a mere schema, that always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they have no meaning. And so it is with all conceptions without distinction.\n\nThe possibility of experience is, then, that which gives objective reality to all our à priori cognitions. Now experience depends upon the synthetical unity of phenomena, that is, upon a synthesis according to conceptions of the object of phenomena in general, a synthesis without which experience never could become knowledge, but would be merely a rhapsody of perceptions, never fitting together into any connected text, according to rules of a thoroughly united (possible) consciousness, and therefore never subjected to the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has therefore for a foundation, à priori principles of its form, that is to say, general rules of unity in the synthesis of phenomena, the objective reality of which rules, as necessary conditions even of the possibility of experience can which rules, as necessary conditions—even of the possibility of experience—can always be shown in experience. But apart from this relation, à priori synthetical propositions are absolutely impossible, because they have no third term, that is, no pure object, in which the synthetical unity can exhibit the objective reality of its conceptions.\n\nAlthough, then, respecting space, or the forms which productive imagination describes therein, we do cognize much à priori in synthetical judgements, and are really in no need of experience for this purpose, such knowledge would nevertheless amount to nothing but a busy trifling with a mere chimera, were not space to be considered as the condition of the phenomena which constitute the material of external experience. Hence those pure synthetical judgements do relate, though but mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience, and upon that alone is founded the objective validity of their synthesis.\n\nWhile then, on the one hand, experience, as empirical synthesis, is the only possible mode of cognition which gives reality to all other synthesis; on the other hand, this latter synthesis, as cognition à priori, possesses truth, that is, accordance with its object, only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetical unity of experience.\n\nAccordingly, the supreme principle of all synthetical judgements is: “Every object is subject to the necessary conditions of the synthetical unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.”\n\nÀ priori synthetical judgements are possible when we apply the formal conditions of the à priori intuition, the synthesis of the imagination, and the necessary unity of that synthesis in a transcendental apperception, to a possible cognition of experience, and say: “The conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and have, for that reason, objective validity in an à priori synthetical judgement.”","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements\n\nThe explanation of the possibility of synthetical judgements is a task with which general logic has nothing to do; indeed she needs not even be acquainted with its name. But in transcendental logic it is the most important matter to be dealt with—indeed the only one, if the question is of the possibility of synthetical judgements à priori, the conditions and extent of their validity. For when this question is fully decided, it can reach its aim with perfect ease, the determination, to wit, of the extent and limits of the pure understanding.\n\nIn an analytical judgement I do not go beyond the given conception, in order to arrive at some decision respecting it. If the judgement is affirmative, I predicate of the conception only that which was already cogitated in it; if negative, I merely exclude from the conception its contrary. But in synthetical judgements, I must go beyond the given conception, in order to cogitate, in relation with it, something quite different from that which was cogitated in it, a relation which is consequently never one either of identity or contradiction, and by means of which the truth or error of the judgement cannot be discerned merely from the judgement itself.\n\nGranted, then, that we must go out beyond a given conception, in order to compare it synthetically with another, a third thing is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two conceptions can originate. Now what is this tertium quid that is to be the medium of all synthetical judgements? It is only a complex in which all our representations are contained, the internal sense to wit, and its form à priori, time.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","text":"The synthesis of our representations rests upon the imagination; their synthetical unity (which is requisite to a judgement), upon the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, is to be sought the possibility of synthetical judgements, and as all three contain the sources of à priori representations, the possibility of pure synthetical judgements also; nay, they are necessary upon these grounds, if we are to possess a knowledge of objects, which rests solely upon the synthesis of representations.\n\nIf a cognition is to have objective reality, that is, to relate to an object, and possess sense and meaning in respect to it, it is necessary that the object be given in some way or another. Without this, our conceptions are empty, and we may indeed have thought by means of them, but by such thinking we have not, in fact, cognized anything, we have merely played with representation. To give an object, if this expression be understood in the sense of “to present” the object, not mediately but immediately in intuition, means nothing else than to apply the representation of it to experience, be that experience real or only possible. Space and time themselves, pure as these conceptions are from all that is empirical, and certain as it is that they are represented fully à priori in the mind, would be completely without objective validity, and without sense and significance, if their necessary use in the objects of experience were not shown. Nay, the representation of them is a mere schema, that always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they have no meaning. And so it is with all conceptions without distinction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","text":"The possibility of experience is, then, that which gives objective reality to all our à priori cognitions. Now experience depends upon the synthetical unity of phenomena, that is, upon a synthesis according to conceptions of the object of phenomena in general, a synthesis without which experience never could become knowledge, but would be merely a rhapsody of perceptions, never fitting together into any connected text, according to rules of a thoroughly united (possible) consciousness, and therefore never subjected to the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has therefore for a foundation, à priori principles of its form, that is to say, general rules of unity in the synthesis of phenomena, the objective reality of which rules, as necessary conditions even of the possibility of experience can which rules, as necessary conditions—even of the possibility of experience—can always be shown in experience. But apart from this relation, à priori synthetical propositions are absolutely impossible, because they have no third term, that is, no pure object, in which the synthetical unity can exhibit the objective reality of its conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","text":"Although, then, respecting space, or the forms which productive imagination describes therein, we do cognize much à priori in synthetical judgements, and are really in no need of experience for this purpose, such knowledge would nevertheless amount to nothing but a busy trifling with a mere chimera, were not space to be considered as the condition of the phenomena which constitute the material of external experience. Hence those pure synthetical judgements do relate, though but mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience, and upon that alone is founded the objective validity of their synthesis.\n\nWhile then, on the one hand, experience, as empirical synthesis, is the only possible mode of cognition which gives reality to all other synthesis; on the other hand, this latter synthesis, as cognition à priori, possesses truth, that is, accordance with its object, only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetical unity of experience.\n\nAccordingly, the supreme principle of all synthetical judgements is: “Every object is subject to the necessary conditions of the synthetical unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.”\n\nÀ priori synthetical judgements are possible when we apply the formal conditions of the à priori intuition, the synthesis of the imagination, and the necessary unity of that synthesis in a transcendental apperception, to a possible cognition of experience, and say: “The conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and have, for that reason, objective validity in an à priori synthetical judgement.”","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"34e74a03848b8cb9ccb2b7b02cef1d0b8912093af51bf75b1a815fa23c089a4f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":47,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SISR","text":"### Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nThat principles exist at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules in regard to that which happens, but is even the source of principles according to which everything that can be presented to us as an object is necessarily subject to rules, because without such rules we never could attain to cognition of an object. Even the laws of nature, if they are contemplated as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, possess also a characteristic of necessity, and we may therefore at least expect them to be determined upon grounds which are valid à priori and antecedent to all experience. But all laws of nature, without distinction, are subject to higher principles of the understanding, inasmuch as the former are merely applications of the latter to particular cases of experience. These higher principles alone therefore give the conception, which contains the necessary condition, and, as it were, the exponent of a rule; experience, on the other hand, gives the case which comes under the rule.\n\nThere is no danger of our mistaking merely empirical principles for principles of the pure understanding, or conversely; for the character of necessity, according to conceptions which distinguish the latter, and the absence of this in every empirical proposition, how extensively valid soever it may be, is a perfect safeguard against confounding them. There are, however, pure principles à priori, which nevertheless I should not ascribe to the pure understanding—for this reason, that they are not derived from pure conceptions, but (although by the mediation of the understanding) from pure intuitions. But understanding is the faculty of conceptions. Such principles mathematical science possesses, but their application to experience, consequently their objective validity, nay the possibility of such à priori synthetical cognitions (the deduction thereof) rests entirely upon the pure understanding.\n\nOn this account, I shall not reckon among my principles those of mathematics; though I shall include those upon the possibility and objective validity à priori, of principles of the mathematical science, which, consequently, are to be looked upon as the principle of these, and which proceed from conceptions to intuition, and not from intuition to conceptions.\n\nIn the application of the pure conceptions of the understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical, for it is directed partly on the intuition alone, partly on the existence of a phenomenon. But the à priori conditions of intuition are in relation to a possible experience absolutely necessary, those of the existence of objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use of the categories will possess a character of absolute necessity, that is, will be apodeictic; those, on the other hand, of the dynamical use, the character of an à priori necessity indeed, but only under the condition of empirical thought in an experience, therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they will not possess that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former, although their application to experience does not, for that reason, lose its truth and certitude. But of this point we shall be better able to judge at the conclusion of this system of principles.\n\nThe table of the categories is naturally our guide to the table of principles, because these are nothing else than rules for the objective employment of the former. Accordingly, all principles of the pure understanding are:\n\n                                1\n                              Axioms\n                           of Intuition\n\n               2                                    3\n          Anticipations                          Analogies\n          of Perception                        of Experience\n                                4\n                          Postulates of\n                        Empirical Thought\n                           in general\n\nThese appellations I have chosen advisedly, in order that we might not lose sight of the distinctions in respect of the evidence and the employment of these principles. It will, however, soon appear that—a fact which concerns both the evidence of these principles, and the à priori determination of phenomena—according to the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend merely to the form of these), the principles of these categories are distinguishable from those of the two others, in as much as the former are possessed of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though in both instances a complete, certitude. I shall therefore call the former mathematical, and the latter dynamical principles.[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-27) It must be observed, however, that by these terms I mean just as little in the one case the principles of mathematics as those of general (physical) dynamics in the other. I have here in view merely the principles of the pure understanding, in their application to the internal sense (without distinction of the representations given therein), by means of which the sciences of mathematics and dynamics become possible. Accordingly, I have named these principles rather with reference to their application than their content; and I shall now proceed to consider them in the order in which they stand in the table.\n\n[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-27) All combination (conjunctio) is either composition (compositio) or connection (nexus). The former is the synthesis of a manifold, the parts of which do not necessarily belong to each other. For example, the two triangles into which a square is divided by a diagonal, do not necessarily belong to each other, and of this kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be mathematically considered. This synthesis can be divided into those of aggregation and coalition, the former of which is applied to extensive, the latter to intensive quantities. The second sort of combination (nexus) is the synthesis of a manifold, in so far as its parts do belong necessarily to each other; for example, the accident to a substance, or the effect to the cause. Consequently it is a synthesis of that which though heterogeneous, is represented as connected à priori. This combination—not an arbitrary one—I entitle dynamical because it concerns the connection of the existence of the manifold. This, again, may be divided into the physical synthesis, of the phenomena divided among each other, and the metaphysical synthesis, or the connection of phenomena à priori in the faculty of cognition.\n\n1. AXIOMS OF INTUITION.\n\nThe principle of these is: All Intuitions are Extensive Quantities.\n\nPROOF.\n\nAll phenomena contain, as regards their form, an intuition in space and time, which lies à priori at the foundation of all without exception. Phenomena, therefore, cannot be apprehended, that is, received into empirical consciousness otherwise than through the synthesis of a manifold, through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated; that is to say, through the composition of the homogeneous and the consciousness of the synthetical unity of this manifold (homogeneous). Now the consciousness of a homogeneous manifold in intuition, in so far as thereby the representation of an object is rendered possible, is the conception of a quantity (quanti). Consequently, even the perception of an object as phenomenon is possible only through the same synthetical unity of the manifold of the given sensuous intuition, through which the unity of the composition of the homogeneous manifold in the conception of a quantity is cogitated; that is to say, all phenomena are quantities, and extensive quantities, because as intuitions in space or time they must be represented by means of the same synthesis through which space and time themselves are determined.\n\nAn extensive quantity I call that wherein the representation of the parts renders possible (and therefore necessarily antecedes) the representation of the whole. I cannot represent to myself any line, however small, without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating from a point all its parts one after another, and in this way alone producing this intuition. Precisely the same is the case with every, even the smallest, portion of time. I cogitate therein only the successive progress from one moment to another, and hence, by means of the different portions of time and the addition of them, a determinate quantity of time is produced. As the pure intuition in all phenomena is either time or space, so is every phenomenon in its character of intuition an extensive quantity, inasmuch as it can only be cognized in our apprehension by successive synthesis (from part to part). All phenomena are, accordingly, to be considered as aggregates, that is, as a collection of previously given parts; which is not the case with every sort of quantities, but only with those which are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.\n\nOn this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of figures, is founded the mathematics of extension, or geometry, with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensuous intuition à priori, under which alone the schema of a pure conception of external intuition can exist; for example, “be tween two points only one straight line is possible,” “two straight lines cannot enclose a space,” etc. These are the axioms which properly relate only to quantities (quanta) as such.\n\nBut, as regards the quantity of a thing (quantitas), that is to say, the answer to the question: “How large is this or that object?” although, in respect to this question, we have various propositions synthetical and immediately certain (indemonstrabilia); we have, in the proper sense of the term, no axioms. For example, the propositions: “If equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal”; “If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal”; are analytical, because I am immediately conscious of the identity of the production of the one quantity with the production of the other; whereas axioms must be à priori synthetical propositions. On the other hand, the self-evident propositions as to the relation of numbers, are certainly synthetical but not universal, like those of geometry, and for this reason cannot be called axioms, but numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytical proposition. For neither in the representation of seven, nor of five, nor of the composition of the two numbers, do I cogitate the number twelve. (Whether I cogitate the number in the addition of both, is not at present the question; for in the case of an analytical proposition, the only point is whether I really cogitate the predicate in the representation of the subject.) But although the proposition is synthetical, it is nevertheless only a singular proposition. In so far as regard is here had merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (the units), it cannot take place except in one manner, although our use of these numbers is afterwards general. If I say: “A triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third,” I exercise merely the pure function of the productive imagination, which may draw the lines longer or shorter and construct the angles at its pleasure. On the contrary, the number seven is possible only in one manner, and so is likewise the number twelve, which results from the synthesis of seven and five. Such propositions, then, cannot be termed axioms (for in that case we should have an infinity of these), but numerical formulae.\n\nThis transcendental principle of the mathematics of phenomena greatly enlarges our à priori cognition. For it is by this principle alone that pure mathematics is rendered applicable in all its precision to objects of experience, and without it the validity of this application would not be so self-evident; on the contrary, contradictions and confusions have often arisen on this very point. Phenomena are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and time); consequently, what geometry affirms of the latter, is indisputably valid of the former. All evasions, such as the statement that objects of sense do not conform to the rules of construction in space (for example, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall to the ground. For, if these objections hold good, we deny to space, and with it to all mathematics, objective validity, and no longer know wherefore, and how far, mathematics can be applied to phenomena. The synthesis of spaces and times as the essential form of all intuition, is that which renders possible the apprehension of a phenomenon, and therefore every external experience, consequently all cognition of the objects of experience; and whatever mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, must necessarily hold good of the latter. All objections are but the chicaneries of an ill-instructed reason, which erroneously thinks to liberate the objects of sense from the formal conditions of our sensibility, and represents these, although mere phenomena, as things in themselves, presented as such to our understanding. But in this case, no à priori synthetical cognition of them could be possible, consequently not through pure conceptions of space and the science which determines these conceptions, that is to say, geometry, would itself be impossible.\n\n2. ANTICIPATIONS OF PERCEPTION.\n\nThe principle of these is: In all phenomena the Real, that which is an object of sensation, has Intensive Quantity, that is, has a Degree.\n\nPROOF.\n\nPerception is empirical consciousness, that is to say, a consciousness which contains an element of sensation. Phenomena as objects of perception are not pure, that is, merely formal intuitions, like space and time, for they cannot be perceived in themselves.[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-28) They contain, then, over and above the intuition, the materials for an object (through which is represented something existing in space or time), that is to say, they contain the real of sensation, as a representation merely subjective, which gives us merely the consciousness that the subject is affected, and which we refer to some external object. Now, a gradual transition from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness is possible, inasmuch as the real in this consciousness entirely vanishes, and there remains a merely formal consciousness (à priori) of the manifold in time and space; consequently there is possible a synthesis also of the production of the quantity of a sensation from its commencement, that is, from the pure intuition = 0 onwards up to a certain quantity of the sensation. Now as sensation in itself is not an objective representation, and in it is to be found neither the intuition of space nor of time, it cannot possess any extensive quantity, and yet there does belong to it a quantity (and that by means of its apprehension, in which empirical consciousness can within a certain time rise from nothing = 0 up to its given amount), consequently an intensive quantity. And thus we must ascribe intensive quantity, that is, a degree of influence on sense to all objects of perception, in so far as this perception contains sensation.\n\n[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-28) They can be perceived only as phenomena, and some part of them must always belong to the non-ego; whereas pure intuitions are entirely the products of the mind itself, and as such are cognized _in themselves.—Tr_\n\nAll cognition, by means of which I am enabled to cognize and determine à priori what belongs to empirical cognition, may be called an anticipation; and without doubt this is the sense in which Epicurus employed his expression prholepsis. But as there is in phenomena something which is never cognized à priori, which on this account constitutes the proper difference between pure and empirical cognition, that is to say, sensation (as the matter of perception), it follows, that sensation is just that element in cognition which cannot be at all anticipated. On the other hand, we might very well term the pure determinations in space and time, as well in regard to figure as to quantity, anticipations of phenomena, because they represent à priori that which may always be given à posteriori in experience. But suppose that in every sensation, as sensation in general, without any particular sensation being thought of, there existed something which could be cognized à priori, this would deserve to be called anticipation in a special sense—special, because it may seem surprising to forestall experience, in that which concerns the matter of experience, and which we can only derive from itself. Yet such really is the case here.\n\nApprehension[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-29), by means of sensation alone, fills only one moment, that is, if I do not take into consideration a succession of many sensations. As that in the phenomenon, the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis advancing from parts to an entire representation, sensation has therefore no extensive quantity; the want of sensation in a moment of time would represent it as empty, consequently = 0. That which in the empirical intuition corresponds to sensation is reality (realitas phaenomenon); that which corresponds to the absence of it, negation = 0. Now every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease, and thus gradually disappear. Therefore, between reality in a phenomenon and negation, there exists a continuous concatenation of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from each other is always smaller than that between the given sensation and zero, or complete negation. That is to say, the real in a phenomenon has always a quantity, which however is not discoverable in apprehension, inasmuch as apprehension take place by means of mere sensation in one instant, and not by the successive synthesis of many sensations, and therefore does not progress from parts to the whole. Consequently, it has a quantity, but not an extensive quantity.\n\n[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-29) Apprehension is the Kantian word for preception, in the largest sense in which we employ that term. It is the genus which includes under i, as species, perception proper and sensation proper—Tr\n\nNow that quantity which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only by approximation to negation = 0, I term intensive quantity. Consequently, reality in a phenomenon has intensive quantity, that is, a degree. If we consider this reality as cause (be it of sensation or of another reality in the phenomenon, for example, a change), we call the degree of reality in its character of cause a momentum, for example, the momentum of weight; and for this reason, that the degree only indicates that quantity the apprehension of which is not successive, but instantaneous. This, however, I touch upon only in passing, for with causality I have at present nothing to do.\n\nAccordingly, every sensation, consequently every reality in phenomena, however small it may be, has a degree, that is, an intensive quantity, which may always be lessened, and between reality and negation there exists a continuous connection of possible realities, and possible smaller perceptions. Every colour—for example, red—has a degree, which, be it ever so small, is never the smallest, and so is it always with heat, the momentum of weight, etc.\n\nThis property of quantities, according to which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part simple), is called their continuity. Space and time are quanta continua, because no part of them can be given, without enclosing it within boundaries (points and moments), consequently, this given part is itself a space or a time. Space, therefore, consists only of spaces, and time of times. Points and moments are only boundaries, that is, the mere places or positions of their limitation. But places always presuppose intuitions which are to limit or determine them; and we cannot conceive either space or time composed of constituent parts which are given before space or time. Such quantities may also be called flowing, because synthesis (of the productive imagination) in the production of these quantities is a progression in time, the continuity of which we are accustomed to indicate by the expression flowing.\n\nAll phenomena, then, are continuous quantities, in respect both to intuition and mere perception (sensation, and with it reality). In the former case they are extensive quantities; in the latter, intensive. When the synthesis of the manifold of a phenomenon is interrupted, there results merely an aggregate of several phenomena, and not properly a phenomenon as a quantity, which is not produced by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain kind, but by the repetition of a synthesis always ceasing. For example, if I call thirteen dollars a sum or quantity of money, I employ the term quite correctly, inasmuch as I understand by thirteen dollars the value of a mark in standard silver, which is, to be sure, a continuous quantity, in which no part is the smallest, but every part might constitute a piece of money, which would contain material for still smaller pieces. If, however, by the words thirteen dollars I understand so many coins (be their value in silver what it may), it would be quite erroneous to use the expression a quantity of dollars; on the contrary, I must call them aggregate, that is, a number of coins. And as in every number we must have unity as the foundation, so a phenomenon taken as unity is a quantity, and as such always a continuous quantity (quantum continuum).\n\nNow, seeing all phenomena, whether considered as extensive or intensive, are continuous quantities, the proposition: “All change (transition of a thing from one state into another) is continuous,” might be proved here easily, and with mathematical evidence, were it not that the causality of a change lies, entirely beyond the bounds of a transcendental philosophy, and presupposes empirical principles. For of the possibility of a cause which changes the condition of things, that is, which determines them to the contrary to a certain given state, the understanding gives us à priori no knowledge; not merely because it has no insight into the possibility of it (for such insight is absent in several à priori cognitions), but because the notion of change concerns only certain determinations of phenomena, which experience alone can acquaint us with, while their cause lies in the unchangeable. But seeing that we have nothing which we could here employ but the pure fundamental conceptions of all possible experience, among which of course nothing empirical can be admitted, we dare not, without injuring the unity of our system, anticipate general physical science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.\n\nNevertheless, we are in no want of proofs of the great influence which the principle above developed exercises in the anticipation of perceptions, and even in supplying the want of them, so far as to shield us against the false conclusions which otherwise we might rashly draw.\n\nIf all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an endless sequence of ever smaller degrees, and if, nevertheless, every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations; no perception, and consequently no experience is possible, which can prove, either immediately or mediately, an entire absence of all reality in a phenomenon; in other words, it is impossible ever to draw from experience a proof of the existence of empty space or of empty time. For in the first place, an entire absence of reality in a sensuous intuition cannot of course be an object of perception; secondly, such absence cannot be deduced from the contemplation of any single phenomenon, and the difference of the degrees in its reality; nor ought it ever to be admitted in explanation of any phenomenon. For if even the complete intuition of a determinate space or time is thoroughly real, that is, if no part thereof is empty, yet because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive quantity of the phenomenon unchanged, can diminish through endless gradations down to nothing (the void), there must be infinitely graduated degrees, with which space or time is filled, and the intensive quantity in different phenomena may be smaller or greater, although the extensive quantity of the intuition remains equal and unaltered.\n\nWe shall give an example of this. Almost all natural philosophers, remarking a great difference in the quantity of the matter of different kinds in bodies with the same volume (partly on account of the momentum of gravity or weight, partly on account of the momentum of resistance to other bodies in motion), conclude unanimously that this volume (extensive quantity of the phenomenon) must be void in all bodies, although in different proportion. But who would suspect that these for the most part mathematical and mechanical inquirers into nature should ground this conclusion solely on a metaphysical hypothesis—a sort of hypothesis which they profess to disparage and avoid? Yet this they do, in assuming that the real in space (I must not here call it impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical conceptions) is always identical, and can only be distinguished according to its extensive quantity, that is, multiplicity. Now to this presupposition, for which they can have no ground in experience, and which consequently is merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental demonstration, which it is true will not explain the difference in the filling up of spaces, but which nevertheless completely does away with the supposed necessity of the above-mentioned presupposition that we cannot explain the said difference otherwise than by the hypothesis of empty spaces. This demonstration, moreover, has the merit of setting the understanding at liberty to conceive this distinction in a different manner, if the explanation of the fact requires any such hypothesis. For we perceive that although two equal spaces may be completely filled by matters altogether different, so that in neither of them is there left a single point wherein matter is not present, nevertheless, every reality has its degree (of resistance or of weight), which, without diminution of the extensive quantity, can become less and less ad infinitum, before it passes into nothingness and disappears. Thus an expansion which fills a space—for example, caloric, or any other reality in the phenomenal world—can decrease in its degrees to infinity, yet without leaving the smallest part of the space empty; on the contrary, filling it with those lesser degrees as completely as another phenomenon could with greater. My intention here is by no means to maintain that this is really the case with the difference of matters, in regard to their specific gravity; I wish only to prove, from a principle of the pure understanding, that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is erroneous to regard the real in a phenomenon as equal quoad its degree, and different only quoad its aggregation and extensive quantity, and this, too, on the pretended authority of an à priori principle of the understanding.\n\nNevertheless, this principle of the anticipation of perception must somewhat startle an inquirer whom initiation into transcendental philosophy has rendered cautious. We must naturally entertain some doubt whether or not the understanding can enounce any such synthetical proposition as that respecting the degree of all reality in phenomena, and consequently the possibility of the internal difference of sensation itself—abstraction being made of its empirical quality. Thus it is a question not unworthy of solution: “How the understanding can pronounce synthetically and à priori respecting phenomena, and thus anticipate these, even in that which is peculiarly and merely empirical, that, namely, which concerns sensation itself?”\n\nThe quality of sensation is in all cases merely empirical, and cannot be represented à priori (for example, colours, taste, etc.). But the real—that which corresponds to sensation—in opposition to negation = 0, only represents something the conception of which in itself contains a being (ein seyn), and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness. That is to say, the empirical consciousness in the internal sense can be raised from 0 to every higher degree, so that the very same extensive quantity of intuition, an illuminated surface, for example, excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other surfaces less illuminated. We can therefore make complete abstraction of the extensive quantity of a phenomenon, and represent to ourselves in the mere sensation in a certain momentum, a synthesis of homogeneous ascension from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness, All sensations therefore as such are given only à posteriori, but this property thereof, namely, that they have a degree, can be known à priori. It is worthy of remark, that in respect to quantities in general, we can cognize à priori only a single quality, namely, continuity; but in respect to all quality (the real in phenomena), we cannot cognize à priori anything more than the intensive quantity thereof, namely, that they have a degree. All else is left to experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","text":"### Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nThat principles exist at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules in regard to that which happens, but is even the source of principles according to which everything that can be presented to us as an object is necessarily subject to rules, because without such rules we never could attain to cognition of an object. Even the laws of nature, if they are contemplated as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, possess also a characteristic of necessity, and we may therefore at least expect them to be determined upon grounds which are valid à priori and antecedent to all experience. But all laws of nature, without distinction, are subject to higher principles of the understanding, inasmuch as the former are merely applications of the latter to particular cases of experience. These higher principles alone therefore give the conception, which contains the necessary condition, and, as it were, the exponent of a rule; experience, on the other hand, gives the case which comes under the rule.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","text":"There is no danger of our mistaking merely empirical principles for principles of the pure understanding, or conversely; for the character of necessity, according to conceptions which distinguish the latter, and the absence of this in every empirical proposition, how extensively valid soever it may be, is a perfect safeguard against confounding them. There are, however, pure principles à priori, which nevertheless I should not ascribe to the pure understanding—for this reason, that they are not derived from pure conceptions, but (although by the mediation of the understanding) from pure intuitions. But understanding is the faculty of conceptions. Such principles mathematical science possesses, but their application to experience, consequently their objective validity, nay the possibility of such à priori synthetical cognitions (the deduction thereof) rests entirely upon the pure understanding.\n\nOn this account, I shall not reckon among my principles those of mathematics; though I shall include those upon the possibility and objective validity à priori, of principles of the mathematical science, which, consequently, are to be looked upon as the principle of these, and which proceed from conceptions to intuition, and not from intuition to conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","text":"In the application of the pure conceptions of the understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical, for it is directed partly on the intuition alone, partly on the existence of a phenomenon. But the à priori conditions of intuition are in relation to a possible experience absolutely necessary, those of the existence of objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use of the categories will possess a character of absolute necessity, that is, will be apodeictic; those, on the other hand, of the dynamical use, the character of an à priori necessity indeed, but only under the condition of empirical thought in an experience, therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they will not possess that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former, although their application to experience does not, for that reason, lose its truth and certitude. But of this point we shall be better able to judge at the conclusion of this system of principles.\n\nThe table of the categories is naturally our guide to the table of principles, because these are nothing else than rules for the objective employment of the former. Accordingly, all principles of the pure understanding are:\n\n                                1\n                              Axioms\n                           of Intuition\n\n               2                                    3\n          Anticipations                          Analogies\n          of Perception                        of Experience\n                                4\n                          Postulates of\n                        Empirical Thought\n                           in general","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","text":"These appellations I have chosen advisedly, in order that we might not lose sight of the distinctions in respect of the evidence and the employment of these principles. It will, however, soon appear that—a fact which concerns both the evidence of these principles, and the à priori determination of phenomena—according to the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend merely to the form of these), the principles of these categories are distinguishable from those of the two others, in as much as the former are possessed of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though in both instances a complete, certitude. I shall therefore call the former mathematical, and the latter dynamical principles.[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-27) It must be observed, however, that by these terms I mean just as little in the one case the principles of mathematics as those of general (physical) dynamics in the other. I have here in view merely the principles of the pure understanding, in their application to the internal sense (without distinction of the representations given therein), by means of which the sciences of mathematics and dynamics become possible. Accordingly, I have named these principles rather with reference to their application than their content; and I shall now proceed to consider them in the order in which they stand in the table.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","text":"[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-27) All combination (conjunctio) is either composition (compositio) or connection (nexus). The former is the synthesis of a manifold, the parts of which do not necessarily belong to each other. For example, the two triangles into which a square is divided by a diagonal, do not necessarily belong to each other, and of this kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be mathematically considered. This synthesis can be divided into those of aggregation and coalition, the former of which is applied to extensive, the latter to intensive quantities. The second sort of combination (nexus) is the synthesis of a manifold, in so far as its parts do belong necessarily to each other; for example, the accident to a substance, or the effect to the cause. Consequently it is a synthesis of that which though heterogeneous, is represented as connected à priori. This combination—not an arbitrary one—I entitle dynamical because it concerns the connection of the existence of the manifold. This, again, may be divided into the physical synthesis, of the phenomena divided among each other, and the metaphysical synthesis, or the connection of phenomena à priori in the faculty of cognition.\n\n1. AXIOMS OF INTUITION.\n\nThe principle of these is: All Intuitions are Extensive Quantities.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","text":"All phenomena contain, as regards their form, an intuition in space and time, which lies à priori at the foundation of all without exception. Phenomena, therefore, cannot be apprehended, that is, received into empirical consciousness otherwise than through the synthesis of a manifold, through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated; that is to say, through the composition of the homogeneous and the consciousness of the synthetical unity of this manifold (homogeneous). Now the consciousness of a homogeneous manifold in intuition, in so far as thereby the representation of an object is rendered possible, is the conception of a quantity (quanti). Consequently, even the perception of an object as phenomenon is possible only through the same synthetical unity of the manifold of the given sensuous intuition, through which the unity of the composition of the homogeneous manifold in the conception of a quantity is cogitated; that is to say, all phenomena are quantities, and extensive quantities, because as intuitions in space or time they must be represented by means of the same synthesis through which space and time themselves are determined.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","text":"An extensive quantity I call that wherein the representation of the parts renders possible (and therefore necessarily antecedes) the representation of the whole. I cannot represent to myself any line, however small, without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating from a point all its parts one after another, and in this way alone producing this intuition. Precisely the same is the case with every, even the smallest, portion of time. I cogitate therein only the successive progress from one moment to another, and hence, by means of the different portions of time and the addition of them, a determinate quantity of time is produced. As the pure intuition in all phenomena is either time or space, so is every phenomenon in its character of intuition an extensive quantity, inasmuch as it can only be cognized in our apprehension by successive synthesis (from part to part). All phenomena are, accordingly, to be considered as aggregates, that is, as a collection of previously given parts; which is not the case with every sort of quantities, but only with those which are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.\n\nOn this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of figures, is founded the mathematics of extension, or geometry, with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensuous intuition à priori, under which alone the schema of a pure conception of external intuition can exist; for example, “be tween two points only one straight line is possible,” “two straight lines cannot enclose a space,” etc. These are the axioms which properly relate only to quantities (quanta) as such.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0008","text":"But, as regards the quantity of a thing (quantitas), that is to say, the answer to the question: “How large is this or that object?” although, in respect to this question, we have various propositions synthetical and immediately certain (indemonstrabilia); we have, in the proper sense of the term, no axioms. For example, the propositions: “If equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal”; “If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal”; are analytical, because I am immediately conscious of the identity of the production of the one quantity with the production of the other; whereas axioms must be à priori synthetical propositions. On the other hand, the self-evident propositions as to the relation of numbers, are certainly synthetical but not universal, like those of geometry, and for this reason cannot be called axioms, but numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytical proposition. For neither in the representation of seven, nor of five, nor of the composition of the two numbers, do I cogitate the number twelve. (Whether I cogitate the number in the addition of both, is not at present the question; for in the case of an analytical proposition, the only point is whether I really cogitate the predicate in the representation of the subject.) But although the proposition is synthetical, it is nevertheless only a singular proposition. In so far as regard is here had merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (the units), it cannot take place except in one manner, although our use of these numbers is afterwards general. If I say: “A triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third,” I exercise merely the pure function of the productive imagination, which may draw the lines longer or shorter and construct the angles at its pleasure. On the contrary, the number seven is possible only in one manner, and so is likewise the number twelve, which results from the synthesis of seven and five. Such propositions, then, cannot be termed axioms (for in that case we should have an infinity of these), but numerical formulae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","text":"This transcendental principle of the mathematics of phenomena greatly enlarges our à priori cognition. For it is by this principle alone that pure mathematics is rendered applicable in all its precision to objects of experience, and without it the validity of this application would not be so self-evident; on the contrary, contradictions and confusions have often arisen on this very point. Phenomena are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and time); consequently, what geometry affirms of the latter, is indisputably valid of the former. All evasions, such as the statement that objects of sense do not conform to the rules of construction in space (for example, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall to the ground. For, if these objections hold good, we deny to space, and with it to all mathematics, objective validity, and no longer know wherefore, and how far, mathematics can be applied to phenomena. The synthesis of spaces and times as the essential form of all intuition, is that which renders possible the apprehension of a phenomenon, and therefore every external experience, consequently all cognition of the objects of experience; and whatever mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, must necessarily hold good of the latter. All objections are but the chicaneries of an ill-instructed reason, which erroneously thinks to liberate the objects of sense from the formal conditions of our sensibility, and represents these, although mere phenomena, as things in themselves, presented as such to our understanding. But in this case, no à priori synthetical cognition of them could be possible, consequently not through pure conceptions of space and the science which determines these conceptions, that is to say, geometry, would itself be impossible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","text":"2. ANTICIPATIONS OF PERCEPTION.\n\nThe principle of these is: In all phenomena the Real, that which is an object of sensation, has Intensive Quantity, that is, has a Degree.\n\nPROOF.\n\nPerception is empirical consciousness, that is to say, a consciousness which contains an element of sensation. Phenomena as objects of perception are not pure, that is, merely formal intuitions, like space and time, for they cannot be perceived in themselves.[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-28) They contain, then, over and above the intuition, the materials for an object (through which is represented something existing in space or time), that is to say, they contain the real of sensation, as a representation merely subjective, which gives us merely the consciousness that the subject is affected, and which we refer to some external object. Now, a gradual transition from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness is possible, inasmuch as the real in this consciousness entirely vanishes, and there remains a merely formal consciousness (à priori) of the manifold in time and space; consequently there is possible a synthesis also of the production of the quantity of a sensation from its commencement, that is, from the pure intuition = 0 onwards up to a certain quantity of the sensation. Now as sensation in itself is not an objective representation, and in it is to be found neither the intuition of space nor of time, it cannot possess any extensive quantity, and yet there does belong to it a quantity (and that by means of its apprehension, in which empirical consciousness can within a certain time rise from nothing = 0 up to its given amount), consequently an intensive quantity. And thus we must ascribe intensive quantity, that is, a degree of influence on sense to all objects of perception, in so far as this perception contains sensation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0011","text":"[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-28) They can be perceived only as phenomena, and some part of them must always belong to the non-ego; whereas pure intuitions are entirely the products of the mind itself, and as such are cognized _in themselves.—Tr_\n\nAll cognition, by means of which I am enabled to cognize and determine à priori what belongs to empirical cognition, may be called an anticipation; and without doubt this is the sense in which Epicurus employed his expression prholepsis. But as there is in phenomena something which is never cognized à priori, which on this account constitutes the proper difference between pure and empirical cognition, that is to say, sensation (as the matter of perception), it follows, that sensation is just that element in cognition which cannot be at all anticipated. On the other hand, we might very well term the pure determinations in space and time, as well in regard to figure as to quantity, anticipations of phenomena, because they represent à priori that which may always be given à posteriori in experience. But suppose that in every sensation, as sensation in general, without any particular sensation being thought of, there existed something which could be cognized à priori, this would deserve to be called anticipation in a special sense—special, because it may seem surprising to forestall experience, in that which concerns the matter of experience, and which we can only derive from itself. Yet such really is the case here.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","text":"Apprehension[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-29), by means of sensation alone, fills only one moment, that is, if I do not take into consideration a succession of many sensations. As that in the phenomenon, the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis advancing from parts to an entire representation, sensation has therefore no extensive quantity; the want of sensation in a moment of time would represent it as empty, consequently = 0. That which in the empirical intuition corresponds to sensation is reality (realitas phaenomenon); that which corresponds to the absence of it, negation = 0. Now every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease, and thus gradually disappear. Therefore, between reality in a phenomenon and negation, there exists a continuous concatenation of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from each other is always smaller than that between the given sensation and zero, or complete negation. That is to say, the real in a phenomenon has always a quantity, which however is not discoverable in apprehension, inasmuch as apprehension take place by means of mere sensation in one instant, and not by the successive synthesis of many sensations, and therefore does not progress from parts to the whole. Consequently, it has a quantity, but not an extensive quantity.\n\n[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-29) Apprehension is the Kantian word for preception, in the largest sense in which we employ that term. It is the genus which includes under i, as species, perception proper and sensation proper—Tr","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","text":"Now that quantity which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only by approximation to negation = 0, I term intensive quantity. Consequently, reality in a phenomenon has intensive quantity, that is, a degree. If we consider this reality as cause (be it of sensation or of another reality in the phenomenon, for example, a change), we call the degree of reality in its character of cause a momentum, for example, the momentum of weight; and for this reason, that the degree only indicates that quantity the apprehension of which is not successive, but instantaneous. This, however, I touch upon only in passing, for with causality I have at present nothing to do.\n\nAccordingly, every sensation, consequently every reality in phenomena, however small it may be, has a degree, that is, an intensive quantity, which may always be lessened, and between reality and negation there exists a continuous connection of possible realities, and possible smaller perceptions. Every colour—for example, red—has a degree, which, be it ever so small, is never the smallest, and so is it always with heat, the momentum of weight, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","text":"This property of quantities, according to which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part simple), is called their continuity. Space and time are quanta continua, because no part of them can be given, without enclosing it within boundaries (points and moments), consequently, this given part is itself a space or a time. Space, therefore, consists only of spaces, and time of times. Points and moments are only boundaries, that is, the mere places or positions of their limitation. But places always presuppose intuitions which are to limit or determine them; and we cannot conceive either space or time composed of constituent parts which are given before space or time. Such quantities may also be called flowing, because synthesis (of the productive imagination) in the production of these quantities is a progression in time, the continuity of which we are accustomed to indicate by the expression flowing.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","text":"All phenomena, then, are continuous quantities, in respect both to intuition and mere perception (sensation, and with it reality). In the former case they are extensive quantities; in the latter, intensive. When the synthesis of the manifold of a phenomenon is interrupted, there results merely an aggregate of several phenomena, and not properly a phenomenon as a quantity, which is not produced by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain kind, but by the repetition of a synthesis always ceasing. For example, if I call thirteen dollars a sum or quantity of money, I employ the term quite correctly, inasmuch as I understand by thirteen dollars the value of a mark in standard silver, which is, to be sure, a continuous quantity, in which no part is the smallest, but every part might constitute a piece of money, which would contain material for still smaller pieces. If, however, by the words thirteen dollars I understand so many coins (be their value in silver what it may), it would be quite erroneous to use the expression a quantity of dollars; on the contrary, I must call them aggregate, that is, a number of coins. And as in every number we must have unity as the foundation, so a phenomenon taken as unity is a quantity, and as such always a continuous quantity (quantum continuum).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","text":"Now, seeing all phenomena, whether considered as extensive or intensive, are continuous quantities, the proposition: “All change (transition of a thing from one state into another) is continuous,” might be proved here easily, and with mathematical evidence, were it not that the causality of a change lies, entirely beyond the bounds of a transcendental philosophy, and presupposes empirical principles. For of the possibility of a cause which changes the condition of things, that is, which determines them to the contrary to a certain given state, the understanding gives us à priori no knowledge; not merely because it has no insight into the possibility of it (for such insight is absent in several à priori cognitions), but because the notion of change concerns only certain determinations of phenomena, which experience alone can acquaint us with, while their cause lies in the unchangeable. But seeing that we have nothing which we could here employ but the pure fundamental conceptions of all possible experience, among which of course nothing empirical can be admitted, we dare not, without injuring the unity of our system, anticipate general physical science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.\n\nNevertheless, we are in no want of proofs of the great influence which the principle above developed exercises in the anticipation of perceptions, and even in supplying the want of them, so far as to shield us against the false conclusions which otherwise we might rashly draw.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","text":"If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an endless sequence of ever smaller degrees, and if, nevertheless, every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations; no perception, and consequently no experience is possible, which can prove, either immediately or mediately, an entire absence of all reality in a phenomenon; in other words, it is impossible ever to draw from experience a proof of the existence of empty space or of empty time. For in the first place, an entire absence of reality in a sensuous intuition cannot of course be an object of perception; secondly, such absence cannot be deduced from the contemplation of any single phenomenon, and the difference of the degrees in its reality; nor ought it ever to be admitted in explanation of any phenomenon. For if even the complete intuition of a determinate space or time is thoroughly real, that is, if no part thereof is empty, yet because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive quantity of the phenomenon unchanged, can diminish through endless gradations down to nothing (the void), there must be infinitely graduated degrees, with which space or time is filled, and the intensive quantity in different phenomena may be smaller or greater, although the extensive quantity of the intuition remains equal and unaltered.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","text":"We shall give an example of this. Almost all natural philosophers, remarking a great difference in the quantity of the matter of different kinds in bodies with the same volume (partly on account of the momentum of gravity or weight, partly on account of the momentum of resistance to other bodies in motion), conclude unanimously that this volume (extensive quantity of the phenomenon) must be void in all bodies, although in different proportion. But who would suspect that these for the most part mathematical and mechanical inquirers into nature should ground this conclusion solely on a metaphysical hypothesis—a sort of hypothesis which they profess to disparage and avoid? Yet this they do, in assuming that the real in space (I must not here call it impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical conceptions) is always identical, and can only be distinguished according to its extensive quantity, that is, multiplicity. Now to this presupposition, for which they can have no ground in experience, and which consequently is merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental demonstration, which it is true will not explain the difference in the filling up of spaces, but which nevertheless completely does away with the supposed necessity of the above-mentioned presupposition that we cannot explain the said difference otherwise than by the hypothesis of empty spaces. This demonstration, moreover, has the merit of setting the understanding at liberty to conceive this distinction in a different manner, if the explanation of the fact requires any such hypothesis. For we perceive that although two equal spaces may be completely filled by matters altogether different, so that in neither of them is there left a single point wherein matter is not present, nevertheless, every reality has its degree (of resistance or of weight), which, without diminution of the extensive quantity, can become less and less ad infinitum, before it passes into nothingness and disappears. Thus an expansion which fills a space—for example, caloric, or any other reality in the phenomenal world—can decrease in its degrees to infinity, yet without leaving the smallest part of the space empty; on the contrary, filling it with those lesser degrees as completely as another phenomenon could with greater. My intention here is by no means to maintain that this is really the case with the difference of matters, in regard to their specific gravity; I wish only to prove, from a principle of the pure understanding, that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is erroneous to regard the real in a phenomenon as equal quoad its degree, and different only quoad its aggregation and extensive quantity, and this, too, on the pretended authority of an à priori principle of the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0019","text":"Nevertheless, this principle of the anticipation of perception must somewhat startle an inquirer whom initiation into transcendental philosophy has rendered cautious. We must naturally entertain some doubt whether or not the understanding can enounce any such synthetical proposition as that respecting the degree of all reality in phenomena, and consequently the possibility of the internal difference of sensation itself—abstraction being made of its empirical quality. Thus it is a question not unworthy of solution: “How the understanding can pronounce synthetically and à priori respecting phenomena, and thus anticipate these, even in that which is peculiarly and merely empirical, that, namely, which concerns sensation itself?”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","text":"The quality of sensation is in all cases merely empirical, and cannot be represented à priori (for example, colours, taste, etc.). But the real—that which corresponds to sensation—in opposition to negation = 0, only represents something the conception of which in itself contains a being (ein seyn), and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness. That is to say, the empirical consciousness in the internal sense can be raised from 0 to every higher degree, so that the very same extensive quantity of intuition, an illuminated surface, for example, excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other surfaces less illuminated. We can therefore make complete abstraction of the extensive quantity of a phenomenon, and represent to ourselves in the mere sensation in a certain momentum, a synthesis of homogeneous ascension from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness, All sensations therefore as such are given only à posteriori, but this property thereof, namely, that they have a degree, can be known à priori. It is worthy of remark, that in respect to quantities in general, we can cognize à priori only a single quality, namely, continuity; but in respect to all quality (the real in phenomena), we cannot cognize à priori anything more than the intensive quantity thereof, namely, that they have a degree. All else is left to experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2c3ccab5c2ed54e19b167aeb5a8b7e23eae0b7c59e7b8b8bb1570b276a62e1e1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","order":48,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CIGD","text":"# Phenomena Noumena\n\n### Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena\n\nWe have now not only traversed the region of the pure understanding and carefully surveyed every part of it, but we have also measured it, and assigned to everything therein its proper place. But this land is an island, and enclosed by nature herself within unchangeable limits. It is the land of truth (an attractive word), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the region of illusion, where many a fog-bank, many an iceberg, seems to the mariner, on his voyage of discovery, a new country, and, while constantly deluding him with vain hopes, engages him in dangerous adventures, from which he never can desist, and which yet he never can bring to a termination. But before venturing upon this sea, in order to explore it in its whole extent, and to arrive at a certainty whether anything is to be discovered there, it will not be without advantage if we cast our eyes upon the chart of the land that we are about to leave, and to ask ourselves, firstly, whether we cannot rest perfectly contented with what it contains, or whether we must not of necessity be contented with it, if we can find nowhere else a solid foundation to build upon; and, secondly, by what title we possess this land itself, and how we hold it secure against all hostile claims? Although, in the course of our analytic, we have already given sufficient answers to these questions, yet a summary recapitulation of these solutions may be useful in strengthening our conviction, by uniting in one point the momenta of the arguments.\n\nWe have seen that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing from experience, it nevertheless possesses only for the behoof and use of experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether constitutive à priori (as the mathematical principles), or merely regulative (as the dynamical), contain nothing but the pure schema, as it were, of possible experience. For experience possesses its unity from the synthetical unity which the understanding, originally and from itself, imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and in à priori relation to and agreement with which phenomena, as data for a possible cognition, must stand. But although these rules of the understanding are not only à priori true, but the very source of all truth, that is, of the accordance of our cognition with objects, and on this ground, that they contain the basis of the possibility of experience, as the ensemble of all cognition, it seems to us not enough to propound what is true—we desire also to be told what we want to know. If, then, we learn nothing more by this critical examination than what we should have practised in the merely empirical use of the understanding, without any such subtle inquiry, the presumption is that the advantage we reap from it is not worth the labour bestowed upon it. It may certainly be answered that no rash curiosity is more prejudicial to the enlargement of our knowledge than that which must know beforehand the utility of this or that piece of information which we seek, before we have entered on the needful investigations, and before one could form the least conception of its utility, even though it were placed before our eyes. But there is one advantage in such transcendental inquiries which can be made comprehensible to the dullest and most reluctant learner—this, namely, that the understanding which is occupied merely with empirical exercise, and does not reflect on the sources of its own cognition, may exercise its functions very well and very successfully, but is quite unable to do one thing, and that of very great importance, to determine, namely, the bounds that limit its employment, and to know what lies within or without its own sphere. This purpose can be obtained only by such profound investigations as we have instituted. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it can never be sure either as to its claims or possessions, but must lay its account with many humiliating corrections, when it transgresses, as it unavoidably will, the limits of its own territory, and loses itself in fanciful opinions and blinding illusions.\n\nThat the understanding, therefore, cannot make of its à priori principles, or even of its conceptions, other than an empirical use, is a proposition which leads to the most important results. A transcendental use is made of a conception in a fundamental proposition or principle, when it is referred to things in general and considered as things in themselves; an empirical use, when it is referred merely to phenomena, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That the latter use of a conception is the only admissible one is evident from the reasons following. For every conception are requisite, firstly, the logical form of a conception (of thought) general; and, secondly, the possibility of presenting to this an object to which it may apply. Failing this latter, it has no sense, and utterly void of content, although it may contain the logical function for constructing a conception from certain data. Now, object cannot be given to a conception otherwise than by intuition, and, even if a pure intuition antecedent to the object is à priori possible, this pure intuition can itself obtain objective validity only from empirical intuition, of which it is itself but the form. All conceptions, therefore, and with them all principles, however high the degree of their à priori possibility, relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to data towards a possible experience. Without this they possess no objective validity, but are mere play of imagination or of understanding with images or notions. Let us take, for example, the conceptions of mathematics, and first in its pure intuitions. “Space has three dimensions”—“Between two points there can be only one straight line,” etc. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science occupies itself, are generated in the mind entirely à priori, they would nevertheless have no significance if we were not always able to exhibit their significance in and by means of phenomena (empirical objects). Hence it is requisite that an abstract conception be made sensuous, that is, that an object corresponding to it in intuition be forthcoming, otherwise the conception remains, as we say, without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this requirement by the construction of the figure, which is a phenomenon evident to the senses. The same science finds support and significance in number; this in its turn finds it in the fingers, or in counters, or in lines and points. The conception itself is always produced à priori, together with the synthetical principles or formulas from such conceptions; but the proper employment of them, and their application to objects, can exist nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which, as regards its form, they contain à priori.\n\nThat this is also the case with all of the categories and the principles based upon them is evident from the fact that we cannot render intelligible the possibility of an object corresponding to them without having recourse to the conditions of sensibility, consequently, to the form of phenomena, to which, as their only proper objects, their use must therefore be confined, inasmuch as, if this condition is removed, all significance, that is, all relation to an object, disappears, and no example can be found to make it comprehensible what sort of things we ought to think under such conceptions.\n\nThe conception of quantity cannot be explained except by saying that it is the determination of a thing whereby it can be cogitated how many times one is placed in it. But this “how many times” is based upon successive repetition, consequently upon time and the synthesis of the homogeneous therein. Reality, in contradistinction to negation, can be explained only by cogitating a time which is either filled therewith or is void. If I leave out the notion of permanence (which is existence in all time), there remains in the conception of substance nothing but the logical notion of subject, a notion of which I endeavour to realize by representing to myself something that can exist only as a subject. But not only am I perfectly ignorant of any conditions under which this logical prerogative can belong to a thing, I can make nothing out of the notion, and draw no inference from it, because no object to which to apply the conception is determined, and we consequently do not know whether it has any meaning at all. In like manner, if I leave out the notion of time, in which something follows upon some other thing in conformity with a rule, I can find nothing in the pure category, except that there is a something of such a sort that from it a conclusion may be drawn as to the existence of some other thing. But in this case it would not only be impossible to distinguish between a cause and an effect, but, as this power to draw conclusions requires conditions of which I am quite ignorant, the conception is not determined as to the mode in which it ought to apply to an object. The so-called principle: “Everything that is contingent has a cause,” comes with a gravity and self-assumed authority that seems to require no support from without. But, I ask, what is meant by contingent? The answer is that the non-existence of which is possible. But I should like very well to know by what means this possibility of non-existence is to be cognized, if we do not represent to ourselves a succession in the series of phenomena, and in this succession an existence which follows a non-existence, or conversely, consequently, change. For to say, that the non-existence of a thing is not self-contradictory is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is no doubt a necessary condition of the existence of the conception, but is far from being sufficient for the real objective possibility of non-existence. I can annihilate in thought every existing substance without self-contradiction, but I cannot infer from this their objective contingency in existence, that is to say, the possibility of their non-existence in itself. As regards the category of community, it may easily be inferred that, as the pure categories of substance and causality are incapable of a definition and explanation sufficient to determine their object without the aid of intuition, the category of reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to each other (commercium) is just as little susceptible thereof. Possibility, existence, and necessity nobody has ever yet been able to explain without being guilty of manifest tautology, when the definition has been drawn entirely from the pure understanding. For the substitution of the logical possibility of the conception—the condition of which is that it be not self-contradictory, for the transcendental possibility of things—the condition of which is that there be an object corresponding to the conception, is a trick which can only deceive the inexperienced.[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-38)\n\n[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-38) In one word, to none of these conceptions belongs a corresponding object, and consequently their real possibility cannot be demonstrated, if we take away sensuous intuition—the only intuition which we possess—and there then remains nothing but the logical possibility, that is, the fact that the conception or thought is possible—which, however, is not the question; what we want to know being, whether it relates to an object and thus possesses any meaning.\n\nIt follows incontestably, that the pure conceptions of the understanding are incapable of transcendental, and must always be of empirical use alone, and that the principles of the pure understanding relate only to the general conditions of a possible experience, to objects of the senses, and never to things in general, apart from the mode in which we intuite them.\n\nTranscendental analytic has accordingly this important result, to wit, that the understanding is competent effect nothing à priori, except the anticipation of the form of a possible experience in general, and that, as that which is not phenomenon cannot be an object of experience, it can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are presented to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of phenomena, and the proud name of an ontology, which professes to present synthetical cognitions à priori of things in general in a systematic doctrine, must give place to the modest title of analytic of the pure understanding.\n\nThought is the act of referring a given intuition to an object. If the mode of this intuition is unknown to us, the object is merely transcendental, and the conception of the understanding is employed only transcendentally, that is, to produce unity in the thought of a manifold in general. Now a pure category, in which all conditions of sensuous intuition—as the only intuition we possess—are abstracted, does not determine an object, but merely expresses the thought of an object in general, according to different modes. Now, to employ a conception, the function of judgement is required, by which an object is subsumed under the conception, consequently the at least formal condition, under which something can be given in intuition. Failing this condition of judgement (schema), subsumption is impossible; for there is in such a case nothing given, which may be subsumed under the conception. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore, in fact, no use at all and has no determined, or even, as regards its form, determinable object. Hence it follows that the pure category is incompetent to establish a synthetical à priori principle, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical and never of transcendental use, and that beyond the sphere of possible experience no synthetical à priori principles are possible.\n\nIt may be advisable, therefore, to express ourselves thus. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have a merely transcendental meaning, but are nevertheless not of transcendental use, because this is in itself impossible, inasmuch as all the conditions of any employment or use of them (in judgements) are absent, to wit, the formal conditions of the subsumption of an object under these conceptions. As, therefore, in the character of pure categories, they must be employed empirically, and cannot be employed transcendentally, they are of no use at all, when separated from sensibility, that is, they cannot be applied to an object. They are merely the pure form of the employment of the understanding in respect of objects in general and of thought, without its being at the same time possible to think or to determine any object by their means. But there lurks at the foundation of this subject an illusion which it is very difficult to avoid. The categories are not based, as regards their origin, upon sensibility, like the forms of intuition, space, and time; they seem, therefore, to be capable of an application beyond the sphere of sensuous objects. But this is not the case. They are nothing but mere forms of thought, which contain only the logical faculty of uniting à priori in consciousness the manifold given in intuition. Apart, then, from the only intuition possible for us, they have still less meaning than the pure sensuous forms, space and time, for through them an object is at least given, while a mode of connection of the manifold, when the intuition which alone gives the manifold is wanting, has no meaning at all. At the same time, when we designate certain objects as phenomena or sensuous existences, thus distinguishing our mode of intuiting them from their own nature as things in themselves, it is evident that by this very distinction we as it were place the latter, considered in this their own nature, although we do not so intuite them, in opposition to the former, or, on the other hand, we do so place other possible things, which are not objects of our senses, but are cogitated by the understanding alone, and call them intelligible existences (noumena). Now the question arises whether the pure conceptions of our understanding do possess significance in respect of these latter, and may possibly be a mode of cognizing them.\n\nBut we are met at the very commencement with an ambiguity, which may easily occasion great misapprehension. The understanding, when it terms an object in a certain relation phenomenon, at the same time forms out of this relation a representation or notion of an object in itself, and hence believes that it can form also conceptions of such objects. Now as the understanding possesses no other fundamental conceptions besides the categories, it takes for granted that an object considered as a thing in itself must be capable of being thought by means of these pure conceptions, and is thereby led to hold the perfectly undetermined conception of an intelligible existence, a something out of the sphere of our sensibility, for a determinate conception of an existence which we can cognize in some way or other by means of the understanding.\n\nIf, by the term noumenon, we understand a thing so far as it is not an object of our sensuous intuition, thus making abstraction of our mode of intuiting it, this is a noumenon in the negative sense of the word. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensuous intuition, we in this case assume a peculiar mode of intuition, an intellectual intuition, to wit, which does not, however, belong to us, of the very possibility of which we have no notion—and this is a noumenon in the positive sense.\n\nThe doctrine of sensibility is also the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense, that is, of things which the understanding is obliged to cogitate apart from any relation to our mode of intuition, consequently not as mere phenomena, but as things in themselves. But the understanding at the same time comprehends that it cannot employ its categories for the consideration of things in themselves, because these possess significance only in relation to the unity of intuitions in space and time, and that they are competent to determine this unity by means of general à priori connecting conceptions only on account of the pure ideality of space and time. Where this unity of time is not to be met with, as is the case with noumena, the whole use, indeed the whole meaning of the categories is entirely lost, for even the possibility of things to correspond to the categories is in this case incomprehensible. On this point, I need only refer the reader to what I have said at the commencement of the General Remark appended to the foregoing chapter. Now, the possibility of a thing can never be proved from the fact that the conception of it is not self-contradictory, but only by means of an intuition corresponding to the conception. If, therefore, we wish to apply the categories to objects which cannot be regarded as phenomena, we must have an intuition different from the sensuous, and in this case the objects would be a noumena in the positive sense of the word. Now, as such an intuition, that is, an intellectual intuition, is no part of our faculty of cognition, it is absolutely impossible for the categories to possess any application beyond the limits of experience. It may be true that there are intelligible existences to which our faculty of sensuous intuition has no relation, and cannot be applied, but our conceptions of the understanding, as mere forms of thought for our sensuous intuition, do not extend to these. What, therefore, we call noumenon must be understood by us as such in a negative sense.\n\nIf I take away from an empirical intuition all thought (by means of the categories), there remains no cognition of any object; for by means of mere intuition nothing is cogitated, and, from the existence of such or such an affection of sensibility in me, it does not follow that this affection or representation has any relation to an object without me. But if I take away all intuition, there still remains the form of thought, that is, the mode of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Thus the categories do in some measure really extend further than sensuous intuition, inasmuch as they think objects in general, without regard to the mode (of sensibility) in which these objects are given. But they do not for this reason apply to and determine a wider sphere of objects, because we cannot assume that such can be given, without presupposing the possibility of another than the sensuous mode of intuition, a supposition we are not justified in making.\n\nI call a conception problematical which contains in itself no contradiction, and which is connected with other cognitions as a limitation of given conceptions, but whose objective reality cannot be cognized in any manner. The conception of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which must be cogitated not as an object of sense, but as a thing in itself (solely through the pure understanding), is not self-contradictory, for we are not entitled to maintain that sensibility is the only possible mode of intuition. Nay, further, this conception is necessary to restrain sensuous intuition within the bounds of phenomena, and thus to limit the objective validity of sensuous cognition; for things in themselves, which lie beyond its province, are called noumena for the very purpose of indicating that this cognition does not extend its application to all that the understanding thinks. But, after all, the possibility of such noumena is quite incomprehensible, and beyond the sphere of phenomena, all is for us a mere void; that is to say, we possess an understanding whose province does problematically extend beyond this sphere, but we do not possess an intuition, indeed, not even the conception of a possible intuition, by means of which objects beyond the region of sensibility could be given us, and in reference to which the understanding might be employed assertorically. The conception of a noumenon is therefore merely a limitative conception and therefore only of negative use. But it is not an arbitrary or fictitious notion, but is connected with the limitation of sensibility, without, however, being capable of presenting us with any positive datum beyond this sphere.\n\nThe division of objects into phenomena and noumena, and of the world into a mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis is therefore quite inadmissible in a positive sense, although conceptions do certainly admit of such a division; for the class of noumena have no determinate object corresponding to them, and cannot therefore possess objective validity. If we abandon the senses, how can it be made conceivable that the categories (which are the only conceptions that could serve as conceptions for noumena) have any sense or meaning at all, inasmuch as something more than the mere unity of thought, namely, a possible intuition, is requisite for their application to an object? The conception of a noumenon, considered as merely problematical, is, however, not only admissible, but, as a limitative conception of sensibility, absolutely necessary. But, in this case, a noumenon is not a particular intelligible object for our understanding; on the contrary, the kind of understanding to which it could belong is itself a problem, for we cannot form the most distant conception of the possibility of an understanding which should cognize an object, not discursively by means of categories, but intuitively in a non-sensuous intuition. Our understanding attains in this way a sort of negative extension. That is to say, it is not limited by, but rather limits, sensibility, by giving the name of noumena to things, not considered as phenomena, but as things in themselves. But it at the same time prescribes limits to itself, for it confesses itself unable to cognize these by means of the categories, and hence is compelled to cogitate them merely as an unknown something.\n\nI find, however, in the writings of modern authors, an entirely different use of the expressions, mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis, which quite departs from the meaning of the ancients—an acceptation in which, indeed, there is to be found no difficulty, but which at the same time depends on mere verbal quibbling. According to this meaning, some have chosen to call the complex of phenomena, in so far as it is intuited, mundus sensibilis, but in so far as the connection thereof is cogitated according to general laws of thought, mundus intelligibilis. Astronomy, in so far as we mean by the word the mere observation of the starry heaven, may represent the former; a system of astronomy, such as the Copernican or Newtonian, the latter. But such twisting of words is a mere sophistical subterfuge, to avoid a difficult question, by modifying its meaning to suit our own convenience. To be sure, understanding and reason are employed in the cognition of phenomena; but the question is, whether these can be applied when the object is not a phenomenon and in this sense we regard it if it is cogitated as given to the understanding alone, and not to the senses. The question therefore is whether, over and above the empirical use of the understanding, a transcendental use is possible, which applies to the noumenon as an object. This question we have answered in the negative.\n\nWhen therefore we say, the senses represent objects as they appear, the understanding as they are, the latter statement must not be understood in a transcendental, but only in an empirical signification, that is, as they must be represented in the complete connection of phenomena, and not according to what they may be, apart from their relation to possible experience, consequently not as objects of the pure understanding. For this must ever remain unknown to us. Nay, it is also quite unknown to us whether any such transcendental or extraordinary cognition is possible under any circumstances, at least, whether it is possible by means of our categories. Understanding and sensibility, with us, can determine objects only in conjunction. If we separate them, we have intuitions without conceptions, or conceptions without intuitions; in both cases, representations, which we cannot apply to any determinate object.\n\nIf, after all our inquiries and explanations, any one still hesitates to abandon the mere transcendental use of the categories, let him attempt to construct with them a synthetical proposition. It would, of course, be unnecessary for this purpose to construct an analytical proposition, for that does not extend the sphere of the understanding, but, being concerned only about what is cogitated in the conception itself, it leaves it quite undecided whether the conception has any relation to objects, or merely indicates the unity of thought—complete abstraction being made of the modi in which an object may be given: in such a proposition, it is sufficient for the understanding to know what lies in the conception—to what it applies is to it indifferent. The attempt must therefore be made with a synthetical and so-called transcendental principle, for example: “Everything that exists, exists as substance,” or, “Everything that is contingent exists as an effect of some other thing, viz., of its cause.” Now I ask, whence can the understanding draw these synthetical propositions, when the conceptions contained therein do not relate to possible experience but to things in themselves (noumena)? Where is to be found the third term, which is always requisite PURE site in a synthetical proposition, which may connect in the same proposition conceptions which have no logical (analytical) connection with each other? The proposition never will be demonstrated, nay, more, the possibility of any such pure assertion never can be shown, without making reference to the empirical use of the understanding, and thus, ipso facto, completely renouncing pure and non-sensuous judgement. Thus the conception of pure and merely intelligible objects is completely void of all principles of its application, because we cannot imagine any mode in which they might be given, and the problematical thought which leaves a place open for them serves only, like a void space, to limit the use of empirical principles, without containing at the same time any other object of cognition beyond their sphere.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0001","text":"# Phenomena Noumena\n\n### Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena\n\nWe have now not only traversed the region of the pure understanding and carefully surveyed every part of it, but we have also measured it, and assigned to everything therein its proper place. But this land is an island, and enclosed by nature herself within unchangeable limits. It is the land of truth (an attractive word), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the region of illusion, where many a fog-bank, many an iceberg, seems to the mariner, on his voyage of discovery, a new country, and, while constantly deluding him with vain hopes, engages him in dangerous adventures, from which he never can desist, and which yet he never can bring to a termination. But before venturing upon this sea, in order to explore it in its whole extent, and to arrive at a certainty whether anything is to be discovered there, it will not be without advantage if we cast our eyes upon the chart of the land that we are about to leave, and to ask ourselves, firstly, whether we cannot rest perfectly contented with what it contains, or whether we must not of necessity be contented with it, if we can find nowhere else a solid foundation to build upon; and, secondly, by what title we possess this land itself, and how we hold it secure against all hostile claims? Although, in the course of our analytic, we have already given sufficient answers to these questions, yet a summary recapitulation of these solutions may be useful in strengthening our conviction, by uniting in one point the momenta of the arguments.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","text":"We have seen that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing from experience, it nevertheless possesses only for the behoof and use of experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether constitutive à priori (as the mathematical principles), or merely regulative (as the dynamical), contain nothing but the pure schema, as it were, of possible experience. For experience possesses its unity from the synthetical unity which the understanding, originally and from itself, imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and in à priori relation to and agreement with which phenomena, as data for a possible cognition, must stand. But although these rules of the understanding are not only à priori true, but the very source of all truth, that is, of the accordance of our cognition with objects, and on this ground, that they contain the basis of the possibility of experience, as the ensemble of all cognition, it seems to us not enough to propound what is true—we desire also to be told what we want to know. If, then, we learn nothing more by this critical examination than what we should have practised in the merely empirical use of the understanding, without any such subtle inquiry, the presumption is that the advantage we reap from it is not worth the labour bestowed upon it. It may certainly be answered that no rash curiosity is more prejudicial to the enlargement of our knowledge than that which must know beforehand the utility of this or that piece of information which we seek, before we have entered on the needful investigations, and before one could form the least conception of its utility, even though it were placed before our eyes. But there is one advantage in such transcendental inquiries which can be made comprehensible to the dullest and most reluctant learner—this, namely, that the understanding which is occupied merely with empirical exercise, and does not reflect on the sources of its own cognition, may exercise its functions very well and very successfully, but is quite unable to do one thing, and that of very great importance, to determine, namely, the bounds that limit its employment, and to know what lies within or without its own sphere. This purpose can be obtained only by such profound investigations as we have instituted. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it can never be sure either as to its claims or possessions, but must lay its account with many humiliating corrections, when it transgresses, as it unavoidably will, the limits of its own territory, and loses itself in fanciful opinions and blinding illusions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","text":"That the understanding, therefore, cannot make of its à priori principles, or even of its conceptions, other than an empirical use, is a proposition which leads to the most important results. A transcendental use is made of a conception in a fundamental proposition or principle, when it is referred to things in general and considered as things in themselves; an empirical use, when it is referred merely to phenomena, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That the latter use of a conception is the only admissible one is evident from the reasons following. For every conception are requisite, firstly, the logical form of a conception (of thought) general; and, secondly, the possibility of presenting to this an object to which it may apply. Failing this latter, it has no sense, and utterly void of content, although it may contain the logical function for constructing a conception from certain data. Now, object cannot be given to a conception otherwise than by intuition, and, even if a pure intuition antecedent to the object is à priori possible, this pure intuition can itself obtain objective validity only from empirical intuition, of which it is itself but the form. All conceptions, therefore, and with them all principles, however high the degree of their à priori possibility, relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to data towards a possible experience. Without this they possess no objective validity, but are mere play of imagination or of understanding with images or notions. Let us take, for example, the conceptions of mathematics, and first in its pure intuitions. “Space has three dimensions”—“Between two points there can be only one straight line,” etc. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science occupies itself, are generated in the mind entirely à priori, they would nevertheless have no significance if we were not always able to exhibit their significance in and by means of phenomena (empirical objects). Hence it is requisite that an abstract conception be made sensuous, that is, that an object corresponding to it in intuition be forthcoming, otherwise the conception remains, as we say, without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this requirement by the construction of the figure, which is a phenomenon evident to the senses. The same science finds support and significance in number; this in its turn finds it in the fingers, or in counters, or in lines and points. The conception itself is always produced à priori, together with the synthetical principles or formulas from such conceptions; but the proper employment of them, and their application to objects, can exist nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which, as regards its form, they contain à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0004","text":"That this is also the case with all of the categories and the principles based upon them is evident from the fact that we cannot render intelligible the possibility of an object corresponding to them without having recourse to the conditions of sensibility, consequently, to the form of phenomena, to which, as their only proper objects, their use must therefore be confined, inasmuch as, if this condition is removed, all significance, that is, all relation to an object, disappears, and no example can be found to make it comprehensible what sort of things we ought to think under such conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","text":"The conception of quantity cannot be explained except by saying that it is the determination of a thing whereby it can be cogitated how many times one is placed in it. But this “how many times” is based upon successive repetition, consequently upon time and the synthesis of the homogeneous therein. Reality, in contradistinction to negation, can be explained only by cogitating a time which is either filled therewith or is void. If I leave out the notion of permanence (which is existence in all time), there remains in the conception of substance nothing but the logical notion of subject, a notion of which I endeavour to realize by representing to myself something that can exist only as a subject. But not only am I perfectly ignorant of any conditions under which this logical prerogative can belong to a thing, I can make nothing out of the notion, and draw no inference from it, because no object to which to apply the conception is determined, and we consequently do not know whether it has any meaning at all. In like manner, if I leave out the notion of time, in which something follows upon some other thing in conformity with a rule, I can find nothing in the pure category, except that there is a something of such a sort that from it a conclusion may be drawn as to the existence of some other thing. But in this case it would not only be impossible to distinguish between a cause and an effect, but, as this power to draw conclusions requires conditions of which I am quite ignorant, the conception is not determined as to the mode in which it ought to apply to an object. The so-called principle: “Everything that is contingent has a cause,” comes with a gravity and self-assumed authority that seems to require no support from without. But, I ask, what is meant by contingent? The answer is that the non-existence of which is possible. But I should like very well to know by what means this possibility of non-existence is to be cognized, if we do not represent to ourselves a succession in the series of phenomena, and in this succession an existence which follows a non-existence, or conversely, consequently, change. For to say, that the non-existence of a thing is not self-contradictory is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is no doubt a necessary condition of the existence of the conception, but is far from being sufficient for the real objective possibility of non-existence. I can annihilate in thought every existing substance without self-contradiction, but I cannot infer from this their objective contingency in existence, that is to say, the possibility of their non-existence in itself. As regards the category of community, it may easily be inferred that, as the pure categories of substance and causality are incapable of a definition and explanation sufficient to determine their object without the aid of intuition, the category of reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to each other (commercium) is just as little susceptible thereof. Possibility, existence, and necessity nobody has ever yet been able to explain without being guilty of manifest tautology, when the definition has been drawn entirely from the pure understanding. For the substitution of the logical possibility of the conception—the condition of which is that it be not self-contradictory, for the transcendental possibility of things—the condition of which is that there be an object corresponding to the conception, is a trick which can only deceive the inexperienced.[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-38)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","text":"[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-38) In one word, to none of these conceptions belongs a corresponding object, and consequently their real possibility cannot be demonstrated, if we take away sensuous intuition—the only intuition which we possess—and there then remains nothing but the logical possibility, that is, the fact that the conception or thought is possible—which, however, is not the question; what we want to know being, whether it relates to an object and thus possesses any meaning.\n\nIt follows incontestably, that the pure conceptions of the understanding are incapable of transcendental, and must always be of empirical use alone, and that the principles of the pure understanding relate only to the general conditions of a possible experience, to objects of the senses, and never to things in general, apart from the mode in which we intuite them.\n\nTranscendental analytic has accordingly this important result, to wit, that the understanding is competent effect nothing à priori, except the anticipation of the form of a possible experience in general, and that, as that which is not phenomenon cannot be an object of experience, it can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are presented to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of phenomena, and the proud name of an ontology, which professes to present synthetical cognitions à priori of things in general in a systematic doctrine, must give place to the modest title of analytic of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","text":"Thought is the act of referring a given intuition to an object. If the mode of this intuition is unknown to us, the object is merely transcendental, and the conception of the understanding is employed only transcendentally, that is, to produce unity in the thought of a manifold in general. Now a pure category, in which all conditions of sensuous intuition—as the only intuition we possess—are abstracted, does not determine an object, but merely expresses the thought of an object in general, according to different modes. Now, to employ a conception, the function of judgement is required, by which an object is subsumed under the conception, consequently the at least formal condition, under which something can be given in intuition. Failing this condition of judgement (schema), subsumption is impossible; for there is in such a case nothing given, which may be subsumed under the conception. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore, in fact, no use at all and has no determined, or even, as regards its form, determinable object. Hence it follows that the pure category is incompetent to establish a synthetical à priori principle, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical and never of transcendental use, and that beyond the sphere of possible experience no synthetical à priori principles are possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","text":"It may be advisable, therefore, to express ourselves thus. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have a merely transcendental meaning, but are nevertheless not of transcendental use, because this is in itself impossible, inasmuch as all the conditions of any employment or use of them (in judgements) are absent, to wit, the formal conditions of the subsumption of an object under these conceptions. As, therefore, in the character of pure categories, they must be employed empirically, and cannot be employed transcendentally, they are of no use at all, when separated from sensibility, that is, they cannot be applied to an object. They are merely the pure form of the employment of the understanding in respect of objects in general and of thought, without its being at the same time possible to think or to determine any object by their means. But there lurks at the foundation of this subject an illusion which it is very difficult to avoid. The categories are not based, as regards their origin, upon sensibility, like the forms of intuition, space, and time; they seem, therefore, to be capable of an application beyond the sphere of sensuous objects. But this is not the case. They are nothing but mere forms of thought, which contain only the logical faculty of uniting à priori in consciousness the manifold given in intuition. Apart, then, from the only intuition possible for us, they have still less meaning than the pure sensuous forms, space and time, for through them an object is at least given, while a mode of connection of the manifold, when the intuition which alone gives the manifold is wanting, has no meaning at all. At the same time, when we designate certain objects as phenomena or sensuous existences, thus distinguishing our mode of intuiting them from their own nature as things in themselves, it is evident that by this very distinction we as it were place the latter, considered in this their own nature, although we do not so intuite them, in opposition to the former, or, on the other hand, we do so place other possible things, which are not objects of our senses, but are cogitated by the understanding alone, and call them intelligible existences (noumena). Now the question arises whether the pure conceptions of our understanding do possess significance in respect of these latter, and may possibly be a mode of cognizing them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","text":"But we are met at the very commencement with an ambiguity, which may easily occasion great misapprehension. The understanding, when it terms an object in a certain relation phenomenon, at the same time forms out of this relation a representation or notion of an object in itself, and hence believes that it can form also conceptions of such objects. Now as the understanding possesses no other fundamental conceptions besides the categories, it takes for granted that an object considered as a thing in itself must be capable of being thought by means of these pure conceptions, and is thereby led to hold the perfectly undetermined conception of an intelligible existence, a something out of the sphere of our sensibility, for a determinate conception of an existence which we can cognize in some way or other by means of the understanding.\n\nIf, by the term noumenon, we understand a thing so far as it is not an object of our sensuous intuition, thus making abstraction of our mode of intuiting it, this is a noumenon in the negative sense of the word. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensuous intuition, we in this case assume a peculiar mode of intuition, an intellectual intuition, to wit, which does not, however, belong to us, of the very possibility of which we have no notion—and this is a noumenon in the positive sense.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","text":"The doctrine of sensibility is also the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense, that is, of things which the understanding is obliged to cogitate apart from any relation to our mode of intuition, consequently not as mere phenomena, but as things in themselves. But the understanding at the same time comprehends that it cannot employ its categories for the consideration of things in themselves, because these possess significance only in relation to the unity of intuitions in space and time, and that they are competent to determine this unity by means of general à priori connecting conceptions only on account of the pure ideality of space and time. Where this unity of time is not to be met with, as is the case with noumena, the whole use, indeed the whole meaning of the categories is entirely lost, for even the possibility of things to correspond to the categories is in this case incomprehensible. On this point, I need only refer the reader to what I have said at the commencement of the General Remark appended to the foregoing chapter. Now, the possibility of a thing can never be proved from the fact that the conception of it is not self-contradictory, but only by means of an intuition corresponding to the conception. If, therefore, we wish to apply the categories to objects which cannot be regarded as phenomena, we must have an intuition different from the sensuous, and in this case the objects would be a noumena in the positive sense of the word. Now, as such an intuition, that is, an intellectual intuition, is no part of our faculty of cognition, it is absolutely impossible for the categories to possess any application beyond the limits of experience. It may be true that there are intelligible existences to which our faculty of sensuous intuition has no relation, and cannot be applied, but our conceptions of the understanding, as mere forms of thought for our sensuous intuition, do not extend to these. What, therefore, we call noumenon must be understood by us as such in a negative sense.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0011","text":"If I take away from an empirical intuition all thought (by means of the categories), there remains no cognition of any object; for by means of mere intuition nothing is cogitated, and, from the existence of such or such an affection of sensibility in me, it does not follow that this affection or representation has any relation to an object without me. But if I take away all intuition, there still remains the form of thought, that is, the mode of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Thus the categories do in some measure really extend further than sensuous intuition, inasmuch as they think objects in general, without regard to the mode (of sensibility) in which these objects are given. But they do not for this reason apply to and determine a wider sphere of objects, because we cannot assume that such can be given, without presupposing the possibility of another than the sensuous mode of intuition, a supposition we are not justified in making.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","text":"I call a conception problematical which contains in itself no contradiction, and which is connected with other cognitions as a limitation of given conceptions, but whose objective reality cannot be cognized in any manner. The conception of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which must be cogitated not as an object of sense, but as a thing in itself (solely through the pure understanding), is not self-contradictory, for we are not entitled to maintain that sensibility is the only possible mode of intuition. Nay, further, this conception is necessary to restrain sensuous intuition within the bounds of phenomena, and thus to limit the objective validity of sensuous cognition; for things in themselves, which lie beyond its province, are called noumena for the very purpose of indicating that this cognition does not extend its application to all that the understanding thinks. But, after all, the possibility of such noumena is quite incomprehensible, and beyond the sphere of phenomena, all is for us a mere void; that is to say, we possess an understanding whose province does problematically extend beyond this sphere, but we do not possess an intuition, indeed, not even the conception of a possible intuition, by means of which objects beyond the region of sensibility could be given us, and in reference to which the understanding might be employed assertorically. The conception of a noumenon is therefore merely a limitative conception and therefore only of negative use. But it is not an arbitrary or fictitious notion, but is connected with the limitation of sensibility, without, however, being capable of presenting us with any positive datum beyond this sphere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","text":"The division of objects into phenomena and noumena, and of the world into a mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis is therefore quite inadmissible in a positive sense, although conceptions do certainly admit of such a division; for the class of noumena have no determinate object corresponding to them, and cannot therefore possess objective validity. If we abandon the senses, how can it be made conceivable that the categories (which are the only conceptions that could serve as conceptions for noumena) have any sense or meaning at all, inasmuch as something more than the mere unity of thought, namely, a possible intuition, is requisite for their application to an object? The conception of a noumenon, considered as merely problematical, is, however, not only admissible, but, as a limitative conception of sensibility, absolutely necessary. But, in this case, a noumenon is not a particular intelligible object for our understanding; on the contrary, the kind of understanding to which it could belong is itself a problem, for we cannot form the most distant conception of the possibility of an understanding which should cognize an object, not discursively by means of categories, but intuitively in a non-sensuous intuition. Our understanding attains in this way a sort of negative extension. That is to say, it is not limited by, but rather limits, sensibility, by giving the name of noumena to things, not considered as phenomena, but as things in themselves. But it at the same time prescribes limits to itself, for it confesses itself unable to cognize these by means of the categories, and hence is compelled to cogitate them merely as an unknown something.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0014","text":"I find, however, in the writings of modern authors, an entirely different use of the expressions, mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis, which quite departs from the meaning of the ancients—an acceptation in which, indeed, there is to be found no difficulty, but which at the same time depends on mere verbal quibbling. According to this meaning, some have chosen to call the complex of phenomena, in so far as it is intuited, mundus sensibilis, but in so far as the connection thereof is cogitated according to general laws of thought, mundus intelligibilis. Astronomy, in so far as we mean by the word the mere observation of the starry heaven, may represent the former; a system of astronomy, such as the Copernican or Newtonian, the latter. But such twisting of words is a mere sophistical subterfuge, to avoid a difficult question, by modifying its meaning to suit our own convenience. To be sure, understanding and reason are employed in the cognition of phenomena; but the question is, whether these can be applied when the object is not a phenomenon and in this sense we regard it if it is cogitated as given to the understanding alone, and not to the senses. The question therefore is whether, over and above the empirical use of the understanding, a transcendental use is possible, which applies to the noumenon as an object. This question we have answered in the negative.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0015","text":"When therefore we say, the senses represent objects as they appear, the understanding as they are, the latter statement must not be understood in a transcendental, but only in an empirical signification, that is, as they must be represented in the complete connection of phenomena, and not according to what they may be, apart from their relation to possible experience, consequently not as objects of the pure understanding. For this must ever remain unknown to us. Nay, it is also quite unknown to us whether any such transcendental or extraordinary cognition is possible under any circumstances, at least, whether it is possible by means of our categories. Understanding and sensibility, with us, can determine objects only in conjunction. If we separate them, we have intuitions without conceptions, or conceptions without intuitions; in both cases, representations, which we cannot apply to any determinate object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","text":"If, after all our inquiries and explanations, any one still hesitates to abandon the mere transcendental use of the categories, let him attempt to construct with them a synthetical proposition. It would, of course, be unnecessary for this purpose to construct an analytical proposition, for that does not extend the sphere of the understanding, but, being concerned only about what is cogitated in the conception itself, it leaves it quite undecided whether the conception has any relation to objects, or merely indicates the unity of thought—complete abstraction being made of the modi in which an object may be given: in such a proposition, it is sufficient for the understanding to know what lies in the conception—to what it applies is to it indifferent. The attempt must therefore be made with a synthetical and so-called transcendental principle, for example: “Everything that exists, exists as substance,” or, “Everything that is contingent exists as an effect of some other thing, viz., of its cause.” Now I ask, whence can the understanding draw these synthetical propositions, when the conceptions contained therein do not relate to possible experience but to things in themselves (noumena)? Where is to be found the third term, which is always requisite PURE site in a synthetical proposition, which may connect in the same proposition conceptions which have no logical (analytical) connection with each other? The proposition never will be demonstrated, nay, more, the possibility of any such pure assertion never can be shown, without making reference to the empirical use of the understanding, and thus, ipso facto, completely renouncing pure and non-sensuous judgement. Thus the conception of pure and merely intelligible objects is completely void of all principles of its application, because we cannot imagine any mode in which they might be given, and the problematical thought which leaves a place open for them serves only, like a void space, to limit the use of empirical principles, without containing at the same time any other object of cognition beyond their sphere.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"71fe80728215b32bb51d4be73eeb467668a7803e8c4da355498cdd8cd01eba88","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX","title":"APPENDIX","order":49,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_APPX","text":"### APPENDIX\n\nOf the Equivocal Nature or Amphiboly of the Conceptions of Reflection from the Confusion of the Transcendental with the Empirical use of the Understanding.\n\nReflection (reflexio) is not occupied about objects themselves, for the purpose of directly obtaining conceptions of them, but is that state of the mind in which we set ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we obtain conceptions. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to the different sources or faculties of cognition, by which alone their relation to each other can be rightly determined. The first question which occurs in considering our representations is to what faculty of cognition do they belong? To the understanding or to the senses? Many judgements are admitted to be true from mere habit or inclination; but, because reflection neither precedes nor follows, it is held to be a judgement that has its origin in the understanding. All judgements do not require examination, that is, investigation into the grounds of their truth. For, when they are immediately certain (for example: “Between two points there can be only one straight line”), no better or less mediate test of their truth can be found than that which they themselves contain and express. But all judgement, nay, all comparisons require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong. The act whereby I compare my representations with the faculty of cognition which originates them, and whereby I distinguish whether they are compared with each other as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensuous intuition, I term transcendental reflection. Now, the relations in which conceptions can stand to each other are those of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, of the internal and external, finally, of the determinable and the determining (matter and form). The proper determination of these relations rests on the question, to what faculty of cognition they subjectively belong, whether to sensibility or understanding? For, on the manner in which we solve this question depends the manner in which we must cogitate these relations.\n\nBefore constructing any objective judgement, we compare the conceptions that are to be placed in the judgement, and observe whether there exists identity (of many representations in one conception), if a general judgement is to be constructed, or difference, if a particular; whether there is agreement when affirmative; and opposition when negative judgements are to be constructed, and so on. For this reason we ought to call these conceptions, conceptions of comparison (conceptus comparationis). But as, when the question is not as to the logical form, but as to the content of conceptions, that is to say, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, and so on, the things can have a twofold relation to our faculty of cognition, to wit, a relation either to sensibility or to the understanding, and as on this relation depends their relation to each other, transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or the other faculty of cognition, can alone determine this latter relation. Thus we shall not be able to discover whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, etc., from the mere conception of the things by means of comparison (comparatio), but only by distinguishing the mode of cognition to which they belong, in other words, by means of transcendental reflection. We may, therefore, with justice say, that logical reflection is mere comparison, for in it no account is taken of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong, and they are consequently, as far as regards their origin, to be treated as homogeneous; while transcendental reflection (which applies to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of objective comparison of representations with each other, and is therefore very different from the former, because the faculties of cognition to which they belong are not even the same. Transcendental reflection is a duty which no one can neglect who wishes to establish an à priori judgement upon things. We shall now proceed to fulfil this duty, and thereby throw not a little light on the question as to the determination of the proper business of the understanding.\n\n1. Identity and Difference. When an object is presented to us several times, but always with the same internal determinations (qualitas et quantitas), it, if an object of pure understanding, is always the same, not several things, but only one thing (numerica identitas); but if a phenomenon, we do not concern ourselves with comparing the conception of the thing with the conception of some other, but, although they may be in this respect perfectly the same, the difference of place at the same time is a sufficient ground for asserting the numerical difference of these objects (of sense). Thus, in the case of two drops of water, we may make complete abstraction of all internal difference (quality and quantity), and, the fact that they are intuited at the same time in different places, is sufficient to justify us in holding them to be numerically different. Leibnitz regarded phenomena as things in themselves, consequently as intelligibilia, that is, objects of pure understanding (although, on account of the confused nature of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena), and in this case his principle of the indiscernible (principium identatis indiscernibilium) is not to be impugned. But, as phenomena are objects of sensibility, and, as the understanding, in respect of them, must be employed empirically and not purely or transcendentally, plurality and numerical difference are given by space itself as the condition of external phenomena. For one part of space, although it may be perfectly similar and equal to another part, is still without it, and for this reason alone is different from the latter, which is added to it in order to make up a greater space. It follows that this must hold good of all things that are in the different parts of space at the same time, however similar and equal one may be to another.\n\n2. Agreement and Opposition. When reality is represented by the pure understanding (realitas noumenon), opposition between realities is incogitable—such a relation, that is, that when these realities are connected in one subject, they annihilate the effects of each other and may be represented in the formula 3 -3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in a phenomenon (realitas phaenomenon) may very well be in mutual opposition, and, when united in the same subject, the one may completely or in part annihilate the effect or consequence of the other; as in the case of two moving forces in the same straight line drawing or impelling a point in opposite directions, or in the case of a pleasure counterbalancing a certain amount of pain.\n\n3. The Internal and External. In an object of the pure understanding, only that is internal which has no relation (as regards its existence) to anything different from itself. On the other hand, the internal determinations of a substantia phaenomenon in space are nothing but relations, and it is itself nothing more than a complex of mere relations. Substance in space we are cognizant of only through forces operative in it, either drawing others towards itself (attraction), or preventing others from forcing into itself (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties that make up the conception of substance phenomenal in space, and which we term matter. On the other hand, as an object of the pure understanding, every substance must have internal determination and forces. But what other internal attributes of such an object can I think than those which my internal sense presents to me? That, to wit, which in either itself thought, or something analogous to it. Hence Leibnitz, who looked upon things as noumena, after denying them everything like external relation, and therefore also composition or combination, declared that all substances, even the component parts of matter, were simple substances with powers of representation, in one word, monads.\n\n4. Matter and Form. These two conceptions lie at the foundation of all other reflection, so inseparably are they connected with every mode of exercising the understanding. The former denotes the determinable in general, the second its determination, both in a transcendental sense, abstraction being made of every difference in that which is given, and of the mode in which it is determined. Logicians formerly termed the universal, matter, the specific difference of this or that part of the universal, form. In a judgement one may call the given conceptions logical matter (for the judgement), the relation of these to each other (by means of the copula), the form of the judgement. In an object, the composite parts thereof (essentialia) are the matter; the mode in which they are connected in the object, the form. In respect to things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, the limitation thereof (negation) as the form, by which one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental conceptions. The understanding demands that something be given (at least in the conception), in order to be able to determine it in a certain manner. Hence, in a conception of the pure understanding, the matter precedes the form, and for this reason Leibnitz first assumed the existence of things (monads) and of an internal power of representation in them, in order to found upon this their external relation and the community their state (that is, of their representations). Hence, with him, space and time were possible—the former through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with each other, as causes and effects. And so would it really be, if the pure understanding were capable of an immediate application to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But being merely sensuous intuitions, in which we determine all objects solely as phenomena, the form of intuition (as a subjective property of sensibility) must antecede all matter (sensations), consequently space and time must antecede all phenomena and all data of experience, and rather make experience itself possible. But the intellectual philosopher could not endure that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility; an objection perfectly correct, if we assume that we intuite things as they are, although with confused representation. But as sensuous intuition is a peculiar subjective condition, which is à priori at the foundation of all perception, and the form of which is primitive, the form must be given per se, and so far from matter (or the things themselves which appear) lying at the foundation of experience (as we must conclude, if we judge by mere conceptions), the very possibility of itself presupposes, on the contrary, a given formal intuition (space and time).\n\nREMARK ON THE AMPHIBOLY OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF REFLECTION.\n\nLet me be allowed to term the position which we assign to a conception either in the sensibility or in the pure understanding, the transcendental place. In this manner, the appointment of the position which must be taken by each conception according to the difference in its use, and the directions for determining this place to all conceptions according to rules, would be a transcendental topic, a doctrine which would thoroughly shield us from the surreptitious devices of the pure understanding and the delusions which thence arise, as it would always distinguish to what faculty of cognition each conception properly belonged. Every conception, every title, under which many cognitions rank together, may be called a logical place. Upon this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, of which teachers and rhetoricians could avail themselves, in order, under certain titles of thought, to observe what would best suit the matter they had to treat, and thus enable themselves to quibble and talk with fluency and an appearance of profundity.\n\nTranscendental topic, on the contrary, contains nothing more than the above-mentioned four titles of all comparison and distinction, which differ from categories in this respect, that they do not represent the object according to that which constitutes its conception (quantity, reality), but set forth merely the comparison of representations, which precedes our conceptions of things. But this comparison requires a previous reflection, that is, a determination of the place to which the representations of the things which are compared belong, whether, to wit, they are cogitated by the pure understanding, or given by sensibility.\n\nConceptions may be logically compared without the trouble of inquiring to what faculty their objects belong, whether as noumena, to the understanding, or as phenomena, to sensibility. If, however, we wish to employ these conceptions in respect of objects, previous transcendental reflection is necessary. Without this reflection I should make a very unsafe use of these conceptions, and construct pretended synthetical propositions which critical reason cannot acknowledge and which are based solely upon a transcendental amphiboly, that is, upon a substitution of an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon.\n\nFor want of this doctrine of transcendental topic, and consequently deceived by the amphiboly of the conceptions of reflection, the celebrated Leibnitz constructed an intellectual system of the world, or rather, believed himself competent to cognize the internal nature of things, by comparing all objects merely with the understanding and the abstract formal conceptions of thought. Our table of the conceptions of reflection gives us the unexpected advantage of being able to exhibit the distinctive peculiarities of his system in all its parts, and at the same time of exposing the fundamental principle of this peculiar mode of thought, which rested upon naught but a misconception. He compared all things with each other merely by means of conceptions, and naturally found no other differences than those by which the understanding distinguishes its pure conceptions one from another. The conditions of sensuous intuition, which contain in themselves their own means of distinction, he did not look upon as primitive, because sensibility was to him but a confused mode of representation and not any particular source of representations. A phenomenon was for him the representation of the thing in itself, although distinguished from cognition by the understanding only in respect of the logical form—the former with its usual want of analysis containing, according to him, a certain mixture of collateral representations in its conception of a thing, which it is the duty of the understanding to separate and distinguish. In one word, Leibnitz intellectualized phenomena, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be allowed to make use of such expressions), sensualized the conceptions of the understanding, that is to say, declared them to be nothing more than empirical or abstract conceptions of reflection. Instead of seeking in the understanding and sensibility two different sources of representations, which, however, can present us with objective judgements of things only in conjunction, each of these great men recognized but one of these faculties, which, in their opinion, applied immediately to things in themselves, the other having no duty but that of confusing or arranging the representations of the former.\n\nAccordingly, the objects of sense were compared by Leibnitz as things in general merely in the understanding.\n\n1st. He compares them in regard to their identity or difference—as judged by the understanding. As, therefore, he considered merely the conceptions of objects, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and left quite out of sight the transcendental locale of these conceptions—whether, that is, their object ought to be classed among phenomena, or among things in themselves, it was to be expected that he should extend the application of the principle of indiscernibles, which is valid solely of conceptions of things in general, to objects of sense (mundus phaenomenon), and that he should believe that he had thereby contributed in no small degree to extend our knowledge of nature. In truth, if I cognize in all its inner determinations a drop of water as a thing in itself, I cannot look upon one drop as different from another, if the conception of the one is completely identical with that of the other. But if it is a phenomenon in space, it has a place not merely in the understanding (among conceptions), but also in sensuous external intuition (in space), and in this case, the physical locale is a matter of indifference in regard to the internal determinations of things, and one place, B, may contain a thing which is perfectly similar and equal to another in a place, A, just as well as if the two things were in every respect different from each other. Difference of place without any other conditions, makes the plurality and distinction of objects as phenomena, not only possible in itself, but even necessary. Consequently, the above so-called law is not a law of nature. It is merely an analytical rule for the comparison of things by means of mere conceptions.\n\n2nd. The principle: “Realities (as simple affirmations) never logically contradict each other,” is a proposition perfectly true respecting the relation of conceptions, but, whether as regards nature, or things in themselves (of which we have not the slightest conception), is without any the least meaning. For real opposition, in which A -B is = 0, exists everywhere, an opposition, that is, in which one reality united with another in the same subject annihilates the effects of the other—a fact which is constantly brought before our eyes by the different antagonistic actions and operations in nature, which, nevertheless, as depending on real forces, must be called realitates phaenomena. General mechanics can even present us with the empirical condition of this opposition in an à priori rule, as it directs its attention to the opposition in the direction of forces—a condition of which the transcendental conception of reality can tell us nothing. Although M. Leibnitz did not announce this proposition with precisely the pomp of a new principle, he yet employed it for the establishment of new propositions, and his followers introduced it into their Leibnitzio-Wolfian system of philosophy. According to this principle, for example, all evils are but consequences of the limited nature of created beings, that is, negations, because these are the only opposite of reality. (In the mere conception of a thing in general this is really the case, but not in things as phenomena.) In like manner, the upholders of this system deem it not only possible, but natural also, to connect and unite all reality in one being, because they acknowledge no other sort of opposition than that of contradiction (by which the conception itself of a thing is annihilated), and find themselves unable to conceive an opposition of reciprocal destruction, so to speak, in which one real cause destroys the effect of another, and the conditions of whose representation we meet with only in sensibility.\n\n3rd. The Leibnitzian monadology has really no better foundation than on this philosopher’s mode of falsely representing the difference of the internal and external solely in relation to the understanding. Substances, in general, must have something inward, which is therefore free from external relations, consequently from that of composition also. The simple—that which can be represented by a unit—is therefore the foundation of that which is internal in things in themselves. The internal state of substances cannot therefore consist in place, shape, contact, or motion, determinations which are all external relations, and we can ascribe to them no other than that whereby we internally determine our faculty of sense itself, that is to say, the state of representation. Thus, then, were constructed the monads, which were to form the elements of the universe, the active force of which consists in representation, the effects of this force being thus entirely confined to themselves.\n\nFor the same reason, his view of the possible community of substances could not represent it but as a predetermined harmony, and by no means as a physical influence. For inasmuch as everything is occupied only internally, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could not stand in active and living connection with that of another, but some third cause operating on all without exception was necessary to make the different states correspond with one another. And this did not happen by means of assistance applied in each particular case (systema assistentiae), but through the unity of the idea of a cause occupied and connected with all substances, in which they necessarily receive, according to the Leibnitzian school, their existence and permanence, consequently also reciprocal correspondence, according to universal laws.\n\n4th. This philosopher’s celebrated doctrine of space and time, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, originated in the same delusion of transcendental reflection. If I attempt to represent by the mere understanding, the external relations of things, I can do so only by employing the conception of their reciprocal action, and if I wish to connect one state of the same thing with another state, I must avail myself of the notion of the order of cause and effect. And thus Leibnitz regarded space as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states. That which space and time possess proper to themselves and independent of things, he ascribed to a necessary confusion in our conceptions of them, whereby that which is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be a self-existent intuition, antecedent even to things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. But things were intelligible substances (substantiae noumena). At the same time, he made these conceptions valid of phenomena, because he did not allow to sensibility a peculiar mode of intuition, but sought all, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to sense naught but the despicable task of confusing and disarranging the representations of the former.\n\nBut even if we could frame any synthetical proposition concerning things in themselves by means of the pure understanding (which is impossible), it could not apply to phenomena, which do not represent things in themselves. In such a case I should be obliged in transcendental reflection to compare my conceptions only under the conditions of sensibility, and so space and time would not be determinations of things in themselves, but of phenomena. What things may be in themselves, I know not and need not know, because a thing is never presented to me otherwise than as a phenomenon.\n\nI must adopt the same mode of procedure with the other conceptions of reflection. Matter is substantia phaenomenon. That in it which is internal I seek to discover in all parts of space which it occupies, and in all the functions and operations it performs, and which are indeed never anything but phenomena of the external sense. I cannot therefore find anything that is absolutely, but only what is comparatively internal, and which itself consists of external relations. The absolutely internal in matter, and as it should be according to the pure understanding, is a mere chimera, for matter is not an object for the pure understanding. But the transcendental object, which is the foundation of the phenomenon which we call matter, is a mere nescio quid, the nature of which we could not understand, even though someone were found able to tell us. For we can understand nothing that does not bring with it something in intuition corresponding to the expressions employed. If, by the complaint of being unable to perceive the internal nature of things, it is meant that we do not comprehend by the pure understanding what the things which appear to us may be in themselves, it is a silly and unreasonable complaint; for those who talk thus really desire that we should be able to cognize, consequently to intuite, things without senses, and therefore wish that we possessed a faculty of cognition perfectly different from the human faculty, not merely in degree, but even as regards intuition and the mode thereof, so that thus we should not be men, but belong to a class of beings, the possibility of whose existence, much less their nature and constitution, we have no means of cognizing. By observation and analysis of phenomena we penetrate into the interior of nature, and no one can say what progress this knowledge may make in time. But those transcendental questions which pass beyond the limits of nature, we could never answer, even although all nature were laid open to us, because we have not the power of observing our own mind with any other intuition than that of our internal sense. For herein lies the mystery of the origin and source of our faculty of sensibility. Its application to an object, and the transcendental ground of this unity of subjective and objective, lie too deeply concealed for us, who cognize ourselves only through the internal sense, consequently as phenomena, to be able to discover in our existence anything but phenomena, the non-sensuous cause of which we at the same time earnestly desire to penetrate to.\n\nThe great utility of this critique of conclusions arrived at by the processes of mere reflection consists in its clear demonstration of the nullity of all conclusions respecting objects which are compared with each other in the understanding alone, while it at the same time confirms what we particularly insisted on, namely, that, although phenomena are not included as things in themselves among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only things by which our cognition can possess objective reality, that is to say, which give us intuitions to correspond with our conceptions.\n\nWhen we reflect in a purely logical manner, we do nothing more than compare conceptions in our understanding, to discover whether both have the same content, whether they are self-contradictory or not, whether anything is contained in either conception, which of the two is given, and which is merely a mode of thinking that given. But if I apply these conceptions to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without first determining whether it is an object of sensuous or intellectual intuition, certain limitations present themselves, which forbid us to pass beyond the conceptions and render all empirical use of them impossible. And thus these limitations prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not only insufficient, but, without sensuous determination and independently of empirical conditions, self-contradictory; that we must therefore make abstraction of all objects, as in logic, or, admitting them, must think them under conditions of sensuous intuition; that, consequently, the intelligible requires an altogether peculiar intuition, which we do not possess, and in the absence of which it is for us nothing; while, on the other hand phenomena cannot be objects in themselves. For, when I merely think things in general, the difference in their external relations cannot constitute a difference in the things themselves; on the contrary, the former presupposes the latter, and if the conception of one of two things is not internally different from that of the other, I am merely thinking the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of one affirmation (reality) to the other, the positive therein is really augmented, and nothing is abstracted or withdrawn from it; hence the real in things cannot be in contradiction with or opposition to itself—and so on.\n\nThe true use of the conceptions of reflection in the employment of the understanding has, as we have shown, been so misconceived by Leibnitz, one of the most acute philosophers of either ancient or modern times, that he has been misled into the construction of a baseless system of intellectual cognition, which professes to determine its objects without the intervention of the senses. For this reason, the exposition of the cause of the amphiboly of these conceptions, as the origin of these false principles, is of great utility in determining with certainty the proper limits of the understanding.\n\nIt is right to say whatever is affirmed or denied of the whole of a conception can be affirmed or denied of any part of it (dictum de omni et nullo); but it would be absurd so to alter this logical proposition as to say whatever is not contained in a general conception is likewise not contained in the particular conceptions which rank under it; for the latter are particular conceptions, for the very reason that their content is greater than that which is cogitated in the general conception. And yet the whole intellectual system of Leibnitz is based upon this false principle, and with it must necessarily fall to the ground, together with all the ambiguous principles in reference to the employment of the understanding which have thence originated.\n\nLeibnitz’s principle of the identity of indiscernibles or indistinguishables is really based on the presupposition that, if in the conception of a thing a certain distinction is not to be found, it is also not to be met with in things themselves; that, consequently, all things are completely identical (numero eadem) which are not distinguishable from each other (as to quality or quantity) in our conceptions of them. But, as in the mere conception of anything abstraction has been made of many necessary conditions of intuition, that of which abstraction has been made is rashly held to be non-existent, and nothing is attributed to the thing but what is contained in its conception.\n\nThe conception of a cubic foot of space, however I may think it, is in itself completely identical. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinct from each other from the sole fact of their being in different places (they are numero diversa); and these places are conditions of intuition, wherein the object of this conception is given, and which do not belong to the conception, but to the faculty of sensibility. In like manner, there is in the conception of a thing no contradiction when a negative is not connected with an affirmative; and merely affirmative conceptions cannot, in conjunction, produce any negation. But in sensuous intuition, wherein reality (take for example, motion) is given, we find conditions (opposite directions)—of which abstraction has been made in the conception of motion in general—which render possible a contradiction or opposition (not indeed of a logical kind)—and which from pure positives produce zero = 0. We are therefore not justified in saying that all reality is in perfect agreement and harmony, because no contradiction is discoverable among its conceptions.[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-39) According to mere conceptions, that which is internal is the substratum of all relations or external determinations. When, therefore, I abstract all conditions of intuition, and confine myself solely to the conception of a thing in general, I can make abstraction of all external relations, and there must nevertheless remain a conception of that which indicates no relation, but merely internal determinations. Now it seems to follow that in everything (substance) there is something which is absolutely internal and which antecedes all external determinations, inasmuch as it renders them possible; and that therefore this substratum is something which does not contain any external relations and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are never anything but relations, at least of their parts external to each other); and, inasmuch as we know of no other absolutely internal determinations than those of the internal sense, this substratum is not only simple, but also, analogously with our internal sense, determined through representations, that is to say, all things are properly monads, or simple beings endowed with the power of representation. Now all this would be perfectly correct, if the conception of a thing were the only necessary condition of the presentation of objects of external intuition. It is, on the contrary, manifest that a permanent phenomenon in space (impenetrable extension) can contain mere relations, and nothing that is absolutely internal, and yet be the primary substratum of all external perception. By mere conceptions I cannot think anything external, without, at the same time, thinking something internal, for the reason that conceptions of relations presuppose given things, and without these are impossible. But, as an intuition there is something (that is, space, which, with all it contains, consists of purely formal, or, indeed, real relations) which is not found in the mere conception of a thing in general, and this presents to us the substratum which could not be cognized through conceptions alone, I cannot say: because a thing cannot be represented by mere conceptions without something absolutely internal, there is also, in the things themselves which are contained under these conceptions, and in their intuition nothing external to which something absolutely internal does not serve as the foundation. For, when we have made abstraction of all the conditions of intuition, there certainly remains in the mere conception nothing but the internal in general, through which alone the external is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded upon abstraction alone, does not obtain in the case of things themselves, in so far as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having anything internal as their foundation; for they are not things in themselves, but only phenomena. What we cognize in matter is nothing but relations (what we call its internal determinations are but comparatively internal). But there are some self-subsistent and permanent, through which a determined object is given. That I, when abstraction is made of these relations, have nothing more to think, does not destroy the conception of a thing as phenomenon, nor the conception of an object in abstracto, but it does away with the possibility of an object that is determinable according to mere conceptions, that is, of a noumenon. It is certainly startling to hear that a thing consists solely of relations; but this thing is simply a phenomenon, and cannot be cogitated by means of the mere categories: it does itself consist in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. In the same way, we cannot cogitate relations of things in abstracto, if we commence with conceptions alone, in any other manner than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is itself the conception of the understanding or category of relation. But, as in this case we make abstraction of all intuition, we lose altogether the mode in which the manifold determines to each of its parts its place, that is, the form of sensibility (space); and yet this mode antecedes all empirical causality.\n\n[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-39) If any one wishes here to have recourse to the usual subterfuge, and to say, that at least realitates noumena cannot be in opposition to each other, it will be requisite for him to adduce an example of this pure and non-sensuous reality, that it may be understood whether the notion represents something or nothing. But an example cannot be found except in experience, which never presents to us anything more than phenomena; and thus the proposition means nothing more than that the conception which contains only affirmatives does not contain anything negative—a proposition nobody ever doubted.\n\nIf by intelligible objects we understand things which can be thought by means of the pure categories, without the need of the schemata of sensibility, such objects are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our conceptions of understanding is the mode of our sensuous intuition, whereby objects are given; and, if we make abstraction of the latter, the former can have no relation to an object. And even if we should suppose a different kind of intuition from our own, still our functions of thought would have no use or signification in respect thereof. But if we understand by the term, objects of a non-sensuous intuition, in respect of which our categories are not valid, and of which we can accordingly have no knowledge (neither intuition nor conception), in this merely negative sense noumena must be admitted. For this is no more than saying that our mode of intuition is not applicable to all things, but only to objects of our senses, that consequently its objective validity is limited, and that room is therefore left for another kind of intuition, and thus also for things that may be objects of it. But in this sense the conception of a noumenon is problematical, that is to say, it is the notion of a thing of which we can neither say that it is possible, nor that it is impossible, inasmuch as we do not know of any mode of intuition besides the sensuous, or of any other sort of conceptions than the categories—a mode of intuition and a kind of conception neither of which is applicable to a non-sensuous object. We are on this account incompetent to extend the sphere of our objects of thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and to assume the existence of objects of pure thought, that is, of noumena, inasmuch as these have no true positive signification. For it must be confessed of the categories that they are not of themselves sufficient for the cognition of things in themselves and, without the data of sensibility, are mere subjective forms of the unity of the understanding. Thought is certainly not a product of the senses, and in so far is not limited by them, but it does not therefore follow that it may be employed purely and without the intervention of sensibility, for it would then be without reference to an object. And we cannot call a noumenon an object of pure thought; for the representation thereof is but the problematical conception of an object for a perfectly different intuition and a perfectly different understanding from ours, both of which are consequently themselves problematical. The conception of a noumenon is therefore not the conception of an object, but merely a problematical conception inseparably connected with the limitation of our sensibility. That is to say, this conception contains the answer to the question: “Are there objects quite unconnected with, and independent of, our intuition?”—a question to which only an indeterminate answer can be given. That answer is: “Inasmuch as sensuous intuition does not apply to all things without distinction, there remains room for other and different objects.” The existence of these problematical objects is therefore not absolutely denied, in the absence of a determinate conception of them, but, as no category is valid in respect of them, neither must they be admitted as objects for our understanding.\n\nUnderstanding accordingly limits sensibility, without at the same time enlarging its own field. While, moreover, it forbids sensibility to apply its forms and modes to things in themselves and restricts it to the sphere of phenomena, it cogitates an object in itself, only, however, as a transcendental object, which is the cause of a phenomenon (consequently not itself a phenomenon), and which cannot be thought either as a quantity or as reality, or as substance (because these conceptions always require sensuous forms in which to determine an object)—an object, therefore, of which we are quite unable to say whether it can be met with in ourselves or out of us, whether it would be annihilated together with sensibility, or, if this were taken away, would continue to exist. If we wish to call this object a noumenon, because the representation of it is non-sensuous, we are at liberty to do so. But as we can apply to it none of the conceptions of our understanding, the representation is for us quite void, and is available only for the indication of the limits of our sensuous intuition, thereby leaving at the same time an empty space, which we are competent to fill by the aid neither of possible experience, nor of the pure understanding.\n\nThe critique of the pure understanding, accordingly, does not permit us to create for ourselves a new field of objects beyond those which are presented to us as phenomena, and to stray into intelligible worlds; nay, it does not even allow us to endeavour to form so much as a conception of them. The specious error which leads to this—and which is a perfectly excusable one—lies in the fact that the employment of the understanding, contrary to its proper purpose and destination, is made transcendental, and objects, that is, possible intuitions, are made to regulate themselves according to conceptions, instead of the conceptions arranging themselves according to the intuitions, on which alone their own objective validity rests. Now the reason of this again is that apperception, and with it thought, antecedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. Accordingly we think something in general and determine it on the one hand sensuously, but, on the other, distinguish the general and in abstracto represented object from this particular mode of intuiting it. In this case there remains a mode of determining the object by mere thought, which is really but a logical form without content, which, however, seems to us to be a mode of the existence of the object in itself (noumenon), without regard to intuition which is limited to our senses.\n\nBefore ending this transcendental analytic, we must make an addition, which, although in itself of no particular importance, seems to be necessary to the completeness of the system. The highest conception, with which a transcendental philosophy commonly begins, is the division into possible and impossible. But as all division presupposes a divided conception, a still higher one must exist, and this is the conception of an object in general—problematically understood and without its being decided whether it is something or nothing. As the categories are the only conceptions which apply to objects in general, the distinguishing of an object, whether it is something or nothing, must proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.\n\n1. To the categories of quantity, that is, the conceptions of all, many, and one, the conception which annihilates all, that is, the conception of none, is opposed. And thus the object of a conception, to which no intuition can be found to correspond, is = nothing. That is, it is a conception without an object (ens rationis), like noumena, which cannot be considered possible in the sphere of reality, though they must not therefore be held to be impossible—or like certain new fundamental forces in matter, the existence of which is cogitable without contradiction, though, as examples from experience are not forthcoming, they must not be regarded as possible.\n\n2. Reality is something; negation is nothing, that is, a conception of the absence of an object, as cold, a shadow (nihil privativum).\n\n3. The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of an object (as phenomenon), as pure space and pure time. These are certainly something, as forms of intuition, but are not themselves objects which are intuited (ens imaginarium).\n\n4. The object of a conception which is self-contradictory, is nothing, because the conception is nothing—is impossible, as a figure composed of two straight lines (nihil negativum).\n\nThe table of this division of the conception of nothing (the corresponding division of the conception of something does not require special description) must therefore be arranged as follows:\n\n                      NOTHING\n                        AS\n\n                        1\n                As Empty Conception\n                 without object,\n                  _ens rationis_\n           2                               3\n     Empty object of               Empty intuition\n      a conception,                without object,\n     _nihil privativum              ens imaginarium_\n                        4\n                   Empty object\n                 without conception,\n                  _nihil negativum_\n\nWe see that the ens rationis is distinguished from the nihil negativum or pure nothing by the consideration that the former must not be reckoned among possibilities, because it is a mere fiction—though not self-contradictory, while the latter is completely opposed to all possibility, inasmuch as the conception annihilates itself. Both, however, are empty conceptions. On the other hand, the nihil privativum and ens imaginarium are empty data for conceptions. If light be not given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness, and if extended objects are not perceived, we cannot represent space. Neither the negation, nor the mere form of intuition can, without something real, be an object.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","text":"### APPENDIX\n\nOf the Equivocal Nature or Amphiboly of the Conceptions of Reflection from the Confusion of the Transcendental with the Empirical use of the Understanding.\n\nReflection (reflexio) is not occupied about objects themselves, for the purpose of directly obtaining conceptions of them, but is that state of the mind in which we set ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we obtain conceptions. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to the different sources or faculties of cognition, by which alone their relation to each other can be rightly determined. The first question which occurs in considering our representations is to what faculty of cognition do they belong? To the understanding or to the senses? Many judgements are admitted to be true from mere habit or inclination; but, because reflection neither precedes nor follows, it is held to be a judgement that has its origin in the understanding. All judgements do not require examination, that is, investigation into the grounds of their truth. For, when they are immediately certain (for example: “Between two points there can be only one straight line”), no better or less mediate test of their truth can be found than that which they themselves contain and express. But all judgement, nay, all comparisons require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong. The act whereby I compare my representations with the faculty of cognition which originates them, and whereby I distinguish whether they are compared with each other as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensuous intuition, I term transcendental reflection. Now, the relations in which conceptions can stand to each other are those of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, of the internal and external, finally, of the determinable and the determining (matter and form). The proper determination of these relations rests on the question, to what faculty of cognition they subjectively belong, whether to sensibility or understanding? For, on the manner in which we solve this question depends the manner in which we must cogitate these relations.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","text":"Before constructing any objective judgement, we compare the conceptions that are to be placed in the judgement, and observe whether there exists identity (of many representations in one conception), if a general judgement is to be constructed, or difference, if a particular; whether there is agreement when affirmative; and opposition when negative judgements are to be constructed, and so on. For this reason we ought to call these conceptions, conceptions of comparison (conceptus comparationis). But as, when the question is not as to the logical form, but as to the content of conceptions, that is to say, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, and so on, the things can have a twofold relation to our faculty of cognition, to wit, a relation either to sensibility or to the understanding, and as on this relation depends their relation to each other, transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or the other faculty of cognition, can alone determine this latter relation. Thus we shall not be able to discover whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, etc., from the mere conception of the things by means of comparison (comparatio), but only by distinguishing the mode of cognition to which they belong, in other words, by means of transcendental reflection. We may, therefore, with justice say, that logical reflection is mere comparison, for in it no account is taken of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong, and they are consequently, as far as regards their origin, to be treated as homogeneous; while transcendental reflection (which applies to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of objective comparison of representations with each other, and is therefore very different from the former, because the faculties of cognition to which they belong are not even the same. Transcendental reflection is a duty which no one can neglect who wishes to establish an à priori judgement upon things. We shall now proceed to fulfil this duty, and thereby throw not a little light on the question as to the determination of the proper business of the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0003","text":"1. Identity and Difference. When an object is presented to us several times, but always with the same internal determinations (qualitas et quantitas), it, if an object of pure understanding, is always the same, not several things, but only one thing (numerica identitas); but if a phenomenon, we do not concern ourselves with comparing the conception of the thing with the conception of some other, but, although they may be in this respect perfectly the same, the difference of place at the same time is a sufficient ground for asserting the numerical difference of these objects (of sense). Thus, in the case of two drops of water, we may make complete abstraction of all internal difference (quality and quantity), and, the fact that they are intuited at the same time in different places, is sufficient to justify us in holding them to be numerically different. Leibnitz regarded phenomena as things in themselves, consequently as intelligibilia, that is, objects of pure understanding (although, on account of the confused nature of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena), and in this case his principle of the indiscernible (principium identatis indiscernibilium) is not to be impugned. But, as phenomena are objects of sensibility, and, as the understanding, in respect of them, must be employed empirically and not purely or transcendentally, plurality and numerical difference are given by space itself as the condition of external phenomena. For one part of space, although it may be perfectly similar and equal to another part, is still without it, and for this reason alone is different from the latter, which is added to it in order to make up a greater space. It follows that this must hold good of all things that are in the different parts of space at the same time, however similar and equal one may be to another.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0004","text":"2. Agreement and Opposition. When reality is represented by the pure understanding (realitas noumenon), opposition between realities is incogitable—such a relation, that is, that when these realities are connected in one subject, they annihilate the effects of each other and may be represented in the formula 3 -3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in a phenomenon (realitas phaenomenon) may very well be in mutual opposition, and, when united in the same subject, the one may completely or in part annihilate the effect or consequence of the other; as in the case of two moving forces in the same straight line drawing or impelling a point in opposite directions, or in the case of a pleasure counterbalancing a certain amount of pain.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0005","text":"3. The Internal and External. In an object of the pure understanding, only that is internal which has no relation (as regards its existence) to anything different from itself. On the other hand, the internal determinations of a substantia phaenomenon in space are nothing but relations, and it is itself nothing more than a complex of mere relations. Substance in space we are cognizant of only through forces operative in it, either drawing others towards itself (attraction), or preventing others from forcing into itself (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties that make up the conception of substance phenomenal in space, and which we term matter. On the other hand, as an object of the pure understanding, every substance must have internal determination and forces. But what other internal attributes of such an object can I think than those which my internal sense presents to me? That, to wit, which in either itself thought, or something analogous to it. Hence Leibnitz, who looked upon things as noumena, after denying them everything like external relation, and therefore also composition or combination, declared that all substances, even the component parts of matter, were simple substances with powers of representation, in one word, monads.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","text":"4. Matter and Form. These two conceptions lie at the foundation of all other reflection, so inseparably are they connected with every mode of exercising the understanding. The former denotes the determinable in general, the second its determination, both in a transcendental sense, abstraction being made of every difference in that which is given, and of the mode in which it is determined. Logicians formerly termed the universal, matter, the specific difference of this or that part of the universal, form. In a judgement one may call the given conceptions logical matter (for the judgement), the relation of these to each other (by means of the copula), the form of the judgement. In an object, the composite parts thereof (essentialia) are the matter; the mode in which they are connected in the object, the form. In respect to things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, the limitation thereof (negation) as the form, by which one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental conceptions. The understanding demands that something be given (at least in the conception), in order to be able to determine it in a certain manner. Hence, in a conception of the pure understanding, the matter precedes the form, and for this reason Leibnitz first assumed the existence of things (monads) and of an internal power of representation in them, in order to found upon this their external relation and the community their state (that is, of their representations). Hence, with him, space and time were possible—the former through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with each other, as causes and effects. And so would it really be, if the pure understanding were capable of an immediate application to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But being merely sensuous intuitions, in which we determine all objects solely as phenomena, the form of intuition (as a subjective property of sensibility) must antecede all matter (sensations), consequently space and time must antecede all phenomena and all data of experience, and rather make experience itself possible. But the intellectual philosopher could not endure that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility; an objection perfectly correct, if we assume that we intuite things as they are, although with confused representation. But as sensuous intuition is a peculiar subjective condition, which is à priori at the foundation of all perception, and the form of which is primitive, the form must be given per se, and so far from matter (or the things themselves which appear) lying at the foundation of experience (as we must conclude, if we judge by mere conceptions), the very possibility of itself presupposes, on the contrary, a given formal intuition (space and time).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0007","text":"REMARK ON THE AMPHIBOLY OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF REFLECTION.\n\nLet me be allowed to term the position which we assign to a conception either in the sensibility or in the pure understanding, the transcendental place. In this manner, the appointment of the position which must be taken by each conception according to the difference in its use, and the directions for determining this place to all conceptions according to rules, would be a transcendental topic, a doctrine which would thoroughly shield us from the surreptitious devices of the pure understanding and the delusions which thence arise, as it would always distinguish to what faculty of cognition each conception properly belonged. Every conception, every title, under which many cognitions rank together, may be called a logical place. Upon this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, of which teachers and rhetoricians could avail themselves, in order, under certain titles of thought, to observe what would best suit the matter they had to treat, and thus enable themselves to quibble and talk with fluency and an appearance of profundity.\n\nTranscendental topic, on the contrary, contains nothing more than the above-mentioned four titles of all comparison and distinction, which differ from categories in this respect, that they do not represent the object according to that which constitutes its conception (quantity, reality), but set forth merely the comparison of representations, which precedes our conceptions of things. But this comparison requires a previous reflection, that is, a determination of the place to which the representations of the things which are compared belong, whether, to wit, they are cogitated by the pure understanding, or given by sensibility.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","text":"Conceptions may be logically compared without the trouble of inquiring to what faculty their objects belong, whether as noumena, to the understanding, or as phenomena, to sensibility. If, however, we wish to employ these conceptions in respect of objects, previous transcendental reflection is necessary. Without this reflection I should make a very unsafe use of these conceptions, and construct pretended synthetical propositions which critical reason cannot acknowledge and which are based solely upon a transcendental amphiboly, that is, upon a substitution of an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","text":"For want of this doctrine of transcendental topic, and consequently deceived by the amphiboly of the conceptions of reflection, the celebrated Leibnitz constructed an intellectual system of the world, or rather, believed himself competent to cognize the internal nature of things, by comparing all objects merely with the understanding and the abstract formal conceptions of thought. Our table of the conceptions of reflection gives us the unexpected advantage of being able to exhibit the distinctive peculiarities of his system in all its parts, and at the same time of exposing the fundamental principle of this peculiar mode of thought, which rested upon naught but a misconception. He compared all things with each other merely by means of conceptions, and naturally found no other differences than those by which the understanding distinguishes its pure conceptions one from another. The conditions of sensuous intuition, which contain in themselves their own means of distinction, he did not look upon as primitive, because sensibility was to him but a confused mode of representation and not any particular source of representations. A phenomenon was for him the representation of the thing in itself, although distinguished from cognition by the understanding only in respect of the logical form—the former with its usual want of analysis containing, according to him, a certain mixture of collateral representations in its conception of a thing, which it is the duty of the understanding to separate and distinguish. In one word, Leibnitz intellectualized phenomena, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be allowed to make use of such expressions), sensualized the conceptions of the understanding, that is to say, declared them to be nothing more than empirical or abstract conceptions of reflection. Instead of seeking in the understanding and sensibility two different sources of representations, which, however, can present us with objective judgements of things only in conjunction, each of these great men recognized but one of these faculties, which, in their opinion, applied immediately to things in themselves, the other having no duty but that of confusing or arranging the representations of the former.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0010","text":"Accordingly, the objects of sense were compared by Leibnitz as things in general merely in the understanding.\n\n1st. He compares them in regard to their identity or difference—as judged by the understanding. As, therefore, he considered merely the conceptions of objects, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and left quite out of sight the transcendental locale of these conceptions—whether, that is, their object ought to be classed among phenomena, or among things in themselves, it was to be expected that he should extend the application of the principle of indiscernibles, which is valid solely of conceptions of things in general, to objects of sense (mundus phaenomenon), and that he should believe that he had thereby contributed in no small degree to extend our knowledge of nature. In truth, if I cognize in all its inner determinations a drop of water as a thing in itself, I cannot look upon one drop as different from another, if the conception of the one is completely identical with that of the other. But if it is a phenomenon in space, it has a place not merely in the understanding (among conceptions), but also in sensuous external intuition (in space), and in this case, the physical locale is a matter of indifference in regard to the internal determinations of things, and one place, B, may contain a thing which is perfectly similar and equal to another in a place, A, just as well as if the two things were in every respect different from each other. Difference of place without any other conditions, makes the plurality and distinction of objects as phenomena, not only possible in itself, but even necessary. Consequently, the above so-called law is not a law of nature. It is merely an analytical rule for the comparison of things by means of mere conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0011","text":"2nd. The principle: “Realities (as simple affirmations) never logically contradict each other,” is a proposition perfectly true respecting the relation of conceptions, but, whether as regards nature, or things in themselves (of which we have not the slightest conception), is without any the least meaning. For real opposition, in which A -B is = 0, exists everywhere, an opposition, that is, in which one reality united with another in the same subject annihilates the effects of the other—a fact which is constantly brought before our eyes by the different antagonistic actions and operations in nature, which, nevertheless, as depending on real forces, must be called realitates phaenomena. General mechanics can even present us with the empirical condition of this opposition in an à priori rule, as it directs its attention to the opposition in the direction of forces—a condition of which the transcendental conception of reality can tell us nothing. Although M. Leibnitz did not announce this proposition with precisely the pomp of a new principle, he yet employed it for the establishment of new propositions, and his followers introduced it into their Leibnitzio-Wolfian system of philosophy. According to this principle, for example, all evils are but consequences of the limited nature of created beings, that is, negations, because these are the only opposite of reality. (In the mere conception of a thing in general this is really the case, but not in things as phenomena.) In like manner, the upholders of this system deem it not only possible, but natural also, to connect and unite all reality in one being, because they acknowledge no other sort of opposition than that of contradiction (by which the conception itself of a thing is annihilated), and find themselves unable to conceive an opposition of reciprocal destruction, so to speak, in which one real cause destroys the effect of another, and the conditions of whose representation we meet with only in sensibility.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0012","text":"3rd. The Leibnitzian monadology has really no better foundation than on this philosopher’s mode of falsely representing the difference of the internal and external solely in relation to the understanding. Substances, in general, must have something inward, which is therefore free from external relations, consequently from that of composition also. The simple—that which can be represented by a unit—is therefore the foundation of that which is internal in things in themselves. The internal state of substances cannot therefore consist in place, shape, contact, or motion, determinations which are all external relations, and we can ascribe to them no other than that whereby we internally determine our faculty of sense itself, that is to say, the state of representation. Thus, then, were constructed the monads, which were to form the elements of the universe, the active force of which consists in representation, the effects of this force being thus entirely confined to themselves.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0013","text":"For the same reason, his view of the possible community of substances could not represent it but as a predetermined harmony, and by no means as a physical influence. For inasmuch as everything is occupied only internally, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could not stand in active and living connection with that of another, but some third cause operating on all without exception was necessary to make the different states correspond with one another. And this did not happen by means of assistance applied in each particular case (systema assistentiae), but through the unity of the idea of a cause occupied and connected with all substances, in which they necessarily receive, according to the Leibnitzian school, their existence and permanence, consequently also reciprocal correspondence, according to universal laws.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0014","text":"4th. This philosopher’s celebrated doctrine of space and time, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, originated in the same delusion of transcendental reflection. If I attempt to represent by the mere understanding, the external relations of things, I can do so only by employing the conception of their reciprocal action, and if I wish to connect one state of the same thing with another state, I must avail myself of the notion of the order of cause and effect. And thus Leibnitz regarded space as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states. That which space and time possess proper to themselves and independent of things, he ascribed to a necessary confusion in our conceptions of them, whereby that which is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be a self-existent intuition, antecedent even to things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. But things were intelligible substances (substantiae noumena). At the same time, he made these conceptions valid of phenomena, because he did not allow to sensibility a peculiar mode of intuition, but sought all, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to sense naught but the despicable task of confusing and disarranging the representations of the former.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","text":"But even if we could frame any synthetical proposition concerning things in themselves by means of the pure understanding (which is impossible), it could not apply to phenomena, which do not represent things in themselves. In such a case I should be obliged in transcendental reflection to compare my conceptions only under the conditions of sensibility, and so space and time would not be determinations of things in themselves, but of phenomena. What things may be in themselves, I know not and need not know, because a thing is never presented to me otherwise than as a phenomenon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","text":"I must adopt the same mode of procedure with the other conceptions of reflection. Matter is substantia phaenomenon. That in it which is internal I seek to discover in all parts of space which it occupies, and in all the functions and operations it performs, and which are indeed never anything but phenomena of the external sense. I cannot therefore find anything that is absolutely, but only what is comparatively internal, and which itself consists of external relations. The absolutely internal in matter, and as it should be according to the pure understanding, is a mere chimera, for matter is not an object for the pure understanding. But the transcendental object, which is the foundation of the phenomenon which we call matter, is a mere nescio quid, the nature of which we could not understand, even though someone were found able to tell us. For we can understand nothing that does not bring with it something in intuition corresponding to the expressions employed. If, by the complaint of being unable to perceive the internal nature of things, it is meant that we do not comprehend by the pure understanding what the things which appear to us may be in themselves, it is a silly and unreasonable complaint; for those who talk thus really desire that we should be able to cognize, consequently to intuite, things without senses, and therefore wish that we possessed a faculty of cognition perfectly different from the human faculty, not merely in degree, but even as regards intuition and the mode thereof, so that thus we should not be men, but belong to a class of beings, the possibility of whose existence, much less their nature and constitution, we have no means of cognizing. By observation and analysis of phenomena we penetrate into the interior of nature, and no one can say what progress this knowledge may make in time. But those transcendental questions which pass beyond the limits of nature, we could never answer, even although all nature were laid open to us, because we have not the power of observing our own mind with any other intuition than that of our internal sense. For herein lies the mystery of the origin and source of our faculty of sensibility. Its application to an object, and the transcendental ground of this unity of subjective and objective, lie too deeply concealed for us, who cognize ourselves only through the internal sense, consequently as phenomena, to be able to discover in our existence anything but phenomena, the non-sensuous cause of which we at the same time earnestly desire to penetrate to.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","text":"The great utility of this critique of conclusions arrived at by the processes of mere reflection consists in its clear demonstration of the nullity of all conclusions respecting objects which are compared with each other in the understanding alone, while it at the same time confirms what we particularly insisted on, namely, that, although phenomena are not included as things in themselves among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only things by which our cognition can possess objective reality, that is to say, which give us intuitions to correspond with our conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0018","text":"When we reflect in a purely logical manner, we do nothing more than compare conceptions in our understanding, to discover whether both have the same content, whether they are self-contradictory or not, whether anything is contained in either conception, which of the two is given, and which is merely a mode of thinking that given. But if I apply these conceptions to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without first determining whether it is an object of sensuous or intellectual intuition, certain limitations present themselves, which forbid us to pass beyond the conceptions and render all empirical use of them impossible. And thus these limitations prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not only insufficient, but, without sensuous determination and independently of empirical conditions, self-contradictory; that we must therefore make abstraction of all objects, as in logic, or, admitting them, must think them under conditions of sensuous intuition; that, consequently, the intelligible requires an altogether peculiar intuition, which we do not possess, and in the absence of which it is for us nothing; while, on the other hand phenomena cannot be objects in themselves. For, when I merely think things in general, the difference in their external relations cannot constitute a difference in the things themselves; on the contrary, the former presupposes the latter, and if the conception of one of two things is not internally different from that of the other, I am merely thinking the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of one affirmation (reality) to the other, the positive therein is really augmented, and nothing is abstracted or withdrawn from it; hence the real in things cannot be in contradiction with or opposition to itself—and so on.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0019","text":"The true use of the conceptions of reflection in the employment of the understanding has, as we have shown, been so misconceived by Leibnitz, one of the most acute philosophers of either ancient or modern times, that he has been misled into the construction of a baseless system of intellectual cognition, which professes to determine its objects without the intervention of the senses. For this reason, the exposition of the cause of the amphiboly of these conceptions, as the origin of these false principles, is of great utility in determining with certainty the proper limits of the understanding.\n\nIt is right to say whatever is affirmed or denied of the whole of a conception can be affirmed or denied of any part of it (dictum de omni et nullo); but it would be absurd so to alter this logical proposition as to say whatever is not contained in a general conception is likewise not contained in the particular conceptions which rank under it; for the latter are particular conceptions, for the very reason that their content is greater than that which is cogitated in the general conception. And yet the whole intellectual system of Leibnitz is based upon this false principle, and with it must necessarily fall to the ground, together with all the ambiguous principles in reference to the employment of the understanding which have thence originated.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0020","text":"Leibnitz’s principle of the identity of indiscernibles or indistinguishables is really based on the presupposition that, if in the conception of a thing a certain distinction is not to be found, it is also not to be met with in things themselves; that, consequently, all things are completely identical (numero eadem) which are not distinguishable from each other (as to quality or quantity) in our conceptions of them. But, as in the mere conception of anything abstraction has been made of many necessary conditions of intuition, that of which abstraction has been made is rashly held to be non-existent, and nothing is attributed to the thing but what is contained in its conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0021","text":"The conception of a cubic foot of space, however I may think it, is in itself completely identical. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinct from each other from the sole fact of their being in different places (they are numero diversa); and these places are conditions of intuition, wherein the object of this conception is given, and which do not belong to the conception, but to the faculty of sensibility. In like manner, there is in the conception of a thing no contradiction when a negative is not connected with an affirmative; and merely affirmative conceptions cannot, in conjunction, produce any negation. But in sensuous intuition, wherein reality (take for example, motion) is given, we find conditions (opposite directions)—of which abstraction has been made in the conception of motion in general—which render possible a contradiction or opposition (not indeed of a logical kind)—and which from pure positives produce zero = 0. We are therefore not justified in saying that all reality is in perfect agreement and harmony, because no contradiction is discoverable among its conceptions.[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-39) According to mere conceptions, that which is internal is the substratum of all relations or external determinations. When, therefore, I abstract all conditions of intuition, and confine myself solely to the conception of a thing in general, I can make abstraction of all external relations, and there must nevertheless remain a conception of that which indicates no relation, but merely internal determinations. Now it seems to follow that in everything (substance) there is something which is absolutely internal and which antecedes all external determinations, inasmuch as it renders them possible; and that therefore this substratum is something which does not contain any external relations and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are never anything but relations, at least of their parts external to each other); and, inasmuch as we know of no other absolutely internal determinations than those of the internal sense, this substratum is not only simple, but also, analogously with our internal sense, determined through representations, that is to say, all things are properly monads, or simple beings endowed with the power of representation. Now all this would be perfectly correct, if the conception of a thing were the only necessary condition of the presentation of objects of external intuition. It is, on the contrary, manifest that a permanent phenomenon in space (impenetrable extension) can contain mere relations, and nothing that is absolutely internal, and yet be the primary substratum of all external perception. By mere conceptions I cannot think anything external, without, at the same time, thinking something internal, for the reason that conceptions of relations presuppose given things, and without these are impossible. But, as an intuition there is something (that is, space, which, with all it contains, consists of purely formal, or, indeed, real relations) which is not found in the mere conception of a thing in general, and this presents to us the substratum which could not be cognized through conceptions alone, I cannot say: because a thing cannot be represented by mere conceptions without something absolutely internal, there is also, in the things themselves which are contained under these conceptions, and in their intuition nothing external to which something absolutely internal does not serve as the foundation. For, when we have made abstraction of all the conditions of intuition, there certainly remains in the mere conception nothing but the internal in general, through which alone the external is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded upon abstraction alone, does not obtain in the case of things themselves, in so far as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having anything internal as their foundation; for they are not things in themselves, but only phenomena. What we cognize in matter is nothing but relations (what we call its internal determinations are but comparatively internal). But there are some self-subsistent and permanent, through which a determined object is given. That I, when abstraction is made of these relations, have nothing more to think, does not destroy the conception of a thing as phenomenon, nor the conception of an object in abstracto, but it does away with the possibility of an object that is determinable according to mere conceptions, that is, of a noumenon. It is certainly startling to hear that a thing consists solely of relations; but this thing is simply a phenomenon, and cannot be cogitated by means of the mere categories: it does itself consist in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. In the same way, we cannot cogitate relations of things in abstracto, if we commence with conceptions alone, in any other manner than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is itself the conception of the understanding or category of relation. But, as in this case we make abstraction of all intuition, we lose altogether the mode in which the manifold determines to each of its parts its place, that is, the form of sensibility (space); and yet this mode antecedes all empirical causality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0022","text":"[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-39) If any one wishes here to have recourse to the usual subterfuge, and to say, that at least realitates noumena cannot be in opposition to each other, it will be requisite for him to adduce an example of this pure and non-sensuous reality, that it may be understood whether the notion represents something or nothing. But an example cannot be found except in experience, which never presents to us anything more than phenomena; and thus the proposition means nothing more than that the conception which contains only affirmatives does not contain anything negative—a proposition nobody ever doubted.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","text":"If by intelligible objects we understand things which can be thought by means of the pure categories, without the need of the schemata of sensibility, such objects are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our conceptions of understanding is the mode of our sensuous intuition, whereby objects are given; and, if we make abstraction of the latter, the former can have no relation to an object. And even if we should suppose a different kind of intuition from our own, still our functions of thought would have no use or signification in respect thereof. But if we understand by the term, objects of a non-sensuous intuition, in respect of which our categories are not valid, and of which we can accordingly have no knowledge (neither intuition nor conception), in this merely negative sense noumena must be admitted. For this is no more than saying that our mode of intuition is not applicable to all things, but only to objects of our senses, that consequently its objective validity is limited, and that room is therefore left for another kind of intuition, and thus also for things that may be objects of it. But in this sense the conception of a noumenon is problematical, that is to say, it is the notion of a thing of which we can neither say that it is possible, nor that it is impossible, inasmuch as we do not know of any mode of intuition besides the sensuous, or of any other sort of conceptions than the categories—a mode of intuition and a kind of conception neither of which is applicable to a non-sensuous object. We are on this account incompetent to extend the sphere of our objects of thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and to assume the existence of objects of pure thought, that is, of noumena, inasmuch as these have no true positive signification. For it must be confessed of the categories that they are not of themselves sufficient for the cognition of things in themselves and, without the data of sensibility, are mere subjective forms of the unity of the understanding. Thought is certainly not a product of the senses, and in so far is not limited by them, but it does not therefore follow that it may be employed purely and without the intervention of sensibility, for it would then be without reference to an object. And we cannot call a noumenon an object of pure thought; for the representation thereof is but the problematical conception of an object for a perfectly different intuition and a perfectly different understanding from ours, both of which are consequently themselves problematical. The conception of a noumenon is therefore not the conception of an object, but merely a problematical conception inseparably connected with the limitation of our sensibility. That is to say, this conception contains the answer to the question: “Are there objects quite unconnected with, and independent of, our intuition?”—a question to which only an indeterminate answer can be given. That answer is: “Inasmuch as sensuous intuition does not apply to all things without distinction, there remains room for other and different objects.” The existence of these problematical objects is therefore not absolutely denied, in the absence of a determinate conception of them, but, as no category is valid in respect of them, neither must they be admitted as objects for our understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","text":"Understanding accordingly limits sensibility, without at the same time enlarging its own field. While, moreover, it forbids sensibility to apply its forms and modes to things in themselves and restricts it to the sphere of phenomena, it cogitates an object in itself, only, however, as a transcendental object, which is the cause of a phenomenon (consequently not itself a phenomenon), and which cannot be thought either as a quantity or as reality, or as substance (because these conceptions always require sensuous forms in which to determine an object)—an object, therefore, of which we are quite unable to say whether it can be met with in ourselves or out of us, whether it would be annihilated together with sensibility, or, if this were taken away, would continue to exist. If we wish to call this object a noumenon, because the representation of it is non-sensuous, we are at liberty to do so. But as we can apply to it none of the conceptions of our understanding, the representation is for us quite void, and is available only for the indication of the limits of our sensuous intuition, thereby leaving at the same time an empty space, which we are competent to fill by the aid neither of possible experience, nor of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0025","text":"The critique of the pure understanding, accordingly, does not permit us to create for ourselves a new field of objects beyond those which are presented to us as phenomena, and to stray into intelligible worlds; nay, it does not even allow us to endeavour to form so much as a conception of them. The specious error which leads to this—and which is a perfectly excusable one—lies in the fact that the employment of the understanding, contrary to its proper purpose and destination, is made transcendental, and objects, that is, possible intuitions, are made to regulate themselves according to conceptions, instead of the conceptions arranging themselves according to the intuitions, on which alone their own objective validity rests. Now the reason of this again is that apperception, and with it thought, antecedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. Accordingly we think something in general and determine it on the one hand sensuously, but, on the other, distinguish the general and in abstracto represented object from this particular mode of intuiting it. In this case there remains a mode of determining the object by mere thought, which is really but a logical form without content, which, however, seems to us to be a mode of the existence of the object in itself (noumenon), without regard to intuition which is limited to our senses.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","text":"Before ending this transcendental analytic, we must make an addition, which, although in itself of no particular importance, seems to be necessary to the completeness of the system. The highest conception, with which a transcendental philosophy commonly begins, is the division into possible and impossible. But as all division presupposes a divided conception, a still higher one must exist, and this is the conception of an object in general—problematically understood and without its being decided whether it is something or nothing. As the categories are the only conceptions which apply to objects in general, the distinguishing of an object, whether it is something or nothing, must proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.\n\n1. To the categories of quantity, that is, the conceptions of all, many, and one, the conception which annihilates all, that is, the conception of none, is opposed. And thus the object of a conception, to which no intuition can be found to correspond, is = nothing. That is, it is a conception without an object (ens rationis), like noumena, which cannot be considered possible in the sphere of reality, though they must not therefore be held to be impossible—or like certain new fundamental forces in matter, the existence of which is cogitable without contradiction, though, as examples from experience are not forthcoming, they must not be regarded as possible.\n\n2. Reality is something; negation is nothing, that is, a conception of the absence of an object, as cold, a shadow (nihil privativum).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","text":"3. The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of an object (as phenomenon), as pure space and pure time. These are certainly something, as forms of intuition, but are not themselves objects which are intuited (ens imaginarium).\n\n4. The object of a conception which is self-contradictory, is nothing, because the conception is nothing—is impossible, as a figure composed of two straight lines (nihil negativum).\n\nThe table of this division of the conception of nothing (the corresponding division of the conception of something does not require special description) must therefore be arranged as follows:\n\n                      NOTHING\n                        AS\n\n                        1\n                As Empty Conception\n                 without object,\n                  _ens rationis_\n           2                               3\n     Empty object of               Empty intuition\n      a conception,                without object,\n     _nihil privativum              ens imaginarium_\n                        4\n                   Empty object\n                 without conception,\n                  _nihil negativum_","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0028","text":"We see that the ens rationis is distinguished from the nihil negativum or pure nothing by the consideration that the former must not be reckoned among possibilities, because it is a mere fiction—though not self-contradictory, while the latter is completely opposed to all possibility, inasmuch as the conception annihilates itself. Both, however, are empty conceptions. On the other hand, the nihil privativum and ens imaginarium are empty data for conceptions. If light be not given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness, and if extended objects are not perceived, we cannot represent space. Neither the negation, nor the mere form of intuition can, without something real, be an object.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"583e55e22bb95e4780d012a79dda033a06aadc14b45c8b6347ae0a337e8662d4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"APPENDIX","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE","title":"Experience","order":50,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_EXPE","text":"# Experience\n\n3. ANALOGIES OF EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of Perceptions.\n\nPROOF.\n\nExperience is an empirical cognition; that is to say, a cognition which determines an object by means of perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, a synthesis which is not itself contained in perception, but which contains the synthetical unity of the manifold of perception in a consciousness; and this unity constitutes the essential of our cognition of objects of the senses, that is, of experience (not merely of intuition or sensation). Now in experience our perceptions come together contingently, so that no character of necessity in their connection appears, or can appear from the perceptions themselves, because apprehension is only a placing together of the manifold of empirical intuition, and no representation of a necessity in the connected existence of the phenomena which apprehension brings together, is to be discovered therein. But as experience is a cognition of objects by means of perceptions, it follows that the relation of the existence of the existence of the manifold must be represented in experience not as it is put together in time, but as it is objectively in time. And as time itself cannot be perceived, the determination of the existence of objects in time can only take place by means of their connection in time in general, consequently only by means of à priori connecting conceptions. Now as these conceptions always possess the character of necessity, experience is possible only by means of a representation of the necessary connection of perception.\n\nThe three modi of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Accordingly, there are three rules of all relations of time in phenomena, according to which the existence of every phenomenon is determined in respect of the unity of all time, and these antecede all experience and render it possible.\n\nThe general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in relation to all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time, consequently, as this unity lies à priori at the foundation of all mental operations, the principle rests on the synthetical unity of all phenomena according to their relation in time. For the original apperception relates to our internal sense (the complex of all representations), and indeed relates à priori to its form, that is to say, the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. Now this manifold must be combined in original apperception according to relations of time—a necessity imposed by the à priori transcendental unity of apperception, to which is subjected all that can belong to my (i.e., my own) cognition, and therefore all that can become an object for me. This synthetical and à priori determined unity in relation of perceptions in time is therefore the rule: “All empirical determinations of time must be subject to rules of the general determination of time”; and the analogies of experience, of which we are now about to treat, must be rules of this nature.\n\nThese principles have this peculiarity, that they do not concern phenomena, and the synthesis of the empirical intuition thereof, but merely the existence of phenomena and their relation to each other in regard to this existence. Now the mode in which we apprehend a thing in a phenomenon can be determined à priori in such a manner that the rule of its synthesis can give, that is to say, can produce this à priori intuition in every empirical example. But the existence of phenomena cannot be known à priori, and although we could arrive by this path at a conclusion of the fact of some existence, we could not cognize that existence determinately, that is to say, we should be incapable of anticipating in what respect the empirical intuition of it would be distinguishable from that of others.\n\nThe two principles above mentioned, which I called mathematical, in consideration of the fact of their authorizing the application of mathematic phenomena, relate to these phenomena only in regard to their possibility, and instruct us how phenomena, as far as regards their intuition or the real in their perception, can be generated according to the rules of a mathematical synthesis. Consequently, numerical quantities, and with them the determination of a phenomenon as a quantity, can be employed in the one case as well as in the other. Thus, for example, out of 200,000 illuminations by the moon, I might compose and give à priori, that is construct, the degree of our sensations of the sun-light.[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-30) We may therefore entitle these two principles constitutive.\n\n[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-30) Kant’s meaning is: The two principles enunciated under the heads of “Axioms of Intuition,” and “Anticipations of Perception,” authorize the application to phenomena of determinations of size and number, that is of mathematic. For example, I may compute the light of the sun, and say that its quantity is a certain number of times greater than that of the moon. In the same way, heat is measured by the comparison of its different effects on water, &c., and on mercury in a thermometer.—Tr\n\nThe case is very different with those principles whose province it is to subject the existence of phenomena to rules à priori. For as existence does not admit of being constructed, it is clear that they must only concern the relations of existence and be merely regulative principles. In this case, therefore, neither axioms nor anticipations are to be thought of. Thus, if a perception is given us, in a certain relation of time to other (although undetermined) perceptions, we cannot then say à priori, what and how great (in quantity) the other perception necessarily connected with the former is, but only how it is connected, quoad its existence, in this given modus of time. Analogies in philosophy mean something very different from that which they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulae, which enounce the equality of two relations of quantity, and are always constitutive, so that if two terms of the proportion are given, the third is also given, that is, can be constructed by the aid of these formulae. But in philosophy, analogy is not the equality of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. In this case, from three given terms, I can give à priori and cognize the relation to a fourth member, but not this fourth term itself, although I certainly possess a rule to guide me in the search for this fourth term in experience, and a mark to assist me in discovering it. An analogy of experience is therefore only a rule according to which unity of experience must arise out of perceptions in respect to objects (phenomena) not as a constitutive, but merely as a regulative principle. The same holds good also of the postulates of empirical thought in general, which relate to the synthesis of mere intuition (which concerns the form of phenomena), the synthesis of perception (which concerns the matter of phenomena), and the synthesis of experience (which concerns the relation of these perceptions). For they are only regulative principles, and clearly distinguishable from the mathematical, which are constitutive, not indeed in regard to the certainty which both possess à priori, but in the mode of evidence thereof, consequently also in the manner of demonstration.\n\nBut what has been observed of all synthetical propositions, and must be particularly remarked in this place, is this, that these analogies possess significance and validity, not as principles of the transcendental, but only as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, and their truth can therefore be proved only as such, and that consequently the phenomena must not be subjoined directly under the categories, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which those principles must be applied were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to cognize aught concerning them synthetically à priori. But they are nothing but phenomena; a complete knowledge of which—a knowledge to which all principles à priori must at last relate—is the only possible experience. It follows that these principles can have nothing else for their aim than the conditions of the empirical cognition in the unity of synthesis of phenomena. But this synthesis is cogitated only in the schema of the pure conception of the understanding, of whose unity, as that of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function unrestricted by any sensuous condition. These principles will therefore authorize us to connect phenomena according to an analogy, with the logical and universal unity of conceptions, and consequently to employ the categories in the principles themselves; but in the application of them to experience, we shall use only their schemata, as the key to their proper application, instead of the categories, or rather the latter as restricting conditions, under the title of “formulae” of the former.\n\nA. FIRST ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Permanence of Substance.\n\nIn all changes of phenomena, substance is permanent, and the quantum thereof in nature is neither increased nor diminished.\n\nPROOF.\n\nAll phenomena exist in time, wherein alone as substratum, that is, as the permanent form of the internal intuition, coexistence and succession can be represented. Consequently time, in which all changes of phenomena must be cogitated, remains and changes not, because it is that in which succession and coexistence can be represented only as determinations thereof. Now, time in itself cannot be an object of perception. It follows that in objects of perception, that is, in phenomena, there must be found a substratum which represents time in general, and in which all change or coexistence can be perceived by means of the relation of phenomena to it. But the substratum of all reality, that is, of all that pertains to the existence of things, is substance; all that pertains to existence can be cogitated only as a determination of substance. Consequently, the permanent, in relation to which alone can all relations of time in phenomena be determined, is substance in the world of phenomena, that is, the real in phenomena, that which, as the substratum of all change, remains ever the same. Accordingly, as this cannot change in existence, its quantity in nature can neither be increased nor diminished.\n\nOur apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon is always successive, is Consequently always changing. By it alone we could, therefore, never determine whether this manifold, as an object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless it had for a foundation something fixed and permanent, of the existence of which all succession and coexistence are nothing but so many modes (modi of time). Only in the permanent, then, are relations of time possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time); that is to say, the permanent is the substratum of our empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all determination of time is possible. Permanence is, in fact, just another expression for time, as the abiding correlate of all existence of phenomena, and of all change, and of all coexistence. For change does not affect time itself, but only the phenomena in time (just as coexistence cannot be regarded as a modus of time itself, seeing that in time no parts are coexistent, but all successive). If we were to attribute succession to time itself, we should be obliged to cogitate another time, in which this succession would be possible. It is only by means of the permanent that existence in different parts of the successive series of time receives a quantity, which we entitle duration. For in mere succession, existence is perpetually vanishing and recommencing, and therefore never has even the least quantity. Without the permanent, then, no relation in time is possible. Now, time in itself is not an object of perception; consequently the permanent in phenomena must be regarded as the substratum of all determination of time, and consequently also as the condition of the possibility of all synthetical unity of perceptions, that is, of experience; and all existence and all change in time can only be regarded as a mode in the existence of that which abides unchangeably. Therefore, in all phenomena, the permanent is the object in itself, that is, the substance (phenomenon); but all that changes or can change belongs only to the mode of the existence of this substance or substances, consequently to its determinations.\n\nI find that in all ages not only the philosopher, but even the common understanding, has preposited this permanence as a substratum of all change in phenomena; indeed, I am compelled to believe that they will always accept this as an indubitable fact. Only the philosopher expresses himself in a more precise and definite manner, when he says: “In all changes in the world, the substance remains, and the accidents alone are changeable.” But of this decidedly synthetical proposition, I nowhere meet with even an attempt at proof; nay, it very rarely has the good fortune to stand, as it deserves to do, at the head of the pure and entirely à priori laws of nature. In truth, the statement that substance is permanent, is tautological. For this very permanence is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to the phenomenon; and we should have been obliged to prove that in all phenomena there is something permanent, of the existence of which the changeable is nothing but a determination. But because a proof of this nature cannot be dogmatical, that is, cannot be drawn from conceptions, inasmuch as it concerns a synthetical proposition à priori, and as philosophers never reflected that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and therefore cannot be proved except by means of a deduction of the possibility of experience, it is no wonder that while it has served as the foundation of all experience (for we feel the need of it in empirical cognition), it has never been supported by proof.\n\nA philosopher was asked: “What is the weight of smoke?” He answered: “Subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the remaining ashes, and you will have the weight of the smoke.” Thus he presumed it to be incontrovertible that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but that only the form of it undergoes a change. In like manner was the saying: “From nothing comes nothing,” only another inference from the principle or permanence, or rather of the ever-abiding existence of the true subject in phenomena. For if that in the phenomenon which we call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, it follows that all existence in past as well as in future time, must be determinable by means of it alone. Hence we are entitled to apply the term substance to a phenomenon, only because we suppose its existence in all time, a notion which the word permanence does not fully express, as it seems rather to be referable to future time. However, the internal necessity perpetually to be, is inseparably connected with the necessity always to have been, and so the expression may stand as it is. “Gigni de nihilo nihil; in nihilum nil posse reverti,”[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-31) are two propositions which the ancients never parted, and which people nowadays sometimes mistakenly disjoin, because they imagine that the propositions apply to objects as things in themselves, and that the former might be inimical to the dependence (even in respect of its substance also) of the world upon a supreme cause. But this apprehension is entirely needless, for the question in this case is only of phenomena in the sphere of experience, the unity of which never could be possible, if we admitted the possibility that new things (in respect of their substance) should arise. For in that case, we should lose altogether that which alone can represent the unity of time, to wit, the identity of the substratum, as that through which alone all change possesses complete and thorough unity. This permanence is, however, nothing but the manner in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things in the phenomenal world.\n\n[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-31) Persius, Satirae, iii.83-84.\n\nThe determinations of a substance, which are only particular modes of its existence, are called accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations, which express the non-existence of something in the substance). Now, if to this real in the substance we ascribe a particular existence (for example, to motion as an accident of matter), this existence is called inherence, in contradistinction to the existence of substance, which we call subsistence. But hence arise many misconceptions, and it would be a more accurate and just mode of expression to designate the accident only as the mode in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Meanwhile, by reason of the conditions of the logical exercise of our understanding, it is impossible to avoid separating, as it were, that which in the existence of a substance is subject to change, whilst the substance remains, and regarding it in relation to that which is properly permanent and radical. On this account, this category of substance stands under the title of relation, rather because it is the condition thereof than because it contains in itself any relation.\n\nNow, upon this notion of permanence rests the proper notion of the conception change. Origin and extinction are not changes of that which originates or becomes extinct. Change is but a mode of existence, which follows on another mode of existence of the same object; hence all that changes is permanent, and only the condition thereof changes. Now since this mutation affects only determinations, which can have a beginning or an end, we may say, employing an expression which seems somewhat paradoxical: “Only the permanent (substance) is subject to change; the mutable suffers no change, but rather alternation, that is, when certain determinations cease, others begin.”\n\nChange, when, cannot be perceived by us except in substances, and origin or extinction in an absolute sense, that does not concern merely a determination of the permanent, cannot be a possible perception, for it is this very notion of the permanent which renders possible the representation of a transition from one state into another, and from non-being to being, which, consequently, can be empirically cognized only as alternating determinations of that which is permanent. Grant that a thing absolutely begins to be; we must then have a point of time in which it was not. But how and by what can we fix and determine this point of time, unless by that which already exists? For a void time—preceding—is not an object of perception; but if we connect this beginning with objects which existed previously, and which continue to exist till the object in question in question begins to be, then the latter can only be a determination of the former as the permanent. The same holds good of the notion of extinction, for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time, in which a phenomenon no longer exists.\n\nSubstances (in the world of phenomena) are the substratum of all determinations of time. The beginning of some, and the ceasing to be of other substances, would utterly do away with the only condition of the empirical unity of time; and in that case phenomena would relate to two different times, in which, side by side, existence would pass; which is absurd. For there is only one time in which all different times must be placed, not as coexistent, but as successive.\n\nAccordingly, permanence is a necessary condition under which alone phenomena, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. But as regards the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of phenomena, we shall find sufficient opportunity to speak in the sequel.\n\nB. SECOND ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Succession of Time According to the Law of Causality. All changes take place according to the law of the connection of Cause and Effect.\n\nPROOF.\n\n(That all phenomena in the succession of time are only changes, that is, a successive being and non-being of the determinations of substance, which is permanent; consequently that a being of substance itself which follows on the non-being thereof, or a non-being of substance which follows on the being thereof, in other words, that the origin or extinction of substance itself, is impossible—all this has been fully established in treating of the foregoing principle. This principle might have been expressed as follows: “All alteration (succession) of phenomena is merely change”; for the changes of substance are not origin or extinction, because the conception of change presupposes the same subject as existing with two opposite determinations, and consequently as permanent. After this premonition, we shall proceed to the proof.)\n\nI perceive that phenomena succeed one another, that is to say, a state of things exists at one time, the opposite of which existed in a former state. In this case, then, I really connect together two perceptions in time. Now connection is not an operation of mere sense and intuition, but is the product of a synthetical faculty of imagination, which determines the internal sense in respect of a relation of time. But imagination can connect these two states in two ways, so that either the one or the other may antecede in time; for time in itself cannot be an object of perception, and what in an object precedes and what follows cannot be empirically determined in relation to it. I am only conscious, then, that my imagination places one state before and the other after; not that the one state antecedes the other in the object. In other words, the objective relation of the successive phenomena remains quite undetermined by means of mere perception. Now in order that this relation may be cognized as determined, the relation between the two states must be so cogitated that it is thereby determined as necessary, which of them must be placed before and which after, and not conversely. But the conception which carries with it a necessity of synthetical unity, can be none other than a pure conception of the understanding which does not lie in mere perception; and in this case it is the conception of “the relation of cause and effect,” the former of which determines the latter in time, as its necessary consequence, and not as something which might possibly antecede (or which might in some cases not be perceived to follow). It follows that it is only because we subject the sequence of phenomena, and consequently all change, to the law of causality, that experience itself, that is, empirical cognition of phenomena, becomes possible; and consequently, that phenomena themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only by virtue of this law.\n\nOur apprehension of the manifold of phenomena is always successive. The representations of parts succeed one another. Whether they succeed one another in the object also, is a second point for reflection, which was not contained in the former. Now we may certainly give the name of object to everything, even to every representation, so far as we are conscious thereof; but what this word may mean in the case of phenomena, not merely in so far as they (as representations) are objects, but only in so far as they indicate an object, is a question requiring deeper consideration. In so far as they, regarded merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not to be distinguished from apprehension, that is, reception into the synthesis of imagination, and we must therefore say: “The manifold of phenomena is always produced successively in the mind.” If phenomena were things in themselves, no man would be able to conjecture from the succession of our representations how this manifold is connected in the object; for we have to do only with our representations. How things may be in themselves, without regard to the representations through which they affect us, is utterly beyond the sphere of our cognition. Now although phenomena are not things in themselves, and are nevertheless the only thing given to us to be cognized, it is my duty to show what sort of connection in time belongs to the manifold in phenomena themselves, while the representation of this manifold in apprehension is always successive. For example, the apprehension of the manifold in the phenomenon of a house which stands before me, is successive. Now comes the question whether the manifold of this house is in itself successive—which no one will be at all willing to grant. But, so soon as I raise my conception of an object to the transcendental signification thereof, I find that the house is not a thing in itself, but only a phenomenon, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which remains utterly unknown. What then am I to understand by the question: “How can the manifold be connected in the phenomenon itself—not considered as a thing in itself, but merely as a phenomenon?” Here that which lies in my successive apprehension is regarded as representation, whilst the phenomenon which is given me, notwithstanding that it is nothing more than a complex of these representations, is regarded as the object thereof, with which my conception, drawn from the representations of apprehension, must harmonize. It is very soon seen that, as accordance of the cognition with its object constitutes truth, the question now before us can only relate to the formal conditions of empirical truth; and that the phenomenon, in opposition to the representations of apprehension, can only be distinguished therefrom as the object of them, if it is subject to a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension, and which renders necessary a mode of connection of the manifold. That in the phenomenon which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension, is the object.\n\nLet us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is to say, that something or some state exists which before was not, cannot be empirically perceived, unless a phenomenon precedes, which does not contain in itself this state. For a reality which should follow upon a void time, in other words, a beginning, which no state of things precedes, can just as little be apprehended as the void time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another perception. But as this is the case with all synthesis of apprehension, as I have shown above in the example of a house, my apprehension of an event is not yet sufficiently distinguished from other apprehensions. But I remark also that if in a phenomenon which contains an occurrence, I call the antecedent state of my perception, A, and the following state, B, the perception B can only follow A in apprehension, and the perception A cannot follow B, but only precede it. For example, I see a ship float down the stream of a river. My perception of its place lower down follows upon my perception of its place higher up the course of the river, and it is impossible that, in the apprehension of this phenomenon, the vessel should be perceived first below and afterwards higher up the stream. Here, therefore, the order in the sequence of perceptions in apprehension is determined; and by this order apprehension is regulated. In the former example, my perceptions in the apprehension of a house might begin at the roof and end at the foundation, or vice versa; or I might apprehend the manifold in this empirical intuition, by going from left to right, and from right to left. Accordingly, in the series of these perceptions, there was no determined order, which necessitated my beginning at a certain point, in order empirically to connect the manifold. But this rule is always to be met with in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions in the apprehension of such a phenomenon necessary.\n\nI must, therefore, in the present case, deduce the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of phenomena, for otherwise the former is quite undetermined, and one phenomenon is not distinguishable from another. The former alone proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in an object, for it is quite arbitrary. The latter must consist in the order of the manifold in a phenomenon, according to which order the apprehension of one thing (that which happens) follows that of another thing (which precedes), in conformity with a rule. In this way alone can I be authorized to say of the phenomenon itself, and not merely of my own apprehension, that a certain order or sequence is to be found therein. That is, in other words, I cannot arrange my apprehension otherwise than in this order.\n\nIn conformity with this rule, then, it is necessary that in that which antecedes an event there be found the condition of a rule, according to which in this event follows always and necessarily; but I cannot reverse this and go back from the event, and determine (by apprehension) that which antecedes it. For no phenomenon goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding point, although it does certainly relate to a preceding point of time; from a given time, on the other hand, there is always a necessary progression to the determined succeeding time. Therefore, because there certainly is something that follows, I must of necessity connect it with something else, which antecedes, and upon which it follows, in conformity with a rule, that is necessarily, so that the event, as conditioned, affords certain indication of a condition, and this condition determines the event.\n\nLet us suppose that nothing precedes an event, upon which this event must follow in conformity with a rule. All sequence of perception would then exist only in apprehension, that is to say, would be merely subjective, and it could not thereby be objectively determined what thing ought to precede, and what ought to follow in perception. In such a case, we should have nothing but a play of representations, which would possess no application to any object. That is to say, it would not be possible through perception to distinguish one phenomenon from another, as regards relations of time; because the succession in the act of apprehension would always be of the same sort, and therefore there would be nothing in the phenomenon to determine the succession, and to render a certain sequence objectively necessary. And, in this case, I cannot say that two states in a phenomenon follow one upon the other, but only that one apprehension follows upon another. But this is merely subjective, and does not determine an object, and consequently cannot be held to be cognition of an object—not even in the phenomenal world.\n\nAccordingly, when we know in experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes, whereupon it follows in conformity with a rule. For otherwise I could not say of the object that it follows; because the mere succession in my apprehension, if it be not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not authorize succession in the object. Only, therefore, in reference to a rule, according to which phenomena are determined in their sequence, that is, as they happen, by the preceding state, can I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and it is only under this presupposition that even the experience of an event is possible.\n\nNo doubt it appears as if this were in thorough contradiction to all the notions which people have hitherto entertained in regard to the procedure of the human understanding. According to these opinions, it is by means of the perception and comparison of similar consequences following upon certain antecedent phenomena that the understanding is led to the discovery of a rule, according to which certain events always follow certain phenomena, and it is only by this process that we attain to the conception of cause. Upon such a basis, it is clear that this conception must be merely empirical, and the rule which it furnishes us with—“Everything that happens must have a cause”—would be just as contingent as experience itself. The universality and necessity of the rule or law would be perfectly spurious attributes of it. Indeed, it could not possess universal validity, inasmuch as it would not in this case be à priori, but founded on deduction. But the same is the case with this law as with other pure à priori representations (e.g., space and time), which we can draw in perfect clearness and completeness from experience, only because we had already placed them therein, and by that means, and by that alone, had rendered experience possible. Indeed, the logical clearness of this representation of a rule, determining the series of events, is possible only when we have made use thereof in experience. Nevertheless, the recognition of this rule, as a condition of the synthetical unity of phenomena in time, was the ground of experience itself and consequently preceded it à priori.\n\nIt is now our duty to show by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute to an object the notion of succession or effect (of an event—that is, the happening of something that did not exist before), and distinguish it from the subjective succession of apprehension, unless when a rule lies at the foundation, which compels us to observe this order of perception in preference to any other, and that, indeed, it is this necessity which first renders possible the representation of a succession in the object.\n\nWe have representations within us, of which also we can be conscious. But, however widely extended, however accurate and thoroughgoing this consciousness may be, these representations are still nothing more than representations, that is, internal determinations of the mind in this or that relation of time. Now how happens it that to these representations we should set an object, or that, in addition to their subjective reality, as modifications, we should still further attribute to them a certain unknown objective reality? It is clear that objective significancy cannot consist in a relation to another representation (of that which we desire to term object), for in that case the question again arises: “How does this other representation go out of itself, and obtain objective significancy over and above the subjective, which is proper to it, as a determination of a state of mind?” If we try to discover what sort of new property the relation to an object gives to our subjective representations, and what new importance they thereby receive, we shall find that this relation has no other effect than that of rendering necessary the connection of our representations in a certain manner, and of subjecting them to a rule; and that conversely, it is only because a certain order is necessary in the relations of time of our representations, that objective significancy is ascribed to them.\n\nIn the synthesis of phenomena, the manifold of our representations is always successive. Now hereby is not represented an object, for by means of this succession, which is common to all apprehension, no one thing is distinguished from another. But so soon as I perceive or assume that in this succession there is a relation to a state antecedent, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, so soon do I represent something as an event, or as a thing that happens; in other words, I cognize an object to which I must assign a certain determinate position in time, which cannot be altered, because of the preceding state in the object. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, there is contained in this representation, in the first place, the fact, that something antecedes; because, it is only in relation to this that the phenomenon obtains its proper relation of time, in other words, exists after an antecedent time, in which it did not exist. But it can receive its determined place in time only by the presupposition that something existed in the foregoing state, upon which it follows inevitably and always, that is, in conformity with a rule. From all this it is evident that, in the first place, I cannot reverse the order of succession, and make that which happens precede that upon which it follows; and that, in the second place, if the antecedent state be posited, a certain determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Hence it follows that there exists a certain order in our representations, whereby the present gives a sure indication of some previously existing state, as a correlate, though still undetermined, of the existing event which is given—a correlate which itself relates to the event as its consequence, conditions it, and connects it necessarily with itself in the series of time.\n\nIf then it be admitted as a necessary law of sensibility, and consequently a formal condition of all perception, that the preceding necessarily determines the succeeding time (inasmuch as I cannot arrive at the succeeding except through the preceding), it must likewise be an indispensable law of empirical representation of the series of time that the phenomena of the past determine all phenomena in the succeeding time, and that the latter, as events, cannot take place, except in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is to say, establish it according to a rule. For it is of course only in phenomena that we can empirically cognize this continuity in the connection of times.\n\nFor all experience and for the possibility of experience, understanding is indispensable, and the first step which it takes in this sphere is not to render the representation of objects clear, but to render the representation of an object in general, possible. It does this by applying the order of time to phenomena, and their existence. In other words, it assigns to each phenomenon, as a consequence, a place in relation to preceding phenomena, determined à priori in time, without which it could not harmonize with time itself, which determines a place à priori to all its parts. This determination of place cannot be derived from the relation of phenomena to absolute time (for it is not an object of perception); but, on the contrary, phenomena must reciprocally determine the places in time of one another, and render these necessary in the order of time. In other words, whatever follows or happens, must follow in conformity with a universal rule upon that which was contained in the foregoing state. Hence arises a series of phenomena, which, by means of the understanding, produces and renders necessary exactly the same order and continuous connection in the series of our possible perceptions, as is found à priori in the form of internal intuition (time), in which all our perceptions must have place.\n\nThat something happens, then, is a perception which belongs to a possible experience, which becomes real only because I look upon the phenomenon as determined in regard to its place in time, consequently as an object, which can always be found by means of a rule in the connected series of my perceptions. But this rule of the determination of a thing according to succession in time is as follows: “In what precedes may be found the condition, under which an event always (that is, necessarily) follows.” From all this it is obvious that the principle of cause and effect is the principle of possible experience, that is, of objective cognition of phenomena, in regard to their relations in the succession of time.\n\nThe proof of this fundamental proposition rests entirely on the following momenta of argument. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, a synthesis which is always successive, that is, in which the representations therein always follow one another. But the order of succession in imagination is not determined, and the series of successive representations may be taken retrogressively as well as progressively. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given phenomenon), then the order is determined in the object, or to speak more accurately, there is therein an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, and according to which something necessarily precedes, and when this is posited, something else necessarily follows. If, then, my perception is to contain the cognition of an event, that is, of something which really happens, it must be an empirical judgement, wherein we think that the succession is determined; that is, it presupposes another phenomenon, upon which this event follows necessarily, or in conformity with a rule. If, on the contrary, when I posited the antecedent, the event did not necessarily follow, I should be obliged to consider it merely as a subjective play of my imagination, and if in this I represented to myself anything as objective, I must look upon it as a mere dream. Thus, the relation of phenomena (as possible perceptions), according to which that which happens is, as to its existence, necessarily determined in time by something which antecedes, in conformity with a rule—in other words, the relation of cause and effect—is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgements in regard to the sequence of perceptions, consequently of their empirical truth, and therefore of experience. The principle of the relation of causality in the succession of phenomena is therefore valid for all objects of experience, because it is itself the ground of the possibility of experience.\n\nHere, however, a difficulty arises, which must be resolved. The principle of the connection of causality among phenomena is limited in our formula to the succession thereof, although in practice we find that the principle applies also when the phenomena exist together in the same time, and that cause and effect may be simultaneous. For example, there is heat in a room, which does not exist in the open air. I look about for the cause, and find it to be the fire, Now the fire as the cause is simultaneous with its effect, the heat of the room. In this case, then, there is no succession as regards time, between cause and effect, but they are simultaneous; and still the law holds good. The greater part of operating causes in nature are simultaneous with their effects, and the succession in time of the latter is produced only because the cause cannot achieve the total of its effect in one moment. But at the moment when the effect first arises, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause, because, if the cause had but a moment before ceased to be, the effect could not have arisen. Here it must be specially remembered that we must consider the order of time and not the lapse thereof. The relation remains, even though no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may entirely vanish, and the cause and effect be thus simultaneous, but the relation of the one to the other remains always determinable according to time. If, for example, I consider a leaden ball, which lies upon a cushion and makes a hollow in it, as a cause, then it is simultaneous with the effect. But I distinguish the two through the relation of time of the dynamical connection of both. For if I lay the ball upon the cushion, then the hollow follows upon the before smooth surface; but supposing the cushion has, from some cause or another, a hollow, there does not thereupon follow a leaden ball.\n\nThus, the law of succession of time is in all instances the only empirical criterion of effect in relation to the causality of the antecedent cause. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two phenomena are contemporaneous. For, as soon as I draw some water with the glass from a larger vessel, an effect follows thereupon, namely, the change of the horizontal state which the water had in the large vessel into a concave, which it assumes in the glass.\n\nThis conception of causality leads us to the conception of action; that of action, to the conception of force; and through it, to the conception of substance. As I do not wish this critical essay, the sole purpose of which is to treat of the sources of our synthetical cognition à priori, to be crowded with analyses which merely explain, but do not enlarge the sphere of our conceptions, I reserve the detailed explanation of the above conceptions for a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis, indeed, executed with great particularity, may already be found in well-known works on this subject. But I cannot at present refrain from making a few remarks on the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as it seems to be more evident and more easily recognized through the conception of action than through that of the permanence of a phenomenon.\n\nWhere action (consequently activity and force) exists, substance also must exist, and in it alone must be sought the seat of that fruitful source of phenomena. Very well. But if we are called upon to explain what we mean by substance, and wish to avoid the vice of reasoning in a circle, the answer is by no means so easy. How shall we conclude immediately from the action to the permanence of that which acts, this being nevertheless an essential and peculiar criterion of substance (phenomenon)? But after what has been said above, the solution of this question becomes easy enough, although by the common mode of procedure—merely analysing our conceptions—it would be quite impossible. The conception of action indicates the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now because all effect consists in that which happens, therefore in the changeable, the last subject thereof is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change in phenomena and, consequently, cannot be a property of a subject which itself changes, because if this were the case, other actions and another subject would be necessary to determine this change. From all this it results that action alone, as an empirical criterion, is a sufficient proof of the presence of substantiality, without any necessity on my part of endeavouring to discover the permanence of substance by a comparison. Besides, by this mode of induction we could not attain to the completeness which the magnitude and strict universality of the conception requires. For that the primary subject of the causality of all arising and passing away, all origin and extinction, cannot itself (in the sphere of phenomena) arise and pass away, is a sound and safe conclusion, a conclusion which leads us to the conception of empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and consequently to the conception of a substance as phenomenon.\n\nWhen something happens, the mere fact of the occurrence, without regard to that which occurs, is an object requiring investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into the existence of it, supposing that this state contains no quality which previously existed in the phenomenon, is a fact of itself demanding inquiry. Such an event, as has been shown in No. A, does not concern substance (for substance does not thus originate), but its condition or state. It is therefore only change, and not origin from nothing. If this origin be regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is termed creation, which cannot be admitted as an event among phenomena, because the very possibility of it would annihilate the unity of experience. If, however, I regard all things not as phenomena, but as things in themselves and objects of understanding alone, they, although substances, may be considered as dependent, in respect of their existence, on a foreign cause. But this would require a very different meaning in the words, a meaning which could not apply to phenomena as objects of possible experience.\n\nHow a thing can be changed, how it is possible that upon one state existing in one point of time, an opposite state should follow in another point of time—of this we have not the smallest conception à priori. There is requisite for this the knowledge of real powers, which can only be given empirically; for example, knowledge of moving forces, or, in other words, of certain successive phenomena (as movements) which indicate the presence of such forces. But the form of every change, the condition under which alone it can take place as the coming into existence of another state (be the content of the change, that is, the state which is changed, what it may), and consequently the succession of the states themselves can very well be considered à priori, in relation to the law of causality and the conditions of time.[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-32)\n\n[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-32) It must be remarked that I do not speak of the change of certain relations, but of the change of the state. Thus, when a body moves in a uniform manner, it does not change its state (of motion); but only when all motion increases or decreases.\n\nWhen a substance passes from one state, a, into another state, b, the point of time in which the latter exists is different from, and subsequent to that in which the former existed. In like manner, the second state, as reality (in the phenomenon), differs from the first, in which the reality of the second did not exist, as b from zero. That is to say, if the state, b, differs from the state, a, only in respect to quantity, the change is a coming into existence of b -a, which in the former state did not exist, and in relation to which that state is = O.\n\nNow the question arises how a thing passes from one state = a, into another state = b. Between two moments there is always a certain time, and between two states existing in these moments there is always a difference having a certain quantity (for all parts of phenomena are in their turn quantities). Consequently, every transition from one state into another, is always effected in a time contained between two moments, of which the first determines the state which the thing leaves, and the second determines the state into which the thing passes. Both moments, then, are limitations of the time of a change, consequently of the intermediate state between both, and as such they belong to the total of the change. Now every change has a cause, which evidences its causality in the whole time during which the charge takes place. The cause, therefore, does not produce the change all at once or in one moment, but in a time, so that, as the time gradually increases from the commencing instant, a, to its completion at b, in like manner also, the quantity of the reality (b - a) is generated through the lesser degrees which are contained between the first and last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of the causality, which, in so far as it is uniform, we call a momentum. The change does not consist of these momenta, but is generated or produced by them as their effect.\n\nSuch is the law of the continuity of all change, the ground of which is that neither time itself nor any phenomenon in time consists of parts which are the smallest possible, but that, notwithstanding, the state of a thing passes in the process of a change through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest degree of reality in a phenomenon, just as there is no smallest degree in the quantity of time; and so the new state of reality grows up out of the former state, through all the infinite degrees thereof, the differences of which one from another, taken all together, are less than the difference between 0 and a.\n\nIt is not our business to inquire here into the utility of this principle in the investigation of nature. But how such a proposition, which appears so greatly to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely à priori, is indeed a question which deserves investigation, although the first view seems to demonstrate the truth and reality of the principle, and the question, how it is possible, may be considered superfluous. For there are so many groundless pretensions to the enlargement of our knowledge by pure reason that we must take it as a general rule to be mistrustful of all such, and without a thoroughgoing and radical deduction, to believe nothing of the sort even on the clearest dogmatical evidence.\n\nEvery addition to our empirical knowledge, and every advance made in the exercise of our perception, is nothing more than an extension of the determination of the internal sense, that is to say, a progression in time, be objects themselves what they may, phenomena, or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing else. That is to say, the parts of the progression exist only in time, and by means of the synthesis thereof, and are not given antecedently to it. For this reason, every transition in perception to anything which follows upon another in time, is a determination of time by means of the production of this perception. And as this determination of time is, always and in all its parts, a quantity, the perception produced is to be considered as a quantity which proceeds through all its degrees—no one of which is the smallest possible—from zero up to its determined degree. From this we perceive the possibility of cognizing à priori a law of changes—a law, however, which concerns their form merely. We merely anticipate our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, inasmuch as it is itself to be found in the mind antecedently to all given phenomena, must certainly be capable of being cognized à priori.\n\nThus, as time contains the sensuous condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous progression of that which exists to that which follows it, the understanding, by virtue of the unity of apperception, contains the condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous determination of the position in time of all phenomena, and this by means of the series of causes and effects, the former of which necessitate the sequence of the latter, and thereby render universally and for all time, and by consequence, objectively, valid the empirical cognition of the relations of time.\n\nC. THIRD ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of Coexistence, According to the Law of Reciprocity or Community.\n\nAll substances, in so far as they can be perceived in space at the same time, exist in a state of complete reciprocity of action.\n\nPROOF.\n\nThings are coexistent, when in empirical intuition the perception of the one can follow upon the perception of the other, and vice versa—which cannot occur in the succession of phenomena, as we have shown in the explanation of the second principle. Thus I can perceive the moon and then the earth, or conversely, first the earth and then the moon; and for the reason that my perceptions of these objects can reciprocally follow each other, I say, they exist contemporaneously. Now coexistence is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But time itself is not an object of perception; and therefore we cannot conclude from the fact that things are placed in the same time, the other fact, that the perception of these things can follow each other reciprocally. The synthesis of the imagination in apprehension would only present to us each of these perceptions as present in the subject when the other is not present, and contrariwise; but would not show that the objects are coexistent, that is to say, that, if the one exists, the other also exists in the same time, and that this is necessarily so, in order that the perceptions may be capable of following each other reciprocally. It follows that a conception of the understanding or category of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations of phenomena (existing, as they do, apart from each other, and yet contemporaneously), is requisite to justify us in saying that the reciprocal succession of perceptions has its foundation in the object, and to enable us to represent coexistence as objective. But that relation of substances in which the one contains determinations the ground of which is in the other substance, is the relation of influence. And, when this influence is reciprocal, it is the relation of community or reciprocity. Consequently the coexistence of substances in space cannot be cognized in experience otherwise than under the precondition of their reciprocal action. This is therefore the condition of the possibility of things themselves as objects of experience.\n\nThings are coexistent, in so far as they exist in one and the same time. But how can we know that they exist in one and the same time? Only by observing that the order in the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold is arbitrary and a matter of indifference, that is to say, that it can proceed from A, through B, C, D, to E, or contrariwise from E to A. For if they were successive in time (and in the order, let us suppose, which begins with A), it is quite impossible for the apprehension in perception to begin with E and go backwards to A, inasmuch as A belongs to past time and, therefore, cannot be an object of apprehension.\n\nLet us assume that in a number of substances considered as phenomena each is completely isolated, that is, that no one acts upon another. Then I say that the coexistence of these cannot be an object of possible perception and that the existence of one cannot, by any mode of empirical synthesis, lead us to the existence of another. For we imagine them in this case to be separated by a completely void space, and thus perception, which proceeds from the one to the other in time, would indeed determine their existence by means of a following perception, but would be quite unable to distinguish whether the one phenomenon follows objectively upon the first, or is coexistent with it.\n\nBesides the mere fact of existence, then, there must be something by means of which A determines the position of B in time and, conversely, B the position of A; because only under this condition can substances be empirically represented as existing contemporaneously. Now that alone determines the position of another thing in time which is the cause of it or of its determinations. Consequently every substance (inasmuch as it can have succession predicated of it only in respect of its determinations) must contain the causality of certain determinations in another substance, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other in itself. That is to say, substances must stand (mediately or immediately) in dynamical community with each other, if coexistence is to be cognized in any possible experience. But, in regard to objects of experience, that is absolutely necessary without which the experience of these objects would itself be impossible. Consequently it is absolutely necessary that all substances in the world of phenomena, in so far as they are coexistent, stand in a relation of complete community of reciprocal action to each other.\n\nThe word community has in our language[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-33) two meanings, and contains the two notions conveyed in the Latin communio and commercium. We employ it in this place in the latter sense—that of a dynamical community, without which even the community of place (communio spatii) could not be empirically cognized. In our experiences it is easy to observe that it is only the continuous influences in all parts of space that can conduct our senses from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eyes and the heavenly bodies produces a mediating community between them and us, and thereby evidences their coexistence with us; that we cannot empirically change our position (perceive this change), unless the existence of matter throughout the whole of space rendered possible the perception of the positions we occupy; and that this perception can prove the contemporaneous existence of these places only through their reciprocal influence, and thereby also the coexistence of even the most remote objects—although in this case the proof is only mediate. Without community, every perception (of a phenomenon in space) is separated from every other and isolated, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, of experience, must, with the appearance of a new object, begin entirely de novo, without the least connection with preceding representations, and without standing towards these even in the relation of time. My intention here is by no means to combat the notion of empty space; for it may exist where our perceptions cannot exist, inasmuch as they cannot reach thereto, and where, therefore, no empirical perception of coexistence takes place. But in this case it is not an object of possible experience.\n\n[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-33) German\n\nThe following remarks may be useful in the way of explanation. In the mind, all phenomena, as contents of a possible experience, must exist in community (communio) of apperception or consciousness, and in so far as it is requisite that objects be represented as coexistent and connected, in so far must they reciprocally determine the position in time of each other and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest upon an objective basis, or to be applied to substances as phenomena, the perception of one substance must render possible the perception of another, and conversely. For otherwise succession, which is always found in perceptions as apprehensions, would be predicated of external objects, and their representation of their coexistence be thus impossible. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is to say, a real community (commercium) of substances, without which therefore the empirical relation of coexistence would be a notion beyond the reach of our minds. By virtue of this commercium, phenomena, in so far as they are apart from, and nevertheless in connection with each other, constitute a compositum reale. Such composita are possible in many different ways. The three dynamical relations then, from which all others spring, are those of inherence, consequence, and composition.\n\nThese, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing more than principles of the determination of the existence of phenomena in time, according to the three modi of this determination; to wit, the relation to time itself as a quantity (the quantity of existence, that is, duration), the relation in time as a series or succession, finally, the relation in time as the complex of all existence (simultaneity). This unity of determination in regard to time is thoroughly dynamical; that is to say, time is not considered as that in which experience determines immediately to every existence its position; for this is impossible, inasmuch as absolute time is not an object of perception, by means of which phenomena can be connected with each other. On the contrary, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of phenomena can receive synthetical unity as regards relations of time, determines for every phenomenon its position in time, and consequently à priori, and with validity for all and every time.\n\nBy nature, in the empirical sense of the word, we understand the totality of phenomena connected, in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, laws. There are therefore certain laws (which are moreover à priori) which make nature possible; and all empirical laws can exist only by means of experience, and by virtue of those primitive laws through which experience itself becomes possible. The purpose of the analogies is therefore to represent to us the unity of nature in the connection of all phenomena under certain exponents, the only business of which is to express the relation of time (in so far as it contains all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can exist in synthesis only according to rules. The combined expression of all is this: “All phenomena exist in one nature, and must so exist, inasmuch as without this à priori unity, no unity of experience, and consequently no determination of objects in experience, is possible.”\n\nAs regards the mode of proof which we have employed in treating of these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiar character of it we must make one remark, which will at the same time be important as a guide in every other attempt to demonstrate the truth of intellectual and likewise synthetical propositions à priori. Had we endeavoured to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from conceptions; that is to say, had we employed this method in attempting to show that everything which exists, exists only in that which is permanent—that every thing or event presupposes the existence of something in a preceding state, upon which it follows in conformity with a rule—lastly, that in the manifold, which is coexistent, the states coexist in connection with each other according to a rule, all our labour would have been utterly in vain. For mere conceptions of things, analyse them as we may, cannot enable us to conclude from the existence of one object to the existence of another. What other course was left for us to pursue? This only, to demonstrate the possibility of experience as a cognition in which at last all objects must be capable of being presented to us, if the representation of them is to possess any objective reality. Now in this third, this mediating term, the essential form of which consists in the synthetical unity of the apperception of all phenomena, we found à priori conditions of the universal and necessary determination as to time of all existences in the world of phenomena, without which the empirical determination thereof as to time would itself be impossible, and we also discovered rules of synthetical unity à priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. For want of this method, and from the fancy that it was possible to discover a dogmatical proof of the synthetical propositions which are requisite in the empirical employment of the understanding, has it happened that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has been so often attempted, and always in vain. The other two analogies nobody has ever thought of, although they have always been silently employed by the mind,[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-34) because the guiding thread furnished by the categories was wanting, the guide which alone can enable us to discover every hiatus, both in the system of conceptions and of principles.\n\n[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-34) The unity of the universe, in which all phenomena to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the admitted principle of the community of all substances which are coexistent. For were substances isolated, they could not as parts constitute a whole, and were their connection (reciprocal action of the manifold) not necessary from the very fact of coexistence, we could not conclude from the fact of the latter as a merely ideal relation to the former as a real one. We have, however, shown in its place that community is the proper ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition of coexistence, and that we may therefore properly reason from the latter to the former as its condition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0001","text":"# Experience","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0002","text":"3. ANALOGIES OF EXPERIENCE.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0003","text":"The principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of Perceptions.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0004","text":"PROOF.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Experience is an empirical cognition; that is to say, a cognition which determines an object by means of perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, a synthesis which is not itself contained in perception, but which contains the synthetical unity of the manifold of perception in a consciousness; and this unity constitutes the essential of our cognition of objects of the senses, that is, of experience (not merely of intuition or sensation). Now in experience our perceptions come together contingently, so that no character of necessity in their connection appears, or can appear from the perceptions themselves, because apprehension is only a placing together of the manifold of empirical intuition, and no representation of a necessity in the connected existence of the phenomena which apprehension brings together, is to be discovered therein. But as experience is a cognition of objects by means of perceptions, it follows that the relation of the existence of the existence of the manifold must be represented in experience not as it is put together in time, but as it is objectively in time. And as time itself cannot be perceived, the determination of the existence of objects in time can only take place by means of their connection in time in general, consequently only by means of à priori connecting conceptions. Now as these conceptions always possess the character of necessity, experience is possible only by means of a representation of the necessary connection of perception.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0006","text":"The three modi of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Accordingly, there are three rules of all relations of time in phenomena, according to which the existence of every phenomenon is determined in respect of the unity of all time, and these antecede all experience and render it possible.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0007","text":"The general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in relation to all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time, consequently, as this unity lies à priori at the foundation of all mental operations, the principle rests on the synthetical unity of all phenomena according to their relation in time. For the original apperception relates to our internal sense (the complex of all representations), and indeed relates à priori to its form, that is to say, the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. Now this manifold must be combined in original apperception according to relations of time—a necessity imposed by the à priori transcendental unity of apperception, to which is subjected all that can belong to my (i.e., my own) cognition, and therefore all that can become an object for me. This synthetical and à priori determined unity in relation of perceptions in time is therefore the rule: “All empirical determinations of time must be subject to rules of the general determination of time”; and the analogies of experience, of which we are now about to treat, must be rules of this nature.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0008","text":"These principles have this peculiarity, that they do not concern phenomena, and the synthesis of the empirical intuition thereof, but merely the existence of phenomena and their relation to each other in regard to this existence. Now the mode in which we apprehend a thing in a phenomenon can be determined à priori in such a manner that the rule of its synthesis can give, that is to say, can produce this à priori intuition in every empirical example. But the existence of phenomena cannot be known à priori, and although we could arrive by this path at a conclusion of the fact of some existence, we could not cognize that existence determinately, that is to say, we should be incapable of anticipating in what respect the empirical intuition of it would be distinguishable from that of others.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0009","text":"The two principles above mentioned, which I called mathematical, in consideration of the fact of their authorizing the application of mathematic phenomena, relate to these phenomena only in regard to their possibility, and instruct us how phenomena, as far as regards their intuition or the real in their perception, can be generated according to the rules of a mathematical synthesis. Consequently, numerical quantities, and with them the determination of a phenomenon as a quantity, can be employed in the one case as well as in the other. Thus, for example, out of 200,000 illuminations by the moon, I might compose and give à priori, that is construct, the degree of our sensations of the sun-light.[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-30) We may therefore entitle these two principles constitutive.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0010","text":"[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-30) Kant’s meaning is: The two principles enunciated under the heads of “Axioms of Intuition,” and “Anticipations of Perception,” authorize the application to phenomena of determinations of size and number, that is of mathematic. For example, I may compute the light of the sun, and say that its quantity is a certain number of times greater than that of the moon. In the same way, heat is measured by the comparison of its different effects on water, &c., and on mercury in a thermometer.—Tr","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0011","text":"The case is very different with those principles whose province it is to subject the existence of phenomena to rules à priori. For as existence does not admit of being constructed, it is clear that they must only concern the relations of existence and be merely regulative principles. In this case, therefore, neither axioms nor anticipations are to be thought of. Thus, if a perception is given us, in a certain relation of time to other (although undetermined) perceptions, we cannot then say à priori, what and how great (in quantity) the other perception necessarily connected with the former is, but only how it is connected, quoad its existence, in this given modus of time. Analogies in philosophy mean something very different from that which they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulae, which enounce the equality of two relations of quantity, and are always constitutive, so that if two terms of the proportion are given, the third is also given, that is, can be constructed by the aid of these formulae. But in philosophy, analogy is not the equality of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. In this case, from three given terms, I can give à priori and cognize the relation to a fourth member, but not this fourth term itself, although I certainly possess a rule to guide me in the search for this fourth term in experience, and a mark to assist me in discovering it. An analogy of experience is therefore only a rule according to which unity of experience must arise out of perceptions in respect to objects (phenomena) not as a constitutive, but merely as a regulative principle. The same holds good also of the postulates of empirical thought in general, which relate to the synthesis of mere intuition (which concerns the form of phenomena), the synthesis of perception (which concerns the matter of phenomena), and the synthesis of experience (which concerns the relation of these perceptions). For they are only regulative principles, and clearly distinguishable from the mathematical, which are constitutive, not indeed in regard to the certainty which both possess à priori, but in the mode of evidence thereof, consequently also in the manner of demonstration.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0012","text":"But what has been observed of all synthetical propositions, and must be particularly remarked in this place, is this, that these analogies possess significance and validity, not as principles of the transcendental, but only as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, and their truth can therefore be proved only as such, and that consequently the phenomena must not be subjoined directly under the categories, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which those principles must be applied were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to cognize aught concerning them synthetically à priori. But they are nothing but phenomena; a complete knowledge of which—a knowledge to which all principles à priori must at last relate—is the only possible experience. It follows that these principles can have nothing else for their aim than the conditions of the empirical cognition in the unity of synthesis of phenomena. But this synthesis is cogitated only in the schema of the pure conception of the understanding, of whose unity, as that of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function unrestricted by any sensuous condition. These principles will therefore authorize us to connect phenomena according to an analogy, with the logical and universal unity of conceptions, and consequently to employ the categories in the principles themselves; but in the application of them to experience, we shall use only their schemata, as the key to their proper application, instead of the categories, or rather the latter as restricting conditions, under the title of “formulae” of the former.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0013","text":"A. FIRST ANALOGY.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0014","text":"Principle of the Permanence of Substance.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0015","text":"In all changes of phenomena, substance is permanent, and the quantum thereof in nature is neither increased nor diminished.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0016","text":"PROOF.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0017","text":"All phenomena exist in time, wherein alone as substratum, that is, as the permanent form of the internal intuition, coexistence and succession can be represented. Consequently time, in which all changes of phenomena must be cogitated, remains and changes not, because it is that in which succession and coexistence can be represented only as determinations thereof. Now, time in itself cannot be an object of perception. It follows that in objects of perception, that is, in phenomena, there must be found a substratum which represents time in general, and in which all change or coexistence can be perceived by means of the relation of phenomena to it. But the substratum of all reality, that is, of all that pertains to the existence of things, is substance; all that pertains to existence can be cogitated only as a determination of substance. Consequently, the permanent, in relation to which alone can all relations of time in phenomena be determined, is substance in the world of phenomena, that is, the real in phenomena, that which, as the substratum of all change, remains ever the same. Accordingly, as this cannot change in existence, its quantity in nature can neither be increased nor diminished.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0018","text":"Our apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon is always successive, is Consequently always changing. By it alone we could, therefore, never determine whether this manifold, as an object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless it had for a foundation something fixed and permanent, of the existence of which all succession and coexistence are nothing but so many modes (modi of time). Only in the permanent, then, are relations of time possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time); that is to say, the permanent is the substratum of our empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all determination of time is possible. Permanence is, in fact, just another expression for time, as the abiding correlate of all existence of phenomena, and of all change, and of all coexistence. For change does not affect time itself, but only the phenomena in time (just as coexistence cannot be regarded as a modus of time itself, seeing that in time no parts are coexistent, but all successive). If we were to attribute succession to time itself, we should be obliged to cogitate another time, in which this succession would be possible. It is only by means of the permanent that existence in different parts of the successive series of time receives a quantity, which we entitle duration. For in mere succession, existence is perpetually vanishing and recommencing, and therefore never has even the least quantity. Without the permanent, then, no relation in time is possible. Now, time in itself is not an object of perception; consequently the permanent in phenomena must be regarded as the substratum of all determination of time, and consequently also as the condition of the possibility of all synthetical unity of perceptions, that is, of experience; and all existence and all change in time can only be regarded as a mode in the existence of that which abides unchangeably. Therefore, in all phenomena, the permanent is the object in itself, that is, the substance (phenomenon); but all that changes or can change belongs only to the mode of the existence of this substance or substances, consequently to its determinations.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0019","text":"I find that in all ages not only the philosopher, but even the common understanding, has preposited this permanence as a substratum of all change in phenomena; indeed, I am compelled to believe that they will always accept this as an indubitable fact. Only the philosopher expresses himself in a more precise and definite manner, when he says: “In all changes in the world, the substance remains, and the accidents alone are changeable.” But of this decidedly synthetical proposition, I nowhere meet with even an attempt at proof; nay, it very rarely has the good fortune to stand, as it deserves to do, at the head of the pure and entirely à priori laws of nature. In truth, the statement that substance is permanent, is tautological. For this very permanence is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to the phenomenon; and we should have been obliged to prove that in all phenomena there is something permanent, of the existence of which the changeable is nothing but a determination. But because a proof of this nature cannot be dogmatical, that is, cannot be drawn from conceptions, inasmuch as it concerns a synthetical proposition à priori, and as philosophers never reflected that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and therefore cannot be proved except by means of a deduction of the possibility of experience, it is no wonder that while it has served as the foundation of all experience (for we feel the need of it in empirical cognition), it has never been supported by proof.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0020","text":"A philosopher was asked: “What is the weight of smoke?” He answered: “Subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the remaining ashes, and you will have the weight of the smoke.” Thus he presumed it to be incontrovertible that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but that only the form of it undergoes a change. In like manner was the saying: “From nothing comes nothing,” only another inference from the principle or permanence, or rather of the ever-abiding existence of the true subject in phenomena. For if that in the phenomenon which we call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, it follows that all existence in past as well as in future time, must be determinable by means of it alone. Hence we are entitled to apply the term substance to a phenomenon, only because we suppose its existence in all time, a notion which the word permanence does not fully express, as it seems rather to be referable to future time. However, the internal necessity perpetually to be, is inseparably connected with the necessity always to have been, and so the expression may stand as it is. “Gigni de nihilo nihil; in nihilum nil posse reverti,”[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-31) are two propositions which the ancients never parted, and which people nowadays sometimes mistakenly disjoin, because they imagine that the propositions apply to objects as things in themselves, and that the former might be inimical to the dependence (even in respect of its substance also) of the world upon a supreme cause. But this apprehension is entirely needless, for the question in this case is only of phenomena in the sphere of experience, the unity of which never could be possible, if we admitted the possibility that new things (in respect of their substance) should arise. For in that case, we should lose altogether that which alone can represent the unity of time, to wit, the identity of the substratum, as that through which alone all change possesses complete and thorough unity. This permanence is, however, nothing but the manner in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things in the phenomenal world.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0021","text":"[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-31) Persius, Satirae, iii.83-84.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0022","text":"The determinations of a substance, which are only particular modes of its existence, are called accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations, which express the non-existence of something in the substance). Now, if to this real in the substance we ascribe a particular existence (for example, to motion as an accident of matter), this existence is called inherence, in contradistinction to the existence of substance, which we call subsistence. But hence arise many misconceptions, and it would be a more accurate and just mode of expression to designate the accident only as the mode in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Meanwhile, by reason of the conditions of the logical exercise of our understanding, it is impossible to avoid separating, as it were, that which in the existence of a substance is subject to change, whilst the substance remains, and regarding it in relation to that which is properly permanent and radical. On this account, this category of substance stands under the title of relation, rather because it is the condition thereof than because it contains in itself any relation.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Now, upon this notion of permanence rests the proper notion of the conception change. Origin and extinction are not changes of that which originates or becomes extinct. Change is but a mode of existence, which follows on another mode of existence of the same object; hence all that changes is permanent, and only the condition thereof changes. Now since this mutation affects only determinations, which can have a beginning or an end, we may say, employing an expression which seems somewhat paradoxical: “Only the permanent (substance) is subject to change; the mutable suffers no change, but rather alternation, that is, when certain determinations cease, others begin.”","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Change, when, cannot be perceived by us except in substances, and origin or extinction in an absolute sense, that does not concern merely a determination of the permanent, cannot be a possible perception, for it is this very notion of the permanent which renders possible the representation of a transition from one state into another, and from non-being to being, which, consequently, can be empirically cognized only as alternating determinations of that which is permanent. Grant that a thing absolutely begins to be; we must then have a point of time in which it was not. But how and by what can we fix and determine this point of time, unless by that which already exists? For a void time—preceding—is not an object of perception; but if we connect this beginning with objects which existed previously, and which continue to exist till the object in question in question begins to be, then the latter can only be a determination of the former as the permanent. The same holds good of the notion of extinction, for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time, in which a phenomenon no longer exists.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Substances (in the world of phenomena) are the substratum of all determinations of time. The beginning of some, and the ceasing to be of other substances, would utterly do away with the only condition of the empirical unity of time; and in that case phenomena would relate to two different times, in which, side by side, existence would pass; which is absurd. For there is only one time in which all different times must be placed, not as coexistent, but as successive.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Accordingly, permanence is a necessary condition under which alone phenomena, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. But as regards the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of phenomena, we shall find sufficient opportunity to speak in the sequel.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0027","text":"B. SECOND ANALOGY.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0028","text":"Principle of the Succession of Time According to the Law of Causality. All changes take place according to the law of the connection of Cause and Effect.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0029","text":"PROOF.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0030","text":"(That all phenomena in the succession of time are only changes, that is, a successive being and non-being of the determinations of substance, which is permanent; consequently that a being of substance itself which follows on the non-being thereof, or a non-being of substance which follows on the being thereof, in other words, that the origin or extinction of substance itself, is impossible—all this has been fully established in treating of the foregoing principle. This principle might have been expressed as follows: “All alteration (succession) of phenomena is merely change”; for the changes of substance are not origin or extinction, because the conception of change presupposes the same subject as existing with two opposite determinations, and consequently as permanent. After this premonition, we shall proceed to the proof.)","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0031","text":"I perceive that phenomena succeed one another, that is to say, a state of things exists at one time, the opposite of which existed in a former state. In this case, then, I really connect together two perceptions in time. Now connection is not an operation of mere sense and intuition, but is the product of a synthetical faculty of imagination, which determines the internal sense in respect of a relation of time. But imagination can connect these two states in two ways, so that either the one or the other may antecede in time; for time in itself cannot be an object of perception, and what in an object precedes and what follows cannot be empirically determined in relation to it. I am only conscious, then, that my imagination places one state before and the other after; not that the one state antecedes the other in the object. In other words, the objective relation of the successive phenomena remains quite undetermined by means of mere perception. Now in order that this relation may be cognized as determined, the relation between the two states must be so cogitated that it is thereby determined as necessary, which of them must be placed before and which after, and not conversely. But the conception which carries with it a necessity of synthetical unity, can be none other than a pure conception of the understanding which does not lie in mere perception; and in this case it is the conception of “the relation of cause and effect,” the former of which determines the latter in time, as its necessary consequence, and not as something which might possibly antecede (or which might in some cases not be perceived to follow). It follows that it is only because we subject the sequence of phenomena, and consequently all change, to the law of causality, that experience itself, that is, empirical cognition of phenomena, becomes possible; and consequently, that phenomena themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only by virtue of this law.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0032","text":"Our apprehension of the manifold of phenomena is always successive. The representations of parts succeed one another. Whether they succeed one another in the object also, is a second point for reflection, which was not contained in the former. Now we may certainly give the name of object to everything, even to every representation, so far as we are conscious thereof; but what this word may mean in the case of phenomena, not merely in so far as they (as representations) are objects, but only in so far as they indicate an object, is a question requiring deeper consideration. In so far as they, regarded merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not to be distinguished from apprehension, that is, reception into the synthesis of imagination, and we must therefore say: “The manifold of phenomena is always produced successively in the mind.” If phenomena were things in themselves, no man would be able to conjecture from the succession of our representations how this manifold is connected in the object; for we have to do only with our representations. How things may be in themselves, without regard to the representations through which they affect us, is utterly beyond the sphere of our cognition. Now although phenomena are not things in themselves, and are nevertheless the only thing given to us to be cognized, it is my duty to show what sort of connection in time belongs to the manifold in phenomena themselves, while the representation of this manifold in apprehension is always successive. For example, the apprehension of the manifold in the phenomenon of a house which stands before me, is successive. Now comes the question whether the manifold of this house is in itself successive—which no one will be at all willing to grant. But, so soon as I raise my conception of an object to the transcendental signification thereof, I find that the house is not a thing in itself, but only a phenomenon, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which remains utterly unknown. What then am I to understand by the question: “How can the manifold be connected in the phenomenon itself—not considered as a thing in itself, but merely as a phenomenon?” Here that which lies in my successive apprehension is regarded as representation, whilst the phenomenon which is given me, notwithstanding that it is nothing more than a complex of these representations, is regarded as the object thereof, with which my conception, drawn from the representations of apprehension, must harmonize. It is very soon seen that, as accordance of the cognition with its object constitutes truth, the question now before us can only relate to the formal conditions of empirical truth; and that the phenomenon, in opposition to the representations of apprehension, can only be distinguished therefrom as the object of them, if it is subject to a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension, and which renders necessary a mode of connection of the manifold. That in the phenomenon which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension, is the object.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0033","text":"Let us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is to say, that something or some state exists which before was not, cannot be empirically perceived, unless a phenomenon precedes, which does not contain in itself this state. For a reality which should follow upon a void time, in other words, a beginning, which no state of things precedes, can just as little be apprehended as the void time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another perception. But as this is the case with all synthesis of apprehension, as I have shown above in the example of a house, my apprehension of an event is not yet sufficiently distinguished from other apprehensions. But I remark also that if in a phenomenon which contains an occurrence, I call the antecedent state of my perception, A, and the following state, B, the perception B can only follow A in apprehension, and the perception A cannot follow B, but only precede it. For example, I see a ship float down the stream of a river. My perception of its place lower down follows upon my perception of its place higher up the course of the river, and it is impossible that, in the apprehension of this phenomenon, the vessel should be perceived first below and afterwards higher up the stream. Here, therefore, the order in the sequence of perceptions in apprehension is determined; and by this order apprehension is regulated. In the former example, my perceptions in the apprehension of a house might begin at the roof and end at the foundation, or vice versa; or I might apprehend the manifold in this empirical intuition, by going from left to right, and from right to left. Accordingly, in the series of these perceptions, there was no determined order, which necessitated my beginning at a certain point, in order empirically to connect the manifold. But this rule is always to be met with in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions in the apprehension of such a phenomenon necessary.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0034","text":"I must, therefore, in the present case, deduce the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of phenomena, for otherwise the former is quite undetermined, and one phenomenon is not distinguishable from another. The former alone proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in an object, for it is quite arbitrary. The latter must consist in the order of the manifold in a phenomenon, according to which order the apprehension of one thing (that which happens) follows that of another thing (which precedes), in conformity with a rule. In this way alone can I be authorized to say of the phenomenon itself, and not merely of my own apprehension, that a certain order or sequence is to be found therein. That is, in other words, I cannot arrange my apprehension otherwise than in this order.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0035","text":"In conformity with this rule, then, it is necessary that in that which antecedes an event there be found the condition of a rule, according to which in this event follows always and necessarily; but I cannot reverse this and go back from the event, and determine (by apprehension) that which antecedes it. For no phenomenon goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding point, although it does certainly relate to a preceding point of time; from a given time, on the other hand, there is always a necessary progression to the determined succeeding time. Therefore, because there certainly is something that follows, I must of necessity connect it with something else, which antecedes, and upon which it follows, in conformity with a rule, that is necessarily, so that the event, as conditioned, affords certain indication of a condition, and this condition determines the event.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Let us suppose that nothing precedes an event, upon which this event must follow in conformity with a rule. All sequence of perception would then exist only in apprehension, that is to say, would be merely subjective, and it could not thereby be objectively determined what thing ought to precede, and what ought to follow in perception. In such a case, we should have nothing but a play of representations, which would possess no application to any object. That is to say, it would not be possible through perception to distinguish one phenomenon from another, as regards relations of time; because the succession in the act of apprehension would always be of the same sort, and therefore there would be nothing in the phenomenon to determine the succession, and to render a certain sequence objectively necessary. And, in this case, I cannot say that two states in a phenomenon follow one upon the other, but only that one apprehension follows upon another. But this is merely subjective, and does not determine an object, and consequently cannot be held to be cognition of an object—not even in the phenomenal world.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Accordingly, when we know in experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes, whereupon it follows in conformity with a rule. For otherwise I could not say of the object that it follows; because the mere succession in my apprehension, if it be not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not authorize succession in the object. Only, therefore, in reference to a rule, according to which phenomena are determined in their sequence, that is, as they happen, by the preceding state, can I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and it is only under this presupposition that even the experience of an event is possible.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0038","text":"No doubt it appears as if this were in thorough contradiction to all the notions which people have hitherto entertained in regard to the procedure of the human understanding. According to these opinions, it is by means of the perception and comparison of similar consequences following upon certain antecedent phenomena that the understanding is led to the discovery of a rule, according to which certain events always follow certain phenomena, and it is only by this process that we attain to the conception of cause. Upon such a basis, it is clear that this conception must be merely empirical, and the rule which it furnishes us with—“Everything that happens must have a cause”—would be just as contingent as experience itself. The universality and necessity of the rule or law would be perfectly spurious attributes of it. Indeed, it could not possess universal validity, inasmuch as it would not in this case be à priori, but founded on deduction. But the same is the case with this law as with other pure à priori representations (e.g., space and time), which we can draw in perfect clearness and completeness from experience, only because we had already placed them therein, and by that means, and by that alone, had rendered experience possible. Indeed, the logical clearness of this representation of a rule, determining the series of events, is possible only when we have made use thereof in experience. Nevertheless, the recognition of this rule, as a condition of the synthetical unity of phenomena in time, was the ground of experience itself and consequently preceded it à priori.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0039","text":"It is now our duty to show by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute to an object the notion of succession or effect (of an event—that is, the happening of something that did not exist before), and distinguish it from the subjective succession of apprehension, unless when a rule lies at the foundation, which compels us to observe this order of perception in preference to any other, and that, indeed, it is this necessity which first renders possible the representation of a succession in the object.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0040","text":"We have representations within us, of which also we can be conscious. But, however widely extended, however accurate and thoroughgoing this consciousness may be, these representations are still nothing more than representations, that is, internal determinations of the mind in this or that relation of time. Now how happens it that to these representations we should set an object, or that, in addition to their subjective reality, as modifications, we should still further attribute to them a certain unknown objective reality? It is clear that objective significancy cannot consist in a relation to another representation (of that which we desire to term object), for in that case the question again arises: “How does this other representation go out of itself, and obtain objective significancy over and above the subjective, which is proper to it, as a determination of a state of mind?” If we try to discover what sort of new property the relation to an object gives to our subjective representations, and what new importance they thereby receive, we shall find that this relation has no other effect than that of rendering necessary the connection of our representations in a certain manner, and of subjecting them to a rule; and that conversely, it is only because a certain order is necessary in the relations of time of our representations, that objective significancy is ascribed to them.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0041","text":"In the synthesis of phenomena, the manifold of our representations is always successive. Now hereby is not represented an object, for by means of this succession, which is common to all apprehension, no one thing is distinguished from another. But so soon as I perceive or assume that in this succession there is a relation to a state antecedent, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, so soon do I represent something as an event, or as a thing that happens; in other words, I cognize an object to which I must assign a certain determinate position in time, which cannot be altered, because of the preceding state in the object. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, there is contained in this representation, in the first place, the fact, that something antecedes; because, it is only in relation to this that the phenomenon obtains its proper relation of time, in other words, exists after an antecedent time, in which it did not exist. But it can receive its determined place in time only by the presupposition that something existed in the foregoing state, upon which it follows inevitably and always, that is, in conformity with a rule. From all this it is evident that, in the first place, I cannot reverse the order of succession, and make that which happens precede that upon which it follows; and that, in the second place, if the antecedent state be posited, a certain determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Hence it follows that there exists a certain order in our representations, whereby the present gives a sure indication of some previously existing state, as a correlate, though still undetermined, of the existing event which is given—a correlate which itself relates to the event as its consequence, conditions it, and connects it necessarily with itself in the series of time.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0042","text":"If then it be admitted as a necessary law of sensibility, and consequently a formal condition of all perception, that the preceding necessarily determines the succeeding time (inasmuch as I cannot arrive at the succeeding except through the preceding), it must likewise be an indispensable law of empirical representation of the series of time that the phenomena of the past determine all phenomena in the succeeding time, and that the latter, as events, cannot take place, except in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is to say, establish it according to a rule. For it is of course only in phenomena that we can empirically cognize this continuity in the connection of times.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0043","text":"For all experience and for the possibility of experience, understanding is indispensable, and the first step which it takes in this sphere is not to render the representation of objects clear, but to render the representation of an object in general, possible. It does this by applying the order of time to phenomena, and their existence. In other words, it assigns to each phenomenon, as a consequence, a place in relation to preceding phenomena, determined à priori in time, without which it could not harmonize with time itself, which determines a place à priori to all its parts. This determination of place cannot be derived from the relation of phenomena to absolute time (for it is not an object of perception); but, on the contrary, phenomena must reciprocally determine the places in time of one another, and render these necessary in the order of time. In other words, whatever follows or happens, must follow in conformity with a universal rule upon that which was contained in the foregoing state. Hence arises a series of phenomena, which, by means of the understanding, produces and renders necessary exactly the same order and continuous connection in the series of our possible perceptions, as is found à priori in the form of internal intuition (time), in which all our perceptions must have place.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0044","text":"That something happens, then, is a perception which belongs to a possible experience, which becomes real only because I look upon the phenomenon as determined in regard to its place in time, consequently as an object, which can always be found by means of a rule in the connected series of my perceptions. But this rule of the determination of a thing according to succession in time is as follows: “In what precedes may be found the condition, under which an event always (that is, necessarily) follows.” From all this it is obvious that the principle of cause and effect is the principle of possible experience, that is, of objective cognition of phenomena, in regard to their relations in the succession of time.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0045","text":"The proof of this fundamental proposition rests entirely on the following momenta of argument. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, a synthesis which is always successive, that is, in which the representations therein always follow one another. But the order of succession in imagination is not determined, and the series of successive representations may be taken retrogressively as well as progressively. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given phenomenon), then the order is determined in the object, or to speak more accurately, there is therein an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, and according to which something necessarily precedes, and when this is posited, something else necessarily follows. If, then, my perception is to contain the cognition of an event, that is, of something which really happens, it must be an empirical judgement, wherein we think that the succession is determined; that is, it presupposes another phenomenon, upon which this event follows necessarily, or in conformity with a rule. If, on the contrary, when I posited the antecedent, the event did not necessarily follow, I should be obliged to consider it merely as a subjective play of my imagination, and if in this I represented to myself anything as objective, I must look upon it as a mere dream. Thus, the relation of phenomena (as possible perceptions), according to which that which happens is, as to its existence, necessarily determined in time by something which antecedes, in conformity with a rule—in other words, the relation of cause and effect—is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgements in regard to the sequence of perceptions, consequently of their empirical truth, and therefore of experience. The principle of the relation of causality in the succession of phenomena is therefore valid for all objects of experience, because it is itself the ground of the possibility of experience.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Here, however, a difficulty arises, which must be resolved. The principle of the connection of causality among phenomena is limited in our formula to the succession thereof, although in practice we find that the principle applies also when the phenomena exist together in the same time, and that cause and effect may be simultaneous. For example, there is heat in a room, which does not exist in the open air. I look about for the cause, and find it to be the fire, Now the fire as the cause is simultaneous with its effect, the heat of the room. In this case, then, there is no succession as regards time, between cause and effect, but they are simultaneous; and still the law holds good. The greater part of operating causes in nature are simultaneous with their effects, and the succession in time of the latter is produced only because the cause cannot achieve the total of its effect in one moment. But at the moment when the effect first arises, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause, because, if the cause had but a moment before ceased to be, the effect could not have arisen. Here it must be specially remembered that we must consider the order of time and not the lapse thereof. The relation remains, even though no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may entirely vanish, and the cause and effect be thus simultaneous, but the relation of the one to the other remains always determinable according to time. If, for example, I consider a leaden ball, which lies upon a cushion and makes a hollow in it, as a cause, then it is simultaneous with the effect. But I distinguish the two through the relation of time of the dynamical connection of both. For if I lay the ball upon the cushion, then the hollow follows upon the before smooth surface; but supposing the cushion has, from some cause or another, a hollow, there does not thereupon follow a leaden ball.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0047","text":"Thus, the law of succession of time is in all instances the only empirical criterion of effect in relation to the causality of the antecedent cause. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two phenomena are contemporaneous. For, as soon as I draw some water with the glass from a larger vessel, an effect follows thereupon, namely, the change of the horizontal state which the water had in the large vessel into a concave, which it assumes in the glass.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0048","text":"This conception of causality leads us to the conception of action; that of action, to the conception of force; and through it, to the conception of substance. As I do not wish this critical essay, the sole purpose of which is to treat of the sources of our synthetical cognition à priori, to be crowded with analyses which merely explain, but do not enlarge the sphere of our conceptions, I reserve the detailed explanation of the above conceptions for a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis, indeed, executed with great particularity, may already be found in well-known works on this subject. But I cannot at present refrain from making a few remarks on the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as it seems to be more evident and more easily recognized through the conception of action than through that of the permanence of a phenomenon.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0049","text":"Where action (consequently activity and force) exists, substance also must exist, and in it alone must be sought the seat of that fruitful source of phenomena. Very well. But if we are called upon to explain what we mean by substance, and wish to avoid the vice of reasoning in a circle, the answer is by no means so easy. How shall we conclude immediately from the action to the permanence of that which acts, this being nevertheless an essential and peculiar criterion of substance (phenomenon)? But after what has been said above, the solution of this question becomes easy enough, although by the common mode of procedure—merely analysing our conceptions—it would be quite impossible. The conception of action indicates the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now because all effect consists in that which happens, therefore in the changeable, the last subject thereof is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change in phenomena and, consequently, cannot be a property of a subject which itself changes, because if this were the case, other actions and another subject would be necessary to determine this change. From all this it results that action alone, as an empirical criterion, is a sufficient proof of the presence of substantiality, without any necessity on my part of endeavouring to discover the permanence of substance by a comparison. Besides, by this mode of induction we could not attain to the completeness which the magnitude and strict universality of the conception requires. For that the primary subject of the causality of all arising and passing away, all origin and extinction, cannot itself (in the sphere of phenomena) arise and pass away, is a sound and safe conclusion, a conclusion which leads us to the conception of empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and consequently to the conception of a substance as phenomenon.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0050","text":"When something happens, the mere fact of the occurrence, without regard to that which occurs, is an object requiring investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into the existence of it, supposing that this state contains no quality which previously existed in the phenomenon, is a fact of itself demanding inquiry. Such an event, as has been shown in No. A, does not concern substance (for substance does not thus originate), but its condition or state. It is therefore only change, and not origin from nothing. If this origin be regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is termed creation, which cannot be admitted as an event among phenomena, because the very possibility of it would annihilate the unity of experience. If, however, I regard all things not as phenomena, but as things in themselves and objects of understanding alone, they, although substances, may be considered as dependent, in respect of their existence, on a foreign cause. But this would require a very different meaning in the words, a meaning which could not apply to phenomena as objects of possible experience.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0051","text":"How a thing can be changed, how it is possible that upon one state existing in one point of time, an opposite state should follow in another point of time—of this we have not the smallest conception à priori. There is requisite for this the knowledge of real powers, which can only be given empirically; for example, knowledge of moving forces, or, in other words, of certain successive phenomena (as movements) which indicate the presence of such forces. But the form of every change, the condition under which alone it can take place as the coming into existence of another state (be the content of the change, that is, the state which is changed, what it may), and consequently the succession of the states themselves can very well be considered à priori, in relation to the law of causality and the conditions of time.[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-32)","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0052","text":"[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-32) It must be remarked that I do not speak of the change of certain relations, but of the change of the state. Thus, when a body moves in a uniform manner, it does not change its state (of motion); but only when all motion increases or decreases.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0053","text":"When a substance passes from one state, a, into another state, b, the point of time in which the latter exists is different from, and subsequent to that in which the former existed. In like manner, the second state, as reality (in the phenomenon), differs from the first, in which the reality of the second did not exist, as b from zero. That is to say, if the state, b, differs from the state, a, only in respect to quantity, the change is a coming into existence of b -a, which in the former state did not exist, and in relation to which that state is = O.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Now the question arises how a thing passes from one state = a, into another state = b. Between two moments there is always a certain time, and between two states existing in these moments there is always a difference having a certain quantity (for all parts of phenomena are in their turn quantities). Consequently, every transition from one state into another, is always effected in a time contained between two moments, of which the first determines the state which the thing leaves, and the second determines the state into which the thing passes. Both moments, then, are limitations of the time of a change, consequently of the intermediate state between both, and as such they belong to the total of the change. Now every change has a cause, which evidences its causality in the whole time during which the charge takes place. The cause, therefore, does not produce the change all at once or in one moment, but in a time, so that, as the time gradually increases from the commencing instant, a, to its completion at b, in like manner also, the quantity of the reality (b - a) is generated through the lesser degrees which are contained between the first and last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of the causality, which, in so far as it is uniform, we call a momentum. The change does not consist of these momenta, but is generated or produced by them as their effect.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0055","text":"Such is the law of the continuity of all change, the ground of which is that neither time itself nor any phenomenon in time consists of parts which are the smallest possible, but that, notwithstanding, the state of a thing passes in the process of a change through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest degree of reality in a phenomenon, just as there is no smallest degree in the quantity of time; and so the new state of reality grows up out of the former state, through all the infinite degrees thereof, the differences of which one from another, taken all together, are less than the difference between 0 and a.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0056","text":"It is not our business to inquire here into the utility of this principle in the investigation of nature. But how such a proposition, which appears so greatly to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely à priori, is indeed a question which deserves investigation, although the first view seems to demonstrate the truth and reality of the principle, and the question, how it is possible, may be considered superfluous. For there are so many groundless pretensions to the enlargement of our knowledge by pure reason that we must take it as a general rule to be mistrustful of all such, and without a thoroughgoing and radical deduction, to believe nothing of the sort even on the clearest dogmatical evidence.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0057","text":"Every addition to our empirical knowledge, and every advance made in the exercise of our perception, is nothing more than an extension of the determination of the internal sense, that is to say, a progression in time, be objects themselves what they may, phenomena, or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing else. That is to say, the parts of the progression exist only in time, and by means of the synthesis thereof, and are not given antecedently to it. For this reason, every transition in perception to anything which follows upon another in time, is a determination of time by means of the production of this perception. And as this determination of time is, always and in all its parts, a quantity, the perception produced is to be considered as a quantity which proceeds through all its degrees—no one of which is the smallest possible—from zero up to its determined degree. From this we perceive the possibility of cognizing à priori a law of changes—a law, however, which concerns their form merely. We merely anticipate our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, inasmuch as it is itself to be found in the mind antecedently to all given phenomena, must certainly be capable of being cognized à priori.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0058","text":"Thus, as time contains the sensuous condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous progression of that which exists to that which follows it, the understanding, by virtue of the unity of apperception, contains the condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous determination of the position in time of all phenomena, and this by means of the series of causes and effects, the former of which necessitate the sequence of the latter, and thereby render universally and for all time, and by consequence, objectively, valid the empirical cognition of the relations of time.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0059","text":"C. THIRD ANALOGY.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0060","text":"Principle of Coexistence, According to the Law of Reciprocity or Community.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0061","text":"All substances, in so far as they can be perceived in space at the same time, exist in a state of complete reciprocity of action.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0062","text":"PROOF.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0063","text":"Things are coexistent, when in empirical intuition the perception of the one can follow upon the perception of the other, and vice versa—which cannot occur in the succession of phenomena, as we have shown in the explanation of the second principle. Thus I can perceive the moon and then the earth, or conversely, first the earth and then the moon; and for the reason that my perceptions of these objects can reciprocally follow each other, I say, they exist contemporaneously. Now coexistence is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But time itself is not an object of perception; and therefore we cannot conclude from the fact that things are placed in the same time, the other fact, that the perception of these things can follow each other reciprocally. The synthesis of the imagination in apprehension would only present to us each of these perceptions as present in the subject when the other is not present, and contrariwise; but would not show that the objects are coexistent, that is to say, that, if the one exists, the other also exists in the same time, and that this is necessarily so, in order that the perceptions may be capable of following each other reciprocally. It follows that a conception of the understanding or category of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations of phenomena (existing, as they do, apart from each other, and yet contemporaneously), is requisite to justify us in saying that the reciprocal succession of perceptions has its foundation in the object, and to enable us to represent coexistence as objective. But that relation of substances in which the one contains determinations the ground of which is in the other substance, is the relation of influence. And, when this influence is reciprocal, it is the relation of community or reciprocity. Consequently the coexistence of substances in space cannot be cognized in experience otherwise than under the precondition of their reciprocal action. This is therefore the condition of the possibility of things themselves as objects of experience.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0064","text":"Things are coexistent, in so far as they exist in one and the same time. But how can we know that they exist in one and the same time? Only by observing that the order in the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold is arbitrary and a matter of indifference, that is to say, that it can proceed from A, through B, C, D, to E, or contrariwise from E to A. For if they were successive in time (and in the order, let us suppose, which begins with A), it is quite impossible for the apprehension in perception to begin with E and go backwards to A, inasmuch as A belongs to past time and, therefore, cannot be an object of apprehension.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0065","text":"Let us assume that in a number of substances considered as phenomena each is completely isolated, that is, that no one acts upon another. Then I say that the coexistence of these cannot be an object of possible perception and that the existence of one cannot, by any mode of empirical synthesis, lead us to the existence of another. For we imagine them in this case to be separated by a completely void space, and thus perception, which proceeds from the one to the other in time, would indeed determine their existence by means of a following perception, but would be quite unable to distinguish whether the one phenomenon follows objectively upon the first, or is coexistent with it.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Besides the mere fact of existence, then, there must be something by means of which A determines the position of B in time and, conversely, B the position of A; because only under this condition can substances be empirically represented as existing contemporaneously. Now that alone determines the position of another thing in time which is the cause of it or of its determinations. Consequently every substance (inasmuch as it can have succession predicated of it only in respect of its determinations) must contain the causality of certain determinations in another substance, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other in itself. That is to say, substances must stand (mediately or immediately) in dynamical community with each other, if coexistence is to be cognized in any possible experience. But, in regard to objects of experience, that is absolutely necessary without which the experience of these objects would itself be impossible. Consequently it is absolutely necessary that all substances in the world of phenomena, in so far as they are coexistent, stand in a relation of complete community of reciprocal action to each other.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0067","text":"The word community has in our language[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-33) two meanings, and contains the two notions conveyed in the Latin communio and commercium. We employ it in this place in the latter sense—that of a dynamical community, without which even the community of place (communio spatii) could not be empirically cognized. In our experiences it is easy to observe that it is only the continuous influences in all parts of space that can conduct our senses from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eyes and the heavenly bodies produces a mediating community between them and us, and thereby evidences their coexistence with us; that we cannot empirically change our position (perceive this change), unless the existence of matter throughout the whole of space rendered possible the perception of the positions we occupy; and that this perception can prove the contemporaneous existence of these places only through their reciprocal influence, and thereby also the coexistence of even the most remote objects—although in this case the proof is only mediate. Without community, every perception (of a phenomenon in space) is separated from every other and isolated, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, of experience, must, with the appearance of a new object, begin entirely de novo, without the least connection with preceding representations, and without standing towards these even in the relation of time. My intention here is by no means to combat the notion of empty space; for it may exist where our perceptions cannot exist, inasmuch as they cannot reach thereto, and where, therefore, no empirical perception of coexistence takes place. But in this case it is not an object of possible experience.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0068","text":"[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-33) German","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0069","text":"The following remarks may be useful in the way of explanation. In the mind, all phenomena, as contents of a possible experience, must exist in community (communio) of apperception or consciousness, and in so far as it is requisite that objects be represented as coexistent and connected, in so far must they reciprocally determine the position in time of each other and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest upon an objective basis, or to be applied to substances as phenomena, the perception of one substance must render possible the perception of another, and conversely. For otherwise succession, which is always found in perceptions as apprehensions, would be predicated of external objects, and their representation of their coexistence be thus impossible. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is to say, a real community (commercium) of substances, without which therefore the empirical relation of coexistence would be a notion beyond the reach of our minds. By virtue of this commercium, phenomena, in so far as they are apart from, and nevertheless in connection with each other, constitute a compositum reale. Such composita are possible in many different ways. The three dynamical relations then, from which all others spring, are those of inherence, consequence, and composition.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0070","text":"These, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing more than principles of the determination of the existence of phenomena in time, according to the three modi of this determination; to wit, the relation to time itself as a quantity (the quantity of existence, that is, duration), the relation in time as a series or succession, finally, the relation in time as the complex of all existence (simultaneity). This unity of determination in regard to time is thoroughly dynamical; that is to say, time is not considered as that in which experience determines immediately to every existence its position; for this is impossible, inasmuch as absolute time is not an object of perception, by means of which phenomena can be connected with each other. On the contrary, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of phenomena can receive synthetical unity as regards relations of time, determines for every phenomenon its position in time, and consequently à priori, and with validity for all and every time.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0071","text":"By nature, in the empirical sense of the word, we understand the totality of phenomena connected, in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, laws. There are therefore certain laws (which are moreover à priori) which make nature possible; and all empirical laws can exist only by means of experience, and by virtue of those primitive laws through which experience itself becomes possible. The purpose of the analogies is therefore to represent to us the unity of nature in the connection of all phenomena under certain exponents, the only business of which is to express the relation of time (in so far as it contains all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can exist in synthesis only according to rules. The combined expression of all is this: “All phenomena exist in one nature, and must so exist, inasmuch as without this à priori unity, no unity of experience, and consequently no determination of objects in experience, is possible.”","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0072","text":"As regards the mode of proof which we have employed in treating of these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiar character of it we must make one remark, which will at the same time be important as a guide in every other attempt to demonstrate the truth of intellectual and likewise synthetical propositions à priori. Had we endeavoured to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from conceptions; that is to say, had we employed this method in attempting to show that everything which exists, exists only in that which is permanent—that every thing or event presupposes the existence of something in a preceding state, upon which it follows in conformity with a rule—lastly, that in the manifold, which is coexistent, the states coexist in connection with each other according to a rule, all our labour would have been utterly in vain. For mere conceptions of things, analyse them as we may, cannot enable us to conclude from the existence of one object to the existence of another. What other course was left for us to pursue? This only, to demonstrate the possibility of experience as a cognition in which at last all objects must be capable of being presented to us, if the representation of them is to possess any objective reality. Now in this third, this mediating term, the essential form of which consists in the synthetical unity of the apperception of all phenomena, we found à priori conditions of the universal and necessary determination as to time of all existences in the world of phenomena, without which the empirical determination thereof as to time would itself be impossible, and we also discovered rules of synthetical unity à priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. For want of this method, and from the fancy that it was possible to discover a dogmatical proof of the synthetical propositions which are requisite in the empirical employment of the understanding, has it happened that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has been so often attempted, and always in vain. The other two analogies nobody has ever thought of, although they have always been silently employed by the mind,[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-34) because the guiding thread furnished by the categories was wanting, the guide which alone can enable us to discover every hiatus, both in the system of conceptions and of principles.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EXPE:DISPLAY-0073","text":"[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-34) The unity of the universe, in which all phenomena to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the admitted principle of the community of all substances which are coexistent. For were substances isolated, they could not as parts constitute a whole, and were their connection (reciprocal action of the manifold) not necessary from the very fact of coexistence, we could not conclude from the fact of the latter as a merely ideal relation to the former as a real one. We have, however, shown in its place that community is the proper ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition of coexistence, and that we may therefore properly reason from the latter to the former as its condition.","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"ec84d34cf70878597afa4cfc1c35e858f50ba1b4d4466dd5bb2d988cb68d675d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"original_title":"Experience","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI","title":"Empicisim","order":51,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_EMPI","text":"# Empicisim\n\n4. THE POSTULATES OF EMPIRICAL THOUGHT.\n\n5. That which agrees with the formal conditions (intuition and conception) of experience, is possible.\n\n6. That which coheres with the material conditions of experience (sensation), is real.\n\n7. That whose coherence with the real is determined according to universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessary.\n\nExplanation.\n\nThe categories of modality possess this peculiarity, that they do not in the least determine the object, or enlarge the conception to which they are annexed as predicates, but only express its relation to the faculty of cognition. Though my conception of a thing is in itself complete, I am still entitled to ask whether the object of it is merely possible, or whether it is also real, or, if the latter, whether it is also necessary. But hereby the object itself is not more definitely determined in thought, but the question is only in what relation it, including all its determinations, stands to the understanding and its employment in experience, to the empirical faculty of judgement, and to the reason of its application to experience.\n\nFor this very reason, too, the categories of modality are nothing more than explanations of the conceptions of possibility, reality, and necessity, as employed in experience, and at the same time, restrictions of all the categories to empirical use alone, not authorizing the transcendental employment of them. For if they are to have something more than a merely logical significance, and to be something more than a mere analytical expression of the form of thought, and to have a relation to things and their possibility, reality, or necessity, they must concern possible experience and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects of cognition can be given.\n\nThe postulate of the possibility of things requires also, that the conception of the things agree with the formal conditions of our experience in general. But this, that is to say, the objective form of experience, contains all the kinds of synthesis which are requisite for the cognition of objects. A conception which contains a synthesis must be regarded as empty and, without reference to an object, if its synthesis does not belong to experience—either as borrowed from it, and in this case it is called an empirical conception, or such as is the ground and à priori condition of experience (its form), and in this case it is a pure conception, a conception which nevertheless belongs to experience, inasmuch as its object can be found in this alone. For where shall we find the criterion or character of the possibility of an object which is cogitated by means of an à priori synthetical conception, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of empirical cognition of objects? That in such a conception no contradiction exists is indeed a necessary logical condition, but very far from being sufficient to establish the objective reality of the conception, that is, the possibility of such an object as is thought in the conception. Thus, in the conception of a figure which is contained within two straight lines, there is no contradiction, for the conceptions of two straight lines and of their junction contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility in such a case does not rest upon the conception in itself, but upon the construction of it in space, that is to say, upon the conditions of space and its determinations. But these have themselves objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain à priori the form of experience in general.\n\nAnd now we shall proceed to point out the extensive utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. When I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything in it which changes belongs merely to its state or condition, from such a conception alone I never can cognize that such a thing is possible. Or, if I represent to myself something which is so constituted that, when it is posited, something else follows always and infallibly, my thought contains no self-contradiction; but whether such a property as causality is to be found in any possible thing, my thought alone affords no means of judging. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) which are so constituted that the state or condition of one causes a change in the state of the other, and reciprocally; but whether such a relation is a property of things cannot be perceived from these conceptions, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these conceptions express à priori the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know that they possess objective reality, that is, transcendental truth; and that independent of experience, though not independent of all relation to form of an experience in general and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects can be empirically cognized.\n\nBut when we fashion to ourselves new conceptions of substances, forces, action, and reaction, from the material presented to us by perception, without following the example of experience in their connection, we create mere chimeras, of the possibility of which we cannot discover any criterion, because we have not taken experience for our instructress, though we have borrowed the conceptions from her. Such fictitious conceptions derive their character of possibility not, like the categories, à priori, as conceptions on which all experience depends, but only, à posteriori, as conceptions given by means of experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized à posteriori and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance which is permanently present in space, yet without filling it (like that tertium quid between matter and the thinking subject which some have tried to introduce into metaphysics), or a peculiar fundamental power of the mind of intuiting the future by anticipation (instead of merely inferring from past and present events), or, finally, a power of the mind to place itself in community of thought with other men, however distant they may be—these are conceptions the possibility of which has no ground to rest upon. For they are not based upon experience and its known laws; and, without experience, they are a merely arbitrary conjunction of thoughts, which, though containing no internal contradiction, has no claim to objective reality, neither, consequently, to the possibility of such an object as is thought in these conceptions. As far as concerns reality, it is self-evident that we cannot cogitate such a possibility in concreto without the aid of experience; because reality is concerned only with sensation, as the matter of experience, and not with the form of thought, with which we can no doubt indulge in shaping fancies.\n\nBut I pass by everything which derives its possibility from reality in experience, and I purpose treating here merely of the possibility of things by means of à priori conceptions. I maintain, then, that the possibility of things is not derived from such conceptions per se, but only when considered as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.\n\nIt seems, indeed, as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from the conception of it alone (which is certainly independent of experience); for we can certainly give to the conception a corresponding object completely à priori, that is to say, we can construct it. But as a triangle is only the form of an object, it must remain a mere product of the imagination, and the possibility of the existence of an object corresponding to it must remain doubtful, unless we can discover some other ground, unless we know that the figure can be cogitated under the conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now, the facts that space is a formal condition à priori of external experience, that the formative synthesis, by which we construct a triangle in imagination, is the very same as that we employ in the apprehension of a phenomenon for the purpose of making an empirical conception of it, are what alone connect the notion of the possibility of such a thing, with the conception of it. In the same manner, the possibility of continuous quantities, indeed of quantities in general, for the conceptions of them are without exception synthetical, is never evident from the conceptions in themselves, but only when they are considered as the formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience. And where, indeed, should we look for objects to correspond to our conceptions, if not in experience, by which alone objects are presented to us? It is, however, true that without antecedent experience we can cognize and characterize the possibility of things, relatively to the formal conditions, under which something is determined in experience as an object, consequently, completely à priori. But still this is possible only in relation to experience and within its limits.\n\nThe postulate concerning the cognition of the reality of things requires perception, consequently conscious sensation, not indeed immediately, that is, of the object itself, whose existence is to be cognized, but still that the object have some connection with a real perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which exhibit all kinds of real connection in experience.\n\nFrom the mere conception of a thing it is impossible to conclude its existence. For, let the conception be ever so complete, and containing a statement of all the determinations of the thing, the existence of it has nothing to do with all this, but only with the question whether such a thing is given, so that the perception of it can in every case precede the conception. For the fact that the conception of it precedes the perception, merely indicates the possibility of its existence; it is perception which presents matter to the conception, that is the sole criterion of reality. Prior to the perception of the thing, however, and therefore comparatively à priori, we are able to cognize its existence, provided it stands in connection with some perceptions according to the principles of the empirical conjunction of these, that is, in conformity with the analogies of perception. For, in this case, the existence of the supposed thing is connected with our perception in a possible experience, and we are able, with the guidance of these analogies, to reason in the series of possible perceptions from a thing which we do really perceive to the thing we do not perceive. Thus, we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attraction of the steel-filings by the magnet, although the constitution of our organs renders an immediate perception of this matter impossible for us. For, according to the laws of sensibility and the connected context of our perceptions, we should in an experience come also on an immediate empirical intuition of this matter, if our senses were more acute—but this obtuseness has no influence upon and cannot alter the form of possible experience in general. Our knowledge of the existence of things reaches as far as our perceptions, and what may be inferred from them according to empirical laws, extend. If we do not set out from experience, or do not proceed according to the laws of the empirical connection of phenomena, our pretensions to discover the existence of a thing which we do not immediately perceive are vain. Idealism, however, brings forward powerful objections to these rules for proving existence mediately. This is, therefore, the proper place for its refutation.\n\nREFUTATION OF IDEALISM.\n\nIdealism—I mean material idealism—is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space without us to be either () doubtful and indemonstrable, or (2) false and impossible. The first is the problematical idealism of Descartes, who admits the undoubted certainty of only one empirical assertion (assertio), to wit, “I am.” The second is the dogmatical idealism of Berkeley, who maintains that space, together with all the objects of which it is the inseparable condition, is a thing which is in itself impossible, and that consequently the objects in space are mere products of the imagination. The dogmatical theory of idealism is unavoidable, if we regard space as a property of things in themselves; for in that case it is, with all to which it serves as condition, a nonentity. But the foundation for this kind of idealism we have already destroyed in the transcendental æsthetic. Problematical idealism, which makes no such assertion, but only alleges our incapacity to prove the existence of anything besides ourselves by means of immediate experience, is a theory rational and evidencing a thorough and philosophical mode of thinking, for it observes the rule not to form a decisive judgement before sufficient proof be shown. The desired proof must therefore demonstrate that we have experience of external things, and not mere fancies. For this purpose, we must prove, that our internal and, to Descartes, indubitable experience is itself possible only under the previous assumption of external experience.\n\nTHEOREM.\n\nThe simple but empirically determined consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of external objects in space.\n\nPROOF\n\nI am conscious of my own existence as determined in time. All determination in regard to time presupposes the existence of something permanent in perception. But this permanent something cannot be something in me, for the very reason that my existence in time is itself determined by this permanent something. It follows that the perception of this permanent existence is possible only through a thing without me and not through the mere representation of a thing without me. Consequently, the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of real things external to me. Now, consciousness in time is necessarily connected with the consciousness of the possibility of this determination in time. Hence it follows that consciousness in time is necessarily connected also with the existence of things without me, inasmuch as the existence of these things is the condition of determination in time. That is to say, the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things without me.\n\nRemark I. The reader will observe, that in the foregoing proof the game which idealism plays is retorted upon itself, and with more justice. It assumed that the only immediate experience is internal and that from this we can only infer the existence of external things. But, as always happens, when we reason from given effects to determined causes, idealism has reasoned with too much haste and uncertainty, for it is quite possible that the cause of our representations may lie in ourselves, and that we ascribe it falsely to external things. But our proof shows that external experience is properly immediate,[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-35) that only by virtue of it—not, indeed, the consciousness of our own existence, but certainly the determination of our existence in time, that is, internal experience—is possible. It is true, that the representation “I am,” which is the expression of the consciousness which can accompany all my thoughts, is that which immediately includes the existence of a subject. But in this representation we cannot find any knowledge of the subject, and therefore also no empirical knowledge, that is, experience. For experience contains, in addition to the thought of something existing, intuition, and in this case it must be internal intuition, that is, time, in relation to which the subject must be determined. But the existence of external things is absolutely requisite for this purpose, so that it follows that internal experience is itself possible only mediately and through external experience.\n\n[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-35) The immediate consciousness of the existence of external things is, in the preceding theorem, not presupposed, but proved, by the possibility of this consciousness understood by us or not. The question as to the possibility of it would stand thus: “Have we an internal sense, but no external sense, and is our belief in external perception a mere delusion?” But it is evident that, in order merely to fancy to ourselves anything as external, that is, to present it to the sense in intuition we must already possess an external sense, and must thereby distinguish immediately the mere receptivity of an external intuition from the spontaneity which characterizes every act of imagination. For merely to imagine also an external sense, would annihilate the faculty of intuition itself which is to be determined by the imagination.\n\nRemark II. Now with this view all empirical use of our faculty of cognition in the determination of time is in perfect accordance. Its truth is supported by the fact that it is possible to perceive a determination of time only by means of a change in external relations (motion) to the permanent in space (for example, we become aware of the sun’s motion by observing the changes of his relation to the objects of this earth). But this is not all. We find that we possess nothing permanent that can correspond and be submitted to the conception of a substance as intuition, except matter. This idea of permanence is not itself derived from external experience, but is an à priori necessary condition of all determination of time, consequently also of the internal sense in reference to our own existence, and that through the existence of external things. In the representation “I,” the consciousness of myself is not an intuition, but a merely intellectual representation produced by the spontaneous activity of a thinking subject. It follows, that this “i” has not any predicate of intuition, which, in its character of permanence, could serve as correlate to the determination of time in the internal sense—in the same way as impenetrability is the correlate of matter as an empirical intuition.\n\nRemark III. From the fact that the existence of external things is a necessary condition of the possibility of a determined consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of external things involves the existence of these things, for their representations may very well be the mere products of the imagination (in dreams as well as in madness); though, indeed, these are themselves created by the reproduction of previous external perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the reality of external objects. The sole aim of our remarks has, however, been to prove that internal experience in general is possible only through external experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience be purely imaginary must be discovered from its particular determinations and by comparing these with the criteria of all real experience.\n\nFinally, as regards the third postulate, it applies to material necessity in existence, and not to merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of conceptions. Now as we cannot cognize completely à priori the existence of any object of sense, though we can do so comparatively à priori, that is, relatively to some other previously given existence—a cognition, however, which can only be of such an existence as must be contained in the complex of experience, of which the previously given perception is a part—the necessity of existence can never be cognized from conceptions, but always, on the contrary, from its connection with that which is an object of perception. But the only existence cognized, under the condition of other given phenomena, as necessary, is the existence of effects from given causes in conformity with the laws of causality. It is consequently not the necessity of the existence of things (as substances), but the necessity of the state of things that we cognize, and that not immediately, but by means of the existence of other states given in perception, according to empirical laws of causality. Hence it follows that the criterion of necessity is to be found only in the law of possible experience—that everything which happens is determined à priori in the phenomenon by its cause. Thus we cognize only the necessity of effects in nature, the causes of which are given us. Moreover, the criterion of necessity in existence possesses no application beyond the field of possible experience, and even in this it is not valid of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be considered as empirical effects, or as something that happens and has a beginning. Necessity, therefore, regards only the relations of phenomena according to the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility grounded thereon, of reasoning from some given existence (of a cause) à priori to another existence (of an effect). “Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary,” is a principle which subjects the changes that take place in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which nature herself could not possibly exist. Hence the proposition, “Nothing happens by blind chance (in mundo non datur casus),” is an à priori law of nature. The case is the same with the proposition, “Necessity in nature is not blind,” that is, it is conditioned, consequently intelligible necessity (non datur fatum). Both laws subject the play of change to “a nature of things (as phenomena),” or, which is the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, and through the understanding alone can changes belong to an experience, as the synthetical unity of phenomena. Both belong to the class of dynamical principles. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality—one of the analogies of experience. The latter belongs to the principles of modality, which to the determination of causality adds the conception of necessity, which is itself, however, subject to a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbids any leap in the series of phenomena regarded as changes (in mundo non datur saltus); and likewise, in the complex of all empirical intuitions in space, any break or hiatus between two phenomena (non datur hiatus)—for we can so express the principle, that experience can admit nothing which proves the existence of a vacuum, or which even admits it as a part of an empirical synthesis. For, as regards a vacuum or void, which we may cogitate as out and beyond the field of possible experience (the world), such a question cannot come before the tribunal of mere understanding, which decides only upon questions that concern the employment of given phenomena for the construction of empirical cognition. It is rather a problem for ideal reason, which passes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and aims at forming a judgement of that which surrounds and circumscribes it, and the proper place for the consideration of it is the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions, “In mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum,” as well as all principles of transcendental origin, we could very easily exhibit in their proper order, that is, in conformity with the order of the categories, and assign to each its proper place. But the already practised reader will do this for himself, or discover the clue to such an arrangement. But the combined result of all is simply this, to admit into the empirical synthesis nothing which might cause a break in or be foreign to the understanding and the continuous connection of all phenomena, that is, the unity of the conceptions of the understanding. For in the understanding alone is the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their assigned place, possible.\n\nWhether the field of possibility be greater than that of reality, and whether the field of the latter be itself greater than that of necessity, are interesting enough questions, and quite capable of synthetic solution, questions, however, which come under the jurisdiction of reason alone. For they are tantamount to asking whether all things as phenomena do without exception belong to the complex and connected whole of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part which therefore cannot be conjoined with any other phenomena—or, whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience? The understanding gives to experience, according to the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, the rules which alone make this experience possible. Other forms of intuition besides those of space and time, other forms of understanding besides the discursive forms of thought, or of cognition by means of conceptions, we can neither imagine nor make intelligible to ourselves; and even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, which is the only mode of cognition by which objects are presented to us. Whether other perceptions besides those which belong to the total of our possible experience, and consequently whether some other sphere of matter exists, the understanding has no power to decide, its proper occupation being with the synthesis of that which is given. Moreover, the poverty of the usual arguments which go to prove the existence of a vast sphere of possibility, of which all that is real (every object of experience) is but a small part, is very remarkable. “All real is possible”; from this follows naturally, according to the logical laws of conversion, the particular proposition: “Some possible is real.” Now this seems to be equivalent to: “Much is possible that is not real.” No doubt it does seem as if we ought to consider the sum of the possible to be greater than that of the real, from the fact that something must be added to the former to constitute the latter. But this notion of adding to the possible is absurd. For that which is not in the sum of the possible, and consequently requires to be added to it, is manifestly impossible. In addition to accordance with the formal conditions of experience, the understanding requires a connection with some perception; but that which is connected with this perception is real, even although it is not immediately perceived. But that another series of phenomena, in complete coherence with that which is given in perception, consequently more than one all-embracing experience is possible, is an inference which cannot be concluded from the data given us by experience, and still less without any data at all. That which is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible, is not possible in any respect. And yet we can find no more certain ground on which to base the discussion of the question whether the sphere of possibility is wider than that of experience.\n\nI have merely mentioned these questions, that in treating of the conception of the understanding, there might be no omission of anything that, in the common opinion, belongs to them. In reality, however, the notion of absolute possibility (possibility which is valid in every respect) is not a mere conception of the understanding, which can be employed empirically, but belongs to reason alone, which passes the bounds of all empirical use of the understanding. We have, therefore, contented ourselves with a merely critical remark, leaving the subject to be explained in the sequel.\n\nBefore concluding this fourth section, and at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, it seems proper to mention the reasons which induced me to term the principles of modality postulates. This expression I do not here use in the sense which some more recent philosophers, contrary to its meaning with mathematicians, to whom the word properly belongs, attach to it—that of a proposition, namely, immediately certain, requiring neither deduction nor proof. For if, in the case of synthetical propositions, however evident they may be, we accord to them without deduction, and merely on the strength of their own pretensions, unqualified belief, all critique of the understanding is entirely lost; and, as there is no want of bold pretensions, which the common belief (though for the philosopher this is no credential) does not reject, the understanding lies exposed to every delusion and conceit, without the power of refusing its assent to those assertions, which, though illegitimate, demand acceptance as veritable axioms. When, therefore, to the conception of a thing an à priori determination is synthetically added, such a proposition must obtain, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion.\n\nThe principles of modality are, however, not objectively synthetical, for the predicates of possibility, reality, and necessity do not in the least augment the conception of that of which they are affirmed, inasmuch as they contribute nothing to the representation of the object. But as they are, nevertheless, always synthetical, they are so merely subjectively. That is to say, they have a reflective power, and apply to the conception of a thing, of which, in other respects, they affirm nothing, the faculty of cognition in which the conception originates and has its seat. So that if the conception merely agree with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible; if it is in connection with perception, and determined thereby, the object is real; if it is determined according to conceptions by means of the connection of perceptions, the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore predicate of a conception nothing more than the procedure of the faculty of cognition which generated it. Now a postulate in mathematics is a practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis by which we present an object to ourselves, and produce the conception of it, for example—“With a given line, to describe a circle upon a plane, from a given point”; and such a proposition does not admit of proof, because the procedure, which it requires, is exactly that by which alone it is possible to generate the conception of such a figure. With the same right, accordingly, can we postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-36) the conception of a thing but merely indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of cognition.\n\n[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-36) When I think the reality of a thing, I do really think more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never contain more in reality than was contained in its complete possibility. But while the notion of possibility is merely the notion of a position of thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), reality is the conjunction of the thing with perception.\n\nGENERAL REMARK ON THE SYSTEM OF PRINCIPLES.\n\nIt is very remarkable that we cannot perceive the possibility of a thing from the category alone, but must always have an intuition, by which to make evident the objective reality of the pure conception of the understanding. Take, for example, the categories of relation. How (1) a thing can exist only as a subject, and not as a mere determination of other things, that is, can be substance; or how (2), because something exists, some other thing must exist, consequently how a thing can be a cause; or how (3), when several things exist, from the fact that one of these things exists, some consequence to the others follows, and reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can be possible—are questions whose solution cannot be obtained from mere conceptions. The very same is the case with the other categories; for example, how a thing can be of the same sort with many others, that is, can be a quantity, and so on. So long as we have not intuition we cannot know whether we do really think an object by the categories, and where an object can anywhere be found to cohere with them, and thus the truth is established, that the categories are not in themselves cognitions, but mere forms of thought for the construction of cognitions from given intuitions. For the same reason is it true that from categories alone no synthetical proposition can be made. For example: “In every existence there is substance,” that is, something that can exist only as a subject and not as mere predicate; or, “Everything is a quantity”—to construct propositions such as these, we require something to enable us to go out beyond the given conception and connect another with it. For the same reason the attempt to prove a synthetical proposition by means of mere conceptions, for example: “Everything that exists contingently has a cause,” has never succeeded. We could never get further than proving that, without this relation to conceptions, we could not conceive the existence of the contingent, that is, could not à priori through the understanding cognize the existence of such a thing; but it does not hence follow that this is also the condition of the possibility of the thing itself that is said to be contingent. If, accordingly; we look back to our proof of the principle of causality, we shall find that we were able to prove it as valid only of objects of possible experience, and, indeed, only as itself the principle of the possibility of experience, Consequently of the cognition of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere conceptions. That, however, the proposition: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause,” is evident to every one merely from conceptions, is not to be denied. But in this case the conception of the contingent is cogitated as involving not the category of modality (as that the non-existence of which can be conceived) but that of relation (as that which can exist only as the consequence of something else), and so it is really an identical proposition: “That which can exist only as a consequence, has a cause.” In fact, when we have to give examples of contingent existence, we always refer to changes, and not merely to the possibility of conceiving the opposite.[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-37) But change is an event, which, as such, is possible only through a cause, and considered per se its non-existence is therefore possible, and we become cognizant of its contingency from the fact that it can exist only as the effect of a cause. Hence, if a thing is assumed to be contingent, it is an analytical proposition to say, it has a cause.\n\n[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-37) We can easily conceive the non-existence of matter; but the ancients did not thence infer its contingency. But even the alternation of the existence and non-existence of a given state in a thing, in which all change consists, by no means proves the contingency of that state—the ground of proof being the reality of its opposite. For example, a body is in a state of rest after motion, but we cannot infer the contingency of the motion from the fact that the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is merely a logical and not a real opposite to the other. If we wish to demonstrate the contingency of the motion, what we ought to prove is that, instead of the motion which took place in the preceding point of time, it was possible for the body to have been then in rest, not, that it is afterwards in rest; for in this case, both opposites are perfectly consistent with each other.\n\nBut it is still more remarkable that, to understand the possibility of things according to the categories and thus to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, we require not merely intuitions, but external intuitions. If, for example, we take the pure conceptions of relation, we find that (1) for the purpose of presenting to the conception of substance something permanent in intuition corresponding thereto and thus of demonstrating the objective reality of this conception, we require an intuition (of matter) in space, because space alone is permanent and determines things as such, while time, and with it all that is in the internal sense, is in a state of continual flow; (2) in order to represent change as the intuition corresponding to the conception of causality, we require the representation of motion as change in space; in fact, it is through it alone that changes, the possibility of which no pure understanding can perceive, are capable of being intuited. Change is the connection of determinations contradictorily opposed to each other in the existence of one and the same thing. Now, how it is possible that out of a given state one quite opposite to it in the same thing should follow, reason without an example can not only not conceive, but cannot even make intelligible without intuition; and this intuition is the motion of a point in space; the existence of which in different spaces (as a consequence of opposite determinations) alone makes the intuition of change possible. For, in order to make even internal change cognitable, we require to represent time, as the form of the internal sense, figuratively by a line, and the internal change by the drawing of that line (motion), and consequently are obliged to employ external intuition to be able to represent the successive existence of ourselves in different states. The proper ground of this fact is that all change to be perceived as change presupposes something permanent in intuition, while in the internal sense no permanent intuition is to be found. Lastly, the objective possibility of the category of community cannot be conceived by mere reason, and consequently its objective reality cannot be demonstrated without an intuition, and that external in space. For how can we conceive the possibility of community, that is, when several substances exist, that some effect on the existence of the one follows from the existence of the other, and reciprocally, and therefore that, because something exists in the latter, something else must exist in the former, which could not be understood from its own existence alone? For this is the very essence of community—which is inconceivable as a property of things which are perfectly isolated. Hence, Leibnitz, in attributing to the substances of the world—as cogitated by the understanding alone—a community, required the mediating aid of a divinity; for, from their existence, such a property seemed to him with justice inconceivable. But we can very easily conceive the possibility of community (of substances as phenomena) if we represent them to ourselves as in space, consequently in external intuition. For external intuition contains in itself à priori formal external relations, as the conditions of the possibility of the real relations of action and reaction, and therefore of the possibility of community. With the same ease can it be demonstrated, that the possibility of things as quantities, and consequently the objective reality of the category of quantity, can be grounded only in external intuition, and that by its means alone is the notion of quantity appropriated by the internal sense. But I must avoid prolixity, and leave the task of illustrating this by examples to the reader’s own reflection.\n\nThe above remarks are of the greatest importance, not only for the confirmation of our previous confutation of idealism, but still more when the subject of self-cognition by mere internal consciousness and the determination of our own nature without the aid of external empirical intuitions is under discussion, for the indication of the grounds of the possibility of such a cognition.\n\nThe result of the whole of this part of the analytic of principles is, therefore: “All principles of the pure understanding are nothing more than à priori principles of the possibility of experience, and to experience alone do all à priori synthetical propositions apply and relate”; indeed, their possibility itself rests entirely on this relation.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","text":"# Empicisim\n\n4. THE POSTULATES OF EMPIRICAL THOUGHT.\n\n5. That which agrees with the formal conditions (intuition and conception) of experience, is possible.\n\n6. That which coheres with the material conditions of experience (sensation), is real.\n\n7. That whose coherence with the real is determined according to universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessary.\n\nExplanation.\n\nThe categories of modality possess this peculiarity, that they do not in the least determine the object, or enlarge the conception to which they are annexed as predicates, but only express its relation to the faculty of cognition. Though my conception of a thing is in itself complete, I am still entitled to ask whether the object of it is merely possible, or whether it is also real, or, if the latter, whether it is also necessary. But hereby the object itself is not more definitely determined in thought, but the question is only in what relation it, including all its determinations, stands to the understanding and its employment in experience, to the empirical faculty of judgement, and to the reason of its application to experience.\n\nFor this very reason, too, the categories of modality are nothing more than explanations of the conceptions of possibility, reality, and necessity, as employed in experience, and at the same time, restrictions of all the categories to empirical use alone, not authorizing the transcendental employment of them. For if they are to have something more than a merely logical significance, and to be something more than a mere analytical expression of the form of thought, and to have a relation to things and their possibility, reality, or necessity, they must concern possible experience and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects of cognition can be given.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","text":"The postulate of the possibility of things requires also, that the conception of the things agree with the formal conditions of our experience in general. But this, that is to say, the objective form of experience, contains all the kinds of synthesis which are requisite for the cognition of objects. A conception which contains a synthesis must be regarded as empty and, without reference to an object, if its synthesis does not belong to experience—either as borrowed from it, and in this case it is called an empirical conception, or such as is the ground and à priori condition of experience (its form), and in this case it is a pure conception, a conception which nevertheless belongs to experience, inasmuch as its object can be found in this alone. For where shall we find the criterion or character of the possibility of an object which is cogitated by means of an à priori synthetical conception, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of empirical cognition of objects? That in such a conception no contradiction exists is indeed a necessary logical condition, but very far from being sufficient to establish the objective reality of the conception, that is, the possibility of such an object as is thought in the conception. Thus, in the conception of a figure which is contained within two straight lines, there is no contradiction, for the conceptions of two straight lines and of their junction contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility in such a case does not rest upon the conception in itself, but upon the construction of it in space, that is to say, upon the conditions of space and its determinations. But these have themselves objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain à priori the form of experience in general.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0003","text":"And now we shall proceed to point out the extensive utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. When I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything in it which changes belongs merely to its state or condition, from such a conception alone I never can cognize that such a thing is possible. Or, if I represent to myself something which is so constituted that, when it is posited, something else follows always and infallibly, my thought contains no self-contradiction; but whether such a property as causality is to be found in any possible thing, my thought alone affords no means of judging. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) which are so constituted that the state or condition of one causes a change in the state of the other, and reciprocally; but whether such a relation is a property of things cannot be perceived from these conceptions, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these conceptions express à priori the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know that they possess objective reality, that is, transcendental truth; and that independent of experience, though not independent of all relation to form of an experience in general and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects can be empirically cognized.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0004","text":"But when we fashion to ourselves new conceptions of substances, forces, action, and reaction, from the material presented to us by perception, without following the example of experience in their connection, we create mere chimeras, of the possibility of which we cannot discover any criterion, because we have not taken experience for our instructress, though we have borrowed the conceptions from her. Such fictitious conceptions derive their character of possibility not, like the categories, à priori, as conceptions on which all experience depends, but only, à posteriori, as conceptions given by means of experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized à posteriori and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance which is permanently present in space, yet without filling it (like that tertium quid between matter and the thinking subject which some have tried to introduce into metaphysics), or a peculiar fundamental power of the mind of intuiting the future by anticipation (instead of merely inferring from past and present events), or, finally, a power of the mind to place itself in community of thought with other men, however distant they may be—these are conceptions the possibility of which has no ground to rest upon. For they are not based upon experience and its known laws; and, without experience, they are a merely arbitrary conjunction of thoughts, which, though containing no internal contradiction, has no claim to objective reality, neither, consequently, to the possibility of such an object as is thought in these conceptions. As far as concerns reality, it is self-evident that we cannot cogitate such a possibility in concreto without the aid of experience; because reality is concerned only with sensation, as the matter of experience, and not with the form of thought, with which we can no doubt indulge in shaping fancies.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0005","text":"But I pass by everything which derives its possibility from reality in experience, and I purpose treating here merely of the possibility of things by means of à priori conceptions. I maintain, then, that the possibility of things is not derived from such conceptions per se, but only when considered as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.\n\nIt seems, indeed, as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from the conception of it alone (which is certainly independent of experience); for we can certainly give to the conception a corresponding object completely à priori, that is to say, we can construct it. But as a triangle is only the form of an object, it must remain a mere product of the imagination, and the possibility of the existence of an object corresponding to it must remain doubtful, unless we can discover some other ground, unless we know that the figure can be cogitated under the conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now, the facts that space is a formal condition à priori of external experience, that the formative synthesis, by which we construct a triangle in imagination, is the very same as that we employ in the apprehension of a phenomenon for the purpose of making an empirical conception of it, are what alone connect the notion of the possibility of such a thing, with the conception of it. In the same manner, the possibility of continuous quantities, indeed of quantities in general, for the conceptions of them are without exception synthetical, is never evident from the conceptions in themselves, but only when they are considered as the formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience. And where, indeed, should we look for objects to correspond to our conceptions, if not in experience, by which alone objects are presented to us? It is, however, true that without antecedent experience we can cognize and characterize the possibility of things, relatively to the formal conditions, under which something is determined in experience as an object, consequently, completely à priori. But still this is possible only in relation to experience and within its limits.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","text":"The postulate concerning the cognition of the reality of things requires perception, consequently conscious sensation, not indeed immediately, that is, of the object itself, whose existence is to be cognized, but still that the object have some connection with a real perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which exhibit all kinds of real connection in experience.\n\nFrom the mere conception of a thing it is impossible to conclude its existence. For, let the conception be ever so complete, and containing a statement of all the determinations of the thing, the existence of it has nothing to do with all this, but only with the question whether such a thing is given, so that the perception of it can in every case precede the conception. For the fact that the conception of it precedes the perception, merely indicates the possibility of its existence; it is perception which presents matter to the conception, that is the sole criterion of reality. Prior to the perception of the thing, however, and therefore comparatively à priori, we are able to cognize its existence, provided it stands in connection with some perceptions according to the principles of the empirical conjunction of these, that is, in conformity with the analogies of perception. For, in this case, the existence of the supposed thing is connected with our perception in a possible experience, and we are able, with the guidance of these analogies, to reason in the series of possible perceptions from a thing which we do really perceive to the thing we do not perceive. Thus, we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attraction of the steel-filings by the magnet, although the constitution of our organs renders an immediate perception of this matter impossible for us. For, according to the laws of sensibility and the connected context of our perceptions, we should in an experience come also on an immediate empirical intuition of this matter, if our senses were more acute—but this obtuseness has no influence upon and cannot alter the form of possible experience in general. Our knowledge of the existence of things reaches as far as our perceptions, and what may be inferred from them according to empirical laws, extend. If we do not set out from experience, or do not proceed according to the laws of the empirical connection of phenomena, our pretensions to discover the existence of a thing which we do not immediately perceive are vain. Idealism, however, brings forward powerful objections to these rules for proving existence mediately. This is, therefore, the proper place for its refutation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0007","text":"REFUTATION OF IDEALISM.\n\nIdealism—I mean material idealism—is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space without us to be either () doubtful and indemonstrable, or (2) false and impossible. The first is the problematical idealism of Descartes, who admits the undoubted certainty of only one empirical assertion (assertio), to wit, “I am.” The second is the dogmatical idealism of Berkeley, who maintains that space, together with all the objects of which it is the inseparable condition, is a thing which is in itself impossible, and that consequently the objects in space are mere products of the imagination. The dogmatical theory of idealism is unavoidable, if we regard space as a property of things in themselves; for in that case it is, with all to which it serves as condition, a nonentity. But the foundation for this kind of idealism we have already destroyed in the transcendental æsthetic. Problematical idealism, which makes no such assertion, but only alleges our incapacity to prove the existence of anything besides ourselves by means of immediate experience, is a theory rational and evidencing a thorough and philosophical mode of thinking, for it observes the rule not to form a decisive judgement before sufficient proof be shown. The desired proof must therefore demonstrate that we have experience of external things, and not mere fancies. For this purpose, we must prove, that our internal and, to Descartes, indubitable experience is itself possible only under the previous assumption of external experience.\n\nTHEOREM.\n\nThe simple but empirically determined consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of external objects in space.\n\nPROOF","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","text":"I am conscious of my own existence as determined in time. All determination in regard to time presupposes the existence of something permanent in perception. But this permanent something cannot be something in me, for the very reason that my existence in time is itself determined by this permanent something. It follows that the perception of this permanent existence is possible only through a thing without me and not through the mere representation of a thing without me. Consequently, the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of real things external to me. Now, consciousness in time is necessarily connected with the consciousness of the possibility of this determination in time. Hence it follows that consciousness in time is necessarily connected also with the existence of things without me, inasmuch as the existence of these things is the condition of determination in time. That is to say, the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things without me.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","text":"Remark I. The reader will observe, that in the foregoing proof the game which idealism plays is retorted upon itself, and with more justice. It assumed that the only immediate experience is internal and that from this we can only infer the existence of external things. But, as always happens, when we reason from given effects to determined causes, idealism has reasoned with too much haste and uncertainty, for it is quite possible that the cause of our representations may lie in ourselves, and that we ascribe it falsely to external things. But our proof shows that external experience is properly immediate,[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-35) that only by virtue of it—not, indeed, the consciousness of our own existence, but certainly the determination of our existence in time, that is, internal experience—is possible. It is true, that the representation “I am,” which is the expression of the consciousness which can accompany all my thoughts, is that which immediately includes the existence of a subject. But in this representation we cannot find any knowledge of the subject, and therefore also no empirical knowledge, that is, experience. For experience contains, in addition to the thought of something existing, intuition, and in this case it must be internal intuition, that is, time, in relation to which the subject must be determined. But the existence of external things is absolutely requisite for this purpose, so that it follows that internal experience is itself possible only mediately and through external experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0010","text":"[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-35) The immediate consciousness of the existence of external things is, in the preceding theorem, not presupposed, but proved, by the possibility of this consciousness understood by us or not. The question as to the possibility of it would stand thus: “Have we an internal sense, but no external sense, and is our belief in external perception a mere delusion?” But it is evident that, in order merely to fancy to ourselves anything as external, that is, to present it to the sense in intuition we must already possess an external sense, and must thereby distinguish immediately the mere receptivity of an external intuition from the spontaneity which characterizes every act of imagination. For merely to imagine also an external sense, would annihilate the faculty of intuition itself which is to be determined by the imagination.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","text":"Remark II. Now with this view all empirical use of our faculty of cognition in the determination of time is in perfect accordance. Its truth is supported by the fact that it is possible to perceive a determination of time only by means of a change in external relations (motion) to the permanent in space (for example, we become aware of the sun’s motion by observing the changes of his relation to the objects of this earth). But this is not all. We find that we possess nothing permanent that can correspond and be submitted to the conception of a substance as intuition, except matter. This idea of permanence is not itself derived from external experience, but is an à priori necessary condition of all determination of time, consequently also of the internal sense in reference to our own existence, and that through the existence of external things. In the representation “I,” the consciousness of myself is not an intuition, but a merely intellectual representation produced by the spontaneous activity of a thinking subject. It follows, that this “i” has not any predicate of intuition, which, in its character of permanence, could serve as correlate to the determination of time in the internal sense—in the same way as impenetrability is the correlate of matter as an empirical intuition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0012","text":"Remark III. From the fact that the existence of external things is a necessary condition of the possibility of a determined consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of external things involves the existence of these things, for their representations may very well be the mere products of the imagination (in dreams as well as in madness); though, indeed, these are themselves created by the reproduction of previous external perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the reality of external objects. The sole aim of our remarks has, however, been to prove that internal experience in general is possible only through external experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience be purely imaginary must be discovered from its particular determinations and by comparing these with the criteria of all real experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","text":"Finally, as regards the third postulate, it applies to material necessity in existence, and not to merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of conceptions. Now as we cannot cognize completely à priori the existence of any object of sense, though we can do so comparatively à priori, that is, relatively to some other previously given existence—a cognition, however, which can only be of such an existence as must be contained in the complex of experience, of which the previously given perception is a part—the necessity of existence can never be cognized from conceptions, but always, on the contrary, from its connection with that which is an object of perception. But the only existence cognized, under the condition of other given phenomena, as necessary, is the existence of effects from given causes in conformity with the laws of causality. It is consequently not the necessity of the existence of things (as substances), but the necessity of the state of things that we cognize, and that not immediately, but by means of the existence of other states given in perception, according to empirical laws of causality. Hence it follows that the criterion of necessity is to be found only in the law of possible experience—that everything which happens is determined à priori in the phenomenon by its cause. Thus we cognize only the necessity of effects in nature, the causes of which are given us. Moreover, the criterion of necessity in existence possesses no application beyond the field of possible experience, and even in this it is not valid of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be considered as empirical effects, or as something that happens and has a beginning. Necessity, therefore, regards only the relations of phenomena according to the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility grounded thereon, of reasoning from some given existence (of a cause) à priori to another existence (of an effect). “Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary,” is a principle which subjects the changes that take place in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which nature herself could not possibly exist. Hence the proposition, “Nothing happens by blind chance (in mundo non datur casus),” is an à priori law of nature. The case is the same with the proposition, “Necessity in nature is not blind,” that is, it is conditioned, consequently intelligible necessity (non datur fatum). Both laws subject the play of change to “a nature of things (as phenomena),” or, which is the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, and through the understanding alone can changes belong to an experience, as the synthetical unity of phenomena. Both belong to the class of dynamical principles. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality—one of the analogies of experience. The latter belongs to the principles of modality, which to the determination of causality adds the conception of necessity, which is itself, however, subject to a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbids any leap in the series of phenomena regarded as changes (in mundo non datur saltus); and likewise, in the complex of all empirical intuitions in space, any break or hiatus between two phenomena (non datur hiatus)—for we can so express the principle, that experience can admit nothing which proves the existence of a vacuum, or which even admits it as a part of an empirical synthesis. For, as regards a vacuum or void, which we may cogitate as out and beyond the field of possible experience (the world), such a question cannot come before the tribunal of mere understanding, which decides only upon questions that concern the employment of given phenomena for the construction of empirical cognition. It is rather a problem for ideal reason, which passes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and aims at forming a judgement of that which surrounds and circumscribes it, and the proper place for the consideration of it is the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions, “In mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum,” as well as all principles of transcendental origin, we could very easily exhibit in their proper order, that is, in conformity with the order of the categories, and assign to each its proper place. But the already practised reader will do this for himself, or discover the clue to such an arrangement. But the combined result of all is simply this, to admit into the empirical synthesis nothing which might cause a break in or be foreign to the understanding and the continuous connection of all phenomena, that is, the unity of the conceptions of the understanding. For in the understanding alone is the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their assigned place, possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","text":"Whether the field of possibility be greater than that of reality, and whether the field of the latter be itself greater than that of necessity, are interesting enough questions, and quite capable of synthetic solution, questions, however, which come under the jurisdiction of reason alone. For they are tantamount to asking whether all things as phenomena do without exception belong to the complex and connected whole of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part which therefore cannot be conjoined with any other phenomena—or, whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience? The understanding gives to experience, according to the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, the rules which alone make this experience possible. Other forms of intuition besides those of space and time, other forms of understanding besides the discursive forms of thought, or of cognition by means of conceptions, we can neither imagine nor make intelligible to ourselves; and even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, which is the only mode of cognition by which objects are presented to us. Whether other perceptions besides those which belong to the total of our possible experience, and consequently whether some other sphere of matter exists, the understanding has no power to decide, its proper occupation being with the synthesis of that which is given. Moreover, the poverty of the usual arguments which go to prove the existence of a vast sphere of possibility, of which all that is real (every object of experience) is but a small part, is very remarkable. “All real is possible”; from this follows naturally, according to the logical laws of conversion, the particular proposition: “Some possible is real.” Now this seems to be equivalent to: “Much is possible that is not real.” No doubt it does seem as if we ought to consider the sum of the possible to be greater than that of the real, from the fact that something must be added to the former to constitute the latter. But this notion of adding to the possible is absurd. For that which is not in the sum of the possible, and consequently requires to be added to it, is manifestly impossible. In addition to accordance with the formal conditions of experience, the understanding requires a connection with some perception; but that which is connected with this perception is real, even although it is not immediately perceived. But that another series of phenomena, in complete coherence with that which is given in perception, consequently more than one all-embracing experience is possible, is an inference which cannot be concluded from the data given us by experience, and still less without any data at all. That which is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible, is not possible in any respect. And yet we can find no more certain ground on which to base the discussion of the question whether the sphere of possibility is wider than that of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0015","text":"I have merely mentioned these questions, that in treating of the conception of the understanding, there might be no omission of anything that, in the common opinion, belongs to them. In reality, however, the notion of absolute possibility (possibility which is valid in every respect) is not a mere conception of the understanding, which can be employed empirically, but belongs to reason alone, which passes the bounds of all empirical use of the understanding. We have, therefore, contented ourselves with a merely critical remark, leaving the subject to be explained in the sequel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0016","text":"Before concluding this fourth section, and at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, it seems proper to mention the reasons which induced me to term the principles of modality postulates. This expression I do not here use in the sense which some more recent philosophers, contrary to its meaning with mathematicians, to whom the word properly belongs, attach to it—that of a proposition, namely, immediately certain, requiring neither deduction nor proof. For if, in the case of synthetical propositions, however evident they may be, we accord to them without deduction, and merely on the strength of their own pretensions, unqualified belief, all critique of the understanding is entirely lost; and, as there is no want of bold pretensions, which the common belief (though for the philosopher this is no credential) does not reject, the understanding lies exposed to every delusion and conceit, without the power of refusing its assent to those assertions, which, though illegitimate, demand acceptance as veritable axioms. When, therefore, to the conception of a thing an à priori determination is synthetically added, such a proposition must obtain, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0017","text":"The principles of modality are, however, not objectively synthetical, for the predicates of possibility, reality, and necessity do not in the least augment the conception of that of which they are affirmed, inasmuch as they contribute nothing to the representation of the object. But as they are, nevertheless, always synthetical, they are so merely subjectively. That is to say, they have a reflective power, and apply to the conception of a thing, of which, in other respects, they affirm nothing, the faculty of cognition in which the conception originates and has its seat. So that if the conception merely agree with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible; if it is in connection with perception, and determined thereby, the object is real; if it is determined according to conceptions by means of the connection of perceptions, the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore predicate of a conception nothing more than the procedure of the faculty of cognition which generated it. Now a postulate in mathematics is a practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis by which we present an object to ourselves, and produce the conception of it, for example—“With a given line, to describe a circle upon a plane, from a given point”; and such a proposition does not admit of proof, because the procedure, which it requires, is exactly that by which alone it is possible to generate the conception of such a figure. With the same right, accordingly, can we postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-36) the conception of a thing but merely indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0018","text":"[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-36) When I think the reality of a thing, I do really think more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never contain more in reality than was contained in its complete possibility. But while the notion of possibility is merely the notion of a position of thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), reality is the conjunction of the thing with perception.\n\nGENERAL REMARK ON THE SYSTEM OF PRINCIPLES.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","text":"It is very remarkable that we cannot perceive the possibility of a thing from the category alone, but must always have an intuition, by which to make evident the objective reality of the pure conception of the understanding. Take, for example, the categories of relation. How (1) a thing can exist only as a subject, and not as a mere determination of other things, that is, can be substance; or how (2), because something exists, some other thing must exist, consequently how a thing can be a cause; or how (3), when several things exist, from the fact that one of these things exists, some consequence to the others follows, and reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can be possible—are questions whose solution cannot be obtained from mere conceptions. The very same is the case with the other categories; for example, how a thing can be of the same sort with many others, that is, can be a quantity, and so on. So long as we have not intuition we cannot know whether we do really think an object by the categories, and where an object can anywhere be found to cohere with them, and thus the truth is established, that the categories are not in themselves cognitions, but mere forms of thought for the construction of cognitions from given intuitions. For the same reason is it true that from categories alone no synthetical proposition can be made. For example: “In every existence there is substance,” that is, something that can exist only as a subject and not as mere predicate; or, “Everything is a quantity”—to construct propositions such as these, we require something to enable us to go out beyond the given conception and connect another with it. For the same reason the attempt to prove a synthetical proposition by means of mere conceptions, for example: “Everything that exists contingently has a cause,” has never succeeded. We could never get further than proving that, without this relation to conceptions, we could not conceive the existence of the contingent, that is, could not à priori through the understanding cognize the existence of such a thing; but it does not hence follow that this is also the condition of the possibility of the thing itself that is said to be contingent. If, accordingly; we look back to our proof of the principle of causality, we shall find that we were able to prove it as valid only of objects of possible experience, and, indeed, only as itself the principle of the possibility of experience, Consequently of the cognition of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere conceptions. That, however, the proposition: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause,” is evident to every one merely from conceptions, is not to be denied. But in this case the conception of the contingent is cogitated as involving not the category of modality (as that the non-existence of which can be conceived) but that of relation (as that which can exist only as the consequence of something else), and so it is really an identical proposition: “That which can exist only as a consequence, has a cause.” In fact, when we have to give examples of contingent existence, we always refer to changes, and not merely to the possibility of conceiving the opposite.[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-37) But change is an event, which, as such, is possible only through a cause, and considered per se its non-existence is therefore possible, and we become cognizant of its contingency from the fact that it can exist only as the effect of a cause. Hence, if a thing is assumed to be contingent, it is an analytical proposition to say, it has a cause.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0020","text":"[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-37) We can easily conceive the non-existence of matter; but the ancients did not thence infer its contingency. But even the alternation of the existence and non-existence of a given state in a thing, in which all change consists, by no means proves the contingency of that state—the ground of proof being the reality of its opposite. For example, a body is in a state of rest after motion, but we cannot infer the contingency of the motion from the fact that the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is merely a logical and not a real opposite to the other. If we wish to demonstrate the contingency of the motion, what we ought to prove is that, instead of the motion which took place in the preceding point of time, it was possible for the body to have been then in rest, not, that it is afterwards in rest; for in this case, both opposites are perfectly consistent with each other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0021","text":"But it is still more remarkable that, to understand the possibility of things according to the categories and thus to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, we require not merely intuitions, but external intuitions. If, for example, we take the pure conceptions of relation, we find that (1) for the purpose of presenting to the conception of substance something permanent in intuition corresponding thereto and thus of demonstrating the objective reality of this conception, we require an intuition (of matter) in space, because space alone is permanent and determines things as such, while time, and with it all that is in the internal sense, is in a state of continual flow; (2) in order to represent change as the intuition corresponding to the conception of causality, we require the representation of motion as change in space; in fact, it is through it alone that changes, the possibility of which no pure understanding can perceive, are capable of being intuited. Change is the connection of determinations contradictorily opposed to each other in the existence of one and the same thing. Now, how it is possible that out of a given state one quite opposite to it in the same thing should follow, reason without an example can not only not conceive, but cannot even make intelligible without intuition; and this intuition is the motion of a point in space; the existence of which in different spaces (as a consequence of opposite determinations) alone makes the intuition of change possible. For, in order to make even internal change cognitable, we require to represent time, as the form of the internal sense, figuratively by a line, and the internal change by the drawing of that line (motion), and consequently are obliged to employ external intuition to be able to represent the successive existence of ourselves in different states. The proper ground of this fact is that all change to be perceived as change presupposes something permanent in intuition, while in the internal sense no permanent intuition is to be found. Lastly, the objective possibility of the category of community cannot be conceived by mere reason, and consequently its objective reality cannot be demonstrated without an intuition, and that external in space. For how can we conceive the possibility of community, that is, when several substances exist, that some effect on the existence of the one follows from the existence of the other, and reciprocally, and therefore that, because something exists in the latter, something else must exist in the former, which could not be understood from its own existence alone? For this is the very essence of community—which is inconceivable as a property of things which are perfectly isolated. Hence, Leibnitz, in attributing to the substances of the world—as cogitated by the understanding alone—a community, required the mediating aid of a divinity; for, from their existence, such a property seemed to him with justice inconceivable. But we can very easily conceive the possibility of community (of substances as phenomena) if we represent them to ourselves as in space, consequently in external intuition. For external intuition contains in itself à priori formal external relations, as the conditions of the possibility of the real relations of action and reaction, and therefore of the possibility of community. With the same ease can it be demonstrated, that the possibility of things as quantities, and consequently the objective reality of the category of quantity, can be grounded only in external intuition, and that by its means alone is the notion of quantity appropriated by the internal sense. But I must avoid prolixity, and leave the task of illustrating this by examples to the reader’s own reflection.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","text":"The above remarks are of the greatest importance, not only for the confirmation of our previous confutation of idealism, but still more when the subject of self-cognition by mere internal consciousness and the determination of our own nature without the aid of external empirical intuitions is under discussion, for the indication of the grounds of the possibility of such a cognition.\n\nThe result of the whole of this part of the analytic of principles is, therefore: “All principles of the pure understanding are nothing more than à priori principles of the possibility of experience, and to experience alone do all à priori synthetical propositions apply and relate”; indeed, their possibility itself rests entirely on this relation.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"148d178b7b7b8519aa16263b4eca42fb32c81eef73770c547f4c71dbafb8e12e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Empicisim","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD","title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.","order":52,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_TRAD","text":"# Second Division\n\n### SECOND DIVISION—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.  \nINTRODUCTION.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","text":"# Second Division\n\n### SECOND DIVISION—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.  \nINTRODUCTION.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7e6d13f0e46f52e05b2fb295ed571f415223fa1255d5370adc17fe3d31c6f4de","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":53,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ITIA","text":"### I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nWe termed dialectic in general a logic of appearance. This does not signify a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, only cognized upon insufficient grounds, and though the information it gives us is imperfect, it is not therefore deceitful. Hence it must not be separated from the analytical part of logic. Still less must phenomenon and appearance be held to be identical. For truth or illusory appearance does not reside in the object, in so far as it is intuited, but in the judgement upon the object, in so far as it is thought. It is, therefore, quite correct to say that the senses do not err, not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth and error, consequently also, illusory appearance as the cause of error, are only to be found in a judgement, that is, in the relation of an object to our understanding. In a cognition which completely harmonizes with the laws of the understanding, no error can exist. In a representation of the senses—as not containing any judgement—there is also no error. But no power of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding per se (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses per se, would fall into error; the former could not, because, if it acts only according to its own laws, the effect (the judgement) must necessarily accord with these laws. But in accordance with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element in all truth. In the senses there is no judgement—neither a true nor a false one. But, as we have no source of cognition besides these two, it follows that error is caused solely by the unobserved influence of the sensibility upon the understanding. And thus it happens that the subjective grounds of a judgement blend and are confounded with the objective, and cause them to deviate from their proper determination,[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-40) just as a body in motion would always of itself proceed in a straight line, but if another impetus gives to it a different direction, it will then start off into a curvilinear line of motion. To distinguish the peculiar action of the understanding from the power which mingles with it, it is necessary to consider an erroneous judgement as the diagonal between two forces, that determine the judgement in two different directions, which, as it were, form an angle, and to resolve this composite operation into the simple ones of the understanding and the sensibility. In pure à priori judgements this must be done by means of transcendental reflection, whereby, as has been already shown, each representation has its place appointed in the corresponding faculty of cognition, and consequently the influence of the one faculty upon the other is made apparent.\n\n[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-40) Sensibility, subjected to the understanding, as the object upon which the understanding employs its functions, is the source of real cognitions. But, in so far as it exercises an influence upon the action of the understanding and determines it to judgement, sensibility is itself the cause of error.\n\nIt is not at present our business to treat of empirical illusory appearance (for example, optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical application of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and in which the judgement is misled by the influence of imagination. Our purpose is to speak of transcendental illusory appearance, which influences principles—that are not even applied to experience, for in this case we should possess a sure test of their correctness—but which leads us, in disregard of all the warnings of criticism, completely beyond the empirical employment of the categories and deludes us with the chimera of an extension of the sphere of the pure understanding. We shall term those principles the application of which is confined entirely within the limits of possible experience, immanent; those, on the other hand, which transgress these limits, we shall call transcendent principles. But by these latter I do not understand principles of the transcendental use or misuse of the categories, which is in reality a mere fault of the judgement when not under due restraint from criticism, and therefore not paying sufficient attention to the limits of the sphere in which the pure understanding is allowed to exercise its functions; but real principles which exhort us to break down all those barriers, and to lay claim to a perfectly new field of cognition, which recognizes no line of demarcation. Thus transcendental and transcendent are not identical terms. The principles of the pure understanding, which we have already propounded, ought to be of empirical and not of transcendental use, that is, they are not applicable to any object beyond the sphere of experience. A principle which removes these limits, nay, which authorizes us to overstep them, is called transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these pretended principles, those which are limited in their employment to the sphere of experience may be called, in opposition to the others, immanent principles of the pure understanding.\n\nLogical illusion, which consists merely in the imitation of the form of reason (the illusion in sophistical syllogisms), arises entirely from a want of due attention to logical rules. So soon as the attention is awakened to the case before us, this illusion totally disappears. Transcendental illusion, on the contrary, does not cease to exist, even after it has been exposed, and its nothingness clearly perceived by means of transcendental criticism. Take, for example, the illusion in the proposition: “The world must have a beginning in time.” The cause of this is as follows. In our reason, subjectively considered as a faculty of human cognition, there exist fundamental rules and maxims of its exercise, which have completely the appearance of objective principles. Now from this cause it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our conceptions, is regarded as an objective necessity of the determination of things in themselves. This illusion it is impossible to avoid, just as we cannot avoid perceiving that the sea appears to be higher at a distance than it is near the shore, because we see the former by means of higher rays than the latter, or, which is a still stronger case, as even the astronomer cannot prevent himself from seeing the moon larger at its rising than some time afterwards, although he is not deceived by this illusion.\n\nTranscendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusory appearance in transcendental judgements, and guarding us against it; but to make it, as in the case of logical illusion, entirely disappear and cease to be illusion is utterly beyond its power. For we have here to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which rests upon subjective principles and imposes these upon us as objective, while logical dialectic, in the detection of sophisms, has to do merely with an error in the logical consequence of the propositions, or with an artificially constructed illusion, in imitation of the natural error. There is, therefore, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason—not that in which the bungler, from want of the requisite knowledge, involves himself, nor that which the sophist devises for the purpose of misleading, but that which is an inseparable adjunct of human reason, and which, even after its illusions have been exposed, does not cease to deceive, and continually to lead reason into momentary errors, which it becomes necessary continually to remove.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","text":"### I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nWe termed dialectic in general a logic of appearance. This does not signify a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, only cognized upon insufficient grounds, and though the information it gives us is imperfect, it is not therefore deceitful. Hence it must not be separated from the analytical part of logic. Still less must phenomenon and appearance be held to be identical. For truth or illusory appearance does not reside in the object, in so far as it is intuited, but in the judgement upon the object, in so far as it is thought. It is, therefore, quite correct to say that the senses do not err, not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth and error, consequently also, illusory appearance as the cause of error, are only to be found in a judgement, that is, in the relation of an object to our understanding. In a cognition which completely harmonizes with the laws of the understanding, no error can exist. In a representation of the senses—as not containing any judgement—there is also no error. But no power of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding per se (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses per se, would fall into error; the former could not, because, if it acts only according to its own laws, the effect (the judgement) must necessarily accord with these laws. But in accordance with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element in all truth. In the senses there is no judgement—neither a true nor a false one. But, as we have no source of cognition besides these two, it follows that error is caused solely by the unobserved influence of the sensibility upon the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","text":"And thus it happens that the subjective grounds of a judgement blend and are confounded with the objective, and cause them to deviate from their proper determination,[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-40) just as a body in motion would always of itself proceed in a straight line, but if another impetus gives to it a different direction, it will then start off into a curvilinear line of motion. To distinguish the peculiar action of the understanding from the power which mingles with it, it is necessary to consider an erroneous judgement as the diagonal between two forces, that determine the judgement in two different directions, which, as it were, form an angle, and to resolve this composite operation into the simple ones of the understanding and the sensibility. In pure à priori judgements this must be done by means of transcendental reflection, whereby, as has been already shown, each representation has its place appointed in the corresponding faculty of cognition, and consequently the influence of the one faculty upon the other is made apparent.\n\n[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-40) Sensibility, subjected to the understanding, as the object upon which the understanding employs its functions, is the source of real cognitions. But, in so far as it exercises an influence upon the action of the understanding and determines it to judgement, sensibility is itself the cause of error.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","text":"It is not at present our business to treat of empirical illusory appearance (for example, optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical application of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and in which the judgement is misled by the influence of imagination. Our purpose is to speak of transcendental illusory appearance, which influences principles—that are not even applied to experience, for in this case we should possess a sure test of their correctness—but which leads us, in disregard of all the warnings of criticism, completely beyond the empirical employment of the categories and deludes us with the chimera of an extension of the sphere of the pure understanding. We shall term those principles the application of which is confined entirely within the limits of possible experience, immanent; those, on the other hand, which transgress these limits, we shall call transcendent principles. But by these latter I do not understand principles of the transcendental use or misuse of the categories, which is in reality a mere fault of the judgement when not under due restraint from criticism, and therefore not paying sufficient attention to the limits of the sphere in which the pure understanding is allowed to exercise its functions; but real principles which exhort us to break down all those barriers, and to lay claim to a perfectly new field of cognition, which recognizes no line of demarcation. Thus transcendental and transcendent are not identical terms. The principles of the pure understanding, which we have already propounded, ought to be of empirical and not of transcendental use, that is, they are not applicable to any object beyond the sphere of experience. A principle which removes these limits, nay, which authorizes us to overstep them, is called transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these pretended principles, those which are limited in their employment to the sphere of experience may be called, in opposition to the others, immanent principles of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","text":"Logical illusion, which consists merely in the imitation of the form of reason (the illusion in sophistical syllogisms), arises entirely from a want of due attention to logical rules. So soon as the attention is awakened to the case before us, this illusion totally disappears. Transcendental illusion, on the contrary, does not cease to exist, even after it has been exposed, and its nothingness clearly perceived by means of transcendental criticism. Take, for example, the illusion in the proposition: “The world must have a beginning in time.” The cause of this is as follows. In our reason, subjectively considered as a faculty of human cognition, there exist fundamental rules and maxims of its exercise, which have completely the appearance of objective principles. Now from this cause it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our conceptions, is regarded as an objective necessity of the determination of things in themselves. This illusion it is impossible to avoid, just as we cannot avoid perceiving that the sea appears to be higher at a distance than it is near the shore, because we see the former by means of higher rays than the latter, or, which is a still stronger case, as even the astronomer cannot prevent himself from seeing the moon larger at its rising than some time afterwards, although he is not deceived by this illusion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","text":"Transcendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusory appearance in transcendental judgements, and guarding us against it; but to make it, as in the case of logical illusion, entirely disappear and cease to be illusion is utterly beyond its power. For we have here to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which rests upon subjective principles and imposes these upon us as objective, while logical dialectic, in the detection of sophisms, has to do merely with an error in the logical consequence of the propositions, or with an artificially constructed illusion, in imitation of the natural error. There is, therefore, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason—not that in which the bungler, from want of the requisite knowledge, involves himself, nor that which the sophist devises for the purpose of misleading, but that which is an inseparable adjunct of human reason, and which, even after its illusions have been exposed, does not cease to deceive, and continually to lead reason into momentary errors, which it becomes necessary continually to remove.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bcd0f86fc4976e35db8565538fdbbf57caddfa32697cf26529e7512c6681f45b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":54,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IPRS","text":"### II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nA. OF REASON IN GENERAL.\n\nAll our knowledge begins with sense, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher can be discovered in the human mind for elaborating the matter of intuition and subjecting it to the highest unity of thought. At this stage of our inquiry it is my duty to give an explanation of this, the highest faculty of cognition, and I confess I find myself here in some difficulty. Of reason, as of the understanding, there is a merely formal, that is, logical use, in which it makes abstraction of all content of cognition; but there is also a real use, inasmuch as it contains in itself the source of certain conceptions and principles, which it does not borrow either from the senses or the understanding. The former faculty has been long defined by logicians as the faculty of mediate conclusion in contradistinction to immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae); but the nature of the latter, which itself generates conceptions, is not to be understood from this definition. Now as a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty presents itself here, it becomes necessary to seek for a higher conception of this source of cognition which shall comprehend both conceptions. In this we may expect, according to the analogy of the conceptions of the understanding, that the logical conception will give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will present us with the clue to the conceptions of reason.\n\nIn the former part of our transcendental logic, we defined the understanding to be the faculty of rules; reason may be distinguished from understanding as the faculty of principles.\n\nThe term principle is ambiguous, and commonly signifies merely a cognition that may be employed as a principle, although it is not in itself, and as regards its proper origin, entitled to the distinction. Every general proposition, even if derived from experience by the process of induction, may serve as the major in a syllogism; but it is not for that reason a principle. Mathematical axioms (for example, there can be only one straight line between two points) are general à priori cognitions, and are therefore rightly denominated principles, relatively to the cases which can be subsumed under them. But I cannot for this reason say that I cognize this property of a straight line from principles—I cognize it only in pure intuition.\n\nCognition from principles, then, is that cognition in which I cognize the particular in the general by means of conceptions. Thus every syllogism is a form of the deduction of a cognition from a principle. For the major always gives a conception, through which everything that is subsumed under the condition thereof is cognized according to a principle. Now as every general cognition may serve as the major in a syllogism, and the understanding presents us with such general à priori propositions, they may be termed principles, in respect of their possible use.\n\nBut if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in relation to their origin, we shall find them to be anything rather than cognitions from conceptions. For they would not even be possible à priori, if we could not rely on the assistance of pure intuition (in mathematics), or on that of the conditions of a possible experience. That everything that happens has a cause, cannot be concluded from the general conception of that which happens; on the contrary the principle of causality instructs us as to the mode of obtaining from that which happens a determinate empirical conception.\n\nSynthetical cognitions from conceptions the understanding cannot supply, and they alone are entitled to be called principles. At the same time, all general propositions may be termed comparative principles.\n\nIt has been a long-cherished wish—that (who knows how late), may one day, be happily accomplished—that the principles of the endless variety of civil laws should be investigated and exposed; for in this way alone can we find the secret of simplifying legislation. But in this case, laws are nothing more than limitations of our freedom upon conditions under which it subsists in perfect harmony with itself; they consequently have for their object that which is completely our own work, and of which we ourselves may be the cause by means of these conceptions. But how objects as things in themselves—how the nature of things is subordinated to principles and is to be determined, according to conceptions, is a question which it seems well nigh impossible to answer. Be this, however, as it may—for on this point our investigation is yet to be made—it is at least manifest from what we have said that cognition from principles is something very different from cognition by means of the understanding, which may indeed precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, but in itself—in so far as it is synthetical—is neither based upon mere thought, nor contains a general proposition drawn from conceptions alone.\n\nThe understanding may be a faculty for the production of unity of phenomena by virtue of rules; the reason is a faculty for the production of unity of rules (of the understanding) under principles. Reason, therefore, never applies directly to experience, or to any sensuous object; its object is, on the contrary, the understanding, to the manifold cognition of which it gives a unity à priori by means of conceptions—a unity which may be called rational unity, and which is of a nature very different from that of the unity produced by the understanding.\n\nThe above is the general conception of the faculty of reason, in so far as it has been possible to make it comprehensible in the absence of examples. These will be given in the sequel.\n\nB. OF THE LOGICAL USE OF REASON.\n\nA distinction is commonly made between that which is immediately cognized and that which is inferred or concluded. That in a figure which is bounded by three straight lines there are three angles, is an immediate cognition; but that these angles are together equal to two right angles, is an inference or conclusion. Now, as we are constantly employing this mode of thought and have thus become quite accustomed to it, we no longer remark the above distinction, and, as in the case of the so-called deceptions of sense, consider as immediately perceived, what has really been inferred. In every reasoning or syllogism, there is a fundamental proposition, afterwards a second drawn from it, and finally the conclusion, which connects the truth in the first with the truth in the second—and that infallibly. If the judgement concluded is so contained in the first proposition that it can be deduced from it without the meditation of a third notion, the conclusion is called immediate (consequentia immediata); I prefer the term conclusion of the understanding. But if, in addition to the fundamental cognition, a second judgement is necessary for the production of the conclusion, it is called a conclusion of the reason. In the proposition: All men are mortal, are contained the propositions: Some men are mortal, Nothing that is not mortal is a man, and these are therefore immediate conclusions from the first. On the other hand, the proposition: all the learned are mortal, is not contained in the main proposition (for the conception of a learned man does not occur in it), and it can be deduced from the main proposition only by means of a mediating judgement.\n\nIn every syllogism I first cogitate a rule (the major) by means of the understanding. In the next place I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (and this is the minor) by means of the judgement. And finally I determine my cognition by means of the predicate of the rule (this is the conclusio), consequently, I determine it à priori by means of the reason. The relations, therefore, which the major proposition, as the rule, represents between a cognition and its condition, constitute the different kinds of syllogisms. These are just threefold—analogously with all judgements, in so far as they differ in the mode of expressing the relation of a cognition in the understanding—namely, categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive.\n\nWhen as often happens, the conclusion is a judgement which may follow from other given judgements, through which a perfectly different object is cogitated, I endeavour to discover in the understanding whether the assertion in this conclusion does not stand under certain conditions according to a general rule. If I find such a condition, and if the object mentioned in the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then this conclusion follows from a rule which is also valid for other objects of cognition. From this we see that reason endeavours to subject the great variety of the cognitions of the understanding to the smallest possible number of principles (general conditions), and thus to produce in it the highest unity.\n\nC. OF THE PURE USE OF REASON.\n\nCan we isolate reason, and, if so, is it in this case a peculiar source of conceptions and judgements which spring from it alone, and through which it can be applied to objects; or is it merely a subordinate faculty, whose duty it is to give a certain form to given cognitions—a form which is called logical, and through which the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated to each other, and lower rules to higher (those, to wit, whose condition comprises in its sphere the condition of the others), in so far as this can be done by comparison? This is the question which we have at present to answer. Manifold variety of rules and unity of principles is a requirement of reason, for the purpose of bringing the understanding into complete accordance with itself, just as understanding subjects the manifold content of intuition to conceptions, and thereby introduces connection into it. But this principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain any ground of the possibility of cognizing or of determining them as such, but is merely a subjective law for the proper arrangement of the content of the understanding. The purpose of this law is, by a comparison of the conceptions of the understanding, to reduce them to the smallest possible number, although, at the same time, it does not justify us in demanding from objects themselves such a uniformity as might contribute to the convenience and the enlargement of the sphere of the understanding, or in expecting that it will itself thus receive from them objective validity. In one word, the question is: “does reason in itself, that is, does pure reason contain à priori synthetical principles and rules, and what are those principles?”\n\nThe formal and logical procedure of reason in syllogisms gives us sufficient information in regard to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason in its pure synthetical cognition will rest.\n\n1. Reason, as observed in the syllogistic process, is not applicable to intuitions, for the purpose of subjecting them to rules—for this is the province of the understanding with its categories—but to conceptions and judgements. If pure reason does apply to objects and the intuition of them, it does so not immediately, but mediately—through the understanding and its judgements, which have a direct relation to the senses and their intuition, for the purpose of determining their objects. The unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this unity, which is that of the understanding. That everything which happens has a cause, is not a principle cognized and prescribed by reason. This principle makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without a reference to possible experience, could never have produced by means of mere conceptions any such synthetical unity.\n\n2. Reason, in its logical use, endeavours to discover the general condition of its judgement (the conclusion), and a syllogism is itself nothing but a judgement by means of the subsumption of its condition under a general rule (the major). Now as this rule may itself be subjected to the same process of reason, and thus the condition of the condition be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as long as the process can be continued, it is very manifest that the peculiar principle of reason in its logical use is to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned whereby the unity of the former is completed.\n\nBut this logical maxim cannot be a principle of pure reason, unless we admit that, if the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another—a series which is consequently itself unconditioned—is also given, that is, contained in the object and its connection.\n\nBut this principle of pure reason is evidently synthetical; for, analytically, the conditioned certainly relates to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. From this principle also there must originate different synthetical propositions, of which the pure understanding is perfectly ignorant, for it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the cognition and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, if it does really exist, must be especially considered in regard to the determinations which distinguish it from whatever is conditioned, and will thus afford us material for many à priori synthetical propositions.\n\nThe principles resulting from this highest principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in relation to phenomena, that is to say, it will be impossible to make any adequate empirical use of this principle. It is therefore completely different from all principles of the understanding, the use made of which is entirely immanent, their object and purpose being merely the possibility of experience. Now our duty in the transcendental dialectic is as follows. To discover whether the principle that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of phenomena, or of thought in general) extends to the unconditioned is objectively true, or not; what consequences result therefrom affecting the empirical use of the understanding, or rather whether there exists any such objectively valid proposition of reason, and whether it is not, on the contrary, a merely logical precept which directs us to ascend perpetually to still higher conditions, to approach completeness in the series of them, and thus to introduce into our cognition the highest possible unity of reason. We must ascertain, I say, whether this requirement of reason has not been regarded, by a misunderstanding, as a transcendental principle of pure reason, which postulates a thorough completeness in the series of conditions in objects themselves. We must show, moreover, the misconceptions and illusions that intrude into syllogisms, the major proposition of which pure reason has supplied—a proposition which has perhaps more of the character of a petitio than of a postulatum—and that proceed from experience upwards to its conditions. The solution of these problems is our task in transcendental dialectic, which we are about to expose even at its source, that lies deep in human reason. We shall divide it into two parts, the first of which will treat of the transcendent conceptions of pure reason, the second of transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","text":"### II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nA. OF REASON IN GENERAL.\n\nAll our knowledge begins with sense, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher can be discovered in the human mind for elaborating the matter of intuition and subjecting it to the highest unity of thought. At this stage of our inquiry it is my duty to give an explanation of this, the highest faculty of cognition, and I confess I find myself here in some difficulty. Of reason, as of the understanding, there is a merely formal, that is, logical use, in which it makes abstraction of all content of cognition; but there is also a real use, inasmuch as it contains in itself the source of certain conceptions and principles, which it does not borrow either from the senses or the understanding. The former faculty has been long defined by logicians as the faculty of mediate conclusion in contradistinction to immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae); but the nature of the latter, which itself generates conceptions, is not to be understood from this definition. Now as a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty presents itself here, it becomes necessary to seek for a higher conception of this source of cognition which shall comprehend both conceptions. In this we may expect, according to the analogy of the conceptions of the understanding, that the logical conception will give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will present us with the clue to the conceptions of reason.\n\nIn the former part of our transcendental logic, we defined the understanding to be the faculty of rules; reason may be distinguished from understanding as the faculty of principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0002","text":"The term principle is ambiguous, and commonly signifies merely a cognition that may be employed as a principle, although it is not in itself, and as regards its proper origin, entitled to the distinction. Every general proposition, even if derived from experience by the process of induction, may serve as the major in a syllogism; but it is not for that reason a principle. Mathematical axioms (for example, there can be only one straight line between two points) are general à priori cognitions, and are therefore rightly denominated principles, relatively to the cases which can be subsumed under them. But I cannot for this reason say that I cognize this property of a straight line from principles—I cognize it only in pure intuition.\n\nCognition from principles, then, is that cognition in which I cognize the particular in the general by means of conceptions. Thus every syllogism is a form of the deduction of a cognition from a principle. For the major always gives a conception, through which everything that is subsumed under the condition thereof is cognized according to a principle. Now as every general cognition may serve as the major in a syllogism, and the understanding presents us with such general à priori propositions, they may be termed principles, in respect of their possible use.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0003","text":"But if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in relation to their origin, we shall find them to be anything rather than cognitions from conceptions. For they would not even be possible à priori, if we could not rely on the assistance of pure intuition (in mathematics), or on that of the conditions of a possible experience. That everything that happens has a cause, cannot be concluded from the general conception of that which happens; on the contrary the principle of causality instructs us as to the mode of obtaining from that which happens a determinate empirical conception.\n\nSynthetical cognitions from conceptions the understanding cannot supply, and they alone are entitled to be called principles. At the same time, all general propositions may be termed comparative principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","text":"It has been a long-cherished wish—that (who knows how late), may one day, be happily accomplished—that the principles of the endless variety of civil laws should be investigated and exposed; for in this way alone can we find the secret of simplifying legislation. But in this case, laws are nothing more than limitations of our freedom upon conditions under which it subsists in perfect harmony with itself; they consequently have for their object that which is completely our own work, and of which we ourselves may be the cause by means of these conceptions. But how objects as things in themselves—how the nature of things is subordinated to principles and is to be determined, according to conceptions, is a question which it seems well nigh impossible to answer. Be this, however, as it may—for on this point our investigation is yet to be made—it is at least manifest from what we have said that cognition from principles is something very different from cognition by means of the understanding, which may indeed precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, but in itself—in so far as it is synthetical—is neither based upon mere thought, nor contains a general proposition drawn from conceptions alone.\n\nThe understanding may be a faculty for the production of unity of phenomena by virtue of rules; the reason is a faculty for the production of unity of rules (of the understanding) under principles. Reason, therefore, never applies directly to experience, or to any sensuous object; its object is, on the contrary, the understanding, to the manifold cognition of which it gives a unity à priori by means of conceptions—a unity which may be called rational unity, and which is of a nature very different from that of the unity produced by the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0005","text":"The above is the general conception of the faculty of reason, in so far as it has been possible to make it comprehensible in the absence of examples. These will be given in the sequel.\n\nB. OF THE LOGICAL USE OF REASON.\n\nA distinction is commonly made between that which is immediately cognized and that which is inferred or concluded. That in a figure which is bounded by three straight lines there are three angles, is an immediate cognition; but that these angles are together equal to two right angles, is an inference or conclusion. Now, as we are constantly employing this mode of thought and have thus become quite accustomed to it, we no longer remark the above distinction, and, as in the case of the so-called deceptions of sense, consider as immediately perceived, what has really been inferred. In every reasoning or syllogism, there is a fundamental proposition, afterwards a second drawn from it, and finally the conclusion, which connects the truth in the first with the truth in the second—and that infallibly. If the judgement concluded is so contained in the first proposition that it can be deduced from it without the meditation of a third notion, the conclusion is called immediate (consequentia immediata); I prefer the term conclusion of the understanding. But if, in addition to the fundamental cognition, a second judgement is necessary for the production of the conclusion, it is called a conclusion of the reason. In the proposition: All men are mortal, are contained the propositions: Some men are mortal, Nothing that is not mortal is a man, and these are therefore immediate conclusions from the first. On the other hand, the proposition: all the learned are mortal, is not contained in the main proposition (for the conception of a learned man does not occur in it), and it can be deduced from the main proposition only by means of a mediating judgement.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","text":"In every syllogism I first cogitate a rule (the major) by means of the understanding. In the next place I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (and this is the minor) by means of the judgement. And finally I determine my cognition by means of the predicate of the rule (this is the conclusio), consequently, I determine it à priori by means of the reason. The relations, therefore, which the major proposition, as the rule, represents between a cognition and its condition, constitute the different kinds of syllogisms. These are just threefold—analogously with all judgements, in so far as they differ in the mode of expressing the relation of a cognition in the understanding—namely, categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive.\n\nWhen as often happens, the conclusion is a judgement which may follow from other given judgements, through which a perfectly different object is cogitated, I endeavour to discover in the understanding whether the assertion in this conclusion does not stand under certain conditions according to a general rule. If I find such a condition, and if the object mentioned in the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then this conclusion follows from a rule which is also valid for other objects of cognition. From this we see that reason endeavours to subject the great variety of the cognitions of the understanding to the smallest possible number of principles (general conditions), and thus to produce in it the highest unity.\n\nC. OF THE PURE USE OF REASON.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","text":"Can we isolate reason, and, if so, is it in this case a peculiar source of conceptions and judgements which spring from it alone, and through which it can be applied to objects; or is it merely a subordinate faculty, whose duty it is to give a certain form to given cognitions—a form which is called logical, and through which the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated to each other, and lower rules to higher (those, to wit, whose condition comprises in its sphere the condition of the others), in so far as this can be done by comparison? This is the question which we have at present to answer. Manifold variety of rules and unity of principles is a requirement of reason, for the purpose of bringing the understanding into complete accordance with itself, just as understanding subjects the manifold content of intuition to conceptions, and thereby introduces connection into it. But this principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain any ground of the possibility of cognizing or of determining them as such, but is merely a subjective law for the proper arrangement of the content of the understanding. The purpose of this law is, by a comparison of the conceptions of the understanding, to reduce them to the smallest possible number, although, at the same time, it does not justify us in demanding from objects themselves such a uniformity as might contribute to the convenience and the enlargement of the sphere of the understanding, or in expecting that it will itself thus receive from them objective validity. In one word, the question is: “does reason in itself, that is, does pure reason contain à priori synthetical principles and rules, and what are those principles?”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","text":"The formal and logical procedure of reason in syllogisms gives us sufficient information in regard to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason in its pure synthetical cognition will rest.\n\n1. Reason, as observed in the syllogistic process, is not applicable to intuitions, for the purpose of subjecting them to rules—for this is the province of the understanding with its categories—but to conceptions and judgements. If pure reason does apply to objects and the intuition of them, it does so not immediately, but mediately—through the understanding and its judgements, which have a direct relation to the senses and their intuition, for the purpose of determining their objects. The unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this unity, which is that of the understanding. That everything which happens has a cause, is not a principle cognized and prescribed by reason. This principle makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without a reference to possible experience, could never have produced by means of mere conceptions any such synthetical unity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","text":"2. Reason, in its logical use, endeavours to discover the general condition of its judgement (the conclusion), and a syllogism is itself nothing but a judgement by means of the subsumption of its condition under a general rule (the major). Now as this rule may itself be subjected to the same process of reason, and thus the condition of the condition be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as long as the process can be continued, it is very manifest that the peculiar principle of reason in its logical use is to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned whereby the unity of the former is completed.\n\nBut this logical maxim cannot be a principle of pure reason, unless we admit that, if the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another—a series which is consequently itself unconditioned—is also given, that is, contained in the object and its connection.\n\nBut this principle of pure reason is evidently synthetical; for, analytically, the conditioned certainly relates to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. From this principle also there must originate different synthetical propositions, of which the pure understanding is perfectly ignorant, for it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the cognition and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, if it does really exist, must be especially considered in regard to the determinations which distinguish it from whatever is conditioned, and will thus afford us material for many à priori synthetical propositions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","text":"The principles resulting from this highest principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in relation to phenomena, that is to say, it will be impossible to make any adequate empirical use of this principle. It is therefore completely different from all principles of the understanding, the use made of which is entirely immanent, their object and purpose being merely the possibility of experience. Now our duty in the transcendental dialectic is as follows. To discover whether the principle that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of phenomena, or of thought in general) extends to the unconditioned is objectively true, or not; what consequences result therefrom affecting the empirical use of the understanding, or rather whether there exists any such objectively valid proposition of reason, and whether it is not, on the contrary, a merely logical precept which directs us to ascend perpetually to still higher conditions, to approach completeness in the series of them, and thus to introduce into our cognition the highest possible unity of reason. We must ascertain, I say, whether this requirement of reason has not been regarded, by a misunderstanding, as a transcendental principle of pure reason, which postulates a thorough completeness in the series of conditions in objects themselves. We must show, moreover, the misconceptions and illusions that intrude into syllogisms, the major proposition of which pure reason has supplied—a proposition which has perhaps more of the character of a petitio than of a postulatum—and that proceed from experience upwards to its conditions. The solution of these problems is our task in transcendental dialectic, which we are about to expose even at its source, that lies deep in human reason. We shall divide it into two parts, the first of which will treat of the transcendent conceptions of pure reason, the second of transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7561933c6aae1e63336dad8d1b21c02f8335ea82774893faefe01ff2db4db767","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI","title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.","order":55,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_TDBI","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.\n\nThe conceptions of pure reason—we do not here speak of the possibility of them—are not obtained by reflection, but by inference or conclusion. The conceptions of understanding are also cogitated à priori antecedently to experience, and render it possible; but they contain nothing but the unity of reflection upon phenomena, in so far as these must necessarily belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are cognition and the determination of an object possible. It is from them, accordingly, that we receive material for reasoning, and antecedently to them we possess no à priori conceptions of objects from which they might be deduced, On the other hand, the sole basis of their objective reality consists in the necessity imposed on them, as containing the intellectual form of all experience, of restricting their application and influence to the sphere of experience.\n\nBut the term, conception of reason, or rational conception, itself indicates that it does not confine itself within the limits of experience, because its object-matter is a cognition, of which every empirical cognition is but a part—nay, the whole of possible experience may be itself but a part of it—a cognition to which no actual experience ever fully attains, although it does always pertain to it. The aim of rational conceptions is the comprehension, as that of the conceptions of understanding is the understanding of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they relate to that to which all experience is subordinate, but which is never itself an object of experience—that towards which reason tends in all its conclusions from experience, and by the standard of which it estimates the degree of their empirical use, but which is never itself an element in an empirical synthesis. If, notwithstanding, such conceptions possess objective validity, they may be called conceptus ratiocinati (conceptions legitimately concluded); in cases where they do not, they have been admitted on account of having the appearance of being correctly concluded, and may be called conceptus ratiocinantes (sophistical conceptions). But as this can only be sufficiently demonstrated in that part of our treatise which relates to the dialectical conclusions of reason, we shall omit any consideration of it in this place. As we called the pure conceptions of the understanding categories, we shall also distinguish those of pure reason by a new name and call them transcendental ideas. These terms, however, we must in the first place explain and justify.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.\n\nThe conceptions of pure reason—we do not here speak of the possibility of them—are not obtained by reflection, but by inference or conclusion. The conceptions of understanding are also cogitated à priori antecedently to experience, and render it possible; but they contain nothing but the unity of reflection upon phenomena, in so far as these must necessarily belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are cognition and the determination of an object possible. It is from them, accordingly, that we receive material for reasoning, and antecedently to them we possess no à priori conceptions of objects from which they might be deduced, On the other hand, the sole basis of their objective reality consists in the necessity imposed on them, as containing the intellectual form of all experience, of restricting their application and influence to the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","text":"But the term, conception of reason, or rational conception, itself indicates that it does not confine itself within the limits of experience, because its object-matter is a cognition, of which every empirical cognition is but a part—nay, the whole of possible experience may be itself but a part of it—a cognition to which no actual experience ever fully attains, although it does always pertain to it. The aim of rational conceptions is the comprehension, as that of the conceptions of understanding is the understanding of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they relate to that to which all experience is subordinate, but which is never itself an object of experience—that towards which reason tends in all its conclusions from experience, and by the standard of which it estimates the degree of their empirical use, but which is never itself an element in an empirical synthesis. If, notwithstanding, such conceptions possess objective validity, they may be called conceptus ratiocinati (conceptions legitimately concluded); in cases where they do not, they have been admitted on account of having the appearance of being correctly concluded, and may be called conceptus ratiocinantes (sophistical conceptions). But as this can only be sufficiently demonstrated in that part of our treatise which relates to the dialectical conclusions of reason, we shall omit any consideration of it in this place. As we called the pure conceptions of the understanding categories, we shall also distinguish those of pure reason by a new name and call them transcendental ideas. These terms, however, we must in the first place explain and justify.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"06c9ead703e3994c2302fe783426482911f5a1b216e7bce4fa88f82a800ee443","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","order":56,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIIG","text":"### Section I—Of Ideas in General\n\nDespite the great wealth of words which European languages possess, the thinker finds himself often at a loss for an expression exactly suited to his conception, for want of which he is unable to make himself intelligible either to others or to himself. To coin new words is a pretension to legislation in language which is seldom successful; and, before recourse is taken to so desperate an expedient, it is advisable to examine the dead and learned languages, with the hope and the probability that we may there meet with some adequate expression of the notion we have in our minds. In this case, even if the original meaning of the word has become somewhat uncertain, from carelessness or want of caution on the part of the authors of it, it is always better to adhere to and confirm its proper meaning—even although it may be doubtful whether it was formerly used in exactly this sense—than to make our labour vain by want of sufficient care to render ourselves intelligible.\n\nFor this reason, when it happens that there exists only a single word to express a certain conception, and this word, in its usual acceptation, is thoroughly adequate to the conception, the accurate distinction of which from related conceptions is of great importance, we ought not to employ the expression improvidently, or, for the sake of variety and elegance of style, use it as a synonym for other cognate words. It is our duty, on the contrary, carefully to preserve its peculiar signification, as otherwise it easily happens that when the attention of the reader is no longer particularly attracted to the expression, and it is lost amid the multitude of other words of very different import, the thought which it conveyed, and which it alone conveyed, is lost with it.\n\nPlato employed the expression idea in a way that plainly showed he meant by it something which is never derived from the senses, but which far transcends even the conceptions of the understanding (with which Aristotle occupied himself), inasmuch as in experience nothing perfectly corresponding to them could be found. Ideas are, according to him, archetypes of things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his view they flow from the highest reason, by which they have been imparted to human reason, which, however, exists no longer in its original state, but is obliged with great labour to recall by reminiscence—which is called philosophy—the old but now sadly obscured ideas. I will not here enter upon any literary investigation of the sense which this sublime philosopher attached to this expression. I shall content myself with remarking that it is nothing unusual, in common conversation as well as in written works, by comparing the thoughts which an author has delivered upon a subject, to understand him better than he understood himself inasmuch as he may not have sufficiently determined his conception, and thus have sometimes spoken, nay even thought, in opposition to his own opinions.\n\nPlato perceived very clearly that our faculty of cognition has the feeling of a much higher vocation than that of merely spelling out phenomena according to synthetical unity, for the purpose of being able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally raises itself to cognitions far too elevated to admit of the possibility of an object given by experience corresponding to them—cognitions which are nevertheless real, and are not mere phantoms of the brain.\n\nThis philosopher found his ideas especially in all that is practical,[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-41) that is, which rests upon freedom, which in its turn ranks under cognitions that are the peculiar product of reason. He who would derive from experience the conceptions of virtue, who would make (as many have really done) that, which at best can but serve as an imperfectly illustrative example, a model for or the formation of a perfectly adequate idea on the subject, would in fact transform virtue into a nonentity changeable according to time and circumstance and utterly incapable of being employed as a rule. On the contrary, every one is conscious that, when any one is held up to him as a model of virtue, he compares this so-called model with the true original which he possesses in his own mind and values him according to this standard. But this standard is the idea of virtue, in relation to which all possible objects of experience are indeed serviceable as examples—proofs of the practicability in a certain degree of that which the conception of virtue demands—but certainly not as archetypes. That the actions of man will never be in perfect accordance with all the requirements of the pure ideas of reason, does not prove the thought to be chimerical. For only through this idea are all judgements as to moral merit or demerit possible; it consequently lies at the foundation of every approach to moral perfection, however far removed from it the obstacles in human nature—indeterminable as to degree—may keep us.\n\n[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-41) He certainly extended the application of his conception to speculative cognitions also, provided they were given pure and completely à priori, nay, even to mathematics, although this science cannot possess an object otherwhere than in Possible experience. I cannot follow him in this, and as little can I follow him in his mystical deduction of these ideas, or in his hypostatization of them; although, in truth, the elevated and exaggerated language which he employed in describing them is quite capable of an interpretation more subdued and more in accordance with fact and the nature of things.\n\nThe Platonic Republic has become proverbial as an example—and a striking one—of imaginary perfection, such as can exist only in the brain of the idle thinker; and Brucker ridicules the philosopher for maintaining that a prince can never govern well, unless he is participant in the ideas. But we should do better to follow up this thought and, where this admirable thinker leaves us without assistance, employ new efforts to place it in clearer light, rather than carelessly fling it aside as useless, under the very miserable and pernicious pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest possible human freedom according to laws, by which the liberty of every individual can consist with the liberty of every other (not of the greatest possible happiness, for this follows necessarily from the former), is, to say the least, a necessary idea, which must be placed at the foundation not only of the first plan of the constitution of a state, but of all its laws. And, in this, it not necessary at the outset to take account of the obstacles which lie in our way—obstacles which perhaps do not necessarily arise from the character of human nature, but rather from the previous neglect of true ideas in legislation. For there is nothing more pernicious and more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to a so-called adverse experience, which indeed would not have existed, if those institutions had been established at the proper time and in accordance with ideas; while, instead of this, conceptions, crude for the very reason that they have been drawn from experience, have marred and frustrated all our better views and intentions. The more legislation and government are in harmony with this idea, the more rare do punishments become and thus it is quite reasonable to maintain, as Plato did, that in a perfect state no punishments at all would be necessary. Now although a perfect state may never exist, the idea is not on that account the less just, which holds up this maximum as the archetype or standard of a constitution, in order to bring legislative government always nearer and nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For at what precise degree human nature must stop in its progress, and how wide must be the chasm which must necessarily exist between the idea and its realization, are problems which no one can or ought to determine—and for this reason, that it is the destination of freedom to overstep all assigned limits between itself and the idea.\n\nBut not only in that wherein human reason is a real causal agent and where ideas are operative causes (of actions and their objects), that is to say, in the region of ethics, but also in regard to nature herself, Plato saw clear proofs of an origin from ideas. A plant, and animal, the regular order of nature—probably also the disposition of the whole universe—give manifest evidence that they are possible only by means of and according to ideas; that, indeed, no one creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, perfectly harmonizes with the idea of the most perfect of its kind—just as little as man with the idea of humanity, which nevertheless he bears in his soul as the archetypal standard of his actions; that, notwithstanding, these ideas are in the highest sense individually, unchangeably, and completely determined, and are the original causes of things; and that the totality of connected objects in the universe is alone fully adequate to that idea. Setting aside the exaggerations of expression in the writings of this philosopher, the mental power exhibited in this ascent from the ectypal mode of regarding the physical world to the architectonic connection thereof according to ends, that is, ideas, is an effort which deserves imitation and claims respect. But as regards the principles of ethics, of legislation, and of religion, spheres in which ideas alone render experience possible, although they never attain to full expression therein, he has vindicated for himself a position of peculiar merit, which is not appreciated only because it is judged by the very empirical rules, the validity of which as principles is destroyed by ideas. For as regards nature, experience presents us with rules and is the source of truth, but in relation to ethical laws experience is the parent of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to limit or to deduce the laws which dictate what I ought to do, from what is done.\n\nWe must, however, omit the consideration of these important subjects, the development of which is in reality the peculiar duty and dignity of philosophy, and confine ourselves for the present to the more humble but not less useful task of preparing a firm foundation for those majestic edifices of moral science. For this foundation has been hitherto insecure from the many subterranean passages which reason in its confident but vain search for treasures has made in all directions. Our present duty is to make ourselves perfectly acquainted with the transcendental use made of pure reason, its principles and ideas, that we may be able properly to determine and value its influence and real worth. But before bringing these introductory remarks to a close, I beg those who really have philosophy at heart—and their number is but small—if they shall find themselves convinced by the considerations following as well as by those above, to exert themselves to preserve to the expression idea its original signification, and to take care that it be not lost among those other expressions by which all sorts of representations are loosely designated—that the interests of science may not thereby suffer. We are in no want of words to denominate adequately every mode of representation, without the necessity of encroaching upon terms which are proper to others. The following is a graduated list of them. The genus is representation in general (representatio). Under it stands representation with consciousness (perceptio). A perception which relates solely to the subject as a modification of its state, is a sensation (sensatio), an objective perception is a cognition (cognitio). A cognition is either an intuition or a conception (intuitus vel conceptus). The former has an immediate relation to the object and is singular and individual; the latter has but a mediate relation, by means of a characteristic mark which may be common to several things. A conception is either empirical or pure. A pure conception, in so far as it has its origin in the understanding alone, and is not the conception of a pure sensuous image, is called notio. A conception formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is an idea, or a conception of reason. To one who has accustomed himself to these distinctions, it must be quite intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red called an idea. It ought not even to be called a notion or conception of understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","text":"### Section I—Of Ideas in General\n\nDespite the great wealth of words which European languages possess, the thinker finds himself often at a loss for an expression exactly suited to his conception, for want of which he is unable to make himself intelligible either to others or to himself. To coin new words is a pretension to legislation in language which is seldom successful; and, before recourse is taken to so desperate an expedient, it is advisable to examine the dead and learned languages, with the hope and the probability that we may there meet with some adequate expression of the notion we have in our minds. In this case, even if the original meaning of the word has become somewhat uncertain, from carelessness or want of caution on the part of the authors of it, it is always better to adhere to and confirm its proper meaning—even although it may be doubtful whether it was formerly used in exactly this sense—than to make our labour vain by want of sufficient care to render ourselves intelligible.\n\nFor this reason, when it happens that there exists only a single word to express a certain conception, and this word, in its usual acceptation, is thoroughly adequate to the conception, the accurate distinction of which from related conceptions is of great importance, we ought not to employ the expression improvidently, or, for the sake of variety and elegance of style, use it as a synonym for other cognate words. It is our duty, on the contrary, carefully to preserve its peculiar signification, as otherwise it easily happens that when the attention of the reader is no longer particularly attracted to the expression, and it is lost amid the multitude of other words of very different import, the thought which it conveyed, and which it alone conveyed, is lost with it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","text":"Plato employed the expression idea in a way that plainly showed he meant by it something which is never derived from the senses, but which far transcends even the conceptions of the understanding (with which Aristotle occupied himself), inasmuch as in experience nothing perfectly corresponding to them could be found. Ideas are, according to him, archetypes of things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his view they flow from the highest reason, by which they have been imparted to human reason, which, however, exists no longer in its original state, but is obliged with great labour to recall by reminiscence—which is called philosophy—the old but now sadly obscured ideas. I will not here enter upon any literary investigation of the sense which this sublime philosopher attached to this expression. I shall content myself with remarking that it is nothing unusual, in common conversation as well as in written works, by comparing the thoughts which an author has delivered upon a subject, to understand him better than he understood himself inasmuch as he may not have sufficiently determined his conception, and thus have sometimes spoken, nay even thought, in opposition to his own opinions.\n\nPlato perceived very clearly that our faculty of cognition has the feeling of a much higher vocation than that of merely spelling out phenomena according to synthetical unity, for the purpose of being able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally raises itself to cognitions far too elevated to admit of the possibility of an object given by experience corresponding to them—cognitions which are nevertheless real, and are not mere phantoms of the brain.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","text":"This philosopher found his ideas especially in all that is practical,[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-41) that is, which rests upon freedom, which in its turn ranks under cognitions that are the peculiar product of reason. He who would derive from experience the conceptions of virtue, who would make (as many have really done) that, which at best can but serve as an imperfectly illustrative example, a model for or the formation of a perfectly adequate idea on the subject, would in fact transform virtue into a nonentity changeable according to time and circumstance and utterly incapable of being employed as a rule. On the contrary, every one is conscious that, when any one is held up to him as a model of virtue, he compares this so-called model with the true original which he possesses in his own mind and values him according to this standard. But this standard is the idea of virtue, in relation to which all possible objects of experience are indeed serviceable as examples—proofs of the practicability in a certain degree of that which the conception of virtue demands—but certainly not as archetypes. That the actions of man will never be in perfect accordance with all the requirements of the pure ideas of reason, does not prove the thought to be chimerical. For only through this idea are all judgements as to moral merit or demerit possible; it consequently lies at the foundation of every approach to moral perfection, however far removed from it the obstacles in human nature—indeterminable as to degree—may keep us.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","text":"[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-41) He certainly extended the application of his conception to speculative cognitions also, provided they were given pure and completely à priori, nay, even to mathematics, although this science cannot possess an object otherwhere than in Possible experience. I cannot follow him in this, and as little can I follow him in his mystical deduction of these ideas, or in his hypostatization of them; although, in truth, the elevated and exaggerated language which he employed in describing them is quite capable of an interpretation more subdued and more in accordance with fact and the nature of things.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","text":"The Platonic Republic has become proverbial as an example—and a striking one—of imaginary perfection, such as can exist only in the brain of the idle thinker; and Brucker ridicules the philosopher for maintaining that a prince can never govern well, unless he is participant in the ideas. But we should do better to follow up this thought and, where this admirable thinker leaves us without assistance, employ new efforts to place it in clearer light, rather than carelessly fling it aside as useless, under the very miserable and pernicious pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest possible human freedom according to laws, by which the liberty of every individual can consist with the liberty of every other (not of the greatest possible happiness, for this follows necessarily from the former), is, to say the least, a necessary idea, which must be placed at the foundation not only of the first plan of the constitution of a state, but of all its laws. And, in this, it not necessary at the outset to take account of the obstacles which lie in our way—obstacles which perhaps do not necessarily arise from the character of human nature, but rather from the previous neglect of true ideas in legislation. For there is nothing more pernicious and more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to a so-called adverse experience, which indeed would not have existed, if those institutions had been established at the proper time and in accordance with ideas; while, instead of this, conceptions, crude for the very reason that they have been drawn from experience, have marred and frustrated all our better views and intentions. The more legislation and government are in harmony with this idea, the more rare do punishments become and thus it is quite reasonable to maintain, as Plato did, that in a perfect state no punishments at all would be necessary. Now although a perfect state may never exist, the idea is not on that account the less just, which holds up this maximum as the archetype or standard of a constitution, in order to bring legislative government always nearer and nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For at what precise degree human nature must stop in its progress, and how wide must be the chasm which must necessarily exist between the idea and its realization, are problems which no one can or ought to determine—and for this reason, that it is the destination of freedom to overstep all assigned limits between itself and the idea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","text":"But not only in that wherein human reason is a real causal agent and where ideas are operative causes (of actions and their objects), that is to say, in the region of ethics, but also in regard to nature herself, Plato saw clear proofs of an origin from ideas. A plant, and animal, the regular order of nature—probably also the disposition of the whole universe—give manifest evidence that they are possible only by means of and according to ideas; that, indeed, no one creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, perfectly harmonizes with the idea of the most perfect of its kind—just as little as man with the idea of humanity, which nevertheless he bears in his soul as the archetypal standard of his actions; that, notwithstanding, these ideas are in the highest sense individually, unchangeably, and completely determined, and are the original causes of things; and that the totality of connected objects in the universe is alone fully adequate to that idea. Setting aside the exaggerations of expression in the writings of this philosopher, the mental power exhibited in this ascent from the ectypal mode of regarding the physical world to the architectonic connection thereof according to ends, that is, ideas, is an effort which deserves imitation and claims respect. But as regards the principles of ethics, of legislation, and of religion, spheres in which ideas alone render experience possible, although they never attain to full expression therein, he has vindicated for himself a position of peculiar merit, which is not appreciated only because it is judged by the very empirical rules, the validity of which as principles is destroyed by ideas. For as regards nature, experience presents us with rules and is the source of truth, but in relation to ethical laws experience is the parent of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to limit or to deduce the laws which dictate what I ought to do, from what is done.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","text":"We must, however, omit the consideration of these important subjects, the development of which is in reality the peculiar duty and dignity of philosophy, and confine ourselves for the present to the more humble but not less useful task of preparing a firm foundation for those majestic edifices of moral science. For this foundation has been hitherto insecure from the many subterranean passages which reason in its confident but vain search for treasures has made in all directions. Our present duty is to make ourselves perfectly acquainted with the transcendental use made of pure reason, its principles and ideas, that we may be able properly to determine and value its influence and real worth. But before bringing these introductory remarks to a close, I beg those who really have philosophy at heart—and their number is but small—if they shall find themselves convinced by the considerations following as well as by those above, to exert themselves to preserve to the expression idea its original signification, and to take care that it be not lost among those other expressions by which all sorts of representations are loosely designated—that the interests of science may not thereby suffer. We are in no want of words to denominate adequately every mode of representation, without the necessity of encroaching upon terms which are proper to others. The following is a graduated list of them. The genus is representation in general (representatio). Under it stands representation with consciousness (perceptio). A perception which relates solely to the subject as a modification of its state, is a sensation (sensatio), an objective perception is a cognition (cognitio). A cognition is either an intuition or a conception (intuitus vel conceptus). The former has an immediate relation to the object and is singular and individual; the latter has but a mediate relation, by means of a characteristic mark which may be common to several things. A conception is either empirical or pure. A pure conception, in so far as it has its origin in the understanding alone, and is not the conception of a pure sensuous image, is called notio. A conception formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is an idea, or a conception of reason. To one who has accustomed himself to these distinctions, it must be quite intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red called an idea. It ought not even to be called a notion or conception of understanding.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b9692662f9b4afa7970e634f19d0b9c12d6f7b349cce4227243f285f841d1996","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","order":57,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SITI","text":"### Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas\n\nTranscendental analytic showed us how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure conceptions à priori, conceptions which represent objects antecedently to all experience, or rather, indicate the synthetical unity which alone renders possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of judgements—converted into a conception of the synthesis of intuitions—produced the categories which direct the employment of the understanding in experience. This consideration warrants us to expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to synthetical unity of intuitions, following the rule of the categories, will contain the origin of particular à priori conceptions, which we may call pure conceptions of reason or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the totality of experience according to principles.\n\nThe function of reason in arguments consists in the universality of a cognition according to conceptions, and the syllogism itself is a judgement which is determined à priori in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition: “Caius is mortal,” is one which may be obtained from experience by the aid of the understanding alone; but my wish is to find a conception which contains the condition under which the predicate of this judgement is given—in this case, the conception of man—and after subsuming under this condition, taken in its whole extent (all men are mortal), I determine according to it the cognition of the object thought, and say: “Caius is mortal.”\n\nHence, in the conclusion of a syllogism we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after having thought it in the major in its whole extent under a certain condition. This complete quantity of the extent in relation to such a condition is called universality (universalitas). To this corresponds totality (universitas) of conditions in the synthesis of intuitions. The transcendental conception of reason is therefore nothing else than the conception of the totality of the conditions of a given conditioned. Now as the unconditioned alone renders possible totality of conditions, and, conversely, the totality of conditions is itself always unconditioned; a pure rational conception in general can be defined and explained by means of the conception of the unconditioned, in so far as it contains a basis for the synthesis of the conditioned.\n\nTo the number of modes of relation which the understanding cogitates by means of the categories, the number of pure rational conceptions will correspond. We must therefore seek for, first, an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; thirdly, of the disjunctive synthesis of parts in a system.\n\nThere are exactly the same number of modes of syllogisms, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned—one to the subject which cannot be employed as predicate, another to the presupposition which supposes nothing higher than itself, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the complete division of a conception. Hence the pure rational conceptions of totality in the synthesis of conditions have a necessary foundation in the nature of human reason—at least as modes of elevating the unity of the understanding to the unconditioned. They may have no valid application, corresponding to their transcendental employment, in concreto, and be thus of no greater utility than to direct the understanding how, while extending them as widely as possible, to maintain its exercise and application in perfect consistence and harmony.\n\nBut, while speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all conceptions of reason, we again light upon an expression which we find it impossible to dispense with, and which nevertheless, owing to the ambiguity attaching to it from long abuse, we cannot employ with safety. The word absolute is one of the few words which, in its original signification, was perfectly adequate to the conception it was intended to convey—a conception which no other word in the same language exactly suits, and the loss—or, which is the same thing, the incautious and loose employment—of which must be followed by the loss of the conception itself. And, as it is a conception which occupies much of the attention of reason, its loss would be greatly to the detriment of all transcendental philosophy. The word absolute is at present frequently used to denote that something can be predicated of a thing considered in itself and intrinsically. In this sense absolutely possible would signify that which is possible in itself (interne)—which is, in fact, the least that one can predicate of an object. On the other hand, it is sometimes employed to indicate that a thing is valid in all respects—for example, absolute sovereignty. Absolutely possible would in this sense signify that which is possible in all relations and in every respect; and this is the most that can be predicated of the possibility of a thing. Now these significations do in truth frequently coincide. Thus, for example, that which is intrinsically impossible, is also impossible in all relations, that is, absolutely impossible. But in most cases they differ from each other toto caelo, and I can by no means conclude that, because a thing is in itself possible, it is also possible in all relations, and therefore absolutely. Nay, more, I shall in the sequel show that absolute necessity does not by any means depend on internal necessity, and that, therefore, it must not be considered as synonymous with it. Of an opposite which is intrinsically impossible, we may affirm that it is in all respects impossible, and that, consequently, the thing itself, of which this is the opposite, is absolutely necessary; but I cannot reason conversely and say, the opposite of that which is absolutely necessary is intrinsically impossible, that is, that the absolute necessity of things is an internal necessity. For this internal necessity is in certain cases a mere empty word with which the least conception cannot be connected, while the conception of the necessity of a thing in all relations possesses very peculiar determinations. Now as the loss of a conception of great utility in speculative science cannot be a matter of indifference to the philosopher, I trust that the proper determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the conception depends will likewise be not indifferent to him.\n\nIn this enlarged signification, then, shall I employ the word absolute, in opposition to that which is valid only in some particular respect; for the latter is restricted by conditions, the former is valid without any restriction whatever.\n\nNow the transcendental conception of reason has for its object nothing else than absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions and does not rest satisfied till it has attained to the absolutely, that is, in all respects and relations, unconditioned. For pure reason leaves to the understanding everything that immediately relates to the object of intuition or rather to their synthesis in imagination. The former restricts itself to the absolute totality in the employment of the conceptions of the understanding and aims at carrying out the synthetical unity which is cogitated in the category, even to the unconditioned. This unity may hence be called the rational unity of phenomena, as the other, which the category expresses, may be termed the unity of the understanding. Reason, therefore, has an immediate relation to the use of the understanding, not indeed in so far as the latter contains the ground of possible experience (for the conception of the absolute totality of conditions is not a conception that can be employed in experience, because no experience is unconditioned), but solely for the purpose of directing it to a certain unity, of which the understanding has no conception, and the aim of which is to collect into an absolute whole all acts of the understanding. Hence the objective employment of the pure conceptions of reason is always transcendent, while that of the pure conceptions of the understanding must, according to their nature, be always immanent, inasmuch as they are limited to possible experience.\n\nI understand by idea a necessary conception of reason, to which no corresponding object can be discovered in the world of sense. Accordingly, the pure conceptions of reason at present under consideration are transcendental ideas. They are conceptions of pure reason, for they regard all empirical cognition as determined by means of an absolute totality of conditions. They are not mere fictions, but natural and necessary products of reason, and have hence a necessary relation to the whole sphere of the exercise of the understanding. And, finally, they are transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experiences, in which, consequently, no object can ever be presented that would be perfectly adequate to a transcendental idea. When we use the word idea, we say, as regards its object (an object of the pure understanding), a great deal, but as regards its subject (that is, in respect of its reality under conditions of experience), exceedingly little, because the idea, as the conception of a maximum, can never be completely and adequately presented in concreto. Now, as in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is properly the sole aim, and as in this case the approximation to a conception, which is never attained in practice, is the same thing as if the conception were non-existent—it is commonly said of the conception of this kind, “it is only an idea.” So we might very well say, “the absolute totality of all phenomena is only an idea,” for, as we never can present an adequate representation of it, it remains for us a problem incapable of solution. On the other hand, as in the practical use of the understanding we have only to do with action and practice according to rules, an idea of pure reason can always be given really in concreto, although only partially, nay, it is the indispensable condition of all practical employment of reason. The practice or execution of the idea is always limited and defective, but nevertheless within indeterminable boundaries, consequently always under the influence of the conception of an absolute perfection. And thus the practical idea is always in the highest degree fruitful, and in relation to real actions indispensably necessary. In the idea, pure reason possesses even causality and the power of producing that which its conception contains. Hence we cannot say of wisdom, in a disparaging way, “it is only an idea.” For, for the very reason that it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible aims, it must be for all practical exertions and endeavours the primitive condition and rule—a rule which, if not constitutive, is at least limitative.\n\nNow, although we must say of the transcendental conceptions of reason, “they are only ideas,” we must not, on this account, look upon them as superfluous and nugatory. For, although no object can be determined by them, they can be of great utility, unobserved and at the basis of the edifice of the understanding, as the canon for its extended and self-consistent exercise—a canon which, indeed, does not enable it to cognize more in an object than it would cognize by the help of its own conceptions, but which guides it more securely in its cognition. Not to mention that they perhaps render possible a transition from our conceptions of nature and the non-ego to the practical conceptions, and thus produce for even ethical ideas keeping, so to speak, and connection with the speculative cognitions of reason. The explication of all this must be looked for in the sequel.\n\nBut setting aside, in conformity with our original purpose, the consideration of the practical ideas, we proceed to contemplate reason in its speculative use alone, nay, in a still more restricted sphere, to wit, in the transcendental use; and here must strike into the same path which we followed in our deduction of the categories. That is to say, we shall consider the logical form of the cognition of reason, that we may see whether reason may not be thereby a source of conceptions which enables us to regard objects in themselves as determined synthetically à priori, in relation to one or other of the functions of reason.\n\nReason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of cognition, is the faculty of conclusion, that is, of mediate judgement—by means of the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgement under the condition of a given judgement. The given judgement is the general rule (major). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgement under the condition of the rule is the minor. The actual judgement, which enounces the assertion of the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (conclusio). The rule predicates something generally under a certain condition. The condition of the rule is satisfied in some particular case. It follows that what was valid in general under that condition must also be considered as valid in the particular case which satisfies this condition. It is very plain that reason attains to a cognition, by means of acts of the understanding which constitute a series of conditions. When I arrive at the proposition, “All bodies are changeable,” by beginning with the more remote cognition (in which the conception of body does not appear, but which nevertheless contains the condition of that conception), “All compound is changeable,” by proceeding from this to a less remote cognition, which stands under the condition of the former, “Bodies are compound,” and hence to a third, which at length connects for me the remote cognition (changeable) with the one before me, “Consequently, bodies are changeable”—I have arrived at a cognition (conclusion) through a series of conditions (premisses). Now every series, whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgement) is given, can be continued; consequently the same procedure of reason conducts us to the ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, which is a series of syllogisms, that can be continued either on the side of the conditions (per prosyllogismos) or of the conditioned (per episyllogismos) to an indefinite extent.\n\nBut we very soon perceive that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of deduced cognitions on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given cognition, in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms must have a very different relation to the faculty of reason from that of the descending series, that is, the progressive procedure of reason on the side of the conditioned by means of episyllogisms. For, as in the former case the cognition (conclusio) is given only as conditioned, reason can attain to this cognition only under the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premisses), because only under this supposition is the judgement we may be considering possible à priori; while on the side of the conditioned or the inferences, only an incomplete and becoming, and not a presupposed or given series, consequently only a potential progression, is cogitated. Hence, when a cognition is contemplated as conditioned, reason is compelled to consider the series of conditions in an ascending line as completed and given in their totality. But if the very same condition is considered at the same time as the condition of other cognitions, which together constitute a series of inferences or consequences in a descending line, reason may preserve a perfect indifference, as to how far this progression may extend _a parte posteriori_, and whether the totality of this series is possible, because it stands in no need of such a series for the purpose of arriving at the conclusion before it, inasmuch as this conclusion is sufficiently guaranteed and determined on grounds a parte priori. It may be the case, that upon the side of the conditions the series of premisses has a first or highest condition, or it may not possess this, and so be a parte priori unlimited; but it must, nevertheless, contain totality of conditions, even admitting that we never could succeed in completely apprehending it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is considered as an inference resulting from it, is to be held as true. This is a requirement of reason, which announces its cognition as determined à priori and as necessary, either in itself—and in this case it needs no grounds to rest upon—or, if it is deduced, as a member of a series of grounds, which is itself unconditionally true.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas\n\nTranscendental analytic showed us how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure conceptions à priori, conceptions which represent objects antecedently to all experience, or rather, indicate the synthetical unity which alone renders possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of judgements—converted into a conception of the synthesis of intuitions—produced the categories which direct the employment of the understanding in experience. This consideration warrants us to expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to synthetical unity of intuitions, following the rule of the categories, will contain the origin of particular à priori conceptions, which we may call pure conceptions of reason or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the totality of experience according to principles.\n\nThe function of reason in arguments consists in the universality of a cognition according to conceptions, and the syllogism itself is a judgement which is determined à priori in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition: “Caius is mortal,” is one which may be obtained from experience by the aid of the understanding alone; but my wish is to find a conception which contains the condition under which the predicate of this judgement is given—in this case, the conception of man—and after subsuming under this condition, taken in its whole extent (all men are mortal), I determine according to it the cognition of the object thought, and say: “Caius is mortal.”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","text":"Hence, in the conclusion of a syllogism we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after having thought it in the major in its whole extent under a certain condition. This complete quantity of the extent in relation to such a condition is called universality (universalitas). To this corresponds totality (universitas) of conditions in the synthesis of intuitions. The transcendental conception of reason is therefore nothing else than the conception of the totality of the conditions of a given conditioned. Now as the unconditioned alone renders possible totality of conditions, and, conversely, the totality of conditions is itself always unconditioned; a pure rational conception in general can be defined and explained by means of the conception of the unconditioned, in so far as it contains a basis for the synthesis of the conditioned.\n\nTo the number of modes of relation which the understanding cogitates by means of the categories, the number of pure rational conceptions will correspond. We must therefore seek for, first, an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; thirdly, of the disjunctive synthesis of parts in a system.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","text":"There are exactly the same number of modes of syllogisms, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned—one to the subject which cannot be employed as predicate, another to the presupposition which supposes nothing higher than itself, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the complete division of a conception. Hence the pure rational conceptions of totality in the synthesis of conditions have a necessary foundation in the nature of human reason—at least as modes of elevating the unity of the understanding to the unconditioned. They may have no valid application, corresponding to their transcendental employment, in concreto, and be thus of no greater utility than to direct the understanding how, while extending them as widely as possible, to maintain its exercise and application in perfect consistence and harmony.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","text":"But, while speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all conceptions of reason, we again light upon an expression which we find it impossible to dispense with, and which nevertheless, owing to the ambiguity attaching to it from long abuse, we cannot employ with safety. The word absolute is one of the few words which, in its original signification, was perfectly adequate to the conception it was intended to convey—a conception which no other word in the same language exactly suits, and the loss—or, which is the same thing, the incautious and loose employment—of which must be followed by the loss of the conception itself. And, as it is a conception which occupies much of the attention of reason, its loss would be greatly to the detriment of all transcendental philosophy. The word absolute is at present frequently used to denote that something can be predicated of a thing considered in itself and intrinsically. In this sense absolutely possible would signify that which is possible in itself (interne)—which is, in fact, the least that one can predicate of an object. On the other hand, it is sometimes employed to indicate that a thing is valid in all respects—for example, absolute sovereignty. Absolutely possible would in this sense signify that which is possible in all relations and in every respect; and this is the most that can be predicated of the possibility of a thing. Now these significations do in truth frequently coincide. Thus, for example, that which is intrinsically impossible, is also impossible in all relations, that is, absolutely impossible. But in most cases they differ from each other toto caelo, and I can by no means conclude that, because a thing is in itself possible, it is also possible in all relations, and therefore absolutely. Nay, more, I shall in the sequel show that absolute necessity does not by any means depend on internal necessity, and that, therefore, it must not be considered as synonymous with it. Of an opposite which is intrinsically impossible, we may affirm that it is in all respects impossible, and that, consequently, the thing itself, of which this is the opposite, is absolutely necessary; but I cannot reason conversely and say, the opposite of that which is absolutely necessary is intrinsically impossible, that is, that the absolute necessity of things is an internal necessity. For this internal necessity is in certain cases a mere empty word with which the least conception cannot be connected, while the conception of the necessity of a thing in all relations possesses very peculiar determinations. Now as the loss of a conception of great utility in speculative science cannot be a matter of indifference to the philosopher, I trust that the proper determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the conception depends will likewise be not indifferent to him.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","text":"In this enlarged signification, then, shall I employ the word absolute, in opposition to that which is valid only in some particular respect; for the latter is restricted by conditions, the former is valid without any restriction whatever.\n\nNow the transcendental conception of reason has for its object nothing else than absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions and does not rest satisfied till it has attained to the absolutely, that is, in all respects and relations, unconditioned. For pure reason leaves to the understanding everything that immediately relates to the object of intuition or rather to their synthesis in imagination. The former restricts itself to the absolute totality in the employment of the conceptions of the understanding and aims at carrying out the synthetical unity which is cogitated in the category, even to the unconditioned. This unity may hence be called the rational unity of phenomena, as the other, which the category expresses, may be termed the unity of the understanding. Reason, therefore, has an immediate relation to the use of the understanding, not indeed in so far as the latter contains the ground of possible experience (for the conception of the absolute totality of conditions is not a conception that can be employed in experience, because no experience is unconditioned), but solely for the purpose of directing it to a certain unity, of which the understanding has no conception, and the aim of which is to collect into an absolute whole all acts of the understanding. Hence the objective employment of the pure conceptions of reason is always transcendent, while that of the pure conceptions of the understanding must, according to their nature, be always immanent, inasmuch as they are limited to possible experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","text":"I understand by idea a necessary conception of reason, to which no corresponding object can be discovered in the world of sense. Accordingly, the pure conceptions of reason at present under consideration are transcendental ideas. They are conceptions of pure reason, for they regard all empirical cognition as determined by means of an absolute totality of conditions. They are not mere fictions, but natural and necessary products of reason, and have hence a necessary relation to the whole sphere of the exercise of the understanding. And, finally, they are transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experiences, in which, consequently, no object can ever be presented that would be perfectly adequate to a transcendental idea. When we use the word idea, we say, as regards its object (an object of the pure understanding), a great deal, but as regards its subject (that is, in respect of its reality under conditions of experience), exceedingly little, because the idea, as the conception of a maximum, can never be completely and adequately presented in concreto. Now, as in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is properly the sole aim, and as in this case the approximation to a conception, which is never attained in practice, is the same thing as if the conception were non-existent—it is commonly said of the conception of this kind, “it is only an idea.” So we might very well say, “the absolute totality of all phenomena is only an idea,” for, as we never can present an adequate representation of it, it remains for us a problem incapable of solution. On the other hand, as in the practical use of the understanding we have only to do with action and practice according to rules, an idea of pure reason can always be given really in concreto, although only partially, nay, it is the indispensable condition of all practical employment of reason. The practice or execution of the idea is always limited and defective, but nevertheless within indeterminable boundaries, consequently always under the influence of the conception of an absolute perfection. And thus the practical idea is always in the highest degree fruitful, and in relation to real actions indispensably necessary. In the idea, pure reason possesses even causality and the power of producing that which its conception contains. Hence we cannot say of wisdom, in a disparaging way, “it is only an idea.” For, for the very reason that it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible aims, it must be for all practical exertions and endeavours the primitive condition and rule—a rule which, if not constitutive, is at least limitative.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","text":"Now, although we must say of the transcendental conceptions of reason, “they are only ideas,” we must not, on this account, look upon them as superfluous and nugatory. For, although no object can be determined by them, they can be of great utility, unobserved and at the basis of the edifice of the understanding, as the canon for its extended and self-consistent exercise—a canon which, indeed, does not enable it to cognize more in an object than it would cognize by the help of its own conceptions, but which guides it more securely in its cognition. Not to mention that they perhaps render possible a transition from our conceptions of nature and the non-ego to the practical conceptions, and thus produce for even ethical ideas keeping, so to speak, and connection with the speculative cognitions of reason. The explication of all this must be looked for in the sequel.\n\nBut setting aside, in conformity with our original purpose, the consideration of the practical ideas, we proceed to contemplate reason in its speculative use alone, nay, in a still more restricted sphere, to wit, in the transcendental use; and here must strike into the same path which we followed in our deduction of the categories. That is to say, we shall consider the logical form of the cognition of reason, that we may see whether reason may not be thereby a source of conceptions which enables us to regard objects in themselves as determined synthetically à priori, in relation to one or other of the functions of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0008","text":"Reason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of cognition, is the faculty of conclusion, that is, of mediate judgement—by means of the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgement under the condition of a given judgement. The given judgement is the general rule (major). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgement under the condition of the rule is the minor. The actual judgement, which enounces the assertion of the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (conclusio). The rule predicates something generally under a certain condition. The condition of the rule is satisfied in some particular case. It follows that what was valid in general under that condition must also be considered as valid in the particular case which satisfies this condition. It is very plain that reason attains to a cognition, by means of acts of the understanding which constitute a series of conditions. When I arrive at the proposition, “All bodies are changeable,” by beginning with the more remote cognition (in which the conception of body does not appear, but which nevertheless contains the condition of that conception), “All compound is changeable,” by proceeding from this to a less remote cognition, which stands under the condition of the former, “Bodies are compound,” and hence to a third, which at length connects for me the remote cognition (changeable) with the one before me, “Consequently, bodies are changeable”—I have arrived at a cognition (conclusion) through a series of conditions (premisses). Now every series, whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgement) is given, can be continued; consequently the same procedure of reason conducts us to the ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, which is a series of syllogisms, that can be continued either on the side of the conditions (per prosyllogismos) or of the conditioned (per episyllogismos) to an indefinite extent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","text":"But we very soon perceive that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of deduced cognitions on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given cognition, in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms must have a very different relation to the faculty of reason from that of the descending series, that is, the progressive procedure of reason on the side of the conditioned by means of episyllogisms. For, as in the former case the cognition (conclusio) is given only as conditioned, reason can attain to this cognition only under the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premisses), because only under this supposition is the judgement we may be considering possible à priori; while on the side of the conditioned or the inferences, only an incomplete and becoming, and not a presupposed or given series, consequently only a potential progression, is cogitated. Hence, when a cognition is contemplated as conditioned, reason is compelled to consider the series of conditions in an ascending line as completed and given in their totality. But if the very same condition is considered at the same time as the condition of other cognitions, which together constitute a series of inferences or consequences in a descending line, reason may preserve a perfect indifference, as to how far this progression may extend _a parte posteriori_, and whether the totality of this series is possible, because it stands in no need of such a series for the purpose of arriving at the conclusion before it, inasmuch as this conclusion is sufficiently guaranteed and determined on grounds a parte priori. It may be the case, that upon the side of the conditions the series of premisses has a first or highest condition, or it may not possess this, and so be a parte priori unlimited; but it must, nevertheless, contain totality of conditions, even admitting that we never could succeed in completely apprehending it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is considered as an inference resulting from it, is to be held as true. This is a requirement of reason, which announces its cognition as determined à priori and as necessary, either in itself—and in this case it needs no grounds to rest upon—or, if it is deduced, as a member of a series of grounds, which is itself unconditionally true.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8285bafc65b4c01c908aac7a2fc0a0324651a27f29bf6023bbbf50bd491192d2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","order":58,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIST","text":"### Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe are not at present engaged with a logical dialectic, which makes complete abstraction of the content of cognition and aims only at unveiling the illusory appearance in the form of syllogisms. Our subject is transcendental dialectic, which must contain, completely à priori, the origin of certain cognitions drawn from pure reason, and the origin of certain deduced conceptions, the object of which cannot be given empirically and which therefore lie beyond the sphere of the faculty of understanding. We have observed, from the natural relation which the transcendental use of our cognition, in syllogisms as well as in judgements, must have to the logical, that there are three kinds of dialectical arguments, corresponding to the three modes of conclusion, by which reason attains to cognitions on principles; and that in all it is the business of reason to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, beyond which the understanding never proceeds, to the unconditioned which the understanding never can reach.\n\nNow the most general relations which can exist in our representations are: 1st, the relation to the subject; 2nd, the relation to objects, either as phenomena, or as objects of thought in general. If we connect this subdivision with the main division, all the relations of our representations, of which we can form either a conception or an idea, are threefold: 1. The relation to the subject; 2. The relation to the manifold of the object as a phenomenon; 3. The relation to all things in general.\n\nNow all pure conceptions have to do in general with the synthetical unity of representations; conceptions of pure reason (transcendental ideas), on the other hand, with the unconditional synthetical unity of all conditions. It follows that all transcendental ideas arrange themselves in three classes, the first of which contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of the conditions of a phenomenon, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.\n\nThe thinking subject is the object-matter of Psychology; the sum total of all phenomena (the world) is the object-matter of Cosmology; and the thing which contains the highest condition of the possibility of all that is cogitable (the being of all beings) is the object-matter of all Theology. Thus pure reason presents us with the idea of a transcendental doctrine of the soul (psychologia rationalis), of a transcendental science of the world (cosmologia rationalis), and finally of a transcendental doctrine of God (theologia transcendentalis). Understanding cannot originate even the outline of any of these sciences, even when connected with the highest logical use of reason, that is, all cogitable syllogisms—for the purpose of proceeding from one object (phenomenon) to all others, even to the utmost limits of the empirical synthesis. They are, on the contrary, pure and genuine products, or problems, of pure reason.\n\nWhat modi of the pure conceptions of reason these transcendental ideas are will be fully exposed in the following chapter. They follow the guiding thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates immediately to objects, but to the conceptions of these contained in the understanding. In like manner, it will be made manifest in the detailed explanation of these ideas—how reason, merely through the synthetical use of the same function which it employs in a categorical syllogism, necessarily attains to the conception of the absolute unity of the thinking subject—how the logical procedure in hypothetical ideas necessarily produces the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally—how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism involves the highest conception of a being of all beings: a thought which at first sight seems in the highest degree paradoxical.\n\nAn objective deduction, such as we were able to present in the case of the categories, is impossible as regards these transcendental ideas. For they have, in truth, no relation to any object, in experience, for the very reason that they are only ideas. But a subjective deduction of them from the nature of our reason is possible, and has been given in the present chapter.\n\nIt is easy to perceive that the sole aim of pure reason is the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions, and that it does not concern itself with the absolute completeness on the Part of the conditioned. For of the former alone does she stand in need, in order to preposit the whole series of conditions, and thus present them to the understanding à priori. But if we once have a completely (and unconditionally) given condition, there is no further necessity, in proceeding with the series, for a conception of reason; for the understanding takes of itself every step downward, from the condition to the conditioned. Thus the transcendental ideas are available only for ascending in the series of conditions, till we reach the unconditioned, that is, principles. As regards descending to the conditioned, on the other hand, we find that there is a widely extensive logical use which reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but that a transcendental use thereof is impossible; and that when we form an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis, for example, of the whole series of all future changes in the world, this idea is a mere ens rationis, an arbitrary fiction of thought, and not a necessary presupposition of reason. For the possibility of the conditioned presupposes the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences. Consequently, this conception is not a transcendental idea—and it is with these alone that we are at present occupied.\n\nFinally, it is obvious that there exists among the transcendental ideas a certain connection and unity, and that pure reason, by means of them, collects all its cognitions into one system. From the cognition of self to the cognition of the world, and through these to the supreme being, the progression is so natural, that it seems to resemble the logical march of reason from the premisses to the conclusion.[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-42) Now whether there lies unobserved at the foundation of these ideas an analogy of the same kind as exists between the logical and transcendental procedure of reason, is another of those questions, the answer to which we must not expect till we arrive at a more advanced stage in our inquiries. In this cursory and preliminary view, we have, meanwhile, reached our aim. For we have dispelled the ambiguity which attached to the transcendental conceptions of reason, from their being commonly mixed up with other conceptions in the systems of philosophers, and not properly distinguished from the conceptions of the understanding; we have exposed their origin and, thereby, at the same time their determinate number, and presented them in a systematic connection, and have thus marked out and enclosed a definite sphere for pure reason.\n\n[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-42) The science of Metaphysics has for the proper object of its inquiries only three grand ideas: GOD, FREEDOM, and IMMORTALITY, and it aims at showing, that the second conception, conjoined with the first, must lead to the third, as a necessary conclusion. All the other subjects with which it occupies itself, are merely means for the attainment and realization of these ideas. It does not require these ideas for the construction of a science of nature, but, on the contrary, for the purpose of passing beyond the sphere of nature. A complete insight into and comprehension of them would render Theology, Ethics, and, through the conjunction of both, Religion, solely dependent on the speculative faculty of reason. In a systematic representation of these ideas the above-mentioned arrangement—the synthetical one—would be the most suitable; but in the investigation which must necessarily precede it, the analytical, which reverses this arrangement, would be better adapted to our purpose, as in it we should proceed from that which experience immediately presents to us—psychology, to cosmology, and thence to theology.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","text":"### Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe are not at present engaged with a logical dialectic, which makes complete abstraction of the content of cognition and aims only at unveiling the illusory appearance in the form of syllogisms. Our subject is transcendental dialectic, which must contain, completely à priori, the origin of certain cognitions drawn from pure reason, and the origin of certain deduced conceptions, the object of which cannot be given empirically and which therefore lie beyond the sphere of the faculty of understanding. We have observed, from the natural relation which the transcendental use of our cognition, in syllogisms as well as in judgements, must have to the logical, that there are three kinds of dialectical arguments, corresponding to the three modes of conclusion, by which reason attains to cognitions on principles; and that in all it is the business of reason to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, beyond which the understanding never proceeds, to the unconditioned which the understanding never can reach.\n\nNow the most general relations which can exist in our representations are: 1st, the relation to the subject; 2nd, the relation to objects, either as phenomena, or as objects of thought in general. If we connect this subdivision with the main division, all the relations of our representations, of which we can form either a conception or an idea, are threefold: 1. The relation to the subject; 2. The relation to the manifold of the object as a phenomenon; 3. The relation to all things in general.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","text":"Now all pure conceptions have to do in general with the synthetical unity of representations; conceptions of pure reason (transcendental ideas), on the other hand, with the unconditional synthetical unity of all conditions. It follows that all transcendental ideas arrange themselves in three classes, the first of which contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of the conditions of a phenomenon, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.\n\nThe thinking subject is the object-matter of Psychology; the sum total of all phenomena (the world) is the object-matter of Cosmology; and the thing which contains the highest condition of the possibility of all that is cogitable (the being of all beings) is the object-matter of all Theology. Thus pure reason presents us with the idea of a transcendental doctrine of the soul (psychologia rationalis), of a transcendental science of the world (cosmologia rationalis), and finally of a transcendental doctrine of God (theologia transcendentalis). Understanding cannot originate even the outline of any of these sciences, even when connected with the highest logical use of reason, that is, all cogitable syllogisms—for the purpose of proceeding from one object (phenomenon) to all others, even to the utmost limits of the empirical synthesis. They are, on the contrary, pure and genuine products, or problems, of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","text":"What modi of the pure conceptions of reason these transcendental ideas are will be fully exposed in the following chapter. They follow the guiding thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates immediately to objects, but to the conceptions of these contained in the understanding. In like manner, it will be made manifest in the detailed explanation of these ideas—how reason, merely through the synthetical use of the same function which it employs in a categorical syllogism, necessarily attains to the conception of the absolute unity of the thinking subject—how the logical procedure in hypothetical ideas necessarily produces the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally—how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism involves the highest conception of a being of all beings: a thought which at first sight seems in the highest degree paradoxical.\n\nAn objective deduction, such as we were able to present in the case of the categories, is impossible as regards these transcendental ideas. For they have, in truth, no relation to any object, in experience, for the very reason that they are only ideas. But a subjective deduction of them from the nature of our reason is possible, and has been given in the present chapter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","text":"It is easy to perceive that the sole aim of pure reason is the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions, and that it does not concern itself with the absolute completeness on the Part of the conditioned. For of the former alone does she stand in need, in order to preposit the whole series of conditions, and thus present them to the understanding à priori. But if we once have a completely (and unconditionally) given condition, there is no further necessity, in proceeding with the series, for a conception of reason; for the understanding takes of itself every step downward, from the condition to the conditioned. Thus the transcendental ideas are available only for ascending in the series of conditions, till we reach the unconditioned, that is, principles. As regards descending to the conditioned, on the other hand, we find that there is a widely extensive logical use which reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but that a transcendental use thereof is impossible; and that when we form an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis, for example, of the whole series of all future changes in the world, this idea is a mere ens rationis, an arbitrary fiction of thought, and not a necessary presupposition of reason. For the possibility of the conditioned presupposes the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences. Consequently, this conception is not a transcendental idea—and it is with these alone that we are at present occupied.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","text":"Finally, it is obvious that there exists among the transcendental ideas a certain connection and unity, and that pure reason, by means of them, collects all its cognitions into one system. From the cognition of self to the cognition of the world, and through these to the supreme being, the progression is so natural, that it seems to resemble the logical march of reason from the premisses to the conclusion.[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-42) Now whether there lies unobserved at the foundation of these ideas an analogy of the same kind as exists between the logical and transcendental procedure of reason, is another of those questions, the answer to which we must not expect till we arrive at a more advanced stage in our inquiries. In this cursory and preliminary view, we have, meanwhile, reached our aim. For we have dispelled the ambiguity which attached to the transcendental conceptions of reason, from their being commonly mixed up with other conceptions in the systems of philosophers, and not properly distinguished from the conceptions of the understanding; we have exposed their origin and, thereby, at the same time their determinate number, and presented them in a systematic connection, and have thus marked out and enclosed a definite sphere for pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0006","text":"[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-42) The science of Metaphysics has for the proper object of its inquiries only three grand ideas: GOD, FREEDOM, and IMMORTALITY, and it aims at showing, that the second conception, conjoined with the first, must lead to the third, as a necessary conclusion. All the other subjects with which it occupies itself, are merely means for the attainment and realization of these ideas. It does not require these ideas for the construction of a science of nature, but, on the contrary, for the purpose of passing beyond the sphere of nature. A complete insight into and comprehension of them would render Theology, Ethics, and, through the conjunction of both, Religion, solely dependent on the speculative faculty of reason. In a systematic representation of these ideas the above-mentioned arrangement—the synthetical one—would be the most suitable; but in the investigation which must necessarily precede it, the analytical, which reverses this arrangement, would be better adapted to our purpose, as in it we should proceed from that which experience immediately presents to us—psychology, to cosmology, and thence to theology.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c60301a30e2a5c72b3e5b9401c66a5f6c4c6e33dbc725ea14c7f48bb1879ae27","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2","title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON","order":59,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_TD_2","text":"# Dialectic\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON\n\nIt may be said that the object of a merely transcendental idea is something of which we have no conception, although the idea may be a necessary product of reason according to its original laws. For, in fact, a conception of an object that is adequate to the idea given by reason, is impossible. For such an object must be capable of being presented and intuited in a Possible experience. But we should express our meaning better, and with less risk of being misunderstood, if we said that we can have no knowledge of an object, which perfectly corresponds to an idea, although we may possess a problematical conception thereof.\n\nNow the transcendental (subjective) reality at least of the pure conceptions of reason rests upon the fact that we are led to such ideas by a necessary procedure of reason. There must therefore be syllogisms which contain no empirical premisses, and by means of which we conclude from something that we do know, to something of which we do not even possess a conception, to which we, nevertheless, by an unavoidable illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such arguments are, as regards their result, rather to be termed sophisms than syllogisms, although indeed, as regards their origin, they are very well entitled to the latter name, inasmuch as they are not fictions or accidental products of reason, but are necessitated by its very nature. They are sophisms, not of men, but of pure reason herself, from which the Wisest cannot free himself. After long labour he may be able to guard against the error, but he can never be thoroughly rid of the illusion which continually mocks and misleads him.\n\nOf these dialectical arguments there are three kinds, corresponding to the number of the ideas which their conclusions present. In the argument or syllogism of the first class, I conclude, from the transcendental conception of the subject which contains no manifold, the absolute unity of the subject itself, of which I cannot in this manner attain to a conception. This dialectical argument I shall call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of sophistical arguments is occupied with the transcendental conception of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given phenomenon, and I conclude, from the fact that I have always a self-contradictory conception of the unconditioned synthetical unity of the series upon one side, the truth of the opposite unity, of which I have nevertheless no conception. The condition of reason in these dialectical arguments, I shall term the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of sophistical argument, I conclude, from the totality of the conditions of thinking objects in general, in so far as they can be given, the absolute synthetical unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general; that is, from things which I do not know in their mere transcendental conception, I conclude a being of all beings which I know still less by means of a transcendental conception, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no conception whatever. This dialectical argument I shall call the ideal of pure reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","text":"# Dialectic\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON\n\nIt may be said that the object of a merely transcendental idea is something of which we have no conception, although the idea may be a necessary product of reason according to its original laws. For, in fact, a conception of an object that is adequate to the idea given by reason, is impossible. For such an object must be capable of being presented and intuited in a Possible experience. But we should express our meaning better, and with less risk of being misunderstood, if we said that we can have no knowledge of an object, which perfectly corresponds to an idea, although we may possess a problematical conception thereof.\n\nNow the transcendental (subjective) reality at least of the pure conceptions of reason rests upon the fact that we are led to such ideas by a necessary procedure of reason. There must therefore be syllogisms which contain no empirical premisses, and by means of which we conclude from something that we do know, to something of which we do not even possess a conception, to which we, nevertheless, by an unavoidable illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such arguments are, as regards their result, rather to be termed sophisms than syllogisms, although indeed, as regards their origin, they are very well entitled to the latter name, inasmuch as they are not fictions or accidental products of reason, but are necessitated by its very nature. They are sophisms, not of men, but of pure reason herself, from which the Wisest cannot free himself. After long labour he may be able to guard against the error, but he can never be thoroughly rid of the illusion which continually mocks and misleads him.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","text":"Of these dialectical arguments there are three kinds, corresponding to the number of the ideas which their conclusions present. In the argument or syllogism of the first class, I conclude, from the transcendental conception of the subject which contains no manifold, the absolute unity of the subject itself, of which I cannot in this manner attain to a conception. This dialectical argument I shall call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of sophistical arguments is occupied with the transcendental conception of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given phenomenon, and I conclude, from the fact that I have always a self-contradictory conception of the unconditioned synthetical unity of the series upon one side, the truth of the opposite unity, of which I have nevertheless no conception. The condition of reason in these dialectical arguments, I shall term the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of sophistical argument, I conclude, from the totality of the conditions of thinking objects in general, in so far as they can be given, the absolute synthetical unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general; that is, from things which I do not know in their mere transcendental conception, I conclude a being of all beings which I know still less by means of a transcendental conception, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no conception whatever. This dialectical argument I shall call the ideal of pure reason.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6888ab871380ce745140d47d2e2886eb552ce6cf7e6f641d93ebc3f4e8ab48dd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","order":60,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CIPP","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason\n\nThe logical paralogism consists in the falsity of an argument in respect of its form, be the content what it may. But a transcendental paralogism has a transcendental foundation, and concludes falsely, while the form is correct and unexceptionable. In this manner the paralogism has its foundation in the nature of human reason, and is the parent of an unavoidable, though not insoluble, mental illusion.\n\nWe now come to a conception which was not inserted in the general list of transcendental conceptions, and yet must be reckoned with them, but at the same time without in the least altering, or indicating a deficiency in that table. This is the conception, or, if the term is preferred, the judgement, “I think.” But it is readily perceived that this thought is as it were the vehicle of all conceptions in general, and consequently of transcendental conceptions also, and that it is therefore regarded as a transcendental conception, although it can have no peculiar claim to be so ranked, inasmuch as its only use is to indicate that all thought is accompanied by consciousness. At the same time, pure as this conception is from empirical content (impressions of the senses), it enables us to distinguish two different kinds of objects. “I,” as thinking, am an object of the internal sense, and am called soul. That which is an object of the external senses is called body. Thus the expression, “I,” as a thinking being, designates the object-matter of psychology, which may be called “the rational doctrine of the soul,” inasmuch as in this science I desire to know nothing of the soul but what, independently of all experience (which determines me in concreto), may be concluded from this conception “I,” in so far as it appears in all thought.\n\nNow, the rational doctrine of the soul is really an undertaking of this kind. For if the smallest empirical element of thought, if any particular perception of my internal state, were to be introduced among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would not be a rational, but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have thus before us a pretended science, raised upon the single proposition, “I think,” whose foundation or want of foundation we may very properly, and agreeably with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, here examine. It ought not to be objected that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of one’s self, an internal experience is asserted, and that consequently the rational doctrine of the soul which is founded upon it, is not pure, but partly founded upon an empirical principle. For this internal perception is nothing more than the mere apperception, “I think,” which in fact renders all transcendental conceptions possible, in which we say, “I think substance, cause, etc.” For internal experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general, and its relation to other perceptions, unless some particular distinction or determination thereof is empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but as cognition of the empirical, and belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is certainly transcendental. The smallest object of experience (for example, only pleasure or pain), that should be included in the general representation of self-consciousness, would immediately change the rational into an empirical psychology.\n\n“I think” is therefore the only text of rational psychology, from which it must develop its whole system. It is manifest that this thought, when applied to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates thereof; because the least empirical predicate would destroy the purity of the science and its independence of all experience.\n\nBut we shall have to follow here the guidance of the categories—only, as in the present case a thing, “I,” as thinking being, is at first given, we shall—not indeed change the order of the categories as it stands in the table—but begin at the category of substance, by which at the a thing in itself is represented and proceeds backwards through the series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else it may contain must be deduced, is accordingly as follows:\n\n            1                          2\n  The Soul is SUBSTANCE       As regards its quality\n                                it is SIMPLE\n\n                      3\n          As regards the different\n          times in which it exists,\n          it is numerically identical,\n          that is UNITY, not Plurality.\n\n                       4\n  It is in relation to possible objects in space[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-43)\n\n[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-43) The reader, who may not so easily perceive the psychological sense of these expressions, taken here in their transcendental abstraction, and cannot guess why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find the expressions sufficiently explained and justified in the sequel. I have, moreover, to apologize for the Latin terms which have been employed, instead of their German synonyms, contrary to the rules of correct writing. But I judged it better to sacrifice elegance to perspicuity.\n\nFrom these elements originate all the conceptions of pure psychology, by combination alone, without the aid of any other principle. This substance, merely as an object of the internal sense, gives the conception of Immateriality; as simple substance, that of Incorruptibility; its identity, as intellectual substance, gives the conception of Personality; all these three together, Spirituality. Its relation to objects in space gives us the conception of connection (commercium) with bodies. Thus it represents thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, that is, as a soul (anima), and as the ground of Animality; and this, limited and determined by the conception of spirituality, gives us that of Immortality.\n\nNow to these conceptions relate four paralogisms of a transcendental psychology, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason, touching the nature of our thinking being. We can, however, lay at the foundation of this science nothing but the simple and in itself perfectly contentless representation “i” which cannot even be called a conception, but merely a consciousness which accompanies all conceptions. By this “I,” or “He,” or “It,” who or which thinks, nothing more is represented than a transcendental subject of thought = x, which is cognized only by means of the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, apart from these, we cannot form the least conception. Hence in a perpetual circle, inasmuch as we must always employ it, in order to frame any judgement respecting it. And this inconvenience we find it impossible to rid ourselves of, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation distinguishing a particular object, as a form of representation in general, in so far as it may be termed cognition; for in and by cognition alone do I think anything.\n\nIt must, however, appear extraordinary at first sight that the condition under which I think, and which is consequently a property of my subject, should be held to be likewise valid for every existence which thinks, and that we can presume to base upon a seemingly empirical proposition a judgement which is apodeictic and universal, to wit, that everything which thinks is constituted as the voice of my consciousness declares it to be, that is, as a self-conscious being. The cause of this belief is to be found in the fact that we necessarily attribute to things à priori all the properties which constitute conditions under which alone we can cogitate them. Now I cannot obtain the least representation of a thinking being by means of external experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Such objects are consequently nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things which can only thus be represented as thinking beings. The proposition, “I think,” is, in the present case, understood in a problematical sense, not in so far as it contains a perception of an existence (like the Cartesian “Cogito, ergo sum”), but in regard to its mere possibility—for the purpose of discovering what properties may be inferred from so simple a proposition and predicated of the subject of it.\n\nIf at the foundation of our pure rational cognition of thinking beings there lay more than the mere Cogito—if we could likewise call in aid observations on the play of our thoughts, and the thence derived natural laws of the thinking self, there would arise an empirical psychology which would be a kind of physiology of the internal sense and might possibly be capable of explaining the phenomena of that sense. But it could never be available for discovering those properties which do not belong to possible experience (such as the quality of simplicity), nor could it make any apodeictic enunciation on the nature of thinking beings: it would therefore not be a rational psychology.\n\nNow, as the proposition “I think” (in the problematical sense) contains the form of every judgement in general and is the constant accompaniment of all the categories, it is manifest that conclusions are drawn from it only by a transcendental employment of the understanding. This use of the understanding excludes all empirical elements; and we cannot, as has been shown above, have any favourable conception beforehand of its procedure. We shall therefore follow with a critical eye this proposition through all the predicaments of pure psychology; but we shall, for brevity’s sake, allow this examination to proceed in an uninterrupted connection.\n\nBefore entering on this task, however, the following general remark may help to quicken our attention to this mode of argument. It is not merely through my thinking that I cognize an object, but only through my determining a given intuition in relation to the unity of consciousness in which all thinking consists. It follows that I cognize myself, not through my being conscious of myself as thinking, but only when I am conscious of the intuition of myself as determined in relation to the function of thought. All the modi of self-consciousness in thought are hence not conceptions of objects (conceptions of the understanding—categories); they are mere logical functions, which do not present to thought an object to be cognized, and cannot therefore present my Self as an object. Not the consciousness of the determining, but only that of the determinable self, that is, of my internal intuition (in so far as the manifold contained in it can be connected conformably with the general condition of the unity of apperception in thought), is the object.\n\n1. In all judgements I am the determining subject of that relation which constitutes a judgement. But that the I which thinks, must be considered as in thought always a subject, and as a thing which cannot be a predicate to thought, is an apodeictic and identical proposition. But this proposition does not signify that I, as an object, am, for myself, a self-subsistent being or substance. This latter statement—an ambitious one—requires to be supported by data which are not to be discovered in thought; and are perhaps (in so far as I consider the thinking self merely as such) not to be discovered in the thinking self at all.\n\n2. That the I or Ego of apperception, and consequently in all thought, is singular or simple, and cannot be resolved into a plurality of subjects, and therefore indicates a logically simple subject—this is self-evident from the very conception of an Ego, and is consequently an analytical proposition. But this is not tantamount to declaring that the thinking Ego is a simple substance—for this would be a synthetical proposition. The conception of substance always relates to intuitions, which with me cannot be other than sensuous, and which consequently lie completely out of the sphere of the understanding and its thought: but to this sphere belongs the affirmation that the Ego is simple in thought. It would indeed be surprising, if the conception of “substance,” which in other cases requires so much labour to distinguish from the other elements presented by intuition—so much trouble, too, to discover whether it can be simple (as in the case of the parts of matter)—should be presented immediately to me, as if by revelation, in the poorest mental representation of all.\n\n3. The proposition of the identity of my Self amidst all the manifold representations of which I am conscious, is likewise a proposition lying in the conceptions themselves, and is consequently analytical. But this identity of the subject, of which I am conscious in all its representations, does not relate to or concern the intuition of the subject, by which it is given as an object. This proposition cannot therefore enounce the identity of the person, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of its own substance as a thinking being in all change and variation of circumstances. To prove this, we should require not a mere analysis of the proposition, but synthetical judgements based upon a given intuition.\n\n4. I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from that of other things external to me—among which my body also is reckoned. This is also an analytical proposition, for other things are exactly those which I think as different or distinguished from myself. But whether this consciousness of myself is possible without things external to me; and whether therefore I can exist merely as a thinking being (without being man)—cannot be known or inferred from this proposition.\n\nThus we have gained nothing as regards the cognition of myself as object, by the analysis of the consciousness of my Self in thought. The logical exposition of thought in general is mistaken for a metaphysical determination of the object.\n\nOur Critique would be an investigation utterly superfluous, if there existed a possibility of proving à priori, that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances, as such, therefore, possess the inseparable attribute of personality, and are conscious of their existence apart from and unconnected with matter. For we should thus have taken a step beyond the world of sense, and have penetrated into the sphere of noumena; and in this case the right could not be denied us of extending our knowledge in this sphere, of establishing ourselves, and, under a favouring star, appropriating to ourselves possessions in it. For the proposition: “Every thinking being, as such, is simple substance,” is an à priori synthetical proposition; because in the first place it goes beyond the conception which is the subject of it, and adds to the mere notion of a thinking being the mode of its existence, and in the second place annexes a predicate (that of simplicity) to the latter conception—a predicate which it could not have discovered in the sphere of experience. It would follow that à priori synthetical propositions are possible and legitimate, not only, as we have maintained, in relation to objects of possible experience, and as principles of the possibility of this experience itself, but are applicable to things in themselves—an inference which makes an end of the whole of this Critique, and obliges us to fall back on the old mode of metaphysical procedure. But indeed the danger is not so great, if we look a little closer into the question.\n\nThere lurks in the procedure of rational Psychology a paralogism, which is represented in the following syllogism:\n\nThat which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject, does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance.\n\nA thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject.\n\nTherefore it exists also as such, that is, as substance.\n\nIn the major we speak of a being that can be cogitated generally and in every relation, consequently as it may be given in intuition. But in the minor we speak of the same being only in so far as it regards itself as subject, relatively to thought and the unity of consciousness, but not in relation to intuition, by which it is presented as an object to thought. Thus the conclusion is here arrived at by a Sophisma figurae dictionis.[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-44)\n\n[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-44) Thought is taken in the two premisses in two totally different senses. In the major it is considered as relating and applying to objects in general, consequently to objects of intuition also. In the minor, we understand it as relating merely to self-consciousness. In this sense, we do not cogitate an object, but merely the relation to the self-consciousness of the subject, as the form of thought. In the former premiss we speak of things which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subjects. In the second, we do not speak of things, but of thought (all objects being abstracted), in which the Ego is always the subject of consciousness. Hence the conclusion cannot be, “I cannot exist otherwise than as subject”; but only “I can, in cogitating my existence, employ my Ego only as the subject of the judgement.” But this is an identical proposition, and throws no light on the mode of my existence.\n\nThat this famous argument is a mere paralogism, will be plain to any one who will consider the general remark which precedes our exposition of the principles of the pure understanding, and the section on noumena. For it was there proved that the conception of a thing, which can exist per se—only as a subject and never as a predicate, possesses no objective reality; that is to say, we can never know whether there exists any object to correspond to the conception; consequently, the conception is nothing more than a conception, and from it we derive no proper knowledge. If this conception is to indicate by the term substance, an object that can be given, if it is to become a cognition, we must have at the foundation of the cognition a permanent intuition, as the indispensable condition of its objective reality. For through intuition alone can an object be given. But in internal intuition there is nothing permanent, for the Ego is but the consciousness of my thought. If then, we appeal merely to thought, we cannot discover the necessary condition of the application of the conception of substance—that is, of a subject existing per se—to the subject as a thinking being. And thus the conception of the simple nature of substance, which is connected with the objective reality of this conception, is shown to be also invalid, and to be, in fact, nothing more than the logical qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thought; whilst we remain perfectly ignorant whether the subject is composite or not.\n\nRefutation of the Argument of Mendelssohn for the Substantiality or Permanence of the Soul.\n\nThis acute philosopher easily perceived the insufficiency of the common argument which attempts to prove that the soul—it being granted that it is a simple being—cannot perish by dissolution or decomposition; he saw it is not impossible for it to cease to be by extinction, or disappearance. He endeavoured to prove in his Phaedo, that the soul cannot be annihilated, by showing that a simple being cannot cease to exist. Inasmuch as, he said, a simple existence cannot diminish, nor gradually lose portions of its being, and thus be by degrees reduced to nothing (for it possesses no parts, and therefore no multiplicity), between the moment in which it is, and the moment in which it is not, no time can be discovered—which is impossible. But this philosopher did not consider that, granting the soul to possess this simple nature, which contains no parts external to each other and consequently no extensive quantity, we cannot refuse to it any less than to any other being, intensive quantity, that is, a degree of reality in regard to all its faculties, nay, to all that constitutes its existence. But this degree of reality can become less and less through an infinite series of smaller degrees. It follows, therefore, that this supposed substance—this thing, the permanence of which is not assured in any other way, may, if not by decomposition, by gradual loss (remissio) of its powers (consequently by elanguescence, if I may employ this expression), be changed into nothing. For consciousness itself has always a degree, which may be lessened.[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-45)Consequently the faculty of being conscious may be diminished; and so with all other faculties. The permanence of the soul, therefore, as an object of the internal sense, remains undemonstrated, nay, even indemonstrable. Its permanence in life is evident, per se, inasmuch as the thinking being (as man) is to itself, at the same time, an object of the external senses. But this does not authorize the rational psychologist to affirm, from mere conceptions, its permanence beyond life.[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-46)\n\n[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-45) Clearness is not, as logicians maintain, the consciousness of a representation. For a certain degree of consciousness, which may not, however, be sufficient for recollection, is to be met with in many dim representations. For without any consciousness at all, we should not be able to recognize any difference in the obscure representations we connect; as we really can do with many conceptions, such as those of right and justice, and those of the musician, who strikes at once several notes in improvising a piece of music. But a representation is clear, in which our consciousness is sufficient for the consciousness of the difference of this representation from others. If we are only conscious that there is a difference, but are not conscious of the difference—that is, what the difference is—the representation must be termed obscure. There is, consequently, an infinite series of degrees of consciousness down to its entire disappearance.\n\n[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-46) There are some who think they have done enough to establish a new possibility in the mode of the existence of souls, when they have shown that there is no contradiction in their hypotheses on this subject. Such are those who affirm the possibility of thought—of which they have no other knowledge than what they derive from its use in connecting empirical intuitions presented in this our human life—after this life has ceased. But it is very easy to embarrass them by the introduction of counter-possibilities, which rest upon quite as good a foundation. Such, for example, is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances; and conversely, of the coalition of several into one simple substance. For, although divisibility presupposes composition, it does not necessarily require a composition of substances, but only of the degrees (of the several faculties) of one and the same substance. Now we can cogitate all the powers and faculties of the soul—even that of consciousness—as diminished by one half, the substance still remaining. In the same way we can represent to ourselves without contradiction, this obliterated half as preserved, not in the soul, but without it; and we can believe that, as in this case every thing that is real in the soul, and has a degree—consequently its entire existence—has been halved, a particular substance would arise out of the soul. For the multiplicity, which has been divided, formerly existed, but not as a multiplicity of substances, but of every reality as the quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was merely a mode of existence, which by this division alone has been transformed into a plurality of subsistence. In the same manner several simple substances might coalesce into one, without anything being lost except the plurality of subsistence, inasmuch as the one substance would contain the degree of reality of all the former substances. Perhaps, indeed, the simple substances, which appear under the form of matter, might (not indeed by a mechanical or chemical influence upon each other, but by an unknown influence, of which the former would be but the phenomenal appearance), by means of such a dynamical division of the parent-souls, as intensive quantities, produce other souls, while the former repaired the loss thus sustained with new matter of the same sort. I am far from allowing any value to such chimeras; and the principles of our analytic have clearly proved that no other than an empirical use of the categories—that of substance, for example—is possible. But if the rationalist is bold enough to construct, on the mere authority of the faculty of thought—without any intuition, whereby an object is given—a self-subsistent being, merely because the unity of apperception in thought cannot allow him to believe it a composite being, instead of declaring, as he ought to do, that he is unable to explain the possibility of a thinking nature; what ought to hinder the materialist, with as complete an independence of experience, to employ the principle of the rationalist in a directly opposite manner—still preserving the formal unity required by his opponent?\n\nIf, now, we take the above propositions—as they must be accepted as valid for all thinking beings in the system of rational psychology—in synthetical connection, and proceed, from the category of relation, with the proposition: “All thinking beings are, as such, substances,” backwards through the series, till the circle is completed; we come at last to their existence, of which, in this system of rational psychology, substances are held to be conscious, independently of external things; nay, it is asserted that, in relation to the permanence which is a necessary characteristic of substance, they can of themselves determine external things. It follows that idealism—at least problematical idealism, is perfectly unavoidable in this rationalistic system. And, if the existence of outward things is not held to be requisite to the determination of the existence of a substance in time, the existence of these outward things at all, is a gratuitous assumption which remains without the possibility of a proof.\n\nBut if we proceed analytically—the “I think” as a proposition containing in itself an existence as given, consequently modality being the principle—and dissect this proposition, in order to ascertain its content, and discover whether and how this Ego determines its existence in time and space without the aid of anything external; the propositions of rationalistic psychology would not begin with the conception of a thinking being, but with a reality, and the properties of a thinking being in general would be deduced from the mode in which this reality is cogitated, after everything empirical had been abstracted; as is shown in the following table:\n\n                        1\n                      I think,\n\n            2                             3\n        as Subject,              as simple Subject,\n\n                        4\n               as identical Subject,\n           in every state of my thought.\n\nNow, inasmuch as it is not determined in this second proposition, whether I can exist and be cogitated only as subject, and not also as a predicate of another being, the conception of a subject is here taken in a merely logical sense; and it remains undetermined, whether substance is to be cogitated under the conception or not. But in the third proposition, the absolute unity of apperception—the simple Ego in the representation to which all connection and separation, which constitute thought, relate, is of itself important; even although it presents us with no information about the constitution or subsistence of the subject. Apperception is something real, and the simplicity of its nature is given in the very fact of its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that is at the same time simple; for points, which are the only simple things in space, are merely limits, but not constituent parts of space. From this follows the impossibility of a definition on the basis of materialism of the constitution of my Ego as a merely thinking subject. But, because my existence is considered in the first proposition as given, for it does not mean, “Every thinking being exists” (for this would be predicating of them absolute necessity), but only, “I exist thinking”; the proposition is quite empirical, and contains the determinability of my existence merely in relation to my representations in time. But as I require for this purpose something that is permanent, such as is not given in internal intuition; the mode of my existence, whether as substance or as accident, cannot be determined by means of this simple self-consciousness. Thus, if materialism is inadequate to explain the mode in which I exist, spiritualism is likewise as insufficient; and the conclusion is that we are utterly unable to attain to any knowledge of the constitution of the soul, in so far as relates to the possibility of its existence apart from external objects.\n\nAnd, indeed, how should it be possible, merely by the aid of the unity of consciousness—which we cognize only for the reason that it is indispensable to the possibility of experience—to pass the bounds of experience (our existence in this life); and to extend our cognition to the nature of all thinking beings by means of the empirical—but in relation to every sort of intuition, perfectly undetermined—proposition, “I think”?\n\nThere does not then exist any rational psychology as a doctrine furnishing any addition to our knowledge of ourselves. It is nothing more than a discipline, which sets impassable limits to speculative reason in this region of thought, to prevent it, on the one hand, from throwing itself into the arms of a soulless materialism, and, on the other, from losing itself in the mazes of a baseless spiritualism. It teaches us to consider this refusal of our reason to give any satisfactory answer to questions which reach beyond the limits of this our human life, as a hint to abandon fruitless speculation; and to direct, to a practical use, our knowledge of ourselves—which, although applicable only to objects of experience, receives its principles from a higher source, and regulates its procedure as if our destiny reached far beyond the boundaries of experience and life.\n\nFrom all this it is evident that rational psychology has its origin in a mere misunderstanding. The unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of the categories, is considered to be an intuition of the subject as an object; and the category of substance is applied to the intuition. But this unity is nothing more than the unity in thought, by which no object is given; to which therefore the category of substance—which always presupposes a given intuition—cannot be applied. Consequently, the subject cannot be cognized. The subject of the categories cannot, therefore, for the very reason that it cogitates these, frame any conception of itself as an object of the categories; for, to cogitate these, it must lay at the foundation its own pure self-consciousness—the very thing that it wishes to explain and describe. In like manner, the subject, in which the representation of time has its basis, cannot determine, for this very reason, its own existence in time. Now, if the latter is impossible, the former, as an attempt to determine itself by means of the categories as a thinking being in general, is no less so.[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-47)\n\n[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-47) The “I think” is, as has been already stated, an empirical proposition, and contains the proposition, “I exist.” But I cannot say, “Everything, which thinks, exists”; for in this case the property of thought would constitute all beings possessing it, necessary beings. Hence my existence cannot be considered as an inference from the proposition, “I think,” as Descartes maintained—because in this case the major premiss, “Everything, which thinks, exists,” must precede—but the two propositions are identical. The proposition, “I think,” expresses an undetermined empirical intuition, that perception (proving consequently that sensation, which must belong to sensibility, lies at the foundation of this proposition); but it precedes experience, whose province it is to determine an object of perception by means of the categories in relation to time; and existence in this proposition is not a category, as it does not apply to an undetermined given object, but only to one of which we have a conception, and about which we wish to know whether it does or does not exist, out of, and apart from this conception. An undetermined perception signifies here merely something real that has been given, only, however, to thought in general—but not as a phenomenon, nor as a thing in itself (noumenon), but only as something that really exists, and is designated as such in the proposition, “I think.” For it must be remarked that, when I call the proposition, “I think,” an empirical proposition, I do not thereby mean that the Ego in the proposition is an empirical representation; on the contrary, it is purely intellectual, because it belongs to thought in general. But without some empirical representation, which presents to the mind material for thought, the mental act, “I think,” would not take place; and the empirical is only the condition of the application or employment of the pure intellectual faculty.\n\nThus, then, appears the vanity of the hope of establishing a cognition which is to extend its rule beyond the limits of experience—a cognition which is one of the highest interests of humanity; and thus is proved the futility of the attempt of speculative philosophy in this region of thought. But, in this interest of thought, the severity of criticism has rendered to reason a not unimportant service, by the demonstration of the impossibility of making any dogmatical affirmation concerning an object of experience beyond the boundaries of experience. She has thus fortified reason against all affirmations of the contrary. Now, this can be accomplished in only two ways. Either our proposition must be proved apodeictically; or, if this is unsuccessful, the sources of this inability must be sought for, and, if these are discovered to exist in the natural and necessary limitation of our reason, our opponents must submit to the same law of renunciation and refrain from advancing claims to dogmatic assertion.\n\nBut the right, say rather the necessity to admit a future life, upon principles of the practical conjoined with the speculative use of reason, has lost nothing by this renunciation; for the merely speculative proof has never had any influence upon the common reason of men. It stands upon the point of a hair, so that even the schools have been able to preserve it from falling only by incessantly discussing it and spinning it like a top; and even in their eyes it has never been able to present any safe foundation for the erection of a theory. The proofs which have been current among men, preserve their value undiminished; nay, rather gain in clearness and unsophisticated power, by the rejection of the dogmatical assumptions of speculative reason. For reason is thus confined within her own peculiar province—the arrangement of ends or aims, which is at the same time the arrangement of nature; and, as a practical faculty, without limiting itself to the latter, it is justified in extending the former, and with it our own existence, beyond the boundaries of experience and life. If we turn our attention to the analogy of the nature of living beings in this world, in the consideration of which reason is obliged to accept as a principle that no organ, no faculty, no appetite is useless, and that nothing is superfluous, nothing disproportionate to its use, nothing unsuited to its end; but that, on the contrary, everything is perfectly conformed to its destination in life—we shall find that man, who alone is the final end and aim of this order, is still the only animal that seems to be excepted from it. For his natural gifts—not merely as regards the talents and motives that may incite him to employ them, but especially the moral law in him—stretch so far beyond all mere earthly utility and advantage, that he feels himself bound to prize the mere consciousness of probity, apart from all advantageous consequences—even the shadowy gift of posthumous fame—above everything; and he is conscious of an inward call to constitute himself, by his conduct in this world—without regard to mere sublunary interests—the citizen of a better. This mighty, irresistible proof—accompanied by an ever-increasing knowledge of the conformability to a purpose in everything we see around us, by the conviction of the boundless immensity of creation, by the consciousness of a certain illimitableness in the possible extension of our knowledge, and by a desire commensurate therewith—remains to humanity, even after the theoretical cognition of ourselves has failed to establish the necessity of an existence after death.\n\nConclusion of the Solution of the Psychological Paralogism.\n\nThe dialectical illusion in rational psychology arises from our confounding an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the conception—in every respect undetermined—of a thinking being in general. I cogitate myself in behalf of a possible experience, at the same time making abstraction of all actual experience; and infer therefrom that I can be conscious of myself apart from experience and its empirical conditions. I consequently confound the possible abstraction of my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a possible separate existence of my thinking self; and I believe that I cognize what is substantial in myself as a transcendental subject, when I have nothing more in thought than the unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of all determination of cognition.\n\nThe task of explaining the community of the soul with the body does not properly belong to the psychology of which we are here speaking; because it proposes to prove the personality of the soul apart from this communion (after death), and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense of the word, although occupying itself with an object of experience—only in so far, however, as it ceases to be an object of experience. But a sufficient answer may be found to the question in our system. The difficulty which lies in the execution of this task consists, as is well known, in the presupposed heterogeneity of the object of the internal sense (the soul) and the objects of the external senses; inasmuch as the formal condition of the intuition of the one is time, and of that of the other space also. But if we consider that both kinds of objects do not differ internally, but only in so far as the one appears externally to the other—consequently, that what lies at the basis of phenomena, as a thing in itself, may not be heterogeneous; this difficulty disappears. There then remains no other difficulty than is to be found in the question—how a community of substances is possible; a question which lies out of the region of psychology, and which the reader, after what in our analytic has been said of primitive forces and faculties, will easily judge to be also beyond the region of human cognition.\n\nGENERAL REMARK\n\nOn the Transition from Rational Psychology to Cosmology.\n\nThe proposition, “I think,” or, “I exist thinking,” is an empirical proposition. But such a proposition must be based on empirical intuition, and the object cogitated as a phenomenon; and thus our theory appears to maintain that the soul, even in thought, is merely a phenomenon; and in this way our consciousness itself, in fact, abuts upon nothing.\n\nThought, per se, is merely the purely spontaneous logical function which operates to connect the manifold of a possible intuition; and it does not represent the subject of consciousness as a phenomenon—for this reason alone, that it pays no attention to the question whether the mode of intuiting it is sensuous or intellectual. I therefore do not represent myself in thought either as I am, or as I appear to myself; I merely cogitate myself as an object in general, of the mode of intuiting which I make abstraction. When I represent myself as the subject of thought, or as the ground of thought, these modes of representation are not related to the categories of substance or of cause; for these are functions of thought applicable only to our sensuous intuition. The application of these categories to the Ego would, however, be necessary, if I wished to make myself an object of knowledge. But I wish to be conscious of myself only as thinking; in what mode my Self is given in intuition, I do not consider, and it may be that I, who think, am a phenomenon—although not in so far as I am a thinking being; but in the consciousness of myself in mere thought I am a being, though this consciousness does not present to me any property of this being as material for thought.\n\nBut the proposition, “I think,” in so far as it declares, “I exist thinking,” is not the mere representation of a logical function. It determines the subject (which is in this case an object also) in relation to existence; and it cannot be given without the aid of the internal sense, whose intuition presents to us an object, not as a thing in itself, but always as a phenomenon. In this proposition there is therefore something more to be found than the mere spontaneity of thought; there is also the receptivity of intuition, that is, my thought of myself applied to the empirical intuition of myself. Now, in this intuition the thinking self must seek the conditions of the employment of its logical functions as categories of substance, cause, and so forth; not merely for the purpose of distinguishing itself as an object in itself by means of the representation “I,” but also for the purpose of determining the mode of its existence, that is, of cognizing itself as noumenon. But this is impossible, for the internal empirical intuition is sensuous, and presents us with nothing but phenomenal data, which do not assist the object of pure consciousness in its attempt to cognize itself as a separate existence, but are useful only as contributions to experience.\n\nBut, let it be granted that we could discover, not in experience, but in certain firmly-established à priori laws of the use of pure reason—laws relating to our existence, authority to consider ourselves as legislating à priori in relation to our own existence and as determining this existence; we should, on this supposition, find ourselves possessed of a spontaneity, by which our actual existence would be determinable, without the aid of the conditions of empirical intuition. We should also become aware that in the consciousness of our existence there was an à priori content, which would serve to determine our own existence—an existence only sensuously determinable—relatively, however, to a certain internal faculty in relation to an intelligible world.\n\nBut this would not give the least help to the attempts of rational psychology. For this wonderful faculty, which the consciousness of the moral law in me reveals, would present me with a principle of the determination of my own existence which is purely intellectual—but by what predicates? By none other than those which are given in sensuous intuition. Thus I should find myself in the same position in rational psychology which I formerly occupied, that is to say, I should find myself still in need of sensuous intuitions, in order to give significance to my conceptions of substance and cause, by means of which alone I can possess a knowledge of myself: but these intuitions can never raise me above the sphere of experience. I should be justified, however, in applying these conceptions, in regard to their practical use, which is always directed to objects of experience—in conformity with their analogical significance when employed theoretically—to freedom and its subject. At the same time, I should understand by them merely the logical functions of subject and predicate, of principle and consequence, in conformity with which all actions are so determined, that they are capable of being explained along with the laws of nature, conformably to the categories of substance and cause, although they originate from a very different principle. We have made these observations for the purpose of guarding against misunderstanding, to which the doctrine of our intuition of self as a phenomenon is exposed. We shall have occasion to perceive their utility in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason\n\nThe logical paralogism consists in the falsity of an argument in respect of its form, be the content what it may. But a transcendental paralogism has a transcendental foundation, and concludes falsely, while the form is correct and unexceptionable. In this manner the paralogism has its foundation in the nature of human reason, and is the parent of an unavoidable, though not insoluble, mental illusion.\n\nWe now come to a conception which was not inserted in the general list of transcendental conceptions, and yet must be reckoned with them, but at the same time without in the least altering, or indicating a deficiency in that table. This is the conception, or, if the term is preferred, the judgement, “I think.” But it is readily perceived that this thought is as it were the vehicle of all conceptions in general, and consequently of transcendental conceptions also, and that it is therefore regarded as a transcendental conception, although it can have no peculiar claim to be so ranked, inasmuch as its only use is to indicate that all thought is accompanied by consciousness. At the same time, pure as this conception is from empirical content (impressions of the senses), it enables us to distinguish two different kinds of objects. “I,” as thinking, am an object of the internal sense, and am called soul. That which is an object of the external senses is called body. Thus the expression, “I,” as a thinking being, designates the object-matter of psychology, which may be called “the rational doctrine of the soul,” inasmuch as in this science I desire to know nothing of the soul but what, independently of all experience (which determines me in concreto), may be concluded from this conception “I,” in so far as it appears in all thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0002","text":"Now, the rational doctrine of the soul is really an undertaking of this kind. For if the smallest empirical element of thought, if any particular perception of my internal state, were to be introduced among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would not be a rational, but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have thus before us a pretended science, raised upon the single proposition, “I think,” whose foundation or want of foundation we may very properly, and agreeably with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, here examine. It ought not to be objected that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of one’s self, an internal experience is asserted, and that consequently the rational doctrine of the soul which is founded upon it, is not pure, but partly founded upon an empirical principle. For this internal perception is nothing more than the mere apperception, “I think,” which in fact renders all transcendental conceptions possible, in which we say, “I think substance, cause, etc.” For internal experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general, and its relation to other perceptions, unless some particular distinction or determination thereof is empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but as cognition of the empirical, and belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is certainly transcendental. The smallest object of experience (for example, only pleasure or pain), that should be included in the general representation of self-consciousness, would immediately change the rational into an empirical psychology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","text":"“I think” is therefore the only text of rational psychology, from which it must develop its whole system. It is manifest that this thought, when applied to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates thereof; because the least empirical predicate would destroy the purity of the science and its independence of all experience.\n\nBut we shall have to follow here the guidance of the categories—only, as in the present case a thing, “I,” as thinking being, is at first given, we shall—not indeed change the order of the categories as it stands in the table—but begin at the category of substance, by which at the a thing in itself is represented and proceeds backwards through the series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else it may contain must be deduced, is accordingly as follows:\n\n            1                          2\n  The Soul is SUBSTANCE       As regards its quality\n                                it is SIMPLE\n\n                      3\n          As regards the different\n          times in which it exists,\n          it is numerically identical,\n          that is UNITY, not Plurality.\n\n                       4\n  It is in relation to possible objects in space[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-43)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0004","text":"[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-43) The reader, who may not so easily perceive the psychological sense of these expressions, taken here in their transcendental abstraction, and cannot guess why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find the expressions sufficiently explained and justified in the sequel. I have, moreover, to apologize for the Latin terms which have been employed, instead of their German synonyms, contrary to the rules of correct writing. But I judged it better to sacrifice elegance to perspicuity.\n\nFrom these elements originate all the conceptions of pure psychology, by combination alone, without the aid of any other principle. This substance, merely as an object of the internal sense, gives the conception of Immateriality; as simple substance, that of Incorruptibility; its identity, as intellectual substance, gives the conception of Personality; all these three together, Spirituality. Its relation to objects in space gives us the conception of connection (commercium) with bodies. Thus it represents thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, that is, as a soul (anima), and as the ground of Animality; and this, limited and determined by the conception of spirituality, gives us that of Immortality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","text":"Now to these conceptions relate four paralogisms of a transcendental psychology, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason, touching the nature of our thinking being. We can, however, lay at the foundation of this science nothing but the simple and in itself perfectly contentless representation “i” which cannot even be called a conception, but merely a consciousness which accompanies all conceptions. By this “I,” or “He,” or “It,” who or which thinks, nothing more is represented than a transcendental subject of thought = x, which is cognized only by means of the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, apart from these, we cannot form the least conception. Hence in a perpetual circle, inasmuch as we must always employ it, in order to frame any judgement respecting it. And this inconvenience we find it impossible to rid ourselves of, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation distinguishing a particular object, as a form of representation in general, in so far as it may be termed cognition; for in and by cognition alone do I think anything.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0006","text":"It must, however, appear extraordinary at first sight that the condition under which I think, and which is consequently a property of my subject, should be held to be likewise valid for every existence which thinks, and that we can presume to base upon a seemingly empirical proposition a judgement which is apodeictic and universal, to wit, that everything which thinks is constituted as the voice of my consciousness declares it to be, that is, as a self-conscious being. The cause of this belief is to be found in the fact that we necessarily attribute to things à priori all the properties which constitute conditions under which alone we can cogitate them. Now I cannot obtain the least representation of a thinking being by means of external experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Such objects are consequently nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things which can only thus be represented as thinking beings. The proposition, “I think,” is, in the present case, understood in a problematical sense, not in so far as it contains a perception of an existence (like the Cartesian “Cogito, ergo sum”), but in regard to its mere possibility—for the purpose of discovering what properties may be inferred from so simple a proposition and predicated of the subject of it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0007","text":"If at the foundation of our pure rational cognition of thinking beings there lay more than the mere Cogito—if we could likewise call in aid observations on the play of our thoughts, and the thence derived natural laws of the thinking self, there would arise an empirical psychology which would be a kind of physiology of the internal sense and might possibly be capable of explaining the phenomena of that sense. But it could never be available for discovering those properties which do not belong to possible experience (such as the quality of simplicity), nor could it make any apodeictic enunciation on the nature of thinking beings: it would therefore not be a rational psychology.\n\nNow, as the proposition “I think” (in the problematical sense) contains the form of every judgement in general and is the constant accompaniment of all the categories, it is manifest that conclusions are drawn from it only by a transcendental employment of the understanding. This use of the understanding excludes all empirical elements; and we cannot, as has been shown above, have any favourable conception beforehand of its procedure. We shall therefore follow with a critical eye this proposition through all the predicaments of pure psychology; but we shall, for brevity’s sake, allow this examination to proceed in an uninterrupted connection.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","text":"Before entering on this task, however, the following general remark may help to quicken our attention to this mode of argument. It is not merely through my thinking that I cognize an object, but only through my determining a given intuition in relation to the unity of consciousness in which all thinking consists. It follows that I cognize myself, not through my being conscious of myself as thinking, but only when I am conscious of the intuition of myself as determined in relation to the function of thought. All the modi of self-consciousness in thought are hence not conceptions of objects (conceptions of the understanding—categories); they are mere logical functions, which do not present to thought an object to be cognized, and cannot therefore present my Self as an object. Not the consciousness of the determining, but only that of the determinable self, that is, of my internal intuition (in so far as the manifold contained in it can be connected conformably with the general condition of the unity of apperception in thought), is the object.\n\n1. In all judgements I am the determining subject of that relation which constitutes a judgement. But that the I which thinks, must be considered as in thought always a subject, and as a thing which cannot be a predicate to thought, is an apodeictic and identical proposition. But this proposition does not signify that I, as an object, am, for myself, a self-subsistent being or substance. This latter statement—an ambitious one—requires to be supported by data which are not to be discovered in thought; and are perhaps (in so far as I consider the thinking self merely as such) not to be discovered in the thinking self at all.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","text":"2. That the I or Ego of apperception, and consequently in all thought, is singular or simple, and cannot be resolved into a plurality of subjects, and therefore indicates a logically simple subject—this is self-evident from the very conception of an Ego, and is consequently an analytical proposition. But this is not tantamount to declaring that the thinking Ego is a simple substance—for this would be a synthetical proposition. The conception of substance always relates to intuitions, which with me cannot be other than sensuous, and which consequently lie completely out of the sphere of the understanding and its thought: but to this sphere belongs the affirmation that the Ego is simple in thought. It would indeed be surprising, if the conception of “substance,” which in other cases requires so much labour to distinguish from the other elements presented by intuition—so much trouble, too, to discover whether it can be simple (as in the case of the parts of matter)—should be presented immediately to me, as if by revelation, in the poorest mental representation of all.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","text":"3. The proposition of the identity of my Self amidst all the manifold representations of which I am conscious, is likewise a proposition lying in the conceptions themselves, and is consequently analytical. But this identity of the subject, of which I am conscious in all its representations, does not relate to or concern the intuition of the subject, by which it is given as an object. This proposition cannot therefore enounce the identity of the person, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of its own substance as a thinking being in all change and variation of circumstances. To prove this, we should require not a mere analysis of the proposition, but synthetical judgements based upon a given intuition.\n\n4. I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from that of other things external to me—among which my body also is reckoned. This is also an analytical proposition, for other things are exactly those which I think as different or distinguished from myself. But whether this consciousness of myself is possible without things external to me; and whether therefore I can exist merely as a thinking being (without being man)—cannot be known or inferred from this proposition.\n\nThus we have gained nothing as regards the cognition of myself as object, by the analysis of the consciousness of my Self in thought. The logical exposition of thought in general is mistaken for a metaphysical determination of the object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0011","text":"Our Critique would be an investigation utterly superfluous, if there existed a possibility of proving à priori, that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances, as such, therefore, possess the inseparable attribute of personality, and are conscious of their existence apart from and unconnected with matter. For we should thus have taken a step beyond the world of sense, and have penetrated into the sphere of noumena; and in this case the right could not be denied us of extending our knowledge in this sphere, of establishing ourselves, and, under a favouring star, appropriating to ourselves possessions in it. For the proposition: “Every thinking being, as such, is simple substance,” is an à priori synthetical proposition; because in the first place it goes beyond the conception which is the subject of it, and adds to the mere notion of a thinking being the mode of its existence, and in the second place annexes a predicate (that of simplicity) to the latter conception—a predicate which it could not have discovered in the sphere of experience. It would follow that à priori synthetical propositions are possible and legitimate, not only, as we have maintained, in relation to objects of possible experience, and as principles of the possibility of this experience itself, but are applicable to things in themselves—an inference which makes an end of the whole of this Critique, and obliges us to fall back on the old mode of metaphysical procedure. But indeed the danger is not so great, if we look a little closer into the question.\n\nThere lurks in the procedure of rational Psychology a paralogism, which is represented in the following syllogism:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0012","text":"That which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject, does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance.\n\nA thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject.\n\nTherefore it exists also as such, that is, as substance.\n\nIn the major we speak of a being that can be cogitated generally and in every relation, consequently as it may be given in intuition. But in the minor we speak of the same being only in so far as it regards itself as subject, relatively to thought and the unity of consciousness, but not in relation to intuition, by which it is presented as an object to thought. Thus the conclusion is here arrived at by a Sophisma figurae dictionis.[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-44)\n\n[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-44) Thought is taken in the two premisses in two totally different senses. In the major it is considered as relating and applying to objects in general, consequently to objects of intuition also. In the minor, we understand it as relating merely to self-consciousness. In this sense, we do not cogitate an object, but merely the relation to the self-consciousness of the subject, as the form of thought. In the former premiss we speak of things which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subjects. In the second, we do not speak of things, but of thought (all objects being abstracted), in which the Ego is always the subject of consciousness. Hence the conclusion cannot be, “I cannot exist otherwise than as subject”; but only “I can, in cogitating my existence, employ my Ego only as the subject of the judgement.” But this is an identical proposition, and throws no light on the mode of my existence.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","text":"That this famous argument is a mere paralogism, will be plain to any one who will consider the general remark which precedes our exposition of the principles of the pure understanding, and the section on noumena. For it was there proved that the conception of a thing, which can exist per se—only as a subject and never as a predicate, possesses no objective reality; that is to say, we can never know whether there exists any object to correspond to the conception; consequently, the conception is nothing more than a conception, and from it we derive no proper knowledge. If this conception is to indicate by the term substance, an object that can be given, if it is to become a cognition, we must have at the foundation of the cognition a permanent intuition, as the indispensable condition of its objective reality. For through intuition alone can an object be given. But in internal intuition there is nothing permanent, for the Ego is but the consciousness of my thought. If then, we appeal merely to thought, we cannot discover the necessary condition of the application of the conception of substance—that is, of a subject existing per se—to the subject as a thinking being. And thus the conception of the simple nature of substance, which is connected with the objective reality of this conception, is shown to be also invalid, and to be, in fact, nothing more than the logical qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thought; whilst we remain perfectly ignorant whether the subject is composite or not.\n\nRefutation of the Argument of Mendelssohn for the Substantiality or Permanence of the Soul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","text":"This acute philosopher easily perceived the insufficiency of the common argument which attempts to prove that the soul—it being granted that it is a simple being—cannot perish by dissolution or decomposition; he saw it is not impossible for it to cease to be by extinction, or disappearance. He endeavoured to prove in his Phaedo, that the soul cannot be annihilated, by showing that a simple being cannot cease to exist. Inasmuch as, he said, a simple existence cannot diminish, nor gradually lose portions of its being, and thus be by degrees reduced to nothing (for it possesses no parts, and therefore no multiplicity), between the moment in which it is, and the moment in which it is not, no time can be discovered—which is impossible. But this philosopher did not consider that, granting the soul to possess this simple nature, which contains no parts external to each other and consequently no extensive quantity, we cannot refuse to it any less than to any other being, intensive quantity, that is, a degree of reality in regard to all its faculties, nay, to all that constitutes its existence. But this degree of reality can become less and less through an infinite series of smaller degrees. It follows, therefore, that this supposed substance—this thing, the permanence of which is not assured in any other way, may, if not by decomposition, by gradual loss (remissio) of its powers (consequently by elanguescence, if I may employ this expression), be changed into nothing. For consciousness itself has always a degree, which may be lessened.[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-45)Consequently the faculty of being conscious may be diminished; and so with all other faculties. The permanence of the soul, therefore, as an object of the internal sense, remains undemonstrated, nay, even indemonstrable. Its permanence in life is evident, per se, inasmuch as the thinking being (as man) is to itself, at the same time, an object of the external senses. But this does not authorize the rational psychologist to affirm, from mere conceptions, its permanence beyond life.[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-46)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0015","text":"[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-45) Clearness is not, as logicians maintain, the consciousness of a representation. For a certain degree of consciousness, which may not, however, be sufficient for recollection, is to be met with in many dim representations. For without any consciousness at all, we should not be able to recognize any difference in the obscure representations we connect; as we really can do with many conceptions, such as those of right and justice, and those of the musician, who strikes at once several notes in improvising a piece of music. But a representation is clear, in which our consciousness is sufficient for the consciousness of the difference of this representation from others. If we are only conscious that there is a difference, but are not conscious of the difference—that is, what the difference is—the representation must be termed obscure. There is, consequently, an infinite series of degrees of consciousness down to its entire disappearance.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","text":"[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-46) There are some who think they have done enough to establish a new possibility in the mode of the existence of souls, when they have shown that there is no contradiction in their hypotheses on this subject. Such are those who affirm the possibility of thought—of which they have no other knowledge than what they derive from its use in connecting empirical intuitions presented in this our human life—after this life has ceased. But it is very easy to embarrass them by the introduction of counter-possibilities, which rest upon quite as good a foundation. Such, for example, is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances; and conversely, of the coalition of several into one simple substance. For, although divisibility presupposes composition, it does not necessarily require a composition of substances, but only of the degrees (of the several faculties) of one and the same substance. Now we can cogitate all the powers and faculties of the soul—even that of consciousness—as diminished by one half, the substance still remaining. In the same way we can represent to ourselves without contradiction, this obliterated half as preserved, not in the soul, but without it; and we can believe that, as in this case every thing that is real in the soul, and has a degree—consequently its entire existence—has been halved, a particular substance would arise out of the soul. For the multiplicity, which has been divided, formerly existed, but not as a multiplicity of substances, but of every reality as the quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was merely a mode of existence, which by this division alone has been transformed into a plurality of subsistence. In the same manner several simple substances might coalesce into one, without anything being lost except the plurality of subsistence, inasmuch as the one substance would contain the degree of reality of all the former substances. Perhaps, indeed, the simple substances, which appear under the form of matter, might (not indeed by a mechanical or chemical influence upon each other, but by an unknown influence, of which the former would be but the phenomenal appearance), by means of such a dynamical division of the parent-souls, as intensive quantities, produce other souls, while the former repaired the loss thus sustained with new matter of the same sort. I am far from allowing any value to such chimeras; and the principles of our analytic have clearly proved that no other than an empirical use of the categories—that of substance, for example—is possible. But if the rationalist is bold enough to construct, on the mere authority of the faculty of thought—without any intuition, whereby an object is given—a self-subsistent being, merely because the unity of apperception in thought cannot allow him to believe it a composite being, instead of declaring, as he ought to do, that he is unable to explain the possibility of a thinking nature; what ought to hinder the materialist, with as complete an independence of experience, to employ the principle of the rationalist in a directly opposite manner—still preserving the formal unity required by his opponent?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0017","text":"If, now, we take the above propositions—as they must be accepted as valid for all thinking beings in the system of rational psychology—in synthetical connection, and proceed, from the category of relation, with the proposition: “All thinking beings are, as such, substances,” backwards through the series, till the circle is completed; we come at last to their existence, of which, in this system of rational psychology, substances are held to be conscious, independently of external things; nay, it is asserted that, in relation to the permanence which is a necessary characteristic of substance, they can of themselves determine external things. It follows that idealism—at least problematical idealism, is perfectly unavoidable in this rationalistic system. And, if the existence of outward things is not held to be requisite to the determination of the existence of a substance in time, the existence of these outward things at all, is a gratuitous assumption which remains without the possibility of a proof.\n\nBut if we proceed analytically—the “I think” as a proposition containing in itself an existence as given, consequently modality being the principle—and dissect this proposition, in order to ascertain its content, and discover whether and how this Ego determines its existence in time and space without the aid of anything external; the propositions of rationalistic psychology would not begin with the conception of a thinking being, but with a reality, and the properties of a thinking being in general would be deduced from the mode in which this reality is cogitated, after everything empirical had been abstracted; as is shown in the following table:\n\n                        1\n                      I think,\n\n            2                             3\n        as Subject,              as simple Subject,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0018","text":"4\n               as identical Subject,\n           in every state of my thought.\n\nNow, inasmuch as it is not determined in this second proposition, whether I can exist and be cogitated only as subject, and not also as a predicate of another being, the conception of a subject is here taken in a merely logical sense; and it remains undetermined, whether substance is to be cogitated under the conception or not. But in the third proposition, the absolute unity of apperception—the simple Ego in the representation to which all connection and separation, which constitute thought, relate, is of itself important; even although it presents us with no information about the constitution or subsistence of the subject. Apperception is something real, and the simplicity of its nature is given in the very fact of its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that is at the same time simple; for points, which are the only simple things in space, are merely limits, but not constituent parts of space. From this follows the impossibility of a definition on the basis of materialism of the constitution of my Ego as a merely thinking subject. But, because my existence is considered in the first proposition as given, for it does not mean, “Every thinking being exists” (for this would be predicating of them absolute necessity), but only, “I exist thinking”; the proposition is quite empirical, and contains the determinability of my existence merely in relation to my representations in time. But as I require for this purpose something that is permanent, such as is not given in internal intuition; the mode of my existence, whether as substance or as accident, cannot be determined by means of this simple self-consciousness. Thus, if materialism is inadequate to explain the mode in which I exist, spiritualism is likewise as insufficient; and the conclusion is that we are utterly unable to attain to any knowledge of the constitution of the soul, in so far as relates to the possibility of its existence apart from external objects.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","text":"And, indeed, how should it be possible, merely by the aid of the unity of consciousness—which we cognize only for the reason that it is indispensable to the possibility of experience—to pass the bounds of experience (our existence in this life); and to extend our cognition to the nature of all thinking beings by means of the empirical—but in relation to every sort of intuition, perfectly undetermined—proposition, “I think”?\n\nThere does not then exist any rational psychology as a doctrine furnishing any addition to our knowledge of ourselves. It is nothing more than a discipline, which sets impassable limits to speculative reason in this region of thought, to prevent it, on the one hand, from throwing itself into the arms of a soulless materialism, and, on the other, from losing itself in the mazes of a baseless spiritualism. It teaches us to consider this refusal of our reason to give any satisfactory answer to questions which reach beyond the limits of this our human life, as a hint to abandon fruitless speculation; and to direct, to a practical use, our knowledge of ourselves—which, although applicable only to objects of experience, receives its principles from a higher source, and regulates its procedure as if our destiny reached far beyond the boundaries of experience and life.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","text":"From all this it is evident that rational psychology has its origin in a mere misunderstanding. The unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of the categories, is considered to be an intuition of the subject as an object; and the category of substance is applied to the intuition. But this unity is nothing more than the unity in thought, by which no object is given; to which therefore the category of substance—which always presupposes a given intuition—cannot be applied. Consequently, the subject cannot be cognized. The subject of the categories cannot, therefore, for the very reason that it cogitates these, frame any conception of itself as an object of the categories; for, to cogitate these, it must lay at the foundation its own pure self-consciousness—the very thing that it wishes to explain and describe. In like manner, the subject, in which the representation of time has its basis, cannot determine, for this very reason, its own existence in time. Now, if the latter is impossible, the former, as an attempt to determine itself by means of the categories as a thinking being in general, is no less so.[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-47)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0021","text":"[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-47) The “I think” is, as has been already stated, an empirical proposition, and contains the proposition, “I exist.” But I cannot say, “Everything, which thinks, exists”; for in this case the property of thought would constitute all beings possessing it, necessary beings. Hence my existence cannot be considered as an inference from the proposition, “I think,” as Descartes maintained—because in this case the major premiss, “Everything, which thinks, exists,” must precede—but the two propositions are identical. The proposition, “I think,” expresses an undetermined empirical intuition, that perception (proving consequently that sensation, which must belong to sensibility, lies at the foundation of this proposition); but it precedes experience, whose province it is to determine an object of perception by means of the categories in relation to time; and existence in this proposition is not a category, as it does not apply to an undetermined given object, but only to one of which we have a conception, and about which we wish to know whether it does or does not exist, out of, and apart from this conception. An undetermined perception signifies here merely something real that has been given, only, however, to thought in general—but not as a phenomenon, nor as a thing in itself (noumenon), but only as something that really exists, and is designated as such in the proposition, “I think.” For it must be remarked that, when I call the proposition, “I think,” an empirical proposition, I do not thereby mean that the Ego in the proposition is an empirical representation; on the contrary, it is purely intellectual, because it belongs to thought in general. But without some empirical representation, which presents to the mind material for thought, the mental act, “I think,” would not take place; and the empirical is only the condition of the application or employment of the pure intellectual faculty.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","text":"Thus, then, appears the vanity of the hope of establishing a cognition which is to extend its rule beyond the limits of experience—a cognition which is one of the highest interests of humanity; and thus is proved the futility of the attempt of speculative philosophy in this region of thought. But, in this interest of thought, the severity of criticism has rendered to reason a not unimportant service, by the demonstration of the impossibility of making any dogmatical affirmation concerning an object of experience beyond the boundaries of experience. She has thus fortified reason against all affirmations of the contrary. Now, this can be accomplished in only two ways. Either our proposition must be proved apodeictically; or, if this is unsuccessful, the sources of this inability must be sought for, and, if these are discovered to exist in the natural and necessary limitation of our reason, our opponents must submit to the same law of renunciation and refrain from advancing claims to dogmatic assertion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","text":"But the right, say rather the necessity to admit a future life, upon principles of the practical conjoined with the speculative use of reason, has lost nothing by this renunciation; for the merely speculative proof has never had any influence upon the common reason of men. It stands upon the point of a hair, so that even the schools have been able to preserve it from falling only by incessantly discussing it and spinning it like a top; and even in their eyes it has never been able to present any safe foundation for the erection of a theory. The proofs which have been current among men, preserve their value undiminished; nay, rather gain in clearness and unsophisticated power, by the rejection of the dogmatical assumptions of speculative reason. For reason is thus confined within her own peculiar province—the arrangement of ends or aims, which is at the same time the arrangement of nature; and, as a practical faculty, without limiting itself to the latter, it is justified in extending the former, and with it our own existence, beyond the boundaries of experience and life. If we turn our attention to the analogy of the nature of living beings in this world, in the consideration of which reason is obliged to accept as a principle that no organ, no faculty, no appetite is useless, and that nothing is superfluous, nothing disproportionate to its use, nothing unsuited to its end; but that, on the contrary, everything is perfectly conformed to its destination in life—we shall find that man, who alone is the final end and aim of this order, is still the only animal that seems to be excepted from it. For his natural gifts—not merely as regards the talents and motives that may incite him to employ them, but especially the moral law in him—stretch so far beyond all mere earthly utility and advantage, that he feels himself bound to prize the mere consciousness of probity, apart from all advantageous consequences—even the shadowy gift of posthumous fame—above everything; and he is conscious of an inward call to constitute himself, by his conduct in this world—without regard to mere sublunary interests—the citizen of a better. This mighty, irresistible proof—accompanied by an ever-increasing knowledge of the conformability to a purpose in everything we see around us, by the conviction of the boundless immensity of creation, by the consciousness of a certain illimitableness in the possible extension of our knowledge, and by a desire commensurate therewith—remains to humanity, even after the theoretical cognition of ourselves has failed to establish the necessity of an existence after death.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0024","text":"Conclusion of the Solution of the Psychological Paralogism.\n\nThe dialectical illusion in rational psychology arises from our confounding an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the conception—in every respect undetermined—of a thinking being in general. I cogitate myself in behalf of a possible experience, at the same time making abstraction of all actual experience; and infer therefrom that I can be conscious of myself apart from experience and its empirical conditions. I consequently confound the possible abstraction of my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a possible separate existence of my thinking self; and I believe that I cognize what is substantial in myself as a transcendental subject, when I have nothing more in thought than the unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of all determination of cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0025","text":"The task of explaining the community of the soul with the body does not properly belong to the psychology of which we are here speaking; because it proposes to prove the personality of the soul apart from this communion (after death), and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense of the word, although occupying itself with an object of experience—only in so far, however, as it ceases to be an object of experience. But a sufficient answer may be found to the question in our system. The difficulty which lies in the execution of this task consists, as is well known, in the presupposed heterogeneity of the object of the internal sense (the soul) and the objects of the external senses; inasmuch as the formal condition of the intuition of the one is time, and of that of the other space also. But if we consider that both kinds of objects do not differ internally, but only in so far as the one appears externally to the other—consequently, that what lies at the basis of phenomena, as a thing in itself, may not be heterogeneous; this difficulty disappears. There then remains no other difficulty than is to be found in the question—how a community of substances is possible; a question which lies out of the region of psychology, and which the reader, after what in our analytic has been said of primitive forces and faculties, will easily judge to be also beyond the region of human cognition.\n\nGENERAL REMARK\n\nOn the Transition from Rational Psychology to Cosmology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0026","text":"The proposition, “I think,” or, “I exist thinking,” is an empirical proposition. But such a proposition must be based on empirical intuition, and the object cogitated as a phenomenon; and thus our theory appears to maintain that the soul, even in thought, is merely a phenomenon; and in this way our consciousness itself, in fact, abuts upon nothing.\n\nThought, per se, is merely the purely spontaneous logical function which operates to connect the manifold of a possible intuition; and it does not represent the subject of consciousness as a phenomenon—for this reason alone, that it pays no attention to the question whether the mode of intuiting it is sensuous or intellectual. I therefore do not represent myself in thought either as I am, or as I appear to myself; I merely cogitate myself as an object in general, of the mode of intuiting which I make abstraction. When I represent myself as the subject of thought, or as the ground of thought, these modes of representation are not related to the categories of substance or of cause; for these are functions of thought applicable only to our sensuous intuition. The application of these categories to the Ego would, however, be necessary, if I wished to make myself an object of knowledge. But I wish to be conscious of myself only as thinking; in what mode my Self is given in intuition, I do not consider, and it may be that I, who think, am a phenomenon—although not in so far as I am a thinking being; but in the consciousness of myself in mere thought I am a being, though this consciousness does not present to me any property of this being as material for thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0027","text":"But the proposition, “I think,” in so far as it declares, “I exist thinking,” is not the mere representation of a logical function. It determines the subject (which is in this case an object also) in relation to existence; and it cannot be given without the aid of the internal sense, whose intuition presents to us an object, not as a thing in itself, but always as a phenomenon. In this proposition there is therefore something more to be found than the mere spontaneity of thought; there is also the receptivity of intuition, that is, my thought of myself applied to the empirical intuition of myself. Now, in this intuition the thinking self must seek the conditions of the employment of its logical functions as categories of substance, cause, and so forth; not merely for the purpose of distinguishing itself as an object in itself by means of the representation “I,” but also for the purpose of determining the mode of its existence, that is, of cognizing itself as noumenon. But this is impossible, for the internal empirical intuition is sensuous, and presents us with nothing but phenomenal data, which do not assist the object of pure consciousness in its attempt to cognize itself as a separate existence, but are useful only as contributions to experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0028","text":"But, let it be granted that we could discover, not in experience, but in certain firmly-established à priori laws of the use of pure reason—laws relating to our existence, authority to consider ourselves as legislating à priori in relation to our own existence and as determining this existence; we should, on this supposition, find ourselves possessed of a spontaneity, by which our actual existence would be determinable, without the aid of the conditions of empirical intuition. We should also become aware that in the consciousness of our existence there was an à priori content, which would serve to determine our own existence—an existence only sensuously determinable—relatively, however, to a certain internal faculty in relation to an intelligible world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0029","text":"But this would not give the least help to the attempts of rational psychology. For this wonderful faculty, which the consciousness of the moral law in me reveals, would present me with a principle of the determination of my own existence which is purely intellectual—but by what predicates? By none other than those which are given in sensuous intuition. Thus I should find myself in the same position in rational psychology which I formerly occupied, that is to say, I should find myself still in need of sensuous intuitions, in order to give significance to my conceptions of substance and cause, by means of which alone I can possess a knowledge of myself: but these intuitions can never raise me above the sphere of experience. I should be justified, however, in applying these conceptions, in regard to their practical use, which is always directed to objects of experience—in conformity with their analogical significance when employed theoretically—to freedom and its subject. At the same time, I should understand by them merely the logical functions of subject and predicate, of principle and consequence, in conformity with which all actions are so determined, that they are capable of being explained along with the laws of nature, conformably to the categories of substance and cause, although they originate from a very different principle. We have made these observations for the purpose of guarding against misunderstanding, to which the doctrine of our intuition of self as a phenomenon is exposed. We shall have occasion to perceive their utility in the sequel.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2623452c01dbafdb4b2153c996d6ea8805add2bdae8e6f46ab9222afa3dd4e14","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","order":61,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CIAP","text":"### Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason\n\nWe showed in the introduction to this part of our work, that all transcendental illusion of pure reason arose from dialectical arguments, the schema of which logic gives us in its three formal species of syllogisms—just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgements. The first kind of these sophistical arguments related to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or soul), in correspondence with the categorical syllogisms, the major of which, as the principle, enounces the relation of a predicate to a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will therefore be concerned, following the analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, with the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in the phenomenon; and, in this way, the theme of the third kind to be treated of in the following chapter will be the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.\n\nBut it is worthy of remark that the transcendental paralogism produced in the mind only a one-third illusion, in regard to the idea of the subject of our thought; and the conceptions of reason gave no ground to maintain the contrary proposition. The advantage is completely on the side of Pneumatism; although this theory itself passes into naught, in the crucible of pure reason.\n\nVery different is the case when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of phenomena. Here, certainly, reason establishes, with much plausibility, its principle of unconditioned unity; but it very soon falls into such contradictions that it is compelled, in relation to cosmology, to renounce its pretensions.\n\nFor here a new phenomenon of human reason meets us—a perfectly natural antithetic, which does not require to be sought for by subtle sophistry, but into which reason of itself unavoidably falls. It is thereby preserved, to be sure, from the slumber of a fancied conviction—which a merely one-sided illusion produces; but it is at the same time compelled, either, on the one hand, to abandon itself to a despairing scepticism, or, on the other, to assume a dogmatical confidence and obstinate persistence in certain assertions, without granting a fair hearing to the other side of the question. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps deserve the title of the euthanasia of pure reason.\n\nBefore entering this region of discord and confusion, which the conflict of the laws of pure reason (antinomy) produces, we shall present the reader with some considerations, in explanation and justification of the method we intend to follow in our treatment of this subject. I term all transcendental ideas, in so far as they relate to the absolute totality in the synthesis of phenomena, cosmical conceptions; partly on account of this unconditioned totality, on which the conception of the world-whole is based—a conception, which is itself an idea—partly because they relate solely to the synthesis of phenomena—the empirical synthesis; while, on the other hand, the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things gives rise to an ideal of pure reason, which is quite distinct from the cosmical conception, although it stands in relation with it. Hence, as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation for a dialectical psychology, the antinomy of pure reason will present us with the transcendental principles of a pretended pure (rational) cosmology—not, however, to declare it valid and to appropriate it, but—as the very term of a conflict of reason sufficiently indicates, to present it as an idea which cannot be reconciled with phenomena and experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason\n\nWe showed in the introduction to this part of our work, that all transcendental illusion of pure reason arose from dialectical arguments, the schema of which logic gives us in its three formal species of syllogisms—just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgements. The first kind of these sophistical arguments related to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or soul), in correspondence with the categorical syllogisms, the major of which, as the principle, enounces the relation of a predicate to a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will therefore be concerned, following the analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, with the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in the phenomenon; and, in this way, the theme of the third kind to be treated of in the following chapter will be the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.\n\nBut it is worthy of remark that the transcendental paralogism produced in the mind only a one-third illusion, in regard to the idea of the subject of our thought; and the conceptions of reason gave no ground to maintain the contrary proposition. The advantage is completely on the side of Pneumatism; although this theory itself passes into naught, in the crucible of pure reason.\n\nVery different is the case when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of phenomena. Here, certainly, reason establishes, with much plausibility, its principle of unconditioned unity; but it very soon falls into such contradictions that it is compelled, in relation to cosmology, to renounce its pretensions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","text":"For here a new phenomenon of human reason meets us—a perfectly natural antithetic, which does not require to be sought for by subtle sophistry, but into which reason of itself unavoidably falls. It is thereby preserved, to be sure, from the slumber of a fancied conviction—which a merely one-sided illusion produces; but it is at the same time compelled, either, on the one hand, to abandon itself to a despairing scepticism, or, on the other, to assume a dogmatical confidence and obstinate persistence in certain assertions, without granting a fair hearing to the other side of the question. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps deserve the title of the euthanasia of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","text":"Before entering this region of discord and confusion, which the conflict of the laws of pure reason (antinomy) produces, we shall present the reader with some considerations, in explanation and justification of the method we intend to follow in our treatment of this subject. I term all transcendental ideas, in so far as they relate to the absolute totality in the synthesis of phenomena, cosmical conceptions; partly on account of this unconditioned totality, on which the conception of the world-whole is based—a conception, which is itself an idea—partly because they relate solely to the synthesis of phenomena—the empirical synthesis; while, on the other hand, the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things gives rise to an ideal of pure reason, which is quite distinct from the cosmical conception, although it stands in relation with it. Hence, as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation for a dialectical psychology, the antinomy of pure reason will present us with the transcendental principles of a pretended pure (rational) cosmology—not, however, to declare it valid and to appropriate it, but—as the very term of a conflict of reason sufficiently indicates, to present it as an idea which cannot be reconciled with phenomena and experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9770308c35dc21fc410f557ac62110eb36a99efb586a914c101d96f3d73459dd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","order":62,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SISC","text":"# Cosmos\n\n### Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas\n\nThat we may be able to enumerate with systematic precision these ideas according to a principle, we must remark, in the first place, that it is from the understanding alone that pure and transcendental conceptions take their origin; that the reason does not properly give birth to any conception, but only frees the conception of the understanding from the unavoidable limitation of a possible experience, and thus endeavours to raise it above the empirical, though it must still be in connection with it. This happens from the fact that, for a given conditioned, reason demands absolute totality on the side of the conditions (to which the understanding submits all phenomena), and thus makes of the category a transcendental idea. This it does that it may be able to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis, by continuing it to the unconditioned (which is not to be found in experience, but only in the idea). Reason requires this according to the principle: If the conditioned is given the whole of the conditions, and consequently the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, whereby alone the former was possible. First, then, the transcendental ideas are properly nothing but categories elevated to the unconditioned; and they may be arranged in a table according to the titles of the latter. But, secondly, all the categories are not available for this purpose, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series—of conditions subordinated to, not co-ordinated with, each other. Absolute totality is required of reason only in so far as concerns the ascending series of the conditions of a conditioned; not, consequently, when the question relates to the descending series of consequences, or to the aggregate of the co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For, in relation to a given conditioned, conditions are presupposed and considered to be given along with it. On the other hand, as the consequences do not render possible their conditions, but rather presuppose them—in the consideration of the procession of consequences (or in the descent from the given condition to the conditioned), we may be quite unconcerned whether the series ceases or not; and their totality is not a necessary demand of reason.\n\nThus we cogitate—and necessarily—a given time completely elapsed up to a given moment, although that time is not determinable by us. But as regards time future, which is not the condition of arriving at the present, in order to conceive it; it is quite indifferent whether we consider future time as ceasing at some point, or as prolonging itself to infinity. Take, for example, the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in relation to m, but at the same time as the condition of o, and let the series proceed upwards from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and also downwards from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.)—I must presuppose the former series, to be able to consider n as given, and n is according to reason (the totality of conditions) possible only by means of that series. But its possibility does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which for this reason cannot be regarded as given, but only as capable of being given (dabilis).\n\nI shall term the synthesis of the series on the side of the conditions—from that nearest to the given phenomenon up to the more remote—regressive; that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the immediate consequence to the more remote, I shall call the progressive synthesis. The former proceeds in antecedentia, the latter in consequentia. The cosmological ideas are therefore occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed in antecedentia, not in consequentia. When the latter takes place, it is an arbitrary and not a necessary problem of pure reason; for we require, for the complete understanding of what is given in a phenomenon, not the consequences which succeed, but the grounds or principles which precede.\n\nIn order to construct the table of ideas in correspondence with the table of categories, we take first the two primitive quanta of all our intuitions, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence, in relation to a given present, we must distinguish à priori in it the antecedentia as conditions (time past) from the consequentia (time future). Consequently, the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned, relates merely to all past time. According to the idea of reason, the whole past time, as the condition of the given moment, is necessarily cogitated as given. But, as regards space, there exists in it no distinction between progressus and regressus; for it is an aggregate and not a series—its parts existing together at the same time. I can consider a given point of time in relation to past time only as conditioned, because this given moment comes into existence only through the past time rather through the passing of the preceding time. But as the parts of space are not subordinated, but co-ordinated to each other, one part cannot be the condition of the possibility of the other; and space is not in itself, like time, a series. But the synthesis of the manifold parts of space—(the syntheses whereby we apprehend space)—is nevertheless successive; it takes place, therefore, in time, and contains a series. And as in this series of aggregated spaces (for example, the feet in a rood), beginning with a given portion of space, those which continue to be annexed form the condition of the limits of the former—the measurement of a space must also be regarded as a synthesis of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. It differs, however, in this respect from that of time, that the side of the conditioned is not in itself distinguishable from the side of the condition; and, consequently, regressus and progressus in space seem to be identical. But, inasmuch as one part of space is not given, but only limited, by and through another, we must also consider every limited space as conditioned, in so far as it presupposes some other space as the condition of its limitation, and so on. As regards limitation, therefore, our procedure in space is also a regressus, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in a series of conditions applies to space also; and I am entitled to demand the absolute totality of the phenomenal synthesis in space as well as in time. Whether my demand can be satisfied is a question to be answered in the sequel.\n\nSecondly, the real in space—that is, matter—is conditioned. Its internal conditions are its parts, and the parts of parts its remote conditions; so that in this case we find a regressive synthesis, the absolute totality of which is a demand of reason. But this cannot be obtained otherwise than by a complete division of parts, whereby the real in matter becomes either nothing or that which is not matter, that is to say, the simple. Consequently we find here also a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.\n\nThirdly, as regards the categories of a real relation between phenomena, the category of substance and its accidents is not suitable for the formation of a transcendental idea; that is to say, reason has no ground, in regard to it, to proceed regressively with conditions. For accidents (in so far as they inhere in a substance) are co-ordinated with each other, and do not constitute a series. And, in relation to substance, they are not properly subordinated to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself. The conception of the substantial might nevertheless seem to be an idea of the transcendental reason. But, as this signifies nothing more than the conception of an object in general, which subsists in so far as we cogitate in it merely a transcendental subject without any predicates; and as the question here is of an unconditioned in the series of phenomena—it is clear that the substantial can form no member thereof. The same holds good of substances in community, which are mere aggregates and do not form a series. For they are not subordinated to each other as conditions of the possibility of each other; which, however, may be affirmed of spaces, the limits of which are never determined in themselves, but always by some other space. It is, therefore, only in the category of causality that we can find a series of causes to a given effect, and in which we ascend from the latter, as the conditioned, to the former as the conditions, and thus answer the question of reason.\n\nFourthly, the conceptions of the possible, the actual, and the necessary do not conduct us to any series—excepting only in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and as indicating, according to a law of the understanding, a condition, under which it is necessary to rise to a higher, till in the totality of the series, reason arrives at unconditioned necessity.\n\nThere are, accordingly, only four cosmological ideas, corresponding with the four titles of the categories. For we can select only such as necessarily furnish us with a series in the synthesis of the manifold.\n\n                      1\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                 COMPOSITION\n     of the given totality of all phenomena.\n\n                      2\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                   DIVISION\n     of given totality in a phenomenon.\n\n                       3\n            The absolute Completeness\n                     of the\n                   ORIGINATION\n                  of a phenomenon.\n\n                       4\n            The absolute Completeness\n         of the DEPENDENCE of the EXISTENCE\n        of what is changeable in a phenomenon.\n\nWe must here remark, in the first place, that the idea of absolute totality relates to nothing but the exposition of phenomena, and therefore not to the pure conception of a totality of things. Phenomena are here, therefore, regarded as given, and reason requires the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these conditions constitute a series—consequently an absolutely (that is, in every respect) complete synthesis, whereby a phenomenon can be explained according to the laws of the understanding.\n\nSecondly, it is properly the unconditioned alone that reason seeks in this serially and regressively conducted synthesis of conditions. It wishes, to speak in another way, to attain to completeness in the series of premisses, so as to render it unnecessary to presuppose others. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we endeavour to form a representation of it in thought. But this absolutely complete synthesis is itself but an idea; for it is impossible, at least before hand, to know whether any such synthesis is possible in the case of phenomena. When we represent all existence in thought by means of pure conceptions of the understanding, without any conditions of sensuous intuition, we may say with justice that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to each other is also given; for the former is only given through the latter. But we find in the case of phenomena a particular limitation of the mode in which conditions are given, that is, through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which must be complete in the regress. Now whether this completeness is sensuously possible, is a problem. But the idea of it lies in the reason—be it possible or impossible to connect with the idea adequate empirical conceptions. Therefore, as in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in a phenomenon (following the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions to a given conditioned) the unconditioned is necessarily contained—it being still left unascertained whether and how this totality exists; reason sets out from the idea of totality, although its proper and final aim is the unconditioned—of the whole series, or of a part thereof.\n\nThis unconditioned may be cogitated—either as existing only in the entire series, all the members of which therefore would be without exception conditioned and only the totality absolutely unconditioned—and in this case the regressus is called infinite; or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the other members are subordinated, but which Is not itself submitted to any other condition.[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-48) In the former case the series is a parte priori unlimited (without beginning), that is, infinite, and nevertheless completely given. But the regress in it is never completed, and can only be called potentially infinite. In the second case there exists a first in the series. This first is called, in relation to past time, the beginning of the world; in relation to space, the limit of the world; in relation to the parts of a given limited whole, the simple; in relation to causes, absolute spontaneity (liberty); and in relation to the existence of changeable things, absolute physical necessity.\n\n[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-48) The absolute totality of the series of conditions to a given conditioned is always unconditioned; because beyond it there exist no other conditions, on which it might depend. But the absolute totality of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematical conception, the possibility of which must be investigated—particularly in relation to the mode in which the unconditioned, as the transcendental idea which is the real subject of inquiry, may be contained therein.\n\nWe possess two expressions, world and nature, which are generally interchanged. The first denotes the mathematical total of all phenomena and the totality of their synthesis—in its progress by means of composition, as well as by division. And the world is termed nature,[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-49) when it is regarded as a dynamical whole—when our attention is not directed to the aggregation in space and time, for the purpose of cogitating it as a quantity, but to the unity in the existence of phenomena. In this case the condition of that which happens is called a cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause in a phenomenon is termed liberty; the conditioned cause is called in a more limited sense a natural cause. The conditioned in existence is termed contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of phenomena may be called natural necessity.\n\n[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-49) Nature, understood adjective (formaliter), signifies the complex of the determinations of a thing, connected according to an internal principle of causality. On the other hand, we understand by nature, substantive (materialiter), the sum total of phenomena, in so far as they, by virtue of an internal principle of causality, are connected with each other throughout. In the former sense we speak of the nature of liquid matter, of fire, etc., and employ the word only adjective; while, if speaking of the objects of nature, we have in our minds the idea of a subsisting whole.\n\nThe ideas which we are at present engaged in discussing I have called cosmological ideas; partly because by the term world is understood the entire content of all phenomena, and our ideas are directed solely to the unconditioned among phenomena; partly also, because world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the content of existing things, and we are directing our attention only to the completeness of the synthesis—although, properly, only in regression. In regard to the fact that these ideas are all transcendent, and, although they do not transcend phenomena as regards their mode, but are concerned solely with the world of sense (and not with noumena), nevertheless carry their synthesis to a degree far above all possible experience—it still seems to me that we can, with perfect propriety, designate them cosmical conceptions. As regards the distinction between the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned which is the aim of the regression of the synthesis, I should call the two former, in a more limited signification, cosmical conceptions, the remaining two transcendent physical conceptions. This distinction does not at present seem to be of particular importance, but we shall afterwards find it to be of some value.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","text":"# Cosmos\n\n### Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas\n\nThat we may be able to enumerate with systematic precision these ideas according to a principle, we must remark, in the first place, that it is from the understanding alone that pure and transcendental conceptions take their origin; that the reason does not properly give birth to any conception, but only frees the conception of the understanding from the unavoidable limitation of a possible experience, and thus endeavours to raise it above the empirical, though it must still be in connection with it. This happens from the fact that, for a given conditioned, reason demands absolute totality on the side of the conditions (to which the understanding submits all phenomena), and thus makes of the category a transcendental idea. This it does that it may be able to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis, by continuing it to the unconditioned (which is not to be found in experience, but only in the idea). Reason requires this according to the principle: If the conditioned is given the whole of the conditions, and consequently the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, whereby alone the former was possible. First, then, the transcendental ideas are properly nothing but categories elevated to the unconditioned; and they may be arranged in a table according to the titles of the latter. But, secondly, all the categories are not available for this purpose, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series—of conditions subordinated to, not co-ordinated with, each other. Absolute totality is required of reason only in so far as concerns the ascending series of the conditions of a conditioned; not, consequently, when the question relates to the descending series of consequences, or to the aggregate of the co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For, in relation to a given conditioned, conditions are presupposed and considered to be given along with it. On the other hand, as the consequences do not render possible their conditions, but rather presuppose them—in the consideration of the procession of consequences (or in the descent from the given condition to the conditioned), we may be quite unconcerned whether the series ceases or not; and their totality is not a necessary demand of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","text":"Thus we cogitate—and necessarily—a given time completely elapsed up to a given moment, although that time is not determinable by us. But as regards time future, which is not the condition of arriving at the present, in order to conceive it; it is quite indifferent whether we consider future time as ceasing at some point, or as prolonging itself to infinity. Take, for example, the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in relation to m, but at the same time as the condition of o, and let the series proceed upwards from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and also downwards from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.)—I must presuppose the former series, to be able to consider n as given, and n is according to reason (the totality of conditions) possible only by means of that series. But its possibility does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which for this reason cannot be regarded as given, but only as capable of being given (dabilis).\n\nI shall term the synthesis of the series on the side of the conditions—from that nearest to the given phenomenon up to the more remote—regressive; that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the immediate consequence to the more remote, I shall call the progressive synthesis. The former proceeds in antecedentia, the latter in consequentia. The cosmological ideas are therefore occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed in antecedentia, not in consequentia. When the latter takes place, it is an arbitrary and not a necessary problem of pure reason; for we require, for the complete understanding of what is given in a phenomenon, not the consequences which succeed, but the grounds or principles which precede.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","text":"In order to construct the table of ideas in correspondence with the table of categories, we take first the two primitive quanta of all our intuitions, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence, in relation to a given present, we must distinguish à priori in it the antecedentia as conditions (time past) from the consequentia (time future). Consequently, the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned, relates merely to all past time. According to the idea of reason, the whole past time, as the condition of the given moment, is necessarily cogitated as given. But, as regards space, there exists in it no distinction between progressus and regressus; for it is an aggregate and not a series—its parts existing together at the same time. I can consider a given point of time in relation to past time only as conditioned, because this given moment comes into existence only through the past time rather through the passing of the preceding time. But as the parts of space are not subordinated, but co-ordinated to each other, one part cannot be the condition of the possibility of the other; and space is not in itself, like time, a series. But the synthesis of the manifold parts of space—(the syntheses whereby we apprehend space)—is nevertheless successive; it takes place, therefore, in time, and contains a series. And as in this series of aggregated spaces (for example, the feet in a rood), beginning with a given portion of space, those which continue to be annexed form the condition of the limits of the former—the measurement of a space must also be regarded as a synthesis of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. It differs, however, in this respect from that of time, that the side of the conditioned is not in itself distinguishable from the side of the condition; and, consequently, regressus and progressus in space seem to be identical. But, inasmuch as one part of space is not given, but only limited, by and through another, we must also consider every limited space as conditioned, in so far as it presupposes some other space as the condition of its limitation, and so on. As regards limitation, therefore, our procedure in space is also a regressus, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in a series of conditions applies to space also; and I am entitled to demand the absolute totality of the phenomenal synthesis in space as well as in time. Whether my demand can be satisfied is a question to be answered in the sequel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","text":"Secondly, the real in space—that is, matter—is conditioned. Its internal conditions are its parts, and the parts of parts its remote conditions; so that in this case we find a regressive synthesis, the absolute totality of which is a demand of reason. But this cannot be obtained otherwise than by a complete division of parts, whereby the real in matter becomes either nothing or that which is not matter, that is to say, the simple. Consequently we find here also a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","text":"Thirdly, as regards the categories of a real relation between phenomena, the category of substance and its accidents is not suitable for the formation of a transcendental idea; that is to say, reason has no ground, in regard to it, to proceed regressively with conditions. For accidents (in so far as they inhere in a substance) are co-ordinated with each other, and do not constitute a series. And, in relation to substance, they are not properly subordinated to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself. The conception of the substantial might nevertheless seem to be an idea of the transcendental reason. But, as this signifies nothing more than the conception of an object in general, which subsists in so far as we cogitate in it merely a transcendental subject without any predicates; and as the question here is of an unconditioned in the series of phenomena—it is clear that the substantial can form no member thereof. The same holds good of substances in community, which are mere aggregates and do not form a series. For they are not subordinated to each other as conditions of the possibility of each other; which, however, may be affirmed of spaces, the limits of which are never determined in themselves, but always by some other space. It is, therefore, only in the category of causality that we can find a series of causes to a given effect, and in which we ascend from the latter, as the conditioned, to the former as the conditions, and thus answer the question of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","text":"Fourthly, the conceptions of the possible, the actual, and the necessary do not conduct us to any series—excepting only in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and as indicating, according to a law of the understanding, a condition, under which it is necessary to rise to a higher, till in the totality of the series, reason arrives at unconditioned necessity.\n\nThere are, accordingly, only four cosmological ideas, corresponding with the four titles of the categories. For we can select only such as necessarily furnish us with a series in the synthesis of the manifold.\n\n                      1\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                 COMPOSITION\n     of the given totality of all phenomena.\n\n                      2\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                   DIVISION\n     of given totality in a phenomenon.\n\n                       3\n            The absolute Completeness\n                     of the\n                   ORIGINATION\n                  of a phenomenon.\n\n                       4\n            The absolute Completeness\n         of the DEPENDENCE of the EXISTENCE\n        of what is changeable in a phenomenon.\n\nWe must here remark, in the first place, that the idea of absolute totality relates to nothing but the exposition of phenomena, and therefore not to the pure conception of a totality of things. Phenomena are here, therefore, regarded as given, and reason requires the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these conditions constitute a series—consequently an absolutely (that is, in every respect) complete synthesis, whereby a phenomenon can be explained according to the laws of the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","text":"Secondly, it is properly the unconditioned alone that reason seeks in this serially and regressively conducted synthesis of conditions. It wishes, to speak in another way, to attain to completeness in the series of premisses, so as to render it unnecessary to presuppose others. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we endeavour to form a representation of it in thought. But this absolutely complete synthesis is itself but an idea; for it is impossible, at least before hand, to know whether any such synthesis is possible in the case of phenomena. When we represent all existence in thought by means of pure conceptions of the understanding, without any conditions of sensuous intuition, we may say with justice that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to each other is also given; for the former is only given through the latter. But we find in the case of phenomena a particular limitation of the mode in which conditions are given, that is, through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which must be complete in the regress. Now whether this completeness is sensuously possible, is a problem. But the idea of it lies in the reason—be it possible or impossible to connect with the idea adequate empirical conceptions. Therefore, as in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in a phenomenon (following the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions to a given conditioned) the unconditioned is necessarily contained—it being still left unascertained whether and how this totality exists; reason sets out from the idea of totality, although its proper and final aim is the unconditioned—of the whole series, or of a part thereof.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","text":"This unconditioned may be cogitated—either as existing only in the entire series, all the members of which therefore would be without exception conditioned and only the totality absolutely unconditioned—and in this case the regressus is called infinite; or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the other members are subordinated, but which Is not itself submitted to any other condition.[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-48) In the former case the series is a parte priori unlimited (without beginning), that is, infinite, and nevertheless completely given. But the regress in it is never completed, and can only be called potentially infinite. In the second case there exists a first in the series. This first is called, in relation to past time, the beginning of the world; in relation to space, the limit of the world; in relation to the parts of a given limited whole, the simple; in relation to causes, absolute spontaneity (liberty); and in relation to the existence of changeable things, absolute physical necessity.\n\n[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-48) The absolute totality of the series of conditions to a given conditioned is always unconditioned; because beyond it there exist no other conditions, on which it might depend. But the absolute totality of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematical conception, the possibility of which must be investigated—particularly in relation to the mode in which the unconditioned, as the transcendental idea which is the real subject of inquiry, may be contained therein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","text":"We possess two expressions, world and nature, which are generally interchanged. The first denotes the mathematical total of all phenomena and the totality of their synthesis—in its progress by means of composition, as well as by division. And the world is termed nature,[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-49) when it is regarded as a dynamical whole—when our attention is not directed to the aggregation in space and time, for the purpose of cogitating it as a quantity, but to the unity in the existence of phenomena. In this case the condition of that which happens is called a cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause in a phenomenon is termed liberty; the conditioned cause is called in a more limited sense a natural cause. The conditioned in existence is termed contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of phenomena may be called natural necessity.\n\n[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-49) Nature, understood adjective (formaliter), signifies the complex of the determinations of a thing, connected according to an internal principle of causality. On the other hand, we understand by nature, substantive (materialiter), the sum total of phenomena, in so far as they, by virtue of an internal principle of causality, are connected with each other throughout. In the former sense we speak of the nature of liquid matter, of fire, etc., and employ the word only adjective; while, if speaking of the objects of nature, we have in our minds the idea of a subsisting whole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","text":"The ideas which we are at present engaged in discussing I have called cosmological ideas; partly because by the term world is understood the entire content of all phenomena, and our ideas are directed solely to the unconditioned among phenomena; partly also, because world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the content of existing things, and we are directing our attention only to the completeness of the synthesis—although, properly, only in regression. In regard to the fact that these ideas are all transcendent, and, although they do not transcend phenomena as regards their mode, but are concerned solely with the world of sense (and not with noumena), nevertheless carry their synthesis to a degree far above all possible experience—it still seems to me that we can, with perfect propriety, designate them cosmical conceptions. As regards the distinction between the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned which is the aim of the regression of the synthesis, I should call the two former, in a more limited signification, cosmical conceptions, the remaining two transcendent physical conceptions. This distinction does not at present seem to be of particular importance, but we shall afterwards find it to be of some value.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"060b7ce790b1759a2bfa8ec5bf21d136a812bb1a809fb0959007e036e9581b9b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","order":63,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIAP","text":"### Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason\n\nThetic is the term applied to every collection of dogmatical propositions. By antithetic I do not understand dogmatical assertions of the opposite, but the self-contradiction of seemingly dogmatical cognitions (thesis cum antithesis), in none of which we can discover any decided superiority. Antithetic is not, therefore, occupied with one-sided statements, but is engaged in considering the contradictory nature of the general cognitions of reason and its causes. Transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we employ our reason not merely in the application of the principles of the understanding to objects of experience, but venture with it beyond these boundaries, there arise certain sophistical propositions or theorems. These assertions have the following peculiarities: They can find neither confirmation nor confutation in experience; and each is in itself not only self-consistent, but possesses conditions of its necessity in the very nature of reason—only that, unluckily, there exist just as valid and necessary grounds for maintaining the contrary proposition.\n\nThe questions which naturally arise in the consideration of this dialectic of pure reason, are therefore: 1st. In what propositions is pure reason unavoidably subject to an antinomy? 2nd. What are the causes of this antinomy? 3rd. Whether and in what way can reason free itself from this self-contradiction?\n\nA dialectical proposition or theorem of pure reason must, according to what has been said, be distinguishable from all sophistical propositions, by the fact that it is not an answer to an arbitrary question, which may be raised at the mere pleasure of any person, but to one which human reason must necessarily encounter in its progress. In the second place, a dialectical proposition, with its opposite, does not carry the appearance of a merely artificial illusion, which disappears as soon as it is investigated, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when we are no longer deceived by it, continues to mock us and, although rendered harmless, can never be completely removed.\n\nThis dialectical doctrine will not relate to the unity of understanding in empirical conceptions, but to the unity of reason in pure ideas. The conditions of this doctrine are—inasmuch as it must, as a synthesis according to rules, be conformable to the understanding, and at the same time as the absolute unity of the synthesis, to the reason—that, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, it is too great for the understanding, if according with the understanding, it is too small for the reason. Hence arises a mutual opposition, which cannot be avoided, do what we will.\n\nThese sophistical assertions of dialectic open, as it were, a battle-field, where that side obtains the victory which has been permitted to make the attack, and he is compelled to yield who has been unfortunately obliged to stand on the defensive. And hence, champions of ability, whether on the right or on the wrong side, are certain to carry away the crown of victory, if they only take care to have the right to make the last attack, and are not obliged to sustain another onset from their opponent. We can easily believe that this arena has been often trampled by the feet of combatants, that many victories have been obtained on both sides, but that the last victory, decisive of the affair between the contending parties, was won by him who fought for the right, only if his adversary was forbidden to continue the tourney. As impartial umpires, we must lay aside entirely the consideration whether the combatants are fighting for the right or for the wrong side, for the true or for the false, and allow the combat to be first decided. Perhaps, after they have wearied more than injured each other, they will discover the nothingness of their cause of quarrel and part good friends.\n\nThis method of watching, or rather of originating, a conflict of assertions, not for the purpose of finally deciding in favour of either side, but to discover whether the object of the struggle is not a mere illusion, which each strives in vain to reach, but which would be no gain even when reached—this procedure, I say, may be termed the sceptical method. It is thoroughly distinct from scepticism—the principle of a technical and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, in order, if possible, to destroy our belief and confidence therein. For the sceptical method aims at certainty, by endeavouring to discover in a conflict of this kind, conducted honestly and intelligently on both sides, the point of misunderstanding; just as wise legislators derive, from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits, information in regard to the defective and ill-defined parts of their statutes. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws, is for our limited wisdom the best criterion of legislation. For the attention of reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become conscious of its errors, is thus roused to the momenta in the determination of its principles.\n\nBut this sceptical method is essentially peculiar to transcendental philosophy, and can perhaps be dispensed with in every other field of investigation. In mathematics its use would be absurd; because in it no false assertions can long remain hidden, inasmuch as its demonstrations must always proceed under the guidance of pure intuition, and by means of an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy, doubt and delay may be very useful; but no misunderstanding is possible, which cannot be easily removed; and in experience means of solving the difficulty and putting an end to the dissension must at last be found, whether sooner or later. Moral philosophy can always exhibit its principles, with their practical consequences, in concreto—at least in possible experiences, and thus escape the mistakes and ambiguities of abstraction. But transcendental propositions, which lay claim to insight beyond the region of possible experience, cannot, on the one hand, exhibit their abstract synthesis in any à priori intuition, nor, on the other, expose a lurking error by the help of experience. Transcendental reason, therefore, presents us with no other criterion than that of an attempt to reconcile such assertions, and for this purpose to permit a free and unrestrained conflict between them. And this we now proceed to arrange.[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-50)\n\n[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-50) The antinomies stand in the order of the four transcendental ideas above detailed.\n\nFIRST CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThe world has a beginning in time, and is also limited in regard to space.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted that the world has no beginning in time; up to every given moment of time, an eternity must have elapsed, and therewith passed away an infinite series of successive conditions or states of things in the world. Now the infinity of a series consists in the fact that it never can be completed by means of a successive synthesis. It follows that an infinite series already elapsed is impossible and that, consequently, a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence. And this was the first thing to be proved.\n\nAs regards the second, let us take the opposite for granted. In this case, the world must be an infinite given total of coexistent things. Now we cannot cogitate the dimensions of a quantity, which is not given within certain limits of an intuition,[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-51) in any other way than by means of the synthesis of its parts, and the total of such a quantity only by means of a completed synthesis, or the repeated addition of unity to itself. Accordingly, to cogitate the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world must be looked upon as completed, that is to say, an infinite time must be regarded as having elapsed in the enumeration of all co-existing things; which is impossible. For this reason an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be considered as a given whole, consequently, not as a contemporaneously given whole. The world is consequently, as regards extension in space, not infinite, but enclosed in limits. And this was the second thing to be proved.\n\n[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-51) We may consider an undetermined quantity as a whole, when it is enclosed within limits, although we cannot construct or ascertain its totality by measurement, that is, by the successive synthesis of its parts. For its limits of themselves determine its completeness as a whole.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThe world has no beginning, and no limits in space, but is, in relation both to time and space, infinite.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor let it be granted that it has a beginning. A beginning is an existence which is preceded by a time in which the thing does not exist. On the above supposition, it follows that there must have been a time in which the world did not exist, that is, a void time. But in a void time the origination of a thing is impossible; because no part of any such time contains a distinctive condition of being, in preference to that of non-being (whether the supposed thing originate of itself, or by means of some other cause). Consequently, many series of things may have a beginning in the world, but the world itself cannot have a beginning, and is, therefore, in relation to past time, infinite.\n\nAs regards the second statement, let us first take the opposite for granted—that the world is finite and limited in space; it follows that it must exist in a void space, which is not limited. We should therefore meet not only with a relation of things in space, but also a relation of things to space. Now, as the world is an absolute whole, out of and beyond which no object of intuition, and consequently no correlate to which can be discovered, this relation of the world to a void space is merely a relation to no object. But such a relation, and consequently the limitation of the world by void space, is nothing. Consequently, the world, as regards space, is not limited, that is, it is infinite in regard to extension.[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-52)\n\n[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-52) Space is merely the form of external intuition (formal intuition), and not a real object which can be externally perceived. Space, prior to all things which determine it (fill or limit it), or, rather, which present an empirical intuition conformable to it, is, under the title of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of external phenomena, in so far as they either exist in themselves, or can annex themselves to given intuitions. Empirical intuition is therefore not a composition of phenomena and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis, but they are vitally connected in the same empirical intuition, as matter and form. If we wish to set one of these two apart from the other—space from phenomena—there arise all sorts of empty determinations of external intuition, which are very far from being possible perceptions. For example, motion or rest of the world in an infinite empty space, or a determination of the mutual relation of both, cannot possibly be perceived, and is therefore merely the predicate of a notional entity.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FIRST ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nIn bringing forward these conflicting arguments, I have not been on the search for sophisms, for the purpose of availing myself of special pleading, which takes advantage of the carelessness of the opposite party, appeals to a misunderstood statute, and erects its unrighteous claims upon an unfair interpretation. Both proofs originate fairly from the nature of the case, and the advantage presented by the mistakes of the dogmatists of both parties has been completely set aside.\n\nThe thesis might also have been unfairly demonstrated, by the introduction of an erroneous conception of the infinity of a given quantity. A quantity is infinite, if a greater than itself cannot possibly exist. The quantity is measured by the number of given units—which are taken as a standard—contained in it. Now no number can be the greatest, because one or more units can always be added. It follows that an infinite given quantity, consequently an infinite world (both as regards time and extension) is impossible. It is, therefore, limited in both respects. In this manner I might have conducted my proof; but the conception given in it does not agree with the true conception of an infinite whole. In this there is no representation of its quantity, it is not said how large it is; consequently its conception is not the conception of a maximum. We cogitate in it merely its relation to an arbitrarily assumed unit, in relation to which it is greater than any number. Now, just as the unit which is taken is greater or smaller, the infinite will be greater or smaller; but the infinity, which consists merely in the relation to this given unit, must remain always the same, although the absolute quantity of the whole is not thereby cognized.\n\nThe true (transcendental) conception of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the measurement of a given quantum can never be completed.[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-53) Hence it follows, without possibility of mistake, that an eternity of actual successive states up to a given (the present) moment cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.\n\n[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-53) The quantum in this sense contains a congeries of given units, which is greater than any number—and this is the mathematical conception of the infinite.\n\nIn regard to the second part of the thesis, the difficulty as to an infinite and yet elapsed series disappears; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is contemporaneously given. But, in order to cogitate the total of this manifold, as we cannot have the aid of limits constituting by themselves this total in intuition, we are obliged to give some account of our conception, which in this case cannot proceed from the whole to the determined quantity of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole by means of a successive synthesis of the parts. But as this synthesis must constitute a series that cannot be completed, it is impossible for us to cogitate prior to it, and consequently not by means of it, a totality. For the conception of totality itself is in the present case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and consequently its conception, is impossible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe proof in favour of the infinity of the cosmical succession and the cosmical content is based upon the consideration that, in the opposite case, a void time and a void space must constitute the limits of the world. Now I am not unaware, that there are some ways of escaping this conclusion. It may, for example, be alleged, that a limit to the world, as regards both space and time, is quite possible, without at the same time holding the existence of an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extending beyond the actual world—which is impossible. I am quite well satisfied with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnitzian school. Space is merely the form of external intuition, but not a real object which can itself be externally intuited; it is not a correlate of phenomena, it is the form of phenomena itself. Space, therefore, cannot be regarded as absolutely and in itself something determinative of the existence of things, because it is not itself an object, but only the form of possible objects. Consequently, things, as phenomena, determine space; that is to say, they render it possible that, of all the possible predicates of space (size and relation), certain may belong to reality. But we cannot affirm the converse, that space, as something self-subsistent, can determine real things in regard to size or shape, for it is in itself not a real thing. Space (filled or void)[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-54) may therefore be limited by phenomena, but phenomena cannot be limited by an empty space without them. This is true of time also. All this being granted, it is nevertheless indisputable, that we must assume these two nonentities, void space without and void time before the world, if we assume the existence of cosmical limits, relatively to space or time.\n\n[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-54) It is evident that what is meant here is, that empty space, in so far as it is limited by phenomena—space, that is, within the world—does not at least contradict transcendental principles, and may therefore, as regards them, be admitted, although its possibility cannot on that account be affirmed.\n\nFor, as regards the subterfuge adopted by those who endeavour to evade the consequence—that, if the world is limited as to space and time, the infinite void must determine the existence of actual things in regard to their dimensions—it arises solely from the fact that instead of a sensuous world, an intelligible world—of which nothing is known—is cogitated; instead of a real beginning (an existence, which is preceded by a period in which nothing exists), an existence which presupposes no other condition than that of time; and, instead of limits of extension, boundaries of the universe. But the question relates to the mundus phaenomenon, and its quantity; and in this case we cannot make abstraction of the conditions of sensibility, without doing away with the essential reality of this world itself. The world of sense, if it is limited, must necessarily lie in the infinite void. If this, and with it space as the à priori condition of the possibility of phenomena, is left out of view, the whole world of sense disappears. In our problem is this alone considered as given. The mundus intelligibilis is nothing but the general conception of a world, in which abstraction has been made of all conditions of intuition, and in relation to which no synthetical proposition—either affirmative or negative—is possible.\n\nSECOND CONFLICT OF TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nEvery composite substance in the world consists of simple parts; and there exists nothing that is not either itself simple, or composed of simple parts.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor, grant that composite substances do not consist of simple parts; in this case, if all combination or composition were annihilated in thought, no composite part, and (as, by the supposition, there do not exist simple parts) no simple part would exist. Consequently, no substance; consequently, nothing would exist. Either, then, it is impossible to annihilate composition in thought; or, after such annihilation, there must remain something that subsists without composition, that is, something that is simple. But in the former case the composite could not itself consist of substances, because with substances composition is merely a contingent relation, apart from which they must still exist as self-subsistent beings. Now, as this case contradicts the supposition, the second must contain the truth—that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.\n\nIt follows, as an immediate inference, that the things in the world are all, without exception, simple beings—that composition is merely an external condition pertaining to them—and that, although we never can separate and isolate the elementary substances from the state of composition, reason must cogitate these as the primary subjects of all composition, and consequently, as prior thereto—and as simple substances.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nNo composite thing in the world consists of simple parts; and there does not exist in the world any simple substance.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Inasmuch as all external relation, consequently all composition of substances, is possible only in space; the space, occupied by that which is composite, must consist of the same number of parts as is contained in the composite. But space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore, every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely primary parts of what is composite are simple. It follows that what is simple occupies a space. Now, as everything real that occupies a space, contains a manifold the parts of which are external to each other, and is consequently composite—and a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot exist external to each other apart from substance), but of substances—it follows that the simple must be a substantial composite, which is self-contradictory.\n\nThe second proposition of the antithesis—that there exists in the world nothing that is simple—is here equivalent to the following: The existence of the absolutely simple cannot be demonstrated from any experience or perception either external or internal; and the absolutely simple is a mere idea, the objective reality of which cannot be demonstrated in any possible experience; it is consequently, in the exposition of phenomena, without application and object. For, let us take for granted that an object may be found in experience for this transcendental idea; the empirical intuition of such an object must then be recognized to contain absolutely no manifold with its parts external to each other, and connected into unity. Now, as we cannot reason from the non-consciousness of such a manifold to the impossibility of its existence in the intuition of an object, and as the proof of this impossibility is necessary for the establishment and proof of absolute simplicity; it follows that this simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatever. As, therefore, an absolutely simple object cannot be given in any experience, and the world of sense must be considered as the sum total of all possible experiences: nothing simple exists in the world.\n\nThis second proposition in the antithesis has a more extended aim than the first. The first merely banishes the simple from the intuition of the composite; while the second drives it entirely out of nature. Hence we were unable to demonstrate it from the conception of a given object of external intuition (of the composite), but we were obliged to prove it from the relation of a given object to a possible experience in general.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE SECOND ANTINOMY. THESIS.\n\nWhen I speak of a whole, which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand thereby only a substantial whole, as the true composite; that is to say, I understand that contingent unity of the manifold which is given as perfectly isolated (at least in thought), placed in reciprocal connection, and thus constituted a unity. Space ought not to be called a compositum but a totum, for its parts are possible in the whole, and not the whole by means of the parts. It might perhaps be called a compositum ideale, but not a compositum reale. But this is of no importance. As space is not a composite of substances (and not even of real accidents), if I abstract all composition therein—nothing, not even a point, remains; for a point is possible only as the limit of a space—consequently of a composite. Space and time, therefore, do not consist of simple parts. That which belongs only to the condition or state of a substance, even although it possesses a quantity (motion or change, for example), likewise does not consist of simple parts. That is to say, a certain degree of change does not originate from the addition of many simple changes. Our inference of the simple from the composite is valid only of self-subsisting things. But the accidents of a state are not self-subsistent. The proof, then, for the necessity of the simple, as the component part of all that is substantial and composite, may prove a failure, and the whole case of this thesis be lost, if we carry the proposition too far, and wish to make it valid of everything that is composite without distinction—as indeed has really now and then happened. Besides, I am here speaking only of the simple, in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite—the latter being capable of solution into the former as its component parts. The proper signification of the word monas (as employed by Leibnitz) ought to relate to the simple, given immediately as simple substance (for example, in consciousness), and not as an element of the composite. As an element, the term atomus would be more appropriate. And as I wish to prove the existence of simple substances, only in relation to, and as the elements of, the composite, I might term the antithesis of the second Antinomy, transcendental Atomistic. But as this word has long been employed to designate a particular theory of corporeal phenomena (moleculae), and thus presupposes a basis of empirical conceptions, I prefer calling it the dialectical principle of Monadology.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAgainst the assertion of the infinite subdivisibility of matter whose ground of proof is purely mathematical, objections have been alleged by the Monadists. These objections lay themselves open, at first sight, to suspicion, from the fact that they do not recognize the clearest mathematical proofs as propositions relating to the constitution of space, in so far as it is really the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them merely as inferences from abstract but arbitrary conceptions, which cannot have any application to real things. Just as if it were possible to imagine another mode of intuition than that given in the primitive intuition of space; and just as if its à priori determinations did not apply to everything, the existence of which is possible, from the fact alone of its filling space. If we listen to them, we shall find ourselves required to cogitate, in addition to the mathematical point, which is simple—not, however, a part, but a mere limit of space—physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but possess the peculiar property, as parts of space, of filling it merely by their aggregation. I shall not repeat here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be found everywhere in numbers: every one knows that it is impossible to undermine the evidence of mathematics by mere discursive conceptions; I shall only remark that, if in this case philosophy endeavours to gain an advantage over mathematics by sophistical artifices, it is because it forgets that the discussion relates solely to Phenomena and their conditions. It is not sufficient to find the conception of the simple for the pure conception of the composite, but we must discover for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple. Now this, according to the laws of sensibility, and consequently in the case of objects of sense, is utterly impossible. In the case of a whole composed of substances, which is cogitated solely by the pure understanding, it may be necessary to be in possession of the simple before composition is possible. But this does not hold good of the Totum substantiale phaenomenon, which, as an empirical intuition in space, possesses the necessary property of containing no simple part, for the very reason that no part of space is simple. Meanwhile, the Monadists have been subtle enough to escape from this difficulty, by presupposing intuition and the dynamical relation of substances as the condition of the possibility of space, instead of regarding space as the condition of the possibility of the objects of external intuition, that is, of bodies. Now we have a conception of bodies only as phenomena, and, as such, they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of all external phenomena. The evasion is therefore in vain; as, indeed, we have sufficiently shown in our Æsthetic. If bodies were things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would be unexceptionable.\n\nThe second dialectical assertion possesses the peculiarity of having opposed to it a dogmatical proposition, which, among all such sophistical statements, is the only one that undertakes to prove in the case of an object of experience, that which is properly a transcendental idea—the absolute simplicity of substance. The proposition is that the object of the internal sense, the thinking Ego, is an absolute simple substance. Without at present entering upon this subject—as it has been considered at length in a former chapter—I shall merely remark that, if something is cogitated merely as an object, without the addition of any synthetical determination of its intuition—as happens in the case of the bare representation, _I_—it is certain that no manifold and no composition can be perceived in such a representation. As, moreover, the predicates whereby I cogitate this object are merely intuitions of the internal sense, there cannot be discovered in them anything to prove the existence of a manifold whose parts are external to each other, and, consequently, nothing to prove the existence of real composition. Consciousness, therefore, is so constituted that, inasmuch as the thinking subject is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself—although it can divide its inhering determinations. For every object in relation to itself is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if the subject is regarded externally, as an object of intuition, it must, in its character of phenomenon, possess the property of composition. And it must always be regarded in this manner, if we wish to know whether there is or is not contained in it a manifold whose parts are external to each other.\n\nTHIRD CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nCausality according to the laws of nature, is not the only causality operating to originate the phenomena of the world. A causality of freedom is also necessary to account fully for these phenomena.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed, that there is no other kind of causality than that according to the laws of nature. Consequently, everything that happens presupposes a previous condition, which it follows with absolute certainty, in conformity with a rule. But this previous condition must itself be something that has happened (that has arisen in time, as it did not exist before), for, if it has always been in existence, its consequence or effect would not thus originate for the first time, but would likewise have always existed. The causality, therefore, of a cause, whereby something happens, is itself a thing that has happened. Now this again presupposes, in conformity with the law of nature, a previous condition and its causality, and this another anterior to the former, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with the laws of nature, there cannot be any real first beginning of things, but only a subaltern or comparative beginning. There cannot, therefore, be a completeness of series on the side of the causes which originate the one from the other. But the law of nature is that nothing can happen without a sufficient à priori determined cause. The proposition therefore—if all causality is possible only in accordance with the laws of nature—is, when stated in this unlimited and general manner, self-contradictory. It follows that this cannot be the only kind of causality.\n\nFrom what has been said, it follows that a causality must be admitted, by means of which something happens, without its cause being determined according to necessary laws by some other cause preceding. That is to say, there must exist an absolute spontaneity of cause, which of itself originates a series of phenomena which proceeds according to natural laws—consequently transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of phenomena on the side of causes is never complete.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThere is no such thing as freedom, but everything in the world happens solely according to the laws of nature.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted, that there does exist freedom in the transcendental sense, as a peculiar kind of causality, operating to produce events in the world—a faculty, that is to say, of originating a state, and consequently a series of consequences from that state. In this case, not only the series originated by this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is to say, the causality itself must have an absolute commencement, such that nothing can precede to determine this action according to unvarying laws. But every beginning of action presupposes in the acting cause a state of inaction; and a dynamically primal beginning of action presupposes a state, which has no connection—as regards causality—with the preceding state of the cause—which does not, that is, in any wise result from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the natural law of cause and effect, and such a conjunction of successive states in effective causes is destructive of the possibility of unity in experience and for that reason not to be found in experience—is consequently a mere fiction of thought.\n\nWe have, therefore, nothing but nature to which we must look for connection and order in cosmical events. Freedom—independence of the laws of nature—is certainly a deliverance from restraint, but it is also a relinquishing of the guidance of law and rule. For it cannot be alleged that, instead of the laws of nature, laws of freedom may be introduced into the causality of the course of nature. For, if freedom were determined according to laws, it would be no longer freedom, but merely nature. Nature, therefore, and transcendental freedom are distinguishable as conformity to law and lawlessness. The former imposes upon understanding the difficulty of seeking the origin of events ever higher and higher in the series of causes, inasmuch as causality is always conditioned thereby; while it compensates this labour by the guarantee of a unity complete and in conformity with law. The latter, on the contrary, holds out to the understanding the promise of a point of rest in the chain of causes, by conducting it to an unconditioned causality, which professes to have the power of spontaneous origination, but which, in its own utter blindness, deprives it of the guidance of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE THIRD ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nThe transcendental idea of freedom is far from constituting the entire content of the psychological conception so termed, which is for the most part empirical. It merely presents us with the conception of spontaneity of action, as the proper ground for imputing freedom to the cause of a certain class of objects. It is, however, the true stumbling-stone to philosophy, which meets with unconquerable difficulties in the way of its admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will, which has for so long a time placed speculative reason in such perplexity, is properly only transcendental, and concerns the question, whether there must be held to exist a faculty of spontaneous origination of a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible is not a necessary inquiry; for in the case of natural causality itself, we are obliged to content ourselves with the à priori knowledge that such a causality must be presupposed, although we are quite incapable of comprehending how the being of one thing is possible through the being of another, but must for this information look entirely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a free first beginning of a series of phenomena, only in so far as it is required for the comprehension of an origin of the world, all following states being regarded as a succession according to laws of nature alone. But, as there has thus been proved the existence of a faculty which can of itself originate a series in time—although we are unable to explain how it can exist—we feel ourselves authorized to admit, even in the midst of the natural course of events, a beginning, as regards causality, of different successions of phenomena, and at the same time to attribute to all substances a faculty of free action. But we ought in this case not to allow ourselves to fall into a common misunderstanding, and to suppose that, because a successive series in the world can only have a comparatively first beginning—another state or condition of things always preceding—an absolutely first beginning of a series in the course of nature is impossible. For we are not speaking here of an absolutely first beginning in relation to time, but as regards causality alone. When, for example, I, completely of my own free will, and independently of the necessarily determinative influence of natural causes, rise from my chair, there commences with this event, including its material consequences in infinitum, an absolutely new series; although, in relation to time, this event is merely the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act of mine do not form part of the succession of effects in nature, and are not mere continuations of it; on the contrary, the determining causes of nature cease to operate in reference to this event, which certainly succeeds the acts of nature, but does not proceed from them. For these reasons, the action of a free agent must be termed, in regard to causality, if not in relation to time, an absolutely primal beginning of a series of phenomena.\n\nThe justification of this need of reason to rest upon a free act as the first beginning of the series of natural causes is evident from the fact, that all philosophers of antiquity (with the exception of the Epicurean school) felt themselves obliged, when constructing a theory of the motions of the universe, to accept a prime mover, that is, a freely acting cause, which spontaneously and prior to all other causes evolved this series of states. They always felt the need of going beyond mere nature, for the purpose of making a first beginning comprehensible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe assertor of the all-sufficiency of nature in regard to causality (transcendental Physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would defend his view of the question somewhat in the following manner. He would say, in answer to the sophistical arguments of the opposite party: If you do not accept a mathematical first, in relation to time, you have no need to seek a dynamical first, in regard to causality. Who compelled you to imagine an absolutely primal condition of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the gradually progressing successions of phenomena—and, as some foundation for this fancy of yours, to set bounds to unlimited nature? Inasmuch as the substances in the world have always existed—at least the unity of experience renders such a supposition quite necessary—there is no difficulty in believing also, that the changes in the conditions of these substances have always existed; and, consequently, that a first beginning, mathematical or dynamical, is by no means required. The possibility of such an infinite derivation, without any initial member from which all the others result, is certainly quite incomprehensible. But, if you are rash enough to deny the enigmatical secrets of nature for this reason, you will find yourselves obliged to deny also the existence of many fundamental properties of natural objects (such as fundamental forces), which you can just as little comprehend; and even the possibility of so simple a conception as that of change must present to you insuperable difficulties. For if experience did not teach you that it was real, you never could conceive à priori the possibility of this ceaseless sequence of being and non-being.\n\nBut if the existence of a transcendental faculty of freedom is granted—a faculty of originating changes in the world—this faculty must at least exist out of and apart from the world; although it is certainly a bold assumption, that, over and above the complete content of all possible intuitions, there still exists an object which cannot be presented in any possible perception. But, to attribute to substances in the world itself such a faculty, is quite inadmissible; for, in this case; the connection of phenomena reciprocally determining and determined according to general laws, which is termed nature, and along with it the criteria of empirical truth, which enable us to distinguish experience from mere visionary dreaming, would almost entirely disappear. In proximity with such a lawless faculty of freedom, a system of nature is hardly cogitable; for the laws of the latter would be continually subject to the intrusive influences of the former, and the course of phenomena, which would otherwise proceed regularly and uniformly, would become thereby confused and disconnected.\n\nFOURTH CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThere exists either in, or in connection with the world—either as a part of it, or as the cause of it—an absolutely necessary being.\n\nPROOF.\n\nThe world of sense, as the sum total of all phenomena, contains a series of changes. For, without such a series, the mental representation of the series of time itself, as the condition of the possibility of the sensuous world, could not be presented to us.[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-55) But every change stands under its condition, which precedes it in time and renders it necessary. Now the existence of a given condition presupposes a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. It follows that something that is absolutely necessary must exist, if change exists as its consequence. But this necessary thing itself belongs to the sensuous world. For suppose it to exist out of and apart from it, the series of cosmical changes would receive from it a beginning, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the world of sense. But this is impossible. For, as the beginning of a series in time is determined only by that which precedes it in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of changes must exist in the time in which this series itself did not exist; for a beginning supposes a time preceding, in which the thing that begins to be was not in existence. The causality of the necessary cause of changes, and consequently the cause itself, must for these reasons belong to time—and to phenomena, time being possible only as the form of phenomena. Consequently, it cannot be cogitated as separated from the world of sense—the sum total of all phenomena. There is, therefore, contained in the world, something that is absolutely necessary—whether it be the whole cosmical series itself, or only a part of it.\n\n[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-55) Objectively, time, as the formal condition of the possibility of change, precedes all changes; but subjectively, and in consciousness, the representation of time, like every other, is given solely by occasion of perception.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAn absolutely necessary being does not exist, either in the world, or out of it—as its cause.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGrant that either the world itself is necessary, or that there is contained in it a necessary existence. Two cases are possible. First, there must either be in the series of cosmical changes a beginning, which is unconditionally necessary, and therefore uncaused—which is at variance with the dynamical law of the determination of all phenomena in time; or, secondly, the series itself is without beginning, and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, is nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned as a whole—which is self-contradictory. For the existence of an aggregate cannot be necessary, if no single part of it possesses necessary existence.\n\nGrant, on the other hand, that an absolutely necessary cause exists out of and apart from the world. This cause, as the highest member in the series of the causes of cosmical changes, must originate or begin[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-56) the existence of the latter and their series. In this case it must also begin to act, and its causality would therefore belong to time, and consequently to the sum total of phenomena, that is, to the world. It follows that the cause cannot be out of the world; which is contradictory to the hypothesis. Therefore, neither in the world, nor out of it (but in causal connection with it), does there exist any absolutely necessary being.\n\n[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-56) The word begin is taken in two senses. The first is active—the cause being regarded as beginning a series of conditions as its effect (infit). The second is passive—the causality in the cause itself beginning to operate (fit). I reason here from the first to the second.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FOURTH ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nTo demonstrate the existence of a necessary being, I cannot be permitted in this place to employ any other than the cosmological argument, which ascends from the conditioned in phenomena to the unconditioned in conception—the unconditioned being considered the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. The proof, from the mere idea of a supreme being, belongs to another principle of reason and requires separate discussion.\n\nThe pure cosmological proof demonstrates the existence of a necessary being, but at the same time leaves it quite unsettled, whether this being is the world itself, or quite distinct from it. To establish the truth of the latter view, principles are requisite, which are not cosmological and do not proceed in the series of phenomena. We should require to introduce into our proof conceptions of contingent beings—regarded merely as objects of the understanding, and also a principle which enables us to connect these, by means of mere conceptions, with a necessary being. But the proper place for all such arguments is a transcendent philosophy, which has unhappily not yet been established.\n\nBut, if we begin our proof cosmologically, by laying at the foundation of it the series of phenomena, and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality, we are not at liberty to break off from this mode of demonstration and to pass over to something which is not itself a member of the series. The condition must be taken in exactly the same signification as the relation of the conditioned to its condition in the series has been taken, for the series must conduct us in an unbroken regress to this supreme condition. But if this relation is sensuous, and belongs to the possible empirical employment of understanding, the supreme condition or cause must close the regressive series according to the laws of sensibility and consequently, must belong to the series of time. It follows that this necessary existence must be regarded as the highest member of the cosmical series.\n\nCertain philosophers have, nevertheless, allowed themselves the liberty of making such a saltus (metabasis eis allo gonos). From the changes in the world they have concluded their empirical contingency, that is, their dependence on empirically-determined causes, and they thus admitted an ascending series of empirical conditions: and in this they are quite right. But as they could not find in this series any primal beginning or any highest member, they passed suddenly from the empirical conception of contingency to the pure category, which presents us with a series—not sensuous, but intellectual—whose completeness does certainly rest upon the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Nay, more, this intellectual series is not tied to any sensuous conditions; and is therefore free from the condition of time, which requires it spontaneously to begin its causality in time. But such a procedure is perfectly inadmissible, as will be made plain from what follows.\n\nIn the pure sense of the categories, that is contingent the contradictory opposite of which is possible. Now we cannot reason from empirical contingency to intellectual. The opposite of that which is changed—the opposite of its state—is actual at another time, and is therefore possible. Consequently, it is not the contradictory opposite of the former state. To be that, it is necessary that, in the same time in which the preceding state existed, its opposite could have existed in its place; but such a cognition is not given us in the mere phenomenon of change. A body that was in motion = A, comes into a state of rest = non-A. Now it cannot be concluded from the fact that a state opposite to the state A follows it, that the contradictory opposite of A is possible; and that A is therefore contingent. To prove this, we should require to know that the state of rest could have existed in the very same time in which the motion took place. Now we know nothing more than that the state of rest was actual in the time that followed the state of motion; consequently, that it was also possible. But motion at one time, and rest at another time, are not contradictorily opposed to each other. It follows from what has been said that the succession of opposite determinations, that is, change, does not demonstrate the fact of contingency as represented in the conceptions of the pure understanding; and that it cannot, therefore, conduct us to the fact of the existence of a necessary being. Change proves merely empirical contingency, that is to say, that the new state could not have existed without a cause, which belongs to the preceding time. This cause—even although it is regarded as absolutely necessary—must be presented to us in time, and must belong to the series of phenomena.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe difficulties which meet us, in our attempt to rise through the series of phenomena to the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, must not originate from our inability to establish the truth of our mere conceptions of the necessary existence of a thing. That is to say, our objections not be ontological, but must be directed against the causal connection with a series of phenomena of a condition which is itself unconditioned. In one word, they must be cosmological and relate to empirical laws. We must show that the regress in the series of causes (in the world of sense) cannot conclude with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the cosmical state—a contingency alleged to arise from change—does not justify us in accepting a first cause, that is, a prime originator of the cosmical series.\n\nThe reader will observe in this antinomy a very remarkable contrast. The very same grounds of proof which established in the thesis the existence of a supreme being, demonstrated in the antithesis—and with equal strictness—the non-existence of such a being. We found, first, that a necessary being exists, because the whole time past contains the series of all conditions, and with it, therefore, the unconditioned (the necessary); secondly, that there does not exist any necessary being, for the same reason, that the whole time past contains the series of all conditions—which are themselves, therefore, in the aggregate, conditioned. The cause of this seeming incongruity is as follows. We attend, in the first argument, solely to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, the one of which determines the other in time, and thus arrive at a necessary unconditioned. In the second, we consider, on the contrary, the contingency of everything that is determined in the series of time—for every event is preceded by a time, in which the condition itself must be determined as conditioned—and thus everything that is unconditioned or absolutely necessary disappears. In both, the mode of proof is quite in accordance with the common procedure of human reason, which often falls into discord with itself, from considering an object from two different points of view. Herr von Mairan regarded the controversy between two celebrated astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty as to the choice of a proper standpoint, as a phenomenon of sufficient importance to warrant a separate treatise on the subject. The one concluded: the moon revolves on its own axis, because it constantly presents the same side to the earth; the other declared that the moon does not revolve on its own axis, for the same reason. Both conclusions were perfectly correct, according to the point of view from which the motions of the moon were considered.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","text":"### Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason\n\nThetic is the term applied to every collection of dogmatical propositions. By antithetic I do not understand dogmatical assertions of the opposite, but the self-contradiction of seemingly dogmatical cognitions (thesis cum antithesis), in none of which we can discover any decided superiority. Antithetic is not, therefore, occupied with one-sided statements, but is engaged in considering the contradictory nature of the general cognitions of reason and its causes. Transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we employ our reason not merely in the application of the principles of the understanding to objects of experience, but venture with it beyond these boundaries, there arise certain sophistical propositions or theorems. These assertions have the following peculiarities: They can find neither confirmation nor confutation in experience; and each is in itself not only self-consistent, but possesses conditions of its necessity in the very nature of reason—only that, unluckily, there exist just as valid and necessary grounds for maintaining the contrary proposition.\n\nThe questions which naturally arise in the consideration of this dialectic of pure reason, are therefore: 1st. In what propositions is pure reason unavoidably subject to an antinomy? 2nd. What are the causes of this antinomy? 3rd. Whether and in what way can reason free itself from this self-contradiction?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","text":"A dialectical proposition or theorem of pure reason must, according to what has been said, be distinguishable from all sophistical propositions, by the fact that it is not an answer to an arbitrary question, which may be raised at the mere pleasure of any person, but to one which human reason must necessarily encounter in its progress. In the second place, a dialectical proposition, with its opposite, does not carry the appearance of a merely artificial illusion, which disappears as soon as it is investigated, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when we are no longer deceived by it, continues to mock us and, although rendered harmless, can never be completely removed.\n\nThis dialectical doctrine will not relate to the unity of understanding in empirical conceptions, but to the unity of reason in pure ideas. The conditions of this doctrine are—inasmuch as it must, as a synthesis according to rules, be conformable to the understanding, and at the same time as the absolute unity of the synthesis, to the reason—that, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, it is too great for the understanding, if according with the understanding, it is too small for the reason. Hence arises a mutual opposition, which cannot be avoided, do what we will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0003","text":"These sophistical assertions of dialectic open, as it were, a battle-field, where that side obtains the victory which has been permitted to make the attack, and he is compelled to yield who has been unfortunately obliged to stand on the defensive. And hence, champions of ability, whether on the right or on the wrong side, are certain to carry away the crown of victory, if they only take care to have the right to make the last attack, and are not obliged to sustain another onset from their opponent. We can easily believe that this arena has been often trampled by the feet of combatants, that many victories have been obtained on both sides, but that the last victory, decisive of the affair between the contending parties, was won by him who fought for the right, only if his adversary was forbidden to continue the tourney. As impartial umpires, we must lay aside entirely the consideration whether the combatants are fighting for the right or for the wrong side, for the true or for the false, and allow the combat to be first decided. Perhaps, after they have wearied more than injured each other, they will discover the nothingness of their cause of quarrel and part good friends.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","text":"This method of watching, or rather of originating, a conflict of assertions, not for the purpose of finally deciding in favour of either side, but to discover whether the object of the struggle is not a mere illusion, which each strives in vain to reach, but which would be no gain even when reached—this procedure, I say, may be termed the sceptical method. It is thoroughly distinct from scepticism—the principle of a technical and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, in order, if possible, to destroy our belief and confidence therein. For the sceptical method aims at certainty, by endeavouring to discover in a conflict of this kind, conducted honestly and intelligently on both sides, the point of misunderstanding; just as wise legislators derive, from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits, information in regard to the defective and ill-defined parts of their statutes. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws, is for our limited wisdom the best criterion of legislation. For the attention of reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become conscious of its errors, is thus roused to the momenta in the determination of its principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","text":"But this sceptical method is essentially peculiar to transcendental philosophy, and can perhaps be dispensed with in every other field of investigation. In mathematics its use would be absurd; because in it no false assertions can long remain hidden, inasmuch as its demonstrations must always proceed under the guidance of pure intuition, and by means of an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy, doubt and delay may be very useful; but no misunderstanding is possible, which cannot be easily removed; and in experience means of solving the difficulty and putting an end to the dissension must at last be found, whether sooner or later. Moral philosophy can always exhibit its principles, with their practical consequences, in concreto—at least in possible experiences, and thus escape the mistakes and ambiguities of abstraction. But transcendental propositions, which lay claim to insight beyond the region of possible experience, cannot, on the one hand, exhibit their abstract synthesis in any à priori intuition, nor, on the other, expose a lurking error by the help of experience. Transcendental reason, therefore, presents us with no other criterion than that of an attempt to reconcile such assertions, and for this purpose to permit a free and unrestrained conflict between them. And this we now proceed to arrange.[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-50)\n\n[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-50) The antinomies stand in the order of the four transcendental ideas above detailed.\n\nFIRST CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThe world has a beginning in time, and is also limited in regard to space.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","text":"Granted that the world has no beginning in time; up to every given moment of time, an eternity must have elapsed, and therewith passed away an infinite series of successive conditions or states of things in the world. Now the infinity of a series consists in the fact that it never can be completed by means of a successive synthesis. It follows that an infinite series already elapsed is impossible and that, consequently, a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence. And this was the first thing to be proved.\n\nAs regards the second, let us take the opposite for granted. In this case, the world must be an infinite given total of coexistent things. Now we cannot cogitate the dimensions of a quantity, which is not given within certain limits of an intuition,[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-51) in any other way than by means of the synthesis of its parts, and the total of such a quantity only by means of a completed synthesis, or the repeated addition of unity to itself. Accordingly, to cogitate the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world must be looked upon as completed, that is to say, an infinite time must be regarded as having elapsed in the enumeration of all co-existing things; which is impossible. For this reason an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be considered as a given whole, consequently, not as a contemporaneously given whole. The world is consequently, as regards extension in space, not infinite, but enclosed in limits. And this was the second thing to be proved.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","text":"[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-51) We may consider an undetermined quantity as a whole, when it is enclosed within limits, although we cannot construct or ascertain its totality by measurement, that is, by the successive synthesis of its parts. For its limits of themselves determine its completeness as a whole.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThe world has no beginning, and no limits in space, but is, in relation both to time and space, infinite.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor let it be granted that it has a beginning. A beginning is an existence which is preceded by a time in which the thing does not exist. On the above supposition, it follows that there must have been a time in which the world did not exist, that is, a void time. But in a void time the origination of a thing is impossible; because no part of any such time contains a distinctive condition of being, in preference to that of non-being (whether the supposed thing originate of itself, or by means of some other cause). Consequently, many series of things may have a beginning in the world, but the world itself cannot have a beginning, and is, therefore, in relation to past time, infinite.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0008","text":"As regards the second statement, let us first take the opposite for granted—that the world is finite and limited in space; it follows that it must exist in a void space, which is not limited. We should therefore meet not only with a relation of things in space, but also a relation of things to space. Now, as the world is an absolute whole, out of and beyond which no object of intuition, and consequently no correlate to which can be discovered, this relation of the world to a void space is merely a relation to no object. But such a relation, and consequently the limitation of the world by void space, is nothing. Consequently, the world, as regards space, is not limited, that is, it is infinite in regard to extension.[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-52)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0009","text":"[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-52) Space is merely the form of external intuition (formal intuition), and not a real object which can be externally perceived. Space, prior to all things which determine it (fill or limit it), or, rather, which present an empirical intuition conformable to it, is, under the title of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of external phenomena, in so far as they either exist in themselves, or can annex themselves to given intuitions. Empirical intuition is therefore not a composition of phenomena and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis, but they are vitally connected in the same empirical intuition, as matter and form. If we wish to set one of these two apart from the other—space from phenomena—there arise all sorts of empty determinations of external intuition, which are very far from being possible perceptions. For example, motion or rest of the world in an infinite empty space, or a determination of the mutual relation of both, cannot possibly be perceived, and is therefore merely the predicate of a notional entity.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FIRST ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nIn bringing forward these conflicting arguments, I have not been on the search for sophisms, for the purpose of availing myself of special pleading, which takes advantage of the carelessness of the opposite party, appeals to a misunderstood statute, and erects its unrighteous claims upon an unfair interpretation. Both proofs originate fairly from the nature of the case, and the advantage presented by the mistakes of the dogmatists of both parties has been completely set aside.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0010","text":"The thesis might also have been unfairly demonstrated, by the introduction of an erroneous conception of the infinity of a given quantity. A quantity is infinite, if a greater than itself cannot possibly exist. The quantity is measured by the number of given units—which are taken as a standard—contained in it. Now no number can be the greatest, because one or more units can always be added. It follows that an infinite given quantity, consequently an infinite world (both as regards time and extension) is impossible. It is, therefore, limited in both respects. In this manner I might have conducted my proof; but the conception given in it does not agree with the true conception of an infinite whole. In this there is no representation of its quantity, it is not said how large it is; consequently its conception is not the conception of a maximum. We cogitate in it merely its relation to an arbitrarily assumed unit, in relation to which it is greater than any number. Now, just as the unit which is taken is greater or smaller, the infinite will be greater or smaller; but the infinity, which consists merely in the relation to this given unit, must remain always the same, although the absolute quantity of the whole is not thereby cognized.\n\nThe true (transcendental) conception of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the measurement of a given quantum can never be completed.[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-53) Hence it follows, without possibility of mistake, that an eternity of actual successive states up to a given (the present) moment cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0011","text":"[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-53) The quantum in this sense contains a congeries of given units, which is greater than any number—and this is the mathematical conception of the infinite.\n\nIn regard to the second part of the thesis, the difficulty as to an infinite and yet elapsed series disappears; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is contemporaneously given. But, in order to cogitate the total of this manifold, as we cannot have the aid of limits constituting by themselves this total in intuition, we are obliged to give some account of our conception, which in this case cannot proceed from the whole to the determined quantity of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole by means of a successive synthesis of the parts. But as this synthesis must constitute a series that cannot be completed, it is impossible for us to cogitate prior to it, and consequently not by means of it, a totality. For the conception of totality itself is in the present case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and consequently its conception, is impossible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","text":"The proof in favour of the infinity of the cosmical succession and the cosmical content is based upon the consideration that, in the opposite case, a void time and a void space must constitute the limits of the world. Now I am not unaware, that there are some ways of escaping this conclusion. It may, for example, be alleged, that a limit to the world, as regards both space and time, is quite possible, without at the same time holding the existence of an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extending beyond the actual world—which is impossible. I am quite well satisfied with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnitzian school. Space is merely the form of external intuition, but not a real object which can itself be externally intuited; it is not a correlate of phenomena, it is the form of phenomena itself. Space, therefore, cannot be regarded as absolutely and in itself something determinative of the existence of things, because it is not itself an object, but only the form of possible objects. Consequently, things, as phenomena, determine space; that is to say, they render it possible that, of all the possible predicates of space (size and relation), certain may belong to reality. But we cannot affirm the converse, that space, as something self-subsistent, can determine real things in regard to size or shape, for it is in itself not a real thing. Space (filled or void)[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-54) may therefore be limited by phenomena, but phenomena cannot be limited by an empty space without them. This is true of time also. All this being granted, it is nevertheless indisputable, that we must assume these two nonentities, void space without and void time before the world, if we assume the existence of cosmical limits, relatively to space or time.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","text":"[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-54) It is evident that what is meant here is, that empty space, in so far as it is limited by phenomena—space, that is, within the world—does not at least contradict transcendental principles, and may therefore, as regards them, be admitted, although its possibility cannot on that account be affirmed.\n\nFor, as regards the subterfuge adopted by those who endeavour to evade the consequence—that, if the world is limited as to space and time, the infinite void must determine the existence of actual things in regard to their dimensions—it arises solely from the fact that instead of a sensuous world, an intelligible world—of which nothing is known—is cogitated; instead of a real beginning (an existence, which is preceded by a period in which nothing exists), an existence which presupposes no other condition than that of time; and, instead of limits of extension, boundaries of the universe. But the question relates to the mundus phaenomenon, and its quantity; and in this case we cannot make abstraction of the conditions of sensibility, without doing away with the essential reality of this world itself. The world of sense, if it is limited, must necessarily lie in the infinite void. If this, and with it space as the à priori condition of the possibility of phenomena, is left out of view, the whole world of sense disappears. In our problem is this alone considered as given. The mundus intelligibilis is nothing but the general conception of a world, in which abstraction has been made of all conditions of intuition, and in relation to which no synthetical proposition—either affirmative or negative—is possible.\n\nSECOND CONFLICT OF TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","text":"Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts; and there exists nothing that is not either itself simple, or composed of simple parts.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor, grant that composite substances do not consist of simple parts; in this case, if all combination or composition were annihilated in thought, no composite part, and (as, by the supposition, there do not exist simple parts) no simple part would exist. Consequently, no substance; consequently, nothing would exist. Either, then, it is impossible to annihilate composition in thought; or, after such annihilation, there must remain something that subsists without composition, that is, something that is simple. But in the former case the composite could not itself consist of substances, because with substances composition is merely a contingent relation, apart from which they must still exist as self-subsistent beings. Now, as this case contradicts the supposition, the second must contain the truth—that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.\n\nIt follows, as an immediate inference, that the things in the world are all, without exception, simple beings—that composition is merely an external condition pertaining to them—and that, although we never can separate and isolate the elementary substances from the state of composition, reason must cogitate these as the primary subjects of all composition, and consequently, as prior thereto—and as simple substances.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nNo composite thing in the world consists of simple parts; and there does not exist in the world any simple substance.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0015","text":"Let it be supposed that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Inasmuch as all external relation, consequently all composition of substances, is possible only in space; the space, occupied by that which is composite, must consist of the same number of parts as is contained in the composite. But space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore, every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely primary parts of what is composite are simple. It follows that what is simple occupies a space. Now, as everything real that occupies a space, contains a manifold the parts of which are external to each other, and is consequently composite—and a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot exist external to each other apart from substance), but of substances—it follows that the simple must be a substantial composite, which is self-contradictory.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0016","text":"The second proposition of the antithesis—that there exists in the world nothing that is simple—is here equivalent to the following: The existence of the absolutely simple cannot be demonstrated from any experience or perception either external or internal; and the absolutely simple is a mere idea, the objective reality of which cannot be demonstrated in any possible experience; it is consequently, in the exposition of phenomena, without application and object. For, let us take for granted that an object may be found in experience for this transcendental idea; the empirical intuition of such an object must then be recognized to contain absolutely no manifold with its parts external to each other, and connected into unity. Now, as we cannot reason from the non-consciousness of such a manifold to the impossibility of its existence in the intuition of an object, and as the proof of this impossibility is necessary for the establishment and proof of absolute simplicity; it follows that this simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatever. As, therefore, an absolutely simple object cannot be given in any experience, and the world of sense must be considered as the sum total of all possible experiences: nothing simple exists in the world.\n\nThis second proposition in the antithesis has a more extended aim than the first. The first merely banishes the simple from the intuition of the composite; while the second drives it entirely out of nature. Hence we were unable to demonstrate it from the conception of a given object of external intuition (of the composite), but we were obliged to prove it from the relation of a given object to a possible experience in general.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE SECOND ANTINOMY. THESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0017","text":"When I speak of a whole, which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand thereby only a substantial whole, as the true composite; that is to say, I understand that contingent unity of the manifold which is given as perfectly isolated (at least in thought), placed in reciprocal connection, and thus constituted a unity. Space ought not to be called a compositum but a totum, for its parts are possible in the whole, and not the whole by means of the parts. It might perhaps be called a compositum ideale, but not a compositum reale. But this is of no importance. As space is not a composite of substances (and not even of real accidents), if I abstract all composition therein—nothing, not even a point, remains; for a point is possible only as the limit of a space—consequently of a composite. Space and time, therefore, do not consist of simple parts. That which belongs only to the condition or state of a substance, even although it possesses a quantity (motion or change, for example), likewise does not consist of simple parts. That is to say, a certain degree of change does not originate from the addition of many simple changes. Our inference of the simple from the composite is valid only of self-subsisting things. But the accidents of a state are not self-subsistent. The proof, then, for the necessity of the simple, as the component part of all that is substantial and composite, may prove a failure, and the whole case of this thesis be lost, if we carry the proposition too far, and wish to make it valid of everything that is composite without distinction—as indeed has really now and then happened. Besides, I am here speaking only of the simple, in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite—the latter being capable of solution into the former as its component parts. The proper signification of the word monas (as employed by Leibnitz) ought to relate to the simple, given immediately as simple substance (for example, in consciousness), and not as an element of the composite. As an element, the term atomus would be more appropriate. And as I wish to prove the existence of simple substances, only in relation to, and as the elements of, the composite, I might term the antithesis of the second Antinomy, transcendental Atomistic. But as this word has long been employed to designate a particular theory of corporeal phenomena (moleculae), and thus presupposes a basis of empirical conceptions, I prefer calling it the dialectical principle of Monadology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0018","text":"ANTITHESIS.\n\nAgainst the assertion of the infinite subdivisibility of matter whose ground of proof is purely mathematical, objections have been alleged by the Monadists. These objections lay themselves open, at first sight, to suspicion, from the fact that they do not recognize the clearest mathematical proofs as propositions relating to the constitution of space, in so far as it is really the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them merely as inferences from abstract but arbitrary conceptions, which cannot have any application to real things. Just as if it were possible to imagine another mode of intuition than that given in the primitive intuition of space; and just as if its à priori determinations did not apply to everything, the existence of which is possible, from the fact alone of its filling space. If we listen to them, we shall find ourselves required to cogitate, in addition to the mathematical point, which is simple—not, however, a part, but a mere limit of space—physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but possess the peculiar property, as parts of space, of filling it merely by their aggregation. I shall not repeat here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be found everywhere in numbers: every one knows that it is impossible to undermine the evidence of mathematics by mere discursive conceptions; I shall only remark that, if in this case philosophy endeavours to gain an advantage over mathematics by sophistical artifices, it is because it forgets that the discussion relates solely to Phenomena and their conditions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0019","text":"It is not sufficient to find the conception of the simple for the pure conception of the composite, but we must discover for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple. Now this, according to the laws of sensibility, and consequently in the case of objects of sense, is utterly impossible. In the case of a whole composed of substances, which is cogitated solely by the pure understanding, it may be necessary to be in possession of the simple before composition is possible. But this does not hold good of the Totum substantiale phaenomenon, which, as an empirical intuition in space, possesses the necessary property of containing no simple part, for the very reason that no part of space is simple. Meanwhile, the Monadists have been subtle enough to escape from this difficulty, by presupposing intuition and the dynamical relation of substances as the condition of the possibility of space, instead of regarding space as the condition of the possibility of the objects of external intuition, that is, of bodies. Now we have a conception of bodies only as phenomena, and, as such, they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of all external phenomena. The evasion is therefore in vain; as, indeed, we have sufficiently shown in our Æsthetic. If bodies were things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would be unexceptionable.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0020","text":"The second dialectical assertion possesses the peculiarity of having opposed to it a dogmatical proposition, which, among all such sophistical statements, is the only one that undertakes to prove in the case of an object of experience, that which is properly a transcendental idea—the absolute simplicity of substance. The proposition is that the object of the internal sense, the thinking Ego, is an absolute simple substance. Without at present entering upon this subject—as it has been considered at length in a former chapter—I shall merely remark that, if something is cogitated merely as an object, without the addition of any synthetical determination of its intuition—as happens in the case of the bare representation, _I_—it is certain that no manifold and no composition can be perceived in such a representation. As, moreover, the predicates whereby I cogitate this object are merely intuitions of the internal sense, there cannot be discovered in them anything to prove the existence of a manifold whose parts are external to each other, and, consequently, nothing to prove the existence of real composition. Consciousness, therefore, is so constituted that, inasmuch as the thinking subject is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself—although it can divide its inhering determinations. For every object in relation to itself is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if the subject is regarded externally, as an object of intuition, it must, in its character of phenomenon, possess the property of composition. And it must always be regarded in this manner, if we wish to know whether there is or is not contained in it a manifold whose parts are external to each other.\n\nTHIRD CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","text":"Causality according to the laws of nature, is not the only causality operating to originate the phenomena of the world. A causality of freedom is also necessary to account fully for these phenomena.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed, that there is no other kind of causality than that according to the laws of nature. Consequently, everything that happens presupposes a previous condition, which it follows with absolute certainty, in conformity with a rule. But this previous condition must itself be something that has happened (that has arisen in time, as it did not exist before), for, if it has always been in existence, its consequence or effect would not thus originate for the first time, but would likewise have always existed. The causality, therefore, of a cause, whereby something happens, is itself a thing that has happened. Now this again presupposes, in conformity with the law of nature, a previous condition and its causality, and this another anterior to the former, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with the laws of nature, there cannot be any real first beginning of things, but only a subaltern or comparative beginning. There cannot, therefore, be a completeness of series on the side of the causes which originate the one from the other. But the law of nature is that nothing can happen without a sufficient à priori determined cause. The proposition therefore—if all causality is possible only in accordance with the laws of nature—is, when stated in this unlimited and general manner, self-contradictory. It follows that this cannot be the only kind of causality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","text":"From what has been said, it follows that a causality must be admitted, by means of which something happens, without its cause being determined according to necessary laws by some other cause preceding. That is to say, there must exist an absolute spontaneity of cause, which of itself originates a series of phenomena which proceeds according to natural laws—consequently transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of phenomena on the side of causes is never complete.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThere is no such thing as freedom, but everything in the world happens solely according to the laws of nature.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted, that there does exist freedom in the transcendental sense, as a peculiar kind of causality, operating to produce events in the world—a faculty, that is to say, of originating a state, and consequently a series of consequences from that state. In this case, not only the series originated by this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is to say, the causality itself must have an absolute commencement, such that nothing can precede to determine this action according to unvarying laws. But every beginning of action presupposes in the acting cause a state of inaction; and a dynamically primal beginning of action presupposes a state, which has no connection—as regards causality—with the preceding state of the cause—which does not, that is, in any wise result from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the natural law of cause and effect, and such a conjunction of successive states in effective causes is destructive of the possibility of unity in experience and for that reason not to be found in experience—is consequently a mere fiction of thought.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0023","text":"We have, therefore, nothing but nature to which we must look for connection and order in cosmical events. Freedom—independence of the laws of nature—is certainly a deliverance from restraint, but it is also a relinquishing of the guidance of law and rule. For it cannot be alleged that, instead of the laws of nature, laws of freedom may be introduced into the causality of the course of nature. For, if freedom were determined according to laws, it would be no longer freedom, but merely nature. Nature, therefore, and transcendental freedom are distinguishable as conformity to law and lawlessness. The former imposes upon understanding the difficulty of seeking the origin of events ever higher and higher in the series of causes, inasmuch as causality is always conditioned thereby; while it compensates this labour by the guarantee of a unity complete and in conformity with law. The latter, on the contrary, holds out to the understanding the promise of a point of rest in the chain of causes, by conducting it to an unconditioned causality, which professes to have the power of spontaneous origination, but which, in its own utter blindness, deprives it of the guidance of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE THIRD ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0024","text":"The transcendental idea of freedom is far from constituting the entire content of the psychological conception so termed, which is for the most part empirical. It merely presents us with the conception of spontaneity of action, as the proper ground for imputing freedom to the cause of a certain class of objects. It is, however, the true stumbling-stone to philosophy, which meets with unconquerable difficulties in the way of its admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will, which has for so long a time placed speculative reason in such perplexity, is properly only transcendental, and concerns the question, whether there must be held to exist a faculty of spontaneous origination of a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible is not a necessary inquiry; for in the case of natural causality itself, we are obliged to content ourselves with the à priori knowledge that such a causality must be presupposed, although we are quite incapable of comprehending how the being of one thing is possible through the being of another, but must for this information look entirely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a free first beginning of a series of phenomena, only in so far as it is required for the comprehension of an origin of the world, all following states being regarded as a succession according to laws of nature alone. But, as there has thus been proved the existence of a faculty which can of itself originate a series in time—although we are unable to explain how it can exist—we feel ourselves authorized to admit, even in the midst of the natural course of events, a beginning, as regards causality, of different successions of phenomena, and at the same time to attribute to all substances a faculty of free action. But we ought in this case not to allow ourselves to fall into a common misunderstanding, and to suppose that, because a successive series in the world can only have a comparatively first beginning—another state or condition of things always preceding—an absolutely first beginning of a series in the course of nature is impossible. For we are not speaking here of an absolutely first beginning in relation to time, but as regards causality alone. When, for example, I, completely of my own free will, and independently of the necessarily determinative influence of natural causes, rise from my chair, there commences with this event, including its material consequences in infinitum, an absolutely new series; although, in relation to time, this event is merely the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act of mine do not form part of the succession of effects in nature, and are not mere continuations of it; on the contrary, the determining causes of nature cease to operate in reference to this event, which certainly succeeds the acts of nature, but does not proceed from them. For these reasons, the action of a free agent must be termed, in regard to causality, if not in relation to time, an absolutely primal beginning of a series of phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0025","text":"The justification of this need of reason to rest upon a free act as the first beginning of the series of natural causes is evident from the fact, that all philosophers of antiquity (with the exception of the Epicurean school) felt themselves obliged, when constructing a theory of the motions of the universe, to accept a prime mover, that is, a freely acting cause, which spontaneously and prior to all other causes evolved this series of states. They always felt the need of going beyond mere nature, for the purpose of making a first beginning comprehensible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0026","text":"The assertor of the all-sufficiency of nature in regard to causality (transcendental Physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would defend his view of the question somewhat in the following manner. He would say, in answer to the sophistical arguments of the opposite party: If you do not accept a mathematical first, in relation to time, you have no need to seek a dynamical first, in regard to causality. Who compelled you to imagine an absolutely primal condition of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the gradually progressing successions of phenomena—and, as some foundation for this fancy of yours, to set bounds to unlimited nature? Inasmuch as the substances in the world have always existed—at least the unity of experience renders such a supposition quite necessary—there is no difficulty in believing also, that the changes in the conditions of these substances have always existed; and, consequently, that a first beginning, mathematical or dynamical, is by no means required. The possibility of such an infinite derivation, without any initial member from which all the others result, is certainly quite incomprehensible. But, if you are rash enough to deny the enigmatical secrets of nature for this reason, you will find yourselves obliged to deny also the existence of many fundamental properties of natural objects (such as fundamental forces), which you can just as little comprehend; and even the possibility of so simple a conception as that of change must present to you insuperable difficulties. For if experience did not teach you that it was real, you never could conceive à priori the possibility of this ceaseless sequence of being and non-being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","text":"But if the existence of a transcendental faculty of freedom is granted—a faculty of originating changes in the world—this faculty must at least exist out of and apart from the world; although it is certainly a bold assumption, that, over and above the complete content of all possible intuitions, there still exists an object which cannot be presented in any possible perception. But, to attribute to substances in the world itself such a faculty, is quite inadmissible; for, in this case; the connection of phenomena reciprocally determining and determined according to general laws, which is termed nature, and along with it the criteria of empirical truth, which enable us to distinguish experience from mere visionary dreaming, would almost entirely disappear. In proximity with such a lawless faculty of freedom, a system of nature is hardly cogitable; for the laws of the latter would be continually subject to the intrusive influences of the former, and the course of phenomena, which would otherwise proceed regularly and uniformly, would become thereby confused and disconnected.\n\nFOURTH CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThere exists either in, or in connection with the world—either as a part of it, or as the cause of it—an absolutely necessary being.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","text":"The world of sense, as the sum total of all phenomena, contains a series of changes. For, without such a series, the mental representation of the series of time itself, as the condition of the possibility of the sensuous world, could not be presented to us.[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-55) But every change stands under its condition, which precedes it in time and renders it necessary. Now the existence of a given condition presupposes a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. It follows that something that is absolutely necessary must exist, if change exists as its consequence. But this necessary thing itself belongs to the sensuous world. For suppose it to exist out of and apart from it, the series of cosmical changes would receive from it a beginning, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the world of sense. But this is impossible. For, as the beginning of a series in time is determined only by that which precedes it in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of changes must exist in the time in which this series itself did not exist; for a beginning supposes a time preceding, in which the thing that begins to be was not in existence. The causality of the necessary cause of changes, and consequently the cause itself, must for these reasons belong to time—and to phenomena, time being possible only as the form of phenomena. Consequently, it cannot be cogitated as separated from the world of sense—the sum total of all phenomena. There is, therefore, contained in the world, something that is absolutely necessary—whether it be the whole cosmical series itself, or only a part of it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","text":"[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-55) Objectively, time, as the formal condition of the possibility of change, precedes all changes; but subjectively, and in consciousness, the representation of time, like every other, is given solely by occasion of perception.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAn absolutely necessary being does not exist, either in the world, or out of it—as its cause.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGrant that either the world itself is necessary, or that there is contained in it a necessary existence. Two cases are possible. First, there must either be in the series of cosmical changes a beginning, which is unconditionally necessary, and therefore uncaused—which is at variance with the dynamical law of the determination of all phenomena in time; or, secondly, the series itself is without beginning, and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, is nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned as a whole—which is self-contradictory. For the existence of an aggregate cannot be necessary, if no single part of it possesses necessary existence.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0030","text":"Grant, on the other hand, that an absolutely necessary cause exists out of and apart from the world. This cause, as the highest member in the series of the causes of cosmical changes, must originate or begin[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-56) the existence of the latter and their series. In this case it must also begin to act, and its causality would therefore belong to time, and consequently to the sum total of phenomena, that is, to the world. It follows that the cause cannot be out of the world; which is contradictory to the hypothesis. Therefore, neither in the world, nor out of it (but in causal connection with it), does there exist any absolutely necessary being.\n\n[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-56) The word begin is taken in two senses. The first is active—the cause being regarded as beginning a series of conditions as its effect (infit). The second is passive—the causality in the cause itself beginning to operate (fit). I reason here from the first to the second.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FOURTH ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nTo demonstrate the existence of a necessary being, I cannot be permitted in this place to employ any other than the cosmological argument, which ascends from the conditioned in phenomena to the unconditioned in conception—the unconditioned being considered the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. The proof, from the mere idea of a supreme being, belongs to another principle of reason and requires separate discussion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0031","text":"The pure cosmological proof demonstrates the existence of a necessary being, but at the same time leaves it quite unsettled, whether this being is the world itself, or quite distinct from it. To establish the truth of the latter view, principles are requisite, which are not cosmological and do not proceed in the series of phenomena. We should require to introduce into our proof conceptions of contingent beings—regarded merely as objects of the understanding, and also a principle which enables us to connect these, by means of mere conceptions, with a necessary being. But the proper place for all such arguments is a transcendent philosophy, which has unhappily not yet been established.\n\nBut, if we begin our proof cosmologically, by laying at the foundation of it the series of phenomena, and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality, we are not at liberty to break off from this mode of demonstration and to pass over to something which is not itself a member of the series. The condition must be taken in exactly the same signification as the relation of the conditioned to its condition in the series has been taken, for the series must conduct us in an unbroken regress to this supreme condition. But if this relation is sensuous, and belongs to the possible empirical employment of understanding, the supreme condition or cause must close the regressive series according to the laws of sensibility and consequently, must belong to the series of time. It follows that this necessary existence must be regarded as the highest member of the cosmical series.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","text":"Certain philosophers have, nevertheless, allowed themselves the liberty of making such a saltus (metabasis eis allo gonos). From the changes in the world they have concluded their empirical contingency, that is, their dependence on empirically-determined causes, and they thus admitted an ascending series of empirical conditions: and in this they are quite right. But as they could not find in this series any primal beginning or any highest member, they passed suddenly from the empirical conception of contingency to the pure category, which presents us with a series—not sensuous, but intellectual—whose completeness does certainly rest upon the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Nay, more, this intellectual series is not tied to any sensuous conditions; and is therefore free from the condition of time, which requires it spontaneously to begin its causality in time. But such a procedure is perfectly inadmissible, as will be made plain from what follows.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0033","text":"In the pure sense of the categories, that is contingent the contradictory opposite of which is possible. Now we cannot reason from empirical contingency to intellectual. The opposite of that which is changed—the opposite of its state—is actual at another time, and is therefore possible. Consequently, it is not the contradictory opposite of the former state. To be that, it is necessary that, in the same time in which the preceding state existed, its opposite could have existed in its place; but such a cognition is not given us in the mere phenomenon of change. A body that was in motion = A, comes into a state of rest = non-A. Now it cannot be concluded from the fact that a state opposite to the state A follows it, that the contradictory opposite of A is possible; and that A is therefore contingent. To prove this, we should require to know that the state of rest could have existed in the very same time in which the motion took place. Now we know nothing more than that the state of rest was actual in the time that followed the state of motion; consequently, that it was also possible. But motion at one time, and rest at another time, are not contradictorily opposed to each other. It follows from what has been said that the succession of opposite determinations, that is, change, does not demonstrate the fact of contingency as represented in the conceptions of the pure understanding; and that it cannot, therefore, conduct us to the fact of the existence of a necessary being. Change proves merely empirical contingency, that is to say, that the new state could not have existed without a cause, which belongs to the preceding time. This cause—even although it is regarded as absolutely necessary—must be presented to us in time, and must belong to the series of phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0034","text":"ON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe difficulties which meet us, in our attempt to rise through the series of phenomena to the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, must not originate from our inability to establish the truth of our mere conceptions of the necessary existence of a thing. That is to say, our objections not be ontological, but must be directed against the causal connection with a series of phenomena of a condition which is itself unconditioned. In one word, they must be cosmological and relate to empirical laws. We must show that the regress in the series of causes (in the world of sense) cannot conclude with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the cosmical state—a contingency alleged to arise from change—does not justify us in accepting a first cause, that is, a prime originator of the cosmical series.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0035","text":"The reader will observe in this antinomy a very remarkable contrast. The very same grounds of proof which established in the thesis the existence of a supreme being, demonstrated in the antithesis—and with equal strictness—the non-existence of such a being. We found, first, that a necessary being exists, because the whole time past contains the series of all conditions, and with it, therefore, the unconditioned (the necessary); secondly, that there does not exist any necessary being, for the same reason, that the whole time past contains the series of all conditions—which are themselves, therefore, in the aggregate, conditioned. The cause of this seeming incongruity is as follows. We attend, in the first argument, solely to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, the one of which determines the other in time, and thus arrive at a necessary unconditioned. In the second, we consider, on the contrary, the contingency of everything that is determined in the series of time—for every event is preceded by a time, in which the condition itself must be determined as conditioned—and thus everything that is unconditioned or absolutely necessary disappears. In both, the mode of proof is quite in accordance with the common procedure of human reason, which often falls into discord with itself, from considering an object from two different points of view. Herr von Mairan regarded the controversy between two celebrated astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty as to the choice of a proper standpoint, as a phenomenon of sufficient importance to warrant a separate treatise on the subject. The one concluded: the moon revolves on its own axis, because it constantly presents the same side to the earth; the other declared that the moon does not revolve on its own axis, for the same reason. Both conclusions were perfectly correct, according to the point of view from which the motions of the moon were considered.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6cfb83cd76b850346afd0a1cbd91daaed332c9addde6b9ea495d3d523174bd36","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","order":64,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIIR","text":"# Contradiction\n\n### Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions\n\nWe have thus completely before us the dialectical procedure of the cosmological ideas. No possible experience can present us with an object adequate to them in extent. Nay, more, reason itself cannot cogitate them as according with the general laws of experience. And yet they are not arbitrary fictions of thought. On the contrary, reason, in its uninterrupted progress in the empirical synthesis, is necessarily conducted to them, when it endeavours to free from all conditions and to comprehend in its unconditioned totality that which can only be determined conditionally in accordance with the laws of experience. These dialectical propositions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There are neither more, nor can there be less, than this number, because there are no other series of synthetical hypotheses, limiting à priori the empirical synthesis.\n\nThe brilliant claims of reason striving to extend its dominion beyond the limits of experience, have been represented above only in dry formulae, which contain merely the grounds of its pretensions. They have, besides, in conformity with the character of a transcendental philosophy, been freed from every empirical element; although the full splendour of the promises they hold out, and the anticipations they excite, manifests itself only when in connection with empirical cognitions. In the application of them, however, and in the advancing enlargement of the employment of reason, while struggling to rise from the region of experience and to soar to those sublime ideas, philosophy discovers a value and a dignity, which, if it could but make good its assertions, would raise it far above all other departments of human knowledge—professing, as it does, to present a sure foundation for our highest hopes and the ultimate aims of all the exertions of reason. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and a limit to its extension in space; whether there exists anywhere, or perhaps, in my own thinking Self, an indivisible and indestructible unity—or whether nothing but what is divisible and transitory exists; whether I am a free agent, or, like other beings, am bound in the chains of nature and fate; whether, finally, there is a supreme cause of the world, or all our thought and speculation must end with nature and the order of external things—are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would willingly exchange his whole science; for in it there is no satisfaction for the highest aspirations and most ardent desires of humanity. Nay, it may even be said that the true value of mathematics—that pride of human reason—consists in this: that she guides reason to the knowledge of nature—in her greater as well as in her less manifestations—in her beautiful order and regularity—guides her, moreover, to an insight into the wonderful unity of the moving forces in the operations of nature, far beyond the expectations of a philosophy building only on experience; and that she thus encourages philosophy to extend the province of reason beyond all experience, and at the same time provides it with the most excellent materials for supporting its investigations, in so far as their nature admits, by adequate and accordant intuitions.\n\nUnfortunately for speculation—but perhaps fortunately for the practical interests of humanity—reason, in the midst of her highest anticipations, finds herself hemmed in by a press of opposite and contradictory conclusions, from which neither her honour nor her safety will permit her to draw back. Nor can she regard these conflicting trains of reasoning with indifference as mere passages at arms, still less can she command peace; for in the subject of the conflict she has a deep interest. There is no other course left open to her than to reflect with herself upon the origin of this disunion in reason—whether it may not arise from a mere misunderstanding. After such an inquiry, arrogant claims would have to be given up on both sides; but the sovereignty of reason over understanding and sense would be based upon a sure foundation.\n\nWe shall at present defer this radical inquiry and, in the meantime, consider for a little what side in the controversy we should most willingly take, if we were obliged to become partisans at all. As, in this case, we leave out of sight altogether the logical criterion of truth, and merely consult our own interest in reference to the question, these considerations, although inadequate to settle the question of right in either party, will enable us to comprehend how those who have taken part in the struggle, adopt the one view rather than the other—no special insight into the subject, however, having influenced their choice. They will, at the same time, explain to us many other things by the way—for example, the fiery zeal on the one side and the cold maintenance of their cause on the other; why the one party has met with the warmest approbations, and the other has always been repulsed by irreconcilable prejudices.\n\nThere is one thing, however, that determines the proper point of view, from which alone this preliminary inquiry can be instituted and carried on with the proper completeness—and that is the comparison of the principles from which both sides, thesis and antithesis, proceed. My readers would remark in the propositions of the antithesis a complete uniformity in the mode of thought and a perfect unity of principle. Its principle was that of pure empiricism, not only in the explication of the phenomena in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas, even of that of the universe itself. The affirmations of the thesis, on the contrary, were based, in addition to the empirical mode of explanation employed in the series of phenomena, on intellectual propositions; and its principles were in so far not simple. I shall term the thesis, in view of its essential characteristic, the dogmatism of pure reason.\n\nOn the side of Dogmatism, or of the thesis, therefore, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, we find:\n\n1. A practical interest, which must be very dear to every right-thinking man. That the word has a beginning—that the nature of my thinking self is simple, and therefore indestructible—that I am a free agent, and raised above the compulsion of nature and her laws—and, finally, that the entire order of things, which form the world, is dependent upon a Supreme Being, from whom the whole receives unity and connection—these are so many foundation-stones of morality and religion. The antithesis deprives us of all these supports—or, at least, seems so to deprive us.\n\n2. A speculative interest of reason manifests itself on this side. For, if we take the transcendental ideas and employ them in the manner which the thesis directs, we can exhibit completely à priori the entire chain of conditions, and understand the derivation of the conditioned—beginning from the unconditioned. This the antithesis does not do; and for this reason does not meet with so welcome a reception. For it can give no answer to our question respecting the conditions of its synthesis—except such as must be supplemented by another question, and so on to infinity. According to it, we must rise from a given beginning to one still higher; every part conducts us to a still smaller one; every event is preceded by another event which is its cause; and the conditions of existence rest always upon other and still higher conditions, and find neither end nor basis in some self-subsistent thing as the primal being.\n\n3. This side has also the advantage of popularity; and this constitutes no small part of its claim to favour. The common understanding does not find the least difficulty in the idea of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis—accustomed, as it is, rather to follow our consequences than to seek for a proper basis for cognition. In the conception of an absolute first, moreover—the possibility of which it does not inquire into—it is highly gratified to find a firmly-established point of departure for its attempts at theory; while in the restless and continuous ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no satisfaction.\n\nOn the side of the antithesis, or Empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas:\n\n1. We cannot discover any such practical interest arising from pure principles of reason as morality and religion present. On the contrary, pure empiricism seems to empty them of all their power and influence. If there does not exist a Supreme Being distinct from the world—if the world is without beginning, consequently without a Creator—if our wills are not free, and the soul is divisible and subject to corruption just like matter—the ideas and principles of morality lose all validity and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.\n\n2. But empiricism, in compensation, holds out to reason, in its speculative interests, certain important advantages, far exceeding any that the dogmatist can promise us. For, when employed by the empiricist, understanding is always upon its proper ground of investigation—the field of possible experience, the laws of which it can explore, and thus extend its cognition securely and with clear intelligence without being stopped by limits in any direction. Here can it and ought it to find and present to intuition its proper object—not only in itself, but in all its relations; or, if it employ conceptions, upon this ground it can always present the corresponding images in clear and unmistakable intuitions. It is quite unnecessary for it to renounce the guidance of nature, to attach itself to ideas, the objects of which it cannot know; because, as mere intellectual entities, they cannot be presented in any intuition. On the contrary, it is not even permitted to abandon its proper occupation, under the pretence that it has been brought to a conclusion (for it never can be), and to pass into the region of idealizing reason and transcendent conceptions, which it is not required to observe and explore the laws of nature, but merely to think and to imagine—secure from being contradicted by facts, because they have not been called as witnesses, but passed by, or perhaps subordinated to the so-called higher interests and considerations of pure reason.\n\nHence the empiricist will never allow himself to accept any epoch of nature for the first—the absolutely primal state; he will not believe that there can be limits to his outlook into her wide domains, nor pass from the objects of nature, which he can satisfactorily explain by means of observation and mathematical thought—which he can determine synthetically in intuition, to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever present in concreto; he will not concede the existence of a faculty in nature, operating independently of the laws of nature—a concession which would introduce uncertainty into the procedure of the understanding, which is guided by necessary laws to the observation of phenomena; nor, finally, will he permit himself to seek a cause beyond nature, inasmuch as we know nothing but it, and from it alone receive an objective basis for all our conceptions and instruction in the unvarying laws of things.\n\nIn truth, if the empirical philosopher had no other purpose in the establishment of his antithesis than to check the presumption of a reason which mistakes its true destination, which boasts of its insight and its knowledge, just where all insight and knowledge cease to exist, and regards that which is valid only in relation to a practical interest, as an advancement of the speculative interests of the mind (in order, when it is convenient for itself, to break the thread of our physical investigations, and, under pretence of extending our cognition, connect them with transcendental ideas, by means of which we really know only that we know nothing)—if, I say, the empiricist rested satisfied with this benefit, the principle advanced by him would be a maxim recommending moderation in the pretensions of reason and modesty in its affirmations, and at the same time would direct us to the right mode of extending the province of the understanding, by the help of the only true teacher, experience. In obedience to this advice, intellectual hypotheses and faith would not be called in aid of our practical interests; nor should we introduce them under the pompous titles of science and insight. For speculative cognition cannot find an objective basis any other where than in experience; and, when we overstep its limits our synthesis, which requires ever new cognitions independent of experience, has no substratum of intuition upon which to build.\n\nBut if—as often happens—empiricism, in relation to ideas, becomes itself dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its phenomenal cognition, it falls itself into the error of intemperance—an error which is here all the more reprehensible, as thereby the practical interest of reason receives an irreparable injury.\n\nAnd this constitutes the opposition between Epicureanism[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-57) and Platonism.\n\n[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-57) It is, however, still a matter of doubt whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as directions for the objective employment of the understanding. If, indeed, they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative exercise of reason, he gives evidence therein a more genuine philosophic spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity. That, in the explanation of phenomena, we must proceed as if the field of inquiry had neither limits in space nor commencement in time; that we must be satisfied with the teaching of experience in reference to the material of which the world is posed; that we must not look for any other mode of the origination of events than that which is determined by the unalterable laws of nature; and finally, that we not employ the hypothesis of a cause distinct from the world to account for a phenomenon or for the world itself—are principles for the extension of speculative philosophy, and the discovery of the true sources of the principles of morals, which, however little conformed to in the present day, are undoubtedly correct. At the same time, any one desirous of ignoring, in mere speculation, these dogmatical propositions, need not for that reason be accused of denying them.\n\nBoth Epicurus and Plato assert more in their systems than they know. The former encourages and advances science—although to the prejudice of the practical; the latter presents us with excellent principles for the investigation of the practical, but, in relation to everything regarding which we can attain to speculative cognition, permits reason to append idealistic explanations of natural phenomena, to the great injury of physical investigation.\n\n3. In regard to the third motive for the preliminary choice of a party in this war of assertions, it seems very extraordinary that empiricism should be utterly unpopular. We should be inclined to believe that the common understanding would receive it with pleasure—promising as it does to satisfy it without passing the bounds of experience and its connected order; while transcendental dogmatism obliges it to rise to conceptions which far surpass the intelligence and ability of the most practised thinkers. But in this, in truth, is to be found its real motive. For the common understanding thus finds itself in a situation where not even the most learned can have the advantage of it. If it understands little or nothing about these transcendental conceptions, no one can boast of understanding any more; and although it may not express itself in so scholastically correct a manner as others, it can busy itself with reasoning and arguments without end, wandering among mere ideas, about which one can always be very eloquent, because we know nothing about them; while, in the observation and investigation of nature, it would be forced to remain dumb and to confess its utter ignorance. Thus indolence and vanity form of themselves strong recommendations of these principles. Besides, although it is a hard thing for a philosopher to assume a principle, of which he can give to himself no reasonable account, and still more to employ conceptions, the objective reality of which cannot be established, nothing is more usual with the common understanding. It wants something which will allow it to go to work with confidence. The difficulty of even comprehending a supposition does not disquiet it, because—not knowing what comprehending means—it never even thinks of the supposition it may be adopting as a principle; and regards as known that with which it has become familiar from constant use. And, at last, all speculative interests disappear before the practical interests which it holds dear; and it fancies that it understands and knows what its necessities and hopes incite it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of transcendentally idealizing reason is robbed of all popularity; and, however prejudicial it may be to the highest practical principles, there is no fear that it will ever pass the limits of the schools, or acquire any favour or influence in society or with the multitude.\n\nHuman reason is by nature architectonic. That is to say, it regards all cognitions as parts of a possible system, and hence accepts only such principles as at least do not incapacitate a cognition to which we may have attained from being placed along with others in a general system. But the propositions of the antithesis are of a character which renders the completion of an edifice of cognitions impossible. According to these, beyond one state or epoch of the world there is always to be found one more ancient; in every part always other parts themselves divisible; preceding every event another, the origin of which must itself be sought still higher; and everything in existence is conditioned, and still not dependent on an unconditioned and primal existence. As, therefore, the antithesis will not concede the existence of a first beginning which might be available as a foundation, a complete edifice of cognition, in the presence of such hypothesis, is utterly impossible. Thus the architectonic interest of reason, which requires a unity—not empirical, but à priori and rational—forms a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis in our antinomy.\n\nBut if any one could free himself entirely from all considerations of interest, and weigh without partiality the assertions of reason, attending only to their content, irrespective of the consequences which follow from them; such a person, on the supposition that he knew no other way out of the confusion than to settle the truth of one or other of the conflicting doctrines, would live in a state of continual hesitation. Today, he would feel convinced that the human will is free; to-morrow, considering the indissoluble chain of nature, he would look on freedom as a mere illusion and declare nature to be all-in-all. But, if he were called to action, the play of the merely speculative reason would disappear like the shapes of a dream, and practical interest would dictate his choice of principles. But, as it well befits a reflective and inquiring being to devote certain periods of time to the examination of its own reason—to divest itself of all partiality, and frankly to communicate its observations for the judgement and opinion of others; so no one can be blamed for, much less prevented from, placing both parties on their trial, with permission to end themselves, free from intimidation, before intimidation, before a sworn jury of equal condition with themselves—the condition of weak and fallible men.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","text":"# Contradiction\n\n### Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions\n\nWe have thus completely before us the dialectical procedure of the cosmological ideas. No possible experience can present us with an object adequate to them in extent. Nay, more, reason itself cannot cogitate them as according with the general laws of experience. And yet they are not arbitrary fictions of thought. On the contrary, reason, in its uninterrupted progress in the empirical synthesis, is necessarily conducted to them, when it endeavours to free from all conditions and to comprehend in its unconditioned totality that which can only be determined conditionally in accordance with the laws of experience. These dialectical propositions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There are neither more, nor can there be less, than this number, because there are no other series of synthetical hypotheses, limiting à priori the empirical synthesis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0002","text":"The brilliant claims of reason striving to extend its dominion beyond the limits of experience, have been represented above only in dry formulae, which contain merely the grounds of its pretensions. They have, besides, in conformity with the character of a transcendental philosophy, been freed from every empirical element; although the full splendour of the promises they hold out, and the anticipations they excite, manifests itself only when in connection with empirical cognitions. In the application of them, however, and in the advancing enlargement of the employment of reason, while struggling to rise from the region of experience and to soar to those sublime ideas, philosophy discovers a value and a dignity, which, if it could but make good its assertions, would raise it far above all other departments of human knowledge—professing, as it does, to present a sure foundation for our highest hopes and the ultimate aims of all the exertions of reason. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and a limit to its extension in space; whether there exists anywhere, or perhaps, in my own thinking Self, an indivisible and indestructible unity—or whether nothing but what is divisible and transitory exists; whether I am a free agent, or, like other beings, am bound in the chains of nature and fate; whether, finally, there is a supreme cause of the world, or all our thought and speculation must end with nature and the order of external things—are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would willingly exchange his whole science; for in it there is no satisfaction for the highest aspirations and most ardent desires of humanity. Nay, it may even be said that the true value of mathematics—that pride of human reason—consists in this: that she guides reason to the knowledge of nature—in her greater as well as in her less manifestations—in her beautiful order and regularity—guides her, moreover, to an insight into the wonderful unity of the moving forces in the operations of nature, far beyond the expectations of a philosophy building only on experience; and that she thus encourages philosophy to extend the province of reason beyond all experience, and at the same time provides it with the most excellent materials for supporting its investigations, in so far as their nature admits, by adequate and accordant intuitions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","text":"Unfortunately for speculation—but perhaps fortunately for the practical interests of humanity—reason, in the midst of her highest anticipations, finds herself hemmed in by a press of opposite and contradictory conclusions, from which neither her honour nor her safety will permit her to draw back. Nor can she regard these conflicting trains of reasoning with indifference as mere passages at arms, still less can she command peace; for in the subject of the conflict she has a deep interest. There is no other course left open to her than to reflect with herself upon the origin of this disunion in reason—whether it may not arise from a mere misunderstanding. After such an inquiry, arrogant claims would have to be given up on both sides; but the sovereignty of reason over understanding and sense would be based upon a sure foundation.\n\nWe shall at present defer this radical inquiry and, in the meantime, consider for a little what side in the controversy we should most willingly take, if we were obliged to become partisans at all. As, in this case, we leave out of sight altogether the logical criterion of truth, and merely consult our own interest in reference to the question, these considerations, although inadequate to settle the question of right in either party, will enable us to comprehend how those who have taken part in the struggle, adopt the one view rather than the other—no special insight into the subject, however, having influenced their choice. They will, at the same time, explain to us many other things by the way—for example, the fiery zeal on the one side and the cold maintenance of their cause on the other; why the one party has met with the warmest approbations, and the other has always been repulsed by irreconcilable prejudices.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","text":"There is one thing, however, that determines the proper point of view, from which alone this preliminary inquiry can be instituted and carried on with the proper completeness—and that is the comparison of the principles from which both sides, thesis and antithesis, proceed. My readers would remark in the propositions of the antithesis a complete uniformity in the mode of thought and a perfect unity of principle. Its principle was that of pure empiricism, not only in the explication of the phenomena in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas, even of that of the universe itself. The affirmations of the thesis, on the contrary, were based, in addition to the empirical mode of explanation employed in the series of phenomena, on intellectual propositions; and its principles were in so far not simple. I shall term the thesis, in view of its essential characteristic, the dogmatism of pure reason.\n\nOn the side of Dogmatism, or of the thesis, therefore, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, we find:\n\n1. A practical interest, which must be very dear to every right-thinking man. That the word has a beginning—that the nature of my thinking self is simple, and therefore indestructible—that I am a free agent, and raised above the compulsion of nature and her laws—and, finally, that the entire order of things, which form the world, is dependent upon a Supreme Being, from whom the whole receives unity and connection—these are so many foundation-stones of morality and religion. The antithesis deprives us of all these supports—or, at least, seems so to deprive us.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","text":"2. A speculative interest of reason manifests itself on this side. For, if we take the transcendental ideas and employ them in the manner which the thesis directs, we can exhibit completely à priori the entire chain of conditions, and understand the derivation of the conditioned—beginning from the unconditioned. This the antithesis does not do; and for this reason does not meet with so welcome a reception. For it can give no answer to our question respecting the conditions of its synthesis—except such as must be supplemented by another question, and so on to infinity. According to it, we must rise from a given beginning to one still higher; every part conducts us to a still smaller one; every event is preceded by another event which is its cause; and the conditions of existence rest always upon other and still higher conditions, and find neither end nor basis in some self-subsistent thing as the primal being.\n\n3. This side has also the advantage of popularity; and this constitutes no small part of its claim to favour. The common understanding does not find the least difficulty in the idea of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis—accustomed, as it is, rather to follow our consequences than to seek for a proper basis for cognition. In the conception of an absolute first, moreover—the possibility of which it does not inquire into—it is highly gratified to find a firmly-established point of departure for its attempts at theory; while in the restless and continuous ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no satisfaction.\n\nOn the side of the antithesis, or Empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0006","text":"1. We cannot discover any such practical interest arising from pure principles of reason as morality and religion present. On the contrary, pure empiricism seems to empty them of all their power and influence. If there does not exist a Supreme Being distinct from the world—if the world is without beginning, consequently without a Creator—if our wills are not free, and the soul is divisible and subject to corruption just like matter—the ideas and principles of morality lose all validity and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","text":"2. But empiricism, in compensation, holds out to reason, in its speculative interests, certain important advantages, far exceeding any that the dogmatist can promise us. For, when employed by the empiricist, understanding is always upon its proper ground of investigation—the field of possible experience, the laws of which it can explore, and thus extend its cognition securely and with clear intelligence without being stopped by limits in any direction. Here can it and ought it to find and present to intuition its proper object—not only in itself, but in all its relations; or, if it employ conceptions, upon this ground it can always present the corresponding images in clear and unmistakable intuitions. It is quite unnecessary for it to renounce the guidance of nature, to attach itself to ideas, the objects of which it cannot know; because, as mere intellectual entities, they cannot be presented in any intuition. On the contrary, it is not even permitted to abandon its proper occupation, under the pretence that it has been brought to a conclusion (for it never can be), and to pass into the region of idealizing reason and transcendent conceptions, which it is not required to observe and explore the laws of nature, but merely to think and to imagine—secure from being contradicted by facts, because they have not been called as witnesses, but passed by, or perhaps subordinated to the so-called higher interests and considerations of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0008","text":"Hence the empiricist will never allow himself to accept any epoch of nature for the first—the absolutely primal state; he will not believe that there can be limits to his outlook into her wide domains, nor pass from the objects of nature, which he can satisfactorily explain by means of observation and mathematical thought—which he can determine synthetically in intuition, to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever present in concreto; he will not concede the existence of a faculty in nature, operating independently of the laws of nature—a concession which would introduce uncertainty into the procedure of the understanding, which is guided by necessary laws to the observation of phenomena; nor, finally, will he permit himself to seek a cause beyond nature, inasmuch as we know nothing but it, and from it alone receive an objective basis for all our conceptions and instruction in the unvarying laws of things.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","text":"In truth, if the empirical philosopher had no other purpose in the establishment of his antithesis than to check the presumption of a reason which mistakes its true destination, which boasts of its insight and its knowledge, just where all insight and knowledge cease to exist, and regards that which is valid only in relation to a practical interest, as an advancement of the speculative interests of the mind (in order, when it is convenient for itself, to break the thread of our physical investigations, and, under pretence of extending our cognition, connect them with transcendental ideas, by means of which we really know only that we know nothing)—if, I say, the empiricist rested satisfied with this benefit, the principle advanced by him would be a maxim recommending moderation in the pretensions of reason and modesty in its affirmations, and at the same time would direct us to the right mode of extending the province of the understanding, by the help of the only true teacher, experience. In obedience to this advice, intellectual hypotheses and faith would not be called in aid of our practical interests; nor should we introduce them under the pompous titles of science and insight. For speculative cognition cannot find an objective basis any other where than in experience; and, when we overstep its limits our synthesis, which requires ever new cognitions independent of experience, has no substratum of intuition upon which to build.\n\nBut if—as often happens—empiricism, in relation to ideas, becomes itself dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its phenomenal cognition, it falls itself into the error of intemperance—an error which is here all the more reprehensible, as thereby the practical interest of reason receives an irreparable injury.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0010","text":"And this constitutes the opposition between Epicureanism[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-57) and Platonism.\n\n[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-57) It is, however, still a matter of doubt whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as directions for the objective employment of the understanding. If, indeed, they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative exercise of reason, he gives evidence therein a more genuine philosophic spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity. That, in the explanation of phenomena, we must proceed as if the field of inquiry had neither limits in space nor commencement in time; that we must be satisfied with the teaching of experience in reference to the material of which the world is posed; that we must not look for any other mode of the origination of events than that which is determined by the unalterable laws of nature; and finally, that we not employ the hypothesis of a cause distinct from the world to account for a phenomenon or for the world itself—are principles for the extension of speculative philosophy, and the discovery of the true sources of the principles of morals, which, however little conformed to in the present day, are undoubtedly correct. At the same time, any one desirous of ignoring, in mere speculation, these dogmatical propositions, need not for that reason be accused of denying them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0011","text":"Both Epicurus and Plato assert more in their systems than they know. The former encourages and advances science—although to the prejudice of the practical; the latter presents us with excellent principles for the investigation of the practical, but, in relation to everything regarding which we can attain to speculative cognition, permits reason to append idealistic explanations of natural phenomena, to the great injury of physical investigation.\n\n3. In regard to the third motive for the preliminary choice of a party in this war of assertions, it seems very extraordinary that empiricism should be utterly unpopular. We should be inclined to believe that the common understanding would receive it with pleasure—promising as it does to satisfy it without passing the bounds of experience and its connected order; while transcendental dogmatism obliges it to rise to conceptions which far surpass the intelligence and ability of the most practised thinkers. But in this, in truth, is to be found its real motive. For the common understanding thus finds itself in a situation where not even the most learned can have the advantage of it. If it understands little or nothing about these transcendental conceptions, no one can boast of understanding any more; and although it may not express itself in so scholastically correct a manner as others, it can busy itself with reasoning and arguments without end, wandering among mere ideas, about which one can always be very eloquent, because we know nothing about them; while, in the observation and investigation of nature, it would be forced to remain dumb and to confess its utter ignorance. Thus indolence and vanity form of themselves strong recommendations of these principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0012","text":"Besides, although it is a hard thing for a philosopher to assume a principle, of which he can give to himself no reasonable account, and still more to employ conceptions, the objective reality of which cannot be established, nothing is more usual with the common understanding. It wants something which will allow it to go to work with confidence. The difficulty of even comprehending a supposition does not disquiet it, because—not knowing what comprehending means—it never even thinks of the supposition it may be adopting as a principle; and regards as known that with which it has become familiar from constant use. And, at last, all speculative interests disappear before the practical interests which it holds dear; and it fancies that it understands and knows what its necessities and hopes incite it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of transcendentally idealizing reason is robbed of all popularity; and, however prejudicial it may be to the highest practical principles, there is no fear that it will ever pass the limits of the schools, or acquire any favour or influence in society or with the multitude.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","text":"Human reason is by nature architectonic. That is to say, it regards all cognitions as parts of a possible system, and hence accepts only such principles as at least do not incapacitate a cognition to which we may have attained from being placed along with others in a general system. But the propositions of the antithesis are of a character which renders the completion of an edifice of cognitions impossible. According to these, beyond one state or epoch of the world there is always to be found one more ancient; in every part always other parts themselves divisible; preceding every event another, the origin of which must itself be sought still higher; and everything in existence is conditioned, and still not dependent on an unconditioned and primal existence. As, therefore, the antithesis will not concede the existence of a first beginning which might be available as a foundation, a complete edifice of cognition, in the presence of such hypothesis, is utterly impossible. Thus the architectonic interest of reason, which requires a unity—not empirical, but à priori and rational—forms a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis in our antinomy.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","text":"But if any one could free himself entirely from all considerations of interest, and weigh without partiality the assertions of reason, attending only to their content, irrespective of the consequences which follow from them; such a person, on the supposition that he knew no other way out of the confusion than to settle the truth of one or other of the conflicting doctrines, would live in a state of continual hesitation. Today, he would feel convinced that the human will is free; to-morrow, considering the indissoluble chain of nature, he would look on freedom as a mere illusion and declare nature to be all-in-all. But, if he were called to action, the play of the merely speculative reason would disappear like the shapes of a dream, and practical interest would dictate his choice of principles. But, as it well befits a reflective and inquiring being to devote certain periods of time to the examination of its own reason—to divest itself of all partiality, and frankly to communicate its observations for the judgement and opinion of others; so no one can be blamed for, much less prevented from, placing both parties on their trial, with permission to end themselves, free from intimidation, before intimidation, before a sworn jury of equal condition with themselves—the condition of weak and fallible men.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"72a4e7532d95f8bedcda48710e94df41504e0b1e78972333c86c0ee0652f1f3c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","order":65,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SINI","text":"### Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems\n\nTo avow an ability to solve all problems and to answer all questions would be a profession certain to convict any philosopher of extravagant boasting and self-conceit, and at once to destroy the confidence that might otherwise have been reposed in him. There are, however, sciences so constituted that every question arising within their sphere must necessarily be capable of receiving an answer from the knowledge already possessed, for the answer must be received from the same sources whence the question arose. In such sciences it is not allowable to excuse ourselves on the plea of necessary and unavoidable ignorance; a solution is absolutely requisite. The rule of right and wrong must help us to the knowledge of what is right or wrong in all possible cases; otherwise, the idea of obligation or duty would be utterly null, for we cannot have any obligation to that which we cannot know. On the other hand, in our investigations of the phenomena of nature, much must remain uncertain, and many questions continue insoluble; because what we know of nature is far from being sufficient to explain all the phenomena that are presented to our observation. Now the question is: Whether there is in transcendental philosophy any question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is unanswerable by this reason; and whether we must regard the subject of the question as quite uncertain, so far as our knowledge extends, and must give it a place among those subjects, of which we have just so much conception as is sufficient to enable us to raise a question—faculty or materials failing us, however, when we attempt an answer.\n\nNow I maintain that, among all speculative cognition, the peculiarity of transcendental philosophy is that there is no question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is insoluble by this reason; and that the profession of unavoidable ignorance—the problem being alleged to be beyond the reach of our faculties—cannot free us from the obligation to present a complete and satisfactory answer. For the very conception which enables us to raise the question must give us the power of answering it; inasmuch as the object, as in the case of right and wrong, is not to be discovered out of the conception.\n\nBut, in transcendental philosophy, it is only the cosmological questions to which we can demand a satisfactory answer in relation to the constitution of their object; and the philosopher is not permitted to avail himself of the pretext of necessary ignorance and impenetrable obscurity. These questions relate solely to the cosmological ideas. For the object must be given in experience, and the question relates to the adequateness of the object to an idea. If the object is transcendental and therefore itself unknown; if the question, for example, is whether the object—the something, the phenomenon of which (internal—in ourselves) is thought—that is to say, the soul, is in itself a simple being; or whether there is a cause of all things, which is absolutely necessary—in such cases we are seeking for our idea an object, of which we may confess that it is unknown to us, though we must not on that account assert that it is impossible.[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-58) The cosmological ideas alone posses the peculiarity that we can presuppose the object of them and the empirical synthesis requisite for the conception of that object to be given; and the question, which arises from these ideas, relates merely to the progress of this synthesis, in so far as it must contain absolute totality—which, however, is not empirical, as it cannot be given in any experience. Now, as the question here is solely in regard to a thing as the object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendental cosmological question need not be sought out of the idea, for the question does not regard an object in itself. The question in relation to a possible experience is not, “What can be given in an experience in concreto” but “what is contained in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis must approximate.” The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone. For the idea is a creation of reason itself, which therefore cannot disclaim the obligation to answer or refer us to the unknown object.\n\n[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-58) The question, “What is the constitution of a transcendental object?” is unanswerable—we are unable to say what it is; but we can perceive that the question itself is nothing; because it does not relate to any object that can be presented to us. For this reason, we must consider all the questions raised in transcendental psychology as answerable and as really answered; for they relate to the transcendental subject of all internal phenomena, which is not itself phenomenon and consequently not given as an object, in which, moreover, none of the categories—and it is to them that the question is properly directed—find any conditions of its application. Here, therefore, is a case where no answer is the only proper answer. For a question regarding the constitution of a something which cannot be cogitated by any determined predicate, being completely beyond the sphere of objects and experience, is perfectly null and void.\n\nIt is not so extraordinary, as it at first sight appears, that a science should demand and expect satisfactory answers to all the questions that may arise within its own sphere (questiones domesticae), although, up to a certain time, these answers may not have been discovered. There are, in addition to transcendental philosophy, only two pure sciences of reason; the one with a speculative, the other with a practical content—pure mathematics and pure ethics. Has any one ever heard it alleged that, from our complete and necessary ignorance of the conditions, it is uncertain what exact relation the diameter of a circle bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? By the former the sum cannot be given exactly, by the latter only approximately; and therefore we decide that the impossibility of a solution of the question is evident. Lambert presented us with a demonstration of this. In the general principles of morals there can be nothing uncertain, for the propositions are either utterly without meaning, or must originate solely in our rational conceptions. On the other hand, there must be in physical science an infinite number of conjectures, which can never become certainties; because the phenomena of nature are not given as objects dependent on our conceptions. The key to the solution of such questions cannot, therefore, be found in our conceptions, or in pure thought, but must lie without us and for that reason is in many cases not to be discovered; and consequently a satisfactory explanation cannot be expected. The questions of transcendental analytic, which relate to the deduction of our pure cognition, are not to be regarded as of the same kind as those mentioned above; for we are not at present treating of the certainty of judgements in relation to the origin of our conceptions, but only of that certainty in relation to objects.\n\nWe cannot, therefore, escape the responsibility of at least a critical solution of the questions of reason, by complaints of the limited nature of our faculties, and the seemingly humble confession that it is beyond the power of our reason to decide, whether the world has existed from all eternity or had a beginning—whether it is infinitely extended, or enclosed within certain limits—whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be capable of infinite divisibility—whether freedom can originate phenomena, or whether everything is absolutely dependent on the laws and order of nature—and, finally, whether there exists a being that is completely unconditioned and necessary, or whether the existence of everything is conditioned and consequently dependent on something external to itself, and therefore in its own nature contingent. For all these questions relate to an object, which can be given nowhere else than in thought. This object is the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of phenomena. If the conceptions in our minds do not assist us to some certain result in regard to these problems, we must not defend ourselves on the plea that the object itself remains hidden from and unknown to us. For no such thing or object can be given—it is not to be found out of the idea in our minds. We must seek the cause of our failure in our idea itself, which is an insoluble problem and in regard to which we obstinately assume that there exists a real object corresponding and adequate to it. A clear explanation of the dialectic which lies in our conception, will very soon enable us to come to a satisfactory decision in regard to such a question.\n\nThe pretext that we are unable to arrive at certainty in regard to these problems may be met with this question, which requires at least a plain answer: “From what source do the ideas originate, the solution of which involves you in such difficulties? Are you seeking for an explanation of certain phenomena; and do you expect these ideas to give you the principles or the rules of this explanation?” Let it be granted, that all nature was laid open before you; that nothing was hid from your senses and your consciousness. Still, you could not cognize in concreto the object of your ideas in any experience. For what is demanded is not only this full and complete intuition, but also a complete synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality; and this is not possible by means of any empirical cognition. It follows that your question—your idea—is by no means necessary for the explanation of any phenomenon; and the idea cannot have been in any sense given by the object itself. For such an object can never be presented to us, because it cannot be given by any possible experience. Whatever perceptions you may attain to, you are still surrounded by conditions—in space, or in time—and you cannot discover anything unconditioned; nor can you decide whether this unconditioned is to be placed in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without beginning. A whole, in the empirical signification of the term, is always merely comparative. The absolute whole of quantity (the universe), of division, of derivation, of the condition of existence, with the question—whether it is to be produced by finite or infinite synthesis, no possible experience can instruct us concerning. You will not, for example, be able to explain the phenomena of a body in the least degree better, whether you believe it to consist of simple, or of composite parts; for a simple phenomenon—and just as little an infinite series of composition—can never be presented to your perception. Phenomena require and admit of explanation, only in so far as the conditions of that explanation are given in perception; but the sum total of that which is given in phenomena, considered as an absolute whole, is itself a perception—and we cannot therefore seek for explanations of this whole beyond itself, in other perceptions. The explanation of this whole is the proper object of the transcendental problems of pure reason.\n\nAlthough, therefore, the solution of these problems is unattainable through experience, we must not permit ourselves to say that it is uncertain how the object of our inquiries is constituted. For the object is in our own mind and cannot be discovered in experience; and we have only to take care that our thoughts are consistent with each other, and to avoid falling into the amphiboly of regarding our idea as a representation of an object empirically given, and therefore to be cognized according to the laws of experience. A dogmatical solution is therefore not only unsatisfactory but impossible. The critical solution, which may be a perfectly certain one, does not consider the question objectively, but proceeds by inquiring into the basis of the cognition upon which the question rests.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","text":"### Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems\n\nTo avow an ability to solve all problems and to answer all questions would be a profession certain to convict any philosopher of extravagant boasting and self-conceit, and at once to destroy the confidence that might otherwise have been reposed in him. There are, however, sciences so constituted that every question arising within their sphere must necessarily be capable of receiving an answer from the knowledge already possessed, for the answer must be received from the same sources whence the question arose. In such sciences it is not allowable to excuse ourselves on the plea of necessary and unavoidable ignorance; a solution is absolutely requisite. The rule of right and wrong must help us to the knowledge of what is right or wrong in all possible cases; otherwise, the idea of obligation or duty would be utterly null, for we cannot have any obligation to that which we cannot know. On the other hand, in our investigations of the phenomena of nature, much must remain uncertain, and many questions continue insoluble; because what we know of nature is far from being sufficient to explain all the phenomena that are presented to our observation. Now the question is: Whether there is in transcendental philosophy any question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is unanswerable by this reason; and whether we must regard the subject of the question as quite uncertain, so far as our knowledge extends, and must give it a place among those subjects, of which we have just so much conception as is sufficient to enable us to raise a question—faculty or materials failing us, however, when we attempt an answer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","text":"Now I maintain that, among all speculative cognition, the peculiarity of transcendental philosophy is that there is no question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is insoluble by this reason; and that the profession of unavoidable ignorance—the problem being alleged to be beyond the reach of our faculties—cannot free us from the obligation to present a complete and satisfactory answer. For the very conception which enables us to raise the question must give us the power of answering it; inasmuch as the object, as in the case of right and wrong, is not to be discovered out of the conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","text":"But, in transcendental philosophy, it is only the cosmological questions to which we can demand a satisfactory answer in relation to the constitution of their object; and the philosopher is not permitted to avail himself of the pretext of necessary ignorance and impenetrable obscurity. These questions relate solely to the cosmological ideas. For the object must be given in experience, and the question relates to the adequateness of the object to an idea. If the object is transcendental and therefore itself unknown; if the question, for example, is whether the object—the something, the phenomenon of which (internal—in ourselves) is thought—that is to say, the soul, is in itself a simple being; or whether there is a cause of all things, which is absolutely necessary—in such cases we are seeking for our idea an object, of which we may confess that it is unknown to us, though we must not on that account assert that it is impossible.[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-58) The cosmological ideas alone posses the peculiarity that we can presuppose the object of them and the empirical synthesis requisite for the conception of that object to be given; and the question, which arises from these ideas, relates merely to the progress of this synthesis, in so far as it must contain absolute totality—which, however, is not empirical, as it cannot be given in any experience. Now, as the question here is solely in regard to a thing as the object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendental cosmological question need not be sought out of the idea, for the question does not regard an object in itself. The question in relation to a possible experience is not, “What can be given in an experience in concreto” but “what is contained in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis must approximate.” The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone. For the idea is a creation of reason itself, which therefore cannot disclaim the obligation to answer or refer us to the unknown object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","text":"[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-58) The question, “What is the constitution of a transcendental object?” is unanswerable—we are unable to say what it is; but we can perceive that the question itself is nothing; because it does not relate to any object that can be presented to us. For this reason, we must consider all the questions raised in transcendental psychology as answerable and as really answered; for they relate to the transcendental subject of all internal phenomena, which is not itself phenomenon and consequently not given as an object, in which, moreover, none of the categories—and it is to them that the question is properly directed—find any conditions of its application. Here, therefore, is a case where no answer is the only proper answer. For a question regarding the constitution of a something which cannot be cogitated by any determined predicate, being completely beyond the sphere of objects and experience, is perfectly null and void.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0005","text":"It is not so extraordinary, as it at first sight appears, that a science should demand and expect satisfactory answers to all the questions that may arise within its own sphere (questiones domesticae), although, up to a certain time, these answers may not have been discovered. There are, in addition to transcendental philosophy, only two pure sciences of reason; the one with a speculative, the other with a practical content—pure mathematics and pure ethics. Has any one ever heard it alleged that, from our complete and necessary ignorance of the conditions, it is uncertain what exact relation the diameter of a circle bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? By the former the sum cannot be given exactly, by the latter only approximately; and therefore we decide that the impossibility of a solution of the question is evident. Lambert presented us with a demonstration of this. In the general principles of morals there can be nothing uncertain, for the propositions are either utterly without meaning, or must originate solely in our rational conceptions. On the other hand, there must be in physical science an infinite number of conjectures, which can never become certainties; because the phenomena of nature are not given as objects dependent on our conceptions. The key to the solution of such questions cannot, therefore, be found in our conceptions, or in pure thought, but must lie without us and for that reason is in many cases not to be discovered; and consequently a satisfactory explanation cannot be expected. The questions of transcendental analytic, which relate to the deduction of our pure cognition, are not to be regarded as of the same kind as those mentioned above; for we are not at present treating of the certainty of judgements in relation to the origin of our conceptions, but only of that certainty in relation to objects.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","text":"We cannot, therefore, escape the responsibility of at least a critical solution of the questions of reason, by complaints of the limited nature of our faculties, and the seemingly humble confession that it is beyond the power of our reason to decide, whether the world has existed from all eternity or had a beginning—whether it is infinitely extended, or enclosed within certain limits—whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be capable of infinite divisibility—whether freedom can originate phenomena, or whether everything is absolutely dependent on the laws and order of nature—and, finally, whether there exists a being that is completely unconditioned and necessary, or whether the existence of everything is conditioned and consequently dependent on something external to itself, and therefore in its own nature contingent. For all these questions relate to an object, which can be given nowhere else than in thought. This object is the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of phenomena. If the conceptions in our minds do not assist us to some certain result in regard to these problems, we must not defend ourselves on the plea that the object itself remains hidden from and unknown to us. For no such thing or object can be given—it is not to be found out of the idea in our minds. We must seek the cause of our failure in our idea itself, which is an insoluble problem and in regard to which we obstinately assume that there exists a real object corresponding and adequate to it. A clear explanation of the dialectic which lies in our conception, will very soon enable us to come to a satisfactory decision in regard to such a question.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","text":"The pretext that we are unable to arrive at certainty in regard to these problems may be met with this question, which requires at least a plain answer: “From what source do the ideas originate, the solution of which involves you in such difficulties? Are you seeking for an explanation of certain phenomena; and do you expect these ideas to give you the principles or the rules of this explanation?” Let it be granted, that all nature was laid open before you; that nothing was hid from your senses and your consciousness. Still, you could not cognize in concreto the object of your ideas in any experience. For what is demanded is not only this full and complete intuition, but also a complete synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality; and this is not possible by means of any empirical cognition. It follows that your question—your idea—is by no means necessary for the explanation of any phenomenon; and the idea cannot have been in any sense given by the object itself. For such an object can never be presented to us, because it cannot be given by any possible experience. Whatever perceptions you may attain to, you are still surrounded by conditions—in space, or in time—and you cannot discover anything unconditioned; nor can you decide whether this unconditioned is to be placed in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without beginning. A whole, in the empirical signification of the term, is always merely comparative. The absolute whole of quantity (the universe), of division, of derivation, of the condition of existence, with the question—whether it is to be produced by finite or infinite synthesis, no possible experience can instruct us concerning. You will not, for example, be able to explain the phenomena of a body in the least degree better, whether you believe it to consist of simple, or of composite parts; for a simple phenomenon—and just as little an infinite series of composition—can never be presented to your perception. Phenomena require and admit of explanation, only in so far as the conditions of that explanation are given in perception; but the sum total of that which is given in phenomena, considered as an absolute whole, is itself a perception—and we cannot therefore seek for explanations of this whole beyond itself, in other perceptions. The explanation of this whole is the proper object of the transcendental problems of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","text":"Although, therefore, the solution of these problems is unattainable through experience, we must not permit ourselves to say that it is uncertain how the object of our inquiries is constituted. For the object is in our own mind and cannot be discovered in experience; and we have only to take care that our thoughts are consistent with each other, and to avoid falling into the amphiboly of regarding our idea as a representation of an object empirically given, and therefore to be cognized according to the laws of experience. A dogmatical solution is therefore not only unsatisfactory but impossible. The critical solution, which may be a perfectly certain one, does not consider the question objectively, but proceeds by inquiring into the basis of the cognition upon which the question rests.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"93954f159052836ebdf511adb0538f04d372cfee2729072c160d5b147bdc078b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","order":66,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVSE","text":"### Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe should be quite willing to desist from the demand of a dogmatical answer to our questions, if we understood beforehand that, be the answer what it may, it would only serve to increase our ignorance, to throw us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into another still greater, and perhaps lead us into irreconcilable contradictions. If a dogmatical affirmative or negative answer is demanded, is it at all prudent to set aside the probable grounds of a solution which lie before us and to take into consideration what advantage we shall gain, if the answer is to favour the one side or the other? If it happens that in both cases the answer is mere nonsense, we have in this an irresistible summons to institute a critical investigation of the question, for the purpose of discovering whether it is based on a groundless presupposition and relates to an idea, the falsity of which would be more easily exposed in its application and consequences than in the mere representation of its content. This is the great utility of the sceptical mode of treating the questions addressed by pure reason to itself. By this method we easily rid ourselves of the confusions of dogmatism, and establish in its place a temperate criticism, which, as a genuine cathartic, will successfully remove the presumptuous notions of philosophy and their consequence—the vain pretension to universal science.\n\nIf, then, I could understand the nature of a cosmological idea and perceive, before I entered on the discussion of the subject at all, that, whatever side of the question regarding the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of phenomena it favoured—it must either be too great or too small for every conception of the understanding—I would be able to comprehend how the idea, which relates to an object of experience—an experience which must be adequate to and in accordance with a possible conception of the understanding—must be completely void and without significance, inasmuch as its object is inadequate, consider it as we may. And this is actually the case with all cosmological conceptions, which, for the reason above mentioned, involve reason, so long as it remains attached to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For suppose:\n\nFirst, that the world has no beginning—in this case it is too large for our conception; for this conception, which consists in a successive regress, cannot overtake the whole eternity that has elapsed. Grant that it has a beginning, it is then too small for the conception of the understanding. For, as a beginning presupposes a time preceding, it cannot be unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding imposes the necessity of looking for a higher condition of time; and the world is, therefore, evidently too small for this law.\n\nThe same is the case with the double answer to the question regarding the extent, in space, of the world. For, if it is infinite and unlimited, it must be too large for every possible empirical conception. If it is finite and limited, we have a right to ask: “What determines these limits?” Void space is not a self-subsistent correlate of things, and cannot be a final condition—and still less an empirical condition, forming a part of a possible experience. For how can we have any experience or perception of an absolute void? But the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis requires that the unconditioned be an empirical conception. Consequently, a finite world is too small for our conception.\n\nSecondly, if every phenomenon (matter) in space consists of an infinite number of parts, the regress of the division is always too great for our conception; and if the division of space must cease with some member of the division (the simple), it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For the member at which we have discontinued our division still admits a regress to many more parts contained in the object.\n\nThirdly, suppose that every event in the world happens in accordance with the laws of nature; the causality of a cause must itself be an event and necessitates a regress to a still higher cause, and consequently the unceasing prolongation of the series of conditions a parte priori. Operative nature is therefore too large for every conception we can form in the synthesis of cosmical events.\n\nIf we admit the existence of spontaneously produced events, that is, of free agency, we are driven, in our search for sufficient reasons, on an unavoidable law of nature and are compelled to appeal to the empirical law of causality, and we find that any such totality of connection in our synthesis is too small for our necessary empirical conception.\n\nFourthly, if we assume the existence of an absolutely necessary being—whether it be the world or something in the world, or the cause of the world—we must place it in a time at an infinite distance from any given moment; for, otherwise, it must be dependent on some other and higher existence. Such an existence is, in this case, too large for our empirical conception, and unattainable by the continued regress of any synthesis.\n\nBut if we believe that everything in the world—be it condition or conditioned—is contingent; every given existence is too small for our conception. For in this case we are compelled to seek for some other existence upon which the former depends.\n\nWe have said that in all these cases the cosmological idea is either too great or too small for the empirical regress in a synthesis, and consequently for every possible conception of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves in a manner exactly the reverse of this and, instead of accusing the cosmological idea of over stepping or of falling short of its true aim, possible experience, say that, in the first case, the empirical conception is always too small for the idea, and in the second too great, and thus attach the blame of these contradictions to the empirical regress? The reason is this. Possible experience can alone give reality to our conceptions; without it a conception is merely an idea, without truth or relation to an object. Hence a possible empirical conception must be the standard by which we are to judge whether an idea is anything more than an idea and fiction of thought, or whether it relates to an object in the world. If we say of a thing that in relation to some other thing it is too large or too small, the former is considered as existing for the sake of the latter, and requiring to be adapted to it. Among the trivial subjects of discussion in the old schools of dialectics was this question: “If a ball cannot pass through a hole, shall we say that the ball is too large or the hole too small?” In this case it is indifferent what expression we employ; for we do not know which exists for the sake of the other. On the other hand, we cannot say: “The man is too long for his coat”; but: “The coat is too short for the man.”\n\nWe are thus led to the well-founded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and all the conflicting sophistical assertions connected with them, are based upon a false and fictitious conception of the mode in which the object of these ideas is presented to us; and this suspicion will probably direct us how to expose the illusion that has so long led us astray from the truth.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","text":"### Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe should be quite willing to desist from the demand of a dogmatical answer to our questions, if we understood beforehand that, be the answer what it may, it would only serve to increase our ignorance, to throw us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into another still greater, and perhaps lead us into irreconcilable contradictions. If a dogmatical affirmative or negative answer is demanded, is it at all prudent to set aside the probable grounds of a solution which lie before us and to take into consideration what advantage we shall gain, if the answer is to favour the one side or the other? If it happens that in both cases the answer is mere nonsense, we have in this an irresistible summons to institute a critical investigation of the question, for the purpose of discovering whether it is based on a groundless presupposition and relates to an idea, the falsity of which would be more easily exposed in its application and consequences than in the mere representation of its content. This is the great utility of the sceptical mode of treating the questions addressed by pure reason to itself. By this method we easily rid ourselves of the confusions of dogmatism, and establish in its place a temperate criticism, which, as a genuine cathartic, will successfully remove the presumptuous notions of philosophy and their consequence—the vain pretension to universal science.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","text":"If, then, I could understand the nature of a cosmological idea and perceive, before I entered on the discussion of the subject at all, that, whatever side of the question regarding the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of phenomena it favoured—it must either be too great or too small for every conception of the understanding—I would be able to comprehend how the idea, which relates to an object of experience—an experience which must be adequate to and in accordance with a possible conception of the understanding—must be completely void and without significance, inasmuch as its object is inadequate, consider it as we may. And this is actually the case with all cosmological conceptions, which, for the reason above mentioned, involve reason, so long as it remains attached to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For suppose:\n\nFirst, that the world has no beginning—in this case it is too large for our conception; for this conception, which consists in a successive regress, cannot overtake the whole eternity that has elapsed. Grant that it has a beginning, it is then too small for the conception of the understanding. For, as a beginning presupposes a time preceding, it cannot be unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding imposes the necessity of looking for a higher condition of time; and the world is, therefore, evidently too small for this law.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","text":"The same is the case with the double answer to the question regarding the extent, in space, of the world. For, if it is infinite and unlimited, it must be too large for every possible empirical conception. If it is finite and limited, we have a right to ask: “What determines these limits?” Void space is not a self-subsistent correlate of things, and cannot be a final condition—and still less an empirical condition, forming a part of a possible experience. For how can we have any experience or perception of an absolute void? But the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis requires that the unconditioned be an empirical conception. Consequently, a finite world is too small for our conception.\n\nSecondly, if every phenomenon (matter) in space consists of an infinite number of parts, the regress of the division is always too great for our conception; and if the division of space must cease with some member of the division (the simple), it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For the member at which we have discontinued our division still admits a regress to many more parts contained in the object.\n\nThirdly, suppose that every event in the world happens in accordance with the laws of nature; the causality of a cause must itself be an event and necessitates a regress to a still higher cause, and consequently the unceasing prolongation of the series of conditions a parte priori. Operative nature is therefore too large for every conception we can form in the synthesis of cosmical events.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","text":"If we admit the existence of spontaneously produced events, that is, of free agency, we are driven, in our search for sufficient reasons, on an unavoidable law of nature and are compelled to appeal to the empirical law of causality, and we find that any such totality of connection in our synthesis is too small for our necessary empirical conception.\n\nFourthly, if we assume the existence of an absolutely necessary being—whether it be the world or something in the world, or the cause of the world—we must place it in a time at an infinite distance from any given moment; for, otherwise, it must be dependent on some other and higher existence. Such an existence is, in this case, too large for our empirical conception, and unattainable by the continued regress of any synthesis.\n\nBut if we believe that everything in the world—be it condition or conditioned—is contingent; every given existence is too small for our conception. For in this case we are compelled to seek for some other existence upon which the former depends.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","text":"We have said that in all these cases the cosmological idea is either too great or too small for the empirical regress in a synthesis, and consequently for every possible conception of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves in a manner exactly the reverse of this and, instead of accusing the cosmological idea of over stepping or of falling short of its true aim, possible experience, say that, in the first case, the empirical conception is always too small for the idea, and in the second too great, and thus attach the blame of these contradictions to the empirical regress? The reason is this. Possible experience can alone give reality to our conceptions; without it a conception is merely an idea, without truth or relation to an object. Hence a possible empirical conception must be the standard by which we are to judge whether an idea is anything more than an idea and fiction of thought, or whether it relates to an object in the world. If we say of a thing that in relation to some other thing it is too large or too small, the former is considered as existing for the sake of the latter, and requiring to be adapted to it. Among the trivial subjects of discussion in the old schools of dialectics was this question: “If a ball cannot pass through a hole, shall we say that the ball is too large or the hole too small?” In this case it is indifferent what expression we employ; for we do not know which exists for the sake of the other. On the other hand, we cannot say: “The man is too long for his coat”; but: “The coat is too short for the man.”\n\nWe are thus led to the well-founded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and all the conflicting sophistical assertions connected with them, are based upon a false and fictitious conception of the mode in which the object of these ideas is presented to us; and this suspicion will probably direct us how to expose the illusion that has so long led us astray from the truth.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e654dc51c08cf8d90a41cb245235091c7e408151ee54f9f69891e5595eaa341a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","order":67,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVTI","text":"### Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic\n\nIn the transcendental æsthetic we proved that everything intuited in space and time, all objects of a possible experience, are nothing but phenomena, that is, mere representations; and that these, as presented to us—as extended bodies, or as series of changes—have no self-subsistent existence apart from human thought. This doctrine I call Transcendental Idealism.[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-59) The realist in the transcendental sense regards these modifications of our sensibility, these mere representations, as things subsisting in themselves.\n\n[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-59) I have elsewhere termed this theory formal idealism, to distinguish it from material idealism, which doubts or denies the existence of external things. To avoid ambiguity, it seems advisable in many cases to employ this term instead of that mentioned in the text.\n\nIt would be unjust to accuse us of holding the long-decried theory of empirical idealism, which, while admitting the reality of space, denies, or at least doubts, the existence of bodies extended in it, and thus leaves us without a sufficient criterion of reality and illusion. The supporters of this theory find no difficulty in admitting the reality of the phenomena of the internal sense in time; nay, they go the length of maintaining that this internal experience is of itself a sufficient proof of the real existence of its object as a thing in itself.\n\nTranscendental idealism allows that the objects of external intuition—as intuited in space, and all changes in time—as represented by the internal sense, are real. For, as space is the form of that intuition which we call external, and, without objects in space, no empirical representation could be given us, we can and ought to regard extended bodies in it as real. The case is the same with representations in time. But time and space, with all phenomena therein, are not in themselves things. They are nothing but representations and cannot exist out of and apart from the mind. Nay, the sensuous internal intuition of the mind (as the object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented by the succession of different states in time, is not the real, proper self, as it exists in itself—not the transcendental subject—but only a phenomenon, which is presented to the sensibility of this, to us, unknown being. This internal phenomenon cannot be admitted to be a self-subsisting thing; for its condition is time, and time cannot be the condition of a thing in itself. But the empirical truth of phenomena in space and time is guaranteed beyond the possibility of doubt, and sufficiently distinguished from the illusion of dreams or fancy—although both have a proper and thorough connection in an experience according to empirical laws. The objects of experience then are not things in themselves, but are given only in experience, and have no existence apart from and independently of experience. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no one has ever observed them, must certainly be admitted; but this assertion means only, that we may in the possible progress of experience discover them at some future time. For that which stands in connection with a perception according to the laws of the progress of experience is real. They are therefore really existent, if they stand in empirical connection with my actual or real consciousness, although they are not in themselves real, that is, apart from the progress of experience.\n\nThere is nothing actually given—we can be conscious of nothing as real, except a perception and the empirical progression from it to other possible perceptions. For phenomena, as mere representations, are real only in perception; and perception is, in fact, nothing but the reality of an empirical representation, that is, a phenomenon. To call a phenomenon a real thing prior to perception means either that we must meet with this phenomenon in the progress of experience, or it means nothing at all. For I can say only of a thing in itself that it exists without relation to the senses and experience. But we are speaking here merely of phenomena in space and time, both of which are determinations of sensibility, and not of things in themselves. It follows that phenomena are not things in themselves, but are mere representations, which if not given in us—in perception—are non-existent.\n\nThe faculty of sensuous intuition is properly a receptivity—a capacity of being affected in a certain manner by representations, the relation of which to each other is a pure intuition of space and time—the pure forms of sensibility. These representations, in so far as they are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The non-sensuous cause of these representations is completely unknown to us and hence cannot be intuited as an object. For such an object could not be represented either in space or in time; and without these conditions intuition or representation is impossible. We may, at the same time, term the non-sensuous cause of phenomena the transcendental object—but merely as a mental correlate to sensibility, considered as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we may attribute the whole connection and extent of our possible perceptions, and say that it is given and exists in itself prior to all experience. But the phenomena, corresponding to it, are not given as things in themselves, but in experience alone. For they are mere representations, receiving from perceptions alone significance and relation to a real object, under the condition that this or that perception—indicating an object—is in complete connection with all others in accordance with the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say: “The things that really existed in past time are given in the transcendental object of experience.” But these are to me real objects, only in so far as I can represent to my own mind, that a regressive series of possible perceptions—following the indications of history, or the footsteps of cause and effect—in accordance with empirical laws—that, in one word, the course of the world conducts us to an elapsed series of time as the condition of the present time. This series in past time is represented as real, not in itself, but only in connection with a possible experience. Thus, when I say that certain events occurred in past time, I merely assert the possibility of prolonging the chain of experience, from the present perception, upwards to the conditions that determine it according to time.\n\nIf I represent to myself all objects existing in all space and time, I do not thereby place these in space and time prior to all experience; on the contrary, such a representation is nothing more than the notion of a possible experience, in its absolute completeness. In experience alone are those objects, which are nothing but representations, given. But, when I say they existed prior to my experience, this means only that I must begin with the perception present to me and follow the track indicated until I discover them in some part or region of experience. The cause of the empirical condition of this progression—and consequently at what member therein I must stop, and at what point in the regress I am to find this member—is transcendental, and hence necessarily incognizable. But with this we have not to do; our concern is only with the law of progression in experience, in which objects, that is, phenomena, are given. It is a matter of indifference, whether I say, “I may in the progress of experience discover stars, at a hundred times greater distance than the most distant of those now visible,” or, “Stars at this distance may be met in space, although no one has, or ever will discover them.” For, if they are given as things in themselves, without any relation to possible experience, they are for me non-existent, consequently, are not objects, for they are not contained in the regressive series of experience. But, if these phenomena must be employed in the construction or support of the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when we are discussing a question that oversteps the limits of possible experience, the proper distinction of the different theories of the reality of sensuous objects is of great importance, in order to avoid the illusion which must necessarily arise from the misinterpretation of our empirical conceptions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","text":"### Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic\n\nIn the transcendental æsthetic we proved that everything intuited in space and time, all objects of a possible experience, are nothing but phenomena, that is, mere representations; and that these, as presented to us—as extended bodies, or as series of changes—have no self-subsistent existence apart from human thought. This doctrine I call Transcendental Idealism.[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-59) The realist in the transcendental sense regards these modifications of our sensibility, these mere representations, as things subsisting in themselves.\n\n[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-59) I have elsewhere termed this theory formal idealism, to distinguish it from material idealism, which doubts or denies the existence of external things. To avoid ambiguity, it seems advisable in many cases to employ this term instead of that mentioned in the text.\n\nIt would be unjust to accuse us of holding the long-decried theory of empirical idealism, which, while admitting the reality of space, denies, or at least doubts, the existence of bodies extended in it, and thus leaves us without a sufficient criterion of reality and illusion. The supporters of this theory find no difficulty in admitting the reality of the phenomena of the internal sense in time; nay, they go the length of maintaining that this internal experience is of itself a sufficient proof of the real existence of its object as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","text":"Transcendental idealism allows that the objects of external intuition—as intuited in space, and all changes in time—as represented by the internal sense, are real. For, as space is the form of that intuition which we call external, and, without objects in space, no empirical representation could be given us, we can and ought to regard extended bodies in it as real. The case is the same with representations in time. But time and space, with all phenomena therein, are not in themselves things. They are nothing but representations and cannot exist out of and apart from the mind. Nay, the sensuous internal intuition of the mind (as the object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented by the succession of different states in time, is not the real, proper self, as it exists in itself—not the transcendental subject—but only a phenomenon, which is presented to the sensibility of this, to us, unknown being. This internal phenomenon cannot be admitted to be a self-subsisting thing; for its condition is time, and time cannot be the condition of a thing in itself. But the empirical truth of phenomena in space and time is guaranteed beyond the possibility of doubt, and sufficiently distinguished from the illusion of dreams or fancy—although both have a proper and thorough connection in an experience according to empirical laws. The objects of experience then are not things in themselves, but are given only in experience, and have no existence apart from and independently of experience. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no one has ever observed them, must certainly be admitted; but this assertion means only, that we may in the possible progress of experience discover them at some future time. For that which stands in connection with a perception according to the laws of the progress of experience is real. They are therefore really existent, if they stand in empirical connection with my actual or real consciousness, although they are not in themselves real, that is, apart from the progress of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0003","text":"There is nothing actually given—we can be conscious of nothing as real, except a perception and the empirical progression from it to other possible perceptions. For phenomena, as mere representations, are real only in perception; and perception is, in fact, nothing but the reality of an empirical representation, that is, a phenomenon. To call a phenomenon a real thing prior to perception means either that we must meet with this phenomenon in the progress of experience, or it means nothing at all. For I can say only of a thing in itself that it exists without relation to the senses and experience. But we are speaking here merely of phenomena in space and time, both of which are determinations of sensibility, and not of things in themselves. It follows that phenomena are not things in themselves, but are mere representations, which if not given in us—in perception—are non-existent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","text":"The faculty of sensuous intuition is properly a receptivity—a capacity of being affected in a certain manner by representations, the relation of which to each other is a pure intuition of space and time—the pure forms of sensibility. These representations, in so far as they are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The non-sensuous cause of these representations is completely unknown to us and hence cannot be intuited as an object. For such an object could not be represented either in space or in time; and without these conditions intuition or representation is impossible. We may, at the same time, term the non-sensuous cause of phenomena the transcendental object—but merely as a mental correlate to sensibility, considered as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we may attribute the whole connection and extent of our possible perceptions, and say that it is given and exists in itself prior to all experience. But the phenomena, corresponding to it, are not given as things in themselves, but in experience alone. For they are mere representations, receiving from perceptions alone significance and relation to a real object, under the condition that this or that perception—indicating an object—is in complete connection with all others in accordance with the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say: “The things that really existed in past time are given in the transcendental object of experience.” But these are to me real objects, only in so far as I can represent to my own mind, that a regressive series of possible perceptions—following the indications of history, or the footsteps of cause and effect—in accordance with empirical laws—that, in one word, the course of the world conducts us to an elapsed series of time as the condition of the present time. This series in past time is represented as real, not in itself, but only in connection with a possible experience. Thus, when I say that certain events occurred in past time, I merely assert the possibility of prolonging the chain of experience, from the present perception, upwards to the conditions that determine it according to time.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","text":"If I represent to myself all objects existing in all space and time, I do not thereby place these in space and time prior to all experience; on the contrary, such a representation is nothing more than the notion of a possible experience, in its absolute completeness. In experience alone are those objects, which are nothing but representations, given. But, when I say they existed prior to my experience, this means only that I must begin with the perception present to me and follow the track indicated until I discover them in some part or region of experience. The cause of the empirical condition of this progression—and consequently at what member therein I must stop, and at what point in the regress I am to find this member—is transcendental, and hence necessarily incognizable. But with this we have not to do; our concern is only with the law of progression in experience, in which objects, that is, phenomena, are given. It is a matter of indifference, whether I say, “I may in the progress of experience discover stars, at a hundred times greater distance than the most distant of those now visible,” or, “Stars at this distance may be met in space, although no one has, or ever will discover them.” For, if they are given as things in themselves, without any relation to possible experience, they are for me non-existent, consequently, are not objects, for they are not contained in the regressive series of experience. But, if these phenomena must be employed in the construction or support of the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when we are discussing a question that oversteps the limits of possible experience, the proper distinction of the different theories of the reality of sensuous objects is of great importance, in order to avoid the illusion which must necessarily arise from the misinterpretation of our empirical conceptions.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c89a6a1fed084f930944e384cdf81c67971fb2db0d8ecef171e8f0507d4d4447","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","order":68,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVCS","text":"# Cosmos Solution\n\n### Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem\n\nThe antinomy of pure reason is based upon the following dialectical argument: “If that which is conditioned is given, the whole series of its conditions is also given; but sensuous objects are given as conditioned; consequently...” This syllogism, the major of which seems so natural and evident, introduces as many cosmological ideas as there are different kinds of conditions in the synthesis of phenomena, in so far as these conditions constitute a series. These ideas require absolute totality in the series, and thus place reason in inextricable embarrassment. Before proceeding to expose the fallacy in this dialectical argument, it will be necessary to have a correct understanding of certain conceptions that appear in it.\n\nIn the first place, the following proposition is evident, and indubitably certain: “If the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby imperatively required.” For the very conception of a conditioned is a conception of something related to a condition, and, if this condition is itself conditioned, to another condition—and so on through all the members of the series. This proposition is, therefore, analytical and has nothing to fear from transcendental criticism. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue, as far as possible, the connection of a conception with its conditions.\n\nIf, in the second place, both the conditioned and the condition are things in themselves, and if the former is given, not only is the regress to the latter requisite, but the latter is really given with the former. Now, as this is true of all the members of the series, the entire series of conditions, and with them the unconditioned, is at the same time given in the very fact of the conditioned, the existence of which is possible only in and through that series, being given. In this case, the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, is a synthesis of the understanding merely, which represents things as they are, without regarding whether and how we can cognize them. But if I have to do with phenomena, which, in their character of mere representations, are not given, if I do not attain to a cognition of them (in other words, to themselves, for they are nothing more than empirical cognitions), I am not entitled to say: “If the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as phenomena) are also given.” I cannot, therefore, from the fact of a conditioned being given, infer the absolute totality of the series of its conditions. For phenomena are nothing but an empirical synthesis in apprehension or perception, and are therefore given only in it. Now, in speaking of phenomena it does not follow that, if the conditioned is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is also thereby given and presupposed; such a synthesis can be established only by an actual regress in the series of conditions. But we are entitled to say in this case that a regress to the conditions of a conditioned, in other words, that a continuous empirical synthesis is enjoined; that, if the conditions are not given, they are at least required; and that we are certain to discover the conditions in this regress.\n\nWe can now see that the major, in the above cosmological syllogism, takes the conditioned in the transcendental signification which it has in the pure category, while the minor speaks of it in the empirical signification which it has in the category as applied to phenomena. There is, therefore, a dialectical fallacy in the syllogism—a sophisma figurae dictionis. But this fallacy is not a consciously devised one, but a perfectly natural illusion of the common reason of man. For, when a thing is given as conditioned, we presuppose in the major its conditions and their series, unperceived, as it were, and unseen; because this is nothing more than the logical requirement of complete and satisfactory premisses for a given conclusion. In this case, time is altogether left out in the connection of the conditioned with the condition; they are supposed to be given in themselves, and contemporaneously. It is, moreover, just as natural to regard phenomena (in the minor) as things in themselves and as objects presented to the pure understanding, as in the major, in which complete abstraction was made of all conditions of intuition. But it is under these conditions alone that objects are given. Now we overlooked a remarkable distinction between the conceptions. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the complete series of the latter (in the major) are not limited by time, and do not contain the conception of succession. On the contrary, the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions in the phenomenal world—subsumed in the minor—are necessarily successive and given in time alone. It follows that I cannot presuppose in the minor, as I did in the major, the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series therein represented; for in the major all the members of the series are given as things in themselves—without any limitations or conditions of time, while in the minor they are possible only in and through a successive regress, which cannot exist, except it be actually carried into execution in the world of phenomena.\n\nAfter this proof of the viciousness of the argument commonly employed in maintaining cosmological assertions, both parties may now be justly dismissed, as advancing claims without grounds or title. But the process has not been ended by convincing them that one or both were in the wrong and had maintained an assertion which was without valid grounds of proof. Nothing seems to be clearer than that, if one maintains: “The world has a beginning,” and another: “The world has no beginning,” one of the two must be right. But it is likewise clear that, if the evidence on both sides is equal, it is impossible to discover on what side the truth lies; and the controversy continues, although the parties have been recommended to peace before the tribunal of reason. There remains, then, no other means of settling the question than to convince the parties, who refute each other with such conclusiveness and ability, that they are disputing about nothing, and that a transcendental illusion has been mocking them with visions of reality where there is none. The mode of adjusting a dispute which cannot be decided upon its own merits, we shall now proceed to lay before our readers.\n\n---\n\nZeno of Elea, a subtle dialectician, was severely reprimanded by Plato as a sophist, who, merely from the base motive of exhibiting his skill in discussion, maintained and subverted the same proposition by arguments as powerful and convincing on the one side as on the other. He maintained, for example, that God (who was probably nothing more, in his view, than the world) is neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor in rest, neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those philosophers who criticized his mode of discussion that his purpose was to deny completely both of two self-contradictory propositions—which is absurd. But I cannot believe that there is any justice in this accusation. The first of these propositions I shall presently consider in a more detailed manner. With regard to the others, if by the word of God he understood merely the Universe, his meaning must have been—that it cannot be permanently present in one place—that is, at rest—nor be capable of changing its place—that is, of moving—because all places are in the universe, and the universe itself is, therefore, in no place. Again, if the universe contains in itself everything that exists, it cannot be similar or dissimilar to any other thing, because there is, in fact, no other thing with which it can be compared. If two opposite judgements presuppose a contingent impossible, or arbitrary condition, both—in spite of their opposition (which is, however, not properly or really a contradiction)—fall away; because the condition, which ensured the validity of both, has itself disappeared.\n\nIf we say: “Everybody has either a good or a bad smell,” we have omitted a third possible judgement—it has no smell at all; and thus both conflicting statements may be false. If we say: “It is either good-smelling or not good-smelling (vel suaveolens vel non-suaveolens),” both judgements are contradictorily opposed; and the contradictory opposite of the former judgement—some bodies are not good-smelling—embraces also those bodies which have no smell at all. In the preceding pair of opposed judgements (per disparata), the contingent condition of the conception of body (smell) attached to both conflicting statements, instead of having been omitted in the latter, which is consequently not the contradictory opposite of the former.\n\nIf, accordingly, we say: “The world is either infinite in extension, or it is not infinite (non est infinitus)”; and if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite—the world is not infinite—must be true. And thus I should deny the existence of an infinite, without, however affirming the existence of a finite world. But if we construct our proposition thus: “The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite),” both statements may be false. For, in this case, we consider the world as per se determined in regard to quantity, and while, in the one judgement, we deny its infinite and consequently, perhaps, its independent existence; in the other, we append to the world, regarded as a thing in itself, a certain determination—that of finitude; and the latter may be false as well as the former, if the world is not given as a thing in itself, and thus neither as finite nor as infinite in quantity. This kind of opposition I may be allowed to term dialectical; that of contradictories may be called analytical opposition. Thus then, of two dialectically opposed judgements both may be false, from the fact, that the one is not a mere contradictory of the other, but actually enounces more than is requisite for a full and complete contradiction.\n\nWhen we regard the two propositions—“The world is infinite in quantity,” and, “The world is finite in quantity,” as contradictory opposites, we are assuming that the world—the complete series of phenomena—is a thing in itself. For it remains as a permanent quantity, whether I deny the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its phenomena. But if we dismiss this assumption—this transcendental illusion—and deny that it is a thing in itself, the contradictory opposition is metamorphosed into a merely dialectical one; and the world, as not existing in itself—independently of the regressive series of my representations—exists in like manner neither as a whole which is infinite nor as a whole which is finite in itself. The universe exists for me only in the empirical regress of the series of phenomena and not per se. If, then, it is always conditioned, it is never completely or as a whole; and it is, therefore, not an unconditioned whole and does not exist as such, either with an infinite, or with a finite quantity.\n\nWhat we have here said of the first cosmological idea—that of the absolute totality of quantity in phenomena—applies also to the others. The series of conditions is discoverable only in the regressive synthesis itself, and not in the phenomenon considered as a thing in itself—given prior to all regress. Hence I am compelled to say: “The aggregate of parts in a given phenomenon is in itself neither finite nor infinite; and these parts are given only in the regressive synthesis of decomposition—a synthesis which is never given in absolute completeness, either as finite, or as infinite.” The same is the case with the series of subordinated causes, or of the conditioned up to the unconditioned and necessary existence, which can never be regarded as in itself, and in its totality, either as finite or as infinite; because, as a series of subordinate representations, it subsists only in the dynamical regress and cannot be regarded as existing previously to this regress, or as a self-subsistent series of things.\n\nThus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas disappears. For the above demonstration has established the fact that it is merely the product of a dialectical and illusory opposition, which arises from the application of the idea of absolute totality—admissible only as a condition of things in themselves—to phenomena, which exist only in our representations, and—when constituting a series—in a successive regress. This antinomy of reason may, however, be really profitable to our speculative interests, not in the way of contributing any dogmatical addition, but as presenting to us another material support in our critical investigations. For it furnishes us with an indirect proof of the transcendental ideality of phenomena, if our minds were not completely satisfied with the direct proof set forth in the Trancendental Æsthetic. The proof would proceed in the following dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it must be either finite or infinite. But it is neither finite nor infinite—as has been shown, on the one side, by the thesis, on the other, by the antithesis. Therefore the world—the content of all phenomena—is not a whole existing in itself. It follows that phenomena are nothing, apart from our representations. And this is what we mean by transcendental ideality.\n\nThis remark is of some importance. It enables us to see that the proofs of the fourfold antinomy are not mere sophistries—are not fallacious, but grounded on the nature of reason, and valid—under the supposition that phenomena are things in themselves. The opposition of the judgements which follow makes it evident that a fallacy lay in the initial supposition, and thus helps us to discover the true constitution of objects of sense. This transcendental dialectic does not favour scepticism, although it presents us with a triumphant demonstration of the advantages of the sceptical method, the great utility of which is apparent in the antinomy, where the arguments of reason were allowed to confront each other in undiminished force. And although the result of these conflicts of reason is not what we expected—although we have obtained no positive dogmatical addition to metaphysical science—we have still reaped a great advantage in the correction of our judgements on these subjects of thought.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","text":"# Cosmos Solution\n\n### Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem\n\nThe antinomy of pure reason is based upon the following dialectical argument: “If that which is conditioned is given, the whole series of its conditions is also given; but sensuous objects are given as conditioned; consequently...” This syllogism, the major of which seems so natural and evident, introduces as many cosmological ideas as there are different kinds of conditions in the synthesis of phenomena, in so far as these conditions constitute a series. These ideas require absolute totality in the series, and thus place reason in inextricable embarrassment. Before proceeding to expose the fallacy in this dialectical argument, it will be necessary to have a correct understanding of certain conceptions that appear in it.\n\nIn the first place, the following proposition is evident, and indubitably certain: “If the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby imperatively required.” For the very conception of a conditioned is a conception of something related to a condition, and, if this condition is itself conditioned, to another condition—and so on through all the members of the series. This proposition is, therefore, analytical and has nothing to fear from transcendental criticism. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue, as far as possible, the connection of a conception with its conditions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","text":"If, in the second place, both the conditioned and the condition are things in themselves, and if the former is given, not only is the regress to the latter requisite, but the latter is really given with the former. Now, as this is true of all the members of the series, the entire series of conditions, and with them the unconditioned, is at the same time given in the very fact of the conditioned, the existence of which is possible only in and through that series, being given. In this case, the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, is a synthesis of the understanding merely, which represents things as they are, without regarding whether and how we can cognize them. But if I have to do with phenomena, which, in their character of mere representations, are not given, if I do not attain to a cognition of them (in other words, to themselves, for they are nothing more than empirical cognitions), I am not entitled to say: “If the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as phenomena) are also given.” I cannot, therefore, from the fact of a conditioned being given, infer the absolute totality of the series of its conditions. For phenomena are nothing but an empirical synthesis in apprehension or perception, and are therefore given only in it. Now, in speaking of phenomena it does not follow that, if the conditioned is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is also thereby given and presupposed; such a synthesis can be established only by an actual regress in the series of conditions. But we are entitled to say in this case that a regress to the conditions of a conditioned, in other words, that a continuous empirical synthesis is enjoined; that, if the conditions are not given, they are at least required; and that we are certain to discover the conditions in this regress.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","text":"We can now see that the major, in the above cosmological syllogism, takes the conditioned in the transcendental signification which it has in the pure category, while the minor speaks of it in the empirical signification which it has in the category as applied to phenomena. There is, therefore, a dialectical fallacy in the syllogism—a sophisma figurae dictionis. But this fallacy is not a consciously devised one, but a perfectly natural illusion of the common reason of man. For, when a thing is given as conditioned, we presuppose in the major its conditions and their series, unperceived, as it were, and unseen; because this is nothing more than the logical requirement of complete and satisfactory premisses for a given conclusion. In this case, time is altogether left out in the connection of the conditioned with the condition; they are supposed to be given in themselves, and contemporaneously. It is, moreover, just as natural to regard phenomena (in the minor) as things in themselves and as objects presented to the pure understanding, as in the major, in which complete abstraction was made of all conditions of intuition. But it is under these conditions alone that objects are given. Now we overlooked a remarkable distinction between the conceptions. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the complete series of the latter (in the major) are not limited by time, and do not contain the conception of succession. On the contrary, the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions in the phenomenal world—subsumed in the minor—are necessarily successive and given in time alone. It follows that I cannot presuppose in the minor, as I did in the major, the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series therein represented; for in the major all the members of the series are given as things in themselves—without any limitations or conditions of time, while in the minor they are possible only in and through a successive regress, which cannot exist, except it be actually carried into execution in the world of phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0004","text":"After this proof of the viciousness of the argument commonly employed in maintaining cosmological assertions, both parties may now be justly dismissed, as advancing claims without grounds or title. But the process has not been ended by convincing them that one or both were in the wrong and had maintained an assertion which was without valid grounds of proof. Nothing seems to be clearer than that, if one maintains: “The world has a beginning,” and another: “The world has no beginning,” one of the two must be right. But it is likewise clear that, if the evidence on both sides is equal, it is impossible to discover on what side the truth lies; and the controversy continues, although the parties have been recommended to peace before the tribunal of reason. There remains, then, no other means of settling the question than to convince the parties, who refute each other with such conclusiveness and ability, that they are disputing about nothing, and that a transcendental illusion has been mocking them with visions of reality where there is none. The mode of adjusting a dispute which cannot be decided upon its own merits, we shall now proceed to lay before our readers.\n\n---","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","text":"Zeno of Elea, a subtle dialectician, was severely reprimanded by Plato as a sophist, who, merely from the base motive of exhibiting his skill in discussion, maintained and subverted the same proposition by arguments as powerful and convincing on the one side as on the other. He maintained, for example, that God (who was probably nothing more, in his view, than the world) is neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor in rest, neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those philosophers who criticized his mode of discussion that his purpose was to deny completely both of two self-contradictory propositions—which is absurd. But I cannot believe that there is any justice in this accusation. The first of these propositions I shall presently consider in a more detailed manner. With regard to the others, if by the word of God he understood merely the Universe, his meaning must have been—that it cannot be permanently present in one place—that is, at rest—nor be capable of changing its place—that is, of moving—because all places are in the universe, and the universe itself is, therefore, in no place. Again, if the universe contains in itself everything that exists, it cannot be similar or dissimilar to any other thing, because there is, in fact, no other thing with which it can be compared. If two opposite judgements presuppose a contingent impossible, or arbitrary condition, both—in spite of their opposition (which is, however, not properly or really a contradiction)—fall away; because the condition, which ensured the validity of both, has itself disappeared.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0006","text":"If we say: “Everybody has either a good or a bad smell,” we have omitted a third possible judgement—it has no smell at all; and thus both conflicting statements may be false. If we say: “It is either good-smelling or not good-smelling (vel suaveolens vel non-suaveolens),” both judgements are contradictorily opposed; and the contradictory opposite of the former judgement—some bodies are not good-smelling—embraces also those bodies which have no smell at all. In the preceding pair of opposed judgements (per disparata), the contingent condition of the conception of body (smell) attached to both conflicting statements, instead of having been omitted in the latter, which is consequently not the contradictory opposite of the former.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","text":"If, accordingly, we say: “The world is either infinite in extension, or it is not infinite (non est infinitus)”; and if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite—the world is not infinite—must be true. And thus I should deny the existence of an infinite, without, however affirming the existence of a finite world. But if we construct our proposition thus: “The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite),” both statements may be false. For, in this case, we consider the world as per se determined in regard to quantity, and while, in the one judgement, we deny its infinite and consequently, perhaps, its independent existence; in the other, we append to the world, regarded as a thing in itself, a certain determination—that of finitude; and the latter may be false as well as the former, if the world is not given as a thing in itself, and thus neither as finite nor as infinite in quantity. This kind of opposition I may be allowed to term dialectical; that of contradictories may be called analytical opposition. Thus then, of two dialectically opposed judgements both may be false, from the fact, that the one is not a mere contradictory of the other, but actually enounces more than is requisite for a full and complete contradiction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","text":"When we regard the two propositions—“The world is infinite in quantity,” and, “The world is finite in quantity,” as contradictory opposites, we are assuming that the world—the complete series of phenomena—is a thing in itself. For it remains as a permanent quantity, whether I deny the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its phenomena. But if we dismiss this assumption—this transcendental illusion—and deny that it is a thing in itself, the contradictory opposition is metamorphosed into a merely dialectical one; and the world, as not existing in itself—independently of the regressive series of my representations—exists in like manner neither as a whole which is infinite nor as a whole which is finite in itself. The universe exists for me only in the empirical regress of the series of phenomena and not per se. If, then, it is always conditioned, it is never completely or as a whole; and it is, therefore, not an unconditioned whole and does not exist as such, either with an infinite, or with a finite quantity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0009","text":"What we have here said of the first cosmological idea—that of the absolute totality of quantity in phenomena—applies also to the others. The series of conditions is discoverable only in the regressive synthesis itself, and not in the phenomenon considered as a thing in itself—given prior to all regress. Hence I am compelled to say: “The aggregate of parts in a given phenomenon is in itself neither finite nor infinite; and these parts are given only in the regressive synthesis of decomposition—a synthesis which is never given in absolute completeness, either as finite, or as infinite.” The same is the case with the series of subordinated causes, or of the conditioned up to the unconditioned and necessary existence, which can never be regarded as in itself, and in its totality, either as finite or as infinite; because, as a series of subordinate representations, it subsists only in the dynamical regress and cannot be regarded as existing previously to this regress, or as a self-subsistent series of things.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","text":"Thus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas disappears. For the above demonstration has established the fact that it is merely the product of a dialectical and illusory opposition, which arises from the application of the idea of absolute totality—admissible only as a condition of things in themselves—to phenomena, which exist only in our representations, and—when constituting a series—in a successive regress. This antinomy of reason may, however, be really profitable to our speculative interests, not in the way of contributing any dogmatical addition, but as presenting to us another material support in our critical investigations. For it furnishes us with an indirect proof of the transcendental ideality of phenomena, if our minds were not completely satisfied with the direct proof set forth in the Trancendental Æsthetic. The proof would proceed in the following dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it must be either finite or infinite. But it is neither finite nor infinite—as has been shown, on the one side, by the thesis, on the other, by the antithesis. Therefore the world—the content of all phenomena—is not a whole existing in itself. It follows that phenomena are nothing, apart from our representations. And this is what we mean by transcendental ideality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","text":"This remark is of some importance. It enables us to see that the proofs of the fourfold antinomy are not mere sophistries—are not fallacious, but grounded on the nature of reason, and valid—under the supposition that phenomena are things in themselves. The opposition of the judgements which follow makes it evident that a fallacy lay in the initial supposition, and thus helps us to discover the true constitution of objects of sense. This transcendental dialectic does not favour scepticism, although it presents us with a triumphant demonstration of the advantages of the sceptical method, the great utility of which is apparent in the antinomy, where the arguments of reason were allowed to confront each other in undiminished force. And although the result of these conflicts of reason is not what we expected—although we have obtained no positive dogmatical addition to metaphysical science—we have still reaped a great advantage in the correction of our judgements on these subjects of thought.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dbbd30ccd687806c47b29b5200f686970efddefa2fa6953d07e32f7a88f5fa3a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","order":69,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVRP","text":"### Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nThe cosmological principle of totality could not give us any certain knowledge in regard to the maximum in the series of conditions in the world of sense, considered as a thing in itself. The actual regress in the series is the only means of approaching this maximum. This principle of pure reason, therefore, may still be considered as valid—not as an axiom enabling us to cogitate totality in the object as actual, but as a problem for the understanding, which requires it to institute and to continue, in conformity with the idea of totality in the mind, the regress in the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. For in the world of sense, that is, in space and time, every condition which we discover in our investigation of phenomena is itself conditioned; because sensuous objects are not things in themselves (in which case an absolutely unconditioned might be reached in the progress of cognition), but are merely empirical representations the conditions of which must always be found in intuition. The principle of reason is therefore properly a mere rule—prescribing a regress in the series of conditions for given phenomena, and prohibiting any pause or rest on an absolutely unconditioned. It is, therefore, not a principle of the possibility of experience or of the empirical cognition of sensuous objects—consequently not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is confined within certain proper limits determined by the given intuition. Still less is it a constitutive principle of reason authorizing us to extend our conception of the sensuous world beyond all possible experience. It is merely a principle for the enlargement and extension of experience as far as is possible for human faculties. It forbids us to consider any empirical limits as absolute. It is, hence, a principle of reason, which, as a rule, dictates how we ought to proceed in our empirical regress, but is unable to anticipate or indicate prior to the empirical regress what is given in the object itself. I have termed it for this reason a regulative principle of reason; while the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as existing in itself and given in the object, is a constitutive cosmological principle. This distinction will at once demonstrate the falsehood of the constitutive principle, and prevent us from attributing (by a transcendental subreptio) objective reality to an idea, which is valid only as a rule.\n\nIn order to understand the proper meaning of this rule of pure reason, we must notice first that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be proceeded with in order to attain to the complete conception of the object. If it gave us any information in respect to the former statement, it would be a constitutive principle—a principle impossible from the nature of pure reason. It will not therefore enable us to establish any such conclusions as: “The series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite,” or, “It is infinite.” For, in this case, we should be cogitating in the mere idea of absolute totality, an object which is not and cannot be given in experience; inasmuch as we should be attributing a reality objective and independent of the empirical synthesis, to a series of phenomena. This idea of reason cannot then be regarded as valid—except as a rule for the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions, according to which we must proceed from the conditioned, through all intermediate and subordinate conditions, up to the unconditioned; although this goal is unattained and unattainable. For the absolutely unconditioned cannot be discovered in the sphere of experience.\n\nWe now proceed to determine clearly our notion of a synthesis which can never be complete. There are two terms commonly employed for this purpose. These terms are regarded as expressions of different and distinguishable notions, although the ground of the distinction has never been clearly exposed. The term employed by the mathematicians is progressus in infinitum. The philosophers prefer the expression progressus in indefinitum. Without detaining the reader with an examination of the reasons for such a distinction, or with remarks on the right or wrong use of the terms, I shall endeavour clearly to determine these conceptions, so far as is necessary for the purpose in this Critique.\n\nWe may, with propriety, say of a straight line, that it may be produced to infinity. In this case the distinction between a progressus in infinitum and a progressus in indefinitum is a mere piece of subtlety. For, although when we say, “Produce a straight line,” it is more correct to say in indefinitum than in infinitum; because the former means, “Produce it as far as you please,” the second, “You must not cease to produce it”; the expression in infinitum is, when we are speaking of the power to do it, perfectly correct, for we can always make it longer if we please—on to infinity. And this remark holds good in all cases, when we speak of a progressus, that is, an advancement from the condition to the conditioned; this possible advancement always proceeds to infinity. We may proceed from a given pair in the descending line of generation from father to son, and cogitate a never-ending line of descendants from it. For in such a case reason does not demand absolute totality in the series, because it does not presuppose it as a condition and as given (datum), but merely as conditioned, and as capable of being given (dabile).\n\nVery different is the case with the problem: “How far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions, must extend”; whether I can say: “It is a regress in infinitum,” or only “in indefinitum”; and whether, for example, setting out from the human beings at present alive in the world, I may ascend in the series of their ancestors, in infinitum—or whether all that can be said is, that so far as I have proceeded, I have discovered no empirical ground for considering the series limited, so that I am justified, and indeed, compelled to search for ancestors still further back, although I am not obliged by the idea of reason to presuppose them.\n\nMy answer to this question is: “If the series is given in empirical intuition as a whole, the regress in the series of its internal conditions proceeds in infinitum; but, if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is to proceed to absolute totality, the regress is possible only in indefinitum.” For example, the division of a portion of matter given within certain limits—of a body, that is—proceeds in infinitum. For, as the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of the part a part of the part, and so on, and as in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned indivisible member of the series of conditions is not to be found; there are no reasons or grounds in experience for stopping in the division, but, on the contrary, the more remote members of the division are actually and empirically given prior to this division. That is to say, the division proceeds to infinity. On the other hand, the series of ancestors of any given human being is not given, in its absolute totality, in any experience, and yet the regress proceeds from every genealogical member of this series to one still higher, and does not meet with any empirical limit presenting an absolutely unconditioned member of the series. But as the members of such a series are not contained in the empirical intuition of the whole, prior to the regress, this regress does not proceed to infinity, but only in indefinitum, that is, we are called upon to discover other and higher members, which are themselves always conditioned.\n\nIn neither case—the regressus in infinitum, nor the regressus in indefinitum, is the series of conditions to be considered as actually infinite in the object itself. This might be true of things in themselves, but it cannot be asserted of phenomena, which, as conditions of each other, are only given in the empirical regress itself. Hence, the question no longer is, “What is the quantity of this series of conditions in itself—is it finite or infinite?” for it is nothing in itself; but, “How is the empirical regress to be commenced, and how far ought we to proceed with it?” And here a signal distinction in the application of this rule becomes apparent. If the whole is given empirically, it is possible to recede in the series of its internal conditions to infinity. But if the whole is not given, and can only be given by and through the empirical regress, I can only say: “It is possible to infinity, to proceed to still higher conditions in the series.” In the first case, I am justified in asserting that more members are empirically given in the object than I attain to in the regress (of decomposition). In the second case, I am justified only in saying, that I can always proceed further in the regress, because no member of the series is given as absolutely conditioned, and thus a higher member is possible, and an inquiry with regard to it is necessary. In the one case it is necessary to find other members of the series, in the other it is necessary to inquire for others, inasmuch as experience presents no absolute limitation of the regress. For, either you do not possess a perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and in this case the regress cannot be regarded as complete; or, you do possess such a limitative perception, in which case it is not a part of your series (for that which limits must be distinct from that which is limited by it), and it is incumbent on you to continue your regress up to this condition, and so on.\n\nThese remarks will be placed in their proper light by their application in the following section.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","text":"### Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nThe cosmological principle of totality could not give us any certain knowledge in regard to the maximum in the series of conditions in the world of sense, considered as a thing in itself. The actual regress in the series is the only means of approaching this maximum. This principle of pure reason, therefore, may still be considered as valid—not as an axiom enabling us to cogitate totality in the object as actual, but as a problem for the understanding, which requires it to institute and to continue, in conformity with the idea of totality in the mind, the regress in the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. For in the world of sense, that is, in space and time, every condition which we discover in our investigation of phenomena is itself conditioned; because sensuous objects are not things in themselves (in which case an absolutely unconditioned might be reached in the progress of cognition), but are merely empirical representations the conditions of which must always be found in intuition. The principle of reason is therefore properly a mere rule—prescribing a regress in the series of conditions for given phenomena, and prohibiting any pause or rest on an absolutely unconditioned. It is, therefore, not a principle of the possibility of experience or of the empirical cognition of sensuous objects—consequently not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is confined within certain proper limits determined by the given intuition. Still less is it a constitutive principle of reason authorizing us to extend our conception of the sensuous world beyond all possible experience. It is merely a principle for the enlargement and extension of experience as far as is possible for human faculties. It forbids us to consider any empirical limits as absolute. It is, hence, a principle of reason, which, as a rule, dictates how we ought to proceed in our empirical regress, but is unable to anticipate or indicate prior to the empirical regress what is given in the object itself. I have termed it for this reason a regulative principle of reason; while the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as existing in itself and given in the object, is a constitutive cosmological principle. This distinction will at once demonstrate the falsehood of the constitutive principle, and prevent us from attributing (by a transcendental subreptio) objective reality to an idea, which is valid only as a rule.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","text":"In order to understand the proper meaning of this rule of pure reason, we must notice first that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be proceeded with in order to attain to the complete conception of the object. If it gave us any information in respect to the former statement, it would be a constitutive principle—a principle impossible from the nature of pure reason. It will not therefore enable us to establish any such conclusions as: “The series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite,” or, “It is infinite.” For, in this case, we should be cogitating in the mere idea of absolute totality, an object which is not and cannot be given in experience; inasmuch as we should be attributing a reality objective and independent of the empirical synthesis, to a series of phenomena. This idea of reason cannot then be regarded as valid—except as a rule for the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions, according to which we must proceed from the conditioned, through all intermediate and subordinate conditions, up to the unconditioned; although this goal is unattained and unattainable. For the absolutely unconditioned cannot be discovered in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0003","text":"We now proceed to determine clearly our notion of a synthesis which can never be complete. There are two terms commonly employed for this purpose. These terms are regarded as expressions of different and distinguishable notions, although the ground of the distinction has never been clearly exposed. The term employed by the mathematicians is progressus in infinitum. The philosophers prefer the expression progressus in indefinitum. Without detaining the reader with an examination of the reasons for such a distinction, or with remarks on the right or wrong use of the terms, I shall endeavour clearly to determine these conceptions, so far as is necessary for the purpose in this Critique.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0004","text":"We may, with propriety, say of a straight line, that it may be produced to infinity. In this case the distinction between a progressus in infinitum and a progressus in indefinitum is a mere piece of subtlety. For, although when we say, “Produce a straight line,” it is more correct to say in indefinitum than in infinitum; because the former means, “Produce it as far as you please,” the second, “You must not cease to produce it”; the expression in infinitum is, when we are speaking of the power to do it, perfectly correct, for we can always make it longer if we please—on to infinity. And this remark holds good in all cases, when we speak of a progressus, that is, an advancement from the condition to the conditioned; this possible advancement always proceeds to infinity. We may proceed from a given pair in the descending line of generation from father to son, and cogitate a never-ending line of descendants from it. For in such a case reason does not demand absolute totality in the series, because it does not presuppose it as a condition and as given (datum), but merely as conditioned, and as capable of being given (dabile).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0005","text":"Very different is the case with the problem: “How far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions, must extend”; whether I can say: “It is a regress in infinitum,” or only “in indefinitum”; and whether, for example, setting out from the human beings at present alive in the world, I may ascend in the series of their ancestors, in infinitum—or whether all that can be said is, that so far as I have proceeded, I have discovered no empirical ground for considering the series limited, so that I am justified, and indeed, compelled to search for ancestors still further back, although I am not obliged by the idea of reason to presuppose them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","text":"My answer to this question is: “If the series is given in empirical intuition as a whole, the regress in the series of its internal conditions proceeds in infinitum; but, if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is to proceed to absolute totality, the regress is possible only in indefinitum.” For example, the division of a portion of matter given within certain limits—of a body, that is—proceeds in infinitum. For, as the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of the part a part of the part, and so on, and as in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned indivisible member of the series of conditions is not to be found; there are no reasons or grounds in experience for stopping in the division, but, on the contrary, the more remote members of the division are actually and empirically given prior to this division. That is to say, the division proceeds to infinity. On the other hand, the series of ancestors of any given human being is not given, in its absolute totality, in any experience, and yet the regress proceeds from every genealogical member of this series to one still higher, and does not meet with any empirical limit presenting an absolutely unconditioned member of the series. But as the members of such a series are not contained in the empirical intuition of the whole, prior to the regress, this regress does not proceed to infinity, but only in indefinitum, that is, we are called upon to discover other and higher members, which are themselves always conditioned.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","text":"In neither case—the regressus in infinitum, nor the regressus in indefinitum, is the series of conditions to be considered as actually infinite in the object itself. This might be true of things in themselves, but it cannot be asserted of phenomena, which, as conditions of each other, are only given in the empirical regress itself. Hence, the question no longer is, “What is the quantity of this series of conditions in itself—is it finite or infinite?” for it is nothing in itself; but, “How is the empirical regress to be commenced, and how far ought we to proceed with it?” And here a signal distinction in the application of this rule becomes apparent. If the whole is given empirically, it is possible to recede in the series of its internal conditions to infinity. But if the whole is not given, and can only be given by and through the empirical regress, I can only say: “It is possible to infinity, to proceed to still higher conditions in the series.” In the first case, I am justified in asserting that more members are empirically given in the object than I attain to in the regress (of decomposition). In the second case, I am justified only in saying, that I can always proceed further in the regress, because no member of the series is given as absolutely conditioned, and thus a higher member is possible, and an inquiry with regard to it is necessary. In the one case it is necessary to find other members of the series, in the other it is necessary to inquire for others, inasmuch as experience presents no absolute limitation of the regress. For, either you do not possess a perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and in this case the regress cannot be regarded as complete; or, you do possess such a limitative perception, in which case it is not a part of your series (for that which limits must be distinct from that which is limited by it), and it is incumbent on you to continue your regress up to this condition, and so on.\n\nThese remarks will be placed in their proper light by their application in the following section.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"76ef6f28bb061aea68dd9e9f44512c4f781c5711082a81c460db78f7fdc0242b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","order":70,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIEU","text":"### Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nWe have shown that no transcendental use can be made either of the conceptions of reason or of understanding. We have shown, likewise, that the demand of absolute totality in the series of conditions in the world of sense arises from a transcendental employment of reason, resting on the opinion that phenomena are to be regarded as things in themselves. It follows that we are not required to answer the question respecting the absolute quantity of a series—whether it is in itself limited or unlimited. We are only called upon to determine how far we must proceed in the empirical regress from condition to condition, in order to discover, in conformity with the rule of reason, a full and correct answer to the questions proposed by reason itself.\n\nThis principle of reason is hence valid only as a rule for the extension of a possible experience—its invalidity as a principle constitutive of phenomena in themselves having been sufficiently demonstrated. And thus, too, the antinomial conflict of reason with itself is completely put an end to; inasmuch as we have not only presented a critical solution of the fallacy lurking in the opposite statements of reason, but have shown the true meaning of the ideas which gave rise to these statements. The dialectical principle of reason has, therefore, been changed into a doctrinal principle. But in fact, if this principle, in the subjective signification which we have shown to be its only true sense, may be guaranteed as a principle of the unceasing extension of the employment of our understanding, its influence and value are just as great as if it were an axiom for the à priori determination of objects. For such an axiom could not exert a stronger influence on the extension and rectification of our knowledge, otherwise than by procuring for the principles of the understanding the most widely expanded employment in the field of experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","text":"### Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nWe have shown that no transcendental use can be made either of the conceptions of reason or of understanding. We have shown, likewise, that the demand of absolute totality in the series of conditions in the world of sense arises from a transcendental employment of reason, resting on the opinion that phenomena are to be regarded as things in themselves. It follows that we are not required to answer the question respecting the absolute quantity of a series—whether it is in itself limited or unlimited. We are only called upon to determine how far we must proceed in the empirical regress from condition to condition, in order to discover, in conformity with the rule of reason, a full and correct answer to the questions proposed by reason itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","text":"This principle of reason is hence valid only as a rule for the extension of a possible experience—its invalidity as a principle constitutive of phenomena in themselves having been sufficiently demonstrated. And thus, too, the antinomial conflict of reason with itself is completely put an end to; inasmuch as we have not only presented a critical solution of the fallacy lurking in the opposite statements of reason, but have shown the true meaning of the ideas which gave rise to these statements. The dialectical principle of reason has, therefore, been changed into a doctrinal principle. But in fact, if this principle, in the subjective signification which we have shown to be its only true sense, may be guaranteed as a principle of the unceasing extension of the employment of our understanding, its influence and value are just as great as if it were an axiom for the à priori determination of objects. For such an axiom could not exert a stronger influence on the extension and rectification of our knowledge, otherwise than by procuring for the principles of the understanding the most widely expanded employment in the field of experience.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ef5acd9fe9a86229db8a689d995ab036f5f953bd5305dde6949b594f8149f4b6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","order":71,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ISCI","text":"### I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe\n\nHere, as well as in the case of the other cosmological problems, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in our empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit, and consequently no experience of a condition, which is itself absolutely unconditioned, is discoverable. And the truth of this proposition itself rests upon the consideration that such an experience must represent to us phenomena as limited by nothing or the mere void, on which our continued regress by means of perception must abut—which is impossible.\n\nNow this proposition, which declares that every condition attained in the empirical regress must itself be considered empirically conditioned, contains the rule in terminis, which requires me, to whatever extent I may have proceeded in the ascending series, always to look for some higher member in the series—whether this member is to become known to me through experience, or not.\n\nNothing further is necessary, then, for the solution of the first cosmological problem, than to decide, whether, in the regress to the unconditioned quantity of the universe (as regards space and time), this never limited ascent ought to be called a regressus in infinitum or indefinitum.\n\nThe general representation which we form in our minds of the series of all past states or conditions of the world, or of all the things which at present exist in it, is itself nothing more than a possible empirical regress, which is cogitated—although in an undetermined manner—in the mind, and which gives rise to the conception of a series of conditions for a given object.[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-60) Now I have a conception of the universe, but not an intuition—that is, not an intuition of it as a whole. Thus I cannot infer the magnitude of the regress from the quantity or magnitude of the world, and determine the former by means of the latter; on the contrary, I must first of all form a conception of the quantity or magnitude of the world from the magnitude of the empirical regress. But of this regress I know nothing more than that I ought to proceed from every given member of the series of conditions to one still higher. But the quantity of the universe is not thereby determined, and we cannot affirm that this regress proceeds in infinitum. Such an affirmation would anticipate the members of the series which have not yet been reached, and represent the number of them as beyond the grasp of any empirical synthesis; it would consequently determine the cosmical quantity prior to the regress (although only in a negative manner)—which is impossible. For the world is not given in its totality in any intuition: consequently, its quantity cannot be given prior to the regress. It follows that we are unable to make any declaration respecting the cosmical quantity in itself—not even that the regress in it is a regress in infinitum; we must only endeavour to attain to a conception of the quantity of the universe, in conformity with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. But this rule merely requires us never to admit an absolute limit to our series—how far soever we may have proceeded in it, but always, on the contrary, to subordinate every phenomenon to some other as its condition, and consequently to proceed to this higher phenomenon. Such a regress is, therefore, the regressus in indefinitum, which, as not determining a quantity in the object, is clearly distinguishable from the regressus in infinitum.\n\n[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-60) The cosmical series can neither be greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, upon which its conception is based. And as this regress cannot be a determinate infinite regress, still less a determinate finite (absolutely limited), it is evident that we cannot regard the world as either finite or infinite, because the regress, which gives us the representation of the world, is neither finite nor infinite.\n\nIt follows from what we have said that we are not justified in declaring the world to be infinite in space, or as regards past time. For this conception of an infinite given quantity is empirical; but we cannot apply the conception of an infinite quantity to the world as an object of the senses. I cannot say, “The regress from a given perception to everything limited either in space or time, proceeds in infinitum,” for this presupposes an infinite cosmical quantity; neither can I say, “It is finite,” for an absolute limit is likewise impossible in experience. It follows that I am not entitled to make any assertion at all respecting the whole object of experience—the world of sense; I must limit my declarations to the rule according to which experience or empirical knowledge is to be attained.\n\nTo the question, therefore, respecting the cosmical quantity, the first and negative answer is: “The world has no beginning in time, and no absolute limit in space.”\n\nFor, in the contrary case, it would be limited by a void time on the one hand, and by a void space on the other. Now, since the world, as a phenomenon, cannot be thus limited in itself for a phenomenon is not a thing in itself; it must be possible for us to have a perception of this limitation by a void time and a void space. But such a perception—such an experience is impossible; because it has no content. Consequently, an absolute cosmical limit is empirically, and therefore absolutely, impossible.[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-61)\n\n[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-61) The reader will remark that the proof presented above is very different from the dogmatical demonstration given in the antithesis of the first antinomy. In that demonstration, it was taken for granted that the world is a thing in itself—given in its totality prior to all regress, and a determined position in space and time was denied to it—if it was not considered as occupying all time and all space. Hence our conclusion differed from that given above; for we inferred in the antithesis the actual infinity of the world.\n\nFrom this follows the affirmative answer: “The regress in the series of phenomena—as a determination of the cosmical quantity, proceeds in indefinitum.” This is equivalent to saying: “The world of sense has no absolute quantity, but the empirical regress (through which alone the world of sense is presented to us on the side of its conditions) rests upon a rule, which requires it to proceed from every member of the series, as conditioned, to one still more remote (whether through personal experience, or by means of history, or the chain of cause and effect), and not to cease at any point in this extension of the possible empirical employment of the understanding.” And this is the proper and only use which reason can make of its principles.\n\nThe above rule does not prescribe an unceasing regress in one kind of phenomena. It does not, for example, forbid us, in our ascent from an individual human being through the line of his ancestors, to expect that we shall discover at some point of the regress a primeval pair, or to admit, in the series of heavenly bodies, a sun at the farthest possible distance from some centre. All that it demands is a perpetual progress from phenomena to phenomena, even although an actual perception is not presented by them (as in the case of our perceptions being so weak as that we are unable to become conscious of them), since they, nevertheless, belong to possible experience.\n\nEvery beginning is in time, and all limits to extension are in space. But space and time are in the world of sense. Consequently phenomena in the world are conditionally limited, but the world itself is not limited, either conditionally or unconditionally.\n\nFor this reason, and because neither the world nor the cosmical series of conditions to a given conditioned can be completely given, our conception of the cosmical quantity is given only in and through the regress and not prior to it—in a collective intuition. But the regress itself is really nothing more than the determining of the cosmical quantity, and cannot therefore give us any determined conception of it—still less a conception of a quantity which is, in relation to a certain standard, infinite. The regress does not, therefore, proceed to infinity (an infinity given), but only to an indefinite extent, for or the of presenting to us a quantity—realized only in and through the regress itself.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","text":"### I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe\n\nHere, as well as in the case of the other cosmological problems, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in our empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit, and consequently no experience of a condition, which is itself absolutely unconditioned, is discoverable. And the truth of this proposition itself rests upon the consideration that such an experience must represent to us phenomena as limited by nothing or the mere void, on which our continued regress by means of perception must abut—which is impossible.\n\nNow this proposition, which declares that every condition attained in the empirical regress must itself be considered empirically conditioned, contains the rule in terminis, which requires me, to whatever extent I may have proceeded in the ascending series, always to look for some higher member in the series—whether this member is to become known to me through experience, or not.\n\nNothing further is necessary, then, for the solution of the first cosmological problem, than to decide, whether, in the regress to the unconditioned quantity of the universe (as regards space and time), this never limited ascent ought to be called a regressus in infinitum or indefinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","text":"The general representation which we form in our minds of the series of all past states or conditions of the world, or of all the things which at present exist in it, is itself nothing more than a possible empirical regress, which is cogitated—although in an undetermined manner—in the mind, and which gives rise to the conception of a series of conditions for a given object.[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-60) Now I have a conception of the universe, but not an intuition—that is, not an intuition of it as a whole. Thus I cannot infer the magnitude of the regress from the quantity or magnitude of the world, and determine the former by means of the latter; on the contrary, I must first of all form a conception of the quantity or magnitude of the world from the magnitude of the empirical regress. But of this regress I know nothing more than that I ought to proceed from every given member of the series of conditions to one still higher. But the quantity of the universe is not thereby determined, and we cannot affirm that this regress proceeds in infinitum. Such an affirmation would anticipate the members of the series which have not yet been reached, and represent the number of them as beyond the grasp of any empirical synthesis; it would consequently determine the cosmical quantity prior to the regress (although only in a negative manner)—which is impossible. For the world is not given in its totality in any intuition: consequently, its quantity cannot be given prior to the regress. It follows that we are unable to make any declaration respecting the cosmical quantity in itself—not even that the regress in it is a regress in infinitum; we must only endeavour to attain to a conception of the quantity of the universe, in conformity with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. But this rule merely requires us never to admit an absolute limit to our series—how far soever we may have proceeded in it, but always, on the contrary, to subordinate every phenomenon to some other as its condition, and consequently to proceed to this higher phenomenon. Such a regress is, therefore, the regressus in indefinitum, which, as not determining a quantity in the object, is clearly distinguishable from the regressus in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","text":"[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-60) The cosmical series can neither be greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, upon which its conception is based. And as this regress cannot be a determinate infinite regress, still less a determinate finite (absolutely limited), it is evident that we cannot regard the world as either finite or infinite, because the regress, which gives us the representation of the world, is neither finite nor infinite.\n\nIt follows from what we have said that we are not justified in declaring the world to be infinite in space, or as regards past time. For this conception of an infinite given quantity is empirical; but we cannot apply the conception of an infinite quantity to the world as an object of the senses. I cannot say, “The regress from a given perception to everything limited either in space or time, proceeds in infinitum,” for this presupposes an infinite cosmical quantity; neither can I say, “It is finite,” for an absolute limit is likewise impossible in experience. It follows that I am not entitled to make any assertion at all respecting the whole object of experience—the world of sense; I must limit my declarations to the rule according to which experience or empirical knowledge is to be attained.\n\nTo the question, therefore, respecting the cosmical quantity, the first and negative answer is: “The world has no beginning in time, and no absolute limit in space.”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","text":"For, in the contrary case, it would be limited by a void time on the one hand, and by a void space on the other. Now, since the world, as a phenomenon, cannot be thus limited in itself for a phenomenon is not a thing in itself; it must be possible for us to have a perception of this limitation by a void time and a void space. But such a perception—such an experience is impossible; because it has no content. Consequently, an absolute cosmical limit is empirically, and therefore absolutely, impossible.[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-61)\n\n[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-61) The reader will remark that the proof presented above is very different from the dogmatical demonstration given in the antithesis of the first antinomy. In that demonstration, it was taken for granted that the world is a thing in itself—given in its totality prior to all regress, and a determined position in space and time was denied to it—if it was not considered as occupying all time and all space. Hence our conclusion differed from that given above; for we inferred in the antithesis the actual infinity of the world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","text":"From this follows the affirmative answer: “The regress in the series of phenomena—as a determination of the cosmical quantity, proceeds in indefinitum.” This is equivalent to saying: “The world of sense has no absolute quantity, but the empirical regress (through which alone the world of sense is presented to us on the side of its conditions) rests upon a rule, which requires it to proceed from every member of the series, as conditioned, to one still more remote (whether through personal experience, or by means of history, or the chain of cause and effect), and not to cease at any point in this extension of the possible empirical employment of the understanding.” And this is the proper and only use which reason can make of its principles.\n\nThe above rule does not prescribe an unceasing regress in one kind of phenomena. It does not, for example, forbid us, in our ascent from an individual human being through the line of his ancestors, to expect that we shall discover at some point of the regress a primeval pair, or to admit, in the series of heavenly bodies, a sun at the farthest possible distance from some centre. All that it demands is a perpetual progress from phenomena to phenomena, even although an actual perception is not presented by them (as in the case of our perceptions being so weak as that we are unable to become conscious of them), since they, nevertheless, belong to possible experience.\n\nEvery beginning is in time, and all limits to extension are in space. But space and time are in the world of sense. Consequently phenomena in the world are conditionally limited, but the world itself is not limited, either conditionally or unconditionally.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0006","text":"For this reason, and because neither the world nor the cosmical series of conditions to a given conditioned can be completely given, our conception of the cosmical quantity is given only in and through the regress and not prior to it—in a collective intuition. But the regress itself is really nothing more than the determining of the cosmical quantity, and cannot therefore give us any determined conception of it—still less a conception of a quantity which is, in relation to a certain standard, infinite. The regress does not, therefore, proceed to infinity (an infinity given), but only to an indefinite extent, for or the of presenting to us a quantity—realized only in and through the regress itself.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"415d87237f1a8a32409b5b02e8fd11b779f7a2787eb2c662ed635308a88bd82c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","order":72,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IS_2","text":"### II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition\n\nWhen I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to its conditions. The division of the parts of the whole (subdivisio or decompositio) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be actually attained and given to the mind, if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continuous decomposition are themselves divisible, the division, that is to say, the regress, proceeds from the conditioned to its conditions in infinitum; because the conditions (the parts) are themselves contained in the conditioned, and, as the latter is given in a limited intuition, the former are all given along with it. This regress cannot, therefore, be called a regressus in indefinitum, as happened in the case of the preceding cosmological idea, the regress in which proceeded from the conditioned to the conditions not given contemporaneously and along with it, but discoverable only through the empirical regress. We are not, however, entitled to affirm of a whole of this kind, which is divisible in infinitum, that it consists of an infinite number of parts. For, although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not contained therein. The division is contained only in the progressing decomposition—in the regress itself, which is the condition of the possibility and actuality of the series. Now, as this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains must be contained in the given whole as an aggregate. But the complete series of division is not contained therein. For this series, being infinite in succession and always incomplete, cannot represent an infinite number of members, and still less a composition of these members into a whole.\n\nTo apply this remark to space. Every limited part of space presented to intuition is a whole, the parts of which are always spaces—to whatever extent subdivided. Every limited space is hence divisible to infinity.\n\nLet us again apply the remark to an external phenomenon enclosed in limits, that is, a body. The divisibility of a body rests upon the divisibility of space, which is the condition of the possibility of the body as an extended whole. A body is consequently divisible to infinity, though it does not, for that reason, consist of an infinite number of parts.\n\nIt certainly seems that, as a body must be cogitated as substance in space, the law of divisibility would not be applicable to it as substance. For we may and ought to grant, in the case of space, that division or decomposition, to any extent, never can utterly annihilate composition (that is to say, the smallest part of space must still consist of spaces); otherwise space would entirely cease to exist—which is impossible. But, the assertion on the other hand that when all composition in matter is annihilated in thought, nothing remains, does not seem to harmonize with the conception of substance, which must be properly the subject of all composition and must remain, even after the conjunction of its attributes in space—which constituted a body—is annihilated in thought. But this is not the case with substance in the phenomenal world, which is not a thing in itself cogitated by the pure category. Phenomenal substance is not an absolute subject; it is merely a permanent sensuous image, and nothing more than an intuition, in which the unconditioned is not to be found.\n\nBut, although this rule of progress to infinity is legitimate and applicable to the subdivision of a phenomenon, as a mere occupation or filling of space, it is not applicable to a whole consisting of a number of distinct parts and constituting a quantum discretum—that is to say, an organized body. It cannot be admitted that every part in an organized whole is itself organized, and that, in analysing it to infinity, we must always meet with organized parts; although we may allow that the parts of the matter which we decompose in infinitum, may be organized. For the infinity of the division of a phenomenon in space rests altogether on the fact that the divisibility of a phenomenon is given only in and through this infinity, that is, an undetermined number of parts is given, while the parts themselves are given and determined only in and through the subdivision; in a word, the infinity of the division necessarily presupposes that the whole is not already divided in se. Hence our division determines a number of parts in the whole—a number which extends just as far as the actual regress in the division; while, on the other hand, the very notion of a body organized to infinity represents the whole as already and in itself divided. We expect, therefore, to find in it a determinate, but at the same time, infinite, number of parts—which is self-contradictory. For we should thus have a whole containing a series of members which could not be completed in any regress—which is infinite, and at the same time complete in an organized composite. Infinite divisibility is applicable only to a quantum continuum, and is based entirely on the infinite divisibility of space, But in a quantum discretum the multitude of parts or units is always determined, and hence always equal to some number. To what extent a body may be organized, experience alone can inform us; and although, so far as our experience of this or that body has extended, we may not have discovered any inorganic part, such parts must exist in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of a phenomenon must extend, we cannot know from experience—it is a question which experience cannot answer; it is answered only by the principle of reason which forbids us to consider the empirical regress, in the analysis of extended body, as ever absolutely complete.\n\nConcluding Remark on the Solution of the Transcendental Mathematical Ideas—and Introductory to the Solution of the Dynamical Ideas.\n\nWe presented the antinomy of pure reason in a tabular form, and we endeavoured to show the ground of this self-contradiction on the part of reason, and the only means of bringing it to a conclusion—namely, by declaring both contradictory statements to be false. We represented in these antinomies the conditions of phenomena as belonging to the conditioned according to relations of space and time—which is the usual supposition of the common understanding. In this respect, all dialectical representations of totality, in the series of conditions to a given conditioned, were perfectly homogeneous. The condition was always a member of the series along with the conditioned, and thus the homogeneity of the whole series was assured. In this case the regress could never be cogitated as complete; or, if this was the case, a member really conditioned was falsely regarded as a primal member, consequently as unconditioned. In such an antinomy, therefore, we did not consider the object, that is, the conditioned, but the series of conditions belonging to the object, and the magnitude of that series. And thus arose the difficulty—a difficulty not to be settled by any decision regarding the claims of the two parties, but simply by cutting the knot—by declaring the series proposed by reason to be either too long or too short for the understanding, which could in neither case make its conceptions adequate with the ideas.\n\nBut we have overlooked, up to this point, an essential difference existing between the conceptions of the understanding which reason endeavours to raise to the rank of ideas—two of these indicating a mathematical, and two a dynamical synthesis of phenomena. Hitherto, it was necessary to signalize this distinction; for, just as in our general representation of all transcendental ideas, we considered them under phenomenal conditions, so, in the two mathematical ideas, our discussion is concerned solely with an object in the world of phenomena. But as we are now about to proceed to the consideration of the dynamical conceptions of the understanding, and their adequateness with ideas, we must not lose sight of this distinction. We shall find that it opens up to us an entirely new view of the conflict in which reason is involved. For, while in the first two antinomies, both parties were dismissed, on the ground of having advanced statements based upon false hypothesis; in the present case the hope appears of discovering a hypothesis which may be consistent with the demands of reason, and, the judge completing the statement of the grounds of claim, which both parties had left in an unsatisfactory state, the question may be settled on its own merits, not by dismissing the claimants, but by a comparison of the arguments on both sides. If we consider merely their extension, and whether they are adequate with ideas, the series of conditions may be regarded as all homogeneous. But the conception of the understanding which lies at the basis of these ideas, contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous (presupposed in every quantity—in its composition as well as in its division) or of the heterogeneous, which is the case in the dynamical synthesis of cause and effect, as well as of the necessary and the contingent.\n\nThus it happens that in the mathematical series of phenomena no other than a sensuous condition is admissible—a condition which is itself a member of the series; while the dynamical series of sensuous conditions admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a member of the series, but, as purely intelligible, lies out of and beyond it. And thus reason is satisfied, and an unconditioned placed at the head of the series of phenomena, without introducing confusion into or discontinuing it, contrary to the principles of the understanding.\n\nNow, from the fact that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of phenomena which does not form a part of the series of phenomena, arises a result which we should not have expected from an antinomy. In former cases, the result was that both contradictory dialectical statements were declared to be false. In the present case, we find the conditioned in the dynamical series connected with an empirically unconditioned, but non-sensuous condition; and thus satisfaction is done to the understanding on the one hand and to the reason on the other.[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-62) While, moreover, the dialectical arguments for unconditioned totality in mere phenomena fall to the ground, both propositions of reason may be shown to be true in their proper signification. This could not happen in the case of the cosmological ideas which demanded a mathematically unconditioned unity; for no condition could be placed at the head of the series of phenomena, except one which was itself a phenomenon and consequently a member of the series.\n\n[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-62) For the understanding cannot admit among phenomena a condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if it is possible to cogitate an intelligible condition—one which is not a member of the series of phenomena—for a conditioned phenomenon, without breaking the series of empirical conditions, such a condition may be admissible as empirically unconditioned, and the empirical regress continue regular, unceasing, and intact.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","text":"### II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition\n\nWhen I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to its conditions. The division of the parts of the whole (subdivisio or decompositio) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be actually attained and given to the mind, if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continuous decomposition are themselves divisible, the division, that is to say, the regress, proceeds from the conditioned to its conditions in infinitum; because the conditions (the parts) are themselves contained in the conditioned, and, as the latter is given in a limited intuition, the former are all given along with it. This regress cannot, therefore, be called a regressus in indefinitum, as happened in the case of the preceding cosmological idea, the regress in which proceeded from the conditioned to the conditions not given contemporaneously and along with it, but discoverable only through the empirical regress. We are not, however, entitled to affirm of a whole of this kind, which is divisible in infinitum, that it consists of an infinite number of parts. For, although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not contained therein. The division is contained only in the progressing decomposition—in the regress itself, which is the condition of the possibility and actuality of the series. Now, as this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains must be contained in the given whole as an aggregate. But the complete series of division is not contained therein. For this series, being infinite in succession and always incomplete, cannot represent an infinite number of members, and still less a composition of these members into a whole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","text":"To apply this remark to space. Every limited part of space presented to intuition is a whole, the parts of which are always spaces—to whatever extent subdivided. Every limited space is hence divisible to infinity.\n\nLet us again apply the remark to an external phenomenon enclosed in limits, that is, a body. The divisibility of a body rests upon the divisibility of space, which is the condition of the possibility of the body as an extended whole. A body is consequently divisible to infinity, though it does not, for that reason, consist of an infinite number of parts.\n\nIt certainly seems that, as a body must be cogitated as substance in space, the law of divisibility would not be applicable to it as substance. For we may and ought to grant, in the case of space, that division or decomposition, to any extent, never can utterly annihilate composition (that is to say, the smallest part of space must still consist of spaces); otherwise space would entirely cease to exist—which is impossible. But, the assertion on the other hand that when all composition in matter is annihilated in thought, nothing remains, does not seem to harmonize with the conception of substance, which must be properly the subject of all composition and must remain, even after the conjunction of its attributes in space—which constituted a body—is annihilated in thought. But this is not the case with substance in the phenomenal world, which is not a thing in itself cogitated by the pure category. Phenomenal substance is not an absolute subject; it is merely a permanent sensuous image, and nothing more than an intuition, in which the unconditioned is not to be found.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","text":"But, although this rule of progress to infinity is legitimate and applicable to the subdivision of a phenomenon, as a mere occupation or filling of space, it is not applicable to a whole consisting of a number of distinct parts and constituting a quantum discretum—that is to say, an organized body. It cannot be admitted that every part in an organized whole is itself organized, and that, in analysing it to infinity, we must always meet with organized parts; although we may allow that the parts of the matter which we decompose in infinitum, may be organized. For the infinity of the division of a phenomenon in space rests altogether on the fact that the divisibility of a phenomenon is given only in and through this infinity, that is, an undetermined number of parts is given, while the parts themselves are given and determined only in and through the subdivision; in a word, the infinity of the division necessarily presupposes that the whole is not already divided in se. Hence our division determines a number of parts in the whole—a number which extends just as far as the actual regress in the division; while, on the other hand, the very notion of a body organized to infinity represents the whole as already and in itself divided. We expect, therefore, to find in it a determinate, but at the same time, infinite, number of parts—which is self-contradictory. For we should thus have a whole containing a series of members which could not be completed in any regress—which is infinite, and at the same time complete in an organized composite. Infinite divisibility is applicable only to a quantum continuum, and is based entirely on the infinite divisibility of space, But in a quantum discretum the multitude of parts or units is always determined, and hence always equal to some number. To what extent a body may be organized, experience alone can inform us; and although, so far as our experience of this or that body has extended, we may not have discovered any inorganic part, such parts must exist in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of a phenomenon must extend, we cannot know from experience—it is a question which experience cannot answer; it is answered only by the principle of reason which forbids us to consider the empirical regress, in the analysis of extended body, as ever absolutely complete.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","text":"Concluding Remark on the Solution of the Transcendental Mathematical Ideas—and Introductory to the Solution of the Dynamical Ideas.\n\nWe presented the antinomy of pure reason in a tabular form, and we endeavoured to show the ground of this self-contradiction on the part of reason, and the only means of bringing it to a conclusion—namely, by declaring both contradictory statements to be false. We represented in these antinomies the conditions of phenomena as belonging to the conditioned according to relations of space and time—which is the usual supposition of the common understanding. In this respect, all dialectical representations of totality, in the series of conditions to a given conditioned, were perfectly homogeneous. The condition was always a member of the series along with the conditioned, and thus the homogeneity of the whole series was assured. In this case the regress could never be cogitated as complete; or, if this was the case, a member really conditioned was falsely regarded as a primal member, consequently as unconditioned. In such an antinomy, therefore, we did not consider the object, that is, the conditioned, but the series of conditions belonging to the object, and the magnitude of that series. And thus arose the difficulty—a difficulty not to be settled by any decision regarding the claims of the two parties, but simply by cutting the knot—by declaring the series proposed by reason to be either too long or too short for the understanding, which could in neither case make its conceptions adequate with the ideas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","text":"But we have overlooked, up to this point, an essential difference existing between the conceptions of the understanding which reason endeavours to raise to the rank of ideas—two of these indicating a mathematical, and two a dynamical synthesis of phenomena. Hitherto, it was necessary to signalize this distinction; for, just as in our general representation of all transcendental ideas, we considered them under phenomenal conditions, so, in the two mathematical ideas, our discussion is concerned solely with an object in the world of phenomena. But as we are now about to proceed to the consideration of the dynamical conceptions of the understanding, and their adequateness with ideas, we must not lose sight of this distinction. We shall find that it opens up to us an entirely new view of the conflict in which reason is involved. For, while in the first two antinomies, both parties were dismissed, on the ground of having advanced statements based upon false hypothesis; in the present case the hope appears of discovering a hypothesis which may be consistent with the demands of reason, and, the judge completing the statement of the grounds of claim, which both parties had left in an unsatisfactory state, the question may be settled on its own merits, not by dismissing the claimants, but by a comparison of the arguments on both sides. If we consider merely their extension, and whether they are adequate with ideas, the series of conditions may be regarded as all homogeneous. But the conception of the understanding which lies at the basis of these ideas, contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous (presupposed in every quantity—in its composition as well as in its division) or of the heterogeneous, which is the case in the dynamical synthesis of cause and effect, as well as of the necessary and the contingent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","text":"Thus it happens that in the mathematical series of phenomena no other than a sensuous condition is admissible—a condition which is itself a member of the series; while the dynamical series of sensuous conditions admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a member of the series, but, as purely intelligible, lies out of and beyond it. And thus reason is satisfied, and an unconditioned placed at the head of the series of phenomena, without introducing confusion into or discontinuing it, contrary to the principles of the understanding.\n\nNow, from the fact that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of phenomena which does not form a part of the series of phenomena, arises a result which we should not have expected from an antinomy. In former cases, the result was that both contradictory dialectical statements were declared to be false. In the present case, we find the conditioned in the dynamical series connected with an empirically unconditioned, but non-sensuous condition; and thus satisfaction is done to the understanding on the one hand and to the reason on the other.[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-62) While, moreover, the dialectical arguments for unconditioned totality in mere phenomena fall to the ground, both propositions of reason may be shown to be true in their proper signification. This could not happen in the case of the cosmological ideas which demanded a mathematically unconditioned unity; for no condition could be placed at the head of the series of phenomena, except one which was itself a phenomenon and consequently a member of the series.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","text":"[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-62) For the understanding cannot admit among phenomena a condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if it is possible to cogitate an intelligible condition—one which is not a member of the series of phenomena—for a conditioned phenomenon, without breaking the series of empirical conditions, such a condition may be admissible as empirically unconditioned, and the empirical regress continue regular, unceasing, and intact.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8ac8e4ae394a76c792a3005be26083a71e5ff5495f63c1f0f8fc3bdec4b78bbd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","order":73,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IS_3","text":"### III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes\n\nThere are only two modes of causality cogitable—the causality of nature or of freedom. The first is the conjunction of a particular state with another preceding it in the world of sense, the former following the latter by virtue of a law. Now, as the causality of phenomena is subject to conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always existed, could not have produced an effect which would make its first appearance at a particular time, the causality of a cause must itself be an effect—must itself have begun to be, and therefore, according to the principle of the understanding, itself requires a cause.\n\nWe must understand, on the contrary, by the term freedom, in the cosmological sense, a faculty of the spontaneous origination of a state; the causality of which, therefore, is not subordinated to another cause determining it in time. Freedom is in this sense a pure transcendental idea, which, in the first place, contains no empirical element; the object of which, in the second place, cannot be given or determined in any experience, because it is a universal law of the very possibility of experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, that consequently the causality of a cause, being itself something that has happened, must also have a cause. In this view of the case, the whole field of experience, how far soever it may extend, contains nothing that is not subject to the laws of nature. But, as we cannot by this means attain to an absolute totality of conditions in reference to the series of causes and effects, reason creates the idea of a spontaneity, which can begin to act of itself, and without any external cause determining it to action, according to the natural law of causality.\n\nIt is especially remarkable that the practical conception of freedom is based upon the transcendental idea, and that the question of the possibility of the former is difficult only as it involves the consideration of the truth of the latter. Freedom, in the practical sense, is the independence of the will of coercion by sensuous impulses. A will is sensuous, in so far as it is pathologically affected (by sensuous impulses); it is termed animal (arbitrium brutum), when it is pathologically necessitated. The human will is certainly an arbitrium sensitivum, not brutum, but liberum; because sensuousness does not necessitate its action, a faculty existing in man of self-determination, independently of all sensuous coercion.\n\nIt is plain that, if all causality in the world of sense were natural—and natural only—every event would be determined by another according to necessary laws, and that, consequently, phenomena, in so far as they determine the will, must necessitate every action as a natural effect from themselves; and thus all practical freedom would fall to the ground with the transcendental idea. For the latter presupposes that although a certain thing has not happened, it ought to have happened, and that, consequently, its phenomenal cause was not so powerful and determinative as to exclude the causality of our will—a causality capable of producing effects independently of and even in opposition to the power of natural causes, and capable, consequently, of spontaneously originating a series of events.\n\nHere, too, we find it to be the case, as we generally found in the self-contradictions and perplexities of a reason which strives to pass the bounds of possible experience, that the problem is properly not physiological, but transcendental. The question of the possibility of freedom does indeed concern psychology; but, as it rests upon dialectical arguments of pure reason, its solution must engage the attention of transcendental philosophy. Before attempting this solution, a task which transcendental philosophy cannot decline, it will be advisable to make a remark with regard to its procedure in the settlement of the question.\n\nIf phenomena were things in themselves, and time and space forms of the existence of things, condition and conditioned would always be members of the same series; and thus would arise in the present case the antinomy common to all transcendental ideas—that their series is either too great or too small for the understanding. The dynamical ideas, which we are about to discuss in this and the following section, possess the peculiarity of relating to an object, not considered as a quantity, but as an existence; and thus, in the discussion of the present question, we may make abstraction of the quantity of the series of conditions, and consider merely the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned. The question, then, suggests itself, whether freedom is possible; and, if it is, whether it can consist with the universality of the natural law of causality; and, consequently, whether we enounce a proper disjunctive proposition when we say: “Every effect must have its origin either in nature or in freedom,” or whether both cannot exist together in the same event in different relations. The principle of an unbroken connection between all events in the phenomenal world, in accordance with the unchangeable laws of nature, is a well-established principle of transcendental analytic which admits of no exception. The question, therefore, is: “Whether an effect, determined according to the laws of nature, can at the same time be produced by a free agent, or whether freedom and nature mutually exclude each other?” And here, the common but fallacious hypothesis of the absolute reality of phenomena manifests its injurious influence in embarrassing the procedure of reason. For if phenomena are things in themselves, freedom is impossible. In this case, nature is the complete and all-sufficient cause of every event; and condition and conditioned, cause and effect are contained in the same series, and necessitated by the same law. If, on the contrary, phenomena are held to be, as they are in fact, nothing more than mere representations, connected with each other in accordance with empirical laws, they must have a ground which is not phenomenal. But the causality of such an intelligible cause is not determined or determinable by phenomena; although its effects, as phenomena, must be determined by other phenomenal existences. This cause and its causality exist therefore out of and apart from the series of phenomena; while its effects do exist and are discoverable in the series of empirical conditions. Such an effect may therefore be considered to be free in relation to its intelligible cause, and necessary in relation to the phenomena from which it is a necessary consequence—a distinction which, stated in this perfectly general and abstract manner, must appear in the highest degree subtle and obscure. The sequel will explain. It is sufficient, at present, to remark that, as the complete and unbroken connection of phenomena is an unalterable law of nature, freedom is impossible—on the supposition that phenomena are absolutely real. Hence those philosophers who adhere to the common opinion on this subject can never succeed in reconciling the ideas of nature and freedom.\n\n_Possibility of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nThat element in a sensuous object which is not itself sensuous, I may be allowed to term intelligible. If, accordingly, an object which must be regarded as a sensuous phenomenon possesses a faculty which is not an object of sensuous intuition, but by means of which it is capable of being the cause of phenomena, the causality of an object or existence of this kind may be regarded from two different points of view. It may be considered to be intelligible, as regards its action—the action of a thing which is a thing in itself, and sensuous, as regards its effects—the effects of a phenomenon belonging to the sensuous world. We should accordingly, have to form both an empirical and an intellectual conception of the causality of such a faculty or power—both, however, having reference to the same effect. This twofold manner of cogitating a power residing in a sensuous object does not run counter to any of the conceptions which we ought to form of the world of phenomena or of a possible experience. Phenomena—not being things in themselves—must have a transcendental object as a foundation, which determines them as mere representations; and there seems to be no reason why we should not ascribe to this transcendental object, in addition to the property of self-phenomenization, a causality whose effects are to be met with in the world of phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon. But every effective cause must possess a character, that is to say, a law of its causality, without which it would cease to be a cause. In the above case, then, every sensuous object would possess an empirical character, which guaranteed that its actions, as phenomena, stand in complete and harmonious connection, conformably to unvarying natural laws, with all other phenomena, and can be deduced from these, as conditions, and that they do thus, in connection with these, constitute a series in the order of nature. This sensuous object must, in the second place, possess an intelligible character, which guarantees it to be the cause of those actions, as phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon nor subordinate to the conditions of the world of sense. The former may be termed the character of the thing as a phenomenon, the latter the character of the thing as a thing in itself.\n\nNow this active subject would, in its character of intelligible subject, be subordinate to no conditions of time, for time is only a condition of phenomena, and not of things in themselves. No action would begin or cease to be in this subject; it would consequently be free from the law of all determination of time—the law of change, namely, that everything which happens must have a cause in the phenomena of a preceding state. In one word, the causality of the subject, in so far as it is intelligible, would not form part of the series of empirical conditions which determine and necessitate an event in the world of sense. Again, this intelligible character of a thing cannot be immediately cognized, because we can perceive nothing but phenomena, but it must be capable of being cogitated in harmony with the empirical character; for we always find ourselves compelled to place, in thought, a transcendental object at the basis of phenomena although we can never know what this object is in itself.\n\nIn virtue of its empirical character, this subject would at the same time be subordinate to all the empirical laws of causality, and, as a phenomenon and member of the sensuous world, its effects would have to be accounted for by a reference to preceding phenomena. Eternal phenomena must be capable of influencing it; and its actions, in accordance with natural laws, must explain to us how its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, is to be cognized in and by means of experience. In a word, all requisites for a complete and necessary determination of these actions must be presented to us by experience.\n\nIn virtue of its intelligible character, on the other hand (although we possess only a general conception of this character), the subject must be regarded as free from all sensuous influences, and from all phenomenal determination. Moreover, as nothing happens in this subject—for it is a noumenon, and there does not consequently exist in it any change, demanding the dynamical determination of time, and for the same reason no connection with phenomena as causes—this active existence must in its actions be free from and independent of natural necessity, for this necessity exists only in the world of phenomena. It would be quite correct to say that it originates or begins its effects in the world of sense from itself, although the action productive of these effects does not begin in itself. We should not be in this case affirming that these sensuous effects began to exist of themselves, because they are always determined by prior empirical conditions—by virtue of the empirical character, which is the phenomenon of the intelligible character—and are possible only as constituting a continuation of the series of natural causes. And thus nature and freedom, each in the complete and absolute signification of these terms, can exist, without contradiction or disagreement, in the same action.\n\n_Exposition of the Cosmological Idea of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nI have thought it advisable to lay before the reader at first merely a sketch of the solution of this transcendental problem, in order to enable him to form with greater ease a clear conception of the course which reason must adopt in the solution. I shall now proceed to exhibit the several momenta of this solution, and to consider them in their order.\n\nThe natural law that everything which happens must have a cause, that the causality of this cause, that is, the action of the cause (which cannot always have existed, but must be itself an event, for it precedes in time some effect which it has originated), must have itself a phenomenal cause, by which it is determined and, and, consequently, all events are empirically determined in an order of nature—this law, I say, which lies at the foundation of the possibility of experience, and of a connected system of phenomena or nature is a law of the understanding, from which no departure, and to which no exception, can be admitted. For to except even a single phenomenon from its operation is to exclude it from the sphere of possible experience and thus to admit it to be a mere fiction of thought or phantom of the brain.\n\nThus we are obliged to acknowledge the existence of a chain of causes, in which, however, absolute totality cannot be found. But we need not detain ourselves with this question, for it has already been sufficiently answered in our discussion of the antinomies into which reason falls, when it attempts to reach the unconditioned in the series of phenomena. If we permit ourselves to be deceived by the illusion of transcendental idealism, we shall find that neither nature nor freedom exists. Now the question is: “Whether, admitting the existence of natural necessity in the world of phenomena, it is possible to consider an effect as at the same time an effect of nature and an effect of freedom—or, whether these two modes of causality are contradictory and incompatible?”\n\nNo phenomenal cause can absolutely and of itself begin a series. Every action, in so far as it is productive of an event, is itself an event or occurrence, and presupposes another preceding state, in which its cause existed. Thus everything that happens is but a continuation of a series, and an absolute beginning is impossible in the sensuous world. The actions of natural causes are, accordingly, themselves effects, and presuppose causes preceding them in time. A primal action which forms an absolute beginning, is beyond the causal power of phenomena.\n\nNow, is it absolutely necessary that, granting that all effects are phenomena, the causality of the cause of these effects must also be a phenomenon and belong to the empirical world? Is it not rather possible that, although every effect in the phenomenal world must be connected with an empirical cause, according to the universal law of nature, this empirical causality may be itself the effect of a non-empirical and intelligible causality—its connection with natural causes remaining nevertheless intact? Such a causality would be considered, in reference to phenomena, as the primal action of a cause, which is in so far, therefore, not phenomenal, but, by reason of this faculty or power, intelligible; although it must, at the same time, as a link in the chain of nature, be regarded as belonging to the sensuous world.\n\nA belief in the reciprocal causality of phenomena is necessary, if we are required to look for and to present the natural conditions of natural events, that is to say, their causes. This being admitted as unexceptionably valid, the requirements of the understanding, which recognizes nothing but nature in the region of phenomena, are satisfied, and our physical explanations of physical phenomena may proceed in their regular course, without hindrance and without opposition. But it is no stumbling-block in the way, even assuming the idea to be a pure fiction, to admit that there are some natural causes in the possession of a faculty which is not empirical, but intelligible, inasmuch as it is not determined to action by empirical conditions, but purely and solely upon grounds brought forward by the understanding—this action being still, when the cause is phenomenized, in perfect accordance with the laws of empirical causality. Thus the acting subject, as a causal phenomenon, would continue to preserve a complete connection with nature and natural conditions; and the phenomenon only of the subject (with all its phenomenal causality) would contain certain conditions, which, if we ascend from the empirical to the transcendental object, must necessarily be regarded as intelligible. For, if we attend, in our inquiries with regard to causes in the world of phenomena, to the directions of nature alone, we need not trouble ourselves about the relation in which the transcendental subject, which is completely unknown to us, stands to these phenomena and their connection in nature. The intelligible ground of phenomena in this subject does not concern empirical questions. It has to do only with pure thought; and, although the effects of this thought and action of the pure understanding are discoverable in phenomena, these phenomena must nevertheless be capable of a full and complete explanation, upon purely physical grounds and in accordance with natural laws. And in this case we attend solely to their empirical and omit all consideration of their intelligible character (which is the transcendental cause of the former) as completely unknown, except in so far as it is exhibited by the latter as its empirical symbol. Now let us apply this to experience. Man is a phenomenon of the sensuous world and, at the same time, therefore, a natural cause, the causality of which must be regulated by empirical laws. As such, he must possess an empirical character, like all other natural phenomena. We remark this empirical character in his actions, which reveal the presence of certain powers and faculties. If we consider inanimate or merely animal nature, we can discover no reason for ascribing to ourselves any other than a faculty which is determined in a purely sensuous manner. But man, to whom nature reveals herself only through sense, cognizes himself not only by his senses, but also through pure apperception; and this in actions and internal determinations, which he cannot regard as sensuous impressions. He is thus to himself, on the one hand, a phenomenon, but on the other hand, in respect of certain faculties, a purely intelligible object—intelligible, because its action cannot be ascribed to sensuous receptivity. These faculties are understanding and reason. The latter, especially, is in a peculiar manner distinct from all empirically-conditioned faculties, for it employs ideas alone in the consideration of its objects, and by means of these determines the understanding, which then proceeds to make an empirical use of its own conceptions, which, like the ideas of reason, are pure and non-empirical.\n\nThat reason possesses the faculty of causality, or that at least we are compelled so to represent it, is evident from the imperatives, which in the sphere of the practical we impose on many of our executive powers. The words I ought express a species of necessity, and imply a connection with grounds which nature does not and cannot present to the mind of man. Understanding knows nothing in nature but that which is, or has been, or will be. It would be absurd to say that anything in nature ought to be other than it is in the relations of time in which it stands; indeed, the ought, when we consider merely the course of nature, has neither application nor meaning. The question, “What ought to happen in the sphere of nature?” is just as absurd as the question, “What ought to be the properties of a circle?” All that we are entitled to ask is, “What takes place in nature?” or, in the latter case, “What are the properties of a circle?”\n\nBut the idea of an ought or of duty indicates a possible action, the ground of which is a pure conception; while the ground of a merely natural action is, on the contrary, always a phenomenon. This action must certainly be possible under physical conditions, if it is prescribed by the moral imperative ought; but these physical or natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will itself, they relate to its effects alone, and the consequences of the effect in the world of phenomena. Whatever number of motives nature may present to my will, whatever sensuous impulses—the moral ought it is beyond their power to produce. They may produce a volition, which, so far from being necessary, is always conditioned—a volition to which the ought enunciated by reason, sets an aim and a standard, gives permission or prohibition. Be the object what it may, purely sensuous—as pleasure, or presented by pure reason—as good, reason will not yield to grounds which have an empirical origin. Reason will not follow the order of things presented by experience, but, with perfect spontaneity, rearranges them according to ideas, with which it compels empirical conditions to agree. It declares, in the name of these ideas, certain actions to be necessary which nevertheless have not taken place and which perhaps never will take place; and yet presupposes that it possesses the faculty of causality in relation to these actions. For, in the absence of this supposition, it could not expect its ideas to produce certain effects in the world of experience.\n\nNow, let us stop here and admit it to be at least possible that reason does stand in a really causal relation to phenomena. In this case it must—pure reason as it is—exhibit an empirical character. For every cause supposes a rule, according to which certain phenomena follow as effects from the cause, and every rule requires uniformity in these effects; and this is the proper ground of the conception of a cause—as a faculty or power. Now this conception (of a cause) may be termed the empirical character of reason; and this character is a permanent one, while the effects produced appear, in conformity with the various conditions which accompany and partly limit them, in various forms.\n\nThus the volition of every man has an empirical character, which is nothing more than the causality of his reason, in so far as its effects in the phenomenal world manifest the presence of a rule, according to which we are enabled to examine, in their several kinds and degrees, the actions of this causality and the rational grounds for these actions, and in this way to decide upon the subjective principles of the volition. Now we learn what this empirical character is only from phenomenal effects, and from the rule of these which is presented by experience; and for this reason all the actions of man in the world of phenomena are determined by his empirical character, and the co-operative causes of nature. If, then, we could investigate all the phenomena of human volition to their lowest foundation in the mind, there would be no action which we could not anticipate with certainty, and recognize to be absolutely necessary from its preceding conditions. So far as relates to this empirical character, therefore, there can be no freedom; and it is only in the light of this character that we can consider the human will, when we confine ourselves to simple observation and, as is the case in anthropology, institute a physiological investigation of the motive causes of human actions.\n\nBut when we consider the same actions in relation to reason—not for the purpose of explaining their origin, that is, in relation to speculative reason, but to practical reason, as the producing cause of these actions—we shall discover a rule and an order very different from those of nature and experience. For the declaration of this mental faculty may be that what has and could not but take place in the course of nature, ought not to have taken place. Sometimes, too, we discover, or believe that we discover, that the ideas of reason did actually stand in a causal relation to certain actions of man; and that these actions have taken place because they were determined, not by empirical causes, but by the act of the will upon grounds of reason.\n\nNow, granting that reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena; can an action of reason be called free, when we know that, sensuously, in its empirical character, it is completely determined and absolutely necessary? But this empirical character is itself determined by the intelligible character. The latter we cannot cognize; we can only indicate it by means of phenomena, which enable us to have an immediate cognition only of the empirical character.[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-63) An action, then, in so far as it is to be ascribed to an intelligible cause, does not result from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, not the conditions of pure reason, but only their effects in the internal sense, precede the act. Pure reason, as a purely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the conditions of time. The causality of reason in its intelligible character does not begin to be; it does not make its appearance at a certain time, for the purpose of producing an effect. If this were not the case, the causality of reason would be subservient to the natural law of phenomena, which determines them according to time, and as a series of causes and effects in time; it would consequently cease to be freedom and become a part of nature. We are therefore justified in saying: “If reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena, it is a faculty which originates the sensuous condition of an empirical series of effects.” For the condition, which resides in the reason, is non-sensuous, and therefore cannot be originated, or begin to be. And thus we find—what we could not discover in any empirical series—a condition of a successive series of events itself empirically unconditioned. For, in the present case, the condition stands out of and beyond the series of phenomena—it is intelligible, and it consequently cannot be subjected to any sensuous condition, or to any time-determination by a preceding cause.\n\n[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-63) The real morality of actions—their merit or demerit, and even that of our own conduct, is completely unknown to us. Our estimates can relate only to their empirical character. How much is the result of the action of free will, how much is to be ascribed to nature and to blameless error, or to a happy constitution of temperament (merito fortunae), no one can discover, nor, for this reason, determine with perfect justice.\n\nBut, in another respect, the same cause belongs also to the series of phenomena. Man is himself a phenomenon. His will has an empirical character, which is the empirical cause of all his actions. There is no condition—determining man and his volition in conformity with this character—which does not itself form part of the series of effects in nature, and is subject to their law—the law according to which an empirically undetermined cause of an event in time cannot exist. For this reason no given action can have an absolute and spontaneous origination, all actions being phenomena, and belonging to the world of experience. But it cannot be said of reason, that the state in which it determines the will is always preceded by some other state determining it. For reason is not a phenomenon, and therefore not subject to sensuous conditions; and, consequently, even in relation to its causality, the sequence or conditions of time do not influence reason, nor can the dynamical law of nature, which determines the sequence of time according to certain rules, be applied to it.\n\nReason is consequently the permanent condition of all actions of the human will. Each of these is determined in the empirical character of the man, even before it has taken place. The intelligible character, of which the former is but the sensuous schema, knows no before or after; and every action, irrespective of the time-relation in which it stands with other phenomena, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which, consequently, enjoys freedom of action, and is not dynamically determined either by internal or external preceding conditions. This freedom must not be described, in a merely negative manner, as independence of empirical conditions, for in this case the faculty of reason would cease to be a cause of phenomena; but it must be regarded, positively, as a faculty which can spontaneously originate a series of events. At the same time, it must not be supposed that any beginning can take place in reason; on the contrary, reason, as the unconditioned condition of all action of the will, admits of no time-conditions, although its effect does really begin in a series of phenomena—a beginning which is not, however, absolutely primal.\n\nI shall illustrate this regulative principle of reason by an example, from its employment in the world of experience; proved it cannot be by any amount of experience, or by any number of facts, for such arguments cannot establish the truth of transcendental propositions. Let us take a voluntary action—for example, a falsehood—by means of which a man has introduced a certain degree of confusion into the social life of humanity, which is judged according to the motives from which it originated, and the blame of which and of the evil consequences arising from it, is imputed to the offender. We at first proceed to examine the empirical character of the offence, and for this purpose we endeavour to penetrate to the sources of that character, such as a defective education, bad company, a shameless and wicked disposition, frivolity, and want of reflection—not forgetting also the occasioning causes which prevailed at the moment of the transgression. In this the procedure is exactly the same as that pursued in the investigation of the series of causes which determine a given physical effect. Now, although we believe the action to have been determined by all these circumstances, we do not the less blame the offender. We do not blame him for his unhappy disposition, nor for the circumstances which influenced him, nay, not even for his former course of life; for we presuppose that all these considerations may be set aside, that the series of preceding conditions may be regarded as having never existed, and that the action may be considered as completely unconditioned in relation to any state preceding, just as if the agent commenced with it an entirely new series of effects. Our blame of the offender is grounded upon a law of reason, which requires us to regard this faculty as a cause, which could have and ought to have otherwise determined the behaviour of the culprit, independently of all empirical conditions. This causality of reason we do not regard as a co-operating agency, but as complete in itself. It matters not whether the sensuous impulses favoured or opposed the action of this causality, the offence is estimated according to its intelligible character—the offender is decidedly worthy of blame, the moment he utters a falsehood. It follows that we regard reason, in spite of the empirical conditions of the act, as completely free, and therefore, as in the present case, culpable.\n\nThe above judgement is complete evidence that we are accustomed to think that reason is not affected by sensuous conditions, that in it no change takes place—although its phenomena, in other words, the mode in which it appears in its effects, are subject to change—that in it no preceding state determines the following, and, consequently, that it does not form a member of the series of sensuous conditions which necessitate phenomena according to natural laws. Reason is present and the same in all human actions and at all times; but it does not itself exist in time, and therefore does not enter upon any state in which it did not formerly exist. It is, relatively to new states or conditions, determining, but not determinable. Hence we cannot ask: “Why did not reason determine itself in a different manner?” The question ought to be thus stated: “Why did not reason employ its power of causality to determine certain phenomena in a different manner?” But this is a question which admits of no answer. For a different intelligible character would have exhibited a different empirical character; and, when we say that, in spite of the course which his whole former life has taken, the offender could have refrained from uttering the falsehood, this means merely that the act was subject to the power and authority—permissive or prohibitive—of reason. Now, reason is not subject in its causality to any conditions of phenomena or of time; and a difference in time may produce a difference in the relation of phenomena to each other—for these are not things and therefore not causes in themselves—but it cannot produce any difference in the relation in which the action stands to the faculty of reason.\n\nThus, then, in our investigation into free actions and the causal power which produced them, we arrive at an intelligible cause, beyond which, however, we cannot go; although we can recognize that it is free, that is, independent of all sensuous conditions, and that, in this way, it may be the sensuously unconditioned condition of phenomena. But for what reason the intelligible character generates such and such phenomena and exhibits such and such an empirical character under certain circumstances, it is beyond the power of our reason to decide. The question is as much above the power and the sphere of reason as the following would be: “Why does the transcendental object of our external sensuous intuition allow of no other form than that of intuition in space?” But the problem, which we were called upon to solve, does not require us to entertain any such questions. The problem was merely this—whether freedom and natural necessity can exist without opposition in the same action. To this question we have given a sufficient answer; for we have shown that, as the former stands in a relation to a different kind of condition from those of the latter, the law of the one does not affect the law of the other and that, consequently, both can exist together in independence of and without interference with each other.\n\nThe reader must be careful to remark that my intention in the above remarks has not been to prove the actual existence of freedom, as a faculty in which resides the cause of certain sensuous phenomena. For, not to mention that such an argument would not have a transcendental character, nor have been limited to the discussion of pure conceptions—all attempts at inferring from experience what cannot be cogitated in accordance with its laws, must ever be unsuccessful. Nay, more, I have not even aimed at demonstrating the possibility of freedom; for this too would have been a vain endeavour, inasmuch as it is beyond the power of the mind to cognize the possibility of a reality or of a causal power by the aid of mere à priori conceptions. Freedom has been considered in the foregoing remarks only as a transcendental idea, by means of which reason aims at originating a series of conditions in the world of phenomena with the help of that which is sensuously unconditioned, involving itself, however, in an antinomy with the laws which itself prescribes for the conduct of the understanding. That this antinomy is based upon a mere illusion, and that nature and freedom are at least not opposed—this was the only thing in our power to prove, and the question which it was our task to solve.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0001","text":"### III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes\n\nThere are only two modes of causality cogitable—the causality of nature or of freedom. The first is the conjunction of a particular state with another preceding it in the world of sense, the former following the latter by virtue of a law. Now, as the causality of phenomena is subject to conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always existed, could not have produced an effect which would make its first appearance at a particular time, the causality of a cause must itself be an effect—must itself have begun to be, and therefore, according to the principle of the understanding, itself requires a cause.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0002","text":"We must understand, on the contrary, by the term freedom, in the cosmological sense, a faculty of the spontaneous origination of a state; the causality of which, therefore, is not subordinated to another cause determining it in time. Freedom is in this sense a pure transcendental idea, which, in the first place, contains no empirical element; the object of which, in the second place, cannot be given or determined in any experience, because it is a universal law of the very possibility of experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, that consequently the causality of a cause, being itself something that has happened, must also have a cause. In this view of the case, the whole field of experience, how far soever it may extend, contains nothing that is not subject to the laws of nature. But, as we cannot by this means attain to an absolute totality of conditions in reference to the series of causes and effects, reason creates the idea of a spontaneity, which can begin to act of itself, and without any external cause determining it to action, according to the natural law of causality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0003","text":"It is especially remarkable that the practical conception of freedom is based upon the transcendental idea, and that the question of the possibility of the former is difficult only as it involves the consideration of the truth of the latter. Freedom, in the practical sense, is the independence of the will of coercion by sensuous impulses. A will is sensuous, in so far as it is pathologically affected (by sensuous impulses); it is termed animal (arbitrium brutum), when it is pathologically necessitated. The human will is certainly an arbitrium sensitivum, not brutum, but liberum; because sensuousness does not necessitate its action, a faculty existing in man of self-determination, independently of all sensuous coercion.\n\nIt is plain that, if all causality in the world of sense were natural—and natural only—every event would be determined by another according to necessary laws, and that, consequently, phenomena, in so far as they determine the will, must necessitate every action as a natural effect from themselves; and thus all practical freedom would fall to the ground with the transcendental idea. For the latter presupposes that although a certain thing has not happened, it ought to have happened, and that, consequently, its phenomenal cause was not so powerful and determinative as to exclude the causality of our will—a causality capable of producing effects independently of and even in opposition to the power of natural causes, and capable, consequently, of spontaneously originating a series of events.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0004","text":"Here, too, we find it to be the case, as we generally found in the self-contradictions and perplexities of a reason which strives to pass the bounds of possible experience, that the problem is properly not physiological, but transcendental. The question of the possibility of freedom does indeed concern psychology; but, as it rests upon dialectical arguments of pure reason, its solution must engage the attention of transcendental philosophy. Before attempting this solution, a task which transcendental philosophy cannot decline, it will be advisable to make a remark with regard to its procedure in the settlement of the question.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","text":"If phenomena were things in themselves, and time and space forms of the existence of things, condition and conditioned would always be members of the same series; and thus would arise in the present case the antinomy common to all transcendental ideas—that their series is either too great or too small for the understanding. The dynamical ideas, which we are about to discuss in this and the following section, possess the peculiarity of relating to an object, not considered as a quantity, but as an existence; and thus, in the discussion of the present question, we may make abstraction of the quantity of the series of conditions, and consider merely the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned. The question, then, suggests itself, whether freedom is possible; and, if it is, whether it can consist with the universality of the natural law of causality; and, consequently, whether we enounce a proper disjunctive proposition when we say: “Every effect must have its origin either in nature or in freedom,” or whether both cannot exist together in the same event in different relations. The principle of an unbroken connection between all events in the phenomenal world, in accordance with the unchangeable laws of nature, is a well-established principle of transcendental analytic which admits of no exception. The question, therefore, is: “Whether an effect, determined according to the laws of nature, can at the same time be produced by a free agent, or whether freedom and nature mutually exclude each other?” And here, the common but fallacious hypothesis of the absolute reality of phenomena manifests its injurious influence in embarrassing the procedure of reason. For if phenomena are things in themselves, freedom is impossible. In this case, nature is the complete and all-sufficient cause of every event; and condition and conditioned, cause and effect are contained in the same series, and necessitated by the same law. If, on the contrary, phenomena are held to be, as they are in fact, nothing more than mere representations, connected with each other in accordance with empirical laws, they must have a ground which is not phenomenal. But the causality of such an intelligible cause is not determined or determinable by phenomena; although its effects, as phenomena, must be determined by other phenomenal existences. This cause and its causality exist therefore out of and apart from the series of phenomena; while its effects do exist and are discoverable in the series of empirical conditions. Such an effect may therefore be considered to be free in relation to its intelligible cause, and necessary in relation to the phenomena from which it is a necessary consequence—a distinction which, stated in this perfectly general and abstract manner, must appear in the highest degree subtle and obscure. The sequel will explain. It is sufficient, at present, to remark that, as the complete and unbroken connection of phenomena is an unalterable law of nature, freedom is impossible—on the supposition that phenomena are absolutely real. Hence those philosophers who adhere to the common opinion on this subject can never succeed in reconciling the ideas of nature and freedom.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","text":"_Possibility of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nThat element in a sensuous object which is not itself sensuous, I may be allowed to term intelligible. If, accordingly, an object which must be regarded as a sensuous phenomenon possesses a faculty which is not an object of sensuous intuition, but by means of which it is capable of being the cause of phenomena, the causality of an object or existence of this kind may be regarded from two different points of view. It may be considered to be intelligible, as regards its action—the action of a thing which is a thing in itself, and sensuous, as regards its effects—the effects of a phenomenon belonging to the sensuous world. We should accordingly, have to form both an empirical and an intellectual conception of the causality of such a faculty or power—both, however, having reference to the same effect. This twofold manner of cogitating a power residing in a sensuous object does not run counter to any of the conceptions which we ought to form of the world of phenomena or of a possible experience. Phenomena—not being things in themselves—must have a transcendental object as a foundation, which determines them as mere representations; and there seems to be no reason why we should not ascribe to this transcendental object, in addition to the property of self-phenomenization, a causality whose effects are to be met with in the world of phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon. But every effective cause must possess a character, that is to say, a law of its causality, without which it would cease to be a cause. In the above case, then, every sensuous object would possess an empirical character, which guaranteed that its actions, as phenomena, stand in complete and harmonious connection, conformably to unvarying natural laws, with all other phenomena, and can be deduced from these, as conditions, and that they do thus, in connection with these, constitute a series in the order of nature. This sensuous object must, in the second place, possess an intelligible character, which guarantees it to be the cause of those actions, as phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon nor subordinate to the conditions of the world of sense. The former may be termed the character of the thing as a phenomenon, the latter the character of the thing as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","text":"Now this active subject would, in its character of intelligible subject, be subordinate to no conditions of time, for time is only a condition of phenomena, and not of things in themselves. No action would begin or cease to be in this subject; it would consequently be free from the law of all determination of time—the law of change, namely, that everything which happens must have a cause in the phenomena of a preceding state. In one word, the causality of the subject, in so far as it is intelligible, would not form part of the series of empirical conditions which determine and necessitate an event in the world of sense. Again, this intelligible character of a thing cannot be immediately cognized, because we can perceive nothing but phenomena, but it must be capable of being cogitated in harmony with the empirical character; for we always find ourselves compelled to place, in thought, a transcendental object at the basis of phenomena although we can never know what this object is in itself.\n\nIn virtue of its empirical character, this subject would at the same time be subordinate to all the empirical laws of causality, and, as a phenomenon and member of the sensuous world, its effects would have to be accounted for by a reference to preceding phenomena. Eternal phenomena must be capable of influencing it; and its actions, in accordance with natural laws, must explain to us how its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, is to be cognized in and by means of experience. In a word, all requisites for a complete and necessary determination of these actions must be presented to us by experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","text":"In virtue of its intelligible character, on the other hand (although we possess only a general conception of this character), the subject must be regarded as free from all sensuous influences, and from all phenomenal determination. Moreover, as nothing happens in this subject—for it is a noumenon, and there does not consequently exist in it any change, demanding the dynamical determination of time, and for the same reason no connection with phenomena as causes—this active existence must in its actions be free from and independent of natural necessity, for this necessity exists only in the world of phenomena. It would be quite correct to say that it originates or begins its effects in the world of sense from itself, although the action productive of these effects does not begin in itself. We should not be in this case affirming that these sensuous effects began to exist of themselves, because they are always determined by prior empirical conditions—by virtue of the empirical character, which is the phenomenon of the intelligible character—and are possible only as constituting a continuation of the series of natural causes. And thus nature and freedom, each in the complete and absolute signification of these terms, can exist, without contradiction or disagreement, in the same action.\n\n_Exposition of the Cosmological Idea of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nI have thought it advisable to lay before the reader at first merely a sketch of the solution of this transcendental problem, in order to enable him to form with greater ease a clear conception of the course which reason must adopt in the solution. I shall now proceed to exhibit the several momenta of this solution, and to consider them in their order.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","text":"The natural law that everything which happens must have a cause, that the causality of this cause, that is, the action of the cause (which cannot always have existed, but must be itself an event, for it precedes in time some effect which it has originated), must have itself a phenomenal cause, by which it is determined and, and, consequently, all events are empirically determined in an order of nature—this law, I say, which lies at the foundation of the possibility of experience, and of a connected system of phenomena or nature is a law of the understanding, from which no departure, and to which no exception, can be admitted. For to except even a single phenomenon from its operation is to exclude it from the sphere of possible experience and thus to admit it to be a mere fiction of thought or phantom of the brain.\n\nThus we are obliged to acknowledge the existence of a chain of causes, in which, however, absolute totality cannot be found. But we need not detain ourselves with this question, for it has already been sufficiently answered in our discussion of the antinomies into which reason falls, when it attempts to reach the unconditioned in the series of phenomena. If we permit ourselves to be deceived by the illusion of transcendental idealism, we shall find that neither nature nor freedom exists. Now the question is: “Whether, admitting the existence of natural necessity in the world of phenomena, it is possible to consider an effect as at the same time an effect of nature and an effect of freedom—or, whether these two modes of causality are contradictory and incompatible?”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0010","text":"No phenomenal cause can absolutely and of itself begin a series. Every action, in so far as it is productive of an event, is itself an event or occurrence, and presupposes another preceding state, in which its cause existed. Thus everything that happens is but a continuation of a series, and an absolute beginning is impossible in the sensuous world. The actions of natural causes are, accordingly, themselves effects, and presuppose causes preceding them in time. A primal action which forms an absolute beginning, is beyond the causal power of phenomena.\n\nNow, is it absolutely necessary that, granting that all effects are phenomena, the causality of the cause of these effects must also be a phenomenon and belong to the empirical world? Is it not rather possible that, although every effect in the phenomenal world must be connected with an empirical cause, according to the universal law of nature, this empirical causality may be itself the effect of a non-empirical and intelligible causality—its connection with natural causes remaining nevertheless intact? Such a causality would be considered, in reference to phenomena, as the primal action of a cause, which is in so far, therefore, not phenomenal, but, by reason of this faculty or power, intelligible; although it must, at the same time, as a link in the chain of nature, be regarded as belonging to the sensuous world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","text":"A belief in the reciprocal causality of phenomena is necessary, if we are required to look for and to present the natural conditions of natural events, that is to say, their causes. This being admitted as unexceptionably valid, the requirements of the understanding, which recognizes nothing but nature in the region of phenomena, are satisfied, and our physical explanations of physical phenomena may proceed in their regular course, without hindrance and without opposition. But it is no stumbling-block in the way, even assuming the idea to be a pure fiction, to admit that there are some natural causes in the possession of a faculty which is not empirical, but intelligible, inasmuch as it is not determined to action by empirical conditions, but purely and solely upon grounds brought forward by the understanding—this action being still, when the cause is phenomenized, in perfect accordance with the laws of empirical causality. Thus the acting subject, as a causal phenomenon, would continue to preserve a complete connection with nature and natural conditions; and the phenomenon only of the subject (with all its phenomenal causality) would contain certain conditions, which, if we ascend from the empirical to the transcendental object, must necessarily be regarded as intelligible. For, if we attend, in our inquiries with regard to causes in the world of phenomena, to the directions of nature alone, we need not trouble ourselves about the relation in which the transcendental subject, which is completely unknown to us, stands to these phenomena and their connection in nature. The intelligible ground of phenomena in this subject does not concern empirical questions. It has to do only with pure thought; and, although the effects of this thought and action of the pure understanding are discoverable in phenomena, these phenomena must nevertheless be capable of a full and complete explanation, upon purely physical grounds and in accordance with natural laws. And in this case we attend solely to their empirical and omit all consideration of their intelligible character (which is the transcendental cause of the former) as completely unknown, except in so far as it is exhibited by the latter as its empirical symbol. Now let us apply this to experience. Man is a phenomenon of the sensuous world and, at the same time, therefore, a natural cause, the causality of which must be regulated by empirical laws. As such, he must possess an empirical character, like all other natural phenomena. We remark this empirical character in his actions, which reveal the presence of certain powers and faculties. If we consider inanimate or merely animal nature, we can discover no reason for ascribing to ourselves any other than a faculty which is determined in a purely sensuous manner. But man, to whom nature reveals herself only through sense, cognizes himself not only by his senses, but also through pure apperception; and this in actions and internal determinations, which he cannot regard as sensuous impressions. He is thus to himself, on the one hand, a phenomenon, but on the other hand, in respect of certain faculties, a purely intelligible object—intelligible, because its action cannot be ascribed to sensuous receptivity. These faculties are understanding and reason. The latter, especially, is in a peculiar manner distinct from all empirically-conditioned faculties, for it employs ideas alone in the consideration of its objects, and by means of these determines the understanding, which then proceeds to make an empirical use of its own conceptions, which, like the ideas of reason, are pure and non-empirical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0012","text":"That reason possesses the faculty of causality, or that at least we are compelled so to represent it, is evident from the imperatives, which in the sphere of the practical we impose on many of our executive powers. The words I ought express a species of necessity, and imply a connection with grounds which nature does not and cannot present to the mind of man. Understanding knows nothing in nature but that which is, or has been, or will be. It would be absurd to say that anything in nature ought to be other than it is in the relations of time in which it stands; indeed, the ought, when we consider merely the course of nature, has neither application nor meaning. The question, “What ought to happen in the sphere of nature?” is just as absurd as the question, “What ought to be the properties of a circle?” All that we are entitled to ask is, “What takes place in nature?” or, in the latter case, “What are the properties of a circle?”","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","text":"But the idea of an ought or of duty indicates a possible action, the ground of which is a pure conception; while the ground of a merely natural action is, on the contrary, always a phenomenon. This action must certainly be possible under physical conditions, if it is prescribed by the moral imperative ought; but these physical or natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will itself, they relate to its effects alone, and the consequences of the effect in the world of phenomena. Whatever number of motives nature may present to my will, whatever sensuous impulses—the moral ought it is beyond their power to produce. They may produce a volition, which, so far from being necessary, is always conditioned—a volition to which the ought enunciated by reason, sets an aim and a standard, gives permission or prohibition. Be the object what it may, purely sensuous—as pleasure, or presented by pure reason—as good, reason will not yield to grounds which have an empirical origin. Reason will not follow the order of things presented by experience, but, with perfect spontaneity, rearranges them according to ideas, with which it compels empirical conditions to agree. It declares, in the name of these ideas, certain actions to be necessary which nevertheless have not taken place and which perhaps never will take place; and yet presupposes that it possesses the faculty of causality in relation to these actions. For, in the absence of this supposition, it could not expect its ideas to produce certain effects in the world of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0014","text":"Now, let us stop here and admit it to be at least possible that reason does stand in a really causal relation to phenomena. In this case it must—pure reason as it is—exhibit an empirical character. For every cause supposes a rule, according to which certain phenomena follow as effects from the cause, and every rule requires uniformity in these effects; and this is the proper ground of the conception of a cause—as a faculty or power. Now this conception (of a cause) may be termed the empirical character of reason; and this character is a permanent one, while the effects produced appear, in conformity with the various conditions which accompany and partly limit them, in various forms.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","text":"Thus the volition of every man has an empirical character, which is nothing more than the causality of his reason, in so far as its effects in the phenomenal world manifest the presence of a rule, according to which we are enabled to examine, in their several kinds and degrees, the actions of this causality and the rational grounds for these actions, and in this way to decide upon the subjective principles of the volition. Now we learn what this empirical character is only from phenomenal effects, and from the rule of these which is presented by experience; and for this reason all the actions of man in the world of phenomena are determined by his empirical character, and the co-operative causes of nature. If, then, we could investigate all the phenomena of human volition to their lowest foundation in the mind, there would be no action which we could not anticipate with certainty, and recognize to be absolutely necessary from its preceding conditions. So far as relates to this empirical character, therefore, there can be no freedom; and it is only in the light of this character that we can consider the human will, when we confine ourselves to simple observation and, as is the case in anthropology, institute a physiological investigation of the motive causes of human actions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0016","text":"But when we consider the same actions in relation to reason—not for the purpose of explaining their origin, that is, in relation to speculative reason, but to practical reason, as the producing cause of these actions—we shall discover a rule and an order very different from those of nature and experience. For the declaration of this mental faculty may be that what has and could not but take place in the course of nature, ought not to have taken place. Sometimes, too, we discover, or believe that we discover, that the ideas of reason did actually stand in a causal relation to certain actions of man; and that these actions have taken place because they were determined, not by empirical causes, but by the act of the will upon grounds of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0017","text":"Now, granting that reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena; can an action of reason be called free, when we know that, sensuously, in its empirical character, it is completely determined and absolutely necessary? But this empirical character is itself determined by the intelligible character. The latter we cannot cognize; we can only indicate it by means of phenomena, which enable us to have an immediate cognition only of the empirical character.[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-63) An action, then, in so far as it is to be ascribed to an intelligible cause, does not result from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, not the conditions of pure reason, but only their effects in the internal sense, precede the act. Pure reason, as a purely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the conditions of time. The causality of reason in its intelligible character does not begin to be; it does not make its appearance at a certain time, for the purpose of producing an effect. If this were not the case, the causality of reason would be subservient to the natural law of phenomena, which determines them according to time, and as a series of causes and effects in time; it would consequently cease to be freedom and become a part of nature. We are therefore justified in saying: “If reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena, it is a faculty which originates the sensuous condition of an empirical series of effects.” For the condition, which resides in the reason, is non-sensuous, and therefore cannot be originated, or begin to be. And thus we find—what we could not discover in any empirical series—a condition of a successive series of events itself empirically unconditioned. For, in the present case, the condition stands out of and beyond the series of phenomena—it is intelligible, and it consequently cannot be subjected to any sensuous condition, or to any time-determination by a preceding cause.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0018","text":"[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-63) The real morality of actions—their merit or demerit, and even that of our own conduct, is completely unknown to us. Our estimates can relate only to their empirical character. How much is the result of the action of free will, how much is to be ascribed to nature and to blameless error, or to a happy constitution of temperament (merito fortunae), no one can discover, nor, for this reason, determine with perfect justice.\n\nBut, in another respect, the same cause belongs also to the series of phenomena. Man is himself a phenomenon. His will has an empirical character, which is the empirical cause of all his actions. There is no condition—determining man and his volition in conformity with this character—which does not itself form part of the series of effects in nature, and is subject to their law—the law according to which an empirically undetermined cause of an event in time cannot exist. For this reason no given action can have an absolute and spontaneous origination, all actions being phenomena, and belonging to the world of experience. But it cannot be said of reason, that the state in which it determines the will is always preceded by some other state determining it. For reason is not a phenomenon, and therefore not subject to sensuous conditions; and, consequently, even in relation to its causality, the sequence or conditions of time do not influence reason, nor can the dynamical law of nature, which determines the sequence of time according to certain rules, be applied to it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","text":"Reason is consequently the permanent condition of all actions of the human will. Each of these is determined in the empirical character of the man, even before it has taken place. The intelligible character, of which the former is but the sensuous schema, knows no before or after; and every action, irrespective of the time-relation in which it stands with other phenomena, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which, consequently, enjoys freedom of action, and is not dynamically determined either by internal or external preceding conditions. This freedom must not be described, in a merely negative manner, as independence of empirical conditions, for in this case the faculty of reason would cease to be a cause of phenomena; but it must be regarded, positively, as a faculty which can spontaneously originate a series of events. At the same time, it must not be supposed that any beginning can take place in reason; on the contrary, reason, as the unconditioned condition of all action of the will, admits of no time-conditions, although its effect does really begin in a series of phenomena—a beginning which is not, however, absolutely primal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","text":"I shall illustrate this regulative principle of reason by an example, from its employment in the world of experience; proved it cannot be by any amount of experience, or by any number of facts, for such arguments cannot establish the truth of transcendental propositions. Let us take a voluntary action—for example, a falsehood—by means of which a man has introduced a certain degree of confusion into the social life of humanity, which is judged according to the motives from which it originated, and the blame of which and of the evil consequences arising from it, is imputed to the offender. We at first proceed to examine the empirical character of the offence, and for this purpose we endeavour to penetrate to the sources of that character, such as a defective education, bad company, a shameless and wicked disposition, frivolity, and want of reflection—not forgetting also the occasioning causes which prevailed at the moment of the transgression. In this the procedure is exactly the same as that pursued in the investigation of the series of causes which determine a given physical effect. Now, although we believe the action to have been determined by all these circumstances, we do not the less blame the offender. We do not blame him for his unhappy disposition, nor for the circumstances which influenced him, nay, not even for his former course of life; for we presuppose that all these considerations may be set aside, that the series of preceding conditions may be regarded as having never existed, and that the action may be considered as completely unconditioned in relation to any state preceding, just as if the agent commenced with it an entirely new series of effects. Our blame of the offender is grounded upon a law of reason, which requires us to regard this faculty as a cause, which could have and ought to have otherwise determined the behaviour of the culprit, independently of all empirical conditions. This causality of reason we do not regard as a co-operating agency, but as complete in itself. It matters not whether the sensuous impulses favoured or opposed the action of this causality, the offence is estimated according to its intelligible character—the offender is decidedly worthy of blame, the moment he utters a falsehood. It follows that we regard reason, in spite of the empirical conditions of the act, as completely free, and therefore, as in the present case, culpable.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0021","text":"The above judgement is complete evidence that we are accustomed to think that reason is not affected by sensuous conditions, that in it no change takes place—although its phenomena, in other words, the mode in which it appears in its effects, are subject to change—that in it no preceding state determines the following, and, consequently, that it does not form a member of the series of sensuous conditions which necessitate phenomena according to natural laws. Reason is present and the same in all human actions and at all times; but it does not itself exist in time, and therefore does not enter upon any state in which it did not formerly exist. It is, relatively to new states or conditions, determining, but not determinable. Hence we cannot ask: “Why did not reason determine itself in a different manner?” The question ought to be thus stated: “Why did not reason employ its power of causality to determine certain phenomena in a different manner?” But this is a question which admits of no answer. For a different intelligible character would have exhibited a different empirical character; and, when we say that, in spite of the course which his whole former life has taken, the offender could have refrained from uttering the falsehood, this means merely that the act was subject to the power and authority—permissive or prohibitive—of reason. Now, reason is not subject in its causality to any conditions of phenomena or of time; and a difference in time may produce a difference in the relation of phenomena to each other—for these are not things and therefore not causes in themselves—but it cannot produce any difference in the relation in which the action stands to the faculty of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","text":"Thus, then, in our investigation into free actions and the causal power which produced them, we arrive at an intelligible cause, beyond which, however, we cannot go; although we can recognize that it is free, that is, independent of all sensuous conditions, and that, in this way, it may be the sensuously unconditioned condition of phenomena. But for what reason the intelligible character generates such and such phenomena and exhibits such and such an empirical character under certain circumstances, it is beyond the power of our reason to decide. The question is as much above the power and the sphere of reason as the following would be: “Why does the transcendental object of our external sensuous intuition allow of no other form than that of intuition in space?” But the problem, which we were called upon to solve, does not require us to entertain any such questions. The problem was merely this—whether freedom and natural necessity can exist without opposition in the same action. To this question we have given a sufficient answer; for we have shown that, as the former stands in a relation to a different kind of condition from those of the latter, the law of the one does not affect the law of the other and that, consequently, both can exist together in independence of and without interference with each other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","text":"The reader must be careful to remark that my intention in the above remarks has not been to prove the actual existence of freedom, as a faculty in which resides the cause of certain sensuous phenomena. For, not to mention that such an argument would not have a transcendental character, nor have been limited to the discussion of pure conceptions—all attempts at inferring from experience what cannot be cogitated in accordance with its laws, must ever be unsuccessful. Nay, more, I have not even aimed at demonstrating the possibility of freedom; for this too would have been a vain endeavour, inasmuch as it is beyond the power of the mind to cognize the possibility of a reality or of a causal power by the aid of mere à priori conceptions. Freedom has been considered in the foregoing remarks only as a transcendental idea, by means of which reason aims at originating a series of conditions in the world of phenomena with the help of that which is sensuously unconditioned, involving itself, however, in an antinomy with the laws which itself prescribes for the conduct of the understanding. That this antinomy is based upon a mere illusion, and that nature and freedom are at least not opposed—this was the only thing in our power to prove, and the question which it was our task to solve.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"66bee8ca39e0336573c359d715a09c281ec4aad7ccdbebf8e02f2153e8f802de","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","order":74,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_IS_4","text":"### IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences\n\nIn the preceding remarks, we considered the changes in the world of sense as constituting a dynamical series, in which each member is subordinated to another—as its cause. Our present purpose is to avail ourselves of this series of states or conditions as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of all changeable phenomena, that is, to a necessary being. Our endeavour to reach, not the unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence, of substance. The series before us is therefore a series of conceptions, and not of intuitions (in which the one intuition is the condition of the other).\n\nBut it is evident that, as all phenomena are subject to change and conditioned in their existence, the series of dependent existences cannot embrace an unconditioned member, the existence of which would be absolutely necessary. It follows that, if phenomena were things in themselves, and—as an immediate consequence from this supposition—condition and conditioned belonged to the same series of phenomena, the existence of a necessary being, as the condition of the existence of sensuous phenomena, would be perfectly impossible.\n\nAn important distinction, however, exists between the dynamical and the mathematical regress. The latter is engaged solely with the combination of parts into a whole, or with the division of a whole into its parts; and therefore are the conditions of its series parts of the series, and to be consequently regarded as homogeneous, and for this reason, as consisting, without exception, of phenomena. If the former regress, on the contrary, the aim of which is not to establish the possibility of an unconditioned whole consisting of given parts, or of an unconditioned part of a given whole, but to demonstrate the possibility of the deduction of a certain state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance from that which exists necessarily, it is not requisite that the condition should form part of an empirical series along with the conditioned.\n\nIn the case of the apparent antinomy with which we are at present dealing, there exists a way of escape from the difficulty; for it is not impossible that both of the contradictory statements may be true in different relations. All sensuous phenomena may be contingent, and consequently possess only an empirically conditioned existence, and yet there may also exist a non-empirical condition of the whole series, or, in other words, a necessary being. For this necessary being, as an intelligible condition, would not form a member—not even the highest member—of the series; the whole world of sense would be left in its empirically determined existence uninterfered with and uninfluenced. This would also form a ground of distinction between the modes of solution employed for the third and fourth antinomies. For, while in the consideration of freedom in the former antinomy, the thing itself—the cause (substantia phaenomenon)—was regarded as belonging to the series of conditions, and only its causality to the intelligible world—we are obliged in the present case to cogitate this necessary being as purely intelligible and as existing entirely apart from the world of sense (as an ens extramundanum); for otherwise it would be subject to the phenomenal law of contingency and dependence.\n\nIn relation to the present problem, therefore, the regulative principle of reason is that everything in the sensuous world possesses an empirically conditioned existence—that no property of the sensuous world possesses unconditioned necessity—that we are bound to expect, and, so far as is possible, to seek for the empirical condition of every member in the series of conditions—and that there is no sufficient reason to justify us in deducing any existence from a condition which lies out of and beyond the empirical series, or in regarding any existence as independent and self-subsistent; although this should not prevent us from recognizing the possibility of the whole series being based upon a being which is intelligible, and for this reason free from all empirical conditions.\n\nBut it has been far from my intention, in these remarks, to prove the existence of this unconditioned and necessary being, or even to evidence the possibility of a purely intelligible condition of the existence of all sensuous phenomena. As bounds were set to reason, to prevent it from leaving the guiding thread of empirical conditions and losing itself in transcendent theories which are incapable of concrete presentation; so it was my purpose, on the other hand, to set bounds to the law of the purely empirical understanding, and to protest against any attempts on its part at deciding on the possibility of things, or declaring the existence of the intelligible to be impossible, merely on the ground that it is not available for the explanation and exposition of phenomena. It has been shown, at the same time, that the contingency of all the phenomena of nature and their empirical conditions is quite consistent with the arbitrary hypothesis of a necessary, although purely intelligible condition, that no real contradiction exists between them and that, consequently, both may be true. The existence of such an absolutely necessary being may be impossible; but this can never be demonstrated from the universal contingency and dependence of sensuous phenomena, nor from the principle which forbids us to discontinue the series at some member of it, or to seek for its cause in some sphere of existence beyond the world of nature. Reason goes its way in the empirical world, and follows, too, its peculiar path in the sphere of the transcendental.\n\nThe sensuous world contains nothing but phenomena, which are mere representations, and always sensuously conditioned; things in themselves are not, and cannot be, objects to us. It is not to be wondered at, therefore, that we are not justified in leaping from some member of an empirical series beyond the world of sense, as if empirical representations were things in themselves, existing apart from their transcendental ground in the human mind, and the cause of whose existence may be sought out of the empirical series. This would certainly be the case with contingent things; but it cannot be with mere representations of things, the contingency of which is itself merely a phenomenon and can relate to no other regress than that which determines phenomena, that is, the empirical. But to cogitate an intelligible ground of phenomena, as free, moreover, from the contingency of the latter, conflicts neither with the unlimited nature of the empirical regress, nor with the complete contingency of phenomena. And the demonstration of this was the only thing necessary for the solution of this apparent antinomy. For if the condition of every conditioned—as regards its existence—is sensuous, and for this reason a part of the same series, it must be itself conditioned, as was shown in the antithesis of the fourth antinomy. The embarrassments into which a reason, which postulates the unconditioned, necessarily falls, must, therefore, continue to exist; or the unconditioned must be placed in the sphere of the intelligible. In this way, its necessity does not require, nor does it even permit, the presence of an empirical condition: and it is, consequently, unconditionally necessary.\n\nThe empirical employment of reason is not affected by the assumption of a purely intelligible being; it continues its operations on the principle of the contingency of all phenomena, proceeding from empirical conditions to still higher and higher conditions, themselves empirical. Just as little does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause, when the question regards merely the pure employment of reason—in relation to ends or aims. For, in this case, an intelligible cause signifies merely the transcendental and to us unknown ground of the possibility of sensuous phenomena, and its existence, necessary and independent of all sensuous conditions, is not inconsistent with the contingency of phenomena, or with the unlimited possibility of regress which exists in the series of empirical conditions.\n\nConcluding Remarks on the Antinomy of Pure Reason.\n\nSo long as the object of our rational conceptions is the totality of conditions in the world of phenomena, and the satisfaction, from this source, of the requirements of reason, so long are our ideas transcendental and cosmological. But when we set the unconditioned—which is the aim of all our inquiries—in a sphere which lies out of the world of sense and possible experience, our ideas become transcendent. They are then not merely serviceable towards the completion of the exercise of reason (which remains an idea, never executed, but always to be pursued); they detach themselves completely from experience and construct for themselves objects, the material of which has not been presented by experience, and the objective reality of which is not based upon the completion of the empirical series, but upon pure à priori conceptions. The intelligible object of these transcendent ideas may be conceded, as a transcendental object. But we cannot cogitate it as a thing determinable by certain distinct predicates relating to its internal nature, for it has no connection with empirical conceptions; nor are we justified in affirming the existence of any such object. It is, consequently, a mere product of the mind alone. Of all the cosmological ideas, however, it is that occasioning the fourth antinomy which compels us to venture upon this step. For the existence of phenomena, always conditioned and never self-subsistent, requires us to look for an object different from phenomena—an intelligible object, with which all contingency must cease. But, as we have allowed ourselves to assume the existence of a self-subsistent reality out of the field of experience, and are therefore obliged to regard phenomena as merely a contingent mode of representing intelligible objects employed by beings which are themselves intelligences—no other course remains for us than to follow analogy and employ the same mode in forming some conception of intelligible things, of which we have not the least knowledge, which nature taught us to use in the formation of empirical conceptions. Experience made us acquainted with the contingent. But we are at present engaged in the discussion of things which are not objects of experience; and must, therefore, deduce our knowledge of them from that which is necessary absolutely and in itself, that is, from pure conceptions. Hence the first step which we take out of the world of sense obliges us to begin our system of new cognition with the investigation of a necessary being, and to deduce from our conceptions of it all our conceptions of intelligible things. This we propose to attempt in the following chapter.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","text":"### IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences\n\nIn the preceding remarks, we considered the changes in the world of sense as constituting a dynamical series, in which each member is subordinated to another—as its cause. Our present purpose is to avail ourselves of this series of states or conditions as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of all changeable phenomena, that is, to a necessary being. Our endeavour to reach, not the unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence, of substance. The series before us is therefore a series of conceptions, and not of intuitions (in which the one intuition is the condition of the other).\n\nBut it is evident that, as all phenomena are subject to change and conditioned in their existence, the series of dependent existences cannot embrace an unconditioned member, the existence of which would be absolutely necessary. It follows that, if phenomena were things in themselves, and—as an immediate consequence from this supposition—condition and conditioned belonged to the same series of phenomena, the existence of a necessary being, as the condition of the existence of sensuous phenomena, would be perfectly impossible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","text":"An important distinction, however, exists between the dynamical and the mathematical regress. The latter is engaged solely with the combination of parts into a whole, or with the division of a whole into its parts; and therefore are the conditions of its series parts of the series, and to be consequently regarded as homogeneous, and for this reason, as consisting, without exception, of phenomena. If the former regress, on the contrary, the aim of which is not to establish the possibility of an unconditioned whole consisting of given parts, or of an unconditioned part of a given whole, but to demonstrate the possibility of the deduction of a certain state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance from that which exists necessarily, it is not requisite that the condition should form part of an empirical series along with the conditioned.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","text":"In the case of the apparent antinomy with which we are at present dealing, there exists a way of escape from the difficulty; for it is not impossible that both of the contradictory statements may be true in different relations. All sensuous phenomena may be contingent, and consequently possess only an empirically conditioned existence, and yet there may also exist a non-empirical condition of the whole series, or, in other words, a necessary being. For this necessary being, as an intelligible condition, would not form a member—not even the highest member—of the series; the whole world of sense would be left in its empirically determined existence uninterfered with and uninfluenced. This would also form a ground of distinction between the modes of solution employed for the third and fourth antinomies. For, while in the consideration of freedom in the former antinomy, the thing itself—the cause (substantia phaenomenon)—was regarded as belonging to the series of conditions, and only its causality to the intelligible world—we are obliged in the present case to cogitate this necessary being as purely intelligible and as existing entirely apart from the world of sense (as an ens extramundanum); for otherwise it would be subject to the phenomenal law of contingency and dependence.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","text":"In relation to the present problem, therefore, the regulative principle of reason is that everything in the sensuous world possesses an empirically conditioned existence—that no property of the sensuous world possesses unconditioned necessity—that we are bound to expect, and, so far as is possible, to seek for the empirical condition of every member in the series of conditions—and that there is no sufficient reason to justify us in deducing any existence from a condition which lies out of and beyond the empirical series, or in regarding any existence as independent and self-subsistent; although this should not prevent us from recognizing the possibility of the whole series being based upon a being which is intelligible, and for this reason free from all empirical conditions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","text":"But it has been far from my intention, in these remarks, to prove the existence of this unconditioned and necessary being, or even to evidence the possibility of a purely intelligible condition of the existence of all sensuous phenomena. As bounds were set to reason, to prevent it from leaving the guiding thread of empirical conditions and losing itself in transcendent theories which are incapable of concrete presentation; so it was my purpose, on the other hand, to set bounds to the law of the purely empirical understanding, and to protest against any attempts on its part at deciding on the possibility of things, or declaring the existence of the intelligible to be impossible, merely on the ground that it is not available for the explanation and exposition of phenomena. It has been shown, at the same time, that the contingency of all the phenomena of nature and their empirical conditions is quite consistent with the arbitrary hypothesis of a necessary, although purely intelligible condition, that no real contradiction exists between them and that, consequently, both may be true. The existence of such an absolutely necessary being may be impossible; but this can never be demonstrated from the universal contingency and dependence of sensuous phenomena, nor from the principle which forbids us to discontinue the series at some member of it, or to seek for its cause in some sphere of existence beyond the world of nature. Reason goes its way in the empirical world, and follows, too, its peculiar path in the sphere of the transcendental.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","text":"The sensuous world contains nothing but phenomena, which are mere representations, and always sensuously conditioned; things in themselves are not, and cannot be, objects to us. It is not to be wondered at, therefore, that we are not justified in leaping from some member of an empirical series beyond the world of sense, as if empirical representations were things in themselves, existing apart from their transcendental ground in the human mind, and the cause of whose existence may be sought out of the empirical series. This would certainly be the case with contingent things; but it cannot be with mere representations of things, the contingency of which is itself merely a phenomenon and can relate to no other regress than that which determines phenomena, that is, the empirical. But to cogitate an intelligible ground of phenomena, as free, moreover, from the contingency of the latter, conflicts neither with the unlimited nature of the empirical regress, nor with the complete contingency of phenomena. And the demonstration of this was the only thing necessary for the solution of this apparent antinomy. For if the condition of every conditioned—as regards its existence—is sensuous, and for this reason a part of the same series, it must be itself conditioned, as was shown in the antithesis of the fourth antinomy. The embarrassments into which a reason, which postulates the unconditioned, necessarily falls, must, therefore, continue to exist; or the unconditioned must be placed in the sphere of the intelligible. In this way, its necessity does not require, nor does it even permit, the presence of an empirical condition: and it is, consequently, unconditionally necessary.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","text":"The empirical employment of reason is not affected by the assumption of a purely intelligible being; it continues its operations on the principle of the contingency of all phenomena, proceeding from empirical conditions to still higher and higher conditions, themselves empirical. Just as little does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause, when the question regards merely the pure employment of reason—in relation to ends or aims. For, in this case, an intelligible cause signifies merely the transcendental and to us unknown ground of the possibility of sensuous phenomena, and its existence, necessary and independent of all sensuous conditions, is not inconsistent with the contingency of phenomena, or with the unlimited possibility of regress which exists in the series of empirical conditions.\n\nConcluding Remarks on the Antinomy of Pure Reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","text":"So long as the object of our rational conceptions is the totality of conditions in the world of phenomena, and the satisfaction, from this source, of the requirements of reason, so long are our ideas transcendental and cosmological. But when we set the unconditioned—which is the aim of all our inquiries—in a sphere which lies out of the world of sense and possible experience, our ideas become transcendent. They are then not merely serviceable towards the completion of the exercise of reason (which remains an idea, never executed, but always to be pursued); they detach themselves completely from experience and construct for themselves objects, the material of which has not been presented by experience, and the objective reality of which is not based upon the completion of the empirical series, but upon pure à priori conceptions. The intelligible object of these transcendent ideas may be conceded, as a transcendental object. But we cannot cogitate it as a thing determinable by certain distinct predicates relating to its internal nature, for it has no connection with empirical conceptions; nor are we justified in affirming the existence of any such object. It is, consequently, a mere product of the mind alone. Of all the cosmological ideas, however, it is that occasioning the fourth antinomy which compels us to venture upon this step. For the existence of phenomena, always conditioned and never self-subsistent, requires us to look for an object different from phenomena—an intelligible object, with which all contingency must cease. But, as we have allowed ourselves to assume the existence of a self-subsistent reality out of the field of experience, and are therefore obliged to regard phenomena as merely a contingent mode of representing intelligible objects employed by beings which are themselves intelligences—no other course remains for us than to follow analogy and employ the same mode in forming some conception of intelligible things, of which we have not the least knowledge, which nature taught us to use in the formation of empirical conceptions. Experience made us acquainted with the contingent. But we are at present engaged in the discussion of things which are not objects of experience; and must, therefore, deduce our knowledge of them from that which is necessary absolutely and in itself, that is, from pure conceptions. Hence the first step which we take out of the world of sense obliges us to begin our system of new cognition with the investigation of a necessary being, and to deduce from our conceptions of it all our conceptions of intelligible things. This we propose to attempt in the following chapter.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4719e7e830156bac8f7399f64684303ad42765f8df09801551f460b471d42628","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","order":75,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_3","text":"# Ideal Of Pure Reason\n\n### Chapter III. The Ideal of Pure Reason\n\n### Section I. Of the Ideal in General\n\nWe have seen that pure conceptions do not present objects to the mind, except under sensuous conditions; because the conditions of objective reality do not exist in these conceptions, which contain, in fact, nothing but the mere form of thought. They may, however, when applied to phenomena, be presented in concreto; for it is phenomena that present to them the materials for the formation of empirical conceptions, which are nothing more than concrete forms of the conceptions of the understanding. But ideas are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no phenomenon can ever present them to the human mind in concreto. They contain a certain perfection, attainable by no possible empirical cognition; and they give to reason a systematic unity, to which the unity of experience attempts to approximate, but can never completely attain.\n\nBut still further removed than the idea from objective reality is the Ideal, by which term I understand the idea, not in concreto, but in individuo—as an individual thing, determinable or determined by the idea alone. The idea of humanity in its complete perfection supposes not only the advancement of all the powers and faculties, which constitute our conception of human nature, to a complete attainment of their final aims, but also everything which is requisite for the complete determination of the idea; for of all contradictory predicates, only one can conform with the idea of the perfect man. What I have termed an ideal was in Plato’s philosophy an idea of the divine mind—an individual object present to its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings, and the archetype of all phenomenal existences.\n\nWithout rising to these speculative heights, we are bound to confess that human reason contains not only ideas, but ideals, which possess, not, like those of Plato, creative, but certainly practical power—as regulative principles, and form the basis of the perfectibility of certain actions. Moral conceptions are not perfectly pure conceptions of reason, because an empirical element—of pleasure or pain—lies at the foundation of them. In relation, however, to the principle, whereby reason sets bounds to a freedom which is in itself without law, and consequently when we attend merely to their form, they may be considered as pure conceptions of reason. Virtue and wisdom in their perfect purity are ideas. But the wise man of the Stoics is an ideal, that is to say, a human being existing only in thought and in complete conformity with the idea of wisdom. As the idea provides a rule, so the ideal serves as an archetype for the perfect and complete determination of the copy. Thus the conduct of this wise and divine man serves us as a standard of action, with which we may compare and judge ourselves, which may help us to reform ourselves, although the perfection it demands can never be attained by us. Although we cannot concede objective reality to these ideals, they are not to be considered as chimeras; on the contrary, they provide reason with a standard, which enables it to estimate, by comparison, the degree of incompleteness in the objects presented to it. But to aim at realizing the ideal in an example in the world of experience—to describe, for instance, the character of the perfectly wise man in a romance—is impracticable. Nay more, there is something absurd in the attempt; and the result must be little edifying, as the natural limitations, which are continually breaking in upon the perfection and completeness of the idea, destroy the illusion in the story and throw an air of suspicion even on what is good in the idea, which hence appears fictitious and unreal.\n\nSuch is the constitution of the ideal of reason, which is always based upon determinate conceptions, and serves as a rule and a model for limitation or of criticism. Very different is the nature of the ideals of the imagination. Of these it is impossible to present an intelligible conception; they are a kind of monogram, drawn according to no determinate rule, and forming rather a vague picture—the production of many diverse experiences—than a determinate image. Such are the ideals which painters and physiognomists profess to have in their minds, and which can serve neither as a model for production nor as a standard for appreciation. They may be termed, though improperly, sensuous ideals, as they are declared to be models of certain possible empirical intuitions. They cannot, however, furnish rules or standards for explanation or examination.\n\nIn its ideals, reason aims at complete and perfect determination according to à priori rules; and hence it cogitates an object, which must be completely determinable in conformity with principles, although all empirical conditions are absent, and the conception of the object is on this account transcendent.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","text":"# Ideal Of Pure Reason\n\n### Chapter III. The Ideal of Pure Reason\n\n### Section I. Of the Ideal in General\n\nWe have seen that pure conceptions do not present objects to the mind, except under sensuous conditions; because the conditions of objective reality do not exist in these conceptions, which contain, in fact, nothing but the mere form of thought. They may, however, when applied to phenomena, be presented in concreto; for it is phenomena that present to them the materials for the formation of empirical conceptions, which are nothing more than concrete forms of the conceptions of the understanding. But ideas are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no phenomenon can ever present them to the human mind in concreto. They contain a certain perfection, attainable by no possible empirical cognition; and they give to reason a systematic unity, to which the unity of experience attempts to approximate, but can never completely attain.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","text":"But still further removed than the idea from objective reality is the Ideal, by which term I understand the idea, not in concreto, but in individuo—as an individual thing, determinable or determined by the idea alone. The idea of humanity in its complete perfection supposes not only the advancement of all the powers and faculties, which constitute our conception of human nature, to a complete attainment of their final aims, but also everything which is requisite for the complete determination of the idea; for of all contradictory predicates, only one can conform with the idea of the perfect man. What I have termed an ideal was in Plato’s philosophy an idea of the divine mind—an individual object present to its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings, and the archetype of all phenomenal existences.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","text":"Without rising to these speculative heights, we are bound to confess that human reason contains not only ideas, but ideals, which possess, not, like those of Plato, creative, but certainly practical power—as regulative principles, and form the basis of the perfectibility of certain actions. Moral conceptions are not perfectly pure conceptions of reason, because an empirical element—of pleasure or pain—lies at the foundation of them. In relation, however, to the principle, whereby reason sets bounds to a freedom which is in itself without law, and consequently when we attend merely to their form, they may be considered as pure conceptions of reason. Virtue and wisdom in their perfect purity are ideas. But the wise man of the Stoics is an ideal, that is to say, a human being existing only in thought and in complete conformity with the idea of wisdom. As the idea provides a rule, so the ideal serves as an archetype for the perfect and complete determination of the copy. Thus the conduct of this wise and divine man serves us as a standard of action, with which we may compare and judge ourselves, which may help us to reform ourselves, although the perfection it demands can never be attained by us. Although we cannot concede objective reality to these ideals, they are not to be considered as chimeras; on the contrary, they provide reason with a standard, which enables it to estimate, by comparison, the degree of incompleteness in the objects presented to it. But to aim at realizing the ideal in an example in the world of experience—to describe, for instance, the character of the perfectly wise man in a romance—is impracticable. Nay more, there is something absurd in the attempt; and the result must be little edifying, as the natural limitations, which are continually breaking in upon the perfection and completeness of the idea, destroy the illusion in the story and throw an air of suspicion even on what is good in the idea, which hence appears fictitious and unreal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","text":"Such is the constitution of the ideal of reason, which is always based upon determinate conceptions, and serves as a rule and a model for limitation or of criticism. Very different is the nature of the ideals of the imagination. Of these it is impossible to present an intelligible conception; they are a kind of monogram, drawn according to no determinate rule, and forming rather a vague picture—the production of many diverse experiences—than a determinate image. Such are the ideals which painters and physiognomists profess to have in their minds, and which can serve neither as a model for production nor as a standard for appreciation. They may be termed, though improperly, sensuous ideals, as they are declared to be models of certain possible empirical intuitions. They cannot, however, furnish rules or standards for explanation or examination.\n\nIn its ideals, reason aims at complete and perfect determination according to à priori rules; and hence it cogitates an object, which must be completely determinable in conformity with principles, although all empirical conditions are absent, and the conception of the object is on this account transcendent.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"19866320f98a877efdf8e21763704d7f3041ee6f7fde6c857360f3f8691e4c15","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","order":76,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_4","text":"### Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)\n\nEvery conception is, in relation to that which is not contained in it, undetermined and subject to the principle of determinability. This principle is that, of every two contradictorily opposed predicates, only one can belong to a conception. It is a purely logical principle, itself based upon the principle of contradiction; inasmuch as it makes complete abstraction of the content and attends merely to the logical form of the cognition.\n\nBut again, everything, as regards its possibility, is also subject to the principle of complete determination, according to which one of all the possible contradictory predicates of things must belong to it. This principle is not based merely upon that of contradiction; for, in addition to the relation between two contradictory predicates, it regards everything as standing in a relation to the sum of possibilities, as the sum total of all predicates of things, and, while presupposing this sum as an à priori condition, presents to the mind everything as receiving the possibility of its individual existence from the relation it bears to, and the share it possesses in, the aforesaid sum of possibilities.[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-64) The principle of complete determination relates the content and not to the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all the predicates which are required to constitute the complete conception of a thing, and not a mere principle analytical representation, which enounces that one of two contradictory predicates must belong to a conception. It contains, moreover, a transcendental presupposition—that, namely, of the material for all possibility, which must contain à priori the data for this or that particular possibility.\n\n[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-64) Thus this principle declares everything to possess a relation to a common correlate—the sum-total of possibility, which, if discovered to exist in the idea of one individual thing, would establish the affinity of all possible things, from the identity of the ground of their complete determination. The determinability of every conception is subordinate to the universality (Allgemeinheit, universalitas) of the principle of excluded middle; the determination of a thing to the totality (Allheit, universitas) of all possible predicates.\n\nThe proposition, everything which exists is completely determined, means not only that one of every pair of given contradictory attributes, but that one of all possible attributes, is always predicable of the thing; in it the predicates are not merely compared logically with each other, but the thing itself is transcendentally compared with the sum-total of all possible predicates. The proposition is equivalent to saying: “To attain to a complete knowledge of a thing, it is necessary to possess a knowledge of everything that is possible, and to determine it thereby in a positive or negative manner.” The conception of complete determination is consequently a conception which cannot be presented in its totality in concreto, and is therefore based upon an idea, which has its seat in the reason—the faculty which prescribes to the understanding the laws of its harmonious and perfect exercise.\n\nNow, although this idea of the sum-total of all possibility, in so far as it forms the condition of the complete determination of everything, is itself undetermined in relation to the predicates which may constitute this sum-total, and we cogitate in it merely the sum-total of all possible predicates—we nevertheless find, upon closer examination, that this idea, as a primitive conception of the mind, excludes a large number of predicates—those deduced and those irreconcilable with others, and that it is evolved as a conception completely determined à priori. Thus it becomes the conception of an individual object, which is completely determined by and through the mere idea, and must consequently be termed an ideal of pure reason.\n\nWhen we consider all possible predicates, not merely logically, but transcendentally, that is to say, with reference to the content which may be cogitated as existing in them à priori, we shall find that some indicate a being, others merely a non-being. The logical negation expressed in the word not does not properly belong to a conception, but only to the relation of one conception to another in a judgement, and is consequently quite insufficient to present to the mind the content of a conception. The expression not mortal does not indicate that a non-being is cogitated in the object; it does not concern the content at all. A transcendental negation, on the contrary, indicates non-being in itself, and is opposed to transcendental affirmation, the conception of which of itself expresses a being. Hence this affirmation indicates a reality, because in and through it objects are considered to be something—to be things; while the opposite negation, on the other hand, indicates a mere want, or privation, or absence, and, where such negations alone are attached to a representation, the non-existence of anything corresponding to the representation.\n\nNow a negation cannot be cogitated as determined, without cogitating at the same time the opposite affirmation. The man born blind has not the least notion of darkness, because he has none of light; the vagabond knows nothing of poverty, because he has never known what it is to be in comfort;[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-65) the ignorant man has no conception of his ignorance, because he has no conception of knowledge. All conceptions of negatives are accordingly derived or deduced conceptions; and realities contain the data, and, so to speak, the material or transcendental content of the possibility and complete determination of all things.\n\n[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-65) The investigations and calculations of astronomers have taught us much that is wonderful; but the most important lesson we have received from them is the discovery of the abyss of our ignorance in relation to the universe—an ignorance the magnitude of which reason, without the information thus derived, could never have conceived. This discovery of our deficiencies must produce a great change in the determination of the aims of human reason.\n\nIf, therefore, a transcendental substratum lies at the foundation of the complete determination of things—a substratum which is to form the fund from which all possible predicates of things are to be supplied, this substratum cannot be anything else than the idea of a sum-total of reality (omnitudo realitatis). In this view, negations are nothing but limitations—a term which could not, with propriety, be applied to them, if the unlimited (the all) did not form the true basis of our conception.\n\nThis conception of a sum-total of reality is the conception of a thing in itself, regarded as completely determined; and the conception of an ens realissimum is the conception of an individual being, inasmuch as it is determined by that predicate of all possible contradictory predicates, which indicates and belongs to being. It is, therefore, a transcendental ideal which forms the basis of the complete determination of everything that exists, and is the highest material condition of its possibility—a condition on which must rest the cogitation of all objects with respect to their content. Nay, more, this ideal is the only proper ideal of which the human mind is capable; because in this case alone a general conception of a thing is completely determined by and through itself, and cognized as the representation of an individuum.\n\nThe logical determination of a conception is based upon a disjunctive syllogism, the major of which contains the logical division of the extent of a general conception, the minor limits this extent to a certain part, while the conclusion determines the conception by this part. The general conception of a reality cannot be divided à priori, because, without the aid of experience, we cannot know any determinate kinds of reality, standing under the former as the genus. The transcendental principle of the complete determination of all things is therefore merely the representation of the sum-total of all reality; it is not a conception which is the genus of all predicates under itself, but one which comprehends them all within itself. The complete determination of a thing is consequently based upon the limitation of this total of reality, so much being predicated of the thing, while all that remains over is excluded—a procedure which is in exact agreement with that of the disjunctive syllogism and the determination of the objects in the conclusion by one of the members of the division. It follows that reason, in laying the transcendental ideal at the foundation of its determination of all possible things, takes a course in exact analogy with that which it pursues in disjunctive syllogisms—a proposition which formed the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are produced in complete parallelism with the three modes of syllogistic reasoning employed by the human mind.\n\nIt is self-evident that reason, in cogitating the necessary complete determination of things, does not presuppose the existence of a being corresponding to its ideal, but merely the idea of the ideal—for the purpose of deducing from the unconditional totality of complete determination, The ideal is therefore the prototype of all things, which, as defective copies (ectypa), receive from it the material of their possibility, and approximate to it more or less, though it is impossible that they can ever attain to its perfection.\n\nThe possibility of things must therefore be regarded as derived—except that of the thing which contains in itself all reality, which must be considered to be primitive and original. For all negations—and they are the only predicates by means of which all other things can be distinguished from the ens realissimum—are mere limitations of a greater and a higher—nay, the highest reality; and they consequently presuppose this reality, and are, as regards their content, derived from it. The manifold nature of things is only an infinitely various mode of limiting the conception of the highest reality, which is their common substratum; just as all figures are possible only as different modes of limiting infinite space. The object of the ideal of reason—an object existing only in reason itself—is also termed the primal being (ens originarium); as having no existence superior to him, the supreme being (ens summum); and as being the condition of all other beings, which rank under it, the being of all beings (ens entium). But none of these terms indicate the objective relation of an actually existing object to other things, but merely that of an idea to conceptions; and all our investigations into this subject still leave us in perfect uncertainty with regard to the existence of this being.\n\nA primal being cannot be said to consist of many other beings with an existence which is derivative, for the latter presuppose the former, and therefore cannot be constitutive parts of it. It follows that the ideal of the primal being must be cogitated as simple.\n\nThe deduction of the possibility of all other things from this primal being cannot, strictly speaking, be considered as a limitation, or as a kind of division of its reality; for this would be regarding the primal being as a mere aggregate—which has been shown to be impossible, although it was so represented in our first rough sketch. The highest reality must be regarded rather as the ground than as the sum-total of the possibility of all things, and the manifold nature of things be based, not upon the limitation of the primal being itself, but upon the complete series of effects which flow from it. And thus all our powers of sense, as well as all phenomenal reality, may be with propriety regarded as belonging to this series of effects, while they could not have formed parts of the idea, considered as an aggregate. Pursuing this track, and hypostatizing this idea, we shall find ourselves authorized to determine our notion of the Supreme Being by means of the mere conception of a highest reality, as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so on—in one word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness by the aid of every possible predicate. The conception of such a being is the conception of God in its transcendental sense, and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object-matter of a transcendental theology.\n\nBut, by such an employment of the transcendental idea, we should be over stepping the limits of its validity and purpose. For reason placed it, as the conception of all reality, at the basis of the complete determination of things, without requiring that this conception be regarded as the conception of an objective existence. Such an existence would be purely fictitious, and the hypostatizing of the content of the idea into an ideal, as an individual being, is a step perfectly unauthorized. Nay, more, we are not even called upon to assume the possibility of such an hypothesis, as none of the deductions drawn from such an ideal would affect the complete determination of things in general—for the sake of which alone is the idea necessary.\n\nIt is not sufficient to circumscribe the procedure and the dialectic of reason; we must also endeavour to discover the sources of this dialectic, that we may have it in our power to give a rational explanation of this illusion, as a phenomenon of the human mind. For the ideal, of which we are at present speaking, is based, not upon an arbitrary, but upon a natural, idea. The question hence arises: How happens it that reason regards the possibility of all things as deduced from a single possibility, that, to wit, of the highest reality, and presupposes this as existing in an individual and primal being?\n\nThe answer is ready; it is at once presented by the procedure of transcendental analytic. The possibility of sensuous objects is a relation of these objects to thought, in which something (the empirical form) may be cogitated à priori; while that which constitutes the matter—the reality of the phenomenon (that element which corresponds to sensation)—must be given from without, as otherwise it could not even be cogitated by, nor could its possibility be presentable to the mind. Now, a sensuous object is completely determined, when it has been compared with all phenomenal predicates, and represented by means of these either positively or negatively. But, as that which constitutes the thing itself—the real in a phenomenon, must be given, and that, in which the real of all phenomena is given, is experience, one, sole, and all-embracing—the material of the possibility of all sensuous objects must be presupposed as given in a whole, and it is upon the limitation of this whole that the possibility of all empirical objects, their distinction from each other and their complete determination, are based. Now, no other objects are presented to us besides sensuous objects, and these can be given only in connection with a possible experience; it follows that a thing is not an object to us, unless it presupposes the whole or sum-total of empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. Now, a natural illusion leads us to consider this principle, which is valid only of sensuous objects, as valid with regard to things in general. And thus we are induced to hold the empirical principle of our conceptions of the possibility of things, as phenomena, by leaving out this limitative condition, to be a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.\n\nWe proceed afterwards to hypostatize this idea of the sum-total of all reality, by changing the distributive unity of the empirical exercise of the understanding into the collective unity of an empirical whole—a dialectical illusion, and by cogitating this whole or sum of experience as an individual thing, containing in itself all empirical reality. This individual thing or being is then, by means of the above-mentioned transcendental subreption, substituted for our notion of a thing which stands at the head of the possibility of all things, the real conditions of whose complete determination it presents.[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-66)\n\n[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-66) This ideal of the ens realissimum—although merely a mental representation—is first objectivized, that is, has an objective existence attributed to it, then hypostatized, and finally, by the natural progress of reason to the completion of unity, personified, as we shall show presently. For the regulative unity of experience is not based upon phenomena themselves, but upon the connection of the variety of phenomena by the understanding in a consciousness, and thus the unity of the supreme reality and the complete determinability of all things, seem to reside in a supreme understanding, and, consequently, in a conscious intelligence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)\n\nEvery conception is, in relation to that which is not contained in it, undetermined and subject to the principle of determinability. This principle is that, of every two contradictorily opposed predicates, only one can belong to a conception. It is a purely logical principle, itself based upon the principle of contradiction; inasmuch as it makes complete abstraction of the content and attends merely to the logical form of the cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","text":"But again, everything, as regards its possibility, is also subject to the principle of complete determination, according to which one of all the possible contradictory predicates of things must belong to it. This principle is not based merely upon that of contradiction; for, in addition to the relation between two contradictory predicates, it regards everything as standing in a relation to the sum of possibilities, as the sum total of all predicates of things, and, while presupposing this sum as an à priori condition, presents to the mind everything as receiving the possibility of its individual existence from the relation it bears to, and the share it possesses in, the aforesaid sum of possibilities.[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-64) The principle of complete determination relates the content and not to the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all the predicates which are required to constitute the complete conception of a thing, and not a mere principle analytical representation, which enounces that one of two contradictory predicates must belong to a conception. It contains, moreover, a transcendental presupposition—that, namely, of the material for all possibility, which must contain à priori the data for this or that particular possibility.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0003","text":"[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-64) Thus this principle declares everything to possess a relation to a common correlate—the sum-total of possibility, which, if discovered to exist in the idea of one individual thing, would establish the affinity of all possible things, from the identity of the ground of their complete determination. The determinability of every conception is subordinate to the universality (Allgemeinheit, universalitas) of the principle of excluded middle; the determination of a thing to the totality (Allheit, universitas) of all possible predicates.\n\nThe proposition, everything which exists is completely determined, means not only that one of every pair of given contradictory attributes, but that one of all possible attributes, is always predicable of the thing; in it the predicates are not merely compared logically with each other, but the thing itself is transcendentally compared with the sum-total of all possible predicates. The proposition is equivalent to saying: “To attain to a complete knowledge of a thing, it is necessary to possess a knowledge of everything that is possible, and to determine it thereby in a positive or negative manner.” The conception of complete determination is consequently a conception which cannot be presented in its totality in concreto, and is therefore based upon an idea, which has its seat in the reason—the faculty which prescribes to the understanding the laws of its harmonious and perfect exercise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0004","text":"Now, although this idea of the sum-total of all possibility, in so far as it forms the condition of the complete determination of everything, is itself undetermined in relation to the predicates which may constitute this sum-total, and we cogitate in it merely the sum-total of all possible predicates—we nevertheless find, upon closer examination, that this idea, as a primitive conception of the mind, excludes a large number of predicates—those deduced and those irreconcilable with others, and that it is evolved as a conception completely determined à priori. Thus it becomes the conception of an individual object, which is completely determined by and through the mere idea, and must consequently be termed an ideal of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","text":"When we consider all possible predicates, not merely logically, but transcendentally, that is to say, with reference to the content which may be cogitated as existing in them à priori, we shall find that some indicate a being, others merely a non-being. The logical negation expressed in the word not does not properly belong to a conception, but only to the relation of one conception to another in a judgement, and is consequently quite insufficient to present to the mind the content of a conception. The expression not mortal does not indicate that a non-being is cogitated in the object; it does not concern the content at all. A transcendental negation, on the contrary, indicates non-being in itself, and is opposed to transcendental affirmation, the conception of which of itself expresses a being. Hence this affirmation indicates a reality, because in and through it objects are considered to be something—to be things; while the opposite negation, on the other hand, indicates a mere want, or privation, or absence, and, where such negations alone are attached to a representation, the non-existence of anything corresponding to the representation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","text":"Now a negation cannot be cogitated as determined, without cogitating at the same time the opposite affirmation. The man born blind has not the least notion of darkness, because he has none of light; the vagabond knows nothing of poverty, because he has never known what it is to be in comfort;[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-65) the ignorant man has no conception of his ignorance, because he has no conception of knowledge. All conceptions of negatives are accordingly derived or deduced conceptions; and realities contain the data, and, so to speak, the material or transcendental content of the possibility and complete determination of all things.\n\n[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-65) The investigations and calculations of astronomers have taught us much that is wonderful; but the most important lesson we have received from them is the discovery of the abyss of our ignorance in relation to the universe—an ignorance the magnitude of which reason, without the information thus derived, could never have conceived. This discovery of our deficiencies must produce a great change in the determination of the aims of human reason.\n\nIf, therefore, a transcendental substratum lies at the foundation of the complete determination of things—a substratum which is to form the fund from which all possible predicates of things are to be supplied, this substratum cannot be anything else than the idea of a sum-total of reality (omnitudo realitatis). In this view, negations are nothing but limitations—a term which could not, with propriety, be applied to them, if the unlimited (the all) did not form the true basis of our conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","text":"This conception of a sum-total of reality is the conception of a thing in itself, regarded as completely determined; and the conception of an ens realissimum is the conception of an individual being, inasmuch as it is determined by that predicate of all possible contradictory predicates, which indicates and belongs to being. It is, therefore, a transcendental ideal which forms the basis of the complete determination of everything that exists, and is the highest material condition of its possibility—a condition on which must rest the cogitation of all objects with respect to their content. Nay, more, this ideal is the only proper ideal of which the human mind is capable; because in this case alone a general conception of a thing is completely determined by and through itself, and cognized as the representation of an individuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","text":"The logical determination of a conception is based upon a disjunctive syllogism, the major of which contains the logical division of the extent of a general conception, the minor limits this extent to a certain part, while the conclusion determines the conception by this part. The general conception of a reality cannot be divided à priori, because, without the aid of experience, we cannot know any determinate kinds of reality, standing under the former as the genus. The transcendental principle of the complete determination of all things is therefore merely the representation of the sum-total of all reality; it is not a conception which is the genus of all predicates under itself, but one which comprehends them all within itself. The complete determination of a thing is consequently based upon the limitation of this total of reality, so much being predicated of the thing, while all that remains over is excluded—a procedure which is in exact agreement with that of the disjunctive syllogism and the determination of the objects in the conclusion by one of the members of the division. It follows that reason, in laying the transcendental ideal at the foundation of its determination of all possible things, takes a course in exact analogy with that which it pursues in disjunctive syllogisms—a proposition which formed the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are produced in complete parallelism with the three modes of syllogistic reasoning employed by the human mind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","text":"It is self-evident that reason, in cogitating the necessary complete determination of things, does not presuppose the existence of a being corresponding to its ideal, but merely the idea of the ideal—for the purpose of deducing from the unconditional totality of complete determination, The ideal is therefore the prototype of all things, which, as defective copies (ectypa), receive from it the material of their possibility, and approximate to it more or less, though it is impossible that they can ever attain to its perfection.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0010","text":"The possibility of things must therefore be regarded as derived—except that of the thing which contains in itself all reality, which must be considered to be primitive and original. For all negations—and they are the only predicates by means of which all other things can be distinguished from the ens realissimum—are mere limitations of a greater and a higher—nay, the highest reality; and they consequently presuppose this reality, and are, as regards their content, derived from it. The manifold nature of things is only an infinitely various mode of limiting the conception of the highest reality, which is their common substratum; just as all figures are possible only as different modes of limiting infinite space. The object of the ideal of reason—an object existing only in reason itself—is also termed the primal being (ens originarium); as having no existence superior to him, the supreme being (ens summum); and as being the condition of all other beings, which rank under it, the being of all beings (ens entium). But none of these terms indicate the objective relation of an actually existing object to other things, but merely that of an idea to conceptions; and all our investigations into this subject still leave us in perfect uncertainty with regard to the existence of this being.\n\nA primal being cannot be said to consist of many other beings with an existence which is derivative, for the latter presuppose the former, and therefore cannot be constitutive parts of it. It follows that the ideal of the primal being must be cogitated as simple.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0011","text":"The deduction of the possibility of all other things from this primal being cannot, strictly speaking, be considered as a limitation, or as a kind of division of its reality; for this would be regarding the primal being as a mere aggregate—which has been shown to be impossible, although it was so represented in our first rough sketch. The highest reality must be regarded rather as the ground than as the sum-total of the possibility of all things, and the manifold nature of things be based, not upon the limitation of the primal being itself, but upon the complete series of effects which flow from it. And thus all our powers of sense, as well as all phenomenal reality, may be with propriety regarded as belonging to this series of effects, while they could not have formed parts of the idea, considered as an aggregate. Pursuing this track, and hypostatizing this idea, we shall find ourselves authorized to determine our notion of the Supreme Being by means of the mere conception of a highest reality, as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so on—in one word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness by the aid of every possible predicate. The conception of such a being is the conception of God in its transcendental sense, and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object-matter of a transcendental theology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","text":"But, by such an employment of the transcendental idea, we should be over stepping the limits of its validity and purpose. For reason placed it, as the conception of all reality, at the basis of the complete determination of things, without requiring that this conception be regarded as the conception of an objective existence. Such an existence would be purely fictitious, and the hypostatizing of the content of the idea into an ideal, as an individual being, is a step perfectly unauthorized. Nay, more, we are not even called upon to assume the possibility of such an hypothesis, as none of the deductions drawn from such an ideal would affect the complete determination of things in general—for the sake of which alone is the idea necessary.\n\nIt is not sufficient to circumscribe the procedure and the dialectic of reason; we must also endeavour to discover the sources of this dialectic, that we may have it in our power to give a rational explanation of this illusion, as a phenomenon of the human mind. For the ideal, of which we are at present speaking, is based, not upon an arbitrary, but upon a natural, idea. The question hence arises: How happens it that reason regards the possibility of all things as deduced from a single possibility, that, to wit, of the highest reality, and presupposes this as existing in an individual and primal being?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","text":"The answer is ready; it is at once presented by the procedure of transcendental analytic. The possibility of sensuous objects is a relation of these objects to thought, in which something (the empirical form) may be cogitated à priori; while that which constitutes the matter—the reality of the phenomenon (that element which corresponds to sensation)—must be given from without, as otherwise it could not even be cogitated by, nor could its possibility be presentable to the mind. Now, a sensuous object is completely determined, when it has been compared with all phenomenal predicates, and represented by means of these either positively or negatively. But, as that which constitutes the thing itself—the real in a phenomenon, must be given, and that, in which the real of all phenomena is given, is experience, one, sole, and all-embracing—the material of the possibility of all sensuous objects must be presupposed as given in a whole, and it is upon the limitation of this whole that the possibility of all empirical objects, their distinction from each other and their complete determination, are based. Now, no other objects are presented to us besides sensuous objects, and these can be given only in connection with a possible experience; it follows that a thing is not an object to us, unless it presupposes the whole or sum-total of empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. Now, a natural illusion leads us to consider this principle, which is valid only of sensuous objects, as valid with regard to things in general. And thus we are induced to hold the empirical principle of our conceptions of the possibility of things, as phenomena, by leaving out this limitative condition, to be a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","text":"We proceed afterwards to hypostatize this idea of the sum-total of all reality, by changing the distributive unity of the empirical exercise of the understanding into the collective unity of an empirical whole—a dialectical illusion, and by cogitating this whole or sum of experience as an individual thing, containing in itself all empirical reality. This individual thing or being is then, by means of the above-mentioned transcendental subreption, substituted for our notion of a thing which stands at the head of the possibility of all things, the real conditions of whose complete determination it presents.[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-66)\n\n[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-66) This ideal of the ens realissimum—although merely a mental representation—is first objectivized, that is, has an objective existence attributed to it, then hypostatized, and finally, by the natural progress of reason to the completion of unity, personified, as we shall show presently. For the regulative unity of experience is not based upon phenomena themselves, but upon the connection of the variety of phenomena by the understanding in a consciousness, and thus the unity of the supreme reality and the complete determinability of all things, seem to reside in a supreme understanding, and, consequently, in a conscious intelligence.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"eadd7e7c5ca2285dcd2dbf2af8257398d10e121bb43aad3b2fe228710e0fee7b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","order":77,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIAE","text":"### Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being\n\nNotwithstanding the pressing necessity which reason feels, to form some presupposition that shall serve the understanding as a proper basis for the complete determination of its conceptions, the idealistic and factitious nature of such a presupposition is too evident to allow reason for a moment to persuade itself into a belief of the objective existence of a mere creation of its own thought. But there are other considerations which compel reason to seek out some resting place in the regress from the conditioned to the unconditioned, which is not given as an actual existence from the mere conception of it, although it alone can give completeness to the series of conditions. And this is the natural course of every human reason, even of the most uneducated, although the path at first entered it does not always continue to follow. It does not begin from conceptions, but from common experience, and requires a basis in actual existence. But this basis is insecure, unless it rests upon the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. And this foundation is itself unworthy of trust, if it leave under and above it empty space, if it do not fill all, and leave no room for a why or a wherefore, if it be not, in one word, infinite in its reality.\n\nIf we admit the existence of some one thing, whatever it may be, we must also admit that there is something which exists necessarily. For what is contingent exists only under the condition of some other thing, which is its cause; and from this we must go on to conclude the existence of a cause which is not contingent, and which consequently exists necessarily and unconditionally. Such is the argument by which reason justifies its advances towards a primal being.\n\nNow reason looks round for the conception of a being that may be admitted, without inconsistency, to be worthy of the attribute of absolute necessity, not for the purpose of inferring à priori, from the conception of such a being, its objective existence (for if reason allowed itself to take this course, it would not require a basis in given and actual existence, but merely the support of pure conceptions), but for the purpose of discovering, among all our conceptions of possible things, that conception which possesses no element inconsistent with the idea of absolute necessity. For that there must be some absolutely necessary existence, it regards as a truth already established. Now, if it can remove every existence incapable of supporting the attribute of absolute necessity, excepting one—this must be the absolutely necessary being, whether its necessity is comprehensible by us, that is, deducible from the conception of it alone, or not.\n\nNow that, the conception of which contains a therefore to every wherefore, which is not defective in any respect whatever, which is all-sufficient as a condition, seems to be the being of which we can justly predicate absolute necessity—for this reason, that, possessing the conditions of all that is possible, it does not and cannot itself require any condition. And thus it satisfies, in one respect at least, the requirements of the conception of absolute necessity. In this view, it is superior to all other conceptions, which, as deficient and incomplete, do not possess the characteristic of independence of all higher conditions. It is true that we cannot infer from this that what does not contain in itself the supreme and complete condition—the condition of all other things—must possess only a conditioned existence; but as little can we assert the contrary, for this supposed being does not possess the only characteristic which can enable reason to cognize by means of an à priori conception the unconditioned and necessary nature of its existence.\n\nThe conception of an ens realissimum is that which best agrees with the conception of an unconditioned and necessary being. The former conception does not satisfy all the requirements of the latter; but we have no choice, we are obliged to adhere to it, for we find that we cannot do without the existence of a necessary being; and even although we admit it, we find it out of our power to discover in the whole sphere of possibility any being that can advance well-grounded claims to such a distinction.\n\nThe following is, therefore, the natural course of human reason. It begins by persuading itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this being it recognizes the characteristics of unconditioned existence. It then seeks the conception of that which is independent of all conditions, and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition of all other things—in other words, in that which contains all reality. But the unlimited all is an absolute unity, and is conceived by the mind as a being one and supreme; and thus reason concludes that the Supreme Being, as the primal basis of all things, possesses an existence which is absolutely necessary.\n\nThis conception must be regarded as in some degree satisfactory, if we admit the existence of a necessary being, and consider that there exists a necessity for a definite and final answer to these questions. In such a case, we cannot make a better choice, or rather we have no choice at all, but feel ourselves obliged to declare in favour of the absolute unity of complete reality, as the highest source of the possibility of things. But if there exists no motive for coming to a definite conclusion, and we may leave the question unanswered till we have fully weighed both sides—in other words, when we are merely called upon to decide how much we happen to know about the question, and how much we merely flatter ourselves that we know—the above conclusion does not appear to be so great advantage, but, on the contrary, seems defective in the grounds upon which it is supported.\n\nFor, admitting the truth of all that has been said, that, namely, the inference from a given existence (my own, for example) to the existence of an unconditioned and necessary being is valid and unassailable; that, in the second place, we must consider a being which contains all reality, and consequently all the conditions of other things, to be absolutely unconditioned; and admitting too, that we have thus discovered the conception of a thing to which may be attributed, without inconsistency, absolute necessity—it does not follow from all this that the conception of a limited being, in which the supreme reality does not reside, is therefore incompatible with the idea of absolute necessity. For, although I do not discover the element of the unconditioned in the conception of such a being—an element which is manifestly existent in the sum-total of all conditions—I am not entitled to conclude that its existence is therefore conditioned; just as I am not entitled to affirm, in a hypothetical syllogism, that where a certain condition does not exist (in the present, completeness, as far as pure conceptions are concerned), the conditioned does not exist either. On the contrary, we are free to consider all limited beings as likewise unconditionally necessary, although we are unable to infer this from the general conception which we have of them. Thus conducted, this argument is incapable of giving us the least notion of the properties of a necessary being, and must be in every respect without result.\n\nThis argument continues, however, to possess a weight and an authority, which, in spite of its objective insufficiency, it has never been divested of. For, granting that certain responsibilities lie upon us, which, as based on the ideas of reason, deserve to be respected and submitted to, although they are incapable of a real or practical application to our nature, or, in other words, would be responsibilities without motives, except upon the supposition of a Supreme Being to give effect and influence to the practical laws: in such a case we should be bound to obey our conceptions, which, although objectively insufficient, do, according to the standard of reason, preponderate over and are superior to any claims that may be advanced from any other quarter. The equilibrium of doubt would in this case be destroyed by a practical addition; indeed, Reason would be compelled to condemn herself, if she refused to comply with the demands of the judgement, no superior to which we know—however defective her understanding of the grounds of these demands might be.\n\nThis argument, although in fact transcendental, inasmuch as it rests upon the intrinsic insufficiency of the contingent, is so simple and natural, that the commonest understanding can appreciate its value. We see things around us change, arise, and pass away; they, or their condition, must therefore have a cause. The same demand must again be made of the cause itself—as a datum of experience. Now it is natural that we should place the highest causality just where we place supreme causality, in that being, which contains the conditions of all possible effects, and the conception of which is so simple as that of an all-embracing reality. This highest cause, then, we regard as absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to rise to it, and do not discover any reason for proceeding beyond it. Thus, among all nations, through the darkest polytheism glimmer some faint sparks of monotheism, to which these idolaters have been led, not from reflection and profound thought, but by the study and natural progress of the common understanding.\n\nThere are only three modes of proving the existence of a Deity, on the grounds of speculative reason.\n\nAll the paths conducting to this end begin either from determinate experience and the peculiar constitution of the world of sense, and rise, according to the laws of causality, from it to the highest cause existing apart from the world—or from a purely indeterminate experience, that is, some empirical existence—or abstraction is made of all experience, and the existence of a supreme cause is concluded from à priori conceptions alone. The first is the physico-theological argument, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. More there are not, and more there cannot be.\n\nI shall show it is as unsuccessful on the one path—the empirical—as on the other—the transcendental, and that it stretches its wings in vain, to soar beyond the world of sense by the mere might of speculative thought. As regards the order in which we must discuss those arguments, it will be exactly the reverse of that in which reason, in the progress of its development, attains to them—the order in which they are placed above. For it will be made manifest to the reader that, although experience presents the occasion and the starting-point, it is the transcendental idea of reason which guides it in its pilgrimage and is the goal of all its struggles. I shall therefore begin with an examination of the transcendental argument, and afterwards inquire what additional strength has accrued to this mode of proof from the addition of the empirical element.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being\n\nNotwithstanding the pressing necessity which reason feels, to form some presupposition that shall serve the understanding as a proper basis for the complete determination of its conceptions, the idealistic and factitious nature of such a presupposition is too evident to allow reason for a moment to persuade itself into a belief of the objective existence of a mere creation of its own thought. But there are other considerations which compel reason to seek out some resting place in the regress from the conditioned to the unconditioned, which is not given as an actual existence from the mere conception of it, although it alone can give completeness to the series of conditions. And this is the natural course of every human reason, even of the most uneducated, although the path at first entered it does not always continue to follow. It does not begin from conceptions, but from common experience, and requires a basis in actual existence. But this basis is insecure, unless it rests upon the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. And this foundation is itself unworthy of trust, if it leave under and above it empty space, if it do not fill all, and leave no room for a why or a wherefore, if it be not, in one word, infinite in its reality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","text":"If we admit the existence of some one thing, whatever it may be, we must also admit that there is something which exists necessarily. For what is contingent exists only under the condition of some other thing, which is its cause; and from this we must go on to conclude the existence of a cause which is not contingent, and which consequently exists necessarily and unconditionally. Such is the argument by which reason justifies its advances towards a primal being.\n\nNow reason looks round for the conception of a being that may be admitted, without inconsistency, to be worthy of the attribute of absolute necessity, not for the purpose of inferring à priori, from the conception of such a being, its objective existence (for if reason allowed itself to take this course, it would not require a basis in given and actual existence, but merely the support of pure conceptions), but for the purpose of discovering, among all our conceptions of possible things, that conception which possesses no element inconsistent with the idea of absolute necessity. For that there must be some absolutely necessary existence, it regards as a truth already established. Now, if it can remove every existence incapable of supporting the attribute of absolute necessity, excepting one—this must be the absolutely necessary being, whether its necessity is comprehensible by us, that is, deducible from the conception of it alone, or not.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","text":"Now that, the conception of which contains a therefore to every wherefore, which is not defective in any respect whatever, which is all-sufficient as a condition, seems to be the being of which we can justly predicate absolute necessity—for this reason, that, possessing the conditions of all that is possible, it does not and cannot itself require any condition. And thus it satisfies, in one respect at least, the requirements of the conception of absolute necessity. In this view, it is superior to all other conceptions, which, as deficient and incomplete, do not possess the characteristic of independence of all higher conditions. It is true that we cannot infer from this that what does not contain in itself the supreme and complete condition—the condition of all other things—must possess only a conditioned existence; but as little can we assert the contrary, for this supposed being does not possess the only characteristic which can enable reason to cognize by means of an à priori conception the unconditioned and necessary nature of its existence.\n\nThe conception of an ens realissimum is that which best agrees with the conception of an unconditioned and necessary being. The former conception does not satisfy all the requirements of the latter; but we have no choice, we are obliged to adhere to it, for we find that we cannot do without the existence of a necessary being; and even although we admit it, we find it out of our power to discover in the whole sphere of possibility any being that can advance well-grounded claims to such a distinction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","text":"The following is, therefore, the natural course of human reason. It begins by persuading itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this being it recognizes the characteristics of unconditioned existence. It then seeks the conception of that which is independent of all conditions, and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition of all other things—in other words, in that which contains all reality. But the unlimited all is an absolute unity, and is conceived by the mind as a being one and supreme; and thus reason concludes that the Supreme Being, as the primal basis of all things, possesses an existence which is absolutely necessary.\n\nThis conception must be regarded as in some degree satisfactory, if we admit the existence of a necessary being, and consider that there exists a necessity for a definite and final answer to these questions. In such a case, we cannot make a better choice, or rather we have no choice at all, but feel ourselves obliged to declare in favour of the absolute unity of complete reality, as the highest source of the possibility of things. But if there exists no motive for coming to a definite conclusion, and we may leave the question unanswered till we have fully weighed both sides—in other words, when we are merely called upon to decide how much we happen to know about the question, and how much we merely flatter ourselves that we know—the above conclusion does not appear to be so great advantage, but, on the contrary, seems defective in the grounds upon which it is supported.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","text":"For, admitting the truth of all that has been said, that, namely, the inference from a given existence (my own, for example) to the existence of an unconditioned and necessary being is valid and unassailable; that, in the second place, we must consider a being which contains all reality, and consequently all the conditions of other things, to be absolutely unconditioned; and admitting too, that we have thus discovered the conception of a thing to which may be attributed, without inconsistency, absolute necessity—it does not follow from all this that the conception of a limited being, in which the supreme reality does not reside, is therefore incompatible with the idea of absolute necessity. For, although I do not discover the element of the unconditioned in the conception of such a being—an element which is manifestly existent in the sum-total of all conditions—I am not entitled to conclude that its existence is therefore conditioned; just as I am not entitled to affirm, in a hypothetical syllogism, that where a certain condition does not exist (in the present, completeness, as far as pure conceptions are concerned), the conditioned does not exist either. On the contrary, we are free to consider all limited beings as likewise unconditionally necessary, although we are unable to infer this from the general conception which we have of them. Thus conducted, this argument is incapable of giving us the least notion of the properties of a necessary being, and must be in every respect without result.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","text":"This argument continues, however, to possess a weight and an authority, which, in spite of its objective insufficiency, it has never been divested of. For, granting that certain responsibilities lie upon us, which, as based on the ideas of reason, deserve to be respected and submitted to, although they are incapable of a real or practical application to our nature, or, in other words, would be responsibilities without motives, except upon the supposition of a Supreme Being to give effect and influence to the practical laws: in such a case we should be bound to obey our conceptions, which, although objectively insufficient, do, according to the standard of reason, preponderate over and are superior to any claims that may be advanced from any other quarter. The equilibrium of doubt would in this case be destroyed by a practical addition; indeed, Reason would be compelled to condemn herself, if she refused to comply with the demands of the judgement, no superior to which we know—however defective her understanding of the grounds of these demands might be.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","text":"This argument, although in fact transcendental, inasmuch as it rests upon the intrinsic insufficiency of the contingent, is so simple and natural, that the commonest understanding can appreciate its value. We see things around us change, arise, and pass away; they, or their condition, must therefore have a cause. The same demand must again be made of the cause itself—as a datum of experience. Now it is natural that we should place the highest causality just where we place supreme causality, in that being, which contains the conditions of all possible effects, and the conception of which is so simple as that of an all-embracing reality. This highest cause, then, we regard as absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to rise to it, and do not discover any reason for proceeding beyond it. Thus, among all nations, through the darkest polytheism glimmer some faint sparks of monotheism, to which these idolaters have been led, not from reflection and profound thought, but by the study and natural progress of the common understanding.\n\nThere are only three modes of proving the existence of a Deity, on the grounds of speculative reason.\n\nAll the paths conducting to this end begin either from determinate experience and the peculiar constitution of the world of sense, and rise, according to the laws of causality, from it to the highest cause existing apart from the world—or from a purely indeterminate experience, that is, some empirical existence—or abstraction is made of all experience, and the existence of a supreme cause is concluded from à priori conceptions alone. The first is the physico-theological argument, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. More there are not, and more there cannot be.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","text":"I shall show it is as unsuccessful on the one path—the empirical—as on the other—the transcendental, and that it stretches its wings in vain, to soar beyond the world of sense by the mere might of speculative thought. As regards the order in which we must discuss those arguments, it will be exactly the reverse of that in which reason, in the progress of its development, attains to them—the order in which they are placed above. For it will be made manifest to the reader that, although experience presents the occasion and the starting-point, it is the transcendental idea of reason which guides it in its pilgrimage and is the goal of all its struggles. I shall therefore begin with an examination of the transcendental argument, and afterwards inquire what additional strength has accrued to this mode of proof from the addition of the empirical element.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"252326d9b6fd52222655e6aced02e4c23ce04426a195963dec5645bcc070d8d0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","order":78,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIIO","text":"### Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt is evident from what has been said that the conception of an absolutely necessary being is a mere idea, the objective reality of which is far from being established by the mere fact that it is a need of reason. On the contrary, this idea serves merely to indicate a certain unattainable perfection, and rather limits the operations than, by the presentation of new objects, extends the sphere of the understanding. But a strange anomaly meets us at the very threshold; for the inference from a given existence in general to an absolutely necessary existence seems to be correct and unavoidable, while the conditions of the understanding refuse to aid us in forming any conception of such a being.\n\nPhilosophers have always talked of an absolutely necessary being, and have nevertheless declined to take the trouble of conceiving whether—and how—a being of this nature is even cogitable, not to mention that its existence is actually demonstrable. A verbal definition of the conception is certainly easy enough: it is something the non-existence of which is impossible. But does this definition throw any light upon the conditions which render it impossible to cogitate the non-existence of a thing—conditions which we wish to ascertain, that we may discover whether we think anything in the conception of such a being or not? For the mere fact that I throw away, by means of the word unconditioned, all the conditions which the understanding habitually requires in order to regard anything as necessary, is very far from making clear whether by means of the conception of the unconditionally necessary I think of something, or really of nothing at all.\n\nNay, more, this chance-conception, now become so current, many have endeavoured to explain by examples which seemed to render any inquiries regarding its intelligibility quite needless. Every geometrical proposition—a triangle has three angles—it was said, is absolutely necessary; and thus people talked of an object which lay out of the sphere of our understanding as if it were perfectly plain what the conception of such a being meant.\n\nAll the examples adduced have been drawn, without exception, from judgements, and not from things. But the unconditioned necessity of a judgement does not form the absolute necessity of a thing. On the contrary, the absolute necessity of a judgement is only a conditioned necessity of a thing, or of the predicate in a judgement. The proposition above-mentioned does not enounce that three angles necessarily exist, but, upon condition that a triangle exists, three angles must necessarily exist—in it. And thus this logical necessity has been the source of the greatest delusions. Having formed an à priori conception of a thing, the content of which was made to embrace existence, we believed ourselves safe in concluding that, because existence belongs necessarily to the object of the conception (that is, under the condition of my positing this thing as given), the existence of the thing is also posited necessarily, and that it is therefore absolutely necessary—merely because its existence has been cogitated in the conception.\n\nIf, in an identical judgement, I annihilate the predicate in thought, and retain the subject, a contradiction is the result; and hence I say, the former belongs necessarily to the latter. But if I suppress both subject and predicate in thought, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing at all, and therefore no means of forming a contradiction. To suppose the existence of a triangle and not that of its three angles, is self-contradictory; but to suppose the non-existence of both triangle and angles is perfectly admissible. And so is it with the conception of an absolutely necessary being. Annihilate its existence in thought, and you annihilate the thing itself with all its predicates; how then can there be any room for contradiction? Externally, there is nothing to give rise to a contradiction, for a thing cannot be necessary externally; nor internally, for, by the annihilation or suppression of the thing itself, its internal properties are also annihilated. God is omnipotent—that is a necessary judgement. His omnipotence cannot be denied, if the existence of a Deity is posited—the existence, that is, of an infinite being, the two conceptions being identical. But when you say, God does not exist, neither omnipotence nor any other predicate is affirmed; they must all disappear with the subject, and in this judgement there cannot exist the least self-contradiction.\n\nYou have thus seen that when the predicate of a judgement is annihilated in thought along with the subject, no internal contradiction can arise, be the predicate what it may. There is no possibility of evading the conclusion—you find yourselves compelled to declare: There are certain subjects which cannot be annihilated in thought. But this is nothing more than saying: There exist subjects which are absolutely necessary—the very hypothesis which you are called upon to establish. For I find myself unable to form the slightest conception of a thing which when annihilated in thought with all its predicates, leaves behind a contradiction; and contradiction is the only criterion of impossibility in the sphere of pure à priori conceptions.\n\nAgainst these general considerations, the justice of which no one can dispute, one argument is adduced, which is regarded as furnishing a satisfactory demonstration from the fact. It is affirmed that there is one and only one conception, in which the non-being or annihilation of the object is self-contradictory, and this is the conception of an ens realissimum. It possesses, you say, all reality, and you feel yourselves justified in admitting the possibility of such a being. (This I am willing to grant for the present, although the existence of a conception which is not self-contradictory is far from being sufficient to prove the possibility of an object.)[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-67) Now the notion of all reality embraces in it that of existence; the notion of existence lies, therefore, in the conception of this possible thing. If this thing is annihilated in thought, the internal possibility of the thing is also annihilated, which is self-contradictory.\n\n[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-67) A conception is always possible, if it is not self-contradictory. This is the logical criterion of possibility, distinguishing the object of such a conception from the nihil negativum. But it may be, notwithstanding, an empty conception, unless the objective reality of this synthesis, but which it is generated, is demonstrated; and a proof of this kind must be based upon principles of possible experience, and not upon the principle of analysis or contradiction. This remark may be serviceable as a warning against concluding, from the possibility of a conception—which is logical—the possibility of a thing—which is real.\n\nI answer: It is absurd to introduce—under whatever term disguised—into the conception of a thing, which is to be cogitated solely in reference to its possibility, the conception of its existence. If this is admitted, you will have apparently gained the day, but in reality have enounced nothing but a mere tautology. I ask, is the proposition, this or that thing (which I am admitting to be possible) exists, an analytical or a synthetical proposition? If the former, there is no addition made to the subject of your thought by the affirmation of its existence; but then the conception in your minds is identical with the thing itself, or you have supposed the existence of a thing to be possible, and then inferred its existence from its internal possibility—which is but a miserable tautology. The word reality in the conception of the thing, and the word existence in the conception of the predicate, will not help you out of the difficulty. For, supposing you were to term all positing of a thing reality, you have thereby posited the thing with all its predicates in the conception of the subject and assumed its actual existence, and this you merely repeat in the predicate. But if you confess, as every reasonable person must, that every existential proposition is synthetical, how can it be maintained that the predicate of existence cannot be denied without contradiction?—a property which is the characteristic of analytical propositions, alone.\n\nI should have a reasonable hope of putting an end for ever to this sophistical mode of argumentation, by a strict definition of the conception of existence, did not my own experience teach me that the illusion arising from our confounding a logical with a real predicate (a predicate which aids in the determination of a thing) resists almost all the endeavours of explanation and illustration. A logical predicate may be what you please, even the subject may be predicated of itself; for logic pays no regard to the content of a judgement. But the determination of a conception is a predicate, which adds to and enlarges the conception. It must not, therefore, be contained in the conception.\n\nBeing is evidently not a real predicate, that is, a conception of something which is added to the conception of some other thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations in it. Logically, it is merely the copula of a judgement. The proposition, God is omnipotent, contains two conceptions, which have a certain object or content; the word is, is no additional predicate—it merely indicates the relation of the predicate to the subject. Now, if I take the subject (God) with all its predicates (omnipotence being one), and say: God is, or, There is a God, I add no new predicate to the conception of God, I merely posit or affirm the existence of the subject with all its predicates—I posit the object in relation to my conception. The content of both is the same; and there is no addition made to the conception, which expresses merely the possibility of the object, by my cogitating the object—in the expression, it is—as absolutely given or existing. Thus the real contains no more than the possible. A hundred real dollars contain no more than a hundred possible dollars. For, as the latter indicate the conception, and the former the object, on the supposition that the content of the former was greater than that of the latter, my conception would not be an expression of the whole object, and would consequently be an inadequate conception of it. But in reckoning my wealth there may be said to be more in a hundred real dollars than in a hundred possible dollars—that is, in the mere conception of them. For the real object—the dollars—is not analytically contained in my conception, but forms a synthetical addition to my conception (which is merely a determination of my mental state), although this objective reality—this existence—apart from my conceptions, does not in the least degree increase the aforesaid hundred dollars.\n\nBy whatever and by whatever number of predicates—even to the complete determination of it—I may cogitate a thing, I do not in the least augment the object of my conception by the addition of the statement: This thing exists. Otherwise, not exactly the same, but something more than what was cogitated in my conception, would exist, and I could not affirm that the exact object of my conception had real existence. If I cogitate a thing as containing all modes of reality except one, the mode of reality which is absent is not added to the conception of the thing by the affirmation that the thing exists; on the contrary, the thing exists—if it exist at all—with the same defect as that cogitated in its conception; otherwise not that which was cogitated, but something different, exists. Now, if I cogitate a being as the highest reality, without defect or imperfection, the question still remains—whether this being exists or not? For, although no element is wanting in the possible real content of my conception, there is a defect in its relation to my mental state, that is, I am ignorant whether the cognition of the object indicated by the conception is possible à posteriori. And here the cause of the present difficulty becomes apparent. If the question regarded an object of sense merely, it would be impossible for me to confound the conception with the existence of a thing. For the conception merely enables me to cogitate an object as according with the general conditions of experience; while the existence of the object permits me to cogitate it as contained in the sphere of actual experience. At the same time, this connection with the world of experience does not in the least augment the conception, although a possible perception has been added to the experience of the mind. But if we cogitate existence by the pure category alone, it is not to be wondered at, that we should find ourselves unable to present any criterion sufficient to distinguish it from mere possibility.\n\nWhatever be the content of our conception of an object, it is necessary to go beyond it, if we wish to predicate existence of the object. In the case of sensuous objects, this is attained by their connection according to empirical laws with some one of my perceptions; but there is no means of cognizing the existence of objects of pure thought, because it must be cognized completely à priori. But all our knowledge of existence (be it immediately by perception, or by inferences connecting some object with a perception) belongs entirely to the sphere of experience—which is in perfect unity with itself; and although an existence out of this sphere cannot be absolutely declared to be impossible, it is a hypothesis the truth of which we have no means of ascertaining.\n\nThe notion of a Supreme Being is in many respects a highly useful idea; but for the very reason that it is an idea, it is incapable of enlarging our cognition with regard to the existence of things. It is not even sufficient to instruct us as to the possibility of a being which we do not know to exist. The analytical criterion of possibility, which consists in the absence of contradiction in propositions, cannot be denied it. But the connection of real properties in a thing is a synthesis of the possibility of which an à priori judgement cannot be formed, because these realities are not presented to us specifically; and even if this were to happen, a judgement would still be impossible, because the criterion of the possibility of synthetical cognitions must be sought for in the world of experience, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. And thus the celebrated Leibnitz has utterly failed in his attempt to establish upon à priori grounds the possibility of this sublime ideal being.\n\nThe celebrated ontological or Cartesian argument for the existence of a Supreme Being is therefore insufficient; and we may as well hope to increase our stock of knowledge by the aid of mere ideas, as the merchant to augment his wealth by the addition of noughts to his cash account.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","text":"### Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt is evident from what has been said that the conception of an absolutely necessary being is a mere idea, the objective reality of which is far from being established by the mere fact that it is a need of reason. On the contrary, this idea serves merely to indicate a certain unattainable perfection, and rather limits the operations than, by the presentation of new objects, extends the sphere of the understanding. But a strange anomaly meets us at the very threshold; for the inference from a given existence in general to an absolutely necessary existence seems to be correct and unavoidable, while the conditions of the understanding refuse to aid us in forming any conception of such a being.\n\nPhilosophers have always talked of an absolutely necessary being, and have nevertheless declined to take the trouble of conceiving whether—and how—a being of this nature is even cogitable, not to mention that its existence is actually demonstrable. A verbal definition of the conception is certainly easy enough: it is something the non-existence of which is impossible. But does this definition throw any light upon the conditions which render it impossible to cogitate the non-existence of a thing—conditions which we wish to ascertain, that we may discover whether we think anything in the conception of such a being or not? For the mere fact that I throw away, by means of the word unconditioned, all the conditions which the understanding habitually requires in order to regard anything as necessary, is very far from making clear whether by means of the conception of the unconditionally necessary I think of something, or really of nothing at all.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","text":"Nay, more, this chance-conception, now become so current, many have endeavoured to explain by examples which seemed to render any inquiries regarding its intelligibility quite needless. Every geometrical proposition—a triangle has three angles—it was said, is absolutely necessary; and thus people talked of an object which lay out of the sphere of our understanding as if it were perfectly plain what the conception of such a being meant.\n\nAll the examples adduced have been drawn, without exception, from judgements, and not from things. But the unconditioned necessity of a judgement does not form the absolute necessity of a thing. On the contrary, the absolute necessity of a judgement is only a conditioned necessity of a thing, or of the predicate in a judgement. The proposition above-mentioned does not enounce that three angles necessarily exist, but, upon condition that a triangle exists, three angles must necessarily exist—in it. And thus this logical necessity has been the source of the greatest delusions. Having formed an à priori conception of a thing, the content of which was made to embrace existence, we believed ourselves safe in concluding that, because existence belongs necessarily to the object of the conception (that is, under the condition of my positing this thing as given), the existence of the thing is also posited necessarily, and that it is therefore absolutely necessary—merely because its existence has been cogitated in the conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","text":"If, in an identical judgement, I annihilate the predicate in thought, and retain the subject, a contradiction is the result; and hence I say, the former belongs necessarily to the latter. But if I suppress both subject and predicate in thought, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing at all, and therefore no means of forming a contradiction. To suppose the existence of a triangle and not that of its three angles, is self-contradictory; but to suppose the non-existence of both triangle and angles is perfectly admissible. And so is it with the conception of an absolutely necessary being. Annihilate its existence in thought, and you annihilate the thing itself with all its predicates; how then can there be any room for contradiction? Externally, there is nothing to give rise to a contradiction, for a thing cannot be necessary externally; nor internally, for, by the annihilation or suppression of the thing itself, its internal properties are also annihilated. God is omnipotent—that is a necessary judgement. His omnipotence cannot be denied, if the existence of a Deity is posited—the existence, that is, of an infinite being, the two conceptions being identical. But when you say, God does not exist, neither omnipotence nor any other predicate is affirmed; they must all disappear with the subject, and in this judgement there cannot exist the least self-contradiction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","text":"You have thus seen that when the predicate of a judgement is annihilated in thought along with the subject, no internal contradiction can arise, be the predicate what it may. There is no possibility of evading the conclusion—you find yourselves compelled to declare: There are certain subjects which cannot be annihilated in thought. But this is nothing more than saying: There exist subjects which are absolutely necessary—the very hypothesis which you are called upon to establish. For I find myself unable to form the slightest conception of a thing which when annihilated in thought with all its predicates, leaves behind a contradiction; and contradiction is the only criterion of impossibility in the sphere of pure à priori conceptions.\n\nAgainst these general considerations, the justice of which no one can dispute, one argument is adduced, which is regarded as furnishing a satisfactory demonstration from the fact. It is affirmed that there is one and only one conception, in which the non-being or annihilation of the object is self-contradictory, and this is the conception of an ens realissimum. It possesses, you say, all reality, and you feel yourselves justified in admitting the possibility of such a being. (This I am willing to grant for the present, although the existence of a conception which is not self-contradictory is far from being sufficient to prove the possibility of an object.)[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-67) Now the notion of all reality embraces in it that of existence; the notion of existence lies, therefore, in the conception of this possible thing. If this thing is annihilated in thought, the internal possibility of the thing is also annihilated, which is self-contradictory.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","text":"[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-67) A conception is always possible, if it is not self-contradictory. This is the logical criterion of possibility, distinguishing the object of such a conception from the nihil negativum. But it may be, notwithstanding, an empty conception, unless the objective reality of this synthesis, but which it is generated, is demonstrated; and a proof of this kind must be based upon principles of possible experience, and not upon the principle of analysis or contradiction. This remark may be serviceable as a warning against concluding, from the possibility of a conception—which is logical—the possibility of a thing—which is real.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","text":"I answer: It is absurd to introduce—under whatever term disguised—into the conception of a thing, which is to be cogitated solely in reference to its possibility, the conception of its existence. If this is admitted, you will have apparently gained the day, but in reality have enounced nothing but a mere tautology. I ask, is the proposition, this or that thing (which I am admitting to be possible) exists, an analytical or a synthetical proposition? If the former, there is no addition made to the subject of your thought by the affirmation of its existence; but then the conception in your minds is identical with the thing itself, or you have supposed the existence of a thing to be possible, and then inferred its existence from its internal possibility—which is but a miserable tautology. The word reality in the conception of the thing, and the word existence in the conception of the predicate, will not help you out of the difficulty. For, supposing you were to term all positing of a thing reality, you have thereby posited the thing with all its predicates in the conception of the subject and assumed its actual existence, and this you merely repeat in the predicate. But if you confess, as every reasonable person must, that every existential proposition is synthetical, how can it be maintained that the predicate of existence cannot be denied without contradiction?—a property which is the characteristic of analytical propositions, alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0007","text":"I should have a reasonable hope of putting an end for ever to this sophistical mode of argumentation, by a strict definition of the conception of existence, did not my own experience teach me that the illusion arising from our confounding a logical with a real predicate (a predicate which aids in the determination of a thing) resists almost all the endeavours of explanation and illustration. A logical predicate may be what you please, even the subject may be predicated of itself; for logic pays no regard to the content of a judgement. But the determination of a conception is a predicate, which adds to and enlarges the conception. It must not, therefore, be contained in the conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","text":"Being is evidently not a real predicate, that is, a conception of something which is added to the conception of some other thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations in it. Logically, it is merely the copula of a judgement. The proposition, God is omnipotent, contains two conceptions, which have a certain object or content; the word is, is no additional predicate—it merely indicates the relation of the predicate to the subject. Now, if I take the subject (God) with all its predicates (omnipotence being one), and say: God is, or, There is a God, I add no new predicate to the conception of God, I merely posit or affirm the existence of the subject with all its predicates—I posit the object in relation to my conception. The content of both is the same; and there is no addition made to the conception, which expresses merely the possibility of the object, by my cogitating the object—in the expression, it is—as absolutely given or existing. Thus the real contains no more than the possible. A hundred real dollars contain no more than a hundred possible dollars. For, as the latter indicate the conception, and the former the object, on the supposition that the content of the former was greater than that of the latter, my conception would not be an expression of the whole object, and would consequently be an inadequate conception of it. But in reckoning my wealth there may be said to be more in a hundred real dollars than in a hundred possible dollars—that is, in the mere conception of them. For the real object—the dollars—is not analytically contained in my conception, but forms a synthetical addition to my conception (which is merely a determination of my mental state), although this objective reality—this existence—apart from my conceptions, does not in the least degree increase the aforesaid hundred dollars.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","text":"By whatever and by whatever number of predicates—even to the complete determination of it—I may cogitate a thing, I do not in the least augment the object of my conception by the addition of the statement: This thing exists. Otherwise, not exactly the same, but something more than what was cogitated in my conception, would exist, and I could not affirm that the exact object of my conception had real existence. If I cogitate a thing as containing all modes of reality except one, the mode of reality which is absent is not added to the conception of the thing by the affirmation that the thing exists; on the contrary, the thing exists—if it exist at all—with the same defect as that cogitated in its conception; otherwise not that which was cogitated, but something different, exists. Now, if I cogitate a being as the highest reality, without defect or imperfection, the question still remains—whether this being exists or not? For, although no element is wanting in the possible real content of my conception, there is a defect in its relation to my mental state, that is, I am ignorant whether the cognition of the object indicated by the conception is possible à posteriori. And here the cause of the present difficulty becomes apparent. If the question regarded an object of sense merely, it would be impossible for me to confound the conception with the existence of a thing. For the conception merely enables me to cogitate an object as according with the general conditions of experience; while the existence of the object permits me to cogitate it as contained in the sphere of actual experience. At the same time, this connection with the world of experience does not in the least augment the conception, although a possible perception has been added to the experience of the mind. But if we cogitate existence by the pure category alone, it is not to be wondered at, that we should find ourselves unable to present any criterion sufficient to distinguish it from mere possibility.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","text":"Whatever be the content of our conception of an object, it is necessary to go beyond it, if we wish to predicate existence of the object. In the case of sensuous objects, this is attained by their connection according to empirical laws with some one of my perceptions; but there is no means of cognizing the existence of objects of pure thought, because it must be cognized completely à priori. But all our knowledge of existence (be it immediately by perception, or by inferences connecting some object with a perception) belongs entirely to the sphere of experience—which is in perfect unity with itself; and although an existence out of this sphere cannot be absolutely declared to be impossible, it is a hypothesis the truth of which we have no means of ascertaining.\n\nThe notion of a Supreme Being is in many respects a highly useful idea; but for the very reason that it is an idea, it is incapable of enlarging our cognition with regard to the existence of things. It is not even sufficient to instruct us as to the possibility of a being which we do not know to exist. The analytical criterion of possibility, which consists in the absence of contradiction in propositions, cannot be denied it. But the connection of real properties in a thing is a synthesis of the possibility of which an à priori judgement cannot be formed, because these realities are not presented to us specifically; and even if this were to happen, a judgement would still be impossible, because the criterion of the possibility of synthetical cognitions must be sought for in the world of experience, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. And thus the celebrated Leibnitz has utterly failed in his attempt to establish upon à priori grounds the possibility of this sublime ideal being.\n\nThe celebrated ontological or Cartesian argument for the existence of a Supreme Being is therefore insufficient; and we may as well hope to increase our stock of knowledge by the aid of mere ideas, as the merchant to augment his wealth by the addition of noughts to his cash account.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1614232f861c711a6d29d60cf6d818ef3da381a0c3cd221ab0ccf33df685a492","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","order":79,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVIC","text":"# Theology\n\n### Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt was by no means a natural course of proceeding, but, on the contrary, an invention entirely due to the subtlety of the schools, to attempt to draw from a mere idea a proof of the existence of an object corresponding to it. Such a course would never have been pursued, were it not for that need of reason which requires it to suppose the existence of a necessary being as a basis for the empirical regress, and that, as this necessity must be unconditioned and à priori, reason is bound to discover a conception which shall satisfy, if possible, this requirement, and enable us to attain to the à priori cognition of such a being. This conception was thought to be found in the idea of an ens realissimum, and thus this idea was employed for the attainment of a better defined knowledge of a necessary being, of the existence of which we were convinced, or persuaded, on other grounds. Thus reason was seduced from her natural courage; and, instead of concluding with the conception of an ens realissimum, an attempt was made to begin with it, for the purpose of inferring from it that idea of a necessary existence which it was in fact called in to complete. Thus arose that unfortunate ontological argument, which neither satisfies the healthy common sense of humanity, nor sustains the scientific examination of the philosopher.\n\nThe cosmological proof, which we are about to examine, retains the connection between absolute necessity and the highest reality; but, instead of reasoning from this highest reality to a necessary existence, like the preceding argument, it concludes from the given unconditioned necessity of some being its unlimited reality. The track it pursues, whether rational or sophistical, is at least natural, and not only goes far to persuade the common understanding, but shows itself deserving of respect from the speculative intellect; while it contains, at the same time, the outlines of all the arguments employed in natural theology—arguments which always have been, and still will be, in use and authority. These, however adorned, and hid under whatever embellishments of rhetoric and sentiment, are at bottom identical with the arguments we are at present to discuss. This proof, termed by Leibnitz the argumentum a contingentia mundi, I shall now lay before the reader, and subject to a strict examination.\n\nIt is framed in the following manner: If something exists, an absolutely necessary being must likewise exist. Now I, at least, exist. Consequently, there exists an absolutely necessary being. The minor contains an experience, the major reasons from a general experience to the existence of a necessary being.[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-68) Thus this argument really begins at experience, and is not completely à priori, or ontological. The object of all possible experience being the world, it is called the cosmological proof. It contains no reference to any peculiar property of sensuous objects, by which this world of sense might be distinguished from other possible worlds; and in this respect it differs from the physico-theological proof, which is based upon the consideration of the peculiar constitution of our sensuous world.\n\n[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-68) This inference is too well known to require more detailed discussion. It is based upon the spurious transcendental law of causality, that everything which is contingent has a cause, which, if itself contingent, must also have a cause; and so on, till the series of subordinated causes must end with an absolutely necessary cause, without which it would not possess completeness.\n\nThe proof proceeds thus: A necessary being can be determined only in one way, that is, it can be determined by only one of all possible opposed predicates; consequently, it must be completely determined in and by its conception. But there is only a single conception of a thing possible, which completely determines the thing à priori: that is, the conception of the ens realissimum. It follows that the conception of the ens realissimum is the only conception by and in which we can cogitate a necessary being. Consequently, a Supreme Being necessarily exists.\n\nIn this cosmological argument are assembled so many sophistical propositions that speculative reason seems to have exerted in it all her dialectical skill to produce a transcendental illusion of the most extreme character. We shall postpone an investigation of this argument for the present, and confine ourselves to exposing the stratagem by which it imposes upon us an old argument in a new dress, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, the one with the credentials of pure reason, and the other with those of empiricism; while, in fact, it is only the former who has changed his dress and voice, for the purpose of passing himself off for an additional witness. That it may possess a secure foundation, it bases its conclusions upon experience, and thus appears to be completely distinct from the ontological argument, which places its confidence entirely in pure à priori conceptions. But this experience merely aids reason in making one step—to the existence of a necessary being. What the properties of this being are cannot be learned from experience; and therefore reason abandons it altogether, and pursues its inquiries in the sphere of pure conception, for the purpose of discovering what the properties of an absolutely necessary being ought to be, that is, what among all possible things contain the conditions (requisita) of absolute necessity. Reason believes that it has discovered these requisites in the conception of an ens realissimum—and in it alone, and hence concludes: The ens realissimum is an absolutely necessary being. But it is evident that reason has here presupposed that the conception of an ens realissimum is perfectly adequate to the conception of a being of absolute necessity, that is, that we may infer the existence of the latter from that of the former—a proposition which formed the basis of the ontological argument, and which is now employed in the support of the cosmological argument, contrary to the wish and professions of its inventors. For the existence of an absolutely necessary being is given in conceptions alone. But if I say: “The conception of the ens realissimum is a conception of this kind, and in fact the only conception which is adequate to our idea of a necessary being,” I am obliged to admit, that the latter may be inferred from the former. Thus it is properly the ontological argument which figures in the cosmological, and constitutes the whole strength of the latter; while the spurious basis of experience has been of no further use than to conduct us to the conception of absolute necessity, being utterly insufficient to demonstrate the presence of this attribute in any determinate existence or thing. For when we propose to ourselves an aim of this character, we must abandon the sphere of experience, and rise to that of pure conceptions, which we examine with the purpose of discovering whether any one contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is thus demonstrated, its existence is also proved; for we may then assert that, of all possible beings there is one which possesses the attribute of necessity—in other words, this being possesses an absolutely necessary existence.\n\nAll illusions in an argument are more easily detected when they are presented in the formal manner employed by the schools, which we now proceed to do.\n\nIf the proposition: “Every absolutely necessary being is likewise an ens realissimum,” is correct (and it is this which constitutes the nervus probandi of the cosmological argument), it must, like all affirmative judgements, be capable of conversion—the conversio per accidens, at least. It follows, then, that some entia realissima are absolutely necessary beings. But no ens realissimum is in any respect different from another, and what is valid of some is valid of all. In this present case, therefore, I may employ simple conversion, and say: “Every ens realissimum is a necessary being.” But as this proposition is determined à priori by the conceptions contained in it, the mere conception of an ens realissimum must possess the additional attribute of absolute necessity. But this is exactly what was maintained in the ontological argument, and not recognized by the cosmological, although it formed the real ground of its disguised and illusory reasoning.\n\nThus the second mode employed by speculative reason of demonstrating the existence of a Supreme Being, is not only, like the first, illusory and inadequate, but possesses the additional blemish of an ignoratio elenchi—professing to conduct us by a new road to the desired goal, but bringing us back, after a short circuit, to the old path which we had deserted at its call.\n\nI mentioned above that this cosmological argument contains a perfect nest of dialectical assumptions, which transcendental criticism does not find it difficult to expose and to dissipate. I shall merely enumerate these, leaving it to the reader, who must by this time be well practised in such matters, to investigate the fallacies residing therein.\n\nThe following fallacies, for example, are discoverable in this mode of proof: 1. The transcendental principle: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause”—a principle without significance, except in the sensuous world. For the purely intellectual conception of the contingent cannot produce any synthetical proposition, like that of causality, which is itself without significance or distinguishing characteristic except in the phenomenal world. But in the present case it is employed to help us beyond the limits of its sphere. 2. “From the impossibility of an infinite ascending series of causes in the world of sense a first cause is inferred”; a conclusion which the principles of the employment of reason do not justify even in the sphere of experience, and still less when an attempt is made to pass the limits of this sphere. 3. Reason allows itself to be satisfied upon insufficient grounds, with regard to the completion of this series. It removes all conditions (without which, however, no conception of Necessity can take place); and, as after this it is beyond our power to form any other conceptions, it accepts this as a completion of the conception it wishes to form of the series. 4. The logical possibility of a conception of the total of reality (the criterion of this possibility being the absence of contradiction) is confounded with the transcendental, which requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis—a principle which again refers us to the world of experience. And so on.\n\nThe aim of the cosmological argument is to avoid the necessity of proving the existence of a necessary being priori from mere conceptions—a proof which must be ontological, and of which we feel ourselves quite incapable. With this purpose, we reason from an actual existence—an experience in general, to an absolutely necessary condition of that existence. It is in this case unnecessary to demonstrate its possibility. For after having proved that it exists, the question regarding its possibility is superfluous. Now, when we wish to define more strictly the nature of this necessary being, we do not look out for some being the conception of which would enable us to comprehend the necessity of its being—for if we could do this, an empirical presupposition would be unnecessary; no, we try to discover merely the negative condition (conditio sine qua non), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would be perfectly admissible in every sort of reasoning, from a consequence to its principle; but in the present case it unfortunately happens that the condition of absolute necessity can be discovered in but a single being, the conception of which must consequently contain all that is requisite for demonstrating the presence of absolute necessity, and thus entitle me to infer this absolute necessity à priori. That is, it must be possible to reason conversely, and say: The thing, to which the conception of the highest reality belongs, is absolutely necessary. But if I cannot reason thus—and I cannot, unless I believe in the sufficiency of the ontological argument—I find insurmountable obstacles in my new path, and am really no farther than the point from which I set out. The conception of a Supreme Being satisfies all questions à priori regarding the internal determinations of a thing, and is for this reason an ideal without equal or parallel, the general conception of it indicating it as at the same time an ens individuum among all possible things. But the conception does not satisfy the question regarding its existence—which was the purpose of all our inquiries; and, although the existence of a necessary being were admitted, we should find it impossible to answer the question: What of all things in the world must be regarded as such?\n\nIt is certainly allowable to admit the existence of an all-sufficient being—a cause of all possible effects—for the purpose of enabling reason to introduce unity into its mode and grounds of explanation with regard to phenomena. But to assert that such a being necessarily exists, is no longer the modest enunciation of an admissible hypothesis, but the boldest declaration of an apodeictic certainty; for the cognition of that which is absolutely necessary must itself possess that character.\n\nThe aim of the transcendental ideal formed by the mind is either to discover a conception which shall harmonize with the idea of absolute necessity, or a conception which shall contain that idea. If the one is possible, so is the other; for reason recognizes that alone as absolutely necessary which is necessary from its conception. But both attempts are equally beyond our power—we find it impossible to satisfy the understanding upon this point, and as impossible to induce it to remain at rest in relation to this incapacity.\n\nUnconditioned necessity, which, as the ultimate support and stay of all existing things, is an indispensable requirement of the mind, is an abyss on the verge of which human reason trembles in dismay. Even the idea of eternity, terrible and sublime as it is, as depicted by Haller, does not produce upon the mental vision such a feeling of awe and terror; for, although it measures the duration of things, it does not support them. We cannot bear, nor can we rid ourselves of the thought that a being, which we regard as the greatest of all possible existences, should say to himself: I am from eternity to eternity; beside me there is nothing, except that which exists by my will; whence then am I? Here all sinks away from under us; and the greatest, as the smallest, perfection, hovers without stay or footing in presence of the speculative reason, which finds it as easy to part with the one as with the other.\n\nMany physical powers, which evidence their existence by their effects, are perfectly inscrutable in their nature; they elude all our powers of observation. The transcendental object which forms the basis of phenomena, and, in connection with it, the reason why our sensibility possesses this rather than that particular kind of conditions, are and must ever remain hidden from our mental vision; the fact is there, the reason of the fact we cannot see. But an ideal of pure reason cannot be termed mysterious or inscrutable, because the only credential of its reality is the need of it felt by reason, for the purpose of giving completeness to the world of synthetical unity. An ideal is not even given as a cogitable object, and therefore cannot be inscrutable; on the contrary, it must, as a mere idea, be based on the constitution of reason itself, and on this account must be capable of explanation and solution. For the very essence of reason consists in its ability to give an account, of all our conceptions, opinions, and assertions—upon objective, or, when they happen to be illusory and fallacious, upon subjective grounds.\n\nDetection and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion in all Transcendental Arguments for the Existence of a Necessary Being.\n\nBoth of the above arguments are transcendental; in other words, they do not proceed upon empirical principles. For, although the cosmological argument professed to lay a basis of experience for its edifice of reasoning, it did not ground its procedure upon the peculiar constitution of experience, but upon pure principles of reason—in relation to an existence given by empirical consciousness; utterly abandoning its guidance, however, for the purpose of supporting its assertions entirely upon pure conceptions. Now what is the cause, in these transcendental arguments, of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the conceptions of necessity and supreme reality, and hypostatizes that which cannot be anything but an idea? What is the cause of this unavoidable step on the part of reason, of admitting that some one among all existing things must be necessary, while it falls back from the assertion of the existence of such a being as from an abyss? And how does reason proceed to explain this anomaly to itself, and from the wavering condition of a timid and reluctant approbation—always again withdrawn—arrive at a calm and settled insight into its cause?\n\nIt is something very remarkable that, on the supposition that something exists, I cannot avoid the inference that something exists necessarily. Upon this perfectly natural—but not on that account reliable—inference does the cosmological argument rest. But, let me form any conception whatever of a thing, I find that I cannot cogitate the existence of the thing as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me—be the thing or being what it may—from cogitating its non-existence. I may thus be obliged to admit that all existing things have a necessary basis, while I cannot cogitate any single or individual thing as necessary. In other words, I can never complete the regress through the conditions of existence, without admitting the existence of a necessary being; but, on the other hand, I cannot make a commencement from this being.\n\nIf I must cogitate something as existing necessarily as the basis of existing things, and yet am not permitted to cogitate any individual thing as in itself necessary, the inevitable inference is that necessity and contingency are not properties of things themselves—otherwise an internal contradiction would result; that consequently neither of these principles are objective, but merely subjective principles of reason—the one requiring us to seek for a necessary ground for everything that exists, that is, to be satisfied with no other explanation than that which is complete à priori, the other forbidding us ever to hope for the attainment of this completeness, that is, to regard no member of the empirical world as unconditioned. In this mode of viewing them, both principles, in their purely heuristic and regulative character, and as concerning merely the formal interest of reason, are quite consistent with each other. The one says: “You must philosophize upon nature,” as if there existed a necessary primal basis of all existing things, solely for the purpose of introducing systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing an idea of this character—a foundation which is arbitrarily admitted to be ultimate; while the other warns you to consider no individual determination, concerning the existence of things, as such an ultimate foundation, that is, as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way always open for further progress in the deduction, and to treat every determination as determined by some other. But if all that we perceive must be regarded as conditionally necessary, it is impossible that anything which is empirically given should be absolutely necessary.\n\nIt follows from this that you must accept the absolutely necessary as out of and beyond the world, inasmuch as it is useful only as a principle of the highest possible unity in experience, and you cannot discover any such necessary existence in the would, the second rule requiring you to regard all empirical causes of unity as themselves deduced.\n\nThe philosophers of antiquity regarded all the forms of nature as contingent; while matter was considered by them, in accordance with the judgement of the common reason of mankind, as primal and necessary. But if they had regarded matter, not relatively—as the substratum of phenomena, but absolutely and in itself—as an independent existence, this idea of absolute necessity would have immediately disappeared. For there is nothing absolutely connecting reason with such an existence; on the contrary, it can annihilate it in thought, always and without self-contradiction. But in thought alone lay the idea of absolute necessity. A regulative principle must, therefore, have been at the foundation of this opinion. In fact, extension and impenetrability—which together constitute our conception of matter—form the supreme empirical principle of the unity of phenomena, and this principle, in so far as it is empirically unconditioned, possesses the property of a regulative principle. But, as every determination of matter which constitutes what is real in it—and consequently impenetrability—is an effect, which must have a cause, and is for this reason always derived, the notion of matter cannot harmonize with the idea of a necessary being, in its character of the principle of all derived unity. For every one of its real properties, being derived, must be only conditionally necessary, and can therefore be annihilated in thought; and thus the whole existence of matter can be so annihilated or suppressed. If this were not the case, we should have found in the world of phenomena the highest ground or condition of unity—which is impossible, according to the second regulative principle. It follows that matter, and, in general, all that forms part of the world of sense, cannot be a necessary primal being, nor even a principle of empirical unity, but that this being or principle must have its place assigned without the world. And, in this way, we can proceed in perfect confidence to deduce the phenomena of the world and their existence from other phenomena, just as if there existed no necessary being; and we can at the same time, strive without ceasing towards the attainment of completeness for our deduction, just as if such a being—the supreme condition of all existences—were presupposed by the mind.\n\nThese remarks will have made it evident to the reader that the ideal of the Supreme Being, far from being an enouncement of the existence of a being in itself necessary, is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, requiring us to regard all connection existing between phenomena as if it had its origin from an all-sufficient necessary cause, and basing upon this the rule of a systematic and necessary unity in the explanation of phenomena. We cannot, at the same time, avoid regarding, by a transcendental subreptio, this formal principle as constitutive, and hypostatizing this unity. Precisely similar is the case with our notion of space. Space is the primal condition of all forms, which are properly just so many different limitations of it; and thus, although it is merely a principle of sensibility, we cannot help regarding it as an absolutely necessary and self-subsistent thing—as an object given à priori in itself. In the same way, it is quite natural that, as the systematic unity of nature cannot be established as a principle for the empirical employment of reason, unless it is based upon the idea of an ens realissimum, as the supreme cause, we should regard this idea as a real object, and this object, in its character of supreme condition, as absolutely necessary, and that in this way a regulative should be transformed into a constitutive principle. This interchange becomes evident when I regard this supreme being, which, relatively to the world, was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as a thing per se. In this case, I find it impossible to represent this necessity in or by any conception, and it exists merely in my own mind, as the formal condition of thought, but not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0001","text":"# Theology\n\n### Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt was by no means a natural course of proceeding, but, on the contrary, an invention entirely due to the subtlety of the schools, to attempt to draw from a mere idea a proof of the existence of an object corresponding to it. Such a course would never have been pursued, were it not for that need of reason which requires it to suppose the existence of a necessary being as a basis for the empirical regress, and that, as this necessity must be unconditioned and à priori, reason is bound to discover a conception which shall satisfy, if possible, this requirement, and enable us to attain to the à priori cognition of such a being. This conception was thought to be found in the idea of an ens realissimum, and thus this idea was employed for the attainment of a better defined knowledge of a necessary being, of the existence of which we were convinced, or persuaded, on other grounds. Thus reason was seduced from her natural courage; and, instead of concluding with the conception of an ens realissimum, an attempt was made to begin with it, for the purpose of inferring from it that idea of a necessary existence which it was in fact called in to complete. Thus arose that unfortunate ontological argument, which neither satisfies the healthy common sense of humanity, nor sustains the scientific examination of the philosopher.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0002","text":"The cosmological proof, which we are about to examine, retains the connection between absolute necessity and the highest reality; but, instead of reasoning from this highest reality to a necessary existence, like the preceding argument, it concludes from the given unconditioned necessity of some being its unlimited reality. The track it pursues, whether rational or sophistical, is at least natural, and not only goes far to persuade the common understanding, but shows itself deserving of respect from the speculative intellect; while it contains, at the same time, the outlines of all the arguments employed in natural theology—arguments which always have been, and still will be, in use and authority. These, however adorned, and hid under whatever embellishments of rhetoric and sentiment, are at bottom identical with the arguments we are at present to discuss. This proof, termed by Leibnitz the argumentum a contingentia mundi, I shall now lay before the reader, and subject to a strict examination.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","text":"It is framed in the following manner: If something exists, an absolutely necessary being must likewise exist. Now I, at least, exist. Consequently, there exists an absolutely necessary being. The minor contains an experience, the major reasons from a general experience to the existence of a necessary being.[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-68) Thus this argument really begins at experience, and is not completely à priori, or ontological. The object of all possible experience being the world, it is called the cosmological proof. It contains no reference to any peculiar property of sensuous objects, by which this world of sense might be distinguished from other possible worlds; and in this respect it differs from the physico-theological proof, which is based upon the consideration of the peculiar constitution of our sensuous world.\n\n[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-68) This inference is too well known to require more detailed discussion. It is based upon the spurious transcendental law of causality, that everything which is contingent has a cause, which, if itself contingent, must also have a cause; and so on, till the series of subordinated causes must end with an absolutely necessary cause, without which it would not possess completeness.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","text":"The proof proceeds thus: A necessary being can be determined only in one way, that is, it can be determined by only one of all possible opposed predicates; consequently, it must be completely determined in and by its conception. But there is only a single conception of a thing possible, which completely determines the thing à priori: that is, the conception of the ens realissimum. It follows that the conception of the ens realissimum is the only conception by and in which we can cogitate a necessary being. Consequently, a Supreme Being necessarily exists.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","text":"In this cosmological argument are assembled so many sophistical propositions that speculative reason seems to have exerted in it all her dialectical skill to produce a transcendental illusion of the most extreme character. We shall postpone an investigation of this argument for the present, and confine ourselves to exposing the stratagem by which it imposes upon us an old argument in a new dress, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, the one with the credentials of pure reason, and the other with those of empiricism; while, in fact, it is only the former who has changed his dress and voice, for the purpose of passing himself off for an additional witness. That it may possess a secure foundation, it bases its conclusions upon experience, and thus appears to be completely distinct from the ontological argument, which places its confidence entirely in pure à priori conceptions. But this experience merely aids reason in making one step—to the existence of a necessary being. What the properties of this being are cannot be learned from experience; and therefore reason abandons it altogether, and pursues its inquiries in the sphere of pure conception, for the purpose of discovering what the properties of an absolutely necessary being ought to be, that is, what among all possible things contain the conditions (requisita) of absolute necessity. Reason believes that it has discovered these requisites in the conception of an ens realissimum—and in it alone, and hence concludes: The ens realissimum is an absolutely necessary being. But it is evident that reason has here presupposed that the conception of an ens realissimum is perfectly adequate to the conception of a being of absolute necessity, that is, that we may infer the existence of the latter from that of the former—a proposition which formed the basis of the ontological argument, and which is now employed in the support of the cosmological argument, contrary to the wish and professions of its inventors. For the existence of an absolutely necessary being is given in conceptions alone. But if I say: “The conception of the ens realissimum is a conception of this kind, and in fact the only conception which is adequate to our idea of a necessary being,” I am obliged to admit, that the latter may be inferred from the former. Thus it is properly the ontological argument which figures in the cosmological, and constitutes the whole strength of the latter; while the spurious basis of experience has been of no further use than to conduct us to the conception of absolute necessity, being utterly insufficient to demonstrate the presence of this attribute in any determinate existence or thing. For when we propose to ourselves an aim of this character, we must abandon the sphere of experience, and rise to that of pure conceptions, which we examine with the purpose of discovering whether any one contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is thus demonstrated, its existence is also proved; for we may then assert that, of all possible beings there is one which possesses the attribute of necessity—in other words, this being possesses an absolutely necessary existence.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","text":"All illusions in an argument are more easily detected when they are presented in the formal manner employed by the schools, which we now proceed to do.\n\nIf the proposition: “Every absolutely necessary being is likewise an ens realissimum,” is correct (and it is this which constitutes the nervus probandi of the cosmological argument), it must, like all affirmative judgements, be capable of conversion—the conversio per accidens, at least. It follows, then, that some entia realissima are absolutely necessary beings. But no ens realissimum is in any respect different from another, and what is valid of some is valid of all. In this present case, therefore, I may employ simple conversion, and say: “Every ens realissimum is a necessary being.” But as this proposition is determined à priori by the conceptions contained in it, the mere conception of an ens realissimum must possess the additional attribute of absolute necessity. But this is exactly what was maintained in the ontological argument, and not recognized by the cosmological, although it formed the real ground of its disguised and illusory reasoning.\n\nThus the second mode employed by speculative reason of demonstrating the existence of a Supreme Being, is not only, like the first, illusory and inadequate, but possesses the additional blemish of an ignoratio elenchi—professing to conduct us by a new road to the desired goal, but bringing us back, after a short circuit, to the old path which we had deserted at its call.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","text":"I mentioned above that this cosmological argument contains a perfect nest of dialectical assumptions, which transcendental criticism does not find it difficult to expose and to dissipate. I shall merely enumerate these, leaving it to the reader, who must by this time be well practised in such matters, to investigate the fallacies residing therein.\n\nThe following fallacies, for example, are discoverable in this mode of proof: 1. The transcendental principle: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause”—a principle without significance, except in the sensuous world. For the purely intellectual conception of the contingent cannot produce any synthetical proposition, like that of causality, which is itself without significance or distinguishing characteristic except in the phenomenal world. But in the present case it is employed to help us beyond the limits of its sphere. 2. “From the impossibility of an infinite ascending series of causes in the world of sense a first cause is inferred”; a conclusion which the principles of the employment of reason do not justify even in the sphere of experience, and still less when an attempt is made to pass the limits of this sphere. 3. Reason allows itself to be satisfied upon insufficient grounds, with regard to the completion of this series. It removes all conditions (without which, however, no conception of Necessity can take place); and, as after this it is beyond our power to form any other conceptions, it accepts this as a completion of the conception it wishes to form of the series. 4. The logical possibility of a conception of the total of reality (the criterion of this possibility being the absence of contradiction) is confounded with the transcendental, which requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis—a principle which again refers us to the world of experience. And so on.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0008","text":"The aim of the cosmological argument is to avoid the necessity of proving the existence of a necessary being priori from mere conceptions—a proof which must be ontological, and of which we feel ourselves quite incapable. With this purpose, we reason from an actual existence—an experience in general, to an absolutely necessary condition of that existence. It is in this case unnecessary to demonstrate its possibility. For after having proved that it exists, the question regarding its possibility is superfluous. Now, when we wish to define more strictly the nature of this necessary being, we do not look out for some being the conception of which would enable us to comprehend the necessity of its being—for if we could do this, an empirical presupposition would be unnecessary; no, we try to discover merely the negative condition (conditio sine qua non), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would be perfectly admissible in every sort of reasoning, from a consequence to its principle; but in the present case it unfortunately happens that the condition of absolute necessity can be discovered in but a single being, the conception of which must consequently contain all that is requisite for demonstrating the presence of absolute necessity, and thus entitle me to infer this absolute necessity à priori. That is, it must be possible to reason conversely, and say: The thing, to which the conception of the highest reality belongs, is absolutely necessary. But if I cannot reason thus—and I cannot, unless I believe in the sufficiency of the ontological argument—I find insurmountable obstacles in my new path, and am really no farther than the point from which I set out. The conception of a Supreme Being satisfies all questions à priori regarding the internal determinations of a thing, and is for this reason an ideal without equal or parallel, the general conception of it indicating it as at the same time an ens individuum among all possible things. But the conception does not satisfy the question regarding its existence—which was the purpose of all our inquiries; and, although the existence of a necessary being were admitted, we should find it impossible to answer the question: What of all things in the world must be regarded as such?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","text":"It is certainly allowable to admit the existence of an all-sufficient being—a cause of all possible effects—for the purpose of enabling reason to introduce unity into its mode and grounds of explanation with regard to phenomena. But to assert that such a being necessarily exists, is no longer the modest enunciation of an admissible hypothesis, but the boldest declaration of an apodeictic certainty; for the cognition of that which is absolutely necessary must itself possess that character.\n\nThe aim of the transcendental ideal formed by the mind is either to discover a conception which shall harmonize with the idea of absolute necessity, or a conception which shall contain that idea. If the one is possible, so is the other; for reason recognizes that alone as absolutely necessary which is necessary from its conception. But both attempts are equally beyond our power—we find it impossible to satisfy the understanding upon this point, and as impossible to induce it to remain at rest in relation to this incapacity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0010","text":"Unconditioned necessity, which, as the ultimate support and stay of all existing things, is an indispensable requirement of the mind, is an abyss on the verge of which human reason trembles in dismay. Even the idea of eternity, terrible and sublime as it is, as depicted by Haller, does not produce upon the mental vision such a feeling of awe and terror; for, although it measures the duration of things, it does not support them. We cannot bear, nor can we rid ourselves of the thought that a being, which we regard as the greatest of all possible existences, should say to himself: I am from eternity to eternity; beside me there is nothing, except that which exists by my will; whence then am I? Here all sinks away from under us; and the greatest, as the smallest, perfection, hovers without stay or footing in presence of the speculative reason, which finds it as easy to part with the one as with the other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","text":"Many physical powers, which evidence their existence by their effects, are perfectly inscrutable in their nature; they elude all our powers of observation. The transcendental object which forms the basis of phenomena, and, in connection with it, the reason why our sensibility possesses this rather than that particular kind of conditions, are and must ever remain hidden from our mental vision; the fact is there, the reason of the fact we cannot see. But an ideal of pure reason cannot be termed mysterious or inscrutable, because the only credential of its reality is the need of it felt by reason, for the purpose of giving completeness to the world of synthetical unity. An ideal is not even given as a cogitable object, and therefore cannot be inscrutable; on the contrary, it must, as a mere idea, be based on the constitution of reason itself, and on this account must be capable of explanation and solution. For the very essence of reason consists in its ability to give an account, of all our conceptions, opinions, and assertions—upon objective, or, when they happen to be illusory and fallacious, upon subjective grounds.\n\nDetection and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion in all Transcendental Arguments for the Existence of a Necessary Being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0012","text":"Both of the above arguments are transcendental; in other words, they do not proceed upon empirical principles. For, although the cosmological argument professed to lay a basis of experience for its edifice of reasoning, it did not ground its procedure upon the peculiar constitution of experience, but upon pure principles of reason—in relation to an existence given by empirical consciousness; utterly abandoning its guidance, however, for the purpose of supporting its assertions entirely upon pure conceptions. Now what is the cause, in these transcendental arguments, of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the conceptions of necessity and supreme reality, and hypostatizes that which cannot be anything but an idea? What is the cause of this unavoidable step on the part of reason, of admitting that some one among all existing things must be necessary, while it falls back from the assertion of the existence of such a being as from an abyss? And how does reason proceed to explain this anomaly to itself, and from the wavering condition of a timid and reluctant approbation—always again withdrawn—arrive at a calm and settled insight into its cause?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","text":"It is something very remarkable that, on the supposition that something exists, I cannot avoid the inference that something exists necessarily. Upon this perfectly natural—but not on that account reliable—inference does the cosmological argument rest. But, let me form any conception whatever of a thing, I find that I cannot cogitate the existence of the thing as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me—be the thing or being what it may—from cogitating its non-existence. I may thus be obliged to admit that all existing things have a necessary basis, while I cannot cogitate any single or individual thing as necessary. In other words, I can never complete the regress through the conditions of existence, without admitting the existence of a necessary being; but, on the other hand, I cannot make a commencement from this being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","text":"If I must cogitate something as existing necessarily as the basis of existing things, and yet am not permitted to cogitate any individual thing as in itself necessary, the inevitable inference is that necessity and contingency are not properties of things themselves—otherwise an internal contradiction would result; that consequently neither of these principles are objective, but merely subjective principles of reason—the one requiring us to seek for a necessary ground for everything that exists, that is, to be satisfied with no other explanation than that which is complete à priori, the other forbidding us ever to hope for the attainment of this completeness, that is, to regard no member of the empirical world as unconditioned. In this mode of viewing them, both principles, in their purely heuristic and regulative character, and as concerning merely the formal interest of reason, are quite consistent with each other. The one says: “You must philosophize upon nature,” as if there existed a necessary primal basis of all existing things, solely for the purpose of introducing systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing an idea of this character—a foundation which is arbitrarily admitted to be ultimate; while the other warns you to consider no individual determination, concerning the existence of things, as such an ultimate foundation, that is, as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way always open for further progress in the deduction, and to treat every determination as determined by some other. But if all that we perceive must be regarded as conditionally necessary, it is impossible that anything which is empirically given should be absolutely necessary.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","text":"It follows from this that you must accept the absolutely necessary as out of and beyond the world, inasmuch as it is useful only as a principle of the highest possible unity in experience, and you cannot discover any such necessary existence in the would, the second rule requiring you to regard all empirical causes of unity as themselves deduced.\n\nThe philosophers of antiquity regarded all the forms of nature as contingent; while matter was considered by them, in accordance with the judgement of the common reason of mankind, as primal and necessary. But if they had regarded matter, not relatively—as the substratum of phenomena, but absolutely and in itself—as an independent existence, this idea of absolute necessity would have immediately disappeared. For there is nothing absolutely connecting reason with such an existence; on the contrary, it can annihilate it in thought, always and without self-contradiction. But in thought alone lay the idea of absolute necessity. A regulative principle must, therefore, have been at the foundation of this opinion. In fact, extension and impenetrability—which together constitute our conception of matter—form the supreme empirical principle of the unity of phenomena, and this principle, in so far as it is empirically unconditioned, possesses the property of a regulative principle. But, as every determination of matter which constitutes what is real in it—and consequently impenetrability—is an effect, which must have a cause, and is for this reason always derived, the notion of matter cannot harmonize with the idea of a necessary being, in its character of the principle of all derived unity. For every one of its real properties, being derived, must be only conditionally necessary, and can therefore be annihilated in thought; and thus the whole existence of matter can be so annihilated or suppressed. If this were not the case, we should have found in the world of phenomena the highest ground or condition of unity—which is impossible, according to the second regulative principle. It follows that matter, and, in general, all that forms part of the world of sense, cannot be a necessary primal being, nor even a principle of empirical unity, but that this being or principle must have its place assigned without the world. And, in this way, we can proceed in perfect confidence to deduce the phenomena of the world and their existence from other phenomena, just as if there existed no necessary being; and we can at the same time, strive without ceasing towards the attainment of completeness for our deduction, just as if such a being—the supreme condition of all existences—were presupposed by the mind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","text":"These remarks will have made it evident to the reader that the ideal of the Supreme Being, far from being an enouncement of the existence of a being in itself necessary, is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, requiring us to regard all connection existing between phenomena as if it had its origin from an all-sufficient necessary cause, and basing upon this the rule of a systematic and necessary unity in the explanation of phenomena. We cannot, at the same time, avoid regarding, by a transcendental subreptio, this formal principle as constitutive, and hypostatizing this unity. Precisely similar is the case with our notion of space. Space is the primal condition of all forms, which are properly just so many different limitations of it; and thus, although it is merely a principle of sensibility, we cannot help regarding it as an absolutely necessary and self-subsistent thing—as an object given à priori in itself. In the same way, it is quite natural that, as the systematic unity of nature cannot be established as a principle for the empirical employment of reason, unless it is based upon the idea of an ens realissimum, as the supreme cause, we should regard this idea as a real object, and this object, in its character of supreme condition, as absolutely necessary, and that in this way a regulative should be transformed into a constitutive principle. This interchange becomes evident when I regard this supreme being, which, relatively to the world, was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as a thing per se. In this case, I find it impossible to represent this necessity in or by any conception, and it exists merely in my own mind, as the formal condition of thought, but not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3521d4a0c73c7af1c63cd2e5a9a482505b5b1552c45eddca2ad3b05cb022b0ec","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","order":80,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVIP","text":"### Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof\n\nIf, then, neither a pure conception nor the general experience of an existing being can provide a sufficient basis for the proof of the existence of the Deity, we can make the attempt by the only other mode—that of grounding our argument upon a determinate experience of the phenomena of the present world, their constitution and disposition, and discover whether we can thus attain to a sound conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being. This argument we shall term the physico-theological argument. If it is shown to be insufficient, speculative reason cannot present us with any satisfactory proof of the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.\n\nIt is evident from the remarks that have been made in the preceding sections, that an answer to this question will be far from being difficult or unconvincing. For how can any experience be adequate with an idea? The very essence of an idea consists in the fact that no experience can ever be discovered congruent or adequate with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary and all-sufficient being is so immeasurably great, so high above all that is empirical, which is always conditioned, that we hope in vain to find materials in the sphere of experience sufficiently ample for our conception, and in vain seek the unconditioned among things that are conditioned, while examples, nay, even guidance is denied us by the laws of empirical synthesis.\n\nIf the Supreme Being forms a link in the chain of empirical conditions, it must be a member of the empirical series, and, like the lower members which it precedes, have its origin in some higher member of the series. If, on the other hand, we disengage it from the chain, and cogitate it as an intelligible being, apart from the series of natural causes—how shall reason bridge the abyss that separates the latter from the former? All laws respecting the regress from effects to causes, all synthetical additions to our knowledge relate solely to possible experience and the objects of the sensuous world, and, apart from them, are without significance.\n\nThe world around us opens before our view so magnificent a spectacle of order, variety, beauty, and conformity to ends, that whether we pursue our observations into the infinity of space in the one direction, or into its illimitable divisions in the other, whether we regard the world in its greatest or its least manifestations—even after we have attained to the highest summit of knowledge which our weak minds can reach, we find that language in the presence of wonders so inconceivable has lost its force, and number its power to reckon, nay, even thought fails to conceive adequately, and our conception of the whole dissolves into an astonishment without power of expression—all the more eloquent that it is dumb. Everywhere around us we observe a chain of causes and effects, of means and ends, of death and birth; and, as nothing has entered of itself into the condition in which we find it, we are constantly referred to some other thing, which itself suggests the same inquiry regarding its cause, and thus the universe must sink into the abyss of nothingness, unless we admit that, besides this infinite chain of contingencies, there exists something that is primal and self-subsistent—something which, as the cause of this phenomenal world, secures its continuance and preservation.\n\nThis highest cause—what magnitude shall we attribute to it? Of the content of the world we are ignorant; still less can we estimate its magnitude by comparison with the sphere of the possible. But this supreme cause being a necessity of the human mind, what is there to prevent us from attributing to it such a degree of perfection as to place it above the sphere of all that is possible? This we can easily do, although only by the aid of the faint outline of an abstract conception, by representing this being to ourselves as containing in itself, as an individual substance, all possible perfection—a conception which satisfies that requirement of reason which demands parsimony in principles, which is free from self-contradiction, which even contributes to the extension of the employment of reason in experience, by means of the guidance afforded by this idea to order and system, and which in no respect conflicts with any law of experience.\n\nThis argument always deserves to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and that most in conformity with the common reason of humanity. It animates the study of nature, as it itself derives its existence and draws ever new strength from that source. It introduces aims and ends into a sphere in which our observation could not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature, by directing our attention to a unity, the principle of which lies beyond nature. This knowledge of nature again reacts upon this idea—its cause; and thus our belief in a divine author of the universe rises to the power of an irresistible conviction.\n\nFor these reasons it would be utterly hopeless to attempt to rob this argument of the authority it has always enjoyed. The mind, unceasingly elevated by these considerations, which, although empirical, are so remarkably powerful, and continually adding to their force, will not suffer itself to be depressed by the doubts suggested by subtle speculation; it tears itself out of this state of uncertainty, the moment it casts a look upon the wondrous forms of nature and the majesty of the universe, and rises from height to height, from condition to condition, till it has elevated itself to the supreme and unconditioned author of all.\n\nBut although we have nothing to object to the reasonableness and utility of this procedure, but have rather to commend and encourage it, we cannot approve of the claims which this argument advances to demonstrative certainty and to a reception upon its own merits, apart from favour or support by other arguments. Nor can it injure the cause of morality to endeavour to lower the tone of the arrogant sophist, and to teach him that modesty and moderation which are the properties of a belief that brings calm and content into the mind, without prescribing to it an unworthy subjection. I maintain, then, that the physico-theological argument is insufficient of itself to prove the existence of a Supreme Being, that it must entrust this to the ontological argument—to which it serves merely as an introduction, and that, consequently, this argument contains the only possible ground of proof (possessed by speculative reason) for the existence of this being.\n\nThe chief momenta in the physico-theological argument are as follow: 1. We observe in the world manifest signs of an arrangement full of purpose, executed with great wisdom, and argument in whole of a content indescribably various, and of an extent without limits. 2. This arrangement of means and ends is entirely foreign to the things existing in the world—it belongs to them merely as a contingent attribute; in other words, the nature of different things could not of itself, whatever means were employed, harmoniously tend towards certain purposes, were they not chosen and directed for these purposes by a rational and disposing principle, in accordance with certain fundamental ideas. 3. There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which is not merely a blind, all-powerful nature, producing the beings and events which fill the world in unconscious fecundity, but a free and intelligent cause of the world. 4. The unity of this cause may be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation existing between the parts of the world, as portions of an artistic edifice—an inference which all our observation favours, and all principles of analogy support.\n\nIn the above argument, it is inferred from the analogy of certain products of nature with those of human art, when it compels Nature to bend herself to its purposes, as in the case of a house, a ship, or a watch, that the same kind of causality—namely, understanding and will—resides in nature. It is also declared that the internal possibility of this freely-acting nature (which is the source of all art, and perhaps also of human reason) is derivable from another and superhuman art—a conclusion which would perhaps be found incapable of standing the test of subtle transcendental criticism. But to neither of these opinions shall we at present object. We shall only remark that it must be confessed that, if we are to discuss the subject of cause at all, we cannot proceed more securely than with the guidance of the analogy subsisting between nature and such products of design—these being the only products whose causes and modes of organization are completely known to us. Reason would be unable to satisfy her own requirements, if she passed from a causality which she does know, to obscure and indemonstrable principles of explanation which she does not know.\n\nAccording to the physico-theological argument, the connection and harmony existing in the world evidence the contingency of the form merely, but not of the matter, that is, of the substance of the world. To establish the truth of the latter opinion, it would be necessary to prove that all things would be in themselves incapable of this harmony and order, unless they were, even as regards their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But this would require very different grounds of proof from those presented by the analogy with human art. This proof can at most, therefore, demonstrate the existence of an architect of the world, whose efforts are limited by the capabilities of the material with which he works, but not of a creator of the world, to whom all things are subject. Thus this argument is utterly insufficient for the task before us—a demonstration of the existence of an all-sufficient being. If we wish to prove the contingency of matter, we must have recourse to a transcendental argument, which the physico-theological was constructed expressly to avoid.\n\nWe infer, from the order and design visible in the universe, as a disposition of a thoroughly contingent character, the existence of a cause proportionate thereto. The conception of this cause must contain certain determinate qualities, and it must therefore be regarded as the conception of a being which possesses all power, wisdom, and so on, in one word, all perfection—the conception, that is, of an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, astonishing, or immeasurable power and excellence, give us no determinate conception of the thing, nor do they inform us what the thing may be in itself. They merely indicate the relation existing between the magnitude of the object and the observer, who compares it with himself and with his own power of comprehension, and are mere expressions of praise and reverence, by which the object is either magnified, or the observing subject depreciated in relation to the object. Where we have to do with the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing, we can discover no determinate conception, except that which comprehends all possible perfection or completeness, and it is only the total (omnitudo) of reality which is completely determined in and through its conception alone.\n\nNow it cannot be expected that any one will be bold enough to declare that he has a perfect insight into the relation which the magnitude of the world he contemplates bears (in its extent as well as in its content) to omnipotence, into that of the order and design in the world to the highest wisdom, and that of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of a Supreme Being. Physico-theology is therefore incapable of presenting a determinate conception of a supreme cause of the world, and is therefore insufficient as a principle of theology—a theology which is itself to be the basis of religion.\n\nThe attainment of absolute totality is completely impossible on the path of empiricism. And yet this is the path pursued in the physico-theological argument. What means shall we employ to bridge the abyss?\n\nAfter elevating ourselves to admiration of the magnitude of the power, wisdom, and other attributes of the author of the world, and finding we can advance no further, we leave the argument on empirical grounds, and proceed to infer the contingency of the world from the order and conformity to aims that are observable in it. From this contingency we infer, by the help of transcendental conceptions alone, the existence of something absolutely necessary; and, still advancing, proceed from the conception of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the completely determined or determining conception thereof—the conception of an all-embracing reality. Thus the physico-theological, failing in its undertaking, recurs in its embarrassment to the cosmological argument; and, as this is merely the ontological argument in disguise, it executes its design solely by the aid of pure reason, although it at first professed to have no connection with this faculty and to base its entire procedure upon experience alone.\n\nThe physico-theologians have therefore no reason to regard with such contempt the transcendental mode of argument, and to look down upon it, with the conceit of clear-sighted observers of nature, as the brain-cobweb of obscure speculatists. For, if they reflect upon and examine their own arguments, they will find that, after following for some time the path of nature and experience, and discovering themselves no nearer their object, they suddenly leave this path and pass into the region of pure possibility, where they hope to reach upon the wings of ideas what had eluded all their empirical investigations. Gaining, as they think, a firm footing after this immense leap, they extend their determinate conception—into the possession of which they have come, they know not how—over the whole sphere of creation, and explain their ideal, which is entirely a product of pure reason, by illustrations drawn from experience—though in a degree miserably unworthy of the grandeur of the object, while they refuse to acknowledge that they have arrived at this cognition or hypothesis by a very different road from that of experience.\n\nThus the physico-theological is based upon the cosmological, and this upon the ontological proof of the existence of a Supreme Being; and as besides these three there is no other path open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, on the ground of pure conceptions of reason, is the only possible one, if any proof of a proposition so far transcending the empirical exercise of the understanding is possible at all.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","text":"### Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof\n\nIf, then, neither a pure conception nor the general experience of an existing being can provide a sufficient basis for the proof of the existence of the Deity, we can make the attempt by the only other mode—that of grounding our argument upon a determinate experience of the phenomena of the present world, their constitution and disposition, and discover whether we can thus attain to a sound conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being. This argument we shall term the physico-theological argument. If it is shown to be insufficient, speculative reason cannot present us with any satisfactory proof of the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.\n\nIt is evident from the remarks that have been made in the preceding sections, that an answer to this question will be far from being difficult or unconvincing. For how can any experience be adequate with an idea? The very essence of an idea consists in the fact that no experience can ever be discovered congruent or adequate with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary and all-sufficient being is so immeasurably great, so high above all that is empirical, which is always conditioned, that we hope in vain to find materials in the sphere of experience sufficiently ample for our conception, and in vain seek the unconditioned among things that are conditioned, while examples, nay, even guidance is denied us by the laws of empirical synthesis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","text":"If the Supreme Being forms a link in the chain of empirical conditions, it must be a member of the empirical series, and, like the lower members which it precedes, have its origin in some higher member of the series. If, on the other hand, we disengage it from the chain, and cogitate it as an intelligible being, apart from the series of natural causes—how shall reason bridge the abyss that separates the latter from the former? All laws respecting the regress from effects to causes, all synthetical additions to our knowledge relate solely to possible experience and the objects of the sensuous world, and, apart from them, are without significance.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0003","text":"The world around us opens before our view so magnificent a spectacle of order, variety, beauty, and conformity to ends, that whether we pursue our observations into the infinity of space in the one direction, or into its illimitable divisions in the other, whether we regard the world in its greatest or its least manifestations—even after we have attained to the highest summit of knowledge which our weak minds can reach, we find that language in the presence of wonders so inconceivable has lost its force, and number its power to reckon, nay, even thought fails to conceive adequately, and our conception of the whole dissolves into an astonishment without power of expression—all the more eloquent that it is dumb. Everywhere around us we observe a chain of causes and effects, of means and ends, of death and birth; and, as nothing has entered of itself into the condition in which we find it, we are constantly referred to some other thing, which itself suggests the same inquiry regarding its cause, and thus the universe must sink into the abyss of nothingness, unless we admit that, besides this infinite chain of contingencies, there exists something that is primal and self-subsistent—something which, as the cause of this phenomenal world, secures its continuance and preservation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0004","text":"This highest cause—what magnitude shall we attribute to it? Of the content of the world we are ignorant; still less can we estimate its magnitude by comparison with the sphere of the possible. But this supreme cause being a necessity of the human mind, what is there to prevent us from attributing to it such a degree of perfection as to place it above the sphere of all that is possible? This we can easily do, although only by the aid of the faint outline of an abstract conception, by representing this being to ourselves as containing in itself, as an individual substance, all possible perfection—a conception which satisfies that requirement of reason which demands parsimony in principles, which is free from self-contradiction, which even contributes to the extension of the employment of reason in experience, by means of the guidance afforded by this idea to order and system, and which in no respect conflicts with any law of experience.\n\nThis argument always deserves to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and that most in conformity with the common reason of humanity. It animates the study of nature, as it itself derives its existence and draws ever new strength from that source. It introduces aims and ends into a sphere in which our observation could not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature, by directing our attention to a unity, the principle of which lies beyond nature. This knowledge of nature again reacts upon this idea—its cause; and thus our belief in a divine author of the universe rises to the power of an irresistible conviction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","text":"For these reasons it would be utterly hopeless to attempt to rob this argument of the authority it has always enjoyed. The mind, unceasingly elevated by these considerations, which, although empirical, are so remarkably powerful, and continually adding to their force, will not suffer itself to be depressed by the doubts suggested by subtle speculation; it tears itself out of this state of uncertainty, the moment it casts a look upon the wondrous forms of nature and the majesty of the universe, and rises from height to height, from condition to condition, till it has elevated itself to the supreme and unconditioned author of all.\n\nBut although we have nothing to object to the reasonableness and utility of this procedure, but have rather to commend and encourage it, we cannot approve of the claims which this argument advances to demonstrative certainty and to a reception upon its own merits, apart from favour or support by other arguments. Nor can it injure the cause of morality to endeavour to lower the tone of the arrogant sophist, and to teach him that modesty and moderation which are the properties of a belief that brings calm and content into the mind, without prescribing to it an unworthy subjection. I maintain, then, that the physico-theological argument is insufficient of itself to prove the existence of a Supreme Being, that it must entrust this to the ontological argument—to which it serves merely as an introduction, and that, consequently, this argument contains the only possible ground of proof (possessed by speculative reason) for the existence of this being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","text":"The chief momenta in the physico-theological argument are as follow: 1. We observe in the world manifest signs of an arrangement full of purpose, executed with great wisdom, and argument in whole of a content indescribably various, and of an extent without limits. 2. This arrangement of means and ends is entirely foreign to the things existing in the world—it belongs to them merely as a contingent attribute; in other words, the nature of different things could not of itself, whatever means were employed, harmoniously tend towards certain purposes, were they not chosen and directed for these purposes by a rational and disposing principle, in accordance with certain fundamental ideas. 3. There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which is not merely a blind, all-powerful nature, producing the beings and events which fill the world in unconscious fecundity, but a free and intelligent cause of the world. 4. The unity of this cause may be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation existing between the parts of the world, as portions of an artistic edifice—an inference which all our observation favours, and all principles of analogy support.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","text":"In the above argument, it is inferred from the analogy of certain products of nature with those of human art, when it compels Nature to bend herself to its purposes, as in the case of a house, a ship, or a watch, that the same kind of causality—namely, understanding and will—resides in nature. It is also declared that the internal possibility of this freely-acting nature (which is the source of all art, and perhaps also of human reason) is derivable from another and superhuman art—a conclusion which would perhaps be found incapable of standing the test of subtle transcendental criticism. But to neither of these opinions shall we at present object. We shall only remark that it must be confessed that, if we are to discuss the subject of cause at all, we cannot proceed more securely than with the guidance of the analogy subsisting between nature and such products of design—these being the only products whose causes and modes of organization are completely known to us. Reason would be unable to satisfy her own requirements, if she passed from a causality which she does know, to obscure and indemonstrable principles of explanation which she does not know.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","text":"According to the physico-theological argument, the connection and harmony existing in the world evidence the contingency of the form merely, but not of the matter, that is, of the substance of the world. To establish the truth of the latter opinion, it would be necessary to prove that all things would be in themselves incapable of this harmony and order, unless they were, even as regards their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But this would require very different grounds of proof from those presented by the analogy with human art. This proof can at most, therefore, demonstrate the existence of an architect of the world, whose efforts are limited by the capabilities of the material with which he works, but not of a creator of the world, to whom all things are subject. Thus this argument is utterly insufficient for the task before us—a demonstration of the existence of an all-sufficient being. If we wish to prove the contingency of matter, we must have recourse to a transcendental argument, which the physico-theological was constructed expressly to avoid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","text":"We infer, from the order and design visible in the universe, as a disposition of a thoroughly contingent character, the existence of a cause proportionate thereto. The conception of this cause must contain certain determinate qualities, and it must therefore be regarded as the conception of a being which possesses all power, wisdom, and so on, in one word, all perfection—the conception, that is, of an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, astonishing, or immeasurable power and excellence, give us no determinate conception of the thing, nor do they inform us what the thing may be in itself. They merely indicate the relation existing between the magnitude of the object and the observer, who compares it with himself and with his own power of comprehension, and are mere expressions of praise and reverence, by which the object is either magnified, or the observing subject depreciated in relation to the object. Where we have to do with the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing, we can discover no determinate conception, except that which comprehends all possible perfection or completeness, and it is only the total (omnitudo) of reality which is completely determined in and through its conception alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","text":"Now it cannot be expected that any one will be bold enough to declare that he has a perfect insight into the relation which the magnitude of the world he contemplates bears (in its extent as well as in its content) to omnipotence, into that of the order and design in the world to the highest wisdom, and that of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of a Supreme Being. Physico-theology is therefore incapable of presenting a determinate conception of a supreme cause of the world, and is therefore insufficient as a principle of theology—a theology which is itself to be the basis of religion.\n\nThe attainment of absolute totality is completely impossible on the path of empiricism. And yet this is the path pursued in the physico-theological argument. What means shall we employ to bridge the abyss?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","text":"After elevating ourselves to admiration of the magnitude of the power, wisdom, and other attributes of the author of the world, and finding we can advance no further, we leave the argument on empirical grounds, and proceed to infer the contingency of the world from the order and conformity to aims that are observable in it. From this contingency we infer, by the help of transcendental conceptions alone, the existence of something absolutely necessary; and, still advancing, proceed from the conception of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the completely determined or determining conception thereof—the conception of an all-embracing reality. Thus the physico-theological, failing in its undertaking, recurs in its embarrassment to the cosmological argument; and, as this is merely the ontological argument in disguise, it executes its design solely by the aid of pure reason, although it at first professed to have no connection with this faculty and to base its entire procedure upon experience alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","text":"The physico-theologians have therefore no reason to regard with such contempt the transcendental mode of argument, and to look down upon it, with the conceit of clear-sighted observers of nature, as the brain-cobweb of obscure speculatists. For, if they reflect upon and examine their own arguments, they will find that, after following for some time the path of nature and experience, and discovering themselves no nearer their object, they suddenly leave this path and pass into the region of pure possibility, where they hope to reach upon the wings of ideas what had eluded all their empirical investigations. Gaining, as they think, a firm footing after this immense leap, they extend their determinate conception—into the possession of which they have come, they know not how—over the whole sphere of creation, and explain their ideal, which is entirely a product of pure reason, by illustrations drawn from experience—though in a degree miserably unworthy of the grandeur of the object, while they refuse to acknowledge that they have arrived at this cognition or hypothesis by a very different road from that of experience.\n\nThus the physico-theological is based upon the cosmological, and this upon the ontological proof of the existence of a Supreme Being; and as besides these three there is no other path open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, on the ground of pure conceptions of reason, is the only possible one, if any proof of a proposition so far transcending the empirical exercise of the understanding is possible at all.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b3bedea08f16a1bcb5b9d61d85cb7f2da84637d459530c6aaeba01f7f97e7b90","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","order":81,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SVCA","text":"### Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason\n\nIf by the term theology I understand the cognition of a primal being, that cognition is based either upon reason alone (theologia rationalis) or upon revelation (theologia revelata). The former cogitates its object either by means of pure transcendental conceptions, as an ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium, and is termed transcendental theology; or, by means of a conception derived from the nature of our own mind, as a supreme intelligence, and must then be entitled natural theology. The person who believes in a transcendental theology alone, is termed a deist; he who acknowledges the possibility of a natural theology also, a theist. The former admits that we can cognize by pure reason alone the existence of a Supreme Being, but at the same time maintains that our conception of this being is purely transcendental, and that all we can say of it is that it possesses all reality, without being able to define it more closely. The second asserts that reason is capable of presenting us, from the analogy with nature, with a more definite conception of this being, and that its operations, as the cause of all things, are the results of intelligence and free will. The former regards the Supreme Being as the cause of the world—whether by the necessity of his nature, or as a free agent, is left undetermined; the latter considers this being as the author of the world.\n\nTranscendental theology aims either at inferring the existence of a Supreme Being from a general experience, without any closer reference to the world to which this experience belongs, and in this case it is called cosmotheology; or it endeavours to cognize the existence of such a being, through mere conceptions, without the aid of experience, and is then termed ontotheology.\n\nNatural theology infers the attributes and the existence of an author of the world, from the constitution of, the order and unity observable in, the world, in which two modes of causality must be admitted to exist—those of nature and freedom. Thus it rises from this world to a supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is termed physico-theology, in the latter, ethical or moral-theology.[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-69)\n\n[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-69) Not theological ethics; for this science contains ethical laws, which presuppose the existence of a Supreme Governor of the world; while moral-theology, on the contrary, is the expression of a conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being, founded upon ethical laws.\n\nAs we are wont to understand by the term God not merely an eternal nature, the operations of which are insensate and blind, but a Supreme Being, who is the free and intelligent author of all things, and as it is this latter view alone that can be of interest to humanity, we might, in strict rigour, deny to the deist any belief in God at all, and regard him merely as a maintainer of the existence of a primal being or thing—the supreme cause of all other things. But, as no one ought to be blamed, merely because he does not feel himself justified in maintaining a certain opinion, as if he altogether denied its truth and asserted the opposite, it is more correct—as it is less harsh—to say, the deist believes in a God, the theist in a living God (summa intelligentia). We shall now proceed to investigate the sources of all these attempts of reason to establish the existence of a Supreme Being.\n\nIt may be sufficient in this place to define theoretical knowledge or cognition as knowledge of that which is, and practical knowledge as knowledge of that which ought to be. In this view, the theoretical employment of reason is that by which I cognize à priori (as necessary) that something is, while the practical is that by which I cognize à priori what ought to happen. Now, if it is an indubitably certain, though at the same time an entirely conditioned truth, that something is, or ought to happen, either a certain determinate condition of this truth is absolutely necessary, or such a condition may be arbitrarily presupposed. In the former case the condition is postulated (per thesin), in the latter supposed (per hypothesin). There are certain practical laws—those of morality—which are absolutely necessary. Now, if these laws necessarily presuppose the existence of some being, as the condition of the possibility of their obligatory power, this being must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which we reason to this determinate condition, is itself cognized à priori as absolutely necessary. We shall at some future time show that the moral laws not merely presuppose the existence of a Supreme Being, but also, as themselves absolutely necessary in a different relation, demand or postulate it—although only from a practical point of view. The discussion of this argument we postpone for the present.\n\nWhen the question relates merely to that which is, not to that which ought to be, the conditioned which is presented in experience is always cogitated as contingent. For this reason its condition cannot be regarded as absolutely necessary, but merely as relatively necessary, or rather as needful; the condition is in itself and à priori a mere arbitrary presupposition in aid of the cognition, by reason, of the conditioned. If, then, we are to possess a theoretical cognition of the absolute necessity of a thing, we cannot attain to this cognition otherwise than à priori by means of conceptions; while it is impossible in this way to cognize the existence of a cause which bears any relation to an existence given in experience.\n\nTheoretical cognition is speculative when it relates to an object or certain conceptions of an object which is not given and cannot be discovered by means of experience. It is opposed to the cognition of nature, which concerns only those objects or predicates which can be presented in a possible experience.\n\nThe principle that everything which happens (the empirically contingent) must have a cause, is a principle of the cognition of nature, but not of speculative cognition. For, if we change it into an abstract principle, and deprive it of its reference to experience and the empirical, we shall find that it cannot with justice be regarded any longer as a synthetical proposition, and that it is impossible to discover any mode of transition from that which exists to something entirely different—termed cause. Nay, more, the conception of a cause likewise that of the contingent—loses, in this speculative mode of employing it, all significance, for its objective reality and meaning are comprehensible from experience alone.\n\nWhen from the existence of the universe and the things in it the existence of a cause of the universe is inferred, reason is proceeding not in the natural, but in the speculative method. For the principle of the former enounces, not that things themselves or substances, but only that which happens or their states—as empirically contingent, have a cause: the assertion that the existence of substance itself is contingent is not justified by experience, it is the assertion of a reason employing its principles in a speculative manner. If, again, I infer from the form of the universe, from the way in which all things are connected and act and react upon each other, the existence of a cause entirely distinct from the universe—this would again be a judgement of purely speculative reason; because the object in this case—the cause—can never be an object of possible experience. In both these cases the principle of causality, which is valid only in the field of experience—useless and even meaningless beyond this region, would be diverted from its proper destination.\n\nNow I maintain that all attempts of reason to establish a theology by the aid of speculation alone are fruitless, that the principles of reason as applied to nature do not conduct us to any theological truths, and, consequently, that a rational theology can have no existence, unless it is founded upon the laws of morality. For all synthetical principles of the understanding are valid only as immanent in experience; while the cognition of a Supreme Being necessitates their being employed transcendentally, and of this the understanding is quite incapable. If the empirical law of causality is to conduct us to a Supreme Being, this being must belong to the chain of empirical objects—in which case it would be, like all phenomena, itself conditioned. If the possibility of passing the limits of experience be admitted, by means of the dynamical law of the relation of an effect to its cause, what kind of conception shall we obtain by this procedure? Certainly not the conception of a Supreme Being, because experience never presents us with the greatest of all possible effects, and it is only an effect of this character that could witness to the existence of a corresponding cause. If, for the purpose of fully satisfying the requirements of Reason, we recognize her right to assert the existence of a perfect and absolutely necessary being, this can be admitted only from favour, and cannot be regarded as the result of irresistible demonstration. The physico-theological proof may add weight to others—if other proofs there are—by connecting speculation with experience; but in itself it rather prepares the mind for theological cognition, and gives it a right and natural direction, than establishes a sure foundation for theology.\n\nIt is now perfectly evident that transcendental questions admit only of transcendental answers—those presented à priori by pure conceptions without the least empirical admixture. But the question in the present case is evidently synthetical—it aims at the extension of our cognition beyond the bounds of experience—it requires an assurance respecting the existence of a being corresponding with the idea in our minds, to which no experience can ever be adequate. Now it has been abundantly proved that all à priori synthetical cognition is possible only as the expression of the formal conditions of a possible experience; and that the validity of all principles depends upon their immanence in the field of experience, that is, their relation to objects of empirical cognition or phenomena. Thus all transcendental procedure in reference to speculative theology is without result.\n\nIf any one prefers doubting the conclusiveness of the proofs of our analytic to losing the persuasion of the validity of these old and time honoured arguments, he at least cannot decline answering the question—how he can pass the limits of all possible experience by the help of mere ideas. If he talks of new arguments, or of improvements upon old arguments, I request him to spare me. There is certainly no great choice in this sphere of discussion, as all speculative arguments must at last look for support to the ontological, and I have, therefore, very little to fear from the argumentative fecundity of the dogmatical defenders of a non-sensuous reason. Without looking upon myself as a remarkably combative person, I shall not decline the challenge to detect the fallacy and destroy the pretensions of every attempt of speculative theology. And yet the hope of better fortune never deserts those who are accustomed to the dogmatical mode of procedure. I shall, therefore, restrict myself to the simple and equitable demand that such reasoners will demonstrate, from the nature of the human mind as well as from that of the other sources of knowledge, how we are to proceed to extend our cognition completely à priori, and to carry it to that point where experience abandons us, and no means exist of guaranteeing the objective reality of our conceptions. In whatever way the understanding may have attained to a conception, the existence of the object of the conception cannot be discovered in it by analysis, because the cognition of the existence of the object depends upon the object’s being posited and given in itself apart from the conception. But it is utterly impossible to go beyond our conception, without the aid of experience—which presents to the mind nothing but phenomena, or to attain by the help of mere conceptions to a conviction of the existence of new kinds of objects or supernatural beings.\n\nBut although pure speculative reason is far from sufficient to demonstrate the existence of a Supreme Being, it is of the highest utility in correcting our conception of this being—on the supposition that we can attain to the cognition of it by some other means—in making it consistent with itself and with all other conceptions of intelligible objects, clearing it from all that is incompatible with the conception of an ens summun, and eliminating from it all limitations or admixtures of empirical elements.\n\nTranscendental theology is still therefore, notwithstanding its objective insufficiency, of importance in a negative respect; it is useful as a test of the procedure of reason when engaged with pure ideas, no other than a transcendental standard being in this case admissible. For if, from a practical point of view, the hypothesis of a Supreme and All-sufficient Being is to maintain its validity without opposition, it must be of the highest importance to define this conception in a correct and rigorous manner—as the transcendental conception of a necessary being, to eliminate all phenomenal elements (anthropomorphism in its most extended signification), and at the same time to overflow all contradictory assertions—be they atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is of course very easy; as the same arguments which demonstrated the inability of human reason to affirm the existence of a Supreme Being must be alike sufficient to prove the invalidity of its denial. For it is impossible to gain from the pure speculation of reason demonstration that there exists no Supreme Being, as the ground of all that exists, or that this being possesses none of those properties which we regard as analogical with the dynamical qualities of a thinking being, or that, as the anthropomorphists would have us believe, it is subject to all the limitations which sensibility imposes upon those intelligences which exist in the world of experience.\n\nA Supreme Being is, therefore, for the speculative reason, a mere ideal, though a faultless one—a conception which perfects and crowns the system of human cognition, but the objective reality of which can neither be proved nor disproved by pure reason. If this defect is ever supplied by a moral theology, the problematic transcendental theology which has preceded, will have been at least serviceable as demonstrating the mental necessity existing for the conception, by the complete determination of it which it has furnished, and the ceaseless testing of the conclusions of a reason often deceived by sense, and not always in harmony with its own ideas. The attributes of necessity, infinitude, unity, existence apart from the world (and not as a world soul), eternity (free from conditions of time), omnipresence (free from conditions of space), omnipotence, and others, are pure transcendental predicates; and thus the accurate conception of a Supreme Being, which every theology requires, is furnished by transcendental theology alone.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","text":"### Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason\n\nIf by the term theology I understand the cognition of a primal being, that cognition is based either upon reason alone (theologia rationalis) or upon revelation (theologia revelata). The former cogitates its object either by means of pure transcendental conceptions, as an ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium, and is termed transcendental theology; or, by means of a conception derived from the nature of our own mind, as a supreme intelligence, and must then be entitled natural theology. The person who believes in a transcendental theology alone, is termed a deist; he who acknowledges the possibility of a natural theology also, a theist. The former admits that we can cognize by pure reason alone the existence of a Supreme Being, but at the same time maintains that our conception of this being is purely transcendental, and that all we can say of it is that it possesses all reality, without being able to define it more closely. The second asserts that reason is capable of presenting us, from the analogy with nature, with a more definite conception of this being, and that its operations, as the cause of all things, are the results of intelligence and free will. The former regards the Supreme Being as the cause of the world—whether by the necessity of his nature, or as a free agent, is left undetermined; the latter considers this being as the author of the world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","text":"Transcendental theology aims either at inferring the existence of a Supreme Being from a general experience, without any closer reference to the world to which this experience belongs, and in this case it is called cosmotheology; or it endeavours to cognize the existence of such a being, through mere conceptions, without the aid of experience, and is then termed ontotheology.\n\nNatural theology infers the attributes and the existence of an author of the world, from the constitution of, the order and unity observable in, the world, in which two modes of causality must be admitted to exist—those of nature and freedom. Thus it rises from this world to a supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is termed physico-theology, in the latter, ethical or moral-theology.[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-69)\n\n[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-69) Not theological ethics; for this science contains ethical laws, which presuppose the existence of a Supreme Governor of the world; while moral-theology, on the contrary, is the expression of a conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being, founded upon ethical laws.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0003","text":"As we are wont to understand by the term God not merely an eternal nature, the operations of which are insensate and blind, but a Supreme Being, who is the free and intelligent author of all things, and as it is this latter view alone that can be of interest to humanity, we might, in strict rigour, deny to the deist any belief in God at all, and regard him merely as a maintainer of the existence of a primal being or thing—the supreme cause of all other things. But, as no one ought to be blamed, merely because he does not feel himself justified in maintaining a certain opinion, as if he altogether denied its truth and asserted the opposite, it is more correct—as it is less harsh—to say, the deist believes in a God, the theist in a living God (summa intelligentia). We shall now proceed to investigate the sources of all these attempts of reason to establish the existence of a Supreme Being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","text":"It may be sufficient in this place to define theoretical knowledge or cognition as knowledge of that which is, and practical knowledge as knowledge of that which ought to be. In this view, the theoretical employment of reason is that by which I cognize à priori (as necessary) that something is, while the practical is that by which I cognize à priori what ought to happen. Now, if it is an indubitably certain, though at the same time an entirely conditioned truth, that something is, or ought to happen, either a certain determinate condition of this truth is absolutely necessary, or such a condition may be arbitrarily presupposed. In the former case the condition is postulated (per thesin), in the latter supposed (per hypothesin). There are certain practical laws—those of morality—which are absolutely necessary. Now, if these laws necessarily presuppose the existence of some being, as the condition of the possibility of their obligatory power, this being must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which we reason to this determinate condition, is itself cognized à priori as absolutely necessary. We shall at some future time show that the moral laws not merely presuppose the existence of a Supreme Being, but also, as themselves absolutely necessary in a different relation, demand or postulate it—although only from a practical point of view. The discussion of this argument we postpone for the present.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","text":"When the question relates merely to that which is, not to that which ought to be, the conditioned which is presented in experience is always cogitated as contingent. For this reason its condition cannot be regarded as absolutely necessary, but merely as relatively necessary, or rather as needful; the condition is in itself and à priori a mere arbitrary presupposition in aid of the cognition, by reason, of the conditioned. If, then, we are to possess a theoretical cognition of the absolute necessity of a thing, we cannot attain to this cognition otherwise than à priori by means of conceptions; while it is impossible in this way to cognize the existence of a cause which bears any relation to an existence given in experience.\n\nTheoretical cognition is speculative when it relates to an object or certain conceptions of an object which is not given and cannot be discovered by means of experience. It is opposed to the cognition of nature, which concerns only those objects or predicates which can be presented in a possible experience.\n\nThe principle that everything which happens (the empirically contingent) must have a cause, is a principle of the cognition of nature, but not of speculative cognition. For, if we change it into an abstract principle, and deprive it of its reference to experience and the empirical, we shall find that it cannot with justice be regarded any longer as a synthetical proposition, and that it is impossible to discover any mode of transition from that which exists to something entirely different—termed cause. Nay, more, the conception of a cause likewise that of the contingent—loses, in this speculative mode of employing it, all significance, for its objective reality and meaning are comprehensible from experience alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","text":"When from the existence of the universe and the things in it the existence of a cause of the universe is inferred, reason is proceeding not in the natural, but in the speculative method. For the principle of the former enounces, not that things themselves or substances, but only that which happens or their states—as empirically contingent, have a cause: the assertion that the existence of substance itself is contingent is not justified by experience, it is the assertion of a reason employing its principles in a speculative manner. If, again, I infer from the form of the universe, from the way in which all things are connected and act and react upon each other, the existence of a cause entirely distinct from the universe—this would again be a judgement of purely speculative reason; because the object in this case—the cause—can never be an object of possible experience. In both these cases the principle of causality, which is valid only in the field of experience—useless and even meaningless beyond this region, would be diverted from its proper destination.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","text":"Now I maintain that all attempts of reason to establish a theology by the aid of speculation alone are fruitless, that the principles of reason as applied to nature do not conduct us to any theological truths, and, consequently, that a rational theology can have no existence, unless it is founded upon the laws of morality. For all synthetical principles of the understanding are valid only as immanent in experience; while the cognition of a Supreme Being necessitates their being employed transcendentally, and of this the understanding is quite incapable. If the empirical law of causality is to conduct us to a Supreme Being, this being must belong to the chain of empirical objects—in which case it would be, like all phenomena, itself conditioned. If the possibility of passing the limits of experience be admitted, by means of the dynamical law of the relation of an effect to its cause, what kind of conception shall we obtain by this procedure? Certainly not the conception of a Supreme Being, because experience never presents us with the greatest of all possible effects, and it is only an effect of this character that could witness to the existence of a corresponding cause. If, for the purpose of fully satisfying the requirements of Reason, we recognize her right to assert the existence of a perfect and absolutely necessary being, this can be admitted only from favour, and cannot be regarded as the result of irresistible demonstration. The physico-theological proof may add weight to others—if other proofs there are—by connecting speculation with experience; but in itself it rather prepares the mind for theological cognition, and gives it a right and natural direction, than establishes a sure foundation for theology.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","text":"It is now perfectly evident that transcendental questions admit only of transcendental answers—those presented à priori by pure conceptions without the least empirical admixture. But the question in the present case is evidently synthetical—it aims at the extension of our cognition beyond the bounds of experience—it requires an assurance respecting the existence of a being corresponding with the idea in our minds, to which no experience can ever be adequate. Now it has been abundantly proved that all à priori synthetical cognition is possible only as the expression of the formal conditions of a possible experience; and that the validity of all principles depends upon their immanence in the field of experience, that is, their relation to objects of empirical cognition or phenomena. Thus all transcendental procedure in reference to speculative theology is without result.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","text":"If any one prefers doubting the conclusiveness of the proofs of our analytic to losing the persuasion of the validity of these old and time honoured arguments, he at least cannot decline answering the question—how he can pass the limits of all possible experience by the help of mere ideas. If he talks of new arguments, or of improvements upon old arguments, I request him to spare me. There is certainly no great choice in this sphere of discussion, as all speculative arguments must at last look for support to the ontological, and I have, therefore, very little to fear from the argumentative fecundity of the dogmatical defenders of a non-sensuous reason. Without looking upon myself as a remarkably combative person, I shall not decline the challenge to detect the fallacy and destroy the pretensions of every attempt of speculative theology. And yet the hope of better fortune never deserts those who are accustomed to the dogmatical mode of procedure. I shall, therefore, restrict myself to the simple and equitable demand that such reasoners will demonstrate, from the nature of the human mind as well as from that of the other sources of knowledge, how we are to proceed to extend our cognition completely à priori, and to carry it to that point where experience abandons us, and no means exist of guaranteeing the objective reality of our conceptions. In whatever way the understanding may have attained to a conception, the existence of the object of the conception cannot be discovered in it by analysis, because the cognition of the existence of the object depends upon the object’s being posited and given in itself apart from the conception. But it is utterly impossible to go beyond our conception, without the aid of experience—which presents to the mind nothing but phenomena, or to attain by the help of mere conceptions to a conviction of the existence of new kinds of objects or supernatural beings.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","text":"But although pure speculative reason is far from sufficient to demonstrate the existence of a Supreme Being, it is of the highest utility in correcting our conception of this being—on the supposition that we can attain to the cognition of it by some other means—in making it consistent with itself and with all other conceptions of intelligible objects, clearing it from all that is incompatible with the conception of an ens summun, and eliminating from it all limitations or admixtures of empirical elements.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","text":"Transcendental theology is still therefore, notwithstanding its objective insufficiency, of importance in a negative respect; it is useful as a test of the procedure of reason when engaged with pure ideas, no other than a transcendental standard being in this case admissible. For if, from a practical point of view, the hypothesis of a Supreme and All-sufficient Being is to maintain its validity without opposition, it must be of the highest importance to define this conception in a correct and rigorous manner—as the transcendental conception of a necessary being, to eliminate all phenomenal elements (anthropomorphism in its most extended signification), and at the same time to overflow all contradictory assertions—be they atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is of course very easy; as the same arguments which demonstrated the inability of human reason to affirm the existence of a Supreme Being must be alike sufficient to prove the invalidity of its denial. For it is impossible to gain from the pure speculation of reason demonstration that there exists no Supreme Being, as the ground of all that exists, or that this being possesses none of those properties which we regard as analogical with the dynamical qualities of a thinking being, or that, as the anthropomorphists would have us believe, it is subject to all the limitations which sensibility imposes upon those intelligences which exist in the world of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","text":"A Supreme Being is, therefore, for the speculative reason, a mere ideal, though a faultless one—a conception which perfects and crowns the system of human cognition, but the objective reality of which can neither be proved nor disproved by pure reason. If this defect is ever supplied by a moral theology, the problematic transcendental theology which has preceded, will have been at least serviceable as demonstrating the mental necessity existing for the conception, by the complete determination of it which it has furnished, and the ceaseless testing of the conclusions of a reason often deceived by sense, and not always in harmony with its own ideas. The attributes of necessity, infinitude, unity, existence apart from the world (and not as a world soul), eternity (free from conditions of time), omnipresence (free from conditions of space), omnipotence, and others, are pure transcendental predicates; and thus the accurate conception of a Supreme Being, which every theology requires, is furnished by transcendental theology alone.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0b22539e0e4947eb74e5c0827620f29a6c38814650179bc168c0b1842999914f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2","title":"Appendix","order":82,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_APP2","text":"# Appendix\n\n### APPENDIX. Of the Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason\n\nThe result of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms the truth of what we have already proved in our Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would lead us beyond the limits of experience are fallacious and groundless, but it at the same time teaches us this important lesson, that human reason has a natural inclination to overstep these limits, and that transcendental ideas are as much the natural property of the reason as categories are of the understanding. There exists this difference, however, that while the categories never mislead us, outward objects being always in perfect harmony therewith, ideas are the parents of irresistible illusions, the severest and most subtle criticism being required to save us from the fallacies which they induce.\n\nWhatever is grounded in the nature of our powers will be found to be in harmony with the final purpose and proper employment of these powers, when once we have discovered their true direction and aim. We are entitled to suppose, therefore, that there exists a mode of employing transcendental ideas which is proper and immanent; although, when we mistake their meaning, and regard them as conceptions of actual things, their mode of application is transcendent and delusive. For it is not the idea itself, but only the employment of the idea in relation to possible experience, that is transcendent or immanent. An idea is employed transcendently, when it is applied to an object falsely believed to be adequate with and to correspond to it; immanently, when it is applied solely to the employment of the understanding in the sphere of experience. Thus all errors of subreptio—of misapplication, are to be ascribed to defects of judgement, and not to understanding or reason.\n\nReason never has an immediate relation to an object; it relates immediately to the understanding alone. It is only through the understanding that it can be employed in the field of experience. It does not form conceptions of objects, it merely arranges them and gives to them that unity which they are capable of possessing when the sphere of their application has been extended as widely as possible. Reason avails itself of the conception of the understanding for the sole purpose of producing totality in the different series. This totality the understanding does not concern itself with; its only occupation is the connection of experiences, by which series of conditions in accordance with conceptions are established. The object of reason is, therefore, the understanding and its proper destination. As the latter brings unity into the diversity of objects by means of its conceptions, so the former brings unity into the diversity of conceptions by means of ideas; as it sets the final aim of a collective unity to the operations of the understanding, which without this occupies itself with a distributive unity alone.\n\nI accordingly maintain that transcendental ideas can never be employed as constitutive ideas, that they cannot be conceptions of objects, and that, when thus considered, they assume a fallacious and dialectical character. But, on the other hand, they are capable of an admirable and indispensably necessary application to objects—as regulative ideas, directing the understanding to a certain aim, the guiding lines towards which all its laws follow, and in which they all meet in one point. This point—though a mere idea (focus imaginarius), that is, not a point from which the conceptions of the understanding do really proceed, for it lies beyond the sphere of possible experience—serves, notwithstanding, to give to these conceptions the greatest possible unity combined with the greatest possible extension. Hence arises the natural illusion which induces us to believe that these lines proceed from an object which lies out of the sphere of empirical cognition, just as objects reflected in a mirror appear to be behind it. But this illusion—which we may hinder from imposing upon us—is necessary and unavoidable, if we desire to see, not only those objects which lie before us, but those which are at a great distance behind us; that is to say, when, in the present case, we direct the aims of the understanding, beyond every given experience, towards an extension as great as can possibly be attained.\n\nIf we review our cognitions in their entire extent, we shall find that the peculiar business of reason is to arrange them into a system, that is to say, to give them connection according to a principle. This unity presupposes an idea—the idea of the form of a whole (of cognition), preceding the determinate cognition of the parts, and containing the conditions which determine à priori to every part its place and relation to the other parts of the whole system. This idea, accordingly, demands complete unity in the cognition of the understanding—not the unity of a contingent aggregate, but that of a system connected according to necessary laws. It cannot be affirmed with propriety that this idea is a conception of an object; it is merely a conception of the complete unity of the conceptions of objects, in so far as this unity is available to the understanding as a rule. Such conceptions of reason are not derived from nature; on the contrary, we employ them for the interrogation and investigation of nature, and regard our cognition as defective so long as it is not adequate to them. We admit that such a thing as pure earth, pure water, or pure air, is not to be discovered. And yet we require these conceptions (which have their origin in the reason, so far as regards their absolute purity and completeness) for the purpose of determining the share which each of these natural causes has in every phenomenon. Thus the different kinds of matter are all referred to earths, as mere weight; to salts and inflammable bodies, as pure force; and finally, to water and air, as the vehicula of the former, or the machines employed by them in their operations—for the purpose of explaining the chemical action and reaction of bodies in accordance with the idea of a mechanism. For, although not actually so expressed, the influence of such ideas of reason is very observable in the procedure of natural philosophers.\n\nIf reason is the faculty of deducing the particular from the general, and if the general be certain in se and given, it is only necessary that the judgement should subsume the particular under the general, the particular being thus necessarily determined. I shall term this the demonstrative or apodeictic employment of reason. If, however, the general is admitted as problematical only, and is a mere idea, the particular case is certain, but the universality of the rule which applies to this particular case remains a problem. Several particular cases, the certainty of which is beyond doubt, are then taken and examined, for the purpose of discovering whether the rule is applicable to them; and if it appears that all the particular cases which can be collected follow from the rule, its universality is inferred, and at the same time, all the causes which have not, or cannot be presented to our observation, are concluded to be of the same character with those which we have observed. This I shall term the hypothetical employment of the reason.\n\nThe hypothetical exercise of reason by the aid of ideas employed as problematical conceptions is properly not constitutive. That is to say, if we consider the subject strictly, the truth of the rule, which has been employed as an hypothesis, does not follow from the use that is made of it by reason. For how can we know all the possible cases that may arise? some of which may, however, prove exceptions to the universality of the rule. This employment of reason is merely regulative, and its sole aim is the introduction of unity into the aggregate of our particular cognitions, and thereby the approximating of the rule to universality.\n\nThe object of the hypothetical employment of reason is therefore the systematic unity of cognitions; and this unity is the criterion of the truth of a rule. On the other hand, this systematic unity—as a mere idea—is in fact merely a unity projected, not to be regarded as given, but only in the light of a problem—a problem which serves, however, as a principle for the various and particular exercise of the understanding in experience, directs it with regard to those cases which are not presented to our observation, and introduces harmony and consistency into all its operations.\n\nAll that we can be certain of from the above considerations is that this systematic unity is a logical principle, whose aim is to assist the understanding, where it cannot of itself attain to rules, by means of ideas, to bring all these various rules under one principle, and thus to ensure the most complete consistency and connection that can be attained. But the assertion that objects and the understanding by which they are cognized are so constituted as to be determined to systematic unity, that this may be postulated à priori, without any reference to the interest of reason, and that we are justified in declaring all possible cognitions—empirical and others—to possess systematic unity, and to be subject to general principles from which, notwithstanding their various character, they are all derivable,—such an assertion can be founded only upon a transcendental principle of reason, which would render this systematic unity not subjectively and logically—in its character of a method, but objectively necessary.\n\nWe shall illustrate this by an example. The conceptions of the understanding make us acquainted, among many other kinds of unity, with that of the causality of a substance, which is termed power. The different phenomenal manifestations of the same substance appear at first view to be so very dissimilar that we are inclined to assume the existence of just as many different powers as there are different effects—as, in the case of the human mind, we have feeling, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, analysis, pleasure, desire and so on. Now we are required by a logical maxim to reduce these differences to as small a number as possible, by comparing them and discovering the hidden identity which exists. We must inquire, for example, whether or not imagination (connected with consciousness), memory, wit, and analysis are not merely different forms of understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental power, the existence of which no effort of logic can assure us of, is the problem to be solved, for the systematic representation of the existing variety of powers. The logical principle of reason requires us to produce as great a unity as is possible in the system of our cognitions; and the more the phenomena of this and the other power are found to be identical, the more probable does it become, that they are nothing but different manifestations of one and the same power, which may be called, relatively speaking, a fundamental power. And so with other cases.\n\nThese relatively fundamental powers must again be compared with each other, to discover, if possible, the one radical and absolutely fundamental power of which they are but the manifestations. But this unity is purely hypothetical. It is not maintained, that this unity does really exist, but that we must, in the interest of reason, that is, for the establishment of principles for the various rules presented by experience, try to discover and introduce it, so far as is practicable, into the sphere of our cognitions.\n\nBut the transcendental employment of the understanding would lead us to believe that this idea of a fundamental power is not problematical, but that it possesses objective reality, and thus the systematic unity of the various powers or forces in a substance is demanded by the understanding and erected into an apodeictic or necessary principle. For, without having attempted to discover the unity of the various powers existing in nature, nay, even after all our attempts have failed, we notwithstanding presuppose that it does exist, and may be, sooner or later, discovered. And this reason does, not only, as in the case above adduced, with regard to the unity of substance, but where many substances, although all to a certain extent homogeneous, are discoverable, as in the case of matter in general. Here also does reason presuppose the existence of the systematic unity of various powers—inasmuch as particular laws of nature are subordinate to general laws; and parsimony in principles is not merely an economical principle of reason, but an essential law of nature.\n\nWe cannot understand, in fact, how a logical principle of unity can of right exist, unless we presuppose a transcendental principle, by which such a systematic unit—as a property of objects themselves—is regarded as necessary à priori. For with what right can reason, in its logical exercise, require us to regard the variety of forces which nature displays, as in effect a disguised unity, and to deduce them from one fundamental force or power, when she is free to admit that it is just as possible that all forces should be different in kind, and that a systematic unity is not conformable to the design of nature? In this view of the case, reason would be proceeding in direct opposition to her own destination, by setting as an aim an idea which entirely conflicts with the procedure and arrangement of nature. Neither can we assert that reason has previously inferred this unity from the contingent nature of phenomena. For the law of reason which requires us to seek for this unity is a necessary law, inasmuch as without it we should not possess a faculty of reason, nor without reason a consistent and self-accordant mode of employing the understanding, nor, in the absence of this, any proper and sufficient criterion of empirical truth. In relation to this criterion, therefore, we must suppose the idea of the systematic unity of nature to possess objective validity and necessity.\n\nWe find this transcendental presupposition lurking in different forms in the principles of philosophers, although they have neither recognized it nor confessed to themselves its presence. That the diversities of individual things do not exclude identity of species, that the various species must be considered as merely different determinations of a few genera, and these again as divisions of still higher races, and so on—that, accordingly, a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical conceptions, in so far as they can be deduced from higher and more general conceptions, must be sought for, is a scholastic maxim or logical principle, without which reason could not be employed by us. For we can infer the particular from the general, only in so far as general properties of things constitute the foundation upon which the particular rest.\n\nThat the same unity exists in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the well-known scholastic maxim, which forbids us unnecessarily to augment the number of entities or principles (entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda). This maxim asserts that nature herself assists in the establishment of this unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity of phenomena should not deter us from the expectation of discovering beneath this diversity a unity of fundamental properties, of which the aforesaid variety is but a more or less determined form. This unity, although a mere idea, thinkers have found it necessary rather to moderate the desire than to encourage it. It was considered a great step when chemists were able to reduce all salts to two main genera—acids and alkalis; and they regard this difference as itself a mere variety, or different manifestation of one and the same fundamental material. The different kinds of earths (stones and even metals) chemists have endeavoured to reduce to three, and afterwards to two; but still, not content with this advance, they cannot but think that behind these diversities there lurks but one genus—nay, that even salts and earths have a common principle. It might be conjectured that this is merely an economical plan of reason, for the purpose of sparing itself trouble, and an attempt of a purely hypothetical character, which, when successful, gives an appearance of probability to the principle of explanation employed by the reason. But a selfish purpose of this kind is easily to be distinguished from the idea, according to which every one presupposes that this unity is in accordance with the laws of nature, and that reason does not in this case request, but requires, although we are quite unable to determine the proper limits of this unity.\n\nIf the diversity existing in phenomena—a diversity not of form (for in this they may be similar) but of content—were so great that the subtlest human reason could never by comparison discover in them the least similarity (which is not impossible), in this case the logical law of genera would be without foundation, the conception of a genus, nay, all general conceptions would be impossible, and the faculty of the understanding, the exercise of which is restricted to the world of conceptions, could not exist. The logical principle of genera, accordingly, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I mean objects presented to our senses), presupposes a transcendental principle. In accordance with this principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the variety of phenomena (although we are unable to determine à priori the degree of this homogeneity), because without it no empirical conceptions, and consequently no experience, would be possible.\n\nThe logical principle of genera, which demands identity in phenomena, is balanced by another principle—that of species, which requires variety and diversity in things, notwithstanding their accordance in the same genus, and directs the understanding to attend to the one no less than to the other. This principle (of the faculty of distinction) acts as a check upon the reason and reason exhibits in this respect a double and conflicting interest—on the one hand, the interest in the extent (the interest of generality) in relation to genera; on the other, that of the content (the interest of individuality) in relation to the variety of species. In the former case, the understanding cogitates more under its conceptions, in the latter it cogitates more in them. This distinction manifests itself likewise in the habits of thought peculiar to natural philosophers, some of whom—the remarkably speculative heads—may be said to be hostile to heterogeneity in phenomena, and have their eyes always fixed on the unity of genera, while others—with a strong empirical tendency—aim unceasingly at the analysis of phenomena, and almost destroy in us the hope of ever being able to estimate the character of these according to general principles.\n\nThe latter mode of thought is evidently based upon a logical principle, the aim of which is the systematic completeness of all cognitions. This principle authorizes me, beginning at the genus, to descend to the various and diverse contained under it; and in this way extension, as in the former case unity, is assured to the system. For if we merely examine the sphere of the conception which indicates a genus, we cannot discover how far it is possible to proceed in the division of that sphere; just as it is impossible, from the consideration of the space occupied by matter, to determine how far we can proceed in the division of it. Hence every genus must contain different species, and these again different subspecies; and as each of the latter must itself contain a sphere (must be of a certain extent, as a conceptus communis), reason demands that no species or sub-species is to be considered as the lowest possible. For a species or sub-species, being always a conception, which contains only what is common to a number of different things, does not completely determine any individual thing, or relate immediately to it, and must consequently contain other conceptions, that is, other sub-species under it. This law of specification may be thus expressed: entium varietates non temere sunt minuendae.\n\nBut it is easy to see that this logical law would likewise be without sense or application, were it not based upon a transcendental law of specification, which certainly does not require that the differences existing in phenomena should be infinite in number, for the logical principle, which merely maintains the indeterminateness of the logical sphere of a conception, in relation to its possible division, does not authorize this statement; while it does impose upon the understanding the duty of searching for subspecies to every species, and minor differences in every difference. For, were there no lower conceptions, neither could there be any higher. Now the understanding cognizes only by means of conceptions; consequently, how far soever it may proceed in division, never by mere intuition, but always by lower and lower conceptions. The cognition of phenomena in their complete determination (which is possible only by means of the understanding) requires an unceasingly continued specification of conceptions, and a progression to ever smaller differences, of which abstraction had been made in the conception of the species, and still more in that of the genus.\n\nThis law of specification cannot be deduced from experience; it can never present us with a principle of so universal an application. Empirical specification very soon stops in its distinction of diversities, and requires the guidance of the transcendental law, as a principle of the reason—a law which imposes on us the necessity of never ceasing in our search for differences, even although these may not present themselves to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds could only be discovered by obeying the anticipatory law of reason, which imposes upon the understanding the task of discovering the differences existing between these earths, and supposes that nature is richer in substances than our senses would indicate. The faculty of the understanding belongs to us just as much under the presupposition of differences in the objects of nature, as under the condition that these objects are homogeneous, because we could not possess conceptions, nor make any use of our understanding, were not the phenomena included under these conceptions in some respects dissimilar, as well as similar, in their character.\n\nReason thus prepares the sphere of the understanding for the operations of this faculty: 1. By the principle of the homogeneity of the diverse in higher genera; 2. By the principle of the variety of the homogeneous in lower species; and, to complete the systematic unity, it adds, 3. A law of the affinity of all conceptions which prescribes a continuous transition from one species to every other by the gradual increase of diversity. We may term these the principles of the homogeneity, the specification, and the continuity of forms. The latter results from the union of the two former, inasmuch as we regard the systematic connection as complete in thought, in the ascent to higher genera, as well as in the descent to lower species. For all diversities must be related to each other, as they all spring from one highest genus, descending through the different gradations of a more and more extended determination.\n\nWe may illustrate the systematic unity produced by the three logical principles in the following manner. Every conception may be regarded as a point, which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has a certain horizon, which may be said to enclose a number of things that may be viewed, so to speak, from that centre. Within this horizon there must be an infinite number of other points, each of which has its own horizon, smaller and more circumscribed; in other words, every species contains sub-species, according to the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points (individuals), which possess no extent. But different horizons or genera, which include under them so many conceptions, may have one common horizon, from which, as from a mid-point, they may be surveyed; and we may proceed thus, till we arrive at the highest genus, or universal and true horizon, which is determined by the highest conception, and which contains under itself all differences and varieties, as genera, species, and subspecies.\n\nTo this highest standpoint I am conducted by the law of homogeneity, as to all lower and more variously-determined conceptions by the law of specification. Now as in this way there exists no void in the whole extent of all possible conceptions, and as out of the sphere of these the mind can discover nothing, there arises from the presupposition of the universal horizon above mentioned, and its complete division, the principle: Non datur vacuum formarum. This principle asserts that there are not different primitive and highest genera, which stand isolated, so to speak, from each other, but all the various genera are mere divisions and limitations of one highest and universal genus; and hence follows immediately the principle: Datur continuum formarum. This principle indicates that all differences of species limit each other, and do not admit of transition from one to another by a saltus, but only through smaller degrees of the difference between the one species and the other. In one word, there are no species or sub-species which (in the view of reason) are the nearest possible to each other; intermediate species or sub-species being always possible, the difference of which from each of the former is always smaller than the difference existing between these.\n\nThe first law, therefore, directs us to avoid the notion that there exist different primal genera, and enounces the fact of perfect homogeneity; the second imposes a check upon this tendency to unity and prescribes the distinction of sub-species, before proceeding to apply our general conceptions to individuals. The third unites both the former, by enouncing the fact of homogeneity as existing even in the most various diversity, by means of the gradual transition from one species to another. Thus it indicates a relationship between the different branches or species, in so far as they all spring from the same stem.\n\nBut this logical law of the continuum specierum (formarum logicarum) presupposes a transcendental principle (lex continui in natura), without which the understanding might be led into error, by following the guidance of the former, and thus perhaps pursuing a path contrary to that prescribed by nature. This law must, consequently, be based upon pure transcendental, and not upon empirical, considerations. For, in the latter case, it would come later than the system; whereas it is really itself the parent of all that is systematic in our cognition of nature. These principles are not mere hypotheses employed for the purpose of experimenting upon nature; although when any such connection is discovered, it forms a solid ground for regarding the hypothetical unity as valid in the sphere of nature—and thus they are in this respect not without their use. But we go farther, and maintain that it is manifest that these principles of parsimony in fundamental causes, variety in effects, and affinity in phenomena, are in accordance both with reason and nature, and that they are not mere methods or plans devised for the purpose of assisting us in our observation of the external world.\n\nBut it is plain that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, to which no adequate object can be discovered in experience. And this for two reasons. First, because the species in nature are really divided, and hence form quanta discreta; and, if the gradual progression through their affinity were continuous, the intermediate members lying between two given species must be infinite in number, which is impossible. Secondly, because we cannot make any determinate empirical use of this law, inasmuch as it does not present us with any criterion of affinity which could aid us in determining how far we ought to pursue the graduation of differences: it merely contains a general indication that it is our duty to seek for and, if possible, to discover them.\n\nWhen we arrange these principles of systematic unity in the order conformable to their employment in experience, they will stand thus: Variety, Affinity, Unity, each of them, as ideas, being taken in the highest degree of their completeness. Reason presupposes the existence of cognitions of the understanding, which have a direct relation to experience, and aims at the ideal unity of these cognitions—a unity which far transcends all experience or empirical notions. The affinity of the diverse, notwithstanding the differences existing between its parts, has a relation to things, but a still closer one to the mere properties and powers of things. For example, imperfect experience may represent the orbits of the planets as circular. But we discover variations from this course, and we proceed to suppose that the planets revolve in a path which, if not a circle, is of a character very similar to it. That is to say, the movements of those planets which do not form a circle will approximate more or less to the properties of a circle, and probably form an ellipse. The paths of comets exhibit still greater variations, for, so far as our observation extends, they do not return upon their own course in a circle or ellipse. But we proceed to the conjecture that comets describe a parabola, a figure which is closely allied to the ellipse. In fact, a parabola is merely an ellipse, with its longer axis produced to an indefinite extent. Thus these principles conduct us to a unity in the genera of the forms of these orbits, and, proceeding farther, to a unity as regards the cause of the motions of the heavenly bodies—that is, gravitation. But we go on extending our conquests over nature, and endeavour to explain all seeming deviations from these rules, and even make additions to our system which no experience can ever substantiate—for example, the theory, in affinity with that of ellipses, of hyperbolic paths of comets, pursuing which, these bodies leave our solar system and, passing from sun to sun, unite the most distant parts of the infinite universe, which is held together by the same moving power.\n\nThe most remarkable circumstance connected with these principles is that they seem to be transcendental, and, although only containing ideas for the guidance of the empirical exercise of reason, and although this empirical employment stands to these ideas in an asymptotic relation alone (to use a mathematical term), that is, continually approximate, without ever being able to attain to them, they possess, notwithstanding, as à priori synthetical propositions, objective though undetermined validity, and are available as rules for possible experience. In the elaboration of our experience, they may also be employed with great advantage, as heuristic[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-70) principles. A transcendental deduction of them cannot be made; such a deduction being always impossible in the case of ideas, as has been already shown.\n\n[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-70) From the Greek, eurhioko.\n\nWe distinguished, in the Transcendental Analytic, the dynamical principles of the understanding, which are regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which are constitutive principles of intuition. These dynamical laws are, however, constitutive in relation to experience, inasmuch as they render the conceptions without which experience could not exist possible à priori. But the principles of pure reason cannot be constitutive even in regard to empirical conceptions, because no sensuous schema corresponding to them can be discovered, and they cannot therefore have an object in concreto. Now, if I grant that they cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, as constitutive principles, how shall I secure for them employment and objective validity as regulative principles, and in what way can they be so employed?\n\nThe understanding is the object of reason, as sensibility is the object of the understanding. The production of systematic unity in all the empirical operations of the understanding is the proper occupation of reason; just as it is the business of the understanding to connect the various content of phenomena by means of conceptions, and subject them to empirical laws. But the operations of the understanding are, without the schemata of sensibility, undetermined; and, in the same manner, the unity of reason is perfectly undetermined as regards the conditions under which, and the extent to which, the understanding ought to carry the systematic connection of its conceptions. But, although it is impossible to discover in intuition a schema for the complete systematic unity of all the conceptions of the understanding, there must be some analogon of this schema. This analogon is the idea of the maximum of the division and the connection of our cognition in one principle. For we may have a determinate notion of a maximum and an absolutely perfect, all the restrictive conditions which are connected with an indeterminate and various content having been abstracted. Thus the idea of reason is analogous with a sensuous schema, with this difference, that the application of the categories to the schema of reason does not present a cognition of any object (as is the case with the application of the categories to sensuous schemata), but merely provides us with a rule or principle for the systematic unity of the exercise of the understanding. Now, as every principle which imposes upon the exercise of the understanding à priori compliance with the rule of systematic unity also relates, although only in an indirect manner, to an object of experience, the principles of pure reason will also possess objective reality and validity in relation to experience. But they will not aim at determining our knowledge in regard to any empirical object; they will merely indicate the procedure, following which the empirical and determinate exercise of the understanding may be in complete harmony and connection with itself—a result which is produced by its being brought into harmony with the principle of systematic unity, so far as that is possible, and deduced from it.\n\nI term all subjective principles, which are not derived from observation of the constitution of an object, but from the interest which Reason has in producing a certain completeness in her cognition of that object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason, which are based solely upon its speculative interest, although they appear to be objective principles.\n\nWhen principles which are really regulative are regarded as constitutive, and employed as objective principles, contradictions must arise; but if they are considered as mere maxims, there is no room for contradictions of any kind, as they then merely indicate the different interests of reason, which occasion differences in the mode of thought. In effect, Reason has only one single interest, and the seeming contradiction existing between her maxims merely indicates a difference in, and a reciprocal limitation of, the methods by which this interest is satisfied.\n\nThis reasoner has at heart the interest of diversity—in accordance with the principle of specification; another, the interest of unity—in accordance with the principle of aggregation. Each believes that his judgement rests upon a thorough insight into the subject he is examining, and yet it has been influenced solely by a greater or less degree of adherence to some one of the two principles, neither of which are objective, but originate solely from the interest of reason, and on this account to be termed maxims rather than principles. When I observe intelligent men disputing about the distinctive characteristics of men, animals, or plants, and even of minerals, those on the one side assuming the existence of certain national characteristics, certain well-defined and hereditary distinctions of family, race, and so on, while the other side maintain that nature has endowed all races of men with the same faculties and dispositions, and that all differences are but the result of external and accidental circumstances—I have only to consider for a moment the real nature of the subject of discussion, to arrive at the conclusion that it is a subject far too deep for us to judge of, and that there is little probability of either party being able to speak from a perfect insight into and understanding of the nature of the subject itself. Both have, in reality, been struggling for the twofold interest of reason; the one maintaining the one interest, the other the other. But this difference between the maxims of diversity and unity may easily be reconciled and adjusted; although, so long as they are regarded as objective principles, they must occasion not only contradictions and polemic, but place hinderances in the way of the advancement of truth, until some means is discovered of reconciling these conflicting interests, and bringing reason into union and harmony with itself.\n\nThe same is the case with the so-called law discovered by Leibnitz, and supported with remarkable ability by Bonnet—the law of the continuous gradation of created beings, which is nothing more than an inference from the principle of affinity; for observation and study of the order of nature could never present it to the mind as an objective truth. The steps of this ladder, as they appear in experience, are too far apart from each other, and the so-called petty differences between different kinds of animals are in nature commonly so wide separations that no confidence can be placed in such views (particularly when we reflect on the great variety of things, and the ease with which we can discover resemblances), and no faith in the laws which are said to express the aims and purposes of nature. On the other hand, the method of investigating the order of nature in the light of this principle, and the maxim which requires us to regard this order—it being still undetermined how far it extends—as really existing in nature, is beyond doubt a legitimate and excellent principle of reason—a principle which extends farther than any experience or observation of ours and which, without giving us any positive knowledge of anything in the region of experience, guides us to the goal of systematic unity.\n\n_Of the Ultimate End of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason._\n\nThe ideas of pure reason cannot be, of themselves and in their own nature, dialectical; it is from their misemployment alone that fallacies and illusions arise. For they originate in the nature of reason itself, and it is impossible that this supreme tribunal for all the rights and claims of speculation should be itself undeserving of confidence and promotive of error. It is to be expected, therefore, that these ideas have a genuine and legitimate aim. It is true, the mob of sophists raise against reason the cry of inconsistency and contradiction, and affect to despise the government of that faculty, because they cannot understand its constitution, while it is to its beneficial influences alone that they owe the position and the intelligence which enable them to criticize and to blame its procedure.\n\nWe cannot employ an à priori conception with certainty, until we have made a transcendental deduction therefore. The ideas of pure reason do not admit of the same kind of deduction as the categories. But if they are to possess the least objective validity, and to represent anything but mere creations of thought (entia rationis ratiocinantis), a deduction of them must be possible. This deduction will complete the critical task imposed upon pure reason; and it is to this part Of our labours that we now proceed.\n\nThere is a great difference between a thing’s being presented to the mind as an object in an absolute sense, or merely as an ideal object. In the former case I employ my conceptions to determine the object; in the latter case nothing is present to the mind but a mere schema, which does not relate directly to an object, not even in a hypothetical sense, but which is useful only for the purpose of representing other objects to the mind, in a mediate and indirect manner, by means of their relation to the idea in the intellect. Thus I say the conception of a supreme intelligence is a mere idea; that is to say, its objective reality does not consist in the fact that it has an immediate relation to an object (for in this sense we have no means of establishing its objective validity), it is merely a schema constructed according to the necessary conditions of the unity of reason—the schema of a thing in general, which is useful towards the production of the highest degree of systematic unity in the empirical exercise of reason, in which we deduce this or that object of experience from the imaginary object of this idea, as the ground or cause of the said object of experience. In this way, the idea is properly a heuristic, and not an ostensive, conception; it does not give us any information respecting the constitution of an object, it merely indicates how, under the guidance of the idea, we ought to investigate the constitution and the relations of objects in the world of experience. Now, if it can be shown that the three kinds of transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological, and theological), although not relating directly to any object nor determining it, do nevertheless, on the supposition of the existence of an ideal object, produce systematic unity in the laws of the empirical employment of the reason, and extend our empirical cognition, without ever being inconsistent or in opposition with it—it must be a necessary maxim of reason to regulate its procedure according to these ideas. And this forms the transcendental deduction of all speculative ideas, not as constitutive principles of the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of our experience, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of empirical cognition, which is by the aid of these ideas arranged and emended within its own proper limits, to an extent unattainable by the operation of the principles of the understanding alone.\n\nI shall make this plainer. Guided by the principles involved in these ideas, we must, in the first place, so connect all the phenomena, actions, and feelings of the mind, as if it were a simple substance, which, endowed with personal identity, possesses a permanent existence (in this life at least), while its states, among which those of the body are to be included as external conditions, are in continual change. Secondly, in cosmology, we must investigate the conditions of all natural phenomena, internal as well as external, as if they belonged to a chain infinite and without any prime or supreme member, while we do not, on this account, deny the existence of intelligible grounds of these phenomena, although we never employ them to explain phenomena, for the simple reason that they are not objects of our cognition. Thirdly, in the sphere of theology, we must regard the whole system of possible experience as forming an absolute, but dependent and sensuously-conditioned unity, and at the same time as based upon a sole, supreme, and all-sufficient ground existing apart from the world itself—a ground which is a self-subsistent, primeval and creative reason, in relation to which we so employ our reason in the field of experience, as if all objects drew their origin from that archetype of all reason. In other words, we ought not to deduce the internal phenomena of the mind from a simple thinking substance, but deduce them from each other under the guidance of the regulative idea of a simple being; we ought not to deduce the phenomena, order, and unity of the universe from a supreme intelligence, but merely draw from this idea of a supremely wise cause the rules which must guide reason in its connection of causes and effects.\n\nNow there is nothing to hinder us from admitting these ideas to possess an objective and hyperbolic existence, except the cosmological ideas, which lead reason into an antinomy: the psychological and theological ideas are not antinomial. They contain no contradiction; and how, then, can any one dispute their objective reality, since he who denies it knows as little about their possibility as we who affirm? And yet, when we wish to admit the existence of a thing, it is not sufficient to convince ourselves that there is no positive obstacle in the way; for it cannot be allowable to regard mere creations of thought, which transcend, though they do not contradict, all our conceptions, as real and determinate objects, solely upon the authority of a speculative reason striving to compass its own aims. They cannot, therefore, be admitted to be real in themselves; they can only possess a comparative reality—that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition. They are to be regarded not as actual things, but as in some measure analogous to them. We abstract from the object of the idea all the conditions which limit the exercise of our understanding, but which, on the other hand, are the sole conditions of our possessing a determinate conception of any given thing. And thus we cogitate a something, of the real nature of which we have not the least conception, but which we represent to ourselves as standing in a relation to the whole system of phenomena, analogous to that in which phenomena stand to each other.\n\nBy admitting these ideal beings, we do not really extend our cognitions beyond the objects of possible experience; we extend merely the empirical unity of our experience, by the aid of systematic unity, the schema of which is furnished by the idea, which is therefore valid—not as a constitutive, but as a regulative principle. For although we posit a thing corresponding to the idea—a something, an actual existence—we do not on that account aim at the extension of our cognition by means of transcendent conceptions. This existence is purely ideal, and not objective; it is the mere expression of the systematic unity which is to be the guide of reason in the field of experience. There are no attempts made at deciding what the ground of this unity may be, or what the real nature of this imaginary being.\n\nThus the transcendental and only determinate conception of God, which is presented to us by speculative reason, is in the strictest sense deistic. In other words, reason does not assure us of the objective validity of the conception; it merely gives us the idea of something, on which the supreme and necessary unity of all experience is based. This something we cannot, following the analogy of a real substance, cogitate otherwise than as the cause of all things operating in accordance with rational laws, if we regard it as an individual object; although we should rest contented with the idea alone as a regulative principle of reason, and make no attempt at completing the sum of the conditions imposed by thought. This attempt is, indeed, inconsistent with the grand aim of complete systematic unity in the sphere of cognition—a unity to which no bounds are set by reason.\n\nHence it happens that, admitting a divine being, I can have no conception of the internal possibility of its perfection, or of the necessity of its existence. The only advantage of this admission is that it enables me to answer all other questions relating to the contingent, and to give reason the most complete satisfaction as regards the unity which it aims at attaining in the world of experience. But I cannot satisfy reason with regard to this hypothesis itself; and this proves that it is not its intelligence and insight into the subject, but its speculative interest alone which induces it to proceed from a point lying far beyond the sphere of our cognition, for the purpose of being able to consider all objects as parts of a systematic whole.\n\nHere a distinction presents itself, in regard to the way in which we may cogitate a presupposition—a distinction which is somewhat subtle, but of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I may have sufficient grounds to admit something, or the existence of something, in a relative point of view (suppositio relativa), without being justified in admitting it in an absolute sense (suppositio absoluta). This distinction is undoubtedly requisite, in the case of a regulative principle, the necessity of which we recognize, though we are ignorant of the source and cause of that necessity, and which we assume to be based upon some ultimate ground, for the purpose of being able to cogitate the universality of the principle in a more determinate way. For example, I cogitate the existence of a being corresponding to a pure transcendental idea. But I cannot admit that this being exists absolutely and in itself, because all of the conceptions by which I can cogitate an object in a determinate manner fall short of assuring me of its existence; nay, the conditions of the objective validity of my conceptions are excluded by the idea—by the very fact of its being an idea. The conceptions of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, have no significance out of the sphere of empirical cognition, and cannot, beyond that sphere, determine any object. They may, accordingly, be employed to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but they are utterly inadequate to explain the possibility of the universe itself considered as a whole; because in this case the ground of explanation must lie out of and beyond the world, and cannot, therefore, be an object of possible experience. Now, I may admit the existence of an incomprehensible being of this nature—the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense; although I have no ground to admit its existence absolutely and in itself. For if an idea (that of a systematic and complete unity, of which I shall presently speak more particularly) lies at the foundation of the most extended empirical employment of reason, and if this idea cannot be adequately represented in concreto, although it is indispensably necessary for the approximation of empirical unity to the highest possible degree—I am not only authorized, but compelled, to realize this idea, that is, to posit a real object corresponding thereto. But I cannot profess to know this object; it is to me merely a something, to which, as the ground of systematic unity in cognition, I attribute such properties as are analogous to the conceptions employed by the understanding in the sphere of experience. Following the analogy of the notions of reality, substance, causality, and necessity, I cogitate a being, which possesses all these attributes in the highest degree; and, as this idea is the offspring of my reason alone, I cogitate this being as self-subsistent reason, and as the cause of the universe operating by means of ideas of the greatest possible harmony and unity. Thus I abstract all conditions that would limit my idea, solely for the purpose of rendering systematic unity possible in the world of empirical diversity, and thus securing the widest possible extension for the exercise of reason in that sphere. This I am enabled to do, by regarding all connections and relations in the world of sense, as if they were the dispositions of a supreme reason, of which our reason is but a faint image. I then proceed to cogitate this Supreme Being by conceptions which have, properly, no meaning or application, except in the world of sense. But as I am authorized to employ the transcendental hypothesis of such a being in a relative respect alone, that is, as the substratum of the greatest possible unity in experience—I may attribute to a being which I regard as distinct from the world, such properties as belong solely to the sphere of sense and experience. For I do not desire, and am not justified in desiring, to cognize this object of my idea, as it exists in itself; for I possess no conceptions sufficient for this task, those of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, losing all significance, and becoming merely the signs of conceptions, without content and without applicability, when I attempt to carry them beyond the limits of the world of sense. I cogitate merely the relation of a perfectly unknown being to the greatest possible systematic unity of experience, solely for the purpose of employing it as the schema of the regulative principle which directs reason in its empirical exercise.\n\nIt is evident, at the first view, that we cannot presuppose the reality of this transcendental object, by means of the conceptions of reality, substance, causality, and so on, because these conceptions cannot be applied to anything that is distinct from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of a Supreme Being or cause is purely relative; it is cogitated only in behalf of the systematic unity of experience; such a being is but a something, of whose existence in itself we have not the least conception. Thus, too, it becomes sufficiently manifest why we required the idea of a necessary being in relation to objects given by sense, although we can never have the least conception of this being, or of its absolute necessity.\n\nAnd now we can clearly perceive the result of our transcendental dialectic, and the proper aim of the ideas of pure reason—which become dialectical solely from misunderstanding and inconsiderateness. Pure reason is, in fact, occupied with itself, and not with any object. Objects are not presented to it to be embraced in the unity of an empirical conception; it is only the cognitions of the understanding that are presented to it, for the purpose of receiving the unity of a rational conception, that is, of being connected according to a principle. The unity of reason is the unity of system; and this systematic unity is not an objective principle, extending its dominion over objects, but a subjective maxim, extending its authority over the empirical cognition of objects. The systematic connection which reason gives to the empirical employment of the understanding not only advances the extension of that employment, but ensures its correctness, and thus the principle of a systematic unity of this nature is also objective, although only in an indefinite respect (principium vagum). It is not, however, a constitutive principle, determining an object to which it directly relates; it is merely a regulative principle or maxim, advancing and strengthening the empirical exercise of reason, by the opening up of new paths of which the understanding is ignorant, while it never conflicts with the laws of its exercise in the sphere of experience.\n\nBut reason cannot cogitate this systematic unity, without at the same time cogitating an object of the idea—an object that cannot be presented in any experience, which contains no concrete example of a complete systematic unity. This being (ens rationis ratiocinatae) is therefore a mere idea and is not assumed to be a thing which is real absolutely and in itself. On the contrary, it forms merely the problematical foundation of the connection which the mind introduces among the phenomena of the sensuous world. We look upon this connection, in the light of the above-mentioned idea, as if it drew its origin from the supposed being which corresponds to the idea. And yet all we aim at is the possession of this idea as a secure foundation for the systematic unity of experience—a unity indispensable to reason, advantageous to the understanding, and promotive of the interests of empirical cognition.\n\nWe mistake the true meaning of this idea when we regard it as an enouncement, or even as a hypothetical declaration of the existence of a real thing, which we are to regard as the origin or ground of a systematic constitution of the universe. On the contrary, it is left completely undetermined what the nature or properties of this so-called ground may be. The idea is merely to be adopted as a point of view, from which this unity, so essential to reason and so beneficial to the understanding, may be regarded as radiating. In one word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of a regulative principle, by means of which Reason, so far as in her lies, extends the dominion of systematic unity over the whole sphere of experience.\n\nThe first object of an idea of this kind is the ego, considered merely as a thinking nature or soul. If I wish to investigate the properties of a thinking being, I must interrogate experience. But I find that I can apply none of the categories to this object, the schema of these categories, which is the condition of their application, being given only in sensuous intuition. But I cannot thus attain to the cognition of a systematic unity of all the phenomena of the internal sense. Instead, therefore, of an empirical conception of what the soul really is, reason takes the conception of the empirical unity of all thought, and, by cogitating this unity as unconditioned and primitive, constructs the rational conception or idea of a simple substance which is in itself unchangeable, possessing personal identity, and in connection with other real things external to it; in one word, it constructs the idea of a simple self-subsistent intelligence. But the real aim of reason in this procedure is the attainment of principles of systematic unity for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul. That is, reason desires to be able to represent all the determinations of the internal sense as existing in one subject, all powers as deduced from one fundamental power, all changes as mere varieties in the condition of a being which is permanent and always the same, and all phenomena in space as entirely different in their nature from the procedure of thought. Essential simplicity (with the other attributes predicated of the ego) is regarded as the mere schema of this regulative principle; it is not assumed that it is the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these properties may rest upon quite different grounds, of which we are completely ignorant; just as the above predicates could not give us any knowledge of the soul as it is in itself, even if we regarded them as valid in respect of it, inasmuch as they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented in concreto. Nothing but good can result from a psychological idea of this kind, if we only take proper care not to consider it as more than an idea; that is, if we regard it as valid merely in relation to the employment of reason, in the sphere of the phenomena of the soul. Under the guidance of this idea, or principle, no empirical laws of corporeal phenomena are called in to explain that which is a phenomenon of the internal sense alone; no windy hypotheses of the generation, annihilation, and palingenesis of souls are admitted. Thus the consideration of this object of the internal sense is kept pure, and unmixed with heterogeneous elements; while the investigation of reason aims at reducing all the grounds of explanation employed in this sphere of knowledge to a single principle. All this is best effected, nay, cannot be effected otherwise than by means of such a schema, which requires us to regard this ideal thing as an actual existence. The psychological idea is, therefore, meaningless and inapplicable, except as the schema of a regulative conception. For, if I ask whether the soul is not really of a spiritual nature—it is a question which has no meaning. From such a conception has been abstracted, not merely all corporeal nature, but all nature, that is, all the predicates of a possible experience; and consequently, all the conditions which enable us to cogitate an object to this conception have disappeared. But, if these conditions are absent, it is evident that the conception is meaningless.\n\nThe second regulative idea of speculative reason is the conception of the universe. For nature is properly the only object presented to us, in regard to which reason requires regulative principles. Nature is twofold—thinking and corporeal nature. To cogitate the latter in regard to its internal possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, no idea is required—no representation which transcends experience. In this sphere, therefore, an idea is impossible, sensuous intuition being our only guide; while, in the sphere of psychology, we require the fundamental idea (I), which contains à priori a certain form of thought namely, the unity of the ego. Pure reason has, therefore, nothing left but nature in general, and the completeness of conditions in nature in accordance with some principle. The absolute totality of the series of these conditions is an idea, which can never be fully realized in the empirical exercise of reason, while it is serviceable as a rule for the procedure of reason in relation to that totality. It requires us, in the explanation of given phenomena (in the regress or ascent in the series), to proceed as if the series were infinite in itself, that is, were prolonged in indefinitum; while on the other hand, where reason is regarded as itself the determining cause (in the region of freedom), we are required to proceed as if we had not before us an object of sense, but of the pure understanding. In this latter case, the conditions do not exist in the series of phenomena, but may be placed quite out of and beyond it, and the series of conditions may be regarded as if it had an absolute beginning from an intelligible cause. All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and not constitutive; and that their aim is not to realize an actual totality in such series. The full discussion of this subject will be found in its proper place in the chapter on the antinomy of pure reason.\n\nThe third idea of pure reason, containing the hypothesis of a being which is valid merely as a relative hypothesis, is that of the one and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, in other words, the idea of God. We have not the slightest ground absolutely to admit the existence of an object corresponding to this idea; for what can empower or authorize us to affirm the existence of a being of the highest perfection—a being whose existence is absolutely necessary—merely because we possess the conception of such a being? The answer is: It is the existence of the world which renders this hypothesis necessary. But this answer makes it perfectly evident that the idea of this being, like all other speculative ideas, is essentially nothing more than a demand upon reason that it shall regulate the connection which it and its subordinate faculties introduce into the phenomena of the world by principles of systematic unity and, consequently, that it shall regard all phenomena as originating from one all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is plain that the only aim of reason in this procedure is the establishment of its own formal rule for the extension of its dominion in the world of experience; that it does not aim at an extension of its cognition beyond the limits of experience; and that, consequently, this idea does not contain any constitutive principle.\n\nThe highest formal unity, which is based upon ideas alone, is the unity of all things—a unity in accordance with an aim or purpose; and the speculative interest of reason renders it necessary to regard all order in the world as if it originated from the intention and design of a supreme reason. This principle unfolds to the view of reason in the sphere of experience new and enlarged prospects, and invites it to connect the phenomena of the world according to teleological laws, and in this way to attain to the highest possible degree of systematic unity. The hypothesis of a supreme intelligence, as the sole cause of the universe—an intelligence which has for us no more than an ideal existence—is accordingly always of the greatest service to reason. Thus, if we presuppose, in relation to the figure of the earth (which is round, but somewhat flattened at the poles),[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-71) or that of mountains or seas, wise designs on the part of an author of the universe, we cannot fail to make, by the light of this supposition, a great number of interesting discoveries. If we keep to this hypothesis, as a principle which is purely regulative, even error cannot be very detrimental. For, in this case, error can have no more serious consequences than that, where we expected to discover a teleological connection (nexus finalis), only a mechanical or physical connection appears. In such a case, we merely fail to find the additional form of unity we expected, but we do not lose the rational unity which the mind requires in its procedure in experience. But even a miscarriage of this sort cannot affect the law in its general and teleological relations. For although we may convict an anatomist of an error, when he connects the limb of some animal with a certain purpose, it is quite impossible to prove in a single case that any arrangement of nature, be it what it may, is entirely without aim or design. And thus medical physiology, by the aid of a principle presented to it by pure reason, extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the different parts of an organized body so far that it may be asserted with the utmost confidence, and with the approbation of all reflecting men, that every organ or bodily part of an animal has its use and answers a certain design. Now, this is a supposition which, if regarded as of a constitutive character, goes much farther than any experience or observation of ours can justify. Hence it is evident that it is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, which aims at the highest degree of systematic unity, by the aid of the idea of a causality according to design in a supreme cause—a cause which it regards as the highest intelligence.\n\n[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-71) The advantages which a circular form, in the case of the earth, has over every other, are well known. But few are aware that the slight flattening at the poles, which gives it the figure of a spheroid, is the only cause which prevents the elevations of continents or even of mountains, perhaps thrown up by some internal convulsion, from continually altering the position of the axis of the earth—and that to some considerable degree in a short time. The great protuberance of the earth under the Equator serves to overbalance the impetus of all other masses of earth, and thus to preserve the axis of the earth, so far as we can observe, in its present position. And yet this wise arrangement has been unthinkingly explained from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass.\n\nIf, however, we neglect this restriction of the idea to a purely regulative influence, reason is betrayed into numerous errors. For it has then left the ground of experience, in which alone are to be found the criteria of truth, and has ventured into the region of the incomprehensible and unsearchable, on the heights of which it loses its power and collectedness, because it has completely severed its connection with experience.\n\nThe first error which arises from our employing the idea of a Supreme Being as a constitutive (in repugnance to the very nature of an idea), and not as a regulative principle, is the error of inactive reason (ignava ratio).[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-72) We may so term every principle which requires us to regard our investigations of nature as absolutely complete, and allows reason to cease its inquiries, as if it had fully executed its task. Thus the psychological idea of the ego, when employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul, and for the extension of our knowledge regarding this subject beyond the limits of experience—even to the condition of the soul after death—is convenient enough for the purposes of pure reason, but detrimental and even ruinous to its interests in the sphere of nature and experience. The dogmatizing spiritualist explains the unchanging unity of our personality through all changes of condition from the unity of a thinking substance, the interest which we take in things and events that can happen only after our death, from a consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so on. Thus he dispenses with all empirical investigations into the cause of these internal phenomena, and with all possible explanations of them upon purely natural grounds; while, at the dictation of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of cognition in experience, greatly to his own ease and convenience, but to the sacrifice of all, genuine insight and intelligence. These prejudicial consequences become still more evident, in the case of the dogmatical treatment of our idea of a Supreme Intelligence, and the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely based upon it. For, in this case, the aims which we observe in nature, and often those which we merely fancy to exist, make the investigation of causes a very easy task, by directing us to refer such and such phenomena immediately to the unsearchable will and counsel of the Supreme Wisdom, while we ought to investigate their causes in the general laws of the mechanism of matter. We are thus recommended to consider the labour of reason as ended, when we have merely dispensed with its employment, which is guided surely and safely only by the order of nature and the series of changes in the world—which are arranged according to immanent and general laws. This error may be avoided, if we do not merely consider from the view-point of final aims certain parts of nature, such as the division and structure of a continent, the constitution and direction of certain mountain-chains, or even the organization existing in the vegetable and animal kingdoms, but look upon this systematic unity of nature in a perfectly general way, in relation to the idea of a Supreme Intelligence. If we pursue this advice, we lay as a foundation for all investigation the conformity to aims of all phenomena of nature in accordance with universal laws, for which no particular arrangement of nature is exempt, but only cognized by us with more or less difficulty; and we possess a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection, which we do not attempt to anticipate or predetermine. All that we do, and ought to do, is to follow out the physico-mechanical connection in nature according to general laws, with the hope of discovering, sooner or later, the teleological connection also. Thus, and thus only, can the principle of final unity aid in the extension of the employment of reason in the sphere of experience, without being in any case detrimental to its interests.\n\n[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-72) This was the term applied by the old dialecticians to a sophistical argument, which ran thus: If it is your fate to die of this disease, you will die, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this mode of reasoning has received this appellation, because, if followed, it puts an end to the employment of reason in the affairs of life. For a similar reason, I have applied this designation to the sophistical argument of pure reason.\n\nThe second error which arises from the misconception of the principle of systematic unity is that of perverted reason (perversa ratio, usteron roteron rationis). The idea of systematic unity is available as a regulative principle in the connection of phenomena according to general natural laws; and, how far soever we have to travel upon the path of experience to discover some fact or event, this idea requires us to believe that we have approached all the more nearly to the completion of its use in the sphere of nature, although that completion can never be attained. But this error reverses the procedure of reason. We begin by hypostatizing the principle of systematic unity, and by giving an anthropomorphic determination to the conception of a Supreme Intelligence, and then proceed forcibly to impose aims upon nature. Thus not only does teleology, which ought to aid in the completion of unity in accordance with general laws, operate to the destruction of its influence, but it hinders reason from attaining its proper aim, that is, the proof, upon natural grounds, of the existence of a supreme intelligent cause. For, if we cannot presuppose supreme finality in nature à priori, that is, as essentially belonging to nature, how can we be directed to endeavour to discover this unity and, rising gradually through its different degrees, to approach the supreme perfection of an author of all—a perfection which is absolutely necessary, and therefore cognizable à priori? The regulative principle directs us to presuppose systematic unity absolutely and, consequently, as following from the essential nature of things—but only as a unity of nature, not merely cognized empirically, but presupposed à priori, although only in an indeterminate manner. But if I insist on basing nature upon the foundation of a supreme ordaining Being, the unity of nature is in effect lost. For, in this case, it is quite foreign and unessential to the nature of things, and cannot be cognized from the general laws of nature. And thus arises a vicious circular argument, what ought to have been proved having been presupposed.\n\nTo take the regulative principle of systematic unity in nature for a constitutive principle, and to hypostatize and make a cause out of that which is properly the ideal ground of the consistent and harmonious exercise of reason, involves reason in inextricable embarrassments. The investigation of nature pursues its own path under the guidance of the chain of natural causes, in accordance with the general laws of nature, and ever follows the light of the idea of an author of the universe—not for the purpose of deducing the finality, which it constantly pursues, from this Supreme Being, but to attain to the cognition of his existence from the finality which it seeks in the existence of the phenomena of nature, and, if possible, in that of all things to cognize this being, consequently, as absolutely necessary. Whether this latter purpose succeed or not, the idea is and must always be a true one, and its employment, when merely regulative, must always be accompanied by truthful and beneficial results.\n\nComplete unity, in conformity with aims, constitutes absolute perfection. But if we do not find this unity in the nature of the things which go to constitute the world of experience, that is, of objective cognition, consequently in the universal and necessary laws of nature, how can we infer from this unity the idea of the supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of a primal being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic unity, and consequently teleological unity, constitutes the very foundation of the possibility of the most extended employment of human reason. The idea of unity is therefore essentially and indissolubly connected with the nature of our reason. This idea is a legislative one; and hence it is very natural that we should assume the existence of a legislative reason corresponding to it, from which the systematic unity of nature—the object of the operations of reason—must be derived.\n\nIn the course of our discussion of the antinomies, we stated that it is always possible to answer all the questions which pure reason may raise; and that the plea of the limited nature of our cognition, which is unavoidable and proper in many questions regarding natural phenomena, cannot in this case be admitted, because the questions raised do not relate to the nature of things, but are necessarily originated by the nature of reason itself, and relate to its own internal constitution. We can now establish this assertion, which at first sight appeared so rash, in relation to the two questions in which reason takes the greatest interest, and thus complete our discussion of the dialectic of pure reason.\n\nIf, then, the question is asked, in relation to transcendental theology,[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-73) first, whether there is anything distinct from the world, which contains the ground of cosmical order and connection according to general laws? The answer is: Certainly. For the world is a sum of phenomena; there must, therefore, be some transcendental basis of these phenomena, that is, a basis cogitable by the pure understanding alone. If, secondly, the question is asked whether this being is substance, whether it is of the greatest reality, whether it is necessary, and so forth? I answer that this question is utterly without meaning. For all the categories which aid me in forming a conception of an object cannot be employed except in the world of sense, and are without meaning when not applied to objects of actual or possible experience. Out of this sphere, they are not properly conceptions, but the mere marks or indices of conceptions, which we may admit, although they cannot, without the help of experience, help us to understand any subject or thing. If, thirdly, the question is whether we may not cogitate this being, which is distinct from the world, in analogy with the objects of experience? The answer is: Undoubtedly, but only as an ideal, and not as a real object. That is, we must cogitate it only as an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order, and finality of the world—a unity which reason must employ as the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Nay, more, we may admit into the idea certain anthropomorphic elements, which are promotive of the interests of this regulative principle. For it is no more than an idea, which does not relate directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, by means, however, of a schema of this unity—the schema of a Supreme Intelligence, who is the wisely-designing author of the universe. What this basis of cosmical unity may be in itself, we know not—we cannot discover from the idea; we merely know how we ought to employ the idea of this unity, in relation to the systematic operation of reason in the sphere of experience.\n\n[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-73) After what has been said of the psychological idea of the ego and its proper employment as a regulative principle of the operations of reason, I need not enter into details regarding the transcendental illusion by which the systematic unity of all the various phenomena of the internal sense is hypostatized. The procedure is in this case very similar to that which has been discussed in our remarks on the theological ideal.\n\nBut, it will be asked again, can we on these grounds, admit the existence of a wise and omnipotent author of the world? Without doubt; and not only so, but we must assume the existence of such a being. But do we thus extend the limits of our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have merely presupposed a something, of which we have no conception, which we do not know as it is in itself; but, in relation to the systematic disposition of the universe, which we must presuppose in all our observation of nature, we have cogitated this unknown being in analogy with an intelligent existence (an empirical conception), that is to say, we have endowed it with those attributes, which, judging from the nature of our own reason, may contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore valid only relatively to the employment in experience of our reason. But if we attribute to it absolute and objective validity, we overlook the fact that it is merely an ideal being that we cogitate; and, by setting out from a basis which is not determinable by considerations drawn from experience, we place ourselves in a position which incapacitates us from applying this principle to the empirical employment of reason.\n\nBut, it will be asked further, can I make any use of this conception and hypothesis in my investigations into the world and nature? Yes, for this very purpose was the idea established by reason as a fundamental basis. But may I regard certain arrangements, which seemed to have been made in conformity with some fixed aim, as the arrangements of design, and look upon them as proceeding from the divine will, with the intervention, however, of certain other particular arrangements disposed to that end? Yes, you may do so; but at the same time you must regard it as indifferent, whether it is asserted that divine wisdom has disposed all things in conformity with his highest aims, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative principle in the investigation of nature, and at the same time a principle of the systematic unity of nature according to general laws, even in those cases where we are unable to discover that unity. In other words, it must be perfectly indifferent to you whether you say, when you have discovered this unity: God has wisely willed it so; or: Nature has wisely arranged this. For it was nothing but the systematic unity, which reason requires as a basis for the investigation of nature, that justified you in accepting the idea of a supreme intelligence as a schema for a regulative principle; and, the farther you advance in the discovery of design and finality, the more certain the validity of your idea. But, as the whole aim of this regulative principle was the discovery of a necessary and systematic unity in nature, we have, in so far as we attain this, to attribute our success to the idea of a Supreme Being; while, at the same time, we cannot, without involving ourselves in contradictions, overlook the general laws of nature, as it was in reference to them alone that this idea was employed. We cannot, I say, overlook the general laws of nature, and regard this conformity to aims observable in nature as contingent or hyperphysical in its origin; inasmuch as there is no ground which can justify us in the admission of a being with such properties distinct from and above nature. All that we are authorized to assert is that this idea may be employed as a principle, and that the properties of the being which is assumed to correspond to it may be regarded as systematically connected in analogy with the causal determination of phenomena.\n\nFor the same reasons we are justified in introducing into the idea of the supreme cause other anthropomorphic elements (for without these we could not predicate anything of it); we may regard it as allowable to cogitate this cause as a being with understanding, the feelings of pleasure and displeasure, and faculties of desire and will corresponding to these. At the same time, we may attribute to this being infinite perfection—a perfection which necessarily transcends that which our knowledge of the order and design in the world authorize us to predicate of it. For the regulative law of systematic unity requires us to study nature on the supposition that systematic and final unity in infinitum is everywhere discoverable, even in the highest diversity. For, although we may discover little of this cosmical perfection, it belongs to the legislative prerogative of reason to require us always to seek for and to expect it; while it must always be beneficial to institute all inquiries into nature in accordance with this principle. But it is evident that, by this idea of a supreme author of all, which I place as the foundation of all inquiries into nature, I do not mean to assert the existence of such a being, or that I have any knowledge of its existence; and, consequently, I do not really deduce anything from the existence of this being, but merely from its idea, that is to say, from the nature of things in this world, in accordance with this idea. A certain dim consciousness of the true use of this idea seems to have dictated to the philosophers of all times the moderate language used by them regarding the cause of the world. We find them employing the expressions wisdom and care of nature, and divine wisdom, as synonymous—nay, in purely speculative discussions, preferring the former, because it does not carry the appearance of greater pretensions than such as we are entitled to make, and at the same time directs reason to its proper field of action—nature and her phenomena.\n\nThus, pure reason, which at first seemed to promise us nothing less than the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of experience, is found, when thoroughly examined, to contain nothing but regulative principles, the virtue and function of which is to introduce into our cognition a higher degree of unity than the understanding could of itself. These principles, by placing the goal of all our struggles at so great a distance, realize for us the most thorough connection between the different parts of our cognition, and the highest degree of systematic unity. But, on the other hand, if misunderstood and employed as constitutive principles of transcendent cognition, they become the parents of illusions and contradictions, while pretending to introduce us to new regions of knowledge.\n\nThus all human cognition begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to conceptions, and ends with ideas. Although it possesses, in relation to all three elements, à priori sources of cognition, which seemed to transcend the limits of all experience, a thoroughgoing criticism demonstrates that speculative reason can never, by the aid of these elements, pass the bounds of possible experience, and that the proper destination of this highest faculty of cognition is to employ all methods, and all the principles of these methods, for the purpose of penetrating into the innermost secrets of nature, by the aid of the principles of unity (among all kinds of which teleological unity is the highest), while it ought not to attempt to soar above the sphere of experience, beyond which there lies nought for us but the void inane. The critical examination, in our Transcendental Analytic, of all the propositions which professed to extend cognition beyond the sphere of experience, completely demonstrated that they can only conduct us to a possible experience. If we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract theorems, if we were not allured by specious and inviting prospects to escape from the constraining power of their evidence, we might spare ourselves the laborious examination of all the dialectical arguments which a transcendent reason adduces in support of its pretensions; for we should know with the most complete certainty that, however honest such professions might be, they are null and valueless, because they relate to a kind of knowledge to which no man can by any possibility attain. But, as there is no end to discussion, if we cannot discover the true cause of the illusions by which even the wisest are deceived, and as the analysis of all our transcendent cognition into its elements is of itself of no slight value as a psychological study, while it is a duty incumbent on every philosopher—it was found necessary to investigate the dialectical procedure of reason in its primary sources. And as the inferences of which this dialectic is the parent are not only deceitful, but naturally possess a profound interest for humanity, it was advisable at the same time, to give a full account of the momenta of this dialectical procedure, and to deposit it in the archives of human reason, as a warning to all future metaphysicians to avoid these causes of speculative error.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","text":"# Appendix\n\n### APPENDIX. Of the Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason\n\nThe result of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms the truth of what we have already proved in our Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would lead us beyond the limits of experience are fallacious and groundless, but it at the same time teaches us this important lesson, that human reason has a natural inclination to overstep these limits, and that transcendental ideas are as much the natural property of the reason as categories are of the understanding. There exists this difference, however, that while the categories never mislead us, outward objects being always in perfect harmony therewith, ideas are the parents of irresistible illusions, the severest and most subtle criticism being required to save us from the fallacies which they induce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","text":"Whatever is grounded in the nature of our powers will be found to be in harmony with the final purpose and proper employment of these powers, when once we have discovered their true direction and aim. We are entitled to suppose, therefore, that there exists a mode of employing transcendental ideas which is proper and immanent; although, when we mistake their meaning, and regard them as conceptions of actual things, their mode of application is transcendent and delusive. For it is not the idea itself, but only the employment of the idea in relation to possible experience, that is transcendent or immanent. An idea is employed transcendently, when it is applied to an object falsely believed to be adequate with and to correspond to it; immanently, when it is applied solely to the employment of the understanding in the sphere of experience. Thus all errors of subreptio—of misapplication, are to be ascribed to defects of judgement, and not to understanding or reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","text":"Reason never has an immediate relation to an object; it relates immediately to the understanding alone. It is only through the understanding that it can be employed in the field of experience. It does not form conceptions of objects, it merely arranges them and gives to them that unity which they are capable of possessing when the sphere of their application has been extended as widely as possible. Reason avails itself of the conception of the understanding for the sole purpose of producing totality in the different series. This totality the understanding does not concern itself with; its only occupation is the connection of experiences, by which series of conditions in accordance with conceptions are established. The object of reason is, therefore, the understanding and its proper destination. As the latter brings unity into the diversity of objects by means of its conceptions, so the former brings unity into the diversity of conceptions by means of ideas; as it sets the final aim of a collective unity to the operations of the understanding, which without this occupies itself with a distributive unity alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","text":"I accordingly maintain that transcendental ideas can never be employed as constitutive ideas, that they cannot be conceptions of objects, and that, when thus considered, they assume a fallacious and dialectical character. But, on the other hand, they are capable of an admirable and indispensably necessary application to objects—as regulative ideas, directing the understanding to a certain aim, the guiding lines towards which all its laws follow, and in which they all meet in one point. This point—though a mere idea (focus imaginarius), that is, not a point from which the conceptions of the understanding do really proceed, for it lies beyond the sphere of possible experience—serves, notwithstanding, to give to these conceptions the greatest possible unity combined with the greatest possible extension. Hence arises the natural illusion which induces us to believe that these lines proceed from an object which lies out of the sphere of empirical cognition, just as objects reflected in a mirror appear to be behind it. But this illusion—which we may hinder from imposing upon us—is necessary and unavoidable, if we desire to see, not only those objects which lie before us, but those which are at a great distance behind us; that is to say, when, in the present case, we direct the aims of the understanding, beyond every given experience, towards an extension as great as can possibly be attained.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0005","text":"If we review our cognitions in their entire extent, we shall find that the peculiar business of reason is to arrange them into a system, that is to say, to give them connection according to a principle. This unity presupposes an idea—the idea of the form of a whole (of cognition), preceding the determinate cognition of the parts, and containing the conditions which determine à priori to every part its place and relation to the other parts of the whole system. This idea, accordingly, demands complete unity in the cognition of the understanding—not the unity of a contingent aggregate, but that of a system connected according to necessary laws. It cannot be affirmed with propriety that this idea is a conception of an object; it is merely a conception of the complete unity of the conceptions of objects, in so far as this unity is available to the understanding as a rule. Such conceptions of reason are not derived from nature; on the contrary, we employ them for the interrogation and investigation of nature, and regard our cognition as defective so long as it is not adequate to them. We admit that such a thing as pure earth, pure water, or pure air, is not to be discovered. And yet we require these conceptions (which have their origin in the reason, so far as regards their absolute purity and completeness) for the purpose of determining the share which each of these natural causes has in every phenomenon. Thus the different kinds of matter are all referred to earths, as mere weight; to salts and inflammable bodies, as pure force; and finally, to water and air, as the vehicula of the former, or the machines employed by them in their operations—for the purpose of explaining the chemical action and reaction of bodies in accordance with the idea of a mechanism. For, although not actually so expressed, the influence of such ideas of reason is very observable in the procedure of natural philosophers.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0006","text":"If reason is the faculty of deducing the particular from the general, and if the general be certain in se and given, it is only necessary that the judgement should subsume the particular under the general, the particular being thus necessarily determined. I shall term this the demonstrative or apodeictic employment of reason. If, however, the general is admitted as problematical only, and is a mere idea, the particular case is certain, but the universality of the rule which applies to this particular case remains a problem. Several particular cases, the certainty of which is beyond doubt, are then taken and examined, for the purpose of discovering whether the rule is applicable to them; and if it appears that all the particular cases which can be collected follow from the rule, its universality is inferred, and at the same time, all the causes which have not, or cannot be presented to our observation, are concluded to be of the same character with those which we have observed. This I shall term the hypothetical employment of the reason.\n\nThe hypothetical exercise of reason by the aid of ideas employed as problematical conceptions is properly not constitutive. That is to say, if we consider the subject strictly, the truth of the rule, which has been employed as an hypothesis, does not follow from the use that is made of it by reason. For how can we know all the possible cases that may arise? some of which may, however, prove exceptions to the universality of the rule. This employment of reason is merely regulative, and its sole aim is the introduction of unity into the aggregate of our particular cognitions, and thereby the approximating of the rule to universality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0007","text":"The object of the hypothetical employment of reason is therefore the systematic unity of cognitions; and this unity is the criterion of the truth of a rule. On the other hand, this systematic unity—as a mere idea—is in fact merely a unity projected, not to be regarded as given, but only in the light of a problem—a problem which serves, however, as a principle for the various and particular exercise of the understanding in experience, directs it with regard to those cases which are not presented to our observation, and introduces harmony and consistency into all its operations.\n\nAll that we can be certain of from the above considerations is that this systematic unity is a logical principle, whose aim is to assist the understanding, where it cannot of itself attain to rules, by means of ideas, to bring all these various rules under one principle, and thus to ensure the most complete consistency and connection that can be attained. But the assertion that objects and the understanding by which they are cognized are so constituted as to be determined to systematic unity, that this may be postulated à priori, without any reference to the interest of reason, and that we are justified in declaring all possible cognitions—empirical and others—to possess systematic unity, and to be subject to general principles from which, notwithstanding their various character, they are all derivable,—such an assertion can be founded only upon a transcendental principle of reason, which would render this systematic unity not subjectively and logically—in its character of a method, but objectively necessary.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0008","text":"We shall illustrate this by an example. The conceptions of the understanding make us acquainted, among many other kinds of unity, with that of the causality of a substance, which is termed power. The different phenomenal manifestations of the same substance appear at first view to be so very dissimilar that we are inclined to assume the existence of just as many different powers as there are different effects—as, in the case of the human mind, we have feeling, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, analysis, pleasure, desire and so on. Now we are required by a logical maxim to reduce these differences to as small a number as possible, by comparing them and discovering the hidden identity which exists. We must inquire, for example, whether or not imagination (connected with consciousness), memory, wit, and analysis are not merely different forms of understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental power, the existence of which no effort of logic can assure us of, is the problem to be solved, for the systematic representation of the existing variety of powers. The logical principle of reason requires us to produce as great a unity as is possible in the system of our cognitions; and the more the phenomena of this and the other power are found to be identical, the more probable does it become, that they are nothing but different manifestations of one and the same power, which may be called, relatively speaking, a fundamental power. And so with other cases.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0009","text":"These relatively fundamental powers must again be compared with each other, to discover, if possible, the one radical and absolutely fundamental power of which they are but the manifestations. But this unity is purely hypothetical. It is not maintained, that this unity does really exist, but that we must, in the interest of reason, that is, for the establishment of principles for the various rules presented by experience, try to discover and introduce it, so far as is practicable, into the sphere of our cognitions.\n\nBut the transcendental employment of the understanding would lead us to believe that this idea of a fundamental power is not problematical, but that it possesses objective reality, and thus the systematic unity of the various powers or forces in a substance is demanded by the understanding and erected into an apodeictic or necessary principle. For, without having attempted to discover the unity of the various powers existing in nature, nay, even after all our attempts have failed, we notwithstanding presuppose that it does exist, and may be, sooner or later, discovered. And this reason does, not only, as in the case above adduced, with regard to the unity of substance, but where many substances, although all to a certain extent homogeneous, are discoverable, as in the case of matter in general. Here also does reason presuppose the existence of the systematic unity of various powers—inasmuch as particular laws of nature are subordinate to general laws; and parsimony in principles is not merely an economical principle of reason, but an essential law of nature.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0010","text":"We cannot understand, in fact, how a logical principle of unity can of right exist, unless we presuppose a transcendental principle, by which such a systematic unit—as a property of objects themselves—is regarded as necessary à priori. For with what right can reason, in its logical exercise, require us to regard the variety of forces which nature displays, as in effect a disguised unity, and to deduce them from one fundamental force or power, when she is free to admit that it is just as possible that all forces should be different in kind, and that a systematic unity is not conformable to the design of nature? In this view of the case, reason would be proceeding in direct opposition to her own destination, by setting as an aim an idea which entirely conflicts with the procedure and arrangement of nature. Neither can we assert that reason has previously inferred this unity from the contingent nature of phenomena. For the law of reason which requires us to seek for this unity is a necessary law, inasmuch as without it we should not possess a faculty of reason, nor without reason a consistent and self-accordant mode of employing the understanding, nor, in the absence of this, any proper and sufficient criterion of empirical truth. In relation to this criterion, therefore, we must suppose the idea of the systematic unity of nature to possess objective validity and necessity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0011","text":"We find this transcendental presupposition lurking in different forms in the principles of philosophers, although they have neither recognized it nor confessed to themselves its presence. That the diversities of individual things do not exclude identity of species, that the various species must be considered as merely different determinations of a few genera, and these again as divisions of still higher races, and so on—that, accordingly, a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical conceptions, in so far as they can be deduced from higher and more general conceptions, must be sought for, is a scholastic maxim or logical principle, without which reason could not be employed by us. For we can infer the particular from the general, only in so far as general properties of things constitute the foundation upon which the particular rest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0012","text":"That the same unity exists in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the well-known scholastic maxim, which forbids us unnecessarily to augment the number of entities or principles (entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda). This maxim asserts that nature herself assists in the establishment of this unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity of phenomena should not deter us from the expectation of discovering beneath this diversity a unity of fundamental properties, of which the aforesaid variety is but a more or less determined form. This unity, although a mere idea, thinkers have found it necessary rather to moderate the desire than to encourage it. It was considered a great step when chemists were able to reduce all salts to two main genera—acids and alkalis; and they regard this difference as itself a mere variety, or different manifestation of one and the same fundamental material. The different kinds of earths (stones and even metals) chemists have endeavoured to reduce to three, and afterwards to two; but still, not content with this advance, they cannot but think that behind these diversities there lurks but one genus—nay, that even salts and earths have a common principle. It might be conjectured that this is merely an economical plan of reason, for the purpose of sparing itself trouble, and an attempt of a purely hypothetical character, which, when successful, gives an appearance of probability to the principle of explanation employed by the reason. But a selfish purpose of this kind is easily to be distinguished from the idea, according to which every one presupposes that this unity is in accordance with the laws of nature, and that reason does not in this case request, but requires, although we are quite unable to determine the proper limits of this unity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0013","text":"If the diversity existing in phenomena—a diversity not of form (for in this they may be similar) but of content—were so great that the subtlest human reason could never by comparison discover in them the least similarity (which is not impossible), in this case the logical law of genera would be without foundation, the conception of a genus, nay, all general conceptions would be impossible, and the faculty of the understanding, the exercise of which is restricted to the world of conceptions, could not exist. The logical principle of genera, accordingly, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I mean objects presented to our senses), presupposes a transcendental principle. In accordance with this principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the variety of phenomena (although we are unable to determine à priori the degree of this homogeneity), because without it no empirical conceptions, and consequently no experience, would be possible.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0014","text":"The logical principle of genera, which demands identity in phenomena, is balanced by another principle—that of species, which requires variety and diversity in things, notwithstanding their accordance in the same genus, and directs the understanding to attend to the one no less than to the other. This principle (of the faculty of distinction) acts as a check upon the reason and reason exhibits in this respect a double and conflicting interest—on the one hand, the interest in the extent (the interest of generality) in relation to genera; on the other, that of the content (the interest of individuality) in relation to the variety of species. In the former case, the understanding cogitates more under its conceptions, in the latter it cogitates more in them. This distinction manifests itself likewise in the habits of thought peculiar to natural philosophers, some of whom—the remarkably speculative heads—may be said to be hostile to heterogeneity in phenomena, and have their eyes always fixed on the unity of genera, while others—with a strong empirical tendency—aim unceasingly at the analysis of phenomena, and almost destroy in us the hope of ever being able to estimate the character of these according to general principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0015","text":"The latter mode of thought is evidently based upon a logical principle, the aim of which is the systematic completeness of all cognitions. This principle authorizes me, beginning at the genus, to descend to the various and diverse contained under it; and in this way extension, as in the former case unity, is assured to the system. For if we merely examine the sphere of the conception which indicates a genus, we cannot discover how far it is possible to proceed in the division of that sphere; just as it is impossible, from the consideration of the space occupied by matter, to determine how far we can proceed in the division of it. Hence every genus must contain different species, and these again different subspecies; and as each of the latter must itself contain a sphere (must be of a certain extent, as a conceptus communis), reason demands that no species or sub-species is to be considered as the lowest possible. For a species or sub-species, being always a conception, which contains only what is common to a number of different things, does not completely determine any individual thing, or relate immediately to it, and must consequently contain other conceptions, that is, other sub-species under it. This law of specification may be thus expressed: entium varietates non temere sunt minuendae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0016","text":"But it is easy to see that this logical law would likewise be without sense or application, were it not based upon a transcendental law of specification, which certainly does not require that the differences existing in phenomena should be infinite in number, for the logical principle, which merely maintains the indeterminateness of the logical sphere of a conception, in relation to its possible division, does not authorize this statement; while it does impose upon the understanding the duty of searching for subspecies to every species, and minor differences in every difference. For, were there no lower conceptions, neither could there be any higher. Now the understanding cognizes only by means of conceptions; consequently, how far soever it may proceed in division, never by mere intuition, but always by lower and lower conceptions. The cognition of phenomena in their complete determination (which is possible only by means of the understanding) requires an unceasingly continued specification of conceptions, and a progression to ever smaller differences, of which abstraction had been made in the conception of the species, and still more in that of the genus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0017","text":"This law of specification cannot be deduced from experience; it can never present us with a principle of so universal an application. Empirical specification very soon stops in its distinction of diversities, and requires the guidance of the transcendental law, as a principle of the reason—a law which imposes on us the necessity of never ceasing in our search for differences, even although these may not present themselves to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds could only be discovered by obeying the anticipatory law of reason, which imposes upon the understanding the task of discovering the differences existing between these earths, and supposes that nature is richer in substances than our senses would indicate. The faculty of the understanding belongs to us just as much under the presupposition of differences in the objects of nature, as under the condition that these objects are homogeneous, because we could not possess conceptions, nor make any use of our understanding, were not the phenomena included under these conceptions in some respects dissimilar, as well as similar, in their character.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0018","text":"Reason thus prepares the sphere of the understanding for the operations of this faculty: 1. By the principle of the homogeneity of the diverse in higher genera; 2. By the principle of the variety of the homogeneous in lower species; and, to complete the systematic unity, it adds, 3. A law of the affinity of all conceptions which prescribes a continuous transition from one species to every other by the gradual increase of diversity. We may term these the principles of the homogeneity, the specification, and the continuity of forms. The latter results from the union of the two former, inasmuch as we regard the systematic connection as complete in thought, in the ascent to higher genera, as well as in the descent to lower species. For all diversities must be related to each other, as they all spring from one highest genus, descending through the different gradations of a more and more extended determination.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0019","text":"We may illustrate the systematic unity produced by the three logical principles in the following manner. Every conception may be regarded as a point, which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has a certain horizon, which may be said to enclose a number of things that may be viewed, so to speak, from that centre. Within this horizon there must be an infinite number of other points, each of which has its own horizon, smaller and more circumscribed; in other words, every species contains sub-species, according to the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points (individuals), which possess no extent. But different horizons or genera, which include under them so many conceptions, may have one common horizon, from which, as from a mid-point, they may be surveyed; and we may proceed thus, till we arrive at the highest genus, or universal and true horizon, which is determined by the highest conception, and which contains under itself all differences and varieties, as genera, species, and subspecies.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0020","text":"To this highest standpoint I am conducted by the law of homogeneity, as to all lower and more variously-determined conceptions by the law of specification. Now as in this way there exists no void in the whole extent of all possible conceptions, and as out of the sphere of these the mind can discover nothing, there arises from the presupposition of the universal horizon above mentioned, and its complete division, the principle: Non datur vacuum formarum. This principle asserts that there are not different primitive and highest genera, which stand isolated, so to speak, from each other, but all the various genera are mere divisions and limitations of one highest and universal genus; and hence follows immediately the principle: Datur continuum formarum. This principle indicates that all differences of species limit each other, and do not admit of transition from one to another by a saltus, but only through smaller degrees of the difference between the one species and the other. In one word, there are no species or sub-species which (in the view of reason) are the nearest possible to each other; intermediate species or sub-species being always possible, the difference of which from each of the former is always smaller than the difference existing between these.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0021","text":"The first law, therefore, directs us to avoid the notion that there exist different primal genera, and enounces the fact of perfect homogeneity; the second imposes a check upon this tendency to unity and prescribes the distinction of sub-species, before proceeding to apply our general conceptions to individuals. The third unites both the former, by enouncing the fact of homogeneity as existing even in the most various diversity, by means of the gradual transition from one species to another. Thus it indicates a relationship between the different branches or species, in so far as they all spring from the same stem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0022","text":"But this logical law of the continuum specierum (formarum logicarum) presupposes a transcendental principle (lex continui in natura), without which the understanding might be led into error, by following the guidance of the former, and thus perhaps pursuing a path contrary to that prescribed by nature. This law must, consequently, be based upon pure transcendental, and not upon empirical, considerations. For, in the latter case, it would come later than the system; whereas it is really itself the parent of all that is systematic in our cognition of nature. These principles are not mere hypotheses employed for the purpose of experimenting upon nature; although when any such connection is discovered, it forms a solid ground for regarding the hypothetical unity as valid in the sphere of nature—and thus they are in this respect not without their use. But we go farther, and maintain that it is manifest that these principles of parsimony in fundamental causes, variety in effects, and affinity in phenomena, are in accordance both with reason and nature, and that they are not mere methods or plans devised for the purpose of assisting us in our observation of the external world.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0023","text":"But it is plain that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, to which no adequate object can be discovered in experience. And this for two reasons. First, because the species in nature are really divided, and hence form quanta discreta; and, if the gradual progression through their affinity were continuous, the intermediate members lying between two given species must be infinite in number, which is impossible. Secondly, because we cannot make any determinate empirical use of this law, inasmuch as it does not present us with any criterion of affinity which could aid us in determining how far we ought to pursue the graduation of differences: it merely contains a general indication that it is our duty to seek for and, if possible, to discover them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0024","text":"When we arrange these principles of systematic unity in the order conformable to their employment in experience, they will stand thus: Variety, Affinity, Unity, each of them, as ideas, being taken in the highest degree of their completeness. Reason presupposes the existence of cognitions of the understanding, which have a direct relation to experience, and aims at the ideal unity of these cognitions—a unity which far transcends all experience or empirical notions. The affinity of the diverse, notwithstanding the differences existing between its parts, has a relation to things, but a still closer one to the mere properties and powers of things. For example, imperfect experience may represent the orbits of the planets as circular. But we discover variations from this course, and we proceed to suppose that the planets revolve in a path which, if not a circle, is of a character very similar to it. That is to say, the movements of those planets which do not form a circle will approximate more or less to the properties of a circle, and probably form an ellipse. The paths of comets exhibit still greater variations, for, so far as our observation extends, they do not return upon their own course in a circle or ellipse. But we proceed to the conjecture that comets describe a parabola, a figure which is closely allied to the ellipse. In fact, a parabola is merely an ellipse, with its longer axis produced to an indefinite extent. Thus these principles conduct us to a unity in the genera of the forms of these orbits, and, proceeding farther, to a unity as regards the cause of the motions of the heavenly bodies—that is, gravitation. But we go on extending our conquests over nature, and endeavour to explain all seeming deviations from these rules, and even make additions to our system which no experience can ever substantiate—for example, the theory, in affinity with that of ellipses, of hyperbolic paths of comets, pursuing which, these bodies leave our solar system and, passing from sun to sun, unite the most distant parts of the infinite universe, which is held together by the same moving power.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","text":"The most remarkable circumstance connected with these principles is that they seem to be transcendental, and, although only containing ideas for the guidance of the empirical exercise of reason, and although this empirical employment stands to these ideas in an asymptotic relation alone (to use a mathematical term), that is, continually approximate, without ever being able to attain to them, they possess, notwithstanding, as à priori synthetical propositions, objective though undetermined validity, and are available as rules for possible experience. In the elaboration of our experience, they may also be employed with great advantage, as heuristic[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-70) principles. A transcendental deduction of them cannot be made; such a deduction being always impossible in the case of ideas, as has been already shown.\n\n[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-70) From the Greek, eurhioko.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","text":"We distinguished, in the Transcendental Analytic, the dynamical principles of the understanding, which are regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which are constitutive principles of intuition. These dynamical laws are, however, constitutive in relation to experience, inasmuch as they render the conceptions without which experience could not exist possible à priori. But the principles of pure reason cannot be constitutive even in regard to empirical conceptions, because no sensuous schema corresponding to them can be discovered, and they cannot therefore have an object in concreto. Now, if I grant that they cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, as constitutive principles, how shall I secure for them employment and objective validity as regulative principles, and in what way can they be so employed?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","text":"The understanding is the object of reason, as sensibility is the object of the understanding. The production of systematic unity in all the empirical operations of the understanding is the proper occupation of reason; just as it is the business of the understanding to connect the various content of phenomena by means of conceptions, and subject them to empirical laws. But the operations of the understanding are, without the schemata of sensibility, undetermined; and, in the same manner, the unity of reason is perfectly undetermined as regards the conditions under which, and the extent to which, the understanding ought to carry the systematic connection of its conceptions. But, although it is impossible to discover in intuition a schema for the complete systematic unity of all the conceptions of the understanding, there must be some analogon of this schema. This analogon is the idea of the maximum of the division and the connection of our cognition in one principle. For we may have a determinate notion of a maximum and an absolutely perfect, all the restrictive conditions which are connected with an indeterminate and various content having been abstracted. Thus the idea of reason is analogous with a sensuous schema, with this difference, that the application of the categories to the schema of reason does not present a cognition of any object (as is the case with the application of the categories to sensuous schemata), but merely provides us with a rule or principle for the systematic unity of the exercise of the understanding. Now, as every principle which imposes upon the exercise of the understanding à priori compliance with the rule of systematic unity also relates, although only in an indirect manner, to an object of experience, the principles of pure reason will also possess objective reality and validity in relation to experience. But they will not aim at determining our knowledge in regard to any empirical object; they will merely indicate the procedure, following which the empirical and determinate exercise of the understanding may be in complete harmony and connection with itself—a result which is produced by its being brought into harmony with the principle of systematic unity, so far as that is possible, and deduced from it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0028","text":"I term all subjective principles, which are not derived from observation of the constitution of an object, but from the interest which Reason has in producing a certain completeness in her cognition of that object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason, which are based solely upon its speculative interest, although they appear to be objective principles.\n\nWhen principles which are really regulative are regarded as constitutive, and employed as objective principles, contradictions must arise; but if they are considered as mere maxims, there is no room for contradictions of any kind, as they then merely indicate the different interests of reason, which occasion differences in the mode of thought. In effect, Reason has only one single interest, and the seeming contradiction existing between her maxims merely indicates a difference in, and a reciprocal limitation of, the methods by which this interest is satisfied.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0029","text":"This reasoner has at heart the interest of diversity—in accordance with the principle of specification; another, the interest of unity—in accordance with the principle of aggregation. Each believes that his judgement rests upon a thorough insight into the subject he is examining, and yet it has been influenced solely by a greater or less degree of adherence to some one of the two principles, neither of which are objective, but originate solely from the interest of reason, and on this account to be termed maxims rather than principles. When I observe intelligent men disputing about the distinctive characteristics of men, animals, or plants, and even of minerals, those on the one side assuming the existence of certain national characteristics, certain well-defined and hereditary distinctions of family, race, and so on, while the other side maintain that nature has endowed all races of men with the same faculties and dispositions, and that all differences are but the result of external and accidental circumstances—I have only to consider for a moment the real nature of the subject of discussion, to arrive at the conclusion that it is a subject far too deep for us to judge of, and that there is little probability of either party being able to speak from a perfect insight into and understanding of the nature of the subject itself. Both have, in reality, been struggling for the twofold interest of reason; the one maintaining the one interest, the other the other. But this difference between the maxims of diversity and unity may easily be reconciled and adjusted; although, so long as they are regarded as objective principles, they must occasion not only contradictions and polemic, but place hinderances in the way of the advancement of truth, until some means is discovered of reconciling these conflicting interests, and bringing reason into union and harmony with itself.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0030","text":"The same is the case with the so-called law discovered by Leibnitz, and supported with remarkable ability by Bonnet—the law of the continuous gradation of created beings, which is nothing more than an inference from the principle of affinity; for observation and study of the order of nature could never present it to the mind as an objective truth. The steps of this ladder, as they appear in experience, are too far apart from each other, and the so-called petty differences between different kinds of animals are in nature commonly so wide separations that no confidence can be placed in such views (particularly when we reflect on the great variety of things, and the ease with which we can discover resemblances), and no faith in the laws which are said to express the aims and purposes of nature. On the other hand, the method of investigating the order of nature in the light of this principle, and the maxim which requires us to regard this order—it being still undetermined how far it extends—as really existing in nature, is beyond doubt a legitimate and excellent principle of reason—a principle which extends farther than any experience or observation of ours and which, without giving us any positive knowledge of anything in the region of experience, guides us to the goal of systematic unity.\n\n_Of the Ultimate End of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason._","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0031","text":"The ideas of pure reason cannot be, of themselves and in their own nature, dialectical; it is from their misemployment alone that fallacies and illusions arise. For they originate in the nature of reason itself, and it is impossible that this supreme tribunal for all the rights and claims of speculation should be itself undeserving of confidence and promotive of error. It is to be expected, therefore, that these ideas have a genuine and legitimate aim. It is true, the mob of sophists raise against reason the cry of inconsistency and contradiction, and affect to despise the government of that faculty, because they cannot understand its constitution, while it is to its beneficial influences alone that they owe the position and the intelligence which enable them to criticize and to blame its procedure.\n\nWe cannot employ an à priori conception with certainty, until we have made a transcendental deduction therefore. The ideas of pure reason do not admit of the same kind of deduction as the categories. But if they are to possess the least objective validity, and to represent anything but mere creations of thought (entia rationis ratiocinantis), a deduction of them must be possible. This deduction will complete the critical task imposed upon pure reason; and it is to this part Of our labours that we now proceed.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","text":"There is a great difference between a thing’s being presented to the mind as an object in an absolute sense, or merely as an ideal object. In the former case I employ my conceptions to determine the object; in the latter case nothing is present to the mind but a mere schema, which does not relate directly to an object, not even in a hypothetical sense, but which is useful only for the purpose of representing other objects to the mind, in a mediate and indirect manner, by means of their relation to the idea in the intellect. Thus I say the conception of a supreme intelligence is a mere idea; that is to say, its objective reality does not consist in the fact that it has an immediate relation to an object (for in this sense we have no means of establishing its objective validity), it is merely a schema constructed according to the necessary conditions of the unity of reason—the schema of a thing in general, which is useful towards the production of the highest degree of systematic unity in the empirical exercise of reason, in which we deduce this or that object of experience from the imaginary object of this idea, as the ground or cause of the said object of experience. In this way, the idea is properly a heuristic, and not an ostensive, conception; it does not give us any information respecting the constitution of an object, it merely indicates how, under the guidance of the idea, we ought to investigate the constitution and the relations of objects in the world of experience. Now, if it can be shown that the three kinds of transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological, and theological), although not relating directly to any object nor determining it, do nevertheless, on the supposition of the existence of an ideal object, produce systematic unity in the laws of the empirical employment of the reason, and extend our empirical cognition, without ever being inconsistent or in opposition with it—it must be a necessary maxim of reason to regulate its procedure according to these ideas. And this forms the transcendental deduction of all speculative ideas, not as constitutive principles of the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of our experience, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of empirical cognition, which is by the aid of these ideas arranged and emended within its own proper limits, to an extent unattainable by the operation of the principles of the understanding alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","text":"I shall make this plainer. Guided by the principles involved in these ideas, we must, in the first place, so connect all the phenomena, actions, and feelings of the mind, as if it were a simple substance, which, endowed with personal identity, possesses a permanent existence (in this life at least), while its states, among which those of the body are to be included as external conditions, are in continual change. Secondly, in cosmology, we must investigate the conditions of all natural phenomena, internal as well as external, as if they belonged to a chain infinite and without any prime or supreme member, while we do not, on this account, deny the existence of intelligible grounds of these phenomena, although we never employ them to explain phenomena, for the simple reason that they are not objects of our cognition. Thirdly, in the sphere of theology, we must regard the whole system of possible experience as forming an absolute, but dependent and sensuously-conditioned unity, and at the same time as based upon a sole, supreme, and all-sufficient ground existing apart from the world itself—a ground which is a self-subsistent, primeval and creative reason, in relation to which we so employ our reason in the field of experience, as if all objects drew their origin from that archetype of all reason. In other words, we ought not to deduce the internal phenomena of the mind from a simple thinking substance, but deduce them from each other under the guidance of the regulative idea of a simple being; we ought not to deduce the phenomena, order, and unity of the universe from a supreme intelligence, but merely draw from this idea of a supremely wise cause the rules which must guide reason in its connection of causes and effects.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0034","text":"Now there is nothing to hinder us from admitting these ideas to possess an objective and hyperbolic existence, except the cosmological ideas, which lead reason into an antinomy: the psychological and theological ideas are not antinomial. They contain no contradiction; and how, then, can any one dispute their objective reality, since he who denies it knows as little about their possibility as we who affirm? And yet, when we wish to admit the existence of a thing, it is not sufficient to convince ourselves that there is no positive obstacle in the way; for it cannot be allowable to regard mere creations of thought, which transcend, though they do not contradict, all our conceptions, as real and determinate objects, solely upon the authority of a speculative reason striving to compass its own aims. They cannot, therefore, be admitted to be real in themselves; they can only possess a comparative reality—that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition. They are to be regarded not as actual things, but as in some measure analogous to them. We abstract from the object of the idea all the conditions which limit the exercise of our understanding, but which, on the other hand, are the sole conditions of our possessing a determinate conception of any given thing. And thus we cogitate a something, of the real nature of which we have not the least conception, but which we represent to ourselves as standing in a relation to the whole system of phenomena, analogous to that in which phenomena stand to each other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0035","text":"By admitting these ideal beings, we do not really extend our cognitions beyond the objects of possible experience; we extend merely the empirical unity of our experience, by the aid of systematic unity, the schema of which is furnished by the idea, which is therefore valid—not as a constitutive, but as a regulative principle. For although we posit a thing corresponding to the idea—a something, an actual existence—we do not on that account aim at the extension of our cognition by means of transcendent conceptions. This existence is purely ideal, and not objective; it is the mere expression of the systematic unity which is to be the guide of reason in the field of experience. There are no attempts made at deciding what the ground of this unity may be, or what the real nature of this imaginary being.\n\nThus the transcendental and only determinate conception of God, which is presented to us by speculative reason, is in the strictest sense deistic. In other words, reason does not assure us of the objective validity of the conception; it merely gives us the idea of something, on which the supreme and necessary unity of all experience is based. This something we cannot, following the analogy of a real substance, cogitate otherwise than as the cause of all things operating in accordance with rational laws, if we regard it as an individual object; although we should rest contented with the idea alone as a regulative principle of reason, and make no attempt at completing the sum of the conditions imposed by thought. This attempt is, indeed, inconsistent with the grand aim of complete systematic unity in the sphere of cognition—a unity to which no bounds are set by reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0036","text":"Hence it happens that, admitting a divine being, I can have no conception of the internal possibility of its perfection, or of the necessity of its existence. The only advantage of this admission is that it enables me to answer all other questions relating to the contingent, and to give reason the most complete satisfaction as regards the unity which it aims at attaining in the world of experience. But I cannot satisfy reason with regard to this hypothesis itself; and this proves that it is not its intelligence and insight into the subject, but its speculative interest alone which induces it to proceed from a point lying far beyond the sphere of our cognition, for the purpose of being able to consider all objects as parts of a systematic whole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","text":"Here a distinction presents itself, in regard to the way in which we may cogitate a presupposition—a distinction which is somewhat subtle, but of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I may have sufficient grounds to admit something, or the existence of something, in a relative point of view (suppositio relativa), without being justified in admitting it in an absolute sense (suppositio absoluta). This distinction is undoubtedly requisite, in the case of a regulative principle, the necessity of which we recognize, though we are ignorant of the source and cause of that necessity, and which we assume to be based upon some ultimate ground, for the purpose of being able to cogitate the universality of the principle in a more determinate way. For example, I cogitate the existence of a being corresponding to a pure transcendental idea. But I cannot admit that this being exists absolutely and in itself, because all of the conceptions by which I can cogitate an object in a determinate manner fall short of assuring me of its existence; nay, the conditions of the objective validity of my conceptions are excluded by the idea—by the very fact of its being an idea. The conceptions of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, have no significance out of the sphere of empirical cognition, and cannot, beyond that sphere, determine any object. They may, accordingly, be employed to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but they are utterly inadequate to explain the possibility of the universe itself considered as a whole; because in this case the ground of explanation must lie out of and beyond the world, and cannot, therefore, be an object of possible experience. Now, I may admit the existence of an incomprehensible being of this nature—the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense; although I have no ground to admit its existence absolutely and in itself. For if an idea (that of a systematic and complete unity, of which I shall presently speak more particularly) lies at the foundation of the most extended empirical employment of reason, and if this idea cannot be adequately represented in concreto, although it is indispensably necessary for the approximation of empirical unity to the highest possible degree—I am not only authorized, but compelled, to realize this idea, that is, to posit a real object corresponding thereto. But I cannot profess to know this object; it is to me merely a something, to which, as the ground of systematic unity in cognition, I attribute such properties as are analogous to the conceptions employed by the understanding in the sphere of experience. Following the analogy of the notions of reality, substance, causality, and necessity, I cogitate a being, which possesses all these attributes in the highest degree; and, as this idea is the offspring of my reason alone, I cogitate this being as self-subsistent reason, and as the cause of the universe operating by means of ideas of the greatest possible harmony and unity. Thus I abstract all conditions that would limit my idea, solely for the purpose of rendering systematic unity possible in the world of empirical diversity, and thus securing the widest possible extension for the exercise of reason in that sphere. This I am enabled to do, by regarding all connections and relations in the world of sense, as if they were the dispositions of a supreme reason, of which our reason is but a faint image. I then proceed to cogitate this Supreme Being by conceptions which have, properly, no meaning or application, except in the world of sense. But as I am authorized to employ the transcendental hypothesis of such a being in a relative respect alone, that is, as the substratum of the greatest possible unity in experience—I may attribute to a being which I regard as distinct from the world, such properties as belong solely to the sphere of sense and experience. For I do not desire, and am not justified in desiring, to cognize this object of my idea, as it exists in itself; for I possess no conceptions sufficient for this task, those of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, losing all significance, and becoming merely the signs of conceptions, without content and without applicability, when I attempt to carry them beyond the limits of the world of sense. I cogitate merely the relation of a perfectly unknown being to the greatest possible systematic unity of experience, solely for the purpose of employing it as the schema of the regulative principle which directs reason in its empirical exercise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0038","text":"It is evident, at the first view, that we cannot presuppose the reality of this transcendental object, by means of the conceptions of reality, substance, causality, and so on, because these conceptions cannot be applied to anything that is distinct from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of a Supreme Being or cause is purely relative; it is cogitated only in behalf of the systematic unity of experience; such a being is but a something, of whose existence in itself we have not the least conception. Thus, too, it becomes sufficiently manifest why we required the idea of a necessary being in relation to objects given by sense, although we can never have the least conception of this being, or of its absolute necessity.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","text":"And now we can clearly perceive the result of our transcendental dialectic, and the proper aim of the ideas of pure reason—which become dialectical solely from misunderstanding and inconsiderateness. Pure reason is, in fact, occupied with itself, and not with any object. Objects are not presented to it to be embraced in the unity of an empirical conception; it is only the cognitions of the understanding that are presented to it, for the purpose of receiving the unity of a rational conception, that is, of being connected according to a principle. The unity of reason is the unity of system; and this systematic unity is not an objective principle, extending its dominion over objects, but a subjective maxim, extending its authority over the empirical cognition of objects. The systematic connection which reason gives to the empirical employment of the understanding not only advances the extension of that employment, but ensures its correctness, and thus the principle of a systematic unity of this nature is also objective, although only in an indefinite respect (principium vagum). It is not, however, a constitutive principle, determining an object to which it directly relates; it is merely a regulative principle or maxim, advancing and strengthening the empirical exercise of reason, by the opening up of new paths of which the understanding is ignorant, while it never conflicts with the laws of its exercise in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0040","text":"But reason cannot cogitate this systematic unity, without at the same time cogitating an object of the idea—an object that cannot be presented in any experience, which contains no concrete example of a complete systematic unity. This being (ens rationis ratiocinatae) is therefore a mere idea and is not assumed to be a thing which is real absolutely and in itself. On the contrary, it forms merely the problematical foundation of the connection which the mind introduces among the phenomena of the sensuous world. We look upon this connection, in the light of the above-mentioned idea, as if it drew its origin from the supposed being which corresponds to the idea. And yet all we aim at is the possession of this idea as a secure foundation for the systematic unity of experience—a unity indispensable to reason, advantageous to the understanding, and promotive of the interests of empirical cognition.\n\nWe mistake the true meaning of this idea when we regard it as an enouncement, or even as a hypothetical declaration of the existence of a real thing, which we are to regard as the origin or ground of a systematic constitution of the universe. On the contrary, it is left completely undetermined what the nature or properties of this so-called ground may be. The idea is merely to be adopted as a point of view, from which this unity, so essential to reason and so beneficial to the understanding, may be regarded as radiating. In one word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of a regulative principle, by means of which Reason, so far as in her lies, extends the dominion of systematic unity over the whole sphere of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0041","text":"The first object of an idea of this kind is the ego, considered merely as a thinking nature or soul. If I wish to investigate the properties of a thinking being, I must interrogate experience. But I find that I can apply none of the categories to this object, the schema of these categories, which is the condition of their application, being given only in sensuous intuition. But I cannot thus attain to the cognition of a systematic unity of all the phenomena of the internal sense. Instead, therefore, of an empirical conception of what the soul really is, reason takes the conception of the empirical unity of all thought, and, by cogitating this unity as unconditioned and primitive, constructs the rational conception or idea of a simple substance which is in itself unchangeable, possessing personal identity, and in connection with other real things external to it; in one word, it constructs the idea of a simple self-subsistent intelligence. But the real aim of reason in this procedure is the attainment of principles of systematic unity for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul. That is, reason desires to be able to represent all the determinations of the internal sense as existing in one subject, all powers as deduced from one fundamental power, all changes as mere varieties in the condition of a being which is permanent and always the same, and all phenomena in space as entirely different in their nature from the procedure of thought. Essential simplicity (with the other attributes predicated of the ego) is regarded as the mere schema of this regulative principle; it is not assumed that it is the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these properties may rest upon quite different grounds, of which we are completely ignorant; just as the above predicates could not give us any knowledge of the soul as it is in itself, even if we regarded them as valid in respect of it, inasmuch as they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented in concreto. Nothing but good can result from a psychological idea of this kind, if we only take proper care not to consider it as more than an idea; that is, if we regard it as valid merely in relation to the employment of reason, in the sphere of the phenomena of the soul. Under the guidance of this idea, or principle, no empirical laws of corporeal phenomena are called in to explain that which is a phenomenon of the internal sense alone; no windy hypotheses of the generation, annihilation, and palingenesis of souls are admitted. Thus the consideration of this object of the internal sense is kept pure, and unmixed with heterogeneous elements; while the investigation of reason aims at reducing all the grounds of explanation employed in this sphere of knowledge to a single principle. All this is best effected, nay, cannot be effected otherwise than by means of such a schema, which requires us to regard this ideal thing as an actual existence. The psychological idea is, therefore, meaningless and inapplicable, except as the schema of a regulative conception. For, if I ask whether the soul is not really of a spiritual nature—it is a question which has no meaning. From such a conception has been abstracted, not merely all corporeal nature, but all nature, that is, all the predicates of a possible experience; and consequently, all the conditions which enable us to cogitate an object to this conception have disappeared. But, if these conditions are absent, it is evident that the conception is meaningless.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0042","text":"The second regulative idea of speculative reason is the conception of the universe. For nature is properly the only object presented to us, in regard to which reason requires regulative principles. Nature is twofold—thinking and corporeal nature. To cogitate the latter in regard to its internal possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, no idea is required—no representation which transcends experience. In this sphere, therefore, an idea is impossible, sensuous intuition being our only guide; while, in the sphere of psychology, we require the fundamental idea (I), which contains à priori a certain form of thought namely, the unity of the ego. Pure reason has, therefore, nothing left but nature in general, and the completeness of conditions in nature in accordance with some principle. The absolute totality of the series of these conditions is an idea, which can never be fully realized in the empirical exercise of reason, while it is serviceable as a rule for the procedure of reason in relation to that totality. It requires us, in the explanation of given phenomena (in the regress or ascent in the series), to proceed as if the series were infinite in itself, that is, were prolonged in indefinitum; while on the other hand, where reason is regarded as itself the determining cause (in the region of freedom), we are required to proceed as if we had not before us an object of sense, but of the pure understanding. In this latter case, the conditions do not exist in the series of phenomena, but may be placed quite out of and beyond it, and the series of conditions may be regarded as if it had an absolute beginning from an intelligible cause. All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and not constitutive; and that their aim is not to realize an actual totality in such series. The full discussion of this subject will be found in its proper place in the chapter on the antinomy of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0043","text":"The third idea of pure reason, containing the hypothesis of a being which is valid merely as a relative hypothesis, is that of the one and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, in other words, the idea of God. We have not the slightest ground absolutely to admit the existence of an object corresponding to this idea; for what can empower or authorize us to affirm the existence of a being of the highest perfection—a being whose existence is absolutely necessary—merely because we possess the conception of such a being? The answer is: It is the existence of the world which renders this hypothesis necessary. But this answer makes it perfectly evident that the idea of this being, like all other speculative ideas, is essentially nothing more than a demand upon reason that it shall regulate the connection which it and its subordinate faculties introduce into the phenomena of the world by principles of systematic unity and, consequently, that it shall regard all phenomena as originating from one all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is plain that the only aim of reason in this procedure is the establishment of its own formal rule for the extension of its dominion in the world of experience; that it does not aim at an extension of its cognition beyond the limits of experience; and that, consequently, this idea does not contain any constitutive principle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0044","text":"The highest formal unity, which is based upon ideas alone, is the unity of all things—a unity in accordance with an aim or purpose; and the speculative interest of reason renders it necessary to regard all order in the world as if it originated from the intention and design of a supreme reason. This principle unfolds to the view of reason in the sphere of experience new and enlarged prospects, and invites it to connect the phenomena of the world according to teleological laws, and in this way to attain to the highest possible degree of systematic unity. The hypothesis of a supreme intelligence, as the sole cause of the universe—an intelligence which has for us no more than an ideal existence—is accordingly always of the greatest service to reason. Thus, if we presuppose, in relation to the figure of the earth (which is round, but somewhat flattened at the poles),[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-71) or that of mountains or seas, wise designs on the part of an author of the universe, we cannot fail to make, by the light of this supposition, a great number of interesting discoveries. If we keep to this hypothesis, as a principle which is purely regulative, even error cannot be very detrimental. For, in this case, error can have no more serious consequences than that, where we expected to discover a teleological connection (nexus finalis), only a mechanical or physical connection appears. In such a case, we merely fail to find the additional form of unity we expected, but we do not lose the rational unity which the mind requires in its procedure in experience. But even a miscarriage of this sort cannot affect the law in its general and teleological relations. For although we may convict an anatomist of an error, when he connects the limb of some animal with a certain purpose, it is quite impossible to prove in a single case that any arrangement of nature, be it what it may, is entirely without aim or design. And thus medical physiology, by the aid of a principle presented to it by pure reason, extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the different parts of an organized body so far that it may be asserted with the utmost confidence, and with the approbation of all reflecting men, that every organ or bodily part of an animal has its use and answers a certain design. Now, this is a supposition which, if regarded as of a constitutive character, goes much farther than any experience or observation of ours can justify. Hence it is evident that it is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, which aims at the highest degree of systematic unity, by the aid of the idea of a causality according to design in a supreme cause—a cause which it regards as the highest intelligence.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0045","text":"[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-71) The advantages which a circular form, in the case of the earth, has over every other, are well known. But few are aware that the slight flattening at the poles, which gives it the figure of a spheroid, is the only cause which prevents the elevations of continents or even of mountains, perhaps thrown up by some internal convulsion, from continually altering the position of the axis of the earth—and that to some considerable degree in a short time. The great protuberance of the earth under the Equator serves to overbalance the impetus of all other masses of earth, and thus to preserve the axis of the earth, so far as we can observe, in its present position. And yet this wise arrangement has been unthinkingly explained from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass.\n\nIf, however, we neglect this restriction of the idea to a purely regulative influence, reason is betrayed into numerous errors. For it has then left the ground of experience, in which alone are to be found the criteria of truth, and has ventured into the region of the incomprehensible and unsearchable, on the heights of which it loses its power and collectedness, because it has completely severed its connection with experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","text":"The first error which arises from our employing the idea of a Supreme Being as a constitutive (in repugnance to the very nature of an idea), and not as a regulative principle, is the error of inactive reason (ignava ratio).[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-72) We may so term every principle which requires us to regard our investigations of nature as absolutely complete, and allows reason to cease its inquiries, as if it had fully executed its task. Thus the psychological idea of the ego, when employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul, and for the extension of our knowledge regarding this subject beyond the limits of experience—even to the condition of the soul after death—is convenient enough for the purposes of pure reason, but detrimental and even ruinous to its interests in the sphere of nature and experience. The dogmatizing spiritualist explains the unchanging unity of our personality through all changes of condition from the unity of a thinking substance, the interest which we take in things and events that can happen only after our death, from a consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so on. Thus he dispenses with all empirical investigations into the cause of these internal phenomena, and with all possible explanations of them upon purely natural grounds; while, at the dictation of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of cognition in experience, greatly to his own ease and convenience, but to the sacrifice of all, genuine insight and intelligence. These prejudicial consequences become still more evident, in the case of the dogmatical treatment of our idea of a Supreme Intelligence, and the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely based upon it. For, in this case, the aims which we observe in nature, and often those which we merely fancy to exist, make the investigation of causes a very easy task, by directing us to refer such and such phenomena immediately to the unsearchable will and counsel of the Supreme Wisdom, while we ought to investigate their causes in the general laws of the mechanism of matter. We are thus recommended to consider the labour of reason as ended, when we have merely dispensed with its employment, which is guided surely and safely only by the order of nature and the series of changes in the world—which are arranged according to immanent and general laws. This error may be avoided, if we do not merely consider from the view-point of final aims certain parts of nature, such as the division and structure of a continent, the constitution and direction of certain mountain-chains, or even the organization existing in the vegetable and animal kingdoms, but look upon this systematic unity of nature in a perfectly general way, in relation to the idea of a Supreme Intelligence. If we pursue this advice, we lay as a foundation for all investigation the conformity to aims of all phenomena of nature in accordance with universal laws, for which no particular arrangement of nature is exempt, but only cognized by us with more or less difficulty; and we possess a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection, which we do not attempt to anticipate or predetermine. All that we do, and ought to do, is to follow out the physico-mechanical connection in nature according to general laws, with the hope of discovering, sooner or later, the teleological connection also. Thus, and thus only, can the principle of final unity aid in the extension of the employment of reason in the sphere of experience, without being in any case detrimental to its interests.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0047","text":"[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-72) This was the term applied by the old dialecticians to a sophistical argument, which ran thus: If it is your fate to die of this disease, you will die, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this mode of reasoning has received this appellation, because, if followed, it puts an end to the employment of reason in the affairs of life. For a similar reason, I have applied this designation to the sophistical argument of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","text":"The second error which arises from the misconception of the principle of systematic unity is that of perverted reason (perversa ratio, usteron roteron rationis). The idea of systematic unity is available as a regulative principle in the connection of phenomena according to general natural laws; and, how far soever we have to travel upon the path of experience to discover some fact or event, this idea requires us to believe that we have approached all the more nearly to the completion of its use in the sphere of nature, although that completion can never be attained. But this error reverses the procedure of reason. We begin by hypostatizing the principle of systematic unity, and by giving an anthropomorphic determination to the conception of a Supreme Intelligence, and then proceed forcibly to impose aims upon nature. Thus not only does teleology, which ought to aid in the completion of unity in accordance with general laws, operate to the destruction of its influence, but it hinders reason from attaining its proper aim, that is, the proof, upon natural grounds, of the existence of a supreme intelligent cause. For, if we cannot presuppose supreme finality in nature à priori, that is, as essentially belonging to nature, how can we be directed to endeavour to discover this unity and, rising gradually through its different degrees, to approach the supreme perfection of an author of all—a perfection which is absolutely necessary, and therefore cognizable à priori? The regulative principle directs us to presuppose systematic unity absolutely and, consequently, as following from the essential nature of things—but only as a unity of nature, not merely cognized empirically, but presupposed à priori, although only in an indeterminate manner. But if I insist on basing nature upon the foundation of a supreme ordaining Being, the unity of nature is in effect lost. For, in this case, it is quite foreign and unessential to the nature of things, and cannot be cognized from the general laws of nature. And thus arises a vicious circular argument, what ought to have been proved having been presupposed.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0049","text":"To take the regulative principle of systematic unity in nature for a constitutive principle, and to hypostatize and make a cause out of that which is properly the ideal ground of the consistent and harmonious exercise of reason, involves reason in inextricable embarrassments. The investigation of nature pursues its own path under the guidance of the chain of natural causes, in accordance with the general laws of nature, and ever follows the light of the idea of an author of the universe—not for the purpose of deducing the finality, which it constantly pursues, from this Supreme Being, but to attain to the cognition of his existence from the finality which it seeks in the existence of the phenomena of nature, and, if possible, in that of all things to cognize this being, consequently, as absolutely necessary. Whether this latter purpose succeed or not, the idea is and must always be a true one, and its employment, when merely regulative, must always be accompanied by truthful and beneficial results.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0050","text":"Complete unity, in conformity with aims, constitutes absolute perfection. But if we do not find this unity in the nature of the things which go to constitute the world of experience, that is, of objective cognition, consequently in the universal and necessary laws of nature, how can we infer from this unity the idea of the supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of a primal being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic unity, and consequently teleological unity, constitutes the very foundation of the possibility of the most extended employment of human reason. The idea of unity is therefore essentially and indissolubly connected with the nature of our reason. This idea is a legislative one; and hence it is very natural that we should assume the existence of a legislative reason corresponding to it, from which the systematic unity of nature—the object of the operations of reason—must be derived.\n\nIn the course of our discussion of the antinomies, we stated that it is always possible to answer all the questions which pure reason may raise; and that the plea of the limited nature of our cognition, which is unavoidable and proper in many questions regarding natural phenomena, cannot in this case be admitted, because the questions raised do not relate to the nature of things, but are necessarily originated by the nature of reason itself, and relate to its own internal constitution. We can now establish this assertion, which at first sight appeared so rash, in relation to the two questions in which reason takes the greatest interest, and thus complete our discussion of the dialectic of pure reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0051","text":"If, then, the question is asked, in relation to transcendental theology,[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-73) first, whether there is anything distinct from the world, which contains the ground of cosmical order and connection according to general laws? The answer is: Certainly. For the world is a sum of phenomena; there must, therefore, be some transcendental basis of these phenomena, that is, a basis cogitable by the pure understanding alone. If, secondly, the question is asked whether this being is substance, whether it is of the greatest reality, whether it is necessary, and so forth? I answer that this question is utterly without meaning. For all the categories which aid me in forming a conception of an object cannot be employed except in the world of sense, and are without meaning when not applied to objects of actual or possible experience. Out of this sphere, they are not properly conceptions, but the mere marks or indices of conceptions, which we may admit, although they cannot, without the help of experience, help us to understand any subject or thing. If, thirdly, the question is whether we may not cogitate this being, which is distinct from the world, in analogy with the objects of experience? The answer is: Undoubtedly, but only as an ideal, and not as a real object. That is, we must cogitate it only as an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order, and finality of the world—a unity which reason must employ as the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Nay, more, we may admit into the idea certain anthropomorphic elements, which are promotive of the interests of this regulative principle. For it is no more than an idea, which does not relate directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, by means, however, of a schema of this unity—the schema of a Supreme Intelligence, who is the wisely-designing author of the universe. What this basis of cosmical unity may be in itself, we know not—we cannot discover from the idea; we merely know how we ought to employ the idea of this unity, in relation to the systematic operation of reason in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0052","text":"[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-73) After what has been said of the psychological idea of the ego and its proper employment as a regulative principle of the operations of reason, I need not enter into details regarding the transcendental illusion by which the systematic unity of all the various phenomena of the internal sense is hypostatized. The procedure is in this case very similar to that which has been discussed in our remarks on the theological ideal.\n\nBut, it will be asked again, can we on these grounds, admit the existence of a wise and omnipotent author of the world? Without doubt; and not only so, but we must assume the existence of such a being. But do we thus extend the limits of our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have merely presupposed a something, of which we have no conception, which we do not know as it is in itself; but, in relation to the systematic disposition of the universe, which we must presuppose in all our observation of nature, we have cogitated this unknown being in analogy with an intelligent existence (an empirical conception), that is to say, we have endowed it with those attributes, which, judging from the nature of our own reason, may contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore valid only relatively to the employment in experience of our reason. But if we attribute to it absolute and objective validity, we overlook the fact that it is merely an ideal being that we cogitate; and, by setting out from a basis which is not determinable by considerations drawn from experience, we place ourselves in a position which incapacitates us from applying this principle to the empirical employment of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0053","text":"But, it will be asked further, can I make any use of this conception and hypothesis in my investigations into the world and nature? Yes, for this very purpose was the idea established by reason as a fundamental basis. But may I regard certain arrangements, which seemed to have been made in conformity with some fixed aim, as the arrangements of design, and look upon them as proceeding from the divine will, with the intervention, however, of certain other particular arrangements disposed to that end? Yes, you may do so; but at the same time you must regard it as indifferent, whether it is asserted that divine wisdom has disposed all things in conformity with his highest aims, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative principle in the investigation of nature, and at the same time a principle of the systematic unity of nature according to general laws, even in those cases where we are unable to discover that unity. In other words, it must be perfectly indifferent to you whether you say, when you have discovered this unity: God has wisely willed it so; or: Nature has wisely arranged this. For it was nothing but the systematic unity, which reason requires as a basis for the investigation of nature, that justified you in accepting the idea of a supreme intelligence as a schema for a regulative principle; and, the farther you advance in the discovery of design and finality, the more certain the validity of your idea. But, as the whole aim of this regulative principle was the discovery of a necessary and systematic unity in nature, we have, in so far as we attain this, to attribute our success to the idea of a Supreme Being; while, at the same time, we cannot, without involving ourselves in contradictions, overlook the general laws of nature, as it was in reference to them alone that this idea was employed. We cannot, I say, overlook the general laws of nature, and regard this conformity to aims observable in nature as contingent or hyperphysical in its origin; inasmuch as there is no ground which can justify us in the admission of a being with such properties distinct from and above nature. All that we are authorized to assert is that this idea may be employed as a principle, and that the properties of the being which is assumed to correspond to it may be regarded as systematically connected in analogy with the causal determination of phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0054","text":"For the same reasons we are justified in introducing into the idea of the supreme cause other anthropomorphic elements (for without these we could not predicate anything of it); we may regard it as allowable to cogitate this cause as a being with understanding, the feelings of pleasure and displeasure, and faculties of desire and will corresponding to these. At the same time, we may attribute to this being infinite perfection—a perfection which necessarily transcends that which our knowledge of the order and design in the world authorize us to predicate of it. For the regulative law of systematic unity requires us to study nature on the supposition that systematic and final unity in infinitum is everywhere discoverable, even in the highest diversity. For, although we may discover little of this cosmical perfection, it belongs to the legislative prerogative of reason to require us always to seek for and to expect it; while it must always be beneficial to institute all inquiries into nature in accordance with this principle. But it is evident that, by this idea of a supreme author of all, which I place as the foundation of all inquiries into nature, I do not mean to assert the existence of such a being, or that I have any knowledge of its existence; and, consequently, I do not really deduce anything from the existence of this being, but merely from its idea, that is to say, from the nature of things in this world, in accordance with this idea. A certain dim consciousness of the true use of this idea seems to have dictated to the philosophers of all times the moderate language used by them regarding the cause of the world. We find them employing the expressions wisdom and care of nature, and divine wisdom, as synonymous—nay, in purely speculative discussions, preferring the former, because it does not carry the appearance of greater pretensions than such as we are entitled to make, and at the same time directs reason to its proper field of action—nature and her phenomena.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0055","text":"Thus, pure reason, which at first seemed to promise us nothing less than the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of experience, is found, when thoroughly examined, to contain nothing but regulative principles, the virtue and function of which is to introduce into our cognition a higher degree of unity than the understanding could of itself. These principles, by placing the goal of all our struggles at so great a distance, realize for us the most thorough connection between the different parts of our cognition, and the highest degree of systematic unity. But, on the other hand, if misunderstood and employed as constitutive principles of transcendent cognition, they become the parents of illusions and contradictions, while pretending to introduce us to new regions of knowledge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","text":"Thus all human cognition begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to conceptions, and ends with ideas. Although it possesses, in relation to all three elements, à priori sources of cognition, which seemed to transcend the limits of all experience, a thoroughgoing criticism demonstrates that speculative reason can never, by the aid of these elements, pass the bounds of possible experience, and that the proper destination of this highest faculty of cognition is to employ all methods, and all the principles of these methods, for the purpose of penetrating into the innermost secrets of nature, by the aid of the principles of unity (among all kinds of which teleological unity is the highest), while it ought not to attempt to soar above the sphere of experience, beyond which there lies nought for us but the void inane. The critical examination, in our Transcendental Analytic, of all the propositions which professed to extend cognition beyond the sphere of experience, completely demonstrated that they can only conduct us to a possible experience. If we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract theorems, if we were not allured by specious and inviting prospects to escape from the constraining power of their evidence, we might spare ourselves the laborious examination of all the dialectical arguments which a transcendent reason adduces in support of its pretensions; for we should know with the most complete certainty that, however honest such professions might be, they are null and valueless, because they relate to a kind of knowledge to which no man can by any possibility attain. But, as there is no end to discussion, if we cannot discover the true cause of the illusions by which even the wisest are deceived, and as the analysis of all our transcendent cognition into its elements is of itself of no slight value as a psychological study, while it is a duty incumbent on every philosopher—it was found necessary to investigate the dialectical procedure of reason in its primary sources. And as the inferences of which this dialectic is the parent are not only deceitful, but naturally possess a profound interest for humanity, it was advisable at the same time, to give a full account of the momenta of this dialectical procedure, and to deposit it in the archives of human reason, as a warning to all future metaphysicians to avoid these causes of speculative error.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bb1ac9495beac35a95112d6aca11b6b40d11941c773ff5cdac6e19d9db40b837","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Appendix","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","order":83,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_ITDM","text":"## II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method\n\nIf we regard the sum of the cognition of pure speculative reason as an edifice, the idea of which, at least, exists in the human mind, it may be said that we have in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements examined the materials and determined to what edifice these belong, and what its height and stability. We have found, indeed, that, although we had purposed to build for ourselves a tower which should reach to Heaven, the supply of materials sufficed merely for a habitation, which was spacious enough for all terrestrial purposes, and high enough to enable us to survey the level plain of experience, but that the bold undertaking designed necessarily failed for want of materials—not to mention the confusion of tongues, which gave rise to endless disputes among the labourers on the plan of the edifice, and at last scattered them over all the world, each to erect a separate building for himself, according to his own plans and his own inclinations. Our present task relates not to the materials, but to the plan of an edifice; and, as we have had sufficient warning not to venture blindly upon a design which may be found to transcend our natural powers, while, at the same time, we cannot give up the intention of erecting a secure abode for the mind, we must proportion our design to the material which is presented to us, and which is, at the same time, sufficient for all our wants.\n\nI understand, then, by the transcendental doctrine of method, the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. We shall accordingly have to treat of the discipline, the canon, the architectonic, and, finally, the history of pure reason. This part of our Critique will accomplish, from the transcendental point of view, what has been usually attempted, but miserably executed, under the name of practical logic. It has been badly executed, I say, because general logic, not being limited to any particular kind of cognition (not even to the pure cognition of the understanding) nor to any particular objects, it cannot, without borrowing from other sciences, do more than present merely the titles or signs of possible methods and the technical expressions, which are employed in the systematic parts of all sciences; and thus the pupil is made acquainted with names, the meaning and application of which he is to learn only at some future time.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","text":"## II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method\n\nIf we regard the sum of the cognition of pure speculative reason as an edifice, the idea of which, at least, exists in the human mind, it may be said that we have in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements examined the materials and determined to what edifice these belong, and what its height and stability. We have found, indeed, that, although we had purposed to build for ourselves a tower which should reach to Heaven, the supply of materials sufficed merely for a habitation, which was spacious enough for all terrestrial purposes, and high enough to enable us to survey the level plain of experience, but that the bold undertaking designed necessarily failed for want of materials—not to mention the confusion of tongues, which gave rise to endless disputes among the labourers on the plan of the edifice, and at last scattered them over all the world, each to erect a separate building for himself, according to his own plans and his own inclinations. Our present task relates not to the materials, but to the plan of an edifice; and, as we have had sufficient warning not to venture blindly upon a design which may be found to transcend our natural powers, while, at the same time, we cannot give up the intention of erecting a secure abode for the mind, we must proportion our design to the material which is presented to us, and which is, at the same time, sufficient for all our wants.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","text":"I understand, then, by the transcendental doctrine of method, the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. We shall accordingly have to treat of the discipline, the canon, the architectonic, and, finally, the history of pure reason. This part of our Critique will accomplish, from the transcendental point of view, what has been usually attempted, but miserably executed, under the name of practical logic. It has been badly executed, I say, because general logic, not being limited to any particular kind of cognition (not even to the pure cognition of the understanding) nor to any particular objects, it cannot, without borrowing from other sciences, do more than present merely the titles or signs of possible methods and the technical expressions, which are employed in the systematic parts of all sciences; and thus the pupil is made acquainted with names, the meaning and application of which he is to learn only at some future time.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"55426f84d09a92ac93d101403535045e517ac91e7d46dc1b09c1a4620bb9cd45","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","order":84,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CIDP","text":"### Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason\n\nNegative judgements—those which are so not merely as regards their logical form, but in respect of their content—are not commonly held in especial respect. They are, on the contrary, regarded as jealous enemies of our insatiable desire for knowledge; and it almost requires an apology to induce us to tolerate, much less to prize and to respect them.\n\nAll propositions, indeed, may be logically expressed in a negative form; but, in relation to the content of our cognition, the peculiar province of negative judgements is solely to prevent error. For this reason, too, negative propositions, which are framed for the purpose of correcting false cognitions where error is absolutely impossible, are undoubtedly true, but inane and senseless; that is, they are in reality purposeless and, for this reason, often very ridiculous. Such is the proposition of the schoolman that Alexander could not have subdued any countries without an army.\n\nBut where the limits of our possible cognition are very much contracted, the attraction to new fields of knowledge great, the illusions to which the mind is subject of the most deceptive character, and the evil consequences of error of no inconsiderable magnitude—the negative element in knowledge, which is useful only to guard us against error, is of far more importance than much of that positive instruction which makes additions to the sum of our knowledge. The restraint which is employed to repress, and finally to extirpate the constant inclination to depart from certain rules, is termed discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which aims at the formation of a certain degree of skill, without attempting to repress or to destroy any other mental power, already existing. In the cultivation of a talent, which has given evidence of an impulse towards self-development, discipline takes a negative,[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-74) culture and doctrine a positive, part.\n\n[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-74) I am well aware that, in the language of the schools, the term discipline is usually employed as synonymous with instruction. But there are so many cases in which it is necessary to distinguish the notion of the former, as a course of corrective training, from that of the latter, as the communication of knowledge, and the nature of things itself demands the appropriation of the most suitable expressions for this distinction, that it is my desire that the former terms should never be employed in any other than a negative signification.\n\nThat natural dispositions and talents (such as imagination and wit), which ask a free and unlimited development, require in many respects the corrective influence of discipline, every one will readily grant. But it may well appear strange that reason, whose proper duty it is to prescribe rules of discipline to all the other powers of the mind, should itself require this corrective. It has, in fact, hitherto escaped this humiliation, only because, in presence of its magnificent pretensions and high position, no one could readily suspect it to be capable of substituting fancies for conceptions, and words for things.\n\nReason, when employed in the field of experience, does not stand in need of criticism, because its principles are subjected to the continual test of empirical observations. Nor is criticism requisite in the sphere of mathematics, where the conceptions of reason must always be presented in concreto in pure intuition, and baseless or arbitrary assertions are discovered without difficulty. But where reason is not held in a plain track by the influence of empirical or of pure intuition, that is, when it is employed in the transcendental sphere of pure conceptions, it stands in great need of discipline, to restrain its propensity to overstep the limits of possible experience and to keep it from wandering into error. In fact, the utility of the philosophy of pure reason is entirely of this negative character. Particular errors may be corrected by particular animadversions, and the causes of these errors may be eradicated by criticism. But where we find, as in the case of pure reason, a complete system of illusions and fallacies, closely connected with each other and depending upon grand general principles, there seems to be required a peculiar and negative code of mental legislation, which, under the denomination of a discipline, and founded upon the nature of reason and the objects of its exercise, shall constitute a system of thorough examination and testing, which no fallacy will be able to withstand or escape from, under whatever disguise or concealment it may lurk.\n\nBut the reader must remark that, in this the second division of our transcendental Critique the discipline of pure reason is not directed to the content, but to the method of the cognition of pure reason. The former task has been completed in the doctrine of elements. But there is so much similarity in the mode of employing the faculty of reason, whatever be the object to which it is applied, while, at the same time, its employment in the transcendental sphere is so essentially different in kind from every other, that, without the warning negative influence of a discipline specially directed to that end, the errors are unavoidable which spring from the unskillful employment of the methods which are originated by reason but which are out of place in this sphere.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason\n\nNegative judgements—those which are so not merely as regards their logical form, but in respect of their content—are not commonly held in especial respect. They are, on the contrary, regarded as jealous enemies of our insatiable desire for knowledge; and it almost requires an apology to induce us to tolerate, much less to prize and to respect them.\n\nAll propositions, indeed, may be logically expressed in a negative form; but, in relation to the content of our cognition, the peculiar province of negative judgements is solely to prevent error. For this reason, too, negative propositions, which are framed for the purpose of correcting false cognitions where error is absolutely impossible, are undoubtedly true, but inane and senseless; that is, they are in reality purposeless and, for this reason, often very ridiculous. Such is the proposition of the schoolman that Alexander could not have subdued any countries without an army.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","text":"But where the limits of our possible cognition are very much contracted, the attraction to new fields of knowledge great, the illusions to which the mind is subject of the most deceptive character, and the evil consequences of error of no inconsiderable magnitude—the negative element in knowledge, which is useful only to guard us against error, is of far more importance than much of that positive instruction which makes additions to the sum of our knowledge. The restraint which is employed to repress, and finally to extirpate the constant inclination to depart from certain rules, is termed discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which aims at the formation of a certain degree of skill, without attempting to repress or to destroy any other mental power, already existing. In the cultivation of a talent, which has given evidence of an impulse towards self-development, discipline takes a negative,[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-74) culture and doctrine a positive, part.\n\n[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-74) I am well aware that, in the language of the schools, the term discipline is usually employed as synonymous with instruction. But there are so many cases in which it is necessary to distinguish the notion of the former, as a course of corrective training, from that of the latter, as the communication of knowledge, and the nature of things itself demands the appropriation of the most suitable expressions for this distinction, that it is my desire that the former terms should never be employed in any other than a negative signification.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","text":"That natural dispositions and talents (such as imagination and wit), which ask a free and unlimited development, require in many respects the corrective influence of discipline, every one will readily grant. But it may well appear strange that reason, whose proper duty it is to prescribe rules of discipline to all the other powers of the mind, should itself require this corrective. It has, in fact, hitherto escaped this humiliation, only because, in presence of its magnificent pretensions and high position, no one could readily suspect it to be capable of substituting fancies for conceptions, and words for things.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","text":"Reason, when employed in the field of experience, does not stand in need of criticism, because its principles are subjected to the continual test of empirical observations. Nor is criticism requisite in the sphere of mathematics, where the conceptions of reason must always be presented in concreto in pure intuition, and baseless or arbitrary assertions are discovered without difficulty. But where reason is not held in a plain track by the influence of empirical or of pure intuition, that is, when it is employed in the transcendental sphere of pure conceptions, it stands in great need of discipline, to restrain its propensity to overstep the limits of possible experience and to keep it from wandering into error. In fact, the utility of the philosophy of pure reason is entirely of this negative character. Particular errors may be corrected by particular animadversions, and the causes of these errors may be eradicated by criticism. But where we find, as in the case of pure reason, a complete system of illusions and fallacies, closely connected with each other and depending upon grand general principles, there seems to be required a peculiar and negative code of mental legislation, which, under the denomination of a discipline, and founded upon the nature of reason and the objects of its exercise, shall constitute a system of thorough examination and testing, which no fallacy will be able to withstand or escape from, under whatever disguise or concealment it may lurk.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","text":"But the reader must remark that, in this the second division of our transcendental Critique the discipline of pure reason is not directed to the content, but to the method of the cognition of pure reason. The former task has been completed in the doctrine of elements. But there is so much similarity in the mode of employing the faculty of reason, whatever be the object to which it is applied, while, at the same time, its employment in the transcendental sphere is so essentially different in kind from every other, that, without the warning negative influence of a discipline specially directed to that end, the errors are unavoidable which spring from the unskillful employment of the methods which are originated by reason but which are out of place in this sphere.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"79d7933a297fd7b07c86b875148a9a5564398670102d0284a4d0545524fdb8cb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","order":85,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIDP","text":"### Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism\n\nThe science of mathematics presents the most brilliant example of the extension of the sphere of pure reason without the aid of experience. Examples are always contagious; and they exert an especial influence on the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other case as fell to its lot in one fortunate instance. Hence pure reason hopes to be able to extend its empire in the transcendental sphere with equal success and security, especially when it applies the same method which was attended with such brilliant results in the science of mathematics. It is, therefore, of the highest importance for us to know whether the method of arriving at demonstrative certainty, which is termed mathematical, be identical with that by which we endeavour to attain the same degree of certainty in philosophy, and which is termed in that science dogmatical.\n\nPhilosophical cognition is the cognition of reason by means of conceptions; mathematical cognition is cognition by means of the construction of conceptions. The construction of a conception is the presentation à priori of the intuition which corresponds to the conception. For this purpose a non-empirical intuition is requisite, which, as an intuition, is an individual object; while, as the construction of a conception (a general representation), it must be seen to be universally valid for all the possible intuitions which rank under that conception. Thus I construct a triangle, by the presentation of the object which corresponds to this conception, either by mere imagination, in pure intuition, or upon paper, in empirical intuition, in both cases completely à priori, without borrowing the type of that figure from any experience. The individual figure drawn upon paper is empirical; but it serves, notwithstanding, to indicate the conception, even in its universality, because in this empirical intuition we keep our eye merely on the act of the construction of the conception, and pay no attention to the various modes of determining it, for example, its size, the length of its sides, the size of its angles, these not in the least affecting the essential character of the conception.\n\nPhilosophical cognition, accordingly, regards the particular only in the general; mathematical the general in the particular, nay, in the individual. This is done, however, entirely à priori and by means of pure reason, so that, as this individual figure is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, the object of the conception, to which this individual figure corresponds as its schema, must be cogitated as universally determined.\n\nThe essential difference of these two modes of cognition consists, therefore, in this formal quality; it does not regard the difference of the matter or objects of both. Those thinkers who aim at distinguishing philosophy from mathematics by asserting that the former has to do with quality merely, and the latter with quantity, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The reason why mathematical cognition can relate only to quantity is to be found in its form alone. For it is the conception of quantities only that is capable of being constructed, that is, presented à priori in intuition; while qualities cannot be given in any other than an empirical intuition. Hence the cognition of qualities by reason is possible only through conceptions. No one can find an intuition which shall correspond to the conception of reality, except in experience; it cannot be presented to the mind à priori and antecedently to the empirical consciousness of a reality. We can form an intuition, by means of the mere conception of it, of a cone, without the aid of experience; but the colour of the cone we cannot know except from experience. I cannot present an intuition of a cause, except in an example which experience offers to me. Besides, philosophy, as well as mathematics, treats of quantities; as, for example, of totality, infinity, and so on. Mathematics, too, treats of the difference of lines and surfaces—as spaces of different quality, of the continuity of extension—as a quality thereof. But, although in such cases they have a common object, the mode in which reason considers that object is very different in philosophy from what it is in mathematics. The former confines itself to the general conceptions; the latter can do nothing with a mere conception, it hastens to intuition. In this intuition it regards the conception in concreto, not empirically, but in an à priori intuition, which it has constructed; and in which, all the results which follow from the general conditions of the construction of the conception are in all cases valid for the object of the constructed conception.\n\nSuppose that the conception of a triangle is given to a philosopher and that he is required to discover, by the philosophical method, what relation the sum of its angles bears to a right angle. He has nothing before him but the conception of a figure enclosed within three right lines, and, consequently, with the same number of angles. He may analyse the conception of a right line, of an angle, or of the number three as long as he pleases, but he will not discover any properties not contained in these conceptions. But, if this question is proposed to a geometrician, he at once begins by constructing a triangle. He knows that two right angles are equal to the sum of all the contiguous angles which proceed from one point in a straight line; and he goes on to produce one side of his triangle, thus forming two adjacent angles which are together equal to two right angles. He then divides the exterior of these angles, by drawing a line parallel with the opposite side of the triangle, and immediately perceives that he has thus got an exterior adjacent angle which is equal to the interior. Proceeding in this way, through a chain of inferences, and always on the ground of intuition, he arrives at a clear and universally valid solution of the question.\n\nBut mathematics does not confine itself to the construction of quantities (quanta), as in the case of geometry; it occupies itself with pure quantity also (quantitas), as in the case of algebra, where complete abstraction is made of the properties of the object indicated by the conception of quantity. In algebra, a certain method of notation by signs is adopted, and these indicate the different possible constructions of quantities, the extraction of roots, and so on. After having thus denoted the general conception of quantities, according to their different relations, the different operations by which quantity or number is increased or diminished are presented in intuition in accordance with general rules. Thus, when one quantity is to be divided by another, the signs which denote both are placed in the form peculiar to the operation of division; and thus algebra, by means of a symbolical construction of quantity, just as geometry, with its ostensive or geometrical construction (a construction of the objects themselves), arrives at results which discursive cognition cannot hope to reach by the aid of mere conceptions.\n\nNow, what is the cause of this difference in the fortune of the philosopher and the mathematician, the former of whom follows the path of conceptions, while the latter pursues that of intuitions, which he represents, à priori, in correspondence with his conceptions? The cause is evident from what has been already demonstrated in the introduction to this Critique. We do not, in the present case, want to discover analytical propositions, which may be produced merely by analysing our conceptions—for in this the philosopher would have the advantage over his rival; we aim at the discovery of synthetical propositions—such synthetical propositions, moreover, as can be cognized à priori. I must not confine myself to that which I actually cogitate in my conception of a triangle, for this is nothing more than the mere definition; I must try to go beyond that, and to arrive at properties which are not contained in, although they belong to, the conception. Now, this is impossible, unless I determine the object present to my mind according to the conditions, either of empirical, or of pure, intuition. In the former case, I should have an empirical proposition (arrived at by actual measurement of the angles of the triangle), which would possess neither universality nor necessity; but that would be of no value. In the latter, I proceed by geometrical construction, by means of which I collect, in a pure intuition, just as I would in an empirical intuition, all the various properties which belong to the schema of a triangle in general, and consequently to its conception, and thus construct synthetical propositions which possess the attribute of universality.\n\nIt would be vain to philosophize upon the triangle, that is, to reflect on it discursively; I should get no further than the definition with which I had been obliged to set out. There are certainly transcendental synthetical propositions which are framed by means of pure conceptions, and which form the peculiar distinction of philosophy; but these do not relate to any particular thing, but to a thing in general, and enounce the conditions under which the perception of it may become a part of possible experience. But the science of mathematics has nothing to do with such questions, nor with the question of existence in any fashion; it is concerned merely with the properties of objects in themselves, only in so far as these are connected with the conception of the objects.\n\nIn the above example, we merely attempted to show the great difference which exists between the discursive employment of reason in the sphere of conceptions, and its intuitive exercise by means of the construction of conceptions. The question naturally arises: What is the cause which necessitates this twofold exercise of reason, and how are we to discover whether it is the philosophical or the mathematical method which reason is pursuing in an argument?\n\nAll our knowledge relates, finally, to possible intuitions, for it is these alone that present objects to the mind. An à priori or non-empirical conception contains either a pure intuition—and in this case it can be constructed; or it contains nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given à priori. In this latter case, it may help us to form synthetical à priori judgements, but only in the discursive method, by conceptions, not in the intuitive, by means of the construction of conceptions.\n\nThe only à priori intuition is that of the pure form of phenomena—space and time. A conception of space and time as quanta may be presented à priori in intuition, that is, constructed, either alone with their quality (figure), or as pure quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous), by means of number. But the matter of phenomena, by which things are given in space and time, can be presented only in perception, à posteriori. The only conception which represents à priori this empirical content of phenomena is the conception of a thing in general; and the à priori synthetical cognition of this conception can give us nothing more than the rule for the synthesis of that which may be contained in the corresponding à posteriori perception; it is utterly inadequate to present an à priori intuition of the real object, which must necessarily be empirical.\n\nSynthetical propositions, which relate to things in general, an à priori intuition of which is impossible, are transcendental. For this reason transcendental propositions cannot be framed by means of the construction of conceptions; they are à priori, and based entirely on conceptions themselves. They contain merely the rule, by which we are to seek in the world of perception or experience the synthetical unity of that which cannot be intuited à priori. But they are incompetent to present any of the conceptions which appear in them in an à priori intuition; these can be given only à posteriori, in experience, which, however, is itself possible only through these synthetical principles.\n\nIf we are to form a synthetical judgement regarding a conception, we must go beyond it, to the intuition in which it is given. If we keep to what is contained in the conception, the judgement is merely analytical—it is merely an explanation of what we have cogitated in the conception. But I can pass from the conception to the pure or empirical intuition which corresponds to it. I can proceed to examine my conception in concreto, and to cognize, either à priori or à posteriori, what I find in the object of the conception. The former—à priori cognition—is rational-mathematical cognition by means of the construction of the conception; the latter—à posteriori cognition—is purely empirical cognition, which does not possess the attributes of necessity and universality. Thus I may analyse the conception I have of gold; but I gain no new information from this analysis, I merely enumerate the different properties which I had connected with the notion indicated by the word. My knowledge has gained in logical clearness and arrangement, but no addition has been made to it. But if I take the matter which is indicated by this name, and submit it to the examination of my senses, I am enabled to form several synthetical—although still empirical—propositions. The mathematical conception of a triangle I should construct, that is, present à priori in intuition, and in this way attain to rational-synthetical cognition. But when the transcendental conception of reality, or substance, or power is presented to my mind, I find that it does not relate to or indicate either an empirical or pure intuition, but that it indicates merely the synthesis of empirical intuitions, which cannot of course be given à priori. The synthesis in such a conception cannot proceed à priori—without the aid of experience—to the intuition which corresponds to the conception; and, for this reason, none of these conceptions can produce a determinative synthetical proposition, they can never present more than a principle of the synthesis[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-75) of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is, therefore, a synthetical cognition of reason by means of pure conceptions and the discursive method, and it renders possible all synthetical unity in empirical cognition, though it cannot present us with any intuition à priori.\n\n[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-75) In the case of the conception of cause, I do really go beyond the empirical conception of an event—but not to the intuition which presents this conception in concreto, but only to the time-conditions, which may be found in experience to correspond to the conception. My procedure is, therefore, strictly according to conceptions; I cannot in a case of this kind employ the construction of conceptions, because the conception is merely a rule for the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions, and which, therefore, cannot be given à priori.\n\nThere is thus a twofold exercise of reason. Both modes have the properties of universality and an à priori origin in common, but are, in their procedure, of widely different character. The reason of this is that in the world of phenomena, in which alone objects are presented to our minds, there are two main elements—the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined completely à priori, and the matter or content—that which is presented in space and time, and which, consequently, contains a something—an existence corresponding to our powers of sensation. As regards the latter, which can never be given in a determinate mode except by experience, there are no à priori notions which relate to it, except the undetermined conceptions of the synthesis of possible sensations, in so far as these belong (in a possible experience) to the unity of consciousness. As regards the former, we can determine our conceptions à priori in intuition, inasmuch as we are ourselves the creators of the objects of the conceptions in space and time—these objects being regarded simply as quanta. In the one case, reason proceeds according to conceptions and can do nothing more than subject phenomena to these—which can only be determined empirically, that is, à posteriori—in conformity, however, with those conceptions as the rules of all empirical synthesis. In the other case, reason proceeds by the construction of conceptions; and, as these conceptions relate to an à priori intuition, they may be given and determined in pure intuition à priori, and without the aid of empirical data. The examination and consideration of everything that exists in space or time—whether it is a quantum or not, in how far the particular something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum, or a mere determination of some other existence, whether it relates to anything else—either as cause or effect, whether its existence is isolated or in reciprocal connection with and dependence upon others, the possibility of this existence, its reality and necessity or opposites—all these form part of the cognition of reason on the ground of conceptions, and this cognition is termed philosophical. But to determine à priori an intuition in space (its figure), to divide time into periods, or merely to cognize the quantity of an intuition in space and time, and to determine it by number—all this is an operation of reason by means of the construction of conceptions, and is called mathematical.\n\nThe success which attends the efforts of reason in the sphere of mathematics naturally fosters the expectation that the same good fortune will be its lot, if it applies the mathematical method in other regions of mental endeavour besides that of quantities. Its success is thus great, because it can support all its conceptions by à priori intuitions and, in this way, make itself a master, as it were, over nature; while pure philosophy, with its à priori discursive conceptions, bungles about in the world of nature, and cannot accredit or show any à priori evidence of the reality of these conceptions. Masters in the science of mathematics are confident of the success of this method; indeed, it is a common persuasion that it is capable of being applied to any subject of human thought. They have hardly ever reflected or philosophized on their favourite science—a task of great difficulty; and the specific difference between the two modes of employing the faculty of reason has never entered their thoughts. Rules current in the field of common experience, and which common sense stamps everywhere with its approval, are regarded by them as axiomatic. From what source the conceptions of space and time, with which (as the only primitive quanta) they have to deal, enter their minds, is a question which they do not trouble themselves to answer; and they think it just as unnecessary to examine into the origin of the pure conceptions of the understanding and the extent of their validity. All they have to do with them is to employ them. In all this they are perfectly right, if they do not overstep the limits of the sphere of nature. But they pass, unconsciously, from the world of sense to the insecure ground of pure transcendental conceptions (instabilis tellus, innabilis unda), where they can neither stand nor swim, and where the tracks of their footsteps are obliterated by time; while the march of mathematics is pursued on a broad and magnificent highway, which the latest posterity shall frequent without fear of danger or impediment.\n\nAs we have taken upon us the task of determining, clearly and certainly, the limits of pure reason in the sphere of transcendentalism, and as the efforts of reason in this direction are persisted in, even after the plainest and most expressive warnings, hope still beckoning us past the limits of experience into the splendours of the intellectual world—it becomes necessary to cut away the last anchor of this fallacious and fantastic hope. We shall, accordingly, show that the mathematical method is unattended in the sphere of philosophy by the least advantage—except, perhaps, that it more plainly exhibits its own inadequacy—that geometry and philosophy are two quite different things, although they go hand in hand in the field of natural science, and, consequently, that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.\n\nThe evidence of mathematics rests upon definitions, axioms, and demonstrations. I shall be satisfied with showing that none of these forms can be employed or imitated in philosophy in the sense in which they are understood by mathematicians; and that the geometrician, if he employs his method in philosophy, will succeed only in building card-castles, while the employment of the philosophical method in mathematics can result in nothing but mere verbiage. The essential business of philosophy, indeed, is to mark out the limits of the science; and even the mathematician, unless his talent is naturally circumscribed and limited to this particular department of knowledge, cannot turn a deaf ear to the warnings of philosophy, or set himself above its direction.\n\nI. Of Definitions. A definition is, as the term itself indicates, the representation, upon primary grounds, of the complete conception of a thing within its own limits.[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-76) Accordingly, an empirical conception cannot be defined, it can only be explained. For, as there are in such a conception only a certain number of marks or signs, which denote a certain class of sensuous objects, we can never be sure that we do not cogitate under the word which indicates the same object, at one time a greater, at another a smaller number of signs. Thus, one person may cogitate in his conception of gold, in addition to its properties of weight, colour, malleability, that of resisting rust, while another person may be ignorant of this quality. We employ certain signs only so long as we require them for the sake of distinction; new observations abstract some and add new ones, so that an empirical conception never remains within permanent limits. It is, in fact, useless to define a conception of this kind. If, for example, we are speaking of water and its properties, we do not stop at what we actually think by the word water, but proceed to observation and experiment; and the word, with the few signs attached to it, is more properly a designation than a conception of the thing. A definition in this case would evidently be nothing more than a determination of the word. In the second place, no à priori conception, such as those of substance, cause, right, fitness, and so on, can be defined. For I can never be sure, that the clear representation of a given conception (which is given in a confused state) has been fully developed, until I know that the representation is adequate with its object. But, inasmuch as the conception, as it is presented to the mind, may contain a number of obscure representations, which we do not observe in our analysis, although we employ them in our application of the conception, I can never be sure that my analysis is complete, while examples may make this probable, although they can never demonstrate the fact. Instead of the word definition, I should rather employ the term exposition—a more modest expression, which the critic may accept without surrendering his doubts as to the completeness of the analysis of any such conception. As, therefore, neither empirical nor à priori conceptions are capable of definition, we have to see whether the only other kind of conceptions—arbitrary conceptions—can be subjected to this mental operation. Such a conception can always be defined; for I must know thoroughly what I wished to cogitate in it, as it was I who created it, and it was not given to my mind either by the nature of my understanding or by experience. At the same time, I cannot say that, by such a definition, I have defined a real object. If the conception is based upon empirical conditions, if, for example, I have a conception of a clock for a ship, this arbitrary conception does not assure me of the existence or even of the possibility of the object. My definition of such a conception would with more propriety be termed a declaration of a project than a definition of an object. There are no other conceptions which can bear definition, except those which contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed à priori. Consequently, the science of mathematics alone possesses definitions. For the object here thought is presented à priori in intuition; and thus it can never contain more or less than the conception, because the conception of the object has been given by the definition—and primarily, that is, without deriving the definition from any other source. Philosophical definitions are, therefore, merely expositions of given conceptions, while mathematical definitions are constructions of conceptions originally formed by the mind itself; the former are produced by analysis, the completeness of which is never demonstratively certain, the latter by a synthesis. In a mathematical definition the conception is formed, in a philosophical definition it is only explained. From this it follows:\n\n[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-76) The definition must describe the conception completely that is, omit none of the marks or signs of which it composed; within its own limits, that is, it must be precise, and enumerate no more signs than belong to the conception; and on primary grounds, that is to say, the limitations of the bounds of the conception must not be deduced from other conceptions, as in this case a proof would be necessary, and the so-called definition would be incapable of taking its place at the head of all the judgements we have to form regarding an object.\n\n(a) That we must not imitate, in philosophy, the mathematical usage of commencing with definitions—except by way of hypothesis or experiment. For, as all so-called philosophical definitions are merely analyses of given conceptions, these conceptions, although only in a confused form, must precede the analysis; and the incomplete exposition must precede the complete, so that we may be able to draw certain inferences from the characteristics which an incomplete analysis has enabled us to discover, before we attain to the complete exposition or definition of the conception. In one word, a full and clear definition ought, in philosophy, rather to form the conclusion than the commencement of our labours.[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-77) In mathematics, on the contrary, we cannot have a conception prior to the definition; it is the definition which gives us the conception, and it must for this reason form the commencement of every chain of mathematical reasoning.\n\n[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-77) Philosophy abounds in faulty definitions, especially such as contain some of the elements requisite to form a complete definition. If a conception could not be employed in reasoning before it had been defined, it would fare ill with all philosophical thought. But, as incompletely defined conceptions may always be employed without detriment to truth, so far as our analysis of the elements contained in them proceeds, imperfect definitions, that is, propositions which are properly not definitions, but merely approximations thereto, may be used with great advantage. In mathematics, definition belongs ad esse, in philosophy ad melius esse. It is a difficult task to construct a proper definition. Jurists are still without a complete definition of the idea of right.\n\n(b) Mathematical definitions cannot be erroneous. For the conception is given only in and through the definition, and thus it contains only what has been cogitated in the definition. But although a definition cannot be incorrect, as regards its content, an error may sometimes, although seldom, creep into the form. This error consists in a want of precision. Thus the common definition of a circle—that it is a curved line, every point in which is equally distant from another point called the centre—is faulty, from the fact that the determination indicated by the word curved is superfluous. For there ought to be a particular theorem, which may be easily proved from the definition, to the effect that every line, which has all its points at equal distances from another point, must be a curved line—that is, that not even the smallest part of it can be straight. Analytical definitions, on the other hand, may be erroneous in many respects, either by the introduction of signs which do not actually exist in the conception, or by wanting in that completeness which forms the essential of a definition. In the latter case, the definition is necessarily defective, because we can never be fully certain of the completeness of our analysis. For these reasons, the method of definition employed in mathematics cannot be imitated in philosophy.\n\n2. Of Axioms. These, in so far as they are immediately certain, are à priori synthetical principles. Now, one conception cannot be connected synthetically and yet immediately with another; because, if we wish to proceed out of and beyond a conception, a third mediating cognition is necessary. And, as philosophy is a cognition of reason by the aid of conceptions alone, there is to be found in it no principle which deserves to be called an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, may possess axioms, because it can always connect the predicates of an object à priori, and without any mediating term, by means of the construction of conceptions in intuition. Such is the case with the proposition: Three points can always lie in a plane. On the other hand, no synthetical principle which is based upon conceptions, can ever be immediately certain (for example, the proposition: Everything that happens has a cause), because I require a mediating term to connect the two conceptions of event and cause—namely, the condition of time-determination in an experience, and I cannot cognize any such principle immediately and from conceptions alone. Discursive principles are, accordingly, very different from intuitive principles or axioms. The former always require deduction, which in the case of the latter may be altogether dispensed with. Axioms are, for this reason, always self-evident, while philosophical principles, whatever may be the degree of certainty they possess, cannot lay any claim to such a distinction. No synthetical proposition of pure transcendental reason can be so evident, as is often rashly enough declared, as the statement, twice two are four. It is true that in the Analytic I introduced into the list of principles of the pure understanding, certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there discussed was not itself an axiom, but served merely to present the principle of the possibility of axioms in general, while it was really nothing more than a principle based upon conceptions. For it is one part of the duty of transcendental philosophy to establish the possibility of mathematics itself. Philosophy possesses, then, no axioms, and has no right to impose its à priori principles upon thought, until it has established their authority and validity by a thoroughgoing deduction.\n\n3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, based upon intuition, can be termed a demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but it cannot convince us that it might not have been otherwise. Hence a proof upon empirical grounds cannot be apodeictic. À priori conceptions, in discursive cognition, can never produce intuitive certainty or evidence, however certain the judgement they present may be. Mathematics alone, therefore, contains demonstrations, because it does not deduce its cognition from conceptions, but from the construction of conceptions, that is, from intuition, which can be given à priori in accordance with conceptions. The method of algebra, in equations, from which the correct answer is deduced by reduction, is a kind of construction—not geometrical, but by symbols—in which all conceptions, especially those of the relations of quantities, are represented in intuition by signs; and thus the conclusions in that science are secured from errors by the fact that every proof is submitted to ocular evidence. Philosophical cognition does not possess this advantage, it being required to consider the general always in abstracto (by means of conceptions), while mathematics can always consider it in concreto (in an individual intuition), and at the same time by means of à priori representation, whereby all errors are rendered manifest to the senses. The former—discursive proofs—ought to be termed acroamatic proofs, rather than demonstrations, as only words are employed in them, while demonstrations proper, as the term itself indicates, always require a reference to the intuition of the object.\n\nIt follows from all these considerations that it is not consonant with the nature of philosophy, especially in the sphere of pure reason, to employ the dogmatical method, and to adorn itself with the titles and insignia of mathematical science. It does not belong to that order, and can only hope for a fraternal union with that science. Its attempts at mathematical evidence are vain pretensions, which can only keep it back from its true aim, which is to detect the illusory procedure of reason when transgressing its proper limits, and by fully explaining and analysing our conceptions, to conduct us from the dim regions of speculation to the clear region of modest self-knowledge. Reason must not, therefore, in its transcendental endeavours, look forward with such confidence, as if the path it is pursuing led straight to its aim, nor reckon with such security upon its premisses, as to consider it unnecessary to take a step back, or to keep a strict watch for errors, which, overlooked in the principles, may be detected in the arguments themselves—in which case it may be requisite either to determine these principles with greater strictness, or to change them entirely.\n\nI divide all apodeictic propositions, whether demonstrable or immediately certain, into dogmata and mathemata. A direct synthetical proposition, based on conceptions, is a dogma; a proposition of the same kind, based on the construction of conceptions, is a mathema. Analytical judgements do not teach us any more about an object than what was contained in the conception we had of it; because they do not extend our cognition beyond our conception of an object, they merely elucidate the conception. They cannot therefore be with propriety termed dogmas. Of the two kinds of à priori synthetical propositions above mentioned, only those which are employed in philosophy can, according to the general mode of speech, bear this name; those of arithmetic or geometry would not be rightly so denominated. Thus the customary mode of speaking confirms the explanation given above, and the conclusion arrived at, that only those judgements which are based upon conceptions, not on the construction of conceptions, can be termed dogmatical.\n\nThus, pure reason, in the sphere of speculation, does not contain a single direct synthetical judgement based upon conceptions. By means of ideas, it is, as we have shown, incapable of producing synthetical judgements, which are objectively valid; by means of the conceptions of the understanding, it establishes certain indubitable principles, not, however, directly on the basis of conceptions, but only indirectly by means of the relation of these conceptions to something of a purely contingent nature, namely, possible experience. When experience is presupposed, these principles are apodeictically certain, but in themselves, and directly, they cannot even be cognized à priori. Thus the given conceptions of cause and event will not be sufficient for the demonstration of the proposition: Every event has a cause. For this reason, it is not a dogma; although from another point of view, that of experience, it is capable of being proved to demonstration. The proper term for such a proposition is principle, and not theorem (although it does require to be proved), because it possesses the remarkable peculiarity of being the condition of the possibility of its own ground of proof, that is, experience, and of forming a necessary presupposition in all empirical observation.\n\nIf then, in the speculative sphere of pure reason, no dogmata are to be found; all dogmatical methods, whether borrowed from mathematics, or invented by philosophical thinkers, are alike inappropriate and inefficient. They only serve to conceal errors and fallacies, and to deceive philosophy, whose duty it is to see that reason pursues a safe and straight path. A philosophical method may, however, be systematical. For our reason is, subjectively considered, itself a system, and, in the sphere of mere conceptions, a system of investigation according to principles of unity, the material being supplied by experience alone. But this is not the proper place for discussing the peculiar method of transcendental philosophy, as our present task is simply to examine whether our faculties are capable of erecting an edifice on the basis of pure reason, and how far they may proceed with the materials at their command.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","text":"### Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism\n\nThe science of mathematics presents the most brilliant example of the extension of the sphere of pure reason without the aid of experience. Examples are always contagious; and they exert an especial influence on the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other case as fell to its lot in one fortunate instance. Hence pure reason hopes to be able to extend its empire in the transcendental sphere with equal success and security, especially when it applies the same method which was attended with such brilliant results in the science of mathematics. It is, therefore, of the highest importance for us to know whether the method of arriving at demonstrative certainty, which is termed mathematical, be identical with that by which we endeavour to attain the same degree of certainty in philosophy, and which is termed in that science dogmatical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","text":"Philosophical cognition is the cognition of reason by means of conceptions; mathematical cognition is cognition by means of the construction of conceptions. The construction of a conception is the presentation à priori of the intuition which corresponds to the conception. For this purpose a non-empirical intuition is requisite, which, as an intuition, is an individual object; while, as the construction of a conception (a general representation), it must be seen to be universally valid for all the possible intuitions which rank under that conception. Thus I construct a triangle, by the presentation of the object which corresponds to this conception, either by mere imagination, in pure intuition, or upon paper, in empirical intuition, in both cases completely à priori, without borrowing the type of that figure from any experience. The individual figure drawn upon paper is empirical; but it serves, notwithstanding, to indicate the conception, even in its universality, because in this empirical intuition we keep our eye merely on the act of the construction of the conception, and pay no attention to the various modes of determining it, for example, its size, the length of its sides, the size of its angles, these not in the least affecting the essential character of the conception.\n\nPhilosophical cognition, accordingly, regards the particular only in the general; mathematical the general in the particular, nay, in the individual. This is done, however, entirely à priori and by means of pure reason, so that, as this individual figure is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, the object of the conception, to which this individual figure corresponds as its schema, must be cogitated as universally determined.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","text":"The essential difference of these two modes of cognition consists, therefore, in this formal quality; it does not regard the difference of the matter or objects of both. Those thinkers who aim at distinguishing philosophy from mathematics by asserting that the former has to do with quality merely, and the latter with quantity, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The reason why mathematical cognition can relate only to quantity is to be found in its form alone. For it is the conception of quantities only that is capable of being constructed, that is, presented à priori in intuition; while qualities cannot be given in any other than an empirical intuition. Hence the cognition of qualities by reason is possible only through conceptions. No one can find an intuition which shall correspond to the conception of reality, except in experience; it cannot be presented to the mind à priori and antecedently to the empirical consciousness of a reality. We can form an intuition, by means of the mere conception of it, of a cone, without the aid of experience; but the colour of the cone we cannot know except from experience. I cannot present an intuition of a cause, except in an example which experience offers to me. Besides, philosophy, as well as mathematics, treats of quantities; as, for example, of totality, infinity, and so on. Mathematics, too, treats of the difference of lines and surfaces—as spaces of different quality, of the continuity of extension—as a quality thereof. But, although in such cases they have a common object, the mode in which reason considers that object is very different in philosophy from what it is in mathematics. The former confines itself to the general conceptions; the latter can do nothing with a mere conception, it hastens to intuition. In this intuition it regards the conception in concreto, not empirically, but in an à priori intuition, which it has constructed; and in which, all the results which follow from the general conditions of the construction of the conception are in all cases valid for the object of the constructed conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0004","text":"Suppose that the conception of a triangle is given to a philosopher and that he is required to discover, by the philosophical method, what relation the sum of its angles bears to a right angle. He has nothing before him but the conception of a figure enclosed within three right lines, and, consequently, with the same number of angles. He may analyse the conception of a right line, of an angle, or of the number three as long as he pleases, but he will not discover any properties not contained in these conceptions. But, if this question is proposed to a geometrician, he at once begins by constructing a triangle. He knows that two right angles are equal to the sum of all the contiguous angles which proceed from one point in a straight line; and he goes on to produce one side of his triangle, thus forming two adjacent angles which are together equal to two right angles. He then divides the exterior of these angles, by drawing a line parallel with the opposite side of the triangle, and immediately perceives that he has thus got an exterior adjacent angle which is equal to the interior. Proceeding in this way, through a chain of inferences, and always on the ground of intuition, he arrives at a clear and universally valid solution of the question.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0005","text":"But mathematics does not confine itself to the construction of quantities (quanta), as in the case of geometry; it occupies itself with pure quantity also (quantitas), as in the case of algebra, where complete abstraction is made of the properties of the object indicated by the conception of quantity. In algebra, a certain method of notation by signs is adopted, and these indicate the different possible constructions of quantities, the extraction of roots, and so on. After having thus denoted the general conception of quantities, according to their different relations, the different operations by which quantity or number is increased or diminished are presented in intuition in accordance with general rules. Thus, when one quantity is to be divided by another, the signs which denote both are placed in the form peculiar to the operation of division; and thus algebra, by means of a symbolical construction of quantity, just as geometry, with its ostensive or geometrical construction (a construction of the objects themselves), arrives at results which discursive cognition cannot hope to reach by the aid of mere conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","text":"Now, what is the cause of this difference in the fortune of the philosopher and the mathematician, the former of whom follows the path of conceptions, while the latter pursues that of intuitions, which he represents, à priori, in correspondence with his conceptions? The cause is evident from what has been already demonstrated in the introduction to this Critique. We do not, in the present case, want to discover analytical propositions, which may be produced merely by analysing our conceptions—for in this the philosopher would have the advantage over his rival; we aim at the discovery of synthetical propositions—such synthetical propositions, moreover, as can be cognized à priori. I must not confine myself to that which I actually cogitate in my conception of a triangle, for this is nothing more than the mere definition; I must try to go beyond that, and to arrive at properties which are not contained in, although they belong to, the conception. Now, this is impossible, unless I determine the object present to my mind according to the conditions, either of empirical, or of pure, intuition. In the former case, I should have an empirical proposition (arrived at by actual measurement of the angles of the triangle), which would possess neither universality nor necessity; but that would be of no value. In the latter, I proceed by geometrical construction, by means of which I collect, in a pure intuition, just as I would in an empirical intuition, all the various properties which belong to the schema of a triangle in general, and consequently to its conception, and thus construct synthetical propositions which possess the attribute of universality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0007","text":"It would be vain to philosophize upon the triangle, that is, to reflect on it discursively; I should get no further than the definition with which I had been obliged to set out. There are certainly transcendental synthetical propositions which are framed by means of pure conceptions, and which form the peculiar distinction of philosophy; but these do not relate to any particular thing, but to a thing in general, and enounce the conditions under which the perception of it may become a part of possible experience. But the science of mathematics has nothing to do with such questions, nor with the question of existence in any fashion; it is concerned merely with the properties of objects in themselves, only in so far as these are connected with the conception of the objects.\n\nIn the above example, we merely attempted to show the great difference which exists between the discursive employment of reason in the sphere of conceptions, and its intuitive exercise by means of the construction of conceptions. The question naturally arises: What is the cause which necessitates this twofold exercise of reason, and how are we to discover whether it is the philosophical or the mathematical method which reason is pursuing in an argument?\n\nAll our knowledge relates, finally, to possible intuitions, for it is these alone that present objects to the mind. An à priori or non-empirical conception contains either a pure intuition—and in this case it can be constructed; or it contains nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given à priori. In this latter case, it may help us to form synthetical à priori judgements, but only in the discursive method, by conceptions, not in the intuitive, by means of the construction of conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","text":"The only à priori intuition is that of the pure form of phenomena—space and time. A conception of space and time as quanta may be presented à priori in intuition, that is, constructed, either alone with their quality (figure), or as pure quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous), by means of number. But the matter of phenomena, by which things are given in space and time, can be presented only in perception, à posteriori. The only conception which represents à priori this empirical content of phenomena is the conception of a thing in general; and the à priori synthetical cognition of this conception can give us nothing more than the rule for the synthesis of that which may be contained in the corresponding à posteriori perception; it is utterly inadequate to present an à priori intuition of the real object, which must necessarily be empirical.\n\nSynthetical propositions, which relate to things in general, an à priori intuition of which is impossible, are transcendental. For this reason transcendental propositions cannot be framed by means of the construction of conceptions; they are à priori, and based entirely on conceptions themselves. They contain merely the rule, by which we are to seek in the world of perception or experience the synthetical unity of that which cannot be intuited à priori. But they are incompetent to present any of the conceptions which appear in them in an à priori intuition; these can be given only à posteriori, in experience, which, however, is itself possible only through these synthetical principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","text":"If we are to form a synthetical judgement regarding a conception, we must go beyond it, to the intuition in which it is given. If we keep to what is contained in the conception, the judgement is merely analytical—it is merely an explanation of what we have cogitated in the conception. But I can pass from the conception to the pure or empirical intuition which corresponds to it. I can proceed to examine my conception in concreto, and to cognize, either à priori or à posteriori, what I find in the object of the conception. The former—à priori cognition—is rational-mathematical cognition by means of the construction of the conception; the latter—à posteriori cognition—is purely empirical cognition, which does not possess the attributes of necessity and universality. Thus I may analyse the conception I have of gold; but I gain no new information from this analysis, I merely enumerate the different properties which I had connected with the notion indicated by the word. My knowledge has gained in logical clearness and arrangement, but no addition has been made to it. But if I take the matter which is indicated by this name, and submit it to the examination of my senses, I am enabled to form several synthetical—although still empirical—propositions. The mathematical conception of a triangle I should construct, that is, present à priori in intuition, and in this way attain to rational-synthetical cognition. But when the transcendental conception of reality, or substance, or power is presented to my mind, I find that it does not relate to or indicate either an empirical or pure intuition, but that it indicates merely the synthesis of empirical intuitions, which cannot of course be given à priori. The synthesis in such a conception cannot proceed à priori—without the aid of experience—to the intuition which corresponds to the conception; and, for this reason, none of these conceptions can produce a determinative synthetical proposition, they can never present more than a principle of the synthesis[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-75) of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is, therefore, a synthetical cognition of reason by means of pure conceptions and the discursive method, and it renders possible all synthetical unity in empirical cognition, though it cannot present us with any intuition à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0010","text":"[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-75) In the case of the conception of cause, I do really go beyond the empirical conception of an event—but not to the intuition which presents this conception in concreto, but only to the time-conditions, which may be found in experience to correspond to the conception. My procedure is, therefore, strictly according to conceptions; I cannot in a case of this kind employ the construction of conceptions, because the conception is merely a rule for the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions, and which, therefore, cannot be given à priori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","text":"There is thus a twofold exercise of reason. Both modes have the properties of universality and an à priori origin in common, but are, in their procedure, of widely different character. The reason of this is that in the world of phenomena, in which alone objects are presented to our minds, there are two main elements—the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined completely à priori, and the matter or content—that which is presented in space and time, and which, consequently, contains a something—an existence corresponding to our powers of sensation. As regards the latter, which can never be given in a determinate mode except by experience, there are no à priori notions which relate to it, except the undetermined conceptions of the synthesis of possible sensations, in so far as these belong (in a possible experience) to the unity of consciousness. As regards the former, we can determine our conceptions à priori in intuition, inasmuch as we are ourselves the creators of the objects of the conceptions in space and time—these objects being regarded simply as quanta. In the one case, reason proceeds according to conceptions and can do nothing more than subject phenomena to these—which can only be determined empirically, that is, à posteriori—in conformity, however, with those conceptions as the rules of all empirical synthesis. In the other case, reason proceeds by the construction of conceptions; and, as these conceptions relate to an à priori intuition, they may be given and determined in pure intuition à priori, and without the aid of empirical data. The examination and consideration of everything that exists in space or time—whether it is a quantum or not, in how far the particular something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum, or a mere determination of some other existence, whether it relates to anything else—either as cause or effect, whether its existence is isolated or in reciprocal connection with and dependence upon others, the possibility of this existence, its reality and necessity or opposites—all these form part of the cognition of reason on the ground of conceptions, and this cognition is termed philosophical. But to determine à priori an intuition in space (its figure), to divide time into periods, or merely to cognize the quantity of an intuition in space and time, and to determine it by number—all this is an operation of reason by means of the construction of conceptions, and is called mathematical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0012","text":"The success which attends the efforts of reason in the sphere of mathematics naturally fosters the expectation that the same good fortune will be its lot, if it applies the mathematical method in other regions of mental endeavour besides that of quantities. Its success is thus great, because it can support all its conceptions by à priori intuitions and, in this way, make itself a master, as it were, over nature; while pure philosophy, with its à priori discursive conceptions, bungles about in the world of nature, and cannot accredit or show any à priori evidence of the reality of these conceptions. Masters in the science of mathematics are confident of the success of this method; indeed, it is a common persuasion that it is capable of being applied to any subject of human thought. They have hardly ever reflected or philosophized on their favourite science—a task of great difficulty; and the specific difference between the two modes of employing the faculty of reason has never entered their thoughts. Rules current in the field of common experience, and which common sense stamps everywhere with its approval, are regarded by them as axiomatic. From what source the conceptions of space and time, with which (as the only primitive quanta) they have to deal, enter their minds, is a question which they do not trouble themselves to answer; and they think it just as unnecessary to examine into the origin of the pure conceptions of the understanding and the extent of their validity. All they have to do with them is to employ them. In all this they are perfectly right, if they do not overstep the limits of the sphere of nature. But they pass, unconsciously, from the world of sense to the insecure ground of pure transcendental conceptions (instabilis tellus, innabilis unda), where they can neither stand nor swim, and where the tracks of their footsteps are obliterated by time; while the march of mathematics is pursued on a broad and magnificent highway, which the latest posterity shall frequent without fear of danger or impediment.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","text":"As we have taken upon us the task of determining, clearly and certainly, the limits of pure reason in the sphere of transcendentalism, and as the efforts of reason in this direction are persisted in, even after the plainest and most expressive warnings, hope still beckoning us past the limits of experience into the splendours of the intellectual world—it becomes necessary to cut away the last anchor of this fallacious and fantastic hope. We shall, accordingly, show that the mathematical method is unattended in the sphere of philosophy by the least advantage—except, perhaps, that it more plainly exhibits its own inadequacy—that geometry and philosophy are two quite different things, although they go hand in hand in the field of natural science, and, consequently, that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.\n\nThe evidence of mathematics rests upon definitions, axioms, and demonstrations. I shall be satisfied with showing that none of these forms can be employed or imitated in philosophy in the sense in which they are understood by mathematicians; and that the geometrician, if he employs his method in philosophy, will succeed only in building card-castles, while the employment of the philosophical method in mathematics can result in nothing but mere verbiage. The essential business of philosophy, indeed, is to mark out the limits of the science; and even the mathematician, unless his talent is naturally circumscribed and limited to this particular department of knowledge, cannot turn a deaf ear to the warnings of philosophy, or set himself above its direction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","text":"I. Of Definitions. A definition is, as the term itself indicates, the representation, upon primary grounds, of the complete conception of a thing within its own limits.[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-76) Accordingly, an empirical conception cannot be defined, it can only be explained. For, as there are in such a conception only a certain number of marks or signs, which denote a certain class of sensuous objects, we can never be sure that we do not cogitate under the word which indicates the same object, at one time a greater, at another a smaller number of signs. Thus, one person may cogitate in his conception of gold, in addition to its properties of weight, colour, malleability, that of resisting rust, while another person may be ignorant of this quality. We employ certain signs only so long as we require them for the sake of distinction; new observations abstract some and add new ones, so that an empirical conception never remains within permanent limits. It is, in fact, useless to define a conception of this kind. If, for example, we are speaking of water and its properties, we do not stop at what we actually think by the word water, but proceed to observation and experiment; and the word, with the few signs attached to it, is more properly a designation than a conception of the thing. A definition in this case would evidently be nothing more than a determination of the word. In the second place, no à priori conception, such as those of substance, cause, right, fitness, and so on, can be defined. For I can never be sure, that the clear representation of a given conception (which is given in a confused state) has been fully developed, until I know that the representation is adequate with its object. But, inasmuch as the conception, as it is presented to the mind, may contain a number of obscure representations, which we do not observe in our analysis, although we employ them in our application of the conception, I can never be sure that my analysis is complete, while examples may make this probable, although they can never demonstrate the fact. Instead of the word definition, I should rather employ the term exposition—a more modest expression, which the critic may accept without surrendering his doubts as to the completeness of the analysis of any such conception. As, therefore, neither empirical nor à priori conceptions are capable of definition, we have to see whether the only other kind of conceptions—arbitrary conceptions—can be subjected to this mental operation. Such a conception can always be defined; for I must know thoroughly what I wished to cogitate in it, as it was I who created it, and it was not given to my mind either by the nature of my understanding or by experience. At the same time, I cannot say that, by such a definition, I have defined a real object. If the conception is based upon empirical conditions, if, for example, I have a conception of a clock for a ship, this arbitrary conception does not assure me of the existence or even of the possibility of the object. My definition of such a conception would with more propriety be termed a declaration of a project than a definition of an object. There are no other conceptions which can bear definition, except those which contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed à priori. Consequently, the science of mathematics alone possesses definitions. For the object here thought is presented à priori in intuition; and thus it can never contain more or less than the conception, because the conception of the object has been given by the definition—and primarily, that is, without deriving the definition from any other source. Philosophical definitions are, therefore, merely expositions of given conceptions, while mathematical definitions are constructions of conceptions originally formed by the mind itself; the former are produced by analysis, the completeness of which is never demonstratively certain, the latter by a synthesis. In a mathematical definition the conception is formed, in a philosophical definition it is only explained. From this it follows:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","text":"[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-76) The definition must describe the conception completely that is, omit none of the marks or signs of which it composed; within its own limits, that is, it must be precise, and enumerate no more signs than belong to the conception; and on primary grounds, that is to say, the limitations of the bounds of the conception must not be deduced from other conceptions, as in this case a proof would be necessary, and the so-called definition would be incapable of taking its place at the head of all the judgements we have to form regarding an object.\n\n(a) That we must not imitate, in philosophy, the mathematical usage of commencing with definitions—except by way of hypothesis or experiment. For, as all so-called philosophical definitions are merely analyses of given conceptions, these conceptions, although only in a confused form, must precede the analysis; and the incomplete exposition must precede the complete, so that we may be able to draw certain inferences from the characteristics which an incomplete analysis has enabled us to discover, before we attain to the complete exposition or definition of the conception. In one word, a full and clear definition ought, in philosophy, rather to form the conclusion than the commencement of our labours.[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-77) In mathematics, on the contrary, we cannot have a conception prior to the definition; it is the definition which gives us the conception, and it must for this reason form the commencement of every chain of mathematical reasoning.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0016","text":"[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-77) Philosophy abounds in faulty definitions, especially such as contain some of the elements requisite to form a complete definition. If a conception could not be employed in reasoning before it had been defined, it would fare ill with all philosophical thought. But, as incompletely defined conceptions may always be employed without detriment to truth, so far as our analysis of the elements contained in them proceeds, imperfect definitions, that is, propositions which are properly not definitions, but merely approximations thereto, may be used with great advantage. In mathematics, definition belongs ad esse, in philosophy ad melius esse. It is a difficult task to construct a proper definition. Jurists are still without a complete definition of the idea of right.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0017","text":"(b) Mathematical definitions cannot be erroneous. For the conception is given only in and through the definition, and thus it contains only what has been cogitated in the definition. But although a definition cannot be incorrect, as regards its content, an error may sometimes, although seldom, creep into the form. This error consists in a want of precision. Thus the common definition of a circle—that it is a curved line, every point in which is equally distant from another point called the centre—is faulty, from the fact that the determination indicated by the word curved is superfluous. For there ought to be a particular theorem, which may be easily proved from the definition, to the effect that every line, which has all its points at equal distances from another point, must be a curved line—that is, that not even the smallest part of it can be straight. Analytical definitions, on the other hand, may be erroneous in many respects, either by the introduction of signs which do not actually exist in the conception, or by wanting in that completeness which forms the essential of a definition. In the latter case, the definition is necessarily defective, because we can never be fully certain of the completeness of our analysis. For these reasons, the method of definition employed in mathematics cannot be imitated in philosophy.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","text":"2. Of Axioms. These, in so far as they are immediately certain, are à priori synthetical principles. Now, one conception cannot be connected synthetically and yet immediately with another; because, if we wish to proceed out of and beyond a conception, a third mediating cognition is necessary. And, as philosophy is a cognition of reason by the aid of conceptions alone, there is to be found in it no principle which deserves to be called an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, may possess axioms, because it can always connect the predicates of an object à priori, and without any mediating term, by means of the construction of conceptions in intuition. Such is the case with the proposition: Three points can always lie in a plane. On the other hand, no synthetical principle which is based upon conceptions, can ever be immediately certain (for example, the proposition: Everything that happens has a cause), because I require a mediating term to connect the two conceptions of event and cause—namely, the condition of time-determination in an experience, and I cannot cognize any such principle immediately and from conceptions alone. Discursive principles are, accordingly, very different from intuitive principles or axioms. The former always require deduction, which in the case of the latter may be altogether dispensed with. Axioms are, for this reason, always self-evident, while philosophical principles, whatever may be the degree of certainty they possess, cannot lay any claim to such a distinction. No synthetical proposition of pure transcendental reason can be so evident, as is often rashly enough declared, as the statement, twice two are four. It is true that in the Analytic I introduced into the list of principles of the pure understanding, certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there discussed was not itself an axiom, but served merely to present the principle of the possibility of axioms in general, while it was really nothing more than a principle based upon conceptions. For it is one part of the duty of transcendental philosophy to establish the possibility of mathematics itself. Philosophy possesses, then, no axioms, and has no right to impose its à priori principles upon thought, until it has established their authority and validity by a thoroughgoing deduction.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","text":"3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, based upon intuition, can be termed a demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but it cannot convince us that it might not have been otherwise. Hence a proof upon empirical grounds cannot be apodeictic. À priori conceptions, in discursive cognition, can never produce intuitive certainty or evidence, however certain the judgement they present may be. Mathematics alone, therefore, contains demonstrations, because it does not deduce its cognition from conceptions, but from the construction of conceptions, that is, from intuition, which can be given à priori in accordance with conceptions. The method of algebra, in equations, from which the correct answer is deduced by reduction, is a kind of construction—not geometrical, but by symbols—in which all conceptions, especially those of the relations of quantities, are represented in intuition by signs; and thus the conclusions in that science are secured from errors by the fact that every proof is submitted to ocular evidence. Philosophical cognition does not possess this advantage, it being required to consider the general always in abstracto (by means of conceptions), while mathematics can always consider it in concreto (in an individual intuition), and at the same time by means of à priori representation, whereby all errors are rendered manifest to the senses. The former—discursive proofs—ought to be termed acroamatic proofs, rather than demonstrations, as only words are employed in them, while demonstrations proper, as the term itself indicates, always require a reference to the intuition of the object.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","text":"It follows from all these considerations that it is not consonant with the nature of philosophy, especially in the sphere of pure reason, to employ the dogmatical method, and to adorn itself with the titles and insignia of mathematical science. It does not belong to that order, and can only hope for a fraternal union with that science. Its attempts at mathematical evidence are vain pretensions, which can only keep it back from its true aim, which is to detect the illusory procedure of reason when transgressing its proper limits, and by fully explaining and analysing our conceptions, to conduct us from the dim regions of speculation to the clear region of modest self-knowledge. Reason must not, therefore, in its transcendental endeavours, look forward with such confidence, as if the path it is pursuing led straight to its aim, nor reckon with such security upon its premisses, as to consider it unnecessary to take a step back, or to keep a strict watch for errors, which, overlooked in the principles, may be detected in the arguments themselves—in which case it may be requisite either to determine these principles with greater strictness, or to change them entirely.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0021","text":"I divide all apodeictic propositions, whether demonstrable or immediately certain, into dogmata and mathemata. A direct synthetical proposition, based on conceptions, is a dogma; a proposition of the same kind, based on the construction of conceptions, is a mathema. Analytical judgements do not teach us any more about an object than what was contained in the conception we had of it; because they do not extend our cognition beyond our conception of an object, they merely elucidate the conception. They cannot therefore be with propriety termed dogmas. Of the two kinds of à priori synthetical propositions above mentioned, only those which are employed in philosophy can, according to the general mode of speech, bear this name; those of arithmetic or geometry would not be rightly so denominated. Thus the customary mode of speaking confirms the explanation given above, and the conclusion arrived at, that only those judgements which are based upon conceptions, not on the construction of conceptions, can be termed dogmatical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","text":"Thus, pure reason, in the sphere of speculation, does not contain a single direct synthetical judgement based upon conceptions. By means of ideas, it is, as we have shown, incapable of producing synthetical judgements, which are objectively valid; by means of the conceptions of the understanding, it establishes certain indubitable principles, not, however, directly on the basis of conceptions, but only indirectly by means of the relation of these conceptions to something of a purely contingent nature, namely, possible experience. When experience is presupposed, these principles are apodeictically certain, but in themselves, and directly, they cannot even be cognized à priori. Thus the given conceptions of cause and event will not be sufficient for the demonstration of the proposition: Every event has a cause. For this reason, it is not a dogma; although from another point of view, that of experience, it is capable of being proved to demonstration. The proper term for such a proposition is principle, and not theorem (although it does require to be proved), because it possesses the remarkable peculiarity of being the condition of the possibility of its own ground of proof, that is, experience, and of forming a necessary presupposition in all empirical observation.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","text":"If then, in the speculative sphere of pure reason, no dogmata are to be found; all dogmatical methods, whether borrowed from mathematics, or invented by philosophical thinkers, are alike inappropriate and inefficient. They only serve to conceal errors and fallacies, and to deceive philosophy, whose duty it is to see that reason pursues a safe and straight path. A philosophical method may, however, be systematical. For our reason is, subjectively considered, itself a system, and, in the sphere of mere conceptions, a system of investigation according to principles of unity, the material being supplied by experience alone. But this is not the proper place for discussing the peculiar method of transcendental philosophy, as our present task is simply to examine whether our faculties are capable of erecting an edifice on the basis of pure reason, and how far they may proceed with the materials at their command.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"544b8adaea96bd4541fe7e3fc67a1ac8126eb3a61916c476bd874545eb4fd284","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","order":86,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_5","text":"# Discipline\n\n### Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics\n\nReason must be subject, in all its operations, to criticism, which must always be permitted to exercise its functions without restraint; otherwise its interests are imperilled and its influence obnoxious to suspicion. There is nothing, however useful, however sacred it may be, that can claim exemption from the searching examination of this supreme tribunal, which has no respect of persons. The very existence of reason depends upon this freedom; for the voice of reason is not that of a dictatorial and despotic power, it is rather like the vote of the citizens of a free state, every member of which must have the privilege of giving free expression to his doubts, and possess even the right of veto.\n\nBut while reason can never decline to submit itself to the tribunal of criticism, it has not always cause to dread the judgement of this court. Pure reason, however, when engaged in the sphere of dogmatism, is not so thoroughly conscious of a strict observance of its highest laws, as to appear before a higher judicial reason with perfect confidence. On the contrary, it must renounce its magnificent dogmatical pretensions in philosophy.\n\nVery different is the case when it has to defend itself, not before a judge, but against an equal. If dogmatical assertions are advanced on the negative side, in opposition to those made by reason on the positive side, its justification kat authrhopon is complete, although the proof of its propositions is kat aletheian unsatisfactory.\n\nBy the polemic of pure reason I mean the defence of its propositions made by reason, in opposition to the dogmatical counter-propositions advanced by other parties. The question here is not whether its own statements may not also be false; it merely regards the fact that reason proves that the opposite cannot be established with demonstrative certainty, nor even asserted with a higher degree of probability. Reason does not hold her possessions upon sufferance; for, although she cannot show a perfectly satisfactory title to them, no one can prove that she is not the rightful possessor.\n\nIt is a melancholy reflection that reason, in its highest exercise, falls into an antithetic; and that the supreme tribunal for the settlement of differences should not be at union with itself. It is true that we had to discuss the question of an apparent antithetic, but we found that it was based upon a misconception. In conformity with the common prejudice, phenomena were regarded as things in themselves, and thus an absolute completeness in their synthesis was required in the one mode or in the other (it was shown to be impossible in both); a demand entirely out of place in regard to phenomena. There was, then, no real self-contradiction of reason in the propositions: The series of phenomena given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: This series is absolutely and in itself without beginning. The two propositions are perfectly consistent with each other, because phenomena as phenomena are in themselves nothing, and consequently the hypothesis that they are things in themselves must lead to self-contradictory inferences.\n\nBut there are cases in which a similar misunderstanding cannot be provided against, and the dispute must remain unsettled. Take, for example, the theistic proposition: There is a Supreme Being; and on the other hand, the atheistic counter-statement: There exists no Supreme Being; or, in psychology: Everything that thinks possesses the attribute of absolute and permanent unity, which is utterly different from the transitory unity of material phenomena; and the counter-proposition: The soul is not an immaterial unity, and its nature is transitory, like that of phenomena. The objects of these questions contain no heterogeneous or contradictory elements, for they relate to things in themselves, and not to phenomena. There would arise, indeed, a real contradiction, if reason came forward with a statement on the negative side of these questions alone. As regards the criticism to which the grounds of proof on the affirmative side must be subjected, it may be freely admitted, without necessitating the surrender of the affirmative propositions, which have, at least, the interest of reason in their favour—an advantage which the opposite party cannot lay claim to.\n\nI cannot agree with the opinion of several admirable thinkers—Sulzer among the rest—that, in spite of the weakness of the arguments hitherto in use, we may hope, one day, to see sufficient demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of pure reason—the existence of a Supreme Being, and the immortality of the soul. I am certain, on the contrary, that this will never be the case. For on what ground can reason base such synthetical propositions, which do not relate to the objects of experience and their internal possibility? But it is also demonstratively certain that no one will ever be able to maintain the contrary with the least show of probability. For, as he can attempt such a proof solely upon the basis of pure reason, he is bound to prove that a Supreme Being, and a thinking subject in the character of a pure intelligence, are impossible. But where will he find the knowledge which can enable him to enounce synthetical judgements in regard to things which transcend the region of experience? We may, therefore, rest assured that the opposite never will be demonstrated. We need not, then, have recourse to scholastic arguments; we may always admit the truth of those propositions which are consistent with the speculative interests of reason in the sphere of experience, and form, moreover, the only means of uniting the speculative with the practical interest. Our opponent, who must not be considered here as a critic solely, we can be ready to meet with a non liquet which cannot fail to disconcert him; while we cannot deny his right to a similar retort, as we have on our side the advantage of the support of the subjective maxim of reason, and can therefore look upon all his sophistical arguments with calm indifference.\n\nFrom this point of view, there is properly no antithetic of pure reason. For the only arena for such a struggle would be upon the field of pure theology and psychology; but on this ground there can appear no combatant whom we need to fear. Ridicule and boasting can be his only weapons; and these may be laughed at, as mere child’s play. This consideration restores to Reason her courage; for what source of confidence could be found, if she, whose vocation it is to destroy error, were at variance with herself and without any reasonable hope of ever reaching a state of permanent repose?\n\nEverything in nature is good for some purpose. Even poisons are serviceable; they destroy the evil effects of other poisons generated in our system, and must always find a place in every complete pharmacopoeia. The objections raised against the fallacies and sophistries of speculative reason, are objections given by the nature of this reason itself, and must therefore have a destination and purpose which can only be for the good of humanity. For what purpose has Providence raised many objects, in which we have the deepest interest, so far above us, that we vainly try to cognize them with certainty, and our powers of mental vision are rather excited than satisfied by the glimpses we may chance to seize? It is very doubtful whether it is for our benefit to advance bold affirmations regarding subjects involved in such obscurity; perhaps it would even be detrimental to our best interests. But it is undoubtedly always beneficial to leave the investigating, as well as the critical reason, in perfect freedom, and permit it to take charge of its own interests, which are advanced as much by its limitation, as by its extension of its views, and which always suffer by the interference of foreign powers forcing it, against its natural tendencies, to bend to certain preconceived designs.\n\nAllow your opponent to say what he thinks reasonable, and combat him only with the weapons of reason. Have no anxiety for the practical interests of humanity—these are never imperilled in a purely speculative dispute. Such a dispute serves merely to disclose the antinomy of reason, which, as it has its source in the nature of reason, ought to be thoroughly investigated. Reason is benefited by the examination of a subject on both sides, and its judgements are corrected by being limited. It is not the matter that may give occasion to dispute, but the manner. For it is perfectly permissible to employ, in the presence of reason, the language of a firmly rooted faith, even after we have been obliged to renounce all pretensions to knowledge.\n\nIf we were to ask the dispassionate David Hume—a philosopher endowed, in a degree that few are, with a well-balanced judgement: What motive induced you to spend so much labour and thought in undermining the consoling and beneficial persuasion that reason is capable of assuring us of the existence, and presenting us with a determinate conception of a Supreme Being?—his answer would be: Nothing but the desire of teaching reason to know its own powers better, and, at the same time, a dislike of the procedure by which that faculty was compelled to support foregone conclusions, and prevented from confessing the internal weaknesses which it cannot but feel when it enters upon a rigid self-examination. If, on the other hand, we were to ask Priestley—a philosopher who had no taste for transcendental speculation, but was entirely devoted to the principles of empiricism—what his motives were for overturning those two main pillars of religion—the doctrines of the freedom of the will and the immortality of the soul (in his view the hope of a future life is but the expectation of the miracle of resurrection)—this philosopher, himself a zealous and pious teacher of religion, could give no other answer than this: I acted in the interest of reason, which always suffers, when certain objects are explained and judged by a reference to other supposed laws than those of material nature—the only laws which we know in a determinate manner. It would be unfair to decry the latter philosopher, who endeavoured to harmonize his paradoxical opinions with the interests of religion, and to undervalue an honest and reflecting man, because he finds himself at a loss the moment he has left the field of natural science. The same grace must be accorded to Hume, a man not less well-disposed, and quite as blameless in his moral character, and who pushed his abstract speculations to an extreme length, because, as he rightly believed, the object of them lies entirely beyond the bounds of natural science, and within the sphere of pure ideas.\n\nWhat is to be done to provide against the danger which seems in the present case to menace the best interests of humanity? The course to be pursued in reference to this subject is a perfectly plain and natural one. Let each thinker pursue his own path; if he shows talent, if he gives evidence of profound thought, in one word, if he shows that he possesses the power of reasoning—reason is always the gainer. If you have recourse to other means, if you attempt to coerce reason, if you raise the cry of treason to humanity, if you excite the feelings of the crowd, which can neither understand nor sympathize with such subtle speculations—you will only make yourselves ridiculous. For the question does not concern the advantage or disadvantage which we are expected to reap from such inquiries; the question is merely how far reason can advance in the field of speculation, apart from all kinds of interest, and whether we may depend upon the exertions of speculative reason, or must renounce all reliance on it. Instead of joining the combatants, it is your part to be a tranquil spectator of the struggle—a laborious struggle for the parties engaged, but attended, in its progress as well as in its result, with the most advantageous consequences for the interests of thought and knowledge. It is absurd to expect to be enlightened by Reason, and at the same time to prescribe to her what side of the question she must adopt. Moreover, reason is sufficiently held in check by its own power, the limits imposed on it by its own nature are sufficient; it is unnecessary for you to place over it additional guards, as if its power were dangerous to the constitution of the intellectual state. In the dialectic of reason there is no victory gained which need in the least disturb your tranquility.\n\nThe strife of dialectic is a necessity of reason, and we cannot but wish that it had been conducted long ere this with that perfect freedom which ought to be its essential condition. In this case, we should have had at an earlier period a matured and profound criticism, which must have put an end to all dialectical disputes, by exposing the illusions and prejudices in which they originated.\n\nThere is in human nature an unworthy propensity—a propensity which, like everything that springs from nature, must in its final purpose be conducive to the good of humanity—to conceal our real sentiments, and to give expression only to certain received opinions, which are regarded as at once safe and promotive of the common good. It is true, this tendency, not only to conceal our real sentiments, but to profess those which may gain us favour in the eyes of society, has not only civilized, but, in a certain measure, moralized us; as no one can break through the outward covering of respectability, honour, and morality, and thus the seemingly-good examples which we see around us form an excellent school for moral improvement, so long as our belief in their genuineness remains unshaken. But this disposition to represent ourselves as better than we are, and to utter opinions which are not our own, can be nothing more than a kind of provisionary arrangement of nature to lead us from the rudeness of an uncivilized state, and to teach us how to assume at least the appearance and manner of the good we see. But when true principles have been developed, and have obtained a sure foundation in our habit of thought, this conventionalism must be attacked with earnest vigour, otherwise it corrupts the heart, and checks the growth of good dispositions with the mischievous weed of fair appearances.\n\nI am sorry to remark the same tendency to misrepresentation and hypocrisy in the sphere of speculative discussion, where there is less temptation to restrain the free expression of thought. For what can be more prejudicial to the interests of intelligence than to falsify our real sentiments, to conceal the doubts which we feel in regard to our statements, or to maintain the validity of grounds of proof which we well know to be insufficient? So long as mere personal vanity is the source of these unworthy artifices—and this is generally the case in speculative discussions, which are mostly destitute of practical interest, and are incapable of complete demonstration—the vanity of the opposite party exaggerates as much on the other side; and thus the result is the same, although it is not brought about so soon as if the dispute had been conducted in a sincere and upright spirit. But where the mass entertains the notion that the aim of certain subtle speculators is nothing less than to shake the very foundations of public welfare and morality—it seems not only prudent, but even praise worthy, to maintain the good cause by illusory arguments, rather than to give to our supposed opponents the advantage of lowering our declarations to the moderate tone of a merely practical conviction, and of compelling us to confess our inability to attain to apodeictic certainty in speculative subjects. But we ought to reflect that there is nothing, in the world more fatal to the maintenance of a good cause than deceit, misrepresentation, and falsehood. That the strictest laws of honesty should be observed in the discussion of a purely speculative subject is the least requirement that can be made. If we could reckon with security even upon so little, the conflict of speculative reason regarding the important questions of God, immortality, and freedom, would have been either decided long ago, or would very soon be brought to a conclusion. But, in general, the uprightness of the defence stands in an inverse ratio to the goodness of the cause; and perhaps more honesty and fairness are shown by those who deny than by those who uphold these doctrines.\n\nI shall persuade myself, then, that I have readers who do not wish to see a righteous cause defended by unfair arguments. Such will now recognize the fact that, according to the principles of this Critique, if we consider not what is, but what ought to be the case, there can be really no polemic of pure reason. For how can two persons dispute about a thing, the reality of which neither can present in actual or even in possible experience? Each adopts the plan of meditating on his idea for the purpose of drawing from the idea, if he can, what is more than the idea, that is, the reality of the object which it indicates. How shall they settle the dispute, since neither is able to make his assertions directly comprehensible and certain, but must restrict himself to attacking and confuting those of his opponent? All statements enounced by pure reason transcend the conditions of possible experience, beyond the sphere of which we can discover no criterion of truth, while they are at the same time framed in accordance with the laws of the understanding, which are applicable only to experience; and thus it is the fate of all such speculative discussions that while the one party attacks the weaker side of his opponent, he infallibly lays open his own weaknesses.\n\nThe critique of pure reason may be regarded as the highest tribunal for all speculative disputes; for it is not involved in these disputes, which have an immediate relation to certain objects and not to the laws of the mind, but is instituted for the purpose of determining the rights and limits of reason.\n\nWithout the control of criticism, reason is, as it were, in a state of nature, and can only establish its claims and assertions by war. Criticism, on the contrary, deciding all questions according to the fundamental laws of its own institution, secures to us the peace of law and order, and enables us to discuss all differences in the more tranquil manner of a legal process. In the former case, disputes are ended by victory, which both sides may claim and which is followed by a hollow armistice; in the latter, by a sentence, which, as it strikes at the root of all speculative differences, ensures to all concerned a lasting peace. The endless disputes of a dogmatizing reason compel us to look for some mode of arriving at a settled decision by a critical investigation of reason itself; just as Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and that we must leave it and submit ourselves to the constraint of law, which indeed limits individual freedom, but only that it may consist with the freedom of others and with the common good of all.\n\nThis freedom will, among other things, permit of our openly stating the difficulties and doubts which we are ourselves unable to solve, without being decried on that account as turbulent and dangerous citizens. This privilege forms part of the native rights of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than the universal reason of humanity; and as this reason is the source of all progress and improvement, such a privilege is to be held sacred and inviolable. It is unwise, moreover, to denounce as dangerous any bold assertions against, or rash attacks upon, an opinion which is held by the largest and most moral class of the community; for that would be giving them an importance which they do not deserve. When I hear that the freedom of the will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God have been overthrown by the arguments of some able writer, I feel a strong desire to read his book; for I expect that he will add to my knowledge and impart greater clearness and distinctness to my views by the argumentative power shown in his writings. But I am perfectly certain, even before I have opened the book, that he has not succeeded in a single point, not because I believe I am in possession of irrefutable demonstrations of these important propositions, but because this transcendental critique, which has disclosed to me the power and the limits of pure reason, has fully convinced me that, as it is insufficient to establish the affirmative, it is as powerless, and even more so, to assure us of the truth of the negative answer to these questions. From what source does this free-thinker derive his knowledge that there is, for example, no Supreme Being? This proposition lies out of the field of possible experience, and, therefore, beyond the limits of human cognition. But I would not read at, all the answer which the dogmatical maintainer of the good cause makes to his opponent, because I know well beforehand, that he will merely attack the fallacious grounds of his adversary, without being able to establish his own assertions. Besides, a new illusory argument, in the construction of which talent and acuteness are shown, is suggestive of new ideas and new trains of reasoning, and in this respect the old and everyday sophistries are quite useless. Again, the dogmatical opponent of religion gives employment to criticism, and enables us to test and correct its principles, while there is no occasion for anxiety in regard to the influence and results of his reasoning.\n\nBut, it will be said, must we not warn the youth entrusted to academical care against such writings, must we not preserve them from the knowledge of these dangerous assertions, until their judgement is ripened, or rather until the doctrines which we wish to inculcate are so firmly rooted in their minds as to withstand all attempts at instilling the contrary dogmas, from whatever quarter they may come?\n\nIf we are to confine ourselves to the dogmatical procedure in the sphere of pure reason, and find ourselves unable to settle such disputes otherwise than by becoming a party in them, and setting counter-assertions against the statements advanced by our opponents, there is certainly no plan more advisable for the moment, but, at the same time, none more absurd and inefficient for the future, than this retaining of the youthful mind under guardianship for a time, and thus preserving it—for so long at least—from seduction into error. But when, at a later period, either curiosity, or the prevalent fashion of thought places such writings in their hands, will the so-called convictions of their youth stand firm? The young thinker, who has in his armoury none but dogmatical weapons with which to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who cannot detect the latent dialectic which lies in his own opinions as well as in those of the opposite party, sees the advance of illusory arguments and grounds of proof which have the advantage of novelty, against as illusory grounds of proof destitute of this advantage, and which, perhaps, excite the suspicion that the natural credulity of his youth has been abused by his instructors. He thinks he can find no better means of showing that he has out grown the discipline of his minority than by despising those well-meant warnings, and, knowing no system of thought but that of dogmatism, he drinks deep draughts of the poison that is to sap the principles in which his early years were trained.\n\nExactly the opposite of the system here recommended ought to be pursued in academical instruction. This can only be effected, however, by a thorough training in the critical investigation of pure reason. For, in order to bring the principles of this critique into exercise as soon as possible, and to demonstrate their perfect even in the presence of the highest degree of dialectical illusion, the student ought to examine the assertions made on both sides of speculative questions step by step, and to test them by these principles. It cannot be a difficult task for him to show the fallacies inherent in these propositions, and thus he begins early to feel his own power of securing himself against the influence of such sophistical arguments, which must finally lose, for him, all their illusory power. And, although the same blows which overturn the edifice of his opponent are as fatal to his own speculative structures, if such he has wished to rear; he need not feel any sorrow in regard to this seeming misfortune, as he has now before him a fair prospect into the practical region in which he may reasonably hope to find a more secure foundation for a rational system.\n\nThere is, accordingly, no proper polemic in the sphere of pure reason. Both parties beat the air and fight with their own shadows, as they pass beyond the limits of nature, and can find no tangible point of attack—no firm footing for their dogmatical conflict. Fight as vigorously as they may, the shadows which they hew down, immediately start up again, like the heroes in Walhalla, and renew the bloodless and unceasing contest.\n\nBut neither can we admit that there is any proper sceptical employment of pure reason, such as might be based upon the principle of neutrality in all speculative disputes. To excite reason against itself, to place weapons in the hands of the party on the one side as well as in those of the other, and to remain an undisturbed and sarcastic spectator of the fierce struggle that ensues, seems, from the dogmatical point of view, to be a part fitting only a malevolent disposition. But, when the sophist evidences an invincible obstinacy and blindness, and a pride which no criticism can moderate, there is no other practicable course than to oppose to this pride and obstinacy similar feelings and pretensions on the other side, equally well or ill founded, so that reason, staggered by the reflections thus forced upon it, finds it necessary to moderate its confidence in such pretensions and to listen to the advice of criticism. But we cannot stop at these doubts, much less regard the conviction of our ignorance, not only as a cure for the conceit natural to dogmatism, but as the settlement of the disputes in which reason is involved with itself. On the contrary, scepticism is merely a means of awakening reason from its dogmatic dreams and exciting it to a more careful investigation into its own powers and pretensions. But, as scepticism appears to be the shortest road to a permanent peace in the domain of philosophy, and as it is the track pursued by the many who aim at giving a philosophical colouring to their contemptuous dislike of all inquiries of this kind, I think it necessary to present to my readers this mode of thought in its true light.\n\n_Scepticism not a Permanent State for Human Reason._\n\nThe consciousness of ignorance—unless this ignorance is recognized to be absolutely necessary ought, instead of forming the conclusion of my inquiries, to be the strongest motive to the pursuit of them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things or of the limits of knowledge. If my ignorance is accidental and not necessary, it must incite me, in the first case, to a dogmatical inquiry regarding the objects of which I am ignorant; in the second, to a critical investigation into the bounds of all possible knowledge. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary and unavoidable, and that it consequently absolves from the duty of all further investigation, is a fact which cannot be made out upon empirical grounds—from observation—but upon critical grounds alone, that is, by a thoroughgoing investigation into the primary sources of cognition. It follows that the determination of the bounds of reason can be made only on à priori grounds; while the empirical limitation of reason, which is merely an indeterminate cognition of an ignorance that can never be completely removed, can take place only à posteriori. In other words, our empirical knowledge is limited by that which yet remains for us to know. The former cognition of our ignorance, which is possible only on a rational basis, is a science; the latter is merely a perception, and we cannot say how far the inferences drawn from it may extend. If I regard the earth, as it really appears to my senses, as a flat surface, I am ignorant how far this surface extends. But experience teaches me that, how far soever I go, I always see before me a space in which I can proceed farther; and thus I know the limits—merely visual—of my actual knowledge of the earth, although I am ignorant of the limits of the earth itself. But if I have got so far as to know that the earth is a sphere, and that its surface is spherical, I can cognize à priori and determine upon principles, from my knowledge of a small part of this surface—say to the extent of a degree—the diameter and circumference of the earth; and although I am ignorant of the objects which this surface contains, I have a perfect knowledge of its limits and extent.\n\nThe sum of all the possible objects of our cognition seems to us to be a level surface, with an apparent horizon—that which forms the limit of its extent, and which has been termed by us the idea of unconditioned totality. To reach this limit by empirical means is impossible, and all attempts to determine it à priori according to a principle, are alike in vain. But all the questions raised by pure reason relate to that which lies beyond this horizon, or, at least, in its boundary line.\n\nThe celebrated David Hume was one of those geographers of human reason who believe that they have given a sufficient answer to all such questions by declaring them to lie beyond the horizon of our knowledge—a horizon which, however, Hume was unable to determine. His attention especially was directed to the principle of causality; and he remarked with perfect justice that the truth of this principle, and even the objective validity of the conception of a cause, was not commonly based upon clear insight, that is, upon à priori cognition. Hence he concluded that this law does not derive its authority from its universality and necessity, but merely from its general applicability in the course of experience, and a kind of subjective necessity thence arising, which he termed habit. From the inability of reason to establish this principle as a necessary law for the acquisition of all experience, he inferred the nullity of all the attempts of reason to pass the region of the empirical.\n\nThis procedure of subjecting the facta of reason to examination, and, if necessary, to disapproval, may be termed the censura of reason. This censura must inevitably lead us to doubts regarding all transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step in our inquiry. The first step in regard to the subjects of pure reason, and which marks the infancy of that faculty, is that of dogmatism. The second, which we have just mentioned, is that of scepticism, and it gives evidence that our judgement has been improved by experience. But a third step is necessary—indicative of the maturity and manhood of the judgement, which now lays a firm foundation upon universal and necessary principles. This is the period of criticism, in which we do not examine the facta of reason, but reason itself, in the whole extent of its powers, and in regard to its capability of à priori cognition; and thus we determine not merely the empirical and ever-shifting bounds of our knowledge, but its necessary and eternal limits. We demonstrate from indubitable principles, not merely our ignorance in respect to this or that subject, but in regard to all possible questions of a certain class. Thus scepticism is a resting place for reason, in which it may reflect on its dogmatical wanderings and gain some knowledge of the region in which it happens to be, that it may pursue its way with greater certainty; but it cannot be its permanent dwelling-place. It must take up its abode only in the region of complete certitude, whether this relates to the cognition of objects themselves, or to the limits which bound all our cognition.\n\nReason is not to be considered as an indefinitely extended plane, of the bounds of which we have only a general knowledge; it ought rather to be compared to a sphere, the radius of which may be found from the curvature of its surface—that is, the nature of à priori synthetical propositions—and, consequently, its circumference and extent. Beyond the sphere of experience there are no objects which it can cognize; nay, even questions regarding such supposititious objects relate only to the subjective principles of a complete determination of the relations which exist between the understanding-conceptions which lie within this sphere.\n\nWe are actually in possession of à priori synthetical cognitions, as is proved by the existence of the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience. If any one cannot comprehend the possibility of these principles, he may have some reason to doubt whether they are really à priori; but he cannot on this account declare them to be impossible, and affirm the nullity of the steps which reason may have taken under their guidance. He can only say: If we perceived their origin and their authenticity, we should be able to determine the extent and limits of reason; but, till we can do this, all propositions regarding the latter are mere random assertions. In this view, the doubt respecting all dogmatical philosophy, which proceeds without the guidance of criticism, is well grounded; but we cannot therefore deny to reason the ability to construct a sound philosophy, when the way has been prepared by a thorough critical investigation. All the conceptions produced, and all the questions raised, by pure reason, do not lie in the sphere of experience, but in that of reason itself, and hence they must be solved, and shown to be either valid or inadmissible, by that faculty. We have no right to decline the solution of such problems, on the ground that the solution can be discovered only from the nature of things, and under pretence of the limitation of human faculties, for reason is the sole creator of all these ideas, and is therefore bound either to establish their validity or to expose their illusory nature.\n\nThe polemic of scepticism is properly directed against the dogmatist, who erects a system of philosophy without having examined the fundamental objective principles on which it is based, for the purpose of evidencing the futility of his designs, and thus bringing him to a knowledge of his own powers. But, in itself, scepticism does not give us any certain information in regard to the bounds of our knowledge. All unsuccessful dogmatical attempts of reason are facia, which it is always useful to submit to the censure of the sceptic. But this cannot help us to any decision regarding the expectations which reason cherishes of better success in future endeavours; the investigations of scepticism cannot, therefore, settle the dispute regarding the rights and powers of human reason.\n\nHume is perhaps the ablest and most ingenious of all sceptical philosophers, and his writings have, undoubtedly, exerted the most powerful influence in awakening reason to a thorough investigation into its own powers. It will, therefore, well repay our labours to consider for a little the course of reasoning which he followed and the errors into which he strayed, although setting out on the path of truth and certitude.\n\nHume was probably aware, although he never clearly developed the notion, that we proceed in judgements of a certain class beyond our conception of the object. I have termed this kind of judgement synthetical. As regard the manner in which I pass beyond my conception by the aid of experience, no doubts can be entertained. Experience is itself a synthesis of perceptions; and it employs perceptions to increment the conception, which I obtain by means of another perception. But we feel persuaded that we are able to proceed beyond a conception, and to extend our cognition à priori. We attempt this in two ways—either, through the pure understanding, in relation to that which may become an object of experience, or, through pure reason, in relation to such properties of things, or of the existence of things, as can never be presented in any experience. This sceptical philosopher did not distinguish these two kinds of judgements, as he ought to have done, but regarded this augmentation of conceptions, and, if we may so express ourselves, the spontaneous generation of understanding and reason, independently of the impregnation of experience, as altogether impossible. The so-called à priori principles of these faculties he consequently held to be invalid and imaginary, and regarded them as nothing but subjective habits of thought originating in experience, and therefore purely empirical and contingent rules, to which we attribute a spurious necessity and universality. In support of this strange assertion, he referred us to the generally acknowledged principle of the relation between cause and effect. No faculty of the mind can conduct us from the conception of a thing to the existence of something else; and hence he believed he could infer that, without experience, we possess no source from which we can augment a conception, and no ground sufficient to justify us in framing a judgement that is to extend our cognition à priori. That the light of the sun, which shines upon a piece of wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens clay, no power of the understanding could infer from the conceptions which we previously possessed of these substances; much less is there any à priori law that could conduct us to such a conclusion, which experience alone can certify. On the other hand, we have seen in our discussion of transcendental logic, that, although we can never proceed immediately beyond the content of the conception which is given us, we can always cognize completely à priori—in relation, however, to a third term, namely, possible experience—the law of its connection with other things. For example, if I observe that a piece of wax melts, I can cognize à priori that there must have been something (the sun’s heat) preceding, which this law; although, without the aid of experience, I could not cognize à priori and in a determinate manner either the cause from the effect, or the effect from the cause. Hume was, therefore, wrong in inferring, from the contingency of the determination according to law, the contingency of the law itself; and the passing beyond the conception of a thing to possible experience (which is an à priori proceeding, constituting the objective reality of the conception), he confounded with our synthesis of objects in actual experience, which is always, of course, empirical. Thus, too, he regarded the principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and indicates a necessary connection, as a mere rule of association, lying in the imitative faculty of imagination, which can present only contingent, and not objective connections.\n\nThe sceptical errors of this remarkably acute thinker arose principally from a defect, which was common to him with the dogmatists, namely, that he had never made a systematic review of all the different kinds of à priori synthesis performed by the understanding. Had he done so, he would have found, to take one example among many, that the principle of permanence was of this character, and that it, as well as the principle of causality, anticipates experience. In this way he might have been able to describe the determinate limits of the à priori operations of understanding and reason. But he merely declared the understanding to be limited, instead of showing what its limits were; he created a general mistrust in the power of our faculties, without giving us any determinate knowledge of the bounds of our necessary and unavoidable ignorance; he examined and condemned some of the principles of the understanding, without investigating all its powers with the completeness necessary to criticism. He denies, with truth, certain powers to the understanding, but he goes further, and declares it to be utterly inadequate to the à priori extension of knowledge, although he has not fully examined all the powers which reside in the faculty; and thus the fate which always overtakes scepticism meets him too. That is to say, his own declarations are doubted, for his objections were based upon facta, which are contingent, and not upon principles, which can alone demonstrate the necessary invalidity of all dogmatical assertions.\n\nAs Hume makes no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which, however, his attacks are mainly directed, reason does not feel itself shut out from all attempts at the extension of à priori cognition, and hence it refuses, in spite of a few checks in this or that quarter, to relinquish such efforts. For one naturally arms oneself to resist an attack, and becomes more obstinate in the resolve to establish the claims he has advanced. But a complete review of the powers of reason, and the conviction thence arising that we are in possession of a limited field of action, while we must admit the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all doubt and dispute, and induces reason to rest satisfied with the undisturbed possession of its limited domain.\n\nTo the uncritical dogmatist, who has not surveyed the sphere of his understanding, nor determined, in accordance with principles, the limits of possible cognition, who, consequently, is ignorant of his own powers, and believes he will discover them by the attempts he makes in the field of cognition, these attacks of scepticism are not only dangerous, but destructive. For if there is one proposition in his chain of reasoning which he cannot prove, or the fallacy in which he cannot evolve in accordance with a principle, suspicion falls on all his statements, however plausible they may appear.\n\nAnd thus scepticism, the bane of dogmatical philosophy, conducts us to a sound investigation into the understanding and the reason. When we are thus far advanced, we need fear no further attacks; for the limits of our domain are clearly marked out, and we can make no claims nor become involved in any disputes regarding the region that lies beyond these limits. Thus the sceptical procedure in philosophy does not present any solution of the problems of reason, but it forms an excellent exercise for its powers, awakening its circumspection, and indicating the means whereby it may most fully establish its claims to its legitimate possessions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","text":"# Discipline\n\n### Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics\n\nReason must be subject, in all its operations, to criticism, which must always be permitted to exercise its functions without restraint; otherwise its interests are imperilled and its influence obnoxious to suspicion. There is nothing, however useful, however sacred it may be, that can claim exemption from the searching examination of this supreme tribunal, which has no respect of persons. The very existence of reason depends upon this freedom; for the voice of reason is not that of a dictatorial and despotic power, it is rather like the vote of the citizens of a free state, every member of which must have the privilege of giving free expression to his doubts, and possess even the right of veto.\n\nBut while reason can never decline to submit itself to the tribunal of criticism, it has not always cause to dread the judgement of this court. Pure reason, however, when engaged in the sphere of dogmatism, is not so thoroughly conscious of a strict observance of its highest laws, as to appear before a higher judicial reason with perfect confidence. On the contrary, it must renounce its magnificent dogmatical pretensions in philosophy.\n\nVery different is the case when it has to defend itself, not before a judge, but against an equal. If dogmatical assertions are advanced on the negative side, in opposition to those made by reason on the positive side, its justification kat authrhopon is complete, although the proof of its propositions is kat aletheian unsatisfactory.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","text":"By the polemic of pure reason I mean the defence of its propositions made by reason, in opposition to the dogmatical counter-propositions advanced by other parties. The question here is not whether its own statements may not also be false; it merely regards the fact that reason proves that the opposite cannot be established with demonstrative certainty, nor even asserted with a higher degree of probability. Reason does not hold her possessions upon sufferance; for, although she cannot show a perfectly satisfactory title to them, no one can prove that she is not the rightful possessor.\n\nIt is a melancholy reflection that reason, in its highest exercise, falls into an antithetic; and that the supreme tribunal for the settlement of differences should not be at union with itself. It is true that we had to discuss the question of an apparent antithetic, but we found that it was based upon a misconception. In conformity with the common prejudice, phenomena were regarded as things in themselves, and thus an absolute completeness in their synthesis was required in the one mode or in the other (it was shown to be impossible in both); a demand entirely out of place in regard to phenomena. There was, then, no real self-contradiction of reason in the propositions: The series of phenomena given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: This series is absolutely and in itself without beginning. The two propositions are perfectly consistent with each other, because phenomena as phenomena are in themselves nothing, and consequently the hypothesis that they are things in themselves must lead to self-contradictory inferences.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0003","text":"But there are cases in which a similar misunderstanding cannot be provided against, and the dispute must remain unsettled. Take, for example, the theistic proposition: There is a Supreme Being; and on the other hand, the atheistic counter-statement: There exists no Supreme Being; or, in psychology: Everything that thinks possesses the attribute of absolute and permanent unity, which is utterly different from the transitory unity of material phenomena; and the counter-proposition: The soul is not an immaterial unity, and its nature is transitory, like that of phenomena. The objects of these questions contain no heterogeneous or contradictory elements, for they relate to things in themselves, and not to phenomena. There would arise, indeed, a real contradiction, if reason came forward with a statement on the negative side of these questions alone. As regards the criticism to which the grounds of proof on the affirmative side must be subjected, it may be freely admitted, without necessitating the surrender of the affirmative propositions, which have, at least, the interest of reason in their favour—an advantage which the opposite party cannot lay claim to.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","text":"I cannot agree with the opinion of several admirable thinkers—Sulzer among the rest—that, in spite of the weakness of the arguments hitherto in use, we may hope, one day, to see sufficient demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of pure reason—the existence of a Supreme Being, and the immortality of the soul. I am certain, on the contrary, that this will never be the case. For on what ground can reason base such synthetical propositions, which do not relate to the objects of experience and their internal possibility? But it is also demonstratively certain that no one will ever be able to maintain the contrary with the least show of probability. For, as he can attempt such a proof solely upon the basis of pure reason, he is bound to prove that a Supreme Being, and a thinking subject in the character of a pure intelligence, are impossible. But where will he find the knowledge which can enable him to enounce synthetical judgements in regard to things which transcend the region of experience? We may, therefore, rest assured that the opposite never will be demonstrated. We need not, then, have recourse to scholastic arguments; we may always admit the truth of those propositions which are consistent with the speculative interests of reason in the sphere of experience, and form, moreover, the only means of uniting the speculative with the practical interest. Our opponent, who must not be considered here as a critic solely, we can be ready to meet with a non liquet which cannot fail to disconcert him; while we cannot deny his right to a similar retort, as we have on our side the advantage of the support of the subjective maxim of reason, and can therefore look upon all his sophistical arguments with calm indifference.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0005","text":"From this point of view, there is properly no antithetic of pure reason. For the only arena for such a struggle would be upon the field of pure theology and psychology; but on this ground there can appear no combatant whom we need to fear. Ridicule and boasting can be his only weapons; and these may be laughed at, as mere child’s play. This consideration restores to Reason her courage; for what source of confidence could be found, if she, whose vocation it is to destroy error, were at variance with herself and without any reasonable hope of ever reaching a state of permanent repose?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0006","text":"Everything in nature is good for some purpose. Even poisons are serviceable; they destroy the evil effects of other poisons generated in our system, and must always find a place in every complete pharmacopoeia. The objections raised against the fallacies and sophistries of speculative reason, are objections given by the nature of this reason itself, and must therefore have a destination and purpose which can only be for the good of humanity. For what purpose has Providence raised many objects, in which we have the deepest interest, so far above us, that we vainly try to cognize them with certainty, and our powers of mental vision are rather excited than satisfied by the glimpses we may chance to seize? It is very doubtful whether it is for our benefit to advance bold affirmations regarding subjects involved in such obscurity; perhaps it would even be detrimental to our best interests. But it is undoubtedly always beneficial to leave the investigating, as well as the critical reason, in perfect freedom, and permit it to take charge of its own interests, which are advanced as much by its limitation, as by its extension of its views, and which always suffer by the interference of foreign powers forcing it, against its natural tendencies, to bend to certain preconceived designs.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","text":"Allow your opponent to say what he thinks reasonable, and combat him only with the weapons of reason. Have no anxiety for the practical interests of humanity—these are never imperilled in a purely speculative dispute. Such a dispute serves merely to disclose the antinomy of reason, which, as it has its source in the nature of reason, ought to be thoroughly investigated. Reason is benefited by the examination of a subject on both sides, and its judgements are corrected by being limited. It is not the matter that may give occasion to dispute, but the manner. For it is perfectly permissible to employ, in the presence of reason, the language of a firmly rooted faith, even after we have been obliged to renounce all pretensions to knowledge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0008","text":"If we were to ask the dispassionate David Hume—a philosopher endowed, in a degree that few are, with a well-balanced judgement: What motive induced you to spend so much labour and thought in undermining the consoling and beneficial persuasion that reason is capable of assuring us of the existence, and presenting us with a determinate conception of a Supreme Being?—his answer would be: Nothing but the desire of teaching reason to know its own powers better, and, at the same time, a dislike of the procedure by which that faculty was compelled to support foregone conclusions, and prevented from confessing the internal weaknesses which it cannot but feel when it enters upon a rigid self-examination. If, on the other hand, we were to ask Priestley—a philosopher who had no taste for transcendental speculation, but was entirely devoted to the principles of empiricism—what his motives were for overturning those two main pillars of religion—the doctrines of the freedom of the will and the immortality of the soul (in his view the hope of a future life is but the expectation of the miracle of resurrection)—this philosopher, himself a zealous and pious teacher of religion, could give no other answer than this: I acted in the interest of reason, which always suffers, when certain objects are explained and judged by a reference to other supposed laws than those of material nature—the only laws which we know in a determinate manner. It would be unfair to decry the latter philosopher, who endeavoured to harmonize his paradoxical opinions with the interests of religion, and to undervalue an honest and reflecting man, because he finds himself at a loss the moment he has left the field of natural science. The same grace must be accorded to Hume, a man not less well-disposed, and quite as blameless in his moral character, and who pushed his abstract speculations to an extreme length, because, as he rightly believed, the object of them lies entirely beyond the bounds of natural science, and within the sphere of pure ideas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","text":"What is to be done to provide against the danger which seems in the present case to menace the best interests of humanity? The course to be pursued in reference to this subject is a perfectly plain and natural one. Let each thinker pursue his own path; if he shows talent, if he gives evidence of profound thought, in one word, if he shows that he possesses the power of reasoning—reason is always the gainer. If you have recourse to other means, if you attempt to coerce reason, if you raise the cry of treason to humanity, if you excite the feelings of the crowd, which can neither understand nor sympathize with such subtle speculations—you will only make yourselves ridiculous. For the question does not concern the advantage or disadvantage which we are expected to reap from such inquiries; the question is merely how far reason can advance in the field of speculation, apart from all kinds of interest, and whether we may depend upon the exertions of speculative reason, or must renounce all reliance on it. Instead of joining the combatants, it is your part to be a tranquil spectator of the struggle—a laborious struggle for the parties engaged, but attended, in its progress as well as in its result, with the most advantageous consequences for the interests of thought and knowledge. It is absurd to expect to be enlightened by Reason, and at the same time to prescribe to her what side of the question she must adopt. Moreover, reason is sufficiently held in check by its own power, the limits imposed on it by its own nature are sufficient; it is unnecessary for you to place over it additional guards, as if its power were dangerous to the constitution of the intellectual state. In the dialectic of reason there is no victory gained which need in the least disturb your tranquility.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0010","text":"The strife of dialectic is a necessity of reason, and we cannot but wish that it had been conducted long ere this with that perfect freedom which ought to be its essential condition. In this case, we should have had at an earlier period a matured and profound criticism, which must have put an end to all dialectical disputes, by exposing the illusions and prejudices in which they originated.\n\nThere is in human nature an unworthy propensity—a propensity which, like everything that springs from nature, must in its final purpose be conducive to the good of humanity—to conceal our real sentiments, and to give expression only to certain received opinions, which are regarded as at once safe and promotive of the common good. It is true, this tendency, not only to conceal our real sentiments, but to profess those which may gain us favour in the eyes of society, has not only civilized, but, in a certain measure, moralized us; as no one can break through the outward covering of respectability, honour, and morality, and thus the seemingly-good examples which we see around us form an excellent school for moral improvement, so long as our belief in their genuineness remains unshaken. But this disposition to represent ourselves as better than we are, and to utter opinions which are not our own, can be nothing more than a kind of provisionary arrangement of nature to lead us from the rudeness of an uncivilized state, and to teach us how to assume at least the appearance and manner of the good we see. But when true principles have been developed, and have obtained a sure foundation in our habit of thought, this conventionalism must be attacked with earnest vigour, otherwise it corrupts the heart, and checks the growth of good dispositions with the mischievous weed of fair appearances.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0011","text":"I am sorry to remark the same tendency to misrepresentation and hypocrisy in the sphere of speculative discussion, where there is less temptation to restrain the free expression of thought. For what can be more prejudicial to the interests of intelligence than to falsify our real sentiments, to conceal the doubts which we feel in regard to our statements, or to maintain the validity of grounds of proof which we well know to be insufficient? So long as mere personal vanity is the source of these unworthy artifices—and this is generally the case in speculative discussions, which are mostly destitute of practical interest, and are incapable of complete demonstration—the vanity of the opposite party exaggerates as much on the other side; and thus the result is the same, although it is not brought about so soon as if the dispute had been conducted in a sincere and upright spirit. But where the mass entertains the notion that the aim of certain subtle speculators is nothing less than to shake the very foundations of public welfare and morality—it seems not only prudent, but even praise worthy, to maintain the good cause by illusory arguments, rather than to give to our supposed opponents the advantage of lowering our declarations to the moderate tone of a merely practical conviction, and of compelling us to confess our inability to attain to apodeictic certainty in speculative subjects. But we ought to reflect that there is nothing, in the world more fatal to the maintenance of a good cause than deceit, misrepresentation, and falsehood. That the strictest laws of honesty should be observed in the discussion of a purely speculative subject is the least requirement that can be made. If we could reckon with security even upon so little, the conflict of speculative reason regarding the important questions of God, immortality, and freedom, would have been either decided long ago, or would very soon be brought to a conclusion. But, in general, the uprightness of the defence stands in an inverse ratio to the goodness of the cause; and perhaps more honesty and fairness are shown by those who deny than by those who uphold these doctrines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","text":"I shall persuade myself, then, that I have readers who do not wish to see a righteous cause defended by unfair arguments. Such will now recognize the fact that, according to the principles of this Critique, if we consider not what is, but what ought to be the case, there can be really no polemic of pure reason. For how can two persons dispute about a thing, the reality of which neither can present in actual or even in possible experience? Each adopts the plan of meditating on his idea for the purpose of drawing from the idea, if he can, what is more than the idea, that is, the reality of the object which it indicates. How shall they settle the dispute, since neither is able to make his assertions directly comprehensible and certain, but must restrict himself to attacking and confuting those of his opponent? All statements enounced by pure reason transcend the conditions of possible experience, beyond the sphere of which we can discover no criterion of truth, while they are at the same time framed in accordance with the laws of the understanding, which are applicable only to experience; and thus it is the fate of all such speculative discussions that while the one party attacks the weaker side of his opponent, he infallibly lays open his own weaknesses.\n\nThe critique of pure reason may be regarded as the highest tribunal for all speculative disputes; for it is not involved in these disputes, which have an immediate relation to certain objects and not to the laws of the mind, but is instituted for the purpose of determining the rights and limits of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","text":"Without the control of criticism, reason is, as it were, in a state of nature, and can only establish its claims and assertions by war. Criticism, on the contrary, deciding all questions according to the fundamental laws of its own institution, secures to us the peace of law and order, and enables us to discuss all differences in the more tranquil manner of a legal process. In the former case, disputes are ended by victory, which both sides may claim and which is followed by a hollow armistice; in the latter, by a sentence, which, as it strikes at the root of all speculative differences, ensures to all concerned a lasting peace. The endless disputes of a dogmatizing reason compel us to look for some mode of arriving at a settled decision by a critical investigation of reason itself; just as Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and that we must leave it and submit ourselves to the constraint of law, which indeed limits individual freedom, but only that it may consist with the freedom of others and with the common good of all.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","text":"This freedom will, among other things, permit of our openly stating the difficulties and doubts which we are ourselves unable to solve, without being decried on that account as turbulent and dangerous citizens. This privilege forms part of the native rights of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than the universal reason of humanity; and as this reason is the source of all progress and improvement, such a privilege is to be held sacred and inviolable. It is unwise, moreover, to denounce as dangerous any bold assertions against, or rash attacks upon, an opinion which is held by the largest and most moral class of the community; for that would be giving them an importance which they do not deserve. When I hear that the freedom of the will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God have been overthrown by the arguments of some able writer, I feel a strong desire to read his book; for I expect that he will add to my knowledge and impart greater clearness and distinctness to my views by the argumentative power shown in his writings. But I am perfectly certain, even before I have opened the book, that he has not succeeded in a single point, not because I believe I am in possession of irrefutable demonstrations of these important propositions, but because this transcendental critique, which has disclosed to me the power and the limits of pure reason, has fully convinced me that, as it is insufficient to establish the affirmative, it is as powerless, and even more so, to assure us of the truth of the negative answer to these questions. From what source does this free-thinker derive his knowledge that there is, for example, no Supreme Being? This proposition lies out of the field of possible experience, and, therefore, beyond the limits of human cognition. But I would not read at, all the answer which the dogmatical maintainer of the good cause makes to his opponent, because I know well beforehand, that he will merely attack the fallacious grounds of his adversary, without being able to establish his own assertions. Besides, a new illusory argument, in the construction of which talent and acuteness are shown, is suggestive of new ideas and new trains of reasoning, and in this respect the old and everyday sophistries are quite useless. Again, the dogmatical opponent of religion gives employment to criticism, and enables us to test and correct its principles, while there is no occasion for anxiety in regard to the influence and results of his reasoning.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","text":"But, it will be said, must we not warn the youth entrusted to academical care against such writings, must we not preserve them from the knowledge of these dangerous assertions, until their judgement is ripened, or rather until the doctrines which we wish to inculcate are so firmly rooted in their minds as to withstand all attempts at instilling the contrary dogmas, from whatever quarter they may come?\n\nIf we are to confine ourselves to the dogmatical procedure in the sphere of pure reason, and find ourselves unable to settle such disputes otherwise than by becoming a party in them, and setting counter-assertions against the statements advanced by our opponents, there is certainly no plan more advisable for the moment, but, at the same time, none more absurd and inefficient for the future, than this retaining of the youthful mind under guardianship for a time, and thus preserving it—for so long at least—from seduction into error. But when, at a later period, either curiosity, or the prevalent fashion of thought places such writings in their hands, will the so-called convictions of their youth stand firm? The young thinker, who has in his armoury none but dogmatical weapons with which to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who cannot detect the latent dialectic which lies in his own opinions as well as in those of the opposite party, sees the advance of illusory arguments and grounds of proof which have the advantage of novelty, against as illusory grounds of proof destitute of this advantage, and which, perhaps, excite the suspicion that the natural credulity of his youth has been abused by his instructors. He thinks he can find no better means of showing that he has out grown the discipline of his minority than by despising those well-meant warnings, and, knowing no system of thought but that of dogmatism, he drinks deep draughts of the poison that is to sap the principles in which his early years were trained.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","text":"Exactly the opposite of the system here recommended ought to be pursued in academical instruction. This can only be effected, however, by a thorough training in the critical investigation of pure reason. For, in order to bring the principles of this critique into exercise as soon as possible, and to demonstrate their perfect even in the presence of the highest degree of dialectical illusion, the student ought to examine the assertions made on both sides of speculative questions step by step, and to test them by these principles. It cannot be a difficult task for him to show the fallacies inherent in these propositions, and thus he begins early to feel his own power of securing himself against the influence of such sophistical arguments, which must finally lose, for him, all their illusory power. And, although the same blows which overturn the edifice of his opponent are as fatal to his own speculative structures, if such he has wished to rear; he need not feel any sorrow in regard to this seeming misfortune, as he has now before him a fair prospect into the practical region in which he may reasonably hope to find a more secure foundation for a rational system.\n\nThere is, accordingly, no proper polemic in the sphere of pure reason. Both parties beat the air and fight with their own shadows, as they pass beyond the limits of nature, and can find no tangible point of attack—no firm footing for their dogmatical conflict. Fight as vigorously as they may, the shadows which they hew down, immediately start up again, like the heroes in Walhalla, and renew the bloodless and unceasing contest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0017","text":"But neither can we admit that there is any proper sceptical employment of pure reason, such as might be based upon the principle of neutrality in all speculative disputes. To excite reason against itself, to place weapons in the hands of the party on the one side as well as in those of the other, and to remain an undisturbed and sarcastic spectator of the fierce struggle that ensues, seems, from the dogmatical point of view, to be a part fitting only a malevolent disposition. But, when the sophist evidences an invincible obstinacy and blindness, and a pride which no criticism can moderate, there is no other practicable course than to oppose to this pride and obstinacy similar feelings and pretensions on the other side, equally well or ill founded, so that reason, staggered by the reflections thus forced upon it, finds it necessary to moderate its confidence in such pretensions and to listen to the advice of criticism. But we cannot stop at these doubts, much less regard the conviction of our ignorance, not only as a cure for the conceit natural to dogmatism, but as the settlement of the disputes in which reason is involved with itself. On the contrary, scepticism is merely a means of awakening reason from its dogmatic dreams and exciting it to a more careful investigation into its own powers and pretensions. But, as scepticism appears to be the shortest road to a permanent peace in the domain of philosophy, and as it is the track pursued by the many who aim at giving a philosophical colouring to their contemptuous dislike of all inquiries of this kind, I think it necessary to present to my readers this mode of thought in its true light.\n\n_Scepticism not a Permanent State for Human Reason._","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","text":"The consciousness of ignorance—unless this ignorance is recognized to be absolutely necessary ought, instead of forming the conclusion of my inquiries, to be the strongest motive to the pursuit of them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things or of the limits of knowledge. If my ignorance is accidental and not necessary, it must incite me, in the first case, to a dogmatical inquiry regarding the objects of which I am ignorant; in the second, to a critical investigation into the bounds of all possible knowledge. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary and unavoidable, and that it consequently absolves from the duty of all further investigation, is a fact which cannot be made out upon empirical grounds—from observation—but upon critical grounds alone, that is, by a thoroughgoing investigation into the primary sources of cognition. It follows that the determination of the bounds of reason can be made only on à priori grounds; while the empirical limitation of reason, which is merely an indeterminate cognition of an ignorance that can never be completely removed, can take place only à posteriori. In other words, our empirical knowledge is limited by that which yet remains for us to know. The former cognition of our ignorance, which is possible only on a rational basis, is a science; the latter is merely a perception, and we cannot say how far the inferences drawn from it may extend. If I regard the earth, as it really appears to my senses, as a flat surface, I am ignorant how far this surface extends. But experience teaches me that, how far soever I go, I always see before me a space in which I can proceed farther; and thus I know the limits—merely visual—of my actual knowledge of the earth, although I am ignorant of the limits of the earth itself. But if I have got so far as to know that the earth is a sphere, and that its surface is spherical, I can cognize à priori and determine upon principles, from my knowledge of a small part of this surface—say to the extent of a degree—the diameter and circumference of the earth; and although I am ignorant of the objects which this surface contains, I have a perfect knowledge of its limits and extent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0019","text":"The sum of all the possible objects of our cognition seems to us to be a level surface, with an apparent horizon—that which forms the limit of its extent, and which has been termed by us the idea of unconditioned totality. To reach this limit by empirical means is impossible, and all attempts to determine it à priori according to a principle, are alike in vain. But all the questions raised by pure reason relate to that which lies beyond this horizon, or, at least, in its boundary line.\n\nThe celebrated David Hume was one of those geographers of human reason who believe that they have given a sufficient answer to all such questions by declaring them to lie beyond the horizon of our knowledge—a horizon which, however, Hume was unable to determine. His attention especially was directed to the principle of causality; and he remarked with perfect justice that the truth of this principle, and even the objective validity of the conception of a cause, was not commonly based upon clear insight, that is, upon à priori cognition. Hence he concluded that this law does not derive its authority from its universality and necessity, but merely from its general applicability in the course of experience, and a kind of subjective necessity thence arising, which he termed habit. From the inability of reason to establish this principle as a necessary law for the acquisition of all experience, he inferred the nullity of all the attempts of reason to pass the region of the empirical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","text":"This procedure of subjecting the facta of reason to examination, and, if necessary, to disapproval, may be termed the censura of reason. This censura must inevitably lead us to doubts regarding all transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step in our inquiry. The first step in regard to the subjects of pure reason, and which marks the infancy of that faculty, is that of dogmatism. The second, which we have just mentioned, is that of scepticism, and it gives evidence that our judgement has been improved by experience. But a third step is necessary—indicative of the maturity and manhood of the judgement, which now lays a firm foundation upon universal and necessary principles. This is the period of criticism, in which we do not examine the facta of reason, but reason itself, in the whole extent of its powers, and in regard to its capability of à priori cognition; and thus we determine not merely the empirical and ever-shifting bounds of our knowledge, but its necessary and eternal limits. We demonstrate from indubitable principles, not merely our ignorance in respect to this or that subject, but in regard to all possible questions of a certain class. Thus scepticism is a resting place for reason, in which it may reflect on its dogmatical wanderings and gain some knowledge of the region in which it happens to be, that it may pursue its way with greater certainty; but it cannot be its permanent dwelling-place. It must take up its abode only in the region of complete certitude, whether this relates to the cognition of objects themselves, or to the limits which bound all our cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0021","text":"Reason is not to be considered as an indefinitely extended plane, of the bounds of which we have only a general knowledge; it ought rather to be compared to a sphere, the radius of which may be found from the curvature of its surface—that is, the nature of à priori synthetical propositions—and, consequently, its circumference and extent. Beyond the sphere of experience there are no objects which it can cognize; nay, even questions regarding such supposititious objects relate only to the subjective principles of a complete determination of the relations which exist between the understanding-conceptions which lie within this sphere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0022","text":"We are actually in possession of à priori synthetical cognitions, as is proved by the existence of the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience. If any one cannot comprehend the possibility of these principles, he may have some reason to doubt whether they are really à priori; but he cannot on this account declare them to be impossible, and affirm the nullity of the steps which reason may have taken under their guidance. He can only say: If we perceived their origin and their authenticity, we should be able to determine the extent and limits of reason; but, till we can do this, all propositions regarding the latter are mere random assertions. In this view, the doubt respecting all dogmatical philosophy, which proceeds without the guidance of criticism, is well grounded; but we cannot therefore deny to reason the ability to construct a sound philosophy, when the way has been prepared by a thorough critical investigation. All the conceptions produced, and all the questions raised, by pure reason, do not lie in the sphere of experience, but in that of reason itself, and hence they must be solved, and shown to be either valid or inadmissible, by that faculty. We have no right to decline the solution of such problems, on the ground that the solution can be discovered only from the nature of things, and under pretence of the limitation of human faculties, for reason is the sole creator of all these ideas, and is therefore bound either to establish their validity or to expose their illusory nature.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0023","text":"The polemic of scepticism is properly directed against the dogmatist, who erects a system of philosophy without having examined the fundamental objective principles on which it is based, for the purpose of evidencing the futility of his designs, and thus bringing him to a knowledge of his own powers. But, in itself, scepticism does not give us any certain information in regard to the bounds of our knowledge. All unsuccessful dogmatical attempts of reason are facia, which it is always useful to submit to the censure of the sceptic. But this cannot help us to any decision regarding the expectations which reason cherishes of better success in future endeavours; the investigations of scepticism cannot, therefore, settle the dispute regarding the rights and powers of human reason.\n\nHume is perhaps the ablest and most ingenious of all sceptical philosophers, and his writings have, undoubtedly, exerted the most powerful influence in awakening reason to a thorough investigation into its own powers. It will, therefore, well repay our labours to consider for a little the course of reasoning which he followed and the errors into which he strayed, although setting out on the path of truth and certitude.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","text":"Hume was probably aware, although he never clearly developed the notion, that we proceed in judgements of a certain class beyond our conception of the object. I have termed this kind of judgement synthetical. As regard the manner in which I pass beyond my conception by the aid of experience, no doubts can be entertained. Experience is itself a synthesis of perceptions; and it employs perceptions to increment the conception, which I obtain by means of another perception. But we feel persuaded that we are able to proceed beyond a conception, and to extend our cognition à priori. We attempt this in two ways—either, through the pure understanding, in relation to that which may become an object of experience, or, through pure reason, in relation to such properties of things, or of the existence of things, as can never be presented in any experience. This sceptical philosopher did not distinguish these two kinds of judgements, as he ought to have done, but regarded this augmentation of conceptions, and, if we may so express ourselves, the spontaneous generation of understanding and reason, independently of the impregnation of experience, as altogether impossible. The so-called à priori principles of these faculties he consequently held to be invalid and imaginary, and regarded them as nothing but subjective habits of thought originating in experience, and therefore purely empirical and contingent rules, to which we attribute a spurious necessity and universality. In support of this strange assertion, he referred us to the generally acknowledged principle of the relation between cause and effect. No faculty of the mind can conduct us from the conception of a thing to the existence of something else; and hence he believed he could infer that, without experience, we possess no source from which we can augment a conception, and no ground sufficient to justify us in framing a judgement that is to extend our cognition à priori. That the light of the sun, which shines upon a piece of wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens clay, no power of the understanding could infer from the conceptions which we previously possessed of these substances; much less is there any à priori law that could conduct us to such a conclusion, which experience alone can certify. On the other hand, we have seen in our discussion of transcendental logic, that, although we can never proceed immediately beyond the content of the conception which is given us, we can always cognize completely à priori—in relation, however, to a third term, namely, possible experience—the law of its connection with other things. For example, if I observe that a piece of wax melts, I can cognize à priori that there must have been something (the sun’s heat) preceding, which this law; although, without the aid of experience, I could not cognize à priori and in a determinate manner either the cause from the effect, or the effect from the cause. Hume was, therefore, wrong in inferring, from the contingency of the determination according to law, the contingency of the law itself; and the passing beyond the conception of a thing to possible experience (which is an à priori proceeding, constituting the objective reality of the conception), he confounded with our synthesis of objects in actual experience, which is always, of course, empirical. Thus, too, he regarded the principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and indicates a necessary connection, as a mere rule of association, lying in the imitative faculty of imagination, which can present only contingent, and not objective connections.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0025","text":"The sceptical errors of this remarkably acute thinker arose principally from a defect, which was common to him with the dogmatists, namely, that he had never made a systematic review of all the different kinds of à priori synthesis performed by the understanding. Had he done so, he would have found, to take one example among many, that the principle of permanence was of this character, and that it, as well as the principle of causality, anticipates experience. In this way he might have been able to describe the determinate limits of the à priori operations of understanding and reason. But he merely declared the understanding to be limited, instead of showing what its limits were; he created a general mistrust in the power of our faculties, without giving us any determinate knowledge of the bounds of our necessary and unavoidable ignorance; he examined and condemned some of the principles of the understanding, without investigating all its powers with the completeness necessary to criticism. He denies, with truth, certain powers to the understanding, but he goes further, and declares it to be utterly inadequate to the à priori extension of knowledge, although he has not fully examined all the powers which reside in the faculty; and thus the fate which always overtakes scepticism meets him too. That is to say, his own declarations are doubted, for his objections were based upon facta, which are contingent, and not upon principles, which can alone demonstrate the necessary invalidity of all dogmatical assertions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0026","text":"As Hume makes no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which, however, his attacks are mainly directed, reason does not feel itself shut out from all attempts at the extension of à priori cognition, and hence it refuses, in spite of a few checks in this or that quarter, to relinquish such efforts. For one naturally arms oneself to resist an attack, and becomes more obstinate in the resolve to establish the claims he has advanced. But a complete review of the powers of reason, and the conviction thence arising that we are in possession of a limited field of action, while we must admit the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all doubt and dispute, and induces reason to rest satisfied with the undisturbed possession of its limited domain.\n\nTo the uncritical dogmatist, who has not surveyed the sphere of his understanding, nor determined, in accordance with principles, the limits of possible cognition, who, consequently, is ignorant of his own powers, and believes he will discover them by the attempts he makes in the field of cognition, these attacks of scepticism are not only dangerous, but destructive. For if there is one proposition in his chain of reasoning which he cannot prove, or the fallacy in which he cannot evolve in accordance with a principle, suspicion falls on all his statements, however plausible they may appear.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0027","text":"And thus scepticism, the bane of dogmatical philosophy, conducts us to a sound investigation into the understanding and the reason. When we are thus far advanced, we need fear no further attacks; for the limits of our domain are clearly marked out, and we can make no claims nor become involved in any disputes regarding the region that lies beyond these limits. Thus the sceptical procedure in philosophy does not present any solution of the problems of reason, but it forms an excellent exercise for its powers, awakening its circumspection, and indicating the means whereby it may most fully establish its claims to its legitimate possessions.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"121ae65b45a191c3b70c72d446a0c234379ba384bc729712fffc2d90217669cc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","order":87,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_6","text":"### Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis\n\nThis critique of reason has now taught us that all its efforts to extend the bounds of knowledge, by means of pure speculation, are utterly fruitless. So much the wider field, it may appear, lies open to hypothesis; as, where we cannot know with certainty, we are at liberty to make guesses and to form suppositions.\n\nImagination may be allowed, under the strict surveillance of reason, to invent suppositions; but, these must be based on something that is perfectly certain—and that is the possibility of the object. If we are well assured upon this point, it is allowable to have recourse to supposition in regard to the reality of the object; but this supposition must, unless it is utterly groundless, be connected, as its ground of explanation, with that which is really given and absolutely certain. Such a supposition is termed a hypothesis.\n\nIt is beyond our power to form the least conception à priori of the possibility of dynamical connection in phenomena; and the category of the pure understanding will not enable us to excogitate any such connection, but merely helps us to understand it, when we meet with it in experience. For this reason we cannot, in accordance with the categories, imagine or invent any object or any property of an object not given, or that may not be given in experience, and employ it in a hypothesis; otherwise, we should be basing our chain of reasoning upon mere chimerical fancies, and not upon conceptions of things. Thus, we have no right to assume the existence of new powers, not existing in nature—for example, an understanding with a non-sensuous intuition, a force of attraction without contact, or some new kind of substances occupying space, and yet without the property of impenetrability—and, consequently, we cannot assume that there is any other kind of community among substances than that observable in experience, any kind of presence than that in space, or any kind of duration than that in time. In one word, the conditions of possible experience are for reason the only conditions of the possibility of things; reason cannot venture to form, independently of these conditions, any conceptions of things, because such conceptions, although not self-contradictory, are without object and without application.\n\nThe conceptions of reason are, as we have already shown, mere ideas, and do not relate to any object in any kind of experience. At the same time, they do not indicate imaginary or possible objects. They are purely problematical in their nature and, as aids to the heuristic exercise of the faculties, form the basis of the regulative principles for the systematic employment of the understanding in the field of experience. If we leave this ground of experience, they become mere fictions of thought, the possibility of which is quite indemonstrable; and they cannot, consequently, be employed as hypotheses in the explanation of real phenomena. It is quite admissible to cogitate the soul as simple, for the purpose of enabling ourselves to employ the idea of a perfect and necessary unity of all the faculties of the mind as the principle of all our inquiries into its internal phenomena, although we cannot cognize this unity in concreto. But to assume that the soul is a simple substance (a transcendental conception) would be enouncing a proposition which is not only indemonstrable—as many physical hypotheses are—but a proposition which is purely arbitrary, and in the highest degree rash. The simple is never presented in experience; and, if by substance is here meant the permanent object of sensuous intuition, the possibility of a simple phenomenon is perfectly inconceivable. Reason affords no good grounds for admitting the existence of intelligible beings, or of intelligible properties of sensuous things, although—as we have no conception either of their possibility or of their impossibility—it will always be out of our power to affirm dogmatically that they do not exist. In the explanation of given phenomena, no other things and no other grounds of explanation can be employed than those which stand in connection with the given phenomena according to the known laws of experience. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason is employed to explain the phenomena of nature, would not give us any better insight into a phenomenon, as we should be trying to explain what we do not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles, by what we do not understand at all. The principles of such a hypothesis might conduce to the satisfaction of reason, but it would not assist the understanding in its application to objects. Order and conformity to aims in the sphere of nature must be themselves explained upon natural grounds and according to natural laws; and the wildest hypotheses, if they are only physical, are here more admissible than a hyperphysical hypothesis, such as that of a divine author. For such a hypothesis would introduce the principle of ignava ratio, which requires us to give up the search for causes that might be discovered in the course of experience and to rest satisfied with a mere idea. As regards the absolute totality of the grounds of explanation in the series of these causes, this can be no hindrance to the understanding in the case of phenomena; because, as they are to us nothing more than phenomena, we have no right to look for anything like completeness in the synthesis of the series of their conditions.\n\nTranscendental hypotheses are therefore inadmissible; and we cannot use the liberty of employing, in the absence of physical, hyperphysical grounds of explanation. And this for two reasons; first, because such hypothesis do not advance reason, but rather stop it in its progress; secondly, because this licence would render fruitless all its exertions in its own proper sphere, which is that of experience. For, when the explanation of natural phenomena happens to be difficult, we have constantly at hand a transcendental ground of explanation, which lifts us above the necessity of investigating nature; and our inquiries are brought to a close, not because we have obtained all the requisite knowledge, but because we abut upon a principle which is incomprehensible and which, indeed, is so far back in the track of thought as to contain the conception of the absolutely primal being.\n\nThe next requisite for the admissibility of a hypothesis is its sufficiency. That is, it must determine à priori the consequences which are given in experience and which are supposed to follow from the hypothesis itself. If we require to employ auxiliary hypotheses, the suspicion naturally arises that they are mere fictions; because the necessity for each of them requires the same justification as in the case of the original hypothesis, and thus their testimony is invalid. If we suppose the existence of an infinitely perfect cause, we possess sufficient grounds for the explanation of the conformity to aims, the order and the greatness which we observe in the universe; but we find ourselves obliged, when we observe the evil in the world and the exceptions to these laws, to employ new hypothesis in support of the original one. We employ the idea of the simple nature of the human soul as the foundation of all the theories we may form of its phenomena; but when we meet with difficulties in our way, when we observe in the soul phenomena similar to the changes which take place in matter, we require to call in new auxiliary hypotheses. These may, indeed, not be false, but we do not know them to be true, because the only witness to their certitude is the hypothesis which they themselves have been called in to explain.\n\nWe are not discussing the above-mentioned assertions regarding the immaterial unity of the soul and the existence of a Supreme Being as dogmata, which certain philosophers profess to demonstrate à priori, but purely as hypotheses. In the former case, the dogmatist must take care that his arguments possess the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For the assertion that the reality of such ideas is probable is as absurd as a proof of the probability of a proposition in geometry. Pure abstract reason, apart from all experience, can either cognize nothing at all; and hence the judgements it enounces are never mere opinions, they are either apodeictic certainties, or declarations that nothing can be known on the subject. Opinions and probable judgements on the nature of things can only be employed to explain given phenomena, or they may relate to the effect, in accordance with empirical laws, of an actually existing cause. In other words, we must restrict the sphere of opinion to the world of experience and nature. Beyond this region opinion is mere invention; unless we are groping about for the truth on a path not yet fully known, and have some hopes of stumbling upon it by chance.\n\nBut, although hypotheses are inadmissible in answers to the questions of pure speculative reason, they may be employed in the defence of these answers. That is to say, hypotheses are admissible in polemic, but not in the sphere of dogmatism. By the defence of statements of this character, I do not mean an attempt at discovering new grounds for their support, but merely the refutation of the arguments of opponents. All à priori synthetical propositions possess the peculiarity that, although the philosopher who maintains the reality of the ideas contained in the proposition is not in possession of sufficient knowledge to establish the certainty of his statements, his opponent is as little able to prove the truth of the opposite. This equality of fortune does not allow the one party to be superior to the other in the sphere of speculative cognition; and it is this sphere, accordingly, that is the proper arena of these endless speculative conflicts. But we shall afterwards show that, in relation to its practical exercise, Reason has the right of admitting what, in the field of pure speculation, she would not be justified in supposing, except upon perfectly sufficient grounds; because all such suppositions destroy the necessary completeness of speculation—a condition which the practical reason, however, does not consider to be requisite. In this sphere, therefore, Reason is mistress of a possession, her title to which she does not require to prove—which, in fact, she could not do. The burden of proof accordingly rests upon the opponent. But as he has just as little knowledge regarding the subject discussed, and is as little able to prove the non-existence of the object of an idea, as the philosopher on the other side is to demonstrate its reality, it is evident that there is an advantage on the side of the philosopher who maintains his proposition as a practically necessary supposition (melior est conditio possidentis). For he is at liberty to employ, in self-defence, the same weapons as his opponent makes use of in attacking him; that is, he has a right to use hypotheses not for the purpose of supporting the arguments in favour of his own propositions, but to show that his opponent knows no more than himself regarding the subject under discussion and cannot boast of any speculative advantage.\n\nHypotheses are, therefore, admissible in the sphere of pure reason only as weapons for self-defence, and not as supports to dogmatical assertions. But the opposing party we must always seek for in ourselves. For speculative reason is, in the sphere of transcendentalism, dialectical in its own nature. The difficulties and objections we have to fear lie in ourselves. They are like old but never superannuated claims; and we must seek them out, and settle them once and for ever, if we are to expect a permanent peace. External tranquility is hollow and unreal. The root of these contradictions, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be destroyed; and this can only be done by giving it, in the first instance, freedom to grow, nay, by nourishing it, that it may send out shoots, and thus betray its own existence. It is our duty, therefore, to try to discover new objections, to put weapons in the bands of our opponent, and to grant him the most favourable position in the arena that he can wish. We have nothing to fear from these concessions; on the contrary, we may rather hope that we shall thus make ourselves master of a possession which no one will ever venture to dispute.\n\nThe thinker requires, to be fully equipped, the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although but leaden weapons (for they have not been steeled in the armoury of experience), are as useful as any that can be employed by his opponents. If, accordingly, we have assumed, from a non-speculative point of view, the immaterial nature of the soul, and are met by the objection that experience seems to prove that the growth and decay of our mental faculties are mere modifications of the sensuous organism—we can weaken the force of this objection by the assumption that the body is nothing but the fundamental phenomenon, to which, as a necessary condition, all sensibility, and consequently all thought, relates in the present state of our existence; and that the separation of soul and body forms the conclusion of the sensuous exercise of our power of cognition and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would, in this view of the question, be regarded, not as the cause of thought, but merely as its restrictive condition, as promotive of the sensuous and animal, but as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the dependence of the animal life on the constitution of the body, would not prove that the whole life of man was also dependent on the state of the organism. We might go still farther, and discover new objections, or carry out to their extreme consequences those which have already been adduced.\n\nGeneration, in the human race as well as among the irrational animals, depends on so many accidents—of occasion, of proper sustenance, of the laws enacted by the government of a country of vice even, that it is difficult to believe in the eternal existence of a being whose life has begun under circumstances so mean and trivial, and so entirely dependent upon our own control. As regards the continuance of the existence of the whole race, we need have no difficulties, for accident in single cases is subject to general laws; but, in the case of each individual, it would seem as if we could hardly expect so wonderful an effect from causes so insignificant. But, in answer to these objections, we may adduce the transcendental hypothesis that all life is properly intelligible, and not subject to changes of time, and that it neither began in birth, nor will end in death. We may assume that this life is nothing more than a sensuous representation of pure spiritual life; that the whole world of sense is but an image, hovering before the faculty of cognition which we exercise in this sphere, and with no more objective reality than a dream; and that if we could intuite ourselves and other things as they really are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, our connection with which did not begin at our birth and will not cease with the destruction of the body. And so on.\n\nWe cannot be said to know what has been above asserted, nor do we seriously maintain the truth of these assertions; and the notions therein indicated are not even ideas of reason, they are purely fictitious conceptions. But this hypothetical procedure is in perfect conformity with the laws of reason. Our opponent mistakes the absence of empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of all that we have asserted; and we have to show him that he has not exhausted the whole sphere of possibility and that he can as little compass that sphere by the laws of experience and nature, as we can lay a secure foundation for the operations of reason beyond the region of experience. Such hypothetical defences against the pretensions of an opponent must not be regarded as declarations of opinion. The philosopher abandons them, so soon as the opposite party renounces its dogmatical conceit. To maintain a simply negative position in relation to propositions which rest on an insecure foundation, well befits the moderation of a true philosopher; but to uphold the objections urged against an opponent as proofs of the opposite statement is a proceeding just as unwarrantable and arrogant as it is to attack the position of a philosopher who advances affirmative propositions regarding such a subject.\n\nIt is evident, therefore, that hypotheses, in the speculative sphere, are valid, not as independent propositions, but only relatively to opposite transcendent assumptions. For, to make the principles of possible experience conditions of the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent a procedure as to maintain the objective reality of ideas which can be applied to no objects except such as lie without the limits of possible experience. The judgements enounced by pure reason must be necessary, or they must not be enounced at all. Reason cannot trouble herself with opinions. But the hypotheses we have been discussing are merely problematical judgements, which can neither be confuted nor proved; while, therefore, they are not personal opinions, they are indispensable as answers to objections which are liable to be raised. But we must take care to confine them to this function, and guard against any assumption on their part of absolute validity, a proceeding which would involve reason in inextricable difficulties and contradictions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","text":"### Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis\n\nThis critique of reason has now taught us that all its efforts to extend the bounds of knowledge, by means of pure speculation, are utterly fruitless. So much the wider field, it may appear, lies open to hypothesis; as, where we cannot know with certainty, we are at liberty to make guesses and to form suppositions.\n\nImagination may be allowed, under the strict surveillance of reason, to invent suppositions; but, these must be based on something that is perfectly certain—and that is the possibility of the object. If we are well assured upon this point, it is allowable to have recourse to supposition in regard to the reality of the object; but this supposition must, unless it is utterly groundless, be connected, as its ground of explanation, with that which is really given and absolutely certain. Such a supposition is termed a hypothesis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","text":"It is beyond our power to form the least conception à priori of the possibility of dynamical connection in phenomena; and the category of the pure understanding will not enable us to excogitate any such connection, but merely helps us to understand it, when we meet with it in experience. For this reason we cannot, in accordance with the categories, imagine or invent any object or any property of an object not given, or that may not be given in experience, and employ it in a hypothesis; otherwise, we should be basing our chain of reasoning upon mere chimerical fancies, and not upon conceptions of things. Thus, we have no right to assume the existence of new powers, not existing in nature—for example, an understanding with a non-sensuous intuition, a force of attraction without contact, or some new kind of substances occupying space, and yet without the property of impenetrability—and, consequently, we cannot assume that there is any other kind of community among substances than that observable in experience, any kind of presence than that in space, or any kind of duration than that in time. In one word, the conditions of possible experience are for reason the only conditions of the possibility of things; reason cannot venture to form, independently of these conditions, any conceptions of things, because such conceptions, although not self-contradictory, are without object and without application.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","text":"The conceptions of reason are, as we have already shown, mere ideas, and do not relate to any object in any kind of experience. At the same time, they do not indicate imaginary or possible objects. They are purely problematical in their nature and, as aids to the heuristic exercise of the faculties, form the basis of the regulative principles for the systematic employment of the understanding in the field of experience. If we leave this ground of experience, they become mere fictions of thought, the possibility of which is quite indemonstrable; and they cannot, consequently, be employed as hypotheses in the explanation of real phenomena. It is quite admissible to cogitate the soul as simple, for the purpose of enabling ourselves to employ the idea of a perfect and necessary unity of all the faculties of the mind as the principle of all our inquiries into its internal phenomena, although we cannot cognize this unity in concreto. But to assume that the soul is a simple substance (a transcendental conception) would be enouncing a proposition which is not only indemonstrable—as many physical hypotheses are—but a proposition which is purely arbitrary, and in the highest degree rash. The simple is never presented in experience; and, if by substance is here meant the permanent object of sensuous intuition, the possibility of a simple phenomenon is perfectly inconceivable. Reason affords no good grounds for admitting the existence of intelligible beings, or of intelligible properties of sensuous things, although—as we have no conception either of their possibility or of their impossibility—it will always be out of our power to affirm dogmatically that they do not exist. In the explanation of given phenomena, no other things and no other grounds of explanation can be employed than those which stand in connection with the given phenomena according to the known laws of experience. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason is employed to explain the phenomena of nature, would not give us any better insight into a phenomenon, as we should be trying to explain what we do not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles, by what we do not understand at all. The principles of such a hypothesis might conduce to the satisfaction of reason, but it would not assist the understanding in its application to objects. Order and conformity to aims in the sphere of nature must be themselves explained upon natural grounds and according to natural laws; and the wildest hypotheses, if they are only physical, are here more admissible than a hyperphysical hypothesis, such as that of a divine author. For such a hypothesis would introduce the principle of ignava ratio, which requires us to give up the search for causes that might be discovered in the course of experience and to rest satisfied with a mere idea. As regards the absolute totality of the grounds of explanation in the series of these causes, this can be no hindrance to the understanding in the case of phenomena; because, as they are to us nothing more than phenomena, we have no right to look for anything like completeness in the synthesis of the series of their conditions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","text":"Transcendental hypotheses are therefore inadmissible; and we cannot use the liberty of employing, in the absence of physical, hyperphysical grounds of explanation. And this for two reasons; first, because such hypothesis do not advance reason, but rather stop it in its progress; secondly, because this licence would render fruitless all its exertions in its own proper sphere, which is that of experience. For, when the explanation of natural phenomena happens to be difficult, we have constantly at hand a transcendental ground of explanation, which lifts us above the necessity of investigating nature; and our inquiries are brought to a close, not because we have obtained all the requisite knowledge, but because we abut upon a principle which is incomprehensible and which, indeed, is so far back in the track of thought as to contain the conception of the absolutely primal being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","text":"The next requisite for the admissibility of a hypothesis is its sufficiency. That is, it must determine à priori the consequences which are given in experience and which are supposed to follow from the hypothesis itself. If we require to employ auxiliary hypotheses, the suspicion naturally arises that they are mere fictions; because the necessity for each of them requires the same justification as in the case of the original hypothesis, and thus their testimony is invalid. If we suppose the existence of an infinitely perfect cause, we possess sufficient grounds for the explanation of the conformity to aims, the order and the greatness which we observe in the universe; but we find ourselves obliged, when we observe the evil in the world and the exceptions to these laws, to employ new hypothesis in support of the original one. We employ the idea of the simple nature of the human soul as the foundation of all the theories we may form of its phenomena; but when we meet with difficulties in our way, when we observe in the soul phenomena similar to the changes which take place in matter, we require to call in new auxiliary hypotheses. These may, indeed, not be false, but we do not know them to be true, because the only witness to their certitude is the hypothesis which they themselves have been called in to explain.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0006","text":"We are not discussing the above-mentioned assertions regarding the immaterial unity of the soul and the existence of a Supreme Being as dogmata, which certain philosophers profess to demonstrate à priori, but purely as hypotheses. In the former case, the dogmatist must take care that his arguments possess the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For the assertion that the reality of such ideas is probable is as absurd as a proof of the probability of a proposition in geometry. Pure abstract reason, apart from all experience, can either cognize nothing at all; and hence the judgements it enounces are never mere opinions, they are either apodeictic certainties, or declarations that nothing can be known on the subject. Opinions and probable judgements on the nature of things can only be employed to explain given phenomena, or they may relate to the effect, in accordance with empirical laws, of an actually existing cause. In other words, we must restrict the sphere of opinion to the world of experience and nature. Beyond this region opinion is mere invention; unless we are groping about for the truth on a path not yet fully known, and have some hopes of stumbling upon it by chance.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","text":"But, although hypotheses are inadmissible in answers to the questions of pure speculative reason, they may be employed in the defence of these answers. That is to say, hypotheses are admissible in polemic, but not in the sphere of dogmatism. By the defence of statements of this character, I do not mean an attempt at discovering new grounds for their support, but merely the refutation of the arguments of opponents. All à priori synthetical propositions possess the peculiarity that, although the philosopher who maintains the reality of the ideas contained in the proposition is not in possession of sufficient knowledge to establish the certainty of his statements, his opponent is as little able to prove the truth of the opposite. This equality of fortune does not allow the one party to be superior to the other in the sphere of speculative cognition; and it is this sphere, accordingly, that is the proper arena of these endless speculative conflicts. But we shall afterwards show that, in relation to its practical exercise, Reason has the right of admitting what, in the field of pure speculation, she would not be justified in supposing, except upon perfectly sufficient grounds; because all such suppositions destroy the necessary completeness of speculation—a condition which the practical reason, however, does not consider to be requisite. In this sphere, therefore, Reason is mistress of a possession, her title to which she does not require to prove—which, in fact, she could not do. The burden of proof accordingly rests upon the opponent. But as he has just as little knowledge regarding the subject discussed, and is as little able to prove the non-existence of the object of an idea, as the philosopher on the other side is to demonstrate its reality, it is evident that there is an advantage on the side of the philosopher who maintains his proposition as a practically necessary supposition (melior est conditio possidentis). For he is at liberty to employ, in self-defence, the same weapons as his opponent makes use of in attacking him; that is, he has a right to use hypotheses not for the purpose of supporting the arguments in favour of his own propositions, but to show that his opponent knows no more than himself regarding the subject under discussion and cannot boast of any speculative advantage.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","text":"Hypotheses are, therefore, admissible in the sphere of pure reason only as weapons for self-defence, and not as supports to dogmatical assertions. But the opposing party we must always seek for in ourselves. For speculative reason is, in the sphere of transcendentalism, dialectical in its own nature. The difficulties and objections we have to fear lie in ourselves. They are like old but never superannuated claims; and we must seek them out, and settle them once and for ever, if we are to expect a permanent peace. External tranquility is hollow and unreal. The root of these contradictions, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be destroyed; and this can only be done by giving it, in the first instance, freedom to grow, nay, by nourishing it, that it may send out shoots, and thus betray its own existence. It is our duty, therefore, to try to discover new objections, to put weapons in the bands of our opponent, and to grant him the most favourable position in the arena that he can wish. We have nothing to fear from these concessions; on the contrary, we may rather hope that we shall thus make ourselves master of a possession which no one will ever venture to dispute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0009","text":"The thinker requires, to be fully equipped, the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although but leaden weapons (for they have not been steeled in the armoury of experience), are as useful as any that can be employed by his opponents. If, accordingly, we have assumed, from a non-speculative point of view, the immaterial nature of the soul, and are met by the objection that experience seems to prove that the growth and decay of our mental faculties are mere modifications of the sensuous organism—we can weaken the force of this objection by the assumption that the body is nothing but the fundamental phenomenon, to which, as a necessary condition, all sensibility, and consequently all thought, relates in the present state of our existence; and that the separation of soul and body forms the conclusion of the sensuous exercise of our power of cognition and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would, in this view of the question, be regarded, not as the cause of thought, but merely as its restrictive condition, as promotive of the sensuous and animal, but as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the dependence of the animal life on the constitution of the body, would not prove that the whole life of man was also dependent on the state of the organism. We might go still farther, and discover new objections, or carry out to their extreme consequences those which have already been adduced.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0010","text":"Generation, in the human race as well as among the irrational animals, depends on so many accidents—of occasion, of proper sustenance, of the laws enacted by the government of a country of vice even, that it is difficult to believe in the eternal existence of a being whose life has begun under circumstances so mean and trivial, and so entirely dependent upon our own control. As regards the continuance of the existence of the whole race, we need have no difficulties, for accident in single cases is subject to general laws; but, in the case of each individual, it would seem as if we could hardly expect so wonderful an effect from causes so insignificant. But, in answer to these objections, we may adduce the transcendental hypothesis that all life is properly intelligible, and not subject to changes of time, and that it neither began in birth, nor will end in death. We may assume that this life is nothing more than a sensuous representation of pure spiritual life; that the whole world of sense is but an image, hovering before the faculty of cognition which we exercise in this sphere, and with no more objective reality than a dream; and that if we could intuite ourselves and other things as they really are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, our connection with which did not begin at our birth and will not cease with the destruction of the body. And so on.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","text":"We cannot be said to know what has been above asserted, nor do we seriously maintain the truth of these assertions; and the notions therein indicated are not even ideas of reason, they are purely fictitious conceptions. But this hypothetical procedure is in perfect conformity with the laws of reason. Our opponent mistakes the absence of empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of all that we have asserted; and we have to show him that he has not exhausted the whole sphere of possibility and that he can as little compass that sphere by the laws of experience and nature, as we can lay a secure foundation for the operations of reason beyond the region of experience. Such hypothetical defences against the pretensions of an opponent must not be regarded as declarations of opinion. The philosopher abandons them, so soon as the opposite party renounces its dogmatical conceit. To maintain a simply negative position in relation to propositions which rest on an insecure foundation, well befits the moderation of a true philosopher; but to uphold the objections urged against an opponent as proofs of the opposite statement is a proceeding just as unwarrantable and arrogant as it is to attack the position of a philosopher who advances affirmative propositions regarding such a subject.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","text":"It is evident, therefore, that hypotheses, in the speculative sphere, are valid, not as independent propositions, but only relatively to opposite transcendent assumptions. For, to make the principles of possible experience conditions of the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent a procedure as to maintain the objective reality of ideas which can be applied to no objects except such as lie without the limits of possible experience. The judgements enounced by pure reason must be necessary, or they must not be enounced at all. Reason cannot trouble herself with opinions. But the hypotheses we have been discussing are merely problematical judgements, which can neither be confuted nor proved; while, therefore, they are not personal opinions, they are indispensable as answers to objections which are liable to be raised. But we must take care to confine them to this function, and guard against any assumption on their part of absolute validity, a proceeding which would involve reason in inextricable difficulties and contradictions.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"91aec189cbc7045f35e5ff69c51251c77cd4f807e55d183771ee2ca42f42203d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","order":88,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SI_7","text":"# Proofs\n\n### Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs\n\nIt is a peculiarity, which distinguishes the proofs of transcendental synthetical propositions from those of all other à priori synthetical cognitions, that reason, in the case of the former, does not apply its conceptions directly to an object, but is first obliged to prove, à priori, the objective validity of these conceptions and the possibility of their syntheses. This is not merely a prudential rule, it is essential to the very possibility of the proof of a transcendental proposition. If I am required to pass, à priori, beyond the conception of an object, I find that it is utterly impossible without the guidance of something which is not contained in the conception. In mathematics, it is à priori intuition that guides my synthesis; and, in this case, all our conclusions may be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as we are dealing only with conceptions of the understanding, we are guided by possible experience. That is to say, a proof in the sphere of transcendental cognition does not show that the given conception (that of an event, for example) leads directly to another conception (that of a cause)—for this would be a saltus which nothing can justify; but it shows that experience itself, and consequently the object of experience, is impossible without the connection indicated by these conceptions. It follows that such a proof must demonstrate the possibility of arriving, synthetically and à priori, at a certain knowledge of things, which was not contained in our conceptions of these things. Unless we pay particular attention to this requirement, our proofs, instead of pursuing the straight path indicated by reason, follow the tortuous road of mere subjective association. The illusory conviction, which rests upon subjective causes of association, and which is considered as resulting from the perception of a real and objective natural affinity, is always open to doubt and suspicion. For this reason, all the attempts which have been made to prove the principle of sufficient reason, have, according to the universal admission of philosophers, been quite unsuccessful; and, before the appearance of transcendental criticism, it was considered better, as this principle could not be abandoned, to appeal boldly to the common sense of mankind (a proceeding which always proves that the problem, which reason ought to solve, is one in which philosophers find great difficulties), rather than attempt to discover new dogmatical proofs.\n\nBut, if the proposition to be proved is a proposition of pure reason, and if I aim at passing beyond my empirical conceptions by the aid of mere ideas, it is necessary that the proof should first show that such a step in synthesis is possible (which it is not), before it proceeds to prove the truth of the proposition itself. The so-called proof of the simple nature of the soul from the unity of apperception, is a very plausible one. But it contains no answer to the objection, that, as the notion of absolute simplicity is not a conception which is directly applicable to a perception, but is an idea which must be inferred—if at all—from observation, it is by no means evident how the mere fact of consciousness, which is contained in all thought, although in so far a simple representation, can conduct me to the consciousness and cognition of a thing which is purely a thinking substance. When I represent to my mind the power of my body as in motion, my body in this thought is so far absolute unity, and my representation of it is a simple one; and hence I can indicate this representation by the motion of a point, because I have made abstraction of the size or volume of the body. But I cannot hence infer that, given merely the moving power of a body, the body may be cogitated as simple substance, merely because the representation in my mind takes no account of its content in space, and is consequently simple. The simple, in abstraction, is very different from the objectively simple; and hence the Ego, which is simple in the first sense, may, in the second sense, as indicating the soul itself, be a very complex conception, with a very various content. Thus it is evident that in all such arguments there lurks a paralogism. We guess (for without some such surmise our suspicion would not be excited in reference to a proof of this character) at the presence of the paralogism, by keeping ever before us a criterion of the possibility of those synthetical propositions which aim at proving more than experience can teach us. This criterion is obtained from the observation that such proofs do not lead us directly from the subject of the proposition to be proved to the required predicate, but find it necessary to presuppose the possibility of extending our cognition à priori by means of ideas. We must, accordingly, always use the greatest caution; we require, before attempting any proof, to consider how it is possible to extend the sphere of cognition by the operations of pure reason, and from what source we are to derive knowledge, which is not obtained from the analysis of conceptions, nor relates, by anticipation, to possible experience. We shall thus spare ourselves much severe and fruitless labour, by not expecting from reason what is beyond its power, or rather by subjecting it to discipline, and teaching it to moderate its vehement desires for the extension of the sphere of cognition.\n\nThe first rule for our guidance is, therefore, not to attempt a transcendental proof, before we have considered from what source we are to derive the principles upon which the proof is to be based, and what right we have to expect that our conclusions from these principles will be veracious. If they are principles of the understanding, it is vain to expect that we should attain by their means to ideas of pure reason; for these principles are valid only in regard to objects of possible experience. If they are principles of pure reason, our labour is alike in vain. For the principles of reason, if employed as objective, are without exception dialectical and possess no validity or truth, except as regulative principles of the systematic employment of reason in experience. But when such delusive proof are presented to us, it is our duty to meet them with the non liquet of a matured judgement; and, although we are unable to expose the particular sophism upon which the proof is based, we have a right to demand a deduction of the principles employed in it; and, if these principles have their origin in pure reason alone, such a deduction is absolutely impossible. And thus it is unnecessary that we should trouble ourselves with the exposure and confutation of every sophistical illusion; we may, at once, bring all dialectic, which is inexhaustible in the production of fallacies, before the bar of critical reason, which tests the principles upon which all dialectical procedure is based. The second peculiarity of transcendental proof is that a transcendental proposition cannot rest upon more than a single proof. If I am drawing conclusions, not from conceptions, but from intuition corresponding to a conception, be it pure intuition, as in mathematics, or empirical, as in natural science, the intuition which forms the basis of my inferences presents me with materials for many synthetical propositions, which I can connect in various modes, while, as it is allowable to proceed from different points in the intention, I can arrive by different paths at the same proposition.\n\nBut every transcendental proposition sets out from a conception, and posits the synthetical condition of the possibility of an object according to this conception. There must, therefore, be but one ground of proof, because it is the conception alone which determines the object; and thus the proof cannot contain anything more than the determination of the object according to the conception. In our Transcendental Analytic, for example, we inferred the principle: Every event has a cause, from the only condition of the objective possibility of our conception of an event. This is that an event cannot be determined in time, and consequently cannot form a part of experience, unless it stands under this dynamical law. This is the only possible ground of proof; for our conception of an event possesses objective validity, that is, is a true conception, only because the law of causality determines an object to which it can refer. Other arguments in support of this principle have been attempted—such as that from the contingent nature of a phenomenon; but when this argument is considered, we can discover no criterion of contingency, except the fact of an event—of something happening, that is to say, the existence which is preceded by the non-existence of an object, and thus we fall back on the very thing to be proved. If the proposition: “Every thinking being is simple,” is to be proved, we keep to the conception of the ego, which is simple, and to which all thought has a relation. The same is the case with the transcendental proof of the existence of a Deity, which is based solely upon the harmony and reciprocal fitness of the conceptions of an ens realissimum and a necessary being, and cannot be attempted in any other manner.\n\nThis caution serves to simplify very much the criticism of all propositions of reason. When reason employs conceptions alone, only one proof of its thesis is possible, if any. When, therefore, the dogmatist advances with ten arguments in favour of a proposition, we may be sure that not one of them is conclusive. For if he possessed one which proved the proposition he brings forward to demonstration—as must always be the case with the propositions of pure reason—what need is there for any more? His intention can only be similar to that of the advocate who had different arguments for different judges; this availing himself of the weakness of those who examine his arguments, who, without going into any profound investigation, adopt the view of the case which seems most probable at first sight and decide according to it.\n\nThe third rule for the guidance of pure reason in the conduct of a proof is that all transcendental proofs must never be apagogic or indirect, but always ostensive or direct. The direct or ostensive proof not only establishes the truth of the proposition to be proved, but exposes the grounds of its truth; the apagogic, on the other hand, may assure us of the truth of the proposition, but it cannot enable us to comprehend the grounds of its possibility. The latter is, accordingly, rather an auxiliary to an argument, than a strictly philosophical and rational mode of procedure. In one respect, however, they have an advantage over direct proofs, from the fact that the mode of arguing by contradiction, which they employ, renders our understanding of the question more clear, and approximates the proof to the certainty of an intuitional demonstration.\n\nThe true reason why indirect proofs are employed in different sciences is this. When the grounds upon which we seek to base a cognition are too various or too profound, we try whether or not we may not discover the truth of our cognition from its consequences. The modus ponens of reasoning from the truth of its inferences to the truth of a proposition would be admissible if all the inferences that can be drawn from it are known to be true; for in this case there can be only one possible ground for these inferences, and that is the true one. But this is a quite impracticable procedure, as it surpasses all our powers to discover all the possible inferences that can be drawn from a proposition. But this mode of reasoning is employed, under favour, when we wish to prove the truth of an hypothesis; in which case we admit the truth of the conclusion—which is supported by analogy—that, if all the inferences we have drawn and examined agree with the proposition assumed, all other possible inferences will also agree with it. But, in this way, an hypothesis can never be established as a demonstrated truth. The modus tollens of reasoning from known inferences to the unknown proposition, is not only a rigorous, but a very easy mode of proof. For, if it can be shown that but one inference from a proposition is false, then the proposition must itself be false. Instead, then, of examining, in an ostensive argument, the whole series of the grounds on which the truth of a proposition rests, we need only take the opposite of this proposition, and if one inference from it be false, then must the opposite be itself false; and, consequently, the proposition which we wished to prove must be true.\n\nThe apagogic method of proof is admissible only in those sciences where it is impossible to mistake a subjective representation for an objective cognition. Where this is possible, it is plain that the opposite of a given proposition may contradict merely the subjective conditions of thought, and not the objective cognition; or it may happen that both propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition, which is incorrectly considered to be objective, and, as the condition is itself false, both propositions may be false, and it will, consequently, be impossible to conclude the truth of the one from the falseness of the other.\n\nIn mathematics such subreptions are impossible; and it is in this science, accordingly, that the indirect mode of proof has its true place. In the science of nature, where all assertion is based upon empirical intuition, such subreptions may be guarded against by the repeated comparison of observations; but this mode of proof is of little value in this sphere of knowledge. But the transcendental efforts of pure reason are all made in the sphere of the subjective, which is the real medium of all dialectical illusion; and thus reason endeavours, in its premisses, to impose upon us subjective representations for objective cognitions. In the transcendental sphere of pure reason, then, and in the case of synthetical propositions, it is inadmissible to support a statement by disproving the counter-statement. For only two cases are possible; either, the counter-statement is nothing but the enouncement of the inconsistency of the opposite opinion with the subjective conditions of reason, which does not affect the real case (for example, we cannot comprehend the unconditioned necessity of the existence of a being, and hence every speculative proof of the existence of such a being must be opposed on subjective grounds, while the possibility of this being in itself cannot with justice be denied); or, both propositions, being dialectical in their nature, are based upon an impossible conception. In this latter case the rule applies: non entis nulla sunt predicata; that is to say, what we affirm and what we deny, respecting such an object, are equally untrue, and the apagogic mode of arriving at the truth is in this case impossible. If, for example, we presuppose that the world of sense is given in itself in its totality, it is false, either that it is infinite, or that it is finite and limited in space. Both are false, because the hypothesis is false. For the notion of phenomena (as mere representations) which are given in themselves (as objects) is self-contradictory; and the infinitude of this imaginary whole would, indeed, be unconditioned, but would be inconsistent (as everything in the phenomenal world is conditioned) with the unconditioned determination and finitude of quantities which is presupposed in our conception.\n\nThe apagogic mode of proof is the true source of those illusions which have always had so strong an attraction for the admirers of dogmatical philosophy. It may be compared to a champion who maintains the honour and claims of the party he has adopted by offering battle to all who doubt the validity of these claims and the purity of that honour; while nothing can be proved in this way, except the respective strength of the combatants, and the advantage, in this respect, is always on the side of the attacking party. Spectators, observing that each party is alternately conqueror and conquered, are led to regard the subject of dispute as beyond the power of man to decide upon. But such an opinion cannot be justified; and it is sufficient to apply to these reasoners the remark:\n\n_Non defensoribus istis  \nTempus eget._\n\nEach must try to establish his assertions by a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof employed in his argument, and thus enable us to see in what way the claims of reason may be supported. If an opponent bases his assertions upon subjective grounds, he may be refuted with ease; not, however to the advantage of the dogmatist, who likewise depends upon subjective sources of cognition and is in like manner driven into a corner by his opponent. But, if parties employ the direct method of procedure, they will soon discover the difficulty, nay, the impossibility of proving their assertions, and will be forced to appeal to prescription and precedence; or they will, by the help of criticism, discover with ease the dogmatical illusions by which they had been mocked, and compel reason to renounce its exaggerated pretensions to speculative insight and to confine itself within the limits of its proper sphere—that of practical principles.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","text":"# Proofs\n\n### Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs\n\nIt is a peculiarity, which distinguishes the proofs of transcendental synthetical propositions from those of all other à priori synthetical cognitions, that reason, in the case of the former, does not apply its conceptions directly to an object, but is first obliged to prove, à priori, the objective validity of these conceptions and the possibility of their syntheses. This is not merely a prudential rule, it is essential to the very possibility of the proof of a transcendental proposition. If I am required to pass, à priori, beyond the conception of an object, I find that it is utterly impossible without the guidance of something which is not contained in the conception. In mathematics, it is à priori intuition that guides my synthesis; and, in this case, all our conclusions may be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as we are dealing only with conceptions of the understanding, we are guided by possible experience. That is to say, a proof in the sphere of transcendental cognition does not show that the given conception (that of an event, for example) leads directly to another conception (that of a cause)—for this would be a saltus which nothing can justify; but it shows that experience itself, and consequently the object of experience, is impossible without the connection indicated by these conceptions. It follows that such a proof must demonstrate the possibility of arriving, synthetically and à priori, at a certain knowledge of things, which was not contained in our conceptions of these things. Unless we pay particular attention to this requirement, our proofs, instead of pursuing the straight path indicated by reason, follow the tortuous road of mere subjective association. The illusory conviction, which rests upon subjective causes of association, and which is considered as resulting from the perception of a real and objective natural affinity, is always open to doubt and suspicion. For this reason, all the attempts which have been made to prove the principle of sufficient reason, have, according to the universal admission of philosophers, been quite unsuccessful; and, before the appearance of transcendental criticism, it was considered better, as this principle could not be abandoned, to appeal boldly to the common sense of mankind (a proceeding which always proves that the problem, which reason ought to solve, is one in which philosophers find great difficulties), rather than attempt to discover new dogmatical proofs.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","text":"But, if the proposition to be proved is a proposition of pure reason, and if I aim at passing beyond my empirical conceptions by the aid of mere ideas, it is necessary that the proof should first show that such a step in synthesis is possible (which it is not), before it proceeds to prove the truth of the proposition itself. The so-called proof of the simple nature of the soul from the unity of apperception, is a very plausible one. But it contains no answer to the objection, that, as the notion of absolute simplicity is not a conception which is directly applicable to a perception, but is an idea which must be inferred—if at all—from observation, it is by no means evident how the mere fact of consciousness, which is contained in all thought, although in so far a simple representation, can conduct me to the consciousness and cognition of a thing which is purely a thinking substance. When I represent to my mind the power of my body as in motion, my body in this thought is so far absolute unity, and my representation of it is a simple one; and hence I can indicate this representation by the motion of a point, because I have made abstraction of the size or volume of the body. But I cannot hence infer that, given merely the moving power of a body, the body may be cogitated as simple substance, merely because the representation in my mind takes no account of its content in space, and is consequently simple. The simple, in abstraction, is very different from the objectively simple; and hence the Ego, which is simple in the first sense, may, in the second sense, as indicating the soul itself, be a very complex conception, with a very various content. Thus it is evident that in all such arguments there lurks a paralogism. We guess (for without some such surmise our suspicion would not be excited in reference to a proof of this character) at the presence of the paralogism, by keeping ever before us a criterion of the possibility of those synthetical propositions which aim at proving more than experience can teach us. This criterion is obtained from the observation that such proofs do not lead us directly from the subject of the proposition to be proved to the required predicate, but find it necessary to presuppose the possibility of extending our cognition à priori by means of ideas. We must, accordingly, always use the greatest caution; we require, before attempting any proof, to consider how it is possible to extend the sphere of cognition by the operations of pure reason, and from what source we are to derive knowledge, which is not obtained from the analysis of conceptions, nor relates, by anticipation, to possible experience. We shall thus spare ourselves much severe and fruitless labour, by not expecting from reason what is beyond its power, or rather by subjecting it to discipline, and teaching it to moderate its vehement desires for the extension of the sphere of cognition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","text":"The first rule for our guidance is, therefore, not to attempt a transcendental proof, before we have considered from what source we are to derive the principles upon which the proof is to be based, and what right we have to expect that our conclusions from these principles will be veracious. If they are principles of the understanding, it is vain to expect that we should attain by their means to ideas of pure reason; for these principles are valid only in regard to objects of possible experience. If they are principles of pure reason, our labour is alike in vain. For the principles of reason, if employed as objective, are without exception dialectical and possess no validity or truth, except as regulative principles of the systematic employment of reason in experience. But when such delusive proof are presented to us, it is our duty to meet them with the non liquet of a matured judgement; and, although we are unable to expose the particular sophism upon which the proof is based, we have a right to demand a deduction of the principles employed in it; and, if these principles have their origin in pure reason alone, such a deduction is absolutely impossible. And thus it is unnecessary that we should trouble ourselves with the exposure and confutation of every sophistical illusion; we may, at once, bring all dialectic, which is inexhaustible in the production of fallacies, before the bar of critical reason, which tests the principles upon which all dialectical procedure is based. The second peculiarity of transcendental proof is that a transcendental proposition cannot rest upon more than a single proof. If I am drawing conclusions, not from conceptions, but from intuition corresponding to a conception, be it pure intuition, as in mathematics, or empirical, as in natural science, the intuition which forms the basis of my inferences presents me with materials for many synthetical propositions, which I can connect in various modes, while, as it is allowable to proceed from different points in the intention, I can arrive by different paths at the same proposition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","text":"But every transcendental proposition sets out from a conception, and posits the synthetical condition of the possibility of an object according to this conception. There must, therefore, be but one ground of proof, because it is the conception alone which determines the object; and thus the proof cannot contain anything more than the determination of the object according to the conception. In our Transcendental Analytic, for example, we inferred the principle: Every event has a cause, from the only condition of the objective possibility of our conception of an event. This is that an event cannot be determined in time, and consequently cannot form a part of experience, unless it stands under this dynamical law. This is the only possible ground of proof; for our conception of an event possesses objective validity, that is, is a true conception, only because the law of causality determines an object to which it can refer. Other arguments in support of this principle have been attempted—such as that from the contingent nature of a phenomenon; but when this argument is considered, we can discover no criterion of contingency, except the fact of an event—of something happening, that is to say, the existence which is preceded by the non-existence of an object, and thus we fall back on the very thing to be proved. If the proposition: “Every thinking being is simple,” is to be proved, we keep to the conception of the ego, which is simple, and to which all thought has a relation. The same is the case with the transcendental proof of the existence of a Deity, which is based solely upon the harmony and reciprocal fitness of the conceptions of an ens realissimum and a necessary being, and cannot be attempted in any other manner.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","text":"This caution serves to simplify very much the criticism of all propositions of reason. When reason employs conceptions alone, only one proof of its thesis is possible, if any. When, therefore, the dogmatist advances with ten arguments in favour of a proposition, we may be sure that not one of them is conclusive. For if he possessed one which proved the proposition he brings forward to demonstration—as must always be the case with the propositions of pure reason—what need is there for any more? His intention can only be similar to that of the advocate who had different arguments for different judges; this availing himself of the weakness of those who examine his arguments, who, without going into any profound investigation, adopt the view of the case which seems most probable at first sight and decide according to it.\n\nThe third rule for the guidance of pure reason in the conduct of a proof is that all transcendental proofs must never be apagogic or indirect, but always ostensive or direct. The direct or ostensive proof not only establishes the truth of the proposition to be proved, but exposes the grounds of its truth; the apagogic, on the other hand, may assure us of the truth of the proposition, but it cannot enable us to comprehend the grounds of its possibility. The latter is, accordingly, rather an auxiliary to an argument, than a strictly philosophical and rational mode of procedure. In one respect, however, they have an advantage over direct proofs, from the fact that the mode of arguing by contradiction, which they employ, renders our understanding of the question more clear, and approximates the proof to the certainty of an intuitional demonstration.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0006","text":"The true reason why indirect proofs are employed in different sciences is this. When the grounds upon which we seek to base a cognition are too various or too profound, we try whether or not we may not discover the truth of our cognition from its consequences. The modus ponens of reasoning from the truth of its inferences to the truth of a proposition would be admissible if all the inferences that can be drawn from it are known to be true; for in this case there can be only one possible ground for these inferences, and that is the true one. But this is a quite impracticable procedure, as it surpasses all our powers to discover all the possible inferences that can be drawn from a proposition. But this mode of reasoning is employed, under favour, when we wish to prove the truth of an hypothesis; in which case we admit the truth of the conclusion—which is supported by analogy—that, if all the inferences we have drawn and examined agree with the proposition assumed, all other possible inferences will also agree with it. But, in this way, an hypothesis can never be established as a demonstrated truth. The modus tollens of reasoning from known inferences to the unknown proposition, is not only a rigorous, but a very easy mode of proof. For, if it can be shown that but one inference from a proposition is false, then the proposition must itself be false. Instead, then, of examining, in an ostensive argument, the whole series of the grounds on which the truth of a proposition rests, we need only take the opposite of this proposition, and if one inference from it be false, then must the opposite be itself false; and, consequently, the proposition which we wished to prove must be true.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","text":"The apagogic method of proof is admissible only in those sciences where it is impossible to mistake a subjective representation for an objective cognition. Where this is possible, it is plain that the opposite of a given proposition may contradict merely the subjective conditions of thought, and not the objective cognition; or it may happen that both propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition, which is incorrectly considered to be objective, and, as the condition is itself false, both propositions may be false, and it will, consequently, be impossible to conclude the truth of the one from the falseness of the other.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","text":"In mathematics such subreptions are impossible; and it is in this science, accordingly, that the indirect mode of proof has its true place. In the science of nature, where all assertion is based upon empirical intuition, such subreptions may be guarded against by the repeated comparison of observations; but this mode of proof is of little value in this sphere of knowledge. But the transcendental efforts of pure reason are all made in the sphere of the subjective, which is the real medium of all dialectical illusion; and thus reason endeavours, in its premisses, to impose upon us subjective representations for objective cognitions. In the transcendental sphere of pure reason, then, and in the case of synthetical propositions, it is inadmissible to support a statement by disproving the counter-statement. For only two cases are possible; either, the counter-statement is nothing but the enouncement of the inconsistency of the opposite opinion with the subjective conditions of reason, which does not affect the real case (for example, we cannot comprehend the unconditioned necessity of the existence of a being, and hence every speculative proof of the existence of such a being must be opposed on subjective grounds, while the possibility of this being in itself cannot with justice be denied); or, both propositions, being dialectical in their nature, are based upon an impossible conception. In this latter case the rule applies: non entis nulla sunt predicata; that is to say, what we affirm and what we deny, respecting such an object, are equally untrue, and the apagogic mode of arriving at the truth is in this case impossible. If, for example, we presuppose that the world of sense is given in itself in its totality, it is false, either that it is infinite, or that it is finite and limited in space. Both are false, because the hypothesis is false. For the notion of phenomena (as mere representations) which are given in themselves (as objects) is self-contradictory; and the infinitude of this imaginary whole would, indeed, be unconditioned, but would be inconsistent (as everything in the phenomenal world is conditioned) with the unconditioned determination and finitude of quantities which is presupposed in our conception.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","text":"The apagogic mode of proof is the true source of those illusions which have always had so strong an attraction for the admirers of dogmatical philosophy. It may be compared to a champion who maintains the honour and claims of the party he has adopted by offering battle to all who doubt the validity of these claims and the purity of that honour; while nothing can be proved in this way, except the respective strength of the combatants, and the advantage, in this respect, is always on the side of the attacking party. Spectators, observing that each party is alternately conqueror and conquered, are led to regard the subject of dispute as beyond the power of man to decide upon. But such an opinion cannot be justified; and it is sufficient to apply to these reasoners the remark:\n\n_Non defensoribus istis  \nTempus eget._\n\nEach must try to establish his assertions by a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof employed in his argument, and thus enable us to see in what way the claims of reason may be supported. If an opponent bases his assertions upon subjective grounds, he may be refuted with ease; not, however to the advantage of the dogmatist, who likewise depends upon subjective sources of cognition and is in like manner driven into a corner by his opponent. But, if parties employ the direct method of procedure, they will soon discover the difficulty, nay, the impossibility of proving their assertions, and will be forced to appeal to prescription and precedence; or they will, by the help of criticism, discover with ease the dogmatical illusions by which they had been mocked, and compel reason to renounce its exaggerated pretensions to speculative insight and to confine itself within the limits of its proper sphere—that of practical principles.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"11f964fe12249560d7e6a154178b85fa39434cc94dc9174fe761eb61f438a46a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","order":89,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CICP","text":"### Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason\n\nIt is a humiliating consideration for human reason that it is incompetent to discover truth by means of pure speculation, but, on the contrary, stands in need of discipline to check its deviations from the straight path and to expose the illusions which it originates. But, on the other hand, this consideration ought to elevate and to give it confidence, for this discipline is exercised by itself alone, and it is subject to the censure of no other power. The bounds, moreover, which it is forced to set to its speculative exercise, form likewise a check upon the fallacious pretensions of opponents; and thus what remains of its possessions, after these exaggerated claims have been disallowed, is secure from attack or usurpation. The greatest, and perhaps the only, use of all philosophy of pure reason is, accordingly, of a purely negative character. It is not an organon for the extension, but a discipline for the determination, of the limits of its exercise; and without laying claim to the discovery of new truth, it has the modest merit of guarding against error.\n\nAt the same time, there must be some source of positive cognitions which belong to the domain of pure reason and which become the causes of error only from our mistaking their true character, while they form the goal towards which reason continually strives. How else can we account for the inextinguishable desire in the human mind to find a firm footing in some region beyond the limits of the world of experience? It hopes to attain to the possession of a knowledge in which it has the deepest interest. It enters upon the path of pure speculation; but in vain. We have some reason, however, to expect that, in the only other way that lies open to it—the path of practical reason—it may meet with better success.\n\nI understand by a canon a list of the à priori principles of the proper employment of certain faculties of cognition. Thus general logic, in its analytical department, is a formal canon for the faculties of understanding and reason. In the same way, Transcendental Analytic was seen to be a canon of the pure understanding; for it alone is competent to enounce true à priori synthetical cognitions. But, when no proper employment of a faculty of cognition is possible, no canon can exist. But the synthetical cognition of pure speculative reason is, as has been shown, completely impossible. There cannot, therefore, exist any canon for the speculative exercise of this faculty—for its speculative exercise is entirely dialectical; and, consequently, transcendental logic, in this respect, is merely a discipline, and not a canon. If, then, there is any proper mode of employing the faculty of pure reason—in which case there must be a canon for this faculty—this canon will relate, not to the speculative, but to the practical use of reason. This canon we now proceed to investigate.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason\n\nIt is a humiliating consideration for human reason that it is incompetent to discover truth by means of pure speculation, but, on the contrary, stands in need of discipline to check its deviations from the straight path and to expose the illusions which it originates. But, on the other hand, this consideration ought to elevate and to give it confidence, for this discipline is exercised by itself alone, and it is subject to the censure of no other power. The bounds, moreover, which it is forced to set to its speculative exercise, form likewise a check upon the fallacious pretensions of opponents; and thus what remains of its possessions, after these exaggerated claims have been disallowed, is secure from attack or usurpation. The greatest, and perhaps the only, use of all philosophy of pure reason is, accordingly, of a purely negative character. It is not an organon for the extension, but a discipline for the determination, of the limits of its exercise; and without laying claim to the discovery of new truth, it has the modest merit of guarding against error.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","text":"At the same time, there must be some source of positive cognitions which belong to the domain of pure reason and which become the causes of error only from our mistaking their true character, while they form the goal towards which reason continually strives. How else can we account for the inextinguishable desire in the human mind to find a firm footing in some region beyond the limits of the world of experience? It hopes to attain to the possession of a knowledge in which it has the deepest interest. It enters upon the path of pure speculation; but in vain. We have some reason, however, to expect that, in the only other way that lies open to it—the path of practical reason—it may meet with better success.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","text":"I understand by a canon a list of the à priori principles of the proper employment of certain faculties of cognition. Thus general logic, in its analytical department, is a formal canon for the faculties of understanding and reason. In the same way, Transcendental Analytic was seen to be a canon of the pure understanding; for it alone is competent to enounce true à priori synthetical cognitions. But, when no proper employment of a faculty of cognition is possible, no canon can exist. But the synthetical cognition of pure speculative reason is, as has been shown, completely impossible. There cannot, therefore, exist any canon for the speculative exercise of this faculty—for its speculative exercise is entirely dialectical; and, consequently, transcendental logic, in this respect, is merely a discipline, and not a canon. If, then, there is any proper mode of employing the faculty of pure reason—in which case there must be a canon for this faculty—this canon will relate, not to the speculative, but to the practical use of reason. This canon we now proceed to investigate.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4c169052351f0888d1cdda6c54bef0ab6ed4cb08f1450459a91b44fc68e46b88","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","order":90,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIUE","text":"### Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason\n\nThere exists in the faculty of reason a natural desire to venture beyond the field of experience, to attempt to reach the utmost bounds of all cognition by the help of ideas alone, and not to rest satisfied until it has fulfilled its course and raised the sum of its cognitions into a self-subsistent systematic whole. Is the motive for this endeavour to be found in its speculative, or in its practical interests alone?\n\nSetting aside, at present, the results of the labours of pure reason in its speculative exercise, I shall merely inquire regarding the problems the solution of which forms its ultimate aim, whether reached or not, and in relation to which all other aims are but partial and intermediate. These highest aims must, from the nature of reason, possess complete unity; otherwise the highest interest of humanity could not be successfully promoted.\n\nThe transcendental speculation of reason relates to three things: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. The speculative interest which reason has in those questions is very small; and, for its sake alone, we should not undertake the labour of transcendental investigation—a labour full of toil and ceaseless struggle. We should be loth to undertake this labour, because the discoveries we might make would not be of the smallest use in the sphere of concrete or physical investigation. We may find out that the will is free, but this knowledge only relates to the intelligible cause of our volition. As regards the phenomena or expressions of this will, that is, our actions, we are bound, in obedience to an inviolable maxim, without which reason cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, to explain these in the same way as we explain all the other phenomena of nature, that is to say, according to its unchangeable laws. We may have discovered the spirituality and immortality of the soul, but we cannot employ this knowledge to explain the phenomena of this life, nor the peculiar nature of the future, because our conception of an incorporeal nature is purely negative and does not add anything to our knowledge, and the only inferences to be drawn from it are purely fictitious. If, again, we prove the existence of a supreme intelligence, we should be able from it to make the conformity to aims existing in the arrangement of the world comprehensible; but we should not be justified in deducing from it any particular arrangement or disposition, or inferring any where it is not perceived. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason that we must not overlook natural causes, or refuse to listen to the teaching of experience, for the sake of deducing what we know and perceive from something that transcends all our knowledge. In one word, these three propositions are, for the speculative reason, always transcendent, and cannot be employed as immanent principles in relation to the objects of experience; they are, consequently, of no use to us in this sphere, being but the valueless results of the severe but unprofitable efforts of reason.\n\nIf, then, the actual cognition of these three cardinal propositions is perfectly useless, while Reason uses her utmost endeavours to induce us to admit them, it is plain that their real value and importance relate to our practical, and not to our speculative interest.\n\nI term all that is possible through free will, practical. But if the conditions of the exercise of free volition are empirical, reason can have only a regulative, and not a constitutive, influence upon it, and is serviceable merely for the introduction of unity into its empirical laws. In the moral philosophy of prudence, for example, the sole business of reason is to bring about a union of all the ends, which are aimed at by our inclinations, into one ultimate end—that of happiness—and to show the agreement which should exist among the means of attaining that end. In this sphere, accordingly, reason cannot present to us any other than pragmatical laws of free action, for our guidance towards the aims set up by the senses, and is incompetent to give us laws which are pure and determined completely à priori. On the other hand, pure practical laws, the ends of which have been given by reason entirely à priori, and which are not empirically conditioned, but are, on the contrary, absolutely imperative in their nature, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws; and these alone belong to the sphere of the practical exercise of reason, and admit of a canon.\n\nAll the powers of reason, in the sphere of what may be termed pure philosophy, are, in fact, directed to the three above-mentioned problems alone. These again have a still higher end—the answer to the question, what we ought to do, if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Now, as this problem relates to our in reference to the highest aim of humanity, it is evident that the ultimate intention of nature, in the constitution of our reason, has been directed to the moral alone.\n\nWe must take care, however, in turning our attention to an object which is foreign[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-78) to the sphere of transcendental philosophy, not to injure the unity of our system by digressions, nor, on the other hand, to fail in clearness, by saying too little on the new subject of discussion. I hope to avoid both extremes, by keeping as close as possible to the transcendental, and excluding all psychological, that is, empirical, elements.\n\n[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-78) All practical conceptions relate to objects of pleasure and pain, and consequently—in an indirect manner, at least—to objects of feeling. But as feeling is not a faculty of representation, but lies out of the sphere of our powers of cognition, the elements of our judgements, in so far as they relate to pleasure or pain, that is, the elements of our practical judgements, do not belong to transcendental philosophy, which has to do with pure à priori cognitions alone.\n\nI have to remark, in the first place, that at present I treat of the conception of freedom in the practical sense only, and set aside the corresponding transcendental conception, which cannot be employed as a ground of explanation in the phenomenal world, but is itself a problem for pure reason. A will is purely animal (arbitrium brutum) when it is determined by sensuous impulses or instincts only, that is, when it is determined in a pathological manner. A will, which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, consequently by motives presented by reason alone, is called a free will (arbitrium liberum); and everything which is connected with this free will, either as principle or consequence, is termed practical. The existence of practical freedom can be proved from experience alone. For the human will is not determined by that alone which immediately affects the senses; on the contrary, we have the power, by calling up the notion of what is useful or hurtful in a more distant relation, of overcoming the immediate impressions on our sensuous faculty of desire. But these considerations of what is desirable in relation to our whole state, that is, is in the end good and useful, are based entirely upon reason. This faculty, accordingly, enounces laws, which are imperative or objective laws of freedom and which tell us what ought to take place, thus distinguishing themselves from the laws of nature, which relate to that which does take place. The laws of freedom or of free will are hence termed practical laws.\n\nWhether reason is not itself, in the actual delivery of these laws, determined in its turn by other influences, and whether the action which, in relation to sensuous impulses, we call free, may not, in relation to higher and more remote operative causes, really form a part of nature—these are questions which do not here concern us. They are purely speculative questions; and all we have to do, in the practical sphere, is to inquire into the rule of conduct which reason has to present. Experience demonstrates to us the existence of practical freedom as one of the causes which exist in nature, that is, it shows the causal power of reason in the determination of the will. The idea of transcendental freedom, on the contrary, requires that reason—in relation to its causal power of commencing a series of phenomena—should be independent of all sensuous determining causes; and thus it seems to be in opposition to the law of nature and to all possible experience. It therefore remains a problem for the human mind. But this problem does not concern reason in its practical use; and we have, therefore, in a canon of pure reason, to do with only two questions, which relate to the practical interest of pure reason: Is there a God? and, Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom is purely speculative, and we may therefore set it entirely aside when we come to treat of practical reason. Besides, we have already discussed this subject in the antinomy of pure reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason\n\nThere exists in the faculty of reason a natural desire to venture beyond the field of experience, to attempt to reach the utmost bounds of all cognition by the help of ideas alone, and not to rest satisfied until it has fulfilled its course and raised the sum of its cognitions into a self-subsistent systematic whole. Is the motive for this endeavour to be found in its speculative, or in its practical interests alone?\n\nSetting aside, at present, the results of the labours of pure reason in its speculative exercise, I shall merely inquire regarding the problems the solution of which forms its ultimate aim, whether reached or not, and in relation to which all other aims are but partial and intermediate. These highest aims must, from the nature of reason, possess complete unity; otherwise the highest interest of humanity could not be successfully promoted.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","text":"The transcendental speculation of reason relates to three things: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. The speculative interest which reason has in those questions is very small; and, for its sake alone, we should not undertake the labour of transcendental investigation—a labour full of toil and ceaseless struggle. We should be loth to undertake this labour, because the discoveries we might make would not be of the smallest use in the sphere of concrete or physical investigation. We may find out that the will is free, but this knowledge only relates to the intelligible cause of our volition. As regards the phenomena or expressions of this will, that is, our actions, we are bound, in obedience to an inviolable maxim, without which reason cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, to explain these in the same way as we explain all the other phenomena of nature, that is to say, according to its unchangeable laws. We may have discovered the spirituality and immortality of the soul, but we cannot employ this knowledge to explain the phenomena of this life, nor the peculiar nature of the future, because our conception of an incorporeal nature is purely negative and does not add anything to our knowledge, and the only inferences to be drawn from it are purely fictitious. If, again, we prove the existence of a supreme intelligence, we should be able from it to make the conformity to aims existing in the arrangement of the world comprehensible; but we should not be justified in deducing from it any particular arrangement or disposition, or inferring any where it is not perceived. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason that we must not overlook natural causes, or refuse to listen to the teaching of experience, for the sake of deducing what we know and perceive from something that transcends all our knowledge. In one word, these three propositions are, for the speculative reason, always transcendent, and cannot be employed as immanent principles in relation to the objects of experience; they are, consequently, of no use to us in this sphere, being but the valueless results of the severe but unprofitable efforts of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","text":"If, then, the actual cognition of these three cardinal propositions is perfectly useless, while Reason uses her utmost endeavours to induce us to admit them, it is plain that their real value and importance relate to our practical, and not to our speculative interest.\n\nI term all that is possible through free will, practical. But if the conditions of the exercise of free volition are empirical, reason can have only a regulative, and not a constitutive, influence upon it, and is serviceable merely for the introduction of unity into its empirical laws. In the moral philosophy of prudence, for example, the sole business of reason is to bring about a union of all the ends, which are aimed at by our inclinations, into one ultimate end—that of happiness—and to show the agreement which should exist among the means of attaining that end. In this sphere, accordingly, reason cannot present to us any other than pragmatical laws of free action, for our guidance towards the aims set up by the senses, and is incompetent to give us laws which are pure and determined completely à priori. On the other hand, pure practical laws, the ends of which have been given by reason entirely à priori, and which are not empirically conditioned, but are, on the contrary, absolutely imperative in their nature, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws; and these alone belong to the sphere of the practical exercise of reason, and admit of a canon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","text":"All the powers of reason, in the sphere of what may be termed pure philosophy, are, in fact, directed to the three above-mentioned problems alone. These again have a still higher end—the answer to the question, what we ought to do, if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Now, as this problem relates to our in reference to the highest aim of humanity, it is evident that the ultimate intention of nature, in the constitution of our reason, has been directed to the moral alone.\n\nWe must take care, however, in turning our attention to an object which is foreign[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-78) to the sphere of transcendental philosophy, not to injure the unity of our system by digressions, nor, on the other hand, to fail in clearness, by saying too little on the new subject of discussion. I hope to avoid both extremes, by keeping as close as possible to the transcendental, and excluding all psychological, that is, empirical, elements.\n\n[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-78) All practical conceptions relate to objects of pleasure and pain, and consequently—in an indirect manner, at least—to objects of feeling. But as feeling is not a faculty of representation, but lies out of the sphere of our powers of cognition, the elements of our judgements, in so far as they relate to pleasure or pain, that is, the elements of our practical judgements, do not belong to transcendental philosophy, which has to do with pure à priori cognitions alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","text":"I have to remark, in the first place, that at present I treat of the conception of freedom in the practical sense only, and set aside the corresponding transcendental conception, which cannot be employed as a ground of explanation in the phenomenal world, but is itself a problem for pure reason. A will is purely animal (arbitrium brutum) when it is determined by sensuous impulses or instincts only, that is, when it is determined in a pathological manner. A will, which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, consequently by motives presented by reason alone, is called a free will (arbitrium liberum); and everything which is connected with this free will, either as principle or consequence, is termed practical. The existence of practical freedom can be proved from experience alone. For the human will is not determined by that alone which immediately affects the senses; on the contrary, we have the power, by calling up the notion of what is useful or hurtful in a more distant relation, of overcoming the immediate impressions on our sensuous faculty of desire. But these considerations of what is desirable in relation to our whole state, that is, is in the end good and useful, are based entirely upon reason. This faculty, accordingly, enounces laws, which are imperative or objective laws of freedom and which tell us what ought to take place, thus distinguishing themselves from the laws of nature, which relate to that which does take place. The laws of freedom or of free will are hence termed practical laws.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","text":"Whether reason is not itself, in the actual delivery of these laws, determined in its turn by other influences, and whether the action which, in relation to sensuous impulses, we call free, may not, in relation to higher and more remote operative causes, really form a part of nature—these are questions which do not here concern us. They are purely speculative questions; and all we have to do, in the practical sphere, is to inquire into the rule of conduct which reason has to present. Experience demonstrates to us the existence of practical freedom as one of the causes which exist in nature, that is, it shows the causal power of reason in the determination of the will. The idea of transcendental freedom, on the contrary, requires that reason—in relation to its causal power of commencing a series of phenomena—should be independent of all sensuous determining causes; and thus it seems to be in opposition to the law of nature and to all possible experience. It therefore remains a problem for the human mind. But this problem does not concern reason in its practical use; and we have, therefore, in a canon of pure reason, to do with only two questions, which relate to the practical interest of pure reason: Is there a God? and, Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom is purely speculative, and we may therefore set it entirely aside when we come to treat of practical reason. Besides, we have already discussed this subject in the antinomy of pure reason.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9fb1d7b278cb7ee1342517ab18a6afc9750a0722b90aa8d3e40667754c77f552","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","order":91,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIIS","text":"### Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason\n\nReason conducted us, in its speculative use, through the field of experience and, as it can never find complete satisfaction in that sphere, from thence to speculative ideas—which, however, in the end brought us back again to experience, and thus fulfilled the purpose of reason, in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accordance with our expectations. It now remains for us to consider whether pure reason can be employed in a practical sphere, and whether it will here conduct us to those ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, as we have just stated them. We shall thus ascertain whether, from the point of view of its practical interest, reason may not be able to supply us with that which, on the speculative side, it wholly denies us.\n\nThe whole interest of reason, speculative as well as practical, is centred in the three following questions:\n\n1. WHAT CAN I KNOW?  \n2. WHAT OUGHT I TO DO?  \n3. WHAT MAY I HOPE?  \n\nThe first question is purely speculative. We have, as I flatter myself, exhausted all the replies of which it is susceptible, and have at last found the reply with which reason must content itself, and with which it ought to be content, so long as it pays no regard to the practical. But from the two great ends to the attainment of which all these efforts of pure reason were in fact directed, we remain just as far removed as if we had consulted our ease and declined the task at the outset. So far, then, as knowledge is concerned, thus much, at least, is established, that, in regard to those two problems, it lies beyond our reach.\n\nThe second question is purely practical. As such it may indeed fall within the province of pure reason, but still it is not transcendental, but moral, and consequently cannot in itself form the subject of our criticism.\n\nThe third question: If I act as I ought to do, what may I then hope?—is at once practical and theoretical. The practical forms a clue to the answer of the theoretical, and—in its highest form—speculative question. For all hoping has happiness for its object and stands in precisely the same relation to the practical and the law of morality as knowing to the theoretical cognition of things and the law of nature. The former arrives finally at the conclusion that something is (which determines the ultimate end), because something ought to take place; the latter, that something is (which operates as the highest cause), because something does take place.\n\nHappiness is the satisfaction of all our desires; extensive, in regard to their multiplicity; intensive, in regard to their degree; and protensive, in regard to their duration. The practical law based on the motive of happiness I term a pragmatical law (or prudential rule); but that law, assuming such to exist, which has no other motive than the worthiness of being happy, I term a moral or ethical law. The first tells us what we have to do, if we wish to become possessed of happiness; the second dictates how we ought to act, in order to deserve happiness. The first is based upon empirical principles; for it is only by experience that I can learn either what inclinations exist which desire satisfaction, or what are the natural means of satisfying them. The second takes no account of our desires or the means of satisfying them, and regards only the freedom of a rational being, and the necessary conditions under which alone this freedom can harmonize with the distribution of happiness according to principles. This second law may therefore rest upon mere ideas of pure reason, and may be cognized à priori.\n\nI assume that there are pure moral laws which determine, entirely à priori (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness), the conduct of a rational being, or in other words, to use which it makes of its freedom, and that these laws are absolutely imperative (not merely hypothetically, on the supposition of other empirical ends), and therefore in all respects necessary. I am warranted in assuming this, not only by the arguments of the most enlightened moralists, but by the moral judgement of every man who will make the attempt to form a distinct conception of such a law.\n\nPure reason, then, contains, not indeed in its speculative, but in its practical, or, more strictly, its moral use, principles of the possibility of experience, of such actions, namely, as, in accordance with ethical precepts, might be met with in the history of man. For since reason commands that such actions should take place, it must be possible for them to take place; and hence a particular kind of systematic unity—the moral—must be possible. We have found, it is true, that the systematic unity of nature could not be established according to speculative principles of reason, because, while reason possesses a causal power in relation to freedom, it has none in relation to the whole sphere of nature; and, while moral principles of reason can produce free actions, they cannot produce natural laws. It is, then, in its practical, but especially in its moral use, that the principles of pure reason possess objective reality.\n\nI call the world a moral world, in so far as it may be in accordance with all the ethical laws—which, by virtue of the freedom of reasonable beings, it can be, and according to the necessary laws of morality it ought to be. But this world must be conceived only as an intelligible world, inasmuch as abstraction is therein made of all conditions (ends), and even of all impediments to morality (the weakness or pravity of human nature). So far, then, it is a mere idea—though still a practical idea—which may have, and ought to have, an influence on the world of sense, so as to bring it as far as possible into conformity with itself. The idea of a moral world has, therefore, objective reality, not as referring to an object of intelligible intuition—for of such an object we can form no conception whatever—but to the world of sense—conceived, however, as an object of pure reason in its practical use—and to a corpus mysticum of rational beings in it, in so far as the liberum arbitrium of the individual is placed, under and by virtue of moral laws, in complete systematic unity both with itself and with the freedom of all others.\n\nThat is the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which relate to its practical interest: Do that which will render thee worthy of happiness. The second question is this: If I conduct myself so as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I hope thereby to obtain happiness? In order to arrive at the solution of this question, we must inquire whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe à priori the law, necessarily also connect this hope with it.\n\nI say, then, that just as the moral principles are necessary according to reason in its practical use, so it is equally necessary according to reason in its theoretical use to assume that every one has ground to hope for happiness in the measure in which he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that therefore the system of morality is inseparably (though only in the idea of pure reason) connected with that of happiness.\n\nNow in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the conception of which we make abstraction of all the impediments to morality (sensuous desires), such a system of happiness, connected with and proportioned to morality, may be conceived as necessary, because freedom of volition—partly incited, and partly restrained by moral laws—would be itself the cause of general happiness; and thus rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would be themselves the authors both of their own enduring welfare and that of others. But such a system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, the carrying out of which depends upon the condition that every one acts as he ought; in other words, that all actions of reasonable beings be such as they would be if they sprung from a Supreme Will, comprehending in, or under, itself all particular wills. But since the moral law is binding on each individual in the use of his freedom of volition, even if others should not act in conformity with this law, neither the nature of things, nor the causality of actions and their relation to morality, determine how the consequences of these actions will be related to happiness; and the necessary connection of the hope of happiness with the unceasing endeavour to become worthy of happiness, cannot be cognized by reason, if we take nature alone for our guide. This connection can be hoped for only on the assumption that the cause of nature is a supreme reason, which governs according to moral laws.\n\nI term the idea of an intelligence in which the morally most perfect will, united with supreme blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, so far as happiness stands in strict relation to morality (as the worthiness of being happy), the ideal of the supreme Good. It is only, then, in the ideal of the supreme original good, that pure reason can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derivative good, and accordingly of an intelligible, that is, moral world. Now since we are necessitated by reason to conceive ourselves as belonging to such a world, while the senses present to us nothing but a world of phenomena, we must assume the former as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense (since the world of sense gives us no hint of it), and therefore as future in relation to us. Thus God and a future life are two hypotheses which, according to the principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation which this reason imposes upon us.\n\nMorality per se constitutes a system. But we can form no system of happiness, except in so far as it is dispensed in strict proportion to morality. But this is only possible in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Such a ruler, together with life in such a world, which we must look upon as future, reason finds itself compelled to assume; or it must regard the moral laws as idle dreams, since the necessary consequence which this same reason connects with them must, without this hypothesis, fall to the ground. Hence also the moral laws are universally regarded as commands, which they could not be did they not connect à priori adequate consequences with their dictates, and thus carry with them promises and threats. But this, again, they could not do, did they not reside in a necessary being, as the Supreme Good, which alone can render such a teleological unity possible.\n\nLeibnitz termed the world, when viewed in relation to the rational beings which it contains, and the moral relations in which they stand to each other, under the government of the Supreme Good, the kingdom of Grace, and distinguished it from the kingdom of Nature, in which these rational beings live, under moral laws, indeed, but expect no other consequences from their actions than such as follow according to the course of nature in the world of sense. To view ourselves, therefore, as in the kingdom of grace, in which all happiness awaits us, except in so far as we ourselves limit our participation in it by actions which render us unworthy of happiness, is a practically necessary idea of reason.\n\nPractical laws, in so far as they are subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are termed maxims. The judgements of moral according to in its purity and ultimate results are framed according ideas; the observance of its laws, according to maxims.\n\nThe whole course of our life must be subject to moral maxims; but this is impossible, unless with the moral law, which is a mere idea, reason connects an efficient cause which ordains to all conduct which is in conformity with the moral law an issue either in this or in another life, which is in exact conformity with our highest aims. Thus, without a God and without a world, invisible to us now, but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are, indeed, objects of approbation and of admiration, but cannot be the springs of purpose and action. For they do not satisfy all the aims which are natural to every rational being, and which are determined à priori by pure reason itself, and necessary.\n\nHappiness alone is, in the view of reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve of it (however much inclination may desire it), except as united with desert. On the other hand, morality alone, and with it, mere desert, is likewise far from being the complete good. To make it complete, he who conducts himself in a manner not unworthy of happiness, must be able to hope for the possession of happiness. Even reason, unbiased by private ends, or interested considerations, cannot judge otherwise, if it puts itself in the place of a being whose business it is to dispense all happiness to others. For in the practical idea both points are essentially combined, though in such a way that participation in happiness is rendered possible by the moral disposition, as its condition, and not conversely, the moral disposition by the prospect of happiness. For a disposition which should require the prospect of happiness as its necessary condition would not be moral, and hence also would not be worthy of complete happiness—a happiness which, in the view of reason, recognizes no limitation but such as arises from our own immoral conduct.\n\nHappiness, therefore, in exact proportion with the morality of rational beings (whereby they are made worthy of happiness), constitutes alone the supreme good of a world into which we absolutely must transport ourselves according to the commands of pure but practical reason. This world is, it is true, only an intelligible world; for of such a systematic unity of ends as it requires, the world of sense gives us no hint. Its reality can be based on nothing else but the hypothesis of a supreme original good. In it independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, founds, maintains, and fulfils the universal order of things, with the most perfect teleological harmony, however much this order may be hidden from us in the world of sense.\n\nThis moral theology has the peculiar advantage, in contrast with speculative theology, of leading inevitably to the conception of a sole, perfect, and rational First Cause, whereof speculative theology does not give us any indication on objective grounds, far less any convincing evidence. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us in these, any ground to warrant us in assuming the existence of one only Being, which stands at the head of all natural causes, and on which these are entirely dependent. On the other hand, if we take our stand on moral unity as a necessary law of the universe, and from this point of view consider what is necessary to give this law adequate efficiency and, for us, obligatory force, we must come to the conclusion that there is one only supreme will, which comprehends all these laws in itself. For how, under different wills, should we find complete unity of ends? This will must be omnipotent, that all nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; omniscient, that it may have knowledge of the most secret feelings and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be at hand to supply every necessity to which the highest weal of the world may give rise; eternal, that this harmony of nature and liberty may never fail; and so on.\n\nBut this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences—which, as mere nature, is only a world of sense, but, as a system of freedom of volition, may be termed an intelligible, that is, moral world (regnum gratiae)—leads inevitably also to the teleological unity of all things which constitute this great whole, according to universal natural laws—just as the unity of the former is according to universal and necessary moral laws—and unites the practical with the speculative reason. The world must be represented as having originated from an idea, if it is to harmonize with that use of reason without which we cannot even consider ourselves as worthy of reason—namely, the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the supreme good. Hence the investigation of nature receives a teleological direction, and becomes, in its widest extension, physico-theology. But this, taking its rise in moral order as a unity founded on the essence of freedom, and not accidentally instituted by external commands, establishes the teleological view of nature on grounds which must be inseparably connected with the internal possibility of things. This gives rise to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity; and this principle connects all things according to universal and necessary natural laws, because all things have their origin in the absolute necessity of the one only Primal Being.\n\nWhat use can we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we do not propose ends to ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and it is only pure reason that can give us the knowledge of these. Though supplied with these, and putting ourselves under their guidance, we can make no teleological use of the knowledge of nature, as regards cognition, unless nature itself has established teleological unity. For without this unity we should not even possess reason, because we should have no school for reason, and no cultivation through objects which afford the materials for its conceptions. But teleological unity is a necessary unity, and founded on the essence of the individual will itself. Hence this will, which is the condition of the application of this unity in concreto, must be so likewise. In this way the transcendental enlargement of our rational cognition would be, not the cause, but merely the effect of the practical teleology which pure reason imposes upon us.\n\nHence, also, we find in the history of human reason that, before the moral conceptions were sufficiently purified and determined, and before men had attained to a perception of the systematic unity of ends according to these conceptions and from necessary principles, the knowledge of nature, and even a considerable amount of intellectual culture in many other sciences, could produce only rude and vague conceptions of the Deity, sometimes even admitting of an astonishing indifference with regard to this question altogether. But the more enlarged treatment of moral ideas, which was rendered necessary by the extreme pure moral law of our religion, awakened the interest, and thereby quickened the perceptions of reason in relation to this object. In this way, and without the help either of an extended acquaintance with nature, or of a reliable transcendental insight (for these have been wanting in all ages), a conception of the Divine Being was arrived at, which we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it accords with the moral principles of reason. Thus it is to pure reason, but only in its practical use, that we must ascribe the merit of having connected with our highest interest a cognition, of which mere speculation was able only to form a conjecture, but the validity of which it was unable to establish—and of having thereby rendered it, not indeed a demonstrated dogma, but a hypothesis absolutely necessary to the essential ends of reason.\n\nBut if practical reason has reached this elevation, and has attained to the conception of a sole Primal Being as the supreme good, it must not, therefore, imagine that it has transcended the empirical conditions of its application, and risen to the immediate cognition of new objects; it must not presume to start from the conception which it has gained, and to deduce from it the moral laws themselves. For it was these very laws, the internal practical necessity of which led us to the hypothesis of an independent cause, or of a wise ruler of the universe, who should give them effect. Hence we are not entitled to regard them as accidental and derived from the mere will of the ruler, especially as we have no conception of such a will, except as formed in accordance with these laws. So far, then, as practical reason has the right to conduct us, we shall not look upon actions as binding on us, because they are the commands of God, but we shall regard them as divine commands, because we are internally bound by them. We shall study freedom under the teleological unity which accords with principles of reason; we shall look upon ourselves as acting in conformity with the divine will only in so far as we hold sacred the moral law which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and we shall believe that we can obey that will only by promoting the weal of the universe in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is, therefore, only of immanent use. It teaches us to fulfil our destiny here in the world, by placing ourselves in harmony with the general system of ends, and warns us against the fanaticism, nay, the crime of depriving reason of its legislative authority in the moral conduct of life, for the purpose of directly connecting this authority with the idea of the Supreme Being. For this would be, not an immanent, but a transcendent use of moral theology, and, like the transcendent use of mere speculation, would inevitably pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason\n\nReason conducted us, in its speculative use, through the field of experience and, as it can never find complete satisfaction in that sphere, from thence to speculative ideas—which, however, in the end brought us back again to experience, and thus fulfilled the purpose of reason, in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accordance with our expectations. It now remains for us to consider whether pure reason can be employed in a practical sphere, and whether it will here conduct us to those ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, as we have just stated them. We shall thus ascertain whether, from the point of view of its practical interest, reason may not be able to supply us with that which, on the speculative side, it wholly denies us.\n\nThe whole interest of reason, speculative as well as practical, is centred in the three following questions:\n\n1. WHAT CAN I KNOW?  \n2. WHAT OUGHT I TO DO?  \n3. WHAT MAY I HOPE?  \n\nThe first question is purely speculative. We have, as I flatter myself, exhausted all the replies of which it is susceptible, and have at last found the reply with which reason must content itself, and with which it ought to be content, so long as it pays no regard to the practical. But from the two great ends to the attainment of which all these efforts of pure reason were in fact directed, we remain just as far removed as if we had consulted our ease and declined the task at the outset. So far, then, as knowledge is concerned, thus much, at least, is established, that, in regard to those two problems, it lies beyond our reach.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0002","text":"The second question is purely practical. As such it may indeed fall within the province of pure reason, but still it is not transcendental, but moral, and consequently cannot in itself form the subject of our criticism.\n\nThe third question: If I act as I ought to do, what may I then hope?—is at once practical and theoretical. The practical forms a clue to the answer of the theoretical, and—in its highest form—speculative question. For all hoping has happiness for its object and stands in precisely the same relation to the practical and the law of morality as knowing to the theoretical cognition of things and the law of nature. The former arrives finally at the conclusion that something is (which determines the ultimate end), because something ought to take place; the latter, that something is (which operates as the highest cause), because something does take place.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","text":"Happiness is the satisfaction of all our desires; extensive, in regard to their multiplicity; intensive, in regard to their degree; and protensive, in regard to their duration. The practical law based on the motive of happiness I term a pragmatical law (or prudential rule); but that law, assuming such to exist, which has no other motive than the worthiness of being happy, I term a moral or ethical law. The first tells us what we have to do, if we wish to become possessed of happiness; the second dictates how we ought to act, in order to deserve happiness. The first is based upon empirical principles; for it is only by experience that I can learn either what inclinations exist which desire satisfaction, or what are the natural means of satisfying them. The second takes no account of our desires or the means of satisfying them, and regards only the freedom of a rational being, and the necessary conditions under which alone this freedom can harmonize with the distribution of happiness according to principles. This second law may therefore rest upon mere ideas of pure reason, and may be cognized à priori.\n\nI assume that there are pure moral laws which determine, entirely à priori (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness), the conduct of a rational being, or in other words, to use which it makes of its freedom, and that these laws are absolutely imperative (not merely hypothetically, on the supposition of other empirical ends), and therefore in all respects necessary. I am warranted in assuming this, not only by the arguments of the most enlightened moralists, but by the moral judgement of every man who will make the attempt to form a distinct conception of such a law.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0004","text":"Pure reason, then, contains, not indeed in its speculative, but in its practical, or, more strictly, its moral use, principles of the possibility of experience, of such actions, namely, as, in accordance with ethical precepts, might be met with in the history of man. For since reason commands that such actions should take place, it must be possible for them to take place; and hence a particular kind of systematic unity—the moral—must be possible. We have found, it is true, that the systematic unity of nature could not be established according to speculative principles of reason, because, while reason possesses a causal power in relation to freedom, it has none in relation to the whole sphere of nature; and, while moral principles of reason can produce free actions, they cannot produce natural laws. It is, then, in its practical, but especially in its moral use, that the principles of pure reason possess objective reality.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","text":"I call the world a moral world, in so far as it may be in accordance with all the ethical laws—which, by virtue of the freedom of reasonable beings, it can be, and according to the necessary laws of morality it ought to be. But this world must be conceived only as an intelligible world, inasmuch as abstraction is therein made of all conditions (ends), and even of all impediments to morality (the weakness or pravity of human nature). So far, then, it is a mere idea—though still a practical idea—which may have, and ought to have, an influence on the world of sense, so as to bring it as far as possible into conformity with itself. The idea of a moral world has, therefore, objective reality, not as referring to an object of intelligible intuition—for of such an object we can form no conception whatever—but to the world of sense—conceived, however, as an object of pure reason in its practical use—and to a corpus mysticum of rational beings in it, in so far as the liberum arbitrium of the individual is placed, under and by virtue of moral laws, in complete systematic unity both with itself and with the freedom of all others.\n\nThat is the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which relate to its practical interest: Do that which will render thee worthy of happiness. The second question is this: If I conduct myself so as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I hope thereby to obtain happiness? In order to arrive at the solution of this question, we must inquire whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe à priori the law, necessarily also connect this hope with it.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","text":"I say, then, that just as the moral principles are necessary according to reason in its practical use, so it is equally necessary according to reason in its theoretical use to assume that every one has ground to hope for happiness in the measure in which he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that therefore the system of morality is inseparably (though only in the idea of pure reason) connected with that of happiness.\n\nNow in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the conception of which we make abstraction of all the impediments to morality (sensuous desires), such a system of happiness, connected with and proportioned to morality, may be conceived as necessary, because freedom of volition—partly incited, and partly restrained by moral laws—would be itself the cause of general happiness; and thus rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would be themselves the authors both of their own enduring welfare and that of others. But such a system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, the carrying out of which depends upon the condition that every one acts as he ought; in other words, that all actions of reasonable beings be such as they would be if they sprung from a Supreme Will, comprehending in, or under, itself all particular wills. But since the moral law is binding on each individual in the use of his freedom of volition, even if others should not act in conformity with this law, neither the nature of things, nor the causality of actions and their relation to morality, determine how the consequences of these actions will be related to happiness; and the necessary connection of the hope of happiness with the unceasing endeavour to become worthy of happiness, cannot be cognized by reason, if we take nature alone for our guide. This connection can be hoped for only on the assumption that the cause of nature is a supreme reason, which governs according to moral laws.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","text":"I term the idea of an intelligence in which the morally most perfect will, united with supreme blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, so far as happiness stands in strict relation to morality (as the worthiness of being happy), the ideal of the supreme Good. It is only, then, in the ideal of the supreme original good, that pure reason can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derivative good, and accordingly of an intelligible, that is, moral world. Now since we are necessitated by reason to conceive ourselves as belonging to such a world, while the senses present to us nothing but a world of phenomena, we must assume the former as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense (since the world of sense gives us no hint of it), and therefore as future in relation to us. Thus God and a future life are two hypotheses which, according to the principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation which this reason imposes upon us.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0008","text":"Morality per se constitutes a system. But we can form no system of happiness, except in so far as it is dispensed in strict proportion to morality. But this is only possible in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Such a ruler, together with life in such a world, which we must look upon as future, reason finds itself compelled to assume; or it must regard the moral laws as idle dreams, since the necessary consequence which this same reason connects with them must, without this hypothesis, fall to the ground. Hence also the moral laws are universally regarded as commands, which they could not be did they not connect à priori adequate consequences with their dictates, and thus carry with them promises and threats. But this, again, they could not do, did they not reside in a necessary being, as the Supreme Good, which alone can render such a teleological unity possible.\n\nLeibnitz termed the world, when viewed in relation to the rational beings which it contains, and the moral relations in which they stand to each other, under the government of the Supreme Good, the kingdom of Grace, and distinguished it from the kingdom of Nature, in which these rational beings live, under moral laws, indeed, but expect no other consequences from their actions than such as follow according to the course of nature in the world of sense. To view ourselves, therefore, as in the kingdom of grace, in which all happiness awaits us, except in so far as we ourselves limit our participation in it by actions which render us unworthy of happiness, is a practically necessary idea of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0009","text":"Practical laws, in so far as they are subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are termed maxims. The judgements of moral according to in its purity and ultimate results are framed according ideas; the observance of its laws, according to maxims.\n\nThe whole course of our life must be subject to moral maxims; but this is impossible, unless with the moral law, which is a mere idea, reason connects an efficient cause which ordains to all conduct which is in conformity with the moral law an issue either in this or in another life, which is in exact conformity with our highest aims. Thus, without a God and without a world, invisible to us now, but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are, indeed, objects of approbation and of admiration, but cannot be the springs of purpose and action. For they do not satisfy all the aims which are natural to every rational being, and which are determined à priori by pure reason itself, and necessary.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","text":"Happiness alone is, in the view of reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve of it (however much inclination may desire it), except as united with desert. On the other hand, morality alone, and with it, mere desert, is likewise far from being the complete good. To make it complete, he who conducts himself in a manner not unworthy of happiness, must be able to hope for the possession of happiness. Even reason, unbiased by private ends, or interested considerations, cannot judge otherwise, if it puts itself in the place of a being whose business it is to dispense all happiness to others. For in the practical idea both points are essentially combined, though in such a way that participation in happiness is rendered possible by the moral disposition, as its condition, and not conversely, the moral disposition by the prospect of happiness. For a disposition which should require the prospect of happiness as its necessary condition would not be moral, and hence also would not be worthy of complete happiness—a happiness which, in the view of reason, recognizes no limitation but such as arises from our own immoral conduct.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","text":"Happiness, therefore, in exact proportion with the morality of rational beings (whereby they are made worthy of happiness), constitutes alone the supreme good of a world into which we absolutely must transport ourselves according to the commands of pure but practical reason. This world is, it is true, only an intelligible world; for of such a systematic unity of ends as it requires, the world of sense gives us no hint. Its reality can be based on nothing else but the hypothesis of a supreme original good. In it independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, founds, maintains, and fulfils the universal order of things, with the most perfect teleological harmony, however much this order may be hidden from us in the world of sense.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","text":"This moral theology has the peculiar advantage, in contrast with speculative theology, of leading inevitably to the conception of a sole, perfect, and rational First Cause, whereof speculative theology does not give us any indication on objective grounds, far less any convincing evidence. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us in these, any ground to warrant us in assuming the existence of one only Being, which stands at the head of all natural causes, and on which these are entirely dependent. On the other hand, if we take our stand on moral unity as a necessary law of the universe, and from this point of view consider what is necessary to give this law adequate efficiency and, for us, obligatory force, we must come to the conclusion that there is one only supreme will, which comprehends all these laws in itself. For how, under different wills, should we find complete unity of ends? This will must be omnipotent, that all nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; omniscient, that it may have knowledge of the most secret feelings and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be at hand to supply every necessity to which the highest weal of the world may give rise; eternal, that this harmony of nature and liberty may never fail; and so on.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","text":"But this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences—which, as mere nature, is only a world of sense, but, as a system of freedom of volition, may be termed an intelligible, that is, moral world (regnum gratiae)—leads inevitably also to the teleological unity of all things which constitute this great whole, according to universal natural laws—just as the unity of the former is according to universal and necessary moral laws—and unites the practical with the speculative reason. The world must be represented as having originated from an idea, if it is to harmonize with that use of reason without which we cannot even consider ourselves as worthy of reason—namely, the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the supreme good. Hence the investigation of nature receives a teleological direction, and becomes, in its widest extension, physico-theology. But this, taking its rise in moral order as a unity founded on the essence of freedom, and not accidentally instituted by external commands, establishes the teleological view of nature on grounds which must be inseparably connected with the internal possibility of things. This gives rise to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity; and this principle connects all things according to universal and necessary natural laws, because all things have their origin in the absolute necessity of the one only Primal Being.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0014","text":"What use can we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we do not propose ends to ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and it is only pure reason that can give us the knowledge of these. Though supplied with these, and putting ourselves under their guidance, we can make no teleological use of the knowledge of nature, as regards cognition, unless nature itself has established teleological unity. For without this unity we should not even possess reason, because we should have no school for reason, and no cultivation through objects which afford the materials for its conceptions. But teleological unity is a necessary unity, and founded on the essence of the individual will itself. Hence this will, which is the condition of the application of this unity in concreto, must be so likewise. In this way the transcendental enlargement of our rational cognition would be, not the cause, but merely the effect of the practical teleology which pure reason imposes upon us.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","text":"Hence, also, we find in the history of human reason that, before the moral conceptions were sufficiently purified and determined, and before men had attained to a perception of the systematic unity of ends according to these conceptions and from necessary principles, the knowledge of nature, and even a considerable amount of intellectual culture in many other sciences, could produce only rude and vague conceptions of the Deity, sometimes even admitting of an astonishing indifference with regard to this question altogether. But the more enlarged treatment of moral ideas, which was rendered necessary by the extreme pure moral law of our religion, awakened the interest, and thereby quickened the perceptions of reason in relation to this object. In this way, and without the help either of an extended acquaintance with nature, or of a reliable transcendental insight (for these have been wanting in all ages), a conception of the Divine Being was arrived at, which we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it accords with the moral principles of reason. Thus it is to pure reason, but only in its practical use, that we must ascribe the merit of having connected with our highest interest a cognition, of which mere speculation was able only to form a conjecture, but the validity of which it was unable to establish—and of having thereby rendered it, not indeed a demonstrated dogma, but a hypothesis absolutely necessary to the essential ends of reason.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","text":"But if practical reason has reached this elevation, and has attained to the conception of a sole Primal Being as the supreme good, it must not, therefore, imagine that it has transcended the empirical conditions of its application, and risen to the immediate cognition of new objects; it must not presume to start from the conception which it has gained, and to deduce from it the moral laws themselves. For it was these very laws, the internal practical necessity of which led us to the hypothesis of an independent cause, or of a wise ruler of the universe, who should give them effect. Hence we are not entitled to regard them as accidental and derived from the mere will of the ruler, especially as we have no conception of such a will, except as formed in accordance with these laws. So far, then, as practical reason has the right to conduct us, we shall not look upon actions as binding on us, because they are the commands of God, but we shall regard them as divine commands, because we are internally bound by them. We shall study freedom under the teleological unity which accords with principles of reason; we shall look upon ourselves as acting in conformity with the divine will only in so far as we hold sacred the moral law which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and we shall believe that we can obey that will only by promoting the weal of the universe in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is, therefore, only of immanent use. It teaches us to fulfil our destiny here in the world, by placing ourselves in harmony with the general system of ends, and warns us against the fanaticism, nay, the crime of depriving reason of its legislative authority in the moral conduct of life, for the purpose of directly connecting this authority with the idea of the Supreme Being. For this would be, not an immanent, but a transcendent use of moral theology, and, like the transcendent use of mere speculation, would inevitably pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ea5a4a54f205a90e41dc74fc7148a4a53e9da5ef0f92aecd71c36352370db6b7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","order":92,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_SIOK","text":"### Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief\n\nThe holding of a thing to be true is a phenomenon in our understanding which may rest on objective grounds, but requires, also, subjective causes in the mind of the person judging. If a judgement is valid for every rational being, then its ground is objectively sufficient, and it is termed a conviction. If, on the other hand, it has its ground in the particular character of the subject, it is termed a persuasion.\n\nPersuasion is a mere illusion, the ground of the judgement, which lies solely in the subject, being regarded as objective. Hence a judgement of this kind has only private validity—is only valid for the individual who judges, and the holding of a thing to be true in this way cannot be communicated. But truth depends upon agreement with the object, and consequently the judgements of all understandings, if true, must be in agreement with each other (consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se). Conviction may, therefore, be distinguished, from an external point of view, from persuasion, by the possibility of communicating it and by showing its validity for the reason of every man; for in this case the presumption, at least, arises that the agreement of all judgements with each other, in spite of the different characters of individuals, rests upon the common ground of the agreement of each with the object, and thus the correctness of the judgement is established.\n\nPersuasion, accordingly, cannot be subjectively distinguished from conviction, that is, so long as the subject views its judgement simply as a phenomenon of its own mind. But if we inquire whether the grounds of our judgement, which are valid for us, produce the same effect on the reason of others as on our own, we have then the means, though only subjective means, not, indeed, of producing conviction, but of detecting the merely private validity of the judgement; in other words, of discovering that there is in it the element of mere persuasion.\n\nIf we can, in addition to this, develop the subjective causes of the judgement, which we have taken for its objective grounds, and thus explain the deceptive judgement as a phenomenon in our mind, apart altogether from the objective character of the object, we can then expose the illusion and need be no longer deceived by it, although, if its subjective cause lies in our nature, we cannot hope altogether to escape its influence.\n\nI can only maintain, that is, affirm as necessarily valid for every one, that which produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if it is agreeable to me; but I cannot, and ought not, to attempt to impose it as binding upon others.\n\nHolding for true, or the subjective validity of a judgement in relation to conviction (which is, at the same time, objectively valid), has the three following degrees: opinion, belief, and knowledge. Opinion is a consciously insufficient judgement, subjectively as well as objectively. Belief is subjectively sufficient, but is recognized as being objectively insufficient. Knowledge is both subjectively and objectively sufficient. Subjective sufficiency is termed conviction (for myself); objective sufficiency is termed certainty (for all). I need not dwell longer on the explanation of such simple conceptions.\n\nI must never venture to be of opinion, without knowing something, at least, by which my judgement, in itself merely problematical, is brought into connection with the truth—which connection, although not perfect, is still something more than an arbitrary fiction. Moreover, the law of such a connection must be certain. For if, in relation to this law, I have nothing more than opinion, my judgement is but a play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In the judgements of pure reason, opinion has no place. For, as they do not rest on empirical grounds and as the sphere of pure reason is that of necessary truth and à priori cognition, the principle of connection in it requires universality and necessity, and consequently perfect certainty—otherwise we should have no guide to the truth at all. Hence it is absurd to have an opinion in pure mathematics; we must know, or abstain from forming a judgement altogether. The case is the same with the maxims of morality. For we must not hazard an action on the mere opinion that it is allowed, but we must know it to be so. In the transcendental sphere of reason, on the other hand, the term opinion is too weak, while the word knowledge is too strong. From the merely speculative point of view, therefore, we cannot form a judgement at all. For the subjective grounds of a judgement, such as produce belief, cannot be admitted in speculative inquiries, inasmuch as they cannot stand without empirical support and are incapable of being communicated to others in equal measure.\n\nBut it is only from the practical point of view that a theoretically insufficient judgement can be termed belief. Now the practical reference is either to skill or to morality; to the former, when the end proposed is arbitrary and accidental, to the latter, when it is absolutely necessary.\n\nIf we propose to ourselves any end whatever, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. The necessity is subjectively, but still only comparatively, sufficient, if I am acquainted with no other conditions under which the end can be attained. On the other hand, it is sufficient, absolutely and for every one, if I know for certain that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions under which the attainment of the proposed end would be possible. In the former case my supposition—my judgement with regard to certain conditions—is a merely accidental belief; in the latter it is a necessary belief. The physician must pursue some course in the case of a patient who is in danger, but is ignorant of the nature of the disease. He observes the symptoms, and concludes, according to the best of his judgement, that it is a case of phthisis. His belief is, even in his own judgement, only contingent: another man might, perhaps come nearer the truth. Such a belief, contingent indeed, but still forming the ground of the actual use of means for the attainment of certain ends, I term Pragmatical belief.\n\nThe usual test, whether that which any one maintains is merely his persuasion, or his subjective conviction at least, that is, his firm belief, is a bet. It frequently happens that a man delivers his opinions with so much boldness and assurance, that he appears to be under no apprehension as to the possibility of his being in error. The offer of a bet startles him, and makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out that his persuasion may be valued at a ducat, but not at ten. For he does not hesitate, perhaps, to venture a ducat, but if it is proposed to stake ten, he immediately becomes aware of the possibility of his being mistaken—a possibility which has hitherto escaped his observation. If we imagine to ourselves that we have to stake the happiness of our whole life on the truth of any proposition, our judgement drops its air of triumph, we take the alarm, and discover the actual strength of our belief. Thus pragmatical belief has degrees, varying in proportion to the interests at stake.\n\nNow, in cases where we cannot enter upon any course of action in reference to some object, and where, accordingly, our judgement is purely theoretical, we can still represent to ourselves, in thought, the possibility of a course of action, for which we suppose that we have sufficient grounds, if any means existed of ascertaining the truth of the matter. Thus we find in purely theoretical judgements an analogon of practical judgements, to which the word belief may properly be applied, and which we may term doctrinal belief. I should not hesitate to stake my all on the truth of the proposition—if there were any possibility of bringing it to the test of experience—that, at least, some one of the planets, which we see, is inhabited. Hence I say that I have not merely the opinion, but the strong belief, on the correctness of which I would stake even many of the advantages of life, that there are inhabitants in other worlds.\n\nNow we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For, although in respect to the theoretical cognition of the universe I do not require to form any theory which necessarily involves this idea, as the condition of my explanation of the phenomena which the universe presents, but, on the contrary, am rather bound so to use my reason as if everything were mere nature, still teleological unity is so important a condition of the application of my reason to nature, that it is impossible for me to ignore it—especially since, in addition to these considerations, abundant examples of it are supplied by experience. But the sole condition, so far as my knowledge extends, under which this unity can be my guide in the investigation of nature, is the assumption that a supreme intelligence has ordered all things according to the wisest ends. Consequently, the hypothesis of a wise author of the universe is necessary for my guidance in the investigation of nature—is the condition under which alone I can fulfil an end which is contingent indeed, but by no means unimportant. Moreover, since the result of my attempts so frequently confirms the utility of this assumption, and since nothing decisive can be adduced against it, it follows that it would be saying far too little to term my judgement, in this case, a mere opinion, and that, even in this theoretical connection, I may assert that I firmly believe in God. Still, if we use words strictly, this must not be called a practical, but a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must also produce in my mind. In the wisdom of a Supreme Being, and in the shortness of life, so inadequate to the development of the glorious powers of human nature, we may find equally sufficient grounds for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.\n\nThe expression of belief is, in such cases, an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but, at the same time, of firm confidence, from the subjective. If I should venture to term this merely theoretical judgement even so much as a hypothesis which I am entitled to assume; a more complete conception, with regard to another world and to the cause of the world, might then be justly required of me than I am, in reality, able to give. For, if I assume anything, even as a mere hypothesis, I must, at least, know so much of the properties of such a being as will enable me, not to form the conception, but to imagine the existence of it. But the word belief refers only to the guidance which an idea gives me, and to its subjective influence on the conduct of my reason, which forces me to hold it fast, though I may not be in a position to give a speculative account of it.\n\nBut mere doctrinal belief is, to some extent, wanting in stability. We often quit our hold of it, in consequence of the difficulties which occur in speculation, though in the end we inevitably return to it again.\n\nIt is quite otherwise with moral belief. For in this sphere action is absolutely necessary, that is, I must act in obedience to the moral law in all points. The end is here incontrovertibly established, and there is only one condition possible, according to the best of my perception, under which this end can harmonize with all other ends, and so have practical validity—namely, the existence of a God and of a future world. I know also, to a certainty, that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions which conduct to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is, at the same time, my maxim (as reason requires that it should be), I am irresistibly constrained to believe in the existence of God and in a future life; and I am sure that nothing can make me waver in this belief, since I should thereby overthrow my moral maxims, the renunciation of which would render me hateful in my own eyes.\n\nThus, while all the ambitious attempts of reason to penetrate beyond the limits of experience end in disappointment, there is still enough left to satisfy us in a practical point of view. No one, it is true, will be able to boast that he knows that there is a God and a future life; for, if he knows this, he is just the man whom I have long wished to find. All knowledge, regarding an object of mere reason, can be communicated; and I should thus be enabled to hope that my own knowledge would receive this wonderful extension, through the instrumentality of his instruction. No, my conviction is not logical, but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral sentiment), I must not even say: It is morally certain that there is a God, etc., but: I am morally certain, that is, my belief in God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral nature that I am under as little apprehension of having the former torn from me as of losing the latter.\n\nThe only point in this argument that may appear open to suspicion is that this rational belief presupposes the existence of moral sentiments. If we give up this assumption, and take a man who is entirely indifferent with regard to moral laws, the question which reason proposes, becomes then merely a problem for speculation and may, indeed, be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by such as will compel the most obstinate scepticism to give way.[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-79) But in these questions no man is free from all interest. For though the want of good sentiments may place him beyond the influence of moral interests, still even in this case enough may be left to make him fear the existence of God and a future life. For he cannot pretend to any certainty of the non-existence of God and of a future life, unless—since it could only be proved by mere reason, and therefore apodeictically—he is prepared to establish the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable man would undertake to do. This would be a negative belief, which could not, indeed, produce morality and good sentiments, but still could produce an analogon of these, by operating as a powerful restraint on the outbreak of evil dispositions.\n\n[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-79) The human mind (as, I believe, every rational being must of necessity do) takes a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided, and may not be practically in preponderance. If you strengthen and increase it, you will find the reason become docile, more enlightened, and more capable of uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care at the outset, or at least midway, to make men good, you will never force them into an honest belief.\n\nBut, it will be said, is this all that pure reason can effect, in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? Nothing more than two articles of belief? Common sense could have done as much as this, without taking the philosophers to counsel in the matter!\n\nI shall not here eulogize philosophy for the benefits which the laborious efforts of its criticism have conferred on human reason—even granting that its merit should turn out in the end to be only negative—for on this point something more will be said in the next section. But, I ask, do you require that that knowledge which concerns all men, should transcend the common understanding, and should only be revealed to you by philosophers? The very circumstance which has called forth your censure, is the best confirmation of the correctness of our previous assertions, since it discloses, what could not have been foreseen, that Nature is not chargeable with any partial distribution of her gifts in those matters which concern all men without distinction and that, in respect to the essential ends of human nature, we cannot advance further with the help of the highest philosophy, than under the guidance which nature has vouchsafed to the meanest understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief\n\nThe holding of a thing to be true is a phenomenon in our understanding which may rest on objective grounds, but requires, also, subjective causes in the mind of the person judging. If a judgement is valid for every rational being, then its ground is objectively sufficient, and it is termed a conviction. If, on the other hand, it has its ground in the particular character of the subject, it is termed a persuasion.\n\nPersuasion is a mere illusion, the ground of the judgement, which lies solely in the subject, being regarded as objective. Hence a judgement of this kind has only private validity—is only valid for the individual who judges, and the holding of a thing to be true in this way cannot be communicated. But truth depends upon agreement with the object, and consequently the judgements of all understandings, if true, must be in agreement with each other (consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se). Conviction may, therefore, be distinguished, from an external point of view, from persuasion, by the possibility of communicating it and by showing its validity for the reason of every man; for in this case the presumption, at least, arises that the agreement of all judgements with each other, in spite of the different characters of individuals, rests upon the common ground of the agreement of each with the object, and thus the correctness of the judgement is established.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0002","text":"Persuasion, accordingly, cannot be subjectively distinguished from conviction, that is, so long as the subject views its judgement simply as a phenomenon of its own mind. But if we inquire whether the grounds of our judgement, which are valid for us, produce the same effect on the reason of others as on our own, we have then the means, though only subjective means, not, indeed, of producing conviction, but of detecting the merely private validity of the judgement; in other words, of discovering that there is in it the element of mere persuasion.\n\nIf we can, in addition to this, develop the subjective causes of the judgement, which we have taken for its objective grounds, and thus explain the deceptive judgement as a phenomenon in our mind, apart altogether from the objective character of the object, we can then expose the illusion and need be no longer deceived by it, although, if its subjective cause lies in our nature, we cannot hope altogether to escape its influence.\n\nI can only maintain, that is, affirm as necessarily valid for every one, that which produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if it is agreeable to me; but I cannot, and ought not, to attempt to impose it as binding upon others.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","text":"Holding for true, or the subjective validity of a judgement in relation to conviction (which is, at the same time, objectively valid), has the three following degrees: opinion, belief, and knowledge. Opinion is a consciously insufficient judgement, subjectively as well as objectively. Belief is subjectively sufficient, but is recognized as being objectively insufficient. Knowledge is both subjectively and objectively sufficient. Subjective sufficiency is termed conviction (for myself); objective sufficiency is termed certainty (for all). I need not dwell longer on the explanation of such simple conceptions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","text":"I must never venture to be of opinion, without knowing something, at least, by which my judgement, in itself merely problematical, is brought into connection with the truth—which connection, although not perfect, is still something more than an arbitrary fiction. Moreover, the law of such a connection must be certain. For if, in relation to this law, I have nothing more than opinion, my judgement is but a play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In the judgements of pure reason, opinion has no place. For, as they do not rest on empirical grounds and as the sphere of pure reason is that of necessary truth and à priori cognition, the principle of connection in it requires universality and necessity, and consequently perfect certainty—otherwise we should have no guide to the truth at all. Hence it is absurd to have an opinion in pure mathematics; we must know, or abstain from forming a judgement altogether. The case is the same with the maxims of morality. For we must not hazard an action on the mere opinion that it is allowed, but we must know it to be so. In the transcendental sphere of reason, on the other hand, the term opinion is too weak, while the word knowledge is too strong. From the merely speculative point of view, therefore, we cannot form a judgement at all. For the subjective grounds of a judgement, such as produce belief, cannot be admitted in speculative inquiries, inasmuch as they cannot stand without empirical support and are incapable of being communicated to others in equal measure.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","text":"But it is only from the practical point of view that a theoretically insufficient judgement can be termed belief. Now the practical reference is either to skill or to morality; to the former, when the end proposed is arbitrary and accidental, to the latter, when it is absolutely necessary.\n\nIf we propose to ourselves any end whatever, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. The necessity is subjectively, but still only comparatively, sufficient, if I am acquainted with no other conditions under which the end can be attained. On the other hand, it is sufficient, absolutely and for every one, if I know for certain that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions under which the attainment of the proposed end would be possible. In the former case my supposition—my judgement with regard to certain conditions—is a merely accidental belief; in the latter it is a necessary belief. The physician must pursue some course in the case of a patient who is in danger, but is ignorant of the nature of the disease. He observes the symptoms, and concludes, according to the best of his judgement, that it is a case of phthisis. His belief is, even in his own judgement, only contingent: another man might, perhaps come nearer the truth. Such a belief, contingent indeed, but still forming the ground of the actual use of means for the attainment of certain ends, I term Pragmatical belief.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0006","text":"The usual test, whether that which any one maintains is merely his persuasion, or his subjective conviction at least, that is, his firm belief, is a bet. It frequently happens that a man delivers his opinions with so much boldness and assurance, that he appears to be under no apprehension as to the possibility of his being in error. The offer of a bet startles him, and makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out that his persuasion may be valued at a ducat, but not at ten. For he does not hesitate, perhaps, to venture a ducat, but if it is proposed to stake ten, he immediately becomes aware of the possibility of his being mistaken—a possibility which has hitherto escaped his observation. If we imagine to ourselves that we have to stake the happiness of our whole life on the truth of any proposition, our judgement drops its air of triumph, we take the alarm, and discover the actual strength of our belief. Thus pragmatical belief has degrees, varying in proportion to the interests at stake.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","text":"Now, in cases where we cannot enter upon any course of action in reference to some object, and where, accordingly, our judgement is purely theoretical, we can still represent to ourselves, in thought, the possibility of a course of action, for which we suppose that we have sufficient grounds, if any means existed of ascertaining the truth of the matter. Thus we find in purely theoretical judgements an analogon of practical judgements, to which the word belief may properly be applied, and which we may term doctrinal belief. I should not hesitate to stake my all on the truth of the proposition—if there were any possibility of bringing it to the test of experience—that, at least, some one of the planets, which we see, is inhabited. Hence I say that I have not merely the opinion, but the strong belief, on the correctness of which I would stake even many of the advantages of life, that there are inhabitants in other worlds.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0008","text":"Now we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For, although in respect to the theoretical cognition of the universe I do not require to form any theory which necessarily involves this idea, as the condition of my explanation of the phenomena which the universe presents, but, on the contrary, am rather bound so to use my reason as if everything were mere nature, still teleological unity is so important a condition of the application of my reason to nature, that it is impossible for me to ignore it—especially since, in addition to these considerations, abundant examples of it are supplied by experience. But the sole condition, so far as my knowledge extends, under which this unity can be my guide in the investigation of nature, is the assumption that a supreme intelligence has ordered all things according to the wisest ends. Consequently, the hypothesis of a wise author of the universe is necessary for my guidance in the investigation of nature—is the condition under which alone I can fulfil an end which is contingent indeed, but by no means unimportant. Moreover, since the result of my attempts so frequently confirms the utility of this assumption, and since nothing decisive can be adduced against it, it follows that it would be saying far too little to term my judgement, in this case, a mere opinion, and that, even in this theoretical connection, I may assert that I firmly believe in God. Still, if we use words strictly, this must not be called a practical, but a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must also produce in my mind. In the wisdom of a Supreme Being, and in the shortness of life, so inadequate to the development of the glorious powers of human nature, we may find equally sufficient grounds for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","text":"The expression of belief is, in such cases, an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but, at the same time, of firm confidence, from the subjective. If I should venture to term this merely theoretical judgement even so much as a hypothesis which I am entitled to assume; a more complete conception, with regard to another world and to the cause of the world, might then be justly required of me than I am, in reality, able to give. For, if I assume anything, even as a mere hypothesis, I must, at least, know so much of the properties of such a being as will enable me, not to form the conception, but to imagine the existence of it. But the word belief refers only to the guidance which an idea gives me, and to its subjective influence on the conduct of my reason, which forces me to hold it fast, though I may not be in a position to give a speculative account of it.\n\nBut mere doctrinal belief is, to some extent, wanting in stability. We often quit our hold of it, in consequence of the difficulties which occur in speculation, though in the end we inevitably return to it again.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","text":"It is quite otherwise with moral belief. For in this sphere action is absolutely necessary, that is, I must act in obedience to the moral law in all points. The end is here incontrovertibly established, and there is only one condition possible, according to the best of my perception, under which this end can harmonize with all other ends, and so have practical validity—namely, the existence of a God and of a future world. I know also, to a certainty, that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions which conduct to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is, at the same time, my maxim (as reason requires that it should be), I am irresistibly constrained to believe in the existence of God and in a future life; and I am sure that nothing can make me waver in this belief, since I should thereby overthrow my moral maxims, the renunciation of which would render me hateful in my own eyes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","text":"Thus, while all the ambitious attempts of reason to penetrate beyond the limits of experience end in disappointment, there is still enough left to satisfy us in a practical point of view. No one, it is true, will be able to boast that he knows that there is a God and a future life; for, if he knows this, he is just the man whom I have long wished to find. All knowledge, regarding an object of mere reason, can be communicated; and I should thus be enabled to hope that my own knowledge would receive this wonderful extension, through the instrumentality of his instruction. No, my conviction is not logical, but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral sentiment), I must not even say: It is morally certain that there is a God, etc., but: I am morally certain, that is, my belief in God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral nature that I am under as little apprehension of having the former torn from me as of losing the latter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0012","text":"The only point in this argument that may appear open to suspicion is that this rational belief presupposes the existence of moral sentiments. If we give up this assumption, and take a man who is entirely indifferent with regard to moral laws, the question which reason proposes, becomes then merely a problem for speculation and may, indeed, be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by such as will compel the most obstinate scepticism to give way.[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-79) But in these questions no man is free from all interest. For though the want of good sentiments may place him beyond the influence of moral interests, still even in this case enough may be left to make him fear the existence of God and a future life. For he cannot pretend to any certainty of the non-existence of God and of a future life, unless—since it could only be proved by mere reason, and therefore apodeictically—he is prepared to establish the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable man would undertake to do. This would be a negative belief, which could not, indeed, produce morality and good sentiments, but still could produce an analogon of these, by operating as a powerful restraint on the outbreak of evil dispositions.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0013","text":"[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-79) The human mind (as, I believe, every rational being must of necessity do) takes a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided, and may not be practically in preponderance. If you strengthen and increase it, you will find the reason become docile, more enlightened, and more capable of uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care at the outset, or at least midway, to make men good, you will never force them into an honest belief.\n\nBut, it will be said, is this all that pure reason can effect, in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? Nothing more than two articles of belief? Common sense could have done as much as this, without taking the philosophers to counsel in the matter!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","text":"I shall not here eulogize philosophy for the benefits which the laborious efforts of its criticism have conferred on human reason—even granting that its merit should turn out in the end to be only negative—for on this point something more will be said in the next section. But, I ask, do you require that that knowledge which concerns all men, should transcend the common understanding, and should only be revealed to you by philosophers? The very circumstance which has called forth your censure, is the best confirmation of the correctness of our previous assertions, since it discloses, what could not have been foreseen, that Nature is not chargeable with any partial distribution of her gifts in those matters which concern all men without distinction and that, in respect to the essential ends of human nature, we cannot advance further with the help of the highest philosophy, than under the guidance which nature has vouchsafed to the meanest understanding.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7ef441dee3b97c8031ca1ee58c49751a6b70fe102aea7f7f2fe50f6e3dc3b433","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","order":93,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CI_2","text":"# Architectonic\n\n### Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason\n\nBy the term architectonic I mean the art of constructing a system. Without systematic unity, our knowledge cannot become science; it will be an aggregate, and not a system. Thus architectonic is the doctrine of the scientific in cognition, and therefore necessarily forms part of our methodology.\n\nReason cannot permit our knowledge to remain in an unconnected and rhapsodistic state, but requires that the sum of our cognitions should constitute a system. It is thus alone that they can advance the ends of reason. By a system I mean the unity of various cognitions under one idea. This idea is the conception—given by reason—of the form of a whole, in so far as the conception determines à priori not only the limits of its content, but the place which each of its parts is to occupy. The scientific idea contains, therefore, the end and the form of the whole which is in accordance with that end. The unity of the end, to which all the parts of the system relate, and through which all have a relation to each other, communicates unity to the whole system, so that the absence of any part can be immediately detected from our knowledge of the rest; and it determines à priori the limits of the system, thus excluding all contingent or arbitrary additions. The whole is thus an organism (articulatio), and not an aggregate (coacervatio); it may grow from within (per intussusceptionem), but it cannot increase by external additions (per appositionem). It is, thus, like an animal body, the growth of which does not add any limb, but, without changing their proportions, makes each in its sphere stronger and more active.\n\nWe require, for the execution of the idea of a system, a schema, that is, a content and an arrangement of parts determined à priori by the principle which the aim of the system prescribes. A schema which is not projected in accordance with an idea, that is, from the standpoint of the highest aim of reason, but merely empirically, in accordance with accidental aims and purposes (the number of which cannot be predetermined), can give us nothing more than technical unity. But the schema which is originated from an idea (in which case reason presents us with aims à priori, and does not look for them to experience), forms the basis of architectonical unity. A science, in the proper acceptation of that term, cannot be formed technically, that is, from observation of the similarity existing between different objects, and the purely contingent use we make of our knowledge in concreto with reference to all kinds of arbitrary external aims; its constitution must be framed on architectonical principles, that is, its parts must be shown to possess an essential affinity, and be capable of being deduced from one supreme and internal aim or end, which forms the condition of the possibility of the scientific whole. The schema of a science must give à priori the plan of it (monogramma), and the division of the whole into parts, in conformity with the idea of the science; and it must also distinguish this whole from all others, according to certain understood principles.\n\nNo one will attempt to construct a science, unless he have some idea to rest on as a proper basis. But, in the elaboration of the science, he finds that the schema, nay, even the definition which he at first gave of the science, rarely corresponds with his idea; for this idea lies, like a germ, in our reason, its parts undeveloped and hid even from microscopical observation. For this reason, we ought to explain and define sciences, not according to the description which the originator gives of them, but according to the idea which we find based in reason itself, and which is suggested by the natural unity of the parts of the science already accumulated. For it will often be found that the originator of a science and even his latest successors remain attached to an erroneous idea, which they cannot render clear to themselves, and that they thus fail in determining the true content, the articulation or systematic unity, and the limits of their science.\n\nIt is unfortunate that, only after having occupied ourselves for a long time in the collection of materials, under the guidance of an idea which lies undeveloped in the mind, but not according to any definite plan of arrangement—nay, only after we have spent much time and labour in the technical disposition of our materials, does it become possible to view the idea of a science in a clear light, and to project, according to architectonical principles, a plan of the whole, in accordance with the aims of reason. Systems seem, like certain worms, to be formed by a kind of generatio aequivoca—by the mere confluence of conceptions, and to gain completeness only with the progress of time. But the schema or germ of all lies in reason; and thus is not only every system organized according to its own idea, but all are united into one grand system of human knowledge, of which they form members. For this reason, it is possible to frame an architectonic of all human cognition, the formation of which, at the present time, considering the immense materials collected or to be found in the ruins of old systems, would not indeed be very difficult. Our purpose at present is merely to sketch the plan of the architectonic of all cognition given by pure reason; and we begin from the point where the main root of human knowledge divides into two, one of which is reason. By reason I understand here the whole higher faculty of cognition, the rational being placed in contradistinction to the empirical.\n\nIf I make complete abstraction of the content of cognition, objectively considered, all cognition is, from a subjective point of view, either historical or rational. Historical cognition is cognitio ex datis, rational, cognitio ex principiis. Whatever may be the original source of a cognition, it is, in relation to the person who possesses it, merely historical, if he knows only what has been given him from another quarter, whether that knowledge was communicated by direct experience or by instruction. Thus the person who has learned a system of philosophy—say the Wolfian—although he has a perfect knowledge of all the principles, definitions, and arguments in that philosophy, as well as of the divisions that have been made of the system, possesses really no more than an historical knowledge of the Wolfian system; he knows only what has been told him, his judgements are only those which he has received from his teachers. Dispute the validity of a definition, and he is completely at a loss to find another. He has formed his mind on another’s; but the imitative faculty is not the productive. His knowledge has not been drawn from reason; and although, objectively considered, it is rational knowledge, subjectively, it is merely historical. He has learned this or that philosophy and is merely a plaster cast of a living man. Rational cognitions which are objective, that is, which have their source in reason, can be so termed from a subjective point of view, only when they have been drawn by the individual himself from the sources of reason, that is, from principles; and it is in this way alone that criticism, or even the rejection of what has been already learned, can spring up in the mind.\n\nAll rational cognition is, again, based either on conceptions, or on the construction of conceptions. The former is termed philosophical, the latter mathematical. I have already shown the essential difference of these two methods of cognition in the first chapter. A cognition may be objectively philosophical and subjectively historical—as is the case with the majority of scholars and those who cannot look beyond the limits of their system, and who remain in a state of pupilage all their lives. But it is remarkable that mathematical knowledge, when committed to memory, is valid, from the subjective point of view, as rational knowledge also, and that the same distinction cannot be drawn here as in the case of philosophical cognition. The reason is that the only way of arriving at this knowledge is through the essential principles of reason, and thus it is always certain and indisputable; because reason is employed in concreto—but at the same time à priori—that is, in pure and, therefore, infallible intuition; and thus all causes of illusion and error are excluded. Of all the à priori sciences of reason, therefore, mathematics alone can be learned. Philosophy—unless it be in an historical manner—cannot be learned; we can at most learn to philosophize.\n\nPhilosophy is the system of all philosophical cognition. We must use this term in an objective sense, if we understand by it the archetype of all attempts at philosophizing, and the standard by which all subjective philosophies are to be judged. In this sense, philosophy is merely the idea of a possible science, which does not exist in concreto, but to which we endeavour in various ways to approximate, until we have discovered the right path to pursue—a path overgrown by the errors and illusions of sense—and the image we have hitherto tried in vain to shape has become a perfect copy of the great prototype. Until that time, we cannot learn philosophy—it does not exist; if it does, where is it, who possesses it, and how shall we know it? We can only learn to philosophize; in other words, we can only exercise our powers of reasoning in accordance with general principles, retaining at the same time, the right of investigating the sources of these principles, of testing, and even of rejecting them.\n\nUntil then, our conception of philosophy is only a scholastic conception—a conception, that is, of a system of cognition which we are trying to elaborate into a science; all that we at present know being the systematic unity of this cognition, and consequently the logical completeness of the cognition for the desired end. But there is also a cosmical conception (conceptus cosmicus) of philosophy, which has always formed the true basis of this term, especially when philosophy was personified and presented to us in the ideal of a philosopher. In this view philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the ultimate and essential aims of human reason (teleologia rationis humanae), and the philosopher is not merely an artist—who occupies himself with conceptions—but a lawgiver, legislating for human reason. In this sense of the word, it would be in the highest degree arrogant to assume the title of philosopher, and to pretend that we had reached the perfection of the prototype which lies in the idea alone.\n\nThe mathematician, the natural philosopher, and the logician—how far soever the first may have advanced in rational, and the two latter in philosophical knowledge—are merely artists, engaged in the arrangement and formation of conceptions; they cannot be termed philosophers. Above them all, there is the ideal teacher, who employs them as instruments for the advancement of the essential aims of human reason. Him alone can we call philosopher; but he nowhere exists. But the idea of his legislative power resides in the mind of every man, and it alone teaches us what kind of systematic unity philosophy demands in view of the ultimate aims of reason. This idea is, therefore, a cosmical conception.[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-80)\n\n[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-80) By a cosmical conception, I mean one in which all men necessarily take an interest; the aim of a science must accordingly be determined according to scholastic conceptions, if it is regarded merely as a means to certain arbitrarily proposed ends.\n\nIn view of the complete systematic unity of reason, there can only be one ultimate end of all the operations of the mind. To this all other aims are subordinate, and nothing more than means for its attainment. This ultimate end is the destination of man, and the philosophy which relates to it is termed moral philosophy. The superior position occupied by moral philosophy, above all other spheres for the operations of reason, sufficiently indicates the reason why the ancients always included the idea—and in an especial manner—of moralist in that of philosopher. Even at the present day, we call a man who appears to have the power of self-government, even although his knowledge may be very limited, by the name of philosopher.\n\nThe legislation of human reason, or philosophy, has two objects—nature and freedom—and thus contains not only the laws of nature, but also those of ethics, at first in two separate systems, which, finally, merge into one grand philosophical system of cognition. The philosophy of nature relates to that which is, that of ethics to that which ought to be.\n\nBut all philosophy is either cognition on the basis of pure reason, or the cognition of reason on the basis of empirical principles. The former is termed pure, the latter empirical philosophy.\n\nThe philosophy of pure reason is either propædeutic, that is, an inquiry into the powers of reason in regard to pure à priori cognition, and is termed critical philosophy; or it is, secondly, the system of pure reason—a science containing the systematic presentation of the whole body of philosophical knowledge, true as well as illusory, given by pure reason—and is called metaphysic. This name may, however, be also given to the whole system of pure philosophy, critical philosophy included, and may designate the investigation into the sources or possibility of à priori cognition, as well as the presentation of the à priori cognitions which form a system of pure philosophy—excluding, at the same time, all empirical and mathematical elements.\n\nMetaphysic is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical use of pure reason, and is, accordingly, either the metaphysic of nature, or the metaphysic of ethics. The former contains all the pure rational principles—based upon conceptions alone (and thus excluding mathematics)—of all theoretical cognition; the latter, the principles which determine and necessitate à priori all action. Now moral philosophy alone contains a code of laws—for the regulation of our actions—which are deduced from principles entirely à priori. Hence the metaphysic of ethics is the only pure moral philosophy, as it is not based upon anthropological or other empirical considerations. The metaphysic of speculative reason is what is commonly called metaphysic in the more limited sense. But as pure moral philosophy properly forms a part of this system of cognition, we must allow it to retain the name of metaphysic, although it is not requisite that we should insist on so terming it in our present discussion.\n\nIt is of the highest importance to separate those cognitions which differ from others both in kind and in origin, and to take great care that they are not confounded with those with which they are generally found connected. What the chemist does in the analysis of substances, what the mathematician in pure mathematics, is, in a still higher degree, the duty of the philosopher, that the value of each different kind of cognition, and the part it takes in the operations of the mind, may be clearly defined. Human reason has never wanted a metaphysic of some kind, since it attained the power of thought, or rather of reflection; but it has never been able to keep this sphere of thought and cognition pure from all admixture of foreign elements. The idea of a science of this kind is as old as speculation itself; and what mind does not speculate—either in the scholastic or in the popular fashion? At the same time, it must be admitted that even thinkers by profession have been unable clearly to explain the distinction between the two elements of our cognition—the one completely à priori, the other à posteriori; and hence the proper definition of a peculiar kind of cognition, and with it the just idea of a science which has so long and so deeply engaged the attention of the human mind, has never been established. When it was said: “Metaphysic is the science of the first principles of human cognition,” this definition did not signalize a peculiarity in kind, but only a difference in degree; these first principles were thus declared to be more general than others, but no criterion of distinction from empirical principles was given. Of these some are more general, and therefore higher, than others; and—as we cannot distinguish what is completely à priori from that which is known to be à posteriori—where shall we draw the line which is to separate the higher and so-called first principles, from the lower and subordinate principles of cognition? What would be said if we were asked to be satisfied with a division of the epochs of the world into the earlier centuries and those following them? “Does the fifth, or the tenth century belong to the earlier centuries?” it would be asked. In the same way I ask: Does the conception of extension belong to metaphysics? You answer, “Yes.” Well, that of body too? “Yes.” And that of a fluid body? You stop, you are unprepared to admit this; for if you do, everything will belong to metaphysics. From this it is evident that the mere degree of subordination—of the particular to the general—cannot determine the limits of a science; and that, in the present case, we must expect to find a difference in the conceptions of metaphysics both in kind and in origin. The fundamental idea of metaphysics was obscured on another side by the fact that this kind of à priori cognition showed a certain similarity in character with the science of mathematics. Both have the property in common of possessing an à priori origin; but, in the one, our knowledge is based upon conceptions, in the other, on the construction of conceptions. Thus a decided dissimilarity between philosophical and mathematical cognition comes out—a dissimilarity which was always felt, but which could not be made distinct for want of an insight into the criteria of the difference. And thus it happened that, as philosophers themselves failed in the proper development of the idea of their science, the elaboration of the science could not proceed with a definite aim, or under trustworthy guidance. Thus, too, philosophers, ignorant of the path they ought to pursue and always disputing with each other regarding the discoveries which each asserted he had made, brought their science into disrepute with the rest of the world, and finally, even among themselves.\n\nAll pure à priori cognition forms, therefore, in view of the peculiar faculty which originates it, a peculiar and distinct unity; and metaphysic is the term applied to the philosophy which attempts to represent that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of metaphysic, which has especially appropriated this appellation—that which we have called the metaphysic of nature—and which considers everything, as it is (not as it ought to be), by means of à priori conceptions, is divided in the following manner.\n\nMetaphysic, in the more limited acceptation of the term, consists of two parts—transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former presents the system of all the conceptions and principles belonging to the understanding and the reason, and which relate to objects in general, but not to any particular given objects (Ontologia); the latter has nature for its subject-matter, that is, the sum of given objects—whether given to the senses, or, if we will, to some other kind of intuition—and is accordingly physiology, although only rationalis. But the use of the faculty of reason in this rational mode of regarding nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, more properly speaking, immanent or transcendent. The former relates to nature, in so far as our knowledge regarding it may be applied in experience (in concreto); the latter to that connection of the objects of experience, which transcends all experience. Transcendent physiology has, again, an internal and an external connection with its object, both, however, transcending possible experience; the former is the physiology of nature as a whole, or transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the connection of the whole of nature with a being above nature, or transcendental cognition of God.\n\nImmanent physiology, on the contrary, considers nature as the sum of all sensuous objects, consequently, as it is presented to us—but still according to à priori conditions, for it is under these alone that nature can be presented to our minds at all. The objects of immanent physiology are of two kinds: 1. Those of the external senses, or corporeal nature; 2. The object of the internal sense, the soul, or, in accordance with our fundamental conceptions of it, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics; but, as it must contain only the principles of an à priori cognition of nature, we must term it rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology, and for the same reason is to be regarded as merely the rational cognition of the soul.\n\nThus the whole system of metaphysics consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology; 2. Rational Physiology; 3. Rational cosmology; and 4. Rational theology. The second part—that of the rational doctrine of nature—may be subdivided into two, physica rationalis[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-81) and psychologia rationalis.\n\n[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-81) It must not be supposed that I mean by this appellation what is generally called physica general is, and which is rather mathematics than a philosophy of nature. For the metaphysic of nature is completely different from mathematics, nor is it so rich in results, although it is of great importance as a critical test of the application of pure understanding—cognition to nature. For want of its guidance, even mathematicians, adopting certain common notions—which are, in fact, metaphysical—have unconsciously crowded their theories of nature with hypotheses, the fallacy of which becomes evident upon the application of the principles of this metaphysic, without detriment, however, to the employment of mathematics in this sphere of cognition.\n\nThe fundamental idea of a philosophy of pure reason of necessity dictates this division; it is, therefore, architectonical—in accordance with the highest aims of reason, and not merely technical, or according to certain accidentally-observed similarities existing between the different parts of the whole science. For this reason, also, is the division immutable and of legislative authority. But the reader may observe in it a few points to which he ought to demur, and which may weaken his conviction of its truth and legitimacy.\n\nIn the first place, how can I desire an à priori cognition or metaphysic of objects, in so far as they are given à posteriori? and how is it possible to cognize the nature of things according to à priori principles, and to attain to a rational physiology? The answer is this. We take from experience nothing more than is requisite to present us with an object (in general) of the external or of the internal sense; in the former case, by the mere conception of matter (impenetrable and inanimate extension), in the latter, by the conception of a thinking being—given in the internal empirical representation, I think. As to the rest, we must not employ in our metaphysic of these objects any empirical principles (which add to the content of our conceptions by means of experience), for the purpose of forming by their help any judgements respecting these objects.\n\nSecondly, what place shall we assign to empirical psychology, which has always been considered a part of metaphysics, and from which in our time such important philosophical results have been expected, after the hope of constructing an à priori system of knowledge had been abandoned? I answer: It must be placed by the side of empirical physics or physics proper; that is, must be regarded as forming a part of applied philosophy, the à priori principles of which are contained in pure philosophy, which is therefore connected, although it must not be confounded, with psychology. Empirical psychology must therefore be banished from the sphere of metaphysics, and is indeed excluded by the very idea of that science. In conformity, however, with scholastic usage, we must permit it to occupy a place in metaphysics—but only as an appendix to it. We adopt this course from motives of economy; as psychology is not as yet full enough to occupy our attention as an independent study, while it is, at the same time, of too great importance to be entirely excluded or placed where it has still less affinity than it has with the subject of metaphysics. It is a stranger who has been long a guest; and we make it welcome to stay, until it can take up a more suitable abode in a complete system of anthropology—the pendant to empirical physics.\n\nThe above is the general idea of metaphysics, which, as more was expected from it than could be looked for with justice, and as these pleasant expectations were unfortunately never realized, fell into general disrepute. Our Critique must have fully convinced the reader that, although metaphysics cannot form the foundation of religion, it must always be one of its most important bulwarks, and that human reason, which naturally pursues a dialectical course, cannot do without this science, which checks its tendencies towards dialectic and, by elevating reason to a scientific and clear self-knowledge, prevents the ravages which a lawless speculative reason would infallibly commit in the sphere of morals as well as in that of religion. We may be sure, therefore, whatever contempt may be thrown upon metaphysics by those who judge a science not by its own nature, but according to the accidental effects it may have produced, that it can never be completely abandoned, that we must always return to it as to a beloved one who has been for a time estranged, because the questions with which it is engaged relate to the highest aims of humanity, and reason must always labour either to attain to settled views in regard to these, or to destroy those which others have already established.\n\nMetaphysic, therefore—that of nature, as well as that of ethics, but in an especial manner the criticism which forms the propædeutic to all the operations of reason—forms properly that department of knowledge which may be termed, in the truest sense of the word, philosophy. The path which it pursues is that of science, which, when it has once been discovered, is never lost, and never misleads. Mathematics, natural science, the common experience of men, have a high value as means, for the most part, to accidental ends—but at last also, to those which are necessary and essential to the existence of humanity. But to guide them to this high goal, they require the aid of rational cognition on the basis of pure conceptions, which, be it termed as it may, is properly nothing but metaphysics.\n\nFor the same reason, metaphysics forms likewise the completion of the culture of human reason. In this respect, it is indispensable, setting aside altogether the influence which it exerts as a science. For its subject-matter is the elements and highest maxims of reason, which form the basis of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That, as a purely speculative science, it is more useful in preventing error than in the extension of knowledge, does not detract from its value; on the contrary, the supreme office of censor which it occupies assures to it the highest authority and importance. This office it administers for the purpose of securing order, harmony, and well-being to science, and of directing its noble and fruitful labours to the highest possible aim—the happiness of all mankind.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","text":"# Architectonic\n\n### Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason\n\nBy the term architectonic I mean the art of constructing a system. Without systematic unity, our knowledge cannot become science; it will be an aggregate, and not a system. Thus architectonic is the doctrine of the scientific in cognition, and therefore necessarily forms part of our methodology.\n\nReason cannot permit our knowledge to remain in an unconnected and rhapsodistic state, but requires that the sum of our cognitions should constitute a system. It is thus alone that they can advance the ends of reason. By a system I mean the unity of various cognitions under one idea. This idea is the conception—given by reason—of the form of a whole, in so far as the conception determines à priori not only the limits of its content, but the place which each of its parts is to occupy. The scientific idea contains, therefore, the end and the form of the whole which is in accordance with that end. The unity of the end, to which all the parts of the system relate, and through which all have a relation to each other, communicates unity to the whole system, so that the absence of any part can be immediately detected from our knowledge of the rest; and it determines à priori the limits of the system, thus excluding all contingent or arbitrary additions. The whole is thus an organism (articulatio), and not an aggregate (coacervatio); it may grow from within (per intussusceptionem), but it cannot increase by external additions (per appositionem). It is, thus, like an animal body, the growth of which does not add any limb, but, without changing their proportions, makes each in its sphere stronger and more active.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","text":"We require, for the execution of the idea of a system, a schema, that is, a content and an arrangement of parts determined à priori by the principle which the aim of the system prescribes. A schema which is not projected in accordance with an idea, that is, from the standpoint of the highest aim of reason, but merely empirically, in accordance with accidental aims and purposes (the number of which cannot be predetermined), can give us nothing more than technical unity. But the schema which is originated from an idea (in which case reason presents us with aims à priori, and does not look for them to experience), forms the basis of architectonical unity. A science, in the proper acceptation of that term, cannot be formed technically, that is, from observation of the similarity existing between different objects, and the purely contingent use we make of our knowledge in concreto with reference to all kinds of arbitrary external aims; its constitution must be framed on architectonical principles, that is, its parts must be shown to possess an essential affinity, and be capable of being deduced from one supreme and internal aim or end, which forms the condition of the possibility of the scientific whole. The schema of a science must give à priori the plan of it (monogramma), and the division of the whole into parts, in conformity with the idea of the science; and it must also distinguish this whole from all others, according to certain understood principles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","text":"No one will attempt to construct a science, unless he have some idea to rest on as a proper basis. But, in the elaboration of the science, he finds that the schema, nay, even the definition which he at first gave of the science, rarely corresponds with his idea; for this idea lies, like a germ, in our reason, its parts undeveloped and hid even from microscopical observation. For this reason, we ought to explain and define sciences, not according to the description which the originator gives of them, but according to the idea which we find based in reason itself, and which is suggested by the natural unity of the parts of the science already accumulated. For it will often be found that the originator of a science and even his latest successors remain attached to an erroneous idea, which they cannot render clear to themselves, and that they thus fail in determining the true content, the articulation or systematic unity, and the limits of their science.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","text":"It is unfortunate that, only after having occupied ourselves for a long time in the collection of materials, under the guidance of an idea which lies undeveloped in the mind, but not according to any definite plan of arrangement—nay, only after we have spent much time and labour in the technical disposition of our materials, does it become possible to view the idea of a science in a clear light, and to project, according to architectonical principles, a plan of the whole, in accordance with the aims of reason. Systems seem, like certain worms, to be formed by a kind of generatio aequivoca—by the mere confluence of conceptions, and to gain completeness only with the progress of time. But the schema or germ of all lies in reason; and thus is not only every system organized according to its own idea, but all are united into one grand system of human knowledge, of which they form members. For this reason, it is possible to frame an architectonic of all human cognition, the formation of which, at the present time, considering the immense materials collected or to be found in the ruins of old systems, would not indeed be very difficult. Our purpose at present is merely to sketch the plan of the architectonic of all cognition given by pure reason; and we begin from the point where the main root of human knowledge divides into two, one of which is reason. By reason I understand here the whole higher faculty of cognition, the rational being placed in contradistinction to the empirical.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","text":"If I make complete abstraction of the content of cognition, objectively considered, all cognition is, from a subjective point of view, either historical or rational. Historical cognition is cognitio ex datis, rational, cognitio ex principiis. Whatever may be the original source of a cognition, it is, in relation to the person who possesses it, merely historical, if he knows only what has been given him from another quarter, whether that knowledge was communicated by direct experience or by instruction. Thus the person who has learned a system of philosophy—say the Wolfian—although he has a perfect knowledge of all the principles, definitions, and arguments in that philosophy, as well as of the divisions that have been made of the system, possesses really no more than an historical knowledge of the Wolfian system; he knows only what has been told him, his judgements are only those which he has received from his teachers. Dispute the validity of a definition, and he is completely at a loss to find another. He has formed his mind on another’s; but the imitative faculty is not the productive. His knowledge has not been drawn from reason; and although, objectively considered, it is rational knowledge, subjectively, it is merely historical. He has learned this or that philosophy and is merely a plaster cast of a living man. Rational cognitions which are objective, that is, which have their source in reason, can be so termed from a subjective point of view, only when they have been drawn by the individual himself from the sources of reason, that is, from principles; and it is in this way alone that criticism, or even the rejection of what has been already learned, can spring up in the mind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","text":"All rational cognition is, again, based either on conceptions, or on the construction of conceptions. The former is termed philosophical, the latter mathematical. I have already shown the essential difference of these two methods of cognition in the first chapter. A cognition may be objectively philosophical and subjectively historical—as is the case with the majority of scholars and those who cannot look beyond the limits of their system, and who remain in a state of pupilage all their lives. But it is remarkable that mathematical knowledge, when committed to memory, is valid, from the subjective point of view, as rational knowledge also, and that the same distinction cannot be drawn here as in the case of philosophical cognition. The reason is that the only way of arriving at this knowledge is through the essential principles of reason, and thus it is always certain and indisputable; because reason is employed in concreto—but at the same time à priori—that is, in pure and, therefore, infallible intuition; and thus all causes of illusion and error are excluded. Of all the à priori sciences of reason, therefore, mathematics alone can be learned. Philosophy—unless it be in an historical manner—cannot be learned; we can at most learn to philosophize.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","text":"Philosophy is the system of all philosophical cognition. We must use this term in an objective sense, if we understand by it the archetype of all attempts at philosophizing, and the standard by which all subjective philosophies are to be judged. In this sense, philosophy is merely the idea of a possible science, which does not exist in concreto, but to which we endeavour in various ways to approximate, until we have discovered the right path to pursue—a path overgrown by the errors and illusions of sense—and the image we have hitherto tried in vain to shape has become a perfect copy of the great prototype. Until that time, we cannot learn philosophy—it does not exist; if it does, where is it, who possesses it, and how shall we know it? We can only learn to philosophize; in other words, we can only exercise our powers of reasoning in accordance with general principles, retaining at the same time, the right of investigating the sources of these principles, of testing, and even of rejecting them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","text":"Until then, our conception of philosophy is only a scholastic conception—a conception, that is, of a system of cognition which we are trying to elaborate into a science; all that we at present know being the systematic unity of this cognition, and consequently the logical completeness of the cognition for the desired end. But there is also a cosmical conception (conceptus cosmicus) of philosophy, which has always formed the true basis of this term, especially when philosophy was personified and presented to us in the ideal of a philosopher. In this view philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the ultimate and essential aims of human reason (teleologia rationis humanae), and the philosopher is not merely an artist—who occupies himself with conceptions—but a lawgiver, legislating for human reason. In this sense of the word, it would be in the highest degree arrogant to assume the title of philosopher, and to pretend that we had reached the perfection of the prototype which lies in the idea alone.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","text":"The mathematician, the natural philosopher, and the logician—how far soever the first may have advanced in rational, and the two latter in philosophical knowledge—are merely artists, engaged in the arrangement and formation of conceptions; they cannot be termed philosophers. Above them all, there is the ideal teacher, who employs them as instruments for the advancement of the essential aims of human reason. Him alone can we call philosopher; but he nowhere exists. But the idea of his legislative power resides in the mind of every man, and it alone teaches us what kind of systematic unity philosophy demands in view of the ultimate aims of reason. This idea is, therefore, a cosmical conception.[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-80)\n\n[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-80) By a cosmical conception, I mean one in which all men necessarily take an interest; the aim of a science must accordingly be determined according to scholastic conceptions, if it is regarded merely as a means to certain arbitrarily proposed ends.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","text":"In view of the complete systematic unity of reason, there can only be one ultimate end of all the operations of the mind. To this all other aims are subordinate, and nothing more than means for its attainment. This ultimate end is the destination of man, and the philosophy which relates to it is termed moral philosophy. The superior position occupied by moral philosophy, above all other spheres for the operations of reason, sufficiently indicates the reason why the ancients always included the idea—and in an especial manner—of moralist in that of philosopher. Even at the present day, we call a man who appears to have the power of self-government, even although his knowledge may be very limited, by the name of philosopher.\n\nThe legislation of human reason, or philosophy, has two objects—nature and freedom—and thus contains not only the laws of nature, but also those of ethics, at first in two separate systems, which, finally, merge into one grand philosophical system of cognition. The philosophy of nature relates to that which is, that of ethics to that which ought to be.\n\nBut all philosophy is either cognition on the basis of pure reason, or the cognition of reason on the basis of empirical principles. The former is termed pure, the latter empirical philosophy.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","text":"The philosophy of pure reason is either propædeutic, that is, an inquiry into the powers of reason in regard to pure à priori cognition, and is termed critical philosophy; or it is, secondly, the system of pure reason—a science containing the systematic presentation of the whole body of philosophical knowledge, true as well as illusory, given by pure reason—and is called metaphysic. This name may, however, be also given to the whole system of pure philosophy, critical philosophy included, and may designate the investigation into the sources or possibility of à priori cognition, as well as the presentation of the à priori cognitions which form a system of pure philosophy—excluding, at the same time, all empirical and mathematical elements.\n\nMetaphysic is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical use of pure reason, and is, accordingly, either the metaphysic of nature, or the metaphysic of ethics. The former contains all the pure rational principles—based upon conceptions alone (and thus excluding mathematics)—of all theoretical cognition; the latter, the principles which determine and necessitate à priori all action. Now moral philosophy alone contains a code of laws—for the regulation of our actions—which are deduced from principles entirely à priori. Hence the metaphysic of ethics is the only pure moral philosophy, as it is not based upon anthropological or other empirical considerations. The metaphysic of speculative reason is what is commonly called metaphysic in the more limited sense. But as pure moral philosophy properly forms a part of this system of cognition, we must allow it to retain the name of metaphysic, although it is not requisite that we should insist on so terming it in our present discussion.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","text":"It is of the highest importance to separate those cognitions which differ from others both in kind and in origin, and to take great care that they are not confounded with those with which they are generally found connected. What the chemist does in the analysis of substances, what the mathematician in pure mathematics, is, in a still higher degree, the duty of the philosopher, that the value of each different kind of cognition, and the part it takes in the operations of the mind, may be clearly defined. Human reason has never wanted a metaphysic of some kind, since it attained the power of thought, or rather of reflection; but it has never been able to keep this sphere of thought and cognition pure from all admixture of foreign elements. The idea of a science of this kind is as old as speculation itself; and what mind does not speculate—either in the scholastic or in the popular fashion? At the same time, it must be admitted that even thinkers by profession have been unable clearly to explain the distinction between the two elements of our cognition—the one completely à priori, the other à posteriori; and hence the proper definition of a peculiar kind of cognition, and with it the just idea of a science which has so long and so deeply engaged the attention of the human mind, has never been established. When it was said: “Metaphysic is the science of the first principles of human cognition,” this definition did not signalize a peculiarity in kind, but only a difference in degree; these first principles were thus declared to be more general than others, but no criterion of distinction from empirical principles was given. Of these some are more general, and therefore higher, than others; and—as we cannot distinguish what is completely à priori from that which is known to be à posteriori—where shall we draw the line which is to separate the higher and so-called first principles, from the lower and subordinate principles of cognition? What would be said if we were asked to be satisfied with a division of the epochs of the world into the earlier centuries and those following them? “Does the fifth, or the tenth century belong to the earlier centuries?” it would be asked. In the same way I ask: Does the conception of extension belong to metaphysics? You answer, “Yes.” Well, that of body too? “Yes.” And that of a fluid body? You stop, you are unprepared to admit this; for if you do, everything will belong to metaphysics. From this it is evident that the mere degree of subordination—of the particular to the general—cannot determine the limits of a science; and that, in the present case, we must expect to find a difference in the conceptions of metaphysics both in kind and in origin. The fundamental idea of metaphysics was obscured on another side by the fact that this kind of à priori cognition showed a certain similarity in character with the science of mathematics. Both have the property in common of possessing an à priori origin; but, in the one, our knowledge is based upon conceptions, in the other, on the construction of conceptions. Thus a decided dissimilarity between philosophical and mathematical cognition comes out—a dissimilarity which was always felt, but which could not be made distinct for want of an insight into the criteria of the difference. And thus it happened that, as philosophers themselves failed in the proper development of the idea of their science, the elaboration of the science could not proceed with a definite aim, or under trustworthy guidance. Thus, too, philosophers, ignorant of the path they ought to pursue and always disputing with each other regarding the discoveries which each asserted he had made, brought their science into disrepute with the rest of the world, and finally, even among themselves.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0013","text":"All pure à priori cognition forms, therefore, in view of the peculiar faculty which originates it, a peculiar and distinct unity; and metaphysic is the term applied to the philosophy which attempts to represent that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of metaphysic, which has especially appropriated this appellation—that which we have called the metaphysic of nature—and which considers everything, as it is (not as it ought to be), by means of à priori conceptions, is divided in the following manner.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0014","text":"Metaphysic, in the more limited acceptation of the term, consists of two parts—transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former presents the system of all the conceptions and principles belonging to the understanding and the reason, and which relate to objects in general, but not to any particular given objects (Ontologia); the latter has nature for its subject-matter, that is, the sum of given objects—whether given to the senses, or, if we will, to some other kind of intuition—and is accordingly physiology, although only rationalis. But the use of the faculty of reason in this rational mode of regarding nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, more properly speaking, immanent or transcendent. The former relates to nature, in so far as our knowledge regarding it may be applied in experience (in concreto); the latter to that connection of the objects of experience, which transcends all experience. Transcendent physiology has, again, an internal and an external connection with its object, both, however, transcending possible experience; the former is the physiology of nature as a whole, or transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the connection of the whole of nature with a being above nature, or transcendental cognition of God.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0015","text":"Immanent physiology, on the contrary, considers nature as the sum of all sensuous objects, consequently, as it is presented to us—but still according to à priori conditions, for it is under these alone that nature can be presented to our minds at all. The objects of immanent physiology are of two kinds: 1. Those of the external senses, or corporeal nature; 2. The object of the internal sense, the soul, or, in accordance with our fundamental conceptions of it, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics; but, as it must contain only the principles of an à priori cognition of nature, we must term it rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology, and for the same reason is to be regarded as merely the rational cognition of the soul.\n\nThus the whole system of metaphysics consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology; 2. Rational Physiology; 3. Rational cosmology; and 4. Rational theology. The second part—that of the rational doctrine of nature—may be subdivided into two, physica rationalis[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-81) and psychologia rationalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0016","text":"[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-81) It must not be supposed that I mean by this appellation what is generally called physica general is, and which is rather mathematics than a philosophy of nature. For the metaphysic of nature is completely different from mathematics, nor is it so rich in results, although it is of great importance as a critical test of the application of pure understanding—cognition to nature. For want of its guidance, even mathematicians, adopting certain common notions—which are, in fact, metaphysical—have unconsciously crowded their theories of nature with hypotheses, the fallacy of which becomes evident upon the application of the principles of this metaphysic, without detriment, however, to the employment of mathematics in this sphere of cognition.\n\nThe fundamental idea of a philosophy of pure reason of necessity dictates this division; it is, therefore, architectonical—in accordance with the highest aims of reason, and not merely technical, or according to certain accidentally-observed similarities existing between the different parts of the whole science. For this reason, also, is the division immutable and of legislative authority. But the reader may observe in it a few points to which he ought to demur, and which may weaken his conviction of its truth and legitimacy.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0017","text":"In the first place, how can I desire an à priori cognition or metaphysic of objects, in so far as they are given à posteriori? and how is it possible to cognize the nature of things according to à priori principles, and to attain to a rational physiology? The answer is this. We take from experience nothing more than is requisite to present us with an object (in general) of the external or of the internal sense; in the former case, by the mere conception of matter (impenetrable and inanimate extension), in the latter, by the conception of a thinking being—given in the internal empirical representation, I think. As to the rest, we must not employ in our metaphysic of these objects any empirical principles (which add to the content of our conceptions by means of experience), for the purpose of forming by their help any judgements respecting these objects.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0018","text":"Secondly, what place shall we assign to empirical psychology, which has always been considered a part of metaphysics, and from which in our time such important philosophical results have been expected, after the hope of constructing an à priori system of knowledge had been abandoned? I answer: It must be placed by the side of empirical physics or physics proper; that is, must be regarded as forming a part of applied philosophy, the à priori principles of which are contained in pure philosophy, which is therefore connected, although it must not be confounded, with psychology. Empirical psychology must therefore be banished from the sphere of metaphysics, and is indeed excluded by the very idea of that science. In conformity, however, with scholastic usage, we must permit it to occupy a place in metaphysics—but only as an appendix to it. We adopt this course from motives of economy; as psychology is not as yet full enough to occupy our attention as an independent study, while it is, at the same time, of too great importance to be entirely excluded or placed where it has still less affinity than it has with the subject of metaphysics. It is a stranger who has been long a guest; and we make it welcome to stay, until it can take up a more suitable abode in a complete system of anthropology—the pendant to empirical physics.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","text":"The above is the general idea of metaphysics, which, as more was expected from it than could be looked for with justice, and as these pleasant expectations were unfortunately never realized, fell into general disrepute. Our Critique must have fully convinced the reader that, although metaphysics cannot form the foundation of religion, it must always be one of its most important bulwarks, and that human reason, which naturally pursues a dialectical course, cannot do without this science, which checks its tendencies towards dialectic and, by elevating reason to a scientific and clear self-knowledge, prevents the ravages which a lawless speculative reason would infallibly commit in the sphere of morals as well as in that of religion. We may be sure, therefore, whatever contempt may be thrown upon metaphysics by those who judge a science not by its own nature, but according to the accidental effects it may have produced, that it can never be completely abandoned, that we must always return to it as to a beloved one who has been for a time estranged, because the questions with which it is engaged relate to the highest aims of humanity, and reason must always labour either to attain to settled views in regard to these, or to destroy those which others have already established.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","text":"Metaphysic, therefore—that of nature, as well as that of ethics, but in an especial manner the criticism which forms the propædeutic to all the operations of reason—forms properly that department of knowledge which may be termed, in the truest sense of the word, philosophy. The path which it pursues is that of science, which, when it has once been discovered, is never lost, and never misleads. Mathematics, natural science, the common experience of men, have a high value as means, for the most part, to accidental ends—but at last also, to those which are necessary and essential to the existence of humanity. But to guide them to this high goal, they require the aid of rational cognition on the basis of pure conceptions, which, be it termed as it may, is properly nothing but metaphysics.\n\nFor the same reason, metaphysics forms likewise the completion of the culture of human reason. In this respect, it is indispensable, setting aside altogether the influence which it exerts as a science. For its subject-matter is the elements and highest maxims of reason, which form the basis of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That, as a purely speculative science, it is more useful in preventing error than in the extension of knowledge, does not detract from its value; on the contrary, the supreme office of censor which it occupies assures to it the highest authority and importance. This office it administers for the purpose of securing order, harmony, and well-being to science, and of directing its noble and fruitful labours to the highest possible aim—the happiness of all mankind.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a33842574fc791d0305815fa5178e3920fee6d678dd0bbf77fa5d548f8b93e09","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","order":94,"chapter_id":"CH_BA94_CIHP","text":"### Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason\n\nThis title is placed here merely for the purpose of designating a division of the system of pure reason of which I do not intend to treat at present. I shall content myself with casting a cursory glance, from a purely transcendental point of view—that of the nature of pure reason—on the labours of philosophers up to the present time. They have aimed at erecting an edifice of philosophy; but to my eye this edifice appears to be in a very ruinous condition.\n\nIt is very remarkable, although naturally it could not have been otherwise, that, in the infancy of philosophy, the study of the nature of God and the constitution of a future world formed the commencement, rather than the conclusion, as we should have it, of the speculative efforts of the human mind. However rude the religious conceptions generated by the remains of the old manners and customs of a less cultivated time, the intelligent classes were not thereby prevented from devoting themselves to free inquiry into the existence and nature of God; and they easily saw that there could be no surer way of pleasing the invisible ruler of the world, and of attaining to happiness in another world at least, than a good and honest course of life in this. Thus theology and morals formed the two chief motives, or rather the points of attraction in all abstract inquiries. But it was the former that especially occupied the attention of speculative reason, and which afterwards became so celebrated under the name of metaphysics.\n\nI shall not at present indicate the periods of time at which the greatest changes in metaphysics took place, but shall merely give a hasty sketch of the different ideas which occasioned the most important revolutions in this sphere of thought. There are three different ends in relation to which these revolutions have taken place.\n\n1. In relation to the object of the cognition of reason, philosophers may be divided into sensualists and intellectualists. Epicurus may be regarded as the head of the former, Plato of the latter. The distinction here signalized, subtle as it is, dates from the earliest times, and was long maintained. The former asserted that reality resides in sensuous objects alone, and that everything else is merely imaginary; the latter, that the senses are the parents of illusion and that truth is to be found in the understanding alone. The former did not deny to the conceptions of the understanding a certain kind of reality; but with them it was merely logical, with the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual conceptions, but declared that sensuous objects alone possessed real existence. The latter maintained that all real objects were intelligible, and believed that the pure understanding possessed a faculty of intuition apart from sense, which, in their opinion, served only to confuse the ideas of the understanding.\n\n2. In relation to the origin of the pure cognitions of reason, we find one school maintaining that they are derived entirely from experience, and another that they have their origin in reason alone. Aristotle may be regarded as the head of the empiricists, and Plato of the noologists. Locke, the follower of Aristotle in modern times, and Leibnitz of Plato (although he cannot be said to have imitated him in his mysticism), have not been able to bring this question to a settled conclusion. The procedure of Epicurus in his sensual system, in which he always restricted his conclusions to the sphere of experience, was much more consequent than that of Aristotle and Locke. The latter especially, after having derived all the conceptions and principles of the mind from experience, goes so far, in the employment of these conceptions and principles, as to maintain that we can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul—both of them objects lying beyond the limits of possible experience—with the same force of demonstration, as any mathematical proposition.\n\n3. In relation to method. Method is procedure according to principles. We may divide the methods at present employed in the field of inquiry into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason lays it down as his principle that common reason, without the aid of science—which he calls sound reason, or common sense—can give a more satisfactory answer to the most important questions of metaphysics than speculation is able to do. He must maintain, therefore, that we can determine the content and circumference of the moon more certainly by the naked eye, than by the aid of mathematical reasoning. But this system is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is the most absurd thing in this doctrine, the neglect of all scientific means is paraded as a peculiar method of extending our cognition. As regards those who are naturalists because they know no better, they are certainly not to be blamed. They follow common sense, without parading their ignorance as a method which is to teach us the wonderful secret, how we are to find the truth which lies at the bottom of the well of Democritus.\n\nQuod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo Esse quod  \nArcesilas aerumnosique Solones. PERSIUS  \n—Satirae, iii. 78-79.  \n\nis their motto, under which they may lead a pleasant and praiseworthy life, without troubling themselves with science or troubling science with them.\n\nAs regards those who wish to pursue a scientific method, they have now the choice of following either the dogmatical or the sceptical, while they are bound never to desert the systematic mode of procedure. When I mention, in relation to the former, the celebrated Wolf, and as regards the latter, David Hume, I may leave, in accordance with my present intention, all others unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If my reader has been kind and patient enough to accompany me on this hitherto untravelled route, he can now judge whether, if he and others will contribute their exertions towards making this narrow footpath a high road of thought, that which many centuries have failed to accomplish may not be executed before the close of the present—namely, to bring Reason to perfect contentment in regard to that which has always, but without permanent results, occupied her powers and engaged her ardent desire for knowledge.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","text":"### Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason\n\nThis title is placed here merely for the purpose of designating a division of the system of pure reason of which I do not intend to treat at present. I shall content myself with casting a cursory glance, from a purely transcendental point of view—that of the nature of pure reason—on the labours of philosophers up to the present time. They have aimed at erecting an edifice of philosophy; but to my eye this edifice appears to be in a very ruinous condition.\n\nIt is very remarkable, although naturally it could not have been otherwise, that, in the infancy of philosophy, the study of the nature of God and the constitution of a future world formed the commencement, rather than the conclusion, as we should have it, of the speculative efforts of the human mind. However rude the religious conceptions generated by the remains of the old manners and customs of a less cultivated time, the intelligent classes were not thereby prevented from devoting themselves to free inquiry into the existence and nature of God; and they easily saw that there could be no surer way of pleasing the invisible ruler of the world, and of attaining to happiness in another world at least, than a good and honest course of life in this. Thus theology and morals formed the two chief motives, or rather the points of attraction in all abstract inquiries. But it was the former that especially occupied the attention of speculative reason, and which afterwards became so celebrated under the name of metaphysics.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","text":"I shall not at present indicate the periods of time at which the greatest changes in metaphysics took place, but shall merely give a hasty sketch of the different ideas which occasioned the most important revolutions in this sphere of thought. There are three different ends in relation to which these revolutions have taken place.\n\n1. In relation to the object of the cognition of reason, philosophers may be divided into sensualists and intellectualists. Epicurus may be regarded as the head of the former, Plato of the latter. The distinction here signalized, subtle as it is, dates from the earliest times, and was long maintained. The former asserted that reality resides in sensuous objects alone, and that everything else is merely imaginary; the latter, that the senses are the parents of illusion and that truth is to be found in the understanding alone. The former did not deny to the conceptions of the understanding a certain kind of reality; but with them it was merely logical, with the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual conceptions, but declared that sensuous objects alone possessed real existence. The latter maintained that all real objects were intelligible, and believed that the pure understanding possessed a faculty of intuition apart from sense, which, in their opinion, served only to confuse the ideas of the understanding.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","text":"2. In relation to the origin of the pure cognitions of reason, we find one school maintaining that they are derived entirely from experience, and another that they have their origin in reason alone. Aristotle may be regarded as the head of the empiricists, and Plato of the noologists. Locke, the follower of Aristotle in modern times, and Leibnitz of Plato (although he cannot be said to have imitated him in his mysticism), have not been able to bring this question to a settled conclusion. The procedure of Epicurus in his sensual system, in which he always restricted his conclusions to the sphere of experience, was much more consequent than that of Aristotle and Locke. The latter especially, after having derived all the conceptions and principles of the mind from experience, goes so far, in the employment of these conceptions and principles, as to maintain that we can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul—both of them objects lying beyond the limits of possible experience—with the same force of demonstration, as any mathematical proposition.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","text":"3. In relation to method. Method is procedure according to principles. We may divide the methods at present employed in the field of inquiry into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason lays it down as his principle that common reason, without the aid of science—which he calls sound reason, or common sense—can give a more satisfactory answer to the most important questions of metaphysics than speculation is able to do. He must maintain, therefore, that we can determine the content and circumference of the moon more certainly by the naked eye, than by the aid of mathematical reasoning. But this system is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is the most absurd thing in this doctrine, the neglect of all scientific means is paraded as a peculiar method of extending our cognition. As regards those who are naturalists because they know no better, they are certainly not to be blamed. They follow common sense, without parading their ignorance as a method which is to teach us the wonderful secret, how we are to find the truth which lies at the bottom of the well of Democritus.\n\nQuod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo Esse quod  \nArcesilas aerumnosique Solones. PERSIUS  \n—Satirae, iii. 78-79.  \n\nis their motto, under which they may lead a pleasant and praiseworthy life, without troubling themselves with science or troubling science with them.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","text":"As regards those who wish to pursue a scientific method, they have now the choice of following either the dogmatical or the sceptical, while they are bound never to desert the systematic mode of procedure. When I mention, in relation to the former, the celebrated Wolf, and as regards the latter, David Hume, I may leave, in accordance with my present intention, all others unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If my reader has been kind and patient enough to accompany me on this hitherto untravelled route, he can now judge whether, if he and others will contribute their exertions towards making this narrow footpath a high road of thought, that which many centuries have failed to accomplish may not be executed before the close of the present—namely, to bring Reason to perfect contentment in regard to that which has always, but without permanent results, occupied her powers and engaged her ardent desire for knowledge.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6860c480e28c741e564738f42a396797ab4aba9856ada37c3f676b0bd7956ae1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","order":1,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IDBP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IDBP@R001","sha256":"549e35e8a40ffe177421b07be3a23d3d72db0fdd193a2f20e5ba82d5161d948e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS1","text":"Human knowledge begins in experience because sensory objects awaken the cognitive faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS2","text":"Experience supplies the occasion and raw material of cognition, but does not thereby account for everything contained in knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS3","text":"Empirical knowledge is potentially composite, consisting of sensory impressions and contributions supplied by the cognitive faculty itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS4","text":"The decisive question is whether any knowledge exists that is independent of all experience rather than merely independent of a particular experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS5","text":"A priori knowledge is defined as absolute independence from experience, whereas empirical knowledge is possible only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AS6","text":"A priori knowledge divides into pure and impure according to whether empirical elements remain mixed within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A1","text":"Knowledge begins with experience in temporal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A2","text":"Sensory objects awaken the faculty of cognition into activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A3","text":"Cognition converts sensuous impressions into knowledge of objects through comparison, connection, and separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A4","text":"Experience provides sensuous impressions that function as the raw material of empirical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A5","text":"The cognitive faculty can supply elements that are not received through sensory impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A6","text":"Empirical knowledge can therefore be a compound of sensory reception and cognitive contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A7","text":"The cognitive contribution may initially be indistinguishable from the sensory element.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A8","text":"Temporal priority of experience does not establish that every element of knowledge originates from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","text":"Knowledge is a priori only when it is independent of all experience, not merely independent of a particular experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A10","text":"Empirical knowledge is possible only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A11","text":"A priori knowledge may contain empirical elements and thereby become impure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A12","text":"Pure a priori knowledge contains no empirical element.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:A13","text":"The concept of change is derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX1","text":"Knowledge does not arise exclusively from sensory impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX2","text":"The fact that knowledge begins with experience does not entail that all knowledge arises out of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX3","text":"A priori knowledge is not knowledge merely acquired without waiting for a particular event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX4","text":"A general rule borrowed from experience is not genuinely independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX5","text":"A priori knowledge is not necessarily pure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX6","text":"Pure a priori knowledge is not knowledge containing an empirical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:NX7","text":"Empirical knowledge is not reducible to the passive reception of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D1","text":"The origin of cognition in experience and the source of every component of knowledge must be analytically distinguished.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A1","CH_BA94_IDBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D2","text":"Sensory impressions are necessary conditions for the awakening of cognition without being sufficient conditions for the resulting knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A2","CH_BA94_IDBP:A4","CH_BA94_IDBP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D3","text":"The possibility of a priori knowledge follows from the presence of cognitive elements not supplied by experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A5","CH_BA94_IDBP:A6","CH_BA94_IDBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D4","text":"Immediate non-derivation from experience is insufficient to establish a priori status when the governing rule itself originates in experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D5","text":"The distinction between pure and impure a priori knowledge depends on whether the content under examination contains an empirical concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A11","CH_BA94_IDBP:A12","CH_BA94_IDBP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:D6","text":"Identifying a priori knowledge requires separating cognitive contribution from the empirical material with which it is initially fused.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A6","CH_BA94_IDBP:A7","CH_BA94_IDBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SD1","text":"The text separates the temporal condition of cognition from the epistemic dependence of knowledge, preventing the beginning of knowledge from determining the origin of all its content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A1","CH_BA94_IDBP:A5","CH_BA94_IDBP:A8","CH_BA94_IDBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SD2","text":"Experience functions simultaneously as the initiating condition of cognition and as an insufficient explanation of the cognitive structure produced from it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A2","CH_BA94_IDBP:A3","CH_BA94_IDBP:A4","CH_BA94_IDBP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SD3","text":"The distinction between empirical and a priori knowledge is established by dependence, not by psychological sequence or the absence of a particular observation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A8","CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SD4","text":"The classification of knowledge requires a layered analysis of source, content, and mixture rather than a single opposition between experience and non-experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A6","CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IDBP:A11","CH_BA94_IDBP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the falsity of the assumption that temporal priority and epistemic derivation are identical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that sensory receptivity is the whole of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the assumption that whatever is not immediately inferred from experience is therefore independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a binary in which knowledge is either wholly empirical or wholly detached from the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the assumption that the empirical and non-empirical components of knowledge are transparently distinguishable at first encounter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the assumption that independence from experience guarantees absence of all empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the impossibility of inferring the complete structure of cognition from the conditions under which cognition is awakened.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SNX2","text":"The text forecloses a purely receptive model of knowledge without yet fully specifying the internal principles supplied by cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SNX3","text":"The possibility of a priori knowledge remains dependent on a separation that ordinary consciousness initially cannot perform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:SNX4","text":"The text presupposes that the boundary between received material and cognitive contribution is real even though its criteria of demarcation remain undeveloped here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS1","text":"Sensory affection → cognitive activation → comparison, connection, and separation → object-directed knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS2","text":"Experience as temporal beginning + cognitive contribution → composite empirical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS3","text":"Composite knowledge → analytical separation of received and supplied elements → possibility of identifying a priori content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS4","text":"Absolute independence from all experience → a priori knowledge; dependence on experience → empirical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS5","text":"A priori knowledge + retained empirical element → impure a priori knowledge; a priori knowledge + absence of empirical element → pure a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS6","text":"Apparent immediate independence → test of the origin of the governing rule → rejection or confirmation of a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AF1","text":"Sensory initiation → cognitive processing → composite knowledge → distinction between temporal beginning and epistemic origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:AF2","text":"Ambiguous meaning of “a priori” → restriction to absolute independence from experience → division into pure and impure a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ME1","text":"Experience is reclassified from the total source of knowledge to the initiating condition and material occasion of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ME2","text":"The opposition between empirical and a priori knowledge is shifted from chronology to dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ME3","text":"The apparent unity of experience is divided into sensory reception and cognitive contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ME4","text":"The category of a priori knowledge is internally differentiated rather than treated as homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ME5","text":"The question of knowledge becomes a problem of component analysis and origin-tracing rather than mere observation of acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes knowledge beginning with experience from knowledge arising wholly out of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is modeled as an operation that organizes sensory material through comparison, connection, and separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes definitional controls against loose uses of “a priori” and redirects inquiry toward absolute rather than local independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time governs the awakening of cognition, while epistemic dependence governs the classification of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Knowledge is treated as potentially heterogeneous in constitution, with sensory and cognitive elements requiring retrospective separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central distinction between knowledge beginning with experience and knowledge wholly derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to derive the definitions of empirical, a priori, pure, and impure knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s decisive movement from temporal sequence to epistemic dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the specific faculties or principles that supply non-empirical elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of distinction and classification, but its promised investigation of a priori knowledge is not completed within the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on the unresolved methodological possibility of separating cognitive contribution from sensory material after their initial fusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE","layer":"CH","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","order":2,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IHIE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IHIE@R001","sha256":"fe989c6e659dc389f61f4d2e339b97a4d287cb64d7b6b1a9eddd968331cb593a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS1","text":"The argument seeks a secure criterion for distinguishing pure cognition from empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS2","text":"Experience establishes how objects are constituted in observed cases but cannot establish that they could not be otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS3","text":"Necessity and strict, exceptionless universality identify cognition as independent of experience and therefore a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS4","text":"Mathematical judgments and causal judgments exhibit the required necessity and universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS5","text":"The certainty of experience itself presupposes non-empirical rules, preventing empirical cognition from serving as its own ultimate ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AS6","text":"The same a priori origin appears in concepts when empirical predicates are removed but space and substance remain unavoidable in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A1","text":"Experience teaches the actual constitution of an object but not the impossibility of its being otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A2","text":"A proposition that contains necessity in its conception is a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A3","text":"A proposition is absolutely a priori when it is not derived from any proposition lacking necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A4","text":"Empirical universality is only assumed and comparative universality established through induction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A5","text":"Strict universality admits no possible exception and therefore cannot be derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","text":"Necessity and strict universality are inseparably connected and each can serve as an infallible test of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A7","text":"Human cognition contains judgments exhibiting necessity and strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A8","text":"The concept of a cause entails a necessary connection with an effect and a universally valid rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A9","text":"The certainty and possibility of experience presuppose rules that are not themselves empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A10","text":"Removing all sensible predicates from the concept of a body leaves the occupied space unremovable in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:A11","text":"Removing empirical properties from the concept of an object leaves the relations through which it is thought as substance or as adhering to substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX1","text":"Pure cognition is not cognition whose validity is established only by observed regularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX2","text":"Strict universality is not an enlarged form of inductive universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX3","text":"Necessity is not generated by the repeated conjunction of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX4","text":"Causality is not reducible to a subjective habit formed by frequent association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX5","text":"A causal judgment is not merely a report of successive occurrences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX6","text":"The concept of a body is not exhausted by color, hardness, softness, weight, or impenetrability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:NX7","text":"The concept of an object is not exhausted by properties supplied through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D1","text":"Modal necessity distinguishes a priori cognition from cognition that merely records actual regularities.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A1","CH_BA94_IHIE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D2","text":"A proposition possesses absolute a priori status only when its necessity is not inherited from a contingent source.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A2","CH_BA94_IHIE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D3","text":"Induction cannot convert comparative universality into strict universality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A4","CH_BA94_IHIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D4","text":"Either necessity or strict universality can operationally identify a priori cognition, even though the two criteria belong together.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D5","text":"Mathematical propositions and the proposition that every change must have a cause function as instances of a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A7","CH_BA94_IHIE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D6","text":"If causality were derived from frequent association, the necessity of causal connection would become merely subjective and the concept of cause would disappear.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D7","text":"Empirical rules cannot serve as first principles of experience when the certainty of experience depends upon those rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D8","text":"Space has an a priori origin because it remains thinkable after empirical bodily predicates have been removed.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D9","text":"Substance has an a priori origin because its determining relations cannot be removed from the thought of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:D10","text":"Human cognition must possess and exercise a faculty of pure a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A7","CH_BA94_IHIE:A9","CH_BA94_IHIE:A10","CH_BA94_IHIE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD1","text":"The distinction between empirical and pure cognition becomes a distinction between contingent acquisition and necessary conditions of intelligibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A1","CH_BA94_IHIE:A2","CH_BA94_IHIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD2","text":"The modal structure of a judgment, rather than its subject matter alone, determines its epistemic source.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A2","CH_BA94_IHIE:A4","CH_BA94_IHIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3","text":"Experience is rendered epistemically dependent upon a priori rules rather than being treated as the ultimate origin of those rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A7","CH_BA94_IHIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD4","text":"The a priori extends beyond judgments into the conceptual conditions under which objects can be thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A10","CH_BA94_IHIE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD5","text":"The critique of associationism preserves objective causality by locating necessity in cognition rather than in repeated succession.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A8","CH_BA94_IHIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SD6","text":"The removal of empirical predicates operates as a negative method for exposing non-empirical cognitive structures.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A10","CH_BA94_IHIE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX1","text":"Experience cannot be the complete ground of the certainty that experience possesses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX2","text":"Finite observation cannot supply the transition from repeated occurrence to impossibility of exception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX3","text":"Objective necessity cannot be reduced to a psychological state produced by habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX4","text":"The conditions governing experience cannot themselves be treated as contingent contents of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX5","text":"The intelligibility of an object cannot be exhausted by predicates that vary with sensory encounter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX6","text":"No purely empirical account can explain why certain structures remain unavoidable when empirical content is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX1","text":"The system excludes an epistemology in which experience supplies both its contents and the ultimate validity of the rules organizing those contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a continuous passage from frequent regularity to strict necessity by accumulation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a causal order whose necessity exists only as a subjective effect of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX4","text":"The system excludes a conception of objects in which all intelligible structure disappears with sensible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX5","text":"The system establishes the existence of a priori cognition without fully specifying the internal constitution of the faculty that supplies it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS1","text":"Actual observation is separated from modal impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS2","text":"Necessity in conception is used to classify a proposition as a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS3","text":"Inductive regularity is separated from strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS4","text":"Necessity and strict universality converge as reciprocal diagnostics of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS5","text":"Mathematical and causal judgments instantiate the diagnostic criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS6","text":"The Humean derivation of causality is rejected because association yields only subjective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS7","text":"The dependence of experience upon rules produces a transcendental reversal: experience presupposes a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS8","text":"Empirical predicates are progressively removed from concepts, revealing residual structures that cannot be thought away.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS9","text":"Judgmental and conceptual residues converge in the conclusion that human cognition possesses a pure a priori faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AF1","text":"Empirical contingency → demand for a criterion → necessity and strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AF2","text":"Comparative induction → failure to establish exceptionlessness → a priori source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AF3","text":"Mathematical and causal judgments → rejection of subjective associationism → objective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:AF4","text":"Conditions of experience → conceptual subtraction → establishment of a priori cognition in judgments and concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME1","text":"Pure and empirical cognition are reclassified by modal force rather than by subject matter or degree of observational support.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME2","text":"The distinction between “true in most cases” and “valid without exception” becomes structurally decisive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME3","text":"Experience is repositioned from foundational source to conditioned field of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME4","text":"Causality is removed from the domain of psychological association and placed within necessary cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME5","text":"The a priori is extended from propositions to the residual structures of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ME6","text":"Abstraction becomes a diagnostic operation: what survives removal of empirical content marks the non-empirical architecture of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ALT1","text":"Surface: Judgments are classified according to necessity, strict universality, and empirical derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ALT2","text":"Structural: Experience is shown to depend upon non-empirical rules that organize its certainty and intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument shifts the burden of proof from empirical accumulation to the conditions that make experience and judgment possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Reality is divided between contingent occurrence and necessary connection, while space and substance persist as unavoidable forms of objectual thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the empirical–pure distinction to the existence of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the sequence from criterion, through examples and transcendental grounding, to conceptual analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if necessity and strict universality are accepted as marks of non-empirical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT4","text":"The relation between necessity and strict universality remains asserted as inseparable while their separate diagnostic use introduces an unresolved operational tension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT5","text":"The argument does not fully specify how conceptual necessity, judgmental necessity, and the structure of the a priori faculty are related.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT6","text":"The subtraction argument establishes the persistence of space and substance in thought but leaves open whether this persistence alone establishes their full objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction depends on the text’s compressed transition from the certainty of experience to the existence of its a priori conditions, but it preserves the argument’s essential generative logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","original_context":"Chapter 02"},"original_title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN","layer":"CH","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","order":3,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IPSN"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IPSN@R001","sha256":"ddbd5f65b615f1dc6c1a963bf0c0136cf9205fc423710f8f85a9dccdea830716","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS1","text":"Human reason forms cognitions that claim validity beyond the bounds of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS2","text":"The importance of supersensible problems makes reason pursue them even without experiential guidance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS3","text":"Metaphysics becomes dogmatic when it attempts to solve these problems before determining whether reason is capable of doing so.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS4","text":"The argument is diagnostic and transcendental: it shifts attention from metaphysical conclusions to the origin, possibility, extent, validity, and worth of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS5","text":"Mathematical success and analytic concept-resolution generate a false impression that reason can extend knowledge without limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AS6","text":"The argument culminates in distinguishing genuine a priori knowledge from the unrecognized addition of foreign concepts to given concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A1","text":"Cognition can claim validity beyond possible experience through concepts for which no corresponding object is given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A2","text":"Pure reason is unavoidably directed toward the problems of God, freedom, and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A3","text":"Metaphysics traditionally attempts to solve supersensible problems without first investigating reason’s capacity for the task.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","text":"A legitimate science of cognition must determine the source, possibility, extent, validity, and worth of a priori knowledge before constructing conclusions from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A5","text":"Experience provides the secure field of cognitive constraint but cannot directly guide inquiry into the supersensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","text":"Mathematics demonstrates that some cognition can be a priori, certain, and productive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","text":"Mathematical a priori cognition depends upon intuition, including intuition that can itself be given a priori, rather than upon mere conceptual analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A8","text":"The success of mathematics can be misinterpreted as evidence that reason has no limit beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A9","text":"The absence of experiential opposition permits speculative reason to extend its claims without an external check.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","text":"Analysis of already possessed concepts can yield a priori clarification without adding new conceptual content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A11","text":"The formal novelty and practical usefulness of analytic results can make clarification appear to be substantive cognitive expansion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A12","text":"Reason can introduce a priori concepts foreign to given concepts without recognizing how those additions arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:A13","text":"Analytic and synthetic modes of a priori cognition must be distinguished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX1","text":"Metaphysics is not initially grounded in a prior critique of reason’s ability or inability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX2","text":"Supersensible inquiry is not secured by instruction or guidance from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX3","text":"Mathematical a priori cognition is not equivalent to cognition derived from pure concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX4","text":"The analysis of a concept is not an addition of new content to that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX5","text":"The absence of contradiction is not proof of speculative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX6","text":"The expansion of judgment beyond experience is not automatically an expansion of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX7","text":"A speculative construction does not cease to be fictional merely because it is carefully constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:NX8","text":"A completed conceptual edifice is not evidence that its foundation is sound.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D1","text":"The possibility of metaphysics must be investigated before metaphysical problems can be legitimately solved.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A3","CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D2","text":"The primary question concerning supersensible cognition is epistemic rather than doctrinal: how such cognition arises and what validity it possesses.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A1","CH_BA94_IPSN:A3","CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D3","text":"Mathematical success cannot by itself authorize speculative metaphysics because mathematical a priori cognition is mediated by intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D4","text":"The lack of experiential resistance removes the principal check against extending concepts beyond their legitimate domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A5","CH_BA94_IPSN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D5","text":"Analytic clarification can generate the appearance of cognitive progress without increasing conceptual content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D6","text":"Analytic success can conceal the unauthorized insertion of synthetic content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:A11","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D7","text":"The absence of an account of origin makes a priori additions epistemically suspect even when they appear formally coherent.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:D8","text":"Critical inquiry must distinguish the clarification of what is already thought from the production of genuinely new a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12","CH_BA94_IPSN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SD1","text":"The critical project does not abolish supersensible questions; it reverses the order of inquiry by requiring the examination of reason before the construction of answers.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A2","CH_BA94_IPSN:A3","CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","CH_BA94_IPSN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SD2","text":"The same general form of a priori cognition can support genuine science in one domain and speculative illusion in another.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:A8","CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3","text":"The limits of reason are not external restrictions added after cognition; they are conditions for determining whether cognition has validity at all.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","CH_BA94_IPSN:A5","CH_BA94_IPSN:A9","CH_BA94_IPSN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SD4","text":"Speculative overreach is produced by transferring a legitimate local method of cognition into a domain where its enabling conditions are absent.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:A8","CH_BA94_IPSN:A9","CH_BA94_IPSN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SD5","text":"The text establishes the necessity of critical investigation without yet supplying the positive criterion by which every valid synthetic a priori claim will be identified.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12","CH_BA94_IPSN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX1","text":"The existence of a priori cognition does not entail unrestricted metaphysical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX2","text":"The certainty of mathematics does not function as a universal warrant for every claim made independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX3","text":"Formal clarification does not constitute material enlargement of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX4","text":"The absence of empirical refutation does not establish validity in a domain beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX5","text":"Escape from experiential limits does not amount to access to supersensible truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX6","text":"The successful completion of a system does not retroactively establish its foundations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX7","text":"Reason cannot treat the origin of its own additions as irrelevant to their validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX1","text":"No unrestricted bridge connects conceptual possibility with the existence or knowability of a corresponding supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX2","text":"No self-authenticating form of pure reason guarantees that its extensions beyond experience are knowledge rather than fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX3","text":"No transfer principle permits mathematical a priori validity to pass automatically into metaphysical a priori validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX4","text":"No experiential tribunal is available to settle supersensible claims directly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX5","text":"No completed edifice can supply the missing investigation of the conditions under which its cognitions are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS1","text":"Supersensible problems arise as unavoidable and elevated aims of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS2","text":"The absence of corresponding objects in experience drives reason beyond the secure ground of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS3","text":"The importance of these problems produces dogmatic metaphysics before reason’s capacity has been examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS4","text":"The demonstrated productivity of mathematical a priori cognition supplies a misleading model for unrestricted reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS5","text":"The obscured role of intuition and the absence of experiential resistance permit conceptual extension beyond its legitimate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS6","text":"Analytic clarification produces apparent novelty without added content, thereby concealing the insertion of foreign synthetic concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS7","text":"The distinction between analytic and synthetic a priori cognition initiates the required critical science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AF1","text":"Unavoidable supersensible problems → dogmatic metaphysical construction → foundation problem → mathematical analogy → overextension beyond experience → analytic illusion → unrecognized synthetic addition → distinction of a priori modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:AF2","text":"The governing question shifts from what reason can construct to how a priori cognition arises and what extent, validity, and worth it possesses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME1","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from a doctrine-producing enterprise into an object of prior epistemic investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME2","text":"A priori cognition is divided into different operative types rather than treated as a single undifferentiated capacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME3","text":"Mathematical success is reclassified as condition-dependent evidence rather than proof of unrestricted rational reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME4","text":"The lack of contradiction loses its status as an adequate test of supersensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME5","text":"Apparent conceptual expansion becomes diagnostically separable into clarification, genuine addition, and unauthorized importation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME6","text":"The temporal order of inquiry is reversed: foundation must precede the construction of the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ME7","text":"The boundary of possible experience becomes a methodological criterion for evaluating claims rather than merely a limit to be overcome.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text concerns reason’s pursuit of God, freedom, immortality, and other supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text analyzes how experience, intuition, concepts, and resistance condition the validity of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces premature doctrinal construction with a prior investigation of reason’s powers, limits, and procedures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Possible experience functions as the boundary of object-directed cognition, while the order of foundation before edifice becomes the governing temporal model of legitimate inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from supersensible ambition through dogmatic metaphysics to the demand for critical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive conditions that would validate synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent because mathematical success, analytic clarification, and speculative overreach are treated as distinct but causally connected phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT4","text":"The strongest component is the diagnosis of how legitimate a priori cognition can become the model for illegitimate extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT5","text":"Reconstruction depends on retaining the distinction between intuition and concept, and between formal clarification and material addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT6","text":"The central unresolved tension is that supersensible problems are presented as unavoidable while experience cannot adjudicate them directly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement from the demand for metaphysical knowledge to the prior demand for a science of the conditions and limits of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","original_context":"Chapter 03"},"original_title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","order":4,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IDBA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IDBA@R001","sha256":"18e25d8bd8cacfe77b28fbd007a33e579be19a6177724a6c9af3e52d05c7fd77","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS1","text":"The argument distinguishes judgments according to whether the predicate is contained in the subject-conception or stands outside it while remaining connected to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS2","text":"Analytical judgments explicate what is already contained in a conception through identity, whereas synthetical judgments augment the conception through a non-identical predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS3","text":"Analytical judgments require no appeal to experience because their predicates can be extracted from concepts through analysis and the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS4","text":"Judgments of experience are synthetical because experience connects separately conceived representations within a larger whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS5","text":"Experience can ground the possibility of empirical synthesis but cannot ground the universality and necessity claimed by synthetic a priori judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS6","text":"The decisive problem is therefore the unknown ground that makes a non-contained predicate necessarily connectable with a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AS7","text":"The argument subordinates analysis to synthesis by treating conceptual clarification as preparation for the genuine acquisition of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A1","text":"A judgment cogitates a relation between a subject-conception and a predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A2","text":"A predicate relates to a subject either by being contained in its conception or by standing outside it while remaining connected to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","text":"An analytical judgment is governed by identity between the predicate and the subject-conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A4","text":"An analytical judgment explicates constituent content already thought in the subject-conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A5","text":"Analysis can disclose an analytical predicate through conceptual clarification and the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A6","text":"A synthetical judgment connects a predicate with a subject without the predicate being contained in the subject-conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A7","text":"A synthetical judgment augments knowledge by adding content unavailable through analysis of the subject-conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A8","text":"Judgments of experience are synthetical because their predicates are connected with their subjects through experience rather than conceptual identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A9","text":"Empirical synthesis establishes contingent connection but cannot establish universal and necessary connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A10","text":"A synthetic a priori judgment connects a non-contained predicate with a conception universally and necessarily without relying on experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A11","text":"The ground of synthetic a priori connection is an unknown condition upon which the understanding relies when it joins a foreign predicate to a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:A12","text":"Analytical judgments are preparatory to the clearer and more extended synthesis on which speculative a priori knowledge depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX1","text":"Analytical judgment is not founded on empirical testimony.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX2","text":"Analytical judgment does not extend the subject-conception by adding new content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX3","text":"A synthetical predicate is not recoverable through analysis of the subject-conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX4","text":"The connection in a synthetical judgment is not identity between predicate and subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX5","text":"Experience does not provide the universality or necessity required by synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX6","text":"The empirical connection of two concepts does not by itself establish their necessary connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX7","text":"The concept of a cause is not contained in the mere concept of something that happens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:NX8","text":"Synthetic a priori knowledge cannot appeal to experience as its sufficient foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D1","text":"Analytical judgments can be established without consulting experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A4","CH_BA94_IDBA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D2","text":"Analytical judgments possess a priori necessity because their truth is disclosed within the subject-conception rather than learned from contingent occurrences.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","CH_BA94_IDBA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D3","text":"Empirical knowledge necessarily involves an addition to concepts that analysis alone cannot supply.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A6","CH_BA94_IDBA:A7","CH_BA94_IDBA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D4","text":"Experience can join independently conceived representations as parts of one experiential totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A8","CH_BA94_IDBA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D5","text":"The distinction between analytical and synthetical judgment is independent of the distinction between a priori and empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","CH_BA94_IDBA:A6","CH_BA94_IDBA:A8","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D6","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori judgment cannot be explained by either conceptual containment or empirical association alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A5","CH_BA94_IDBA:A9","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D7","text":"The proposition that everything that happens has a cause functions as a test case for a predicate that is non-contained yet claimed to be necessarily connected.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A6","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10","CH_BA94_IDBA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:D8","text":"The clarification of concepts is a necessary condition for reliable synthesis but is not itself the principal acquisition of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A5","CH_BA94_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD1","text":"The argument transforms a classificatory distinction between judgments into a foundational problem concerning the possibility of necessary knowledge that extends concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A2","CH_BA94_IDBA:A7","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD2","text":"The system assigns different epistemic functions to analysis and synthesis: analysis secures intelligibility, while synthesis produces augmentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A4","CH_BA94_IDBA:A7","CH_BA94_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD3","text":"Experience occupies an intermediate epistemic position: it is sufficient for contingent synthesis but structurally insufficient for necessary synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A8","CH_BA94_IDBA:A9","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD4","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment requires a mode of connection that is neither conceptual identity nor empirical conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","CH_BA94_IDBA:A8","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD5","text":"The entire project of speculative a priori knowledge depends on explaining how augmentation can acquire necessity without becoming reducible to analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A7","CH_BA94_IDBA:A9","CH_BA94_IDBA:A10","CH_BA94_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SD6","text":"The passage establishes the problem of synthetic a priori cognition without yet determining the nature of its unknown ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A10","CH_BA94_IDBA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX1","text":"Necessary knowledge cannot be reduced to repeated empirical conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX2","text":"Augmentative knowledge cannot be reduced to the explication of concepts already possessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX3","text":"The relation between subject and predicate cannot be exhaustively modeled either as containment or as contingent association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX4","text":"The source of universal necessity cannot be located in the testimony of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX5","text":"The possibility of speculative knowledge cannot be secured by conceptual analysis alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX6","text":"A non-contained predicate cannot become necessary merely by being repeatedly found alongside the subject in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:DNX7","text":"The distinction between analysis and synthesis cannot be collapsed into the distinction between verbal clarification and factual observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX1","text":"The ground that makes synthetic a priori connection possible remains unexhibited within the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX2","text":"Neither the subject-conception nor experience, taken alone, contains the principle of necessary augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX3","text":"The system excludes any account in which all genuine knowledge is either conceptual repetition or empirical accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX4","text":"The possibility of necessary synthesis is treated as indispensable but not yet positively formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX5","text":"The argument’s force depends on the absence of an available foundation for connecting foreign predicates with concepts universally and necessarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS1","text":"Subject–predicate relation → classification by containment versus external connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS2","text":"Containment → identity → analysis → explication → a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS3","text":"External connection → non-identity → synthesis → augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS4","text":"Synthesis grounded in experience → contingent connection within an experiential whole → empirical judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS5","text":"Non-contained predicate claimed with universality and necessity → exclusion of experience as sufficient ground → synthetic a priori problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS6","text":"Synthetic a priori problem → search for an unknown condition of necessary augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:FGS7","text":"Conceptual analysis → clarification of conditions for judgment → preparation for extended synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF1","text":"A subject–predicate relation is divided into conceptual containment and non-identical connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF2","text":"Containment yields analytical, explicative, and a priori judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF3","text":"Non-identical connection yields synthetical and augmentative judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF4","text":"When non-identical connection is supplied by experience, it yields contingent empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF5","text":"When non-identical connection is asserted universally and necessarily without experience, it produces the synthetic a priori problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF6","text":"The synthetic a priori problem redirects the argument from classification toward the unknown ground of speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:AF7","text":"The argument closes by assigning analysis a preparatory function within the larger task of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME1","text":"Judgment is reclassified according to the mode of connection between subject and predicate rather than according to subject matter alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME2","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition is separated from the opposition between analytical and synthetical judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME3","text":"Experience is reclassified as a source of contingent synthesis rather than a source of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME4","text":"The distinction between analysis and synthesis becomes a theory of epistemic functions: clarification versus augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME5","text":"Synthetic a priori propositions become the central problem of speculative knowledge rather than a marginal hybrid category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ME6","text":"The apparent opposition between conceptual reasoning and experience is displaced by a threefold structure of analysis, empirical synthesis, and synthetic a priori synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ALT1","text":"Surface — Judgments differ according to whether the predicate is contained in or external to the subject-conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Analysis operates through conceptual identity and contradiction, while synthesis operates through the connection of distinct representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument shifts inquiry from determining judgment-types to locating the ground that authorizes necessary expansion of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Experience organizes representations as parts of a whole, while synthetic a priori cognition claims necessity concerning relations such as cause and effect beyond mere temporal succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between analytical and synthetical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT2","text":"The structure is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from conceptual taxonomy to the problem of synthetic a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined because the unknown ground of synthetic a priori connection is identified but not explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT4","text":"The central tension is that analysis supplies necessity without augmentation, while experience supplies augmentation without necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT5","text":"The passage is internally coherent as a problem-setting argument but incomplete as an account of the solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text because the final priority of synthesis is generated from the limits of both analysis and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS","layer":"CH","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","order":5,"parents":["RAW_BA94_VATS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_VATS@R001","sha256":"09905a947a5c1493234bac4e187984844a2b110e36eaa9ed80c044dedaf7d799","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS1","text":"The text contests the inference from mathematics’ conformity to contradiction to the analyticity of its principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS2","text":"It distinguishes logical discernibility of a conclusion from the synthetic ground required to generate that conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS3","text":"It establishes mathematics as a priori yet synthetic by showing that conceptual analysis must be supplemented by intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS4","text":"It extends the model from mathematics to physics, whose necessary principles add predicates not contained in their concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS5","text":"It defines metaphysics by its necessary attempt to enlarge a priori knowledge beyond analytic clarification and empirical limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AS6","text":"The argument moves from a diagnostic correction of analytic misclassification to a general architecture of theoretical science grounded in synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A1","text":"Necessity cannot be supplied by experience and therefore marks a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A2","text":"An analytical judgement contains its predicate in the concept of its subject and is governed by the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A3","text":"A synthetical judgement adds to a concept a predicate not thought within that concept itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A4","text":"Conformity with the principle of contradiction does not prove that a judgement is analytical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A5","text":"Mathematical cognition requires intuition to determine what conceptual combination alone does not contain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A6","text":"Proper mathematical propositions are necessary, non-empirical, and synthetical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A7","text":"Principles of pure physics necessarily add determinations not contained in the concepts to which they apply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A8","text":"Metaphysics necessarily seeks an enlargement of a priori cognition beyond the analytic exposition of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:A9","text":"A science whose proper aim is a priori enlargement consists, as such, in synthetical judgements a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX1","text":"Mathematical propositions are not analytical deductions from definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX2","text":"The principle of contradiction is not a productive source of synthetic mathematical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX3","text":"Necessity is not an empirical acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX4","text":"The numerical result of a sum is not contained in the mere concept of combining its addends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX5","text":"The quantitative determination of a geometrical relation is not contained in the merely qualitative concept of straightness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX6","text":"Metaphysics is not exhausted by analytical dissection of a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:NX7","text":"Analytically valid mathematical propositions are not the generative principles of mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:D1","text":"Apodeictic certainty can depend on a synthetic ground even when its conclusions are tested or ordered by contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A2","CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:D2","text":"Arithmetic requires an intuitive construction to determine the sum that is not recoverable by analysing the concepts of the addends.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:D3","text":"Geometry requires intuition because its necessary quantitative predicates exceed the qualitative content of its elementary concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:D4","text":"Conservation and reciprocal-action principles in physics are synthetic because they determine matter and motion beyond their given concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A1","CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:D5","text":"Metaphysics can count as a distinctive science only insofar as it supplies principles that extend rather than merely clarify a priori concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A8","CH_BA94_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SD1","text":"The division between analytic and synthetic judgement reorganizes theoretical science by separating logical legitimacy from cognitive production.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A2","CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A4","CH_BA94_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SD2","text":"Theoretical science is constituted not by deduction from concepts alone but by necessary additions to concepts under non-empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A1","CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:A7","CH_BA94_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SD3","text":"Metaphysics is structurally compelled by reason’s demand for a priori extension, even before its particular claims are validated.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A1","CH_BA94_VATS:A8","CH_BA94_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SD4","text":"The same formal category, synthetic a priori judgement, is made to underwrite mathematical construction, physical law, and metaphysical ambition despite their differing objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:A6","CH_BA94_VATS:A7","CH_BA94_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the model in which logical non-contradiction alone can generate substantive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the model in which all cognition extending a concept must derive from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a strict equivalence between a priori cognition and analytic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the reduction of science to either empirical generalization or conceptual tautology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the confinement of metaphysics to conceptual clarification if metaphysics is to retain its proper aim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the denial that reason can be both necessarily truth-bearing and merely formal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses the empiricist restriction under which necessity is unavailable before experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SNX3","text":"The argument presupposes that a non-empirical source of synthetic determination is available, while leaving its unified account outside the present demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:SNX4","text":"The argument cannot admit that every claim beyond experience is thereby cognitively void, because metaphysics is defined through such extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS1","text":"Necessary cognition → exclusion of empirical origin → classification as a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS2","text":"Predicate not contained in subject-concept → classification as synthetic → insufficiency of conceptual analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS3","text":"Mathematical insufficiency of concepts → recourse to intuition → construction of necessary synthetic judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS4","text":"Physical law exceeds object-concept → necessary synthetic principle → pure natural science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS5","text":"Reason seeks a priori enlargement beyond analysis and experience → metaphysical principles must be synthetic a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:FGS6","text":"Synthetic a priori principle across distinct sciences → theoretical science is reclassified by its mode of grounding rather than by its object-domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AF1","text":"Mathematical apodeicticity → mistaken identification with analyticity → distinction between logical test and cognitive ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AF2","text":"Conceptual insufficiency in arithmetic and geometry → intuition → mathematical synthesis a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AF3","text":"Necessary physical laws → predicates exceeding empirical object-concepts → physical synthesis a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AF4","text":"Metaphysical demand for a priori enlargement → rejection of merely analytic metaphysics → metaphysics as synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:AF5","text":"Mathematics, physics, and metaphysics → shared synthetic a priori principle-form → unified account of theoretical sciences of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ME1","text":"Logical contradiction is relocated from the role of knowledge-generator to the role of criterion operative upon already grounded cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ME2","text":"Mathematics is reclassified from conceptual analysis to intuitive construction under conditions of a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ME3","text":"Physics is reclassified from empirical description to a discipline containing necessary principles irreducible to empirical observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ME4","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from analysis of inherited concepts to the attempted extension of a priori cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ME5","text":"The analytic/synthetic distinction cuts across disciplinary objects and becomes the principal map of theoretical legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Arithmetic, geometry, physical laws, and metaphysical propositions are classified according to whether their predicates are conceptually contained or added.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ALT2","text":"Structural — The possibility of theoretical science depends on distinguishing conceptual containment, intuitive supplementation, empirical origin, and a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text displaces the question from whether a proposition is logically consistent to what ground permits necessary cognitive extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Epistemic — Objects of mathematics, nature, and metaphysics become knowable in science only insofar as they can be determined through synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:ALT5","text":"Temporal — Metaphysical claims concerning the world’s beginning exemplify reason’s movement beyond experiential conditions toward a priori determination of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central claim that theoretical sciences contain synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s decisive distinction between contradiction as a test of judgement and synthesis as a source of cognitive extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT3","text":"The mathematical demonstration is structurally stronger than the physical and metaphysical extensions because it explicitly exhibits the need for intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT4","text":"The account of physics asserts but does not derive the conditions under which its synthetic a priori principles are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT5","text":"The account of metaphysics establishes its required form from its aim but does not establish the validity of particular metaphysical syntheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether one account of intuition and synthesis can ground mathematics, physics, and metaphysics alike.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VATS:MRT7","text":"The principal structural tension is that metaphysics must exceed experience to fulfill its aim while the demonstrated model of synthesis is supplied through mathematical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP","layer":"CH","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","order":6,"parents":["RAW_BA94_VUPP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_VUPP@R001","sha256":"dbcb01475e4f56788e5cf9709744b3804a162990ae3cb7f8f387cd878308f2d0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS1","text":"The decisive problem of pure reason is whether synthetic judgments a priori are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS2","text":"The scientific standing of metaphysics depends on resolving this problem or proving its impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS3","text":"Hume’s causal scepticism is diagnostically important but fails because it treats one synthetic a priori claim as the whole problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science are existing instances whose possibility requires an account of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS5","text":"Metaphysics exists naturally as an inextinguishable rational disposition but remains unestablished as a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS6","text":"Recurrent contradiction requires reason to examine its own competence before extending itself beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AS7","text":"Critique converts metaphysics from dogmatic construction into a decision concerning the legitimate scope and limits of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A1","text":"Metaphysics can be a science only if synthetic judgments a priori are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A2","text":"Analytic judgment explicates what is already contained in concepts and does not extend a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A3","text":"Mathematics necessarily contains synthetic judgments a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A4","text":"Pure natural science necessarily contains synthetic judgments a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A5","text":"The actual existence of mathematics and pure natural science establishes that their possibility must be explainable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A6","text":"Human reason naturally generates questions that empirical reasoning cannot answer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A7","text":"The natural generation of metaphysical questions produces some metaphysical system in every exercise of speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","text":"Previous attempts to answer metaphysical questions have generated unavoidable contradictions within reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A9","text":"A science of metaphysics requires a determinate judgment concerning reason’s ability or inability to know its objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A10","text":"Determining reason’s competence requires determining both the extent and the limits of its a priori use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A11","text":"Critique concerns Reason and its self-generated problems rather than an inexhaustible plurality of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A12","text":"Uncritical dogmatic use of reason generates groundless assertions and culminates in scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:A13","text":"The natural root of metaphysics in human reason persists even when particular metaphysical systems fail.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX1","text":"Metaphysics is not established as a science merely because metaphysical systems have historically been produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX2","text":"The universal problem of pure reason is not reducible to the causal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX3","text":"A priori necessity is not adequately explained as experiential habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX4","text":"The natural disposition toward metaphysics is not proof that metaphysical knowledge is valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX5","text":"Metaphysical questions are not answerable by empirical applications of reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX6","text":"Analysis of concepts is not metaphysics proper because it cannot explain the a priori origin or valid extension of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX7","text":"Dogmatic construction without critique is not a route to metaphysical science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX8","text":"Contradiction among metaphysical claims is not a basis for confident doctrinal extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX9","text":"The critique of reason is not primarily an investigation of a manifold of objects beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:NX10","text":"Limiting pure reason is not equivalent to abandoning the rational demand for metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D1","text":"The possibility of mathematics and pure natural science becomes evidentially prior to the possibility of metaphysics as a science.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A3","CH_BA94_VUPP:A4","CH_BA94_VUPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D2","text":"Hume’s denial of synthetic a priori causality cannot be universalized without also undermining pure mathematics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A3","CH_BA94_VUPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D3","text":"Metaphysics has a double status: it is unavoidable as a rational disposition and unverified as a science.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A6","CH_BA94_VUPP:A7","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D4","text":"The persistence of metaphysical questioning does not license any answer to those questions.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A6","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D5","text":"Contradiction changes the philosophical task from constructing metaphysical doctrines to adjudicating the powers of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9","CH_BA94_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D6","text":"The legitimate extension of a priori cognition is conditional upon a prior determination of its validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A10","CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:D7","text":"Earlier dogmatic metaphysical systems count as failures of metaphysics proper, even where they contain useful conceptual analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A2","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SD1","text":"The argument differentiates three modes of metaphysical existence: actual science, natural rational disposition, and dogmatic historical system.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A5","CH_BA94_VUPP:A7","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9","CH_BA94_VUPP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SD2","text":"Critical limitation is the enabling condition of valid a priori extension rather than a merely negative restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9","CH_BA94_VUPP:A10","CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SD3","text":"Reason must become both the investigator and the object of investigation because the source of metaphysical problems lies within reason itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A6","CH_BA94_VUPP:A7","CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SD4","text":"Historical metaphysical failure functions as methodological evidence against dogmatism without functioning as evidence against the irreducible rational need for metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:A12","CH_BA94_VUPP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SD5","text":"The critique must account simultaneously for the reality of successful a priori sciences and the failure of metaphysics to attain comparable scientific status.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A3","CH_BA94_VUPP:A4","CH_BA94_VUPP:A5","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX1","text":"No domain of alleged objects beyond experience can independently authorize pure metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX2","text":"No question is answerable merely because reason necessarily poses it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX3","text":"No conceptual decomposition can establish the a priori genesis or objective validity of synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX4","text":"No unrestricted use of pure reason can claim legitimacy before reason determines its own competence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX5","text":"No historical accumulation of metaphysical systems can substitute for a transcendental account of their possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX6","text":"No escape from metaphysics is available through scepticism because the source of metaphysical questioning is internal to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an uncriticized transition from the natural fact of rational questioning to the scientific validity of metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX2","text":"The system excludes both final dogmatic possession of metaphysical objects and sceptical cancellation of reason’s metaphysical demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX3","text":"The absent object organizing the argument is a metaphysics that could be scientific without first becoming a critique of its own source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX4","text":"The system denies that reason can secure its transcendence of experience by spontaneous assertion while preserving rational necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS1","text":"Existing synthetic a priori sciences plus unavoidable metaphysical questioning generate the universal problem of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS2","text":"The universal problem separates actual scientific existence from mere natural rational disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS3","text":"The distinction between analytic explication and synthetic extension excludes conceptual analysis as a sufficient foundation for metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS4","text":"Recurrent contradiction converts doctrinal production into a problem of rational jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS5","text":"Rational jurisdiction requires critique of reason’s powers in relation to experience and beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6","text":"Critique yields the alternative of warranted extension or strictly delimited restraint, thereby making metaphysics as science conditionally thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AF1","text":"Vacillating metaphysics → universalization of the synthetic a priori problem → mathematics and pure natural science as proof cases → metaphysics as natural disposition → contradiction in dogmatic metaphysics → critique of rational competence → determination of legitimate extension and limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:AF2","text":"Hume’s partial sceptical diagnosis → failure of its universal implications → necessity of a more comprehensive critique of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ME1","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from a body of disputed doctrines into a problem concerning the conditions of valid a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ME2","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science are reclassified from unquestioned bodies of knowledge into evidence requiring transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ME3","text":"The opposition between extension and limitation collapses because valid extension depends on prior limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ME4","text":"Scepticism is reclassified from a final philosophical position into the terminal effect of failed dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ME5","text":"Reason is reclassified from a faculty that merely applies principles to objects into a self-problematizing source of its own questions and standards.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit inquiry asks how synthetic judgments a priori, mathematics, pure natural science, and metaphysics are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument distinguishes analytic containment from synthetic extension and distinguishes actual science from natural rational propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces dogmatic construction of metaphysical claims with critique of the authority entitled to make them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reason is presented as an enduring self-generative power whose metaphysical demand persists across the historical collapse of particular systems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from the universal problem of synthetic a priori judgment to the necessity of critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive conditions that make synthetic judgments a priori possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as it distinguishes the inevitability of metaphysical questioning from the legitimacy of metaphysical knowing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where the actual existence of mathematics and pure natural science blocks a universal sceptical denial of a priori synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the compressed inference that actual scientific existence guarantees the availability of a satisfactory account of its possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT6","text":"The system leaves a reflexive tension because reason must determine the limits of reason through an inquiry conducted by reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement from doctrinal failure through self-critique toward conditional scientific legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","original_context":"Chapter 06"},"original_title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP","layer":"CH","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","order":7,"parents":["RAW_BA94_VIDP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_VIDP@R001","sha256":"206455f0bbb83963343949b5dc18c0bc748ae69d85d7ae67dad6479482f33db6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine whether and how knowledge claimed as absolutely a priori can be validly extended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS2","text":"Pure reason is reframed as a faculty whose a priori principles require critical examination before they can ground a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS3","text":"A Critique of Pure Reason is constituted as a preliminary investigation of reason’s sources, scope, and limits rather than as a completed doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS4","text":"The argument moves from the uncertain possibility of an organon to the necessary establishment of a negative touchstone for judging a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS5","text":"Transcendental inquiry shifts attention from objects to the a priori conditions under which objects are given and thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AS6","text":"The projected science is divided according to the faculties that give objects and think them, because conditions of givenness precede conditions of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A1","text":"Reason supplies principles of knowledge a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A2","text":"Pure reason contains principles for cognizing objects absolutely a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A3","text":"An organon of pure reason would contain the principles sufficient for producing all pure a priori cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","text":"The possibility and legitimate scope of an organon cannot be assumed before reason’s capacity for a priori cognition is critically assessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A5","text":"A critique of pure reason investigates the sources and limits of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A6","text":"Transcendental knowledge concerns the a priori mode of cognition of objects rather than objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A7","text":"The critical task is to assess synthetic a priori cognition, while analysis is pursued only as required for that assessment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A8","text":"Transcendental philosophy is the architectonic system of all principles of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A9","text":"A complete transcendental philosophy requires a complete analysis of human a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A10","text":"Transcendental philosophy admits only completely pure a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A11","text":"Practical cognition involving motives depends on feeling and therefore on empirical sources of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A12","text":"Human knowledge arises from sensibility, through which objects are given, and understanding, through which objects are thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A13","text":"A priori conditions of sensibility are conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A14","text":"Conditions under which objects are given must precede conditions under which they are thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:A15","text":"The a priori cognitive capacities of the mind form a bounded investigable field even if the nature of external objects is indefinitely extensive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX1","text":"Critique is not a doctrine that directly enlarges knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX2","text":"Critique is not an organon that already supplies procedures for generating all pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX3","text":"Transcendental inquiry is not inquiry into the empirical nature of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX4","text":"The Critique of Pure Reason is not identical with completed transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX5","text":"The critical examination of reason is not a critique of philosophical books, systems, or their historical authors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX6","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not a mixed field containing empirical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX7","text":"Pure morality is not included within transcendental philosophy insofar as its construction necessarily incorporates empirical motives and feelings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX8","text":"The critique does not require a complete analysis and deduction of every derived a priori concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:NX9","text":"Sensibility is not reducible to understanding, because giving an object differs from thinking one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D1","text":"Critical inquiry must precede any legitimate claim to possess a system capable of extending pure a priori knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A3","CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D2","text":"The immediate use of critique is negative because it purifies reason from error before it authorizes doctrinal expansion.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D3","text":"Critique functions as a universal touchstone for the worth or worthlessness of claims to a priori knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A5","CH_BA94_VIDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D4","text":"A future canon remains possible even if an organon proves unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A3","CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D5","text":"The Critique can claim completeness only at the level of the radical concepts required to judge synthesis a priori.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A7","CH_BA94_VIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D6","text":"Moral principles may be a priori without belonging to transcendental philosophy.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A10","CH_BA94_VIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:D7","text":"The Doctrine of Elements must begin with transcendental sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A12","CH_BA94_VIDP:A13","CH_BA94_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SD1","text":"The argument converts uncertainty about metaphysical knowledge into a bounded jurisdictional inquiry over the faculties that make such knowledge possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A5","CH_BA94_VIDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SD2","text":"The possibility of a positive system is made conditional upon a prior negative delimitation of illegitimate cognitive claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A3","CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A5","CH_BA94_VIDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SD3","text":"The field of philosophy is redrawn by separating the purity of a principle from the empirical conditions required for its practical operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A10","CH_BA94_VIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SD4","text":"The architectonic order of the science follows the dependency order of cognition rather than the order in which objects appear to consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A6","CH_BA94_VIDP:A12","CH_BA94_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SD5","text":"The finitude of the subject’s a priori cognitive field makes systematic completion conceivable without requiring exhaustive knowledge of external reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A8","CH_BA94_VIDP:A9","CH_BA94_VIDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX1","text":"Validity cannot be secured by inherited metaphysical systems, historical authority, or the mutual correction of unsupported assertions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX2","text":"The form of cognition cannot be treated as transparent merely because cognition is exercised.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX3","text":"No claim to universality belongs to cognition whose constitutive concepts require empirical material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX4","text":"The systematic unity of reason cannot be presupposed as accomplished before its elementary sources and limits are discriminated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX5","text":"Objectivity cannot be analyzed solely through objects, because object-givenness itself depends on subjective a priori conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX1","text":"The text excludes immediate access to an unrestricted absolute knowledge generated by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX2","text":"The common root of sensibility and understanding is treated as unavailable to the inquiry, although their coordinated operation is required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX3","text":"The completed system of pure reason is held open as a regulative possibility while denied as a present possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX4","text":"The ultimate ground of the faculties is not supplied, so the science begins from functional division rather than from a demonstrated first origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS1","text":"Claim to pure a priori cognition → uncertainty concerning its legitimate extension → necessity of critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS2","text":"Critique of sources and limits → negative purification from error → touchstone for the validity of a priori claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS3","text":"Transcendental redirection from objects to cognitive conditions → isolation of pure a priori elements → determination of the field of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS4","text":"Requirement of systematic completeness → restriction of present analysis to conditions necessary for judging synthetic a priori cognition → critique as incomplete idea of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS5","text":"Purity requirement → exclusion of empirical practical motives → restriction to speculative pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS6","text":"Sensibility gives objects and understanding thinks them → givenness precedes thought → transcendental doctrine of sensibility becomes the first element.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:AF1","text":"Uncertain a priori extension → critique of reason’s sources and limits → negative touchstone of validity → transcendental relocation to conditions of cognition → restriction to pure speculative principles → architectonic division into elements and method → priority of sensibility over understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME1","text":"Philosophical evaluation is relocated from comparison among doctrines to examination of the faculty that makes doctrinal claims possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME2","text":"The distinction between critique and doctrine separates the validation of cognitive authority from the production of new cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME3","text":"The distinction between an organon and a canon separates unrestricted constructive power from rule-governed legitimate use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME4","text":"The distinction between pure a priori principle and empirically conditioned practical application removes morality from the transcendental field without denying its a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME5","text":"The distinction between givenness and thought establishes a non-interchangeable division of cognitive labor between sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ME6","text":"The mind’s a priori constitution becomes a finite field of inquiry, while the empirical infinity of external nature ceases to determine the scope of the critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines the Critique of Pure Reason as an investigation into the validity, sources, and limits of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text orders cognition through the distinct functions of sensibility, which gives objects, and understanding, which thinks them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text requires philosophy to establish a criterion of judgment before it authorizes systems, histories, or doctrinal constructions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The argument locates systematic possibility in the bounded structure of the cognizing mind while deferring completed transcendental philosophy to a future achievement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the central transition from the doubtful organon to the necessary critique because they preserve the distinction between productive doctrine and limiting examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive inventory of radical concepts, because the text asserts their required completeness without supplying them here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent insofar as the priority of critique follows from uncertainty about the scope of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT4","text":"The system depends on a tension between calling the critique complete as an idea and withholding the complete analysis required for transcendental philosophy as a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT5","text":"The exclusion of morality depends on a further tension between its a priori principles and its necessary relation to empirical motives in practical construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT6","text":"The architecture relies on an unproved coordination of sensibility and understanding, whose common root is admitted but declared unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if negative delimitation is retained as the condition for every later positive system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 07","original_title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","original_context":"Chapter 07"},"original_title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT","layer":"CH","title":"§ I. Introductory.","order":8,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IINT"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IINT@R001","sha256":"52f66dc74736ab781c5ccae04d053cb3e41d394e2fd3ba7ab5642651da285e8f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS1","text":"Human knowledge immediately relates to objects through intuition, not through thought alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS2","text":"Intuition requires an object to be given through the mind’s receptivity to affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS3","text":"Sensibility supplies intuitions, while understanding supplies concepts through which intuitions are thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS4","text":"Empirical cognition is divided into sensation as matter and form as the a priori ordering of phenomenal content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS5","text":"A transcendental reduction removes conceptual determinations and sensation in order to isolate the pure form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AS6","text":"The investigation thereby establishes transcendental æsthetic as the science of a priori sensibility and prepares the identification of space and time as its pure forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A1","text":"Knowledge immediately relates to objects through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A2","text":"Human intuition occurs only when an object is given to the mind through affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A3","text":"Sensibility is the receptive capacity through which objects are given and intuitions arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A4","text":"Understanding thinks what sensibility gives and produces conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A5","text":"Sensation is the effect of an object upon the faculty of representation insofar as the mind is affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A6","text":"An intuition related to an object through sensation is an empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A7","text":"The matter of a phenomenon is what corresponds to sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A8","text":"The form of a phenomenon is what enables its content to be arranged under relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A9","text":"Phenomenal matter is given a posteriori, while phenomenal form lies a priori in the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A10","text":"Pure intuition is the a priori form of sensibility in which phenomenal multiplicity is arranged, independently of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A11","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is the science of the a priori principles of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:A12","text":"Empirical criteria of taste cannot function as determinate a priori laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX1","text":"Knowledge does not immediately relate to objects through understanding or conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX2","text":"Intuition is not possible without the givenness of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX3","text":"Sensibility is not understanding, and receptivity is not conceptual thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX4","text":"The form of a phenomenon is not itself sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX5","text":"Pure intuition is not empirical content, sensory quality, or conceptual determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX6","text":"Phenomenal form is not received a posteriori in the same manner as phenomenal matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX7","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is not a science of taste or the beautiful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:NX8","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is not transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D1","text":"Human cognition cannot obtain an object through conceptual thought without an ultimate relation to intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A1","CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D2","text":"An empirical phenomenon is constituted by sensory matter organized under form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:A6","CH_BA94_IINT:A7","CH_BA94_IINT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D3","text":"The relational organization of phenomenal content cannot itself be identified with sensory content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A7","CH_BA94_IINT:A8","CH_BA94_IINT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D4","text":"The a priori form of sensibility is isolated by subtracting conceptual determinations and then sensory content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D5","text":"Pure intuition is the residual structure through which phenomenal multiplicity can be arranged and viewed.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D6","text":"Sensibility is epistemically prior to understanding because understanding requires what sensibility gives.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D7","text":"Extension and shape remain after conceptual and sensory predicates are removed from the representation of a body.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D8","text":"The reduction of sensibility yields space and time as the two pure forms of sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10","CH_BA94_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:D9","text":"The term æsthetic is redirected from the critique of taste to the science of a priori laws of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A11","CH_BA94_IINT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD1","text":"Human object-cognition is generated through a layered sequence in which affection produces sensation, sensation supplies empirical intuition, pure form orders the intuition, and understanding conceptualizes it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A1","CH_BA94_IINT:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4","CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:A6","CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD2","text":"Sensibility is not merely receptive material but a priori structured receptivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD3","text":"The object is cognitively available only through conditions that belong partly to the object’s affection and partly to the mind’s form of reception.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD4","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding becomes a division between two distinct sources of cognition rather than a distinction between passive and active versions of one faculty.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4","CH_BA94_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD5","text":"Transcendental inquiry changes the apparent given into a problem of the conditions under which givenness is possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SD6","text":"Phenomenal objectivity is neither raw sensation nor pure conceptual construction but sensory content ordered through a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:A7","CH_BA94_IINT:A8","CH_BA94_IINT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX1","text":"The system excludes an unmediated relation between the knowing subject and the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the reduction of phenomenal form to an accumulation of sensations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the derivation of object-directed cognition from concepts without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a homogeneous faculty in which receiving, intuiting, thinking, and conceiving are indistinguishable operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the treatment of pure intuition as a contingent sensory product.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the use of “æsthetic” as a designation for empirically grounded rules of taste.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SNX1","text":"No single source of cognition can independently supply both the object and the conditions under which it is thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SNX2","text":"There is no cognitively sufficient “raw phenomenon” prior to the distinction between sensory matter and organizing form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SNX3","text":"Unconditioned object-relation is unavailable within human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SNX4","text":"A unified doctrine of cognition is impossible without separating the principles of sensibility from the principles of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:SNX5","text":"An account of the object apart from the conditions of its givenness remains outside the operative scope of this introductory analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:FGS1","text":"Object affects mind → sensation arises → empirical intuition relates sensation to an object → phenomenon is constituted.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:FGS2","text":"Sensory matter + a priori relational form → phenomenal content arranged under relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A7","CH_BA94_IINT:A8","CH_BA94_IINT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:FGS3","text":"Sensibility gives intuitions → understanding thinks them through concepts → thought ultimately returns to intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A1","CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:FGS4","text":"Remove conceptual determinations → isolate empirical intuition; remove sensation → isolate pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:FGS5","text":"Pure intuition → space and time; a priori sensibility → transcendental æsthetic; pure thought → transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A10","CH_BA94_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AF1","text":"Object-relation → intuition → givenness → affection → sensibility → empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AF2","text":"Empirical content → distinction of matter and form → a posteriori matter / a priori form → pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:AF3","text":"Isolation of sensibility → removal of conceptual additions → removal of sensation → identification of space and time → constitution of transcendental æsthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME1","text":"Object-cognition is reclassified from direct apprehension to intuition-mediated givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A1","CH_BA94_IINT:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME2","text":"Sensibility is reclassified from mere receptivity to a faculty with a priori organizing form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME3","text":"The phenomenon is reclassified from undifferentiated appearance into a composite of matter and form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A6","CH_BA94_IINT:A7","CH_BA94_IINT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME4","text":"The boundary between empirical and a priori cognition is relocated within sensibility itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A9","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME5","text":"Æsthetic inquiry is reclassified from taste criticism to transcendental analysis of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A11","CH_BA94_IINT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ME6","text":"The architecture of cognition is divided into the conditions of intuition and the principles of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A3","CH_BA94_IINT:A4","CH_BA94_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ALT1","text":"Surface: The passage asks how knowledge immediately relates to objects and answers through intuition, sensation, and conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ALT2","text":"Structural: Cognition is organized as a division between receptivity, sensation, empirical intuition, pure form, and conceptual thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument replaces description of experienced objects with a reductive inquiry into the conditions that make experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Human objectivity is conditioned by affection, while the formal ordering of phenomenal multiplicity is a priori and culminates in space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central movement from object-affection to intuition, from intuition to phenomenon, and from phenomenon to pure form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between what sensibility receives and what the mind contributes a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT3","text":"The exact argument establishing why the pure forms are specifically space and time is announced rather than demonstrated in this introductory passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT4","text":"The relation between external affection and the mind’s a priori form is asserted functionally but not further explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT5","text":"The tension between object-dependent empirical intuition and pure intuition without sensation is contained by distinguishing empirical content from the form of sensibility, but the reconciliation is not fully developed here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT6","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of division and method, but its conclusions depend on the legitimacy of the two-stage subtraction that isolates pure form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IINT:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argument provided that space and time are treated as the announced result of the reduction rather than as conclusions fully proven within the section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"§ I. Introductory.","context":"Chapter 08","original_title":"§ I. Introductory.","original_context":"Chapter 08"},"original_title":"§ I. Introductory.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET","layer":"CH","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":9,"parents":["RAW_BA94_2MET"],"revision":"CH_BA94_2MET@R001","sha256":"eb73e521e10c506f9adf643e1ee62d7b5ffc1b177ba1fdccb7232765aabd74be","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS1","text":"The exposition determines whether space is an empirical acquisition, a relation belonging to things independently of intuition, or an a priori form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS2","text":"External objects can be represented as outside, separate, and spatially related only if spatial representation already precedes external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS3","text":"Space is therefore a necessary a priori condition of the possibility of external phenomena rather than a determination derived from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS4","text":"The inability to represent the nonexistence of space distinguishes its necessity from the contingent absence of objects within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS5","text":"The unity, non-discursive character, and infinite givenness of space identify it as a pure intuition rather than a general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AS6","text":"The apodeictic certainty of geometry is grounded in this a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A1","text":"A metaphysical exposition represents what belongs to a conception insofar as that content is given a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A2","text":"External sense represents objects as outside the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A3","text":"The shape, dimensions, and mutual relations of external objects are determined or determinable only in space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A4","text":"Representing sensations as external, separate, and differently located requires an antecedent representation of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A5","text":"External experience is possible only through an antecedent representation of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A6","text":"Space is a necessary a priori representation that grounds all external intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A7","text":"The nonexistence of space cannot be represented, although the absence of objects within space can be thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A8","text":"Space is a pure intuition rather than a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A9","text":"Only one space can be represented, while apparent plurality consists of parts of that one space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A10","text":"Particular spaces can be represented only as limitations within the one all-embracing space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A11","text":"Space is represented as an infinite given quantity whose parts can be extended without limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:A12","text":"Geometrical principles derive their apodeictic certainty from a priori intuition rather than from general concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX1","text":"Space is not derived from outward experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX2","text":"Space is not produced by abstracting relations from external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX3","text":"Space is not a determination dependent on phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX4","text":"Space is not a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX5","text":"Space is not an aggregate constructed from independently prior spaces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX6","text":"Space is not a finite concept containing an infinite multitude of subordinate representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX7","text":"Space is not abolished by the absence of objects within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:NX8","text":"Space is not presented as an object through internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D1","text":"External experience functions as an effect of spatial representation rather than as its source.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A4","CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D2","text":"The distinction between outside, near, separate, and differently located sensations already presupposes spatial form.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A2","CH_BA94_2MET:A3","CH_BA94_2MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D3","text":"The necessity of space is modal rather than merely regular: space makes external intuition possible instead of merely accompanying it.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A6","CH_BA94_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D4","text":"The possibility of an empty spatial field confirms that spatial form is independent of the contingent presence of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A6","CH_BA94_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D5","text":"Spatial plurality is derivative because particular spaces are bounded portions of one prior space.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:A9","CH_BA94_2MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D6","text":"The infinite character of space belongs to the original intuition rather than to a concept that progressively accumulates infinite contents.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D7","text":"Geometry obtains apodeictic force because its principles are grounded in the intuition of space rather than inferred from abstract definitions alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:D8","text":"External phenomena are spatially ordered only under the condition supplied by a priori intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A3","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SD1","text":"The explanatory direction is reversed from objects producing space to spatial form making objects available as external phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A4","CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SD2","text":"Empirical derivation is blocked at the origin of external experience, while conceptual derivation is blocked at the origin of geometrical certainty.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SD3","text":"All local spatial determinations become derivative limitations within a single prior field.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A9","CH_BA94_2MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SD4","text":"The distinction between empty space and nonexistent space makes spatial form independent of occupancy.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A7","CH_BA94_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SD5","text":"Infinity is treated as a structural property of the given spatial field rather than as the result of summing indefinitely many finite regions.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A10","CH_BA94_2MET:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX1","text":"No intelligible externality exists prior to the spatial form that organizes sensations as outside and apart.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX2","text":"No empirical sequence can explain the prior availability of spatial relations without presupposing what it seeks to explain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX3","text":"No spatial framework can be constructed from relations among objects that are already spatially individuated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX4","text":"No total space can be generated by assembling independently given parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX5","text":"No apodeictic geometry can be grounded in discursive generality alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:DNX6","text":"No representation of external phenomena is possible from a standpoint that first removes the spatial form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses a knowable pre-spatial domain of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses the use of spatiality as an explanatory property established independently of possible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses the possibility that geometrical necessity originates in observation of external regularities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4","text":"The relation between spatial form and things considered independently of intuition remains outside the completed exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:SNX5","text":"The announced analogy between space and time remains programmatic because the present exposition develops only the metaphysical account of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS1","text":"External sense presents objects as outside the mind and thereby requires distinctions of location, separation, and relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS2","text":"The required distinctions presuppose a representation of space that cannot itself be obtained from those distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS3","text":"The presupposed representation is therefore necessary, a priori, and constitutive of external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS4","text":"The inability to represent the absence of space, together with the ability to represent empty space, separates spatial necessity from object occupancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS5","text":"The unity and infinite givenness of space distinguish a pure intuition from a general concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:FGS6","text":"Geometrical certainty follows from the a priori intuition that grounds spatial determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AF1","text":"Ontological question concerning space → definition of a priori exposition → regress from external experience to antecedent spatial representation → necessity of space → pure intuition → unity and infinite givenness → geometrical apodeicticity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:AF2","text":"External location requires space → space cannot be empirically derived → space conditions external phenomena → spatial multiplicity depends on one intuition → geometry depends on that intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME1","text":"Externality is reclassified from an immediate feature of objects into a relation made possible by a priori spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME2","text":"Space is relocated from the status of an empirical object-property to the status of a condition of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME3","text":"The opposition between empirical acquisition and conceptual abstraction is replaced by the category of pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME4","text":"Separate spaces are reclassified as limitations within one space rather than as independently fundamental spatial units.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME5","text":"Geometry is detached from derivation through general concepts and attached to the certainty of a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ME6","text":"The absence of objects is separated from the absence of spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ALT1","text":"Surface: Space determines the shape, dimensions, separation, and relations of externally represented objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ALT2","text":"Structural: Spatial representation is the antecedent condition under which external experience and phenomenal ordering become possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument reverses ordinary explanation by deriving the possibility of object-experience from the form of intuition rather than deriving space from experienced objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Spatial predicates are assigned within the conditions of intuition, while the status of space as a reality independent of intuition remains an unresolved boundary of the exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the exposition’s principal movement from the presupposition of space to its status as pure a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive regress from external experience to the antecedent representation that makes external experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the distinction between empirical content and a priori form is accepted as its governing condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4","text":"The status of space as a real existence or as a predicate of things independently of intuition is posed but not fully resolved by this subsection alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT5","text":"The transition from necessary a priori representation to the full doctrine of subjective constitution is suggested by the framing but not separately demonstrated in the numbered exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT6","text":"The relation between infinite given space and the finite act of representing particular spatial regions remains compressed into the distinction between intuition and concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_2MET:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement: empirical externality is made dependent on a priori spatial intuition, spatial plurality is subordinated to unity, and geometrical certainty is derived from that structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","context":"Chapter 09","original_title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","original_context":"Chapter 09"},"original_title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","order":10,"parents":["RAW_BA94_3TEC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_3TEC@R001","sha256":"ff608184bb7efc4580fac32009b32a2cad73df4e4ad2882c631192fc9e69fb07","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine what conception of space makes synthetic a priori geometrical cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS2","text":"A transcendental exposition explains a conception as a principle from which the possibility of other synthetic a priori cognitions can be discerned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS3","text":"Geometry exceeds conceptual analysis while retaining apodeictic necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS4","text":"Because geometry exceeds a mere conception, its ground must be intuition rather than conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS5","text":"Because geometrical cognition is necessary and a priori, its intuition must be pure and prior to empirical perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS6","text":"Pure spatial intuition is possible only as the subject’s form of receptivity to external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AS7","text":"The subjective form of external sense explains the possibility of geometry and discriminates the adequate explanation from merely similar alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A1","text":"A transcendental exposition must explain a conception as a principle of possible synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A2","text":"The cognitions derived from the conception must actually flow from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A3","text":"The cognitions must be possible only under the proposed mode of explaining the conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","text":"Geometry determines properties of space synthetically and a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A5","text":"A mere conception cannot yield propositions that extend beyond the content of that conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A6","text":"Cognition that extends beyond a conception requires intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A7","text":"Geometrical principles possess apodeictic necessity and therefore cannot be empirical judgments or conclusions from empirical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A8","text":"The intuition grounding geometry must be available prior to perception of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A9","text":"An intuition prior to objects must be pure rather than empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A10","text":"Space is an intuition belonging to the subject’s mode of receptivity to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A11","text":"The subject’s receptivity to objects provides the form of external sense in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:A12","text":"The possibility of geometry becomes comprehensible only when space is explained as the pure form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX1","text":"Space is not merely a conception from which geometrical propositions are analytically extracted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX2","text":"Geometrical cognition is not generated by conceptual decomposition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX3","text":"The ground of geometry is not empirical perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX4","text":"Geometrical necessity is not inferred from empirical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX5","text":"The intuition of space is not an empirical representation acquired after encountering objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX6","text":"Space is not posited here as an independently existing external object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:NX7","text":"An explanation that fails to establish the possibility of synthetic a priori geometry is not an adequate transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D1","text":"Geometry requires a non-conceptual ground because it produces synthetic propositions that exceed the content of a mere conception.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","CH_BA94_3TEC:A5","CH_BA94_3TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D2","text":"The ground of geometrical cognition must be intuitive rather than merely conceptual.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A5","CH_BA94_3TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D3","text":"The geometrical ground must be a priori because geometrical principles are apodeictically necessary.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","CH_BA94_3TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D4","text":"The geometrical ground must be pure because it precedes perception of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A8","CH_BA94_3TEC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D5","text":"The required pure intuition cannot be explained as an object-independent external entity if it is to account for the subject’s cognition of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A8","CH_BA94_3TEC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D6","text":"Space functions as the condition under which external objects can be immediately represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A10","CH_BA94_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D7","text":"Geometry is possible because it articulates the formal structure of external intuition rather than extracting properties from empirical objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","CH_BA94_3TEC:A9","CH_BA94_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:D8","text":"The proposed explanation is transcendental because it connects the conception of space to the possibility of a specific synthetic a priori science.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A1","CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","CH_BA94_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD1","text":"The necessity of geometrical cognition is relocated from the independently given world to the subject’s a priori mode of receiving external objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A7","CH_BA94_3TEC:A9","CH_BA94_3TEC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD2","text":"Space becomes both objectively applicable to external appearances and subjectively grounded as the form through which those appearances are received.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A10","CH_BA94_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD3","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge depends on a distinction between the content supplied by objects and the formal conditions supplied by the subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A6","CH_BA94_3TEC:A9","CH_BA94_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD4","text":"The validity of geometry is secured by explanatory necessity rather than by empirical confirmation.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A2","CH_BA94_3TEC:A3","CH_BA94_3TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD5","text":"Transcendental explanation functions as a discriminating test: an account of space is admissible only if it generates the possibility of geometry.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A1","CH_BA94_3TEC:A3","CH_BA94_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SD6","text":"The subject is constituted as receptive rather than productive in relation to external objects, while nevertheless supplying the form of their intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A10","CH_BA94_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX1","text":"The possibility of necessary geometrical knowledge cannot depend on the contingent occurrence of particular objects or perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX2","text":"The formal condition of external experience cannot be derived from the empirical contents whose appearance it makes possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX3","text":"The universality of geometry cannot be secured by treating spatial relations as inductive generalizations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX4","text":"The subject cannot be reduced to a passive container receiving already spatially constituted representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX5","text":"Space cannot be treated as a purely subjective fiction lacking objective applicability to appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX6","text":"The explanation cannot separate the possibility of space from the possibility of external object-representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes a reality in which geometrical necessity is wholly independent of the subject’s cognitive constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes a subject that encounters external objects without any prior formal condition of externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes the possibility of grounding synthetic a priori cognition without a constitutive relation between subject and object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX4","text":"The argument leaves no admissible explanatory space between empirical realism and the subject’s pure form of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX5","text":"The argument presupposes that the conditions of object-experience can be identified independently of the objects empirically encountered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS1","text":"Synthetic a priori geometry → propositions exceeding conceptual content → requirement of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS2","text":"Apodeictic necessity → exclusion of empirical derivation → requirement of a priori grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS3","text":"A priori intuition → exclusion of empirical intuition → requirement of purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS4","text":"Pure intuition of externality → location in the subject’s receptivity → identification with the form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS5","text":"Form of external sense → possibility of immediate spatial representation → possibility of geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS6","text":"Possibility of geometry → validation of the conception of space → rejection of rival explanations that cannot generate that possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS7","text":"Transcendental exposition = conception → conditions of synthetic a priori cognition → necessity of the proposed explanatory mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AF1","text":"Synthetic a priori geometry → demand for a ground beyond conceptual analysis → intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AF2","text":"Intuition → demand for a priori necessity → purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AF3","text":"Pure a priori intuition of externality → demand for a subject-relative locus → form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AF4","text":"Form of external sense → immediate representation of objects → possibility of geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:AF5","text":"Possibility of geometry → transcendental validation → exclusion of competing explanations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an apparently given object or concept into a formal condition of external representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME2","text":"Geometry is reclassified from an empirical description of spatial objects into a science grounded in a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME3","text":"The opposition between subjective form and objective validity is reorganized: space is subject-grounded while remaining valid for external appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME4","text":"Necessity is relocated from observed regularity to the formal conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME5","text":"Rival theories of space are judged by whether they can generate the possibility of synthetic a priori geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ME6","text":"The subject-object relation is rendered structurally prior to empirical cognition because external objects are representable only within the subject’s spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT1","text":"Surface — Geometry contains synthetic, necessary, a priori propositions concerning space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The possibility of geometry depends on pure intuition as the form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — An explanation is validated by the possibility it makes intelligible, not by resemblance to the phenomenon explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Space is not established as a thing independently of cognition; it is prior to empirical objects as the subject’s a priori condition for their external appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Synthetic expansion beyond concepts requires intuition, while necessity requires that intuition to be a priori and pure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from geometry’s synthetic a priori character to the conception of space as pure external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between conceptual analysis, intuition, empirical perception, and a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT3","text":"The central explanatory mechanism is internally coherent: geometry requires pure intuition, and pure intuition is identified with the form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how the subject’s form of receptivity relates to objects beyond their status as appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the transition from the necessity of geometry to the subjectivity of space into the claim that only the proposed explanation makes geometry possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT6","text":"The principal tension is that space is treated as prior to objects for cognition while being described through the subject’s receptivity to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s decisive operation: the possibility of geometry serves as the criterion for the adequate conception of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","context":"Chapter 10","original_title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","original_context":"Chapter 10"},"original_title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","order":11,"parents":["RAW_BA94_4CFC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_4CFC@R001","sha256":"169e8b2d30f47741f2cf1ca26c59cf4c5c6fabe469699181128c0ac04942f0a8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS1","text":"The text asks whether space belongs to objects independently of the subject or arises from the conditions of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS2","text":"It reframes space as the form of external sensibility rather than as a property or relation of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS3","text":"It grounds this form in the subject’s receptivity, which precedes actual perception and makes space a pure a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS4","text":"It derives the possibility of spatial principles and objective validity for all objects of possible external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS5","text":"It restricts that validity to phenomena and denies its extension to things in themselves or to the unknown intuitions of other thinking beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS6","text":"It concludes that space is empirically real for experience but transcendentally ideal with respect to things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AS7","text":"The argument is transcendental, classificatory, and boundary-setting: it secures spatial cognition by limiting its ontological reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A1","text":"Space does not represent a property of objects considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A2","text":"Space does not represent relations belonging to objects independently of the subject’s mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A3","text":"Space is the form of all phenomena of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A4","text":"External intuition is possible only under the subjective condition of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A5","text":"The subject’s receptivity precedes actual intuitions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A6","text":"Space is a pure intuition given a priori in the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A7","text":"All objects of possible external experience are determined in space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A8","text":"Spatial judgments possess universal validity only when their objects are limited to external phenomena of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A9","text":"Space has empirical reality with respect to all possible external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","text":"Space has transcendental ideality with respect to objects considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A11","text":"The conditions of sensibility determine only the possibility of phenomenal existence, not the possibility of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A12","text":"Things in themselves cannot be cognized through spatial representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:A13","text":"Sensations such as colour, sound, and heat are subjective changes rather than intuitions that independently yield object cognition or synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX1","text":"Space is not a property attached to objects in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX2","text":"Space is not an independently existing relation among objects in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX3","text":"Absolute or relative spatial determinations are not intuitable a priori as determinations of things existing independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX4","text":"The special conditions of human sensibility are not conditions of the possibility of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX5","text":"Space does not apply without qualification to all things whatsoever.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX6","text":"The spatial conditions binding human intuition cannot be projected without judgment onto the intuitions of other thinking beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX7","text":"Colour, taste, sound, and heat are not adequate illustrations of the ideality of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX8","text":"Sensations are not themselves intuitions that disclose objects or generate a priori object cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX9","text":"Nothing intuited in space is thereby established as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:NX10","text":"The representation of space has no meaning once the condition of possible external experience is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D1","text":"Because receptivity precedes actual intuition, space can be present a priori before particular perceptions occur.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A3","CH_BA94_4CFC:A5","CH_BA94_4CFC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D2","text":"Because all externally experienced objects are determined in space, spatial relations can provide principles governing objects prior to particular experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D3","text":"Spatial judgments become universally valid when their objects are explicitly conceived as external phenomena rather than as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D4","text":"When the subjective condition of external intuition is removed, the concept of space loses its applicability rather than becoming a property of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","CH_BA94_4CFC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D5","text":"The proposition that all objects are beside one another in space requires restriction to objects of human sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D6","text":"The universal validity of space is restricted to the conditions of human sensibility and cannot be extended to other thinking beings without evidence about their modes of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D7","text":"Space alone among subjective representations referring to the external has the status of an a priori intuition capable of grounding synthetic a priori propositions.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D8","text":"Treating the spatially presented object as a thing in itself converts a phenomenon into an illegitimate metaphysical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A1","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","CH_BA94_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D9","text":"The spatial representation gives access to the structure of appearance without giving access to the intrinsic constitution of what appears.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A3","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","CH_BA94_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:D10","text":"The empirical understanding can mistake phenomenal presentation for a thing in itself when it treats the form of sensibility as an objective property of the object.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A1","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","CH_BA94_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD1","text":"Space acquires a dual status: it is objectively valid for every possible external experience while remaining non-constitutive of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A3","CH_BA94_4CFC:A7","CH_BA94_4CFC:A9","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD2","text":"The argument preserves the necessity of spatial cognition by relocating its ground from the ontology of objects to the constitution of the experiencing subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A5","CH_BA94_4CFC:A6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD3","text":"Universal spatial claims are valid only through an indexed universality whose domain is human external phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A7","CH_BA94_4CFC:A8","CH_BA94_4CFC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD4","text":"The distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality prevents the choice between unrestricted spatial realism and the claim that spatial experience is meaningless.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A9","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD5","text":"The framework treats space as subjective in origin but objective in phenomenal validity, thereby assigning two distinct senses to objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A8","CH_BA94_4CFC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD6","text":"The thing in itself functions as the inaccessible boundary of representation: it is asserted as the real correlate of appearance while being excluded from cognition through appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A10","CH_BA94_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD7","text":"The universality of space is humanly universal but not established as universal for thinking beings as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A8","CH_BA94_4CFC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SD8","text":"The system remains coherent only by preventing a priori priority from being reinterpreted as an ontological property of reality independent of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A1","CH_BA94_4CFC:A6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX1","text":"No standpoint outside sensibility is available from which objects could be spatially compared as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX2","text":"No condition governing appearance can be converted into a condition governing things independently of appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX3","text":"No unqualified spatial universality is available beyond the domain of human external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX4","text":"No representation of an object can establish that the represented object is identical with the object in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX5","text":"No symmetry exists between space and sensations such as colour, sound, or heat with respect to a priori object cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX6","text":"No inference from the conditions of human sensibility to the conditions of other possible subjects is licensed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX7","text":"No phenomenal description can disclose the intrinsic constitution of the represented object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX1","text":"The possibility that spatial structure belongs to things in themselves is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX2","text":"A non-sensuous access to objects that would bypass the conditions of intuition is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX3","text":"An unconditioned standpoint from which space could be judged as a property of reality as such is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX4","text":"The identity of appearance and thing in itself is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX5","text":"The possibility that all thinking beings share the human form of external intuition remains structurally undecidable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX6","text":"Complete objectivity, understood as validity independent of every mode of sensibility, is unavailable to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX7","text":"The positive constitution of the thing in itself remains absent; only its function as the limit of phenomenal cognition is retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS1","text":"The argument begins by separating objects as things in themselves from objects as given in external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS2","text":"Subjective receptivity → form of sensibility → possibility of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS3","text":"Form of external sensibility → pure intuition → a priori determination of spatial relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS4","text":"A priori spatial form + objects of external experience → universal principles valid for phenomenal objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS5","text":"Phenomenal restriction → empirical reality of space + transcendental ideality of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS6","text":"Withdrawal of the subjective condition → withdrawal of the meaning and applicability of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS7","text":"Comparison with sensations → exclusion of colour, sound, heat, and taste from the role of a priori object-producing intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS8","text":"Phenomenal cognition secured → noumenal cognition excluded → space retained as a condition of appearance rather than a property of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AF1","text":"Question of space as an object-property → distinction between things in themselves and appearances → receptivity as the ground of external intuition → space as a pure a priori form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AF2","text":"Pure a priori form → spatial principles for possible experience → restricted objective validity → empirical reality plus transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:AF3","text":"Phenomenal restriction → rejection of noumenal spatiality → exclusion of sensory analogies → boundary of cognition at the thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an independently existing feature of objects to the form under which external objects can appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME2","text":"The opposition between spatial realism and subjective unreality is replaced by the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME3","text":"Universal judgments are reclassified as domain-dependent judgments whose validity changes with the conception of their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME4","text":"Sensory qualities are separated from space because subjectivity alone does not constitute an a priori intuition or object cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME5","text":"The boundary between appearance and thing in itself becomes the governing boundary of spatial knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ME6","text":"Human sensibility becomes the reference frame for universal spatial validity without being established as the form of intuition for all possible subjects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT1","text":"Surface: The immediate issue is whether space belongs to objects independently of the subject or belongs to the mode in which objects are intuited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT2","text":"Structural: The operative mechanism is the dependence of external intuition on receptivity and the form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument preserves objective spatial cognition by restricting the domain in which spatial judgments may claim validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Space is treated as a priori with respect to actual perception, while the being of objects independently of sensibility remains inaccessible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central movement from the question of spatial objectivity to the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction requires preserving the difference between space as a form of sensibility and space as a property of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if “objective validity” is restricted to possible external experience rather than extended to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT4","text":"Its strongest operation is the qualification of universal spatial judgments by the conception of their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive status of the thing in itself and the precise relation between the inaccessible correlate and phenomenal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT6","text":"Its principal tensions are the coexistence of subjective form with objective validity, the assertion of a real correlate with the denial of its cognition, and human universality with uncertainty about other subjects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text if it retains the sequence: subjective condition → a priori spatial form → phenomenal validity → transcendental limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","context":"Chapter 11","original_title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","original_context":"Chapter 11"},"original_title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET","layer":"CH","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":12,"parents":["RAW_BA94_5MET"],"revision":"CH_BA94_5MET@R001","sha256":"f08f65ff3ff711d94bb8b1ef7bb88e07ae87c1e342ecb9a346a9bdffad7465b0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS1","text":"The account seeks the ground of temporal coexistence, succession, and temporal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS2","text":"It relocates that ground from empirical perception to an a priori representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS3","text":"The necessity of temporal representation establishes time as the universal condition of possible phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS4","text":"The apodeictic principles of time are treated as conditions governing experience rather than conclusions extracted from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS5","text":"The distinction between intuition and conception establishes time as a pure form of sensuous intuition rather than a general concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AS6","text":"The structure of temporal infinity is reconstructed through one unlimited time and the limitation that yields determinate temporal quantities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A1","text":"Temporal coexistence and succession presuppose an a priori representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A2","text":"Time is a necessary representation underlying all human intuitions of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A3","text":"Phenomena cannot be represented apart from time, whereas time can be represented without phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A4","text":"Time is the universal condition under which the reality of phenomena is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A5","text":"The fundamental principles of temporal relation possess apodeictic validity because they ground the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A6","text":"Time is a pure form of sensuous intuition, not a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A7","text":"All determinate times are parts or limitations within one time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A8","text":"Temporal succession is immediately contained in the intuition of time and cannot be derived from concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A9","text":"The representation of time is originally unlimited, while determinate temporal quantities arise through limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:A10","text":"Concepts require immediate intuition as their basis in order to represent determinate temporal quantities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX1","text":"Time is not an empirical conception derived from perceived coexistence or succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX2","text":"Temporal principles are not generalizations from accumulated experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX3","text":"Time is not a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX4","text":"Phenomena cannot be represented as outside or disconnected from time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX5","text":"Time itself cannot be annulled as phenomena can be annihilated in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX6","text":"Determinate temporal representation cannot be furnished by concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:NX7","text":"Different times are not coexistent in the manner of different spaces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D1","text":"Coexistence and succession are intelligible only after temporal representation has been presupposed.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A1","CH_BA94_5MET:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D2","text":"The temporal order of phenomena cannot be established with strict universality or apodeictic certainty by experience alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A1","CH_BA94_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D3","text":"Temporal axioms function as rules for the possibility of experience rather than as findings produced within experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A4","CH_BA94_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D4","text":"The annihilation of all phenomena in thought does not entail the annihilation of the condition under which phenomena can be represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A3","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D5","text":"The proposition that different times are successive is synthetic because it is supplied by temporal intuition rather than conceptual analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D6","text":"Every determinate temporal interval presupposes a prior unity of time from which it is delimited.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A7","CH_BA94_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:D7","text":"Concepts can represent temporal determinations only by depending on an immediately given temporal intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SD1","text":"Time functions simultaneously as the enabling condition of every temporal phenomenon and as a representation that is not itself constituted by those phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A2","CH_BA94_5MET:A3","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SD2","text":"The universality and necessity of temporal order are secured by relocating their source from empirical regularity to the form of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A1","CH_BA94_5MET:A5","CH_BA94_5MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SD3","text":"Temporal multiplicity is generated as internal differentiation of one time rather than as a plurality of independently given times.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:A7","CH_BA94_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SD4","text":"The possibility of determinate temporal knowledge depends on a prior non-conceptual synthesis that concepts cannot produce by themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:A8","CH_BA94_5MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SD5","text":"Temporal infinity is not an independently completed totality but the structural availability of indefinite determination through successive limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A7","CH_BA94_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:DNX1","text":"The possibility of experience cannot be grounded in the temporal objects that experience already presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:DNX2","text":"Temporal order cannot be reduced to a habit, regularity, or inductive expectation formed from prior observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:DNX3","text":"The unity of time cannot be reconstructed by aggregating discrete temporal parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:DNX4","text":"Temporal determination cannot originate exclusively in logical division or conceptual classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:DNX5","text":"Phenomenal reality cannot be conceived as temporally unconditioned while remaining phenomenal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a temporal order whose validity would depend on contingent empirical confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a plurality of times existing as independent coexistent wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a completed representation of time assembled from finite conceptual representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SNX4","text":"The system leaves no admissible account of phenomena that would be genuinely outside temporal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:SNX5","text":"The system excludes any account in which temporal necessity is derived from the contents of experience rather than presupposed as their condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS1","text":"A priori temporal representation grounds the possibility of representing coexistence and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS2","text":"Grounded temporal representation establishes time as the necessary condition of phenomenal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS3","text":"The necessary condition of phenomenal intuition yields apodeictic principles governing temporal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS4","text":"The non-empirical and non-discursive status of those principles identifies time as a pure form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS5","text":"Pure temporal intuition generates one-dimensional succession and the unity of all temporal parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS6","text":"The unlimited unity of time generates determinate intervals through limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:FGS7","text":"The dependence of determination on intuition subordinates conceptual representation to immediate temporal givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:AF1","text":"Empirical insufficiency → a priori temporal ground → necessary condition of phenomena → apodeictic temporal principles → pure intuition rather than concept → unified unlimited time → determinate quantities through limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ME1","text":"Temporal order is reclassified from an observed feature of phenomena into a condition of their possible observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ME2","text":"The opposition between experience and necessity is reorganized: experience supplies the field governed by temporal principles, but not the ground of their necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ME3","text":"The distinction between concept and intuition becomes a distinction between secondary determination and original temporal givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ME4","text":"Temporal plurality is mapped as limitation within a single form rather than coexistence among separate temporal substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ME5","text":"Infinity is reclassified from an indefinitely large quantity into the unlimited ground from which finite temporal quantities are delimited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ALT1","text":"Surface: Temporal coexistence and succession require a prior representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ALT2","text":"Structural: Time supplies the universal form within which phenomenal reality and temporal experience become possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The validity of temporal principles is secured by reversing the direction of explanation from experience-to-rule into condition-to-experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Time is treated as one unlimited form whose internal limitations generate determinate temporal parts without reducing the form to those parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the failure of empiricism to the a priori status of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT2","text":"The distinction between pure intuition and general conception is indispensable for reconstructing the argument’s middle transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT3","text":"The account of unlimited time and limitation is necessary to reconstruct the final treatment of temporal infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if time is treated as the form of phenomenal intuition rather than as an empirical object among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT5","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that time is represented independently of phenomena while its operative significance is defined as the condition of phenomenal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between the claim that time is one unlimited whole and the production of determinate times through limitation; the text provides the relation but not a further account of the limiting act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_5MET:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement, but depends on accepting the distinction between transcendental condition and empirical content as its governing premise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","context":"Chapter 12","original_title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","original_context":"Chapter 12"},"original_title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","order":13,"parents":["RAW_BA94_6TEC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_6TEC@R001","sha256":"5abaa79efd546e6c4ae1a1b673084b034538f60f11c5b6789083ec0efb2bcd63","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AS1","text":"The possibility of change and motion requires a prior representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AS2","text":"Time is reframed as an internal a priori intuition rather than an abstract concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AS3","text":"Temporal succession makes it possible for one object to bear contradictorily opposed determinations without simultaneous contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AS4","text":"The temporal condition of change grounds the possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge concerning motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AS5","text":"The argument moves from the transcendental status of time to its explanatory function in the general doctrine of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","text":"Change is possible only through and in the representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A2","text":"Motion is change of place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","text":"Time is an internal a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A4","text":"Without time as an intuition, the possibility of change cannot be rendered comprehensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A5","text":"Change involves the succession of contradictorily opposed determinations in one and the same object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","text":"Temporal succession permits opposed determinations to belong to one thing at different times.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","text":"The conception of time explains the possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge displayed in the general doctrine of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:NX1","text":"Time is not merely a concept through which change becomes intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:NX2","text":"Change is not comprehensible independently of an a priori intuition of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:NX3","text":"Opposed determinations in one object are not simultaneous contradictory predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:NX4","text":"Motion is not conceptually primary over time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:NX5","text":"Synthetic a priori knowledge of motion is not explained without reference to the temporal form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:D1","text":"Because motion is change of place and change requires time, motion presupposes temporal intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","CH_BA94_6TEC:A2","CH_BA94_6TEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:D2","text":"Because opposed determinations can belong to one object successively, temporal succession makes change intelligible without treating contradiction as simultaneous.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A4","CH_BA94_6TEC:A5","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:D3","text":"The intelligibility of change depends on a form of representation that precedes the changing empirical determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_6TEC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:D4","text":"The general doctrine of motion contains synthetic a priori knowledge because its object is constituted through the temporal form of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:D5","text":"Time functions as the condition that unifies successive states under the identity of one object.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A5","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SD1","text":"Temporal succession converts an otherwise unintelligible conjunction of opposed determinations into the intelligible structure of change.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_6TEC:A4","CH_BA94_6TEC:A5","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SD2","text":"The transcendental analysis of time establishes a condition for objective alteration rather than merely describing the order in which perceptions occur.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SD3","text":"The possibility of a priori knowledge of motion is transferred from the objects of motion to the temporal form through which alteration can be represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SD4","text":"Identity through change is possible only as identity maintained across temporally successive determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A5","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that change could be explained by empirical observation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that time is derived from relations among changing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a model in which contradictory determinations can belong to one object without temporal differentiation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the principles of motion are merely analytic consequences of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a conception of objects whose identity is independent of their temporal ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SNX1","text":"The argument forecloses a reality of change that is intelligible prior to the temporal form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SNX2","text":"It denies that contradiction alone can explain alteration; contradiction becomes intelligible only through succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SNX3","text":"It leaves no explanatory route from motion to time, since time must already condition the possibility of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:SNX4","text":"It treats a non-temporal structure of changing identity as unavailable to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS1","text":"Internal a priori intuition of time → condition for succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS2","text":"Succession → possibility of opposed determinations belonging to one object at different times.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS3","text":"Temporally ordered opposed determinations → intelligibility of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS4","text":"Change of place → motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS5","text":"Temporal condition of motion → possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge concerning motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS6","text":"The explanatory order runs from transcendental condition to experienced alteration, not from alteration to the derivation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:AF1","text":"Incomprehensibility of change without time → time identified as internal a priori intuition → succession distinguished from simultaneous contradiction → change and motion rendered possible → synthetic a priori knowledge of motion grounded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ME1","text":"Change is reclassified from an unexplained feature of objects into an event structured by temporal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ME2","text":"Contradiction is divided into simultaneous incompatibility and successive opposed determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ME3","text":"Motion is relocated from an independent empirical occurrence to a phenomenon conditioned by time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ME4","text":"The source of synthetic a priori knowledge is mapped to the subject’s temporal form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ME5","text":"Objectual identity is redescribed as persistence across successive determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ALT1","text":"Surface — Change and motion require representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Time organizes opposed determinations as successive states of one object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The explanatory direction is reversed from empirical change to its transcendental condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time functions as an internal a priori form through which alteration, persistence, and synthetic a priori knowledge become possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the a priori status of time to the possibility of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between temporal succession and simultaneous contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT3","text":"The central dependency is internally coherent: change requires time, while time is not derived from change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT4","text":"The argument remains underdetermined regarding the further justification of time’s a priori and internal character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT5","text":"Its strongest point is the conversion of succession into the condition of intelligible alteration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT6","text":"Its compressed step is the passage from the temporal condition of motion to synthetic a priori knowledge in the general doctrine of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_6TEC:MRT7","text":"The extracted structure preserves the original movement from transcendental condition to the possibility of change and motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","context":"Chapter 13","original_title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","original_context":"Chapter 13"},"original_title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","order":14,"parents":["RAW_BA94_7CAC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_7CAC@R001","sha256":"02b913cf5745b09fd6f063fb9cfc03dba089821d9da897d33a64be9d1fb2edcb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS1","text":"The argument asks how time can possess universal a priori necessity without subsisting independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS2","text":"The argument is transcendental and reconstructive: it determines the status of time by analyzing the conditions under which objects can be experienced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS3","text":"Time is reframed from an intrinsic property of things into the form of internal, human, sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS4","text":"Because every representation belongs to internal state, the temporal condition extends from inner phenomena to all phenomena, including outward phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS5","text":"The argument distinguishes empirical reality, which time possesses with respect to phenomena, from absolute reality, which time lacks independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AS6","text":"The conclusion preserves the necessity of temporal experience while denying that time belongs intrinsically to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A1","text":"Time does not subsist independently as a real entity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A2","text":"Time does not inhere in things as an objective determination belonging to them independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","text":"Time is the a priori form of internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A4","text":"Time determines relations among representations in internal state rather than the shape or position of outward objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","text":"Every representation, including representation of an external object, belongs as a determination of the mind to internal state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","text":"Time is the a priori formal condition of all phenomena, immediately for internal phenomena and mediately for external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A7","text":"Space is the pure form of external intuition and is therefore limited to external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A8","text":"Human intuition is always sensuous and therefore receptive to objects only under the conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","text":"Time has objective validity for every object that can be presented to the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A10","text":"Time has no absolute reality independently of the form of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A11","text":"When sensuous intuition is abstracted, time is nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:A12","text":"Properties belonging to things in themselves cannot be presented through the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX1","text":"Time is not a self-subsisting reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX2","text":"Time is not an objective determination inhering in things independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX3","text":"Time is not a determination of outward phenomena in the manner of shape or position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX4","text":"Time is not an external spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX5","text":"Time is not absolutely real apart from the conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX6","text":"The proposition that all things are in time is not valid when “things” means things in general independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX7","text":"Time is not a predicate that can be transferred from phenomena to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:NX8","text":"The transcendental ideality of time cannot be established through analogies that already presuppose the objective reality at issue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D1","text":"Time can be represented prior to particular objects because it is a subjective form of intuition rather than a feature received from those objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D2","text":"The a priori status of time makes synthetic a priori propositions about temporal relations possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D3","text":"Temporal succession belongs directly to the ordering of representations in internal state rather than to the external geometry of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D4","text":"The temporal representation can be analogically depicted as a one-dimensional line, although the line’s parts coexist while temporal parts succeed one another.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D5","text":"Every possible object of experience must stand in temporal relations because every representation of an object belongs to internal state.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","CH_BA94_7CAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D6","text":"External phenomena are temporally conditioned through the internal intuition in which they are represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D7","text":"The universally valid proposition is that all things as phenomena are in time, not that all things without qualification are in time.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10","CH_BA94_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:D8","text":"Empirical reality and transcendental ideality describe different relations of time rather than mutually exclusive properties.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10","CH_BA94_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD1","text":"The universality of time is generated by the universality of the subject’s mode of intuition, not by an inference from the intrinsic structure of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","CH_BA94_7CAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD2","text":"The account converts temporal objectivity from an absolute ontology into a necessary condition of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3","text":"Externality does not produce a temporal regime separate from internality because external objects are accessible only through representations belonging to internal state.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD4","text":"The distinction between phenomena and things in themselves limits the reach of temporal necessity to objects as they can be experienced.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A8","CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD5","text":"Time is simultaneously subject-dependent and objectively valid, with the tension stabilized by assigning objectivity to phenomena rather than to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SD6","text":"The structure permits necessary knowledge of the temporal conditions of experience without permitting knowledge of time as an intrinsic feature of reality beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10","CH_BA94_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a subject-independent temporal standpoint available within human sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the conversion of conditions of appearing into properties of objects as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes any external temporality that would bypass the internal intuition through which external objects are represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the inference from empirical objectivity to absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a conception of “things in general” that silently retains the conditions of sensuous intuition while claiming to have abstracted from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX1","text":"The argument forecloses a time that remains meaningful after every relation to a mode of intuition has been removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses a subject-free temporal order that could be asserted without reference to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX3","text":"The argument leaves no available cognitive route from sensuous phenomena to the temporal constitution of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX4","text":"The argument forecloses a representation that would make internal succession fully identical with an external spatial line.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX5","text":"The argument excludes the synthesis of empirical temporal objectivity with absolute temporal realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS1","text":"Independent subsistence and intrinsic inherence are rejected as inadequate accounts of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS2","text":"Time is assigned to the form of internal intuition, thereby acquiring priority over particular objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS3","text":"Internal intuition supplies the ordering relation of representations as succession, with the line serving only as an imperfect external analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS4","text":"Because every representation belongs to internal state, the temporal condition extends from inner representations to all phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS5","text":"The extension produces empirical objective validity for phenomena while denying absolute reality beyond sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS6","text":"The universal proposition is restricted from things without qualification to things as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS7","text":"The restriction blocks the illicit passage from the conditions of experience to the properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the failure of self-subsistence and intrinsic inherence to the identification of time with the form of internal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AF2","text":"The argument moves from internal intuition to temporal succession and the possibility of a priori temporal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AF3","text":"The argument moves from the internal belonging of every representation to the temporal conditioning of all phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AF4","text":"The argument moves from universal phenomenal applicability to the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:AF5","text":"The argument ends by qualifying temporal universality and excluding any claim about time as an intrinsic property of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ME1","text":"Time is reclassified from an object or objective property into a condition of object-presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ME2","text":"The opposition between inner and outer phenomena is reorganized as a distinction between immediate and mediate temporal conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ME3","text":"Objectivity is divided into empirical validity within experience and absolute reality beyond the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ME4","text":"The qualifier “as phenomena” becomes necessary for universal claims about temporal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ME5","text":"The boundary of temporal knowledge is relocated from the limits of experience to the distinction between appearance and thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ALT1","text":"Surface level: the status, structure, and universality of time are determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ALT2","text":"Structural level: internal sense mediates the temporal ordering of every representation, including representations of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level: the argument resolves the apparent opposition between subjective form and objective validity by assigning each to a distinct domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:ALT4","text":"Ontological and temporal level: time is necessary for phenomenal experience but has no status as an independently subsisting feature of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the rejection of realist accounts of time to the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction depends on preserving the distinction between objective validity for phenomena and absolute reality independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent only if “subjective” denotes the necessary form of human sensuous intuition rather than an arbitrary private construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT4","text":"The strongest inferential link is the passage from the internal belonging of every representation to the mediate temporal conditioning of external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT5","text":"The principal structural tension is that time is subject-conditioned while remaining universally and objectively valid for experience; the text resolves this tension through domain qualification rather than elimination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT6","text":"The line analogy remains underdetermined as a representation of succession because it spatializes temporal order while failing to reproduce non-coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if temporal necessity, phenomenal objectivity, and noumenal non-applicability remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","context":"Chapter 14","original_title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","original_context":"Chapter 14"},"original_title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU","layer":"CH","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","order":15,"parents":["RAW_BA94_8ELU"],"revision":"CH_BA94_8ELU@R001","sha256":"8c156defacca593b1e54c42bf939550a08f3c13c81a0766cabcbe33b2e3db468","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS1","text":"The objection infers the absolute reality of time from the empirical reality of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS2","text":"The reply concedes that change and time are real within experience while denying that this establishes time as a reality independent of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS3","text":"Time is reclassified from an object or property of things into the form through which inner states are represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS4","text":"The apparent force of inner consciousness is limited by distinguishing phenomenal reality from knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS5","text":"Space and time ground synthetic a priori cognition only because they are pure forms of sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS6","text":"Competing theories fail because they must sacrifice either experiential coherence or the apodeictic validity of mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AS7","text":"The argument closes by excluding motion and change from the a priori inventory of transcendental aesthetic because both require empirical perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A1","text":"Change is empirically real only as a determination represented in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A2","text":"Time is the real form of inner intuition and therefore has subjective empirical reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A3","text":"Time is neither a thing in itself nor an objective determination inhering in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A4","text":"The concepts of time and change vanish when the sensible condition of inner intuition is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","text":"A phenomenon includes both an unknowable thing considered in itself and the subjective form through which it appears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A6","text":"The forms of intuition belong necessarily and really to phenomenal objects without belonging to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","text":"Space and time are the two pure forms of all sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","text":"Space and time make synthetic a priori cognition possible, including pure mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A9","text":"Pure forms of sensibility have objective validity only within the sphere of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A10","text":"Empirical knowledge remains valid whether space and time are treated as forms of intuition rather than as features of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","text":"Motion and change require empirical perception of an existing movable object and its successive determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:A12","text":"No pure datum of sensibility beyond space and time can be admitted into transcendental aesthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX1","text":"Time is not an independently existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX2","text":"Time is not a property or relation objectively inhering in things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX3","text":"The reality of experienced change does not prove the absolute reality of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX4","text":"Inner consciousness is not knowledge of the self as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX5","text":"Space and time are not objectively applicable beyond sensible phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX6","text":"Space and time are not substances existing infinitely and eternally apart from things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX7","text":"Space and time are not empirical abstractions whose apparent necessity is produced by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:NX8","text":"Motion and change are not pure a priori forms or concepts of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D1","text":"The empirical reality of change entails the empirical reality of time as a condition of experience, not the absolute reality of time.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A2","CH_BA94_8ELU:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D2","text":"Consciousness certifies the actuality of inner representations without certifying that temporal succession belongs to the self as it is in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A2","CH_BA94_8ELU:A3","CH_BA94_8ELU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D3","text":"Mathematical cognition has necessity and objective validity only for objects insofar as they are given under the forms of space and time.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D4","text":"The ideality of space and time does not undermine empirical knowledge because empirical objects are constituted within those forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A6","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9","CH_BA94_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D5","text":"A substantival theory of space and time preserves mathematical scope only by positing empty self-subsisting entities.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D6","text":"A relational-empiricist theory of space and time cannot ground apodeictic mathematical cognition because empirical abstraction cannot yield a priori necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:D7","text":"Motion and change can be cognized only as empirical determinations within spatial and temporal forms rather than as additional pure forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SD1","text":"Transcendental ideality and empirical realism are mutually required: the forms must be subjective enough to avoid reifying them and objective enough to govern every possible phenomenon.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A2","CH_BA94_8ELU:A6","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9","CH_BA94_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SD2","text":"The conditions that make objects knowable also delimit knowledge to appearances, so epistemic constitution functions as a boundary on ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:A6","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3","text":"The validity of mathematics is relocated from correspondence with things in themselves to necessity within the universally binding form of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SD4","text":"The purported completeness of transcendental aesthetic depends on converting every sensible concept beyond space and time into an empirical determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX1","text":"Immediate awareness does not provide an exception to the restriction of knowledge to phenomenal appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX2","text":"The self cannot be known independently of the temporal form under which its states are internally represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX3","text":"Mathematical necessity cannot arise from relations abstracted from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX4","text":"Objective validity does not require that the form of cognition be a property of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX5","text":"No determinate claim about non-sensible intuition is available, even though its possibility is used to mark the contingency of temporal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX1","text":"No cognition can legitimately convert the necessary conditions of human experience into determinations of being as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX2","text":"No absolute temporal order is available from which phenomenal succession could be measured or justified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX3","text":"No third pure sensible source can mediate between the formal conditions of intuition and empirical contents such as motion or change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4","text":"The thing in itself is structurally required as the limit of appearance but is unavailable as an object of determinate temporal or spatial knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS1","text":"Real change → succession in inner sense → empirical reality of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS2","text":"Empirical reality of time → subjective form of intuition → denial of absolute temporal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS3","text":"Phenomenon as appearance → subjective form plus indeterminate thing in itself → restriction of objective validity to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS4","text":"Pure forms of intuition → synthetic a priori mathematics → validity for possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS5","text":"Substantival absolutism or empirical relationalism → loss of experiential coherence or mathematical necessity → transcendental ideality as the required position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS6","text":"Requirement of empirical movable content → motion and change → exclusion from the pure inventory of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF1","text":"The inference from real change to absolute time is confronted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF2","text":"Time is reframed as the real but subjective form of inner intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF3","text":"Inner consciousness is subordinated to the phenomenon–thing-in-itself distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF4","text":"Space and time are established as the exclusive grounds of synthetic a priori sensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF5","text":"Their validity is confined to appearances while empirical certainty is retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF6","text":"Rival ontologies are shown to divide mathematical necessity from experiential legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:AF7","text":"Motion and change are assigned to empirical experience, completing the boundary of transcendental aesthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ME1","text":"The opposition between the ideality of time and the reality of change collapses because empirical reality is separated from absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ME2","text":"Inner experience is reclassified from privileged access to an independently real self into phenomenal representation governed by inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ME3","text":"Mathematics is no longer mapped onto things in themselves but onto the universal conditions under which objects can appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ME4","text":"The dispute over space and time is redrawn as a forced choice among ontological reification, empirical derivation, and transcendental formalism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ME5","text":"Motion and change are relocated from candidates for transcendental forms to empirical contents presupposing those forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text answers the claim that actual change proves that time is absolutely real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes subjective forms of intuition, phenomenal objects, and indeterminate things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text resolves rival failures by retaining empirical realism and mathematical necessity while denying transcendent ontological claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time is not a self-subsisting entity or a feature of being in itself, but the necessary condition under which temporal succession can appear to a sensible subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT5","text":"Taxonomic — The text closes the field of pure sensibility at space and time by treating motion and change as empirically conditioned derivatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the reply to the objection, the restriction of temporal validity, the defense of mathematics, and the exclusion of motion and change from transcendental aesthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the distinction between phenomenal objectivity and thing-in-itself reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as subjective forms are treated as objectively valid conditions of possible experience rather than as private psychological states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT4","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how the thing in itself relates to appearance while prohibiting determinate knowledge of that relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT5","text":"The claim that non-sensible intuition would remove temporal representation functions as a limiting counterfactual rather than as positive knowledge of another mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT6","text":"The closure of transcendental aesthetic depends on the compressed classificatory claim that every sensible concept other than space and time presupposes empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","context":"Chapter 15","original_title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","original_context":"Chapter 15"},"original_title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT","layer":"CH","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":16,"parents":["RAW_BA94_9GRT"],"revision":"CH_BA94_9GRT@R001","sha256":"be2088aa63e93ca34dda89fb09bcc4b1bfbf2071538bdfbeaf0a809f56e9cff4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS1","text":"The text addresses how necessary and universally valid cognition of spatial objects is possible if sensibility does not disclose things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS2","text":"It asserts that human intuition presents phenomena under subjective forms rather than objects with their noumenal constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS3","text":"It relocates the distinction between sensibility and understanding from degrees of clarity to different origins and contents of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS4","text":"It proceeds by a transcendental argument from the synthetic a priori necessity of geometry to the subjective ideality of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS5","text":"It confirms this ideality by showing that intuition supplies relations and that internal sense gives the subject only as temporally affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS6","text":"It protects phenomenal reality from illusion by making illusion arise from objectifying space and time rather than from treating them as subjective forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AS7","text":"It closes by delimiting finite, derivative intuition against an original intellectual intuition, using natural theology as illustration rather than proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A1","text":"Human intuition represents objects only as phenomena and not as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","text":"Space and time are pure a priori forms of human sensibility, while sensation supplies its empirical matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A3","text":"The constitution of objects independently of human sensibility is unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A4","text":"The difference between sensibility and understanding concerns the origin and content of cognition rather than its logical clarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","text":"Validity for every human sensibility remains an empirical universality and does not disclose a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A6","text":"Cognitive intuition presents only relations and therefore cannot reveal the intrinsic nature of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","text":"Apodeictic synthetic cognition in geometry requires pure a priori intuition rather than empirical experience or concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A8","text":"Necessary synthetic cognition of external objects is possible only because space, and correspondingly time, are subjective a priori conditions under which objects can appear to us.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A9","text":"Internal intuition is temporal self-affection through which the subject represents itself as it appears rather than as it is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A10","text":"Phenomena are genuinely given objects in relation to sensibility and are not illusory appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A11","text":"Treating space and time as objective conditions of things in themselves turns them into self-subsistent necessities and destabilizes the reality of all empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:A12","text":"Finite human intuition is derivative, depending on objects that affect the subject, rather than original intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX1","text":"Sensibility is not a confused or less distinct cognition of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX2","text":"The sensuous and the intellectual are not separated by degrees of conceptual clearness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX3","text":"No sensory representation becomes noumenal cognition through exhaustive empirical clarification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX4","text":"No feature shared by all human observers establishes a property of an object independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX5","text":"Synthetic a priori geometry cannot arise from empirical intuition or from concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX6","text":"Space and time are not predicates belonging to objects considered independently of their relation to a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX7","text":"Internal self-consciousness is not an intellectual intuition through which the subject immediately knows itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX8","text":"A phenomenon is not an illusory appearance merely because its predicates depend on sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:NX9","text":"The possibility of an original divine intuition is not offered as proof of the theory of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D1","text":"Increasing empirical clarity can disclose only the structure of human intuition and cannot approach cognition of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D2","text":"Intersubjective agreement within humanity remains phenomenal because it is still conditioned by a shared sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D3","text":"Geometrical necessity determines how externally experienced objects must appear and does not determine objects as they are in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","CH_BA94_9GRT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D4","text":"The ideality of space and time permits empirical objectivity without granting transcendental reality to spatial and temporal predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A8","CH_BA94_9GRT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D5","text":"External cognition and self-cognition have the same phenomenal status, although one is ordered spatially and the other temporally.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D6","text":"The subjective ideality of space and time prevents the reduction of empirical objects and human existence to mere illusory appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A8","CH_BA94_9GRT:A10","CH_BA94_9GRT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:D7","text":"Natural theology can withhold spatial and temporal conditions from divine cognition only if those conditions are forms of finite sensibility rather than forms of being as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A8","CH_BA94_9GRT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SD1","text":"Necessary cognition acquires objective validity precisely by remaining within the conditions of possible experience rather than by transcending them.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","CH_BA94_9GRT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SD2","text":"The subject is simultaneously the formal condition of appearances and inaccessible to itself as it is independently of temporal self-affection.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SD3","text":"Objectivity divides into empirical validity for phenomena and unavailable knowledge of the transcendental object.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A3","CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_9GRT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SD4","text":"Phenomenal dependence on the subject does not abolish reality; it defines the condition under which reality can be distinguished from illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A8","CH_BA94_9GRT:A10","CH_BA94_9GRT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SD5","text":"Even if all finite thinking beings shared human spatiotemporal intuition, that universality would not convert derivative sensibility into original intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_9GRT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX1","text":"No comparison between a sensuous representation and a thing in itself can escape the forms of sensibility through which the comparison is made.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX2","text":"No empirical invariant, including universal human agreement, can serve as evidence for noumenal structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX3","text":"No transparent self-presence can ground self-knowledge because the self appears only through temporal affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX4","text":"No spatially external object can be known as absolutely external to the subject, because spatial externality is itself a form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX1","text":"The ground of appearances must remain without positive determination, because every determination available to cognition belongs to phenomenal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX2","text":"A cognition that gives its object immediately and through its own spontaneity is unavailable to finite human knowers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX3","text":"A nonrelational cognition of either object or self is foreclosed for a subject whose intuition is receptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX4","text":"An object-independent spatiotemporal order cannot be admitted without collapsing the distinction between phenomenal reality and illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS1","text":"Receptive sensibility + space and time as a priori forms → relational intuition → objects given only as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS2","text":"Phenomenal object + empirical investigation → greater clarity of human intuition → no cognition of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS3","text":"Apodeictic synthetic geometry → exclusion of empirical and merely conceptual sources → required pure intuition → subjective conditions of external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS4","text":"Internal perception + temporal self-affection → self-intuition → subject given as appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS5","text":"Subjective ideality of forms + real givenness of phenomena → empirical objectivity without illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS6","text":"Objective space and time → self-subsistent conditions of all being → ontological absurdity and generalized illusion → rejection of their objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS7","text":"Finite derivative intuition → exclusion of intellectual intuition → theological contrast that illustrates but does not establish the theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:AF1","text":"Phenomenal intuition → rejection of the confused-noumenal model → synthetic a priori geometry → subjective forms of space and time → relational and temporal confirmation → empirical reality without illusion → boundary between finite and original intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ME1","text":"Spatial and temporal predicates are reclassified from properties of things in themselves to relational predicates valid for objects of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ME2","text":"The distinction between empirical reality and transcendental reality becomes visible, so that an empirically real object can remain transcendentally phenomenal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ME3","text":"Geometry is reclassified from knowledge of independently spatial objects into evidence for the a priori conditions under which objects can be externally given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ME4","text":"Self-consciousness is reclassified from immediate self-possession into temporally mediated self-appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ME5","text":"Illusion is relocated from the subjective ideality of forms to the attempt to treat those forms as independently objective conditions of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the text concerns space, time, sensation, geometry, external objects, and self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, cognition is organized by receptive subjectivity, pure forms of intuition, relational presentation, and phenomenal objecthood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the transcendental question displaces inquiry from what objects are to the conditions under which they can be given and known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, finite beings inhabit derivative spatiotemporal intuition, while things in themselves and original intuition remain outside that mode of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from phenomenal limitation through geometrical necessity to the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT2","text":"The system remains internally coherent only if objective reality is restricted to objects as given under universal forms rather than extended to predicates independent of every subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT3","text":"The argument is structurally strongest where the necessity of synthetic geometry excludes both empirical experience and concepts alone as its source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT4","text":"The inference from relational intuition to the unknowability of intrinsic nature depends on a distinction between relation and inner constitution that the section asserts rather than independently establishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT5","text":"The account of self-affection leaves underdetermined how the subject can actively apprehend itself while receiving itself passively in temporal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT6","text":"The scope of the forms remains unresolved beyond humanity, since possible agreement among all finite beings is admitted but not decidable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT7","text":"The reductio of objective space and time relies on treating their self-subsistence and applicability to all being as unavoidable consequences, which concentrates the polemical force of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT8","text":"The theological contrast preserves the system’s boundary only as illustration, so reconstruction does not require it as an independent proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","context":"Chapter 16","original_title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","original_context":"Chapter 16"},"original_title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS","layer":"CH","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":17,"parents":["RAW_BA94_1CTS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_1CTS@R001","sha256":"b95e4f2ce593d3ffb9f95a71454821b8a489dca28687f1f972ffb0636b69b1f2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AS1","text":"The transcendental-philosophical problem concerns the possibility of synthetic a priori propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AS2","text":"Pure a priori intuitions are identified as the conditions through which synthetic a priori cognition becomes possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AS3","text":"Space and time supply a priori content that exceeds what is contained in a concept while remaining capable of synthetic unification with it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AS4","text":"The resulting judgments possess validity only with respect to objects of the senses and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AS5","text":"The argument therefore establishes the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition together with the experiential boundary of its validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A1","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori propositions is the governing problem of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","text":"Space and time are pure a priori intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A3","text":"An a priori judgment can pass beyond the content of a given conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A4","text":"Pure intuition contains a priori material not discoverable in the corresponding conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A5","text":"The content supplied by pure intuition can be synthetically united with a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A6","text":"Judgments enabled by pure intuition apply only to objects of the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:A7","text":"The validity of these judgments extends only to objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:NX1","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment is not exhausted by analysis of the given conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:NX2","text":"Pure intuition is not empirical content received from actual experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:NX3","text":"The validity of judgments grounded in space and time does not extend beyond sensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:NX4","text":"The judgments do not possess unrestricted validity independently of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:NX5","text":"The pure intuitions do not authorize cognition of objects outside the conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:D1","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment is possible because pure intuition contributes content beyond the concept while remaining connectable to it.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","CH_BA94_1CTS:A3","CH_BA94_1CTS:A4","CH_BA94_1CTS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:D2","text":"Space and time function as enabling conditions rather than merely as objects or contents within empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","CH_BA94_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:D3","text":"The existence of synthetic a priori cognition does not entail cognition of reality without reference to sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A5","CH_BA94_1CTS:A6","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:D4","text":"The solution to the possibility-question is simultaneously a delimitation of the domain in which the solution holds.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A1","CH_BA94_1CTS:A6","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:D5","text":"A priori validity is compatible with experiential restriction because the relevant a priori conditions structure possible experience rather than transcend it.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","CH_BA94_1CTS:A6","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SD1","text":"The transcendental account converts the opposition between a priori necessity and experiential limitation into a single structure: necessity belongs to the conditions of experience, not to objects considered independently of those conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","CH_BA94_1CTS:A5","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SD2","text":"The argument produces a bounded conception of knowledge in which extension beyond concepts is possible only through the forms of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A3","CH_BA94_1CTS:A4","CH_BA94_1CTS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SD3","text":"The partial solution of transcendental philosophy is inseparable from the exposure of a limit on metaphysical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A1","CH_BA94_1CTS:A6","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SD4","text":"The same conditions that generate synthetic a priori knowledge also prevent that knowledge from claiming validity beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A2","CH_BA94_1CTS:A5","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX1","text":"The system cannot treat a priori validity as evidence that cognition reaches objects independently of the forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX2","text":"The system cannot identify the source of synthetic content with concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX3","text":"The system cannot preserve both the transcendental account and an unrestricted application of its judgments beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a conception of knowledge in which necessity and objectivity require access to objects apart from sensible conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX1","text":"Cognition of objects wholly independent of possible experience is structurally unavailable within the framework.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX2","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition is denied as a route to absolute or unrestricted knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX3","text":"The system leaves no legitimate standpoint from which the conditions of sensibility can be bypassed while retaining the same form of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX4","text":"The uncompleted remainder of transcendental philosophy is the excluded possibility of knowledge beyond the sensible field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS1","text":"Governing problem: determine how a judgment can be both synthetic and a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS2","text":"Enabling condition: pure intuitions provide a priori content exceeding the given conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS3","text":"Synthetic operation: the additional intuitive content is united with the conception in an a priori judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS4","text":"Domain restriction: the judgment is valid only for objects presented under sensible conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS5","text":"Terminal consequence: the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition is established as a conditionally valid and experience-bound achievement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:AF1","text":"Possibility of synthetic a priori propositions → identification of pure intuitions → supplementation of concepts by a priori intuitive content → synthetic unification → restriction to sensible objects → validity within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ME1","text":"The source of synthetic a priori cognition is relocated from conceptual analysis alone to the relation between concepts and pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ME2","text":"The distinction between a priori validity and unrestricted applicability becomes explicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ME3","text":"The boundary between possible experience and cognition beyond sensibility becomes the decisive limit of transcendental validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ME4","text":"The problem of synthetic a priori knowledge is transformed from a binary possibility-question into a possibility-plus-domain question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ALT1","text":"**Surface:** Synthetic a priori judgments are possible through the pure intuitions of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ALT2","text":"**Structural:** Pure intuition supplies non-conceptual a priori content that can be synthetically integrated with concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ALT3","text":"**Metastrategic:** The argument solves the possibility problem by simultaneously restricting the scope of the resulting cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:ALT4","text":"**Ontological/Temporal:** Space and time function as a priori conditions of sensible objectivity, while objects outside possible experience remain beyond the established field of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the problem of synthetic a priori judgment to its restricted solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between conceptual content and intuitive contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the dual result: synthetic a priori cognition is possible, but only within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT4","text":"The argument is internally coherent if pure intuition is accepted as both a priori and sensible in function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5","text":"The remaining underdetermination concerns the further account of how space and time themselves ground particular judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT6","text":"The compressed system preserves the argument’s central tension: the same a priori conditions that enable cognition also delimit its reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","context":"Chapter 17","original_title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","original_context":"Chapter 17"},"original_title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","order":18,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ILG0"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ILG0@R001","sha256":"09e4a22f1d609d2737f93e3cd15f85f21cbd74c701fcc18d785a20fcf26663cc","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1","text":"Cognition requires the joint operation of receptivity, through which objects are given, and spontaneity, through which they are thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2","text":"The irreducible division between sensibility and understanding requires a corresponding division between æsthetic and logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS3","text":"Logic must be stratified by whether it governs all thought or thought concerning a determinate class of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS4","text":"General logic becomes scientifically valid only when purified of empirical conditions governing the actual exercise of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS5","text":"Pure general logic is delimited as an a priori canon of the formal use of understanding and reason, not as a doctrine of objects or subjective mental life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AS6","text":"Applied logic is reclassified as an empirical discipline concerning the hindrances and conditions of human thinking rather than as logic proper.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A1","text":"Objects are given through receptivity and thought through spontaneity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","text":"Intuition and conception are jointly necessary elements of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A3","text":"Human intuition is necessarily sensuous and expresses the manner in which subjects are affected by objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A4","text":"Understanding thinks objects of sensuous intuition through conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A5","text":"Sensibility cannot think, and understanding cannot intuite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A6","text":"Knowledge arises only from the united operation of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A7","text":"The science of sensibility is distinct from the science of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A8","text":"General logic concerns laws of thought necessary for every use of understanding, independently of differences among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A9","text":"Particular logic concerns correct thought only in relation to a determinate class of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A10","text":"Pure general logic abstracts from all cognitive content, objects, and empirical conditions of the exercise of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A11","text":"Pure general logic contains only completely a priori principles governing the formal use of understanding and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A12","text":"Applied logic concerns understanding under empirically given subjective conditions that can hinder or promote its use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:A13","text":"Applied logic necessarily depends on empirical and psychological principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX1","text":"Cognition is not produced by conceptions without corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX2","text":"Cognition is not produced by intuition without conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX3","text":"Understanding is not a faculty of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX4","text":"Sensibility is not a faculty of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX5","text":"General logic is not concerned with the content of cognition or with the specific objects thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX6","text":"Pure general logic is not derived from psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX7","text":"Pure general logic is not an organon for extending cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX8","text":"Applied logic is not a demonstrated science or a canon of understanding in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:NX9","text":"Particular logic is not primary in the order by which a science of its objects is acquired.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D1","text":"Thoughts lacking intuition are formally thinkable but cognitively empty.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A4","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D2","text":"Intuitions lacking conceptions provide sensible givenness without cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A3","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D3","text":"The distinction between æsthetic and logic follows from the non-interchangeability of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D4","text":"Pure intuition and pure conception can be a priori because they contain form without sensation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A3","CH_BA94_ILG0:A4","CH_BA94_ILG0:A10","CH_BA94_ILG0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D5","text":"General logic has universal validity only by abstracting from every distinction among objects and every empirical source of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A8","CH_BA94_ILG0:A10","CH_BA94_ILG0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D6","text":"A particular organon can be articulated only after a substantial cognition of its determinate object-domain has been acquired.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:D7","text":"Applied logic can diagnose attention, error, doubt, hesitation, and conviction only as empirical modifications of human understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A12","CH_BA94_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SD1","text":"The possibility of objective cognition is located neither in receptivity alone nor in spontaneity alone, but in their ordered synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A1","CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SD2","text":"Formal universality is purchased by the exclusion of object-content, psychological causation, and concrete error from pure logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A8","CH_BA94_ILG0:A10","CH_BA94_ILG0:A11","CH_BA94_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SD3","text":"Logic bifurcates into a canon that legislates necessary formal conditions of thinking and an empirical cathartic that regulates defective human performance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A11","CH_BA94_ILG0:A12","CH_BA94_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SD4","text":"The alleged autonomy of a special science is retrospective, because its logical organon presupposes prior acquaintance with the object-domain it organizes.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5","text":"The systematic separation of faculties is required precisely because cognition depends on their cooperation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6","CH_BA94_ILG0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX1","text":"No purely intellectual access to objects is available to finite human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX2","text":"No sensible datum counts as knowledge independently of conceptual determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX3","text":"No account of the psychological origin of thinking can establish the validity of logical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX4","text":"No formal logical canon can determine the material truth of cognition by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX5","text":"No universal logic can retain its universality while incorporating the contingent conditions of individual human thinkers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a standpoint from which cognition could be grounded by a single self-sufficient faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the reduction of normatively valid thought to the empirical history, psychology, or pathology of thinkers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a logic that would simultaneously be universally formal, materially cognitive, and psychologically concrete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX4","text":"The system forecloses any direct transition from formal conditions of thought to knowledge of determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS1","text":"Receptivity gives an object in intuition; spontaneity thinks that object under conceptions; their synthesis yields cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS2","text":"The non-interchangeability of the two faculties requires the separation of laws of sensibility from laws of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS3","text":"Laws of understanding divide according to scope: universal laws of every thought and special laws for determinate object-domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS4","text":"Universal logic divides according to purification: abstraction from empirical conditions yields pure logic, while retention of subjective conditions yields applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS5","text":"Pure abstraction yields an a priori formal canon; empirical retention yields a practical diagnosis of the human use and misuse of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS6","text":"The distinction between canon, organon, and cathartic prevents formal logic, special science, and empirical psychology from being conflated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AF1","text":"The problem of cognition → the dual-source constitution of cognition → the necessary cooperation and separation of intuition and conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AF2","text":"The division of cognitive faculties → the division of sciences into æsthetic and logic → the delimitation of logic as the science of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AF3","text":"Logic as science of understanding → general versus particular use → canon versus organon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AF4","text":"General logic → abstraction from empirical conditions or attention to them → pure logic or applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:AF5","text":"Pure logic as a priori formal canon → applied logic as empirical cathartic → the exclusion of psychology from logical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ME1","text":"The distinction between having an intuition and cognizing an object becomes visible as the distinction between sensible affection and conceptual determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ME2","text":"Logic is reclassified from a general account of mental activity into a formal science whose purity depends on exclusion of empirical psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ME3","text":"Error, doubt, attention, habit, and inclination are displaced from the foundation of logic to the empirical conditions of its human application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ME4","text":"The opposition between universal logic and specialized scientific method is reorganized as the difference between a canon of all thought and an organon for determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between faculty cooperation and faculty separation collapses, because cooperation in cognition requires differentiation of functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes intuition, conception, sensibility, understanding, general logic, particular logic, pure logic, and applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is organized by a two-faculty architecture in which object-givenness and object-thought have distinct necessary functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Logical validity is secured by separating formal conditions of thought from empirical accounts of how thinkers actually succeed or fail in thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human finitude is presupposed because objects can be known only insofar as they are sensibly given and subsequently thought through conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central movement from the dual source of cognition to the stratification of logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as faculty distinction, cognitive cooperation, and logical purification perform different functions rather than mutually cancelling one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning how conceptions are connected to intuitions in a cognition of determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it separates the normative validity of pure logic from empirical descriptions of human cognitive performance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT5","text":"The structure depends on an asserted asymmetry between formal laws of thought and psychological laws of mental occurrence without yet demonstrating that asymmetry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT6","text":"The designation of applied logic as merely cathartic creates a tension because it treats empirically conditioned thought as indispensable for human use while denying it the status of proper logical science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","context":"Chapter 18","original_title":"I. Of Logic in General.","original_context":"Chapter 18"},"original_title":"I. Of Logic in General.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0","layer":"CH","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","order":19,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ITL0"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ITL0@R001","sha256":"a81a5474c83e88cee0648b7f63a01fb0b0a4b074b28494bd5e162a3e259fa8de","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS1","text":"General logic abstracts from the content of cognition and from cognition’s relation to objects, retaining only the formal relations among cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS2","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical intuition entails a corresponding distinction between pure and empirical thought of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS3","text":"A logic of pure thought cannot abstract from all content because it investigates the a priori relation of thought to objects while excluding empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS4","text":"Transcendental status belongs not to every a priori cognition but to cognition of the a priori possibility or use of representations in relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS5","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction therefore concerns the critique of cognition rather than a division among objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AS6","text":"A science determining the origin, extent, and objective validity of pure object-directed cognition is designated transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A1","text":"General logic abstracts from all content of cognition and from cognition’s relation to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A2","text":"General logic examines only the formal laws governing the relations among cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A3","text":"General logic treats representations according to their logical use regardless of whether their source is pure a priori or empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A4","text":"Pure and empirical intuition permit a corresponding distinction between pure and empirical thought of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A5","text":"A logic of pure thought of objects cannot abstract from all content because it excludes cognitions containing empirical content while retaining a priori object-directed cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A6","text":"A logic distinct from general logic must examine the origin of cognition insofar as that origin cannot be attributed to objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","text":"Transcendental cognition concerns the a priori possibility or a priori use of representations in relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A8","text":"The mere a priori character of a representation does not make that representation transcendental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A9","text":"Space and a priori geometrical determinations are not themselves transcendental representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A10","text":"Knowledge that a representation is non-empirical and can relate to objects of experience a priori is transcendental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A11","text":"Applying space to objects in general is transcendental, whereas restricting its application to objects of sense is empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A12","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction belongs to the critique of cognitions and does not classify objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A13","text":"Pure concepts can function as acts of pure thought that are neither empirical nor aesthetic in origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A14","text":"A science of pure understanding must determine the origin, extent, and objective validity of pure object-directed cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:A15","text":"Transcendental logic concerns the a priori relation of pure understanding and reason to objects, whereas general logic treats empirical and pure cognitions without distinction of this kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX1","text":"General logic is not an inquiry into the origin of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX2","text":"General logic is not restricted to pure cognition or to pure thought of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX3","text":"Transcendental cognition is not identical with cognition that merely happens to be a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX4","text":"Space is not itself a transcendental representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX5","text":"An a priori geometrical determination is not itself transcendental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX6","text":"The application of space to objects of sense is not transcendental but empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX7","text":"Transcendentality is not a property assigned directly to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX8","text":"Transcendental logic does not include empirical content as constitutive material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX9","text":"Pure concepts are not derived from empirical or aesthetic sources.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:NX10","text":"Formal laws governing thought in general do not by themselves constitute a science of a priori object cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D1","text":"Because general logic brackets both content and origin, it can apply its formal laws to representations from any source.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A1","CH_BA94_ITL0:A2","CH_BA94_ITL0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D2","text":"A priori origin is insufficient for transcendental status; the representation must also be considered according to its a priori applicability or possibility in relation to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D3","text":"The same basic representation can receive transcendental or empirical status according to the scope of its application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A10","CH_BA94_ITL0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D4","text":"Transcendental inquiry must combine an account of origin with an account of possibility, use, extent, and objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A6","CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D5","text":"Transcendental logic retains object-directed content at the level of a priori relation while excluding empirical content from the pure science.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A4","CH_BA94_ITL0:A5","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D6","text":"The decisive difference between general and transcendental logic is not the presence of a priori cognition but the examination of its a priori relation to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A3","CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D7","text":"Formal logical validity cannot establish the objective validity of pure object-directed cognition without a separate transcendental inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A2","CH_BA94_ITL0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:D8","text":"Pure understanding can be investigated as a source of object-directed concepts without reducing those concepts to sensory intuition or empirical acquisition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A13","CH_BA94_ITL0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD1","text":"Transcendental logic occupies an intermediate structural position between source-indifferent formal logic and empirical cognition of particular objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A1","CH_BA94_ITL0:A5","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD2","text":"The notion of content is internally divided: empirical material is excluded, while the a priori mode of object-directed cognition remains operative.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A1","CH_BA94_ITL0:A5","CH_BA94_ITL0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD3","text":"Transcendentality is relocated from the mere possession of representations to the conditions governing their a priori use and objective reach.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A8","CH_BA94_ITL0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD4","text":"The system makes the scope of application an epistemic determinant: universal object-reference and sensible-object reference do not carry the same status.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A10","CH_BA94_ITL0:A11","CH_BA94_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD5","text":"The proposed science converts pure understanding from a presumed faculty into an object of inquiry whose origin, limits, and validity must be established.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A6","CH_BA94_ITL0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SD6","text":"The argument displaces the question of object cognition from formal consistency alone to the conditions under which pure thought can have objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A2","CH_BA94_ITL0:A14","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX1","text":"Pure a priori origin cannot by itself ground transcendental status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX2","text":"Empirical origin cannot serve as the explanatory ground of the pure object-directed cognition under investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX3","text":"Empirical content cannot constitute the material of a science of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX4","text":"Formal logical form cannot by itself determine the objective validity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX5","text":"Transcendental analysis cannot be reduced to an ontology of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX6","text":"A representation cannot be identified with the conditions governing its use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX7","text":"The distinction between transcendental and empirical cannot be settled by source alone; it requires analysis of object-directed applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX1","text":"The system excludes both the reduction of cognition to empirical derivation and the reduction of transcendentality to bare a priori form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX2","text":"No direct passage from pure thought to objectively valid cognition is admitted without an intervening inquiry into possibility, extent, and validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX3","text":"No object-independent formal logic exhausts the conditions of object cognition, yet no empirical object-content can ground the pure science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX4","text":"The transcendental is not an ontological realm of objects but a critical status assigned to cognition’s a priori relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX5","text":"The text forecloses any framework in which the distinction between transcendental and empirical is determined solely by the objects considered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS1","text":"Cognition has content and object-relation → general logic abstracts from both → only formal relations among cognitions remain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS2","text":"Pure and empirical intuition are distinguished → pure and empirical thought of objects become distinguishable → a non-general logic becomes necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS3","text":"Pure thought of objects is isolated → empirical content is excluded → a priori object-directed content remains as the domain of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS4","text":"A representation is a priori → its a priori applicability or possibility in relation to objects is examined → transcendental status is assigned only where that relation is established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS5","text":"Application is universal or object-general → the application is transcendental; application is restricted to sensible objects → the application is empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:FGS6","text":"Origin, extent, and objective validity are made into explicit questions → a science of pure understanding and rational cognition is constituted → that science is named transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:AF1","text":"Abstraction of cognition’s content and object-relation → distinction between pure and empirical thought → retention of a priori object-directed content → criterion of transcendental use → separation of universal from sensible application → science of origin, extent, and objective validity → transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME1","text":"Transcendentality is reclassified from a feature of an isolated representation to a feature of its a priori possibility or use in relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME2","text":"The boundary between general logic and transcendental logic is redrawn around object-directed a priori validity rather than around the simple presence or absence of formal thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME3","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical is made scope-sensitive: one representation can receive different statuses according to the domain of its application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME4","text":"The origin of cognition becomes philosophically relevant only when distinguished from derivation through objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME5","text":"Objective validity is transformed from an implicit assumption of pure thought into a problem that the proposed science must determine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ME6","text":"The object of logical inquiry expands from the form of thinking in general to the conditions under which pure thought can relate to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes general logic from transcendental logic and pure thought from empirical thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT2","text":"Structural — The distinction is generated by different treatments of content, origin, object-relation, applicability, scope, and validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes a criterion for identifying which a priori cognitions belong to a critique of object-directed cognition rather than to formal logic in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The system treats objects as termini of cognition rather than as the direct determinants of transcendental status, and treats a priori priority as a condition of use rather than as sufficient proof of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between general logic, pure thought, empirical thought, and transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT2","text":"The formal sequence preserves the argument’s movement from abstraction through differentiation to the proposal of a new science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT3","text":"The extracted system leaves the specific contents of pure understanding and the detailed proof of their objective validity underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT4","text":"The structure is internally coherent provided transcendentality is treated as a critical status of cognition rather than as a property of representations or objects in isolation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT5","text":"The system is strongest in distinguishing a priori givenness from transcendental use and in making application-scope epistemically decisive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT6","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that transcendental logic must retain object-directed content while simultaneously excluding empirical content from its domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITL0:MRT7","text":"A second unresolved tension is that the science is defined by its task of determining objective validity before that validity has itself been demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","original_context":"Chapter 19"},"original_title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL","layer":"CH","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","order":20,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IDGL"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IDGL@R001","sha256":"5d056c110e0ba4b18df9f8de20f22f3cea37714f387bb7797eec2870f1aa016e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS1","text":"The demand for a universal and secure criterion of truth is tested against the definition of truth as accordance between cognition and its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS2","text":"A criterion valid for all cognitions must abstract from their objects and therefore from the content upon which material truth depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS3","text":"The demand for a universal material criterion of truth is therefore self-contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS4","text":"General logic can supply only formal criteria that exclude self-contradiction, not criteria that establish agreement with objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS5","text":"Logic is consequently a negative canon for testing cognition, not an organon for producing objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AS6","text":"Dialectic arises when formal logic is misused as an organon and is therefore recast as the critique of this illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A1","text":"Truth consists in the accordance of cognition with its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A2","text":"The truth or falsity of a cognition depends on the particular object to which that cognition relates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A3","text":"A universal criterion of truth must apply to cognitions without distinction of their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A4","text":"A criterion that abstracts from all objects also abstracts from the content of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A5","text":"Material truth depends on the content of cognition and its relation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","text":"The universal laws of understanding provide criteria for the formal consistency of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","text":"Contradiction with the universal laws of understanding is sufficient for formal falsity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","text":"Formal consistency does not establish agreement between cognition and its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A9","text":"Objective judgment requires information about objects obtained independently of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A10","text":"General logic becomes dialectical when it is treated as an instrument for extending objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:A11","text":"Dialectic, in its legitimate sense, is the critique of the illusion produced by the organonic misuse of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX1","text":"No universal criterion can establish the material truth of all cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX2","text":"Formal accordance with logical laws is not objective truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX3","text":"General logic is not a source of cognition about objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX4","text":"General logic is not an organon for the extension of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX5","text":"Logical non-contradiction does not exclude material error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:NX6","text":"Dialectic is not a positive method for generating objective assertions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D1","text":"The universality of a truth-criterion can be retained only by restricting it to form, thereby abandoning any claim to certify material truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A3","CH_BA94_IDGL:A4","CH_BA94_IDGL:A5","CH_BA94_IDGL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D2","text":"Logical laws function as necessary conditions of truth because every objectively valid cognition must at least avoid self-contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D3","text":"Analytic is the legitimate function of general logic because it resolves formal cognition into elements and tests those elements negatively.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D4","text":"Material investigation must follow logical testing because logic can invalidate incoherent cognition but cannot establish its objective content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A5","CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDGL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D5","text":"Any apparently objective assertion generated from formal logic alone possesses only the semblance of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDGL:A9","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:D6","text":"Dialectical illusion is structurally enabled by the fact that formal correctness can imitate the appearance of truth without supplying objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SD1","text":"The very abstraction that makes logic universally applicable also prevents it from determining the truth of object-directed cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A3","CH_BA94_IDGL:A4","CH_BA94_IDGL:A5","CH_BA94_IDGL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SD2","text":"The distinction between canon and organon separates the regulation of already given cognition from the production of cognition about objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDGL:A9","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SD3","text":"Error divides into formal contradiction, which logic can detect, and material discordance with objects, which logic cannot detect by itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A1","CH_BA94_IDGL:A5","CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SD4","text":"Critique becomes necessary not because logic is invalid, but because valid formal procedure can be illegitimately converted into apparent objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10","CH_BA94_IDGL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX1","text":"Objective reference cannot be generated from the universal form of thought alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX2","text":"The laws governing thought cannot, merely by governing thought, guarantee that thought reaches objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX3","text":"Truth is not reducible to consistency within cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX4","text":"A universally valid procedure cannot supply universal material knowledge merely by being universally valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX5","text":"The absence of contradiction is not a positive ground of objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX1","text":"No direct transition from subjective logical form to objective truth is available within general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX2","text":"The positive source of objectively valid content remains outside the resources of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses the possibility that logic alone could be self-authorizing as knowledge of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX4","text":"The discourse is organized around the unavailability of a universal positive mark of material truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS1","text":"Truth as cognition–object accordance → object-relative content becomes indispensable to material truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS2","text":"Demand for a universal criterion → abstraction from objects and content → incapacity to certify material truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS3","text":"Retention of formal laws → exclusion of contradiction → necessary but insufficient condition of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS4","text":"Formal canon treated as objective organon → production of merely apparent knowledge → dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS5","text":"Dialectical illusion identified → organonic pretension withdrawn → dialectic becomes critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AF1","text":"Truth defined as object-relative accordance → universal truth-criterion demanded → content necessarily abstracted → material criterion shown impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AF2","text":"Material criterion denied → formal logical laws retained → contradiction excluded → logic established as negative test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:AF3","text":"Negative test mistaken for productive knowledge → formal appearance mistaken for objectivity → dialectic identified as illusion → critique instituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ME1","text":"Truth is divided into material truth concerning objects and formal truth concerning consistency of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ME2","text":"Logic is reclassified from a purported producer of knowledge into a restrictive test governing the permissible form of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ME3","text":"Dialectic is reclassified from a technique of argument into a diagnostic category for the illusion of formal knowledge-production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ME4","text":"Error is redrawn as containing an irreducibly material dimension that cannot be resolved by formal analysis alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between rigorous logic and empty sophistry is reorganized, since sophistry can borrow rigor’s formal appearance without its objective ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines whether general logic can provide a universal criterion for the truth of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes formal laws of thought from the object-related content required for material truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text limits the legitimate use of logic by separating critical regulation from the production of objective claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT4","text":"Epistemic — The text presupposes that objective validity requires a relation to objects not supplied by universal formal laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT5","text":"Temporal-Methodological — The text orders inquiry so that formal testing precedes, but cannot replace, examination of the material validity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the argument from the definition of truth through the impossibility of a universal material criterion to the critical delimitation of logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent if material truth and formal consistency are maintained as distinct conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT3","text":"The structure is strongest where it derives the impossibility of a universal material truth-criterion from the abstraction required by universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT4","text":"The system leaves underdetermined the source, status, and validation of the independently obtained information that objective judgment requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the unargued persistence of the correspondence definition of truth while using that definition to restrict what logic can establish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT6","text":"The decisive tension is that logic is universally necessary for judging cognition but structurally incapable of furnishing the objectivity that truth requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the negative function of logic is kept distinct from any positive theory of how cognition reaches objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","original_context":"Chapter 20"},"original_title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","order":21,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IDTL"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IDTL@R001","sha256":"505eb8ead43ad9ad9669b24600a9c4d725f1443b94b3d7f5d9a46114b37890ff","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS1","text":"Transcendental logic isolates the understanding and examines cognition originating from it alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS2","text":"Pure understanding has objective validity only when its concepts are applied to objects given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS3","text":"Transcendental analytic identifies the elements and principles required for any object to be thought and thereby functions as a logic of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS4","text":"The understanding becomes dialectical when it treats its formal principles as materially valid beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS5","text":"Transcendental dialectic critiques the illusion produced by this hyperphysical use of understanding and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AS6","text":"The proper role of transcendental logic is therefore to regulate empirical judgment and prevent sophistical overreach, not to generate unrestricted cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A1","text":"Transcendental logic isolates the understanding from sensibility and examines its pure cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","text":"The application of pure cognition requires objects to be given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A3","text":"Cognition without intuition has no objects and is therefore void of content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","text":"Transcendental analytic contains the elements and principles without which no object can be thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A5","text":"The principles of transcendental analytic constitute the conditions of objective validity and truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A6","text":"Experience is the source from which the matter or objects required by pure concepts is obtained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","text":"Pure understanding is prone to extend its formal principles beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A8","text":"The use of pure understanding beyond possible experience produces judgments about objects that are not given and may not be giveable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","text":"Transcendental logic is properly a canon regulating the empirical use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:A10","text":"Transcendental dialectic critiques the hyperphysical use of understanding and reason and guards judgment against sophistical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX1","text":"Pure cognition is not self-sufficient apart from intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX2","text":"Cognition without reference to an object is not genuine cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX3","text":"Formal principles of understanding are not by themselves material knowledge of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX4","text":"Transcendental logic is not an organon for the universal and unlimited exercise of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX5","text":"Pure understanding cannot legitimately determine objects in general without their being given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX6","text":"Transcendental dialectic is not an art for producing metaphysical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:NX7","text":"Transcendental principles do not by themselves discover or enlarge cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D1","text":"A pure concept becomes objectively empty when no intuition supplies an object for its application.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D2","text":"The truth of a cognition depends not only on formal consistency but also on reference to an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A3","CH_BA94_IDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D3","text":"Transcendental analytic can function as a logic of truth only within the conditions that make object-reference possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","CH_BA94_IDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D4","text":"Extending the categories beyond possible experience converts a canon of judgment into an illegitimate organon of object-production.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D5","text":"Judgments concerning objects that cannot be given lack the material conditions required for objective determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D6","text":"Dialectical illusion arises when the understanding mistakes the formal conditions of thinking for sufficient grounds of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:D7","text":"Critique must assess the legitimacy of judgments made through pure principles rather than extend those principles into new domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SD1","text":"The distinction between analytic and dialectic is generated by the difference between legitimate object-directed use and illegitimate object-independent use of pure principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SD2","text":"The same formal structures that make objective cognition possible become sources of illusion when detached from the conditions of their application.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SD3","text":"The boundary of possible experience is simultaneously the boundary between truth-producing cognition and empty metaphysical pretension.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A3","CH_BA94_IDTL:A5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SD4","text":"Transcendental critique transforms the task of reason from expanding cognition by pure principles to policing the conditions and limits of their valid use.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX1","text":"The validity of a concept is not secured by its logical form alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX2","text":"The possibility of thinking an object does not entail the possibility of knowing or encountering that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX3","text":"A faculty’s possession of formal principles does not authorize unrestricted synthesis concerning objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX4","text":"The absence of intuition cannot be compensated for by increasing abstraction, formal rigor, or transcendental speculation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX5","text":"The critique of metaphysics cannot itself operate as an alternative metaphysical organon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX1","text":"Unconditioned cognition of objects through pure understanding alone is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX2","text":"A domain of objectivity independent of intuition and possible experience is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX3","text":"The self-authorizing expansion of cognition through transcendental principles is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX4","text":"The possibility that formal thought can generate its own objective matter is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS1","text":"The understanding is isolated as the source of pure cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS2","text":"Pure cognition is subordinated to intuition as the condition of object-reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS3","text":"The principles securing object-thought are constituted as transcendental analytic and as a logic of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS4","text":"The detachment of these principles from intuition and experience produces empty synthetic judgments and dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS5","text":"Transcendental dialectic is instituted as the critique that exposes this detachment and restores the principles to their regulative, empirical function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AF1","text":"Isolation of pure understanding → dependence on intuition → constitution of analytic truth → misuse beyond experience → production of dialectical illusion → critical restriction of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:AF2","text":"Formal condition of thought → required object-reference → boundary of possible experience → exposure of hyperphysical overreach → canon rather than organon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ME1","text":"The distinction between analytic and dialectic is relocated from two subjects of logic to two uses of pure cognitive principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ME2","text":"Objectivity is reclassified as requiring both formal conditions of thought and intuitively given content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ME3","text":"Metaphysical error is reclassified from simple falsehood to the misuse of valid formal principles outside their domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ME4","text":"Critique is reclassified from a generator of higher knowledge into a regulator of the legitimacy of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ME5","text":"The boundary of experience becomes a functional boundary separating truth-bearing judgment from empty synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure understanding is distinguished from intuition, and transcendental logic is divided into analytic and dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objective cognition requires the conjunction of formal principles with intuitively supplied matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The proper critical strategy is to test the use of principles and block their conversion from canon into unlimited organon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are cognitively available only under conditions of possible intuition and experience; beyond those conditions, formal thought loses objective reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the division between transcendental analytic and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as objective validity requires both formal conditions and given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT3","text":"The central unresolved tension is that pure understanding supplies necessary conditions for object-thought while remaining unable to produce objects or their matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT4","text":"The relation between intuition, experience, and objects that may never be given is compressed rather than fully differentiated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT5","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement from the conditions of truth to the critique of their hyperphysical misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","original_context":"Chapter 21"},"original_title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL","layer":"CH","title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","order":22,"parents":["RAW_BA94_FDTL"],"revision":"CH_BA94_FDTL@R001","sha256":"47eb807659c51616d52e599dea1ae92ed589625b172f49a34065324345725caf","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS1","text":"Transcendental analytic seeks to dissect a priori cognition into the elementary constituents of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS2","text":"Its objects must be pure, intellectual, elementary, and exhaustive of the sphere of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS3","text":"Exhaustiveness cannot be established through an empirical aggregate of repeated observations or attempts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS4","text":"Completeness requires an idea of the totality of a priori understanding and a systematic division determined by that idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS5","text":"Pure understanding is autonomous from both empirical cognition and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS6","text":"The systematic completeness and articulation of pure understanding provide a criterion for testing the legitimacy of its components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AS7","text":"The system is organized into the concepts and principles of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A1","text":"Transcendental analytic concerns the elements of a priori cognition belonging to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A2","text":"The proper conceptions of transcendental analytic are pure rather than empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A3","text":"The proper conceptions belong to thought and understanding rather than to intuition and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A4","text":"The proper conceptions are elementary rather than deduced or compound.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A5","text":"The table of elementary conceptions must be complete across the whole sphere of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A6","text":"Empirical aggregation cannot establish the completeness of a science of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A7","text":"Completeness requires an idea of the totality of a priori cognition of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A8","text":"The division of elementary conceptions must be determined through their connection within a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A9","text":"Pure understanding is self-subsistent and self-sufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A10","text":"Pure understanding cannot be enlarged by additions from outside itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","text":"The cognition of pure understanding forms a system governed by an idea of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A12","text":"Systematic completeness and articulation can test the correctness and genuineness of individual cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:A13","text":"The system of transcendental analytic contains separate divisions for the conceptions and principles of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX1","text":"Pure understanding is not empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX2","text":"The elementary conceptions are not derived from intuition or sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX3","text":"The elementary conceptions are not deduced or compound conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX4","text":"Completeness is not an empirical aggregate produced by repeated experiments and attempts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX5","text":"Pure understanding is not dependent on additions from outside itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX6","text":"The table of pure concepts is not a partial or merely provisional inventory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX7","text":"The system is not an unconnected collection of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:NX8","text":"The legitimacy of a cognition is not secured independently of its systematic place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D1","text":"The objects of transcendental analytic must be selected by their relation to pure understanding rather than by their occurrence in experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A1","CH_BA94_FDTL:A2","CH_BA94_FDTL:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D2","text":"A conception qualifies as elementary only when it is neither composed from other conceptions nor obtained through derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D3","text":"A complete table requires a principle capable of determining the entire domain to be tabulated.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A5","CH_BA94_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D4","text":"The criterion of completeness is architectonic rather than inductive.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A6","CH_BA94_FDTL:A7","CH_BA94_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D5","text":"The relations among conceptions are constitutive of the table’s completeness, not merely explanatory after the table has been assembled.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A7","CH_BA94_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D6","text":"The autonomy of pure understanding entails that its elements must be sought within a self-contained domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A9","CH_BA94_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D7","text":"The systematic form of cognition allows the whole to function as a criterion for its parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:D8","text":"The division between conceptions and principles is required by the internal organization of the system of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_FDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SD1","text":"The system of pure understanding is generated deductively from an anticipated totality rather than reconstructed retrospectively from collected instances.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A5","CH_BA94_FDTL:A6","CH_BA94_FDTL:A7","CH_BA94_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SD2","text":"The validity of individual categories is inseparable from the completeness and articulation of the system that contains them.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A8","CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SD3","text":"Pure understanding is treated as a closed epistemic domain whose boundaries are established by its own form rather than by external material.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A3","CH_BA94_FDTL:A9","CH_BA94_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SD4","text":"The argument converts classification into a test of legitimacy: a conception is authenticated through its necessary place within the complete system.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A5","CH_BA94_FDTL:A8","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SD5","text":"The subsequent architecture of transcendental logic is predetermined as a movement from elementary conceptions to the principles they organize.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_FDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX1","text":"The system cannot be grounded in an open-ended accumulation of observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX2","text":"The system cannot admit sensibility as a constitutive source of the pure concepts under investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX3","text":"The system cannot treat its elementary units as arbitrary analytical subdivisions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX4","text":"The system cannot recognize an external supplement capable of completing pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX5","text":"The system cannot validate its parts through isolated inspection detached from totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX6","text":"The system cannot regard incompleteness as a neutral condition of the science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX1","text":"The possibility that the domain of pure understanding is fundamentally unbounded is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX2","text":"The possibility that experience supplies the final standard for the classification of pure concepts is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX3","text":"The possibility of a legitimate pure cognition lacking systematic necessity is disallowed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX4","text":"The argument presupposes that the totality of pure understanding can be conceptually determined before every element has been exhibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX5","text":"The system leaves no internal place for an unassimilated remainder within pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS1","text":"A priori cognition of understanding → isolation of its elements → restriction to pure, intellectual, elementary conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS2","text":"Requirement of completeness → rejection of empirical aggregation → appeal to an idea of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS3","text":"Idea of totality → systematic division of conceptions → determination of the complete table.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS4","text":"Systematic totality → reciprocal test of whole and parts → validation of the correctness and genuineness of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS5","text":"Autonomous pure understanding → self-contained system → division into conceptions and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF1","text":"Dissection of a priori understanding → specification of purity, intellectuality, elementarity, and completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF2","text":"Demand for completeness → exclusion of empirical enumeration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF3","text":"Exclusion of enumeration → positing of totality as the organizing idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF4","text":"Organizing totality → systematic division and interconnection of elementary conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF5","text":"Systematic interconnection → whole-to-part test of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:AF6","text":"Self-sufficient understanding → twofold architecture of concepts and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ME1","text":"Completeness is relocated from the accumulation of content to the structure of a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ME2","text":"The boundary between pure understanding and sensibility becomes a constitutive boundary of the science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ME3","text":"Classification is transformed from inventory-making into the determination of necessary relations within a totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ME4","text":"Individual cognitions become assessable through their systematic position rather than through isolated examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ME5","text":"Transcendental logic is mapped as an internally articulated domain with concepts as one level and principles as another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate task is the analysis of a priori cognition into elementary conceptions of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT2","text":"Structural — The possibility of a complete table depends on systematic totality, internal division, and reciprocal relation between whole and parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces empirical discovery with architectonic determination as the method for securing completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Pure understanding is presupposed as an autonomous, self-sufficient unity whose totality can guide the determination of its elements prior to their complete exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from transcendental analysis to systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between purity, intellectuality, elementarity, and completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s central methodological opposition between systematic determination and empirical aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT4","text":"The exact contents of the elementary conceptions and principles remain underdetermined because the table itself is deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as autonomy, totality, systematic division, and whole-to-part validation mutually reinforce one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT6","text":"A structural tension remains between the claim that pure understanding is self-sufficient and the need for an idea of totality to determine its completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT7","text":"A second tension remains between using systematic completeness to validate the parts and presupposing the totality required to construct that system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement but cannot independently establish whether the promised table is actually complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","context":"Chapter 22","original_title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","original_context":"Chapter 22"},"original_title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC","layer":"CH","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","order":23,"parents":["RAW_BA94_BIAC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_BIAC@R001","sha256":"dbe59fb67be678d9547f357a2b4d7a92bd7e8a77d32215417bc5fdf6f868a09e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine how conceptions à priori are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AS2","text":"The inquiry is reframed from analysing given conceptions to analysing the faculty of understanding that produces them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AS3","text":"The understanding is treated as the proper birthplace of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AS4","text":"Transcendental philosophy investigates the pure use of this faculty, while general philosophy retains the logical treatment of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AS5","text":"Pure conceptions are traced from their germs in understanding through their empirical occasions to their separation from empirical conditions and presentation in purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A1","text":"An analytic of conceptions is not primarily the dissection of the content of given conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A2","text":"The faculty of understanding can itself be analysed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A3","text":"The possibility of conceptions à priori must be investigated through the understanding alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A4","text":"The understanding is the birthplace of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A5","text":"Transcendental philosophy is concerned with the pure use of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A6","text":"The logical treatment of conceptions belongs to philosophy in general rather than to the distinctive task of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","text":"Pure conceptions possess germs and beginnings in the human understanding prior to their empirical development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","text":"Experience provides occasions on which conceptions already rooted in understanding are developed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A9","text":"The understanding can remove empirical conditions from conceptions developed through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:A10","text":"A conception can be distinguished from the empirical conditions through which it becomes manifest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:NX1","text":"An analytic of conceptions is not the ordinary clarification of conceptions by dissecting their content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:NX2","text":"The origin of conceptions à priori is not sought in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:NX3","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not reducible to the logical treatment of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:NX4","text":"Pure conceptions are not constituted by the empirical conditions under which they are first developed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:NX5","text":"The inquiry does not begin with already presented conceptions as its ultimate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D1","text":"The possibility of a priori cognition requires an inquiry into the producing faculty rather than only into the products of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A1","CH_BA94_BIAC:A2","CH_BA94_BIAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D2","text":"The analysis of the understanding must be transcendental because it concerns the conditions under which conceptions can be possible prior to experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A2","CH_BA94_BIAC:A3","CH_BA94_BIAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D3","text":"The purity of a conception is determined by the understanding’s capacity to distinguish its own contribution from empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A4","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9","CH_BA94_BIAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D4","text":"Experience functions as an activating occasion for pure conceptual germs rather than as their sufficient origin.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D5","text":"The investigation must proceed genetically from the beginnings of conceptions in understanding to their developed and purified form.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:D6","text":"The distinction between transcendental and general philosophy is established by separating the investigation of the faculty’s pure operation from the logical handling of its conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A5","CH_BA94_BIAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SD1","text":"The text establishes a layered model of conception in which origin, activation, empirical contamination, and purification are distinct moments.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A3","CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SD2","text":"The understanding is simultaneously the source of a priori form, the agent of conceptual development, and the instrument of purification.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A2","CH_BA94_BIAC:A4","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SD3","text":"The text converts the relation between a priori and empirical cognition from a simple opposition into a sequence of germination, occasion, development, and abstraction.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A3","CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SD4","text":"The legitimacy of transcendental philosophy depends on preserving both the non-empirical origin of pure conceptions and their empirical occasions of manifestation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A3","CH_BA94_BIAC:A5","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the claim that conceptual necessity can be established by analysing only the content of concepts already in use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes an empiricist account in which experience generates the conceptual forms it merely occasions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a faculty-transcendent source of pure conceptions; the relevant source must be internal to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a conception of purity as absolute isolation from experience; purity is achieved through the removal of empirical conditions from an empirically occasioned development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a static account of a priori conceptions by requiring their recovery through germs, development, and purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX1","text":"The argument cannot permit experience to be either the complete origin or an irrelevant background to pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses a separation between the understanding as source and the understanding as historically activated by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX3","text":"The system leaves no legitimate account of conception in which empirical development alters the a priori ground itself; development must expose or elaborate what understanding already contains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX4","text":"The text’s force depends on the unavailability of a neutral standpoint outside both the understanding and its empirical occasions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS1","text":"Given conceptions are encountered → their content-based analysis is rejected as insufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS2","text":"The object of inquiry shifts from conceptual products to the faculty that produces them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS3","text":"The understanding is isolated as the birthplace of conceptions à priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS4","text":"The pure use of understanding is assigned to transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS5","text":"Pure conceptual germs are traced through occasions supplied by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS6","text":"Empirical conditions are removed by the same understanding that developed the conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS7","text":"The resulting conceptions are presented as unalloyed and a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF1","text":"Content-analysis of given conceptions → inadequacy of the ordinary analytic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF2","text":"Inadequacy → analysis of the understanding itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF3","text":"Analysis of understanding → identification of its a priori conceptual germs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF4","text":"A priori germs + empirical occasions → developmental account of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF5","text":"Developmental account → separation of conceptual form from empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:AF6","text":"Separation → transcendental presentation of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ME1","text":"The object of analysis is reclassified from concepts as contents to understanding as a generative faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ME2","text":"Experience is reclassified from origin of a priori conceptions to occasion for their development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3","text":"Purity is reclassified from absence of experiential contact to abstraction from empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ME4","text":"Transcendental philosophy is separated from general logical analysis by assigning each a distinct operative task.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ME5","text":"The history of a conception is divided into its internal germ, empirical activation, and transcendental purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines what is meant by an “Analytic of Conceptions.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument distinguishes the understanding, experience, and the logical treatment of concepts as different functions within one conceptual process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The inquiry is redirected from analysing products to recovering the conditions and operations that generate them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Conceptions are treated as possessing an a priori origin, an empirical developmental occasion, and a later purification into unalloyed form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the shift from ordinary conceptual analysis to transcendental analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT2","text":"The axioms preserve the distinction between a priori origin and empirical occasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT3","text":"The axioms preserve the distinction between transcendental philosophy and general logical treatment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if empirical development is understood as activation rather than production of pure conceptual germs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT5","text":"The principal unresolved tension lies between the claim that conceptions are found in understanding alone and the claim that they develop on occasions supplied by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction depends on treating purification as an operation performed by understanding rather than as the discovery of concepts untouched by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT7","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s movement from rejected content-analysis to faculty-analysis, genetic tracing, and purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","context":"Chapter 23","original_title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","original_context":"Chapter 23"},"original_title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3","layer":"CH","title":"Introductory § 3","order":24,"parents":["RAW_BA94_INT3"],"revision":"CH_BA94_INT3@R001","sha256":"5a8cf9d97ca04082d59fba2b10509d1e345b93787ff509ef378d7cf3e2b3b270","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AS1","text":"The problem is that mechanically discovered conceptions do not yield a determinate or systematic totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AS2","text":"Mere resemblance and graded complexity organize conceptions without establishing their necessary order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AS3","text":"Transcendental philosophy reframes the task as the principled discovery of conceptions arising purely from the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AS4","text":"Because pure conceptions are treated as expressions of an absolute unity, their relations must be governed by a single ordering conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AS5","text":"The governing principle makes possible both the assignment of each conception to its proper place and the a priori determination of the system’s completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A1","text":"The uncontrolled exercise of a cognitive faculty produces conceptions according to variable circumstances and degrees of investigative effort.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A2","text":"Mechanically discovered conceptions do not disclose in advance where their accumulation will end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A3","text":"Conceptions discovered without a governing principle lack systematic order and unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A4","text":"Resemblance and increasing quantity of content can arrange conceptions into series without generating a systematic structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A5","text":"Transcendental philosophy must seek pure conceptions according to a principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A6","text":"Pure conceptions arise unmixed from the understanding as an absolute unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A7","text":"Conceptions arising from an absolute unity must be connected according to one conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A8","text":"A governing connection supplies a rule for assigning each pure conception its proper place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:A9","text":"The same governing connection permits the a priori determination of the system’s completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:NX1","text":"The discovery of conceptions is not sufficient merely because additional conceptions continue to manifest themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:NX2","text":"A mechanically accumulated collection is not a determinate system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:NX3","text":"Resemblance is not a sufficient principle of systematic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:NX4","text":"A series from the simpler to the more complex is not thereby a systematic order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:NX5","text":"The completeness of the system is not to depend on choice or chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:D1","text":"Without a governing principle, the inventory of conceptions remains open-ended and its endpoint cannot be established.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A1","CH_BA94_INT3:A2","CH_BA94_INT3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:D2","text":"Similarity can produce classificatory proximity without establishing the necessary relation among conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A3","CH_BA94_INT3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:D3","text":"Systematic unity requires more than ordering conceptions by their amount or complexity of content.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A3","CH_BA94_INT3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:D4","text":"If pure conceptions originate in one unity, their systematic articulation must be generated from that unity rather than assembled externally.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A5","CH_BA94_INT3:A6","CH_BA94_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:D5","text":"The principle that orders the conceptions also functions as a criterion for identifying what belongs to the system.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A7","CH_BA94_INT3:A8","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SD1","text":"The passage converts the problem of discovering conceptions into the problem of deriving and locating them within a closed systematic order.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A2","CH_BA94_INT3:A5","CH_BA94_INT3:A8","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SD2","text":"Systematicity is treated as a condition of epistemic legitimacy rather than as an optional improvement in presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A3","CH_BA94_INT3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SD3","text":"The same unity that grounds the conceptions supplies both their internal relations and the possibility of proving that none are missing.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A6","CH_BA94_INT3:A7","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SD4","text":"A priori completeness becomes possible only when classification is subordinated to derivation from a common principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A4","CH_BA94_INT3:A7","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the assumption that the totality of pure conceptions can be secured by indefinite empirical or psychological observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that accumulation eventually reveals its own completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a pluralism of conceptions lacking a unifying ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:DNX4","text":"The system excludes external or accidental relations as adequate grounds for the internal organization of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the separation of classification from the question of whether the inventory is complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SNX1","text":"The argument depends on the impossibility of treating the understanding as a merely additive source of independent conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SNX2","text":"It forecloses a conception of knowledge in which order is imposed only after discovery and has no grounding in the faculty itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SNX3","text":"It treats an unprovable boundary between the known and the still undiscovered as incompatible with a complete system of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:SNX4","text":"The argument’s force depends on denying that contingency can remain at the foundation of transcendental classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:FGS1","text":"Variable cognitive exercise → contingent manifestation of conceptions → indeterminate accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:FGS2","text":"Indeterminate accumulation → resemblance-based association and complexity-based sequencing → non-systematic collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:FGS3","text":"Non-systematic collection → demand for a governing principle supplied by transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:FGS4","text":"Pure origin in the understanding → presupposed absolute unity → connection through one conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:FGS5","text":"Governing connection → rule of proper placement → a priori criterion of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AF1","text":"Mechanical discovery → open-ended accumulation → resemblance-based ordering → failure of systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AF2","text":"Failure of systematic unity → transcendental demand for a principle → derivation from absolute unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:AF3","text":"Derivation from absolute unity → rule for assigning proper places → a priori determination of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ME1","text":"The object of inquiry shifts from collecting conceptions to determining the principle that generates and orders them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ME2","text":"Classification is reclassified as a test of systematic necessity rather than a convenient arrangement of similarities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ME3","text":"Completeness becomes an a priori structural question rather than an empirical result of continued investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ME4","text":"The understanding is mapped as an internally unified source whose conceptions are related by derivational order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The passage contrasts the accidental discovery of conceptions with their principled ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ALT2","text":"Structural — A system requires an internal rule that determines relations, placement, and boundaries among its elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Transcendental philosophy claims authority by replacing contingent collection with principled derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Pure conceptions are treated as originating in an absolute unity of the understanding, allowing systematic completeness to be determined a priori rather than through temporal accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from uncontrolled discovery to principled systematicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between associative classification and necessary systematic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the coupled functions of the governing principle: proper placement and determination of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT4","text":"The central tension is that the passage asserts an absolute unity and a single ordering idea without yet specifying their content or derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of method, but its concrete completeness remains underdetermined until the governing principle is exhibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_INT3:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement: contingent manifestation → failure of accidental order → principled unity → a priori systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"Introductory § 3","context":"Chapter 24","original_title":"Introductory § 3","original_context":"Chapter 24"},"original_title":"Introductory § 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","order":25,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SILU"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SILU@R001","sha256":"8dcb7db421cf7bfe60c48bc8cc3d76cb47fe62fe87df44852fc5f7a12affc2c7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine the understanding independently of sensibility without treating it as an intuitive faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS2","text":"The argument distinguishes intuition, conception, and judgment as successive modes of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS3","text":"Human cognition is reframed as discursive because the understanding cognizes through conceptions rather than intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS4","text":"Conceptions are grounded in spontaneous functions that unify diverse representations under common representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS5","text":"Because concepts relate to objects only mediately, the understanding operates through judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AS6","text":"The complete classification of judgment-functions is presented as the route to exhibiting all functions of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A1","text":"The understanding is a non-sensuous faculty of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A2","text":"Independent of sensibility, the understanding cannot possess intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A3","text":"Human cognition proceeds either through intuition or through conception, and cognition through conception is discursive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A4","text":"Sensuous intuitions depend on receptivity to affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A5","text":"Conceptions depend on the spontaneity of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A6","text":"A function is the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A7","text":"The understanding can use conceptions only by judging through them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A8","text":"Only intuition relates immediately to an object; a conception relates to an object through another representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A9","text":"A judgment is the mediate cognition of an object and therefore represents a representation of that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A10","text":"Every judgment contains a conception that applies to multiple conceptions and includes a representation immediately connected with an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A11","text":"Judgments unify representations by subsuming multiple possible cognitions under a higher representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A12","text":"All acts of the understanding can be reduced to judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A13","text":"A conception is a predicate of a possible judgment because other representations are contained under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:A14","text":"The functions of the understanding can be discovered by completely exhibiting the functions of unity in judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX1","text":"The understanding is not a sensuous faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX2","text":"The understanding is not a faculty of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX3","text":"Human cognition through the understanding is not intuitive but discursive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX4","text":"A conception does not relate immediately to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX5","text":"A judgment is not immediate cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX6","text":"The understanding does not use conceptions apart from judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:NX7","text":"A conception is not an isolated representation detached from relations of containment and predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D1","text":"The distinction between receptivity and spontaneity assigns intuition and conception to different sources of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A4","CH_BA94_SILU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D2","text":"Since conceptions depend on functions, conceptual cognition requires the unification of otherwise diverse representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A5","CH_BA94_SILU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D3","text":"Since the understanding can use conceptions only in judging, thought by the understanding is structurally judgmental.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D4","text":"Since concepts lack immediate object-relation, object-cognition by the understanding requires mediation through representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A8","CH_BA94_SILU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D5","text":"A judgment connects a higher-order conceptual unity with a representation that is directly related to an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A9","CH_BA94_SILU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D6","text":"The unity of a judgment permits many possible cognitions to be collected into one cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A10","CH_BA94_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D7","text":"The analysis of judgment is sufficient in principle for the analysis of the understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SILU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:D8","text":"A concept relates to an object only through the representations subsumed under it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A8","CH_BA94_SILU:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SD1","text":"The understanding is constituted not by direct access to objects but by organized mediation among representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6","CH_BA94_SILU:A8","CH_BA94_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SD2","text":"Judgment is the fundamental operational form through which spontaneity becomes determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A5","CH_BA94_SILU:A7","CH_BA94_SILU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SD3","text":"The logical structure of judgments functions as the access-point for reconstructing the general structure of conceptual thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SILU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SD4","text":"Object-directed cognition by the understanding is necessarily layered: object-related representation, conceptual subsumption, and judgmental unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A8","CH_BA94_SILU:A9","CH_BA94_SILU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:DNX1","text":"Cognition by the understanding is not a self-sufficient intuition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:DNX2","text":"Conceptual universality is not produced by immediate resemblance between concept and object but by the subsumption of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:DNX3","text":"The unity of cognition is not supplied by the object alone; it is enacted through the spontaneous activity of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:DNX4","text":"The understanding is not analyzed through its contents alone but through the functions by which it unifies representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SNX1","text":"Immediate intellectual intuition is structurally unavailable to the human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SNX2","text":"An object cannot enter conceptual cognition without passing through a representational order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:SNX3","text":"No account of the understanding can bypass the formal organization of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:FGS1","text":"Sensible affection → receptive intuition → representation immediately related to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:FGS2","text":"Spontaneous thought → function of unification → conception under which diverse representations are arranged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:FGS3","text":"Conception → predication and subsumption → judgment as mediate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:FGS4","text":"Judgmental unity → collection of multiple possible cognitions → systematic analysis of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:FGS5","text":"Complete exhibition of judgment-functions → complete exhibition of understanding-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:AF1","text":"Negative definition of understanding → exclusion of intellectual intuition → distinction between receptivity and spontaneity → conception as function of unification → judgment as the only use of conception → mediate object-cognition → reduction of understanding to judgment → classification of judgment-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ME1","text":"The understanding is reclassified from a faculty that might receive objects directly into a faculty that organizes representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ME2","text":"Judgment becomes the structural hinge between conceptual thought and object-directed cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ME3","text":"The analysis of cognition shifts from the relation of isolated concepts to the functions of unity operating in judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ME4","text":"The distinction between receptivity and spontaneity replaces a simple subject-object model with a two-source architecture of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ALT1","text":"Surface — The understanding is defined as a faculty that cognizes through conceptions and judges by means of them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is organized through the relation among affection, intuition, representation, conception, and judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The route to the general structure of the understanding is shifted from introspective description to the systematic enumeration of judgment-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human cognition presupposes a non-immediate relation to objects in which object-directedness is constituted through ordered mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the negative definition of understanding to its reduction to judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the distinction between intuition as receptive and conception as spontaneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT3","text":"The derivational structure explains why judgment mediates between concept and object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT4","text":"The final transition to the next section remains programmatic because the actual classification of judgment-functions is not included here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as every higher-order claim depends on the exclusion of immediate conceptual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILU:MRT6","text":"The unresolved tension is that cognition is object-directed while the understanding never relates to the object immediately; this tension is retained as the constitutive condition of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","context":"Chapter 25","original_title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","original_context":"Chapter 25"},"original_title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","order":26,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SILF"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SILF@R001","sha256":"d668d99110636e301231a059cc1caeb293b139eb8b105f1e40097eb4eeefdd48","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS1","text":"Judgement is analysed by abstracting from its material content and isolating the intellectual functions through which thought orders a given cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS2","text":"The complete logical function of judgement divides into quantity, quality, relation, and modality, each with three irreducible momenta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS3","text":"General logic’s classifications are insufficient where they suppress distinctions required for evaluating cognition according to its transcendental significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS4","text":"Singular and infinite judgements acquire independent status when judgement is considered as cognition rather than merely as an instrument of syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS5","text":"Relations of judgement organize predicates, consequences, and mutually delimiting divisions into determinate forms of conceptual connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS6","text":"Modality orders the status of a judgement for thought as possible, actual, or necessary without adding determinate material content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AS7","text":"The table establishes a complete set of elementary functions through which the understanding judges and through which pure cognition can subsequently be investigated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A1","text":"The intellectual form of judgement can be considered independently of the content judged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A2","text":"The functions of thought in judgement are exhaustively ordered under quantity, quality, relation, and modality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A3","text":"Each fundamental function of judgement contains three distinct momenta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A4","text":"The logical treatment of a judgement may differ from its transcendental treatment because general logic abstracts from cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A5","text":"A singular judgement is formally usable as universal in syllogistic inference but is quantitatively distinct as cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A6","text":"An infinite judgement affirms a negative predicate while providing only a limitative determination of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A7","text":"Categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive judgements respectively express subject-predicate connection, ground-consequence connection, and the reciprocal ordering of divided cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A8","text":"A hypothetical judgement asserts the connection of propositions without deciding the truth of its component propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A9","text":"A disjunctive judgement combines mutual exclusion among alternatives with their collective completion of one cognitive sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A10","text":"Modality concerns the validity of the copula in relation to thought rather than the content of the judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A11","text":"Problematical, assertorical, and apodeictical judgements represent a proposition respectively as logically possible, logically actual, and logically necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:A12","text":"Logical necessity arises when an assertorical judgement is represented as determined by the laws of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX1","text":"The table is not limited to the classifications needed for syllogistic use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX2","text":"Singular judgement is not identical with universal judgement merely because both can function equivalently in inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX3","text":"Infinite judgement is not simply affirmative judgement despite its affirmative logical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX4","text":"The exclusion of a predicate-class is not a positive determination of the subject’s content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX5","text":"Hypothetical relation is not an assertion that either antecedent or consequent is true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX6","text":"Disjunction is not merely an aggregate of alternatives; it is a structured totality of reciprocal exclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX7","text":"Problematical judgement is not objective possibility or objective assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:NX8","text":"Modality is not an additional constituent of a judgement’s material content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D1","text":"A complete transcendental table must preserve distinctions that formal logic may legitimately collapse for inferential purposes.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A4","CH_BA94_SILF:A5","CH_BA94_SILF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D2","text":"Singular judgement marks cognition as a unity rather than as an instance of general extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D3","text":"Infinite judgement narrows the field in which an object may be placed without positively specifying what the object is.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D4","text":"Hypothetical judgement permits conditional cognition to be used even when its component propositions remain undecided or false.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A8","CH_BA94_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D5","text":"Disjunctive judgement determines a cognition by assigning it to one complementary sphere and thereby excluding the others.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D6","text":"Logical possibility functions as a provisional admissibility of a proposition within thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A10","CH_BA94_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:D7","text":"The progression from problematic to assertorical to apodeictical judgement represents increasing incorporation of a proposition into the understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A11","CH_BA94_SILF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SD1","text":"The table transforms logical forms from a taxonomy of propositions into functions that measure distinct ways cognition can be determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A2","CH_BA94_SILF:A4","CH_BA94_SILF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SD2","text":"Negative determination is structurally indispensable because cognition can be delimited without being materially enriched.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A6","CH_BA94_SILF:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SD3","text":"The understanding can organize truth-directed inquiry through propositions that are not themselves asserted as true.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A8","CH_BA94_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SD4","text":"Completeness of cognition requires both positive connections and an articulated field of exclusions among possible determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A6","CH_BA94_SILF:A7","CH_BA94_SILF:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SD5","text":"Necessity is represented as a relation to the understanding’s lawful activity rather than as an additional predicate belonging to the object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A10","CH_BA94_SILF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:DNX1","text":"Cognitive quantity cannot be reduced to the extension relevant to formal inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:DNX2","text":"Logical affirmation cannot be equated with positive cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:DNX3","text":"Conditional intelligibility does not require prior truth of every proposition employed in a relation of consequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:DNX4","text":"Completeness is not obtained by eliminating alternatives but by preserving their reciprocal boundaries within a common sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:DNX5","text":"Necessity is not derived from repeated assertion but from lawful determination by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SNX1","text":"No unmediated access to an object’s positive determination is available through infinite judgement, which only removes an excluded region.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SNX2","text":"No proposition is granted objective standing merely because the understanding can provisionally admit it as possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SNX3","text":"No complete logical architecture can rest on isolated propositions, because determination requires relations of consequence, exclusion, and totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:SNX4","text":"No modal status is treated as a property added to objects independently of their relation to thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS1","text":"Abstraction from judged content → isolation of intellectual form → four fundamental functions of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS2","text":"Formal-logical equivalence → transcendental differentiation of cognitive worth → independent status of singular and infinite judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS3","text":"Predicate relation / consequence relation / divisional reciprocity → categorical / hypothetical / disjunctive forms of connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS4","text":"Logical possibility → accepted logical actuality → law-determined logical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS5","text":"Mutual exclusion among alternatives → collective completion of a cognitive sphere → determinate assignment of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:FGS6","text":"Limitation without positive enrichment → negative determination → transcendental relevance of infinite judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF1","text":"Abstraction from content → classification of forms → fourfold table of judgement-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF2","text":"Formal-logical simplification → recognition of cognitive distinctions → separation of singular from universal judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF3","text":"Logical affirmation by negation → limitative cognitive value → separation of infinite from affirmative judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF4","text":"Forms of relation → predicate, consequence, and reciprocal division → categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF5","text":"Copular validity for thought → possibility, actuality, necessity → modal sequence of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:AF6","text":"Complete functional table → groundwork for inquiry into pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ME1","text":"The distinction between formal validity and transcendental cognitive significance redraws logic as more than an instrument of syllogistic classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ME2","text":"Negative predicates become visible as operators of limitation rather than as sources of positive object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ME3","text":"Disjunction becomes a structure of jointly exhaustive and mutually exclusive cognitive positions rather than a merely alternative syntax.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ME4","text":"Modal terms become statuses of a proposition’s relation to thought rather than degrees of objective being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ME5","text":"Provisional propositions become legitimate components of truth-directed cognition without being mistaken for truths.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ALT1","text":"Surface — Judgements are classified by quantity, quality, relation, and modality, each containing three momenta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ALT2","text":"Structural — The classification differentiates inferential form, cognitive quantity, limitative content, relational architecture, and modal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — General logic is subordinated to a transcendental procedure that asks what each form contributes to possible a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Necessity is produced through the progressive integration of judgement into lawful understanding, while possibility remains a merely logical admission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the fourfold table and the justification for its departures from ordinary logical classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT2","text":"The system depends centrally on the distinction between logical form and the transcendental worth of a judgement for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT3","text":"The treatment of singular and infinite judgement is coherent only if inferential equivalence is kept distinct from cognitive identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT4","text":"The account of relation is strongest where consequence and reciprocal exclusion are treated as different forms of conceptual connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT5","text":"The account of modality remains structurally tense because modality contributes nothing to content while still functions as a necessary moment of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT6","text":"The transition from logical possibility to logical necessity relies on the unexplicated authority of the laws of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SILF:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if negative limitation, provisional assumption, and disjunctive totalization remain distinct operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 26","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","context":"Chapter 26","original_title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","original_context":"Chapter 26"},"original_title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","order":27,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIPC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIPC@R001","sha256":"9d106db0672e8cc40badde6a69cea7337519c4b4e2c06db177ddb98c37904f4c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how pure concepts of the understanding can yield a priori cognition of objects rather than merely formal conceptual order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS2","text":"The argument relocates the origin of cognition from analysis of given concepts to synthesis of a manifold supplied through pure sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS3","text":"Cognition requires a sequence in which intuition supplies diversity, imagination combines it, and understanding represents its necessary unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS4","text":"The unifying function exercised in judgment is identified with the function that unifies the manifold of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS5","text":"The logical forms of all possible judgments therefore determine the exact inventory of pure concepts applicable a priori to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS6","text":"The category table is defended as a systematic and exhaustive derivation, then extended downward into deduced concepts while excluding illegitimate additions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AS7","text":"The argument moves from conditions of cognition to an architectonic program for rational science and a restriction on metaphysical predicate-making.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A1","text":"General logic abstracts from all content of cognition and can only analyse representations received from another source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A2","text":"Transcendental logic requires the manifold of pure a priori sensibility as its content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A3","text":"Representations must be given before they can be analysed, and analysis cannot generate the content of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A4","text":"Synthesis joins a manifold of representations into one cognition and is the first requisite of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A5","text":"Imagination performs the indispensable synthesis of the manifold without thereby producing cognition in the proper sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A6","text":"Pure synthesis requires an a priori synthetic unity in order to yield a pure concept of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A7","text":"Understanding supplies concepts that represent the necessary synthetic unity required for cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","text":"The function that unifies representations in judgment also unifies the manifold in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","text":"There are exactly as many pure concepts of the understanding as there are logical functions in all possible judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A10","text":"The category table is derived systematically from the faculty of judgment rather than assembled inductively from encountered concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A11","text":"Original categories generate pure but deduced concepts through their combination with one another and with modes of pure sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A12","text":"The third category in each triad arises through combination of the first two while requiring a distinct and primitive act of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A13","text":"Mathematical categories concern objects of intuition, whereas dynamical categories concern existence and relations of objects to one another or to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A14","text":"Community represents reciprocal co-ordination among independently existing substances rather than a one-way causal sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:A15","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection are logical criteria of cognition derived from transformed categories of quantity, not additional categories or properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX1","text":"General logic is not a source of transcendental content or of pure concepts applicable a priori to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX2","text":"Analysis is not the origin of conceptual content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX3","text":"Mere receptivity to an intuitive manifold is not cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX4","text":"Imaginative synthesis alone is not cognition in the proper sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX5","text":"The category catalogue is not an empirical, rhapsodic, or Aristotelian-style inventory gathered without a guiding principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX6","text":"Modes of pure sensibility, empirical concepts, and deduced concepts are not original categories of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX7","text":"Predicables are not primitive concepts even when they are pure and a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX8","text":"Third categories are not merely derivative composites of the first and second categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX9","text":"Community is not unilateral determination of an effect by a cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX10","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection are not transcendental predicates that enlarge the category table.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX11","text":"Logical conditions of coherent cognition are not properties attributable to objects as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:NX12","text":"A complete vocabulary and definitions of categories are not required for the immediate critical task.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D1","text":"Analysis can operate only upon a manifold already synthesized into a provisional cognitive unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A3","CH_BA94_SIPC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D2","text":"Cognition of an object requires the ordered cooperation of sensibility, imagination, and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A5","CH_BA94_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D3","text":"Pure concepts acquire transcendental rather than merely logical significance by representing the necessary unity of intuitive synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D4","text":"The logical table of judgments becomes a complete source-table for categories because judgment and intuition are unified by the same function of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D5","text":"The category inventory can claim completeness only if the logical functions of judgment are themselves complete and exhaustive.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D6","text":"Deduced concepts can be generated systematically without expanding the inventory of original categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D7","text":"A category may be structurally composite while remaining primitive because the act of combination has irreducible cognitive content.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D8","text":"The distinction between mathematical and dynamical categories determines two different domains of a priori conceptual ordering.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D9","text":"Reciprocal interaction constitutes a whole of co-ordinated substances that cannot be reduced to a linear causal relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:D10","text":"Scholastic transcendental predicates are reclassified as tests of the consistency and completeness of cognition rather than as ontological additions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD1","text":"The objective applicability of categories is grounded in their role as conditions for synthesizing an intuitive manifold into object-cognition, not in empirical abstraction or independent ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A4","CH_BA94_SIPC:A7","CH_BA94_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2","text":"The system combines finite closure at the level of original categories with indefinite derivational expansion at the level of predicables.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD3","text":"The category table functions simultaneously as an inventory of primitive concepts, a rule for classifying derivative concepts, and a blueprint for rational science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:A10","CH_BA94_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD4","text":"The triadic form of the table prevents higher conceptual unities from being reduced to an additive sum of simpler determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD5","text":"The relation category of community makes simultaneous mutual determination intelligible without assimilating it to causal succession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A13","CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6","text":"The completed table limits metaphysics by preventing logical norms of thought from being converted into predicates of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A10","CH_BA94_SIPC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX1","text":"The intuitive manifold must not contain or generate its own necessary unity independently of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX2","text":"Pure concepts must not receive cognitive content independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX3","text":"Logical forms of judgment must not be merely subjective classificatory devices disconnected from the synthesis of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX4","text":"The category inventory must not exceed, fall short of, or remain independent from the complete table of logical functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX5","text":"A metaphysical predicate must not be validated merely because it expresses a formal condition of coherent cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX6","text":"The faculties of cognition must be sufficiently determinate and finite for an architectonic enumeration of their primitive functions to be possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses cognitive entitlement to characterize objects wholly independently of the syntheses through which they become objects of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses an ontology whose primitive vocabulary is selected by inherited metaphysical usage, semantic convenience, or empirical accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses a passage from pure logical form alone to determinate cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX4","text":"The system forecloses an indefinitely revisable category list whose completeness cannot be grounded in a prior systematic principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS1","text":"A priori sensibility supplies a manifold of intuition that is given but not yet cognized as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS2","text":"Imagination synthesizes the manifold into a connected representation without yet supplying objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3","text":"Understanding represents the necessary unity of synthesis through pure concepts and thereby makes object-cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS4","text":"The unifying operation in judgment is identified with the unifying operation imposed upon intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS5","text":"The complete table of logical judgment-functions determines the complete inventory of original categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS6","text":"Original categories generate subordinate predicables through regulated combinations with one another and with modes of pure sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7","text":"Taxonomic exclusions remove empirical concepts, sensible modes, derived concepts, and merely logical criteria from the register of primitive categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS8","text":"The purified category table becomes an architectonic schema for the division, completion, and critique of a priori rational science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AF1","text":"General logic’s abstraction from content → pure sensible manifold → priority of synthesis over analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AF2","text":"Given manifold → imaginative connection → conceptual representation of necessary unity → cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AF3","text":"Unity in judgment → unity in intuition → logical functions → original categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AF4","text":"Original categories → derivable predicables → systematic conceptual genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:AF5","text":"Systematic table → exclusion of false categories and predicates → architectonic plan for metaphysics and rational science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME1","text":"The origin of cognition is relocated from analytic decomposition to pre-analytic synthetic unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME2","text":"Categories are reclassified from a collection of high-order concepts into functions required for thinking an intuitive object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME3","text":"The distinction between original categories and predicables permits conceptual expansion without inflation of the primitive inventory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME4","text":"The distinction between mathematical and dynamical categories redraws the field between the constitution of objects of intuition and the ordering of existence and relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME5","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection are shifted from alleged ontological predicates to formal criteria governing cognition’s consistency with itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ME6","text":"Community becomes visible as reciprocal co-ordination within a whole rather than as a variant of one-directional causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text enumerates categories, distinguishes them from predicables, and organizes their classes, triads, and relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is produced through the ordered dependence of sensible manifold, imaginative synthesis, and conceptual unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — A systematic derivation from judgment replaces empirical cataloguing and regulates which concepts may enter transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Epistemic — Objects are cognizable only under a priori conditions of synthesis, while logical conditions of cognition are denied direct status as properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central movement from sensible manifold through synthesis to categories and systematic conceptual architecture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT2","text":"The derivation of categories depends on the asserted identity between unification in judgment and unification in intuition, but this identity is stated more than demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT3","text":"The claim of exhaustive completeness depends on the completeness of the logical table of judgments, which functions as a premise rather than a result of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT4","text":"The triadic doctrine creates a pressure point because third categories are described as generated through combination while also retained as primitive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT5","text":"The distinction between categories, predicables, sensible modes, empirical concepts, and logical criteria is structurally necessary but depends on classificatory judgments not fully formalized in the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT6","text":"The correspondence of community to disjunctive judgment is the explicitly difficult case and reveals the weakest point in the category-to-judgment mapping.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT7","text":"The restriction against treating logical criteria as properties of things in themselves coheres with the system only if applicability to objects is confined to objects as cognized under synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement, but detailed definitions of categories and the full derivation of their objective validity remain deliberately deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 27","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","context":"Chapter 27","original_title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","original_context":"Chapter 27"},"original_title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","order":28,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIPT"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIPT@R001","sha256":"024b26b3943c1fd58f6a9727a8a57da8ef8b35b7984ecf72692d33e1275494ab","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS1","text":"Pure concepts require a juridical justification of their objective employment, not merely an account of their factual acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS2","text":"Transcendental deduction is required because pure a priori concepts claim applicability to objects independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS3","text":"Space and time can be justified through their role as forms under which objects are given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS4","text":"Categories generate a distinct problem because they are conditions of thought rather than conditions under which objects are intuitively given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS5","text":"The category of causality exposes the problem because necessary lawful connection cannot be derived from observed regularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AS6","text":"Critical philosophy must either establish the objective validity and limits of pure concepts or abandon claims to cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A1","text":"The factual origin of a concept does not establish its right to apply to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A2","text":"A deduction establishes the legitimate objective employment of a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A3","text":"Pure a priori concepts claim application to objects without derivation from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A4","text":"The legitimate a priori application of a pure concept requires a transcendental deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A5","text":"An empirical genealogy explains the occasioned production of a concept but cannot justify its a priori validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","text":"Space and time are pure forms of sensibility that condition the appearance of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A7","text":"Objects given in sensuous intuition necessarily conform to the forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","text":"Categories are pure concepts of understanding that determine synthetic unity in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A9","text":"Objects can be given in intuition without thereby being shown to conform to the synthetic conditions of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A10","text":"The objective validity of categories depends on showing that subjective conditions of thought are conditions of cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","text":"The concept of cause asserts necessary and strictly universal lawful connection between distinct events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A12","text":"Experience and induction yield observed regularities but cannot yield necessity or strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A13","text":"Unjustified categories can extend the use of spatial concepts beyond the conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:A14","text":"Cognition claiming validity beyond possible experience requires completed critical justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX1","text":"A transcendental deduction is not an empirical account of how concepts arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX2","text":"Psychological or physiological derivation is not proof of a pure concept’s objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX3","text":"The possession of a concept is not its authorization for objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX4","text":"Intuitive givenness under space and time is not equivalent to categorial validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX5","text":"Observed succession is not necessary causal production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX6","text":"Inductive universality is not strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX7","text":"Geometry’s secure use of space is not a model sufficient to vindicate the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX8","text":"The categories are not empirically manufactured abstractions without loss of their distinctive a priori character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:NX9","text":"Pure reason is not entitled to proceed beyond experience without first determining the legitimate scope of its concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D1","text":"The question of transcendental philosophy concerns the entitlement of concepts to determine objects rather than the history of their acquisition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A1","CH_BA94_SIPT:A2","CH_BA94_SIPT:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D2","text":"The transcendental deduction of space and time can proceed through their constitutive role in the possibility of appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","CH_BA94_SIPT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D3","text":"The transcendental deduction of the categories cannot appeal to an object already given through a corresponding pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_SIPT:A9","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D4","text":"Causality must be grounded in the understanding or else be discarded as an empty concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A3","CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D5","text":"The distinction between empirical law and causal law rests on the distinction between repeated conjunction and necessary production.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D6","text":"The deduction of the categories must simultaneously delimit the legitimate use of space when space is thought under categorial predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_SIPT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:D7","text":"The possibility of experience does not by itself establish the categories unless experience is shown to require their synthetic unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A9","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SD1","text":"Objectivity is redefined as justified necessary applicability to objects, not as the mere presence of an object before consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A1","CH_BA94_SIPT:A2","CH_BA94_SIPT:A9","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SD2","text":"The problem of pure cognition is displaced from the origin of representations to the conditions under which representations can bind objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A3","CH_BA94_SIPT:A4","CH_BA94_SIPT:A5","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SD3","text":"Necessity becomes the decisive mark separating the understanding’s pure synthesis from empirical generalization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SD4","text":"The categories become conditions to be vindicated as constitutive of objective cognition rather than optional conceptual instruments imposed upon independently given phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_SIPT:A9","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SD5","text":"Critique functions as a condition of rational ambition because unexamined a priori concepts otherwise oscillate between empty formalism and illegitimate extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A4","CH_BA94_SIPT:A13","CH_BA94_SIPT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX1","text":"The conformity of appearances to the forms of intuition does not entail their conformity to the laws of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX2","text":"Subjective necessity in thinking cannot be presupposed to be objective necessity in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX3","text":"The order found in experience cannot be treated as self-certifying evidence that experience is necessarily ordered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX4","text":"A priori concepts cannot obtain objective content merely by being internally coherent or universally employed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX5","text":"The authority of pure reason cannot be assumed prior to its self-limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX1","text":"There is no immediate passage from thought’s formal unity to the objectivity of what is thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX2","text":"There is no precritical guarantee that reality must exhibit the lawful unity demanded by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX3","text":"There is no admissible cognition beyond possible experience unless the conditions making experience objectively unified have first been demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX4","text":"The category is structurally threatened by emptiness whenever its relation to intuition remains unproved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS1","text":"Conceptual possession → distinction between factual acquisition and juridical entitlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS2","text":"A priori claim to objective use → insufficiency of empirical genealogy → necessity of transcendental deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS3","text":"Pure intuition conditions appearances directly → demonstrable validity of space and time for phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS4","text":"Pure concepts of synthesis lack corresponding intuitive givenness → uncertainty concerning their objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS5","text":"Causality claims necessity and strict universality → empirical regularity proves insufficient → a priori ground becomes necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS6","text":"Unproved categorial application enables transcendent extension → critique must establish both validity and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:FGS7","text":"Failure of deduction → categories become empty claims → cognition beyond possible experience must be relinquished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AF1","text":"Factual possession of concepts → juridical question of their right → transcendental rather than empirical deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AF2","text":"Space and time as conditions of intuitive givenness → immediate applicability to appearances → contrast with categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AF3","text":"Categories as conditions of thought without intuitive objects → doubt concerning objective validity → causality as decisive test case.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AF4","text":"Empirical regularity lacks necessity → categories require an a priori ground → critical deduction becomes unavoidable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:AF5","text":"Deduction of objective validity and limits → legitimate pure cognition of experience; failure → renunciation of claims beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ME1","text":"Epistemic legitimacy is separated from conceptual origin, converting the problem of knowledge into a problem of right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ME2","text":"Pure intuition and pure understanding are reclassified as non-equivalent a priori powers because one conditions object-givenness and the other claims synthetic determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ME3","text":"Causality is reclassified from an observed empirical pattern into a claim of necessary production requiring transcendental authorization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ME4","text":"Objectivity becomes dependent on a demonstrated relation between conditions of cognition and objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ME5","text":"Critique redraws the boundary between legitimate a priori cognition and transcendent conceptual projection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure concepts, especially causality, require proof that they apply to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sensibility supplies the forms under which objects appear, while understanding supplies a claimed unity not immediately guaranteed by appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument displaces empirical explanation with juridical transcendental justification as the proper procedure for pure cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Necessary lawfulness is distinguished from merely repeated succession, making objective temporal connection irreducible to induction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT5","text":"Critical-Limiting — The validity of pure reason is inseparable from a determination of the boundary beyond which its concepts lack authorized use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from conceptual possession to the necessity of transcendental deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT2","text":"The system depends chiefly on the distinction between conditions of intuitive givenness and conditions of synthetic thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined at the decisive point because it states the required proof that categories condition objects without yet supplying that proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural operation is the separation of empirical origin, empirical confirmation, and transcendental right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT5","text":"The central fault line is that phenomena are declared conceivable without categorial unity while the forthcoming deduction must establish categories as necessary for objective cognition of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT6","text":"The argument relies on necessity as irreducible to induction, but the warrant for that irreducibility is asserted here as a task for deduction rather than established within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if the section is treated as a problem-formulation and methodological closure rather than as the completed deduction itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 28","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","context":"Chapter 28","original_title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","original_context":"Chapter 28"},"original_title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC","layer":"CH","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","order":29,"parents":["RAW_BA94_TTDC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_TTDC@R001","sha256":"7c06a66127ae3ac017fbc03dfd8dd6c74bb3aac142417921bc1a51463a2ba682","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS1","text":"The problem is to establish how pure concepts can necessarily apply to objects without being derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS2","text":"Necessary object-representation correspondence can arise either from objects producing representations or from representations determining objects as objects of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS3","text":"Since intuition supplies objects only as phenomena and conceptual thought supplies their objecthood, experience requires both sensible givenness and a priori conditions for thinking an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS4","text":"The transcendental deduction must prove that categories are conditions of possible experience rather than empirical generalizations found within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS5","text":"Locke’s empiricism and Hume’s habitualism respectively yield uncontrolled transcendence and sceptical restriction because neither grounds the necessary objective validity of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the conditions of object-cognition to the justified limitation of reason to its legitimate experiential domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AS7","text":"Categories are specified as concepts that determine an intuition as an object through the logical functions of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A1","text":"A synthetic representation can necessarily correspond to an object only if the object makes the representation possible or the representation makes the object possible as an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A2","text":"When the object makes representation possible, the representation is empirical and cannot possess a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","text":"A representation can make an object possible without producing the object’s existence by determining the conditions under which it can be cognized as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","text":"Cognition of an object requires intuition through which an object is given and conception through which the given is thought as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A5","text":"The formal conditions of sensibility are a priori conditions under which phenomena can be given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","text":"If concepts of the understanding are conditions under which anything can be thought as an object, every empirical cognition of objects must conform to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A7","text":"Experience contains both sensible intuition and the conception of an object given in that intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","text":"Concepts that ground the possibility of experience possess necessary objective validity for objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A9","text":"The necessity of a priori concepts cannot be deduced from their occurrence in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A10","text":"Scientific a priori cognition in mathematics and general physics cannot be reconciled with an empirical derivation of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A11","text":"Categories are concepts of an object in general that determine intuition in relation to logical functions of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:A12","text":"A category fixes a determinate objective role within an intuition that remains indeterminate under merely logical judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX1","text":"Categories are not empirical concepts abstracted from recurrent experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX2","text":"A priori determination of an object is not production of the object’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX3","text":"The analysis of experiences containing pure concepts is not a transcendental deduction of their necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX4","text":"Objective necessity is not subjective expectation generated by repeated association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX5","text":"The application of categories is not intelligible as an externally imposed relation between independently constituted objects and concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","text":"Pure concepts are not licenses for cognition beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX7","text":"Logical subject-predicate form alone does not determine which empirical concept must function as subject or predicate in an object-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:NX8","text":"Reason is not to be confined by vague moderation, nor abandoned to universal scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D1","text":"The objective validity of the categories depends on their constitutive role in the form of possible experience rather than on empirical confirmation.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A7","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D2","text":"An object of experience can be thought only insofar as its intuition is subsumed under concepts that determine objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D3","text":"The categories apply necessarily to all possible objects of experience, while their validity does not thereby extend to objects outside possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D4","text":"The deduction of categories must be transcendental because its task is to identify enabling conditions, not to report psychological or empirical origins.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D5","text":"Locke’s position cannot explain the necessity of pure concepts while retaining their use in cognition beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A2","CH_BA94_TTDC:A9","CH_BA94_TTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D6","text":"Hume’s reduction of necessity to habit consistently blocks any warranted use of pure concepts beyond experienced conjunctions.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A2","CH_BA94_TTDC:A9","CH_BA94_TTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:D7","text":"Categories transform logical functions of judgment into determinate ways of regarding an intuition as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A11","CH_BA94_TTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SD1","text":"Objectivity is relocated from a correspondence between a ready-made object and a contingent representation to the universal conditions under which anything can count as an object of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A1","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SD2","text":"The understanding is positioned as a formal co-author of experience because it determines the objecthood of what sensibility gives without creating the existence of what is given.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_TTDC:A5","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SD3","text":"The same structure that secures scientific necessity also establishes a principled boundary against transcendent cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A10","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SD4","text":"A legitimate middle position becomes possible only by separating constitutive a priori conditions of experience from both empirical derivation and unrestricted metaphysical use.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A9","CH_BA94_TTDC:A10","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX1","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX1","text":"Objectivity is not grounded in access to objects independently of the conditions through which they can be experienced as objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX2","text":"Necessity is not a stronger form of empirical regularity, repetition, expectation, or psychological compulsion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX3","text":"The understanding is not a passive recipient that merely records relations already given among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX4","text":"The domain of possible cognition is not wider than the domain in which intuition and conceptual determination can be united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX5","text":"The legitimacy of reason is not measured by its capacity to think beyond experience but by its lawful applicability within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX1","text":"A cognition of objects wholly independent of the conditions of possible experience is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX2","text":"A final foundation for categories in either empirical psychology or an independently knowable object-order is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX3","text":"The alternative between dogmatic metaphysical expansion and sceptical renunciation is treated as inadmissible because both follow from misconstruing the source of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS1","text":"Necessary object-representation relation → either empirical affection by objects or a priori determination of objecthood by representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS2","text":"Sensible givenness → a priori forms of intuition → phenomenal availability of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS3","text":"Given intuition + concept of an object → experience as objectively thinkable cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS4","text":"Categories as a priori conditions of object-thought → possibility of experience → necessary objective validity for experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS5","text":"Transcendental grounding of necessity → rejection of empirical derivation → simultaneous exclusion of dogmatism and scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:FGS6","text":"Logical functions of judgment → categorical determination of intuition → determinate objective roles within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF1","text":"The problem of necessary correspondence between representations and objects leads to a distinction between empirical dependence and a priori determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF2","text":"The distinction between intuition and conception establishes the dual condition under which an object can be given and thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF3","text":"The possibility that concepts condition object-thought reframes categories as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF4","text":"The grounding of categories in possible experience yields their objective validity and restricts their use to experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF5","text":"The failure of empirical derivation diagnoses Locke’s excess and Hume’s scepticism as opposite consequences of the same missing ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:AF6","text":"The definition of categories through judgment functions supplies the formal mechanism by which intuition becomes objectively determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ME1","text":"The question of whether categories match objects is reclassified as the question of what makes objects of experience thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ME2","text":"Objectivity becomes a transcendental status grounded in universally necessary conditions of experience rather than an empirical relation inferred from repeated encounters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ME3","text":"Empiricism and scepticism are mapped as structurally linked failures rather than independent philosophical options.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ME4","text":"The boundary of legitimate cognition is redrawn as internal to the conditions that make experience possible, not as an externally imposed prohibition on reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ME5","text":"Logical judgment is reclassified from a merely formal ordering of concepts into the source-form of categorical determination of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines categories and asks how they can validly apply to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Experience requires the conjunction of sensible intuition and conceptual conditions that make what is given thinkable as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The deduction is framed as a route between empiricist overextension and sceptical withdrawal by grounding limits in conditions of legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are cognizable as phenomena only within a structure in which sensible givenness is a priori formed and conceptual objecthood is a priori determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from the problem of correspondence to the claim that categories condition possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT2","text":"The system depends centrally on the distinction between making an object possible as cognized and producing its existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined at the point where the existence and completeness of the categories are asserted rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it distinguishes transcendental justification from empirical illustration and psychological genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses a possible circularity because experience grounds the validity of categories while categories are said to ground the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT6","text":"The relation between logical functions of judgment and their objective determination of intuition is announced as foundational but receives only an initial example.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if its restriction of categories to possible experience is retained alongside their claim to necessary objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 29","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","context":"Chapter 29","original_title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","original_context":"Chapter 29"},"original_title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","order":30,"parents":["RAW_BA94_PCMR"],"revision":"CH_BA94_PCMR@R001","sha256":"4d63c42025f7b772cce3345835806660cd396b1ee9a7d6378ab0b16540ff2513","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS1","text":"The passage determines how conjunction is possible when sense supplies only manifold content and receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS2","text":"It separates the passive givenness of sensibility from the spontaneous activity required to combine representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS3","text":"It identifies this spontaneous combining activity with understanding and names its operation synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS4","text":"It establishes that representing an object as conjoined requires a prior conjunction performed by the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS5","text":"It makes synthesis the condition of analysis and treats conjunction as the representation of the manifold’s synthetical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS6","text":"It distinguishes the unity represented within conjunction from the prior unity that makes conjunction possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AS7","text":"It ascends from categorical unity to a higher qualitative unity that grounds the unity of concepts in judgment and the logical use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","text":"Sensuous intuition can give manifold content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A2","text":"The form of sensuous intuition can exist a priori as the mode in which the subject is affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","text":"Conjunction is not given by the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","text":"Conjunction is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A5","text":"Understanding is the faculty distinguished from sensibility by spontaneous conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A6","text":"Every conjunction is an act of understanding, whether conscious or unconscious, sensuous or non-sensuous, and intuitive or conceptual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A7","text":"Synthesis is the general name for the act of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","text":"Representing anything as conjoined in an object presupposes that the subject has previously conjoined it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A9","text":"The possibility of conjunction is grounded in the nature of the spontaneous act that produces it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A10","text":"The ground of conjunction must hold for every form of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A11","text":"Analysis presupposes a prior synthesis because only what has been conjoined can be analyzed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","text":"Conjunction is the representation of the synthetical unity of the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","text":"The unity represented in conjunction does not arise from conjunction but precedes it as its enabling ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","text":"The unity that precedes conjunction is not the category of unity, because categorical functions of judgment already presuppose conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:A15","text":"A higher qualitative unity grounds the unity of diverse concepts in judgments and thereby grounds the possibility of understanding in its logical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX1","text":"Conjunction is not a sensory datum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX2","text":"Conjunction is not contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX3","text":"Understanding is not identical with sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX4","text":"The conjunction of representations is not given through objects independently of subjective activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX5","text":"Synthesis is not restricted to conscious combinations of sensory intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX6","text":"Analysis is not an operation independent of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX7","text":"Synthetical unity is not produced by the mere aggregation of a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX8","text":"The enabling unity of conjunction is not reducible to the category of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:NX9","text":"The argument does not depend on deciding whether the representations are identical or analytically derivable from one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D1","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding is generated by the difference between receiving a manifold and spontaneously combining it.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D2","text":"All forms of conjunction are unified under the operation called synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D3","text":"Object-conjunction is representationally dependent on prior subjective synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A7","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D4","text":"Analysis is a derivative operation because its object must already have been synthesized.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A9","CH_BA94_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D5","text":"Conjunction is not merely combination but combination under the representation of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D6","text":"The category of unity cannot be the ultimate ground of conjunction because categorical judgment already presupposes conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D7","text":"The transcendental inquiry must move from conjunction and categorical unity to a higher ground of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D8","text":"The passage brackets the identity or analytic reducibility of representations and restricts its claim to their possible synthesis in consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:D9","text":"The logical use of understanding depends on a unity more fundamental than the logical functions expressed in judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD1","text":"A conjoined object is neither a raw sensory manifold nor an independently given combination but the result of receptivity operating together with spontaneity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD2","text":"The argument establishes a stratified order of dependence in which sensible givenness, synthesis, synthetical unity, and higher qualitative unity occupy distinct foundational levels.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A2","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD3","text":"Logical judgment and categorical unity are downstream from a more primitive operation of conjunction and cannot serve as its ultimate explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD4","text":"Subjective synthesis is simultaneously the condition for representing an object as conjoined and the condition for subsequently analyzing it.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD5","text":"The universality of synthesis derives from the structure of the spontaneous act rather than from the particular content being combined.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A9","CH_BA94_PCMR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD6","text":"The apparent circularity between conjunction and unity is resolved by distinguishing the synthetical unity represented in conjunction from the prior unity that enables conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SD7","text":"The argument relocates the foundational problem from the possibility of conjunction to the nature of the higher unity that makes conjunction possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX1","text":"The system cannot be grounded in passive reception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX2","text":"The system cannot treat the conjunction of representations as an independently object-given relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX3","text":"The system cannot derive synthesis directly from sensuous form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX4","text":"The system cannot treat analysis as primitive or as the symmetrical opposite of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX5","text":"The system cannot treat the category of unity as the ultimate ground of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX6","text":"The system cannot explain unity as an aggregate produced after conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX7","text":"The system cannot require separate fundamental mechanisms for conscious, unconscious, sensuous, non-sensuous, intuitive, and conceptual conjunctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX8","text":"The system cannot allow the identity or analytic reducibility of representations to determine the possibility of their synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a purely receptive epistemology in which sensation directly yields a conjoined object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX2","text":"The text excludes a self-grounding logical understanding whose categories could generate conjunction without presupposing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX3","text":"The text excludes any unlayered identity between the unity represented in conjunction and the unity that makes conjunction possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX4","text":"The text excludes self-sufficient poles in which either sensibility or understanding alone produces complete representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX5","text":"The higher qualitative unity is functionally required but not fully specified in its own content or mode of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX6","text":"The passage leaves unavailable a final account of how the subject’s spontaneous synthesis secures the object-directed validity of the conjunction it produces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS1","text":"Sensuous receptivity supplies manifold content under an a priori form of affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS2","text":"The absence of conjunction from sensuous givenness necessitates a spontaneous act of combination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS3","text":"The spontaneous act of combination is assigned to understanding and designated synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS4","text":"Synthesis produces the representation of synthetical unity and makes object-conjunction depend on prior subjective conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS5","text":"Prior synthesis makes analysis possible, while conjunction makes the category of unity possible rather than resulting from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS6","text":"The dependency of categorical unity on conjunction requires a higher unity that is not itself categorical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:FGS7","text":"Higher qualitative unity grounds the unity of concepts in judgment and thereby grounds the logical use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AF1","text":"Sensibly given manifold → distinction between receptivity and spontaneity → conjunction as synthesis → prior subjective conjunction as condition of object-conjunction → synthetical unity → higher qualitative unity beyond categorical unity → ground of judgment and logical understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:AF2","text":"Apparent unity/conjunction circularity → distinction between unity represented in conjunction and unity enabling conjunction → relocation of the explanatory ground to qualitative unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME1","text":"Conjunction is reclassified from an object-side datum to a spontaneous operation of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME2","text":"Sensibility and understanding are mapped as complementary conditions: sensibility supplies the manifold, while understanding supplies its combination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME3","text":"The opposition between synthesis and analysis is collapsed into an asymmetric dependence in which analysis presupposes synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME4","text":"The category of unity is displaced from foundational status and made derivative of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME5","text":"Object representation is reclassified as mediated by subjective synthesis rather than directly read from the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME6","text":"Unity is divided into a represented synthetical unity and a prior enabling unity, preventing their conflation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ME7","text":"The ground of logical understanding is displaced upward from judgmental functions to a higher qualitative condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ALT1","text":"Surface: Sense gives a manifold, but conjunction is not given through sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ALT2","text":"Structural: Receptivity and spontaneity form a dependency architecture in which understanding synthesizes what sensibility supplies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The inquiry must move beneath conjunction and categorical unity to identify the conditions that make both possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The subject’s spontaneous activity is a constitutive condition for representing an object as conjoined, while the priority of unity is transcendental rather than asserted as a chronological event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:ALT5","text":"Terminal: The system reaches a higher qualitative unity as the ground of logical understanding but does not further determine its internal constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the local argument from sensible givenness to higher qualitative unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction requires preserving the distinction between sensuous form, spontaneous conjunction, synthetical unity, categorical unity, and higher qualitative unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent only if the unity represented in conjunction is distinguished from the prior unity that enables conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest in establishing the dependence of object-conjunction, analysis, and categorical unity on prior synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT5","text":"The passage leaves the nature of the higher qualitative unity underdetermined and specifies it primarily by its grounding function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT6","text":"The passage does not fully establish how subjective synthesis secures the objective validity of the conjunction represented in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text but cannot supply the larger account of judgment, categories, or the constitution of higher unity that the passage presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 30","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","context":"Chapter 30","original_title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","original_context":"Chapter 30"},"original_title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","order":31,"parents":["RAW_BA94_OSUA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_OSUA@R001","sha256":"52fca42bcff50789823032a593d30366376dbe3aab542e2c53ff89a547a8fe44","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS1","text":"The problem is how a manifold of intuitions can belong to one subject as representations that are thinkable and identifiable as mine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS2","text":"The “I think” is reframed as pure, spontaneous apperception rather than an empirical or sensory occurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS3","text":"The unity of self-consciousness is grounded in the capacity to synthesize the manifold of intuition within one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS4","text":"Empirical consciousness cannot supply this unity because it is fragmentary and lacks a necessary relation among representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS5","text":"Analytical identity of consciousness is shown to presuppose a prior synthetical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AS6","text":"This synthetical unity becomes the highest principle of understanding, concept formation, logic, and a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A1","text":"A representation can exist for the subject only if the “I think” can accompany it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A2","text":"The “I think” is an act of spontaneity rather than an element of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A3","text":"Pure apperception is one and identical throughout the acts of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A4","text":"The manifold given in intuition has a necessary relation to one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A5","text":"Empirical consciousness is fragmentary and does not by itself establish the identity of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A6","text":"The identity of consciousness requires the synthesis of representations into one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A7","text":"Analytical unity of apperception presupposes synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A8","text":"The synthetical unity of the manifold in intuition is a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A9","text":"The conjunction of representations into a concept is an operation of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","text":"The understanding is the faculty of conjoining representations a priori under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A11","text":"The synthetical unity of apperception is the highest principle governing the operations of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","text":"The ego, considered as a simple representation, contains no manifold content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A13","text":"The manifold content of cognition is supplied in intuition, which is distinct from the representation of the ego.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A14","text":"Human understanding can think the manifold but cannot intuit it; its intuition must therefore be supplied by sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:A15","text":"The principle of the necessary unity of apperception is analytically identical while explaining the necessity of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX1","text":"The subject is not capable of representing something for itself independently of the possible accompaniment of the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX2","text":"Pure apperception is not a product or component of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX3","text":"The unity of self-consciousness is not produced by merely accompanying each representation with consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX4","text":"The identity of the subject is not secured by empirical consciousness alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX5","text":"The conjunction of representations is not borrowed from objects or passively extracted through perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX6","text":"The ego as a simple representation is not itself a manifold of determinate content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX7","text":"Human understanding is not an intuitive understanding in which the manifold is given through consciousness itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX8","text":"Concepts are not formed through a conjunction already contained in objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:NX9","text":"Analytical unity is not independent of synthetical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D1","text":"Every representation that belongs to the subject must be structurally includable within one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A1","CH_BA94_OSUA:A3","CH_BA94_OSUA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D2","text":"The unity of representations arises from their connection, not from the subject’s simultaneous awareness of them.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A5","CH_BA94_OSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D3","text":"Calling representations “mine” means that they can be synthesized within one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A3","CH_BA94_OSUA:A4","CH_BA94_OSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D4","text":"The consciousness of an identical subject is possible only after a manifold has been synthesized.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A6","CH_BA94_OSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D5","text":"A representation can function as a common concept only after it has been placed within a possible synthesis with other representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A7","CH_BA94_OSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D6","text":"Every operation of the understanding must be connected to the synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","CH_BA94_OSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D7","text":"A priori cognition depends on the possibility of synthesizing the manifold under one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A8","CH_BA94_OSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D8","text":"The formal identity of the ego cannot by itself produce the content required for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","CH_BA94_OSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D9","text":"Human cognition requires the cooperation of receptive intuition and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A2","CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","CH_BA94_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:D10","text":"The identity of consciousness is a result of the subject’s synthesizing activity rather than an immediately given empirical fact.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A5","CH_BA94_OSUA:A6","CH_BA94_OSUA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD1","text":"Subjective identity is not primitive content but a formal identity constituted through the synthesis of an intuitively given manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A3","CH_BA94_OSUA:A6","CH_BA94_OSUA:A8","CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","CH_BA94_OSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD2","text":"Representation, concept formation, logic, and a priori cognition share one generative condition: the synthesis of the manifold under apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A7","CH_BA94_OSUA:A9","CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","CH_BA94_OSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD3","text":"Human cognition has a twofold architecture in which sensibility supplies multiplicity and understanding supplies unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A2","CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","CH_BA94_OSUA:A13","CH_BA94_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD4","text":"The boundary of what belongs to the subject is determined by possible synthesis within one consciousness rather than by explicit empirical awareness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A1","CH_BA94_OSUA:A4","CH_BA94_OSUA:A5","CH_BA94_OSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD5","text":"The unity principle governs cognition without supplying the manifold content that cognition requires.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A8","CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","CH_BA94_OSUA:A13","CH_BA94_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SD6","text":"The formal unity of self-consciousness and the plurality of intuition are mutually dependent aspects of human cognition rather than independently complete sources of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A6","CH_BA94_OSUA:A10","CH_BA94_OSUA:A13","CH_BA94_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX1","text":"The subject is not originally a plurality of separate representations requiring later aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX2","text":"Self-identity is not inferred from the repeated occurrence of empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX3","text":"The unity of consciousness is not an empirical regularity among already independent mental contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX4","text":"Universal concepts are not possible as mere abstractions from common features without prior synthetic combination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX5","text":"The “I” is not a substantive inner intuition containing the manifold of cognition within itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX6","text":"Human understanding does not generate the manifold that it synthesizes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX7","text":"Logical unity is not autonomous from the transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX8","text":"The formal unity of the subject cannot be separated from the operation that connects representations under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX1","text":"Unconnected multiplicity cannot count as a unified field of representations for a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX2","text":"There is no self-consciousness available as a fully content-bearing ground prior to synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX3","text":"Cognition cannot arise through passive reception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX4","text":"The identity of the subject cannot be established by analytical reflection without a prior synthetic condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX5","text":"Objects cannot be treated as the ultimate source of the conjunction that makes them conceptually cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX6","text":"Human cognition cannot collapse receptivity and spontaneity into a single undifferentiated faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX7","text":"No determinate cognition is possible in the absence of a manifold supplied through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS1","text":"Intuition supplies a manifold of representations that is not yet unified under self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS2","text":"Pure apperception establishes the formal possibility that the manifold can belong to one subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS3","text":"The understanding actively synthesizes the manifold by connecting representations under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS4","text":"Consciousness of this synthesis makes it possible to represent the identity of consciousness across the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS5","text":"The resulting synthetical unity precedes and grounds analytical unity, common concepts, and the operations of logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS6","text":"Because human understanding does not intuit, the completed structure requires the division between sensible intuition and spontaneous understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AF1","text":"Manifold intuition → necessary relation to the “I think” → spontaneous apperception → synthesis of representations → synthetical unity of consciousness → analytical identity of the subject → concept formation and logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AF2","text":"Fragmentary empirical consciousness → insufficiency of mere accompaniment → active conjunction → possibility of calling representations “mine” → transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:AF3","text":"Simple ego representation without manifold → dependence on intuition for content → understanding as synthesizing faculty → human cognition as receptive-spontaneous cooperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME1","text":"Unity is reclassified from simultaneous awareness to an active operation of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME2","text":"Self-identity is displaced from an immediately given inner content to a formal relation organizing a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME3","text":"“My representations” becomes a structural designation for possible inclusion in one self-consciousness rather than a report of empirical ownership.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME4","text":"Concepts are reclassified as products of the understanding’s a priori conjunction rather than copies or abstractions passively obtained from objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME5","text":"Logic and transcendental philosophy are placed under the same generative condition: synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME6","text":"Human cognition is mapped as a relation between receptive material and spontaneous formal unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ME7","text":"The distinction between empirical and transcendental apperception becomes decisive for explaining how representations can belong to consciousness without being explicitly recognized as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT1","text":"**Surface:** The “I think” must be capable of accompanying every representation that exists for the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT2","text":"**Structural:** The identity of consciousness is produced through the synthesis of an intuitively given manifold rather than through empirical accompaniment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT3","text":"**Metastrategic:** The argument relocates the foundation of cognition from objects and isolated representations to the activity that unifies them under apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT4","text":"**Ontological/Temporal:** A priori priority is structural rather than chronological; synthetical unity must precede determinate thought as its condition, while the ego remains formally empty without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the necessity of the “I think” through synthesis to the primacy of synthetical apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT2","text":"The central generative hinge is the claim that analytical identity presupposes synthetical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent once pure apperception is distinguished from fragmentary empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT4","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that the fundamental principle is called analytically identical while functioning as the ground of a necessary synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT5","text":"A second tension is that apperception supplies the formal condition of unity but cannot supply the manifold content required for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT6","text":"The argument leaves underdetermined how consciousness of synthesis itself is to be explained beyond the asserted capacity for synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement from representation, through synthesis and self-identity, to concepts, logic, and the division between sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 31","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","context":"Chapter 31","original_title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","original_context":"Chapter 31"},"original_title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA","layer":"CH","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","order":32,"parents":["RAW_BA94_PSUA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_PSUA@R001","sha256":"a736f39823835c9ad22e2b2af21063960a0782dc3f0e9f076b2eebf8248335fd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS1","text":"Sensibility subjects its manifold to space and time, while understanding subjects its manifold to the original synthetic unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS2","text":"The central problem is how a given manifold becomes cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS3","text":"An object requires the unification of manifold representations, and such unification requires their conjunction in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS4","text":"Synthetic unity of consciousness therefore constitutes the objective condition of cognition and the possibility of the understanding itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS5","text":"The unifying act is expressed through the possibility of attaching the identical “I think” to representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS6","text":"The principle is restricted to human understanding, which thinks but cannot intuit, and is contrasted with an intuitive understanding that would not require this synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AS7","text":"The argument is transcendental and reconstructive: it derives the conditions of object cognition and then limits their universality by distinguishing kinds of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A1","text":"The manifold of intuition is subject to the formal conditions of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A2","text":"The manifold relevant to understanding is subject to the original synthetic unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A3","text":"Given representations must be capable of conjunction in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A4","text":"Without the common act of the “I think,” representations cannot be connected in one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A5","text":"Understanding is the faculty of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A6","text":"Cognition consists in the determinate relation of a given representation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A7","text":"An object is that in the conception of which the manifold of a given intuition is united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A8","text":"Every union of representations requires unity of consciousness in their synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A9","text":"Unity of consciousness constitutes the possibility of objective validity, cognition, and the existence of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A10","text":"Space, as the mere form of external intuition, supplies a manifold but affords no cognition by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A11","text":"Cognition of a determinate object requires the synthetic production of a determined conjunction of the given manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A12","text":"Every intuition must be subject to synthetic unity in order to become an object for the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A13","text":"The principle of apperception analytically states the condition that enables representations to be connected with an identical self through the expression “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A14","text":"The principle applies to an understanding whose pure apperception contains no manifold content in the thought “I am.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","text":"An understanding that intuited its objects through its own representational act would not require a separate synthesis of manifold representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:A16","text":"Human understanding thinks but cannot intuit and therefore has an absolute need for synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX1","text":"Space and time alone are not sufficient for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX2","text":"A given manifold is not yet an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX3","text":"Representations that cannot be connected with the identical “I think” do not form one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX4","text":"Unity of consciousness is not a mere aggregate of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX5","text":"The principle of synthetic unity is not a principle for every possible understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX6","text":"Human understanding is not an understanding that intuits its objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX7","text":"Human self-consciousness does not contain the manifold of intuition through the act of self-consciousness itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX8","text":"The principle is not derived from conditions of mere sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:NX9","text":"Human understanding cannot positively conceive an understanding with a different form of intuition from space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D1","text":"Only representations unified in one consciousness can become cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A3","CH_BA94_PSUA:A4","CH_BA94_PSUA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D2","text":"Objective reference depends on the apperceptive synthesis of representations rather than on their mere givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A6","CH_BA94_PSUA:A7","CH_BA94_PSUA:A8","CH_BA94_PSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D3","text":"A spatial manifold becomes cognition of a determinate object only through a constructive act of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A10","CH_BA94_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D4","text":"Every intuition becomes an object for the subject only by entering synthetic unity of consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A9","CH_BA94_PSUA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D5","text":"The expression “I think” designates the form through which representations are connected with an identical self.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A3","CH_BA94_PSUA:A4","CH_BA94_PSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D6","text":"The original synthetic unity of apperception is the first pure cognition on which all other exercises of the understanding depend.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A9","CH_BA94_PSUA:A13","CH_BA94_PSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D7","text":"The necessity of synthesis follows from the separation of intuition and understanding in human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:D8","text":"An intuitive understanding would bypass the human requirement for a distinct synthesis of manifold representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A14","CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD1","text":"Objectivity is generated through the formal unification of a manifold rather than received as a property of an uncombined manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A6","CH_BA94_PSUA:A7","CH_BA94_PSUA:A8","CH_BA94_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD2","text":"Human cognition requires the irreducible cooperation of receptivity, which supplies the manifold, and apperception, which unifies it.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A1","CH_BA94_PSUA:A2","CH_BA94_PSUA:A10","CH_BA94_PSUA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD3","text":"Human finitude is defined by the separation between intuition and thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A14","CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD4","text":"The identical self functions as the invariant pole through which discrete representations acquire unity and objective relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A3","CH_BA94_PSUA:A4","CH_BA94_PSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD5","text":"The same principle grounds both the formal unity of thought and the objective validity of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A8","CH_BA94_PSUA:A9","CH_BA94_PSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SD6","text":"The claim to supremacy is universal only within the structural limits of human understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A5","CH_BA94_PSUA:A14","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX1","text":"A manifold without a unifying act cannot count as cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX2","text":"An object cannot function as an object for finite cognition prior to its synthesis in consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX3","text":"Objective validity cannot be separated from unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX4","text":"A purely receptive model of cognition is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX5","text":"A purely spatial account of cognition is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX6","text":"Human understanding cannot be grounded in an intuition that supplies its own manifold through self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX7","text":"The human form of cognition cannot be abstracted from the necessity of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX8","text":"The human cognitive condition cannot be projected without qualification onto every possible intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX1","text":"The possibility of objectivity without a synthesizing unity of consciousness is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX2","text":"The possibility of an unmediated identity between representation and object is unavailable to human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX3","text":"A cognition wholly independent of both apperceptive synthesis and the human forms of intuition cannot be positively specified from the human standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX4","text":"The original unity of consciousness is treated as primitive rather than generated by a deeper cognitive condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX5","text":"The human subject cannot occupy the standpoint of an intuitive understanding that would unite representation and existence in one act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX6","text":"The argument forecloses any account of cognition in which representations remain objective without belonging to one identical self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS1","text":"Space and time condition the reception of a sensible manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS2","text":"The received manifold must be connectable in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS3","text":"The “I think” supplies the form of identity through which representations are synthesized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS4","text":"Synthetic unity converts the manifold into a determinate representation of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS5","text":"Determinate object-relation yields cognition and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS6","text":"The resulting condition grounds every exercise of human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS7","text":"The condition is bounded by the distinction between a discursive understanding that must synthesize and an intuitive understanding that would not need such synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AF1","text":"Given manifold → spatial-temporal conditioning → requirement of conjunction → synthetic unity of apperception → determinate object → cognition and objective validity → foundational principle of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:AF2","text":"Human separation of intuition and thought → necessity of synthesis → restriction of the principle to human understanding → intuitive understanding as limiting contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME1","text":"The argument separates the conditions of receiving representations from the conditions of relating them to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME2","text":"The object is reclassified from a merely given content to a unity produced through synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME3","text":"Self-conscious unity becomes constitutive of objective validity rather than an accompanying awareness of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME4","text":"Space and time are reclassified as necessary forms of intuition but insufficient grounds of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME5","text":"Understanding is reclassified from passive reception into an operation that actively unifies a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME6","text":"The scope of universality is redrawn: the principle is supreme for human understanding but not for every possible intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ME7","text":"The distinction between discursive and intuitive understanding becomes a boundary on what human cognition can positively conceive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT1","text":"Surface: Intuitions are conditioned by space and time, while cognition requires synthetic unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT2","text":"Structural: Objectivity emerges from the synthesis of a manifold in one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument shifts inquiry from empirical contents to the conditions that make object cognition possible, then limits the result by differentiating cognitive types.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Human cognition presupposes a finite separation between intuition and thought, with space and time defining the form of the human sensible horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from manifold, through synthesis, to object, cognition, and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT2","text":"The apophatic structure preserves the essential exclusions of space alone, passive givenness, and unrestricted universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the analytical character of the proposition is distinguished from the synthetic function it establishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT4","text":"The primitive status of synthetic unity remains underived; the argument establishes its necessity without explaining its deeper ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between the principle’s independence from sensuous intuition and the dependence of actual cognition on sensible material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between the principle’s status as supreme for all human understanding and its restriction to one type of possible understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the transcendental movement of the argument, but its force depends on necessity claims rather than empirical demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 32","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","context":"Chapter 32","original_title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","original_context":"Chapter 32"},"original_title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS","layer":"CH","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","order":33,"parents":["RAW_BA94_WOUS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_WOUS@R001","sha256":"cfc5dc7f89ca5990c1c4e00e382d5e8edb5ed025a2e55f2411adddd8ac6fb848","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish the objective unity of consciousness from the empirical unity through which representations are contingently connected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AS2","text":"Objective unity is grounded in the transcendental unity of apperception, through which the manifold of intuition is united in a conception of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AS3","text":"Empirical unity belongs to internal sense and depends upon contingent conditions of association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AS4","text":"The necessary relation of the manifold to the “I think” grounds all empirical synthesis a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AS5","text":"The argument moves from the distinction between kinds of unity to the priority and objective validity of transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A1","text":"The manifold given in intuition must be united into a conception of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A2","text":"The transcendental unity of apperception performs the unification of the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A3","text":"The unity produced by transcendental apperception is objective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A4","text":"Subjective unity of consciousness is a determination of internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A5","text":"Empirical consciousness of the manifold as coexistent or successive depends on empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","text":"Empirical unity of consciousness arises through association of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A7","text":"The empirical unity of consciousness relates to a phenomenal world and is contingent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A8","text":"The manifold in pure intuition is necessarily related to the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A9","text":"The pure synthesis of the understanding lies a priori at the foundation of empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A10","text":"Transcendental unity of apperception alone possesses objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A11","text":"Empirical unity is derived from transcendental unity under determinate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:A12","text":"Empirical unity has only subjective validity and lacks necessary universal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX1","text":"Objective unity is not identical with subjective unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX2","text":"Objective unity is not reducible to the empirical association of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX3","text":"Empirical unity is not necessary or universally valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX4","text":"Empirical unity is not independent of circumstances or empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX5","text":"The unity of consciousness in relation to experience is not identical across subjects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX6","text":"The unity grounded in internal sense is not the original ground of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:NX7","text":"The pure synthesis of the understanding is not itself an empirical process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D1","text":"The unity of an object requires a synthesis that exceeds the contingent succession or coexistence of representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A1","CH_BA94_WOUS:A2","CH_BA94_WOUS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D2","text":"The same manifold can receive different empirical unifications without thereby altering the objective ground of its synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A5","CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","CH_BA94_WOUS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D3","text":"Empirical synthesis presupposes a prior non-empirical condition of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A8","CH_BA94_WOUS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D4","text":"The objectivity of a conception depends on the necessary relation of the manifold to the “I think,” rather than on the subject’s contingent awareness of its representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A3","CH_BA94_WOUS:A8","CH_BA94_WOUS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D5","text":"Differences in the meanings connected with the same word exhibit the subjective limitation of empirical unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","CH_BA94_WOUS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:D6","text":"The empirical unity of consciousness is derivative rather than constitutive of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A9","CH_BA94_WOUS:A10","CH_BA94_WOUS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SD1","text":"Objectivity is constituted by a universal condition of synthesis that remains operative beneath variable empirical combinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A3","CH_BA94_WOUS:A8","CH_BA94_WOUS:A9","CH_BA94_WOUS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SD2","text":"The distinction between transcendental and empirical unity separates the conditions of possible object-experience from the contingent contents and connections found within experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A4","CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","CH_BA94_WOUS:A7","CH_BA94_WOUS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SD3","text":"Subjective variation does not undermine objective unity because variation occurs at the empirical level presupposing a shared transcendental condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A8","CH_BA94_WOUS:A10","CH_BA94_WOUS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SD4","text":"The “I think” functions as the formal point of integration through which a manifold becomes synthesizable as belonging to an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A1","CH_BA94_WOUS:A2","CH_BA94_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX1","text":"Objectivity cannot be grounded solely in the contents of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX2","text":"Necessary unity cannot be explained by temporal order as empirically observed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX3","text":"Universal validity cannot arise from privately variable associations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX4","text":"Consciousness cannot be treated as a merely receptive succession of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX5","text":"The conditions of empirical synthesis cannot themselves be exhausted by empirical description.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX6","text":"The unity of an object cannot be identified with the fact that a subject happens to experience representations as connected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX1","text":"The possibility that objectivity is produced by contingent association is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX2","text":"The possibility that individual empirical consciousness supplies the universal ground of objecthood is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX3","text":"A conception of experience without an original synthetic unity is rendered inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX4","text":"The text leaves no objective status for a unity that cannot be related to the “I think” through necessary synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX5","text":"The empirical subject is denied the power to establish, by its variable associations alone, the universal validity of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS1","text":"Manifold in intuition → requirement of unification → relation to the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS2","text":"Relation to the “I think” → pure synthesis of the understanding → a priori ground of empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS3","text":"A priori synthesis → transcendental unity of apperception → conception of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS4","text":"Empirical conditions → association of representations → contingent subjective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS5","text":"Transcendental unity → objective and universal validity; empirical unity → derivative and merely subjective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS6","text":"Variable empirical connections → demonstration of the insufficiency of association as the ground of objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AF1","text":"Manifold of intuition → need for synthesis → relation to the “I think” → pure synthesis of understanding → transcendental unity of apperception → objective conception of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AF2","text":"Empirical givenness → internal sense → association under contingent conditions → subjective unity → lack of necessary universal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:AF3","text":"Two forms of unity → distinction between constitutive ground and empirical derivative → restriction of objective validity to transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ME1","text":"Unity is divided into a transcendental condition of objectivity and an empirical condition of subjective connectedness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ME2","text":"The source of objectivity is relocated from experienced association to a priori synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ME3","text":"Interpersonal and intrapersonal variation in representation is reclassified as evidence of empirical subjectivity rather than a refutation of objective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ME4","text":"The “I think” is mapped as a formal condition of object-reference rather than as an empirical item within the phenomenal sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ME5","text":"Empirical consciousness is positioned as dependent upon, rather than foundational for, the unity it appears to enact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT1","text":"Surface: Consciousness contains a manifold whose unity may be experienced as successive or coexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT2","text":"Structural: Transcendental synthesis grounds object-conception, while association produces only contingent empirical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument resolves the conflict between subjective variation and objective validity by assigning them to different levels of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Time supplies the pure form in which a manifold is given, but the unity of that manifold depends on an a priori relation to the “I think,” not on temporal succession alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between transcendental and empirical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT2","text":"The structure preserves the movement from manifold, through synthesis, to objectivity and from association, through contingency, to subjectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if objective validity means validity grounded in necessary synthesis rather than empirical agreement alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT4","text":"The relation between the “I think,” pure intuition, and the pure synthesis of the understanding remains compressed rather than fully demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT5","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that empirical unity is described as a unity derived from transcendental unity while also being characterized by variable, condition-dependent associations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real force: empirical consciousness cannot account for the universal validity of object-conceptions that it presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 33","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","context":"Chapter 33","original_title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","original_context":"Chapter 33"},"original_title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ","layer":"CH","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","order":34,"parents":["RAW_BA94_LFAJ"],"revision":"CH_BA94_LFAJ@R001","sha256":"35f568564f4a8157190ff1c46ae57a45d9a4cde593c960668d1b547c4d55bce3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS1","text":"The conventional definition of judgment as a relation between two conceptions is inadequate because it excludes non-categorical forms and leaves the nature of the relation undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS2","text":"Judgment is reframed as a relation belonging to understanding rather than as an association produced by reproductive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS3","text":"Judgment consists in bringing given cognitions under the objective unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS4","text":"Objective unity is grounded in the necessary synthesis of representations according to principles derived from transcendental unity of apperception, even when the empirical judgment is contingent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS5","text":"The distinction between objective judgment and subjective association is established by contrasting an object-predicate with a subject-conditioned report of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A1","text":"The definition of judgment as a relation between two conceptions is insufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A2","text":"Judgments may relate judgments to one another, not only conceptions to one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A3","text":"A definition of judgment must determine the kind of relation that constitutes it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A4","text":"The understanding and reproductive imagination generate different kinds of unity among representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A5","text":"Reproductive imagination produces a unity with merely subjective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","text":"Judgment brings given cognitions under the objective unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","text":"Objective unity relates representations to the original apperception and unifies them necessarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A8","text":"The necessary unity of judgment belongs to the synthesis of intuitions, not to necessary conjunction in empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9","text":"The principles of objective determination are derived from transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A10","text":"Subjective association licenses only a subject-conditioned report of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:A11","text":"Objective predication treats representations as conjoined in the object independently of the subject’s particular condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX1","text":"Judgment is not merely a relation between two conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX2","text":"Judgment is not restricted to the categorical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX3","text":"The unity constituting judgment is not the unity produced by reproductive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX4","text":"Objective validity is not produced by subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX5","text":"The necessary unity of a judgment is not empirical necessity in the perceived conjunction of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX6","text":"Repeated perception does not by itself establish an objective predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX7","text":"Hypothetical and disjunctive judgments are not reducible to categorical judgments as their exclusive basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX8","text":"Saying that an object is heavy is not equivalent to reporting that carrying it produces an impression of weight.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D1","text":"A judgment has objective validity when the relation among its representations is grounded in the objective unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D2","text":"An empirical judgment may be contingent in content while necessary in the form of its synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A8","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D3","text":"The same representations can form either an objective judgment or a subject-conditioned association depending on the mode of their synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A4","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A5","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D4","text":"Objective determination abstracts from the particular condition of the perceiving subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A10","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D5","text":"Association can express how representations occur together for a subject but cannot establish that they belong together in the object.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A5","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A10","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:D6","text":"The insufficiency of the traditional definition lies not only in its narrow scope but in its failure to identify the ground of objective relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A1","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD1","text":"Objectivity is generated by the rule-governed synthesis of representations rather than supplied by their mere coexistence in perception.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A8","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD2","text":"Transcendental unity of apperception functions as the criterion that converts a sequence of representations into cognition with objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD3","text":"Judgment is the mediating operation between subjective apprehension and object-directed cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A4","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD4","text":"The text relocates necessity from the empirical content of a judgment to the transcendental form under which that content can count as cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A8","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD5","text":"The logical form of judgment is treated as dependent upon a prior unity of consciousness that makes objective reference possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX1","text":"The system excludes psychologism as an account of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX2","text":"The system excludes empiricism that identifies objectivity with repeated conjunction in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX3","text":"The system excludes formalism that defines judgment without specifying the ground of its relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a subject-bound conception of judgment in which validity remains restricted to the perceiver’s condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the inference from frequent association to necessary objective connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the reduction of all judgmental forms to categorical predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX1","text":"No objective judgment is possible without a unity that exceeds the merely successive occurrence of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX2","text":"No direct passage exists from subjective impression to objective predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX3","text":"Objectivity cannot be established independently of the synthesizing conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX4","text":"A relation between representations cannot remain philosophically indeterminate if it is to ground judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX5","text":"The system forecloses a conception of cognition in which the object is authenticated by perception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS1","text":"An over-narrow definition of judgment is exposed by the plurality of judgmental forms and by the undefined character of relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS2","text":"The relation among representations is divided into subjective association and objective synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS3","text":"Objective synthesis is referred to the original apperception and its necessary unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS4","text":"The necessary unity of synthesis is derived from principles of transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS5","text":"Empirical contingency is separated from transcendental necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS6","text":"The distinction is operationalized by contrasting an object-predicate with a report of subject-conditioned sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AF1","text":"Inadequate definition → unspecified relation → distinction between subjective and objective unity → transcendental grounding of objective synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:AF2","text":"Transcendental unity of apperception → necessary synthesis of representations → objective validity despite empirical contingency → exclusion of association-only reports as judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME1","text":"Judgment is reclassified from a static relation between concepts into an operation that unifies cognitions objectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME2","text":"Objectivity is relocated from empirical repetition to the conditions governing synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME3","text":"The distinction between subjective and objective cognition becomes a distinction between modes of unity rather than merely different contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME4","text":"Empirical contingency and objective validity are no longer opposites; contingency concerns content, while necessity concerns the form of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME5","text":"The exclusive primacy of categorical judgments is displaced by an account of judgment that includes relations among judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT1","text":"**Surface:** Judgment is the objective relation among given cognitions, contrasted with a subjective report of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT2","text":"**Structural:** Understanding produces objective unity through synthesis, whereas reproductive imagination produces subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT3","text":"**Metastrategic:** The analysis shifts the question from the classification of judgments to the conditions under which any relation can possess objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT4","text":"**Ontological/Temporal:** Object-directed cognition is not grounded in the temporal repetition of impressions but in a necessary synthesis that orders representations according to transcendental principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the critique of the traditional definition to the distinction between objective judgment and subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the central movement from logical classification to transcendental grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT3","text":"The passage does not independently derive the full structure of transcendental unity of apperception; it presupposes that structure from the surrounding argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent because it distinguishes empirical contingency from necessity in synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT5","text":"Its strongest point is the contrast between an object-predicate and a subject-conditioned impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT6","text":"Its principal fault line lies in asserting objective unity and object-conjunction as consequences of transcendental apperception without fully re-establishing those premises within the passage itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 34","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","context":"Chapter 34","original_title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","original_context":"Chapter 34"},"original_title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA","layer":"CH","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","order":35,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ASIA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ASIA@R001","sha256":"bcef09296d9233989aba4a7f0ca984bc60db617f67c518ea425bab5c0ea507b0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AS1","text":"The problem is how the manifold content of a sensuous intuition can possess unity in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AS2","text":"Unity of intuition is grounded in the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AS3","text":"The act that unites manifold representations under one apperception is the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AS4","text":"Categories are these functions of judgement insofar as they determine the manifold of a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AS5","text":"The argument therefore moves from the possibility of intuitive unity, through apperception and judgement, to the necessary subjection of the manifold to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A1","text":"The manifold content of a sensuous intuition is necessarily subject to the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A2","text":"The unity of intuition is possible only through the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A3","text":"The understanding brings the manifold content of given representations under one apperception through an act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A4","text":"The act of bringing manifold representations under one apperception is the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","text":"Every manifold given in one empirical intuition is determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","text":"Categories are logical functions of judgement insofar as they determine the manifold of a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:A7","text":"The manifold of a given intuition is united in one consciousness only through its determination in relation to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX1","text":"The unity of intuition is not possible through sensuous givenness alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX2","text":"The manifold of intuition is not united in consciousness independently of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX3","text":"The synthesis of the manifold is not an arbitrary combination of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX4","text":"The act of uniting representations is not external to the logical functions of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX5","text":"Categories are not presented as independent contents added to intuition from outside.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:NX6","text":"The manifold of empirical intuition is not exempt from determination by the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:D1","text":"Any manifold that is to count as united in one intuition must be referred to the unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A1","CH_BA94_ASIA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:D2","text":"Since the understanding unites manifold representations through judgement, the unity of intuition has a logical form.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A3","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:D3","text":"Because every empirical manifold is brought under a function of judgement, its unity is determined by a category.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:D4","text":"The categories have objective applicability to intuition because they are the functions through which the manifold is united in consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A4","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:D5","text":"The necessary relation of intuition to categories follows from the conditions of synthesis rather than from an empirical comparison between intuitions and concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A2","CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SD1","text":"The possibility of a unified empirical object is inseparable from the activity of the understanding that determines its manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A1","CH_BA94_ASIA:A3","CH_BA94_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SD2","text":"The categories function as conditions of objectual unity because no manifold can become one object of consciousness without categorical determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A2","CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SD3","text":"The argument converts the relation between sensibility and understanding from an external cooperation into a necessary constitutive dependence.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A1","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SD4","text":"The unity of consciousness and the unity of intuition are generated through the same synthetic-logical operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A2","CH_BA94_ASIA:A3","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX1","text":"A unified intuition cannot be grounded in receptivity considered apart from synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX2","text":"The manifold cannot be treated as already unified before its relation to apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX3","text":"The logical functions of judgement cannot be confined to abstract conceptual combination detached from intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX4","text":"The categories cannot be reduced to empirical generalizations extracted from already constituted objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX5","text":"Conscious unity cannot be explained without a prior formal condition supplied by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX1","text":"A manifold that is unified in consciousness without categorical determination is structurally unavailable to the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX2","text":"Pure intuition without an act of synthesis is excluded as a sufficient ground of objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX3","text":"An account of empirical objects that bypasses the unity of apperception is rendered inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX4","text":"The possibility that categories might be merely optional interpretive schemes is foreclosed by the necessity of unified consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS1","text":"Manifold given in intuition → requirement of unity → original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS2","text":"Original synthetical unity of apperception → act of bringing representations under one consciousness → synthesis by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS3","text":"Synthesis by the understanding → logical function of judgement → determination of the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS4","text":"Logical function of judgement applied to an intuitive manifold → category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS5","text":"Category-mediated determination → unity of the manifold in one consciousness → necessary subjection of sensuous intuition to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:AF1","text":"Manifold content of intuition → need for unity → apperceptive synthesis → logical function of judgement → categorical determination → unity in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ME1","text":"Intuitive unity is reclassified from a sensory feature into a product of synthesis under apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ME2","text":"Judgement is reclassified from a merely conceptual operation into the formal mechanism that unifies intuitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ME3","text":"Categories are reclassified from abstract logical forms into necessary conditions for the unity of empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ME4","text":"The boundary between sensibility and understanding is redrawn as a relation of necessary dependence rather than separate domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ME5","text":"Objectual unity is displaced from the given manifold itself to the activity that determines the manifold in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The manifold content of sensuous intuition is subject to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Apperception, synthesis, judgement, and categorical determination form the mechanism by which intuitive multiplicity becomes unified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes categorical necessity by tracing it back to the conditions under which representation can belong to one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Unified empirical objects are constituted through a priori forms of synthesis rather than encountered as independently unified givens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the unity of intuition to the necessary applicability of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the required sequence from apperception through synthesis and judgement to categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent within the stated framework of transcendental conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT4","text":"The argument depends on the prior identification of categories with logical functions of judgement and does not independently re-establish that identification here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT5","text":"The passage leaves underdetermined how particular categories correspond to particular forms of intuitive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT6","text":"The strongest point is the necessity claim linking unified consciousness to categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT7","text":"The main compression occurs in the transition from logical function to category and from categorical determination to objective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 35","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","context":"Chapter 35","original_title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","original_context":"Chapter 35"},"original_title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1","layer":"CH","title":"Observation § 17","order":36,"parents":["RAW_BA94_OBS1"],"revision":"CH_BA94_OBS1@R001","sha256":"9ac11edb56d0920f55cc249058e54238829fbeea210bcfeeee7f9509c48df043","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AS1","text":"The problem is to establish the a priori validity of the pure concepts of understanding for objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AS2","text":"The problem is reframed as the production of unity: a category subjects an intuitively given manifold to the unity of pure self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AS3","text":"The proof rests on the claim that an object-giving represented unity necessarily contains both synthesis of the manifold and its relation to apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AS4","text":"The deduction initially brackets the empirical mode of givenness while retaining the prior independent givenness of the manifold as a boundary condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AS5","text":"The argument proceeds from formal isolation of understanding’s unity to its identification with the unity of sensibility, then establishes category validity and terminates at the unexplained finitude of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","text":"A manifold in intuition is represented as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness through a synthesis of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","text":"The category is the means by which the understanding synthesizes the manifold under the unity of self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A3","text":"Empirical consciousness of a given manifold is subject to a pure a priori self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","text":"Categories originate in the understanding independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","text":"The deduction can abstract from the empirical mode in which the manifold is given in order to isolate the unity contributed by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A6","text":"The represented unity through which an object is given includes both synthesis of the manifold and its relation to the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","text":"The manifold to be synthesized must be given previously and independently of the synthesis of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","text":"The relevant understanding is a faculty whose power consists in thought rather than intuitive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","text":"The understanding cognizes nothing per se but only connects and arranges intuition supplied from elsewhere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A10","text":"The unity belonging to empirical intuition is identical with the unity imposed by the category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:A11","text":"The precise number of functions of judgment, categories, and forms of intuition cannot be explained from within the cognitive system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX1","text":"The deduction is not initially grounded in the empirical mode by which the manifold is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX2","text":"Categories are not derived from sensibility or from empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX3","text":"Human understanding is not an intuitive faculty that produces objects through its own representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX4","text":"Categories do not have the same significance for an intuitive understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX5","text":"The understanding is not a self-sufficient source of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:NX6","text":"The exact inventory of cognitive functions and forms is not deduced by the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D1","text":"A category is the condition under which a given manifold can belong to empirical consciousness as unified self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D2","text":"Object cognition requires both an independently given manifold and a synthesis that relates it to apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A6","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D3","text":"Because categories originate independently of sensibility, their deduction must first isolate formal unity rather than explain empirical givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D4","text":"Categories function as rules for organizing intuition rather than as producers of the material organized.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D5","text":"The a priori validity of categories for objects of sense follows when the unity supplied by sensibility is shown to coincide with category-imposed unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:D6","text":"The argument can establish the applicability of categories without explaining why the human cognitive apparatus has precisely its given structure.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SD1","text":"Category validity is relationally established through the correspondence between the unity required by apperception and the unity present in sensible intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SD2","text":"The deduction has a two-stage structure: formal abstraction from givenness followed by transcendental reintegration with sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3","text":"Human cognition is constituted by a division of labor in which sensibility supplies the manifold and understanding supplies its unified organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SD4","text":"The scope of the categories is determined by the finite, discursive type of understanding for which they are rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SD5","text":"The argument achieves transcendental validity without achieving complete explanatory closure concerning the origin and inventory of cognitive forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX1","text":"No account of human cognition may make the understanding generate the material of its own intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX2","text":"No category can be treated as valid independently of the type of understanding to which it belongs.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX3","text":"No deduction of categories can proceed from empirical material alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX4","text":"No complete self-explanation of the cognitive faculties is available within the deduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX5","text":"No object of cognition is constituted by an ununified manifold alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX1","text":"A divine or intuitive understanding is excluded as the operative model for explaining human category-use, even though it remains conceptually imaginable.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a fully self-grounding passage from the source of the manifold to the act of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX3","text":"The ultimate reason for the specific forms and number of cognitive functions remains unavailable to the argument.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX4","text":"The possibility of deriving the finite architecture of cognition from a deeper explanatory principle is left foreclosed.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS1","text":"Independently given manifold in intuition → synthesis by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS2","text":"Synthesis → category-mediated relation to the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS3","text":"Unity of apperception → represented object and empirical consciousness of the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS4","text":"Abstraction from empirical givenness → isolation of the understanding’s formal unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS5","text":"Sensibility’s mode of givenness → identification of its unity with category-imposed unity → a priori validity for objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS6","text":"Discursive understanding → dependence on externally supplied intuition → category relevance and explanatory limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AF1","text":"Given manifold → synthesis under apperception → category-mediated unity → represented object and empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AF2","text":"Formal abstraction from givenness → recovery of the sensibility–category correspondence → a priori validity for objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:AF3","text":"Discursive limitation of understanding → exclusion of intuitive intellect as the human model → unresolved ground of the cognitive inventory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ME1","text":"Object cognition is reclassified as synthesized unity rather than mere reception of a manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ME2","text":"A priori validity is relocated from isolated concepts to their necessary operation upon objects of sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ME3","text":"Sensibility and understanding become distinct but coordinated sources of cognition: one supplies the manifold and the other supplies unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ME4","text":"The category is mapped as a rule specific to discursive cognition rather than as a universal feature of every possible intellect.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ME5","text":"The explanatory map terminates in the facticity of the cognitive apparatus rather than deriving its exact architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT1","text":"Surface level: a category unifies a manifold in intuition under the unity of self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT2","text":"Structural level: cognition depends on the coordination of two distinct faculties, with sensibility providing material and understanding organizing it.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level: the deduction alternates between abstraction from givenness and reconnection with the conditions of givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT4","text":"Ontological and temporal level: human cognition presupposes pre-given material, a finite discursive subject, and irreducible a priori forms of intuition whose ultimate specification is not explained.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the core deduction from manifold, synthesis, apperception, category, and sensibility to a priori validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT2","text":"The extracted sequence preserves the argument’s actual movement from formal isolation to sensibility-mediated confirmation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT3","text":"The mode by which the manifold is initially given remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if category validity is distinguished from the production of the manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT5","text":"The strongest point is the formal dependency of object cognition on synthesis and apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT6","text":"The weakest point is the deferred transition from abstractly isolated unity to the asserted identity of sensibility’s unity with category-imposed unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT7","text":"The unresolved tension lies between the independent origin of categories in understanding and their necessary applicability to material supplied by sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 36","title":"Observation § 17","context":"Chapter 36","original_title":"Observation § 17","original_context":"Chapter 36"},"original_title":"Observation § 17","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE","layer":"CH","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","order":37,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CAOE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CAOE@R001","sha256":"07edf8f768c42e8a69cb2a430ed2e0d2ba447fdcc00aaa3e04c305a6caaefd8b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS1","text":"The argument addresses how a pure concept can become cognition of an object rather than remain thought without an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS2","text":"Cognition requires both a conception through which an object is thought and an intuition through which the object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS3","text":"Because human intuition is sensuous, categories acquire cognitive significance only through application to empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS4","text":"Pure intuition and mathematics yield cognition only of the formal structure of phenomena and do not establish the existence of corresponding things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS5","text":"Categories extended beyond empirical intuition become empty forms because no available intuition can provide them with objective significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AS6","text":"The transcendental argument moves from the conditions of cognition to the delimitation of legitimate categorical use and the exclusion of positive knowledge beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A1","text":"Thinking an object and cognizing an object are distinct acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","text":"Cognition consists of a conception through which an object is thought and an intuition through which the object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A3","text":"A conception without a corresponding intuition remains formally thinkable but yields cognition of no object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A4","text":"All intuition available to human cognition is sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A5","text":"Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition of space and time or empirical intuition involving sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A6","text":"Pure intuition yields a priori cognition only of the formal conditions under which objects appear as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A7","text":"Pure mathematical conceptions become cognition only under the presupposition that objects can be represented according to the forms of pure sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","text":"Categories produce cognition only insofar as they can be applied to empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A9","text":"Categories render empirical cognition possible, and this category-enabled empirical cognition is experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A10","text":"The legitimate use of categories is restricted to objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A11","text":"Pure concepts of the understanding can be extended formally to objects of any sensuous intuition, but extension beyond human intuition provides no cognitive advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A12","text":"Empirical intuition alone gives categories significance and meaning for human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A13","text":"Merely specifying what a supposed object is not does not establish proper knowledge of that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:A14","text":"No category can be validly applied to a supposed object whose intuition is non-sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX1","text":"Cognition is not identical with thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX2","text":"A category without corresponding intuition is not cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX3","text":"Categories do not by themselves provide cognition of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX4","text":"Pure intuition alone does not establish that things corresponding to its formal conditions exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX5","text":"Mathematical conceptions are not cognition independently of their relation to representable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX6","text":"Space and time have no validity as conditions of objects beyond sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX7","text":"Extending categories beyond available intuition does not reveal the possibility or impossibility of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX8","text":"Negative predicates concerning a non-sensuous object do not constitute knowledge of that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:NX9","text":"A non-sensuous intuition cannot supply a legitimate field of categorical application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D1","text":"Objective cognition requires the joint operation of conceptual determination and object-giving intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A1","CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","CH_BA94_CAOE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D2","text":"Human cognition can acquire objects only through sensuous givenness, while conceptual determination organizes what is given.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","CH_BA94_CAOE:A4","CH_BA94_CAOE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D3","text":"Mathematical a priori cognition is restricted to the formal structure of possible phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A5","CH_BA94_CAOE:A6","CH_BA94_CAOE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D4","text":"Categories become objectively meaningful only when their synthesis can determine empirical intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_CAOE:A9","CH_BA94_CAOE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D5","text":"Experience is neither bare sensation nor pure conceptual form but their synthetically ordered combination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_CAOE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D6","text":"The formal universality of categories does not entail their legitimate cognitive use beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A10","CH_BA94_CAOE:A11","CH_BA94_CAOE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D7","text":"A non-sensuous object can be marked by exclusion of sensuous predicates without being positively determined as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A11","CH_BA94_CAOE:A13","CH_BA94_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:D8","text":"The category of substance has cognitive meaning only where empirical intuition supplies an occasion for its application.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_CAOE:A12","CH_BA94_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD1","text":"Cognitive validity is produced by the relation between concept and intuition rather than possessed by the concept in isolation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","CH_BA94_CAOE:A3","CH_BA94_CAOE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD2","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding establishes a dual limit: sensibility restricts what can be given, while understanding restricts how what is given can be determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A2","CH_BA94_CAOE:A4","CH_BA94_CAOE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD3","text":"The system permits formal thought beyond experience while denying that such thought can become cognition without corresponding intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A3","CH_BA94_CAOE:A10","CH_BA94_CAOE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD4","text":"A priori form acquires objective force only through an empirical condition that it cannot generate by itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A6","CH_BA94_CAOE:A7","CH_BA94_CAOE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD5","text":"Mathematical necessity cannot independently establish the existence of objects corresponding to mathematical forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A6","CH_BA94_CAOE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD6","text":"Discourse about non-sensuous objects is reduced to a boundary operation: it can negate sensuous determinations but cannot provide positive cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A13","CH_BA94_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SD7","text":"The restriction of categorical use converts the question of metaphysical objecthood into a question of available conditions for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_CAOE:A10","CH_BA94_CAOE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX1","text":"Human cognition has no intellectual intuition through which objects could be given independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX2","text":"No object can become cognitively available through conceptual determination alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX3","text":"No category carries autonomous objective reference apart from a mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX4","text":"No formal consistency of a conception guarantees the existence of a corresponding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX5","text":"No transition from pure form to actual existence is available without empirical givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX6","text":"No negative determination can be converted into positive content merely by multiplying exclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX7","text":"No extension of categorical concepts can overcome the absence of an intuition capable of giving them meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX1","text":"Positive cognition of objects beyond possible human experience is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX2","text":"An ontology that treats categories as self-sufficient determinations of things is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX3","text":"A non-sensuous object can function only as an indeterminate limit of thought, not as a cognized object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX4","text":"A purely intellectual route from conception to object is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX5","text":"The existence of things corresponding to formal conditions cannot be secured by the forms of thought or intuition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS1","text":"Distinguish formal thought from cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS2","text":"Require the conjunction of conceptual determination and object-giving intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS3","text":"Restrict object-giving intuition, for human cognition, to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS4","text":"Separate pure formal determination from empirical object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS5","text":"Permit categories to generate cognition only through empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS6","text":"Treat extension beyond empirical intuition as empty formal possibility rather than cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS7","text":"Convert non-sensuous object-talk into negative boundary-marking without positive determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:FGS8","text":"Conclude that the legitimate use of categories terminates at objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the distinction between thinking and cognizing to the requirement of both conception and intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AF2","text":"It moves from the concept–intuition requirement to the restriction of human intuition to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AF3","text":"It moves from sensibility to the distinction between pure formal cognition and empirical object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AF4","text":"It moves from the category’s formal role to its empirical application as the condition of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:AF5","text":"It moves from attempted extension beyond experience to empty conceptions, negative determinations, and the restriction of legitimate categorical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME1","text":"The category is reclassified from an independently object-producing concept into a condition of empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME2","text":"Experience is reclassified from immediate givenness into a synthesis of empirical intuition and categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME3","text":"Mathematics is separated from unrestricted ontology and assigned a formal validity concerning phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME4","text":"The distinction between formal conceivability and objective cognition becomes structurally decisive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME5","text":"Non-sensuous discourse is reclassified from possible positive knowledge into apophatic limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ME6","text":"The boundary of knowledge is redrawn according to conditions of application rather than according to the abstract scope of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text distinguishes thinking an object from cognizing an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ALT2","text":"Structural: Cognition is organized as the synthesis of conceptual form and intuitive givenness, with empirical intuition supplying objective significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument redirects metaphysical inquiry from unrestricted conceptual extension toward the conditions under which concepts can legitimately determine objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Space and time are treated as forms belonging to sensuous appearance, while objects beyond these forms remain unavailable to positive human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central argument from the distinction between thought and cognition to the restriction of categorical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive sequence: concept and intuition, sensibility, empirical application, experience, and exclusion of cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if formal extension and legitimate cognitive use are treated as distinct levels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT4","text":"Its strongest point is the dependence of objective cognition on the conjunction of conceptual determination and intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined the precise status of objects of non-sensuous intuition and the relation between categories and intuitions unlike those available to humans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT6","text":"Its principal fault line is the coexistence of the categories’ formal extension to intuition in general with their cognitive restriction to empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT7","text":"The argument depends on the compressed transition from the absence of corresponding intuition to the emptiness of the concept and from negative characterization to the impossibility of positive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text because the final limitation is generated by the initial distinction between thinking and cognizing rather than appended as an external restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 37","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","context":"Chapter 37","original_title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","original_context":"Chapter 37"},"original_title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","order":38,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ACOS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ACOS@R001","sha256":"57d38560491a242e3d9b7a776850a238e0d4c95e3dad616efebde35664f1c6e1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS1","text":"Pure categories require sensuous intuition to yield cognition of determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS2","text":"The understanding secures objective application by determining sensibility through a transcendental synthesis of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS3","text":"Figurative synthesis mediates between the intellectual unity of apperception and the manifold given in sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS4","text":"Internal sense is receptive and formally temporal, while understanding actively affects and synthetically determines it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS5","text":"Self-consciousness as “I think” establishes existence only as thought, not as an intuition of the self as it is in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the applicability of categories to phenomena toward the restriction of both outer and inner cognition to appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A1","text":"Categories are pure forms of thought and cannot cognize a determinate object without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A2","text":"Intellectual synthesis unifies a manifold under apperception and grounds the possibility of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A3","text":"Human intuition is sensuous and supplies its manifold only through receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A4","text":"Internal sense has the a priori form of time but contains no original synthetic conjunction of its manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","text":"The understanding is spontaneous and can determine internal sense in conformity with the synthetic unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","text":"Imagination belongs to sensibility insofar as it provides intuition, but is spontaneous insofar as it synthesizes and determines sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A7","text":"The synthesis of imagination under the categories is the first application of understanding to possible objects of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A8","text":"The objective reality of the categories extends only to objects given as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A9","text":"Productive imagination supplies a priori synthesis, whereas reproductive imagination follows empirical association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A10","text":"Apperception is the source of synthetic unity and is distinct from internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A11","text":"Self-cognition requires not only the thought “I think” but also an intuition through which the self’s manifold can be determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:A12","text":"Human self-intuition is sensuous and temporal rather than intellectual; therefore the self is cognized only as appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX1","text":"Categories are not independently cognitive representations of determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX2","text":"Intellectual synthesis is not identical with figurative synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX3","text":"Imagination is not merely receptive sensibility or merely understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX4","text":"Productive imagination is not reproductive association governed by empirical psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX5","text":"Internal sense is not identical with apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX6","text":"The understanding does not discover a pre-given synthetic order within internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX7","text":"Inner intuition does not disclose the self as it is in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX8","text":"The “I think” is not knowledge of a substantial, intuitively given self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX9","text":"Human cognition is not intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:NX10","text":"Motion as an empirical determination of an object is not a priori geometric cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D1","text":"The categories acquire objective validity only through their application to sensuously given appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A1","CH_BA94_ACOS:A3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D2","text":"A priori cognition requires a synthesis that is both intellectual in its rule and sensible in its presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A2","CH_BA94_ACOS:A3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D3","text":"Figurative synthesis is the operative mediation by which categories determine the sensible manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D4","text":"The temporal order of inner experience is produced through the understanding’s determination of internal sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A4","CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D5","text":"Geometrical and temporal representation presuppose an active construction of intuition rather than passive reception of completed forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D6","text":"The self can be conscious of its synthetic spontaneity without possessing intuitive knowledge of itself as a spontaneous being.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A11","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:D7","text":"The distinction between self as thinker and self as intuited does not divide the subject into two substances.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A10","CH_BA94_ACOS:A11","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SD1","text":"Objectivity is constituted neither by bare concepts nor by bare sensation, but by lawful synthesis whereby spontaneity orders receptivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A1","CH_BA94_ACOS:A3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SD2","text":"The same structure that enables cognition of objects limits cognition to appearance, because the required intuition is sensuous.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7","CH_BA94_ACOS:A8","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SD3","text":"Self-relation is structurally asymmetric: the subject determines its inner sense while receiving itself only as temporally affected.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A4","CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A11","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SD4","text":"Time is not a transparent medium of self-presence, because temporal self-givenness depends upon an act that cannot itself become intellectual self-intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A4","CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SD5","text":"The transcendental account excludes psychology from explaining the conditions of a priori cognition, except where psychological processes are subordinated to transcendental synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A7","CH_BA94_ACOS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:DNX1","text":"Cognition cannot arise from a self-sufficient conceptual intellect that determines objects without receiving intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:DNX2","text":"The unity of consciousness cannot be explained by a passive inner faculty already containing synthesized representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:DNX3","text":"Self-consciousness cannot yield metaphysical knowledge of the soul merely by reflecting on the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:DNX4","text":"The distinction between appearance and thing in itself cannot be cancelled by shifting inquiry from external objects to the inner self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:DNX5","text":"A priori necessity cannot be grounded in empirical association, repetition, or psychological habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX1","text":"Immediate self-possession is unavailable because the subject encounters itself only through a form of receptivity it actively determines but does not intellectually originate as intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX2","text":"An absolute coincidence of thinker, intuited self, and known self is foreclosed for finite cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX3","text":"Noumenal self-knowledge is structurally inadmissible because its possibility would require an intellectual intuition denied to human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX4","text":"The ultimate ground of the capacity by which spontaneity affects its own receptivity remains unobjectified within the account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS1","text":"Category as empty thought-form → requirement of an intuitively given manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS2","text":"Sensuous manifold under temporal form → insufficiency of receptivity for synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS3","text":"Spontaneous understanding → determination of internal sense according to apperceptive unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS4","text":"Productive imagination → figurative synthesis connecting categories to sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS5","text":"Figurative synthesis → objective validity of categories for phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS6","text":"Reflexive application to the self → consciousness of existence without cognition of the self in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS7","text":"Temporal self-intuition → self-knowledge restricted to phenomenal appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:AF1","text":"Empty categories → necessity of sensuous intuition → transcendental imagination as mediator → understanding’s affection of internal sense → temporal constitution of self-experience → phenomenal restriction of object- and self-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ME1","text":"Objectivity is relocated from correspondence between concepts and independently known things to the lawful synthesis of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ME2","text":"Imagination is reclassified from an auxiliary image-making power into the constitutive mediator of understanding and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ME3","text":"Internal experience is reclassified from immediate self-disclosure into an affection-mediated phenomenal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ME4","text":"The opposition between active understanding and passive sensibility is rendered non-simple, because spontaneity actively determines the receptive faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ME5","text":"The “I think” is separated from empirical or metaphysical self-knowledge, preserving existence-consciousness while denying determinate noumenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Categories apply to sensible objects only when imagination synthesizes the manifold of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Apperception supplies synthetic unity, understanding determines internal sense, and time receives the resulting inner determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The apparent paradox of self-affection is resolved by distinguishing the determining intellect from the receptive mode in which its activity becomes intuitable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Finite subjects exist and think spontaneously, yet can determine and know their existence only under temporal conditions of appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from empty categories to their objective application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT2","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the mediating necessity of productive imagination and figurative synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT3","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the distinction between apperception, internal sense, and phenomenal self-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT4","text":"The system is coherent if spontaneity can determine receptivity without thereby converting human intuition into intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT5","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns how one and the same subject can be both spontaneous determinant and receptively affected without an account of their ultimate common ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT6","text":"The system depends on the asserted distinction between thought of existence and intuitive determination of existence; without it, the restriction of self-knowledge to appearance fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the restriction on cognition is treated as generated by the very mechanism that makes objective cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 38","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","context":"Chapter 38","original_title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","original_context":"Chapter 38"},"original_title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","order":39,"parents":["RAW_BA94_TDUP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_TDUP@R001","sha256":"858da53a619d2c51d38df57057438741616612c943891dbffaf39959bea90678","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS1","text":"The problem is to establish why categories, though originating a priori in understanding, necessarily govern every object that can appear in sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS2","text":"Perception is reframed as an achieved unity of apprehended sensible manifold rather than as immediately given awareness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS3","text":"Space and time supply the sensible manifold and its a priori formal unity, but the conjunction that makes perception possible requires intellectual synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS4","text":"Because every empirical apprehension must conform to categorial synthesis, every possible perception is subject to categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5","text":"Experience is cognition through conjoined perceptions; categories are therefore a priori conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AS6","text":"Nature, as the lawful connection of phenomena, is possible only under categorial conditions, although its particular empirical laws cannot be derived from categories alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A1","text":"Categories are a priori forms of intellectual synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","text":"Perception is possible only through a synthesis of apprehension that combines the manifold of empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A3","text":"Every synthesis of apprehension must conform to the a priori forms of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A4","text":"Space and time, as formal intuitions, contain an a priori unity of the manifold they present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A5","text":"The unity required for apprehending a manifold is the same primitive unity of consciousness expressed intellectually through categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A6","text":"The same spontaneity conjoins sensible manifold under the name of imagination and gives this conjunction intellectual unity under the name of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A7","text":"Experience is cognition produced through conjoined perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A8","text":"Whatever is a condition of possible perception is a condition of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A9","text":"Phenomena exist only in relation to a subject possessing sensibility, and their lawful conjunction exists only in relation to a subject possessing understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","text":"Categories prescribe a priori only the universal conditions of lawfulness required for nature in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A11","text":"Particular empirical laws require experience for their determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:A12","text":"Things in themselves remain unknown with respect to what they are independently of their phenomenal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX1","text":"Categories are not abstracted from nature or empirically regulated by nature as their model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX2","text":"Perception is not a merely receptive registration of a sensibly given manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX3","text":"Space and time are not merely empty forms lacking manifold or unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX4","text":"The formal unity of space and time is not reducible to sensibility understood as passive receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX5","text":"Laws of phenomenal conjunction do not subsist in phenomena independently of their relation to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX6","text":"Categories do not yield particular laws governing empirically specified phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX7","text":"Nature as appearance is not identical with things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:NX8","text":"The validity of categories does not extend by this deduction to objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D1","text":"Every possible perception is necessarily categorially structured because apprehension requires a synthesis whose unity is intellectual.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A1","CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D2","text":"Categories are valid a priori for every object of possible experience because experience consists of conjoined perceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A7","CH_BA94_TDUP:A8","CH_BA94_TDUP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D3","text":"The perception of spatial magnitude necessarily presupposes the category of quantity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A3","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D4","text":"The perception of an event as temporally successive necessarily presupposes the category of causality.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A3","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D5","text":"Nature in the material sense, as the totality of phenomena, is subject to categories insofar as all phenomenal perception requires categorial conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5","CH_BA94_TDUP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D6","text":"Nature in the formal sense, as necessary lawfulness among phenomena, depends on categories as its a priori ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A1","CH_BA94_TDUP:A9","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:D7","text":"Empirical investigation is necessary for particular laws even though its objects must already conform to universal categorial lawfulness.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SD1","text":"The deduction converts the necessity of natural law from a property inferred from independently given objects into a condition of objects as objects of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A5","CH_BA94_TDUP:A9","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SD2","text":"The understanding legislates only the form of possible nature, while sensibility supplies the manifold to which that legislation applies.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A3","CH_BA94_TDUP:A6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SD3","text":"Objective necessity within experience is generated through the cooperation of receptivity, apprehension, imagination, and understanding rather than secured by access to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A9","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SD4","text":"The categories function as limits on possible empirical objectivity: they make experience possible while also restricting their own legitimate use to experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A7","CH_BA94_TDUP:A8","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX1","text":"No independently knowable order of objects can ground the validity of categories, because the relevant objects are objects only as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX2","text":"No empirical fact can establish the universal necessity of categories, because empirical cognition already presupposes categorially unified perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX3","text":"No purely sensible intuition can constitute perception as objective experience without a non-sensible act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX4","text":"No deduction of particular scientific laws from pure understanding is possible, because pure categories determine only the form of lawful nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX5","text":"No claim about the intrinsic constitution of things in themselves can be licensed by the demonstrated necessity of phenomenal lawfulness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an unmediated encounter with an object before the subjective conditions of its apprehension, synthesis, and consciousness have operated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses a nature that is both empirically accessible and wholly independent of the forms through which experience is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX3","text":"The system treats the ultimate ground of correspondence between phenomenal lawfulness and things in themselves as unavailable rather than demonstrable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX4","text":"The system cannot admit a complete a priori science of materially determinate nature, because empirical specification remains irreducible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:FGS1","text":"Sensible manifold in space and time → synthesis of apprehension → perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:FGS2","text":"Formal unity of space and time + primitive unity of consciousness → categorial determination of apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:FGS3","text":"Categorially determined perception → conjoined perceptions → possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:FGS4","text":"Possible experience → objects as phenomena → universal lawfulness of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:FGS5","text":"Universal categorial lawfulness → formal nature only; empirical determination → particular laws through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AF1","text":"A priori origin of categories → demand for their universal experiential validity → analysis of perception as synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AF2","text":"Synthesis of apprehension → dependence on spatial-temporal formal unity → identification of this unity with categorial synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AF3","text":"Categorial synthesis of perception → experience as connected perception → a priori validity of categories for experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AF4","text":"Phenomena as subject-relative representations → categories as laws of their conjunction → nature as formally dependent on understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:AF5","text":"Formal legislation of nature → exclusion of derivation of particular laws → experience retained as the source of empirical specification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ME1","text":"Nature is reclassified from an independently given law-governed domain into the lawful unity of phenomena under conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ME2","text":"Categories are reclassified from abstract logical concepts into necessary operations that constitute the unity required for perception and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ME3","text":"The opposition between subjective conditions and objective validity is reorganized: subjective conditions become precisely the conditions of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ME4","text":"The opposition between a priori necessity and empirical inquiry is redistributed: the former governs nature in general, while the latter determines particular laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ME5","text":"Space and time are repositioned as neither merely passive receptivity nor independent objects, but as formal intuitions whose unity presupposes synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text establishes the applicability of categories to all objects that can be presented in sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Perception is produced through the coordinated functions of sensibility, apprehension, imagination, and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The deduction secures objectivity by reconstructing its conditions of possibility rather than by deriving concepts from objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomena have being only in relation to sensibility and understanding, while temporal succession becomes objectively determinable only through categorial synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT5","text":"Epistemic-Limiting — The same argument that grounds necessary laws for experience limits cognition of nature to appearances and leaves things in themselves indeterminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central deduction from perceptual synthesis to the universal validity of categories for possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT2","text":"The system depends critically on identifying the unity of apprehension with categorial unity while distinguishing formal intuition from the empirical forms apprehended within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT3","text":"The strongest transition is from the dependence of perception on synthesis to the dependence of experience on categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT4","text":"The main compression occurs in the claim that the unity belonging a priori to space and time nevertheless presupposes a synthesis not belonging to sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between assigning formal-intuitive unity to space and time and locating the relevant synthetic unity in understanding through categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between the subject-relativity of phenomenal laws and the claim that things in themselves must independently possess law-conformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT7","text":"The argument remains coherent if its conclusion is restricted to the formal conditions of possible experience rather than extended to particular empirical laws or things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 39","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","context":"Chapter 39","original_title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","original_context":"Chapter 39"},"original_title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC","layer":"CH","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","order":40,"parents":["RAW_BA94_RTDC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_RTDC@R001","sha256":"b582cef4cd14c511a5ad970c7051fb73a4274a7fbb62b96c47d1efef866e4a15","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS1","text":"The argument seeks to explain how necessary a priori concepts can validly govern experience without being derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS2","text":"It distinguishes thinking an object through categories from cognizing an object through a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS3","text":"Because human intuition is sensuous, cognition of given objects is empirical and therefore belongs to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS4","text":"Experience nevertheless contains a priori elements, so its conformity with the categories must be explained transcendental-ly rather than empirically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS5","text":"The alternatives are reduced to empirical derivation, a priori constitution, or a preformation model; empirical derivation and preformation are rejected because they cannot preserve necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AS6","text":"The deduction concludes that categories are principles of the possibility of experience, grounded in the synthetic unity of apperception and related to space and time, while their concrete application is deferred to transcendental judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A1","text":"An object can be thought only through categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","text":"A thought can be cognized only through an intuition corresponding to its conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A3","text":"Human intuitions are sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A4","text":"Cognition of an object insofar as it is given is empirical, and empirical cognition is experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A5","text":"Pure intuitions and pure conceptions of the understanding contain a priori elements of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A6","text":"Necessary harmony between experience and the conceptions of its objects must arise either because experience makes the conceptions possible or because the conceptions make experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","text":"The categories are a priori conceptions independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A8","text":"An empirical origin cannot account for the categories without reducing their origin to an unintelligible generation from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A9","text":"The categories contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A10","text":"Determination of an object requires intuition, although the exercise of thought through the categories is not itself limited to sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A11","text":"Thought without intuition can have consequences for the subject’s reasoning and volition without constituting cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A12","text":"The necessity expressed by the categories must possess objective validity rather than merely subjective psychological necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A13","text":"A preformation account treats the categories as subjective aptitudes harmonized with nature by contingent constitution and thereby removes their genuine necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:A14","text":"Pure conceptions of the understanding function as principles of the possibility of experience, whose ultimate formal ground is the original synthetic unity of apperception in relation to space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX1","text":"Categories alone do not cognize an object without corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX2","text":"Human a priori cognition does not extend to objects outside possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX3","text":"The categories are not derived entirely from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX4","text":"The categories are not explained by an empirical generatio aequivoca.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX5","text":"The categories are not merely subjective aptitudes implanted alongside human existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX6","text":"Necessity is not reducible to the fact that a subject is constitutionally compelled to connect representations in a certain way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX7","text":"Useful consequences of thought for reason or volition do not amount to cognition of the thought object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:NX8","text":"This section does not yet provide the principles governing the concrete application of the categories to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D1","text":"Human object-cognition is restricted to possible experience because cognition requires intuition and human intuition is sensuous.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","CH_BA94_RTDC:A3","CH_BA94_RTDC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D2","text":"The empirical character of experience does not imply that all of its constitutive elements originate empirically.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A4","CH_BA94_RTDC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D3","text":"Since the categories are independent of experience and empirical origin is unavailable, they must be conditions under which experience is possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A6","CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","CH_BA94_RTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D4","text":"An empirical account of the categories would fail to explain the necessary conformity between experience and the categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A6","CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","CH_BA94_RTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D5","text":"The preformation hypothesis converts objective necessity into a contingent fact about subjective organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A12","CH_BA94_RTDC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D6","text":"Once category-connections are treated as merely subjective, objective knowledge becomes vulnerable to the status of illusion and loses a basis for rational dispute.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A12","CH_BA94_RTDC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D7","text":"The absence of intuition blocks object-determination but does not block every rational use of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A10","CH_BA94_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D8","text":"The deduction presents the categories as principles of experience rather than as contents acquired from experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A5","CH_BA94_RTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:D9","text":"The grounding of experience in synthetic unity, space, and time establishes the framework within which the categories must later be applied to phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD1","text":"Objectivity is secured immanently within the conditions of possible experience rather than by appeal either to empirical acquisition or to cognition beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A5","CH_BA94_RTDC:A6","CH_BA94_RTDC:A9","CH_BA94_RTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD2","text":"The system produces an epistemic asymmetry in which categorical thought can exceed intuition while determinate cognition cannot exceed possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A1","CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","CH_BA94_RTDC:A10","CH_BA94_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD3","text":"Necessity functions as the decisive criterion separating transcendental validity from both empiricism and subjective psychological compulsion.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","CH_BA94_RTDC:A8","CH_BA94_RTDC:A12","CH_BA94_RTDC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD4","text":"The argument is transcendental because it infers the conditions of experience from the necessary conformity of experience with cognition rather than reconstructing a causal history of the categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A6","CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","CH_BA94_RTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD5","text":"Synthetic unity of apperception functions as the formal bridge between the understanding’s categories and the spatiotemporal field in which experience is given.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A3","CH_BA94_RTDC:A5","CH_BA94_RTDC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SD6","text":"The argument preserves a rational domain beyond theoretical object-cognition while formally excluding that domain from the present deduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A10","CH_BA94_RTDC:A11","CH_BA94_RTDC:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the falsity of the claim that experience is the sole source of every element present in cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the identification of all nonempirical thought with cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes any account in which the necessity of the categories is merely a contingent feature of human psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a genetic or causal explanation of the categories as sufficient to establish their validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes objective validity from being independent of the conditions under which objects can be experienced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the reduction of rationality to theoretical determination of objects, since reason may also determine the subject and its volition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX1","text":"Unconditioned cognition of objects beyond sensibility is rendered inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX2","text":"The possibility that categories disclose objects independently of the conditions of possible experience is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX3","text":"The opposition between subjective form and objective validity is rendered untenable when the subject’s a priori structures are treated as conditions of objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX4","text":"The positive account of how the categories operate in experience remains absent from this section and appears only as a deferred task.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS1","text":"Object thought → categories; object cognition → corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS2","text":"Sensuous intuition → empirical cognition → experience; therefore human a priori cognition is restricted to objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS3","text":"Necessary harmony between experience and categories → empirical-origin alternative or a priori-condition alternative; empirical origin rejected → categories as conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS4","text":"Preformation alternative → subjective aptitude → loss of necessity → loss of objective validity → skeptical consequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS5","text":"Categories plus pure intuition plus synthetic unity of apperception → experience understood as the determination of phenomena in space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS6","text":"General transcendental grounding → deferred inquiry into the principles governing the categories’ application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:AF1","text":"Problem of necessary conformity → distinction between thought and cognition → sensuous limitation of intuition → separation of empirical cognition from a priori conditions → elimination of empirical origin → rejection of subjective preformation → categories as conditions of possible experience → deferral to transcendental judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME1","text":"A priori cognition is reclassified from cognition of a supersensible object to cognition of the conditions governing possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME2","text":"Thinking and cognizing are separated into distinct operations rather than treated as equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME3","text":"Objectivity is relocated in necessary validity for possible experience rather than in empirical derivation or private subjective constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME4","text":"The apparent opposition between empiricism and a priori cognition is reorganized as a question of the conditions that make experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME5","text":"The epistemic field is divided between unrestricted categorical thought and intuition-bound object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ME6","text":"The investigation of category validity is redirected from origin theories toward the transcendental exercise of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text establishes the relation among categories, intuitions, cognition, and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT2","text":"Structural: The categories are positioned as conditions that make the necessary organization of experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument secures objective validity by exhausting and rejecting competing origin models rather than by describing the psychological acquisition of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Reality as experienced is constituted as phenomena determined within space and time, while the unity of apperception provides the formal relation between understanding and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central argument from the distinction between categories and intuition through the conclusion that categories condition possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT2","text":"The extracted sequence preserves the actual movement from epistemic limitation to transcendental grounding and then to the rejection of rival origin accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the specific principles by which each category makes experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT4","text":"The argument is internally coherent only if thought without intuition is distinguished from cognition rather than treated as meaningless.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT5","text":"The strongest inference is the incompatibility between subjective preformation and the necessity required by objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT6","text":"The system depends on treating the two principal origin alternatives as exhaustive and on using necessity as the criterion of transcendental status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT7","text":"A structural tension remains between the categories’ unbounded sphere of thought and the strict restriction of cognition to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 40","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","context":"Chapter 40","original_title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","original_context":"Chapter 40"},"original_title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP","layer":"CH","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","order":41,"parents":["RAW_BA94_BIAP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_BIAP@R001","sha256":"ca223c67e984cc76a0acb8c232c855dbe8913173909d127ddf6a100debf311cc","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine which higher cognitive faculties can supply objectively valid principles within transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS2","text":"The division of general logic cannot be transferred unchanged to transcendental logic because transcendental logic has a determinate content: pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS3","text":"Understanding, judgement, and reason must therefore be separated according to their distinct modes of transcendental employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS4","text":"Understanding and judgement can yield objectively valid cognition of phenomena, whereas reason becomes dialectical when it seeks cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS5","text":"The analytic of principles is consequently restricted to the valid application of pure concepts of understanding to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AS6","text":"The chapter reframes itself as a doctrine of the faculty of judgement because its governing task is the application of principles rather than the mere inventory of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A1","text":"General logic is organized according to the higher faculties of cognition: understanding, judgement, and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A2","text":"General logic abstracts from all content of cognition and concerns only the form of discursive thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A3","text":"The form of reason has a law that can be discovered a priori by analysing the operations of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A4","text":"Transcendental logic concerns the determinate content of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A5","text":"A division valid for general logic cannot be transferred without modification to transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","text":"Understanding and judgement possess objectively valid functions within transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","text":"Pure concepts of the understanding provide necessary conditions for establishing a priori laws concerning phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","text":"Reason becomes dialectical when it seeks a priori cognition of objects beyond the bounds of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A9","text":"Illusory assertions of reason cannot constitute a canon of objectively valid cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A10","text":"The analytic of principles is a canon for the faculty of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A11","text":"The proper object of the doctrine is the application of pure concepts of the understanding to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:A12","text":"Transcendental dialectic is the proper department for the illusory transcendental employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX1","text":"Transcendental logic is not a merely formal logic that abstracts from every determinate content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX2","text":"The transcendental analytic is not a canon for reason in its attempt to determine objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX3","text":"Reason’s transcendental employment is not objectively valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX4","text":"The analytic of principles is not an unrestricted theory of all a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX5","text":"Pure concepts of the understanding are not self-applying principles whose validity is independent of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX6","text":"The principles of the understanding are not valid as determinations of objects outside possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:NX7","text":"Transcendental dialectic is not part of the logic of truth, although it belongs to the systematic treatment of transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D1","text":"The faculties must be assigned different logical functions because transcendental logic retains determinate cognitive content.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A1","CH_BA94_BIAP:A4","CH_BA94_BIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D2","text":"The a priori analysis of reason’s formal operations does not establish the objective validity of reason’s transcendental conclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A3","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D3","text":"The existence of a formal law of reason does not entail the existence of a valid transcendental canon for reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A2","CH_BA94_BIAP:A3","CH_BA94_BIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D4","text":"The validity of pure concepts depends on their application within the field of phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D5","text":"The analytic of principles must instruct judgement in the application of concepts rather than merely describe the concepts themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","CH_BA94_BIAP:A10","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D6","text":"The boundary of possible experience separates objectively valid understanding from dialectical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:D7","text":"The distinction between analytic and dialectic is determined by objective validity rather than by the mere presence of a priori reasoning.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A4","CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SD1","text":"Transcendental logic is internally stratified into a logic of truth for understanding and judgement and a logic of illusion for reason’s overextension.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A4","CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:A9","CH_BA94_BIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SD2","text":"The boundary of possible experience functions as the jurisdictional limit that converts otherwise unified cognition into separate regimes of validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A4","CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SD3","text":"The analytic of principles is an operation of controlled application: it transforms pure conceptual conditions into valid laws only through judgement’s relation to phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","CH_BA94_BIAP:A10","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SD4","text":"A priori necessity is divided between valid conditions of phenomenal cognition and invalid claims about objects transcending experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SD5","text":"The same systematic architecture that orders cognition also produces its critical boundary by showing that reason cannot occupy the same valid role in transcendental logic as understanding and judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A1","CH_BA94_BIAP:A5","CH_BA94_BIAP:A6","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the assumption that a priori form alone guarantees objective reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that every faculty recognized by general logic has an equally valid transcendental employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a unitary conception of reason in which formal rational necessity and objective cognition coincide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that pure concepts establish laws without a determinate domain of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the identification of systematic completeness with unrestricted cognitive legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX6","text":"The system excludes cognition of supersensible objects as a possible extension of the analytic of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX7","text":"The system excludes the reduction of judgement to a passive intermediary between concepts and objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX1","text":"The system denies that cognition can secure objectivity merely by extending formal necessity beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX2","text":"The system leaves no valid analytic route from pure reason to objects that cannot be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses the possibility of a single canon governing understanding, judgement, and reason without differentiating their objective reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX4","text":"The system makes the absence of an experiential field decisive: without possible experience, a priori synthesis loses its status as objectively valid cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX5","text":"The system treats unbounded metaphysical cognition not as an incomplete science but as a structurally different and illusory employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS1","text":"Higher faculties of cognition → division of logical functions → distinction between understanding, judgement, and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS2","text":"Formal abstraction from content → general logic → a priori analysis of the form of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS3","text":"Determinate content of pure cognition → transcendental logic → restriction of validity conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS4","text":"Pure concepts of understanding + application to phenomena → necessary conditions of a priori laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS5","text":"A priori attempt to cognize objects beyond possible experience → loss of objective validity → transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS6","text":"Objective validity of understanding and judgement + invalid transcendental employment of reason → division between analytic of truth and dialectic of illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS7","text":"Need to regulate application rather than merely enumerate concepts → analytic of principles → doctrine of the faculty of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF1","text":"Faculty-based division of general logic → mismatch with determinate-content transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF2","text":"Mismatch → differentiation of valid and invalid transcendental employments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF3","text":"Differentiation → restriction of objective validity to understanding and judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF4","text":"Restriction → confinement of pure concepts to their application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF5","text":"Confinement → exclusion of reason’s beyond-experience claims from the analytic of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:AF6","text":"Exclusion → designation of the analytic of principles as a doctrine of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME1","text":"The conceptual field is reorganized from a uniform hierarchy of faculties into differentiated regimes of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME2","text":"The boundary between analytic and dialectic is relocated from the presence of a priori reasoning to the possibility of valid application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME3","text":"Pure concepts are reclassified from independently sufficient forms into conditions whose lawful force depends on judgement and phenomenal application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME4","text":"Reason’s metaphysical overreach is reclassified from failed analytic cognition into a distinct logic of illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME5","text":"The analytic of principles is shifted from a static catalogue of principles to a regulatory theory of their use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ME6","text":"Possible experience becomes the boundary-marker determining whether a priori cognition belongs to truth or illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter identifies the proper subject and scope of the analytic of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Transcendental logic is divided according to the different validity conditions governing understanding, judgement, and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument prevents the analytic from being captured by metaphysical claims that exceed the domain of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objective cognition is restricted to objects as they can stand within possible experience, while a priori necessity is denied unrestricted access to transcendent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Validity is produced by the relation among pure concepts, judgement, and phenomena rather than by formal cognition in isolation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between general logic and transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to derive the separation of analytic truth from transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT3","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of objective validity to understanding and judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT4","text":"The structure remains underdetermined regarding the specific principles that the subsequent analytic will establish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent if formal reason and objectively valid transcendental cognition are treated as distinct functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT6","text":"Its central fault line is that reason possesses an a priori formal law while lacking a valid transcendental canon for objects beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT7","text":"A second tension remains between the chapter’s designation as an analytic of principles and its final reclassification as a doctrine of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement from faculty-based classification through validity restriction to the regulation of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 41","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","context":"Chapter 41","original_title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","original_context":"Chapter 41"},"original_title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ","layer":"CH","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","order":42,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ITFJ"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ITFJ@R001","sha256":"271a7377a8d45ab54cb51b6e922d27c8cf9d6510b9cb4280dc60d27b5fcfe76d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS1","text":"Understanding operates through rules, whereas judgment determines whether a particular case falls under a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS2","text":"General logic cannot instruct subsumption because any rule for applying rules would itself require judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS3","text":"Judgment is therefore an irreducible talent that formal learning cannot guarantee and that exercise alone can develop.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS4","text":"Examples train judgment but can weaken the understanding’s grasp of rules in their universal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS5","text":"Transcendental logic differs from general logic because it can determine the conditions under which pure concepts may be correctly applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS6","text":"Philosophy must abandon positive expansion of pure cognition in favor of critique that negatively prevents misuse of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS7","text":"This critique proceeds from conditions of possible object-givenness to schematism and then to the principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A1","text":"Understanding is the faculty of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","text":"Judgment is the faculty of subsuming a given case under a rule and distinguishing whether the case belongs under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A3","text":"General logic abstracts from the content of cognition and establishes only formal rules of concepts, judgments, and conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4","text":"Any rule intended to direct subsumption would itself require judgment for its correct application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","text":"Judgment is a natural talent that cannot be supplied by tuition and can be developed only through exercise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A6","text":"Education can transmit rules borrowed from other minds but cannot guarantee their correct application when judgment is deficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A7","text":"Examples sharpen judgment but can weaken the understanding’s apprehension of rules in their universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8","text":"Transcendental logic is charged with securing and directing the employment of pure understanding through determinate rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A9","text":"Philosophy as a doctrine aimed at enlarging pure a priori cognition yields little or no gain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A10","text":"Philosophy as critique can guard against errors of judgment in the employment of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A11","text":"Transcendental philosophy can indicate both the general condition of a rule and, a priori, the case to which the rule applies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A12","text":"Pure concepts must relate a priori to objects if they are to possess objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13","text":"Objective validity requires general conditions under which objects can be given in conformity with pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:A14","text":"The transcendental doctrine of judgment proceeds from schematism to the synthetic a priori principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX1","text":"General logic is not a doctrine that directs the act of subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX2","text":"Possession of general rules is not equivalent to correct judgment in particular cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX3","text":"Scholastic discipline cannot compensate for a deficiency in natural judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX4","text":"No prescribed rule can guarantee correct application in the absence or deficiency of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX5","text":"Examples are not adequate substitutes for the universal apprehension of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX6","text":"Philosophy is not justified here as a positive enlargement of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX7","text":"Critique is not primarily a positive production of new pure cognition but a guard against error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX8","text":"Pure concepts are not objectively valid merely as formal logical structures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX9","text":"Objective validity cannot be established by a posteriori demonstration alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D1","text":"General logic cannot provide a non-circular rule for subsumption because such a rule would itself have to be subsumed and applied by judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A3","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D2","text":"A person may possess extensive scientific rules and still fail to apply them correctly to concrete cases.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D3","text":"Exercise and examples are compensatory supports for judgment but cannot create the judgmental faculty itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D4","text":"Examples have a divided function: they improve the application of rules while risking dependence on particular circumstances.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D5","text":"The impossibility of directing judgment through general logic does not preclude a restricted direction of judgment within the domain of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D6","text":"The philosophical task shifts from expanding pure cognition to preventing invalid applications of the pure concepts already possessed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A9","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D7","text":"The employment of pure concepts requires an a priori account of the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A11","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A12","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:D8","text":"The doctrine must first determine the sensuous conditions of application and only then derive the synthetic a priori principles that depend upon them.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD1","text":"Rule possession, rule formulation, and rule application are distinct cognitive functions that cannot be reduced to one another.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A3","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD2","text":"Formal education can increase understanding without producing reliable judgment, creating a structural gap between learnedness and correct use.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD3","text":"Transcendental logic functions as a bounded exception to the general non-teachability of judgment: it does not manufacture judgment but specifies conditions governing the use of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4","text":"Critique acquires methodological force through negation: it secures cognition by excluding invalid applications rather than by enlarging the stock of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A9","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD5","text":"Examples occupy an intrinsically ambivalent pedagogical position because they are necessary for exercising judgment while potentially obstructing apprehension of universal rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD6","text":"Objective validity is generated by the relation among pure concept, object, and conditions of possible givenness, not by the concept in isolation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A11","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A12","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD7","text":"The transition from judgment to schematism and principles converts the problem of application into a doctrine of the conditions governing pure cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD8","text":"The system remains dependent on an incompletely formalized boundary between the general impossibility of teaching judgment and the transcendental possibility of directing its pure employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a complete algorithm for determining every case of subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX2","text":"It excludes the reduction of judgment to the possession or manipulation of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX3","text":"It excludes an educational mechanism capable of reliably manufacturing judgment where natural judgment is absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX4","text":"It excludes the equivalence of theoretical mastery and competent application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX5","text":"It excludes empirical examples as sufficient grounds for the universal validity of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX6","text":"It excludes positive philosophical construction as a secure route to the enlargement of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX7","text":"It excludes objective validity from formal conceptual structure without conditions of possible object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX8","text":"It excludes an unrestricted employment of pure understanding independent of sensuous conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX1","text":"A complete formal closure of judgment is unavailable; the system requires an irreducible act of distinguishing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX2","text":"A transferable educational technology capable of guaranteeing judgment is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX3","text":"Positive philosophical sovereignty over pure cognition is foreclosed; critique retains authority only as a regulator of misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX4","text":"Unconditioned conceptual access to objects is impossible; pure thought cannot supply object-givenness by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX5","text":"The text leaves no fully articulated criterion for where ordinary non-teachability of judgment ends and transcendental direction of judgment begins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS1","text":"Rule-governed understanding produces the problem of subsumption because a rule does not determine its own application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS2","text":"General logic abstracts from content and therefore retains only the formal structure of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS3","text":"Any attempt to add a general rule for subsumption reproduces the original problem at a higher level and presupposes judgment again.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS4","text":"The resulting gap between rule possession and rule application is expressed pedagogically as the difference between learnedness and judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS5","text":"Exercise and examples can train the operation of judgment but cannot replace the natural capacity required for it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS6","text":"The domain of pure understanding introduces a restricted alternative in which transcendental logic specifies conditions for the employment of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS7","text":"Because pure concepts must relate a priori to objects, their validity is tested through conditions under which objects can be given in conformity with them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS8","text":"The resulting doctrine proceeds from sensuous conditions of application to schematism and from schematism to synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF1","text":"Rule-governed understanding → subsumptive judgment → abstraction of general logic → recursive failure of a rule for subsumption → irreducibility of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF2","text":"Irreducibility of judgment → exercise and examples → persistent gap between learned rules and correct application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF3","text":"Gap in application → distinction between positive doctrine and negative critique → prevention of misuse of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF4","text":"A priori conditions of object-givenness → schematism → synthetic a priori principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME1","text":"Judgment is reclassified from rule acquisition to the determination of whether a particular case falls under a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME2","text":"Logic is divided into formal general logic, which cannot direct subsumption, and transcendental logic, which can specify conditions for the pure employment of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME3","text":"Examples are reclassified from evidentiary instances of rules into instruments for exercising judgment, with a simultaneous risk to universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME4","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from a constructive expansion of pure cognition into a critique that prevents invalid use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME5","text":"Objective validity is relocated from logical form alone to the relation between concept and conditions of possible object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME6","text":"The problem of judgment is transformed into a two-stage doctrine: conditions of application first, principles of pure understanding second.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text distinguishes understanding, judgment, rules, subsumption, examples, schematism, and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT2","text":"Structural: The argument separates rule production, rule possession, rule application, and the conditions that make application valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The proper philosophical strategy is shifted from constructing more pure cognition to critically policing the use of existing pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Objectivity depends on a priori conditions under which objects can be given, and the doctrine advances sequentially from sensible conditions to principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the distinction between understanding and judgment to the architecture of transcendental doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT2","text":"The precise nature of schemata, principles, and the a priori determination of cases remains underdetermined because those elements are announced rather than developed here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if transcendental direction is treated as a restricted domain-specific exception rather than as a universal teachability of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT4","text":"Its strongest structural claim is the non-equivalence of rule possession and correct subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT5","text":"Its principal fault line lies between the asserted non-teachability of judgment and the claimed capacity of transcendental logic to direct its employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT6","text":"The argument relies on compressed assertions concerning the failure of positive doctrine and the necessity of a priori conditions for objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the actual movement from formal insufficiency, through critique, to schematism and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 42","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","context":"Chapter 42","original_title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","original_context":"Chapter 42"},"original_title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","order":43,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CISP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CISP@R001","sha256":"d4ec929fa35cc3d95cf40e410729548637f1e58259982dc574e914a24c03b9f8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS1","text":"The application of pure concepts to sensible objects requires an account of how heterogeneous representations can enter into subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS2","text":"A third representation must mediate between categories and phenomena by being homogeneous with both.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS3","text":"Transcendental time-determinations supply this mediation as schemata produced by the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS4","text":"Each category receives objective applicability only through its rule-governed temporal schema.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS5","text":"The same sensible mediation that realizes categories also confines their objective use to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AS6","text":"The argument moves from heterogeneity, through temporal mediation, to empirical restriction of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A1","text":"Subsumption requires homogeneity between the concept and the object represented under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A2","text":"Categories are heterogeneous with empirical intuition and cannot be found in sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A3","text":"A mediating representation must be intellectual in relation to categories and sensible in relation to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A4","text":"Time is the formal condition of inner sense and is present in every empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A5","text":"A transcendental time-determination is homogeneous with categories through its a priori rule and with phenomena through temporal sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A6","text":"The transcendental schema is a pure product of imagination that determines inner sense according to a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A7","text":"A schema represents a general procedure for producing intuition in conformity with a concept rather than an image of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A8","text":"Pure concepts attain objective significance only when an object can be given under the formal conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A9","text":"Objects can be given to cognition only through modifications of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A10","text":"The schemata of categories are a priori determinations of time ordered by the categorical functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A11","text":"The unity of schematized intuition indirectly grounds the unity of apperception required for experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:A12","text":"Categories without schemata retain logical function but lack determinate objective meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX1","text":"Categories are not sensible contents that can be intuited in phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX2","text":"Schemas are not images, because an image is singular whereas a schema governs the production of possible images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX3","text":"A pure concept is not objectively meaningful merely by being thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX4","text":"Categories are not validly applicable to things in themselves as objects independently given beyond sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX5","text":"The schema is not an empirical representation, because it must mediate a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX6","text":"The schema of a category is not reducible to a spatial figure or any other image.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX7","text":"The category of substance is not a determinate object-concept once its temporal condition of permanence is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:NX8","text":"Transcendental truth is not cognition of supersensible objects but the condition of possible empirical truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D1","text":"The problem of applying categories is transformed from a question of resemblance between concept and object into a question of rule-governed temporal mediation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A1","CH_BA94_CISP:A2","CH_BA94_CISP:A3","CH_BA94_CISP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D2","text":"Imagination is assigned a transcendental function because it produces the temporal rule that connects understanding with sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A3","CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D3","text":"Quantity becomes objectively applicable through number as the successive generation of homogeneous time.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D4","text":"Quality becomes objectively applicable through degrees of temporal filling, including the continuous transition between reality and negation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D5","text":"Relation becomes objectively applicable through temporal rules of permanence, succession, and rule-governed coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D6","text":"Modality becomes objectively applicable through temporal determination of possibility, actual existence, and existence in all time.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D7","text":"Experience is possible only as a unified field in which intuitions are synthesized under temporal rules and connected in one apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A10","CH_BA94_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:D8","text":"The empirical restriction of categories is not an external limitation added after their formation but follows from the conditions required for their objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A8","CH_BA94_CISP:A9","CH_BA94_CISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SD1","text":"Objectivity is constituted as the lawful temporal synthesizeability of appearances rather than as access to objects independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A8","CH_BA94_CISP:A10","CH_BA94_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SD2","text":"The categories possess a double status: they are unrestricted logical functions in abstraction but objectively valid rules only within sensible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A8","CH_BA94_CISP:A9","CH_BA94_CISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SD3","text":"Time functions as the universal conversion medium through which intellectual unity acquires phenomenal determinacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A3","CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A5","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SD4","text":"The unity of experience depends on a hidden productive operation that cannot itself be replaced by either discursive concepts or empirical images.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISP:A7","CH_BA94_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SD5","text":"Transcendental truth precedes empirical truth by establishing the formal conditions under which empirical judgments can form one connected experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A8","CH_BA94_CISP:A10","CH_BA94_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:DNX1","text":"No direct intellectual intuition of categories in objects is available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:DNX2","text":"No object can be objectively determined by a category outside the temporal form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:DNX3","text":"No singular sensible presentation can exhaust the universality of a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:DNX4","text":"No purely logical determination supplies the material criteria by which an object can be cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:DNX5","text":"No account of experience can dispense with a mediating synthesis between receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SNX1","text":"Cognition of things as they are independently of the conditions under which they are given is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SNX2","text":"A category’s apparent metaphysical extension beyond experience is denied objective content while remaining thinkable in a merely logical sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3","text":"The source of the mediating synthesis remains unavailable to transparent theoretical inspection, because schematism is posited as an obscure art of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:SNX4","text":"The hoped-for coincidence of pure thought with objecthood without sensible mediation is structurally inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS1","text":"Heterogeneity of category and intuition → impossibility of immediate subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS2","text":"Requirement of homogeneity → necessity of a third, mixed representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS3","text":"Universality of time in sensibility + rule-governed unity of categories → transcendental temporal schema.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS4","text":"Schema as imaginative rule → temporal specification of quantity, quality, relation, and modality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS5","text":"Temporal specification → lawful synthesis of appearances into possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS6","text":"Dependence on temporal sensibility → realization and restriction of categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:FGS7","text":"Removal of sensibility → residual logical function without objective object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF1","text":"Heterogeneity of pure concepts and intuition → demand for homogeneous mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF2","text":"Demand for mediation → identification of transcendental time-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF3","text":"Time-determination → schema as rule of productive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF4","text":"Schema-rule → categorical ordering of appearances in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF5","text":"Temporal ordering → unified possible experience and transcendental truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:AF6","text":"Sensible condition of validity → exclusion of objective categorical knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME1","text":"The category is reclassified from a self-sufficient object-concept into a function requiring sensible schematization for objective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME2","text":"Imagination is reclassified from a merely reproductive image-making faculty into the mediator of understanding and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME3","text":"Time is reclassified from one form of intuition among others into the universal field in which every categorical schema receives applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME4","text":"The image/concept opposition is displaced by a threefold structure of image, schema, and concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME5","text":"The opposition between unrestricted metaphysical use and empirical use collapses into a distinction between logical significance and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ME6","text":"Transcendental truth is located in the architecture enabling experience rather than in a correspondence claim about things beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter explains how categories such as quantity, causality, substance, possibility, actuality, and necessity can apply to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Schematism organizes categorical application through rule-governed determinations of time produced by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument resolves the threat of a gap between pure thought and experience by relocating validity in mediation rather than direct conceptual possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects of cognition are determinable only insofar as they enter temporal conditions of appearance and unified consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the necessity of schematism from the heterogeneity of categories and intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT2","text":"The system reconstructs the schema’s role only if time is retained as universally present in empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT3","text":"The categorical schemata reconstruct the movement from abstract functions to determinate temporal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as objective validity is made conditional upon possible sensible givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive mechanism by which imagination generates specific schemata, since that operation is declared obscure rather than derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT6","text":"The strongest structural closure is the reciprocal thesis that sensibility both enables and limits understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISP:MRT7","text":"A residual tension persists between the categories’ asserted logical significance apart from sensibility and the claim that no object-concept remains once schematization is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 43","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","context":"Chapter 43","original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","original_context":"Chapter 43"},"original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":44,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CISA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CISA@R001","sha256":"d338b31613737466e09e366b4345acacb47cdaa4ff48a04d0a08c6b4728db6b3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS1","text":"The task is to exhibit systematically the pure a priori judgments actually produced by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS2","text":"The categories provide the natural and secure basis for identifying these judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS3","text":"Their application to possible experience determines the complete catalogue of transcendental principles belonging to the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS4","text":"A priori principles require proof even when no higher principle can objectively ground them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS5","text":"The required proof proceeds from the subjective conditions that make cognition of an object possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS6","text":"The inquiry is delimited by excluding principles grounded in sensibility or intuition while retaining their possibility as an issue of transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AS7","text":"The distinction between analytical and synthetical judgments is introduced to delimit and clarify the proper subject of the investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A1","text":"Pure a priori cognition of the understanding arises through the application of the categories to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A2","text":"The relation between the categories and sensibility determines the systematic catalogue of the understanding’s transcendental principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A3","text":"A principle may ground other judgments without being grounded in a higher or more general cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A4","text":"Foundational status does not exempt an a priori principle from the need for proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A5","text":"The possibility of cognition of an object can be established through the subjective conditions of that cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A6","text":"The categories govern the principles within the present system, whereas space and time belong to the conditions supplied by transcendental sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A7","text":"Mathematical principles are synthetic a priori but derive from intuition rather than from pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A8","text":"The possibility of mathematical synthetic a priori cognition can be treated without making mathematical proof itself the subject of this system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A9","text":"Analytical judgments must be considered as a contrastive boundary for identifying synthetic judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:A10","text":"The principles under investigation concern the use of the understanding in relation to possible experience, not objects considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX1","text":"The system is not an inventory of every a priori principle whatsoever.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX2","text":"The present inquiry does not investigate the principles of transcendental aesthetics as its proper subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX3","text":"Principles concerning space and time are not treated as principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX4","text":"The principles of transcendental aesthetics cannot be applied to objects as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX5","text":"Mathematical principles are not derived from pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX6","text":"The inquiry does not aim to prove the accuracy or apodeictic certainty of mathematical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX7","text":"Analytical judgments are not the proper subject of the investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX8","text":"A foundational a priori principle is not thereby a gratuitous assertion exempt from justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:NX9","text":"The categories do not license cognition of objects independently of their relation to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D1","text":"The categories can function as a complete classificatory guide for the principles of the pure understanding because their application to possible experience exhausts the relevant domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D2","text":"A system of principles can be both foundational for object-cognition and subject to transcendental proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A3","CH_BA94_CISA:A4","CH_BA94_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D3","text":"The proof of a first principle need not appeal to a still higher objective principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A3","CH_BA94_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D4","text":"The relevant proof establishes the conditions under which cognition of an object is possible rather than deducing the principle from an externally superior proposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A4","CH_BA94_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D5","text":"The system distinguishes the source of a principle from the conditions under which its application is legitimate.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A6","CH_BA94_CISA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D6","text":"Mathematical cognition can be included as an object of transcendental possibility without being assimilated to cognition generated by the categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A7","CH_BA94_CISA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D7","text":"The opposition between analytical and synthetical judgment prevents the theory of synthetic a priori principles from being left conceptually ambiguous.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:D8","text":"The scope of the system is determined by the intersection of pure understanding, categorical form, and possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A2","CH_BA94_CISA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SD1","text":"The system converts the question of first principles from a search for higher premises into an analysis of the conditions that make object-cognition possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A3","CH_BA94_CISA:A4","CH_BA94_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SD2","text":"The categories function simultaneously as principles of synthesis, a rule for systematic classification, and a boundary on legitimate application.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A2","CH_BA94_CISA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SD3","text":"The system produces a layered architecture in which sensibility supplies conditions of appearance, intuition supplies the basis of mathematics, and the categories supply principles for the understanding’s synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A6","CH_BA94_CISA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SD4","text":"The possibility of a complete catalogue depends on restricting completeness to the domain of the understanding’s categorical relation to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A2","CH_BA94_CISA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SD5","text":"The distinction between analytical and synthetical judgment operates as a control mechanism that prevents foundational analysis from collapsing into mere conceptual unpacking.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A7","CH_BA94_CISA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX1","text":"The system cannot be grounded on an unrestricted theory of all a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX2","text":"The understanding cannot claim principles whose validity is detached from their relation to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX3","text":"The categories cannot be treated as interchangeable with the forms of sensibility or the sources of mathematical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX4","text":"Objectivity cannot be secured solely by positing a principle as foundational.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX5","text":"Transcendental proof cannot be reduced to derivation from a higher empirical or metaphysical proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX6","text":"Systematic completeness cannot mean inclusion of every form of synthetic a priori cognition under one faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:DNX7","text":"The distinction between appearance and thing in itself cannot be suspended when specifying the valid domain of transcendental principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SNX1","text":"Unconditioned cognition of things in themselves is unavailable to the principles established through the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SNX2","text":"No single undifferentiated source of a priori cognition is admissible; sensibility, intuition, and understanding must remain functionally distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SNX3","text":"The legitimacy of first principles cannot rest on authority, assertion, or mere absence of a higher ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SNX4","text":"Completeness cannot be achieved by erasing the boundary between the conditions of appearance and the conditions of conceptual synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:SNX5","text":"The system presupposes that cognition requires a finite field of possible application rather than access to reality without conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS1","text":"Categories + possible experience → domain of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS2","text":"Domain of pure understanding + relation to sensibility → systematic catalogue of transcendental principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS3","text":"Foundational status + absence of higher cognition → need for transcendental rather than higher-level proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS4","text":"Subjective conditions of object-cognition → proof of the objective validity of foundational principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS5","text":"Separation of sensibility, intuition, and understanding → exclusion of aesthetic and mathematical principles from the category-system proper.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS6","text":"Excluded mathematical principles + synthetic a priori status → investigation of their possibility without mathematical demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS7","text":"Contrast with analytical judgment → determinate identification of synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:FGS8","text":"Restricted domain + transcendental proof + categorical ordering → system of the principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF1","text":"General conditions for using pure concepts → systematic exhibition of actual a priori judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF2","text":"Categories → catalogue of principles governing the understanding’s relation to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF3","text":"Foundational status without higher ground → requirement of proof from subjective conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF4","text":"Separation of sensibility, intuition, and understanding → restriction of the system’s proper object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF5","text":"Mathematical exception → transcendental treatment of possibility without mathematical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF6","text":"Analytical/synthetic opposition → clarification of the system’s proper subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:AF7","text":"Delimited domain + categorical structure + transcendental justification → system of the principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME1","text":"A priori principles are reclassified from unsupported foundations into objects requiring transcendental justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME2","text":"The categories become the organizing grid for the understanding’s principles rather than merely a list of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME3","text":"The boundary between sensibility and understanding becomes a condition of systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME4","text":"Mathematical cognition is relocated from the category-system proper to a neighboring domain whose possibility still requires transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME5","text":"The opposition between analytical and synthetical judgment becomes a methodological instrument for determining the field of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ME6","text":"The valid scope of object-cognition is mapped onto possible experience rather than onto objects considered independently of conditions of appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text announces a systematic inventory of the pure a priori judgments produced by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ALT2","text":"Structural — The inventory is generated by correlating categories with their application to possible experience and their relation to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces appeal to higher principles with transcendental proof from the conditions of cognition, while using exclusions to delimit the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Cognition is restricted to objects as phenomena within possible experience; things in themselves remain outside the valid application of the principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s central movement from categorical classification to a systematic theory of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between the source of a principle, the proof of its validity, and the domain of its application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT3","text":"The structure remains coherent because completeness is restricted to the principles of the pure understanding in relation to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT4","text":"The main unresolved tension is that a priori principles are foundational yet require justification, which the text resolves by shifting from objective derivation to transcendental grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT5","text":"A second tension remains between excluding mathematical principles from the system and treating their possibility as necessarily relevant to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CISA:MRT6","text":"The chapter’s final system depends on the maintained separation of analytical and synthetical judgment, sensibility and understanding, and phenomena and things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 44","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","context":"Chapter 44","original_title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","original_context":"Chapter 44"},"original_title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","order":45,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SISP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SISP@R001","sha256":"c35a3bcf02aab3275fde7e623103bedee2fe9dda3c437b73f9c9a5945f98ae6c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS1","text":"The central problem is to determine what the principle of contradiction can and cannot establish about judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS2","text":"Every judgment is first subjected to noncontradiction as a universal negative condition of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS3","text":"Noncontradiction is then separated from correspondence with the object and from the possession of grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS4","text":"The principle receives a positive function only within analytical cognition, where the predicate belongs to the subject-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS5","text":"Synthetic cognition remains outside the principle’s capacity to establish truth, although it remains bound by its prohibition of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS6","text":"The temporalized formula of contradiction is rejected because it confuses logical contradiction with the successive alteration of predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AS7","text":"The corrected formulation must express contradiction through the relation between a predicate and the subject-concept rather than through temporal simultaneity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A1","text":"A judgment that contradicts itself is nullified as cognition independently of its relation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A2","text":"Noncontradiction is a universal negative condition of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A3","text":"A noncontradictory judgment may nevertheless be false or groundless.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A4","text":"The principle of contradiction is formal and applies to cognitions merely as cognitions, without regard to their content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A5","text":"In an analytical judgment, the predicate is contained in or constitutive of the conception of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A6","text":"In an analytical judgment, the contrary of a constituent predicate conflicts with the subject-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A7","text":"The principle of contradiction is sufficient for recognizing the truth of analytical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A8","text":"The principle of contradiction is a necessary condition of truth but not, in general, the determining ground of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A9","text":"The principle of contradiction cannot directly establish the truth of a synthetic proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A10","text":"The formula that a thing cannot be and not be at the same time introduces both a temporal condition and a synthetic relation between predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A11","text":"Predicates that cannot belong to the same thing at the same time may belong to it at different times.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A12","text":"Logical contradiction must not be restricted to simultaneous temporal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:A13","text":"An analytical negative judgment does not require a temporal qualification when the negated predicate is excluded by the subject-concept itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX1","text":"The absence of contradiction is not equivalent to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX2","text":"The absence of contradiction is not equivalent to correspondence with the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX3","text":"The absence of contradiction is not equivalent to the possession of grounds, whether a priori or a posteriori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX4","text":"The principle of contradiction is not a content-bearing or object-determining principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX5","text":"The principle of contradiction is not a sufficient criterion for synthetic truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX6","text":"Logical contradiction is not identical with the incompatibility of predicates at one moment in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX7","text":"The temporalized formula is not an adequate formulation of the purely logical principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:NX8","text":"An analytical contradiction is not produced by separating a predicate from the subject-concept and opposing that predicate to its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D1","text":"The principle of contradiction functions as a preliminary nullification test before any question of object-relation arises.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A1","CH_BA94_SISP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D2","text":"A judgment that survives the contradiction test remains epistemically admissible but not thereby established as true.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A3","CH_BA94_SISP:A4","CH_BA94_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D3","text":"The principle acquires positive cognitive force when contradiction with the subject-concept is sufficient to exclude the contrary predicate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A5","CH_BA94_SISP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D4","text":"Analytical truth can be recognized through contradiction because conceptual containment supplies the missing relation between predicate and subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A5","CH_BA94_SISP:A6","CH_BA94_SISP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D5","text":"The same principle operates negatively for all cognition but positively only where the judgment is analytical.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A2","CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D6","text":"The distinction between analytical and synthetic judgment determines whether contradiction can function as a truth-recognition procedure or only as a prohibition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:A8","CH_BA94_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D7","text":"Temporal succession shows that opposed predicates do not by themselves constitute a contradiction in the subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A10","CH_BA94_SISP:A11","CH_BA94_SISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D8","text":"An analytical negative proposition is atemporal in its logical force when the predicate’s exclusion follows from the subject-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A5","CH_BA94_SISP:A12","CH_BA94_SISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:D9","text":"Correcting the formula requires removing the temporal condition and restoring contradiction to the subject-predicate structure of analytical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A5","CH_BA94_SISP:A10","CH_BA94_SISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SD1","text":"The principle of contradiction is simultaneously universal as a boundary condition and restricted as a truth-producing principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A2","CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SD2","text":"The positive use of logic does not abolish its formal character; it becomes possible only when the content of the judgment is already structurally contained in the subject-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A4","CH_BA94_SISP:A5","CH_BA94_SISP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SD3","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction marks two different epistemic powers of contradiction: determination in the first case and constraint in the second.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:A8","CH_BA94_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SD4","text":"The temporalized formula commits a category error by treating a change in predication as though it were the self-nullification of a judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A10","CH_BA94_SISP:A11","CH_BA94_SISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SD5","text":"Truth is internally stratified into logical admissibility, analytical recognizability, and synthetic determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A3","CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:A8","CH_BA94_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the falsity of the equation between consistency and truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the derivation of object-correspondence from formal logical validity alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the reduction of grounds of cognition to the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the use of temporal succession as the defining structure of logical contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the detachment of predicates from their conceptual relation to the subject when determining analytical contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a single undifferentiated operation of contradiction across analytical and synthetic judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SNX1","text":"A purely formal principle cannot by itself bridge cognition and the object cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SNX2","text":"No general positive criterion of synthetic truth is supplied by the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SNX3","text":"The unification of analytical and synthetic truth under one formal rule is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SNX4","text":"Temporal change is inadmissible as the ground of logical contradiction, even though it remains relevant to changing predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:SNX5","text":"The determining ground of synthetic cognition remains external to the logical principle and is left unprovided in the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS1","text":"Any judgment enters the system through a contradiction test: judgment → self-consistency check → nullification if contradiction is present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS2","text":"Survival of the contradiction test produces only formal admissibility: noncontradiction → continued status as cognition → unresolved truth-status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS3","text":"The unresolved truth-status is divided by judgment type: analytical judgment versus synthetic judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS4","text":"Analytical cognition activates conceptual containment: subject-concept → contained predicate → exclusion of contrary predicate → recognizable truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS5","text":"Synthetic cognition activates only logical restriction: proposition → noncontradiction requirement → no direct truth-establishment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS6","text":"The temporal formula is tested against succession: simultaneous predicate opposition → possible successive compatibility → failure of the formula as a general logical principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:FGS7","text":"The corrected formulation restores the subject-concept as the site of contradiction and removes temporal qualification from analytical negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AF1","text":"Universal demand for self-consistency → nullification of contradiction → insufficiency of consistency for truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AF2","text":"Formal logical principle → distinction between analytical and synthetic cognition → restricted positive use in analytical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:AF3","text":"Temporalized contradiction formula → distinction between predicate incompatibility and conceptual contradiction → reformulation through the subject-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME1","text":"Truth is reclassified from a single criterion into differentiated levels of logical admissibility, analytical recognition, and synthetic grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME2","text":"The principle of contradiction is repositioned from a universal truth-generator to a universal boundary condition with a limited positive application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME3","text":"The opposition between “true” and “contradictory” is separated from the opposition between “grounded” and “groundless.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME4","text":"Temporal change is removed from the definition of logical contradiction and reassigned to the domain of successive predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME5","text":"The analytical judgment becomes identifiable through conceptual containment rather than through the mere simultaneity of predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ME6","text":"Synthetic truth is redrawn as a separate epistemic problem that logic can constrain but cannot solve.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument evaluates the principle that a subject cannot possess a contradictory predicate and corrects its temporalized formulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The argument differentiates formal logic, analytical judgment, synthetic judgment, object-correspondence, and epistemic grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument converts a seemingly universal truth criterion into a bounded instrument whose function depends on the type of cognition being assessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The argument separates logical contradiction from temporal succession, allowing predicates to change across time without making the cognition self-contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central distinction between noncontradiction as a necessary condition and truth as a further determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT2","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s movement from negative logical validity to the restricted positive function of contradiction in analytical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest where it differentiates subject-conceptual contradiction from successive changes in predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT4","text":"The apparent tension between calling the principle a criterion of truth and denying its sufficiency is resolved by restricting its positive authority to analytical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT5","text":"The boundary between conceptual containment and synthetic predication remains compressed rather than fully formalized in the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT6","text":"The grounds of synthetic truth are structurally excluded from the principle but not independently supplied, leaving the larger epistemological problem open.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the internal coherence of the passage, including its deliberate refusal to make logical noncontradiction the determining ground of all truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 45","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","context":"Chapter 45","original_title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","original_context":"Chapter 45"},"original_title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","order":46,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_2"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_2@R001","sha256":"70f5a37b9418c498143bcff44e0b3d60170f0e323718a7f143df3f7c75deeef2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS1","text":"The possibility and objective validity of synthetic a priori judgement cannot be determined by general logic because such judgement exceeds conceptual containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS2","text":"Synthetic judgement requires a mediating field in which heterogeneous representations can be combined rather than merely compared by identity or contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS3","text":"The mediating conditions of synthesis are a priori intuition, imagination, and the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS4","text":"A representation acquires objective reality only through possible experience, in which an object can be given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS5","text":"Possible experience requires rules that synthesize appearances into a unified consciousness of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS6","text":"Synthetic a priori judgements are valid only as conditions necessary for the possibility of experience and therefore for objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AS7","text":"The supreme principle subjects every object of possible experience to the necessary synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A1","text":"Analytic judgement determines a concept without exceeding what is already thought in it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A2","text":"Synthetic judgement connects a concept with something not already thought in that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A3","text":"The truth or falsity of a synthetic judgement cannot be determined from the judgement’s concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A4","text":"Every synthesis of representations requires a mediating domain in which the representations can be combined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A5","text":"Time, as the a priori form of inner sense, contains the representations whose combination makes synthesis possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A6","text":"Imagination performs the synthesis of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","text":"Apperception supplies the unity required for synthesized representations to constitute judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","text":"Cognition has objective reality only if its object can be given in intuition within real or possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A9","text":"Concepts without possible experiential application are empty of objective meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","text":"Experience is possible only through a rule-governed synthesis of appearances under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A11","text":"A priori principles have objective validity only insofar as they express necessary conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:A12","text":"Objects of experience are necessarily subject to the conditions that make unified experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX1","text":"The possibility of synthetic judgement is not a problem for general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX2","text":"Synthetic judgement is not reducible to conceptual identity, conceptual containment, or contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX3","text":"Synthetic truth is not decidable through formal inspection of concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX4","text":"A synthesis is not possible through two concepts alone without a mediating third term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX5","text":"Mere thinking is not cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX6","text":"Pure concepts, including space and time, are not objectively valid merely because they are represented a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX7","text":"Experience is not an aggregate or rhapsody of perceptions lacking necessary unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX8","text":"Synthetic a priori propositions detached from possible experience are impossible as objectively valid cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:NX9","text":"A priori synthesis is not valid when it claims more than the necessary unity of possible experience requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D1","text":"The investigation of synthetic a priori judgement becomes an investigation of the conditions under which representations can be objectively unified.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A2","CH_BA94_SI_2:A3","CH_BA94_SI_2:A4","CH_BA94_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D2","text":"The conditions of a priori intuition, imagination, and apperception jointly supply the architecture required for pure synthetic judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A5","CH_BA94_SI_2:A6","CH_BA94_SI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D3","text":"The objective validity of space and time depends on their functioning as conditions under which objects can appear in possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","CH_BA94_SI_2:A9","CH_BA94_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D4","text":"Experience becomes cognition only when perceptions are synthesized according to rules into one possible consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A6","CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D5","text":"The possibility of experience confers objective reality on a priori cognition because such cognition articulates experience’s necessary form.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:D6","text":"The supreme principle of synthetic judgement follows because an experiential object cannot be given except under the conditions of unified synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SD1","text":"Objectivity is relocated from correspondence between an isolated concept and an independent object to lawful synthesizability within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A3","CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SD2","text":"The same transcendental conditions ground both the unity of experience and the objecthood of what is experienced.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3","text":"Pure cognition is constrained rather than expanded by its a priori status, because its validity ends where the conditions of possible experience end.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A8","CH_BA94_SI_2:A9","CH_BA94_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SD4","text":"The question of whether synthetic a priori knowledge is possible is transformed into the question of how a unified experience of objects is possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A2","CH_BA94_SI_2:A4","CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX1","text":"Objective cognition is not grounded in concepts whose relation to objects can be established independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX2","text":"Objects of cognition are not available prior to, or apart from, the formal conditions under which they can be experienced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX3","text":"Necessity is not licensed by mere conceptual coherence; it requires grounding in the necessary form of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX4","text":"A priori knowledge is not a route to objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX5","text":"Empirical multiplicity is not self-unifying and cannot yield cognition without transcendental synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a cognition of things as objects independently of the conditions through which a subject can receive and unify them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses an unconditioned standpoint from which the validity of synthetic cognition could be established outside possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX3","text":"The system denies that an object can serve as a cognitive ground while remaining unavailable to intuition and synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX4","text":"The system disallows a separation between the conditions of experiential objecthood and the conditions of valid objective judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS1","text":"Conceptual non-containment → need for synthetic connection → impossibility of purely logical validation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS2","text":"Need for synthetic connection → mediating intuition → imaginative synthesis → apperceptive unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS3","text":"Intuition plus synthesis plus unity → possible experience as connected cognition rather than perceptual aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS4","text":"Possible experience → conditions for objects to be given → objective validity of a priori representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS5","text":"Objective validity of a priori synthesis → restriction to necessary conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:FGS6","text":"Restriction to experiential conditions → every object of experience is subject to the synthetic unity of intuition’s manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:AF1","text":"Synthetic judgement exceeds conceptual analysis → a mediating synthesis is required → intuition, imagination, and apperception provide its conditions → possible experience supplies objectivity → necessary experiential conditions define valid a priori synthesis → objects of experience fall under synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ME1","text":"The problem of synthetic a priori knowledge is reclassified from a logical problem of concepts to a transcendental problem of experiential objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ME2","text":"Space and time are reclassified from independently meaningful representations to forms whose objective significance depends on possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ME3","text":"Experience is reclassified from received perception to rule-governed synthesis under unified consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ME4","text":"Objectivity is mapped as dependence on conditions of possible givenness rather than as a property secured by thought alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ME5","text":"The boundary between legitimate a priori cognition and empty speculation is drawn at the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text establishes the supreme principle governing all synthetic judgements concerning objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds judgement in the coordinated functions of intuition, imagination, apperception, and rule-governed experiential synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text displaces the validation of a priori knowledge from conceptual analysis to an account of the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are cognizable only as appearances ordered within a priori forms of intuition and unified in temporal inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT5","text":"Limitative — The text restricts every objectively valid synthetic a priori claim to what is necessary for possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from the insufficiency of conceptual analysis to the principle governing synthetic a priori judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the claim that possible experience is the sole source of objective reality for a priori representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT3","text":"The argument is internally coherent if objectivity means valid applicability to possible experience rather than access to objects independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT4","text":"The principal compression occurs in the transition from the unity required for experience to the claim that the same unity necessarily governs every object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between time as the stated universal mediating field of representation and space as an indispensable form of external experiential objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT6","text":"A further tension remains between the claim that an object must be given and the appeal to merely possible experience as sufficient for objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the limiting function of possible experience is retained alongside its grounding function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 46","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","context":"Chapter 46","original_title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","original_context":"Chapter 46"},"original_title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":47,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SISR"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SISR@R001","sha256":"f4f0c4ef41597365a4f6ded28330a237bc4d8192ffa4cda1bad1561b7921bfdd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS1","text":"The chapter determines how synthetic a priori principles can govern every object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS2","text":"It locates the source of such principles in pure understanding because object-cognition requires necessary rule-governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS3","text":"It separates principles of pure understanding from empirical laws and from mathematical principles derived through pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS4","text":"It derives the system of principles from the category table as rules for the categories’ objective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS5","text":"It divides categorical synthesis into mathematical determination of intuition and dynamical determination of phenomenal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS6","text":"It proves the first two mathematical principles by deriving extensive quantity from spatial-temporal apprehension and intensive quantity from the graduated reality of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AS7","text":"The movement is transcendental-systematic: objectivity requirement → principial source → categorical classification → quantitative proofs → empirical constraints and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A1","text":"Cognition of an object of possible experience requires necessary rules governing its presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A2","text":"Pure understanding is the source of the principles supplying these necessary rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A3","text":"Pure understanding is the faculty of concepts, so its proper principles proceed from pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A4","text":"Empirical laws of nature apply higher a priori principles and cannot ground their own necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A5","text":"Principles of pure understanding are rules for the objective employment of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A6","text":"Quantity, quality, relation, and modality generate respectively the Axioms of Intuition, Anticipations of Perception, Analogies of Experience, and Postulates of Empirical Thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A7","text":"Mathematical principles originate in pure intuition, but their possibility and objective validity for experience depend on pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A8","text":"Categorical synthesis is mathematical when directed to intuition and dynamical when directed to phenomenal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A9","text":"Mathematical synthesis composes homogeneous manifolds, whereas dynamical synthesis connects heterogeneous existences as necessarily related.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A10","text":"Conditions of intuition are absolutely necessary, whereas conditions of phenomenal existence are necessary only under the condition of empirical thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A11","text":"The form of every phenomenon is given a priori through continuous intuition in space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A12","text":"Apprehension of determinate space or time requires successive synthesis of a homogeneous manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A13","text":"A quantity is extensive when representation of its parts necessarily precedes and makes possible representation of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A14","text":"All intuitions, and therefore all phenomena considered as intuitions, are extensive quantities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A15","text":"Perception contains sensation as empirical matter, while the determinate quality of sensation is cognizable only a posteriori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A16","text":"Sensation is apprehended as a unity rather than successively composed from parts and therefore has no extensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A17","text":"Every sensation permits continuous diminution from its given reality toward negation at zero.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:A18","text":"The real in every phenomenon has intensive quantity because sensation is representable by a degree relative to zero.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX1","text":"Extensive empirical validity does not confer the necessity characteristic of a pure principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX2","text":"Empirical laws are not coordinate with the higher principles that make their rule-governed application possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX3","text":"Principles originating in pure intuition are not themselves principles of pure understanding, even when their objective validity depends on understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX4","text":"“Mathematical” and “dynamical” designate modes of application, not the substantive principles of mathematics or physical dynamics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX5","text":"Discursive and mediated certainty is not thereby incomplete or uncertain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX6","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX7","text":"Objects of sense cannot be exempted from the formal conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX8","text":"Neither analytical equality rules nor singular numerical formulae qualify as synthetic universal axioms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX9","text":"The determinate quality of sensation cannot be anticipated a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX10","text":"Sensation is not an objective spatial or temporal representation and does not possess extensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX11","text":"Points and moments are not simple constituents of space and time but boundaries presupposing continuous intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX12","text":"Repetition of interrupted syntheses produces an aggregate of phenomena, not one phenomenon as a continuous quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX13","text":"The continuity of change is not established here because determination of its cause would exceed the available pure principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX14","text":"Failure to perceive reality does not constitute experiential proof of empty space or empty time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:NX15","text":"Variable intensive filling is presented as a possible explanation of material difference, not as an established physical fact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D1","text":"Necessity distinguishes principles of pure understanding from empirical generalizations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A1","CH_BA94_SISR:A2","CH_BA94_SISR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D2","text":"Higher principles provide the necessary form of a rule, while experience supplies the particular case falling under it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A2","CH_BA94_SISR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D3","text":"The category table functions as the systematic generator rather than merely the classifier of pure principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A5","CH_BA94_SISR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D4","text":"The quantity and quality principles possess intuitive and apodeictic evidence, while relation and modality possess mediated but complete discursive certainty.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A8","CH_BA94_SISR:A9","CH_BA94_SISR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D5","text":"Every phenomenon must be apprehended through part-to-part synthesis as an extensive quantity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A12","CH_BA94_SISR:A13","CH_BA94_SISR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D6","text":"What pure mathematics establishes concerning spatial-temporal intuition applies necessarily to phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A7","CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D7","text":"A phenomenal whole cannot antecede the synthesis of the homogeneous parts through which it is apprehended.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A12","CH_BA94_SISR:A13","CH_BA94_SISR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D8","text":"Every phenomenal reality possesses degree despite lacking extensive composition within the sensation itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A16","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D9","text":"The quality of a sensation remains empirical, but the fact that it has a degree is anticipatable a priori.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D10","text":"Phenomena are continuous both as extensive intuitions and as intensive realities.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A14","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D11","text":"Neither spatial-temporal extension nor phenomenal reality contains a smallest possible positive part.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D12","text":"Experience cannot prove a void because complete negation is not itself perceived and arbitrarily diminished reality remains possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D13","text":"Equal extensive spaces can be completely filled while differing through the intensive degrees of their realities.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A14","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:D14","text":"Transcendental principles determine the possibility-space of empirical explanation without determining sensation’s qualities, actual causes, or physical facts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A7","CH_BA94_SISR:A10","CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD1","text":"Objectivity consists in necessary conformity to the synthetic rules under which an appearance can become an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A1","CH_BA94_SISR:A2","CH_BA94_SISR:A5","CH_BA94_SISR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD2","text":"The objective applicability of mathematics is secured because the synthesis determining pure space and time also determines possible phenomenal apprehension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A7","CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A12","CH_BA94_SISR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD3","text":"A priori cognition reaches the matter of experience only through an invariant quantitative condition, not through prior knowledge of what is sensed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A16","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD4","text":"The phenomenal field is doubly quantitative: form is extensive and sensible reality is intensive, while both dimensions are continuous.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A14","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD5","text":"Nonperception underdetermines the distinction between absolute absence and reality below the degree required for perception.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A17","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD6","text":"Transcendental analysis can invalidate a claimed necessity in physical explanation while leaving the competing empirical explanation undecided.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A7","CH_BA94_SISR:A10","CH_BA94_SISR:A15","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SD7","text":"The system stratifies evidence by mode of synthesis without stratifying truth or certainty into secure and insecure forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A8","CH_BA94_SISR:A9","CH_BA94_SISR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX1","text":"An object cannot become cognizable independently of necessary synthetic rule-governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX2","text":"Necessity cannot be generated by induction from repeated empirical cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX3","text":"The system cannot tolerate an incomplete or non-exhaustive category table without losing the systematic completeness of its principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX4","text":"Spatial-temporal predicates cannot be treated as properties of things independently of the conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX5","text":"Space and time cannot be constructed from antecedently given simple points or moments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX6","text":"Sensation cannot consist of indivisible least positive degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX7","text":"Absence of perception cannot be identified with perception of absence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX8","text":"Differences among phenomenal realities cannot be reduced necessarily to multiplicity, aggregation, or extensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:DNX9","text":"Pure understanding cannot determine the empirical quality of sensation or select an actual physical cause from transcendental conditions alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SNX1","text":"Unmediated cognition of objects apart from sensibility and categorical synthesis is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SNX2","text":"The qualitative content of empirical reality remains absent from a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SNX3","text":"Absolute nothing cannot appear as a positive datum; zero functions only as the limiting negation of possible degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SNX4","text":"No ultimate phenomenal simple is available either as a spatial-temporal constituent or as a least degree of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:SNX5","text":"A completed physical ontology cannot be extracted from the transcendental conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS1","text":"Object-cognition → necessary rule-governance → pure understanding → a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS2","text":"Empirical regularity / pure intuition / pure concept → exclusion of empirical and mathematical principles from the proper principles of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS3","text":"Category table → objective-use rules → quantity/Axioms, quality/Anticipations, relation/Analogies, modality/Postulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS4","text":"Intuition-directed composition → mathematical principles → immediate apodeictic evidence; existence-directed nexus → dynamical principles → mediated conditional necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS5","text":"Space-time form → homogeneous manifold → successive synthesis → antecedent parts → extensive phenomenal quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS6","text":"Perception → sensation → non-extensive unity → continuous variation from zero → intensive degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS7","text":"Extensive continuity + intensive continuity → no smallest phenomenal parts → no experiential proof of absolute void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:FGS8","text":"Transcendental condition → exclusion of false necessity → enlarged empirical possibility-space → suspension of factual physical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:AF1","text":"Need for principles → pure understanding as source → separation from empirical and mathematical principles → categorical systematization → mathematical/dynamical division → proof of extensive quantity → validation of mathematical application → proof of intensive quantity → continuity → restriction of void-inferences → empirical restraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME1","text":"Laws of nature are reclassified from self-grounding necessities into empirical applications of higher principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME2","text":"Mathematics is repositioned from an independently applicable science to a body of pure-intuitive cognition whose objective use requires transcendental grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME3","text":"The category table becomes a generative map of possible principial forms rather than a static taxonomy of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME4","text":"Quantity divides into extensive composition and intensive degree, preventing the quantitative field from being reduced to aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME5","text":"Sensation divides into an empirically unknowable quality and an a priori knowable structure of degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME6","text":"Nonperception is reclassified from evidence of nothingness into an indeterminate relation between zero and imperceptibly small reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME7","text":"Points, moments, and zero are reclassified as limits or boundaries rather than constitutive phenomenal elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME8","text":"Differences in material filling become conceivably intensive rather than necessarily products of empty intervals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ME9","text":"Immediate and mediated evidence become distinct modes of certainty rather than opposing degrees of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ALT1","text":"Surface: The chapter classifies the principles of pure understanding and proves that phenomenal intuition is extensive while phenomenal reality is intensive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ALT2","text":"Structural: The category table and the modes of synthesis determine the architecture, evidence, and domain of each principial class.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument regulates legitimate a priori claims by separating formal conditions of experience from empirical qualities, causes, and physical hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomena exist for cognition only through spatial-temporal synthesis, part-to-part succession, continuous magnitude, and graduated sensible reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s taxonomy and its developed arguments for the first two principle classes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT2","text":"The Analogies of Experience and Postulates of Empirical Thought remain underdetermined because they are classified but not developed in the supplied text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if the completeness of the category table and the prior doctrines of pure intuition and phenomena are accepted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT4","text":"The strongest inferential junction is the identity of the synthesis determining space and time with the synthesis required for apprehending phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT5","text":"The anticipation argument depends on the asserted continuous diminutability of every sensation, which is not independently demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT6","text":"The account treats sensation as apprehended in one moment while grounding its degree in a temporal rise of consciousness from zero; the relation between instantaneous degree and diachronic synthesis remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT7","text":"“Aggregate” shifts between an internally synthesized extensive phenomenon and a mere collection of separate phenomena, leaving the criterion of phenomenal unity partly implicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT8","text":"The polemic against exempting sensible objects from geometry adds no independent ground beyond the imported doctrine that phenomena conform to pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT9","text":"The anti-void conclusion is epistemic rather than ontological, and the intensive account of material difference is modal rather than factual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISR:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if mediated certainty, empirical underdetermination, and the distinction between possibility and actuality remain intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 47","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","context":"Chapter 47","original_title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","original_context":"Chapter 47"},"original_title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","order":48,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CIGD"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CIGD@R001","sha256":"b8ac3ac6ad6f156ca6d26575465d3de2d380815db8cd916b73ab82c46f8086d0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS1","text":"The chapter asks whether pure understanding can validly determine objects beyond possible experience and thereby ground a division into phenomena and noumena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2","text":"Its central claim is that categories and principles possess objective use only under the conditions of sensibility within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS3","text":"Objective cognition requires both an intuition that gives an object and a schema that permits categorial subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS4","text":"The ambiguity of noumenon is resolved by distinguishing a negative limit-concept from a positive object of intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS5","text":"Positive noumena are unavailable because human cognition lacks intellectual intuition, while negative noumena remain necessary to bound sensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS6","text":"The argument is critical, transcendental, diagnostic, and reconstructive: it grounds empirical validity by restricting transcendental pretension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AS7","text":"The movement proceeds from the secured territory of experience through the conditions of objective meaning to the collapse of transcendental use and the retention of a merely limitative beyond.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A1","text":"The unity of experience is produced by the understanding’s a priori synthesis of the sensible manifold in relation to apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A2","text":"Pure concepts and principles possess objective validity through their function as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","text":"Objective reference requires that an object be given through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","text":"The only intuition available to human cognition is sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A5","text":"Space and time possess objective validity only as forms of empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","text":"Categories are forms for uniting a given manifold and do not themselves supply that manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","text":"Categorial judgment requires a schema as the formal condition for subsuming an intuited object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A8","text":"Noncontradiction establishes only logical possibility; real objective possibility requires a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A9","text":"Empirical use refers concepts to phenomena, whereas transcendental use would refer them to things in general as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A10","text":"Designating an object as phenomenon distinguishes it from the thing considered apart from the human mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","text":"A noumenon in the negative sense is a thing considered insofar as it is not an object of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A12","text":"A noumenon in the positive sense is an object of nonsensuous intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A13","text":"Human cognition possesses neither intellectual intuition nor a determinate conception of its possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A14","text":"The negative noumenon is necessary only as a problematic concept limiting sensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A15","text":"Every synthetic principle requires a third term connecting concepts that are not analytically related.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:A16","text":"Possible experience is the only available mediator for synthetic categorial principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX1","text":"The a priori origin of a concept does not authorize its application beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX2","text":"Pure categories separated from sensibility do not determine objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX3","text":"Logical noncontradiction is not proof of real possibility or existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX4","text":"A merely transcendental meaning does not constitute a valid transcendental use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX5","text":"A noumenon is not a determinate intelligible object for human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX6","text":"The negative noumenon is neither an arbitrary fiction nor a source of positive supersensible data.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX7","text":"Phenomena and noumena do not form two positively cognizable classes of objects or worlds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX8","text":"Human sensibility cannot be asserted to be the only possible mode of intuition absolutely.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX9","text":"The lawful conceptual organization of phenomena is not an intelligible world in the noumenal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX10","text":"The claim that understanding represents objects “as they are” cannot signify cognition of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:NX11","text":"A synthetic ontology of things in general cannot be constructed from pure categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D1","text":"Pure understanding can anticipate only the formal structure of possible experience, not the intrinsic constitution of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A1","CH_BA94_CIGD:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D2","text":"Every objectively valid use of the categories is empirical even though the categories originate a priori.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A2","CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D3","text":"Transcendental use of a category is no determinate use because it lacks an object capable of subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D4","text":"Without sensible and temporal schemata, categories collapse into indeterminate logical functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A5","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D5","text":"Ontology must be replaced by an analytic of the understanding’s contribution to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A2","CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D6","text":"Positive noumenal cognition is unavailable because its required mode of givenness is absent from human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A12","CH_BA94_CIGD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D7","text":"The negative noumenon is admissible and necessary while yielding no positive cognition beyond phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A10","CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D8","text":"A positive division of objects into sensible and intelligible classes is invalid because the alleged noumenal class has no determinable objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A12","CH_BA94_CIGD:A13","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D9","text":"Categories extend beyond sensibility only as forms for thinking an object in general, not as means of determining a wider domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D10","text":"Sensibility without understanding and understanding without sensibility each fail to produce cognition of a determinate object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:D11","text":"No synthetic principle about noumena can be demonstrated because no non-empirical mediator connects its concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A9","CH_BA94_CIGD:A15","CH_BA94_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD1","text":"The phenomena–noumena distinction is asymmetrical: phenomena constitute a cognizable domain, while noumena mark only that sensible cognition is not cognition of things as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A10","CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD2","text":"Understanding acquires a negative extension by limiting sensibility while simultaneously renouncing any determination of what lies beyond it.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A9","CH_BA94_CIGD:A10","CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A12","CH_BA94_CIGD:A13","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD3","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when independence from sensibility in categorial origin is mistaken for independence from sensibility in categorial application.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A1","CH_BA94_CIGD:A2","CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD4","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is redirected from determining supersensible objects to determining the conditions under which object-reference has meaning.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A8","CH_BA94_CIGD:A9","CH_BA94_CIGD:A15","CH_BA94_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD5","text":"Possible experience exhausts the domain of human objective cognition without exhausting every possible mode of reality or intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A2","CH_BA94_CIGD:A3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A8","CH_BA94_CIGD:A13","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6","text":"The thing in itself functions as a necessary unknown remainder that limits appearance but cannot receive positive predication from the categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A10","CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A14","CH_BA94_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX1","text":"Pure thought cannot generate the givenness of its own object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX2","text":"Logical forms such as subject, consequence, and combination cannot function as an ontology without sensible conditions of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX3","text":"The distinction between appearance and thing in itself cannot support a symmetrical two-world model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX4","text":"The conditions of human givenness cannot be converted into conditions of all possible being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX5","text":"Generic thinkability cannot substitute for determinate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX6","text":"Ignorance of noumena cannot be converted into proof of their nonexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX7","text":"The systematic intelligibility of phenomena cannot resolve the question of supersensible cognition by terminological reclassification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX1","text":"The constitutive absence organizing the system is intellectual intuition: without it no positive beyond can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX2","text":"No standpoint is available from which human cognition can compare an object as appearance with the same object as it is independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX3","text":"A positive human science of supersensible objects is structurally foreclosed rather than merely incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4","text":"The noumenal place must remain empty: filling it violates the critical boundary, while eliminating it erases the distinction that bounds sensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS1","text":"A priori synthesis of the sensible manifold → unity of possible experience → empirical objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS2","text":"Concept + corresponding intuition + schema → subsumption → determinate object-reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS3","text":"Category − sensible manifold − schema → logical form without objective application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS4","text":"A priori origin → apparent unrestricted range → attempted transcendental use → indeterminacy and illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS5","text":"Designation as phenomenon → abstraction from the sensible mode of intuition → negative noumenon → limitation of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS6","text":"Positive noumenon → requirement of intellectual intuition → absence of that faculty → impossibility of positive noumenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS7","text":"Noumenal synthetic claim → requirement of a mediating third term → absence of a non-empirical mediator → failure of transcendental ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AF1","text":"Secured territory of experience → question of jurisdiction and boundary → recapitulation of the ground of empirical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AF2","text":"Conditions of concept-use → necessity of intuition and schema → restriction of categories to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AF3","text":"Abstraction from sensibility → loss of determinate meaning → collapse of pure ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AF4","text":"Phenomenon–thing-in-itself distinction → ambiguity of noumenon → separation of negative and positive senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:AF5","text":"Absence of intellectual intuition → rejection of positive noumena → retention of the negative limit-concept → self-limitation of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME1","text":"The distinction between a concept’s origin and its range of valid application becomes decisive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME2","text":"Possibility divides into logical consistency and objective possibility grounded in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME3","text":"The apparent division between two worlds is redrawn as an asymmetry between a domain of cognition and a boundary-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME4","text":"Ontology is reclassified as an illicit expansion of what can legitimately be only an analytic of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME5","text":"Cognitive extension beyond experience is reclassified from acquisition of territory to negative delimitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ME6","text":"Objectivity is relocated from either sensibility or understanding alone to their schematized conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT1","text":"**Surface —** The immediate issue is whether pure categories can determine noumena and support a division of objects into sensible and intelligible kinds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT2","text":"**Structural —** Objective cognition is generated through the conjunction of sensible givenness, categorial synthesis, and schematic subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT3","text":"**Metastrategic —** Critique secures the jurisdiction of empirical knowledge by diagnosing transcendental claims as misapplications of valid cognitive forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT4","text":"**Ontological/Temporal —** Object-determination is conditioned by sensible givenness in space and time, while reality apart from those conditions remains thinkable only as an indeterminate limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of pure understanding, the noumenal distinction, and the final rejection of transcendental ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT2","text":"The ontological status of things in themselves and the possibility of nonhuman intellectual intuition remain intentionally underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent only if logical thinkability, objective possibility, transcendental meaning, and objective use remain strictly distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT4","text":"Its strongest inferential mechanism is the requirement that categorial application have both an intuited object and a schema of subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT5","text":"The universal restriction to possible experience depends on earlier transcendental deductions that this chapter recapitulates rather than independently reproduces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT6","text":"A structural tension remains between the necessity of thinking the thing in itself and the prohibition against determining it as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT7","text":"A semantic fault line remains between attributing transcendental meaning to pure categories and declaring their separation from sensibility devoid of meaningful use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement from empirical grounding through semantic restriction to the negative, non-territorial function of noumena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 48","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","context":"Chapter 48","original_title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","original_context":"Chapter 48"},"original_title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX","layer":"CH","title":"Appendix","order":49,"parents":["RAW_BA94_APPX"],"revision":"CH_BA94_APPX@R001","sha256":"281f01d24594a981e944f629c1fda8c9f1a211eeaaace10ffc7bb189e196bcd0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS1","text":"The argument addresses the false objective judgments produced when conceptions of comparison are used without determining their cognitive source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS2","text":"Objective comparison requires transcendental reflection because sensibility and understanding condition representations differently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS3","text":"The procedure is critical-diagnostic: legitimate comparison is reconstructed by locating the amphiboly generated through faculty-confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS4","text":"The argument moves from the distinction between logical and transcendental reflection to four source-dependent modes of comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS5","text":"The four comparisons expose Leibnitz’s intellectualization of phenomena as the common generator of his metaphysical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS6","text":"The diagnosis establishes that categories acquire objective validity only under conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AS7","text":"The restriction of cognition to phenomena yields the noumenon as a negative boundary-concept and culminates in a categorical division of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A1","text":"Reflection determines the relation of representations to their cognitive sources.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A2","text":"Sensibility and understanding are irreducibly distinct sources of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A3","text":"Logical reflection compares conceptions while abstracting from their cognitive source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A4","text":"Transcendental reflection assigns representations to sensibility or understanding and thereby grounds objective comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A5","text":"Identity and difference, agreement and opposition, internal and external, and matter and form acquire objective significance only relative to transcendental place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A6","text":"Conceptions of the understanding acquire objective validity only through corresponding sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A7","text":"Space and time are primitive a priori forms of sensibility that condition phenomena and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A8","text":"Human objective cognition is restricted to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A9","text":"Spatial difference is sufficient for the numerical difference of phenomena even when their internal conceptions are identical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A10","text":"Phenomenal realities can oppose and reciprocally cancel their effects without logical contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A11","text":"Matter as phenomenon is cognized through relations and need contain no absolutely internal determination available to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A12","text":"Pure understanding places the determinable before determination, whereas sensible cognition presupposes formal intuition before sensory matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A13","text":"Transcendental amphiboly substitutes an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon when transcendental place remains undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A14","text":"The precedence of thought over determinate intuition permits logical form to appear as a determination of an object in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A15","text":"A noumenon is legitimate only negatively and problematically as a boundary marking the non-exhaustiveness of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A16","text":"Understanding limits sensibility without thereby acquiring a positive non-sensuous domain of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A17","text":"The division into possible and impossible presupposes the problematic conception of an object in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:A18","text":"The categorical order generates four distinct modes of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX1","text":"Reflection is not the direct acquisition of conceptions from objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX2","text":"Logical comparison alone is not sufficient for objective comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX3","text":"Sensibility is not a confused or analytically deficient form of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX4","text":"Understanding does not cognize things in themselves by immediate application of its pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX5","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX6","text":"Space and time are not determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX7","text":"Conceptual indiscernibility does not establish the numerical identity of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX8","text":"The absence of logical contradiction between realities does not establish their real agreement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX9","text":"The external relations of phenomenal matter do not require a cognizable absolutely internal substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX10","text":"Categories without schemata or sensuous data do not constitute cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX11","text":"The representation of an object in general is not sufficient to present an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX12","text":"Critique does not authorize the construction of intelligible worlds beyond phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX13","text":"A negative noumenon is not asserted to be actual, possible, impossible, or cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX14","text":"What is absent from a general conception is not thereby absent from its particular objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:NX15","text":"Negation and the pure forms of intuition are not self-standing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D1","text":"A transcendental topic is required to assign conceptions to their proper faculties and prevent equivocal objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A1","CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_APPX:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D2","text":"Omitting transcendental reflection converts merely logical comparisons into pretended synthetic propositions about objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A3","CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D3","text":"Leibnitz intellectualizes phenomena by treating sensibility as confused understanding and objects of sense as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D4","text":"Locke’s sensualization of conceptions and Leibnitz’s intellectualization of phenomena are inverse reductions of the two-source architecture of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D5","text":"The identity of indiscernibles is an analytical rule for conceptions but not a law governing spatial phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A3","CH_BA94_APPX:A9","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D6","text":"The non-opposition of affirmative conceptions is logically valid, phenomenally false when generalized, and cognitively empty when asserted of noumenal realities.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A10","CH_BA94_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D7","text":"Leibnitz’s restriction of substances to an absolutely internal character generates simple representational monads.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A3","CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D8","text":"Within Leibnitz’s construction, internally self-contained representational substances exclude physical interaction and require a universal coordinating cause for their correspondence.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D9","text":"Leibnitz’s reduction of space to substantial coexistence and time to causal succession follows from treating sensible forms as intellectual relations among things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_APPX:A12","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D10","text":"No synthetic cognition of things in themselves can be produced by pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A8","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D11","text":"Analysis of phenomena may penetrate indefinitely into nature, but it cannot become cognition of the non-sensuous internal nature of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A8","CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D12","text":"The noumenon preserves the limitation of sensibility while withholding every positive determination of a supersensible object.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A15","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D13","text":"The transcendental object marks an unknown ground or cause of phenomena while remaining unavailable to categorial determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A8","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D14","text":"An ens rationis is a non-self-contradictory empty conception without a corresponding object and is neither established as possible nor condemned as impossible.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A17","CH_BA94_APPX:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D15","text":"A nihil privativum is the conceived absence of an object and depends upon the contrast supplied by something real.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A17","CH_BA94_APPX:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D16","text":"An ens imaginarium is an empty intuition without an object, as in pure space or pure time considered apart from appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_APPX:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:D17","text":"A nihil negativum is an impossible object because its conception annihilates itself through contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A17","CH_BA94_APPX:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD1","text":"Any reduction of sensibility and understanding to a single faculty produces systematic metaphysical error rather than an isolated false judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD2","text":"Empirical plurality and real antagonism cannot be derived from logical analysis because their differentiating conditions enter through sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A3","CH_BA94_APPX:A9","CH_BA94_APPX:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD3","text":"Leibnitzian indiscernibility, monadology, predetermined harmony, and relational space-time form a coordinated system generated by one amphibolous substitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_APPX:A9","CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD4","text":"Restriction to phenomena secures objective cognition because sensuous intuition supplies the object-reference that pure thought lacks.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A6","CH_BA94_APPX:A8","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD5","text":"The understanding’s legitimate transcendental function is self-limitation rather than expansion into a supersensible ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A15","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD6","text":"A phenomenon may be constituted by relations without licensing inference to an analogous relational or non-relational constitution of the thing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A8","CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SD7","text":"The fourfold division of nothing prevents non-givenness, privation, formal conditionality, and contradiction from collapsing into a single notion of nonbeing.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A17","CH_BA94_APPX:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX1","text":"The cognitive faculties cannot form a continuum differentiated only by degrees of clarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX2","text":"Logical conditions of thinkability cannot serve as constitutive conditions of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX3","text":"Abstraction from conditions of intuition cannot eliminate those conditions from the objects given through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX4","text":"Mere representability by thought cannot establish objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX5","text":"The forms of human sensibility cannot legitimately be extended to all possible being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX6","text":"The independence of thought from sensation does not authorize an intuition-free employment of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX7","text":"Human cognition cannot leave its own conditions in order to compare phenomena directly with things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:DNX8","text":"The demand for an explanatory ground cannot convert an unknown ground into a positively determined entity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SNX1","text":"The unavailable possibility of non-sensuous intuition is the constitutive absence around which the noumenon is organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SNX2","text":"The transcendental ground unifying subjective forms of cognition with objective givenness remains inaccessible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SNX3","text":"No standpoint is available from which the subject can cognize itself otherwise than as phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SNX4","text":"The absolutely internal nature demanded by metaphysics is absent from every possible empirical presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:SNX5","text":"A supersensible field is left open as an empty logical space while being foreclosed as a domain of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS1","text":"Representation → reflection on cognitive source → assignment of transcendental place → legitimate objective comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS2","text":"Transcendental place + four conceptions of comparison → source-relative determinations of identity, opposition, internality, and form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS3","text":"Omitted source-assignment → homogeneous intellectual comparison → substitution of noumenon for phenomenon → transcendental amphiboly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS4","text":"Reiterated amphiboly → indiscernibility principle + non-opposition of realities + representational monads + predetermined harmony + intellectualized space-time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS5","text":"Restoration of sensible form + categorial synthesis → objective cognition → restriction to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS6","text":"Restriction to phenomena → limitation of sensibility → negative noumenon → no enlargement of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:FGS7","text":"Problematic object in general + categorical modes of emptiness → ens rationis / nihil privativum / ens imaginarium / nihil negativum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:AF1","text":"Unreflected comparison → faculty distinction → transcendental-place assignment → four bifurcated relations → diagnosis of Leibnitzian amphiboly → phenomenal restriction of cognition → negative noumenon → fourfold classification of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME1","text":"Cognitive provenance becomes a necessary component of every legitimate objective comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME2","text":"Logical relations and objective relations are separated into distinct levels of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME3","text":"Numerical identity is reclassified from conceptual indiscernibility to source-relative individuation through space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME4","text":"Opposition is expanded beyond contradiction to include the reciprocal cancellation of phenomenal forces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME5","text":"Internality is divided into the absolute internal demanded by pure conception and the comparative internal available within phenomenal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME6","text":"The priority of matter over form in pure conception is displaced by the priority of sensible form over empirical matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME7","text":"Leibnitz’s doctrines are reclassified as coordinated effects of faculty-confusion rather than independent metaphysical discoveries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME8","text":"The noumenon is reclassified from a supersensible object into a problematic marker of cognitive limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ME9","text":"Nothing is pluralized into distinct failures of object, datum, intuition, and conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument determines the legitimate use of four conceptions of comparison and tests their misuse in Leibnitzian philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ALT2","text":"Structural: Objective cognition is generated by the conjunction of irreducible faculties, while amphiboly results from suppressing their difference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Metaphysical construction is replaced by transcendental topic, which regulates claims through source-assignment and boundary enforcement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomena are conditioned by a priori forms that antecede empirical data, while things in themselves remain indeterminate and noumenal temporality or spatiality remains unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between logical comparison, transcendental reflection, amphiboly, phenomenal cognition, and noumenal limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT2","text":"The relation among thing in itself, transcendental object, and negative noumenon remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if the irreducibility of the two faculties and the intuition-dependence of objective validity are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where the four reflection-concepts demonstrate concrete divergences between logical and sensible determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses rather than independently establishes the exhaustiveness of sensibility and categories for human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT6","text":"The noumenon must be thought to mark sensibility’s limit even though the categories required for determinate object-thought are denied application to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT7","text":"The description of the transcendental object as the cause of phenomena employs causal language where categorial determination is otherwise prohibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT8","text":"The fourfold table of nothing is reconstructible from the categorical order but remains only loosely integrated with the main diagnosis of amphiboly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APPX:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the actual movement from reflective provenance through metaphysical error to critical limitation and modal classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 49","title":"Appendix","context":"Chapter 49","original_title":"Appendix","original_context":"Chapter 49"},"original_title":"Appendix","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE","layer":"CH","title":"Experience","order":50,"parents":["RAW_BA94_EXPE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_EXPE@R001","sha256":"e1e09b8b96d748495d9432e9d49bfd22109f26691176fb7e1ff61196a7bd97ce","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS1","text":"The chapter addresses how contingent perceptions can yield objective cognition of phenomena in one time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS2","text":"Objective temporal determination requires necessary connections that perception itself cannot supply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS3","text":"The synthetic unity of apperception supplies these connections through temporally schematized categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","text":"Permanence, succession, and coexistence generate respectively the rules of substance, causality, and reciprocal community.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS5","text":"These rules regulate relations of phenomenal existence without constructing particular existents or reaching things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AS6","text":"The argument is transcendental and reconstructive, moving from the possibility of experience to its necessary temporal conditions and thence to the unity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A1","text":"Experience is object-determining empirical cognition constituted by the synthetic unity of perceptions in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A2","text":"Perception and apprehension supply only contingent conjunctions and contain no necessity of connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A3","text":"Empirical cognition of an object requires relations of existence to be represented as objective relations in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","text":"Time itself is not an object of perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A5","text":"Phenomena receive determinate temporal positions only through a priori rules of connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A6","text":"The a priori unity of apperception requires all perceptions belonging to one cognition to be synthesized according to temporal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","text":"Permanence, succession, and coexistence exhaust the modes under which phenomenal existence is temporally determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A8","text":"Analogies are regulative rules for relations of phenomenal existence rather than constitutive procedures for constructing existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A9","text":"The analogies are valid only for empirical use, and categories apply to phenomena only through temporal schemata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","text":"A permanent phenomenal substratum is necessary for the representation of duration, coexistence, and change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A11","text":"The total quantum of phenomenal substance is permanent and can neither increase nor diminish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A12","text":"Empirical change is the alteration of determinations belonging to a permanent substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","text":"Objective succession exists only where an antecedent state determines a succeeding state necessarily according to a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","text":"The law of cause and effect is an a priori condition of experiencing events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A15","text":"Objective coexistence requires reciprocal temporal determination grounded in interaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A16","text":"All phenomenal substances perceived as coexistent stand, mediately or immediately, in complete dynamical community.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A17","text":"Time and phenomenal reality are continuous magnitudes without smallest parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:A18","text":"Synthetic a priori principles governing phenomenal existence can be justified only by deduction from the possibility of experience, not by dogmatic analysis of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX1","text":"Experience is not mere intuition, sensation, apprehension, or an aggregate of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX2","text":"Necessary connection is not discoverable within perceptions themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX3","text":"Absolute time, void time, and temporal position considered apart from phenomena are not objects of perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX4","text":"The existence or determinate character of a phenomenon cannot be constructed a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX5","text":"Philosophical analogy is not a mathematical proportion from which an unknown term can be constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX6","text":"The analogies do not constitute phenomenal existence as mathematical principles constitute intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX7","text":"The analogies do not authorize transcendental application to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX8","text":"Categories are not applied directly to phenomena independently of their temporal schemata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX9","text":"The permanence of substance cannot be proved by tautology or by analysis of the concept of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX10","text":"Succession in apprehension is not by itself succession in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX11","text":"Causal necessity is not a generalization produced by repeated observation of similar sequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX12","text":"Absolute origination or extinction of substance is not an empirically possible event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX13","text":"The empirical exclusion of creation does not decide whether things considered in themselves depend on a foreign cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX14","text":"Simultaneity of cause and effect does not abolish their asymmetrical temporal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX15","text":"Reversible apprehension or location in an assumed common time is insufficient to establish objective coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:NX16","text":"Empty space is not denied absolutely, but space inaccessible to perception is denied status as an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D1","text":"The unity that converts perceptions into experience must be supplied by synthesis rather than extracted from perception.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A1","CH_BA94_EXPE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D2","text":"Every empirical determination of time must be subordinated to an a priori rule of general temporal determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A3","CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:A5","CH_BA94_EXPE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D3","text":"Permanence, causal succession, and reciprocal coexistence exhaust the rules required to assign phenomenal existence a position in one time.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A5","CH_BA94_EXPE:A6","CH_BA94_EXPE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D4","text":"A philosophical analogy determines the necessary relation to an empirically sought but a priori unspecified counterpart.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A8","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D5","text":"The permanent functions as the empirical representation of time by supporting duration, succession, and coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:A5","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D6","text":"Substantial existence subsists while accidents positively determine its changing modes.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D7","text":"Absolute generation or annihilation cannot be experienced because change presupposes one enduring subject across opposed states.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A11","CH_BA94_EXPE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D8","text":"Necessary temporal ordering, rather than mere succession of representations, confers objective significance on an event.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A1","CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D9","text":"An experienced event indicates an antecedent state that determines it according to a universal rule.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D10","text":"Cause and effect may be simultaneous in elapsed time while remaining asymmetrically ordered by which state determines the other.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D11","text":"Action is an empirical criterion of substance because causality produces change and the ultimate subject of change must be permanent.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A12","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D12","text":"Every change is generated through intermediate degrees by causality acting continuously throughout its temporal interval.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D13","text":"Reciprocal perceptual order counts as objective coexistence only when each substance can determine the temporal position of the other.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A15","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D14","text":"All phenomena belonging to one possible experience form one nature under permanence, causality, and community.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A6","CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D15","text":"Empirical laws presuppose rather than ground the primitive laws that make experience possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16","CH_BA94_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:D16","text":"The analogies anticipate only the form of temporal connection; particular existents, states, magnitudes, and powers remain empirically discoverable.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A8","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9","CH_BA94_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD1","text":"Objective significance arises when synthesis is constrained by a necessary rule that excludes alternative temporal orderings.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A1","CH_BA94_EXPE:A2","CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD2","text":"Phenomena determine one another’s temporal positions relationally, allowing one objective time to be represented without perception of absolute time.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A3","CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:A5","CH_BA94_EXPE:A13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD3","text":"The triad converts categories of relation into temporal conditions of experience: substance into inherence, causality into consequence, and community into composition.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD4","text":"The system combines a priori determination of experiential form with empirical openness of experiential content.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A8","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9","CH_BA94_EXPE:A17","CH_BA94_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD5","text":"The unity of nature is a precondition for objects to share one experience rather than an empirical totalization assembled after their discovery.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A6","CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16","CH_BA94_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD6","text":"Complete isolation is not merely empirically unknown but uncognizable as coexistence because no temporal relation could connect the isolated perceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A15","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD7","text":"The principles that authorize objective empirical judgments simultaneously restrict those judgments to phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A9","CH_BA94_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SD8","text":"No phenomenal existence is cognizable except as enduring substance, causally ordered event, or coexistent member of a reciprocal whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX1","text":"Time cannot function as a self-ordering container independently of rule-governed relations among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX2","text":"Change cannot be fundamental without a permanent phenomenal bearer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX3","text":"A temporally ordered but non-rule-governed sequence cannot count as an objectively experienced event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX4","text":"Causally independent coexistents cannot be cognized as members of one empirical world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX5","text":"Mutually disconnected temporal domains cannot be synthesized as one possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX6","text":"An independently accessible object cannot supply the criterion distinguishing objective connection from subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX7","text":"Repetition cannot generate the strict universality and necessity presupposed by empirical objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX8","text":"Formal a priori principles cannot determine which empirical powers, states, or existents occur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX9","text":"Conditions necessary for experiencing phenomena cannot be converted into determinations of noumenal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX1","text":"Unmediated access to objective temporal reality is unavailable; only rule-governed phenomenal relations can represent it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX2","text":"The nonappearance of time itself is the absence around which permanence, causality, and community are organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX3","text":"Absolute becoming from nothing and absolute disappearance into nothing are foreclosed within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX4","text":"Isolated existence is unavailable as a member of one empirically cognizable nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX5","text":"The thing in itself remains the inaccessible remainder that prevents empirical objectivity from becoming absolute ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX6","text":"A priori cognition of temporal form is purchased by renouncing a priori cognition of determinate empirical existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS1","text":"The insufficiency sequence is: contingent perceptual manifold → no represented necessity → unperceivable time → no objective temporal position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS2","text":"The objectivity sequence is: unity of apperception → a priori temporal rule → necessary synthesis → object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS3","text":"The permanence branch is: temporal duration → required permanent correlate → phenomenal substance → alteration of accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS4","text":"The succession branch is: successive apprehension → irreversible rule → cause and effect → objectively experienced event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS5","text":"The coexistence branch is: reversible apprehension → reciprocal temporal determination → dynamical interaction → compositum reale.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS6","text":"The regulative operation is: given perception + temporal mode + analogy → necessary relation to an unspecified counterpart → directed empirical search without construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS7","text":"The totalizing operation is: three temporal analogies + one apperception → unified experience → law-governed empirical nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS8","text":"The validation procedure is: assume possible experience → isolate its necessary condition → show that denial eliminates objectivity → restrict the resulting principle to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AF1","text":"Contingent perceptions → failure of perceptual necessity → unperceivability of time → a priori synthesis under apperception → objective temporal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:AF2","text":"General regulative principle → permanence and substance → succession and causality → coexistence and reciprocity → unity of nature → transcendental restriction to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME1","text":"Experience is reclassified from an accumulation of perceptions into a necessary synthesis that makes objects empirically cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME2","text":"Temporal position is relocated from an absolute time-container to lawful relations among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME3","text":"Substance is reclassified from a noumenal entity into the permanent phenomenal correlate required for duration and change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME4","text":"Causality is reclassified from an observed regularity into the condition under which succession can count as an objective event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME5","text":"Coexistence is reclassified from mere simultaneous placement into membership in a dynamical community.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME6","text":"Analogy is reclassified from a constructive quantitative proportion into a regulative qualitative relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME7","text":"Nature is reclassified from an aggregate of phenomena into the single law-governed unity required by possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ME8","text":"A priori cognition is restricted to formal relations of existence while determinate empirical content remains unconstrued.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT1","text":"Surface: The chapter establishes permanence, causality, and reciprocal action as the three principles governing temporal relations among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT2","text":"Structural: The principles operate through apperception, imagination, temporal schemata, and rule-governed synthesis to distinguish objects from subjective sequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument replaces dogmatic proof and empirical induction with transcendental deduction from the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomenal reality is presupposed as one temporally unified field of enduring substances, causally ordered changes, and reciprocally connected coexistents, without extension to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the general principle, the three analogies, their scope, and the concluding unity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT2","text":"The empirical identification of particular substances, causes, powers, and reciprocal influences remains underdetermined by design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent when every principle remains restricted to phenomena and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT4","text":"The strongest inference is the distinction between subjective perceptual succession and objective event-order through a necessary rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT5","text":"The designation of the analogies as merely regulative stands in tension with their role as conditions making empirical objects possible; the distinction holds only if constitution is restricted to construction of intuition or existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT6","text":"The First Analogy compresses the transition from the need for some permanent correlate of time to the conservation of the total quantum of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT7","text":"The Third Analogy moves from an epistemic condition for cognizing coexistence to an assertoric claim of complete dynamical reciprocity among coexistent substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT8","text":"The continuity of change depends on inherited premises concerning continuous time and phenomenal magnitude rather than on causality alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT9","text":"Simultaneous causation is preserved by replacing temporal lapse with asymmetric order, but the ground of that asymmetry remains compressed into the determining relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s real movement if it retains the sequence from perceptual insufficiency through the three temporal rules to phenomenal nature and the final restriction of scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 50","title":"Experience","context":"Chapter 50","original_title":"Experience","original_context":"Chapter 50"},"original_title":"Experience","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI","layer":"CH","title":"Empicisim","order":51,"parents":["RAW_BA94_EMPI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_EMPI@R001","sha256":"fd93af9dad65ecd1c7a4970d0dfc554aaf29e6a2de78f2d67fed22d4f73f7ddf","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS1","text":"The argument determines how possibility, reality, and necessity can possess objective validity without being inferred from conceptual completeness or extended beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS2","text":"Modal predicates express an object’s relation to the faculties and procedures of cognition rather than additional determinations of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3","text":"Possibility is grounded in formal experiential conditions, reality in perception or lawful connection with perception, and necessity in universal causal determination within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS4","text":"The criterion of reality generates a refutation of problematic idealism by making temporally determined internal experience dependent on external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS5","text":"The dependence of categories on intuition is generalized into the claim that external intuition supplies the indispensable presentation of permanence, change, community, and quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AS6","text":"The argument is transcendental-critical and reconstructive, moving from modal predicates through their experiential conditions to the restriction of all principles of pure understanding to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","text":"Possible experience is the sole domain in which objects can be presented for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A2","text":"The understanding supplies the a priori formal rules that synthesize intuitions into unified experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","text":"Categories are forms of thought rather than cognitions and acquire objective reality only through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A4","text":"Modal predicates determine the relation of a complete conception to the faculty of cognition rather than the object represented by that conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","text":"The objective use of modal categories is restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","text":"An object is possible when its conception agrees with the formal conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A7","text":"Freedom from contradiction is necessary but insufficient for objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","text":"Reality requires conscious sensation, whether through direct perception or through lawful connection with an actual perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A9","text":"Existence cannot be established from a conception but only from perception and empirically lawful inference from perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A10","text":"Empirically determined consciousness of one’s existence in time presupposes something permanent in perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","text":"Internal sense contains no permanent intuition capable of determining time; the required permanence is presented through matter in external space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A12","text":"Determinate internal experience is possible only through the existence of external objects and external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A13","text":"The necessity of external experience in general does not validate every particular representation of an external object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A14","text":"Material necessity concerns causally determined states and effects rather than the existence of substances as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A15","text":"Necessity within nature is conditioned and intelligible, and empirical synthesis excludes lawless chance, blind fate, leaps, and hiatuses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A16","text":"External spatial intuition supplies the presentations required to establish the objective reality of relational categories and to represent internal change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:A17","text":"All principles of pure understanding are a priori principles of the possibility of experience and apply only to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX1","text":"Modal predicates do not enlarge or further determine the conception of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX2","text":"Logical noncontradiction does not establish the objective possibility of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX3","text":"Arbitrary recombinations of empirically borrowed concepts do not acquire possibility merely because they are thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX4","text":"Existence is not contained in even a complete conception of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX5","text":"Space is not a property of things in themselves from which Berkeleyan idealism could infer the impossibility of spatial objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX6","text":"Internal experience is not the sole immediate experience from which external existence must be uncertainly inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX7","text":"The dependence of experience on external objects does not make dreams, hallucinations, or every apparent perception veridical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX8","text":"Material necessity is neither logical necessity nor absolute necessity nor the necessary existence of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX9","text":"Absolute possibility is not a conception that the understanding can employ empirically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX10","text":"A postulate is not an indemonstrable speculative assertion entitled to acceptance through self-evidence alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:NX11","text":"Categories alone cannot produce cognition or establish synthetic propositions about objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D1","text":"A synthetic conception has objective possibility only when its synthesis is borrowed from experience or constitutes an a priori formal condition of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D2","text":"A conception assembled from empirical materials without conformity to experiential laws remains a chimera lacking a criterion of possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D3","text":"A priori mathematical construction establishes objective possibility only because its formative synthesis is identical with a synthesis governing possible external experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D4","text":"An unperceived entity can be cognized as real when empirical laws connect it to an actual perception within one possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","CH_BA94_EMPI:A9","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D5","text":"Problematic idealism reverses its own priority because temporally determined self-consciousness presupposes rather than proves from itself the external field of permanence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A10","CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D6","text":"Particular external representations require comparison with the criteria of coherent experience because external experience is necessary in general without every representation being real.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","CH_BA94_EMPI:A13","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D7","text":"Natural necessity is hypothetical: given a cause under empirical law, its effect is necessary, but neither cause nor substance is thereby absolutely necessary.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A14","CH_BA94_EMPI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D8","text":"Questions concerning whether possibility exceeds reality or whether several all-embracing experiences are possible cannot be decided by the understanding from experiential data.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D9","text":"Modal postulates are subjectively synthetic procedures that classify a conception according to formal conformity, perceptual connection, or lawful determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A4","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","CH_BA94_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:D10","text":"The proposition that every contingent thing has a cause is objectively valid only within possible experience; if contingency already means existence solely as a consequence, the proposition becomes analytic.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A14","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD1","text":"Objective validity is constituted neither by conceptual coherence nor by access to things in themselves, but by constructibility or lawful connectability within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD2","text":"The progression from possibility to reality to necessity adds determinations of cognitive relation rather than additional content to the object itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A4","CH_BA94_EMPI:A6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A8","CH_BA94_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD3","text":"Externality is a constitutive condition of empirically determinate subjectivity rather than a doubtful object inferred from an independently complete interior consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A10","CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4","text":"The same experiential restriction that legitimates a priori cognition also blocks its conversion into a transcendent ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD5","text":"Nature is rendered law-governed without being rendered absolutely necessary because necessity attaches to conditioned phenomenal transitions rather than self-subsisting things.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A14","CH_BA94_EMPI:A15","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SD6","text":"The understanding can unify what is given into possible experience but cannot determine whether the total modal field contains other forms of intuition, other experiential wholes, or unrealized possibilities.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX1","text":"Pure concepts cannot authenticate their own objective reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX2","text":"Conceivability cannot function as a sufficient criterion of being or possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX3","text":"Internal consciousness cannot provide a self-sufficient temporal foundation for empirical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX4","text":"Existence cannot be treated as conceptual content added to a possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX5","text":"Phenomenal nature cannot contain brute events, unconditioned transitions, or ruptures that evade the unity of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX6","text":"Modal domains cannot be measured or compared independently of the conditions under which objects are experientially given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX7","text":"Alternative forms of intuition or understanding cannot extend cognition for us beyond the conditions of possible human experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX1","text":"No standpoint is available from which possibility, reality, and necessity can be surveyed as absolute domains independent of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX2","text":"No permanent inward presence exists through which the subject could determine itself temporally without externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX3","text":"No object can attain cognitive validity while remaining unrelated to every possible mode of givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX4","text":"The modal constitution of things in themselves remains unavailable because every usable modal criterion terminates at the boundary of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:FGS1","text":"The possibility circuit is: candidate conception → formal synthesis test → agreement with possible experience → objective possibility; failed agreement → emptiness or chimera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:FGS2","text":"The reality circuit is: possible object → direct perception or lawful connection with perception → empirical givenness → reality; absence of perceptual connection → merely conceived existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:FGS3","text":"The necessity circuit is: real state → universal empirical law → determination by a given cause → hypothetical necessity of the effect; absence of lawful determination → no cognizable material necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:FGS4","text":"The anti-idealist circuit is: temporal self-consciousness → requirement of permanence → absence of permanent internal intuition → external spatial permanence → external experience as condition of internal experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:FGS5","text":"The critical closure is: category → corresponding intuition → objective reality → experiential principle; category without intuition → empty form; application beyond experience → illegitimate transcendence or a problem reserved for reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AF1","text":"Modal predicates → cognitive-relational reinterpretation → formal criterion of possibility → perceptual criterion of reality → causal criterion of necessity → empirical restriction of modality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:AF2","text":"Reality criterion → idealist challenge → temporal analysis of self-consciousness → dependence on external permanence → general dependence of categories on external intuition → reduction of pure principles to conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME1","text":"Modality is reclassified from a set of intrinsic object-properties into a set of relations between conceptions and cognitive procedures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME2","text":"The boundary between the thinkable and the possible is redrawn through formal compatibility with possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME3","text":"Reality is relocated from conceptual completeness to the perceptually connected field of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME4","text":"The epistemic priority of inner over outer experience is reversed by making temporal self-determination depend on external permanence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME5","text":"Necessity is narrowed from absolute or substantial being to law-governed dependence among phenomenal states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME6","text":"Categories are reclassified from self-sufficient representations of being into formal operators requiring intuition for objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ME7","text":"Questions about absolute possibility, alternative experiential worlds, and modal totality are transferred from understanding to reason and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the text establishes distinct criteria for possibility, reality, and necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, modal validity is generated by the cooperation of understanding, intuition, perception, judgment, and empirical law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the text converts idealist doubt and metaphysical assertion into tests of the conditions required for experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, phenomenal reality forms a lawfully unified field in which temporal self-consciousness depends on external permanence and necessity remains conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the three modal postulates, the refutation of idealism, and the final restriction of pure understanding to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction remains dependent on prior accounts of space and time, the analogies of experience, causality, apperception, and the distinction between phenomena and things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT3","text":"The system is most coherent where one experiential domain supplies parallel criteria for modal validity and category use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT4","text":"The refutation of idealism compresses the transition from the need for permanence in temporal determination to the claim that only external matter can supply it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT5","text":"The designation of external experience as immediate remains structurally tense because its necessity is established through a transcendental argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT6","text":"The claim that modality is merely subjectively synthetic coexists with claims of objective validity; coherence depends on treating objectivity as validity under experiential conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT7","text":"The exclusion of leaps and hiatuses extends the requirement of unified experience into continuity claims whose complete deduction is not supplied here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the idealism proof and the dependence on external intuition remain internal consequences of the modal analysis rather than detachable digressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 51","title":"Empicisim","context":"Chapter 51","original_title":"Empicisim","original_context":"Chapter 51"},"original_title":"Empicisim","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Dialectic.","order":52,"parents":["RAW_BA94_TRAD"],"revision":"CH_BA94_TRAD@R001","sha256":"b2a1f06ff89ae3a9b6db7182d2a20cf3f4517e507bc8070f9bf80013d339efb8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:AS1","text":"The supplied unit establishes a textual position within a larger division rather than presenting a developed argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:AS2","text":"The unit is nested under Transcendental Logic and specifically designated Transcendental Dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:AS3","text":"The designation “Introduction” marks the material as preliminary to a subsequent treatment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4","text":"No substantive problem, thesis, proof, or argumentative resolution is supplied in the available text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:A1","text":"The material belongs to a section designated “Second Division.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:A2","text":"The Second Division is designated “Transcendental Logic.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:A3","text":"The present subsection is designated “Transcendental Dialectic.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:A4","text":"The supplied unit is designated “Introduction.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:NX1","text":"The supplied material is not a developed exposition of Transcendental Dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:NX2","text":"The supplied material does not state a determinate thesis concerning dialectical reason, illusion, contradiction, or knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:NX3","text":"The supplied material does not provide premises from which a substantive conclusion can be derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:NX4","text":"The supplied material does not identify a method, dispute, or resolution beyond its sectional designation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:D1","text":"The text’s primary function is classificatory and positional rather than demonstrative.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A1","CH_BA94_TRAD:A2","CH_BA94_TRAD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:D2","text":"The subsection is structurally preparatory to an argument not included in the supplied unit.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A3","CH_BA94_TRAD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:D3","text":"Any reconstruction of the doctrine of Transcendental Dialectic from this unit alone would exceed the supplied evidence.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A4","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX1","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX2","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX3","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:SD1","text":"The available text establishes an argumentative container but withholds the conceptual operations that would fill it.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A1","CH_BA94_TRAD:A2","CH_BA94_TRAD:A3","CH_BA94_TRAD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:SD2","text":"The hierarchy of divisions supplies interpretive scope while leaving the internal logic of the subsection underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A1","CH_BA94_TRAD:A2","CH_BA94_TRAD:A3","CH_BA94_TRAD:A4","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX1","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX2","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX3","CH_BA94_TRAD:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX1","text":"The text cannot support an inferred theory of dialectical error, transcendental illusion, or antinomy without additional material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX2","text":"The sectional hierarchy cannot be treated as a substantive argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX3","text":"The introductory status excludes treating the supplied unit as the completed exposition of its subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX4","text":"The absence of argumentative predicates prevents attribution of a specific conclusion to the text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:SNX1","text":"The operative absence is the missing transition from classification to demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:SNX2","text":"The text presupposes a forthcoming conceptual development without disclosing its premises or direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:SNX3","text":"The substantive object of the dialectic remains unavailable within the supplied evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:FGS1","text":"Division designation → logical domain designation → subsection designation → introductory status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:FGS2","text":"Hierarchical placement → interpretive delimitation → preparatory function → argumentative indeterminacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:FGS3","text":"Missing premises → unavailable derivation → unavailable substantive reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:AF1","text":"Second Division → Transcendental Logic → Transcendental Dialectic → Introduction → withheld exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ME1","text":"The text reclassifies the available material from argument to sectional threshold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ME2","text":"The hierarchy distinguishes the general logical domain from the specific dialectical subsection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ME3","text":"The designation “Introduction” prevents the heading from being mistaken for the completed doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ME4","text":"The conceptual map is bounded by explicit placement but remains substantively incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT1","text":"**Surface:** The text presents titles and sectional headings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT2","text":"**Structural:** The headings establish a nested relation between division, domain, subsection, and introductory function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT3","text":"**Metastrategic:** The available material limits interpretation to textual placement and prohibits reconstruction of an unprovided argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT4","text":"**Ontological/Temporal:** The text implies a before-and-after structure in which the introduction precedes a fuller exposition, but it supplies no doctrine of reality, subject, or time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the supplied text’s hierarchy and editorial function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT2","text":"The axioms are insufficient to reconstruct a substantive theory of Transcendental Dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT3","text":"The internal structure is coherent at the level of textual classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT4","text":"The argument remains underdetermined because no conceptual premises, distinctions, or conclusions are supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT5","text":"The reconstruction preserves the available movement from division to subsection to introduction without inventing missing content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TRAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 52","title":"Transcendental Dialectic.","context":"Chapter 52","original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic.","original_context":"Chapter 52"},"original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":53,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ITIA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ITIA@R001","sha256":"c999c4e7b2a079c33016f9aa448407e351ee29348c006508486d113ca58e31f3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS1","text":"Truth, error, and illusory appearance arise only in judgement, where an object is related to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS2","text":"Error is produced when sensibility covertly supplies subjective grounds that understanding treats as objective grounds of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS3","text":"The inquiry excludes empirical deception in order to isolate an illusion that drives reason beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS4","text":"The argument distinguishes immanent employment within experience from transcendent principles that authorize passage beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS5","text":"Transcendental illusion is generated when subjective necessities of reason are taken as objective necessities of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AS6","text":"Transcendental dialectic can expose and regulate this illusion but cannot abolish its recurrent production by human reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A1","text":"Truth and error exist only in judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A2","text":"Sensibility does not judge and therefore does not itself err.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A3","text":"Understanding acting solely under its own laws produces formally lawful judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A4","text":"Error arises when sensibility influences understanding without that influence being recognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A5","text":"Transcendental reflection can distinguish the respective contributions of sensibility and understanding within a judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A6","text":"Principles restricted to possible experience are immanent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A7","text":"Principles that authorize cognition beyond possible experience are transcendent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A8","text":"A transcendent principle differs from a merely erroneous transcendental use of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A9","text":"Logical illusion results from inattention to logical rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A10","text":"Transcendental illusion results from subjective principles of reason appearing as objective principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","text":"Transcendental illusion persists after criticism has disclosed its groundlessness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:A12","text":"Transcendental dialectic can expose and guard against illusion but cannot terminate its natural recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX1","text":"Dialectic is not a doctrine of probability, because probability remains defective but non-deceptive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX2","text":"Phenomenon is not identical with illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX3","text":"Sensible representation is neither true nor false because it contains no judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX4","text":"Error is not attributable to sensibility alone or understanding alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX5","text":"The inquiry is not concerned with empirical illusion occurring within otherwise valid empirical judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX6","text":"Transcendental illusion is not identical with transcendent principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX7","text":"The legitimate principles of pure understanding are not licensed for objects beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:NX8","text":"Transcendental illusion is not removable by the same attentive correction that dissolves logical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D1","text":"Error must be analyzed as a composite judgement rather than assigned to an isolated cognitive faculty.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A1","CH_BA94_ITIA:A2","CH_BA94_ITIA:A3","CH_BA94_ITIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D2","text":"The proper critical procedure resolves an erroneous judgement into subjective sensory influence and lawful intellectual form.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D3","text":"The boundary of possible experience functions as the criterion separating legitimate pure-understanding principles from transcendent claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A6","CH_BA94_ITIA:A7","CH_BA94_ITIA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D4","text":"Reason can experience subjective necessity as if it disclosed the necessary structure of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D5","text":"Recognition that a transcendent claim lacks objective validity does not extinguish the disposition to generate that claim.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A10","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:D6","text":"Critique changes the status of illusion from an unrecognized source of error into a permanently monitored tendency.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SD1","text":"The system relocates transcendental error from defective empirical evidence to a structural misattribution of rational subjectivity as objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A1","CH_BA94_ITIA:A4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SD2","text":"Critical philosophy cannot deliver a final state free of metaphysical illusion, because the source of illusion is constitutive of reason’s ordinary operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A10","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SD3","text":"The immanent–transcendent distinction converts the limit of experience from a mere absence of knowledge into a juridical limit on valid cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A6","CH_BA94_ITIA:A7","CH_BA94_ITIA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SD4","text":"Dialectic becomes a discipline of recurrent containment rather than a technique for permanently eliminating error.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A9","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX1","text":"No felt necessity of thought can count as immediate evidence of an objective necessity in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX2","text":"No cognition extending beyond possible experience can acquire validity merely by being generated from pure concepts or rational maxims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX3","text":"No faculty-level innocence guarantees truth when faculties are combined in an unexamined judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX4","text":"No critical disclosure can transform finite human reason into a faculty that ceases to solicit transcendent conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses a standpoint from which reason could retain its constitutive demand for unconditioned cognition without producing transcendent illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SNX2","text":"The system denies that objective reality can be secured by the mere internal coherence, subjective necessity, or formal inevitability of rational thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:SNX3","text":"The system leaves unavailable any positive theoretical domain beyond experience in which transcendent principles could be validated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS1","text":"Sensibility supplies intuition and understanding supplies judgement; their unobserved interference produces ordinary error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS2","text":"Transcendental reflection decomposes the composite judgement and assigns each representation to its proper cognitive faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS3","text":"Possible experience establishes the boundary within which pure-understanding principles retain immanent validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS4","text":"Subjective maxims of reason acquire the appearance of objective principles and generate transcendent claims beyond that boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS5","text":"Criticism exposes the projection of subjectivity as objectivity, while dialectic continually guards against its renewed operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AF1","text":"Error is relocated from objects and isolated faculties to the judgement in which sensibility and understanding become confused.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AF2","text":"The analysis moves from ordinary error to the distinction between empirical illusion and transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AF3","text":"The distinction between immanent and transcendent principles fixes possible experience as the boundary of legitimate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AF4","text":"Subjective rational necessity is then diagnosed as the source of an objective-seeming transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:AF5","text":"The final position assigns dialectic the continuing task of exposure and restraint rather than eradication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ME1","text":"Illusion is reclassified from a property of objects or sensations into a defect of judgement and its attribution of grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ME2","text":"The limit of experience is reclassified from an empirical shortfall into the constitutive boundary of valid theoretical principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ME3","text":"The opposition between error and critique is redrawn: critique can neutralize the authority of illusion without eliminating its appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ME4","text":"Dialectic is reclassified from sophistical failure into an unavoidable and natural tendency internal to pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate object is the distinction between logical, empirical, and transcendental forms of illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Error is generated by an unrecognized crossing of cognitive functions, especially sensibility’s determination of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Criticism operates by demarcating valid employment, exposing illegitimate extension, and instituting permanent vigilance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human reason necessarily and repeatedly projects subjective rational demands onto things beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s central movement from the location of error to the necessity of critical restraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the distinction between subjective necessity and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding how criticism reliably identifies the boundary between a merely subjective maxim and a valid objective principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural relation is the distinction between correctable logical illusion and ineradicable transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT5","text":"The system contains a productive tension because reason must employ the very subjective principles that criticism identifies as the source of its recurring illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if dialectic is treated as ongoing regulation of an unavoidable tendency rather than final removal of false appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 53","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","context":"Chapter 53","original_title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","original_context":"Chapter 53"},"original_title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS","layer":"CH","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":54,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IPRS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IPRS@R001","sha256":"17dab027544eb137ab33a9d8064c809840d9401a793950eba108bd47fc6f52a6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS1","text":"Human cognition rises from sensibility through understanding to reason, whose proper task is the highest unity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS2","text":"Reason must be distinguished from understanding because understanding unifies intuitions under rules, whereas reason unifies rules under principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS3","text":"The logical form of syllogism reveals reason’s operative demand to seek conditions for conditioned judgements until an unconditioned condition is reached.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","text":"The transition from a logical demand for completeness to an assertion that the unconditioned exists in objects is synthetic and cannot be secured by logic alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5","text":"Transcendental illusion originates when the subjective maxim of ascending toward higher conditions is mistaken for an objective principle determining objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AS6","text":"Transcendental dialectic must determine whether reason’s principle has objective validity or only regulates the systematic ordering of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A1","text":"All human cognition begins in sensibility, is processed by understanding, and is ordered toward unity by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A2","text":"Understanding is the faculty of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A3","text":"Reason is the faculty of principles because it produces unity among the rules of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A4","text":"Cognition from principles cognizes a particular through its subsumption under a general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A5","text":"A merely general proposition can function comparatively as a principle without being a principle by origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A6","text":"Synthetic cognition from concepts alone is required for principles in the strict sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A7","text":"The principles of understanding obtain their objective use only through the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A8","text":"Reason does not directly determine intuitions or sensuous objects; it acts mediately through understanding and its judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A9","text":"Logical reason seeks the general condition under which a conditioned judgement follows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","text":"The repeated search for conditions leads reason to demand the unconditioned as the completion of conditioned cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A11","text":"The claim that the whole series of conditions is given whenever the conditioned is given is a synthetic principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A12","text":"Any objective use of this principle exceeds the immanent limits of understanding and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A13","text":"Reason’s demand for systematic unity is initially a subjective law governing the arrangement of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:A14","text":"Transcendental dialectic investigates whether reason’s subjective demand can legitimately determine objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX1","text":"Reason is not merely the faculty of mediate inference, because that definition excludes its purported real and transcendental use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX2","text":"Reason is not a faculty that directly applies concepts to intuition or sensuous objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX3","text":"The unity of reason is not the empirical unity that makes experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX4","text":"The principle that every event has a cause is not generated by reason independently of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX5","text":"A subjective requirement for conceptual economy is not an objective law of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX6","text":"Logical ascent from a conditioned cognition to its conditions does not by itself establish an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX7","text":"Transcendent principles are not legitimately usable as immanent principles of empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:NX8","text":"The completeness of the series of conditions cannot be postulated as an objective feature of objects merely because reason requires completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D1","text":"Reason’s proper object is understanding itself, since rational unity orders the manifold rules and cognitions produced by understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A2","CH_BA94_IPRS:A3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D2","text":"The syllogism formalizes reason’s operation by moving from a rule, through subsumption under its condition, to a determined conclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A4","CH_BA94_IPRS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D3","text":"The demand for the unconditioned is generated recursively because every discovered condition can itself be treated as conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A9","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D4","text":"Reason seeks to reduce the plurality of cognitions and rules to the smallest possible number of higher principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D5","text":"The unconditioned cannot be supplied by the understanding, because understanding cognizes only under conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A7","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D6","text":"The assertion of a complete unconditioned series introduces propositions that exceed what the categories and principles of understanding can establish.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A11","CH_BA94_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:D7","text":"Transcendental illusion is structurally possible because a necessary logical maxim can be misread as a constitutive principle of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:A11","CH_BA94_IPRS:A13","CH_BA94_IPRS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SD1","text":"Reason produces a systematic totality-demand that is necessary for cognition’s organization but indeterminate with respect to the existence of a corresponding object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SD2","text":"The same rational movement that advances cognition toward systematic unity also produces transcendent claims when its formal requirement is objectified.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A8","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:A11","CH_BA94_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SD3","text":"Dialectical error is generated not by accidental irrationality but by reason’s lawful tendency to complete the series of conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A9","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4","text":"The critique of reason must preserve the regulative demand for unity while suspending its unauthorized constitutive extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A12","CH_BA94_IPRS:A13","CH_BA94_IPRS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX1","text":"The system excludes any direct passage from the form of inference to knowledge of unconditioned being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the identity of subjective cognitive necessity with objective ontological necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX3","text":"The system excludes an empirical object that could adequately exhibit or verify the totality of all conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that understanding’s experiential principles can determine what lies beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX5","text":"The system excludes closure of rational inquiry through a final object whenever that closure is claimed as empirically knowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the unavailable coincidence between reason’s demand for absolute completion and cognition’s capacity for objective verification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses an unmediated intellectual access to the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX3","text":"The argument treats absolute totality as thinkable through reason’s demand yet inadmissible as a directly determinable object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX4","text":"The argument denies that human cognition can occupy a standpoint from which the entire series of conditions is simply given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS1","text":"Conditioned judgement → search for its general condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS2","text":"General condition → renewed treatment of that condition as conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS3","text":"Recursive ascent through conditions → logical demand for the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS4","text":"Logical demand for completion → possible synthetic assertion that the complete series exists in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS5","text":"Objectification of subjective rational demand → transcendence beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS6","text":"Transcendent objectification → transcendental illusion and dialectical syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS7","text":"Dialectical critique → separation of regulative systematic unity from constitutive objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF1","text":"The distinction between sensibility, understanding, and reason establishes reason as the problem of highest cognitive unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF2","text":"The distinction between rules and principles redefines reason as the faculty that systematizes understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF3","text":"The analysis of syllogism reveals reason’s formal movement from conditioned cognition toward higher conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF4","text":"The regress toward higher conditions generates the idea of an unconditioned completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF5","text":"The unconditioned becomes dialectically problematic when logical completion is asserted as objective totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:AF6","text":"Transcendental dialectic tests the legitimacy of that assertion and isolates the illusion produced by its misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ME1","text":"Reason is reclassified from a faculty that merely infers conclusions into a faculty that demands systematic unity among rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ME2","text":"The distinction between logical and transcendental use separates formal inferential validity from objective validity concerning objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is recast from an immediately available object of cognition into a problem generated by rational regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ME4","text":"Systematic unity is separated from empirical determination, allowing rational organization without immediate ontological commitment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ME5","text":"Error is reclassified as a lawful effect of reason’s own drive toward completeness rather than as a mere empirical mistake.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Reason is differentiated from understanding through its role in syllogism, principles, and the pursuit of higher unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is organized as a hierarchy in which sensibility supplies intuition, understanding supplies rules, and reason orders rules into a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical inquiry shifts from asking whether reason can infer correctly to asking whether its demand for completion has objective authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The regress through conditions projects a totality of conditions whose completed givenness cannot be presumed within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from the hierarchy of faculties to the dialectical problem of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT2","text":"The structure depends principally on the distinction between a logical maxim of regression and a synthetic claim about objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it separates understanding’s immanent experiential use from reason’s demand for systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT4","text":"The central fault line is that reason’s necessary pursuit of completion continually pressures cognition toward claims that its own critical limits prohibit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT5","text":"The status of the unconditioned remains underdetermined until dialectic establishes whether it is objectively valid, merely regulative, or the source of unavoidable illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if the logical necessity of seeking conditions is not conflated with knowledge that an unconditioned totality is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 54","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","context":"Chapter 54","original_title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","original_context":"Chapter 54"},"original_title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of the Conceptions of Pure Reason.","order":55,"parents":["RAW_BA94_TDBI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_TDBI@R001","sha256":"a23c3e3152ff1eea59607e0d3cd64c0386fec9f9bb0256a3ff7388ffe00428f6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish the origin, scope, and possible objective validity of the conceptions of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS2","text":"The text separates conceptions of reason from conceptions of understanding by their mode of production and function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS3","text":"Conceptions of understanding organize phenomena within possible experience, whereas conceptions of reason seek cognition exceeding any complete empirical attainment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS4","text":"Reason is defined by its tendency toward the unconditioned underlying or governing conditioned cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS5","text":"The objective validity of rational conceptions remains conditional and is distinguished from their merely apparent derivability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AS6","text":"The argument moves from distinction of faculties, through delimitation of experience, toward the classification of rational conceptions as transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A1","text":"Conceptions of pure reason are obtained through inference or conclusion rather than reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A2","text":"Conceptions of understanding are a priori conditions that provide the unity of reflection required for possible empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A3","text":"Cognition and determination of an object require the conceptions of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","text":"The objective reality of conceptions of understanding depends on restricting their application to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A5","text":"Rational conceptions seek a cognition of which empirical cognition and possible experience are only parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A6","text":"The aim of rational conceptions is comprehension, whereas the aim of conceptions of understanding is the understanding of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A7","text":"The unconditioned is that to which all experience is subordinate but which is never itself given as an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A8","text":"Reason tends toward the unconditioned in its conclusions from experience and uses it as a standard for estimating empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A9","text":"A rational conception has objective validity only if it is legitimately concluded rather than merely admitted because it appears correctly concluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:A10","text":"The pure conceptions of reason are designated transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX1","text":"Pure rational conceptions are not generated by reflection in the manner of the conceptions of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX2","text":"Conceptions of understanding are not derived from prior a priori conceptions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX3","text":"The conceptions of understanding do not possess unrestricted application beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX4","text":"The unconditioned is not an element in an empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX5","text":"No actual experience fully attains the cognition toward which rational conceptions tend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX6","text":"Apparent derivability does not establish objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:NX7","text":"The text does not yet determine whether particular rational conceptions are legitimate or sophistical; that determination is deferred to the dialectical analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D1","text":"Because conceptions of understanding constitute the intellectual form of experience, their objective reality is inseparable from empirical restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A2","CH_BA94_TDBI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D2","text":"Because rational conceptions aim at cognition exceeding empirical cognition, they cannot receive their complete object from any possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A5","CH_BA94_TDBI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D3","text":"The unconditioned functions as a regulative terminus of reasoning from experience without becoming an empirical object within that reasoning.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A7","CH_BA94_TDBI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D4","text":"Rational conceptions divide into legitimate conceptions and sophistical conceptions according to whether their inferred objective validity is established or merely presumed.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D5","text":"The distinction between understanding and reason is simultaneously a distinction between the determination of objects within experience and the comprehension of the totality or condition of such objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A3","CH_BA94_TDBI:A5","CH_BA94_TDBI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:D6","text":"The proper classification of rational conceptions requires a later analysis of the dialectical conclusions produced by reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A9","CH_BA94_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SD1","text":"Reason is structurally oriented beyond the domain that alone can confer direct objective reality, creating a permanent distinction between its aim and its possible evidentiary ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_TDBI:A5","CH_BA94_TDBI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SD2","text":"The unconditioned operates as the horizon that organizes the evaluation of conditioned cognition without entering the empirical series as one more condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A7","CH_BA94_TDBI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SD3","text":"The system makes the legitimacy of reason depend on a future adjudication of inference rather than on the mere presence of an a priori conception.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A1","CH_BA94_TDBI:A9","CH_BA94_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SD4","text":"The distinction between understanding and reason converts the boundary of experience from a limitation on cognition into the criterion for separating valid object-determination from speculative overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A3","CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_TDBI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX1","text":"The system cannot treat all a priori conceptions as having the same origin, function, or entitlement to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the identification of the unconditioned with any empirically discoverable item within the chain of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX3","text":"The system disqualifies unrestricted extension from the domain of possible experience as a sufficient ground of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX4","text":"The system cannot infer legitimacy from the psychological or formal appearance of a conclusion alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a completed empirical possession of the total cognition toward which reason is directed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a form of reason whose objects would be immediately available within experience while still functioning as the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SNX2","text":"The text excludes the possibility that the demand for total comprehension can be satisfied by extending empirical cognition without altering its epistemic status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SNX3","text":"The text withholds any final guarantee that the content generated by reason corresponds to an objectively valid object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:SNX4","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of an empirical presentation adequate to reason’s complete object-matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS1","text":"A priori cognition divides into understanding-based conceptions and reason-based conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS2","text":"Understanding-based conceptions unify phenomena and thereby make object-determination within possible experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS3","text":"Reason-based conceptions infer a cognition that exceeds any particular empirical cognition and potentially exceeds the whole of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS4","text":"The unconditioned is introduced as the terminal orientation of reasoning from conditioned experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS5","text":"The inferred conception is tested by the distinction between legitimate objective validity and sophistical appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS6","text":"The resulting class of reason-conceptions is named transcendental ideas, whose legitimacy is reserved for subsequent dialectical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:AF1","text":"A priori conceptual plurality → distinction of origin → understanding as condition of experience → reason as inference beyond experience → orientation toward the unconditioned → separation of legitimate and sophistical inference → designation as transcendental ideas → deferred dialectical adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ME1","text":"The conceptual field is divided between object-determining cognition within experience and comprehension directed beyond the limits of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ME2","text":"The boundary of experience becomes an epistemic criterion rather than merely a restriction on the quantity of knowable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a possible object among objects into the terminal standard or horizon of rational inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ME4","text":"The distinction between valid and invalid metaphysical cognition is relocated from content alone to the mode by which the conception is inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ME5","text":"Pure reason is mapped as simultaneously indispensable for total comprehension and unable to obtain its aim through direct empirical presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text introduces and differentiates the conceptions of understanding and the conceptions of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ALT2","text":"Structural: Understanding supplies the form through which phenomena become objects, while reason organizes conditioned cognition in relation to an unconditioned terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The text establishes a procedure for adjudicating rational concepts by separating legitimate inference from merely apparent inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The unconditioned is positioned as a non-empirical limit toward which cognition tends, while its objective status remains unresolved until later dialectical analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the primary distinction between understanding and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT2","text":"The axioms preserve the dependence of objectivity on possible experience and the orientation of reason toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT3","text":"The classification of rational conceptions as legitimate or sophistical remains deliberately underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT4","text":"The central tension is that reason seeks cognition beyond experience while objective validity is introduced through a criterion not yet supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of functional distinction but incomplete at the level of validating particular transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from faculty-differentiation to transcendental classification and deferred dialectical judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 55","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of the Conceptions of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 55","original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason.","original_context":"Chapter 55"},"original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG","layer":"CH","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","order":56,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIIG"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIIG@R001","sha256":"207216b3101362c27f8b0ca08b488719411c82ab71a34dbded7fe9cb758f0796","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS1","text":"Linguistic indeterminacy threatens the preservation, communication, and scientific use of distinct conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS2","text":"The term “idea” must be recovered from Platonic usage and reserved for a determinate rational function rather than dispersed across representations generally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS3","text":"Reason generates cognitions whose objects cannot be given in possible experience, yet whose practical operation is real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS4","text":"Practical ideas function as archetypal standards by which empirical actions, persons, laws, and institutions are judged without being derived from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5","text":"The discrepancy between an idea and its realization establishes a task of approximation rather than a refutation of the idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AS6","text":"The argument moves from terminological discipline through the practical authority of ideas to a critical inquiry into the legitimate transcendental use of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A1","text":"Distinct conceptions require distinct and stable terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A2","text":"A representation becomes cognitively determinate only through its place within a graduated taxonomy of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A3","text":"An idea is a concept of reason that transcends the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A4","text":"Ideas are not derived from the senses and admit of no perfectly corresponding empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A5","text":"A cognition may be real even when no object corresponding to it can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","text":"Practical reason, because it concerns freedom, can make ideas operative causes of actions and their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A7","text":"Empirical instances can illustrate an idea but cannot constitute its archetype or determine its content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A8","text":"Imperfect realization of an idea does not invalidate its normative authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A9","text":"The idea of a lawful constitution of mutually compatible freedom is prior to and regulative of legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A10","text":"Ethical and juridical laws cannot be derived from empirical conduct because experience supplies what is done rather than what ought to be done.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A11","text":"Experience is a source of rules and truth for nature but a source of illusion when treated as the ground of ethical law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A12","text":"The legitimate use, influence, and worth of ideas require transcendental critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:A13","text":"Platonic insight into ideas must be separated from mystical deduction, hypostatization, and unrestricted extension into speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX1","text":"Philosophical precision is not secured by indiscriminate neologism or stylistic variation in the use of technical terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX2","text":"An idea is not a generic name for every representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX3","text":"An idea is not a sensation, intuition, empirical concept, pure concept of understanding, or category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX4","text":"An empirical example is not an original model of virtue, justice, or lawful freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX5","text":"An idea is not disqualified by the absence of complete realization in human action or political institutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX6","text":"The political end is not the greatest possible happiness as an independently grounding principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX7","text":"Adverse historical experience is not decisive evidence against a rational ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX8","text":"Ethical obligation is not inferable from prevailing conduct, customary institutions, or observed human behavior.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:NX9","text":"A critical theory of ideas is not identical with Plato’s speculative, mystical, or hypostatizing doctrine of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D1","text":"The representation “idea” occupies a terminal position in the taxonomy of representations and cannot be applied to merely sensible or intellectual contents.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A1","CH_BA94_SIIG:A2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D2","text":"Moral appraisal presupposes a rational standard that precedes every comparison among empirical actions or persons.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SIIG:A7","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D3","text":"Virtuous persons function as graded examples only because an antecedent idea of virtue measures their adequacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D4","text":"Political reform remains rationally directed by the idea of reciprocal freedom even when no state fully realizes it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A8","CH_BA94_SIIG:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D5","text":"Institutional failure can indicate defective legislation or neglected rational principles rather than the impracticability of freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A8","CH_BA94_SIIG:A9","CH_BA94_SIIG:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D6","text":"The relation between nature and ethics divides the epistemic authority of experience: descriptive authority belongs to nature, while normative authority belongs to reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A10","CH_BA94_SIIG:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D7","text":"Reason can legitimately exceed experience in practical use only under a critique that distinguishes such use from metaphysical reification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A5","CH_BA94_SIIG:A12","CH_BA94_SIIG:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:D8","text":"The impossibility of assigning the final distance between an idea and realization prevents any empirical limit from fixing the destination of freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8","CH_BA94_SIIG:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SD1","text":"The system establishes two irreducible regimes of validity: empirical cognition governs natural actuality, while rational ideas govern practical assessment and direction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A3","CH_BA94_SIIG:A10","CH_BA94_SIIG:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SD2","text":"Nonconformity to an ideal becomes the condition for moral and political judgment rather than evidence that judgment lacks an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A7","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SD3","text":"Terminological taxonomy becomes an epistemic border-policing mechanism that prevents the ascent from sensation to reason from being collapsed into one undifferentiated field of representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A1","CH_BA94_SIIG:A2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SD4","text":"The authority of practical ideals depends simultaneously on their nonempirical origin and on their capacity to order empirical transformation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SD5","text":"Critique is required because reason must exceed experience to ground obligation while being prevented from converting that excess into knowledge of independently existing ideal objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A5","CH_BA94_SIIG:A12","CH_BA94_SIIG:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX1","text":"Cognition is not exhausted by the reception and synthesis of objects available in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX2","text":"Normative universality is not produced by generalizing from examples, customs, or historical regularities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX3","text":"The actuality of institutions is not the measure of their legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX4","text":"The historical failure of freedom is not proof of an immutable natural limit on freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX5","text":"No empirical state of affairs can determine the ultimate degree of approximation required by a rational ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX6","text":"Rational standards cannot be preserved if ordinary linguistic usage is permitted to erase distinctions among modes of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around an absent empirical object that must nevertheless be present as a standard for judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX2","text":"The system excludes any final empirical reconciliation between ideal law and its complete realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses both empiricist closure, which denies rational ideals, and dogmatic metaphysical closure, which treats ideals as independently given beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX4","text":"The destination of freedom is binding precisely because no authority can assign a final boundary between freedom and its idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS1","text":"Linguistic indeterminacy produces conceptual loss; taxonomic differentiation preserves determinate thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS2","text":"Taxonomic differentiation isolates the idea as a rational concept exceeding possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS3","text":"The practical relation to freedom gives the nonempirical idea operational force in actions, judgments, and institutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS4","text":"The idea functions as archetype; empirical persons and institutions function as examples, approximations, or deviations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS5","text":"Imperfect realization is reclassified from disproof into the measurable task of progressive approximation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS6","text":"Empirical appeals to existing practice are displaced from normative grounding and confined to their proper natural-descriptive domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS7","text":"Transcendental critique limits rational excess by distinguishing practical validity from speculative hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AF1","text":"Lexical imprecision → loss of conceptual distinctions → preservation of technical vocabulary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AF2","text":"Platonic naming of ideas → critical recovery of rational archetypes → separation from Platonic metaphysical excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AF3","text":"Ideas beyond experience → freedom as practical causality → ideals of virtue and reciprocal legal freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AF4","text":"Empirical inadequacy → rejection of empirical refutation → approximation under an unreached rational standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:AF5","text":"Practical authority of ideas → exclusion of empirical ethical grounding → transcendental examination of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME1","text":"Representations are reorganized into a hierarchy in which an idea is no longer confused with perception, intuition, concept, or category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME2","text":"The relation between ideal and instance is redrawn: the ideal judges the instance, while the instance can only exhibit partial practicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME3","text":"Political theory is shifted from maximizing happiness or recording existing institutions to constructing conditions of mutually compatible freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME4","text":"Historical failure is reclassified from a veto on ideals into possible evidence of prior legislative and institutional failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME5","text":"Experience is divided into two functions: it grounds truth about nature but cannot ground laws of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ME6","text":"Plato is reclassified from a doctrinal authority to a source of a recoverable structural insight requiring critical restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit task is to reserve “idea” for a concept of reason and to distinguish it from every lower mode of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text constructs a hierarchy in which reason, understanding, sensibility, experience, freedom, and legislation possess noninterchangeable functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument blocks empirical objections to moral and political ideals by redescribing them as failures of realization rather than failures of principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ideas lack complete empirical instantiation yet orient an open-ended temporal movement of freedom beyond every assigned limit of realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:ALT5","text":"Critical — Pure reason is granted practical reach beyond experience while being subjected to a tribunal that denies it unrestricted speculative objecthood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from semantic discipline to the practical authority and critical limitation of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT2","text":"The system depends centrally on the distinction between empirical objects, rational standards, and the practical causality of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT3","text":"The argument is internally coherent if ideas are treated as practical archetypes rather than as empirically available objects or independently existing entities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural operation is the conversion of nonrealization from an objection into the very condition of normative judgment and reform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how a cognition without a corresponding possible object is established as real rather than merely subjective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT6","text":"The system also leaves a tension between the asserted practical efficacy of ideas and the required critical prohibition on their hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if the terminological taxonomy, practical application, antiempirical normativity, and critical restriction remain jointly operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 56","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","context":"Chapter 56","original_title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","original_context":"Chapter 56"},"original_title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","order":57,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SITI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SITI@R001","sha256":"b8afdaf4fa30bfd009f6b4a561d2e9d17a3b0cdbe4792ba23a58c8f2b3c92ca9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine whether reason’s logical form generates pure a priori conceptions analogous to the categories generated from judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS2","text":"Reason is reconstructed as the faculty that seeks the totality of conditions for every given conditioned cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS3","text":"The demand for totality entails the conception of the unconditioned as the ground of a completed series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS4","text":"The three syllogistic relations generate three transcendental ideas: the unconditioned subject, the unconditioned ground of a series, and the complete system of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS5","text":"Transcendental ideas necessarily direct the understanding toward systematic unity but cannot determine objects within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AS6","text":"The argument moves from logical inference to the necessary regulative demand for unconditioned totality, then restricts that demand from transcendent cognition to the ordered use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A1","text":"Logical forms of cognition can yield pure a priori conceptions when applied to the synthesis of intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A2","text":"The logical form of judgement yields categories that make empirical cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A3","text":"The logical form of syllogism yields pure conceptions of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A4","text":"Reason functions by subsuming a conditioned cognition under the universal condition that grounds it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A5","text":"A syllogism seeks a condition under which a predicate is valid in its whole extent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A6","text":"The universality of a condition in judgement corresponds to the totality of conditions in the synthesis of intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A7","text":"A transcendental conception of reason is the conception of the totality of conditions for a given conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A8","text":"The totality of conditions is possible only through the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A9","text":"The categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive forms of synthesis require respectively an unconditioned subject, an unconditioned ground of a series, and an unconditioned system of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A10","text":"Absolute totality concerns what is unconditioned in every respect and relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A11","text":"A transcendental idea is a necessary conception of reason for which no adequate object can be presented in the world of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A12","text":"Pure reason seeks absolute unity of the understanding’s acts rather than immediate cognition of objects of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A13","text":"The objective employment of transcendental ideas is transcendent because no experience contains the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A14","text":"Transcendental ideas can regulate the extended and self-consistent use of understanding without constituting further cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A15","text":"In an ascending series of conditions, reason presupposes totality as necessary for the truth of the conditioned conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:A16","text":"In a descending series of consequences, reason requires only an indefinitely extendable progression and not a completed totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX1","text":"Transcendental ideas are not empirical concepts because no possible experience presents the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX2","text":"Transcendental ideas are not arbitrary fictions because they arise necessarily from reason’s own logical demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX3","text":"The unconditioned is not an object immediately cognized through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX4","text":"The objective use of reason is not immanent in the manner of the understanding’s empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX5","text":"Absolute possibility is not identical with intrinsic possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX6","text":"Absolute necessity is not identical with internal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX7","text":"The absence of an adequate sensible object does not make an idea superfluous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:NX8","text":"The completed totality demanded on the side of conditions is not a totality that finite cognition can fully apprehend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D1","text":"Reason necessarily transforms any given conditioned cognition into a demand for its complete ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A4","CH_BA94_SITI:A7","CH_BA94_SITI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D2","text":"The forms of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive inference determine the exhaustive architecture of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A3","CH_BA94_SITI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D3","text":"Reason’s demand for unconditioned totality is logically necessary even where its corresponding object cannot be experienced.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A8","CH_BA94_SITI:A11","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D4","text":"The transcendental use of reason cannot add determinate objects to experience because its proper object exceeds all possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A11","CH_BA94_SITI:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D5","text":"Ideas retain a legitimate use by ordering the understanding’s cognitions toward maximal systematic unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D6","text":"A conditioned conclusion is treated as necessary only through reference to an unconditionally valid totality of grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A4","CH_BA94_SITI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D7","text":"The asymmetry between conditions and consequences makes completion necessary in regress but unnecessary in progress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A15","CH_BA94_SITI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:D8","text":"The distinction between absolute and intrinsic necessity blocks the inference from unrestricted necessity to necessity grounded merely in a thing’s inner constitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SD1","text":"Reason is structurally compelled to exceed experience while being denied constitutive cognition beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A8","CH_BA94_SITI:A11","CH_BA94_SITI:A13","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SD2","text":"The system converts reason’s apparent metaphysical excess into a regulative requirement for systematic coherence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A13","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SD3","text":"The demand for the unconditioned is generated internally by the logic of conditioned judgement rather than imported from sensory objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A3","CH_BA94_SITI:A4","CH_BA94_SITI:A7","CH_BA94_SITI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SD4","text":"The truth-claim of inferential cognition depends on a totality that cannot itself be given as an empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A11","CH_BA94_SITI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SD5","text":"Systematic unity becomes the legitimate surrogate for unavailable knowledge of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A13","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that all legitimate cognition must be directly grounded in sensible presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that reason can be reduced to a merely formal instrument for deriving consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the conditioned can be finally intelligible without reference to an unconditioned totality of grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the unconditioned can be known as a determinate empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes any simple equivalence between logical necessity, intrinsic necessity, and absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around an unavailable completion: reason must posit totality precisely where no adequate presentation of totality is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses a final coincidence between the structure required for cognition and an object that could satisfy that structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:SNX3","text":"The system denies reason both metaphysical possession of the unconditioned and freedom from the demand for it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS1","text":"Logical form of judgement → categories → conditions of possible empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS2","text":"Logical form of syllogism → search for universal conditions → demand for totality of grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS3","text":"Given conditioned → regress through conditions → conception of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS4","text":"Three forms of relation → unconditioned subject / unconditioned series-ground / complete systematic division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS5","text":"No adequate sensible object for the unconditioned → transcendent objective use is blocked.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS6","text":"Necessary demand for totality + prohibition on constitutive transcendence → regulative unity of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:FGS7","text":"Ascending regress requires completed grounds → descending progression remains indefinitely open.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AF1","text":"Categories derived from judgement **→** reason examined through syllogism **→** totality of conditions identified as reason’s object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AF2","text":"Totality of conditions **→** unconditioned **→** three classes of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AF3","text":"Transcendental ideas as necessary products of reason **→** absence of adequate experience **→** denial of constitutive objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AF4","text":"Denial of constitutive use **→** retention of regulative use **→** systematic unity of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:AF5","text":"Conditioned conclusion **→** required totality of premises **→** asymmetry between completed regress and open progress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME1","text":"Metaphysical ideas are reclassified from candidate objects of knowledge into necessary functions of systematic orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME2","text":"The unconditioned is relocated from empirical content to the formal horizon required by reason’s treatment of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME3","text":"The distinction between understanding and reason is redrawn as a distinction between possible-experience cognition and totalizing regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME4","text":"The distinction between legitimate and illegitimate transcendence is redrawn as regulative guidance versus constitutive object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME5","text":"Syllogistic forms are reclassified as generators of transcendental ideas rather than merely formal patterns of inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ME6","text":"Inferential regress and inferential progress are separated into structurally unequal temporal directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure conceptions of reason are defined as transcendental ideas oriented toward absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ALT2","text":"Structural — Syllogistic inference compels reason to seek completed grounds for conditioned cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reason’s transcendent tendency is retained as a rule for organizing understanding rather than licensed as knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The unconditioned functions as a necessary but unpresentable horizon of completed regress, while consequences remain capable of indefinite progression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the central transition from syllogistic form to transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT2","text":"The axioms reconstruct the derivation of the unconditioned from reason’s demand for totality of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how a merely regulative idea can secure the specific unity it prescribes without covert objective commitment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes the logical necessity of seeking totality from the empirical impossibility of presenting totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the compressed transition from forms of syllogism to exhaustive classes of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT6","text":"The distinction between absolute and internal necessity blocks equivocation but leaves the positive determinations of absolute necessity deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SITI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the regulative validity of ideas is maintained together with the prohibition on their constitutive objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 57","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 57","original_title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 57"},"original_title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","order":58,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIST"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIST@R001","sha256":"abe30d061325fcad40f495dc049eee2a1ab1f12b8f97eae57ddb83d2ce1fd12e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS1","text":"Transcendental dialectic must be distinguished from formal logical dialectic because it concerns the a priori origin of reason’s non-empirical ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS2","text":"Reason moves from conditioned syntheses toward an unconditioned that understanding cannot reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS3","text":"The possible relations of representation yield a threefold architecture of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS4","text":"The three ideas organize rational inquiry into soul, world, and supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS5","text":"The ideas can be subjectively deduced from reason’s syllogistic activity but cannot be objectively deduced from possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AS6","text":"The chapter closes by delimiting pure reason’s proper sphere as a systematic ascent to conditions rather than an extension of empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A1","text":"Transcendental dialectic concerns a priori cognitions and ideas originating in pure reason whose objects cannot be empirically given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A2","text":"Reason necessarily ascends from the conditioned to the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A3","text":"Understanding remains within conditioned synthesis and cannot reach the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A4","text":"Pure concepts of understanding concern the synthetic unity of representations, while transcendental ideas concern the unconditioned synthetic unity of all conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A5","text":"Representations relate to the subject, to the manifold of phenomena, or to all things in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A6","text":"The first class of transcendental ideas concerns the absolute unity of the thinking subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A7","text":"The second class of transcendental ideas concerns the absolute unity of the series of conditions of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A8","text":"The third class of transcendental ideas concerns the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A9","text":"Pure reason relates immediately to concepts contained in understanding rather than immediately to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A10","text":"The categorical syllogism generates the idea of the absolute unity of the thinking subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A11","text":"The hypothetical syllogism generates the idea of an absolutely unconditioned series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A12","text":"The disjunctive syllogism generates the idea of a being of all beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A13","text":"Transcendental ideas admit no objective deduction because they lack objects that can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A14","text":"Transcendental ideas admit a subjective deduction from the nature and necessary activity of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A15","text":"Pure reason seeks absolute totality on the side of conditions rather than absolute completeness on the side of the conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A16","text":"Reason has only logical, not transcendental, use in descending from conditions to conditioned consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A17","text":"Pure reason collects its ideas into a system progressing from self through world to supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:A18","text":"The analogy between logical forms and transcendental procedures remains unestablished at this stage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX1","text":"Transcendental dialectic is not logical dialectic abstracted from all cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX2","text":"Transcendental ideas are not concepts whose objects can be supplied in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX3","text":"Transcendental ideas are not products that understanding can generate, even through maximal logical inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX4","text":"The rational doctrines of soul, world, and supreme being are not objective sciences constituted through empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX5","text":"The totality of consequences is not a necessary presupposition of the possibility of the conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX6","text":"The idea of the complete series of future changes is not a transcendental idea but an ens rationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX7","text":"Logical descent from condition to conditioned is not a transcendental employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:NX8","text":"The formal correspondence of syllogistic logic and transcendental reason is not yet demonstrated as an objective analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D1","text":"The architecture of transcendental ideas is necessarily triadic because reason’s demand for unconditioned unity is distributed across the three fundamental relations of representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A4","CH_BA94_SIST:A5","CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_SIST:A7","CH_BA94_SIST:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D2","text":"Rational psychology, rational cosmology, and transcendental theology are differentiated by the object-type posited by each idea rather than by empirically given domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_SIST:A7","CH_BA94_SIST:A8","CH_BA94_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D3","text":"The subjective deduction of transcendental ideas proceeds through the necessary synthetic use of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive inference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A9","CH_BA94_SIST:A10","CH_BA94_SIST:A11","CH_BA94_SIST:A12","CH_BA94_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D4","text":"The necessity of transcendental ideas belongs to reason’s demand for conditions and does not establish the existence or cognition of their corresponding objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_SIST:A14","CH_BA94_SIST:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D5","text":"Reason’s legitimate transcendental direction is upward toward conditions, whereas its movement toward consequences remains confined to logical ordering.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_SIST:A15","CH_BA94_SIST:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:D6","text":"The systematic unity of pure reason is a classification of its necessary ideas, not a completed proof that their objects form a knowable totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_SIST:A17","CH_BA94_SIST:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SD1","text":"Pure reason is constituted as a system-producing faculty whose necessity exceeds the limits of possible object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_SIST:A14","CH_BA94_SIST:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SD2","text":"Logical form supplies the generative grammar of transcendental ideas without supplying their objective warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A9","CH_BA94_SIST:A10","CH_BA94_SIST:A11","CH_BA94_SIST:A12","CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_SIST:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SD3","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is reclassified from inquiry into empirically available objects to inquiry into the necessary totalizing demands of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A1","CH_BA94_SIST:A4","CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_SIST:A7","CH_BA94_SIST:A8","CH_BA94_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SD4","text":"The system generates an asymmetry in which reason must posit an unconditioned ground for conditions while being barred from treating that ground as empirically knowable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_SIST:A3","CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_SIST:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SD5","text":"The enclosure of pure reason’s sphere simultaneously legitimates transcendental ideas as necessary products and suspends their conversion into speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A14","CH_BA94_SIST:A17","CH_BA94_SIST:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:DNX1","text":"Empirical cognition cannot supply its own absolute totality of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:DNX2","text":"The unconditioned cannot become an object of experience without ceasing to function as the unconditioned sought by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:DNX3","text":"The subjective necessity of reason cannot be equated with objective validity for objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:DNX4","text":"The order of grounds and conditions cannot be replaced by an equally necessary totality of effects and consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:DNX5","text":"The triadic system presupposes that every transcendental demand of reason can be exhaustively situated under subject, world, or supreme condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SNX1","text":"No speculative procedure can transform reason’s need for unconditioned unity into direct cognition of an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SNX2","text":"No established homology yet licenses the transfer of validity from logical syllogistic form to transcendental reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SNX3","text":"Freedom is excluded from the chapter’s operative triad of subject, world, and supreme being, although footnote 42 represents metaphysics as ordered around God, freedom, and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:SNX4","text":"The system cannot provide a final metaphysical object while preserving the distinction between an idea of reason and a concept of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS1","text":"The distinction between logical form and transcendental content isolates pure reason as the source of non-empirical ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS2","text":"The ascent from conditioned synthesis to unconditioned unity establishes the functional demand governing those ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS3","text":"The three relations of representation distribute that demand into subject, phenomenal series, and all objects of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS4","text":"The three syllogistic forms generate the corresponding ideas of soul, world, and being of all beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS5","text":"The lack of empirical objects blocks objective deduction while permitting a subjective deduction from reason’s own necessary operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:FGS6","text":"The restriction of transcendental use to ascent toward conditions excludes consequential totalities and encloses pure reason within a determinate system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF1","text":"Formal logical dialectic is separated from transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF2","text":"Reason’s ascent from conditioned synthesis is identified as the source of transcendental demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF3","text":"Relations of representation are reduced to a threefold classificatory matrix.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF4","text":"Unconditioned unity is distributed into the ideas of self, world, and supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF5","text":"Syllogistic forms provide a subjective genesis for the ideas while empirical objectivity is denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF6","text":"The asymmetry between conditions and consequences restricts legitimate transcendental use to ascent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:AF7","text":"The resulting ideas are unified as a closed system of pure reason whose logical-transcendental analogy remains provisional.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ME1","text":"The distinction between concepts of understanding and ideas of reason redraws the boundary between empirical cognition and rational totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ME2","text":"Psychology, cosmology, and theology are reclassified as the three structural destinations of reason’s demand for unconditioned unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ME3","text":"The contrast between ascending toward conditions and descending toward consequences separates necessary transcendental ideas from arbitrary rational fictions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ME4","text":"Logical syllogistic form becomes visible as a source of transcendental architecture without becoming evidence for transcendent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ME5","text":"Systematic unity becomes a feature of reason’s self-organization rather than a map of a directly knowable metaphysical domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter classifies transcendental ideas as ideas of soul, world, and supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ALT2","text":"Structural — The classification is generated by the relation between conditioned synthesis, unconditioned unity, and the three forms of syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument secures a legitimate field for rational ideas by deriving them subjectively while withholding objective deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The unconditioned functions as a required ground of conditions but cannot be treated as an empirically completed totality of objects or future consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from the distinction of dialectics to the systematic triad of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction depends on the irreducible distinction between subjective rational necessity and objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT3","text":"The architecture is internally coherent insofar as the same restriction that blocks objective deduction also explains the need for subjective deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it derives a determinate threefold classification from the relation between conditioned synthesis and unconditioned unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT5","text":"The system compresses rather than resolves the warrant for deriving transcendental structures from logical syllogistic forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT6","text":"The unresolved status of the logical-transcendental analogy prevents the formal derivation from functioning as an objective proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIST:MRT7","text":"Footnote 42 introduces a second metaphysical triad of God, freedom, and immortality that is not integrated with the chapter’s operative triad of soul, world, and supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIST:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 58","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 58","original_title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 58"},"original_title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book II—Of the Dialectical Procedure of Pure Reason","order":59,"parents":["RAW_BA94_TD_2"],"revision":"CH_BA94_TD_2@R001","sha256":"3b11b1980be2570e60a4f1411d5ba116cdfceb43ef834bc4a32f62ecf547a42d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS1","text":"Pure reason necessarily produces transcendental ideas whose objects cannot be adequately conceived through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS2","text":"The absence of adequate object-knowledge does not eliminate the ideas, because their reality as products of reason is procedurally necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS3","text":"Pure reason converts its necessary ideas into non-empirical syllogisms that infer from something known to something unknowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS4","text":"These inferences are sophistical in result but syllogistic in origin, since their illusion arises from reason’s own laws rather than from individual fabrication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS5","text":"The resulting dialectic divides into paralogism, antinomy, and the ideal of pure reason according to the unconditioned unity each argument seeks.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AS6","text":"Critical awareness can restrain error but cannot abolish the illusion generated by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A1","text":"Pure reason produces transcendental ideas according to its original laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A2","text":"An object adequate to a transcendental idea would have to be presentable and intuitable in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A3","text":"No object perfectly corresponding to a transcendental idea can be adequately conceived or known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A4","text":"The transcendental subjective reality of pure concepts of reason consists in their necessary generation by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A5","text":"Pure reason generates syllogisms containing no empirical premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A6","text":"These syllogisms infer from something known to something for which no adequate conception is available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A7","text":"Pure reason unavoidably attributes objective reality to the conclusions of these inferences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A8","text":"The arguments are sophistical in result but syllogistic in origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","text":"The dialectical illusion is produced by reason’s nature rather than by accidental fiction or individual error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A10","text":"The illusion cannot be eliminated, although its errors can be guarded against.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","text":"The paralogism infers the absolute unity of the subject from the transcendental conception of a subject containing no manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","text":"The antinomy concerns the absolute totality of the conditions of a phenomenon and infers one unconditioned unity from the self-contradiction of another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A13","text":"The ideal of pure reason infers the absolute synthetical unity of all conditions of things from the totality of conditions of thinking objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:A14","text":"The ideal of pure reason culminates in the representation of a being of all beings whose unconditioned necessity cannot be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX1","text":"The transcendental idea is not an empirically presented object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX2","text":"The object corresponding to the idea is not adequately conceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX3","text":"The idea does not yield knowledge of an object perfectly corresponding to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX4","text":"The dialectical conclusion is not thereby an ordinary piece of valid object-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX5","text":"The dialectical syllogisms are not grounded in empirical premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX6","text":"The dialectical arguments are not accidental fictions invented by individual thinkers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX7","text":"The illusion is not removable through superior intelligence or completed reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX8","text":"The absolute unity of the subject is not obtained as a concept through the transcendental conception of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX9","text":"The unconditioned synthetical unity of the series of conditions is not available as a coherent conception on the initially asserted side.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX10","text":"The being of all beings and its unconditioned necessity are not available through a transcendental conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:NX11","text":"The subjective reality of a rational idea is not identical with knowledge of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D1","text":"The necessity with which reason generates an idea does not establish the knowability of the idea’s object.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A1","CH_BA94_TD_2:A2","CH_BA94_TD_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D2","text":"Rational necessity can produce an unavoidable representation without producing an adequate object-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A3","CH_BA94_TD_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D3","text":"The absence of empirical premises does not prevent pure reason from generating an objective-reality attribution.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A5","CH_BA94_TD_2:A6","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D4","text":"The same reasoning process can possess legitimate syllogistic form at its origin and sophistical force in its conclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A8","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D5","text":"Dialectical error is structural rather than merely psychological because it follows from the operation of pure reason itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A8","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D6","text":"Critique can suspend assent to dialectical conclusions without eliminating the conditions that generate them.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D7","text":"The three dialectical forms are differentiated by the object of unconditioned unity that each constructs.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","CH_BA94_TD_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D8","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and the ideal of pure reason are distinct applications of one procedure: extending a necessary rational structure beyond possible cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A3","CH_BA94_TD_2:A6","CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","CH_BA94_TD_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:D9","text":"The progression from subject, through conditioned series, to totality of beings increases the scope of the unconditioned claim while preserving the same epistemic failure.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","CH_BA94_TD_2:A13","CH_BA94_TD_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD1","text":"Pure reason is simultaneously the source of necessary rational organization and the source of unavoidable overreach beyond possible knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A1","CH_BA94_TD_2:A4","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD2","text":"The objective appearance of metaphysical objects is generated by the formal demand for unconditioned unity rather than by access to an independently given object.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A2","CH_BA94_TD_2:A5","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD3","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and the ideal of pure reason form a systematic taxonomy of reason’s attempts to totalize the subject, conditions, and beings.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","CH_BA94_TD_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD4","text":"The epistemic boundary imposed by possible experience functions not only as a restriction but also as the condition for identifying dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A2","CH_BA94_TD_2:A3","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD5","text":"Wisdom changes the practical relation to dialectical illusion without changing its generative existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SD6","text":"The system preserves a structural tension between the necessary legitimacy of reason’s procedure and the sophistical character of its result without fully specifying the rule that separates valid extension from dialectical overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A8","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX1","text":"The necessity of a representation does not guarantee the existence of a correspondingly knowable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX2","text":"Pure reason is not transparent to itself in its own operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX3","text":"Empirical verification cannot adjudicate the origin of these ideas because their generating syllogisms contain no empirical premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX4","text":"Dialectical illusion cannot be reduced to defective education, individual carelessness, or intellectual weakness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX5","text":"The validity of a reasoning form cannot be inferred solely from the necessity with which reason produces it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX6","text":"The unconditioned is not a hidden empirical object waiting to be discovered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX7","text":"The three metaphysical targets do not become knowable merely because reason can organize them as totalities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX8","text":"The removal of error does not entail the removal of the conditions that make error possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX1","text":"No final object corresponding to reason’s totalizing demand is available within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX2","text":"No external standpoint wholly outside pure reason is available from which its dialectical tendency could be permanently abolished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX3","text":"No simple opposition between valid reasoning and irrational illusion can organize the system, because the illusion originates in reason’s own syllogistic activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX4","text":"The unconditioned remains operative as a demand even where it is unavailable as a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX5","text":"The absence of a knowable unconditioned unity is the concealed condition underlying the production of all three dialectical forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX6","text":"Pure reason cannot attain the object it posits, but it cannot cease positing the demand for such an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS1","text":"Original laws of pure reason generate a transcendental idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS2","text":"The transcendental idea exceeds the conditions of presentation and intuition in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS3","text":"The absence of possible intuition prevents adequate object-knowledge while leaving a problematic conception available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS4","text":"Pure reason constructs a syllogism without empirical premises that moves from something known toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS5","text":"The syllogism attributes objective reality to what cannot be adequately conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS6","text":"The resulting inference is classified as sophistical in conclusion but necessary in origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS7","text":"The same mechanism differentiates into subject-unity, series-unity, and totality-of-beings forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS8","text":"Critical reflection blocks the error of assent while leaving the underlying illusion operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF1","text":"Necessary production of transcendental ideas → absence of an adequately conceivable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF2","text":"Absence of adequate object-knowledge → reliance on non-empirical syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF3","text":"Non-empirical inference → attribution of objective reality to the unknowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF4","text":"Objective-reality attribution → dialectical sophism generated by pure reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF5","text":"Common dialectical mechanism → paralogism, antinomy, and the ideal of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:AF6","text":"Recognition of structural illusion → error-control without elimination of illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME1","text":"The object of critique shifts from isolated false propositions to the procedure by which pure reason generates them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME2","text":"Subjective transcendental reality is separated from objective knowledge of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME3","text":"Metaphysical errors are reclassified from accidental inventions to necessary products of rational structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME4","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and the ideal cease to appear as unrelated disputes and become forms of one totalizing operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME5","text":"The opposition between illusion and reason collapses into a model in which reason itself generates unavoidable illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME6","text":"Critique is redefined from eradication of dialectic to regulation of its conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ME7","text":"The boundary of possible experience becomes a diagnostic instrument for distinguishing problematic thought from knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure reason produces three dialectical arguments concerning the subject, the totality of conditions, and the being of all beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Non-empirical syllogistic reasoning converts the demand for unconditioned unity into objective-reality claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The proper response to dialectic is to prevent error while accepting that the generative illusion cannot be removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Possible experience defines the limit of knowability, while the demand for an unconditioned totality remains continuously active beyond that limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s central mechanism from necessary idea-production to unavoidable dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between subjective transcendental reality, objective-reality attribution, and actual knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT3","text":"The classification of paralogism, antinomy, and ideal is recoverable from the three forms of unconditioned unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT4","text":"The strongest point is the linkage between reason’s intrinsic necessity and the non-accidental character of its error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT5","text":"The relation between “no conception” and “problematical conception” remains compressed and depends on distinguishing inadequate object-conception from merely problematic thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT6","text":"The tension between syllogistic origin and sophistical result is asserted clearly but not fully resolved by a formal criterion of valid versus dialectical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT7","text":"The antinomy’s “opposite unity” remains directionally specified but structurally underdeveloped in this passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT8","text":"The extracted system preserves the real movement from necessary rational generation through objective illusion to limited critical containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 59","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book II—Of the Dialectical Procedure of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 59","original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 59"},"original_title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","order":60,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CIPP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CIPP@R001","sha256":"a8da8423e7bd913db35f3eccc07e004b073ae77689e424895404572a48ca3576","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS1","text":"The inquiry tests whether pure self-consciousness can yield a priori knowledge of the soul as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS2","text":"Pure self-consciousness supplies only the formal unity of thought, not an intuition determining the subject’s mode of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS3","text":"The argument is a transcendental diagnosis of an unavoidable illusion produced by valid logical forms used beyond their objective conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS4","text":"Rational psychology projects the categories onto “I think” and converts logical subjecthood, simplicity, identity, and distinction into metaphysical properties of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS5","text":"The conversion fails because it equivocates between the subject of thought and an object given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS6","text":"Rational psychology therefore survives only as a critical discipline limiting both materialist and spiritualist dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AS7","text":"The failure of speculative self-knowledge displaces claims concerning future existence from theoretical proof to practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A1","text":"A transcendental paralogism can preserve valid logical form while deriving a false conclusion from a necessary propensity of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A2","text":"A pure rational psychology must exclude every empirical determination and therefore has only “I think” as its generative text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","text":"“I think” is the formal accompaniment and vehicle of all concepts rather than a determinate concept of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A4","text":"Pure thought represents the thinking subject only as a transcendental subject = x, cognizable solely through its thoughts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","text":"Objective cognition requires a given intuition determined through concepts under the unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","text":"Categories acquire objective reality only in relation to sensuous intuition and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A7","text":"Self-cognition as an object requires intuition of the determinable self rather than consciousness of the determining function of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A8","text":"The modes of pure self-consciousness are logical functions and do not themselves determine an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","text":"Internal intuition is sensuous and temporal and presents the self only through phenomenal data.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A10","text":"The objective application of the category of substance requires a permanent intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A11","text":"Internal intuition supplies no permanent presentation corresponding to the thinking Ego.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A12","text":"Abstraction from empirical conditions does not establish existence independently of those conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A13","text":"The existential occurrence of “I think” requires empirical material even though its Ego-representation is purely intellectual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A14","text":"The subject cannot cognize itself through categories whose employment already presupposes its unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A15","text":"A simple being may possess intensive magnitude capable of continuous diminution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A16","text":"Mere non-contradiction cannot privilege either of two opposed speculative hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A17","text":"Practical warrant extending beyond experience does not constitute theoretical cognition of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:A18","text":"Difference between internal and external appearances does not establish heterogeneity between their grounds as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX1","text":"A transcendental paralogism is not merely an argument defective in logical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX2","text":"“I think” is not a determinate transcendental concept or an intuition of the thinking subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX3","text":"Pure rational psychology is not empirical psychology based on observations of internal states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX4","text":"Necessary use of the Ego as the subject of judgment is not proof that the Ego exists as substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX5","text":"Logical simplicity of apperception is not proof of a simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX6","text":"Analytical identity of consciousness across representations is not proof of personal identity through changing states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX7","text":"Distinguishing the self from external objects is not proof that the self can exist without body or external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX8","text":"“I think” is not a syllogistic inference from thought to existence or a universal assertion that every thinking being exists necessarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX9","text":"Mere thought presents the self neither as it is in itself nor as it appears in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX10","text":"Possible abstraction from empirical existence is not consciousness of a possible separate existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX11","text":"Simplicity does not entail incorruptibility, permanence, or immunity from extinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX12","text":"Freedom from contradiction is not objective knowledge that a proposed mode of existence is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX13","text":"Rational psychology is not a positive doctrine that adds knowledge of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:NX14","text":"Critical restriction neither establishes soulless materialism nor refutes the practical admission of future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D1","text":"The exclusion of empirical predicates leaves rational psychology to generate its system by projecting the category table onto formal apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A2","CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D2","text":"Logical subjecthood, simplicity, identity, and distinction yield no corresponding determinations of substance, simple substance, personality, or separability.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D3","text":"The foundational syllogism of rational psychology commits a sophisma figurae dictionis by shifting from an object that can exist only as subject to the Ego’s merely formal position as subject of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A1","CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D4","text":"The category of substance cannot determine the thinking self because the permanent intuition required for its objective application is absent from inner sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A10","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D5","text":"Immateriality, incorruptibility, personality, spirituality, and immortality lose their foundation because they are combinations downstream from the invalid substantiation of the Ego.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A2","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8","CH_BA94_CIPP:A10","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D6","text":"Mendelssohn’s proof of permanence fails because exclusion of decomposition does not exclude extinction through infinitely diminishing degrees of reality and consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D7","text":"Speculation licensed only by non-contradiction admits contrary constructions with equal right and therefore cannot decide between rationalist and materialist hypotheses.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D8","text":"Treating the thinking substance as temporally determinable without external objects makes external existence gratuitous and renders problematic idealism unavoidable within rational psychology.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D9","text":"The real unity of apperception prevents a spatially reductive definition of the thinking subject without establishing that subject as a spiritual substance.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8","CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D10","text":"Self-objectification through the categories is circular because the unity to be explained is already presupposed in every categorical judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A4","CH_BA94_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D11","text":"Rational psychology can function only as a discipline that prohibits unsupported determinations of the soul.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A2","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D12","text":"The demonstrated limits of speculative reason impose the same renunciation on dogmatic denials as on dogmatic affirmations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D13","text":"The failure of speculative proof leaves intact the claimed practical necessity of admitting an existence extending beyond present life.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D14","text":"Reclassifying space and time as forms of appearance removes the presupposed proof that soul and body are internally heterogeneous while leaving their ultimate community unknowable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:D15","text":"Moral consciousness may disclose practical spontaneity without supplying the theoretical predicates required for noumenal self-knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD1","text":"Rational psychology is generated by converting a condition of possible experience into a metaphysical property of an object purportedly existing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8","CH_BA94_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD2","text":"The attribution of substance is the load-bearing error from which the remaining ontology of the soul is produced by combinatory amplification.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A2","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A8","CH_BA94_CIPP:A10","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD3","text":"The thinking subject is transcendentally indispensable as a condition of cognition and theoretically indeterminate as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A4","CH_BA94_CIPP:A7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD4","text":"A successful rational psychology would license synthetic a priori knowledge of things in themselves and thereby dissolve the critical restriction of knowledge to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD5","text":"Speculative underdetermination does not make arbitrary hypotheses cognitively available, because objective assertion remains constrained by the conditions of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD6","text":"The subject grounding the representation of time cannot derive its own substantial persistence in time from that grounding function.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SD7","text":"Critical restriction removes both speculative proof and speculative disproof, thereby preserving a distinct domain for practical commitment without converting it into knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A16","CH_BA94_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX1","text":"Conditions under which an object is represented cannot be transferred without mediation into properties of the represented object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX2","text":"Self-consciousness cannot be treated as immediate self-intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX3","text":"Formal continuity of consciousness cannot guarantee the diachronic persistence of a substantial person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX4","text":"Removing empirical conditions from a representation cannot remove those conditions from the represented being’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX5","text":"The thinking subject cannot occupy an unconditioned standpoint from which it cognizes the ground of its own cognitive functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX6","text":"Logical thinkability cannot exhaust the conditions of real possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX7","text":"Failure to establish one metaphysical account cannot establish its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX8","text":"Practical necessity cannot be converted into theoretical determination of the soul’s constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX1","text":"An immediately self-present and theoretically transparent substantial Ego is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX2","text":"The noumenal constitution of the thinking subject is excluded from speculative adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX3","text":"Neither immortality nor extinction beyond life is available as an object of dogmatic theoretical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX4","text":"The subject cannot complete theoretical reason by turning the presupposed unity of reason into its own self-grounding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS1","text":"The purity requirement removes all empirical predicates and leaves “I think” as the sole datum of rational psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS2","text":"The category table is projected onto that datum to generate substance, simplicity, identity, and external relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS3","text":"Combinations of those projected determinations generate immateriality, incorruptibility, personality, spirituality, communion with bodies, and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS4","text":"Critical analysis separates the determining unity of consciousness from the determinable self that alone could be given as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS5","text":"Each metaphysical predicate is tested against its required intuition and fails because pure apperception supplies no corresponding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS6","text":"The central paralogism is exposed as an equivocation between existence only as subject and use only as the subject of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS7","text":"Auxiliary proofs are neutralized by intensive diminution, contrary possibilities, and the absence of permanent inner intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS8","text":"The failed doctrine is converted into a reciprocal prohibition against materialist and spiritualist dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS9","text":"Claims extending beyond life are relocated from speculative cognition to practical orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:AF1","text":"Transcendental illusion → empirical exclusion → “I think” as sole datum → categorical construction of the soul → distinction between logical function and object-determination → exposure of equivocation → collapse of speculative soul-ontology → reciprocal dogmatic restraint → practical relocation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME1","text":"Error is reclassified from defective logical form to an unavoidable transcendental misuse of valid form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME2","text":"The Ego is reclassified from a cognized substance to the formal vehicle of cognition and an indeterminate transcendental subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME3","text":"Substance, simplicity, personality, and separability are reclassified as illicit metaphysical expansions of logical functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME4","text":"Materialism and spiritualism cease to be exhaustive theoretical alternatives and become equally unsupported determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME5","text":"Rational psychology is reclassified from a positive science into a boundary-discipline of speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME6","text":"Immortality is displaced from demonstrable soul-property to an object of practical reason protected from speculative disproof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ME7","text":"The soul–body problem is redrawn from a presumed interaction between heterogeneous substances to an unknowable relation beneath differently structured appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument tests the derivation of substance, simplicity, personality, separability, permanence, and immortality from “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The argument identifies a categorical transfer from logical self-consciousness to metaphysical object-determination without mediating intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The critique converts failed speculative doctrine into symmetrical limits on opposed dogmatisms and redirects reason toward practical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The self is necessary as the unity of cognition, given empirically only through temporal inner sense, and indeterminate regarding its noumenal constitution and persistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from pure self-consciousness through categorical projection to critical limitation and practical relocation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT2","text":"The exact correspondence between every category and every traditional predicate of the soul remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if objective cognition requires intuition and the categories are restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT4","text":"The argument is structurally strongest in its distinction between determining consciousness and the determinable self and in its diagnosis of the syllogistic equivocation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT5","text":"The proof depends on results imported from the Analytic concerning categories, permanence, noumena, and the limits of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT6","text":"The status of “I think” shifts among pure apperception, empirical existential proposition, and undetermined perception; the distinction between intellectual form and empirical occurrence manages but does not eliminate this ambiguity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT7","text":"The use of the real simplicity of apperception against materialist definition approaches the ontological use of simplicity rejected elsewhere, although it is restricted to a negative conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT8","text":"The practical admission of future life depends on moral and teleological premises not established within this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the text’s actual transition from transcendental diagnosis to disciplinary closure rather than reducing it to a denial of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 60","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 60","original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 60"},"original_title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","order":61,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CIAP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CIAP@R001","sha256":"b08f5153f4d5c130fd9b3a01302862b5180e9f933e8adec3fadf0d29ac98e1f7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS1","text":"Transcendental illusion is generated by dialectical arguments whose forms correspond to the formal species of syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS2","text":"The dialectical sequence progresses from the unconditioned unity of the thinking subject to the unconditioned unity of phenomenal conditions and then to the conditions of possible objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS3","text":"Unlike the paralogism, which produces only a partial illusion, the application of reason to phenomenal synthesis produces an apparently valid principle that turns into contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS4","text":"The antinomy is a natural and unavoidable conflict of reason rather than an error introduced by sophistical reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS5","text":"The conflict forces either skeptical renunciation or dogmatic insistence, and both responses destroy sound philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AS6","text":"The proper method is to treat cosmical conceptions as ideas generated by reason but irreconcilable with phenomena and experience, rather than as valid cosmological doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A1","text":"Transcendental illusion of pure reason arises from dialectical arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A2","text":"The formal species of syllogism provide the logical schemas for the principal dialectical arguments of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A3","text":"The first dialectical argument concerns the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A4","text":"The second dialectical argument concerns the unconditioned unity of objective conditions in phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A5","text":"The third dialectical argument concerns the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A6","text":"The paralogism concerning the subject of thought produces only a partial illusion and does not establish its contrary proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A7","text":"The theory of pneumatism gains the argumentative advantage in the paralogism but ultimately fails before pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","text":"Reason applies a principle of unconditioned unity to the objective synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","text":"The application of unconditioned unity to phenomenal synthesis generates contradictions within reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","text":"The antinomy arises naturally and unavoidably from the operation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A11","text":"The antinomy prevents both one-sided conviction and stable philosophical resolution through unqualified assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A12","text":"Cosmical conceptions concern the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A13","text":"The ideal of pure reason concerns the absolute totality of the conditions of all possible things and is distinct from, though related to, the cosmical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","text":"The antinomy supplies principles for a pretended pure rational cosmology rather than a valid cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:A15","text":"Cosmical conceptions must be presented as ideas irreconcilable with phenomena and experience rather than appropriated as valid objects of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX1","text":"The antinomy is not a merely sophistical conflict introduced by subtle argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX2","text":"The cosmological illusion is not merely a one-sided illusion of the kind produced by the paralogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX3","text":"The unconditioned unity of phenomenal conditions is not secured by the plausibility of the principle that generates it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX4","text":"The antinomy is not resolved by granting one side of the conflict exclusive authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX5","text":"Skeptical surrender is not a sound philosophical resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX6","text":"Dogmatic confidence and obstinate persistence are not sound philosophical resolutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX7","text":"The cosmical conception is not identical with the ideal of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX8","text":"Rational cosmology is not to be declared valid or appropriated as doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:NX9","text":"Absolute totality in the synthesis of phenomena is not reconciled with phenomena and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D1","text":"The formal structure of syllogistic reasoning determines the ordered taxonomy of transcendental illusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A1","CH_BA94_CIAP:A2","CH_BA94_CIAP:A3","CH_BA94_CIAP:A4","CH_BA94_CIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D2","text":"The movement from subjective conditions to phenomenal conditions marks a transition from dialectical psychology to dialectical cosmology.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A3","CH_BA94_CIAP:A4","CH_BA94_CIAP:A6","CH_BA94_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D3","text":"The cosmological case is more destabilizing than the paralogical case because the principle of unity appears plausible before producing contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A6","CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D4","text":"The natural origin of the antinomy prevents its dismissal as a contingent defect in reasoning.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D5","text":"The antinomy exposes a conflict between reason’s demand for unconditioned unity and the conditions of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D6","text":"The two immediate responses to antinomy convert a cognitive conflict into either renunciation of reason or illegitimate closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","CH_BA94_CIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D7","text":"The distinction between cosmical conception and ideal of pure reason prevents different forms of totality from being treated as one object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A12","CH_BA94_CIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:D8","text":"The proper result of the cosmological analysis is a restriction of doctrinal validity rather than the elimination of the idea that generates the conflict.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD1","text":"Reason’s demand for unconditioned unity is both the generative source of cosmological thought and the source of its self-contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A1","CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD2","text":"The contradiction functions diagnostically by revealing that the totality demanded by reason cannot be straightforwardly converted into an object within phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","CH_BA94_CIAP:A12","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD3","text":"The text relocates cosmology from the level of doctrine to the level of critical analysis of reason’s totalizing activity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A11","CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD4","text":"The same rational architecture that organizes valid inference also produces transcendental illusion when its demand for totality is extended to phenomenal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A1","CH_BA94_CIAP:A2","CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD5","text":"The argument preserves the necessity of reason’s demand for unity while denying that this demand yields a coherent phenomenal world-whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A12","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SD6","text":"Sound philosophy requires a position that neither abandons reason in skepticism nor satisfies its demand through dogmatic assertion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","CH_BA94_CIAP:A11","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX1","text":"The completed totality of phenomenal conditions is not assumed to be directly available as an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX2","text":"The formal schema of reasoning is not assumed to guarantee legitimate ontological reference for the ideas generated through it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX3","text":"Contradiction in cosmology is not treated as an accidental defect removable by improved sophistical technique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX4","text":"No single cosmological assertion is permitted to suppress the opposing assertion merely by persistence or confidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX5","text":"The analogy among dialectical forms does not entail equal validity for the objects or totalities associated with them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX6","text":"The demand for absolute totality does not entail the existence of a corresponding phenomenal totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX7","text":"Pure reason is not permitted to transform its regulative demand for unity into an experience-grounded cosmological possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SNX1","text":"A fully experience-compatible world-whole is structurally unavailable to the cosmological use of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SNX2","text":"A final reconciliation between absolute totality and phenomenal experience is excluded within the disputed cosmological framework.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SNX3","text":"A stable doctrinal settlement of the antinomy is foreclosed by the internal conflict of reason’s own principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:SNX4","text":"The possibility that reason could transparently master the totality it demands is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS1","text":"Logical forms of judgment and syllogism → schemas of transcendental reasoning → dialectical production of totalizing ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A1","CH_BA94_CIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS2","text":"Subjective conditions of representation → unconditioned unity of the subject → paralogism → partial illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A3","CH_BA94_CIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS3","text":"Objective conditions in phenomena → unconditioned unity of phenomenal synthesis → cosmological principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A4","CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS4","text":"Plausible cosmological unity → mutually conflicting consequences → natural antinomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A8","CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS5","text":"Natural antinomy → skeptical renunciation or dogmatic persistence → destruction of sound philosophy.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","CH_BA94_CIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS6","text":"Cosmical conception distinguished from the ideal of pure reason → cosmological totality restricted to an idea → refusal of doctrinal appropriation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A12","CH_BA94_CIAP:A13","CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:FGS7","text":"Conflict of pure reason → exposure of non-reconciliation with phenomena and experience → critical presentation without cosmological validation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","CH_BA94_CIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AF1","text":"Dialectical form → transcendental illusion → ordered domains of totality → plausible unconditioned unity → unavoidable contradiction → skepticism or dogmatism → critical non-appropriation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:AF2","text":"Partial paralogical illusion → stronger cosmological antinomy → failure of both unilateral alternatives → cosmical conception retained as irreconcilable idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME1","text":"Antinomy is reclassified from an accidental argumentative failure into a necessary structural effect of reason’s operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME2","text":"Contradiction becomes evidence about the limits of cosmological cognition rather than merely an obstacle to be overcome.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME3","text":"Subjective dialectic and objective cosmological dialectic are separated by the different strength and structure of their illusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME4","text":"The world-whole is distinguished from the totality of conditions for all possible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME5","text":"Rational cosmology is moved from the status of asserted science to the status of a critical object generated by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ME6","text":"The opposition between skepticism and dogmatism is collapsed as a false alternative for dealing with antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Reason’s synthesis of objective phenomenal conditions produces contradictory cosmological claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Syllogistic forms organize a hierarchy of totalizing demands concerning the subject, phenomena, and possible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The proper response to contradiction is neither to select a side nor to abandon reason, but to diagnose the status of the idea producing the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological — Absolute totality belongs to reason’s conception of conditions but cannot be reconciled as a phenomenal object with experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from dialectical form through cosmological antinomy to critical restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive distinction between the partial illusion of the paralogism and the contradictory structure of the antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent as a diagnosis of reason’s totalizing demand and its phenomenal limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the concrete antinomies and the subsequent positive resolution of the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT5","text":"Its strongest point is the rejection of both skeptical surrender and dogmatic closure as responses to necessary contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT6","text":"Its central unresolved tension lies between the necessity of the demand for unconditioned unity and the impossibility of satisfying that demand within phenomenal experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction depends on retaining the formal analogy between syllogistic species and dialectical ideas without treating that analogy as proof of cosmological validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 61","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 61","original_title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 61"},"original_title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","order":62,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SISC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SISC@R001","sha256":"65077b5e1c817ad397dd9c26e9b64f4c000490e069af9293748096b91a4d7a99","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS1","text":"The text seeks a systematic principle for enumerating transcendental ideas rather than treating them as an unstructured set of metaphysical questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS2","text":"It grounds transcendental ideas in categories supplied by understanding and transformed by reason through the demand for unconditioned completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS3","text":"It restricts legitimate cosmological ideas to categorial syntheses that form subordinated serial conditions for a given conditioned phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS4","text":"It reorients rational inquiry from consequences to antecedent conditions because only regressive synthesis is necessary for explaining what is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS5","text":"It derives four cosmological ideas by applying regressive totalization to composition, division, causality, and existential dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AS6","text":"It suspends the empirical realizability of absolute totality while distinguishing two possible forms of the unconditioned: an infinite whole of conditioned members or a first unconditioned member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A1","text":"Understanding originates pure concepts, while reason does not originate concepts but releases concepts from the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A2","text":"Reason demands absolute totality of conditions whenever a conditioned is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A3","text":"A category becomes a transcendental idea when reason extends its conditioned synthesis toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A4","text":"Only a synthesis ordered as a series of subordinated conditions can support a transcendental idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A5","text":"Reason necessarily demands completeness only in the ascending regress from a conditioned to its antecedent conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A6","text":"The progressive series of consequences is not required for the possibility or explanation of a given conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A7","text":"Cosmological ideas concern the totality of conditions for phenomena and do not concern a totality of things independently of phenomenal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A8","text":"Time supplies a genuine antecedent series because a present moment is conditioned by preceding time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A9","text":"Space is an aggregate of coexisting parts, but its apprehension and limitation require a successive synthesis that permits regressive totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A10","text":"Matter supplies a regressive series because its parts condition the given real whole and each part can be further divided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A11","text":"Causality supplies a regressive series because an effect is conditioned by causes antecedent to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A12","text":"Contingent existence supplies a regressive series because it requires a condition through which it becomes necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A13","text":"The four admissible cosmological ideas concern absolute completeness in composition, division, origination, and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A14","text":"Absolute totality is an idea whose possibility in phenomenal synthesis remains unestablished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A15","text":"The unconditioned can be represented either as the complete infinite series of conditioned members or as an unconditioned first member within the series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A16","text":"World denotes the mathematical totality of phenomena, while nature denotes their dynamical unity under internal causal connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A17","text":"Cosmological ideas remain directed to phenomena while extending synthesis beyond every possible empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:A18","text":"Mathematical cosmological ideas concern composition and division, while dynamical cosmological ideas concern origination and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX1","text":"Reason is not an independent source of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX2","text":"A transcendental idea is not a concept detached from categories or from empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX3","text":"A category does not generate a cosmological idea merely by being a category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX4","text":"Co-ordinated aggregates do not constitute the serial subordination required for rational regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX5","text":"The descending series of consequences does not impose a necessary demand for absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX6","text":"Substance and accident do not form the required series of conditions because accidents are modes of substance rather than antecedent conditions of it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX7","text":"Substances in reciprocal community do not form a cosmological series because they are aggregated rather than subordinated conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX8","text":"Possibility, actuality, and necessity do not independently yield cosmological ideas unless contingency introduces a conditioned existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX9","text":"Cosmological totality is not a totality of noumenal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX10","text":"Absolute completeness is not established as an empirically realizable synthesis merely because reason necessarily thinks it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX11","text":"Infinite regress is not equivalent to a completed empirical traversal of infinitely many conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:NX12","text":"Nature is not merely a spatial-temporal aggregate when it is considered as a causal whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D1","text":"The systematic table of cosmological ideas must be derived from the categorial table by selecting only categories capable of expressing regressive serial conditioning.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A3","CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SISC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D2","text":"Rational completeness concerns explanatory grounds rather than an exhaustive prediction or enumeration of future consequences.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D3","text":"The totality of past time is necessarily thought as conditioning the present, whereas the termination or infinitude of future time is indifferent to the present’s possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A6","CH_BA94_SISC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D4","text":"Spatial magnitude becomes a cosmological problem only through the successive synthesis of delimitation, not through space considered merely as a simultaneous aggregate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D5","text":"Division of matter directs reason toward the simple because exhaustive division is the only route by which the conditionality of material parts could terminate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D6","text":"Causal inquiry directs reason from a given effect toward an antecedent ground capable of terminating the regress in causation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D7","text":"Inquiry into contingent existence directs reason toward an unconditioned necessity because contingency cannot provide its own sufficient condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D8","text":"The four cosmological ideas divide into two mathematical questions concerning phenomenal quantity and two dynamical questions concerning phenomenal existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SISC:A16","CH_BA94_SISC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D9","text":"The first possible form of the unconditioned permits a series without a first member while requiring that the entire series be thought as complete.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A14","CH_BA94_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:D10","text":"The second possible form of the unconditioned yields the concepts of a temporal beginning, spatial limit, simple part, spontaneous cause, and absolutely necessary existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD1","text":"Cosmological reason transforms ordinary categorial determination into a demand for a total explanatory ground that no finite empirical inquiry can guarantee.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A1","CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:A14","CH_BA94_SISC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD2","text":"The system converts metaphysical questions about the world into questions about the form and possible completion of regressive phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A7","CH_BA94_SISC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD3","text":"The unconditioned is structurally required as the endpoint of explanation even when its instantiation within experience cannot be known.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD4","text":"The four cosmological problems are generated not by four independently discovered objects but by four admissible modes through which conditioned phenomena can be regressed toward completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SISC:A10","CH_BA94_SISC:A11","CH_BA94_SISC:A12","CH_BA94_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD5","text":"The mathematical-dynamical division separates questions concerning the completeness of phenomenal magnitude from questions concerning the completeness of phenomenal causal existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SISC:A16","CH_BA94_SISC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SD6","text":"Reason is internally driven beyond experience without being licensed to leave the domain of phenomena for noumenal objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A1","CH_BA94_SISC:A7","CH_BA94_SISC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the denial that a conditioned phenomenon can be fully intelligible through its immediately given state alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX2","text":"The system depends on the denial that explanation can terminate arbitrarily within a series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that coexisting elements are explanatory grounds of one another merely because they belong to one whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a symmetrical treatment of antecedents and consequences because only antecedents are constitutive conditions of what is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes direct intellectual access to an unconditioned object outside phenomenal conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the inference that rational necessity establishes empirical existence or empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:DNX7","text":"The system excludes any cosmology not mediated by the categorial forms of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the unavailable standpoint from which an entire phenomenal series could be empirically given as absolutely complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SNX2","text":"The argument presupposes that the unconditioned is necessary for reason but unavailable as an object of completed experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SNX3","text":"The argument forecloses a final metaphysical inventory of reality by relocating cosmological totality into the problematic form of rational idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:SNX4","text":"The argument withholds any guarantee that the rational demand for completion corresponds to a structure actually possessed by phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS1","text":"A conditioned phenomenon is given, and reason applies the demand that its complete set of antecedent conditions be thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS2","text":"A category is eligible for transcendental elevation only if its synthesis orders conditions in a subordinated series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS3","text":"The eligible series is taken regressively from the given conditioned toward increasingly remote antecedent conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS4","text":"The regressive demand produces an idea of absolute totality rather than an established object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS5","text":"The idea of totality yields two alternative representations of the unconditioned: an unlimited complete whole or a first unconditioned condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS6","text":"The serial filter yields four cosmological problem-fields: composition, division, origination, and existential dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:FGS7","text":"The resulting cosmological ideas remain phenomenal in reference while exceeding every empirically completable synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the need for a principle of enumeration to the distinction between understanding’s concepts and reason’s totalizing demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF2","text":"The distinction between concepts and ideas leads to the criterion of serially subordinated conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF3","text":"The serial criterion leads to the privileging of regressus over progressus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF4","text":"Regressive synthesis is differentiated across time, space, matter, causality, and contingent existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF5","text":"The differentiation excludes nonserial categories and yields four cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF6","text":"The four ideas are reframed as problematic demands for an unconditioned whose phenomenal realizability remains undecided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:AF7","text":"The argument terminates by dividing the resulting ideas into mathematical and dynamical classes within the world of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME1","text":"Metaphysical totality is reclassified from a property immediately attributable to the world into a rational demand generated by conditioned experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME2","text":"The distinction between regressus and progressus redraws explanation as dependence on antecedent grounds rather than extension toward consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME3","text":"Categories are divided into those that can generate cosmological problems and those that cannot because they lack serial subordination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME4","text":"Space is made available for cosmological regression despite not being intrinsically temporal or serial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME5","text":"Infinity is reclassified as the possible form of a complete idea rather than as a completed sequence empirically traversed by cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME6","text":"The opposition between finite first condition and infinite regress becomes internal to the representation of the unconditioned rather than an immediate dispute about empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME7","text":"World and nature are separated into mathematical totality and dynamical causal unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ME8","text":"Cosmological inquiry is confined to phenomenal conditions while being recognized as necessarily trans-empirical in its demand for completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text enumerates four cosmological ideas concerning world magnitude, material division, causal origin, and necessary dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The enumeration is generated by a filter that admits only categories expressing a regressively ordered series of subordinate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text converts speculative questions into an investigation of the rational procedure that produces them and of the limits governing that procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomenal reality is treated as conditionally ordered in time, spatial delimitation, material parts, causation, and existence, while absolute completion remains only ideationally available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Reason necessarily represents the unconditioned as required for explanatory completeness but cannot establish whether corresponding phenomenal totality is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the text’s derivation of cosmological ideas from categories through the demand for regressive totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the conditions under which an idea of totality could correspond to an actually possible phenomenal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it distinguishes the rational requirement for totality from empirical proof that totality is realizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it derives the admissible cosmological questions from the formal distinction between serial subordination and mere aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT5","text":"The system relies on an unresolved tension between the claim that the whole series of conditions is required as given and the claim that completion of phenomenal regress may be impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT6","text":"The system also retains tension between space as a nonserial aggregate and spatial limitation as a regressively serial synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT7","text":"The system preserves a further tension between ideas that remain restricted to phenomena and their necessary extension beyond all possible empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SISC:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the four cosmological ideas are treated as procedural products of reason rather than as independently given metaphysical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 62","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 62","original_title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 62"},"original_title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","order":63,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIAP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIAP@R001","sha256":"436a72a966e221cea136f0d9d6b17514462272edd069a874bb3fe2789d1072d0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS1","text":"Pure reason necessarily generates contradictory cosmological claims when it extends the demand for completeness beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS2","text":"Transcendental antithetic treats these oppositions as reason’s self-contradiction rather than as an external contest between unrelated dogmas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS3","text":"The contradiction arises because reason demands absolute unity while understanding permits only synthesis governed by the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS4","text":"This conflict recurs as opposed determinations of cosmical magnitude, composition, causality, and necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS5","text":"Because neither experience nor a priori intuition can adjudicate transcendent claims, unrestricted conflict becomes the diagnostic criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AS6","text":"The argument moves from necessary cosmological question to paired demonstration, from paired demonstration to rational deadlock, and from deadlock to the search for a concealed misunderstanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A1","text":"The objective employment of understanding is restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","text":"Pure reason necessarily demands absolute unity and completeness for every conditioned synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A3","text":"Cosmological synthesis must conform to the rules of understanding while answering reason’s demand for totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A4","text":"A synthesis adequate to reason is too great for understanding, while a synthesis adequate to understanding is too small for reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A5","text":"Extending reason beyond possible experience generates transcendental cosmological propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A6","text":"The questions answered by dialectical propositions are necessarily encountered by human reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","text":"Each side of a transcendental conflict is internally consistent and possesses grounds of necessity in reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","text":"The contrary side possesses grounds that are equally valid and necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","text":"Transcendental illusion is natural, unavoidable, and recurrent even after its deception has been exposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A10","text":"Transcendental propositions admit neither empirical confirmation or refutation nor exhibition in a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A11","text":"Free and unrestricted conflict is transcendental reason’s only available criterion for locating the source of disagreement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A12","text":"The sceptical method seeks certainty by diagnosing misunderstanding rather than by destroying the grounds of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","text":"Space and time are forms of sensible intuition rather than independently existing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","text":"The sensible world consists only of phenomena under the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A15","text":"A world abstracted from all conditions of intuition permits no synthetic proposition, whether affirmative or negative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A16","text":"Empirical nature requires a connected and law-governed causal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:A17","text":"A regress begun among empirical conditions cannot validly terminate by changing without warrant to an intellectual condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX1","text":"Antithetic is not a collection of dogmatic counterassertions and is not a one-sided defence of either proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX2","text":"A dialectical proposition is not an answer to an arbitrarily invented question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX3","text":"Transcendental illusion is not an artificial sophism that disappears when detected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX4","text":"The sceptical method is not scepticism and does not institute universal scientific ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX5","text":"The conflict is not decided by doctrinal allegiance, practical preference, defensive success, or possession of the final attack.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX6","text":"Transcendental disputes cannot be adjudicated by mathematical construction, experimental testing, or practical exemplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX7","text":"Infinity is not a maximum magnitude greater than which none can be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX8","text":"Space and time are not substances, empty containers, or external correlates of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX9","text":"The mundus intelligibilis is not a knowable substitute for the phenomenal world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX10","text":"Failure to perceive a manifold does not establish the absolute simplicity of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX11","text":"Empirical change does not establish intellectual contingency or justify an inference to a transcendent necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:NX12","text":"Transcendental freedom is not identical with the complete empirical or psychological conception of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D1","text":"Treating the world as a completed temporal-spatial whole excludes both an elapsed infinite succession and an infinite coexistent totality requiring completed successive synthesis, thereby generating the first thesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A3","CH_BA94_SIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D2","text":"Treating the world as phenomenal excludes a beginning preceded by empty time and a spatial boundary related to empty space, thereby generating the first antithesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D3","text":"Once composition is treated as a contingent relation among self-subsisting substances, the removal of composition must leave simple substances or annihilate substance altogether, thereby generating the second thesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D4","text":"Because every spatially intuited real part occupies further divisible space, no absolutely simple part can be given in possible experience, thereby generating the second antithesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A1","CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D5","text":"The demand for completeness in a causal series whose natural causes are themselves conditioned generates the postulate of a spontaneous causality, thereby producing the third thesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D6","text":"A spontaneous event introduced within experience would sever causal connection with preceding states and destroy empirical unity, thereby producing the third antithesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A1","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D7","text":"The demand for a complete series of conditions for change generates an absolutely necessary condition and, because its efficacy is construed temporally, tends to place that condition within the cosmical series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D8","text":"Since every temporal member remains conditioned, the aggregate cannot become necessary through conditioned parts, while an extra-world cause that begins to act becomes temporally implicated in the world, thereby producing the fourth antithesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16","CH_BA94_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D9","text":"The absence of an external criterion makes dogmatic victory indefinitely reversible and allows argumentative sequence to masquerade as truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","CH_BA94_SIAP:A10","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:D10","text":"Staging each opposition at full strength converts antinomy from a contest of doctrines into evidence concerning defects in reason’s principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD1","text":"The four antinomies are recurrent outputs of one structural mismatch between absolute rational totalization and empirically conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A3","CH_BA94_SIAP:A4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD2","text":"Dogmatic opposition is self-sustaining because each side can invalidate the other’s governing standpoint without establishing its own objective reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","CH_BA94_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD3","text":"The sceptical method seeks critical certainty concerning cognitive boundaries rather than positive knowledge of cosmological totalities.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A10","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12","CH_BA94_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD4","text":"The unconditioned is successively instantiated as completed magnitude, simple substrate, spontaneous causality, and necessary existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5","text":"Antinomy functions as a criterion of transcendental legislation by forcing abstract principles to reveal their defective scope through contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6","text":"Because transcendental illusion cannot be eliminated, critique must remain a recurrent discipline rather than a completed eradication of error.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD7","text":"Restriction to phenomena conditionally leaves thing-in-itself alternatives open while denying that such alternatives can supply synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SD8","text":"The declared parity of the opposed proofs is strained by the text’s local reliance on mathematical evidence, the transcendental status of space, and the rejection of monadist constructions as inadmissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","CH_BA94_SIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX1","text":"The world as an absolutely completed whole must not be assumed to be an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX2","text":"Internal consistency and subjective rational necessity must not be equated with objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX3","text":"Principles valid for members of an empirical series must not be assumed to determine the series as an absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX4","text":"Forms of intuition must not be converted into empty objects capable of bounding or preceding phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX5","text":"The incomprehensibility of an infinite regress must not by itself be treated as proof of its impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX6","text":"Antinomy must not be interpreted as evidence that the world itself possesses contradictory predicates independently of reason’s mode of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX7","text":"A noumenal redescription must not be used as an answer to a question posed under phenomenal conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absence of any possible intuition of the world-whole whose properties reason attempts to determine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX2","text":"No transcendent metacriterion exists from which thesis and antithesis can be directly compared as objects of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX3","text":"A single undifferentiated object cannot simultaneously satisfy reason’s demand for the unconditioned and understanding’s demand for empirical conditionedness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX4","text":"Speculative reason has no final state in which its drive toward the unconditioned is permanently extinguished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5","text":"A positive rule assigning the opposed propositions to distinct standpoints remains absent from this section, although the need for such a distinction structures the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS1","text":"Understanding-rules → possible experience → legitimate but indefinitely conditioned synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS2","text":"Conditioned synthesis → reason’s demand for absolute completeness → representation of the cosmical series as a totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS3","text":"Transgression beyond experience → loss of intuition and empirical test → natural transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS4","text":"Application of the completion operator yields temporal-spatial limits, simple substances, spontaneous causality, and necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS5","text":"Reapplication of sensible and empirical conditions yields temporal-spatial infinity, indefinite divisibility, universal natural causality, and the absence of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS6","text":"Equal rational grounding plus unavailable objective criterion yields dogmatic deadlock.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS7","text":"Unrestricted conflict → exposure of standpoint or level-shift → diagnosis of misunderstanding → attempted neutralization of recurrent illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AF1","text":"Necessary demand for the unconditioned → extension beyond possible experience → paired cosmological assertions → equal demonstrations → antinomial deadlock → sceptical diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AF2","text":"Assumption of the opposite → derivation of contradiction → establishment of thesis → reversal of governing standpoint → renewed assumption of the opposite → establishment of antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:AF3","text":"Magnitude of the world → elements of the world → origin of cosmical causality → ground of cosmical existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME1","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from disagreement between doctrines to an endogenous self-conflict of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME2","text":"The world-whole is reclassified from a possible object of cosmological description to a problematic totality generated by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME3","text":"Thesis and antithesis become symmetrical products of opposed requirements rather than simple truth and error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME4","text":"Sceptical suspension is divided into destructive scepticism and a diagnostic method directed toward certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME5","text":"Philosophical combat is converted from a mechanism of doctrinal victory into an experiment for detecting defective principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME6","text":"Boundaries, simples, freedom, and necessity become variant forms of one demand for the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ME7","text":"Space and time cease to function as independent cosmical containers and become conditions governing what can count as a phenomenal claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface: Four paired conflicts determine whether the world is finite or infinite, simple or indefinitely divisible, freely originated or naturally determined, and grounded in necessary existence or not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The antinomies are generated by the incompatibility between reason’s demand for absolute totality and understanding’s restriction to conditioned experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Doctrinal adjudication is replaced by the deliberate production of maximal conflict as a method for exposing illicit assumptions and level-shifts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Objects are available only as phenomena under space, time, and causal order, while the unconditioned cannot be temporally or spatially located without becoming conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the common generator, the four principal outputs, and the sceptical procedure applied to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT2","text":"The final rule by which the antinomies are to be resolved remains underdetermined because this section arranges and diagnoses the conflict without completing its general solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent as an account of why reason produces opposed necessities in the absence of experiential adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it identifies illicit transitions between empirical conditions, absolute totality, and purely intellectual grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT5","text":"The individual proofs depend on compressed assumptions concerning completed synthesis, spatial divisibility, causal completeness, and the relation between aggregate necessity and the necessity of members.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT6","text":"The claim of strict parity depends partly on rhetorical construction because the observations introduce substantive transcendental commitments that locally privilege some arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT7","text":"The freedom thesis establishes at most a cosmological demand for a first causal beginning, then extends that result toward intraworld agency without an equivalent demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT8","text":"The fourth thesis tends to place necessary existence within the temporal series while its observation leaves the relation between the necessary being and the world unsettled.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the text’s real movement only if it refuses to select either dogmatic side or import a positive resolution not yet supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 63","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 63","original_title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 63"},"original_title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","order":64,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIIR"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIIR@R001","sha256":"6b385ebcdb0bc0a3d39b0620052e06a5248a42aa09dcebf3d851f62ec6f55345","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS1","text":"Reason necessarily generates cosmological ideas by seeking unconditioned totality for conditionally determined experience, although no possible experience can supply their adequate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS2","text":"The four cosmological antinomies are treated as the exhaustive and unavoidable forms in which this demand for unconditioned completion becomes self-contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS3","text":"The conflict cannot be dismissed because its opposing conclusions bear practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS4","text":"The preliminary inquiry suspends logical adjudication in order to diagnose why reason adheres to either side of the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS5","text":"The thesis is classified as dogmatism because it supplements empirical explanation with intellectual claims about the unconditioned, while the antithesis is classified as empiricism because it universalizes explanation by possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS6","text":"Empirical restriction is valid as a maxim of speculative inquiry but becomes invalid when transformed into a denial of what lies beyond phenomenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AS7","text":"The alternation between freedom and natural necessity leaves the conflict unresolved and redirects inquiry toward the origin of reason’s own disunion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A1","text":"No possible experience can furnish an object adequate to cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","text":"Reason necessarily reaches cosmological ideas when it seeks the unconditioned totality of conditional empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A3","text":"The cosmological problems are limited to four because only four a priori series of synthetic conditions delimit empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A4","text":"The thesis supplements empirical explanation with intellectual propositions concerning the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A5","text":"The antithesis applies pure empiricism to transcendental ideas as well as to natural phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","text":"Objective speculative cognition requires a basis in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","text":"Methodological empiricism directs understanding to indefinitely extend natural inquiry through experience and necessary laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A8","text":"Empiricism becomes dogmatic when phenomenal unknowability is converted into a denial of what exceeds phenomenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A9","text":"The thesis claims that the conditioned can be derived from an unconditioned terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A10","text":"The antithesis represents every condition as dependent on a further condition without a self-subsistent first basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A11","text":"The thesis’s claims function as theoretical supports for morality and religion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A12","text":"Reason seeks to place cognition within an a priori rational unity of system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","text":"Interest can govern preliminary partisan choice while logical truth is deliberately bracketed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A14","text":"Practical interest selects principles for action when speculative reason remains undecided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A15","text":"A proper preliminary inquiry compares the principles from which thesis and antithesis proceed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A16","text":"Detached speculative judgment oscillates between opposed cosmological conclusions when it presumes that one side alone must be true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:A17","text":"Radical settlement requires inquiry into the origin of reason’s disunion rather than victory by either party.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX1","text":"Cosmological ideas are not arbitrary inventions, despite their lack of adequate experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX2","text":"Interest is not a logical criterion of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX3","text":"The thesis is not governed by empirical explanation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX4","text":"The antithesis is not merely a local theory of natural phenomena but an empiricist treatment of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX5","text":"Possible experience does not license speculative explanation by a first epoch, a spatial limit, a supersensible free faculty, or a cause beyond nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX6","text":"Transcendent concepts do not acquire objective speculative validity without corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX7","text":"Methodological suspension of supersensible claims is not equivalent to their dogmatic denial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX8","text":"Infinite regress is not an architectonically complete system of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX9","text":"Familiarity with a conception is not comprehension of its objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX10","text":"Practical need is not speculative evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX11","text":"Neither party may claim objective knowledge beyond what its principle can warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:NX12","text":"The conflict cannot be ended by indifference, suppression, or partisan intimidation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D1","text":"Cosmological ideas must be diagnosed as unavoidable products of reason rather than rejected as optional errors.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A1","CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","CH_BA94_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D2","text":"The opposition between thesis and antithesis is determined by their generating principles rather than by the particular cosmological propositions they defend.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A4","CH_BA94_SIIR:A5","CH_BA94_SIIR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D3","text":"The thesis gains its natural attractiveness by promising moral support, terminal explanation, and architectonic completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A9","CH_BA94_SIIR:A11","CH_BA94_SIIR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D4","text":"Empiricism retains speculative legitimacy only when it remains a rule for inquiry within experience rather than a doctrine denying the supersensible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SIIR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D5","text":"The analysis of interests explains adherence to opposing doctrines without establishing either doctrine as true.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","CH_BA94_SIIR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D6","text":"Action can terminate speculative hesitation through practical selection without theoretically resolving the antinomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A14","CH_BA94_SIIR:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D7","text":"The chapter divides inquiry into a provisional diagnosis of partisan interests and a deferred investigation of the source of contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","CH_BA94_SIIR:A15","CH_BA94_SIIR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:D8","text":"The central fault is not attachment either to nature or to practical hope, but conversion of either orientation into unwarranted speculative doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A4","CH_BA94_SIIR:A5","CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SIIR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SD1","text":"Reason generates its own cosmological conflict because its necessary demand for unconditioned totality exceeds the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A1","CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","CH_BA94_SIIR:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SD2","text":"The system separates epistemic authorization from practical commitment: possible experience authorizes speculative cognition, while practical interest explains action and allegiance.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SIIR:A11","CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","CH_BA94_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SD3","text":"Architectonic reason converts the endlessness of empirical conditioning into an apparent defect and thereby gives the thesis a structural advantage independent of proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A10","CH_BA94_SIIR:A12","CH_BA94_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SD4","text":"The same empirical principle produces scientific modesty when used methodologically and practical-spiritual damage when used as a dogmatic metaphysical negation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SIIR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SD5","text":"A resolution requires a third operation that neither dogmatic affirmation nor empirical denial can provide: an account of how reason produces the opposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A15","CH_BA94_SIIR:A16","CH_BA94_SIIR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX1","text":"Phenomenal unknowability cannot be silently equated with nonexistence without converting empiricism into dogmatic denial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX2","text":"Reason’s need for foundation, moral support, and systematic unity cannot by itself establish the objective constitution of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX3","text":"Architectonic demand cannot supply an experiential object for the unconditioned terminus it requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX4","text":"Partisan psychology cannot explain the source of self-contradiction, because interest explains adherence after the conflict has already arisen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX5","text":"Practical indispensability cannot cancel the distinction between what must be assumed for action and what can be known in speculation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX6","text":"An infinite empirical series cannot be treated as either a completed object or an achieved systematic whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX1","text":"Direct speculative possession of unconditioned cosmological totality is unavailable, and this absence structures every cosmological question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX2","text":"No final speculative choice between thesis and antithesis is supplied within the chapter’s present frame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX3","text":"No guaranteed correspondence exists between reason’s demand for a final ground and the form in which nature is knowable as an indefinitely conditioned order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX4","text":"A non-dogmatic relation between practical commitment and speculative limitation is withheld as the unresolved target of the deferred radical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS1","text":"Conditional empirical synthesis + reason’s demand for unconditioned totality → necessary production of cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS2","text":"Cosmological ideas lacking adequate possible objects + their treatment as determinative knowledge → opposed cosmological conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS3","text":"Opposed conclusions → thesis as intellectual dogmatism / antithesis as universalized empiricism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS4","text":"Thesis and antithesis + suspension of immediate truth adjudication → comparison of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS5","text":"Empirical restriction as methodological maxim → secure natural inquiry; empirical restriction as metaphysical denial → dogmatic overreach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS6","text":"Speculative oscillation + practical necessity → practical selection without theoretical settlement → demand for an origin-diagnosis of reason’s disunion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AF1","text":"The movement begins with reason’s necessary search for the unconditioned and reaches the four forms of cosmological self-contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AF2","text":"Cosmological self-contradiction → suspension of immediate truth-claims → comparison of the principles of dogmatism and empiricism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AF3","text":"Principle-comparison → differentiation of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests → explanation of partisan preference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:AF4","text":"Qualified empiricism versus dogmatic empiricism + thesis’s architectonic attraction → speculative oscillation and practical selection → deferred inquiry into the source of the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME1","text":"Cosmological propositions are reclassified from candidate descriptions of the world into unavoidable products of reason’s demand for totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME2","text":"Dogmatism and empiricism are reclassified by their relation to possible experience and the unconditioned rather than by their historical or rhetorical identities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME3","text":"Practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests become distinct explanatory variables rather than interchangeable reasons for truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME4","text":"The boundary of experience is split into two functions: it constrains objective assertion but does not authorize denial of everything beyond it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME5","text":"Infinite regression becomes double-coded as a legitimate horizon of empirical inquiry and an obstacle to architectonic completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ME6","text":"Speculative indecision becomes evidence of a structural defect in the framing of the dispute rather than evidence for either cosmological side.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit object is the preference between thesis and antithesis concerning world, self, freedom, causality, and supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT2","text":"Structural — The conflict is generated by the collision between conditional experience, unconditioned reason, empirical objectivity, and architectonic system-building.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The inquiry brackets truth temporarily in order to distinguish doctrinal proof from the interests that recruit adherents to doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is contested as either an indefinitely conditioned natural order or an order grounded in unconditioned beginnings, simple substances, free causes, and ultimate cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from necessary cosmological ideas through antinomial conflict to an interest-analysis and deferred origin-diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT2","text":"The extraction does not reconstruct the detailed proofs of the four individual antinomies, because this section presupposes their prior dialectical derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT3","text":"The system is most coherent where it distinguishes methodological empiricism from dogmatic empirical denial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT4","text":"The primary tension is that thesis-side practical and architectonic attractions explain its appeal while being explicitly insufficient to prove its speculative claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT5","text":"A second tension is that reason necessarily demands an unconditioned unity while possible experience cannot provide the object required to know such unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT6","text":"The final appeal to impartial trial is under pressure because the chapter has already shown that practical and architectonic interests condition preference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT7","text":"The proposed radical resolution remains underdetermined because the inquiry into the source of the contradiction is announced but deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s actual movement only if it ends in suspended adjudication and diagnostic redirection rather than in victory for either thesis or antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 64","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","context":"Chapter 64","original_title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","original_context":"Chapter 64"},"original_title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","order":65,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SINI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SINI@R001","sha256":"0a141dc651bf927e2b7bfd3dc0685c2e4aa92ed2ee0d10089cc1be07c94c2c05","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS1","text":"Pure reason must determine whether its transcendental questions are internally answerable or merely exceed its competence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS2","text":"Questions generated by a science’s own constitutive concepts require solutions from those same concepts when their objects are determined within that sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS3","text":"Cosmological ideas uniquely concern the absolute totality of an empirical synthesis rather than a transcendent object given independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS4","text":"Because no possible experience can present absolute totality, cosmological problems cannot receive dogmatic determinations of their supposed objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","text":"The proper solution transfers inquiry from the constitution of a presumed object to the dialectical basis and legitimate employment of the idea that generates the question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AS6","text":"Reason’s apparent failure is therefore diagnosed as an internal illusion that mistakes its own idea for an empirically determinable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A1","text":"A question arising from concepts constitutive of a pure science must be answerable from those concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A2","text":"Transcendental philosophy contains questions whose source is pure reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A3","text":"Cosmological ideas demand the absolute totality of the synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A4","text":"Absolute totality cannot be given in any possible empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A5","text":"Cosmological questions concern the required progress of empirical synthesis toward an idea, not the constitution of a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A6","text":"An object not presentable in possible experience cannot be determined by predicates whose application requires experiential conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A7","text":"A question concerning such an indeterminable object is null rather than merely unanswered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A8","text":"The ideas of reason are not required for the explanation of particular phenomena because no empirical intuition can exhibit their unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A9","text":"Dogmatic solutions treat an idea as though it represented an empirically given object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A10","text":"Critical solutions investigate the cognitive ground and dialectic of the question rather than determine its supposed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:A11","text":"Reason cannot discharge its obligation by appealing to the limitation of its faculties when the problem originates in reason’s own ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX1","text":"Transcendental cosmological questions are not questions about a transcendent thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX2","text":"Necessary ignorance is not an admissible response to a genuine question generated by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX3","text":"Absolute totality is not an empirical whole obtainable through the accumulation of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX4","text":"The unconditioned is not discoverable at any point within a series of conditioned appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX5","text":"A transcendental object is not a legitimate object of categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX6","text":"A dogmatic answer is not a deficient version of a critical answer; it is impossible for the relevant problems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX7","text":"The cosmological idea is not a principle required to explain any determinate phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:NX8","text":"The failure to determine an unconditioned object is not evidence that such an object remains obscurely available to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D1","text":"Cosmological problems require resolution despite the impossibility of empirically exhibiting their unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A1","CH_BA94_SINI:A2","CH_BA94_SINI:A3","CH_BA94_SINI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D2","text":"The answer to a cosmological question must be drawn from the idea’s conditions of use rather than from experience or transcendent metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SINI:A5","CH_BA94_SINI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D3","text":"Questions concerning the soul as a simple thing or a necessary being as a thing in itself fail by lacking an object to which the relevant categories could apply.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A6","CH_BA94_SINI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D4","text":"The antinomial demand for an absolute beginning, absolute infinity, ultimate simplicity, freedom, or an unconditioned necessary being cannot be settled by further empirical investigation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A3","CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SINI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D5","text":"The adequate resolution of transcendental problems consists in exposing the idea’s dialectical overreach rather than choosing among rival object-level claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A9","CH_BA94_SINI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:D6","text":"The distinction between a legitimate and a null transcendental question depends on whether the question concerns the synthesis of possible experience or an object beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A5","CH_BA94_SINI:A6","CH_BA94_SINI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SD1","text":"Pure reason’s necessity to answer its own questions is fulfilled reflexively: it resolves its problems by determining the limits and illusion of the concepts that generate them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A1","CH_BA94_SINI:A2","CH_BA94_SINI:A10","CH_BA94_SINI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SD2","text":"The unconditioned functions as a regulative demand for completeness of synthesis while being unavailable as an object of determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A3","CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SINI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SD3","text":"The apparent alternative between metaphysical knowledge and skeptical suspension is displaced by a critical verdict on the legitimacy of the question itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A7","CH_BA94_SINI:A9","CH_BA94_SINI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SD4","text":"Transcendental error arises when reason converts a necessary subjective demand for totality into a claim about an independently existing total object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A3","CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SINI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:DNX1","text":"Pure reason is not licensed to infer an object’s existence from the mere intelligibility or necessity of an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:DNX2","text":"The totality sought by cosmological reason is not a hidden empirical datum awaiting more complete observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:DNX3","text":"Conceptual origin does not authorize objective application beyond the conditions under which an object can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:DNX4","text":"The limit of cognition is not merely an external boundary imposed by deficient evidence but an internal boundary generated by the form of reason’s own demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:DNX5","text":"A question’s grammatical form does not guarantee that it has a valid object or admits an object-level answer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SNX1","text":"No completed standpoint can occupy the absolute totality that reason necessarily thinks but cannot experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SNX2","text":"No final metaphysical object corresponds to the demand that every conditioned series be grounded in the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3","text":"Reason cannot become theoretically complete by extending cognition beyond possible experience; its completion consists only in critical self-limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:FGS1","text":"Pure reason generates an idea of unconditioned totality from the demand to complete conditioned synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:FGS2","text":"The idea is misread as representing an independently given object capable of empirical or categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:FGS3","text":"This misreading produces cosmological problems that demand an absolute determination unavailable in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:FGS4","text":"The impossibility of empirical determination redirects inquiry from the supposed object to the idea’s cognitive source, function, and dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:FGS5","text":"Critical analysis dissolves dogmatic object-claims while preserving reason’s obligation to account for the necessity of its own questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AF1","text":"Reason’s demand for answers to its own questions → distinction between internally generated and externally contingent questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AF2","text":"Internally generated questions → isolation of cosmological ideas as demands for absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AF3","text":"Absolute totality beyond experience → impossibility of dogmatic determination of a cosmological object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AF4","text":"Dogmatic impossibility → diagnosis of the idea-object confusion that generates transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:AF5","text":"Dialectical diagnosis → critical resolution through analysis of the cognitive basis of the question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ME1","text":"The field of transcendental questions is divided between legitimate questions about possible experience and null questions about transcendent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ME2","text":"Ignorance is reclassified: it is legitimate in empirical inquiry, inadmissible for genuine questions of pure reason, and replaced by nullity where no object can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ME3","text":"Cosmological antinomies are reclassified from disputes about the world’s hidden constitution into effects of reason’s demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ME4","text":"The opposition between empirical solution and metaphysical speculation is displaced by critical examination of the conditions under which a question counts as meaningful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ME5","text":"The object of transcendental inquiry shifts from the world as totality to reason’s representation of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text addresses whether reason can answer questions concerning the world’s beginning, magnitude, composition, freedom, and necessary ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ALT2","text":"Structural — The questions arise because reason requires the conditioned synthesis of appearances to be completed through an unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces evasive ignorance and dogmatic metaphysical decision with critique of the question-producing concepts themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The world as an absolute whole, whether temporally begun or beginningless and spatially finite or infinite, cannot be constituted as an experiential object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Objective validity depends not on the thinkability of an object but on the possibility of giving that object under conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from reason’s obligation to answer its questions to the critical dissolution of dogmatic cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the distinction between an idea of totality and an object given in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent if “solution” includes diagnosing a question as null or exposing the dialectical source of its apparent object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT4","text":"The decisive compression occurs when the text treats the concept that generates a question as also supplying the resources for its resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between the claim that reason must answer its own questions and the claim that certain such questions receive only a verdict of nullity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT6","text":"A further tension remains between cosmological ideas as necessarily generated demands for totality and their exclusion from any constitutive determination of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SINI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if critical limitation is treated as reason’s positive self-resolution rather than as skeptical refusal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 65","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","context":"Chapter 65","original_title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","original_context":"Chapter 65"},"original_title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE","layer":"CH","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","order":66,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVSE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVSE@R001","sha256":"8f230516b57c2024782a4564fbd4bd1c9f2e56bca26bd284bbc727728f976cc4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS1","text":"Dogmatic answers to cosmological questions are irrational when either answer intensifies incomprehensibility or yields contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS2","text":"The mutual failure of opposed cosmological answers requires criticism of the question’s presupposition rather than adjudication between its alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS3","text":"Cosmological ideas demand an unconditioned totality within a regressive synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS4","text":"Every cosmological alternative makes the demanded totality either exceed or fall short of the conditions of possible empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5","text":"Possible experience is the sole criterion by which a conception can possess objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AS6","text":"Cosmological antinomy therefore exposes a fictitious conception of how its alleged object is presented, not a conflict between two knowable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A1","text":"A conception has objective reality only through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","text":"An empirical conception must be adequate to the conditions of a possible empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A3","text":"Cosmological ideas seek the unconditioned for the regressive synthesis of conditioned phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","text":"A successive empirical regress cannot complete an infinite elapsed series as a given whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","text":"A conditioned empirical beginning, limit, simple part, cause, or contingent existence requires regress to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A6","text":"The unconditioned required as an empirical totality must itself be available within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A7","text":"In each cosmological antinomy, one alternative exceeds the empirical synthesis and the opposed alternative leaves that synthesis conditionally incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A8","text":"When both opposed answers fail the criterion of possible experience, the question rests on a groundless presupposition concerning its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:A9","text":"Sceptical examination functions critically by converting dogmatic conflict into diagnosis of its generating illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX1","text":"Cosmological questions are not to be settled by a dogmatic affirmative or negative answer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX2","text":"An infinite temporal, spatial, causal, or divisional regress is not a completed object available to empirical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX3","text":"A finite beginning, boundary, simple, spontaneous event, or contingent existence is not unconditioned when it still demands a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX4","text":"Absolute void is not an empirical object or a final empirical condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX5","text":"Spontaneous causality is not cognizable as an empirical exception that terminates the demand for causal grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX6","text":"A conception lacking relation to possible experience is not knowledge of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:NX7","text":"Antinomy is not evidence that opposed cosmological doctrines are jointly valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D1","text":"The alternatives of world-beginning and world-eternity both fail because the former remains conditioned and the latter cannot be completed in successive regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D2","text":"The alternatives of finite and infinite world-extension both fail because finite limits demand determination and infinite extension cannot be empirically synthesized as a whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D3","text":"The alternatives of infinitely divisible matter and simple ultimate parts both fail because infinite division exceeds completed synthesis and a simple member still permits regress into its parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D4","text":"The alternatives of universal natural causality and free spontaneous events both fail as cosmological totalities because each preserves an unclosed demand for conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D5","text":"The alternatives of an absolutely necessary being and universal contingency both fail because the former exceeds empirical regress and the latter indefinitely renews it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D6","text":"Cosmological ideas lack objective significance when their alleged objects cannot be made adequate to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A1","CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A6","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:D7","text":"Scepticism becomes a cathartic criticism when the incoherence of answers is used to expose the false construction of the question.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A8","CH_BA94_SVSE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SD1","text":"The antinomies are generated by applying a valid demand for conditions beyond the domain in which empirical synthesis can supply an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A3","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SD2","text":"The conflict of thesis and antithesis is a symptom of transcendental illusion rather than a contest between equally established cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A1","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SD3","text":"The empirical regress becomes the normative measure of cosmological thought, while the alleged totality becomes answerable to the conditions of that regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A1","CH_BA94_SVSE:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SD4","text":"Critique displaces metaphysical decision by testing whether the question can present an object under the conditions it itself invokes.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A1","CH_BA94_SVSE:A8","CH_BA94_SVSE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX1","text":"The unconditioned is not available as an empirically given completion of the regress through conditioned appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX2","text":"The world is not legitimately cognized as a thing whose total temporal, spatial, material, causal, or modal constitution can be given as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX3","text":"The demand for a final condition is not authorized merely because empirical understanding requires conditions for each given appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX4","text":"The form of a question does not guarantee that its object, alternatives, or presupposed mode of presentation are valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SNX1","text":"No standpoint is available within possible experience from which the whole series of conditions can be surveyed as unconditionally complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SNX2","text":"No dogmatic resolution can convert the absence of an empirically presentable cosmological object into knowledge of such an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:SNX3","text":"The final exclusion is the possibility of universal science founded on cosmological totalities treated as objects of theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS1","text":"Demand for an unconditional cosmological answer → examination of the opposed answers’ cognitive yield.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS2","text":"Opposed answers → either an infinite regress exceeds possible synthesis or a finite terminus remains conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS3","text":"Double inadequacy → failure of the alleged object to meet the criterion of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS4","text":"Failure of objectivity → diagnosis of a false presupposition about the presentation of the cosmological object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS5","text":"Diagnosis of transcendental illusion → replacement of dogmatism with sceptical-critical limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF1","text":"Dogmatic demand for cosmological decision → recognition that either answer can be meaningless or contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF2","text":"Failure of both answers → critical interrogation of the question’s presupposed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF3","text":"Cosmological idea of the unconditioned → application to temporal, spatial, divisional, causal, and modal regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF4","text":"Infinite alternative exceeds empirical conception; finite alternative remains conditionally deficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF5","text":"Possible experience as criterion of reality → cosmological object loses objective significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:AF6","text":"Loss of objective significance → antinomy is reclassified as transcendental illusion requiring criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ME1","text":"Cosmological antinomies are reclassified from disputes over the world’s determinate constitution into tests of the objective validity of cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ME2","text":"“Too large” and “too small” become measures of adequacy to possible empirical synthesis rather than comparative properties of independently given objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is detached from empirical objecthood, because every empirical object remains within the order of conditional regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ME4","text":"Scepticism is transformed from suspension between rival doctrines into an instrument for exposing invalid question-formation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ME5","text":"The demand for totality is shown to produce illusion when it converts a rule of regress into a completed empirical whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text examines four pairs of opposed cosmological claims concerning the world’s beginning and extent, divisibility, causality, and necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT2","text":"Structural — Each pair reproduces one mechanism: infinite regress cannot be completed, while an alleged terminus remains subject to further conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects philosophical procedure from defending a thesis or antithesis toward examining the validity of the object presupposed by both.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects of experience are treated as conditionally given within regress, while unconditional totality is denied empirical presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Possible experience supplies the standard of objective reality and disqualifies ideas that cannot be related to a possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the argument because they generate both poles of each antinomy and the critical transition from contradiction to illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the claim that possible experience is the exclusive source of objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent if the demand for conditions is valid only within empirical synthesis and invalid when converted into an object of completed totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT4","text":"The strongest compression occurs in the common formal structure assigned to all four cosmological ideas: excess through infinity and deficiency through conditional termination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how an idea can regulate inquiry without acquiring the objective significance denied to cosmological totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT6","text":"The system also relies on, rather than fully establishes here, the boundary between a legitimate empirical regress and an illegitimate demand for its unconditioned completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if scepticism is treated as a diagnostic procedure that dissolves the question’s false object rather than merely withholding judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 66","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 66","original_title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 66"},"original_title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI","layer":"CH","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","order":67,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVTI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVTI@R001","sha256":"813d944c8ac712eac11eba122b76d57fb9c3910d7e9d8ddf46942ea54fa4ba0c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS1","text":"Pure cosmological dialectic arises when objects of possible experience are treated as self-subsistent things existing independently of the conditions under which they can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS2","text":"Transcendental idealism preserves the empirical reality of objects while denying that space, time, and phenomena belong to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS3","text":"The text advances a diagnostic and dissolutive argument that locates cosmological illusion in a misapplication of empirical concepts beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS4","text":"Reality is reframed from independent subsistence into lawful givenness within perception and the possible progression of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS5","text":"Unperceived, past, distant, and future objects are rendered empirically meaningful only as determinable nodes in a possible experiential regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS6","text":"The transcendental ground of appearances is retained as an unknowable correlate, preventing phenomenal order from being converted into knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AS7","text":"Cosmological totality is reduced from an independently existing object to the idea of a completed possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A1","text":"Space and time are pure forms of human sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A2","text":"Everything intuited in space and time is phenomenon rather than thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A3","text":"Phenomena are representations without self-subsistent existence apart from the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","text":"An empirical object is real insofar as it is given in perception or lawfully connected to possible perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A5","text":"The unity of experience supplies the criterion distinguishing empirical reality from dream, fancy, and illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A6","text":"The temporally determined inner self is appearance rather than the transcendental subject as it exists in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A7","text":"The non-sensuous cause of appearances cannot be intuited or known as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A8","text":"The transcendental object is only a mental correlate of sensibility and not a knowable phenomenal object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A9","text":"Claims about unperceived objects are meaningful only through a possible lawful progression of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A10","text":"The transcendental condition determining the course and endpoint of empirical regress is necessarily incognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A11","text":"The representation of all objects in all space and time is only the idea of possible experience in absolute completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:A12","text":"Cosmological illusion results when phenomenal objects are treated as things in themselves outside possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX1","text":"Transcendental idealism is not empirical idealism, because it does not deny the reality of external bodies in space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX2","text":"Empirical reality is not self-subsistent reality independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX3","text":"Space and time are not determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX4","text":"The object of inner sense is not the proper self as it exists in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX5","text":"The non-sensuous ground of appearances is not an object available to intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX6","text":"A phenomenon not given in perception or in a possible empirical progression is not an object for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX7","text":"A past event is not affirmed as a self-subsisting fact detached from the possible regress of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX8","text":"The totality of objects in space and time is not given as an already constituted object prior to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:NX9","text":"Cosmological concepts are not legitimate when severed from the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D1","text":"Empirical realism and transcendental idealism are compatible because phenomenal objects can be fully real within experience without being things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A2","CH_BA94_SVTI:A3","CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D2","text":"The reality of an unobserved object consists in its lawful possible discoverability rather than in its independent phenomenal presence outside experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A5","CH_BA94_SVTI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D3","text":"Assertions about the past express the possible extension of present experience through a law-governed regress to temporal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A9","CH_BA94_SVTI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D4","text":"Assertions about indefinitely distant objects retain meaning only as assertions about the possible extension of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A9","CH_BA94_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D5","text":"The empirical self can be known only as a succession of appearances and cannot ground knowledge of the transcendental subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A1","CH_BA94_SVTI:A2","CH_BA94_SVTI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D6","text":"The transcendental object can mark the dependence of appearances on an unknown ground without furnishing knowledge of that ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A7","CH_BA94_SVTI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:D7","text":"A cosmological absolute whole cannot be cognized as an object because its alleged objecthood exceeds the possible regress through which objects acquire empirical reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A9","CH_BA94_SVTI:A11","CH_BA94_SVTI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SD1","text":"Cosmological dialectic is generated by an equivocation between empirical reality within experience and transcendental reality independent of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A3","CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SD2","text":"The world is cognitively available only as an open rule-governed field of possible regress, never as a completed object standing before cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A9","CH_BA94_SVTI:A10","CH_BA94_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SD3","text":"Transcendental idealism dissolves rather than answers cosmological claims that require a standpoint external to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A7","CH_BA94_SVTI:A10","CH_BA94_SVTI:A11","CH_BA94_SVTI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SD4","text":"Objectivity is relocated from independent ontological subsistence to lawful integration within the unity of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A3","CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SD5","text":"The system preserves an unknowable ground of appearances while prohibiting that ground from explaining cosmological objects as knowable things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A7","CH_BA94_SVTI:A8","CH_BA94_SVTI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a correspondence model in which cognition compares appearances with spatial-temporal things as they exist independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes an ontological inventory of objects whose existence can be determined independently of their possible relation to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the inference from the continuity of empirical regress to the existence of a completed cosmological totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that temporal self-consciousness yields direct knowledge of a noumenal self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes any cognitive passage from the lawful order of appearances to knowledge of their non-sensuous cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a determinate empirical endpoint at which the regress of experience could become unconditioned knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX1","text":"No standpoint exists from which the whole of space, time, and objects can be known as a completed reality outside the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX2","text":"No phenomenal object can possess the mode of absolute being required by dogmatic cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX3","text":"No self-transparent subject remains available beneath the temporal succession through which the empirical self is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX4","text":"No final empirical regress can convert the transcendental condition of experience into an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS1","text":"Sensibility structured by space and time → appearances constituted as representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS2","text":"Appearances plus lawful unity of experience → empirical objects and empirical reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS3","text":"Present perception plus possible empirical regress → warranted reference to past, distant, and unperceived phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS4","text":"Unknown non-sensuous ground → transcendental object as limiting correlate rather than cognized object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS5","text":"Possible experience considered as complete → cosmological idea without a given cosmological object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS6","text":"Phenomenal reality misread as independent reality → cosmological dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS7","text":"Restoration of the distinction between phenomenon and thing in itself → dissolution of the dialectical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AF1","text":"Apparent conflict over the reality of external and cosmological objects → distinction between empirical and transcendental reality → validation of phenomena through lawful experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AF2","text":"Lawful experience → possible regress from perception → conditional legitimacy of claims about past, distant, and unobserved objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AF3","text":"Possible regress mistaken for completed totality → attribution of independent reality to phenomena → cosmological illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:AF4","text":"Transcendental idealist restriction of objectivity to possible experience → removal of the alleged cosmological object → dissolution of pure cosmological dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME1","text":"Reality is divided into empirical validity within experience and forbidden transcendental attribution to phenomena as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME2","text":"The past, the distant, and the unobserved are reclassified from absent entities to possible termini of lawful experiential progression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME3","text":"The external world is preserved as empirically real without being granted independent spatial-temporal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME4","text":"The self is split between an empirically knowable temporal appearance and an unknowable transcendental subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME5","text":"Cosmological totality is reclassified from an object to be known into an idea generated by the demand for completed experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ME6","text":"Dialectical contradiction is relocated from the world to a category error in the use of empirical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ALT1","text":"Surface: External bodies, temporal events, and inner states are affirmed as empirically real appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ALT2","text":"Structural: Space, time, receptivity, perception, and the unity of experience constitute the conditions under which objects can be given and counted as real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Cosmological disputes are neutralized by restricting the scope of object-claims rather than by selecting one cosmological doctrine over another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Being in space and time belongs only to appearance, while temporal succession never discloses either a noumenal self or an unconditioned world-totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:ALT5","text":"Epistemic: The transcendental ground is necessary as a limiting thought but unavailable as a positive object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument that empirical realism depends upon transcendental idealism rather than contradicting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT2","text":"The system reconstructs the transition from perceptual givenness to lawful possible experience as the criterion of phenomenal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT3","text":"The system reconstructs the dissolution of cosmological objecthood by denying independent phenomenal existence outside possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning why the forms of sensibility and the unity of experience have the authority attributed to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT5","text":"The system depends on a compressed distinction between a merely possible experiential regress and a determinate assertion of actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT6","text":"The transcendental object is structurally unstable because it is invoked as an in-itself ground while being denied all objectual cognizability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT7","text":"The argument is strongest where it exposes the equivocation between empirical reality and independent existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the text’s movement only if the limiting function of the unknowable ground is retained without converting it into positive metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 67","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","context":"Chapter 67","original_title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","original_context":"Chapter 67"},"original_title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS","layer":"CH","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","order":68,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVCS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVCS@R001","sha256":"fbee5e7c0fe8f599defd0d78de891c5876563ccd6f63b318c64fd84882669cf8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS1","text":"Cosmological antinomy arises when the logical requirement to seek conditions is converted into a claim that the total series of conditions is already given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS2","text":"The conversion depends on equivocating between conditioned things in themselves and conditioned phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS3","text":"Phenomena are given only through successive empirical synthesis, so their full conditioned series cannot be given as a completed whole prior to regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS4","text":"Cosmological thesis and antithesis therefore conflict only because both presuppose that the world is a self-subsistent whole determined independently of empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS5","text":"Once this presupposition is removed, opposed cosmological assertions become dialectically opposed judgments that may both be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AS6","text":"The dissolution of antinomy supplies an indirect proof of transcendental ideality and reassigns dialectic from dogmatic knowledge-production to critical error-diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A1","text":"A conditioned object necessarily refers reason to its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","text":"Reason is logically required to continue the search for conditions as far as possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A3","text":"If conditioned objects and their conditions are things in themselves, the condition-series is given with the conditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A4","text":"If the entire condition-series is given with the conditioned object, the unconditioned is given with that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A5","text":"Phenomena are representations rather than things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","text":"Phenomena are given only in empirical apprehension and synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","text":"Empirical synthesis is necessarily successive and temporally conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A8","text":"A condition not empirically given can be required without being already given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A9","text":"The cosmological syllogism uses the conditioned transcendentally in its major premise and empirically in its minor premise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A10","text":"A judgment concerning the complete quantity or totality of the world presupposes that the world is a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A11","text":"A series of phenomena exists only in the regressive synthesis through which it is sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A12","text":"Opposed judgments may both be false when each predicates a determination under an invalid shared condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A13","text":"The removal of a false presupposition can transform apparent contradiction into merely dialectical opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:A14","text":"The failure of cosmological dogmatism supports the transcendental ideality of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX1","text":"The logical demand for regress is not a proof that all conditions are simultaneously given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX2","text":"Phenomena are not objects available to pure understanding independently of intuition and empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX3","text":"The empirical series of conditions is not an atemporal series of co-given members.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX4","text":"The world is not a self-subsistent whole existing independently of the regress of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX5","text":"The world is not determinable in itself as either finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX6","text":"The negation of infinitude is not equivalent to the assertion of finitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX7","text":"Cosmological thesis and antithesis are not necessarily contradictory judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:NX8","text":"The critical dissolution of antinomy is not a sceptical denial of empirical inquiry or rational regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D1","text":"Reason may require an indefinitely continued regress toward conditions without possessing the completed totality of that regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A1","CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D2","text":"The cosmological inference from a given conditioned phenomenon to an absolutely complete condition-series is invalid.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A5","CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D3","text":"The difference between things in themselves and phenomena is the decisive condition governing whether totality can be inferred from conditionedness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A3","CH_BA94_SVCS:A5","CH_BA94_SVCS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D4","text":"No phenomenal series can be represented as a completed unconditioned whole, because its members are available only through successive regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D5","text":"The propositions that the world is finite and that it is infinite can both fail when the world is treated as a phenomenal series rather than a thing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A10","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11","CH_BA94_SVCS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D6","text":"The same critical restriction applies to quantitative, compositional, causal, and necessary-condition cosmological series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:D7","text":"Antinomy identifies an error in the common premise of opposed arguments rather than establishing either opposed conclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A12","CH_BA94_SVCS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SD1","text":"The unconditioned functions within phenomenal inquiry as a regulative demand for regress rather than as an object given in experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A8","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SD2","text":"Cosmological reason generates antinomy by importing the ontological conditions of things in themselves into the temporal procedure of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A3","CH_BA94_SVCS:A5","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SD3","text":"The world is reclassified from an object possessing a determinate total magnitude into the open field of empirically executable regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A10","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SD4","text":"Rational conflict becomes evidence for the limits of objectification when equally compelling opposed proofs depend upon the same illicit presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A12","CH_BA94_SVCS:A13","CH_BA94_SVCS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SD5","text":"Critical reason preserves the validity of empirical investigation by denying that its procedural demands authorize transcendent conclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVCS:A8","CH_BA94_SVCS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX1","text":"No object of possible experience can be given as a completed absolute totality independently of the synthesis that constitutes it as experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX2","text":"No transition is licensed from the necessity of seeking conditions to the existence of a completed unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX3","text":"No temporal series can be treated as if all of its members were co-present outside temporal succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX4","text":"No cosmological predicate of finitude, infinitude, beginning, non-beginning, simplicity, composition, freedom, necessity, or contingency applies to the world as a whole independently of the conditions of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX5","text":"No formal opposition guarantees that one of the opposed judgments corresponds to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SNX1","text":"The system excludes any standpoint from which the totality of appearances could be known as an object while remaining an appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a final empirical completion of the regress that would convert reason’s demand for conditions into knowledge of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:SNX3","text":"The system excludes an unmediated identity between the logical form of explanation and the ontological structure of what is given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS1","text":"Given conditionedness, reason demands regress toward conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS2","text":"The demand for regress becomes a claim of completed totality only if the conditioned is treated as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS3","text":"Phenomenal conditionedness is given only through temporally successive empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS4","text":"Successive synthesis permits required regress but forbids the prior givenness of the whole regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS5","text":"The cosmological syllogism produces antinomy by substituting the thing-in-itself model of conditionedness for the phenomenal model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS6","text":"Removal of the thing-in-itself presupposition converts cosmological contradiction into a pair of jointly invalid dialectical predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS7","text":"The collapse of the predicates finite and infinite as determinations of the world in itself establishes the ideality of the phenomenal world-whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS8","text":"Critical reason retains empirical regress while suspending every dogmatic claim to the completed unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AF1","text":"Given conditioned phenomenon → logical demand for conditions → illicit assertion that the entire series is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AF2","text":"Illicit assertion of totality → equivocation between transcendental and empirical conditionedness → apparent cosmological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AF3","text":"Successive phenomenal synthesis → impossibility of pre-given total series → invalidation of the cosmological inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AF4","text":"Invalid shared presupposition → dialectical rather than contradictory opposition → joint dissolution of thesis and antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:AF5","text":"Dissolved antinomy → transcendental ideality of phenomena → critical preservation of empirical regress without dogmatic cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ME1","text":"The distinction between seeking every condition and possessing all conditions separates methodological infinity from an actually given infinite totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ME2","text":"Cosmological oppositions are reclassified from disputes over rival world-properties into symptoms of an invalid object-model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ME3","text":"The world becomes thinkable as a regressive order of appearances without becoming an objectifiable whole of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ME4","text":"The opposition between dogmatism and scepticism is displaced by a critical position that rejects transcendent assertion while retaining empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ME5","text":"Contradiction becomes diagnostically productive because it can expose a common presupposition rather than merely defeat one contestant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text resolves the opposed claims that the world is finite or infinite, begun or unbegun, composed or simple, and causally conditioned or unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text locates antinomy in the temporal and synthetic conditions under which phenomena are given rather than in the objects apparently disputed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces adjudication between opposed cosmological doctrines with diagnosis of the presupposition that generates both doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text denies that phenomenal reality possesses an atemporal, self-subsistent totality and defines its objectivity through temporally successive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The text confines cognition of phenomenal conditions to actual or possible regress while reserving the unconditioned from constitutive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from the logical demand for conditions to the critical rejection of cosmological totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT2","text":"The decisive reconstructive distinction is between a condition-series given with things in themselves and a condition-series sought through phenomenal regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if phenomenal objectivity depends on synthesis without requiring that synthesis to be complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT4","text":"The system depends on maintaining a strict distinction between logical necessity, empirical availability, and ontological givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how indefinitely required regress can be positively characterized without reintroducing a concealed totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT6","text":"The system’s central fault line is that the indirect proof of transcendental ideality uses the failure of world-totality predicates while relying on a prior account of phenomenal representation to diagnose that failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if antinomy is treated as a diagnostic instrument rather than as a set of cosmological propositions awaiting settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 68","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","context":"Chapter 68","original_title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","original_context":"Chapter 68"},"original_title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP","layer":"CH","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","order":69,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVRP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVRP@R001","sha256":"f8f9dfaf22a70c6dc9f78e472ff1c6140afa039bd7731b1e223f12692ae50fe9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS1","text":"Cosmological totality cannot yield knowledge of a maximum of conditions as an object existing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS2","text":"The idea of totality is reclassified from a claim about objects into a rule directing empirical regress from conditioned to condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS3","text":"The argument is transcendental and diagnostic: it exposes the illicit conversion of a subjective rule of inquiry into objective cosmological knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS4","text":"Phenomenal conditionedness requires continued regress while excluding the empirical discovery of an absolutely unconditioned condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS5","text":"The distinction between regressus in infinitum and regressus in indefinitum is determined by how the relevant series is empirically given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AS6","text":"The argument concludes that no empirical regress licenses a claim that the series of conditions is itself finite or actually infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A1","text":"Sensuous objects are empirical representations rather than things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A2","text":"Every condition discovered within experience is itself conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A3","text":"The absolutely unconditioned cannot be discovered within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","text":"The idea of absolute totality belongs to reason and is not given as an object in empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","text":"Reason requires regress from a given conditioned through subordinate conditions toward completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A6","text":"A principle that prescribes empirical procedure without determining its object is regulative rather than constitutive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A7","text":"A constitutive cosmological principle would illegitimately attribute objective reality to absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A8","text":"Empirical limits cannot be treated as absolute limits merely because regress has not yet surpassed them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A9","text":"A regress through internal conditions proceeds in infinitum when the relevant whole is empirically given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A10","text":"A regress toward higher external conditions proceeds only in indefinitum when the whole series is not empirically given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:A11","text":"Neither infinite regress nor indefinite regress establishes an actually infinite series in the object itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX1","text":"The cosmological idea is not an axiom for cognizing totality as actual in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX2","text":"The regulative principle is not a principle of the possibility of experience or of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX3","text":"The regulative principle is not authorization to extend cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX4","text":"The rule of regress does not determine whether a series of conditions is finite in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX5","text":"The rule of regress does not determine whether a series of conditions is infinite in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX6","text":"The empirical search for conditions must not halt at an allegedly absolute unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX7","text":"The absence of a discovered limit is not evidence that an actually infinite totality is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:NX8","text":"A limitative perception is not identical with the series that it limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D1","text":"The demand for totality functions as an obligation to continue empirical inquiry rather than as knowledge of a completed totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","CH_BA94_SVRP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D2","text":"The unconditioned operates as an unattainable terminus of regress rather than as an empirically discoverable member of the series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A3","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D3","text":"Empirical cognition is enlarged by the rule of reason without being converted into transcendent cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","CH_BA94_SVRP:A6","CH_BA94_SVRP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D4","text":"The form of permissible infinity depends on whether inquiry begins from an empirically given whole or from a single given member.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A9","CH_BA94_SVRP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D5","text":"Division of a bounded body permits regress in infinitum because further internal parts are given as conditions of the whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D6","text":"Genealogical ascent permits regress in indefinitum because higher conditions must be sought without the completed series being empirically given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","CH_BA94_SVRP:A8","CH_BA94_SVRP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:D7","text":"The question of a series’s intrinsic numerical magnitude is displaced by the question of how inquiry must proceed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A6","CH_BA94_SVRP:A7","CH_BA94_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SD1","text":"Cosmological ideas acquire legitimate use only through their transformation from object-claims into procedural constraints on empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","CH_BA94_SVRP:A6","CH_BA94_SVRP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SD2","text":"Reason generates an indefinitely extensible task of explanation while withholding any empirical completion capable of satisfying that task.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A3","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5","CH_BA94_SVRP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SD3","text":"Infinity is relocated from a property of phenomenal objects to a mode of authorized empirical continuation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A6","CH_BA94_SVRP:A9","CH_BA94_SVRP:A10","CH_BA94_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SD4","text":"The apparent alternative between a finite world-series and an infinite world-series is structurally invalid because both alternatives presuppose objectual totality beyond empirical givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A1","CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","CH_BA94_SVRP:A7","CH_BA94_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX1","text":"Phenomena do not possess a cognizable totality independently of the empirical regress through which their conditions are sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX2","text":"Empirical inquiry cannot derive an ontological conclusion from the mere necessity of continuing inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX3","text":"The absence of a stopping point cannot become a positive intuition of limitless reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX4","text":"A completed explanation of phenomenal conditions cannot be available as an empirical datum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX5","text":"The form of a rational demand cannot determine the ontological constitution of its empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX1","text":"No standpoint is available from within experience from which the total series of phenomenal conditions can be surveyed as a completed whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX2","text":"No absolute beginning, final condition, or terminal explanatory ground is admissible within the phenomenal order as empirically known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX3","text":"The system excludes any direct passage from the idea of the unconditioned to the existence of an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX4","text":"The demand for systematic completeness is organized around an end that must guide cognition without becoming cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS1","text":"Phenomenal representation → every encountered condition is conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS2","text":"Conditionedness → reason demands regress toward condition-completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS3","text":"Idea of totality without empirical object → regulative rule rather than constitutive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS4","text":"Regulative rule → prohibition against arresting regress at an allegedly unconditioned condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS5","text":"Whole empirically given → regress through internal conditions in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS6","text":"Only a series-member empirically given → regress toward higher conditions in indefinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS7","text":"Either mode of regress → suspension of claims about the series’s finite or actual-infinite being in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AF1","text":"Claim to cosmological totality → denial of constitutive validity → conversion into a regulative rule → compulsory empirical regress → exclusion of empirical closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AF2","text":"Mode of empirical givenness → distinction between internal decomposition and external ascent → infinitum or indefinitum as procedural forms → rejection of actual infinity in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:AF3","text":"Empirical boundary encountered or absent → boundary distinguished from the series → regress continued toward its condition → no absolute empirical stopping point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ME1","text":"Cosmological questions are reclassified from questions about the world’s intrinsic total quantity into questions about the lawful conduct of empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ME2","text":"Infinity is divided into two non-equivalent procedural forms: infinite regression within an empirically given whole and indefinite regression beyond an uncompleted series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ME3","text":"The opposition between finite and infinite world-series loses legitimacy as an empirical-objectual classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ME4","text":"An empirical limit becomes intelligible either as a provisional absence of further perception or as a condition requiring further regress, never automatically as absolute closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ME5","text":"Reason’s legitimacy is located in the extension of experience rather than in the production of supersensible cosmological objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines how far one may regress from a conditioned phenomenon to its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The distinction between phenomena and things in themselves blocks the transfer of rational totality into an objective property of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Rational ideas are preserved by changing their use from constituting objects to regulating inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomenal series cannot be assigned a knowable completed totality, first condition, final condition, or actual infinity independently of empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central conversion of cosmological totality from a constitutive claim into a regulative rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT2","text":"The distinction between infinitum and indefinitum is reconstructible only if empirical givenness is retained as the determining criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it separates the demand for completeness from knowledge of a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT4","text":"The system depends on the compressed distinction between an empirically given whole and a totality allegedly given in the object itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT5","text":"A tension remains between reason’s demand for a complete conception of the object and the principled unattainability of the unconditioned that would complete regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT6","text":"A further tension remains because regress in infinitum is licensed for an empirically given whole while actual infinity in the object is denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if procedural infinity is not silently reconverted into an ontological predicate of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 69","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 69","original_title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 69"},"original_title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU","layer":"CH","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","order":70,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIEU"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIEU@R001","sha256":"cc0105c9c718e3ddc9bb8055f0f63c70a4e75d35e71fca788e458851ef2a5592","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS1","text":"The cosmological demand for absolute totality arises from treating appearances as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS2","text":"Once this assumption is rejected, reason need not determine whether the world-series is absolutely finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS3","text":"Reason must instead determine how far empirical regress must proceed to answer its own questions correctly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS4","text":"The principle of reason is therefore restricted from constitutive metaphysical use to regulative extension of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS5","text":"This restriction dissolves the antinomial conflict by exposing the fallacy and clarifying the meaning of the ideas that produced it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AS6","text":"The regulative principle retains practical epistemic efficacy comparable to that of an a priori constitutive axiom because it maximizes the employment and correction of understanding within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A1","text":"Neither reason nor understanding can be employed transcendentally to determine objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A2","text":"The demand for absolute totality in the conditions of appearances results from regarding phenomena as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A3","text":"The absolute quantity of a cosmological series is not determinable as a property of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","text":"Reason legitimately governs the empirical regress from condition to condition as a rule for extending possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","text":"The principle of reason is regulative rather than constitutive with respect to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A6","text":"The antinomy arises from a fallacious transcendental interpretation of reason’s ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A7","text":"Clarifying the true meaning of reason’s ideas converts dialectical conflict into a valid doctrinal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:A8","text":"A regulative principle can produce epistemic effects equivalent in practical force to those of a constitutive axiom without determining objects a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX1","text":"Reason is not entitled to transcendental knowledge of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX2","text":"Understanding is not entitled to transcendental knowledge of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX3","text":"Phenomena are not to be treated as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX4","text":"The world-series is not required to possess an empirically determinable absolute finitude or infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX5","text":"The principle of reason is not constitutive of phenomena in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:NX6","text":"The antinomial opposition is not an irreducible contradiction between two objectively valid descriptions of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D1","text":"Cosmological inquiry must shift from determining the absolute extent of a series to determining the necessary extent of empirical investigation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A2","CH_BA94_SIEU:A3","CH_BA94_SIEU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D2","text":"The legitimate object of reason’s cosmological use is the procedure of regress, not the completed totality of the series as an independently existing object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A3","CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D3","text":"The validity of reason’s principle depends on its capacity to extend possible experience rather than on its capacity to describe noumenal reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A1","CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D4","text":"Resolving the antinomy requires both rejecting the fallacious inference and reinterpreting the ideas that generated it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A6","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D5","text":"The transformation of reason’s principle from dialectical to doctrinal preserves its directive function while eliminating its dogmatic object-claim.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:D6","text":"The understanding receives a wider and more corrective field of application when reason regulates inquiry rather than constituting objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SD1","text":"Critical limitation does not diminish reason’s epistemic authority; it relocates that authority from metaphysical determination to the regulation of inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A1","CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SD2","text":"The antinomy is produced by confusing a rule for indefinitely advancing knowledge with a principle that determines a completed totality of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A2","CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SD3","text":"The critical solution preserves the motivating demand for systematic completeness while denying that completeness is an object already given in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A3","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SD4","text":"The system makes epistemic progress depend on an unending movement toward conditions rather than on possession of an absolute totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:DNX1","text":"No completed metaphysical inventory of the world can serve as the legitimate endpoint of cosmological reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:DNX2","text":"No opposition between finite and infinite totality can be treated as objectively decidable independently of the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:DNX3","text":"No epistemic gain follows from extending reason beyond the domain in which its principles can regulate experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:DNX4","text":"No constitutive object corresponds to the totality demanded by reason when that demand is detached from empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SNX1","text":"Absolute totality is unavailable as a completed object of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SNX2","text":"The final ground of the cosmological series cannot function as an empirically established terminus of explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a standpoint from which reason could survey the world-series as a completed whole in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS1","text":"Transcendental treatment of appearances as things in themselves generates the demand for absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS2","text":"The demand for absolute totality generates opposed claims concerning the absolute quantity of the world-series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS3","text":"Critical separation of appearances from things in themselves removes the object-domain required by those opposed claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS4","text":"Removal of the illegitimate object-domain converts reason’s demand from metaphysical determination into a rule for empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5","text":"The regulative rule extends and corrects the employment of understanding throughout possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS6","text":"The resulting expansion of empirical cognition supplies the practical force formerly attributed to a constitutive a priori axiom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AF1","text":"Transcendental misclassification of appearances → demand for absolute totality → cosmological antinomy → critical restriction of reason → empirical-regulative rule → expanded employment of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:AF2","text":"Absolute object-question → procedural question about the necessary extent of regress → dialectical principle → doctrinal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ME1","text":"Cosmological totality is reclassified from an object of determination into a horizon governing inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ME2","text":"The finite-versus-infinite opposition is displaced from an absolute ontological dispute to a question concerning the legitimate progress of empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ME3","text":"Antinomy is transformed from a conflict within the objects of reason into a conflict generated by the misuse of reason’s ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ME4","text":"Limitation of metaphysical reach becomes a condition for the wider and more exact use of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ALT1","text":"Surface — The issue is how far empirical inquiry must proceed through a series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument distinguishes constitutive principles that determine objects from regulative principles that organize the extension of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reason’s apparent defeat in antinomy is converted into a method for correcting the scope and direction of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Totality is denied as a completed thing in itself and retained as an indefinitely approached horizon of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from transcendental error to empirical regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between constitutive object-knowledge and regulative guidance of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT3","text":"The dissolution of the antinomy follows from denying that appearances possess the status of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT4","text":"The system remains dependent on the compressed identification of dialectical reason with a misapplied demand for totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT5","text":"The strongest point is the conversion of limitation into epistemic productivity; the unresolved tension is that a merely regulative principle is claimed to possess force comparable to a constitutive axiom without determining any object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 70","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 70","original_title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 70"},"original_title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI","layer":"CH","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","order":71,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ISCI"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ISCI@R001","sha256":"092f936a88f20564dbbe2e97560010f7943ac5db1e396a61b8da64bc932b533a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS1","text":"Cosmological totality poses a problem because reason seeks an unconditioned limit for an empirical series whose members are always conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS2","text":"The problem is reframed from determining the world’s completed magnitude to determining the legitimate rule of empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS3","text":"No experience can exhibit an absolutely unconditioned condition, because such exhibition would require intuition of a boundary against empty time or space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS4","text":"The world is represented only through a possible empirical regress and is never given as a completed intuition of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS5","text":"The absence of an empirically discoverable absolute limit requires continued ascent from each conditioned appearance to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AS6","text":"The legitimate cosmological conclusion is an indefinite regress governed by a rule, not an assertion that the world is either finitely bounded or actually infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A1","text":"Every condition reached in empirical regress is itself empirically conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A2","text":"No experience can present an absolutely unconditioned condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A3","text":"An absolute temporal or spatial limit of phenomena would have to be experienced as a boundary against void time or void space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A4","text":"Experience of a boundary against mere void is impossible because such an experience has no content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A5","text":"The world of sense is a totality of appearances and is not given as a whole in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","text":"The conception of cosmical totality is constituted through possible empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A7","text":"The magnitude of the world cannot be determined before the empirical regress through which it is represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A8","text":"A claim of actual infinite regress presupposes a determinate infinite cosmical quantity already given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A9","text":"A claim of finite cosmical magnitude presupposes an empirically possible absolute limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A10","text":"Reason’s proper cosmological use is to regulate empirical inquiry by requiring ascent from each conditioned phenomenon to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:A11","text":"The regulative rule requires no termination of possible empirical extension, not an actually unceasing series of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX1","text":"The unconditioned is not an object discoverable within empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX2","text":"The world is not a thing in itself given in totality prior to all regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX3","text":"Cosmical magnitude is not an independently intuited quantity from which the extent of regress can be inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX4","text":"The regress is not regressus in infinitum, understood as a determinate infinite series or quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX5","text":"The world is not finitely bounded by an empirically accessible beginning in time or limit in space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX6","text":"The rule of regress is not a demand that every empirical lineage or phenomenal sequence lack a relative beginning or endpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:NX7","text":"The regulative principle is not a constitutive determination of the world’s magnitude in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D1","text":"Every attained empirical condition remains subject to further conditioning and therefore cannot terminate inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A1","CH_BA94_ISCI:A2","CH_BA94_ISCI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D2","text":"An absolute beginning in time or absolute spatial boundary cannot be empirically established.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A3","CH_BA94_ISCI:A4","CH_BA94_ISCI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D3","text":"The world cannot be classified as either finite or infinite by means of empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A5","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7","CH_BA94_ISCI:A8","CH_BA94_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D4","text":"Cosmical quantity is generated only through the activity of regress and is never available as a completed object before that activity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A5","CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D5","text":"The appropriate designation of cosmological regress is indefiniteness rather than actual infinity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A7","CH_BA94_ISCI:A8","CH_BA94_ISCI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:D6","text":"Relative empirical limits may occur within experience without constituting an absolute limit of the world.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A10","CH_BA94_ISCI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SD1","text":"Cosmological reason changes status when it ceases to determine a completed object and instead governs the open continuation of empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A5","CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SD2","text":"The antinomy of finite versus infinite world arises from treating a rule for possible inquiry as a predicate of a completed object.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7","CH_BA94_ISCI:A8","CH_BA94_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SD3","text":"The world has no absolute empirical quantity because the conditions under which it is represented cannot yield a completed totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A2","CH_BA94_ISCI:A5","CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SD4","text":"Empirical openness is compatible with locally completed investigations because indefinite regress governs the form of inquiry rather than the duration of every factual sequence.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A10","CH_BA94_ISCI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX1","text":"No standpoint external to possible experience can supply the world as a completed object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX2","text":"No determinate cosmical magnitude can precede the synthesis through which magnitude is empirically constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX3","text":"No empirical datum can function as an absolutely final condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX4","text":"No inference from the inexhaustibility of inquiry to the actual infinity of its object is licensed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX5","text":"No local boundary within appearances can become a boundary of appearance as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX1","text":"The completed world-whole is unavailable as an object for theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX2","text":"The unconditioned remains unavailable as an empirical terminus even while it structures reason’s demand for conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX3","text":"Cosmological closure is foreclosed because the condition of representing totality is the regress that prevents its completed presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:FGS1","text":"Conditioned appearance → demand for its condition → further empirically conditioned appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:FGS2","text":"Supposed absolute limit → required intuition of void boundary → contentless experience → impossibility of empirical confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:FGS3","text":"World-whole treated as prior object → determinate finite-or-infinite magnitude → illegitimate anticipation of unreached synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:FGS4","text":"World-whole treated as possible regress → nonterminal rule of conditioning → regressus in indefinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:FGS5","text":"Regulative continuation of inquiry → denial of absolute cosmical limits → suspension of finite/infinite predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AF1","text":"Demand for an unconditioned cosmical totality → impossibility of experiencing an absolute boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AF2","text":"Impossibility of absolute boundary → every attained condition remains subordinated to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AF3","text":"World not given in collective intuition → cosmical magnitude cannot precede empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AF4","text":"No pre-given finite or infinite magnitude → rejection of regressus in infinitum and absolute finitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:AF5","text":"Regress governed by nontermination → cosmological totality designated as indefinite rather than determinate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ME1","text":"The finite/infinite opposition is reclassified from an exhaustive cosmological alternative into a shared misuse of totality as a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ME2","text":"The world is relocated from an object presumed to possess a knowable total magnitude to a horizon represented through possible empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is relocated from an obtainable empirical item to the negative boundary regulating the search for conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ME4","text":"Empirical inquiry is distinguished from dogmatic cosmology by its capacity for indefinite extension without commitment to actual infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ME5","text":"Relative beginnings and limits become compatible with the absence of an absolute beginning or limit of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines the proper answer to whether the world has a temporal beginning, a spatial limit, or an infinite magnitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds cosmological judgment in the conditions of possible empirical regress rather than in properties attributed to a completed world-whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text converts reason from a faculty that declares cosmical facts into a faculty that legislates the nonterminal procedure of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Appearances are temporally and spatially conditioned, while the world as their totality lacks a determinable absolute magnitude within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Totality is cognizable only as the rule-governed openness of synthesis, not as an intuition or concept determinative of a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from the impossibility of empirical absolute limits to indefinite empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT2","text":"The structure depends principally on the distinction between appearances and a world treated as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT3","text":"The decisive compression is the inference that all absolute limits would require experience of void, whose impossibility carries the anti-finitist conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent when “no beginning” and “no absolute limit” are read as restrictions on empirical determination rather than predicates of a completed world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT5","text":"A residual tension remains because the text formulates negative claims about the world while denying that the world as a whole is an object available for determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT6","text":"A further tension remains between the demand for continued conditioning and the allowance that particular empirical series may possess relative initial members or terminal limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if regressus in indefinitum is maintained as a regulative form of possible inquiry rather than converted into an ontological thesis of infinite reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 71","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","context":"Chapter 71","original_title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","original_context":"Chapter 71"},"original_title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2","layer":"CH","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","order":72,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IS_2"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IS_2@R001","sha256":"1724dd37a60877c1f0c77872870846097b394886c76a9c3fcd872adfd7bb695b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS1","text":"Division of an intuitively given whole regresses from a conditioned whole to its constitutive conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS2","text":"Infinite divisibility entails an infinite regress of subdivision, not an actually given infinite multitude of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS3","text":"The distinction between continuous magnitude and discrete multiplicity determines whether infinite division is coherent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS4","text":"Phenomenal substance cannot secure an indivisible remainder because it is not an absolute subject or thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS5","text":"Mathematical antinomies arise from treating a homogeneous phenomenal series as if it admitted completed unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AS6","text":"Dynamical antinomies may permit a non-sensuous intelligible condition outside the phenomenal series, allowing qualified satisfaction of reason without interrupting empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A1","text":"Division of a whole given in intuition is a regress from the conditioned to its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A2","text":"In a continuous decomposition, every attained part remains divisible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A3","text":"Conditions contained together with a given whole generate a regress in infinitum rather than a merely indefinite empirical search.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A4","text":"An infinite regress of division does not contain a completed infinite series of divided members.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A5","text":"Space is a continuum, and every limited space is divisible to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A6","text":"Bodies, as extended phenomena, are divisible to infinity because their divisibility derives from space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A7","text":"Phenomenal substance is a permanent sensuous intuition rather than an absolute subject cognized by a pure category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A8","text":"Infinite divisibility belongs only to a quantum continuum whose parts are determined through progressive division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A9","text":"A quantum discretum contains a determinate numerical multitude of distinct units.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A10","text":"An organized whole presupposed as already divided into infinitely many determinate organized parts is self-contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A11","text":"Mathematical cosmological ideas concern homogeneous sensuous conditions whose members all belong to one phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A12","text":"No homogeneous phenomenal series can contain a mathematically unconditioned member without falsely treating a conditioned member as unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A13","text":"Dynamical synthesis may admit a heterogeneous intelligible condition that is not a member of the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:A14","text":"An intelligible condition can be empirically unconditioned only if its admission leaves empirical regress regular and uninterrupted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX1","text":"Infinite divisibility is not equivalent to an infinite number of parts actually composing a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX2","text":"The regress of decomposition is not a regressus in indefinitum grounded in conditions merely discoverable through subsequent experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX3","text":"A body as phenomenon is not an independently subsisting substance that remains after all spatial composition is removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX4","text":"Infinite division is not applicable to an organized discrete whole as an already determinate infinite organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX5","text":"A determinate multitude of units is not infinitely large merely because its material can be further divided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX6","text":"Mathematical antinomies are not resolved by awarding truth to either contradictory assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX7","text":"An unconditioned mathematical condition is not admissible within the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:NX8","text":"An intelligible dynamical condition is not a sensuous member inserted into the empirical chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D1","text":"The given whole contains every part reachable through division, but it does not contain the complete infinite division as an accomplished series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A1","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D2","text":"The infinity of spatial division signifies inexhaustible determinability of parts through regress rather than actual infinite composition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4","CH_BA94_IS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D3","text":"The divisibility of bodies licenses no inference to an actually infinite aggregate of bodily parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A4","CH_BA94_IS_2:A5","CH_BA94_IS_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D4","text":"No indivisible substantial residue can be inferred from the removal of bodily composition in thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A6","CH_BA94_IS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D5","text":"Experience can determine only how far organization has been encountered, not the transcendental endpoint of the regress of division.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A8","CH_BA94_IS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D6","text":"The principle of reason forbids treating empirical analysis of an extended body as absolutely complete.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A1","CH_BA94_IS_2:A2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D7","text":"Contradictory mathematical totality claims are both false when each presupposes a completed homogeneous phenomenal series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:D8","text":"Dynamical contradiction can be conditionally reframed because its unconditioned ground need not belong to the phenomenal series it conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A13","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SD1","text":"The text relocates infinity from the ontology of completed parts to the rule-governed procedure of regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4","CH_BA94_IS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SD2","text":"The apparent conflict between finite given whole and infinite divisibility dissolves once the whole is distinguished from the never-completed operation of division.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A1","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SD3","text":"The legitimacy of a cosmological idea depends on the kind of synthesis underlying it, not on a uniform demand for totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SD4","text":"Mathematical and dynamical antinomies require non-identical resolutions because homogeneous and heterogeneous conditions have different admissibility structures.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A12","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SD5","text":"Reason can seek an unconditioned ground without converting that ground into an empirically discoverable terminus of natural explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A12","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX1","text":"Phenomenal intuition cannot present a completed infinite multiplicity as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX2","text":"The categories cannot establish the nature of a phenomenal object as though it were a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX3","text":"Empirical regress cannot culminate in an absolutely first or unconditioned phenomenal member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX4","text":"The same model of totality cannot govern both spatial quantity and dynamical dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX5","text":"The unconditioned required by reason cannot be supplied by a completed sensuous series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX1","text":"No final phenomenal inventory can coincide with the idea of absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2","text":"No intelligible ground may be admitted if it functions as a hidden empirical object or disrupts the continuity of empirical explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX3","text":"The demand for unconditioned explanation is foreclosed as an object-level demand within mathematical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX4","text":"Absolute completion is unavailable wherever the conditions are constituted only through the regress that seeks them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS1","text":"Given intuitive whole → division into conditions → each condition remains divisible → infinite regress of determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS2","text":"Infinite regress of determination → absence of completed division → rejection of an actually infinite aggregate of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS3","text":"Spatial continuity → infinite divisibility of extended phenomena → no indivisible phenomenal substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS4","text":"Continuous division versus discrete organization → indeterminate progressive parts versus determinate numerical plurality → exclusion of infinitely pre-organized composite wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS5","text":"Homogeneous phenomenal series → impossibility of mathematical unconditioned totality → reciprocal falsity of opposed mathematical theses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:FGS6","text":"Heterogeneous dynamical synthesis → possible intelligible non-member condition → empirically uninterrupted regress with conditionally satisfied rational demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AF1","text":"Given whole in intuition → regress of division → distinction between infinite regress and infinite aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AF2","text":"Spatial continuum → infinitely divisible body → denial of an absolute phenomenal substratum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AF3","text":"Continuous magnitude versus discrete organization → contradiction in completed infinite organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:AF4","text":"Mathematical homogeneity → both totality claims invalid → dynamical heterogeneity → conditional admissibility of an intelligible ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ME1","text":"Infinity is reclassified from a property of a completed object into a structure of permissible regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ME2","text":"Bodies are reclassified from substances with potentially surviving simple cores into spatial phenomena whose analysis lacks an absolute internal terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ME3","text":"The distinction between continuum and discrete multiplicity prevents infinite divisibility from being transferred to determinate organized plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ME4","text":"Antinomies are divided into mathematical conflicts requiring reciprocal rejection and dynamical conflicts permitting qualified bilateral validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ME5","text":"The unconditioned is repositioned from the head of a phenomenal sequence to a possible intelligible ground outside that sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Limited spaces and bodies are divisible without yielding an actually infinite stock of constituent parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The status of a series depends on whether its conditions are given simultaneously in intuition, constituted through regress, or heterogeneous to the series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Contradictions of reason are resolved by diagnosing the illicit form of totality presupposed by a claim rather than by selecting a victorious thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Completed infinity is denied to phenomenal composition, while infinity is retained as an unending temporal-procedural regress of determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT5","text":"Transcendental — The admissibility of an unconditioned condition is determined by whether it is represented as phenomenal or intelligible and whether it preserves empirical lawfulness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the central distinction between infinite divisibility and an actually infinite plurality of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT2","text":"The axioms also reconstruct the exclusion of an indivisible phenomenal substance and of infinitely pre-given organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT3","text":"The transition from mathematical to dynamical antinomies is reconstructible only if homogeneous and heterogeneous synthesis remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT4","text":"The dynamical resolution remains conditional because the text permits an intelligible condition only under the requirement that empirical regress remain intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT5","text":"The system is coherent at the level of phenomenal series, but it leaves the positive status of the intelligible condition underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on a strict separation between completed totality, procedural regress, phenomenal conditions, and intelligible grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 72","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","context":"Chapter 72","original_title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","original_context":"Chapter 72"},"original_title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3","layer":"CH","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","order":73,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IS_3"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IS_3@R001","sha256":"3ba3b69b50354351d8dad5e8158356558e69ffab331c9cfb5dd9d9df7772928b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS1","text":"The central problem is whether one action can be universally necessitated as a phenomenon and free in relation to its cause without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS2","text":"The apparent contradiction arises when phenomena and their temporal causal series are treated as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS3","text":"The argument is transcendental, reconstructive, and critical rather than empirical or physiological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS4","text":"The distinction between appearance and nonphenomenal ground reindexes causality into empirical and intelligible relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS5","text":"Empirical character secures complete natural determination, while intelligible character permits timeless spontaneity with respect to the same effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS6","text":"Practical ought and blame motivate the representation of reason as causally free without establishing freedom as an object of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AS7","text":"The argument moves from causal antinomy through standpoint differentiation and practical application to the restricted conclusion that nature and freedom are not mutually contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A1","text":"Causality is cogitable only as causality through nature or causality through freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","text":"Every phenomenal event, including a cause’s action, is temporally determined by a preceding phenomenal event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","text":"Unbroken phenomenal causation is a condition of possible experience and admits no exception within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","text":"Cosmological freedom is the faculty of originating a causal series without determination by a preceding sensuous cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A5","text":"Practical freedom from sensuous coercion depends on the possibility of transcendental spontaneity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A6","text":"Phenomena are representations rather than things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A7","text":"Phenomena require a nonphenomenal transcendental ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","text":"The same causal subject can be represented through an empirical character as appearance and an intelligible character as thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","text":"Empirical character is the stable causal rule by which every action of a phenomenal subject is integrated into nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","text":"An intelligible character, if ascribed, is exempt from time, change, and sensuous determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A11","text":"Any effect attributed to intelligible causality must remain fully conditioned and explicable within the empirical causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","text":"Intelligible character cannot be cognized directly and can be thought only through its empirical manifestation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A13","text":"Ought expresses a necessity grounded in a pure conception rather than in the course of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A14","text":"Ought presupposes that reason can produce effects in the phenomenal world according to ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A15","text":"Human self-consciousness represents reason as a nonempirical faculty distinct from sensuous receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:A16","text":"The transcendental argument can establish only the non-opposition of freedom and nature, not freedom’s actuality or real possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX1","text":"No third mode of causality is admitted beyond nature and freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX2","text":"Natural causality admits no exceptional phenomenon within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX3","text":"Freedom is not an empirical property, event, or object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX4","text":"Freedom is not merely the absence of empirical determination but a positive power of causal origination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX5","text":"Free causality is not an absolute temporal beginning within the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX6","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX7","text":"Nature and freedom are not exclusive predicates of an action when attributed under different causal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX8","text":"Intelligible causality is not an additional phenomenal cause cooperating with natural causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX9","text":"Ought is not a description, prediction, or modification of what occurs in nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX10","text":"Intelligible character is not available to theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:NX11","text":"The compatibility argument is not a proof that freedom exists or is really possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D1","text":"The phenomenal causal chain admits neither an absolute beginning nor an attainable absolute totality of conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D2","text":"Treating phenomena as things in themselves makes natural necessity ontologically exhaustive and thereby excludes freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D3","text":"The disjunction between nature and freedom ceases to be exclusive at the level of a single event once causal attribution is indexed to empirical and intelligible relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A1","CH_BA94_IS_3:A6","CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D4","text":"Complete empirical knowledge of an agent’s character and conditions would make every phenomenal action predictable as naturally necessary in principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D5","text":"An intelligible cause can condition the temporal beginning of an empirical series without itself beginning in time.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D6","text":"Physical inquiry can explain actions completely through empirical causes while omitting their unknowable intelligible ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11","CH_BA94_IS_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D7","text":"Practical freedom collapses if phenomenal natural causality is treated as the exhaustive form of causality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D8","text":"Normative necessity both exceeds natural description and presupposes the causal efficacy of rational ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A13","CH_BA94_IS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D9","text":"Rational imputation brackets empirical antecedents for purposes of blame without denying their explanatory necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A13","CH_BA94_IS_3:A14","CH_BA94_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D10","text":"No empirical observation can prove freedom because observation reaches only temporally conditioned effects and empirical character.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","CH_BA94_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:D11","text":"Freedom and natural necessity can coexist because each governs the action under a different kind of condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11","CH_BA94_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD1","text":"The cosmological antinomy is generated by converting the empirical validity of natural causality into a claim about reality in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A6","CH_BA94_IS_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD2","text":"The two-character model assigns complete natural explanation and complete rational attribution to the same action without construing them as competing empirical causes.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD3","text":"Freedom is relocated from a gap within nature to a nonempirical relation underlying an uninterrupted natural series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD4","text":"Normative judgment functions as a practical reason for representing rational causality, not as theoretical evidence of a noumenal fact.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A13","CH_BA94_IS_3:A14","CH_BA94_IS_3:A15","CH_BA94_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD5","text":"Natural causality is constitutive and knowable within experience, whereas freedom remains regulative and merely thinkable beyond empirical determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","CH_BA94_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD6","text":"The framework licenses practices of imputation while withholding knowledge of the actual proportion attributable to freedom, nature, error, or temperament.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","CH_BA94_IS_3:A13","CH_BA94_IS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SD7","text":"The system preserves the conceptual availability of both physical determinism and responsibility by assigning them noninterfering explanatory jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_3:A5","CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_3:A9","CH_BA94_IS_3:A10","CH_BA94_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX1","text":"The causal form governing possible experience does not govern things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX2","text":"Empirical explanatory completeness is not ontological completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX3","text":"Temporal priority is not a necessary form of every conceivable causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX4","text":"Normative necessity is not reducible to natural necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX5","text":"An intelligible cause is not itself an event requiring a prior temporal cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX6","text":"Coexistence of nature and freedom does not require causal competition within one phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX7","text":"Establishing noncontradiction does not require cognition of the mechanism linking intelligible character to empirical effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX1","text":"Freedom occupies the epistemically unavailable ground left when phenomenal causality is denied ontological exhaustiveness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX2","text":"No cognizable bridge connects timeless intelligible determination to its temporally determinate empirical manifestation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX3","text":"An absolute beginning is unavailable both as a first phenomenal event and as a temporal act within reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX4","text":"The system supplies no answer to why an intelligible character produces one empirical character rather than another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX5","text":"Perfect knowledge of moral merit and demerit remains structurally foreclosed because the contribution of freedom cannot be separated from natural determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS1","text":"Exceptionless phenomenal causality → every causal action becomes a conditioned event → no absolute empirical beginning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS2","text":"Reason’s demand for an unconditioned condition → idea of spontaneous causality → conflict with the phenomenal causal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS3","text":"Absolute reality of phenomena → single causal series → mutual exclusion of nature and freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS4","text":"Appearance–thing-in-itself distinction → empirical–intelligible character distinction → dual causal attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS5","text":"Empirical character → temporal conditioning → complete natural necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS6","text":"Intelligible character → exemption from time and sensuous conditions → spontaneous rational causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS7","text":"Same phenomenal effect → full physical explanation plus nonempirical rational imputation → compatibility without causal interference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS8","text":"Ought and blame → practical representation of rational causality → epistemic limitation → conclusion restricted to non-opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AF1","text":"Universal natural causality → impossibility of an empirical first cause → transcendental idea of freedom → antinomy under absolute phenomenal reality → dual-character reframing → compatibility of nature and freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AF2","text":"Human action as phenomenon → empirical determination → rational ought → attribution to intelligible reason → blame without denial of natural explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:AF3","text":"Compatibility established → intelligible ground remains unknowable → actuality and real possibility remain unproved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME1","text":"The exclusive opposition between nature and freedom is replaced by relation-indexed coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME2","text":"Natural determinism is reclassified as exhaustive within experience but not exhaustive of reality in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME3","text":"Freedom is relocated from interruption of natural law to the intelligible grounding of law-governed effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME4","text":"The human subject is reclassified as both a naturally explicable phenomenon and a rationally imputable agent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME5","text":"Physical explanation and practical imputation become distinct operations directed at the same action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME6","text":"Ought is reclassified from a possible natural predicate into the mark of a nonempirical rational order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ME7","text":"Logical compatibility, real possibility, actuality, and empirical provability become distinct epistemic statuses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument asks whether natural necessity and freedom can apply to the same human action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT2","text":"Structural: The empirical and intelligible characters distribute causal determination across phenomenal and nonphenomenal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The antinomy is dissolved by exposing absolute phenomenal reality as the assumption that falsely makes causal modes exclusive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomena exist under temporal succession, whereas intelligible causality is represented as timeless and unchanging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT5","text":"Epistemic/Practical: Knowledge remains confined to empirical character, while practical reason requires the representation of a causality it cannot theoretically cognize.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT1","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the causal antinomy, the dual-character resolution, the practical application, and the restricted conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT2","text":"The relation by which intelligible character determines empirical character remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if the appearance–thing-in-itself distinction and the domain restriction of natural causality are accepted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it preserves exceptionless empirical causality without allowing empirical causality to become ontologically exhaustive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT5","text":"The transition from an unknowable transcendental ground to the attribution of genuine intelligible causality depends on modal compression rather than demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT6","text":"The claim that freedom is not opposed to nature does not by itself establish the real possibility that the text expressly declines to prove.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT7","text":"Complete empirical determination remains in tension with the practical claim that the agent could and ought to have acted otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT8","text":"Decisive blame remains in tension with the admitted impossibility of knowing the actual moral contribution of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT9","text":"The isolated phrase “illusion of transcendental idealism” conflicts with the argument’s reliance on transcendental idealism and its rejection of the absolute reality of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from these elements preserves the movement from antinomy to standpoint differentiation, practical instantiation, and epistemic closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 73","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","context":"Chapter 73","original_title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","original_context":"Chapter 73"},"original_title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","order":74,"parents":["RAW_BA94_IS_4"],"revision":"CH_BA94_IS_4@R001","sha256":"396523816ff300d4ca76d7f324b02957e49a239e2ed48c6266826c72a5597757","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS1","text":"The apparent antinomy arises when the contingent existences of phenomena are treated as requiring an unconditioned existent within their own empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS2","text":"The dynamical regress differs from the mathematical regress because its condition need not be a homogeneous member of the series it conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS3","text":"A necessary condition can be thought without contradiction only as purely intelligible and extramundane, not as the highest phenomenal condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS4","text":"Empirical reason must continue to seek empirical conditions for every phenomenon despite the admissibility of an intelligible ground of the whole series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS5","text":"The solution establishes only compatibility between phenomenal contingency and an intelligible necessary being, not the being’s existence or determinable nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AS6","text":"The fourth antinomy drives reason from cosmological inquiry toward transcendent ideas and the projected investigation of a necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A1","text":"All objects in the sensuous world are phenomena and are conditioned in their existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A2","text":"No phenomenal existence possesses unconditioned necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A3","text":"If condition and conditioned belong to one phenomenal series, every condition is itself conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A4","text":"A dynamical condition need not belong to the empirical series of the conditioned existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","text":"A necessary condition of phenomenal existence must be purely intelligible if it is not to fall under phenomenal contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A6","text":"A purely intelligible necessary being is not a member, including a highest member, of the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A7","text":"Phenomenal contingency is compatible with an intelligible condition independent of empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A8","text":"Reason is required to seek an empirical condition for every empirical member of the series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","text":"The empirical regress may proceed without limit through further empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A10","text":"No existence may be inferred from a condition outside the empirical series by means of empirical reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A11","text":"The absence of empirical explanatory use cannot demonstrate the impossibility of the intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","text":"An intelligible object outside possible experience cannot be cognized through determinate predicates or affirmed as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A13","text":"Transcendent ideas arise when the unconditioned is placed outside possible experience and treated as an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:A14","text":"The necessary being functions initially as a limiting thought required to resolve an antinomy, not as an established object of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX1","text":"The necessary being is not a phenomenal substance, a worldly cause, or the highest member of an empirical causal sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX2","text":"The solution is not an assertion that a necessary being exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX3","text":"Phenomenal contingency is not evidence that an intelligible necessary condition is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX4","text":"The unrestricted demand for empirical conditions is not permission to seek an empirical cause beyond the empirical world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX5","text":"The intelligible is not invalidated merely because it cannot explain or be exhibited within phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX6","text":"A transcendent intelligible object is not determinable by predicates borrowed from empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:NX7","text":"The unconditioned is not obtainable as a completed object through the empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D1","text":"The fourth antinomy dissolves when its opposed claims are assigned to heterogeneous relations: empirical dependence within phenomena and intelligible independence outside them.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A1","CH_BA94_IS_4:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D2","text":"The necessary being can be conceived as unconditioned only by being excluded from the domain in which every existence is empirically conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A2","CH_BA94_IS_4:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D3","text":"The empirical investigation of nature remains methodologically unchanged by the thought of an intelligible ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A8","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_IS_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D4","text":"The possibility of an intelligible ground is defensible only negatively, through the failure of empirical contingency to disprove it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A7","CH_BA94_IS_4:A10","CH_BA94_IS_4:A11","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D5","text":"Reason may admit an unknown transcendental ground of phenomena without possessing knowledge of its existence or inner constitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A11","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:D6","text":"The transition from cosmological ideas to transcendent ideas occurs when the unconditioned is relocated from the totality of empirical conditions to an object beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_IS_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SD1","text":"The antinomy is transformed from a dispute about competing objects into a jurisdictional dispute about whether empirical and intelligible conditions may occupy the same explanatory field without belonging to one series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6","CH_BA94_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SD2","text":"The system protects empirical science by making its regress indefinitely exhaustive within experience while preventing that regress from deciding the status of what lies beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A8","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_IS_4:A10","CH_BA94_IS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SD3","text":"The necessary being is preserved as a permissible boundary-concept precisely by being deprived of empirical membership, empirical causal efficacy, and determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6","CH_BA94_IS_4:A10","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SD4","text":"The fourth antinomy becomes the threshold at which a merely regulative restriction on reason prepares a new transcendent inquiry whose object cannot yet be known.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A13","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14","CH_BA94_IS_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the assumption that empirical dependence and absolute dependence are one and the same relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX2","text":"The system excludes any inference from the incompleteness of empirical explanation to the existence of a supersensible explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the converse inference that the empirical world’s self-contained methodological order exhausts all possible being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a necessary being that could be empirically located, empirically conditioned, or empirically known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the conversion of the unconditioned from a limiting demand of reason into a presented object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX1","text":"The discourse is organized around the unavailable object of an unconditioned existence that reason must think in order to close dependence but cannot know in order to avoid transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX2","text":"The resolution depends on the foreclosure of any single domain in which phenomenal contingency and absolute necessity could confront one another as properties of the same object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX3","text":"The projected cognition of intelligible things is structured by an absence of intuition, leaving analogy and pure conception without constitutive access to their object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS1","text":"Phenomenal existence → universal empirical conditioning → impossibility of an unconditioned phenomenal member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS2","text":"Dynamical regress → non-homogeneity of condition and conditioned → admissibility of a condition outside the empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS3","text":"Intelligible relocation of the unconditioned → removal from contingency → formal compatibility with phenomenal dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS4","text":"Empirical regulative law → unbounded search for empirical conditions → prohibition on empirical transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS5","text":"Compatibility without proof → intelligible ground as permissible but indeterminate → suspension of ontological assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS6","text":"Relocation beyond experience → transcendent idea → projected inquiry into the necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:AF1","text":"Conditioned phenomenal series → impossibility of necessary phenomenal existence → distinction between mathematical and dynamical regress → intelligible extramundane condition → coexistence of empirical contingency and possible necessity → restriction of empirical inference → transition to transcendent investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ME1","text":"The necessary being is reclassified from a missing empirical cause into a possible intelligible ground that cannot enter empirical explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ME2","text":"The opposition between universal phenomenal contingency and necessary existence is recoded as non-contradictory because the claims concern different orders of conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ME3","text":"Infinite empirical regress becomes compatible with a non-empirical ground, rather than functioning as evidence either for or against such a ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ME4","text":"Empirical reason and pure reason receive distinct jurisdictions: the former investigates conditions of appearances, while the latter may think a limiting intelligible condition without knowing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ME5","text":"The fourth antinomy is converted from a cosmological impasse into a passage toward rational theology, while retaining the prohibition on constitutive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text resolves whether a necessary being can coexist with the universal contingency of phenomenal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — The resolution depends on separating empirical causal series from an intelligible condition that does not belong to their membership.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text limits both dogmatic rationalism, which infers supersensible existence, and empirical exclusivism, which declares the supersensible impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomena are constituted as endlessly conditionable representations, whereas the unconditioned can be thought only as outside the temporal-empirical order of dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Thinkability exceeds knowability: an intelligible ground may be admitted as possible while its existence and predicates remain unavailable to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the argument if phenomenal contingency, the non-membership of dynamical conditions, and the restriction of empirical inference are retained together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT2","text":"The system is coherent at the level of compatibility because it does not derive necessary existence from phenomenal dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes the methodological infinity of empirical regress from an ontological assertion about what may lie beyond it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how an intelligible ground can be positively conceived without importing empirical predicates by analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT5","text":"The movement toward a subsequent investigation of a necessary being strains the prior prohibition against affirming the existence or determinate nature of transcendent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT6","text":"The distinction between permissible thought and illicit cognition bears the full burden of the resolution and cannot itself be established by empirical evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 74","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","context":"Chapter 74","original_title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","original_context":"Chapter 74"},"original_title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","order":75,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_3"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_3@R001","sha256":"02eecdabffeaff021f3beac7a8a312add7aeaaffa4c6e10fd9ac8eaabc93f198","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS1","text":"Pure conceptions provide forms of thought but require sensuous conditions for objective presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS2","text":"Ideas exceed possible phenomenal presentation and organize experience through a unity that experience never fully attains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3","text":"An ideal is an idea individualized and completely determined by a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS4","text":"Unlike a merely formal idea, an ideal has practical force as an archetype for judging and perfecting action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS5","text":"The ideal remains non-objective and unattainable, yet functions as a standard for measuring empirical incompleteness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AS6","text":"The argument preserves rational ideals by excluding both empirical embodiment and indeterminate imaginative representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A1","text":"Objective presentation to the mind requires sensuous conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A2","text":"Pure conceptions contain the form of thought without independently containing objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A3","text":"Pure conceptions can acquire concrete application when related to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A4","text":"Ideas cannot be presented in concreto by any phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A5","text":"Ideas provide a perfection unattainable by empirical cognition and a unity toward which experience can only approximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A6","text":"An ideal is an idea considered as an individual object determined by the idea alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A7","text":"Complete ideal determination requires selecting one conforming predicate from each set of contradictory predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A8","text":"Human reason contains ideals in addition to ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A9","text":"Ideals possess practical and regulative power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A10","text":"An idea provides a rule, while an ideal provides an archetype for the complete determination of its copy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","text":"An ideal can lack objective reality without being a chimera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A12","text":"An ideal enables reason to estimate the incompleteness of objects through comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A13","text":"Empirical realization of the ideal as an exemplary object is impracticable and absurd.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A14","text":"Ideals of imagination lack determinate rules and cannot function as standards of production or appreciation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:A15","text":"Reason conceives its ideal object as completely determinable by a priori principles despite the absence of empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX1","text":"Pure conceptions are not independently objective objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX2","text":"Ideas are not phenomenal contents available in concrete intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX3","text":"The ideal is not merely an abstract rule but an idea individualized as a complete object of determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX4","text":"Rational ideals are not creative or constitutive objects in the Platonic sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX5","text":"Lack of objective reality does not reduce the ideal to a chimera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX6","text":"The ideal is not realizable as a perfect empirical example.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX7","text":"The rational ideal is not an indeterminate image produced by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX8","text":"Imaginative ideals are not rules or standards for explanation, examination, production, or appreciation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:NX9","text":"Moral conceptions are not perfectly pure conceptions insofar as empirical pleasure or pain enters their foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D1","text":"The movement from pure conception to idea to ideal progressively withdraws objective presentation while increasing systematic and determinative function.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A2","CH_BA94_SI_3:A4","CH_BA94_SI_3:A6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D2","text":"An ideal converts a general rational rule into an individual archetype against which copies can be judged.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A7","CH_BA94_SI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D3","text":"The ideal can measure empirical deficiency without itself being an empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D4","text":"Unattainability does not cancel practical efficacy because the ideal operates as a regulative standard rather than as an attainable state.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D5","text":"Moral action can be regulated by an ideal even when moral concepts retain empirical elements.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:A10","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D6","text":"Attempted empirical embodiment destroys the ideal’s perfection by introducing conditions that the ideal excludes.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:A13","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D7","text":"Imaginative representation cannot perform the rational function of an ideal because it lacks determinate rule-governed structure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A10","CH_BA94_SI_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:D8","text":"The ideal is transcendent because it lacks empirical conditions but remains non-arbitrary because its determination follows a priori principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A1","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD1","text":"Rational judgment can possess objective standardizing force without possessing an objectively given standard-object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD2","text":"The ideal’s distance from empirical realization is what preserves its function as a universal measure of incompleteness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD3","text":"Perfectibility is constituted as an indefinite orientation toward a standard rather than as the achievement of a final empirical condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD4","text":"The ideal separates normative validity from ontological existence: it lacks actuality while retaining critical and practical authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD5","text":"The system makes the absence of a sensible image a condition for preserving the rational ideal’s determinacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A13","CH_BA94_SI_3:A14","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SD6","text":"The ideal is simultaneously an individual object of thought and a non-objective standard, with the tension contained by locating its individuality in rational determination rather than intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX1","text":"The validity of the ideal does not depend on its being given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX2","text":"No completed empirical cognition can supply the perfection or unity prescribed by the ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX3","text":"No empirical specimen can ground the ideal’s normative authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX4","text":"No sensible image can preserve complete rational determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX5","text":"Normative force does not require creative, constitutive, or object-producing causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX6","text":"Empirical content cannot be allowed to determine the formal structure of the rational ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX1","text":"The empirical world is structurally incapable of containing the ideal as a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX2","text":"Experience cannot become the self-sufficient ground of rational normativity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX3","text":"The permanent gap between ideal and experience cannot be closed without abolishing the ideal’s function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX4","text":"A sensible representation cannot substitute for a determinate a priori archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX5","text":"The ideal cannot be converted from a regulative standard into a constitutively existing entity without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS1","text":"Sensuous condition → objective presentation; absence of sensuous condition → pure form of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS2","text":"Phenomenal application → concrete empirical conception; impossibility of phenomenal presentation → idea beyond empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS3","text":"Idea + individuation + exhaustive a priori determination → ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS4","text":"Ideal + archetypal function → comparison, criticism, and practical perfectibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS5","text":"Denial of objective reality + retention of regulative function → non-chimerical transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS6","text":"Attempted empirical embodiment or imaginative depiction → loss of determinate perfection and failure of standardization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AF1","text":"Sensuous condition of objectivity → formal conception → idea beyond possible intuition → individualized ideal → archetypal comparison → practical perfectibility → exclusion of empirical embodiment and imaginative substitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:AF2","text":"Objective reality is progressively withdrawn while systematic unity, determination, criticism, and practical regulation are retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME1","text":"The ideal is reclassified from a possible object of knowledge into a regulative standard of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME2","text":"Non-attainment is reclassified from failure into the condition of practical perfectibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME3","text":"Empirical incompleteness becomes measurable through comparison with a non-empirical archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME4","text":"The rational ideal is separated from the sensible prototype and the imaginative image.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME5","text":"Individuality is detached from empirical existence and relocated in complete rational determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ME6","text":"The attempted representation of perfection is exposed as a degradation of the standard it seeks to exhibit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The passage distinguishes pure conceptions, ideas, ideals of reason, and ideals of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument organizes the relation between sensibility, phenomenal cognition, understanding, and reason according to degrees of objective presentation and determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The passage converts unattainable perfection into a governing standard for criticism, comparison, and self-reformation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The ideal is transcendent and non-objective, while experience remains an indefinitely incomplete approximation to rational unity and perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the hierarchy from pure conception through idea to ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT2","text":"The extracted system preserves the distinction between objective reality and regulative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the exclusion of both empirical exemplification and imaginative indeterminacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether the ideal’s individuality is epistemic, ontological, or a deliberately suspended hybrid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT5","text":"The central unresolved tension is that the ideal must be sufficiently individual for complete determination while lacking the objective reality of an individual empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT6","text":"The argument is internally coherent once the ideal’s authority is assigned a regulative rather than constitutive status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction depends on retaining the text’s distinction between rational determination and sensible depiction; collapsing that distinction dissolves the machine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 75","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","context":"Chapter 75","original_title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","original_context":"Chapter 75"},"original_title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","order":76,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_4"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_4@R001","sha256":"8b77a7fde639fbb224a9aae6e3831688a8766acfb00e967ec1cb7a40656336f2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS1","text":"Complete determination appears to require a total field of possible predicates rather than merely the logical exclusion of contradictories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS2","text":"The material condition of complete determination is represented as the sum-total of reality, because negations are intelligible only as limitations of affirmative reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS3","text":"The sum-total of reality becomes an individual transcendental ideal, the ens realissimum, only within reason’s demand for complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS4","text":"Reason analogically treats the ideal as the prototype and ground of the possibility of determinate things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS5","text":"The ideal supplies a regulative condition for thinking objects but supplies no warrant for asserting an objectively existing primal being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS6","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when a principle valid for empirical objects is extended to things in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AS7","text":"The reified totality of empirical reality is successively objectivized, hypostatized, and personified as a supreme intelligence or God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A1","text":"Logical determinability requires that only one predicate of each contradictory pair belong to a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","text":"Complete determination requires that a thing be related to the whole domain of possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A3","text":"Complete determination concerns the material content of possibility, not merely the logical form of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A4","text":"The totality required for complete determination cannot be given in concreto and therefore has the status of an idea of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A5","text":"Transcendental affirmation signifies reality or being, whereas transcendental negation signifies privation, absence, or limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A6","text":"Negative determinations are cognizable only through their relation to corresponding affirmative determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A7","text":"The material of all possibility is therefore the sum-total of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","text":"The idea of the sum-total of reality determines an individual ideal of pure reason, the ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A9","text":"Determinate things are thinkable through partial participation in, and limitation of, the totality of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","text":"The transcendental ideal is a condition for the thought of complete determination, not an assertion of the existence of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A11","text":"The ideal must be thought as simple because derivative beings cannot constitute the primal condition from which they derive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A12","text":"Sensuous objects are possible for cognition only in relation to the unified whole of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A13","text":"Reason naturally mistakes the empirical condition of possible experience for a transcendental condition of things in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:A14","text":"The conversion of distributive empirical unity into a collective individual whole produces dialectical hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX1","text":"Complete determination is not identical with the principle of contradiction or the law of excluded middle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX2","text":"Logical negation is not transcendental negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX3","text":"A negative predicate is not a positive representation of an independent non-being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX4","text":"The sum-total of reality is not a genus containing predicates as subordinate species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX5","text":"The ens realissimum is not an aggregate composed from derivative beings or partial realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX6","text":"The transcendental ideal is not evidence that a corresponding being objectively exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX7","text":"Empirical totality is not an individual object containing all reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:NX8","text":"The regulative unity of experience is not a property inhering in phenomena independently of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D1","text":"Complete knowledge of a thing would require its positive and negative determination against the total field of possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_4:A3","CH_BA94_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D2","text":"Every determinate negation presupposes a prior affirmative reality of which it is a limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D3","text":"The totality of possibility is represented as omnitudo realitatis rather than as a totality of positive and negative items of equal rank.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_4:A6","CH_BA94_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D4","text":"The ens realissimum functions as an archetype from which finite things are conceived as imperfectly determined copies.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A7","CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D5","text":"The logical form of disjunctive determination supplies the analogue through which reason thinks finite determination by exclusion from a total field.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A1","CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D6","text":"The predicates of unity, simplicity, sufficiency, and eternity can be attached to the ideal only after it is treated as an individual being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A11","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:D7","text":"The demand for an unconditioned ground of possibility exceeds the legitimate use for which the ideal is required.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SI_4:A13","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD1","text":"Finite plurality is structurally subordinated to a prior affirmative totality because difference is construed as graduated limitation rather than independent positivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_4:A7","CH_BA94_SI_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD2","text":"The transcendental ideal mediates between the logical demand for exhaustive determination and theology’s representation of a supreme being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A1","CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD3","text":"The same structure that permits systematic empirical determination also generates the illusion of an ontologically supreme object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SI_4:A12","CH_BA94_SI_4:A13","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD4","text":"The ideal is indispensable as a condition of reason’s systematic completion while invalid as an object of theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A4","CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD5","text":"Transcendental theology is generated by a category error in which a subjective condition of complete determination is converted into an objectively existing cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SI_4:A13","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SD6","text":"The critique of the ideal does not abolish the idea of totality but relocates it from constitutive ontology to regulative reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A4","CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SI_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX1","text":"Possibility is not grounded by a collection of independent determinations lacking a common material condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX2","text":"Negation is not ontologically co-original with affirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX3","text":"Empirical conditions of objectivity are not licensed to determine the structure of supersensible being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX4","text":"A conceptual necessity for totality is not a proof of an object corresponding to that totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX5","text":"The unity required by cognition is not thereby a unity possessed by reality independently of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX1","text":"No theoretical passage is available from the necessity of reason’s ideal to the existence of an unconditioned being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX2","text":"No finite cognition can possess the totality of possibility required for complete determination in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX3","text":"No positive representation of absolute non-being is available within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX4","text":"No theological object can be secured merely by completing the systematic unity demanded by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS1","text":"Contradictory-predicate exclusion → logical determinability of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS2","text":"Demand for exhaustive determination → relation of each thing to all possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS3","text":"Transcendental distinction between reality and privation → primacy of affirmative reality as the material of possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS4","text":"Totality of affirmative reality → idea of omnitudo realitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS5","text":"Idea of total reality → individual ideal of ens realissimum and prototype of finite determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS6","text":"Restriction to possible experience → legitimate regulative use of the ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:FGS7","text":"Extension beyond possible experience → reification, hypostatization, personification, and transcendental theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF1","text":"Logical determinability → complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF2","text":"Complete determination → totality of possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF3","text":"Totality of predicates → primacy of affirmative reality over negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF4","text":"Totality of reality → transcendental ideal of an ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF5","text":"Ideal as condition of thought → regulative prototype of determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF6","text":"Empirical-condition transference → supposed primal being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:AF7","text":"Supposed primal being → dialectical theology without objective warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME1","text":"Determination is reclassified from pairwise logical exclusion to a relation between each object and a total field of possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME2","text":"Negativity is reclassified from an equal logical opposite into privative limitation of affirmative reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME3","text":"The ens realissimum is reclassified from a theological object into a transcendental ideal required by reason’s demand for completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME4","text":"The possibility of empirical objects is linked to the unity of possible experience rather than to isolated sensory items.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME5","text":"Theological personification is reclassified as a progressive transformation of an epistemic-regulative representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ME6","text":"The decisive boundary is relocated from the conceivability of a supreme being to the legitimacy of converting an idea into an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes logical determinability from transcendental complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Complete determination depends upon a totality of reality in which finite predicates function as partial limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique proceeds by preserving reason’s regulative need for totality while blocking its constitutive and theological use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The primal, supreme, and all-encompassing being is treated as a projected endpoint of reason’s demand for unconditioned completeness rather than as an established entity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the transition from logical exclusion to transcendental complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT2","text":"The axioms reconstruct the derivation of the ideal from the asymmetry between affirmative reality and privative negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning why the totality of reality must be represented as an individual rather than merely as an ideal whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent only if the ideal’s regulative necessity is kept distinct from its alleged objective existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT5","text":"The principal fault line lies in the movement from the condition for empirical determination to the claim that all possibility requires a single highest ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT6","text":"The argument relies on the contested priority of reality over negation, since the derivation of omnitudo realitatis depends upon negation being merely privative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement when it ends with the diagnosis of hypostatization rather than with the establishment or denial of God’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 76","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","context":"Chapter 76","original_title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","original_context":"Chapter 76"},"original_title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","order":77,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIAE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIAE@R001","sha256":"9b17434b5b3d74c11dd83a6e0f0c5ceffb7d37c852d9946744850558735e0c8f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1","text":"Reason seeks an unconditioned ground because regress through contingent conditions cannot yield complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS2","text":"The natural ascent from contingent existence to necessary existence selects complete reality as the sole candidate for unconditioned necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS3","text":"The selection of complete reality depends on an invalid exclusion: failure to derive necessity from a limited concept does not prove that limited being conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS4","text":"The cosmological argument therefore identifies neither the determinate properties nor the unique concept of a necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS5","text":"Practical reason can conditionally lend authority to an objectively insufficient speculative conclusion when obligation requires a Supreme Being as its effective ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AS6","text":"Speculative proofs of Deity exhaust three routes—physico-theological, cosmological, and ontological—and empirical argument is ultimately directed by transcendental reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","text":"Reason requires an unconditioned condition to complete a series of conditioned grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A2","text":"Contingent existence is represented as requiring a cause, and the causal regress is represented as requiring an unconditionally necessary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A3","text":"The existence of a necessary being cannot be established merely by conceiving such a being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A4","text":"Once necessary existence is presupposed, reason seeks a concept compatible with absolute necessity rather than a concept that produces existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A5","text":"A being containing all reality is conceived as containing the conditions of all possible things and as independent of higher conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A6","text":"Complete reality is the concept most compatible with unconditioned necessity among available concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A7","text":"The absence of an intelligible mark of unconditioned necessity in a concept does not entail that its object is conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A8","text":"Compatibility with necessary existence does not establish either the existence or the determinate properties of a necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A9","text":"Practical obligation may supply subjective rational authority to a speculative proposition that lacks objective sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A10","text":"Every speculative proof of a Supreme Being proceeds from determinate experience, indeterminate empirical existence, or pure a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A11","text":"The transcendental idea of an unconditioned ground guides the empirical ascent toward a highest cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:A12","text":"The felt necessity of terminating inquiry does not itself constitute cognition of an objectively necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX1","text":"A concept manufactured by reason is not thereby an objectively existing being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX2","text":"Absolute necessity is not derivable from the mere concept of a possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX3","text":"The ens realissimum is not shown to satisfy every requirement of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX4","text":"A limited being is not disproved as necessary merely because its necessity cannot be read from its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX5","text":"The inability to locate the unconditioned in a concept is not proof of the opposite predicate, conditioned existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX6","text":"The cosmological argument is not entitled to infer the attributes of a necessary being from its selected candidate concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7","text":"Practical reinforcement is not an objective speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8","text":"No speculative proof lies outside the physico-theological, cosmological, and ontological alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D1","text":"The cosmological argument presupposes necessary existence before it begins to identify the being alleged to possess it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A3","CH_BA94_SIAE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D2","text":"Complete reality functions as a preferred candidate for necessity rather than as a demonstrated existent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A5","CH_BA94_SIAE:A6","CH_BA94_SIAE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D3","text":"The purported uniqueness of the ens realissimum collapses because other concepts may be necessary without making their necessity conceptually intelligible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A6","CH_BA94_SIAE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D4","text":"The inference from a highest explanatory condition to a uniquely necessary Supreme Being exceeds what causal regress can establish.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIAE:A2","CH_BA94_SIAE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D5","text":"Practical reason can alter the rational balance of assent without converting subjective need into theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A8","CH_BA94_SIAE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:D6","text":"Empirical theology is structurally dependent on a prior transcendental demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A10","CH_BA94_SIAE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SD1","text":"The proof-machine converts a demand for explanatory closure into a candidate ontology, then mistakes the candidate’s comparative adequacy for existential determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIAE:A4","CH_BA94_SIAE:A5","CH_BA94_SIAE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SD2","text":"The cosmological proof depends on the ontological register it appears to avoid, because its identification of the necessary being proceeds through the privileged concept of complete reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A3","CH_BA94_SIAE:A4","CH_BA94_SIAE:A5","CH_BA94_SIAE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SD3","text":"The three proof-types differ by their starting material but converge on the same transcendental aspiration to transform conditioned cognition into knowledge of an unconditioned total ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIAE:A10","CH_BA94_SIAE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4","text":"The practical supplementation of speculative insufficiency preserves the regulative force of the Supreme Being while leaving its theoretical objectivity unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A8","CH_BA94_SIAE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:DNX1","text":"Logical completeness is not identical with ontological necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:DNX2","text":"Explanatory termination is not identical with a real terminus existing independently of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:DNX3","text":"Conceptual superiority among possible beings is not identical with existential singularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:DNX4","text":"Human reason has no speculative entitlement to convert its inability to proceed further into knowledge that no further ground is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX1","text":"A final answer to the regress of conditions is unavailable as speculative cognition even where reason cannot cease demanding one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX2","text":"The unconditioned cannot be made present as an object of knowledge by any combination of experience, causal ascent, and pure conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX3","text":"The desired coincidence of rational necessity, conceptual necessity, and real existence is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS1","text":"Conditioned existence → causal regress → demand for an unconditioned and necessary ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS2","text":"Presupposed necessary existence → comparison of possible concepts → complete reality selected as the most adequate candidate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS3","text":"Failure to derive necessity from limited concepts → illicit exclusion of limited candidates → false uniqueness of the ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS4","text":"Recognition that non-derivability does not entail conditioned existence → collapse of uniqueness → incapacity to determine the necessary being’s properties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS5","text":"Objective speculative insufficiency → possible practical supplementation → rational adherence without theoretical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS6","text":"Exhaustion of proof-paths → reduction to ontological, cosmological, and physico-theological forms → announced failure of speculative theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AF1","text":"Contingency → regress toward necessity → search for an unconditioned concept → ens realissimum → invalid exclusion of alternatives → indeterminate necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AF2","text":"Practical need → conditional reinforcement of assent → distinction between rational obligation and theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:AF3","text":"Three speculative routes → transcendental idea as their hidden guide → reverse critical order beginning with the transcendental proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ME1","text":"The question shifts from whether a highest being can be imagined to whether conceptual adequacy can warrant existential attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ME2","text":"Necessary existence is divided into a presupposed existential claim, a conceptually intelligible predicate, and a practical postulate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ME3","text":"The ens realissimum is reclassified from demonstrated ultimate reality to a maximally suitable but non-exclusive conceptual candidate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ME4","text":"Empirical theology is reclassified as derivative of transcendental reason rather than as an independent ascent from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between empirical and a priori proofs is subordinated to a shared demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text tests the inference from contingent existence to an absolutely necessary Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument separates causal regress, conceptual selection, existential assertion, and practical endorsement as non-equivalent operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical examination reverses the order of ordinary reason’s ascent in order to expose the transcendental idea governing empirical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Conditioned beings appear as mutable and dependent, while the demanded unconditioned is posited as self-sufficient, complete, and exempt from further regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Reason’s unavoidable need for totality exceeds its capacity to cognize an unconditioned object as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the transition from causal dependence to the demand for necessary existence and from necessary existence to the concept of complete reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT2","text":"The decisive critical operation is preserved only if conceptual non-derivability is kept distinct from proof of the contrary predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent as a diagnosis of the cosmological proof because it distinguishes presupposition, selection, and demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether the initial causal regress to a necessary being is itself valid, because that premise is provisionally conceded for the argument’s internal critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT5","text":"The principal fault line lies between reason’s demand for an ultimate explanatory ground and its lack of entitlement to identify that demand with an existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT6","text":"A second fault line lies between practical necessity, which may authorize commitment, and speculative necessity, which would require objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the text’s movement only if the classification of three proof-types is treated as a closure of possible speculative routes rather than as an additional proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 77","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","context":"Chapter 77","original_title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","original_context":"Chapter 77"},"original_title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","order":78,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIIO"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIIO@R001","sha256":"ef424f1c69a69fa402f9015a6dfb5fb456bfc90dd1e8d9a81b52c04b2e7801c4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS1","text":"The problem is whether the concept of an absolutely necessary being can establish that such a being exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS2","text":"The argument distinguishes necessity belonging to judgements from necessity belonging to things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS3","text":"The argument tests absolute necessity by contradiction and finds that suppressing a thing together with its predicates yields no contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS4","text":"The ontological proof attempts to derive existence from the concept of an ens realissimum containing all reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS5","text":"The proof fails because existence is not a real predicate that augments the concept of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS6","text":"Existence can be cognized only through connection with possible experience, whereas a Supreme Being is an idea beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AS7","text":"The movement is from apparent logical necessity to the exposure of its illicit conversion into real existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A1","text":"An absolutely necessary being is initially only an idea of reason, not an object whose reality has been established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A2","text":"Logical necessity concerns the relation of predicate to subject within a judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A3","text":"The necessity of a judgement does not establish the absolute necessity of the thing judged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A4","text":"Contradiction is the criterion of impossibility for pure a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A5","text":"No contradiction arises when a subject and all its predicates are simultaneously suppressed in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A6","text":"The non-existence of a thing cannot be rendered contradictory merely by making existence belong to its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A7","text":"Logical possibility, defined by freedom from contradiction, does not establish the real possibility of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","text":"Existence is not a real predicate that adds a determination to the concept of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A9","text":"An existential judgement posits an object in relation to a concept without enlarging the concept’s content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A10","text":"The existence of an object requires grounds beyond its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A11","text":"Knowledge of existence belongs to experience, whether immediately through perception or mediately through empirical inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:A12","text":"No a priori criterion establishes the real possibility or existence of an object of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX1","text":"Absolute necessity is not established by reason’s need for an unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX2","text":"The impossibility of denying a predicate under a posited subject is not the impossibility of denying the subject itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX3","text":"Geometrical necessity is not evidence for the existence of an absolutely necessary thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX4","text":"The ens realissimum is not shown to exist by containing existence within its alleged totality of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX5","text":"The absence of contradiction in a concept is not evidence that its object is really possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX6","text":"Existence is not a property, perfection, or determining content added to an object’s concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX7","text":"Complete conceptual determination is not existential determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:NX8","text":"An idea of reason is not an instrument for extending cognition to supersensible existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D1","text":"Every alleged proof of absolute necessity must show that the subject itself cannot be suppressed, not merely that a predicate follows from a posited subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A2","CH_BA94_SIIO:A3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D2","text":"The claim that God’s non-existence is contradictory fails because negating God negates omnipotence and every other predicate together with the subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A4","CH_BA94_SIIO:A5","CH_BA94_SIIO:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D3","text":"The ontological proof equivocates between the necessity of positing predicates under an assumed concept and the necessity of positing the object itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A2","CH_BA94_SIIO:A3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D4","text":"Defining a possible being as containing all reality cannot establish that the being is actual.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A7","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","CH_BA94_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D5","text":"If existence were analytically contained in a concept, existential propositions would be tautological rather than synthetic.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","CH_BA94_SIIO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D6","text":"A concept remains the same in content whether its object is merely possible or actually given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","CH_BA94_SIIO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D7","text":"The concept of a most real being leaves unresolved whether any corresponding object is given in experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A10","CH_BA94_SIIO:A11","CH_BA94_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:D8","text":"The possibility of a Supreme Being cannot be demonstrated by analysis alone because the real synthesis of its predicates lacks an a priori warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A7","CH_BA94_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SD1","text":"The argument converts the question of divine existence from conceptual inclusion to the conditions under which an object can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","CH_BA94_SIIO:A10","CH_BA94_SIIO:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SD2","text":"Reason’s demand for the unconditioned functions regulatively as a limit-idea rather than constitutively as knowledge of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A1","CH_BA94_SIIO:A11","CH_BA94_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SD3","text":"The purported transition from ideal completeness to actual being is structurally blocked because no amount of conceptual content supplies the act of existential positing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A8","CH_BA94_SIIO:A9","CH_BA94_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SD4","text":"The ontological proof mistakes a rule governing thought under an assumption for a rule governing what exists independently of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A2","CH_BA94_SIIO:A3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SD5","text":"The critique preserves the thinkability of the Supreme Being as an idea while foreclosing its theoretical demonstration as an existent object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A1","CH_BA94_SIIO:A7","CH_BA94_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX1","text":"Reality is not exhaustively determined by what can be analytically unpacked from a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX2","text":"Thought does not create or secure an object merely by representing it without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX3","text":"The unconditioned is not available as an object under the conditions that make objects cognitively determinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX4","text":"Pure reason cannot cross from logical consistency to objective existence without an independent mode of givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX5","text":"A predicate’s necessary dependence on a subject does not imply that the subject has necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX1","text":"No conceptual totality can abolish the gap between possible representation and actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX2","text":"No purely a priori cognition can authorize a determinate claim about supersensible existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX3","text":"The absolute ground sought by reason remains unavailable as theoretically knowable being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX4","text":"The argument excludes a metaphysics in which being is derivable from essence by analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS1","text":"Reason requires an unconditioned ground → the concept of an absolutely necessary being is formed as an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS2","text":"Necessary judgement → conditional predicate-necessity → illicit conversion into necessary existence of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS3","text":"Suppression of predicate alone → contradiction; suppression of subject and predicates together → no contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS4","text":"Ens realissimum contains all reality → existence is inserted as conceptual content → the proof becomes tautological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS5","text":"Existence is mere positing rather than a real predicate → conceptual completeness cannot yield actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:FGS6","text":"Existence requires possible experience → supersensible idea lacks an existential criterion → ontological proof is invalid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AF1","text":"Demand for an absolutely necessary being → distinction between logical and real necessity → test by contradiction → dissolution of necessary-subject claim → analysis of existence → restriction of existential cognition to experience → invalidation of ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AF2","text":"Conceptual possibility → alleged inclusion of existence → exposure of tautological positing → unresolved objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:AF3","text":"Idea of Supreme Being → regulative intelligibility → theoretical non-demonstrability of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ME1","text":"Necessity is redistributed from objects to the conditional structure of judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ME2","text":"Existence is reclassified from conceptual content to the relation between a concept and an object’s givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ME3","text":"The opposition between possible and real objects becomes decisive without making actuality an additional predicate of the real object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ME4","text":"The Supreme Being is reclassified from a demonstrable object of theoretical knowledge to an idea whose objective reality remains unproved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5","text":"The field of legitimate existential claims is restricted to objects connected with possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text denies that the ontological or Cartesian argument proves God’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text separates analytic relations among concepts from synthetic claims that an object exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects metaphysical proof from conceptual definition toward an inquiry into the conditions of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Actuality is not an intensified form of possibility but requires an object’s placement within experiential reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT5","text":"Limitative — The unconditioned remains thinkable as reason’s limiting demand while unavailable as an object of determinate theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central refutation because they establish the distinction between logical necessity, real possibility, and existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT2","text":"The argument depends decisively on the claim that contradiction is the sole a priori criterion of impossibility and that subject-plus-predicate suppression produces none.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if existential cognition is restricted to possible experience and existence is denied predicate-status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it exposes the equivocation between conditional necessity within judgement and unconditional necessity of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT5","text":"The argument leaves underdetermined how a supersensible existence might be thought without being cognized, since such existence is declared not impossible but unverifiable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT6","text":"The restriction of real possibility to experiential warrant creates a structural tension with the retained intelligibility of ideas that exceed experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the critique of the ontological proof is treated as a critique of illicit inference, not as a proof of divine non-existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 78","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","context":"Chapter 78","original_title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","original_context":"Chapter 78"},"original_title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC","layer":"CH","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","order":79,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVIC"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVIC@R001","sha256":"33a0e15c3d9f1b666598a1f42110ac8801a714f118ce44e8b8aee1ab1df8e8a3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS1","text":"The argument tests whether existence in general can establish an absolutely necessary Supreme Being without deriving existence from a mere concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS2","text":"The cosmological proof moves from empirical existence to an indeterminate necessary ground and then from that ground to the ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS3","text":"The first transition exceeds the valid employment of causality and cannot establish termination in an absolutely necessary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS4","text":"The second transition covertly reinstates the ontological claim that the concept of the ens realissimum entails necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS5","text":"The resulting illusion originates in reason’s simultaneous demand for an unconditioned ground and inability to identify any determinate existence as unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AS6","text":"The argument proceeds by transcendental diagnosis from apparent empirical proof through concealed conceptual dependency to a regulative reinterpretation of absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","text":"The principle of causality has objective significance only within possible sensible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","text":"The impossibility of an infinite ascending series of empirical causes does not authorize inference to a first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A3","text":"The removal of all conditions does not produce a positive conception of absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A4","text":"The logical non-contradictoriness of a concept does not establish its transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","text":"Experience supplies actual existence but cannot identify the properties or determinate existence of an absolutely necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A6","text":"No determinate thing can be conceived as absolutely necessary because its non-existence remains thinkable without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A7","text":"Reason requires the regress through conditions to be oriented toward a complete and necessary ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A8","text":"Reason must treat every empirical determination as conditioned and leave open the possibility of further derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","text":"Necessity and contingency, in their transcendental employment, function as subjective regulative principles rather than properties of things themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A10","text":"The cosmological proof depends upon the proposition that every absolutely necessary being is an ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A11","text":"The assumed uniqueness of the ens realissimum permits the proposition that some ens realissimum is necessary to be generalized to every ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A12","text":"Attributing necessary existence to the ens realissimum through its concept reproduces the ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A13","text":"The idea of a Supreme Being is legitimate only as an as-if ground for the highest systematic unity of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A14","text":"An ideal of pure reason is not given as a cogitable object and possesses no credential beyond reason’s need for systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:A15","text":"Transcendental subreptio converts a formal regulative principle into a constitutive and hypostatized condition of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX1","text":"The existence of an object cannot be inferred from the mere concept of that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX2","text":"The cosmological proof is not independent of the ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX3","text":"The empirical beginning of the cosmological proof does not make its decisive inferences empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX4","text":"Causality cannot legitimately be extended from phenomena to a supersensible necessary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX5","text":"An allegedly incomplete causal regress does not establish an objectively existing first member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX6","text":"The absence of further represented conditions is not a positive cognition of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX7","text":"The non-contradictoriness of the totality of reality does not prove that such a synthesis is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX8","text":"The concept of the ens realissimum determines an ideal essence but does not answer whether it exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX9","text":"The methodological admission of an all-sufficient cause is not an apodeictic assertion of its existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX10","text":"No member of the sensible world can be treated as an absolutely necessary primal being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:NX11","text":"The ideal of pure reason is not an inscrutable object whose existence is independently given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D1","text":"Experience occasions the cosmological inference but supplies no objective warrant for its transition to an absolutely necessary being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D2","text":"Determining the alleged necessary ground as the ens realissimum requires the abandonment of experience in favor of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D3","text":"The entire determinate force of the cosmological proof is supplied by the ontological argument it professes to avoid.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A10","CH_BA94_SVIC:A11","CH_BA94_SVIC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D4","text":"Neither causal regress, deconditioning, nor conceptual non-contradiction establishes the existence of an unconditioned cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","CH_BA94_SVIC:A3","CH_BA94_SVIC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D5","text":"Matter and every other sensible existence remain only conditionally necessary and cannot instantiate the required absolute ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A6","CH_BA94_SVIC:A8","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D6","text":"Reason must pursue complete explanation as if a necessary ground existed while refusing to terminate inquiry at any empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A7","CH_BA94_SVIC:A8","CH_BA94_SVIC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D7","text":"The transformation of methodological unity into a Supreme Being is a natural but objectively invalid hypostatization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A13","CH_BA94_SVIC:A14","CH_BA94_SVIC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D8","text":"An all-sufficient cause may regulate explanation as a hypothesis without becoming an object of apodeictic cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIC:A6","CH_BA94_SVIC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:D9","text":"The representation of the necessary ground as beyond the world marks its non-instantiability within experience rather than cognition of a supersensible object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A8","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVIC:A13","CH_BA94_SVIC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SD1","text":"The cosmological proof fails twice: it cannot establish an absolutely necessary ground, and it cannot determine such a ground as the ens realissimum without circular dependence on ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIC:A10","CH_BA94_SVIC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SD2","text":"The apparent agreement of empirical and rational witnesses collapses into a single unsupported conceptual witness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIC:A10","CH_BA94_SVIC:A11","CH_BA94_SVIC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SD3","text":"Critical reason preserves inquiry by projecting completeness as a horizon while prohibiting every empirical closure of the regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A7","CH_BA94_SVIC:A8","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVIC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SD4","text":"The Supreme Being retains a formal place in reason’s architecture after losing the status of a speculatively knowable entity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVIC:A13","CH_BA94_SVIC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SD5","text":"Dialectical illusion is generated structurally whenever reason mistakes the unavoidable form of its explanatory demand for an independently existing object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A7","CH_BA94_SVIC:A14","CH_BA94_SVIC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX1","text":"An epistemic demand for complete explanation cannot confer existence upon its projected terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX2","text":"Conceptual uniqueness and maximal determination cannot substitute for instantiation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX3","text":"Placement beyond experience cannot preserve the objective use of categories whose validity is restricted to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX4","text":"Systematic unity does not require possession of its ultimate ground as an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX5","text":"The natural or unavoidable character of an inference does not establish its objective truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX6","text":"The refusal to hypostatize the unconditioned does not require abandoning the search for increasingly complete empirical explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX1","text":"Speculative cognition of an absolutely necessary being is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX2","text":"No positive concept mediates between the demand for a necessary basis and the determinate existence of the ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX3","text":"Absolute necessity lacks any representable instance either within phenomena or as a cognized thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX4","text":"Final explanatory closure is denied even though reason cannot cease orienting itself toward such closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX5","text":"The system retains the structural place of an ultimate ground while withholding every object capable of occupying it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS1","text":"The overt proof runs: actual existence → absolutely necessary ground → complete determination → ens realissimum → necessarily existing Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS2","text":"The first critical decomposition runs: empirical existence → illicit transcendental causality → forced termination of regress → pseudo-necessary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS3","text":"The second critical decomposition runs: necessary being → ens realissimum → conversion through uniqueness → ens realissimum as necessary by concept → concealed ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS4","text":"The supporting fallacy cluster runs: category exported beyond experience + regress declared incomplete + conditions removed + logical possibility substituted for transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS5","text":"The natural dialectic runs: demand for an unconditioned ground → projection of a necessary object → inability to conceive any object as necessary → withdrawal → renewed demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS6","text":"The critical resolution runs: objective necessity denied → regulative necessity retained → empirical regress kept open → systematic unity pursued as if grounded in an all-sufficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AF1","text":"The claimed proof moves from existence through necessary ground and complete reality to the necessary existence of the Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AF2","text":"The critique moves from empirical appearance through transcendental overreach and logical conversion to exposure of the hidden ontological premise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:AF3","text":"The explanatory resolution moves from unavoidable rational demand through constitutive illusion to regulative reinterpretation and prohibition of hypostasis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME1","text":"The distinction between ontological and cosmological proofs is reduced from independent warrants to different entrances into the same conceptual dependency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME2","text":"Causality is relocated from a universal law of beings to a rule valid only for phenomenal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME3","text":"Absolute necessity is reclassified from an objective predicate of a determinate being to a regulative demand for explanatory completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME4","text":"The ens realissimum is reclassified from a demonstrable Supreme Being to an ideal organizing systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME5","text":"The exclusion of an empirical first ground opens rather than closes the indefinite deduction of phenomena from further phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ME6","text":"The apparent mystery of the Supreme Being is converted into an explainable projection generated by reason’s own formal needs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT1","text":"Surface: The cosmological proof is examined as an inference from actual existence to an absolutely necessary Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT2","text":"Structural: The proof depends on extending causal principles beyond experience and importing the ontological identification of necessity with complete reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Critique preserves the pursuit of systematic unity while separating methodological orientation from existential assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Every empirical existence remains conditioned, the unconditioned lacks object-status, and the regress of explanation remains indefinitely open under the projected horizon of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the proof, its two-stage invalidation, and the regulative explanation of its persistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT2","text":"The chapter presupposes rather than fully reconstructs the prior critique of the ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent only if regulative necessity and constitutive necessity remain strictly distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT4","text":"Its strongest structural operation is the conversion that exposes the cosmological proof’s dependence on the ontological proposition it disavows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT5","text":"The claim that reason cannot avoid inferring a necessary basis is asserted as a natural tendency more than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT6","text":"The permission to admit an all-sufficient being as a hypothesis conflicts verbally with the denial that the ideal is given as a cogitable object unless the hypothesis is read exclusively as methodological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT7","text":"The assignment of the necessary ground beyond the world risks renewed hypostasis unless interpreted only as a negative boundary on empirical instantiation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if it includes both the destruction of the proof and the positive retention of the Supreme Being as a regulative ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 79","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","context":"Chapter 79","original_title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","original_context":"Chapter 79"},"original_title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP","layer":"CH","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","order":80,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVIP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVIP@R001","sha256":"cd43b12a6658fe1c4229f88c0690cb535a5738d32a286e8edefa2709a5770328","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS1","text":"The physico-theological proof is tested as the remaining empirical route to a Supreme Being after pure conceptual and general-experiential routes fail.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS2","text":"The proof begins from observed order, purposiveness, and unity in nature and infers an intelligent cause proportionate to those appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS3","text":"Empirical order can establish only the contingency of worldly form and at most an intelligent architect operating upon given material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS4","text":"An all-sufficient creator requires a determinate concept of absolute perfection, which comparative empirical judgments cannot provide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS5","text":"The proof therefore abandons experience and secretly depends on cosmological inference to an absolutely necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS6","text":"Cosmological inference reduces to ontological inference; hence the physico-theological proof has no independent demonstrative force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AS7","text":"The argument moves from empirical admiration through inferential limitation to the exposure of pure reason as the concealed ground of empirical theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A1","text":"A transcendental idea of an unconditioned being cannot be adequately presented in any conditioned experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A2","text":"Causal laws and synthetic inference from effects to causes have validity only within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A3","text":"A cause placed within the empirical series is conditioned by that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A4","text":"A cause placed outside the empirical series cannot be reached by empirical causal regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A5","text":"The analogy between natural order and human art can warrant only inference to an intelligent ordering cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A6","text":"Order and purposiveness in the world establish contingency of form, not contingency of matter or substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A7","text":"An intelligent architect limited by material conditions is not an all-sufficient creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A8","text":"Comparative predicates of greatness, wisdom, and power do not determine the concept of an absolutely perfect being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A9","text":"Only the totality of all possible reality yields a completely determinate concept of supreme perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A10","text":"Empirical inquiry cannot attain absolute totality or establish unconditioned necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A11","text":"The physico-theological proof reaches absolute necessity only by importing cosmological reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A12","text":"The cosmological proof depends upon the ontological proof for its decisive transition from necessity to supreme reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:A13","text":"The practical reasonableness and heuristic utility of an argument do not confer demonstrative certainty upon it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX1","text":"The Supreme Being is not an object whose transcendental content can be matched by empirical observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX2","text":"The unconditioned is not a member of the causal series of conditioned appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX3","text":"Empirical causality is not a bridge from sensible nature to an intelligible cause beyond nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX4","text":"Natural purposiveness is not proof that the matter of the world was created by supreme wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX5","text":"A world-architect is not thereby a world-creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX6","text":"Indefinitely magnified finite predicates are not equivalent to absolute perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX7","text":"The physico-theological proof is not demonstratively self-sufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:NX8","text":"Respect for the moral or heuristic usefulness of belief is not assent to its speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D1","text":"The inference from natural order establishes a cause proportionate to observed arrangement, not a cause proportionate to all possible reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A5","CH_BA94_SVIP:A6","CH_BA94_SVIP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D2","text":"The proof can infer intelligence and purposive disposition only under the analogy of art, while leaving the ontological status of the material world undetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A5","CH_BA94_SVIP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D3","text":"The move from an ordering cause to an all-sufficient being requires a transition from relative predicates to absolute totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A7","CH_BA94_SVIP:A8","CH_BA94_SVIP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D4","text":"Because experience cannot establish absolute necessity, the proof cannot remain empirical once it seeks a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A1","CH_BA94_SVIP:A10","CH_BA94_SVIP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D5","text":"The supposed empirical proof derives its theological conclusion only through an unacknowledged transcendental supplement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A10","CH_BA94_SVIP:A11","CH_BA94_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:D6","text":"The proof’s popular persuasiveness and its demonstrative insufficiency are compatible because affective conviction exceeds speculative warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A13","CH_BA94_SVIP:A5","CH_BA94_SVIP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SD1","text":"Every empirical ascent from nature to an unconditioned deity terminates in a category-boundary problem: remaining within experience yields only a conditioned cause, while leaving experience removes causal warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A2","CH_BA94_SVIP:A3","CH_BA94_SVIP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SD2","text":"The proof’s apparent independence from metaphysics is illusory because its final object is constituted by the pure concept of all reality rather than by empirical data.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A8","CH_BA94_SVIP:A9","CH_BA94_SVIP:A10","CH_BA94_SVIP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SD3","text":"Nature can regulate the search for unity and purposive order without furnishing theoretical cognition of a transcendent creator.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A1","CH_BA94_SVIP:A5","CH_BA94_SVIP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SD4","text":"The argumentative hierarchy is inverted: the proof presented as grounded in experience is valid, if at all, only through the proof it professes to avoid.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A11","CH_BA94_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX1","text":"The magnitude, order, and unity of the world are not measures of omnipotence, absolute wisdom, or divine unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX2","text":"Finite observational comparison is not capable of yielding an absolute predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX3","text":"A causal explanation of phenomenal arrangement is not an ontological explanation of the existence of phenomenal substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX4","text":"The intelligible ground of nature is not available as an empirical object of causal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX5","text":"The transition from contingent appearance to necessary being is not licensed by the same reasoning that connects empirical effects with empirical causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SNX1","text":"No speculative proof can both begin exclusively from experience and conclude with cognition of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SNX2","text":"The absolute totality required for a determinate theology is unavailable to empirical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SNX3","text":"The desired object of physico-theology—a creator known through nature as absolutely perfect—is structurally foreclosed by the limits that make natural cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:SNX4","text":"The proof cannot preserve both the transcendence of its deity and the empirical legitimacy of its causal inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS1","text":"Observed order and purposiveness → analogy with human art → inference to intelligent disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS2","text":"Intelligent disposition → contingency of worldly form → hypothesis of a world-architect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS3","text":"World-architect constrained by material possibility → failure to establish creation, omnipotence, or all-sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS4","text":"Comparative excellence → demand for complete determination → concept of all possible reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS5","text":"Empirical insufficiency → cosmological inference to absolute necessity → ontological determination of supreme reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:FGS6","text":"Concealed ontological dependence → collapse of the physico-theological proof’s claim to independent demonstrative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AF1","text":"Natural order and purposiveness → intelligent cause by analogy → architect of contingent form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AF2","text":"Architect of form → inability to establish contingency of matter → inability to establish a creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AF3","text":"Relative greatness of the cause → need for absolute perfection → failure of empirical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:AF4","text":"Failure of empirical determination → cosmological necessity → ontological all-reality → denial of physico-theological autonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ME1","text":"The distinction between ordering form and creating matter becomes the decisive boundary between an architect and a creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ME2","text":"Natural theology is reclassified from an empirical proof into a mixed argument whose decisive operations belong to transcendental metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ME3","text":"The opposition between empirical and ontological proofs collapses because the empirical proof covertly requires ontological completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ME4","text":"Persuasive encounter with cosmic order is separated from demonstrative entitlement to assert a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ME5","text":"Absolute divine predicates are reclassified as concepts requiring totality rather than as extrapolations from observed magnitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The argument assesses whether observed natural order proves the existence of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument distinguishes the contingency of form from the contingency of matter and thereby limits the causal conclusion available from design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument exposes a purportedly empirical theology as dependent on the transcendental proofs it disparages.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The argument separates conditioned empirical succession from unconditioned necessity and denies that causal regression can traverse their divide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The argument distinguishes relative, observer-dependent judgments of magnitude from a completely determinate concept of absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s central conclusion that the physico-theological proof cannot independently establish an all-sufficient Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT2","text":"The structure depends upon the prior establishment that cosmological reasoning reduces to ontological reasoning; that reduction is invoked here rather than re-demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as it applies the restriction of causal cognition to experience against every attempted ascent from natural order to transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT4","text":"The decisive structural hinge is the distinction between contingent arrangement and contingent substance, because that distinction blocks the passage from designer to creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT5","text":"The system retains a tension between conceding the rational legitimacy of design analogy for empirical explanation and denying its adequacy for transcendental theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT6","text":"The conclusion that the ontological proof is the only possible speculative route does not itself establish that such a proof succeeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from empirical elevation, through determination failure, to the disclosure of concealed ontological dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 80","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","context":"Chapter 80","original_title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","original_context":"Chapter 80"},"original_title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA","layer":"CH","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","order":81,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SVCA"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SVCA@R001","sha256":"132332cefa5acc76b8ebaf6522da6daf2fc92b01e7b161d6eac15b1ef6a2841b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS1","text":"Theology divides according to its alleged source of cognition: reason alone or revelation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS2","text":"Rational theology divides into transcendental theology, which thinks a primal being through pure concepts, and natural theology, which infers a world-author through analogy with nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS3","text":"Speculative reason attempts to establish a Supreme Being by extending causal, conceptual, or experiential principles beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS4","text":"Such extension is invalid because the principles that yield objective cognition are valid only for objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS5","text":"Speculative theology cannot prove or disprove the existence of a Supreme Being, but can negatively discipline the concept of such a being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AS6","text":"Theology can attain objective validity only, if at all, through practical reason and moral laws rather than speculative demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A1","text":"Theoretical cognition concerns what is, whereas practical cognition concerns what ought to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A2","text":"Absolutely necessary practical laws may postulate conditions required for their obligatory force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A3","text":"Objects given in experience are conditioned and contingently presented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A4","text":"A theoretical cognition of absolute necessity must be a priori and conceptual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A5","text":"Existence cannot be analytically extracted from a concept, because existence requires the object to be independently posited or given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","text":"Synthetic principles of the understanding possess objective validity only under the formal conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A7","text":"The principle of causality objectively applies only to empirical events or states, not to existence as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A8","text":"Cause and contingency lose determinate objective meaning when detached from possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A9","text":"A cause wholly distinct from the world cannot be presented as an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A10","text":"No experience can present the greatest possible effect required to infer a corresponding absolutely supreme cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A11","text":"A transcendental question concerning supersensible existence demands a transcendental answer adequate to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A12","text":"Pure concepts cannot furnish objective assurance of the existence of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A13","text":"Pure speculative reason can neither demonstrate nor refute the objective reality of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A14","text":"Pure reason can regulate and purify a concept even where it cannot establish the concept’s object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:A15","text":"Moral laws can require the postulation of a Supreme Being from a practical standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX1","text":"Theology is not a unitary rational procedure; its claims differ according to whether they arise from pure concepts, experience, natural analogy, morality, or revelation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX2","text":"A deistic primal being is not thereby a living intelligent author of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX3","text":"The empirical causal principle is not a warrant for inferring a cause of the universe as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX4","text":"The contingency of empirical states is not evidence that substance or the world itself is contingent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX5","text":"A supersensible cause is not an object to which natural-scientific causal reasoning can be extended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX6","text":"The existence of a Supreme Being is not contained in, or derivable from, its concept by analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX7","text":"Physico-theology is not a sufficient foundation for theology, even where it directs thought toward theological questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX8","text":"A speculative ideal of reason is not an objectively cognized being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX9","text":"The speculative inability to prove God is not a speculative proof of atheism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:NX10","text":"The concept of a Supreme Being is not legitimately anthropomorphic or phenomenally conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D1","text":"Cosmological inference from the universe to its cause illegitimately converts a principle governing empirical alterations into a principle governing the existence of the world.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A7","CH_BA94_SVCA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D2","text":"Inference from worldly order to a cause outside the world exceeds possible experience because the inferred cause cannot be experienced as its object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D3","text":"Physico-theology cannot infer an absolutely supreme cause because no empirical effect can establish maximality among all possible effects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A9","CH_BA94_SVCA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D4","text":"Ontological theology fails because conceptual analysis can clarify predicates but cannot posit the existence of their object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A4","CH_BA94_SVCA:A5","CH_BA94_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D5","text":"All speculative proofs of a Supreme Being ultimately require an ontological transition from concept to existence and therefore inherit its failure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A5","CH_BA94_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D6","text":"Theoretical reason cannot establish absolute necessity by beginning with contingent experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A4","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D7","text":"Theological existence-claims based on speculation remain subjective ideals rather than objective cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D8","text":"Moral theology occupies a distinct logical position because it derives a postulate from practical necessity rather than an object of knowledge from theoretical inference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A1","CH_BA94_SVCA:A2","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:D9","text":"Negative speculative critique can preserve the practical admissibility of God by removing invalid affirmations and invalid denials alike.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A13","CH_BA94_SVCA:A14","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1","text":"The failure of speculative theology is not merely a failure of particular proofs but a jurisdictional limit on every theoretical route from experience or concepts to supersensible existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A5","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:A7","CH_BA94_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SD2","text":"The Supreme Being is transformed from a theoretically knowable object into a regulative ideal whose determinate concept is conditionally available without corresponding theoretical existence-knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:A13","CH_BA94_SVCA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SD3","text":"The argument redistributes theological labor: speculative reason supplies conceptual purification, while practical reason alone may supply justified positability.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A1","CH_BA94_SVCA:A14","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4","text":"The same restriction that blocks dogmatic theism also blocks dogmatic atheism insofar as both claim theoretical knowledge of supersensible existence or nonexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SD5","text":"Divine predicates become legitimate only as purified transcendental determinations, not as empirical features magnified from finite human or natural experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:A9","CH_BA94_SVCA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX1","text":"The system excludes any theoretical standpoint from which the totality of empirical conditions can be treated as an empirical object requiring an external empirical cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX2","text":"The system excludes any direct passage from the logical completeness of a concept to the ontological actuality of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a single univocal use of necessity across empirical explanation, conceptual determination, and moral obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a positive speculative science of the supersensible even when reason necessarily forms ideas of the supersensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the assimilation of God to a worldly intelligence enlarged without limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the conclusion that the limits of theoretical proof determine the limits of rational assent in practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the unavailability of an object adequate to the idea of an absolutely necessary and all-sufficient being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses speculative possession of the unconditioned while preserving reason’s unavoidable demand to think it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX3","text":"The argument denies that human cognition can occupy a perspective external to the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX4","text":"The argument leaves the Supreme Being necessarily thinkable for reason yet inaccessible as a theoretical object of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS1","text":"Rational-theological claim → distinction between theoretical and practical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS2","text":"Theoretical existence-claim → requirement of objective validity for synthetic principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS3","text":"Objective validity → restriction to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS4","text":"Restriction to possible experience → invalidation of cosmological, physico-theological, and ontological transitions to supersensible existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS5","text":"Invalidated proof and invalidated disproof → Supreme Being classified as a speculative ideal rather than theoretical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS6","text":"Speculative ideal → negative purification of divine predicates and exclusion of anthropomorphism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:FGS7","text":"Practical necessity of moral law → conditional postulation of a Supreme Being without conversion into speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF1","text":"Taxonomy of theology → distinction between rational and revealed sources → distinction between transcendental and natural theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF2","text":"Distinction between theoretical being and practical ought → distinction between hypothetical presupposition and practical postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF3","text":"Empirical causality restricted to appearances → cosmological and physico-theological inference disqualified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF4","text":"Conceptual analysis unable to posit existence → ontological inference disqualified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF5","text":"Collapse of speculative proof → reciprocal collapse of speculative disproof → Supreme Being retained as an ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:AF6","text":"Negative speculative discipline → purified concept of a necessary being → possible practical theological use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME1","text":"Theology is reclassified from one domain of knowledge into differentiated modes of rational orientation with unequal epistemic status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME2","text":"The distinction between deism and theism becomes a distinction between an indeterminate primal ground and an intelligent free world-author.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME3","text":"Causality is redrawn as a rule for empirical events rather than a bridge from the world to its transcendent source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME4","text":"The opposition between theism and atheism is displaced because speculative reason authorizes neither existential assertion nor existential denial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME5","text":"The Supreme Being becomes visible as a limiting ideal of systematic reason rather than an available object of theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME6","text":"Anthropomorphic divine representation is reclassified as an empirical contamination of a concept that must be purified of sensible conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ME7","text":"Moral theology is separated from theological ethics because the former derives a divine postulate from moral law, whereas the latter presupposes divine governance for moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text evaluates whether reason can establish the existence and attributes of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds theological failure in the restriction of synthetic cognition, causality, and objective validity to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text converts speculative theology from a constructive proof-project into a negative tribunal regulating the permissible concept of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text separates conditioned empirical existence from the thought of an unconditioned necessary being and denies theoretical cognition any transition between them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT5","text":"Practical — The text relocates possible rational warrant for a Supreme Being from what exists to the conditions implicated by what ought to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central restriction: theoretical cognition cannot establish supersensible existence because its objective principles are immanent to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT2","text":"The distinction between theoretical cognition and practical postulation is necessary to reconstruct why moral theology survives the critique without becoming speculative theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as the same jurisdictional limit invalidates both speculative theism and speculative atheism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it distinguishes causal explanation of empirical events from causal explanation of existence as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT5","text":"The argument depends on an underdeveloped transition from moral obligation to the postulation of a Supreme Being, which is deferred rather than demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT6","text":"The status of the purified divine concept remains structurally tense because it is declared indispensable as an ideal while denied objective theoretical reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement only if negative speculative critique is treated as preparatory for practical theology rather than as the final theological conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 81","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","context":"Chapter 81","original_title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","original_context":"Chapter 81"},"original_title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2","layer":"CH","title":"Appendix","order":82,"parents":["RAW_BA94_APP2"],"revision":"CH_BA94_APP2@R001","sha256":"83104e0888529ef8e30f6d2de014ae98358b662faecafc0c2952d1e7d32fdf10","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS1","text":"Transcendental ideas must be legitimized despite the impossibility of speculative cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS2","text":"The legitimacy of transcendental ideas lies in their regulative direction of empirical inquiry, not in their constitution of transcendent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS3","text":"Reason relates to objects only through the understanding and therefore properly orders cognitions rather than cognizing supersensible beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS4","text":"The argument is diagnostic, reconstructive, and critical: it locates dialectic in misemployment and recovers the proper function concealed within illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS5","text":"The argument moves from failed transcendent inference to systematic unity as reason’s immanent function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS6","text":"Regulative ideas acquire indirect and indeterminate objective validity by organizing possible experience toward maximal unity, diversity, and continuity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AS7","text":"Psychological, cosmological, and theological objects are retained only as analogical schemata whose hypostatization produces contradiction, arrested inquiry, and circular explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A1","text":"Speculative cognition cannot pass beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A2","text":"Transcendental ideas arise necessarily from the nature of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A3","text":"Transcendental ideas become dialectical through misemployment rather than through their intrinsic form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A4","text":"Reason relates immediately to the understanding rather than to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A5","text":"The understanding connects experience through concepts and empirical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A6","text":"Reason’s proper occupation is the systematic unity and totalization of the understanding’s cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A7","text":"A transcendental idea lacks a sensuous schema and therefore cannot determine an object in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A8","text":"Constitutive employment falsely treats an idea as the conception of an adequate object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A9","text":"Regulative employment directs the understanding within experience toward maximal unity and extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A10","text":"The idea of a systematic whole precedes and regulates the determination of the place and relation of its parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A11","text":"Hypothetical reason treats the universal rule as problematic and uses certain particulars only to approximate its universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A12","text":"The empirical use of systematic principles presupposes a priori and indeterminately that nature is amenable to systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A13","text":"Reason requires homogeneity among diverse phenomena in order to form higher genera and general concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A14","text":"Reason requires unceasing specification within every genus and forbids treating any species as the lowest possible determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A15","text":"Reason requires affinity between forms by directing inquiry toward gradual transitions between unity and diversity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A16","text":"Complete systematic unity remains an asymptotic goal that experience can approach but never attain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A17","text":"Regulative ideas possess objective validity only indirectly and indeterminately through their governance of possible empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:A18","text":"The ego, world-totality, and God may be posited only as relative and analogical schemata of systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX1","text":"Transcendental ideas are not conceptions of actual supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX2","text":"Reason does not directly produce cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX3","text":"The focus imaginarius is not the real source from which empirical concepts proceed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX4","text":"Systematic unity is not a given or completed empirical totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX5","text":"The conformity of observed cases to a hypothetical rule does not prove the rule’s universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX6","text":"Homogeneity, specification, and affinity are not universal principles derived from accumulated observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX7","text":"The principle of continuity does not establish an actually infinite series of empirical intermediate species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX8","text":"The principle of continuity supplies no determinate empirical criterion for measuring affinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX9","text":"Categories such as substance, causality, reality, and necessity do not determine objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX10","text":"Relative realization of an ideal object is not knowledge of its absolute existence or intrinsic constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX11","text":"The psychological idea does not establish the soul as a simple, immortal, or self-subsistent substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX12","text":"The cosmological idea does not constitute a completed totality of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX13","text":"The theological idea does not provide speculative proof of a supreme intelligence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX14","text":"Teleological regulation does not replace investigation through immanent mechanical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:NX15","text":"The maxims of unity and diversity are not mutually contradictory objective laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D1","text":"Critique must redirect transcendental ideas rather than abolish them because their necessity and their dialectical misuse have different grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:A3","CH_BA94_APP2:A8","CH_BA94_APP2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D2","text":"Reason’s relation to empirical objects is mediated by its organization of the understanding’s cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A4","CH_BA94_APP2:A5","CH_BA94_APP2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D3","text":"Ideas legitimately determine the procedure of cognition without determining any object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A7","CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D4","text":"The projected whole can assign systematic relations among cognitions before that whole is empirically available.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A10","CH_BA94_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D5","text":"Systematic fit supplies a regulative criterion for a hypothetical rule while leaving possible exceptions unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A11","CH_BA94_APP2:A12","CH_BA94_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D6","text":"Empirical concepts require phenomena to be sufficiently similar for subsumption and sufficiently different for determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A12","CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D7","text":"Affinity coordinates the opposing demands for generality and individuality without eliminating either.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D8","text":"The objective force of an idea consists in the coherence and extension it produces within experience, not in correspondence to a transcendent object.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D9","text":"Relative realization permits reason to represent systematic unity as if it radiated from an ideal ground while withholding absolute existence claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A10","CH_BA94_APP2:A17","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D10","text":"The psychological idea regulates inquiry by treating mental phenomena as if they belonged to one permanent subject and arose from unified powers.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D11","text":"The cosmological idea regulates natural explanation by requiring indefinite continuation through conditioned phenomena without constituting an actual completed series.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A16","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D12","text":"The theological idea regulates inquiry by treating nature as if its lawful order expressed one supremely systematic intelligence.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A17","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D13","text":"Disputes between unifying and differentiating investigators become reconcilable when their positions are reclassified as reciprocally limiting maxims.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_APP2:A15","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D14","text":"Constitutive hypostatization either terminates empirical investigation prematurely or imposes preconceived purposes upon nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A8","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:D15","text":"Critical diagnosis remains necessary because a natural and unavoidable illusion can be restrained without being eliminated.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD1","text":"Reason generates objectification pressure from within its proper regulative activity because it cannot represent complete systematic unity without representing an ideal source of that unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A10","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD2","text":"The system stratifies objectivity into determinate constitutive validity and indirect regulative validity rather than opposing objectivity to subjectivity without qualification.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A7","CH_BA94_APP2:A8","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD3","text":"Nature’s systematic intelligibility functions as a transcendental condition of empirical inquiry without becoming a determinate ontology of nature in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A12","CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_APP2:A15","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD4","text":"Rational inquiry is structurally interminable because every achieved unity generates further demands for specification, affinity, and higher unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A11","CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_APP2:A15","CH_BA94_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD5","text":"Critical metaphysics preserves the functions of traditional metaphysical objects by withdrawing their status as objects of speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_APP2:A6","CH_BA94_APP2:A17","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD6","text":"Divine wisdom and nature’s systematic arrangement become methodologically interchangeable formulations when both designate only the regulative demand for lawful unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A17","CH_BA94_APP2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SD7","text":"The distinction between legitimate and illegitimate reason is operational: legitimate ideas extend and coordinate inquiry, whereas hypostatized ideas arrest or distort it.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A3","CH_BA94_APP2:A8","CH_BA94_APP2:A9","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX1","text":"Radical heterogeneity cannot be the form of nature available to experience because it would make general concepts and understanding impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX2","text":"Absolute homogeneity cannot be the form of experience because cognition also requires distinguishable contents and lower determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX3","text":"No achieved empirical classification can possess an absolutely terminal species or an absolutely isolated highest genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX4","text":"The practical indispensability of an idea cannot entail the absolute existence of a corresponding being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX5","text":"The successful organization of phenomena cannot disclose the intrinsic constitution of the projected ground of that organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX6","text":"Failure to attain complete systematic unity cannot make that unity dispensable as a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX7","text":"Teleological inquiry cannot legitimately authorize exemption from mechanical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:DNX8","text":"Reason’s natural production of illusion cannot justify either abandoning reason or treating its spontaneous representations as knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SNX1","text":"The absent object corresponding to complete systematic unity organizes the entire system without ever becoming available as an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SNX2","text":"Absolute knowledge of the soul, the completed world, and God is the foreclosed possibility around which the regulative reconstruction is organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SNX3","text":"Final empirical completion must remain unavailable because its attainment would terminate the open-ended function assigned to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SNX4","text":"No external standpoint is available from which the conformity of nature to reason’s ideal system can be directly verified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:SNX5","text":"Positive transcendent ontology is replaced by an irreducible “as if” whose necessity cannot be converted into knowledge of what lies beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS1","text":"Sensibility supplies intuitions → understanding connects intuitions through concepts and laws → reason unifies the understanding’s cognitions into a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS2","text":"Empirical multiplicity → projected systematic maximum → directed search for rules → expanded connection of cognitions → renewed approximation to unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS3","text":"Homogeneity ascends toward genera → specification descends toward lower differences → affinity mediates the two through anticipated continuity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS4","text":"Absence of an adequate sensuous schema → impossibility of constitutive cognition → substitution of an analogical schema → indirect regulation of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS5","text":"Unity of mental phenomena → psychological schema; indefinite totality of conditions → cosmological schema; purposive unity of nature → theological schema.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS6","text":"Projected ideal object → hypostatization → constitutive attribution → premature explanatory closure or imposed finality → contradiction and dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:FGS7","text":"Critique of transcendent claims → recovery of regulative function → extension and correction of empirical inquiry → confinement of cognition to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AF1","text":"Failure of transcendent inference → naturality of transcendental ideas → distinction between constitutive and regulative employment → mediated function of reason → systematic unity as proper destination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:AF2","text":"Logical maxims of system → transcendental presupposition of nature’s intelligibility → relative realization of ideal objects → psychological, cosmological, and theological regulation → diagnosis of inactive and perverted reason → critical closure within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME1","text":"Transcendental ideas are reclassified from failed metaphysical objects into indispensable rules of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME2","text":"Reason is relocated from direct cognition of the world to metalevel organization of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME3","text":"Dialectical error is relocated from the existence of ideas to the judgment that determines their employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME4","text":"Objectivity is divided into determinate object-constitution and indirect regulation of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME5","text":"Unity and diversity cease to be exclusive explanatory alternatives and become reciprocally limiting demands of one systematic interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME6","text":"Psychology, cosmology, and theology are relocated from supersensible knowledge to schemata for empirical organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME7","text":"Teleology is reclassified from a constitutive account of natural production into a heuristic for discovering lawful connections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ME8","text":"Metaphysical disagreement is partially reclassified as conflict between methodological maxims rather than insight into opposed objective structures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ALT1","text":"Surface: The immediate distinction is between transcendent constitutive misuse and immanent regulative employment of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ALT2","text":"Structural: The faculty architecture assigns sensibility the manifold, understanding empirical connection, and reason systematic totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Inquiry is governed by coordinated maxims of unity, specification, affinity, and purposiveness while dogmatic closure is prohibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Nature must be presupposed as indefinitely systematizable, while complete unity and its ideal ground remain asymptotic and unknowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the boundary of cognition, the mediated function of reason, the regulative status of ideas, and the principal modes of dialectical failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT2","text":"The exact status of nature’s systematic unity remains underdetermined because it is presented both as a subjective maxim and as possessing indirect objective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent so long as constitutive determination and regulative direction remain rigorously distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where it derives inactive and perverted reason from the hypostatization of methodological ideals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT5","text":"The term “deduction” shifts between an impossible category-like deduction of ideas and an admitted functional deduction of their regulative use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT6","text":"Relative supposition remains unstable because reason is said both to posit a real ideal object necessarily and to make no assertion of its absolute existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT7","text":"The continuity and teleology claims risk constitutive leakage when regulative directives are expressed as necessary features of nature despite lacking adequate empirical objects or criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_APP2:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from dialectical failure through regulative recovery to the confinement and extension of empirical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 82","title":"Appendix","context":"Chapter 82","original_title":"Appendix","original_context":"Chapter 82"},"original_title":"Appendix","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM","layer":"CH","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","order":83,"parents":["RAW_BA94_ITDM"],"revision":"CH_BA94_ITDM@R001","sha256":"c13270742fd908365e623eda1b6dba2598ea84ba0741ef6778f319edf9d9cfa4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AS1","text":"Pure speculative reason is assessed as an edifice whose materials, scope, height, and stability have already been determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AS2","text":"The attempted construction of a heaven-reaching system fails because human cognition lacks sufficient materials and because disputes over its design prevent unified construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AS3","text":"The remaining task is to design a secure system proportioned to the materials actually available and sufficient for terrestrial cognitive needs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AS4","text":"Transcendental doctrine of method determines the formal conditions under which a complete system of pure reason can be constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AS5","text":"Discipline, canon, architectonic, and history replace an inadequately executed practical logic by determining method from the standpoint of transcendental cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A1","text":"Pure reason possesses a systematic form that can be represented as an edifice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A2","text":"The available materials of cognition determine the possible scope, height, and stability of the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A3","text":"Human reason lacks sufficient materials for a system that transcends the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A4","text":"A secure cognitive system must be proportioned to the materials actually available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A5","text":"The available materials are sufficient for the legitimate requirements of terrestrial cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","text":"The doctrine of method concerns the plan and formal conditions of a complete system, not the provision of its cognitive materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A7","text":"A complete system of pure reason requires determinate formal conditions of organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A8","text":"The method of pure reason must be articulated through discipline, canon, architectonic, and history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A9","text":"General logic cannot determine the concrete methods of particular kinds of cognition or particular objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A10","text":"General logic supplies methodological titles and technical expressions without supplying their substantive meaning or application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:A11","text":"A transcendental doctrine of method can perform the systematic task inadequately assigned to practical logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX1","text":"The system is not a tower capable of reaching beyond the limits of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX2","text":"The available cognitive materials are not sufficient for unrestricted speculative construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX3","text":"Doctrine of method is not an inquiry into the materials of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX4","text":"A secure system is not designed independently of the materials supplied by cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX5","text":"General logic is not a sufficient method for every kind of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX6","text":"Technical terminology is not equivalent to knowledge of meaning or application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:NX7","text":"A complete system is not produced by disconnected constructions based on private plans and inclinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D1","text":"The failure of transcendent speculation requires methodological limitation rather than abandonment of systematic reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A3","CH_BA94_ITDM:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D2","text":"The legitimate aim of reason is a bounded but secure cognitive dwelling rather than an unlimited speculative edifice.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A3","CH_BA94_ITDM:A4","CH_BA94_ITDM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D3","text":"Method must be derived from the conditions of the cognition being organized, because general formal rules cannot determine particular applications.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","CH_BA94_ITDM:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D4","text":"A system of pure reason requires both material delimitation and formal organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A2","CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","CH_BA94_ITDM:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D5","text":"The four divisions of the doctrine of method specify distinct functions within the construction and regulation of pure reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A7","CH_BA94_ITDM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D6","text":"Practical logic fails when it presents methodological vocabulary without enabling the learner to determine its substantive use.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A9","CH_BA94_ITDM:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:D7","text":"Transcendental method must connect formal system-building with the specific conditions and limits of pure cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","CH_BA94_ITDM:A9","CH_BA94_ITDM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SD1","text":"The limitation of reason becomes the enabling condition for a more stable and complete system of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A3","CH_BA94_ITDM:A4","CH_BA94_ITDM:A5","CH_BA94_ITDM:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SD2","text":"Systematic unity is treated as a constructed order governed by cognitive conditions rather than as an unlimited projection of reason’s aspirations.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A1","CH_BA94_ITDM:A2","CH_BA94_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SD3","text":"Methodological disagreement is transformed from a dispute over competing private designs into a problem of determining universally valid formal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A7","CH_BA94_ITDM:A8","CH_BA94_ITDM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SD4","text":"The critique of general logic produces a theory of method that is simultaneously formal, object-sensitive, and cognitively bounded.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","CH_BA94_ITDM:A9","CH_BA94_ITDM:A10","CH_BA94_ITDM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX1","text":"The system cannot rest on the assumption that the mere idea of a totality guarantees the materials required to construct it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX2","text":"Formal systematization cannot compensate for the absence of valid cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX3","text":"Reason cannot determine its legitimate reach by aspiration alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX4","text":"A universal method detached from the type of cognition and object at issue is structurally incapable of guiding scientific cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX5","text":"Systematic unity cannot be secured through individually improvised constructions lacking a common plan.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX6","text":"Instruction in methodological nomenclature cannot substitute for the conditions that make methodological application possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX1","text":"Unlimited speculative completion is foreclosed as a legitimate ideal of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX2","text":"The possibility of a secure system is made dependent on accepting finitude rather than overcoming it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX3","text":"There is no method capable of generating valid cognition independently of the limits and materials of cognition itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX4","text":"The unity of reason is unavailable as an unregulated totality and must instead be instituted through formal conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS1","text":"Available cognitive materials → determination of legitimate scope → rejection of transcendent construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS2","text":"Rejection of transcendent construction → proportional design → secure habitation for legitimate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS3","text":"Proportional design → specification of formal conditions → complete system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS4","text":"Formal conditions → discipline + canon + architectonic + history → transcendental doctrine of method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS5","text":"General logic’s abstraction from particular cognition and objects → methodological indeterminacy → transcendental correction through object- and cognition-specific method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:AF1","text":"Failed transcendent construction → recognition of insufficient materials → bounded systematic design → formal doctrine of method → differentiated organization of pure reason → correction of practical logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ME1","text":"The conceptual field shifts from the quantity of speculative reach to the relation between available materials and permissible construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ME2","text":"Failure is reclassified from the collapse of systematic reason to the failure of an improperly scaled design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ME3","text":"Method is separated from generic logical form and relocated within the specific conditions of cognition and objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ME4","text":"Systematic unity is reclassified as an architectonic achievement requiring differentiated functions rather than as a mere aggregate of propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ME5","text":"Methodological education is distinguished from the memorization of technical names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines the task and divisions of the transcendental doctrine of method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument organizes cognition as an edifice whose possible form depends on the adequacy of its materials and plan.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects philosophical method from unlimited construction toward disciplined, materially proportioned system-building.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human cognition is finite and situated within experience; systematic reason must therefore be bounded without becoming unsystematic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the limits of cognition to the need for a transcendental doctrine of method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between cognitive materials and the formal plan imposed upon them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as bounded cognition is treated as the condition of secure system-building.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT4","text":"The strongest linkage is between the insufficiency of transcendent materials and the proportional design of a terrestrial system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT5","text":"The principal tension lies between the demand for a complete system and the finite, experience-bound materials available to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT6","text":"A secondary tension remains between the formal universality sought by method and the claim that methods must be specific to kinds of cognition and objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction depends on compressed transitions from the architectural metaphor to the formal divisions of method, but these transitions remain structurally specified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 83","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","context":"Chapter 83","original_title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","original_context":"Chapter 83"},"original_title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","order":84,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CIDP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CIDP@R001","sha256":"5fd957490445ca08005109dc6acfa297adbbafdd936127b929884bb00307d955","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS1","text":"The text addresses the undervaluation of negative judgments as mere obstacles to the expansion of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS2","text":"It reframes negativity as an indispensable protection against error when cognition is limited, illusion is powerful, and mistakes are consequential.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS3","text":"It defines discipline as negative restraint and distinguishes it from the positive formation of skill or the communication of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS4","text":"It extends the need for discipline to reason itself because reason can replace concepts with fancies and things with words.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS5","text":"It distinguishes empirically and mathematically constrained reason from transcendental reason, which lacks an external track and tends to exceed possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AS6","text":"It concludes that pure reason requires a method-directed negative code capable of exposing interconnected fallacies rather than merely correcting isolated errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A1","text":"Content-negative judgments primarily prevent error rather than add positive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A2","text":"A negative proposition correcting an error that is impossible is true but purposeless.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A3","text":"The value of negative cognition increases when possible cognition is restricted, illusion is deceptive, and error has serious consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A4","text":"Discipline is restraint directed against the inclination to violate governing rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A5","text":"Culture develops existing powers positively, whereas discipline corrects or suppresses their misapplication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A6","text":"Natural talents that seek unrestricted development can nevertheless require corrective discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A7","text":"Reason can substitute fancies for concepts and words for things despite its authoritative status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A8","text":"Reason employed in experience is tested by empirical observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A9","text":"Mathematical cognition presents concepts in pure intuition, exposing arbitrary or baseless assertions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","text":"Transcendental reason operates through pure concepts without the empirical or intuitive constraints available in other domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A11","text":"Pure reason generates a connected system of illusions and fallacies grounded in general principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A12","text":"The discipline of pure reason concerns the method of cognition rather than the content already treated by the doctrine of elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:A13","text":"A connected system of pure-reason fallacies requires a general negative code of examination and testing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX1","text":"Logical negativity is not identical with content-negative judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX2","text":"Negative judgment is not ordinarily a means of increasing the stock of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX3","text":"Correction of an impossible error is not a meaningful cognitive task.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX4","text":"Discipline is not culture or positive skill formation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX5","text":"Discipline is not instruction understood as the communication of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX6","text":"Reason is not exempt from the corrective scrutiny it applies to other mental powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX7","text":"Empirical and mathematical reason are not exposed to the same undisciplined transcendental wandering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX8","text":"The discipline of pure reason is not directed primarily at cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX9","text":"Pure reason is not permitted to extend itself beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:NX10","text":"A systematic network of fallacies is not adequately addressed by isolated corrections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D1","text":"The epistemic value of negative judgment is conditional rather than intrinsically uniform.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:A2","CH_BA94_CIDP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D2","text":"Under conditions of epistemic danger, restraint becomes more important than further positive acquisition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A3","CH_BA94_CIDP:A4","CH_BA94_CIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D3","text":"Reason’s authority makes its own errors difficult to detect because its prestige conceals the possibility of substitution and distortion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A6","CH_BA94_CIDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D4","text":"The need for criticism varies according to whether a domain supplies empirical or intuitive tests.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A8","CH_BA94_CIDP:A9","CH_BA94_CIDP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D5","text":"The absence of external constraint makes transcendental reason especially vulnerable to illegitimate extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A9","CH_BA94_CIDP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D6","text":"The generality of pure reason’s principles allows separate fallacies to reinforce one another as a system.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D7","text":"Systematic transcendental error requires legislative discipline rather than episodic refutation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A11","CH_BA94_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D8","text":"Methods that are legitimate under empirical or intuitive constraint can become illegitimate when transferred into the transcendental sphere.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A8","CH_BA94_CIDP:A9","CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:D9","text":"Separating content from method assigns positive doctrinal construction and negative methodological control to distinct tasks.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SD1","text":"Negative restriction becomes a constructive condition for reliable cognition when positive expansion is exposed to systematic illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:A3","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2","text":"Pure reason becomes both the source of governing principles and the object requiring correction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A7","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11","CH_BA94_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SD3","text":"Critique is transformed from the correction of individual mistakes into the regulation of a whole mode of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A11","CH_BA94_CIDP:A12","CH_BA94_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4","text":"The boundary of possible experience functions as a jurisdictional limit on the legitimate use of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A12","CH_BA94_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SD5","text":"Reason cannot secure transcendental cognition merely through the internal coherence or generality of its own principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A7","CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX1","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that the desire for more knowledge justifies crossing cognitive limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX2","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that reason’s prestige guarantees the validity of its concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX3","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that unrestricted development of a power is always beneficial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX4","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that validity established in one cognitive domain transfers automatically to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX5","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that interconnected fallacies can be neutralized through isolated objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX6","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that regulating cognitive content and regulating cognitive method are the same operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of an external empirical or intuitive test for pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX2","text":"It forecloses the possibility that reason can certify its transcendental products merely by generating them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX3","text":"It excludes a seamless continuity between the positive expansion of knowledge and the control of error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX4","text":"It renders unrestricted transcendental speculation inadmissible as a secure route to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX5","text":"It denies the possibility of a discipline-free autonomy of reason in the transcendental sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS1","text":"Logical negativity is separated from content-negative judgment, isolating error prevention as the operative function of the latter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS2","text":"Restricted cognition, deceptive illusion, and consequential error elevate error prevention above positive accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS3","text":"The elevated negative function is formalized as discipline, while positive development is assigned to culture and doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS4","text":"The requirement of discipline is extended from ordinary powers to reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS5","text":"Empirical observation and pure intuition partition domains where reason is externally constrained from the transcendental domain where it is not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS6","text":"The absence of external constraint produces a tendency toward overstepping possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS7","text":"Overstepping is treated as a connected system of fallacies rather than as a collection of independent mistakes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS8","text":"A connected system of fallacies generates the need for a general negative code of examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS9","text":"The code is directed at the method of pure cognition and functions by preventing illegitimate methodological transfer and transcendental overreach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:AF1","text":"Negative judgment as error prevention → heightened value under epistemic risk → discipline as corrective restraint → reason subjected to discipline → separation of externally constrained and transcendental cognition → method-directed code against systematic illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME1","text":"Negativity is reclassified from an impediment to knowledge into a condition of epistemic security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME2","text":"The conceptual field is divided between positive power formation and negative power regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME3","text":"Reason’s exemption from criticism is removed; reason becomes an object of critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME4","text":"Cognitive domains are reclassified according to the presence or absence of empirical and intuitive constraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME5","text":"Error is reclassified from a local defect into a system generated by general principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ME6","text":"Critique shifts from correcting propositions to governing the methods by which propositions are produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ALT1","text":"Surface: Negative judgments are assigned the function of preventing error rather than adding knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ALT2","text":"Structural: Discipline organizes the relation between mental powers, cognitive domains, and permissible methods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Critique becomes a regulatory operation that blocks the misuse of reason-generated methods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Possible experience establishes the boundary of legitimate cognition, while transcendental reason tends toward an ungrounded extension beyond that boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the limited value of negative judgment to the necessity of disciplining pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent because the distinction between positive cognition and negative error-control governs every major transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT3","text":"Its strongest structural point is the distinction between externally constrained cognition and transcendental cognition lacking such constraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4","text":"The system contains a productive tension because discipline must regulate reason while being founded on reason’s own nature and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT5","text":"A second tension arises because the discipline of pure reason is methodologically necessary, while the text does not specify its concrete tests with the precision available in empirical or mathematical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT6","text":"The relation between the positive construction of cognition in the doctrine of elements and the negative regulation of cognition in discipline remains partially underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the original argument’s movement because systematic illusion, domain separation, and method-directed restriction jointly generate the final position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 84","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 84","original_title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 84"},"original_title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","order":85,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIDP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIDP@R001","sha256":"f14b4e82f1ab14cf47ee47caa51bef9705acef5ddb13a889d019ab82bcdaba5b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS1","text":"Mathematical success induces pure reason to expect an equally secure extension into the transcendental sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS2","text":"The governing problem is whether mathematical demonstration and philosophical dogmatism employ the same method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS3","text":"Mathematical cognition constructs concepts in a priori intuition, whereas philosophical cognition proceeds discursively through concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS4","text":"This methodological division arises because space and time are available a priori for construction, while the matter of appearances is available only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS5","text":"Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations depend in their mathematical form upon construction and therefore lack strict philosophical equivalents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS6","text":"The argument is diagnostic, transcendental, and disciplinary, moving from methodological temptation through formal differentiation and evidential testing to prohibition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AS7","text":"Philosophy may remain systematic, but its proper procedure is deduction, conceptual exposition, limit-detection, and correction rather than mathematical dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A1","text":"Objects are presented to cognition only through possible intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","text":"Philosophical cognition is rational cognition through concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","text":"Mathematical cognition is rational cognition through the construction of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A4","text":"Construction is the a priori presentation of an intuition corresponding to a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A5","text":"An individual construction yields universal cognition when only its universal conditions of construction determine the result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A6","text":"Philosophical and mathematical cognition can both possess universality and an a priori origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A7","text":"The difference between philosophy and mathematics lies in their cognitive form rather than their subject matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","text":"Space and time are the only a priori intuitions and constitute the pure form of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","text":"The matter of appearances is given only a posteriori through perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A10","text":"Analysis clarifies a concept but does not extend cognition beyond its contained marks.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A11","text":"Synthetic extension requires passage beyond a concept to an intuition corresponding to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A12","text":"A concept is constructible only insofar as its corresponding intuition can be presented a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","text":"Transcendental concepts represent rules for synthesizing possible empirical intuitions rather than a priori intuitions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","text":"Transcendental principles possess objective validity only as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A15","text":"The completeness of an analysis of a given concept cannot be established with demonstrative certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","text":"A synthetic connection made through concepts requires mediating cognition and deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A17","text":"A strict definition can initiate cognition only when it generates a concept that can be constructed a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:A18","text":"Demonstration is apodeictic proof grounded in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX1","text":"Philosophy and mathematics are not distinguished by an opposition between quality and quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX2","text":"Shared universality and a priori origin do not establish methodological identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX3","text":"Mathematical success does not warrant transferring mathematical procedure into transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX4","text":"The matter, reality, or existence of an object cannot be constructed a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX5","text":"Conceptual analysis does not produce synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX6","text":"Empirical cognition does not possess necessity or strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX7","text":"Empirical concepts cannot receive complete and permanently bounded definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX8","text":"Given a priori concepts cannot receive demonstrably complete definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX9","text":"The definition of an arbitrary project does not establish the existence or possibility of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX10","text":"Philosophy contains no immediately certain synthetic principles in the mathematical sense of axioms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX11","text":"Discursive philosophical proofs are not demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX12","text":"Transcendental principles are not direct synthetic dogmata grounded in concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:NX13","text":"Rejection of the dogmatic method does not entail rejection of philosophical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D1","text":"Mathematical construction converts an individual a priori presentation into universally valid synthetic cognition by retaining only universal construction conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A4","CH_BA94_SIDP:A5","CH_BA94_SIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D2","text":"Mathematics concerns quantity because quantities admit a priori construction; quantity is therefore an effect of mathematical form rather than the original basis of the mathematics–philosophy distinction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","CH_BA94_SIDP:A7","CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D3","text":"Common objects and common a priori validity do not erase the difference between construction and conceptual consideration.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","CH_BA94_SIDP:A6","CH_BA94_SIDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D4","text":"Discursive reflection confined to a concept can reorder or clarify cognition but cannot discover properties not already contained in that concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D5","text":"Recourse to empirical intuition can produce synthetic propositions, but it forfeits necessity and strict universality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D6","text":"Transcendental propositions determine rules for the unity of possible experience without presenting their objects in a priori intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A1","CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D7","text":"Mathematical definitions can generate their concepts at the beginning of inquiry, whereas philosophical definitions remain uncertain expositions that properly approach completion only at the end.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","CH_BA94_SIDP:A4","CH_BA94_SIDP:A15","CH_BA94_SIDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D8","text":"Philosophy possesses no axioms because synthetic connections between concepts require mediation and deduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D9","text":"Philosophy possesses no demonstrations because it cannot present its transcendental concepts through constructed a priori intuitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12","CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D10","text":"Speculative pure reason contains no dogmata because its objectively valid synthetic principles are mediated through their relation to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D11","text":"Philosophy can form a systematic investigation under principles of unity even though experience supplies its material and deduction supplies its authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D12","text":"Philosophy’s proper transcendental task is to establish validity, delimit legitimate employment, detect illusion, and revise defective principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SIDP:A15","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:D13","text":"Importing mathematical evidence into philosophy suppresses the mediations through which philosophical claims must be justified and thereby conceals error.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A7","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","CH_BA94_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD1","text":"A priori cognition is internally heterogeneous: common origin and universality divide into intuitive construction and discursive conditioning rather than forming one transferable method.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","CH_BA94_SIDP:A6","CH_BA94_SIDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD2","text":"Mathematical mastery is confined to formal objects whose determinations the subject can construct; it does not extend to empirical matter, existence, or reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A4","CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3","text":"Possible experience functions simultaneously as the limit of transcendental principles and as the ground through which their objective validity is established.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD4","text":"Philosophical inquiry must operate with incompletely exposed concepts before it can approach complete exposition, making its order of cognition inverse to mathematical definition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A2","CH_BA94_SIDP:A15","CH_BA94_SIDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD5","text":"Philosophical certainty, where attainable, is deduced and mediated rather than self-evident, so apodeictic force does not entail mathematical form.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A6","CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","CH_BA94_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD6","text":"The dogmatic method is structurally error-preserving because it treats revisable conceptual premises as though they possessed intuitive transparency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A15","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","CH_BA94_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SD7","text":"The absence of constructible intuitions for transcendental concepts blocks mathematical passage from possible experience to a supersensible intellectual world.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A1","CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12","CH_BA94_SIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX1","text":"No intellectual intuition beyond space and time is available to speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX2","text":"No method remains invariant when transferred between constructed intuition and discursive concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX3","text":"No concept guarantees the existence, possibility, or determinate reality of its object merely by being clearly formed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX4","text":"No material content of appearance can be generated from pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX5","text":"No transcendental principle is entitled to acceptance through self-evidence alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX6","text":"No complete philosophical vocabulary can be secured before philosophical inquiry begins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX7","text":"No idea of pure reason produces objectively valid synthetic knowledge of a supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX8","text":"No technical success within mathematics exempts mathematical reason from philosophical determination of its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX1","text":"The unavailable intuition of transcendental objects is the central absence around which the prohibition of dogmatic metaphysics is organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX2","text":"Philosophy lacks an Archimedean beginning composed of complete definitions, self-evident axioms, and intuitive demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX3","text":"The system forecloses a universal apodeictic method capable of moving unchanged from mathematics to every object of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX4","text":"Pure reason cannot erect a speculative edifice from self-supplied material because the material of cognition remains dependent upon experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX5","text":"The intellectual world promised by transcendental dogmatism remains structurally inaccessible rather than merely undiscovered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS1","text":"Mathematical success generates analogical expectation, which generates the question of methodological transfer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS2","text":"Object-presentation divides into pure formal intuition and empirically given matter, thereby producing two legitimate employments of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS3","text":"Pure intuition → a priori construction → individual schema under universal conditions → synthetic universality → mathematical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS4","text":"Empirical matter → transcendental rule of synthesis → relation to possible experience → mediated deduction → philosophical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS5","text":"The distinction yields three evidential oppositions: concept-generation versus exposition, immediate construction versus deduction, and intuitive demonstration versus discursive proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS6","text":"Failure of philosophical construction eliminates mathematical definitions, axioms, demonstrations, and direct synthetic dogmata from speculative philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS7","text":"The resulting closure redirects philosophy from transcendental expansion toward systematic criticism, boundary-determination, error detection, and revision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:AF1","text":"The argument moves through mathematical success → transcendental imitation → method-identity question → construction–concept distinction → pure-form–empirical-matter division → test of definitions, axioms, and demonstrations → elimination of speculative dogmata → critical-systematic discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME1","text":"Mathematics and philosophy are reclassified by their modes of cognition rather than by separate inventories of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME2","text":"Quantity becomes a consequence of constructibility rather than the primitive definition of mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME3","text":"The a priori divides into constructible intuition and discursive conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME4","text":"Certainty divides into immediate intuitive evidence and mediated validity established through deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME5","text":"Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations cease to function as universal norms of rational proof and become domain-specific effects of construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME6","text":"Transcendental principles are reclassified from direct dogmata into conditions governing possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME7","text":"Philosophy’s inability to imitate mathematics is reclassified from cognitive deficiency into the condition of its critical function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ME8","text":"Dogmatism is reclassified as an illicit transfer of evidential form rather than merely excessive confidence in particular doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text tests whether philosophical reasoning can employ mathematical definitions, axioms, and demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The legitimacy of each method is determined by the relation among concepts, pure intuition, empirical matter, and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Philosophy is redirected from imitating mathematical expansion toward policing validity, limits, and inferential error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Appearances divide into the a priori forms of space and time and the a posteriori matter of sensation, while causality and other transcendental concepts govern temporal synthesis without presenting objects independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the methodological distinction, its transcendental ground, the tripartite evidential test, and the critical conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT2","text":"The exclusivity of space and time as a priori intuitions and the objective validity of the categories remain underdetermined because their proofs are imported from earlier stages of the Critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent only if direct synthetic cognition from concepts is distinguished rigorously from mediated synthetic principles validated through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where differences in available intuition generate differences in method, evidence, and legitimate scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT5","text":"The claim that mathematics alone possesses definitions depends on restricting strict definition to object-valid, a priori constructible concepts, despite admitting definitions of arbitrary projects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT6","text":"The coexistence of apodeictically certain philosophical principles with the denial of philosophical demonstrations depends upon a distinction between deduced certainty and intuitive evidence whose full defense is not supplied here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT7","text":"The use of possible experience both as the proof-ground of transcendental principles and as what those principles make possible creates a reflexive dependence that requires the prior transcendental deduction to avoid circularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT8","text":"The assertion that dogmatic methods merely conceal error is stronger than the demonstrated conclusion that mathematical evidence cannot be transferred unchanged into philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement from temptation to restriction, but it cannot reproduce the argument’s full warrant without the antecedent accounts of intuition, categories, and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 85","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","context":"Chapter 85","original_title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","original_context":"Chapter 85"},"original_title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","order":86,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_5"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_5@R001","sha256":"6d981d001c153e9d8f80f0a966b557350957ccb4e278f74853d26423a7415acd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS1","text":"The governing problem is how pure reason can confront unavoidable disputes over supersensible claims without dogmatic proof, censorship, or terminal scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS2","text":"Unrestricted criticism, rather than partisan victory, secures reason’s legitimate domain and the possibility of lasting intellectual peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS3","text":"Objective speculative validity is restricted to possible experience, rendering both affirmative and negative transcendent claims indemonstrable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS4","text":"Polemical success is reduced from proving a transcendent proposition to showing that its contrary cannot establish itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS5","text":"Free contest corrects reason by exposing dialectical illusion, whereas coercive protection preserves dogmatic fragility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS6","text":"Scepticism functions as censure and preparation but must yield to systematic a priori determination of reason’s necessary limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AS7","text":"The argument is juridical-critical, diagnostic, and reconstructive, moving from speculative conflict through jurisdictional diagnosis and sceptical interruption to bounded rational self-possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","text":"Every exercise of reason must remain open to unrestricted criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","text":"Universal human reason is the sole legitimate tribunal for rational claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","text":"Critique determines reason’s rights and limits by examining reason’s own sources and powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","text":"Possible experience supplies the only objective criterion of speculative truth available to human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A5","text":"The understanding possesses synthetic a priori principles whose objective validity consists in governing possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","text":"Ideas of supersensible objects cannot determine either the actuality or the impossibility of their objects for human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A7","text":"A polemical defence establishes at most the failure of a dogmatic counterclaim, not the truth of the defended proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A8","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A9","text":"Treating phenomena as things in themselves and demanding their unconditioned completion generates apparent rational contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A10","text":"Practical rational interest can warrant faith in transcendent propositions without increasing their speculative demonstrability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A11","text":"Free reciprocal testing advances reason by exposing illusion and restricting unjustified claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A12","text":"Rational adjudication requires honesty concerning doubts, proof deficiencies, and actual convictions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A13","text":"Sceptical censure of failed dogmatic attempts produces doubt but cannot establish necessary limits to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:A14","text":"Necessary limits and unavoidable ignorance can be established only through systematic a priori investigation of reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX1","text":"No claim, institution, interest, or sacred doctrine is exempt from criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX2","text":"Reason is not a despotic authority and cannot be legitimately governed by foreign coercive powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX3","text":"Refuting an opponent is not equivalent to proving one’s own transcendent assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX4","text":"Phenomena must not be interpreted as independently existing things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX5","text":"Speculative pure reason cannot demonstrate the existence of God, an immortal soul, or freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX6","text":"Speculative pure reason cannot demonstrate the nonexistence or impossibility of those objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX7","text":"Rational faith is not speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX8","text":"Practical usefulness does not legitimate deceptive proof, concealment of doubt, or censorship.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX9","text":"Protective guardianship and dogmatic instruction do not produce durable rational conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX10","text":"Scepticism is not a permanent dwelling-place for reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX11","text":"Empirical awareness of ignorance is not knowledge of cognition’s necessary boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:NX12","text":"The necessity of causal law is not reducible to habitual association among observed events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D1","text":"Opposed assertions concerning supersensible objects are symmetrical in speculative undecidability, even when one side has greater practical interest.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D2","text":"A transcendent affirmative may be preserved against dogmatic refutation without thereby receiving a satisfactory speculative title.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A7","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D3","text":"No proper object-level polemic is possible beyond experience because neither party possesses a criterion capable of fixing the disputed object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D4","text":"Cosmological antinomy is dissolved by correcting the status assigned to phenomena rather than by selecting one dogmatic proposition over its contrary.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A8","CH_BA94_SI_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D5","text":"Speculative attacks cannot overthrow practically warranted commitments when those commitments are not misrepresented as speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10","CH_BA94_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D6","text":"Suppressing opposition deprives reason of its corrective mechanism and leaves inherited convictions vulnerable to later dialectical attack.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:A11","CH_BA94_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D7","text":"Critique replaces the unstable victories of dogmatic warfare with jurisdictional decisions concerning which claims reason is entitled to make.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D8","text":"Scepticism is a necessary interruption of dogmatism but becomes illegitimate when it converts failed attempts into a universal verdict on reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A13","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D9","text":"Accidental ignorance motivates further inquiry, whereas only critically demonstrated necessary ignorance can terminate inquiry as knowledge of limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A13","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D10","text":"The contingency of particular causal determinations does not entail the contingency of causal law, because the law is a condition of possible experience rather than an inference from repeated experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A5","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:D11","text":"Critical education must expose students to opposed speculative arguments and train them to detect the dialectical defects shared by both sides.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:A11","CH_BA94_SI_5:A13","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD1","text":"Critique transforms metaphysical warfare over supersensible objects into constitutional inquiry concerning the conditions under which any claim may possess authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD2","text":"Rational freedom and rational limitation become mutually constitutive because reason remains autonomous only by submitting itself to universally valid self-criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD3","text":"Supersensible doctrines survive critique only through a change of status from speculative cognition to practically grounded rational commitment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD4","text":"The dogmatic opponent becomes an internal instrument of reason’s self-correction rather than an external danger to be silenced.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:A11","CH_BA94_SI_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD5","text":"Lasting speculative peace is produced by terminating illegitimate jurisdiction, not by answering questions about supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD6","text":"Epistemic symmetry between transcendent affirmation and negation coexists with a practical asymmetry favouring affirmation, while the criterion separating rational faith from interest-driven preference remains incompletely formalized here.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10","CH_BA94_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD7","text":"Critique claims necessary and enduring jurisdiction while requiring unrestricted criticizability, so its final authority remains conditional on demonstrations supplied outside this section.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SD8","text":"The functionalization of error, conflict, and hypocrisy as purposive contributions to humanity invokes a teleological assurance that the restriction on supersensible cognition cannot itself certify.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A6","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX1","text":"Human cognition possesses no intellectual intuition through which supersensible objects could be directly presented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX2","text":"A rational idea cannot generate or certify the existence of its corresponding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX3","text":"No criterion of objective speculative truth is available independently of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX4","text":"Repeated failure or observed limitation cannot establish necessary incapacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX5","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition cannot be wholly reducible to empirical habit without destroying the possibility of critique itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX6","text":"Anticipated social benefit or harm cannot determine the speculative truth of a proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX7","text":"Reason cannot acquire durable security through insulation from opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX1","text":"A completed theoretical metaphysics of God, freedom, and immortality is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX2","text":"No standpoint external to reason is available from which reason can be authoritatively judged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX3","text":"Final object-level victory in transcendent dispute is unavailable because the disputed object can never enter the common evidential field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX4","text":"Stable dogmatic innocence is impossible once reason encounters opposed arguments generated by its own structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX5","text":"The possibility that critique is merely another revisable combatant rather than the final tribunal is structurally inadmissible to the chapter’s juridical closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX6","text":"The practical foundation expected to secure supersensible commitments remains deferred beyond the speculative argument developed here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS1","text":"The constitutional mechanism is unrestricted criticizability → universal rational tribunal → determination of rights and limits → lawful possession of a bounded domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS2","text":"The polemical mechanism is transcendent assertion → absence of experiential criterion → reciprocal inability to prove → defensive invalidation of the contrary → dissolution of proper polemic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS3","text":"The antinomy mechanism is appearance treated as thing in itself → demand for unconditioned completion → contradictory inference → restoration of the transcendental distinction → dissolution of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS4","text":"The civic-pedagogical mechanism is perceived doctrinal threat → censorship or deceptive apologetics → dogmatic fragility, whereas free exposure → critical testing → resistance to dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS5","text":"The developmental mechanism is dogmatism → sceptical censure → recognition that failed facta do not determine necessary bounds → systematic a priori self-investigation → criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS6","text":"The correction of Hume proceeds from separating contingent empirical causal determination from the necessary causal form of possible experience and thereby restoring limited synthetic a priori validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS7","text":"Strategic closure proceeds through boundary determination → renunciation of transcendent knowledge → retention of practical rational orientation → cessation of speculative war.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:AF1","text":"Unrestricted criticism → dogmatic antithetic → experiential criterion of validity → reciprocal speculative impotence → polemic reduced to counter-refutation → critique established as jurisdiction → free contest and critical education → scepticism subordinated → Humean overreach corrected → bounded certainty and legal peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME1","text":"Supersensible disputes are reclassified from contests over objects into inquiries concerning the jurisdiction of cognitive faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME2","text":"Affirmation and negation cease to form a speculative winner-loser opposition because both exceed the available criterion of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME3","text":"Faith and knowledge become formally separable modes of assent rather than competing degrees of the same speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME4","text":"Freedom and discipline cease to be opposites because criticism limits reason through reason’s own universal legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME5","text":"Scepticism is repositioned from philosophical conclusion to transitional instrument between dogmatism and criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME6","text":"Ignorance is divided into indefinite empirical lack and determinate critical knowledge of necessary limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME7","text":"Hostile argument is reclassified from a danger to doctrine into an exercise that reveals reason’s legitimate possessions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ME8","text":"A priori cognition is separated from transcendent speculation and reassigned to the formal conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the argument governs disputes concerning God, the soul, freedom, immortality, causal necessity, and freedom of speculative expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, criticism functions as a juridical institution that replaces intellectual war, censorship, and doctrinal guardianship with lawful adjudication and critical education.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the argument refuses partisan entry into transcendent disputes and redirects inquiry toward the conditions that make claims valid or inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT4","text":"At the ontological-epistemic level, the system distinguishes phenomena from things in themselves and confines objective cognition to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT5","text":"At the temporal level, rational maturity develops from dogmatic assertion through sceptical interruption to critical knowledge of a permanently bounded domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s transition from free polemic to critical jurisdiction and from scepticism to bounded cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT2","text":"The objective validity of synthetic a priori principles remains dependent on transcendental demonstrations supplied elsewhere in the work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT3","text":"The practical warrant for faith in God, freedom, and immortality is invoked but not established within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT4","text":"The structure is internally coherent if possible experience is accepted as the exclusive criterion of objective speculative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT5","text":"The system is strongest in distinguishing reciprocal transcendent unprovability from critical knowledge of necessary limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT6","text":"The juridical analogy and the teleological treatment of conflict perform argumentative work not fully reducible to the stated epistemic premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT7","text":"The principal fault lines are the practical preference for affirmative doctrines despite speculative symmetry, and the tribunal’s claim to finality despite the universal requirement of criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if Humean scepticism is retained as the necessary middle stage rather than treated as the chapter’s final adversary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 86","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","context":"Chapter 86","original_title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","original_context":"Chapter 86"},"original_title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","order":87,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_6"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_6@R001","sha256":"ad49421b907273620749b1aed52e3baedda5127d697a6ddddbc5dbc1b5188b4d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS1","text":"The failure of pure speculation to extend knowledge creates an apparent opening for unrestricted hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS2","text":"Hypothesis is legitimate only where an object’s possibility is secured and the supposition explains what is certainly given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS3","text":"The conditions of possible experience delimit both theoretical objecthood and admissible explanatory causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS4","text":"Transcendental hypotheses are excluded from explanation because they replace inquiry with incomprehensible grounds beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS5","text":"Hypotheses concerning transcendent matters retain a strictly defensive use against equally ungrounded dogmatic objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AS6","text":"Reason achieves durable discipline only by eliciting, confronting, and neutralizing its own dialectical objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A1","text":"Pure speculative reason cannot extend cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A2","text":"A legitimate hypothesis presupposes certainty of the possible object to which it refers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A3","text":"A legitimate hypothesis must connect its explanatory ground to something actually given and certain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A4","text":"The categories determine dynamical connections only when such connections are or can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","text":"The conditions of possible experience are the only theoretical conditions under which things can be cognized as possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A6","text":"Ideas of reason are problematical regulative principles for systematizing experience rather than cognitions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A7","text":"The explanation of phenomena may employ only grounds connected with them through known laws of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A8","text":"A hypothesis is sufficient only if it determines in advance the experienced consequences attributed to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A9","text":"Auxiliary hypotheses weaken a hypothesis when their necessity lacks independent justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","text":"Pure reason can issue either necessary cognition or a declaration of non-cognition, but not theoretical opinion beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","text":"A hypothesis may defend against a transcendent assertion without serving as proof of its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A12","text":"Equal speculative ignorance of opposed transcendent claims prevents either party from claiming theoretical superiority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:A13","text":"Speculative reason is inherently dialectical and must subject its own claims to adversarial examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX1","text":"Hypothesis is not permission to invent objects, powers, properties, substances, relations, durations, or presences unavailable to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX2","text":"Logical non-contradiction is not sufficient to establish the objective possibility of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX3","text":"Ideas of reason are not representations of imaginary, merely possible, or independently knowable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX4","text":"A transcendental hypothesis is not an explanation of natural phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX5","text":"Hyperphysical grounds are not admissible substitutes for empirical causal investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX6","text":"The absolute totality of explanatory conditions is not a requirement that may obstruct empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX7","text":"Probable judgement and opinion are not valid forms of pure speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX8","text":"Defensive hypotheses are not personal opinions, dogmatic theses, or proofs of transcendent reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:NX9","text":"External controversy is not the primary source of transcendental conflict, because the opposition originates within reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D1","text":"Theoretical hypotheses are restricted to empirical possibilities whose explanatory relation to given appearances can be specified.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A2","CH_BA94_SI_6:A3","CH_BA94_SI_6:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D2","text":"The soul may be treated as a regulative unity for investigating mental appearances without being asserted as a simple substance.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A6","CH_BA94_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D3","text":"Natural order and purposiveness must be investigated through natural laws even when a hyperphysical ground appears explanatorily tempting.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D4","text":"The multiplication of ad hoc auxiliaries functions as evidence that an original hypothesis lacks demonstrated sufficiency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A8","CH_BA94_SI_6:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D5","text":"Neither the existence nor the non-existence of intelligible entities can be established by speculative reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","CH_BA94_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D6","text":"A counter-hypothesis may exhibit the incompleteness of an opponent’s appeal to empirical conditions without acquiring positive theoretical validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:D7","text":"The philosopher must abandon a defensive transcendent hypothesis once the opposing dogmatic assertion is withdrawn.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SD1","text":"The discipline of hypothesis divides reason into three non-interchangeable functions: empirical explanation, regulative systematization, and polemical self-defence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A6","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SD2","text":"Explanatory closure is reclassified as a methodological danger when it is achieved by appeal to a ground that terminates rather than advances inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SD3","text":"The inability to prove transcendent claims generates a relative equality in speculative conflict rather than a licence for either affirmation or denial.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SD4","text":"Reason’s permanent security depends not on suppressing objections but on making their strongest form visible and dissolving their source.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:A12","CH_BA94_SI_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SD5","text":"Transcendental fictions acquire conditional legitimacy only as instruments for preventing the conversion of empirical limitation into absolute impossibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX1","text":"The system excludes any theoretical route from a merely thinkable predicate to an objectively possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the demand that empirical explanation must culminate in an unconditioned explanatory ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the inference that the absence of empirical conditions proves the impossibility of what exceeds them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a protected doctrine that may evade the objections it would direct against its opponent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the conversion of methodological usefulness into ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX1","text":"No theoretical possession of transcendent reality is available to either affirmative metaphysics or its empirical negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX2","text":"No final speculative peace is available through external victory, because the adversary is the self-dividing structure of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX3","text":"No positive knowledge of an intelligible order can be extracted from the inadequacy of sensuous explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX4","text":"No absolute validity may attach to a hypothesis whose only role is to neutralize an opponent’s unwarranted certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS1","text":"Speculative cognitive failure produces an apparent surplus of hypothetical freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS2","text":"Secured object-possibility, empirical connection, and anticipatory sufficiency convert supposition into an admissible physical hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS3","text":"Absence of experiential objecthood converts transcendental supposition into fiction without explanatory entitlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS4","text":"Hyperphysical explanation terminates empirical causal inquiry and is therefore methodologically excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS5","text":"Symmetrical ignorance concerning transcendent claims permits a relative counter-hypothesis solely for defensive refutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS6","text":"Defensive counter-hypothesis is withdrawn when dogmatic opposition ceases, preventing polemic from becoming doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7","text":"Reason internalizes opposition, intensifies its objections, and seeks settlement through critique rather than suppression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AF1","text":"The failure of speculative extension leads to the question of whether hypothesis can occupy the space left by knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AF2","text":"The question is constrained by the distinction between empirically grounded physical hypotheses and transcendental explanatory inventions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AF3","text":"The exclusion of transcendental explanation leads to standards of sufficiency, predictive consequence, and suspicion toward auxiliary repair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AF4","text":"The prohibition on dogmatic transcendent assertion is converted into a limited right of polemical counter-supposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:AF5","text":"Polemical symmetry culminates in a discipline requiring reason to stage and overcome its own dialectical opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ME1","text":"The distinction between possible experience and bare thinkability becomes the boundary between legitimate hypothesis and empty invention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ME2","text":"The distinction between regulative ideas and constitutive objects separates systematic inquiry from metaphysical object-assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ME3","text":"The distinction between physical and hyperphysical explanation reclassifies appeals to ultimate grounds as impediments to inquiry rather than explanatory achievements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ME4","text":"The distinction between dogmatic proof and defensive counter-hypothesis creates a narrow polemical space without reopening speculative metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ME5","text":"Opposition is relocated from an external adversary to the internal dialectical operations of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text establishes conditions under which hypotheses concerning natural and transcendent matters may be used.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT2","text":"Structural — The possibility of objects, the authority of categories, and the scope of explanatory laws are all subordinated to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text distinguishes inquiry from closure, defence from proof, and self-critique from merely external controversy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomenal existence is conditioned by space, time, and sensibility, while intelligible existence remains neither theoretically cognizable nor theoretically disprovable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central restriction of hypothesis to empirically possible objects and empirically connected explanations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes regulative use, explanatory use, and polemical use without allowing one to substitute for another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT3","text":"The criterion of secured object-possibility remains dependent on prior critical doctrine concerning the limits of categories and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT4","text":"The defensive use of transcendental fictions creates a controlled tension with the general prohibition on hypotheses lacking experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT5","text":"The claimed equality of speculative ignorance suspends dogmatic verdicts but cannot itself determine which practical suppositions may be admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT6","text":"The announced practical exception remains structurally necessary but is not developed within this argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if polemical hypotheses remain relative, retractable, and incapable of becoming positive doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 87","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","context":"Chapter 87","original_title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","original_context":"Chapter 87"},"original_title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","order":88,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SI_7"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SI_7@R001","sha256":"042adc6fc9703bae8474c3777580819839710144fa5a807d3c79be37bd78e198","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS1","text":"Transcendental synthetic proof must establish the objective validity of its concepts before it can establish a proposition formed from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS2","text":"The proof-problem is reframed from deriving predicates out of concepts to showing the conditions under which experience and its objects are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS3","text":"Pure reason is disciplined by requiring a deduction of the source, right, and objective scope of every principle used in speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS4","text":"The uniqueness of the conceptual condition of an object entails the uniqueness of any valid transcendental ground of proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS5","text":"Indirect proof is excluded from transcendental inquiry because subjective conditions can be mistaken for objective conditions and can invalidate both opposed propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AS6","text":"Criticism converts the failure of dogmatic proof into a restriction of speculative reason and a relocation of reason to practical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A1","text":"A transcendental synthetic proposition requires prior proof of the objective validity of its concepts and of the possibility of their synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A2","text":"No cognition can pass a priori beyond a concept without a guide not contained in that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A3","text":"Possible experience guides transcendental cognition when concepts of the understanding are in question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A4","text":"A transcendental proof establishes a conceptual connection by showing that experience and its object would be impossible without it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A5","text":"Principles of the understanding have validity only for objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A6","text":"Principles of pure reason employed as objectively constitutive are dialectical and lack objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A7","text":"Subjective association and subjective conditions of thought can be mistaken for objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A8","text":"A proof using pure ideas must first establish the possibility of the proposed synthetic extension of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A9","text":"A transcendental proposition has only one possible ground of proof because its concept alone determines its possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A10","text":"A direct proof discloses the ground of a proposition’s truth, whereas an indirect proof can at most establish truth through the falsity of its opposite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A11","text":"Indirect proof is valid only where subjective representation cannot be confused with objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A12","text":"Where an object-conception is impossible, both its affirmative and negative predicates are equally invalid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:A13","text":"A transcendental deduction of the principles used is the necessary tribunal for every speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX1","text":"Transcendental proof is not an immediate transition from one concept to another concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX2","text":"Conceptual simplicity in abstraction is not objective simplicity of the thing represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX3","text":"The unity of apperception is not a proof that the soul is a simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX4","text":"Principles of the understanding are not grounds for cognition of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX5","text":"Ideas of pure reason are not objectively valid principles of speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX6","text":"A plurality of persuasive arguments is not a plurality of valid transcendental proofs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX7","text":"The falsity of a counter-proposition is not sufficient to prove a transcendental proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:NX8","text":"The sensible world considered as a totality given in itself is not a coherent object of determination as either finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D1","text":"Causality is established transcendentally only as a condition under which an event can be temporally determined within experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A3","CH_BA94_SI_7:A4","CH_BA94_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D2","text":"A proof of a supersensible object fails unless it first justifies the possibility of extending cognition through the idea of that object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A1","CH_BA94_SI_7:A6","CH_BA94_SI_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D3","text":"The demand for a deduction can reject an entire class of dialectical proofs without locating the particular sophism in each proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A6","CH_BA94_SI_7:A7","CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D4","text":"Multiple proofs of the same transcendental thesis signal that none has isolated the unique objective condition determined by its concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A4","CH_BA94_SI_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D5","text":"An indirect disproof in transcendental inquiry may reveal only an incompatibility with subjective reason rather than an impossibility in the object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A7","CH_BA94_SI_7:A10","CH_BA94_SI_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D6","text":"Antinomial alternatives can both be false when they predicate opposed determinations of an object constituted by an impossible presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A11","CH_BA94_SI_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:D7","text":"Speculative reason must renounce claims whose proof depends on treating regulative ideas as constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A6","CH_BA94_SI_7:A8","CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SD1","text":"The proper object of transcendental proof is not a supersensible thing but the lawful possibility of experience as the field in which concepts acquire objective reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A1","CH_BA94_SI_7:A3","CH_BA94_SI_7:A4","CH_BA94_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SD2","text":"Critique changes proof from an accumulation of conclusions into a jurisdiction over the conditions under which conclusion-making can claim objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A7","CH_BA94_SI_7:A9","CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SD3","text":"Dialectical conflict is structurally produced when reason converts the subjective demand for the unconditioned into an objective determination of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A6","CH_BA94_SI_7:A7","CH_BA94_SI_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SD4","text":"The discipline of proof displaces dogmatic victory over an opponent with critical determination of the admissible domain of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A10","CH_BA94_SI_7:A11","CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX1","text":"Logical coherence of a representation is not sufficient for objective reference to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX2","text":"The subjective necessity with which reason seeks completion is not evidence that an unconditioned object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX3","text":"Contradictory predicates do not necessarily divide a real object into exhaustive alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX4","text":"Speculative reason has no self-authorizing capacity to determine what lies beyond the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX5","text":"The defeat of an opposing proof is not identical with the objective establishment of its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SNX1","text":"No theoretical procedure can transform the idea of the unconditioned into cognition of an unconditioned object merely by formal inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SNX2","text":"No speculative proof can occupy a standpoint outside the subjective conditions whose unnoticed operation generates transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SNX3","text":"The absolute totality sought by dogmatic metaphysics is unavailable as an object for determinate theoretical predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:SNX4","text":"The final restriction of speculative reason silently forecloses the identification of rational demand with theoretical possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:FGS1","text":"Conceptual claim → demand for objective validity → demand for possible synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:FGS2","text":"Possible synthesis → identification of its guide → possible experience as the condition of objective reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:FGS3","text":"Objective-reference condition → single legitimate transcendental ground → direct proof of the condition’s necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:FGS4","text":"Failure to deduce the principle’s source and right → classification as dialectical appearance → suspension of speculative assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:FGS5","text":"Exposure of dialectical appearance → abandonment of constitutive speculative pretension → confinement of reason to regulative and practical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF1","text":"Transcendental proposition → prior validation of concepts → possible experience as mediating ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF2","text":"Pure idea → unproved synthetic extension → paralogism or dialectical assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF3","text":"Demand for deduction → invalidation of unsupported principles → economy of critical refutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF4","text":"Conceptual determination of an object → unique ground of proof → exposure of dogmatic proof-plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF5","text":"Subjective-objective confusion → failure of indirect proof → necessity of ostensive procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:AF6","text":"Failure of dogmatic proof → critical self-limitation → practical destination of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ME1","text":"Proof is reclassified from a relation among propositions into a demonstration of the conditions that make objective reference possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ME2","text":"Causality, substance, soul, deity, and world-whole are separated according to whether their concepts can receive objective validity through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ME3","text":"The opposition between direct proof and refutation is reorganized: refutation becomes subordinate to prior critique of the principles used by both sides.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ME4","text":"The apparent opposition between finite and infinite world-totality collapses when the allegedly given total object is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ME5","text":"Dogmatic metaphysical controversy is redescribed as a conflict generated by shared subjective conditions rather than by rival knowledge of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — Transcendental propositions require special rules governing their proof, number of proofs, and permissible form of inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objective cognition depends on conditions of possible experience, while dialectic arises from the misattribution of subjective conditions to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical reason does not defeat every dogmatic claim individually but tests the legitimacy of the principles from which all such claims proceed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are determinable only within conditions that permit experiential temporal ordering; unconditioned totalities and supersensible substances lack this theoretical mode of determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from proof as inference to proof as deduction of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT2","text":"The structure depends principally on the distinction between subjective representation and objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent if possible experience is accepted as the exclusive court of objective validity for theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where it derives causal necessity from the possibility of temporal experience rather than from conceptual association or contingent observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT5","text":"The claim that every transcendental proposition has only one proof-ground compresses the distinction between a unique objective ground and potentially diverse valid expositions of that ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT6","text":"The transition from speculative restriction to practical reason is asserted as the final destination of discipline but is not independently derived within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the critique of indirect proof is retained as a consequence of subjective-objective subreption rather than as a general rejection of reductio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 88","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","context":"Chapter 88","original_title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","original_context":"Chapter 88"},"original_title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","order":89,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CICP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CICP@R001","sha256":"271b490bd627f3e088cf62ae3e739a9382a34db88c0debac8fe4f896b884614f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS1","text":"Pure reason cannot securely discover truth through speculation and therefore requires discipline against its own deviations and illusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS2","text":"Self-imposed limits both restrain reason and protect its legitimate cognitive possessions from rival claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS3","text":"The primary use of philosophy of pure reason is negative: it determines the limits of speculative employment and guards against error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS4","text":"Reason nevertheless contains a positive orientation toward cognitions beyond experience, whose mischaracterization produces speculative error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS5","text":"Speculative reason fails to attain its desired knowledge, whereas practical reason remains the possible site of reason’s proper employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AS6","text":"A canon can govern only a faculty with a possible proper use; because speculative pure reason has no such use, its canon belongs to practical rather than speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A1","text":"Pure speculative reason is incompetent to establish truth by speculation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A2","text":"Pure reason requires discipline to restrain its deviations and expose its self-generated illusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A3","text":"The discipline of pure reason is exercised by reason itself rather than by an external authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A4","text":"The limitation of speculative claims protects whatever legitimate cognition remains from opposing usurpation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A5","text":"The principal use of philosophy of pure reason is negative and consists in guarding against error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A6","text":"Pure reason contains positive cognitions whose misidentified status becomes a source of error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A7","text":"Human reason is oriented toward knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A8","text":"Speculative reason cannot successfully satisfy this orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A9","text":"A canon is a set of a priori principles governing the proper employment of a cognitive faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A10","text":"A canon can exist only where a proper employment of the relevant faculty is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A11","text":"The synthetic cognition of pure speculative reason is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A12","text":"The speculative exercise of pure reason is dialectical rather than cognitively legitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:A13","text":"The proper employment of pure reason, if it exists, belongs to its practical rather than speculative use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX1","text":"Pure reason is not a self-sufficient instrument for extending knowledge through speculation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX2","text":"Philosophy of pure reason is not primarily an organon for discovering new truths.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX3","text":"Speculative limitation is not an external suppression imposed by another cognitive authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX4","text":"Pure speculative reason is not capable of a valid synthetic canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX5","text":"Transcendental logic, insofar as it concerns speculative reason, is not a canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX6","text":"The dialectical activity of speculative reason is not a proper employment of the faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX7","text":"The failure of speculation does not eliminate reason’s positive orientation toward supersensible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:NX8","text":"Practical reason is not treated as merely another unsuccessful route of speculation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D1","text":"Because speculative reason generates illusions through its own activity, critique must function as internal self-discipline rather than as external correction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A1","CH_BA94_CICP:A2","CH_BA94_CICP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D2","text":"Because limiting speculative claims removes illegitimate pretensions, critique converts restriction into protection of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A4","CH_BA94_CICP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D3","text":"Because the positive content of pure reason is misused rather than simply absent, critique must distinguish its proper character from its speculative distortion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A6","CH_BA94_CICP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D4","text":"Because reason’s desire exceeds experience while speculation cannot satisfy it, the failure of speculative knowledge does not terminate reason’s orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A7","CH_BA94_CICP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D5","text":"Because a canon presupposes a possible proper employment, the impossibility of speculative synthetic cognition excludes a speculative canon.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A9","CH_BA94_CICP:A10","CH_BA94_CICP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D6","text":"Because speculative reason has no legitimate synthetic use, transcendental logic can discipline its speculative activity but cannot prescribe it as a canon.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A10","CH_BA94_CICP:A11","CH_BA94_CICP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:D7","text":"Because practical employment remains the only possible proper use of pure reason, the search for a canon must be redirected from speculation to practice.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A10","CH_BA94_CICP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SD1","text":"Reason’s limitation is simultaneously epistemic restraint, internal self-government, and protection of a residual domain of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A2","CH_BA94_CICP:A3","CH_BA94_CICP:A4","CH_BA94_CICP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SD2","text":"The critique of pure reason transforms failure to know beyond experience into a criterion for distinguishing legitimate from illegitimate uses of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A1","CH_BA94_CICP:A5","CH_BA94_CICP:A8","CH_BA94_CICP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SD3","text":"The absence of a speculative canon does not abolish rational normativity; it relocates normativity from speculative cognition to practical employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A9","CH_BA94_CICP:A11","CH_BA94_CICP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SD4","text":"Pure reason is structurally divided between a negative speculative function that limits error and a possible practical function that supplies its proper positive employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A5","CH_BA94_CICP:A6","CH_BA94_CICP:A12","CH_BA94_CICP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SD5","text":"The system preserves reason’s aspiration beyond experience by denying speculative possession of its object while retaining the aspiration as a motive for practical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A6","CH_BA94_CICP:A7","CH_BA94_CICP:A8","CH_BA94_CICP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX1","text":"Legitimate cognition cannot be secured by unrestricted speculative confidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX2","text":"Rational autonomy does not mean unrestricted permission for reason to legislate beyond its competence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX3","text":"The existence of rational concepts does not entail the possibility of speculative knowledge of their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX4","text":"The persistence of metaphysical desire does not validate the speculative method used to satisfy it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX5","text":"A faculty’s possession of cognitive content does not by itself establish a proper rule for using that content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX6","text":"The failure of speculative reason cannot be interpreted as the failure of reason as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:DNX7","text":"Practical legitimacy cannot be inferred merely from speculative aspiration; it must arise from a distinct mode of employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that speculation can provide final metaphysical security on its own terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SNX2","text":"The system excludes an external tribunal capable of disciplining reason more fundamentally than reason’s own critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the reduction of reason’s supersensible orientation to an error that can simply be discarded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the identity of reason’s desire for supersensible knowledge with an actual speculative possession of it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:SNX5","text":"The system leaves unavailable a positive speculative science of the supersensible while preserving the demand for a non-speculative completion of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:FGS1","text":"Speculative aspiration exceeds experience → speculation generates illusion → self-discipline becomes necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:FGS2","text":"Self-discipline limits speculative claims → illegitimate pretensions are removed → legitimate cognition is secured against attack.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:FGS3","text":"Pure reason’s negative function is established → reason’s positive orientation remains unexplained by speculation alone → practical reason becomes the alternative site of fulfillment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:FGS4","text":"Canon is defined as a rule for possible proper employment → speculative synthetic cognition is declared impossible → no speculative canon can exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:FGS5","text":"Speculative reason is classified as dialectical → transcendental logic functions as discipline rather than canon → the canon of pure reason is sought in practical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AF1","text":"Speculative incompetence → internal discipline → limitation of claims → protection of legitimate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AF2","text":"Negative critique → persistence of supersensible aspiration → failure of speculative satisfaction → redirection toward practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AF3","text":"Definition of canon → requirement of possible proper employment → impossibility of speculative synthesis → exclusion of a speculative canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:AF4","text":"Dialectical status of speculation → disciplinary role of transcendental logic → practical canon of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME1","text":"The boundary of reason becomes a positive epistemic instrument rather than a mere deprivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME2","text":"Critique reclassifies philosophical success from acquiring metaphysical truths to preventing illegitimate claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME3","text":"The distinction between cognition and aspiration becomes necessary: reason may be oriented toward what speculation cannot know.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME4","text":"The concept of canon is separated from the general activity of critique and restricted to a faculty’s possible proper employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME5","text":"The failure of speculative metaphysics redirects the normative center of pure reason toward practical use rather than toward abandonment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ME6","text":"Transcendental logic is repositioned as a boundary-discipline for speculative reason rather than a source of speculative extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Speculative reason fails to establish truth and must be disciplined by limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Reason’s self-critique separates legitimate cognition from dialectical overreach and distinguishes discipline from canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Philosophical authority is relocated from the production of new speculative truths to the regulation of claims and the protection of reason’s legitimate domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reason is constituted by an enduring orientation beyond experience whose speculative fulfillment is impossible but whose practical fulfillment remains open.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative failure to practical redirection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT2","text":"The decisive hinge is the definition of a canon as a rule for a possible proper employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent in distinguishing the negative task of critique from the positive possibility of practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT4","text":"The relation between positive cognitions and their practical status remains underdetermined within the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT5","text":"The transition from the persistence of supersensible desire to the promise of practical reason is asserted more than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on maintaining a tension between reason’s aspiration beyond experience and the impossibility of speculative access to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CICP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement: speculative limitation → protection of legitimate cognition → distinction between discipline and canon → practical relocation of reason’s proper use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 89","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 89","original_title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 89"},"original_title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","order":90,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIUE"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIUE@R001","sha256":"50778a3c381203cd491a1efa22eb17b2a8406bc71b9f506c4716e908bb6d75e0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS1","text":"Reason naturally exceeds experience in pursuit of a self-subsistent systematic unity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS2","text":"The ideas of freedom, immortality, and God constitute reason’s highest speculative problems but yield no immanent knowledge of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS3","text":"The practical interest of reason, rather than its speculative interest, supplies the real significance of these transcendent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS4","text":"Practical reason divides into empirically conditioned prudence and pure moral legislation determined a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS5","text":"Moral law converts the ultimate question of reason into the question of what ought to be done under the ideas of freedom, God, and a future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AS6","text":"Practical freedom is admissible through experience as reason’s causal determination of the will, while transcendental freedom remains a speculative problem excluded from the practical canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A1","text":"Reason possesses a natural drive to transcend experience and organize cognition into systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A2","text":"The highest ends of reason must possess complete unity if they are to serve the highest interest of humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A3","text":"Transcendental speculation is concentrated on freedom of the will, immortality of the soul, and the existence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A4","text":"Transcendent ideas cannot serve as immanent principles for explaining objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A5","text":"Experience must be explained through natural causes and unchangeable laws rather than through transcendent grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A6","text":"The speculative cognition of freedom, immortality, and God is without practical utility for physical or empirical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A7","text":"The value of the cardinal transcendent ideas therefore belongs primarily to the practical interest of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A8","text":"Practical cognition concerns what is possible through free will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A9","text":"Empirically conditioned willing permits reason only a regulative role in unifying inclination-based ends and their means.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","text":"Pure practical laws are determined a priori by reason and command absolutely rather than conditionally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A11","text":"Moral laws alone constitute a canon of pure practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A12","text":"Practical freedom consists in the will’s capacity to be determined by rational motives independently of sensuous impulses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A13","text":"Experience establishes practical freedom by displaying reason as a causal power in the determination of the human will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A14","text":"Transcendental freedom concerns independence from every sensuous determining cause and remains a speculative problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:A15","text":"Transcendental philosophy excludes empirical psychological and affective elements because it concerns pure a priori cognition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX1","text":"Speculative reason is not authorized to use freedom, immortality, or God as explanatory principles of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX2","text":"Knowledge of an incorporeal soul is not positive knowledge of either present life or future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX3","text":"The existence of a supreme intelligence is not a warrant for inferring particular natural arrangements where experience does not disclose them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX4","text":"Transcendent explanation is not permitted to displace natural causal explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX5","text":"Prudential unification of happiness-seeking inclinations is not pure moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX6","text":"A will determined solely by sensuous impulse is not free will but animal arbitrium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX7","text":"Practical freedom is not identical with transcendental freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX8","text":"The practical canon is not required to resolve whether rational causality itself belongs to a higher natural causal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:NX9","text":"Empirical feeling is not an element of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D1","text":"The three cardinal ideas retain rational importance only after their theoretical employment has been converted into a practical concern.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A3","CH_BA94_SIUE:A4","CH_BA94_SIUE:A6","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D2","text":"Reason’s practical use separates conditional guidance toward happiness from unconditional moral command.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A8","CH_BA94_SIUE:A9","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D3","text":"Only moral law can supply the pure practical canon because only moral law originates entirely a priori in reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D4","text":"The ultimate end of pure reason is reordered from theoretical completion to moral deliberation concerning what ought to be done.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A1","CH_BA94_SIUE:A2","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D5","text":"Practical agency can be treated as rationally law-governed without settling the metaphysical origin of rational causality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A12","CH_BA94_SIUE:A13","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:D6","text":"The practical canon narrows its remaining transcendent questions to God and a future life because practical freedom is already established in the relevant practical sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A12","CH_BA94_SIUE:A13","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SD1","text":"The text subordinates metaphysical speculation to morality without converting morality into a branch of empirical explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A4","CH_BA94_SIUE:A5","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SD2","text":"The system preserves the autonomy of the practical standpoint by assigning causal explanation and moral obligation to distinct rational functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A5","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A12","CH_BA94_SIUE:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SD3","text":"Reason’s demand for systematic unity survives the failure of speculative cognition by relocating final unity in the moral end of humanity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A1","CH_BA94_SIUE:A2","CH_BA94_SIUE:A6","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SD4","text":"The argument permits practical commitment to questions whose theoretical objects remain unavailable as objects of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A4","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX1","text":"The ultimate interest of reason is not exhausted by the expansion of theoretical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX2","text":"Moral obligation is not reducible to the empirical coordination of pleasure, pain, inclination, or happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX3","text":"The legitimacy of practical law is not dependent upon a prior speculative proof of transcendental freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX4","text":"Systematic unity is not obtainable through unrestricted metaphysical explanation of empirical phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX5","text":"The rational subject is not treated solely as a phenomenon governed by natural law, even though its actions as phenomena must be so explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX1","text":"No theoretical standpoint is available from which rational causality can be reconciled with the total causal order of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX2","text":"No positive speculative cognition is available of the transcendent objects that orient reason’s highest practical questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX3","text":"No final speculative system can fulfill reason’s demand for totality without exceeding the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX4","text":"No empirical account of motivation can supply the unconditional authority claimed by moral law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS1","text":"Natural demand for systematic totality → transcendence beyond experience through ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS2","text":"Transcendent ideas → attempted speculative cognition of freedom, immortality, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS3","text":"Speculative non-immanence → prohibition on using transcendent ideas to explain experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS4","text":"Speculative impotence → transfer of the ideas’ significance to practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS5","text":"Practical reason → division between empirical prudence and pure moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS6","text":"Pure moral legislation → canon of what ought to be done.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:FGS7","text":"Empirical proof of practical freedom plus suspension of transcendental freedom → restriction of the practical canon to God and future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AF1","text":"Reason’s demand for totality → transcendent ideas → speculative non-usefulness → practical revaluation → distinction between prudence and moral law → moral primacy → practical freedom → suspension of transcendental freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AF2","text":"Natural-law explanation of phenomena → exclusion of transcendent causal explanation → preservation of empirical inquiry alongside practical obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:AF3","text":"Sensuous determination of the will → rational resistance to impulse → practical freedom → objective laws of what ought to occur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ME1","text":"Freedom, immortality, and God are reclassified from possible theoretical explanations into practical objects of reason’s highest interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ME2","text":"The distinction between what happens and what ought to happen redraws the boundary between natural law and practical law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ME3","text":"Freedom is divided into an empirically established practical capacity and a metaphysically unresolved transcendental condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ME4","text":"Happiness is reclassified as the terminal end of prudence rather than the source of pure moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ME5","text":"The demand for systematic unity is redirected from speculative completion toward the moral end of humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines the ultimate end served by pure reason’s concern with freedom, immortality, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text partitions reason into speculative, empirical-practical, and pure-practical uses with distinct evidentiary and legislative powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text blocks metaphysical explanations of experience while preserving transcendent ideas as practically indispensable questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human action is simultaneously treated as phenomenal nature subject to causal law and as practically attributable to rational freedom, while the reconciliation of these orders is deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the argument if the central operation is understood as a transfer of ultimate rational interest from speculation to morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how practical freedom can be empirically established without resolving its relation to transcendental freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it separates empirical explanation from practical legislation rather than requiring one to derive from the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it establishes the non-immanent status of transcendent ideas and the a priori status of moral law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT5","text":"The system depends on a compression where the practical authority of moral law is asserted before the later grounding of its relation to God and future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the unresolved problem of transcendental freedom remains suspended rather than silently solved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 90","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","context":"Chapter 90","original_title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","original_context":"Chapter 90"},"original_title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","order":91,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIIS"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIIS@R001","sha256":"e591b9e4be2f142a53cc18951336a50bfe7cbc8a7cc3137b35ad78b5e4c41626","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS1","text":"Pure reason’s highest interest requires an account of how moral obligation can authorize hope when speculative cognition cannot reach the required supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS2","text":"The argument performs a practical-transcendental deduction in which moral law, rather than theoretical knowledge, becomes the route to reason’s ultimate end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS3","text":"The distinction between prudential happiness and moral worthiness separates duty from inclination while making hope a distinct rational problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS4","text":"The supreme good is defined as happiness distributed in exact proportion to moral worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS5","text":"Nature supplies no cognizable causal order that guarantees proportionality between virtue and happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS6","text":"The required unity of morality and happiness therefore produces the practical hypotheses of God and future life, and thereby moral theology and teleology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AS7","text":"The argument closes by subordinating theology to antecedent moral law and restricting its legitimate use to immanent moral practice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","text":"Speculative reason cannot establish theoretical knowledge of the supersensible conditions of reason’s highest ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","text":"Pure moral laws determine rational freedom a priori, categorically, and independently of happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A3","text":"Happiness concerns the satisfaction of desires, whereas morality concerns worthiness to be happy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A4","text":"The moral disposition must be the condition of participation in happiness, not an effect of the prospect of happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","text":"The highest good requires happiness to correspond exactly with the moral worthiness of rational beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A6","text":"Natural causality cannot establish that morally worthy action will produce happiness proportionate to worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","text":"A moral world is an intelligible systematic unity of free rational beings under moral laws and a practical idea for transforming the world of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","text":"The ideal of the supreme good requires a morally perfect and supremely blessed intelligence to cause happiness in proportion to moral worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","text":"God and future life are practically necessary hypotheses inseparable from the complete realization of moral obligation’s final end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","text":"Practical necessity does not yield theoretical cognition, demonstrated dogma, or intuitive access to supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","text":"Moral law is prior to the representation of God and determines the sense in which divine commands can be recognized as binding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A12","text":"Moral unity of ends gives natural inquiry a teleological orientation without authorizing transcendent theoretical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:A13","text":"Moral theology has legitimate force only insofar as it preserves reason’s legislative authority in moral action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX1","text":"The highest objects of reason are not available as speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX2","text":"Happiness alone is not the complete good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX3","text":"Morality is not prudence directed toward the acquisition of happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX4","text":"Moral worthiness is not produced by the expectation of reward.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX5","text":"The moral world is not an empirically given order or an object of intelligible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX6","text":"Nature alone is not a sufficient ground for hope that virtue and happiness will coincide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX7","text":"God and future life are not theoretical discoveries or demonstrated metaphysical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX8","text":"Moral obligations are not binding because they are externally commanded by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:NX9","text":"Moral theology is not permitted to replace moral legislation with transcendent religious authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D1","text":"Moral obligation entails the practical possibility of free action and of a systematic moral unity among rational beings.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","CH_BA94_SIIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D2","text":"Hope is rational only as hope for happiness conditioned by prior moral worthiness rather than as expectation generated by desire.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A3","CH_BA94_SIIS:A4","CH_BA94_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D3","text":"The highest good cannot be completed within nature alone and therefore requires the practical assumption of a supersensible moral order.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A6","CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D4","text":"The representation of a single supreme will arises from the requirement that one universal order coordinate moral laws, natural conditions, and the distribution of happiness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D5","text":"Divine attributes are inferred functionally from the requirements of the highest good rather than known independently through speculative theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","CH_BA94_SIIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D6","text":"Moral theology gives nature a teleological interpretation because moral ends require nature to be thinkable as compatible with freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","CH_BA94_SIIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D7","text":"Moral law remains the source of obligation even when reason represents morally required action as conformity with divine will.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","CH_BA94_SIIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:D8","text":"Practical reason extends rational orientation beyond speculative limits without converting its postulates into objects of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","CH_BA94_SIIS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SD1","text":"The three questions of reason form a hierarchy in which the unconditional “ought” governs the conditional “may I hope,” and practical necessity thereby directs speculative orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SD2","text":"The moral world has a double status: it lacks theoretical objecthood while possessing practical reality as the rule for transforming sensible conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","CH_BA94_SIIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SD3","text":"Theology is structurally dependent on autonomous morality because it is required to make the highest good thinkable yet forbidden to legislate the moral law from which it was derived.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","CH_BA94_SIIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SD4","text":"Practical teleology totalizes nature under moral ends while withholding any theoretical warrant for asserting that this totality is known as actual.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","CH_BA94_SIIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5","text":"The future becomes the temporal form of the highest good because its required harmony is absent from present sensible experience but demanded by practical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A6","CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX1","text":"No immanent empirical relation can guarantee the final coincidence of moral worth and happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX2","text":"No desire, reward, punishment, observed consequence, or external command can constitute the ground of moral obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX3","text":"No practical need can be converted into theoretical cognition of the object postulated to satisfy that need.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX4","text":"No merely natural order contains enough intrinsic teleological unity to validate the final ends of moral reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX5","text":"No plurality of independent supreme wills can secure the complete unity of ends required by the highest good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absence of any empirically accessible world in which the moral law’s ultimate end is visibly fulfilled.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX2","text":"Finite reason cannot close the gap between unconditional duty and conditioned happiness; it can only postulate their unifying ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX3","text":"The divine ground of the highest good is unavailable as an external source of moral authority because such a source would displace reason’s own legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX4","text":"The complete moral order required by reason is unavailable as present knowledge and can appear only as a future intelligible horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS1","text":"Speculative limitation removes theoretical cognition as the route to reason’s highest supersensible ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS2","text":"Pure moral law supplies an unconditional practical norm independent of inclination and empirical reward.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS3","text":"Moral law generates the idea of worthiness, which combines with happiness to define the highest good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS4","text":"Nature fails to guarantee the required proportion between worthiness and happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5","text":"The failure of nature generates the practical postulation of a supreme moral cause and a future moral world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS6","text":"The postulate yields moral theology and a teleological orientation of nature without producing speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS7","text":"The priority of moral law returns as a limiting condition that prevents theology from becoming the source of obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:AF1","text":"Speculative boundary → three interests of reason → categorical moral law → distinction between happiness and worthiness → highest good → natural insufficiency → God and future life as practical hypotheses → moral theology and teleology → restoration of moral autonomy through immanent use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME1","text":"Happiness is reclassified from the final good to one required component of the highest good, subordinate to moral worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME2","text":"God and future life are reclassified from speculative objects of proof to practically necessary hypotheses of hope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME3","text":"The moral world is reclassified from a possible object of knowledge to a practical idea governing the transformation of sensible life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME4","text":"Theology is subordinated to morality, so divine will is interpreted through moral law rather than moral law derived from divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME5","text":"Natural teleology is reclassified as an effect of practical reason’s demand for systematic moral ends rather than an independent proof of theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ME6","text":"The relation between freedom and nature is redescribed as a coordination problem requiring a postulated unity rather than as a theoretically solved causal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text answers how one should act and what one may rationally hope after speculative reason has reached its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument constructs the highest good by linking free moral agency, distributive happiness, natural causality, and a supreme causal ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Practical reason gains priority over speculative reason by supplying the regulative conditions under which theology and teleology may be employed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The required unity of freedom and happiness belongs to an intelligible moral order represented as future relative to sensible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT5","text":"Juridical — Moral law retains legislative sovereignty by treating divine commands as recognizable only through independently binding rational obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative limitation through moral law to the practical postulation of the highest good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT2","text":"The system depends on the unproven transition from the practical need for proportional happiness to the assumption of a supreme causal ground capable of realizing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT3","text":"The structure remains coherent only if practical necessity and theoretical cognition are held apart as different modalities of rational validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT4","text":"A persistent tension arises because moral law is declared independently binding while the complete efficacy of moral maxims is said to require God and a future world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT5","text":"A second tension arises because the unity and attributes of a sole supreme being are functionally required by moral teleology but cannot be independently known by speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT6","text":"The architecture is most determinate at the sequence moral law → worthiness → highest good → postulate, where each transition performs a distinct structural function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if the final restriction on transcendent theology is retained as constitutive rather than appended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 91","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 91","original_title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 91"},"original_title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","order":92,"parents":["RAW_BA94_SIOK"],"revision":"CH_BA94_SIOK@R001","sha256":"2d32dec91a83826cb9510305f6bcd7357fa2783dc29a914cbce59aa53779d469","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS1","text":"Holding a proposition true must be divided according to whether its ground is objectively sufficient for every rational being or merely subjectively compelling for an individual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS2","text":"The possibility of universal communication functions as the external test that distinguishes conviction from private persuasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS3","text":"The degrees of holding true are ordered by subjective and objective sufficiency into opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS4","text":"Where theoretical certainty is unavailable, belief can be legitimate only insofar as an idea is required for practical or regulative rational guidance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS5","text":"Moral obligation transforms belief in God and a future life from a speculative hypothesis into morally necessary subjective certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AS6","text":"Critique limits transcendent knowledge while preserving universally available practical orientation beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A1","text":"Holding a proposition true has both objective grounds and subjective causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A2","text":"A judgement is conviction when its ground is objectively sufficient for every rational being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A3","text":"A judgement is persuasion when it rests on the particular character of the judging subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A4","text":"Truth consists in agreement of judgement with its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A5","text":"Judgements that agree with an object must be mutually communicable and mutually consistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A6","text":"Opinion is consciously insufficient both subjectively and objectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","text":"Belief is subjectively sufficient while acknowledged to be objectively insufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A8","text":"Knowledge is sufficient both subjectively and objectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A9","text":"Opinion requires a known lawful connection between a merely problematical judgement and truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A10","text":"Pure reason and morality require necessity and certainty rather than opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A11","text":"Theoretical insufficiency can support belief only through a practical or regulative relation to rational ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A12","text":"Pragmatical belief concerns contingent means required for arbitrarily adopted ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A13","text":"Doctrinal belief concerns theoretically unprovable ideas that guide the use of reason without supplying speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A14","text":"Moral law necessarily obligates action and requires the practical unity of all ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A15","text":"Belief in God and a future life is morally necessary when these are the only conceivable conditions of the unity required by moral law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A16","text":"Transcendent objects cannot be known through speculative reason beyond the limits of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:A17","text":"Essential practical orientation must remain available to common understanding rather than depend on philosophical privilege.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX1","text":"Persuasion is not conviction because private subjective compulsion lacks objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX2","text":"Private assurance is not truth because truth requires agreement with the object rather than intensity of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX3","text":"The inability to communicate a judgement is not proof of its falsehood but excludes its claim to objective conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX4","text":"Opinion is not arbitrary invention because even problematical judgement requires a certain rule of connection to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX5","text":"Opinion has no legitimate place in pure mathematics, pure reason, or moral permission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX6","text":"Doctrinal belief is not knowledge because it cannot provide a speculative account of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX7","text":"Belief in God is not a theoretical hypothesis because it supplies rational guidance without furnishing an imaginable object determined by predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX8","text":"Moral certainty is not logical certainty because it rests on the subject’s necessary moral disposition rather than demonstrable cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:NX9","text":"Critical philosophy is not an esoteric substitute for common understanding because its practical result cannot exceed universally shareable rational orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D1","text":"Communicability supplies a fallible external test for detecting whether an apparently objective judgement is merely private persuasion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A4","CH_BA94_SIOK:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D2","text":"Analysis of a judgement’s subjective causes can expose an illusion even when the disposition generating it cannot be wholly removed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D3","text":"No person may impose persuasion upon others as binding because persuasion lacks the objective sufficiency required for universal validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D4","text":"A judgement concerning a practical end can be rationally held despite theoretical insufficiency when action cannot wait for complete cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A11","CH_BA94_SIOK:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D5","text":"The degree of pragmatical belief varies with the stake one is prepared rationally to risk on the judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D6","text":"Teleological unity licenses belief in a wise author of nature only as a regulative condition for investigating nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A11","CH_BA94_SIOK:A13","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D7","text":"Moral belief remains firm because abandoning its objects would dissolve the moral maxims through which the subject must act.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A14","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D8","text":"Denial of God and a future life cannot attain certainty unless speculative reason proves their impossibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:D9","text":"Even negative belief can restrain evil dispositions without generating morality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A8","CH_BA94_SIOK:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SD1","text":"Rational assent is reorganized as a two-axis system in which subjective commitment and objective warrant can diverge without collapsing into irrationality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:A6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SD2","text":"Critique converts the apparent choice between transcendent knowledge and sceptical abandonment into a differentiated field of opinion, doctrinal belief, moral belief, and knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A8","CH_BA94_SIOK:A13","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SD3","text":"Practical necessity can stabilize commitment to objects that speculative reason cannot cognize, without converting that commitment into objective proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A11","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SD4","text":"The limitation of theoretical reason becomes the condition for an ethical orientation that is universal in demand but non-dogmatic in cognitive status.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A10","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SD5","text":"Rational universality is distributed unequally across domains: cognition requires communicable proof, while moral action requires subjective commitment under objectively unprovable conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A5","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX1","text":"Objective validity cannot be constituted by sincerity, confidence, psychological compulsion, or the practical convenience of a judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX2","text":"Universal assent cannot be presumed from a merely shared human disposition unless that disposition is connected to the object or to a necessary rational law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX3","text":"Theoretical reason cannot legitimately determine supersensible reality through teleological usefulness, moral desirability, or the absence of disproof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX4","text":"Moral agency cannot remain intact if it is indifferent to the conditions under which the final unity of its ends is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX5","text":"A rational system of belief cannot rest wholly on either empirical evidence or speculative demonstration when its governing objects exceed experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX6","text":"Practical universality cannot depend on specialist philosophical knowledge without contradicting its relevance to all rational agents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX1","text":"No theoretical standpoint exists from which reason can possess God, freedom’s final order, or immortality as objects of demonstrable knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2","text":"No morally serious standpoint permits action to be suspended until transcendent certainty is obtained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX3","text":"No final reconciliation is supplied between the subjective ground of moral certainty and the objective insufficiency acknowledged by speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX4","text":"No philosophical achievement is allowed to create a privileged access to the essential practical ends of human reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS1","text":"Holding-true claim → inquiry into objective ground and subjective cause → distinction between conviction and persuasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS2","text":"Agreement with object → required intersubjective communicability → external detection of merely private assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS3","text":"Subjective sufficiency × objective sufficiency → opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS4","text":"Theoretical insufficiency + practical end → pragmatical or doctrinal belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS5","text":"Moral law + necessary action + unity of ends → moral belief in conditions of that unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS6","text":"Speculative limitation → prohibition of transcendent knowledge → preservation of practical orientation for common reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF1","text":"Private assent → communicability test → distinction between persuasion and conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF2","text":"Distinction of validity → degrees of sufficiency → opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF3","text":"Exclusion of opinion from necessity-governed domains → confinement of belief to practical or regulative use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF4","text":"Contingent practical guidance → pragmatical belief → stake-sensitive assurance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF5","text":"Regulative theoretical guidance → doctrinal belief → non-cognitive assent to supersensible ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF6","text":"Moral necessity → required unity of ends → moral certainty without speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:AF7","text":"Limitation of philosophy → accessibility of practical faith → parity of common and philosophical reason in essential human ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME1","text":"Assent is no longer mapped by a simple true-or-false opposition but by the independent dimensions of subjective and objective sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME2","text":"Communicability is reclassified from a social convenience into a diagnostic indicator of possible objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME3","text":"Belief is separated from both arbitrary opinion and failed knowledge and is assigned a determinate rational function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME4","text":"God and immortality are reclassified from speculative objects to practically necessary conditions of moral orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5","text":"The boundary of theoretical reason becomes a condition of practical legitimacy rather than merely a deficit of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ME6","text":"Philosophy is deprived of exclusive authority over practical commitments that concern every rational person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT1","text":"Surface — Judgements are classified as persuasion, conviction, opinion, belief, or knowledge according to their sufficiency and validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objective validity depends on relation to the object and is tested indirectly through possible agreement among rational judges.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical reason redirects failed speculative ambition toward disciplined practical and regulative use of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Supersensible objects remain unavailable to knowledge, while finite human life and moral action require orientation toward an unexperienced final order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT5","text":"Political-epistemic — Essential rational orientation is universalized by denying that philosophical expertise can monopolize access to practical ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the central distinction between subjective assent and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT2","text":"The axioms reconstruct the tripartite ordering of opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT3","text":"The axioms reconstruct the transition from theoretical insufficiency to pragmatical, doctrinal, and moral belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning why moral unity has only the stated supersensible conditions and no alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT5","text":"The system is structurally coherent only if practical necessity does not covertly become theoretical evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT6","text":"The system is strongest in separating cognitive warrant from rationally necessary commitment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT7","text":"The system depends on the compressed premise that moral law necessarily generates both an end-unity requirement and belief in its stated conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement from epistemic discrimination through practical authorization to the democratization of rational orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 92","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","context":"Chapter 92","original_title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","original_context":"Chapter 92"},"original_title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","order":93,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CI_2"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CI_2@R001","sha256":"076b1e466e19825d8cdd3195d751f0999870c9ede5070f848428e15095c89420","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS1","text":"Cognition cannot become science while its parts remain an aggregate lacking necessary unity, limits, and placement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS2","text":"Architectonic converts cognition into science by deriving its organization from a reason-given idea of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS3","text":"The argument is reconstructive, taxonomic, teleological, and critically polemical against empirical aggregation and inherited disciplinary definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS4","text":"The argument moves from the conditions of systematic unity to the distinction between architectonic and technical organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS5","text":"It then relocates rationality from the objective content of cognition to the subject’s mode of deriving cognition from principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS6","text":"Philosophy is reconstructed as an unattained legislative ideal ordered by the ultimate ends of reason, rather than as a transmissible body of doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AS7","text":"The resulting hierarchy defines metaphysics by its a priori conceptual origin and vindicates it as reason’s indispensable critical and regulative completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","text":"Scientific cognition requires systematic unity rather than aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A2","text":"A system is the unity of cognitions under one reason-given idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","text":"The idea of a system determines a priori its end, form, limits, and the position of every part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A4","text":"The execution of a system requires a schema whose content and arrangement are determined a priori by its end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","text":"Architectonic unity requires the essential affinity of parts to be derivable from one supreme internal end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A6","text":"Organization produced from empirical similarities and accidental aims possesses only technical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A7","text":"The rational idea of a science can remain germinal and can correct both its founder’s definition and its historically inherited organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A8","text":"Particular systems are members of one comprehensive system of human cognition whose organizing ground lies in reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","text":"Cognition is subjectively historical when possessed from data or authority and subjectively rational when derived by the knower from principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A10","text":"Rational cognition proceeds either philosophically through concepts or mathematically through the construction of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A11","text":"Received philosophical doctrine remains historical, whereas mathematical cognition can retain subjective rationality through reconstruction in pure a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A12","text":"Philosophy in the objective sense is the unattained archetype and standard of every actual attempt to philosophize.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","text":"Philosophy in the cosmical sense legislates the relation of all cognition to the ultimate ends in which human reason necessarily takes an interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","text":"Reason has one ultimate end, the destination of humanity, to which all subordinate ends stand as means and through which moral philosophy receives priority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A15","text":"The legislation of reason concerns nature and freedom, whose initially separate systems must ultimately belong to one philosophical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","text":"Philosophy divides according to pure or empirical grounds, while pure philosophy divides into criticism and the systematic cognition of pure reason designated as metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A17","text":"Metaphysics systematizes a priori philosophical cognition, excludes empirical and mathematical elements, and divides into ethics and the speculative domains of ontology, rational physics, rational psychology, cosmology, and theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:A18","text":"Metaphysics is unavoidable because criticism of pure reason must restrain dialectical speculation, order the sciences, and direct cognition toward humanity’s highest end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX1","text":"Science is not an aggregate, rhapsody, or externally accumulated collection of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX2","text":"Systematic unity is not produced by contingent additions whose places are undetermined by the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX3","text":"Architectonic unity is not technical unity based on observed similarity or arbitrary practical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX4","text":"The founder’s explicit definition of a science is not the final authority concerning its true idea, content, or limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX5","text":"Objective rationality of content does not by itself make a person’s possession of that content subjectively rational.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX6","text":"Philosophy is not learnable as a completed doctrine independently of the autonomous exercise of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX7","text":"Technical mastery of mathematics, natural philosophy, logic, or conceptual arrangement does not constitute philosophical legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX8","text":"Metaphysics is not adequately defined as the science of more general or higher first principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX9","text":"Mathematical construction and empirical cognition do not belong to metaphysics merely because mathematics is a priori or empirical principles may be highly general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX10","text":"Empirical psychology is not an internal part of metaphysics, even when scholastic economy provisionally retains it as an appendix.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX11","text":"Metaphysics is not the foundation of religion and cannot legitimately operate as lawless speculative dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:NX12","text":"The historical failure or accidental misuse of metaphysics does not establish that reason can abandon it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D1","text":"Knowledge of the system’s idea makes omissions detectable and excludes arbitrary additions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A2","CH_BA94_CI_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D2","text":"A scientific whole develops organically by strengthening internally articulated parts rather than by appending unrelated members.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CI_2:A4","CH_BA94_CI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D3","text":"Empirical classification can provisionally arrange materials but cannot establish the necessary plan of a science.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A4","CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","CH_BA94_CI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D4","text":"Mature scientific organization can disclose a governing idea that invalidates the science’s original self-description.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D5","text":"Criticism and rejection of inherited doctrines become possible only when cognition is regenerated from principles rather than retained from authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D6","text":"Philosophy can be learned only historically, while philosophizing requires autonomous reasoning under principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CI_2:A10","CH_BA94_CI_2:A11","CH_BA94_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D7","text":"Specialists remain conceptual artists when their disciplines are not legislatively related to the ultimate ends of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A12","CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D8","text":"Moral philosophy occupies the highest position because it concerns the single end under which every cognitive and practical operation is subordinated.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D9","text":"Metaphysics must be distinguished by the origin and method of its cognition rather than by the relative generality of its propositions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A10","CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","CH_BA94_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D10","text":"Rational physiology preserves its claimed purity by taking from experience only the presentation of an object in general and excluding empirical principles from judgments about it.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","CH_BA94_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D11","text":"Empirical psychology must be relocated to applied philosophy or anthropology because its empirical origin violates the constitutive boundary of metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","CH_BA94_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D12","text":"The secure value of metaphysics lies more in preventing speculative error than in extending positive knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:D13","text":"Metaphysics remains necessary despite its failures because the ultimate questions that generate it are constitutive interests of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD1","text":"Systematization is a legislative operation because the idea of the whole determines which cognitions belong, where they belong, and which additions are inadmissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A2","CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD2","text":"A science possesses a rational identity irreducible to the intentions or self-understanding of its historical practitioners.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD3","text":"Philosophical education must cultivate the production, testing, and rejection of principles rather than the faithful reproduction of conclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CI_2:A10","CH_BA94_CI_2:A11","CH_BA94_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD4","text":"The cosmical conception totalizes specialist sciences as instruments within a moral-teleological hierarchy rather than recognizing them as independent final ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A8","CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","text":"Critique functions as an internal organ of metaphysics because delimiting speculative reason preserves metaphysics from its own dialectical excesses.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","CH_BA94_CI_2:A17","CH_BA94_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","text":"The metaphysical taxonomy is jurisdictional: it allocates objects, methods, and valid sources of cognition while expelling foreign elements.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","CH_BA94_CI_2:A16","CH_BA94_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","text":"Architectonic reason operates through a dual temporality in which the idea precedes the system as its ground but becomes explicit only after historical accumulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8","text":"The completion of reason takes the form of negative governance: censorship of error, regulation of disciplinary relations, and subordination of inquiry to a universal end.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX1","text":"Bottom-up convergence of cognitions cannot by itself generate scientific unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX2","text":"No externally imposed classification can substitute for an internal end that necessitates the placement of every part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX3","text":"Authorial intention and disciplinary convention cannot constitute the rational identity of a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX4","text":"Correct propositions cannot confer rational autonomy when they are possessed only through imitation or authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX5","text":"Specialist sciences cannot be self-sufficient within the complete economy of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX6","text":"The ultimate ends of reason cannot remain plural, coordinate, or mutually independent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX7","text":"The distinction between a priori and empirical cognition cannot be reduced to a continuous scale of generality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX8","text":"Philosophical and mathematical cognition cannot be assimilated merely because both possess an a priori origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX9","text":"Reason cannot permanently suspend metaphysical inquiry or regulate its dialectical tendencies without criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX1","text":"Complete philosophy is absent in concreto, and this absence permits the rational prototype to judge every actual philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX2","text":"The ideal philosopher is absent as an actual person, while the idea of philosophical legislation remains operative in every rational subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX3","text":"An explicit architectonic plan is absent at the historical beginning of science, although the system presupposes its germinal presence in reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4","text":"The final unity of nature and freedom is posited as necessary but remains unconstructed within the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX5","text":"The possibility that science could remain neutral toward the destination of humanity is excluded at the highest architectonic level.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX6","text":"A secure positive expansion of speculative metaphysical knowledge recedes behind the negative functions of limitation, error-prevention, and disciplinary order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS1","text":"Aggregate cognition → reason-given idea → a priori limits and part-positions → schema → organic systematic unity → science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS2","text":"Accumulated materials → provisional technical arrangement → disclosure of the germinal idea → correction of inherited definitions → architectonic reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS3","text":"Reception from data or authority → historical possession; autonomous derivation from principles → rational possession → capacity for criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS4","text":"Rational cognition → concepts → philosophy as autonomous practice; rational cognition → construction of concepts in pure intuition → mathematics as learnable rational cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS5","text":"Objective philosophical prototype → cosmical legislation → one ultimate human end → moral priority → subordination of specialist sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS6","text":"Philosophy → empirical philosophy / pure philosophy → criticism / metaphysical system → metaphysics of ethics / metaphysics of nature → ontology / rational physics / rational psychology / cosmology / theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:FGS7","text":"Natural dialectical tendency → critical restriction → purified metaphysics → regulation of sciences → orientation toward the happiness and destination of humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AF1","text":"Aggregate cognition → definition of architectonic → reason-given idea → a priori schema → organic science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AF2","text":"Germinal rational unity → historical accumulation → retrospective clarification → correction of disciplinary self-definitions → architectonic of all cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:AF3","text":"Historical/rational distinction → philosophical/mathematical distinction → philosophy as unattained legislative ideal → cosmical and moral hierarchy → metaphysical taxonomy → critical vindication of metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME1","text":"Science is reclassified from a quantity of accumulated knowledge into a teleologically articulated whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME2","text":"Disciplinary boundaries become consequences of an internal idea rather than products of convention, utility, or resemblance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME3","text":"The identity of a science is detached from the declared intentions of its founder and relocated in a rational structure discoverable through its parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME4","text":"The historical/rational distinction is relocated from the objective source of propositions to the subject’s mode of possessing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME5","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from a doctrinal corpus into an autonomous practice governed by an unattained archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME6","text":"Specialist disciplines are repositioned as instruments under philosophical legislation rather than as independent embodiments of wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME7","text":"Morality becomes the terminal organizing end of the entire cognitive system rather than one discipline among others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME8","text":"Metaphysics is differentiated from mathematics and empirical science by kind and origin rather than by superior generality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME9","text":"Empirical psychology is displaced from metaphysics into applied philosophy and prospective anthropology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ME10","text":"The disrepute of metaphysics is reframed as a consequence of uncriticized speculation rather than evidence of metaphysics’ dispensability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text defines architectonic and distributes cognition, philosophy, and metaphysics into systematic divisions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural: A reason-given end governs the constitution, boundaries, internal affinities, and jurisdictional relations of sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The text replaces inherited doctrine and empirical classification with autonomous critical reconstruction while preserving metaphysics by limiting its claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Systems are conceived as organisms whose wholes ideally precede their parts, while their governing ideas become historically explicit only through retrospective development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT1","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from systematic unity through cognitive taxonomy to metaphysics’ critical office.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT2","text":"The criterion by which the true rational idea is distinguished from a retrospectively imposed interpretation remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent wherever idea, schema, internal end, systematic division, and disciplinary limits are treated as mutually determining.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT4","text":"The architecture is strongest in distinguishing architectonic unity from technical aggregation and differences of kind from differences of degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT5","text":"The a priori authority of the system is strained by the claim that its plan generally becomes visible only after empirical and historical accumulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT6","text":"The mathematical exception depends on an asserted infallibility of pure intuition that is not established within this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT7","text":"The priority of one moral-human end and the final merger of nature with freedom are asserted as architectonic necessities rather than demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT8","text":"The purity of rational physiology depends on an incompletely defended distinction between empirical presentation of an object and empirical determination of its content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT9","text":"The provisional admission of empirical psychology as an appendix conflicts with the claimed immutability and legislative authority of the architectonic division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT10","text":"The term metaphysics alternately designates a system coordinate with criticism and the whole of pure philosophy including criticism, leaving the highest taxonomic boundary unstable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 93","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 93","original_title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 93"},"original_title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","order":94,"parents":["RAW_BA94_CIHP"],"revision":"CH_BA94_CIHP@R001","sha256":"294d21d1cfecf138d4430367da13b28c76c5fe719952e39a784ee5c00626854c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS1","text":"The history of philosophy must be judged transcendentally by the nature and capacity of pure reason rather than narrated chronologically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS2","text":"The ruinous condition of metaphysics is diagnosed through persistent unresolved alternatives concerning its objects, sources, and methods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS3","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is historically propelled by theology and morality, but speculative reason makes theology its primary object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS4","text":"Philosophical conflict is reduced to three classificatory axes: what reason knows, whence pure cognition arises, and how inquiry proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS5","text":"Naturalistic reliance on unaided common sense is excluded from scientific metaphysics because it repudiates the means of rational examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AS6","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism remain systematic alternatives, but criticism alone is presented as the route capable of permanently satisfying reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A1","text":"Pure reason must be evaluated according to its own nature and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A2","text":"The historical condition of metaphysics is one of recurrent construction without durable settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A3","text":"The central historical disputes of metaphysics concern the objects of reason, the origin of pure cognition, and the method of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A4","text":"Sensualism locates reality in sensuous objects, while intellectualism locates truth in understanding independently of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A5","text":"Empiricism derives pure cognition from experience, while noology derives it from reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A6","text":"A doctrine becomes incoherent when it derives cognition from experience but uses that cognition to demonstrate objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A7","text":"Naturalism elevates unaided common reason above scientific inquiry in answering metaphysical questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A8","text":"Scientific metaphysical inquiry must proceed systematically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A9","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism are opposed systematic procedures within scientific inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:A10","text":"Criticism is the remaining path through which reason may attain stable satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX1","text":"Naturalism is not a scientific method of metaphysical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX2","text":"Common sense is not an autonomous tribunal competent to settle the principal questions of metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX3","text":"The derivation of concepts from experience does not authorize proofs concerning God or immortality as objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX4","text":"Historical succession is not itself a criterion of philosophical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX5","text":"The rejection of science is not a method for extending cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:NX6","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism are not final solutions to the demands of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D1","text":"The history of metaphysics becomes a diagnostic taxonomy of rational failures rather than a chronology of doctrines.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A2","CH_BA94_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D2","text":"The dispute over the objects of cognition is independent of the dispute over the origin of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A3","CH_BA94_CIHP:A4","CH_BA94_CIHP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D3","text":"Empiricism becomes self-undermining when it crosses from experiential derivation to transcendent demonstration.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A5","CH_BA94_CIHP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D4","text":"The distinction between ordinary practical reasoning and scientific metaphysical procedure is necessary to prevent ignorance from becoming a doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A7","CH_BA94_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D5","text":"Methodological systematicity is a condition of legitimacy even where scientific procedures diverge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A8","CH_BA94_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:D6","text":"Criticism is positioned as a third-order procedure that judges the admissibility of both dogmatic and sceptical procedures.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1","text":"The history of pure reason functions as a tribunal that classifies philosophical positions by the conditions under which they overreach their own claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A3","CH_BA94_CIHP:A6","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2","text":"Metaphysical failure is relocated from isolated doctrinal error to an unexamined relation between cognitive source, cognitive object, and cognitive method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A2","CH_BA94_CIHP:A3","CH_BA94_CIHP:A6","CH_BA94_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3","text":"Reason can obtain durable satisfaction only by making its own powers of cognition, rather than transcendent objects alone, the primary object of examination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A6","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4","text":"Philosophical progress requires the conversion of a narrow critical procedure into a collectively sustained public method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A8","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX1","text":"No doctrine is validated merely by its historical persistence, antiquity, or cultural authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX2","text":"No cognition can legitimately exceed the conditions under which its source is established without further critical warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX3","text":"No appeal to ordinary reason can replace an examination of reason’s own competence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX4","text":"No permanent settlement of metaphysics follows from choosing between empirical derivation and rational derivation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX5","text":"No systematic procedure is adequate if it leaves the authority and limits of pure reason unexamined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX1","text":"Immediate possession of metaphysical truth is unavailable; reason must first determine whether its demand for such truth is cognitively authorized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX2","text":"The positive content of the critical path is withheld in the historical sketch, so criticism is asserted here as necessary before it is demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3","text":"The final satisfaction of reason is unavailable as a private achievement because its realization depends on the collective transformation of method into a common road of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS1","text":"Recurrent metaphysical dissatisfaction → transcendental diagnosis of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS2","text":"Transcendental diagnosis → classification of disputes by object, origin, and method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS3","text":"Classification of disputes → exposure of positions that exceed their own epistemic basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS4","text":"Exposure of empirical transcendence and naturalistic misology → exclusion of unaudited cognitive authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS5","text":"Exclusion of unaudited authority → requirement of systematic procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS6","text":"Systematic dogmatism and systematic scepticism → critical examination of reason as the remaining route.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:FGS7","text":"Critical examination → conditional possibility of durable rational satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AF1","text":"Ruinous metaphysical inheritance → transcendental reframing of history → threefold taxonomy of rational conflict → exposure of experiential and naturalistic overreach → systematic-method requirement → critical path → conditional completion of reason’s demand for knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:AF2","text":"Theology and morality as original attractions of inquiry → metaphysics as the concentrated labor of speculative reason → necessity of judging speculative reason’s authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ME1","text":"Philosophical history is reclassified from a sequence of schools into a structured field of positions defined by epistemic commitments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ME2","text":"The opposition between sensualism and intellectualism is separated from the opposition between empiricism and noology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ME3","text":"Method ceases to be a secondary technique and becomes a determining condition of metaphysical legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ME4","text":"Common sense is reclassified from a practical orientation for life into an insufficient ground for scientific metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ME5","text":"Criticism is positioned neither as another doctrine about objects nor as a compromise between schools, but as an examination of the authority of rational inquiry itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter distinguishes historical schools by their accounts of reality, cognition, and philosophical method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The chapter locates metaphysical instability in the disconnection between a doctrine’s claimed objects and the source and procedure of its cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The chapter converts the history of philosophy into a selective preparation for the critical method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The chapter treats reason as historically recurrent in its metaphysical desires yet capable of a future durable settlement through self-limitation and collective methodological development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from metaphysical failure to the necessity of criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined because the positive procedures and criteria of criticism are announced but not supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent where it distinguishes the object, origin, and method of cognition and treats their misalignment as diagnostic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT4","text":"The system depends on compression where historical schools are assigned stable positions within a threefold classificatory grid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT5","text":"A tension remains between the declared availability of dogmatic and sceptical systematic methods and the claim that criticism alone remains open.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT6","text":"A tension remains between the promised perfect contentment of reason and the conditional, future, collective labor required to make the critical path generally available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if criticism is treated as a tribunal of rational legitimacy rather than as one more historical school.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_BA94_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 94","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 94","original_title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","original_context":"Chapter 94"},"original_title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_BA94_IDBP","CH_BA94_IHIE","CH_BA94_IPSN"],"revision":"CL_BA94_01@R001","sha256":"f92220de280a79d664e2d6acddb4aec85bb794ee6fcb61dbf90ddfc6820e1b4a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-01","text":"Knowledge begins through experience, but its temporal awakening does not establish experience as the source of every cognitive component.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-02","text":"The system converts the empirical–a priori distinction from a chronological contrast into a test of absolute dependence, necessity, and cognitive origin.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-03","text":"Necessity and strict universality identify structures that experience cannot generate, even where those structures govern the certainty of experience itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","CH_BA94_IHIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-04","text":"The a priori expands from an initially separable cognitive contribution into the condition of judgment, object-thought, and experiential intelligibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:D6","CH_BA94_IHIE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-05","text":"The confirmation that a priori cognition exists produces a new danger: legitimate non-empirical cognition can be transferred illicitly beyond the conditions that validate it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-06","text":"Metaphysics must therefore suspend doctrinal construction and become an investigation into the source, possibility, extent, validity, and worth of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: experience requires a priori conditions, while the validity of a priori claims must be tested by the conditions under which experience is possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AS-08","text":"The cluster closes not with metaphysical knowledge but with a critical science that distinguishes valid cognitive production from conceptual extension without warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Knowledge may begin with experience without being wholly derived from experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Empirical cognition is composite: sensory reception supplies material while cognition contributes ordering elements.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The a priori is first defined by independence from experience, then operationally identified through necessity, strict universality, and unavoidable conceptual residue.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","CH_BA94_IHIE:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Necessity and strict universality cannot be produced by induction from contingent observation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Experience is not the ultimate ground of its own certainty because its intelligibility presupposes non-empirical rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The a priori concerns both judgments and the irreducible forms through which objects can be thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Validity depends not merely on formal coherence but on the origin and enabling conditions of cognitive additions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The existence of a priori cognition does not authorize unlimited cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Critical inquiry must determine the possibility and extent of reason before reason constructs metaphysical conclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The distinction between analytic clarification and synthetic addition is necessary to expose unauthorized conceptual importation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-01","text":"Experience retains its role as the temporal occasion of cognition but mutates into a conditioned field whose certainty depends upon a priori rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A1","CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-02","text":"A priori cognition recurs first as a separable contribution within empirical knowledge, then as necessity in judgment, and finally as the object of critical science.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:D6","CH_BA94_IHIE:D1","CH_BA94_IPSN:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-03","text":"Necessity changes from a criterion distinguishing empirical cognition into a boundary mechanism limiting speculative reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D1","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-04","text":"Conceptual subtraction migrates from a method for identifying residual forms of object-thought into a general diagnostic for distinguishing clarification from genuine addition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD6","CH_BA94_IPSN:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-05","text":"Mathematics reverses valence: it initially certifies productive a priori cognition but becomes a misleading model when detached from the intuition that enables it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-06","text":"The distinction between empirical and a priori knowledge is absorbed into the larger distinction between conditions of cognition and claims that exceed those conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:ME3","CH_BA94_IPSN:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RMD-07","text":"Pure and impure a priori cognition performs local classificatory work, then recedes as the stronger distinction between legitimate and illegitimate a priori extension takes priority.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A11","CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-01","text":"The division between pure and impure a priori knowledge remains a local taxonomy because it does not by itself determine the validity or extent of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:D5","CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-02","text":"The examples of causality, space, and substance scale upward because they establish that cognitive necessity structures both judgment and object-directed thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A8","CH_BA94_IHIE:D8","CH_BA94_IHIE:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-03","text":"The separation of temporal beginning from epistemic origin becomes a cluster-wide bridge between experiential initiation and transcendental grounding.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:SD1","CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-04","text":"Necessity and strict universality become transition mechanisms because they convert the mere possibility of a priori cognition into a criterion for investigating its authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:AS3","CH_BA94_IPSN:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-05","text":"The local rejection of associationism is recoded as a general prohibition against deriving conditions of intelligibility from contingent psychological or observational regularities.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD5","CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-06","text":"The mathematical example becomes obsolete as a universal model once its dependence on a priori intuition is exposed.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:CGD-07","text":"The cluster-level architecture begins where a priori cognition is treated not as an achieved possession but as a problem of authorization, scope, and boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D10","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects the inference from the temporal priority of experience to the empirical derivation of all knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects the reduction of necessity and universality to accumulated regularity or subjective habit.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:NX2","CH_BA94_IHIE:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the claim that objects are exhausted by sensible predicates or that cognition is exhausted by sensory reception.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:NX7","CH_BA94_IHIE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects the inference from logical consistency, conceptual completion, or analytic clarity to substantive cognitive validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:NX5","CH_BA94_IPSN:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects unrestricted transfer from the validated productivity of mathematics to supersensible metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects speculative expansion that lacks an account of the origin and conditions of its synthetic additions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:DBN-01","text":"The cluster depends on the falsity of the assumption that the conditions awakening cognition reveal the source of all cognitive content.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:DBN-02","text":"The cluster disqualifies any account in which experience supplies both the content of cognition and the ultimate warrant for its organizing rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:DBN-03","text":"The cluster disqualifies every continuous passage from finite observation to exceptionless necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:DBN-04","text":"The cluster disqualifies every metaphysics that treats the absence of experiential resistance as evidence of supersensible validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:DBN-05","text":"The cluster disqualifies the assumption that pure reason carries a self-authenticating authority wherever empirical constraint is absent.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded possibility is a cognition whose claim to objective validity could be established without conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSA-02","text":"The system circles the unavailable identity of conceptual possibility and objective reality, refusing to convert thinkability into knowability.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSA-03","text":"The system cannot admit an a priori faculty whose productive operations need not be traced to their source, scope, and conditions of use.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSA-04","text":"The decisive absence is an external experiential tribunal for supersensible claims, which necessitates critique as an internal examination of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX4","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-01","text":"The distinction between experiential onset and epistemic origin becomes the basis for distinguishing the empirical material of knowledge from the non-empirical conditions that make experience intelligible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:SD1","CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-02","text":"Necessity operates doubly: it identifies a priori cognition and prevents that cognition from being treated as valid beyond the conditions that ground its necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-03","text":"The demonstration that experience presupposes a priori rules converts the question of a priori cognition from one of possession into one of transcendental authorization.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A9","CH_BA94_IPSN:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-04","text":"Conceptual residues such as space and substance establish the need for non-empirical forms of thought, but cannot alone determine the legitimate application of those forms beyond possible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D8","CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-05","text":"The same a priori productivity that secures mathematics generates speculative risk when its dependence on intuition is concealed and its method is generalized.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-06","text":"Analytic clarification becomes epistemically dangerous when the appearance of novelty masks foreign synthetic content and thus bypasses inquiry into origin.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A11","CH_BA94_IPSN:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:XD-07","text":"A critical science is required because neither empirical derivation nor conceptual coherence alone can distinguish valid synthetic a priori cognition from illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:NX5","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-01","text":"The cluster transforms transcendental inquiry into a theory of conditioned validity: cognitive authority exists only through determinable conditions of origin, use, and scope.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A4","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-02","text":"Experience is reconceived as a field constituted by a priori rules rather than as the source from which those rules could be inductively extracted.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-03","text":"The a priori is neither an escape from experience nor a deposit of ready-made doctrine; it is the structural condition whose legitimacy remains domain-bound.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX1","CH_BA94_IPSN:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-04","text":"Metaphysical error is generated not by reason’s absence but by the overextension of locally valid cognitive powers into domains lacking their enabling conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-05","text":"Critique becomes a positive epistemic discipline because delimitation is the means by which valid cognition is distinguished from formally coherent fiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3","CH_BA94_IPSN:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:BSD-06","text":"The central political-metastrategic effect is the reversal of philosophical authority: no system may claim its conclusions before accounting for the conditions that authorize its construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-01","text":"Experience awakens cognition; cognition supplies conditions of experience; the resulting dependence requires a retrospective separation of received material from cognitive contribution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A2","CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3","CH_BA94_IDBP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-02","text":"Empirical regularity generates the demand for necessity; necessity identifies a priori structure; a priori structure then becomes the condition for explaining empirical certainty.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D3","CH_BA94_IHIE:D1","CH_BA94_IHIE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-03","text":"Mathematical success produces confidence in a priori cognition; concealed dependence on intuition permits generalization; generalized confidence becomes speculative overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:A7","CH_BA94_IPSN:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-04","text":"Conceptual analysis produces clarification; clarification appears productive; apparent productivity conceals synthetic insertion; concealed insertion triggers critical investigation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:A11","CH_BA94_IPSN:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience functions as the principal attractor because it simultaneously grounds object-directed cognition and limits the legitimacy of its extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A5","CH_BA94_IPSN:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:RLE-06","text":"Critique functions as a recursive closure: it investigates the conditions of cognition using the very cognitive powers whose limits it must determine.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:AS4","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B01","text":"Sensory affection initiates cognition without supplying the complete source of what cognition produces.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B02","text":"Composite empirical knowledge requires analytical separation of received impressions from cognitive contribution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B03","text":"Separation yields the possibility of identifying a priori content through independence, necessity, strict universality, and conceptual non-removability.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B04","text":"Identified a priori structures explain the certainty and intelligibility of experience by functioning as its conditions rather than its empirical products.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B05","text":"Productive a priori cognition acquires an illusion of unrestricted scope when mathematics and analysis are abstracted from their enabling conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D3","CH_BA94_IPSN:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B06","text":"Speculative extension without a corresponding account of objects, origin, or conditions becomes epistemically indeterminate rather than cognitively valid.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D7","CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FGS-B07","text":"Critical science reconstructs the boundary of valid cognition by distinguishing legitimate synthesis from dogmatic conceptual construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:D8","CH_BA94_IPSN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B01","text":"Experiential awakening → composite cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B02","text":"Composite cognition → distinction between temporal origin and epistemic derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B03","text":"Epistemic derivation → criteria of necessity, strict universality, and non-removable conceptual form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B04","text":"A priori criteria → transcendental reversal in which experience presupposes non-empirical rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B05","text":"Productive a priori cognition → mathematical analogy and analytic illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B06","text":"Mathematical analogy and analytic illusion → unauthorized conceptual extension beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:AFM-B07","text":"Unauthorized extension → critical investigation of the possibility, validity, extent, and worth of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-01","text":"Experience is reclassified from the total source of knowledge into an initiated, materially supplied, and transcendentally conditioned field.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:ME1","CH_BA94_IHIE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-02","text":"The empirical–a priori distinction is reorganized around dependence and modal force rather than psychological acquisition or observational frequency.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:ME2","CH_BA94_IHIE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-03","text":"Objectivity is relocated from sensory predicates and associative habits to the non-empirical relations required for thinking objects and causal connections.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD5","CH_BA94_IHIE:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-04","text":"Mathematics becomes condition-dependent evidence rather than a passport for metaphysical construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-05","text":"Conceptual analysis is divided into clarification, legitimate synthesis, and unauthorized importation, making apparent novelty diagnostically unstable.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MME-06","text":"The boundary of possible experience becomes an internal criterion of legitimacy rather than a merely negative barrier to speculative ambition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system distinguishes empirical cognition, necessary judgment, causal connection, object-thought, mathematics, and supersensible metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:AS4","CH_BA94_IPSN:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Cognition organizes sensible material through rules that cannot be derived from the empirical material they condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A3","CH_BA94_IHIE:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The system reverses philosophical procedure by requiring investigation of cognitive authority before doctrinal construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Time governs the awakening of cognition, while possible experience governs the validity of object-directed claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:ALT4","CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The argument performs critique by using subtraction, modal testing, and genealogical tracing to expose the hidden conditions of apparent cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD6","CH_BA94_IPSN:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FL-01","text":"Necessity is treated as an infallible sign of a priori cognition, yet the cluster still requires a further account of the source and valid scope of necessary claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FL-02","text":"The system establishes that a priori cognition exists before it has fully established the positive criterion that validates each synthetic a priori claim.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D10","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:FL-03","text":"Supersensible problems remain unavoidable for reason even though no direct experiential tribunal can adjudicate their objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A2","CH_BA94_IPSN:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from independence from experience into a condition of experience, thereby shifting from classificatory status to transcendental function.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A9","CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MP-02","text":"Mathematics mutates from a proof that productive a priori cognition is possible into a diagnostic case of how valid cognition can be overgeneralized.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MP-03","text":"Analysis mutates from harmless conceptual clarification into a site where apparent formal progress can conceal unauthorized synthetic addition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:A10","CH_BA94_IPSN:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an ordered field of possible experience whose intelligibility depends upon rules not derivable from the sensory contents that occupy it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as an active but finite cognitive operator that receives material, synthesizes it under non-empirical conditions, and risks mistaking its own conceptual productivity for unrestricted knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:A6","CH_BA94_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:HSI-03","text":"Time appears bifurcated between the temporal activation of cognition and the non-temporal conditions determining the validity of cognitive claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:SD1","CH_BA94_IPSN:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:HSI-04","text":"Struggle takes the form of critical navigation: reason must preserve the conditions of its genuine productivity while refusing their dogmatic extension beyond possible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPSN:SD2","CH_BA94_IPSN:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from experiential initiation through a priori conditions to the demand for critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT1","CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where it links the conditions of empirical certainty to the limitation of speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:D7","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined regarding the internal constitution of the a priori faculty and the positive validation of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBP:MRT4","CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if necessity, universality, intuition, and possible experience are retained as differentiated conditions rather than compressed into a single notion of rationality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:MRT4","CH_BA94_IPSN:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when its recursive form is retained: the discovery of a priori cognition simultaneously establishes its necessity and problematizes its unrestricted use.","refs":["CH_BA94_IHIE:SD3","CH_BA94_IPSN:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”"},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_BA94_IDBA","CH_BA94_VATS","CH_BA94_VUPP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_02@R001","sha256":"8dcd01a131dcb8c747f458501b0e3c805482012703e081d2f08df01d5de3c36e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B01","text":"Analysis yields necessity without cognitive augmentation, while empirical synthesis yields augmentation without universal necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:D2","CH_BA94_IDBA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B02","text":"The unresolved problem is how a predicate not contained in a concept can nevertheless be necessarily connected with it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B03","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment shifts from a logical anomaly into the principle-form through which theoretical science must be understood.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B04","text":"Mathematics, pure natural science, and metaphysics are unified not by common objects but by their demand for non-empirical cognitive extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:SD4","CH_BA94_VATS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B05","text":"Mathematical intuition supplies a determinate model of necessary synthesis, but its sufficiency as a unified ground for all theoretical sciences remains unsettled.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B06","text":"Metaphysics is simultaneously unavoidable as a rational disposition and unestablished as a science.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B07","text":"Recurrent metaphysical contradiction converts the task from doctrinal construction to an examination of reason’s own competence.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS6","CH_BA94_VUPP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AS-B08","text":"The cluster closes conditionally: critique can authorize warranted extension or impose restraint, but it does not yet provide the positive ground of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6","CH_BA94_VUPP:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Analytic judgment can secure conceptual necessity but cannot produce knowledge that augments the subject-conception.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:D2","CH_BA94_IDBA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Empirical synthesis can connect representations and extend cognition, but cannot supply the universality and necessity required for a priori knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A9","CH_BA94_VATS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Synthetic a priori judgment begins as an unexplained connection between heterogeneous terms and becomes the common formal requirement of theoretical science.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A11","CH_BA94_VATS:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Logical contradiction tests the legitimacy of a judgment but cannot generate the synthetic ground of substantive cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A4","CH_BA94_VATS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Theoretical science is constituted by necessary additions to concepts under non-empirical conditions rather than by deduction or observation alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Mathematics and pure natural science are existing proof-cases whose possibility requires transcendental explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Intuition is the productive mediator of mathematical synthesis, yet its extension into a universal account of science and metaphysics remains unproven.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Metaphysics is naturally generated by reason but acquires scientific standing only through a valid account of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS5","CH_BA94_VUPP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Critical limitation is the enabling condition of legitimate a priori extension rather than a merely negative restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Reason must become the object of inquiry because its metaphysical problems, claims, and limits are self-generated.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-01","text":"Analytic judgment retains its identity as conceptual explication, but its function mutates into a limiting baseline that marks what science and metaphysics cannot accomplish by analysis alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","CH_BA94_VATS:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-02","text":"Synthesis expands from a subject–predicate relation into a transdisciplinary principle-form governing mathematics, physics, and metaphysical aspiration.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A11","CH_BA94_VATS:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-03","text":"Experience recurs first as the source of contingent connection and later as the boundary against which reason’s jurisdiction must be determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:SD3","CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-04","text":"Contradiction reverses function: logically, it excludes invalid analytic relations; historically, it diagnoses the failure of dogmatic metaphysical construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:ME1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-05","text":"Intuition appears as the concrete productive condition of mathematics but becomes a mutation point when treated as a possible universal ground of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RMD-06","text":"Metaphysics changes from an attempt to enlarge a priori knowledge into a problem concerning the authority, scope, and limits of that enlargement.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:AS5","CH_BA94_VUPP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-01","text":"The containment relation between subject and predicate remains local machinery until it is converted into a diagnosis of the limits of analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between analytic explication and synthetic extension scales upward because it reorganizes the possibility of necessary knowledge across theoretical science.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:SD1","CH_BA94_VATS:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-03","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science function as bridge-structures because their actual existence turns the possibility of synthesis into an unavoidable explanatory task.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-04","text":"Recurrent contradiction is a transition mechanism that redirects inquiry from metaphysical objects toward the competence of the faculty claiming knowledge of them.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-05","text":"A merely classificatory account of judgment becomes obsolete once contradiction transforms doctrinal production into a question of rational jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:CGD-06","text":"Conceptual analysis is recoded as preparatory: it clarifies the conditions of judgment but cannot replace the synthetic production of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS7","CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-01","text":"A priori cognition cannot be equated with analytic cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-02","text":"Experience cannot provide the universality and necessity claimed by synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-03","text":"The principle of contradiction cannot serve as a productive source of synthetic knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-04","text":"Science cannot be reduced either to empirical generalization or to conceptual tautology.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-05","text":"The natural fact that reason generates metaphysical questions cannot establish the validity of metaphysical answers.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:GNX-06","text":"Neither dogmatic possession of metaphysical objects nor sceptical cancellation of metaphysical demand is admissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX2","CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:DBN-01","text":"Neither conceptual containment nor empirical association can explain necessary cognitive augmentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:DBN-02","text":"Reason cannot authorize unrestricted a priori use before determining its own competence.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:DBN-03","text":"The inevitability with which reason poses a question cannot make any answer to that question cognitively valid.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:DBN-04","text":"Successful sciences cannot remain self-evident bodies of knowledge; they must become evidence requiring an account of their possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:DBN-05","text":"A mathematical account of intuitive synthesis cannot, by itself, count as a completed unified warrant for physics and metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an available foundation for necessarily connecting predicates that are foreign to their concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSA-02","text":"A metaphysics that becomes scientific without first becoming critique of its own source is structurally unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSA-03","text":"The system excludes both final metaphysical possession and a sceptical escape from the rational demand for metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSA-04","text":"The deepest unresolved demand is a reflexive authorization in which reason determines its own limits without being able to appeal to an external tribunal.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-01","text":"The paired deficits of analysis and experience transform the problem of synthesis into a critical inquiry into the conditions under which augmentation can possess necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-01","CL_BA94_02:GA-02","CL_BA94_02:GA-09","CH_BA94_IDBA:D6","CH_BA94_VUPP:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-02","text":"Mathematical intuition supplies a partial model of synthetic production, but its non-transferability to metaphysics makes critique necessary rather than optional.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-03","CL_BA94_02:GA-07","CL_BA94_02:GA-08","CH_BA94_VATS:A5","CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-03","text":"Existing a priori sciences block universal scepticism, but their existence creates an explanatory burden rather than an automatic validation of metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-05","CL_BA94_02:GA-06","CL_BA94_02:GA-08","CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4","CH_BA94_VUPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-04","text":"Contradiction becomes bifunctional: it remains a logical test of consistency while becoming a historical symptom of reason’s uncritical overextension.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-04","CL_BA94_02:GA-10","CH_BA94_VATS:ME1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-05","text":"Metaphysics’ double status as unavoidable disposition and unverified science requires a distinction between the inevitability of questioning and the legitimacy of knowing.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-08","CL_BA94_02:GA-09","CH_BA94_VUPP:D3","CH_BA94_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:XD-06","text":"Inquiry shifts from the relation of concepts to objects toward the authority of the operations by which reason connects, extends, and limits concepts.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-01","CL_BA94_02:GA-10","CH_BA94_IDBA:AS1","CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-01","text":"The burden of proof shifts from whether reason can formulate necessary claims to what ground permits necessary cognitive extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-02","text":"Theoretical science is redefined by its mode of grounding rather than by the object-domain to which its propositions refer.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-03","text":"Metaphysics becomes a threefold formation: natural disposition, historical dogmatic system, and conditionally possible science.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-04","text":"Historical metaphysical failure becomes methodological evidence against dogmatism while preserving the rational need that repeatedly regenerates metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-05","text":"Limitation becomes a positive condition of rational power because only delimited reason can distinguish warranted extension from assertion.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:BSD-06","text":"The absent positive ground of synthesis becomes operationally productive by forcing reason into a procedure of self-critique and conditional authorization.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT2","CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-01","text":"Analysis clarifies concepts, discovers its inability to augment them, and therefore returns as preparation for the synthesis it cannot itself produce.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A12","CH_BA94_IDBA:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-02","text":"Experience connects representations, fails to yield necessity, and thereby returns the argument to the demand for a non-empirical ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS4","CH_BA94_IDBA:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-03","text":"Existing sciences generate the question of their possibility, and the demand to explain their possibility redirects science into critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4","CH_BA94_VUPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-04","text":"Natural metaphysical questioning produces systems, systems generate contradiction, contradiction culminates in scepticism, and scepticism reactivates critique rather than ending metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A7","CH_BA94_VUPP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-05","text":"Contradiction is a phase-transformer that turns doctrinal production into jurisdictional self-examination.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:A8","CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:RLE-06","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment functions as the cluster’s principal attractor because every disciplinary case and every critical restriction converges on its possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:AF5","CH_BA94_VUPP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B01","text":"Subject–predicate relation → distinction between conceptual containment and externally connected predicate.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B02","text":"Conceptual containment → analytic explication → necessity without augmentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A3","CH_BA94_IDBA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B03","text":"Experiential connection → empirical synthesis → augmentation without universal necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AS4","CH_BA94_IDBA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B04","text":"Necessary non-contained predicate → synthetic a priori problem → mathematics and pure natural science as proof-cases requiring explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4","CH_BA94_VATS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B05","text":"Natural metaphysical demand plus recurrent contradiction → conversion of doctrinal claims into an inquiry into rational jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS4","CH_BA94_VUPP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FGS-B06","text":"Critique of rational competence → conditional authorization of extension or determinate restraint.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B01","text":"Subject–predicate classification → analytic containment or non-contained synthetic connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B02","text":"Analytic necessity without augmentation plus experiential augmentation without necessity → structural deficit of the available models of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B03","text":"Structural deficit → universal problem of necessary synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B04","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science → existing proof-cases that demand explanation of synthetic a priori possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B05","text":"Natural metaphysical demand plus historical contradiction → distinction between rational disposition and scientific legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS5","CH_BA94_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:AFM-B06","text":"Critique of reason → warranted extension or restraint under determinate limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:AS7","CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MME-01","text":"The field of cognition is reorganized into analytic explication, empirical synthesis, and synthetic a priori synthesis rather than divided simply between a priori and empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:ME2","CH_BA94_IDBA:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MME-02","text":"Logical contradiction is relocated from a supposed generator of knowledge to a criterion governing already grounded cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MME-03","text":"Mathematics, physics, and metaphysics become differentiations of one problem of grounding rather than isolated disciplines defined by distinct objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MME-04","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from a contested doctrinal archive into a problem concerning the conditions of valid a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MME-05","text":"The opposition between extension and limitation collapses because valid extension depends upon prior critical limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system classifies analytic judgments, synthetic judgments, mathematics, pure natural science, and metaphysics by the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, the system distributes cognition among conceptual containment, intuitive supplementation, empirical connection, and a priori necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the system replaces dogmatic construction with an inquiry into the authority entitled to make metaphysical claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reason is an enduring self-generative power whose metaphysical demand persists through the historical collapse of particular systems.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, distinctions, proof-cases, and contradictions progressively force reason to make its own powers the object of inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FL-01","text":"The argument depends on the compressed inference that the actual existence of mathematics and pure natural science guarantees the availability of an account of their possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FL-02","text":"Mathematical intuition is the strongest exhibited model of synthesis, while metaphysics requires a form of extension that exceeds the demonstrated mathematical model.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:FL-03","text":"Reason must determine the limits of reason through an inquiry that is itself conducted by reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MP-01","text":"Synthesis mutates from a relation between subject and predicate into the formal condition underwriting distinct theoretical sciences.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:A11","CH_BA94_VATS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MP-02","text":"Contradiction mutates from a logical criterion of invalidity into a historical signal that doctrinal metaphysics has exceeded its jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:ME1","CH_BA94_VUPP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MP-03","text":"Metaphysics mutates from a project of a priori enlargement into a critique of the scope and limits of the faculty seeking that enlargement.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:AS5","CH_BA94_VUPP:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is intelligible only as a field of connections whose necessity cannot be reduced either to conceptual containment or to empirical conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is reason acting simultaneously as claimant, investigator, and tribunal of its own authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is recursive rather than merely successive because metaphysical demand persists through repeated historical failure and returns in altered critical form.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:HSI-04","text":"Rational struggle is neither conquest of transcendent objects nor sceptical withdrawal, but navigation between warranted extension and strictly delimited restraint.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from analytic–synthetic distinction through scientific proof-cases to critique of rational competence.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined because it identifies the demand for a positive ground of synthetic a priori judgment without supplying that ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where the paired limits of analysis and experience are joined to the actual existence of mathematics and pure natural science.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDBA:MRT4","CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_02:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction preserves the cluster’s movement only if it retains the unresolved transition from mathematical intuition to a universal account of synthesis and the reflexive problem of reason limiting itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_VATS:MRT6","CH_BA94_VUPP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason."},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":3,"parents":["CH_BA94_VIDP","CH_BA94_IINT","CH_BA94_2MET"],"revision":"CL_BA94_03@R001","sha256":"7a30c6cfa54a769cc0bc4c3ee178a43c3800a752247b2438e87fce8460cf9661","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how absolutely a priori cognition can be validly extended when human knowledge reaches objects only through intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_IINT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B02","text":"The claim to a priori knowledge is converted from a presumed possession into a jurisdictional problem requiring critique of reason’s sources, scope, and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS4","CH_BA94_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B03","text":"Transcendental inquiry redirects explanation from objects as given entities to the a priori conditions under which givenness and thought become possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS5","CH_BA94_IINT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B04","text":"The critical division of faculties becomes an order of dependence: sensibility gives the object through intuition before understanding can think it through concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS6","CH_BA94_IINT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B05","text":"The distinction between sensory matter and a priori form makes sensibility a structured receptivity rather than a merely passive intake of impressions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B06","text":"The analysis of externality specifies space as the a priori form required for anything to appear outside, separate from, or related to anything else.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:AS2","CH_BA94_2MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B07","text":"Space’s status as pure intuition grounds geometrical necessity while blocking both empirical derivation and conceptual abstraction as sufficient explanations.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AS-B08","text":"The cluster closes by establishing a bounded phenomenal jurisdiction: critical inquiry can determine conditions of appearance without settling the status of things independently of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Valid a priori cognition requires prior determination of the faculties and conditions that make its object-relation possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D1","CH_BA94_IINT:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Human cognition has no object-directed use of concepts that is independent of intuition and givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:AS1","CH_BA94_IINT:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility and understanding are irreducibly distinct sources of cognition, because giving an object and thinking an object are non-interchangeable operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A12","CH_BA94_IINT:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Critique begins as a negative limitation of pure reason but becomes an operative method for isolating the pure conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D2","CH_BA94_IINT:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Phenomenal objectivity arises neither from raw sensation nor from conceptual construction alone, but from sensory matter ordered through a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Space is a necessary a priori form of external intuition and therefore a condition of possible external experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Geometrical apodeicticity is possible because spatial determination is grounded in pure intuition rather than in observed regularity or general concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A12","CH_BA94_2MET:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Systematic completion is conceivable only because the subject’s a priori field is bounded, although its full architectonic completion remains deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A15","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Critical validity terminates at the conditions of possible appearance and cannot establish the independent ontological status of space or of things beyond intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The a priori is not coextensive with conceptual generality, because the form that conditions external experience is singular, infinite, and intuitive.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A8","CH_BA94_2MET:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-01","text":"Critique recurs first as a negative touchstone against illegitimate a priori claims and then as the reductive procedure that isolates pure sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D2","CH_BA94_IINT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-02","text":"The a priori mutates from a general predicate of pure reason into the formal structure of sensibility and finally into the determinate condition of external intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A2","CH_BA94_IINT:A10","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-03","text":"Object-relation is displaced from the broad question of pure cognition to the mediated sequence of affection, intuition, phenomenal ordering, and conceptual thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ME1","CH_BA94_IINT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-04","text":"Form mutates from a relational principle organizing phenomenal content into the singular and infinite spatial intuition within which all external relations are possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-05","text":"The faculty division persists, but its function expands from an architectonic classification into the dependency structure governing objectivity and externality.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A12","CH_BA94_IINT:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-06","text":"Necessity shifts from the critical demand to justify pure reason’s scope into the modal impossibility of representing external experience without space.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A4","CH_BA94_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-07","text":"Completeness remains an organizing aspiration, but the cluster replaces the unavailable total system with a completed local demonstration of the conditions of external intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A9","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RMD-08","text":"Morality and taste perform boundary work by excluding empirical practical motives and empirical aesthetic criteria, then recede once the field of pure sensibility is isolated.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX7","CH_BA94_IINT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between organon and canon remains local machinery, while the cluster-level structure retains only the more general rule that validation must precede constructive extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX2","CH_BA94_VIDP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-02","text":"The exclusion of morality is locally specific to the definition of transcendental philosophy and scales upward only as an instance of the purity requirement.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX7","CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-03","text":"The terminological reassignment of æsthetic from taste to sensibility is local, but its structural consequence scales upward as the separation of empirical criteria from a priori law.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding scales upward because it supplies both the order of critical inquiry and the architecture of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D7","CH_BA94_IINT:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-05","text":"The reductive isolation of pure intuition bridges the critical demand for purity with the metaphysical exposition of space.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS3","CH_BA94_IINT:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-06","text":"The priority of givenness over thought becomes the catalytic transition through which an abstract division of faculties becomes a demonstration of spatial necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A14","CH_BA94_2MET:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-07","text":"Completed transcendental philosophy remains deferred, whereas transcendental æsthetic becomes the presently executable first determination of the wider system.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:CGD-08","text":"The independent status of space and things considered apart from intuition is not superseded but retained as the terminal boundary of the cluster’s claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4","CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects any doctrine of pure reason that claims productive authority before the legitimacy and limits of that authority have been examined.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX1","CH_BA94_VIDP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects conceptual thought as an independently sufficient route to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the reduction of phenomenal form to sensation, empirical accumulation, or contingent association.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:NX4","CH_BA94_IINT:DNX2","CH_BA94_IINT:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects the derivation of space from external experience because external differentiation already presupposes spatial representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:NX1","CH_BA94_2MET:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects the treatment of space as a general concept, an aggregate of separate spaces, or a finite container of infinitely many representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:NX4","CH_BA94_2MET:NX5","CH_BA94_2MET:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects the grounding of geometrical necessity in empirical regularity or discursive generality alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:DNX5","CH_BA94_2MET:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:GNX-07","text":"The system rejects the collapse of sensibility and understanding into a homogeneous cognitive faculty.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:NX9","CH_BA94_IINT:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:DBN-01","text":"Neither inherited doctrine, historical authority, nor the recurrence of unsupported assertion can validate a priori knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX1","CH_BA94_VIDP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:DBN-02","text":"No externality is intelligible prior to the form that makes outside, separation, location, and relation representable.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:DNX1","CH_BA94_2MET:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:DBN-03","text":"No account of cognition can treat its own forms as transparent, because their operation must be critically isolated before their authority can be claimed.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX2","CH_BA94_IINT:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:DBN-04","text":"No systematic totality can be generated merely by aggregating independently given parts, whether those parts are doctrines, spaces, or empirical objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX4","CH_BA94_2MET:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:DBN-05","text":"No unrestricted transition from conditions of appearance to claims about things independently of intuition is licensed.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:DNX5","CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSA-01","text":"Unrestricted absolute knowledge is unavailable because every legitimate object-claim is conditioned by a finite architecture of intuition and thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_VIDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSA-02","text":"A pre-form external object is unavailable to cognition, because removing spatial form removes the conditions under which externality can be represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:DNX6","CH_BA94_2MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSA-03","text":"A final common origin of sensibility and understanding is unavailable, even though their coordinated difference is indispensable to cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX2","CH_BA94_IINT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSA-04","text":"A completed system remains a regulative horizon rather than a presently possessed total doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX3","CH_BA94_VIDP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-01","text":"Critical limitation is not merely preparatory: it is enacted as the method that strips cognition down to the formal conditions required for valid object-relation.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-01","CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CH_BA94_VIDP:D2","CH_BA94_IINT:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-02","text":"The architectonic priority of sensibility becomes a proof-order requirement, because external concepts can be assessed only after the intuition that gives their objects has been determined.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-02","CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CH_BA94_VIDP:D7","CH_BA94_2MET:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-03","text":"Externality and geometrical certainty are co-grounded in spatial intuition: the same form that makes objects appear outside one another also makes geometry apodeictic.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CL_BA94_03:GA-07","CH_BA94_2MET:A5","CH_BA94_2MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-04","text":"The category of the a priori divides internally between conceptual principles and non-discursive intuitive forms, preventing pure cognition from being equated with abstraction.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:GA-10","CH_BA94_VIDP:A10","CH_BA94_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-05","text":"The negative exclusion of empirical derivation is productive because it reveals spatial form as the condition that empirical external experience silently uses.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CH_BA94_2MET:NX1","CH_BA94_2MET:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-06","text":"The bounded field of a priori cognition permits systematic inquiry without authorizing exhaustive cognition of external reality as such.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-08","CL_BA94_03:GA-09","CH_BA94_VIDP:SD5","CH_BA94_VIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-07","text":"Transcendental reduction brackets the independent nature of objects without eliminating empirical affection, thereby preserving mediation while refusing metaphysical possession.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CL_BA94_03:GA-09","CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:XD-08","text":"Spatial plurality is derivative rather than primitive, because every determinate region is a limitation of one prior spatial field.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CL_BA94_03:GA-10","CH_BA94_2MET:A9","CH_BA94_2MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as conditioned validity: an object is knowable neither through direct access nor through free conceptual production, but through lawful mediation by intuition and form.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-02","CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CH_BA94_IINT:SD3","CH_BA94_2MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-02","text":"Justification becomes regressive rather than accumulative: cognition is validated by recovering its enabling conditions, not by adding further empirical instances or doctrinal assertions.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-01","CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CH_BA94_VIDP:SD1","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes neither an unrestricted creator of the object nor a passive receiver of it, but the finite site in which affection is formally ordered and conceptually thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CH_BA94_IINT:SD6","CH_BA94_IINT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-04","text":"Scientific necessity becomes intelligible as the expression of a priori formal conditions rather than as a generalization extracted from repeated observation.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CL_BA94_03:GA-07","CH_BA94_2MET:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-05","text":"The distinction between critique and doctrine is transformed into an internal hierarchy of inquiry in which the determination of sensibility conditions every subsequent doctrine of thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-01","CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CH_BA94_VIDP:ME2","CH_BA94_VIDP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-06","text":"Purification is generative rather than merely restrictive, because subtraction produces the positive identification of pure form.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CH_BA94_IINT:D4","CH_BA94_IINT:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-07","text":"The system converts the apparent independence of external objects into a question of the conditions under which independence can appear as externality.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CL_BA94_03:GA-06","CL_BA94_03:GA-09","CH_BA94_2MET:ME2","CH_BA94_2MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:BSD-08","text":"The architectonic unity of the system is not derived from a first origin but assembled from functional divisions, regressions, and necessary presuppositions.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:GA-08","CL_BA94_03:GA-09","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX4","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-01","text":"Claim to pure cognition → critique of its legitimacy → isolation of its conditions → renewed criterion for evaluating claims to pure cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS1","CH_BA94_VIDP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-02","text":"Affection → sensation → empirical intuition → formal ordering → conceptual thought → return of thought to intuition as its indispensable object-relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:FGS1","CH_BA94_IINT:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-03","text":"External differentiation presupposes space → spatial form enables external experience → external experience displays distinctions that cannot explain their own spatial condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:D2","CH_BA94_2MET:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-04","text":"Spatial plurality → limitation within one space → confirmation of spatial unity → grounding of the non-discursive status of space.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:D5","CH_BA94_2MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-05","text":"Empirical subtraction → residual pure form → restriction of legitimate claims to that form’s domain → exclusion of empirical derivation from the account of the form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:FGS4","CH_BA94_2MET:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-06","text":"Bounded a priori faculties make systematic completion thinkable → incompleteness blocks possession of the completed system → deferred completion sustains further critical analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:SD5","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:RLE-07","text":"The condition of possibility functions as the cluster’s conceptual attractor, repeatedly converting apparent facts into presuppositions requiring transcendental reconstruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:SD5","CH_BA94_2MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B01","text":"Assertion of pure a priori cognition → uncertainty over its legitimate extension → critical examination of sources, scope, and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_VIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B02","text":"Critical examination → distinction between conditions of givenness and conditions of thought → priority of sensibility within the doctrine of elements.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A14","CH_BA94_VIDP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B03","text":"Object-affection → sensation and empirical intuition → distinction between phenomenal matter and a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B04","text":"Subtraction of conceptual and sensory additions → isolation of pure intuition → constitution of transcendental æsthetic.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B05","text":"Analysis of external representation → regress to antecedent space → determination of space as necessary a priori intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:AS2","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B06","text":"Unity and infinite givenness of space → exclusion of conceptual and aggregate models → grounding of geometrical apodeicticity.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A9","CH_BA94_2MET:A11","CH_BA94_2MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FGS-B07","text":"Determination of phenomenal conditions → validation of restricted objective claims → suspension of claims concerning things independently of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D3","CH_BA94_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B01","text":"Doubt concerning a priori extension → critical delimitation of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B02","text":"Critical delimitation of reason → transcendental relocation from objects to conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B03","text":"Conditions of cognition → division of sensibility and understanding according to givenness and thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B04","text":"Sensibility → distinction between sensory matter and a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:AS4","CH_BA94_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B05","text":"A priori form → reductive isolation of pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B06","text":"Pure intuition → space as the necessary form of external experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:D8","CH_BA94_2MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:AFM-B07","text":"Spatial intuition → unity, infinity, geometrical necessity, and a terminal boundary against claims about things independently of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:SD5","CH_BA94_2MET:D7","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-01","text":"Philosophical authority is relocated from the possession of a doctrine to the prior examination of the faculty that would authorize doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:ME1","CH_BA94_VIDP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-02","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from direct access to an object into intuition-mediated givenness under a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ME1","CH_BA94_2MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-03","text":"Sensibility is reclassified from bare receptivity into a source of a priori organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ME2","CH_BA94_IINT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-04","text":"The empirical and the a priori are no longer separated simply by faculties, because sensibility itself contains empirical matter and a priori form.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-05","text":"Space is reclassified from an object-property or empirical relation into the condition through which external objects can appear as external.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:ME2","CH_BA94_2MET:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-06","text":"Spatial multiplicity is reclassified as derivative limitation within one prior field rather than as a plurality of independently fundamental spaces.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:ME4","CH_BA94_2MET:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MME-07","text":"Geometry is separated from empirical observation and conceptual generality and attached to the constitutive form of external intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The explicit problem concerns the relation of knowledge to objects through intuition, sensation, conception, and space.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ALT1","CH_BA94_2MET:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Cognition is organized through the ordered mediation of affection, sensibility, pure form, intuition, and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The system replaces object-description and doctrinal comparison with a regressive inquiry into the conditions that make experience and valid judgment possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:ALT3","CH_BA94_2MET:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — External reality is available only as phenomenally ordered under antecedent spatial form, while independent reality remains a suspended question rather than a resolved object.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:ALT4","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The system performs its thesis by subtracting empirical and conceptual additions, then treating the residual condition as the explanatory ground of what was subtracted.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:FGS4","CH_BA94_2MET:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FL-01","text":"Empirical intuition requires an object to affect the mind, but the relation between that affection and the mind’s a priori formal contribution is functionally asserted rather than explained.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FL-02","text":"Critique is described as complete at the level required for judgment, while completed transcendental philosophy is withheld as requiring a fuller analysis than the critique supplies.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D5","CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FL-03","text":"Object-directed cognition requires givenness and sensation, yet pure intuition is isolated by removing sensation and can therefore appear independent of the empirical object it conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FL-04","text":"Space is established as necessary for external experience, but its status as a reality or predicate independent of intuition remains deliberately unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A6","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:FL-05","text":"The unity and infinite givenness of space are asserted together with finite representations of particular spaces, leaving the relation between infinite field and finite delimitation compressed.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A11","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from pure reason’s principle of cognition into sensibility’s formal ordering and then into the intuition of one space.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:A1","CH_BA94_IINT:A10","CH_BA94_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MP-02","text":"The object mutates from the affecting correlate of sensation into the phenomenon ordered under form and then into externally represented appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:A5","CH_BA94_IINT:D2","CH_BA94_2MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MP-03","text":"Necessity mutates from a demand for critical legitimacy into the modal nonrepresentability of the absence of space.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D1","CH_BA94_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MP-04","text":"Form mutates from the relational arrangement of phenomenal content into the unbounded unity whose limitations constitute particular spaces.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned processing field in which affection supplies material, sensibility orders it through form, and understanding thinks the resulting intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite relay rather than a sovereign creator or transparent observer: it receives affection, supplies form, and thinks only what can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:HSI-03","text":"External space is not an independently mapped container within the system but the preconditioned field through which externality first becomes representable.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:ME2","CH_BA94_2MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:HSI-04","text":"Time is present only as an announced co-form of sensibility, while the cluster’s operative temporal logic is one of transcendental antecedence rather than chronological succession.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:D8","CH_BA94_2MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in navigating the boundary between legitimate phenomenal construction and illegitimate claims to unconditioned cognitive possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:D3","CH_BA94_VIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s decisive movement from the critique of a priori claims to the determination of space as pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT1","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the proof that space and time are specifically the pure forms and concerning the independent status of space.","refs":["CH_BA94_IINT:MRT3","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if the distinction between empirical matter and a priori form is retained as the governing condition of its regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_2MET:MRT3","CH_BA94_IINT:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where critical delimitation, reductive isolation, and the regress from external experience to spatial form operate as one continuous machine.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT7","CH_BA94_2MET:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction remains dependent on unresolved tensions between affection and form, pure intuition and empirical givenness, and functional faculty-division and unavailable common origin.","refs":["CH_BA94_VIDP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","original_title":"Chapters 07–09","original_context":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."},"original_title":"Chapters 07–09","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":4,"parents":["CH_BA94_3TEC","CH_BA94_4CFC","CH_BA94_5MET"],"revision":"CL_BA94_04@R001","sha256":"ec38004e5f707e3ec84b2eb1cbd39401126d190ee0ceefcab6879a5ce012d074","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-01","text":"The cluster reconstructs space and time as non-empirical conditions required for the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition and phenomenal representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS1","CH_BA94_5MET:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-02","text":"Its argument reverses explanation: spatial and temporal order do not arise from experience but condition the possibility of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD3","CH_BA94_5MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-03","text":"Geometry establishes that external representation requires pure spatial intuition, while temporal succession establishes that all phenomena require pure temporal intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D7","CH_BA94_5MET:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-04","text":"Intuition becomes the shared generative medium through which necessity can extend cognition beyond conceptual analysis without becoming empirical.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-05","text":"Space and time are structurally asymmetric: space organizes external appearances, whereas time conditions phenomenal reality as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A11","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-06","text":"The system secures objectivity by restricting it to appearances, thereby distinguishing empirical validity from knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A8","CH_BA94_4CFC:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-07","text":"The movement is recursive and boundary-producing: the conditions that enable necessary cognition also mark the limit beyond which cognition cannot legitimately claim ontological reach.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS6","CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ABS-08","text":"Its closure is conditional rather than absolute: phenomenal order is rendered necessary for us, while the positive constitution of things independently of sensibility remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD6","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Synthetic a priori cognition requires pure intuition whenever cognition must extend beyond what concepts analytically contain.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A5","CH_BA94_5MET:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Necessity belongs to the formal conditions of possible experience rather than to empirical regularities extracted from experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD4","CH_BA94_5MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Space functions as the pure form of external intuition, making external appearances immediately representable without constituting things in themselves spatially.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Time functions as the universal form of phenomenal intuition, making succession, coexistence, and temporal determination possible without deriving them from phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:D1","CH_BA94_5MET:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Objective validity is domain-indexed: it is secured for possible experience precisely by being denied as a determination of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD3","CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Receptivity is not passive empirical intake but the subject’s contribution of the formal field in which objects can appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The form/content distinction governs both spatial and temporal cognition: empirical objects provide material only within forms that precede their appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD3","CH_BA94_5MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The possibility of experience is the discriminating criterion by which representations are classified as pure forms, empirical contents, or illegitimate metaphysical projections.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD5","CH_BA94_5MET:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Phenomenal cognition is complete only as cognition of appearances; any transition from conditions of appearance to the intrinsic constitution of things is invalid.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX7","CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Unity precedes determination: one spatially and temporally enabling form is presupposed before particular magnitudes, relations, or intervals can be represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A7","CH_BA94_5MET:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-01","text":"The requirement of pure intuition recurs identically as the non-conceptual ground of synthetic a priori cognition, but expands from geometry’s spatial case into the universal temporal condition of phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D2","CH_BA94_5MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-02","text":"A priori priority retains its function as independence from empirical derivation, but its domain mutates from external representation in space to all phenomenal representation in time.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A8","CH_BA94_5MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-03","text":"Objectivity reverses from apparent object-independence into phenomenal validity: what is objective is not what belongs to things in themselves but what necessarily governs possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ME3","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-04","text":"The subject’s receptivity migrates from the formal source of externality to the general architecture in which necessary forms condition phenomenal givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A11","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-05","text":"Infinity is displaced from a merely negative absence of limits into the positive structural unity from which determinate temporal quantities arise by limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A9","CH_BA94_5MET:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-06","text":"The thing in itself does not become a positive object of inquiry; it is absorbed into the higher-order role of a boundary that prevents phenomenal conditions from becoming ontological predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD6","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RMD-07","text":"Empiricism disappears as a viable ground at each local site after performing its structural role as the excluded rival model of spatial and temporal necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX3","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-01","text":"The specifically geometrical proof that spatial cognition exceeds conceptual analysis remains local, but its non-conceptual requirement scales into the cluster’s general theory of pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS3","CH_BA94_5MET:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between space and sensations remains a local classificatory refinement because the cluster’s higher architecture concerns forms of intuition rather than every species of subjective representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A13","CH_BA94_4CFC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-03","text":"The unity and unlimitedness of time begin as temporal-specific machinery but scale upward as the general priority of formal unity over empirical or conceptual determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:A9","CH_BA94_5MET:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-04","text":"The empirical-reality/transcendental-ideality distinction bridges the spatial and temporal accounts by supplying the cluster’s general rule for restricted objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:AS6","CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-05","text":"The transcendental exposition of space catalyzes the cluster-wide methodological transition from explaining objects through experience to explaining experience through its conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS2","CH_BA94_5MET:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:CGD-06","text":"Any account treating pure forms as independently existing entities becomes obsolete once formal priority is recoded as a condition of receptivity rather than an ontological property of reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX6","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-01","text":"Neither space nor time may be derived from empirical perceptions, observed sequences, or inductive generalization.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX5","CH_BA94_5MET:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-02","text":"Neither space nor time is a discursive concept from which necessary relations can be analytically extracted.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX1","CH_BA94_5MET:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-03","text":"Formal conditions of appearance cannot be converted into properties, relations, or structures of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:NX1","CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-04","text":"No universal claim about intuition may be extended beyond the domain for which its subjective conditions have been established.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:NX6","CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-05","text":"Phenomenal contents cannot ground the conditions that render those contents representable as ordered objects or events.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX2","CH_BA94_5MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:GNX-06","text":"Formal unity cannot be assembled from the determinate parts that presuppose it.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:DNX3","CH_BA94_5MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:DBN-01","text":"The cluster disqualifies any theory in which the necessity of cognition is inferred from a world already given as spatially and temporally ordered.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX1","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:DBN-02","text":"It rules out a spectator-subject that merely receives fully constituted objects, because receptivity must already supply the forms of their possible appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:DBN-03","text":"It rules out unrestricted realism, since treating formal conditions as ontological determinations collapses the difference between appearance and thing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:D8","CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:DBN-04","text":"It rules out unrestricted idealism, since subjective origin does not cancel the necessity and objective applicability of forms within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX5","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:DBN-05","text":"It rules out a conceptualism that attempts to generate determinate spatial or temporal cognition without immediate intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A6","CH_BA94_5MET:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable position organizing the cluster is an unconditioned standpoint from which reality could be known independently of every form of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX3","CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSA-02","text":"The cluster excludes the possibility of knowing whether the forms necessary for human experience also belong to reality in itself or to every possible thinking being.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX5","CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSA-03","text":"It denies any cognition that would bypass the subject-object relation while retaining claims to necessary objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SNX3","CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSA-04","text":"It excludes an account of phenomena without formal conditions, because removing time and space removes the intelligibility of ordered appearance rather than revealing an unconstrained empirical world.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:NX10","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-01","text":"Spatial and temporal forms jointly establish that synthetic a priori knowledge is possible only where intuition supplies a pre-empirical field for determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D7","CH_BA94_5MET:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-02","text":"The difference between space and time prevents pure intuition from becoming a uniform abstraction: externality is spatially conditioned, while phenomenal succession and coexistence are temporally conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D6","CH_BA94_5MET:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-03","text":"The necessity of geometry and the necessity of temporal order have the same transcendental status: each is valid because it expresses a condition for possible experience rather than an observed feature of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD4","CH_BA94_5MET:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-04","text":"The subject is neither an empirical creator of spatial and temporal contents nor a passive receiver of their order; it is receptive through forms that precondition empirical givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD6","CH_BA94_4CFC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-05","text":"The restriction of spatial validity to appearances generalizes across the cluster: formal necessity licenses phenomenal objectivity while blocking ontological inference from form to thing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD1","CH_BA94_5MET:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:XD-06","text":"The temporal doctrine of unlimited unity clarifies the cluster-wide priority of form over determinate content: particular representations are limitations within conditions that cannot be reconstructed from particulars.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:D6","CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSD-01","text":"The cluster transforms necessity from a predicate of independently ordered reality into the lawful invariance of the conditions under which reality can appear to a finite sensuous subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD1","CH_BA94_5MET:SD2","CL_BA94_04:GA-02","CL_BA94_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes relational rather than absolute: it names validity for every object within a specified field of possible experience, not access to being apart from conditions of appearing.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD3","CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX7","CL_BA94_04:GA-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSD-03","text":"The cluster produces a transcendental realism of appearances in which empirical objects are fully law-governed and knowable as phenomena without becoming transparent as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:A9","CH_BA94_4CFC:D9","CL_BA94_04:GA-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSD-04","text":"The form/content distinction becomes an anti-regressive principle: no empirical item may ground the condition that first makes it available as an empirical item.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX2","CH_BA94_5MET:DNX1","CL_BA94_04:GA-07","CL_BA94_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:BSD-05","text":"The cluster’s method becomes diagnostic: rival metaphysical accounts are adjudicated by whether they can generate necessary cognition while preserving the boundary between appearance and thing in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD5","CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS8","CL_BA94_04:GA-08","CL_BA94_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RLE-01","text":"The primary recursive loop runs from necessary cognition to pure form, from pure form to possible experience, and from possible experience back to the validation of necessary cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS5","CH_BA94_5MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RLE-02","text":"The central escalation path moves from the insufficiency of conceptual analysis and empirical observation toward a theory of receptivity as the condition of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D1","CH_BA94_5MET:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RLE-03","text":"The appearance/thing-in-itself distinction is a conceptual attractor because every assertion of formal necessity is forced back toward a restriction on its ontological scope.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD1","CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RLE-04","text":"Unity becomes a generative attractor: one prior form yields manifold determinations through differentiation or limitation without being reducible to them.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:SD3","CH_BA94_5MET:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:RLE-05","text":"Repeated exclusion of empiricism is not redundant but phase-changing: it first establishes a priori form, then protects that form from reduction to ontology, psychology, or induction.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX4","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FGS-B01","text":"Necessary synthetic cognition → inadequacy of empirical derivation and conceptual analysis → demand for pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A4","CH_BA94_5MET:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FGS-B02","text":"Pure intuition → subject’s receptive formal constitution → spatial externality and temporal phenomenal order.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:A10","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FGS-B03","text":"Formal constitution + empirical affection → ordered appearances → objectively valid principles for possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD2","CH_BA94_5MET:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FGS-B04","text":"Objective phenomenal validity → restriction of ontological reach → empirical reality without transcendental realism.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:AS5","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FGS-B05","text":"Restriction of ontological reach → thing in itself as limiting correlate → recurrent prohibition on converting conditions of appearance into predicates of being.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD6","CH_BA94_4CFC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:AFM-B01","text":"Synthetic a priori science → failure of conceptual and empirical derivation → pure intuition as necessary ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS4","CH_BA94_5MET:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:AFM-B02","text":"Pure intuition → receptive form of sensibility → space as external form and time as phenomenal form.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS6","CH_BA94_5MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:AFM-B03","text":"Formal sensibility → necessary ordering of appearances → geometry and temporal principles as conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D7","CH_BA94_5MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:AFM-B04","text":"Necessary phenomenal validity → empirical reality → transcendental ideality and the delimitation of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:AS6","CH_BA94_4CFC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:AFM-B05","text":"Delimitation of cognition → exclusion of noumenal spatiality and temporal derivation → stable but conditioned objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:AF3","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MME-01","text":"The cluster reclassifies space and time from contents within experience into conditions that organize the possibility of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ME1","CH_BA94_5MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MME-02","text":"It collapses the false opposition between subjective origin and objective validity by separating transcendental source from empirical domain of application.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ME3","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MME-03","text":"It redraws the boundary between science and metaphysics: necessary science concerns appearances under pure forms, while metaphysical claims about things in themselves lack warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD4","CH_BA94_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MME-04","text":"It makes formal dependence visible as a method of explanation, requiring inquiry to identify what must be presupposed before a phenomenon can be represented or judged.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AS2","CH_BA94_5MET:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MME-05","text":"It reclassifies infinity as structural inexhaustibility of formal determination rather than empirical magnitude or a completed aggregate.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:ME5","CH_BA94_5MET:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The explicit objects are geometry, external objects, temporal coexistence, succession, and the validity of spatial and temporal judgments.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT1","CH_BA94_5MET:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Pure forms of sensibility mediate between empirical affection and ordered appearances, making externality and temporality conditions rather than discovered properties.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT2","CH_BA94_5MET:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Explanation proceeds by identifying conditions of possibility and testing rival accounts by their capacity to generate necessary cognition without illicit ontological extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT3","CH_BA94_5MET:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Reality for cognition is phenomenal, temporally conditioned, and spatially externalized, while being independently of sensibility remains inaccessible.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:ALT4","CH_BA94_4CFC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The cluster performs its thesis by repeatedly converting apparent properties of objects into enabling conditions that must be presupposed by any representation of those objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:FGS7","CH_BA94_5MET:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FL-01","text":"The system requires space and time to be independent of particular phenomena as conditions of representation while defining their operative significance through their role in representing phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT6","CH_BA94_5MET:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FL-02","text":"The claim that forms are subject-grounded yet objectively valid remains stable only through the phenomenal restriction that prevents either term from acquiring unrestricted meaning.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT6","CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FL-03","text":"The thing in itself is required as the limit of phenomenal cognition, yet its assertion as a real correlate exceeds what the system can positively determine.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD6","CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:FL-04","text":"The unity of unlimited time generates determinate temporal quantities through limitation, but the source does not supply a further account of the limiting act.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:MRT6","CH_BA94_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MP-01","text":"“Objectivity” mutates from apparent independence of the subject into universal validity for objects under the subject’s conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:SD5","CH_BA94_4CFC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MP-02","text":"“Subjectivity” mutates from the possible unreality of a representation into the transcendental source of empirically binding formal order.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX5","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MP-03","text":"“Infinity” mutates from an indeterminate absence of boundary into the productive unity that makes finite temporal determination possible through limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:SD5","CH_BA94_5MET:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned field: empirical objects and events become knowable only after being received within spatial externality and temporal order.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:D6","CH_BA94_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a formally receptive node rather than a sovereign producer or empty container, supplying the conditions under which objects can appear without producing their empirical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:SD6","CH_BA94_3TEC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is the irreversible dimension of phenomenal differentiation, while space is the field of external coexistence; neither is licensed as a predicate of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_5MET:NX7","CH_BA94_4CFC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:HSI-04","text":"Struggle at this scale is methodological: thought must navigate between empirical reduction, conceptual abstraction, and transcendent projection in order to preserve conditioned objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:NX7","CH_BA94_4CFC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from synthetic a priori cognition to pure forms of intuition and from pure forms to the restricted objectivity of appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT1","CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction is strongest where spatial and temporal arguments converge on the priority of formal conditions over empirical contents and conceptual derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT2","CH_BA94_5MET:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if “objective validity” remains indexed to possible experience and never becomes a claim about things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_4CFC:MRT3","CH_BA94_4CFC:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MRT-B04","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive status of the thing in itself, the relation between forms and independently existing objects, and the act by which unlimited time is limited.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:MRT4","CH_BA94_5MET:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_04:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction must preserve recursive return from formal conditions to their empirical validity and then to their ontological restriction; linear restatement alone loses the architecture’s decisive movement.","refs":["CH_BA94_3TEC:AF5","CH_BA94_4CFC:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","original_title":"Chapters 10–12","original_context":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."},"original_title":"Chapters 10–12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":5,"parents":["CH_BA94_6TEC","CH_BA94_7CAC","CH_BA94_8ELU"],"revision":"CL_BA94_05@R001","sha256":"ddc0f3b8e4f806d40aac7bdbba6351f2ba663d396e5459ce7048959ea9eab5d2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-01","text":"The cluster resolves how time can be necessary for change, motion, and cognition while lacking independent ontological subsistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:AS1","CH_BA94_7CAC:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-02","text":"Time is reconstructed as an a priori form of internal intuition rather than an abstract concept, object, or property received from things.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:AS2","CH_BA94_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-03","text":"Temporal succession makes change intelligible by assigning opposed determinations to one object at different times, and motion follows as change of place.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","CH_BA94_6TEC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-04","text":"Because every object is represented in internal state, temporal conditioning extends from inner appearance to all phenomena, including external ones.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-05","text":"The empirical reality of change confirms temporal ordering within experience but cannot establish an absolutely real time outside sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:AS2","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-06","text":"Phenomenal objectivity is secured by the forms of intuition while things in themselves remain unavailable as temporally determinable objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-07","text":"Necessary cognition is retained by locating mathematical and temporal validity in universally binding forms of possible experience rather than in correspondence with things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:AS6","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AS-08","text":"The movement terminates by recoding motion and change as empirical determinations that presuppose pure forms rather than augmenting the pure inventory of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Time is the internal a priori form without which succession, change, and motion cannot be represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A4","CH_BA94_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Temporal succession converts opposed determinations of one object from simultaneous contradiction into intelligible change.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","CH_BA94_6TEC:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Motion presupposes temporal intuition as change of place, yet particular motion remains an empirical determination rather than a pure form.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:D1","CH_BA94_8ELU:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The internality of every representation makes time universally valid for phenomena without making time an external form.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Temporal necessity has objective validity only for things as phenomena and cannot be extended to things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:D7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Empirical reality does not entail absolute reality: actual change is real within experience without proving that time exists independently of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:D1","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Synthetic a priori cognition is possible because temporal and spatial forms precede empirical objects and determine the field in which objects can appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Space and time exhaust the pure forms of sensibility; every further sensible determination requires empirical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A12","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Time is a condition of object-presentation, not an object, relation, or objective property inhering in things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:ME1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Subjectivity denotes the necessary human form of receptivity that establishes empirical objectivity, not a private or arbitrary psychological construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT3","CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-01","text":"Time retains its identity as a priori intuition while expanding from the condition of change into the universal form of internal sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3","CH_BA94_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-02","text":"Time mutates from an explanatory condition for alteration into the boundary condition that prevents temporal objectivity from becoming absolute ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:SD2","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-03","text":"Change shifts from the phenomenon requiring temporal explanation to the empirical fact invoked against transcendental ideality and then reabsorbed as its conditioned effect.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","CH_BA94_8ELU:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-04","text":"Motion migrates from an object whose possibility is grounded by time into an empirical derivative that cannot itself count as a pure form of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-05","text":"Subjectivity reverses valence: what might appear as merely subjective becomes the basis of universally valid phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:SD5","CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-06","text":"The inner–outer distinction is displaced from a division between temporal and non-temporal domains into immediate and mediate temporal conditioning.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A6","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-07","text":"Contradiction is recoded from simultaneous incompatibility into a temporal relation whose ordered non-coexistence constitutes change.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:ME2","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-08","text":"Time’s isolated analysis is absorbed into the paired architecture of space and time, where both function as exhaustive pure forms of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RMD-09","text":"The thing in itself enters only after temporal universality has been established and persists as a limit that blocks the conversion of phenomenal form into ontological predicate.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A12","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-01","text":"The definition of motion as change of place remains local; the cluster retains only motion’s dependency on temporal and empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A2","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-02","text":"The line analogy remains a local illustrative device whose current-level function is only to preserve the distinction between spatial coexistence and temporal succession.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between a priori conditions for cognition concerning motion and the empirical status of particular motions becomes a central bridge between the first and final units.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-04","text":"The claim that every representation belongs to internal state scales upward because it universalizes temporal conditioning across external as well as internal phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-05","text":"The phenomenon–thing-in-itself distinction becomes a catalytic current-level structure because it converts epistemic constitution into an ontological limit.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-06","text":"The distinction between empirical reality and absolute reality bridges the treatment of change, temporal objectivity, and transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:D8","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-07","text":"Rival accounts of time become structurally relevant only when their opposed failures are combined: absolutism reifies empty entities, while empiricism cannot yield apodeictic necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:D5","CH_BA94_8ELU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:CGD-08","text":"The exclusion of every pure sensible source beyond space and time is the terminal classificatory result of the cluster rather than a merely local doctrinal claim.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A12","CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-01","text":"Time is rejected as a self-subsisting entity independent of the sensible conditions under which it is represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-02","text":"Time is rejected as an objective determination inhering in things, because conditions of appearing cannot be transferred into predicates of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A2","CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects every derivation of time from empirical change, since change already presupposes time and cannot prove its absolute reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:DNX2","CH_BA94_8ELU:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects an external temporality that could bypass the internal state in which every object is represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX3","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects the inference from empirical objectivity to absolute ontological reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX4","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects the privilege of inner consciousness as a route to the self independently of phenomenal appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:NX4","CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:GNX-07","text":"The system rejects motion, change, and any third datum of sensibility as additional pure forms alongside space and time.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:NX8","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-01","text":"No subject-independent temporal standpoint is available from within human sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-02","text":"No necessary condition of human experience may be converted into a determination of being as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-03","text":"The thing in itself must remain structurally posited as the limit of appearance while remaining unavailable for determinate temporal cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-04","text":"Universal temporal propositions become illegitimate whenever “things in general” silently retains the conditions of sensuous intuition after claiming to abstract from them.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:DNX5","CH_BA94_7CAC:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-05","text":"Neither substantival absolutism nor empirical relationalism can preserve both experiential coherence and a priori necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:D5","CH_BA94_8ELU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:DBN-06","text":"Mathematical necessity cannot arise from empirical relations and therefore cannot be justified by abstraction from experienced change.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX3","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSA-01","text":"The system forecloses a time that remains cognitively meaningful after every relation to sensuous intuition has been removed.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSA-02","text":"The system requires an outside of appearance only as an inaccessible limit, never as a positive temporal order available to cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSA-03","text":"The possibility of non-sensible intuition functions only as a limiting counterfactual and cannot supply positive cognition of another temporal or non-temporal regime.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSA-04","text":"A self or object temporally determinate in itself is unavailable to cognition because both self-representation and object-presentation remain conditioned by sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:DNX2","CH_BA94_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSA-05","text":"No third pure sensible source can bridge formal intuition and empirical content without collapsing the closure of transcendental aesthetic.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-01","text":"Real change certifies only the empirical reality of temporal ordering; it cannot reverse the explanatory direction by deriving time from change.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-01","CL_BA94_05:GA-06","CH_BA94_6TEC:AS4","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-02","text":"A priori cognition concerning motion concerns the formal temporal conditions of movable objects, whereas particular motion and change remain empirically given determinations.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-03","CL_BA94_05:GA-07","CL_BA94_05:GA-08","CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-03","text":"External objects cannot supply an independent source of time because their representation already belongs to the internally ordered field of appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-04","CL_BA94_05:GA-09","CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-04","text":"Identity through changing states is valid only for an object as it appears, because succession unifies determinations that cannot be assigned to a thing in itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-02","CL_BA94_05:GA-05","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","CH_BA94_8ELU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-05","text":"Empirical realism is stabilized precisely by transcendental ideality: the temporal form must be subject-conditioned enough to avoid reification and universally binding enough to govern phenomena.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-05","CL_BA94_05:GA-06","CL_BA94_05:GA-10","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD5","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-06","text":"Necessary mathematical cognition and temporal cognition share a formal source, but neither can claim correspondence with things independently of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-05","CL_BA94_05:GA-07","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-07","text":"Absolutist and empiricist theories fail in opposite directions, forcing a position in which formal conditions are neither independent entities nor abstractions from experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-06","CL_BA94_05:GA-07","CL_BA94_05:GA-09","CH_BA94_8ELU:D5","CH_BA94_8ELU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:XD-08","text":"The closure of pure sensibility follows from a dependency test: any determination requiring an existing movable object belongs to empirical experience rather than to pure intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-03","CL_BA94_05:GA-08","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-01","text":"The condition that constitutes phenomenal objectivity simultaneously withholds every claim that time belongs to reality independently of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:SD2","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes a universal operator of intelligibility that jointly produces change, persistence, motion, and the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:SD1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-03","text":"The cluster reverses evidentiary priority: observed alteration is not the ground of time but an appearance made possible by temporal form.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS6","CH_BA94_8ELU:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-04","text":"The subject is recoded as the site of universal receptive form rather than as a private psychological observer or a self-transparent substance.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-05","text":"Reality is stratified into objectively valid appearances and an inaccessible thing in itself, with the latter functioning as a limit rather than a second knowable domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-06","text":"The pure inventory of sensibility is determined negatively by excluding everything whose representation depends on empirical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:SD4","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:BSD-07","text":"Necessary knowledge is redefined as validity within possible experience rather than as knowledge of being without qualification.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:SD6","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-01","text":"Time as form generates succession; succession makes change real in experience; real change produces the objection of absolute time; the objection is resolved by returning change to its formal condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A1","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-02","text":"Internal representation universalizes temporal order across phenomena, and this universalization is then restricted back to phenomena rather than extended to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-03","text":"Change undergoes a phase transition from transcendental effect, to empirical datum, to apparent counterexample, and finally to confirmation of phenomenal temporal reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:AS1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-04","text":"Identity persists only by recursively integrating successive differences under one object, making temporal ordering the machine of persistence through alteration.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","CH_BA94_6TEC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-05","text":"The attractor of pure-form status draws mathematical necessity toward space and time while expelling every determination dependent upon empirical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-06","text":"Rival theories form a forced escalation path in which absolutism loses formal humility and empiricism loses necessity, driving the reconstruction toward transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS5","CH_BA94_8ELU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:RLE-07","text":"The final exclusion of motion and change completes a hierarchical loop: they depend upon pure forms, but their dependency disqualifies them from being pure forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B01","text":"A priori inner intuition of time establishes the condition under which succession can be represented before particular objects are given.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B02","text":"Succession distributes opposed determinations across different times and thereby transforms incompatibility into the possibility of change.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B03","text":"Change and motion enter experience as empirically real determinations constituted within temporal and spatial forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B04","text":"The internal belonging of every representation extends temporal conditioning from inner states to all possible phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B05","text":"Universal phenomenal validity is secured by restricting time to appearances and denying absolute temporal reality beyond sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS5","CH_BA94_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B06","text":"Pure forms make synthetic a priori cognition possible while fixing its validity within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_7CAC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B07","text":"Absolutist and empiricist alternatives are rejected because each sacrifices a condition that transcendental formalism retains.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FGS-B08","text":"The requirement of empirical movable content excludes motion and change from pure sensibility and closes the transcendental-aesthetic inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS6","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B01","text":"Internal a priori form of time → representable temporal succession.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B02","text":"Temporal succession → opposed determinations can belong to one object without simultaneous contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B03","text":"Ordered opposed determinations → empirically real change and conditionally possible motion.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:D1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B04","text":"Internal ordering of representation → temporal conditioning of all phenomena, including external ones.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B05","text":"Universal phenomenal conditioning → empirical reality of time without absolute temporal reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:D7","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B06","text":"Pure forms of intuition → synthetic a priori cognition valid for possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B07","text":"Rival failures of absolutism and empiricism → transcendental ideality as the only retained structure.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:AFM-B08","text":"Empirical dependency of motion and change → closure of pure sensibility at space and time.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A11","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is divided into empirical validity within experience and absolute reality beyond the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:ME3","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-02","text":"Externality is redescribed as mediately temporal because external objects are represented only through internal state.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:ME2","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-03","text":"Motion and change are reclassified from candidates for transcendental forms into empirical contents that presuppose those forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:ME3","CH_BA94_8ELU:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-04","text":"Mathematics is relocated from correspondence with things in themselves to universally binding conditions under which objects can appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ME3","CH_BA94_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-05","text":"Contradiction is divided into simultaneous incompatibility and temporally ordered opposition, making persistence through difference conceptually available.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:ME2","CH_BA94_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-06","text":"The dispute over time is transformed from a choice between realism and idealism into a forced comparison of reification, empirical abstraction, and transcendental formalism.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:FGS5","CH_BA94_8ELU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MME-07","text":"The field of pure sensibility is closed through a dependency criterion rather than by merely enumerating forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT5","CH_BA94_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] Actual change, motion, and the status of time are the explicit objects through which the cluster tests whether temporal reality is absolute or phenomenal.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Internal sense mediates every representation, while space and time organize the formal field in which empirical objects can appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:ALT2","CH_BA94_8ELU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system retains empirical realism and mathematical necessity by assigning objectivity and subject-dependence to distinct but coordinated domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT3","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Time is necessary for phenomenal succession but has no status as a self-subsisting feature of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ALT4","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The argument preserves a claim only by regressing from empirical appearance to its condition of representation and then restricting that condition to its legitimate domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS6","CH_BA94_7CAC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FL-01","text":"Time is subject-conditioned while objectively valid for all phenomena, and the cluster stabilizes this tension by restricting objectivity to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT5","CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FL-02","text":"The cluster grants synthetic a priori cognition concerning motion while denying motion and change the status of pure forms; coherence depends on distinguishing formal conditions from empirically given instances.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A7","CH_BA94_8ELU:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FL-03","text":"Temporal necessity is universal only under the condition of human sensuous intuition, leaving the scope of its universality bounded rather than unqualified.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A8","CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:FL-04","text":"The thing in itself is required to mark the limit of appearance, but its relation to appearance remains structurally underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT4","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MP-01","text":"Change mutates from the effect that proves the necessity of temporal succession into the empirical premise of an objection and finally into a conditionally valid phenomenal fact.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:AS1","CH_BA94_8ELU:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MP-02","text":"Objectivity mutates from a possible claim about intrinsic reality into validity for every object insofar as it can be presented to the senses.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A9","CH_BA94_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MP-03","text":"Inner sense mutates from a register of private inwardness into the universal medium through which even external objects are temporally represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MP-04","text":"Time mutates from the real form of inner intuition into nothing once the condition of sensuous intuition is abstracted.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A2","CH_BA94_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a two-level machine: a formally organized phenomenal field and an inaccessible limit that cannot be converted into a second knowable temporal domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:A5","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a receptive node in which every representation enters internal state and becomes ordered under universally binding forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:A5","CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:HSI-03","text":"Time functions as a processor that converts opposed determinations into successive states and thereby produces intelligible alteration.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:A6","CH_BA94_6TEC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:HSI-04","text":"Objects are stable only as identities maintained across successive determinations, while motion names an empirically given alteration within the forms of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:SD4","CH_BA94_8ELU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:HSI-05","text":"Cognition proceeds by regression from empirical determination to its transcendental condition and terminates at an unknowable limit rather than an external temporal standpoint.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS6","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from temporal form, through phenomenal change, to the closure of pure sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is coherent only if phenomenal objectivity remains distinct from absolute reality and subjective form remains distinct from private psychology.","refs":["CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT2","CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where it converts the apparent incompatibility of temporal ideality and empirical change into a reciprocal structure of formal condition and phenomenal validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:ME1","CH_BA94_8ELU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined regarding the relation between appearance and the thing in itself, and the spatial line analogy remains inadequate to temporal non-coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_8ELU:MRT4","CH_BA94_7CAC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_05:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when transcendental priority is followed by phenomenal validation, ontological restriction, and taxonomic closure; the limit of the thing in itself remains irreducible to positive determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_6TEC:FGS6","CH_BA94_8ELU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation.","original_title":"Chapters 13–15","original_context":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation."},"original_title":"Chapters 13–15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":6,"parents":["CH_BA94_9GRT","CH_BA94_1CTS","CH_BA94_ILG0"],"revision":"CL_BA94_06@R001","sha256":"a1f0d3da8e55fa0b718665609939c258030627342af06d1c27def43c18def1c1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-01","text":"Necessary cognition of sensible objects must be explained without converting appearances into things in themselves or allowing conceptual thought to operate independently of sensible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AS1","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-02","text":"Pure intuition supplies non-empirical content under spatial and temporal forms, while understanding converts sensible givenness into cognition through conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS3","CH_BA94_ILG0:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-03","text":"The cooperation required for cognition presupposes the non-interchangeability of sensibility and understanding and therefore generates the division between æsthetic and logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-04","text":"The same a priori conditions that make necessary cognition possible also restrict its objective validity to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:SD4","CH_BA94_9GRT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-05","text":"Empirical objectivity arises from the synthesis of conditioned givenness and conceptual thought, not from access to an independently constituted noumenal object.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D4","CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-06","text":"The movement is cumulative and recursive: delimitation of sensible cognition closes the æsthetic only by reopening the problem at the level of understanding and logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS5","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-07","text":"Purification operates differently across the two faculties: æsthetic isolates a priori forms from sensation, while logic isolates formal laws from object-content and psychology.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AS-08","text":"The cluster closes provisionally with a dual-source architecture of bounded cognition while leaving the determinate connection between intuitions and conceptions unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires both receptive givenness and spontaneous conceptual thought; neither faculty produces cognition alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Space and time are a priori forms through which objects are given only as phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Pure intuition contributes a priori content beyond a given conception and thereby makes synthetic a priori judgment possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS3","CH_BA94_1CTS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Necessity signifies the structure objects must exhibit under the conditions of experience rather than the constitution of objects in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:SD1","CH_BA94_9GRT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Faculty cooperation requires functional differentiation because sensibility and understanding cannot exchange their respective operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5","CH_BA94_ILG0:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Objective cognition requires both formally conditioned sensible presentation and conceptual determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D4","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Universality is condition-relative: sensible universality arises from shared forms of intuition, whereas logical universality arises through abstraction from object-differences and empirical circumstances.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] The constitution of objects independently of sensible conditions remains cognitively unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A3","CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Cognitive enabling and cognitive limitation are one structure because the conditions that generate validity also determine its boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:SD4","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Purity names distinct exclusions: pure intuition excludes empirical sensation while retaining non-conceptual form, whereas pure logic excludes object-content and empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:NX2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Pure general logic is an a priori canon of formal thought and cannot by itself extend cognition of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:AS5","CH_BA94_ILG0:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The subject conditions appearances while remaining available to itself only through temporally conditioned self-presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SD2","CH_BA94_9GRT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The dual-source architecture establishes the need for synthesis without yet explaining how determinate conceptions connect to intuitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Human finitude consists in the absence of both a self-sufficient cognitive faculty and purely intellectual access to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX1","CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-01","text":"Receptivity retains stable identity as the mode through which objects are given, but its function expands from explaining appearance to supplying one indispensable source of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A12","CH_BA94_ILG0:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-02","text":"Space and time mutate from forms delimiting phenomenal presentation into sources of synthetic content and boundaries governing the validity of judgments.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ME1","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-03","text":"Conception changes from an insufficient rival to pure intuition into the necessary spontaneous operation by which intuitive content becomes cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-04","text":"A priori necessity loses any apparent implication of unrestricted metaphysical reach and becomes evidence of cognition’s dependence on the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D3","CH_BA94_1CTS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-05","text":"Subjectivity reverses valence from a suspected source of illusion into the formal condition of empirical objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX8","CH_BA94_9GRT:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-06","text":"Universality migrates from shared sensible constitution to formal laws of every use of understanding, while in neither domain becoming knowledge of noumenal structure.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-07","text":"Purity recurs with altered function: in æsthetic it isolates a priori sensible form, while in logic it removes content and psychological contingency.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS2","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-08","text":"The model of sensibility as merely confused thought disappears after faculty differences are recoded as differences of cognitive origin and function.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-09","text":"Geometry ceases to be the terminal object of analysis and is absorbed as evidence that non-empirical intuition is required for necessary cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","CH_BA94_9GRT:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-10","text":"Original intellectual intuition performs a boundary-setting function and then recedes once finite cognition has been reconstructed through receptive intuition and conceptual spontaneity.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AS7","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RMD-11","text":"Relation changes from a mark of intuition’s inability to disclose intrinsic nature into the structured interface through which intuitive content and conceptions can produce cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A6","CH_BA94_1CTS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-01","text":"Geometry remains a local demonstration, but its upward-scaling function is to establish that necessity requires a priori intuition rather than empirical induction or conceptual analysis alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX5","CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-02","text":"Temporal self-affection remains a local account of self-intuition, while its cluster-level function is to show that even self-cognition falls under finite receptive conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A9","CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-03","text":"The theological contrast with original intuition remains illustrative and disappears once it has marked the boundary of finite cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX9","CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-04","text":"The detailed division of logic into general, particular, pure, and applied forms remains locally taxonomic, but its scalable principle is that validity depends on disciplined exclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT1","CH_BA94_ILG0:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-05","text":"The relation between pure intuition and conception bridges the æsthetic and logical units because it converts sensible form into a condition of synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:D1","CH_BA94_ILG0:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-06","text":"The conclusion of the æsthetic catalyzes the transition by turning a solution to synthetic a priori cognition into a demand to distinguish sensible contribution from conceptual operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:D4","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-07","text":"Applied logic remains a local empirical discipline, but its exclusion from pure logic clarifies the cluster-wide distinction between conditions of validity and conditions of actual human performance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A12","CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-08","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding scales upward because it organizes both the possibility of cognition and the architecture of the sciences that investigate it.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A7","CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:CGD-09","text":"The clarity-based model becomes obsolete because no increase in empirical distinctness can cross the difference between phenomenal givenness and noumenal constitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D1","CH_BA94_9GRT:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-01","text":"Sensibility cannot be reduced to confused intellectual cognition of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-02","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition cannot arise from concepts alone or from empirical intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX5","CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-03","text":"A priori validity does not authorize application beyond sensible objects and possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:NX3","CH_BA94_1CTS:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-04","text":"Neither receptivity nor spontaneity can constitute cognition as a self-sufficient faculty.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:NX1","CH_BA94_ILG0:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-05","text":"Formal logic cannot function as an organon for producing material knowledge of determinate objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:NX7","CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-06","text":"Psychological descriptions of thinking cannot establish the normative validity of logical laws.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:NX6","CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-07","text":"The subjective ideality of sensible forms cannot be equated with the unreality of empirical objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX8","CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-08","text":"Finite human cognition cannot possess original intellectual intuition of either object or self.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX7","CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:GNX-09","text":"Intersubjective agreement cannot establish that spatial and temporal predicates belong to things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX4","CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-01","text":"The architecture collapses if either sensibility or understanding is treated as a complete and independent source of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX1","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-02","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any direct realism that transfers spatial, temporal, or relational predicates from appearances to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX6","CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-03","text":"The architecture excludes a rationalism in which conceptual analysis generates the synthetic content required for knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:NX1","CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-04","text":"The architecture excludes psychologism because contingent mental occurrence cannot ground universally valid formal laws.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A13","CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-05","text":"The architecture excludes logical omnipotence because formal correctness cannot supply object-givenness or determine material truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:D1","CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:DBN-06","text":"The architecture cannot admit a transparent, unconditioned subject because self-presentation is temporal and cognition remains divided between receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX3","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSA-01","text":"No unconditioned standpoint is available from which sensible forms, conceptual laws, and objects in themselves could be compared at once.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX1","CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSA-02","text":"Original intuition remains the constitutive absence against which receptive and derivative cognition becomes intelligible.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX2","CH_BA94_9GRT:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSA-03","text":"A direct correspondence between representation and noumenal object is inadmissible because every available comparison already occurs within sensible and conceptual conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSA-04","text":"No logic can simultaneously remain universally formal, materially productive, and psychologically concrete.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX3","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSA-05","text":"Mediation-free unity is unavailable, yet the system must seek unity through synthesis without erasing the difference between the faculties being synthesized.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX3","CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-01","text":"Subjective forms can ground objective cognition only when the objects given through those forms are conceptually determined.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-01","CL_BA94_06:GA-02","CL_BA94_06:GA-06","CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-02","text":"Mathematical necessity is neither empirical generalization nor conceptual analysis, yet it becomes cognition of objects only through the cooperation of pure intuition and conception.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-01","CL_BA94_06:GA-03","CL_BA94_06:GA-04","CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","CH_BA94_ILG0:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-03","text":"The separation of cognitive faculties is not opposed to their unity but is the condition under which their cooperation can be specified without reduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-01","CL_BA94_06:GA-05","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5","CH_BA94_ILG0:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-04","text":"Sensible universality and logical universality are distinct products of conditioning and abstraction, so neither can be converted into unrestricted ontological universality.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-07","CL_BA94_06:GA-08","CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-05","text":"Empirical objectivity cannot be secured by subjective form alone or by formal thought alone; it requires their ordered synthesis around a genuinely given phenomenon.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-02","CL_BA94_06:GA-06","CL_BA94_06:GA-12","CH_BA94_9GRT:D4","CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-06","text":"Pure intuition extends cognition beyond conceptual analysis precisely by restricting the resulting extension to objects that can be sensibly given.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-03","CL_BA94_06:GA-04","CL_BA94_06:GA-09","CH_BA94_1CTS:D1","CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-07","text":"The shared term “pure” conceals an asymmetry: pure intuition retains a priori material relative to concepts, whereas pure logic attains universality by removing object-content.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-10","CL_BA94_06:GA-11","CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:XD-08","text":"Completion of the æsthetic generates rather than resolves the problem of mediation, because it identifies both necessary sources without explaining their determinate connection.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-05","CL_BA94_06:GA-13","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes condition-relative without becoming arbitrary: its necessity follows from universal cognitive conditions rather than from noumenal access.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SD4","CH_BA94_1CTS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-02","text":"Finitude becomes productive because receptive limitation supplies the field within which synthetic extension, empirical reality, and conceptual cognition are possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A12","CH_BA94_1CTS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-03","text":"The subject is neither a passive recorder nor an original creator of objects, but a divided condition of cognition that receives appearances and thinks them.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A3","CH_BA94_9GRT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-04","text":"A priori necessity becomes diagnostic of the knower’s constitutive contribution rather than proof of access to reality independent of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX1","CH_BA94_9GRT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-05","text":"Transcendental inquiry acquires a possibility-and-domain method in which every account of how cognition is possible must also specify where its validity ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:ME4","CH_BA94_1CTS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-06","text":"Cognitive disciplines are organized by selective purification, but their isolated purity remains subordinate to the composite act of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-07","text":"The settlement of sensible conditions necessarily produces a further problem concerning the rules and acts by which understanding determines what sensibility gives.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:ALT3","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:BSD-08","text":"Self-cognition is doubly mediated because the subject must first be temporally given to itself and then conceptually determined as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A9","CH_BA94_ILG0:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned givenness produces empirical objectivity; attempted conversion of its conditions into noumenal predicates produces illusion; transcendental delimitation returns cognition to conditioned givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AS6","CH_BA94_9GRT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-02","text":"Faculty separation enables cooperation, cooperation demonstrates the necessity of both faculties, and that necessity reinforces their functional separation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5","CH_BA94_ILG0:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-03","text":"Purification secures universality by excluding content, but exclusion makes pure logic materially empty and therefore returns cognition to the need for sensible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:SD2","CH_BA94_ILG0:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-04","text":"Geometrical necessity escalates into pure intuition, pure intuition into experiential restriction, and experiential restriction into the logical problem of conceptual determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AS4","CH_BA94_1CTS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-05","text":"Subjective conditioning generates the threat of illusion, but analysis reverses that threat by locating illusion in the objectification of subjective conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX8","CH_BA94_9GRT:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-06","text":"Bounded objectivity functions as the principal attractor: every route to valid cognition converges on necessity within possible experience and indeterminacy beyond it.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SD1","CH_BA94_1CTS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-07","text":"“Pure” becomes a generative operator that isolates a priori sensible form in one domain and universally formal thought in another.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:RLE-08","text":"The architecture escalates from the forms of appearance to the duality of faculties and then to the division and purification of the sciences governing those faculties.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AF1","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible affection → a priori spatial-temporal formatting → relational presentation → phenomenon.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT2","CH_BA94_9GRT:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B02","text":"Pure intuition + conception → a priori intuitive excess joined to conceptual unity → synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:A4","CH_BA94_1CTS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B03","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment → necessary validity for sensible objects → restriction to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS4","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B04","text":"Phenomenal givenness + conceptual spontaneity → cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A1","CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B05","text":"Non-interchangeable cognitive functions → separation of æsthetic and logic → coordinated dual-source architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:FGS2","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B06","text":"Abstraction from object-content and psychology → pure general logic → universally formal canon without material extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:AS4","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FGS-B07","text":"Independent purification of intuition and thought → exposure of their mutual insufficiency → unresolved demand for determinate synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B01","text":"Phenomenal limitation → rejection of sensibility as confused noumenal cognition → isolation of a priori sensible form.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX1","CH_BA94_9GRT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B02","text":"Geometrical necessity → exclusion of empirical and conceptual sources → pure intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A7","CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B03","text":"Subjective ideality → empirical reality of appearances → prohibition on noumenal extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ME2","CH_BA94_1CTS:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B04","text":"Synthetic a priori possibility → intuitive content beyond concepts → validity bounded by possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:D1","CH_BA94_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B05","text":"Bounded sensible cognition → distinction of receptivity and spontaneity → necessary faculty cooperation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:AFM-B06","text":"Faculty cooperation → division of æsthetic and logic → purification of formal thought → unresolved mediation of intuition and conception.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:AS2","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence with independently constituted objects into validity for phenomena jointly given and thought under universal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ME2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-02","text":"The a priori is reclassified from unrestricted independence of experience into the formal constitution of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:D5","CH_BA94_1CTS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-03","text":"Subjectivity is reclassified from distortion into a constitutive condition that can ground empirical reality without determining noumenal being.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SD4","CH_BA94_9GRT:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-04","text":"The opposition between faculty separation and cognitive unity collapses because unity becomes possible only through differentiated contributions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:ME5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-05","text":"Logic is reclassified from a general psychology of thinking into a formal canon whose universality depends on excluding empirical mental conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:ME2","CH_BA94_ILG0:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-06","text":"Universality divides into transcendental universality within shared sensible conditions and formal universality abstracted from every object-domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MME-07","text":"Cognitive boundaries cease to be external prohibitions and become internal consequences of the same conditions that produce valid cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:SD4","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system coordinates space, time, geometry, synthetic a priori judgments, sensibility, understanding, and the divisions of logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT1","CH_BA94_ILG0:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, cognition is a dual-source operation in which a priori sensible formatting supplies givenness and spontaneous conception supplies thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry shifts from asking what objects are independently to determining the conditions, scope, and exclusions governing their possible cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:ALT3","CH_BA94_1CTS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality for finite knowers is phenomenal, the self appears through temporal affection, and noumenal constitution remains indeterminate.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D5","CH_BA94_9GRT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, successive acts of purification delimit sensibility and logic separately while the transition between them performs the necessity of their eventual synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS5","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-01","text":"The inference from relational intuition to unknowable intrinsic constitution remains asserted more strongly than it is independently established.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-02","text":"Receptivity presupposes affection by objects even though the constitution of those objects independently of sensibility is declared unknown.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A12","CH_BA94_9GRT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-03","text":"The subject is passive in temporal self-affection yet active in apprehension and conceptual thought, without the relation between these roles being fully explained.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT5","CH_BA94_ILG0:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-04","text":"Faculty cooperation is architecturally indispensable, but the determinate connection between conceptions and intuitions remains underdescribed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-05","text":"Pure logic claims universal normative authority while its abstraction from content prevents it from determining material truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:D5","CH_BA94_ILG0:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:FL-06","text":"The forms are universal for humanity, but their scope across all possible finite knowers remains undecidable.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MP-01","text":"Sensibility mutates from a faculty defined primarily by limitation into a positive source of the a priori content required for synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:NX1","CH_BA94_1CTS:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MP-02","text":"Subjective form mutates from an apparent threat to reality into the condition that distinguishes empirical objectivity from illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:D6","CH_BA94_9GRT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MP-03","text":"Purity mutates from the isolation of non-empirical intuitive form into the exclusion of all object-content and psychological conditions from logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:NX2","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MP-04","text":"Universality mutates from validity for every human sensibility into validity for every formal use of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MP-05","text":"The conclusion of the æsthetic mutates from closure into transition because delimiting intuitive cognition necessitates a separate account of conceptual thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:AS5","CH_BA94_ILG0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a two-stage interface in which affection is spatially and temporally formatted before spontaneous thought determines the given as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A2","CH_BA94_ILG0:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided gate rather than a sovereign spectator: it conditions appearance, receives itself temporally, and thinks what it cannot originally create or intuit intellectually.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:SD2","CH_BA94_9GRT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is the medium of finite self-appearance rather than a self-subsistent container belonging to reality independently of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:A9","CH_BA94_9GRT:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible through disciplined boundary maintenance: forms must not be objectified, faculties must not be conflated, and their cooperation must not be replaced by either faculty alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:AS6","CH_BA94_ILG0:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from phenomenal limitation through synthetic a priori possibility to the dual-source architecture of cognition and the delimitation of logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT1","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the point where determinate conceptions must be connected to intuitions and particular judgments.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if faculty distinction, faculty cooperation, and domain restriction are treated as mutually supporting rather than mutually cancelling operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT2","CH_BA94_ILG0:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it makes cognitive possibility and cognitive limitation consequences of the same a priori conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_1CTS:SD4","CH_BA94_1CTS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on the sequence from purified sensibility to purified logic because each isolated faculty exposes the insufficiency that requires the other.","refs":["CH_BA94_ILG0:D1","CH_BA94_ILG0:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_06:MRT-B06","text":"Geometry, theological contrast, and the detailed taxonomy of applied logic are dispensable locally, but the unresolved act of synthesis remains resistant to complete axiomatic reduction at this layer.","refs":["CH_BA94_9GRT:MRT8","CH_BA94_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General.","original_title":"Chapters 16–18","original_context":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General."},"original_title":"Chapters 16–18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":7,"parents":["CH_BA94_ITL0","CH_BA94_IDGL","CH_BA94_IDTL"],"revision":"CL_BA94_07@R001","sha256":"f8f32156cce8dd59643c60d5bdb736055b9969804fea91c4b48de47099408f6d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B01","text":"Logic is necessary for assessing cognition yet cannot, through universal formal laws alone, establish the objective truth it assesses.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS5","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B02","text":"The system separates source-indifferent formal logic from transcendental inquiry into the a priori relation of pure thought to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:AS1","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B03","text":"Transcendental status is reassigned from bare a priori possession to the possibility and valid use of representations in relation to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B04","text":"The universal demand for a material truth-criterion defeats itself because universality requires abstraction from the object-relative content that material truth requires.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS2","CH_BA94_IDGL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B05","text":"Transcendental analytic supplies the pure conditions under which an object can be thought, but intuition and experience must supply the object to which those conditions apply.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B06","text":"Dialectic begins whenever formal or pure conditions are converted from conditions of possible object-cognition into independent instruments of object-production.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS6","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive: abstraction establishes formal necessity, insufficiency exposes the need for object-reference, and the renewed attempt to exceed that reference generates illusion requiring critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:D6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AS-B08","text":"Closure is not a positive possession of universal truth but the restoration of pure principles to their bounded canon of empirical judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Formal coherence is a necessary negative condition of cognition but cannot establish agreement between cognition and object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDGL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires pure conceptual conditions and objects given through intuition or experience; neither factor produces objective knowledge alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:AS2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Content is internally divided: empirical content is excluded from the pure science, while intuitively supplied matter remains indispensable to the objective use of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Transcendentality concerns the a priori possibility, scope, and object-directed use of cognition rather than the mere a priori origin of a representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A7","CH_BA94_ITL0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Analytic and dialectic distinguish legitimate and illegitimate uses of cognitive principles rather than separate inventories of propositions or faculties.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SD1","CH_BA94_IDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The canon–organon distinction governs the system: a rule may regulate already given cognition without authorizing the production of objects or objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SD2","CH_BA94_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Dialectic is the critique of illusion generated when valid formal or transcendental conditions are detached from the conditions of their application.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A11","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the functional boundary at which pure thought can retain objective validity and beyond which it becomes empty or hyperphysical.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SD3","CH_BA94_IDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Critique culminates in policing the legitimacy, scope, and limits of pure principles rather than extending cognition through them.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SD4","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Truth remains defined through cognition’s accordance with objects, while no universal positive criterion can certify that accordance.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A1","CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Formal logic retains its role as the universal examination of relations among cognitions, but its universality remains inseparable from its formal limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A2","CH_BA94_IDGL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Content changes from what general logic brackets to what transcendental inquiry must distinguish between excluded empirical material and required intuitive matter.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:SD2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-03","text":"[Displacement] Object-relation moves from the factor abstracted by general logic to the criterion of transcendental validity and finally to intuition-conditioned objective cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:AS1","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Dialectic changes from the appearance of formal knowledge-production into a critical name for the misuse of formal and transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS6","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-05","text":"[Escalation] The distinction between universal and sensible application expands into the boundary between possible experience and hyperphysical object-claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A11","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] Inquiry into cognitive origin is retained only after being subordinated to the broader question of how pure cognition acquires valid object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] A logic of truth survives only as a conditional analytic of possible object-cognition, not as an unrestricted truth-producing discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:AS3","CH_BA94_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The case of space applied universally or only to objects of sense remains a local demonstration of scope-sensitive transcendental status.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The distinction between pure and empirical thought remains a local taxonomy until it is converted into the current-level distinction between pure conditions and empirical object-givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A4","CH_BA94_ITL0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-03","text":"[Scales] The difference between formal consistency and objective validity becomes global because it governs both general logic and transcendental analytic.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A8","CH_BA94_IDTL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Object-reference links the inquiry into transcendental use with the requirement that objects be given in intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A15","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-05","text":"[Catalyst] The canon–organon distinction drives the transition from legitimate logical testing to the diagnosis of dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SD2","CH_BA94_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-06","text":"[Recoded] Cognitive origin no longer functions as a sufficient ground of transcendental status but as one condition requiring validation through scope and use.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D2","CH_BA94_ITL0:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] A logic of truth understood as an unqualified source of truth is superseded by a bounded analytic whose validity depends on possible object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:AS3","CH_BA94_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-01","text":"Formal accordance with logical laws does not constitute material or objective truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-02","text":"A representation does not become transcendental merely by being a priori.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-03","text":"Pure cognition is not self-sufficient when no intuition supplies an object for its concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-04","text":"No universal criterion can establish the material truth of all object-directed cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-05","text":"General logic is not an organon for extending knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-06","text":"Transcendental logic is not an unrestricted organon for the universal exercise of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:GNX-07","text":"Dialectic is not a productive art for generating metaphysical assertions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes self-authorizing logic: no form of cognition becomes knowledge of objects merely by satisfying its own rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes the production of objective matter by formal thought, however pure, rigorous, or universally valid that thought may be.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes an ontology in which transcendental distinctions classify objects independently of cognition’s conditions of use.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:DBN-04","text":"The possibility of thinking an object does not entail the possibility of knowing, encountering, or determining it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:DBN-05","text":"Critique cannot evade metaphysical overreach by becoming an alternative metaphysical organon.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSA-01","text":"A domain of objectivity independent of intuition and possible experience is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSA-02","text":"A universally available positive mark of material truth is unavailable in principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSA-03","text":"No direct transition from subjective logical form to objectively valid cognition is admitted.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSA-04","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the system is immediate access to objects through pure thought without mediating conditions of application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-01","text":"General and transcendental logic form nested restrictions: the first prevents formal contradiction, while the second regulates whether pure principles can be objectively used within experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_07:GA-09","CH_BA94_IDGL:AS5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-02","text":"Dialectical illusion has a repeated structure across levels: general logic falsely claims to yield objects from form, while pure understanding falsely claims to yield objects beyond possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-05","CL_BA94_07:GA-07","CL_BA94_07:GA-08","CH_BA94_IDGL:AS6","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-03","text":"The impossibility of a universal material truth-criterion prevents transcendental analytic from functioning as a universal producer of truth; its logic of truth is conditional upon possible object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-08","CL_BA94_07:GA-10","CH_BA94_IDGL:NX1","CH_BA94_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-04","text":"Object-reference divides into a priori conditions of applicability and empirical fulfillment through intuition, so transcendental validity is neither reducible to empirical derivation nor separable from empirical givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-03","CL_BA94_07:GA-04","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-05","text":"Critique has a double function: it preserves formal and pure principles as necessary conditions while withdrawing every claim that those principles independently generate objective knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_07:GA-09","CH_BA94_IDGL:D2","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:XD-06","text":"Universality itself becomes a trigger for self-limitation because abstraction from objects secures formal reach only by requiring a separate inquiry into valid object-directed use.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-04","CL_BA94_07:GA-10","CH_BA94_IDGL:AS2","CH_BA94_ITL0:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-01","text":"Validation becomes three-tiered: formal non-contradiction excludes incoherence, transcendental principles condition object-thought, and intuition supplies the object for which validity is claimed.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-08","CH_BA94_IDGL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-02","text":"Error becomes stratified into formal contradiction, material discordance, and dialectical illusion produced by the illegitimate extension of valid principles.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-07","CH_BA94_IDGL:SD3","CH_BA94_IDTL:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-03","text":"Critique acquires regulatory rather than certifying authority: it can delimit the valid use of cognition without yielding a universal material certificate of truth.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_07:GA-09","CL_BA94_07:GA-10","CH_BA94_IDGL:NX4","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-04","text":"Objects are reconfigured as cognitively available only under conditions of possible intuition and experience, not as self-certifying termini for formal thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-08","CH_BA94_ITL0:A12","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-05","text":"Universality becomes a negative achievement: formal laws govern every cognition precisely because they cannot supply the particular object-relative content of material truth.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:GA-10","CH_BA94_IDGL:SD1","CH_BA94_IDGL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysical error is reclassified from mere false content into a misuse of otherwise valid formal or transcendental rules.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-05","CL_BA94_07:GA-07","CH_BA94_IDTL:ME3","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-01","text":"Formal law functions as a negative test, is misrecognized as an organon, produces the semblance of knowledge, and is restored through dialectical critique to canon-status.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A6","CH_BA94_IDGL:A10","CH_BA94_IDGL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-02","text":"Pure principles make object-thought possible, detach from intuition, generate empty synthetic judgments, and are returned to empirical use by transcendental dialectic.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7","CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-03","text":"Object-relation is first abstracted to produce universal form, then reintroduced as the condition that exposes formal universality as insufficient for objective cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:AS1","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-04","text":"The canon–organon distinction is the system’s primary conceptual attractor because it converts every question of logical power into a question of authorized use.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SD2","CH_BA94_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-05","text":"Content is recursively repartitioned: empirical content is excluded from pure analysis, yet empirical givenness returns as the indispensable condition of pure concepts’ objective application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:SD2","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:RLE-06","text":"Critique closes through constrained return rather than final expansion: it restores principles to empirical judgment while preserving the temptation to exceed that boundary as dialectic’s recurrent object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A10","CH_BA94_IDTL:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B01","text":"Object-related content is abstracted from cognition, leaving general logic with universally applicable formal relations and no material criterion of truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:AS1","CH_BA94_IDGL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B02","text":"Pure object-directed cognition is isolated, and transcendental inquiry asks how its a priori relation to objects can be possible and valid.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A5","CH_BA94_ITL0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B03","text":"A priori origin is tested through possible use and scope of application, so transcendental status becomes a critical relation rather than an intrinsic property.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D2","CH_BA94_ITL0:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B04","text":"Pure concepts provide conditions for thinking objects, intuition gives those concepts objects, and transcendental analytic becomes a bounded logic of truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A4","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2","CH_BA94_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B05","text":"Formal or pure principles are detached from their object-giving conditions, converting a canon of judgment into an apparent organon of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:D4","CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B06","text":"Dialectic identifies the detachment as illusion and blocks the conversion of conditions of thought into unrestricted claims about objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:D6","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FGS-B07","text":"The machine closes by preserving pure principles as conditions of valid empirical judgment while denying them independent object-producing force.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B01","text":"Formal abstraction establishes a universal negative test of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B02","text":"Universal formal reach removes the object-related content required for material truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS2","CH_BA94_IDGL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B03","text":"Transcendental inquiry restores the question of a priori object-directed applicability without reintroducing empirical content as its constitutive material.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D5","CH_BA94_ITL0:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B04","text":"Transcendental analytic identifies the pure conditions of object-thought but subordinates their objective validity to intuition and possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B05","text":"The attempt to treat formal or pure conditions as unrestricted object-producing powers generates dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A10","CH_BA94_IDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:AFM-B06","text":"Critical restriction returns both logical and transcendental principles to their proper role as canons of valid empirical judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:A9","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-01","text":"Logic is redistributed into distinct regimes of formal consistency, transcendental validity, and empirical object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:ME1","CH_BA94_IDTL:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-02","text":"Transcendentality is reclassified from a property of isolated representations into a relation of possible objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:ME1","CH_BA94_ITL0:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-03","text":"Experience becomes a functional boundary of valid cognition rather than merely an external source of empirical material.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SD3","CH_BA94_IDTL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-04","text":"Dialectic becomes a diagnostic of rule-misuse rather than a body of false propositions or a technique for generating knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:ME3","CH_BA94_IDTL:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-05","text":"The opposition between rigor and illusion collapses because rigorously valid forms can generate sophistical appearance when their scope is ignored.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:ME5","CH_BA94_IDGL:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MME-06","text":"Critical method is reorganized as a sequence of formal testing, transcendental delimitation, and assessment of possible object-givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT5","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system divides general from transcendental logic and analytic from dialectic.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT1","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, cognition requires the conjunction of formal principles, object-directed applicability, and intuitively supplied material.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:ALT2","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique regulates the use of principles by separating legitimate canons from illegitimate organons.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:ALT3","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological level, objects are cognitively available only under conditions of possible intuition and experience, while formal thought alone lacks objective reach.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT4","CH_BA94_IDGL:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the system performs its own claim by repeatedly abstracting, exposing the insufficiency of abstraction, and restoring bounded object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:FGS2","CH_BA94_IDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FL-01","text":"Pure analysis excludes empirical content while objective validity requires intuition and experience; the distinction between methodological isolation and valid application remains compressed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:NX8","CH_BA94_IDTL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FL-02","text":"Transcendental analytic is called a logic of truth even though no universal positive criterion of material truth is available, making its truth-function necessarily conditional.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:AS3","CH_BA94_IDGL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FL-03","text":"Possible experience serves as the decisive boundary of objectivity, but the relation among intuition, experience, and objects that may never be given is left underdifferentiated.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:ME5","CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:FL-04","text":"The science is defined by determining objective validity while the validity of its own pure principles is still the matter requiring critical establishment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:A14","CH_BA94_IDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MP-01","text":"Content mutates from an element excluded by general and pure logic into the necessary intuitive matter through which pure concepts acquire objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MP-02","text":"Truth mutates from correspondence with objects into a bounded analytic status whose validity depends on the conditions of possible object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A1","CH_BA94_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MP-03","text":"Dialectic mutates from the misuse of general logical form into the critique of hyperphysical extension by pure understanding and reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:A11","CH_BA94_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MP-04","text":"The canon mutates from a merely formal negative test into a transcendental regulator of the empirical use of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:AS5","CH_BA94_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:HSI-01","text":"The system images cognition as a gated circuit in which rules authorize thought only when an object can enter through intuition and possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:AS2","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not an autonomous producer of objects but a bounded user of formal and pure principles whose validity depends on conditions it does not generate.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX4","CH_BA94_IDTL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:HSI-03","text":"Reality is not an independently accessible metaphysical field but the field of objects available under the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX2","CH_BA94_IDTL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:HSI-04","text":"The operative horizon is modal rather than merely formal: possible experience, not universal logical reach, determines where object-claims remain valid.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:SD1","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in retaining necessary rules while refusing their self-expansion into an organon of unrestricted cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:D2","CH_BA94_IDTL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms sufficiently reconstruct the transition from formal logic to transcendental analytic and the critical diagnosis of dialectical misuse.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT1","CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive source and validation of the object-information that objective judgment requires.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT4","CH_BA94_IDGL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if excluded empirical content is distinguished from empirically given objects required for the valid application of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITL0:D5","CH_BA94_IDTL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it derives the limits of logical and transcendental claims from the very abstractions and principles that make those claims possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT3","CH_BA94_IDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_07:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when the recurring conversion of canon into organon, and its critical reversal, remains dynamic rather than a static distinction.","refs":["CH_BA94_IDGL:MRT7","CH_BA94_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","original_title":"Chapters 19–21","original_context":"II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic."},"original_title":"Chapters 19–21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_08","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 22–24","order":8,"parents":["CH_BA94_FDTL","CH_BA94_BIAC","CH_BA94_INT3"],"revision":"CL_BA94_08@R001","sha256":"616148434bb64dcc240ac64ef6cd07b61a5a84edbce9303083efe7be415974db","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-01","text":"The current-level system must establish a complete order of pure conceptions without relying on empirical accumulation or mechanically extended discovery.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS3","CH_BA94_INT3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-02","text":"The inquiry therefore shifts from inspecting conceptual products to reconstructing the faculty and principle that generate and order them.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AS2","CH_BA94_INT3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-03","text":"Pure understanding functions as the autonomous source of conceptual form, while experience supplies occasions for its development without becoming its sufficient origin.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS5","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-04","text":"Totality becomes the governing ground through which conceptions receive their places and the system determines its own completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS4","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-05","text":"Systematicity converts classification into a test of legitimacy by making the whole capable of validating its parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A12","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-06","text":"The movement is recursive: exclusion of accumulation produces a principle of totality, which organizes the elements whose systematic order then confirms the principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS2","CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AS-07","text":"The cluster closes around a self-contained but developmentally activated system whose completeness is asserted before its concrete inventory is fully exhibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX4","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Completeness is architectonic rather than the result of accumulated instances.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Pure understanding is a self-sufficient source of the forms under investigation.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The object of analysis shifts from given conceptual contents to the faculty and principle that generate them.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AS2","CH_BA94_INT3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The relevant conceptions must be pure, intellectual, and elementary rather than empirical or compound.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The governing connection both assigns each conception its proper place and determines the system’s completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A9","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Experience is recoded from possible origin into activating occasion, after which understanding removes empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The systematic whole functions as a reciprocal criterion for the legitimacy of its parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Similarity, quantity, and graded complexity cannot supply necessary systematic connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Pure conceptions are organized as expressions of an underlying unity that precedes their complete exhibition.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A6","CH_BA94_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental analysis culminates in an internally differentiated architecture of conceptions and principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Purity means abstraction from empirical conditions, not absolute separation from experiential activation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3","CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The system seeks closure without admitting an unassimilated remainder within pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-01","text":"[Persistence] Totality recurs first as the condition of completeness and then as the principle that orders the complete field.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Autonomy migrates from a property of pure understanding into an operation by which understanding generates, develops, and purifies conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A9","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-03","text":"[Reversal] The exclusion of the empirical as origin becomes a qualified inclusion of experience as an activating occasion.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:NX1","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Classification changes from inventory-making into derivational placement within a necessary order.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME3","CH_BA94_INT3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-05","text":"[Displacement] Elementarity is absorbed into a developmental account in which conceptual germs become explicit through activation and purification.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A7","CH_BA94_BIAC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] Systematic completeness shifts from a criterion used to test cognitions into an effect generated by the governing principle itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A12","CH_BA94_INT3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] The local division between conceptions and principles is absorbed into the larger relation between source, ordering rule, and complete system.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A13","CH_BA94_INT3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Mechanical discovery is not merely rejected; it is replaced by principled derivation as the only route to closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A2","CH_BA94_INT3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-01","text":"The division between conceptions and principles remains a lower-layer architectural specification; at cluster scale it becomes part of a broader generative relation between elements and ordering totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-02","text":"Autonomy, totality, completeness, and systematic validation scale upward because they organize all three input units rather than one local argument.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A11","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-03","text":"The relation between empirical occasion and conceptual purity bridges the units by converting an apparent opposition into a developmental sequence.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-04","text":"The decisive transition is from analysing given concepts to identifying the faculty or principle that makes their systematic order possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-05","text":"Open-ended accumulation, resemblance, and variable investigative effort remain local negative mechanisms that are superseded by the cluster’s principle of derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A3","CH_BA94_INT3:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:CGD-06","text":"The current-level architecture is not a larger inventory but a closure mechanism linking origin, purification, placement, and validation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SD1","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects empirical aggregation as a sufficient ground for completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-02","text":"It rejects ordinary content-analysis as an adequate account of the origin or necessity of pure conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-03","text":"It rejects resemblance and quantitative complexity as principles of necessary connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-04","text":"It rejects external supplementation as capable of completing pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-05","text":"It rejects an unconnected collection of cognitions as a system.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-06","text":"It rejects the validation of individual cognitions independently of their systematic position.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:GNX-07","text":"It rejects contingency at the foundation of transcendental classification.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:DBN-01","text":"The system must exclude the possibility that continued discovery can reveal its own endpoint.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:DBN-02","text":"It must exclude arbitrary subdivisions of the conceptual field.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:DBN-03","text":"It must exclude external material as the condition that completes or authorizes pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:DBN-04","text":"It must exclude the possibility that a mechanically accumulated collection already constitutes a determinate system.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:DBN-05","text":"It must exclude a neutral standpoint outside both understanding and its empirical occasions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSA-01","text":"The system circles the inadmissibility of an unassimilated remainder within the domain of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSA-02","text":"It forecloses the possibility that the domain of pure understanding is fundamentally unbounded.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSA-03","text":"It cannot allow experience to become either the complete origin or an irrelevant background to pure conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSA-04","text":"It disallows a foundation in which contingency remains constitutive of transcendental order.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable possibility is an open-ended conceptual field lacking an internally determinable boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-01","text":"Completeness becomes derivable rather than merely demanded when the totality principle is made responsible for both ordering the elements and determining the system’s boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-01","CL_BA94_08:GA-05","CH_BA94_FDTL:AS4","CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-02","text":"The developmental role of experience can be retained without opening the system to external completion because empirical occasions activate forms already assigned to understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-02","CL_BA94_08:GA-06","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-03","text":"The failure of mechanical discovery and the analysis of the faculty converge on a method that seeks the generator of conceptual order rather than a larger inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-03","CL_BA94_08:GA-08","CH_BA94_INT3:A2","CH_BA94_BIAC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-04","text":"Classification becomes a legitimacy test only when placement within a governing order is treated as constitutive of conceptual identity.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-05","CL_BA94_08:GA-07","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-05","text":"The cluster temporalizes an otherwise static doctrine of pure unity: conceptions originate in unity but become explicit through experiential occasions.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-06","CL_BA94_08:GA-09","CH_BA94_INT3:A6","CH_BA94_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-06","text":"The exclusion of external supplementation and the inclusion of empirical activation jointly produce a closed system with an internal developmental interface.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-02","CL_BA94_08:GA-06","CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX4","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:XD-07","text":"Whole-to-part validation generates a productive circularity: the system authenticates its elements through the totality that those elements help instantiate.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-05","CL_BA94_08:GA-07","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12","CH_BA94_INT3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-01","text":"The system transforms classification into a self-authenticating generative operation rather than a neutral act of arrangement.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-02","text":"Purity becomes an operation performed by understanding upon historically activated material, not a condition untouched by experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-03","text":"The system produces an anticipatory epistemic temporality in which totality is conceptually available before every element has been displayed.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-04","text":"The legitimacy of transcendental inquiry depends on preserving a closed source while allowing empirical development to expose its forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-05","text":"The knowing subject is reclassified from an observer of conceptual contents into the operator that distinguishes, purifies, places, and validates them.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:BSD-06","text":"Systematicity becomes the condition under which conceptual multiplicity can count as knowledge at all.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-01","text":"Incompleteness produces the demand for totality; totality organizes the elements; organized elements return as evidence for totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS4","CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-02","text":"Empirical occasion activates conceptual germs, purification removes empirical conditions, and the purified result reconfirms understanding as the source.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:D4","CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-03","text":"Mechanical discovery yields open-ended accumulation, whose failure escalates into the demand for a governing principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:AF1","CH_BA94_INT3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-04","text":"The presupposed unity generates connection, while connection retrospectively makes the unity legible as a systematic ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:FGS4","CH_BA94_INT3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-05","text":"The distinction between conceptions and principles becomes a feedback relation in which principles order concepts and concepts instantiate the system’s principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A13","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:RLE-06","text":"Closure is the principal attractor: every local distinction is directed toward eliminating an unassimilated remainder.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B01","text":"Variable discovery produces an open-ended field whose accumulation cannot determine its own boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B02","text":"The failure of accumulation redirects inquiry from conceptual products toward the faculty and principle that generate them.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B03","text":"A governing connection is posited to assign proper places within a unity that precedes complete exhibition.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B04","text":"Empirical occasions activate pure germs, after which understanding abstracts the empirical conditions from their developed expression.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A8","CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B05","text":"The governing principle generates both systematic division and the a priori criterion of completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FGS-B06","text":"The completed system tests the legitimacy of its elements, thereby converting circular validation into the engine of closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B01","text":"Mechanical discovery → open-ended accumulation → failure of determinate order.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B02","text":"Failure of accumulation → analysis of the understanding and demand for a governing principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AF2","CH_BA94_INT3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B03","text":"Governing principle → derivational placement of pure conceptions within an underlying unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:AF2","CH_BA94_INT3:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B04","text":"Pure conceptual germ + empirical occasion → developmental purification.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:AF4","CH_BA94_BIAC:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B05","text":"Purification → internally ordered system → a priori determination of completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:FGS7","CH_BA94_INT3:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:AFM-B06","text":"Systematic completeness → reciprocal validation of parts and whole → closure without external supplement.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:FGS4","CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-01","text":"Completeness is relocated from the quantity of discovered material to the architecture that determines its possible elements.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-02","text":"The boundary between pure understanding and experience becomes a functional interface rather than a simple exclusionary wall.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME2","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-03","text":"Classification is redrawn as derivation, placement, and legitimacy-testing rather than resemblance-based arrangement.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME3","CH_BA94_INT3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-04","text":"The object of inquiry becomes a generative faculty whose products cannot be understood apart from their conditions of production.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ME1","CH_BA94_BIAC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-05","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition collapses into a sequence of origin, activation, development, and purification.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SD3","CH_BA94_BIAC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MME-06","text":"The conceptual field is redrawn as a closed, internally articulated domain whose completeness is both presupposed and operationally produced.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SD3","CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit problem is the determination of pure, elementary conceptions and their complete order.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:AS1","CH_BA94_FDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] The operative mechanism is the relation among faculty, empirical occasion, governing principle, systematic placement, and whole-to-part validation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT2","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system claims methodological authority by replacing contingent collection with principled derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT3","CH_BA94_INT3:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Pure understanding is treated as an autonomous unity whose forms are temporally activated and purified through experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ALT4","CH_BA94_BIAC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The text performs closure by repeatedly excluding alternatives until systematic totality appears as the only admissible ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:NX7","CH_BA94_INT3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FL-01","text":"The system uses totality to construct the table while using the completed table to validate totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FL-02","text":"Pure understanding is declared self-sufficient even though empirical occasions are required for the development of its conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:MRT5","CH_BA94_FDTL:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FL-03","text":"The system excludes external supplementation but depends on a developmental process that appears to introduce material from outside pure form.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:DNX4","CH_BA94_BIAC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:FL-04","text":"Absolute unity and the single governing idea are asserted before their concrete content or derivation is exhibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MP-01","text":"Purity mutates from exclusion of experience into the active removal of empirical conditions from experience-activated conceptions.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:ME3","CH_BA94_BIAC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MP-02","text":"Completeness mutates from a demanded precondition into an effect generated by systematic connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME1","CH_BA94_INT3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MP-03","text":"Experience mutates from a rejected source into an activating occasion that makes pure conceptual germs explicit.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:NX2","CH_BA94_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MP-04","text":"Understanding mutates from an autonomous repository into the operator of generation, development, abstraction, and validation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SD2","CH_BA94_BIAC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a self-sealing architectonic machine that converts contingent manifestations into necessary systematic positions.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SD1","CH_BA94_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes an internal operator of conceptual differentiation and purification rather than a passive receiver of conceptual contents.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:A2","CH_BA94_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is recursive and anticipatory: the totality guides the exposition of elements, while later exposition retroactively confirms the totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX4","CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is not discovery of external material but navigation of internal relations, boundaries, purification, and systematic placement.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:ME3","CH_BA94_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from uncontrolled discovery through faculty-analysis and principled derivation to systematic closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:MRT1","CH_BA94_INT3:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between autonomous origin, empirical occasion, purification, and systematic validation.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at cluster scale, but concrete completeness remains underdetermined until the promised governing principle and table are exhibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_INT3:MRT5","CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MRT-B04","text":"The strongest structure is the recursive coupling of totality, derivation, and whole-to-part validation; the weakest point is the circular presupposition of the totality it must prove.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT6","CH_BA94_FDTL:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement, but its final closure depends on sequencing, recursive return, and the continued exclusion of an unassimilated remainder.","refs":["CH_BA94_FDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 22–24","context":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3","original_title":"Chapters 22–24","original_context":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3"},"original_title":"Chapters 22–24","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_09","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 25–27","order":9,"parents":["CH_BA94_SILU","CH_BA94_SILF","CH_BA94_SIPC"],"revision":"CL_BA94_09@R001","sha256":"94da17c07030175bab1b0466e04c9a4d5e4246c89833417f082f4dc5f44d8da7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS1","text":"The governing problem is how a finite understanding that lacks intuition can possess pure concepts with legitimate a priori application to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A2","CH_BA94_SIPC:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS2","text":"Human cognition is discursive because conceptual thought reaches objects only through representational mediation rather than intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A3","CH_BA94_SILU:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS3","text":"Cognition consequently requires a distributed cooperation in which sensibility gives a manifold, imagination synthesizes it, and understanding represents its necessary unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:D2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS4","text":"The argument becomes transcendental and architectonic by reducing the understanding’s activity to judgment and reconstructing conceptual thought through judgmental functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SILU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS5","text":"Abstraction from content yields a complete fourfold and triadic table of functions rather than a collection of isolated logical forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS2","CH_BA94_SILF:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS6","text":"Transcendental analysis then reopens distinctions that formal logic may suppress whenever inferential equivalence conceals differences in cognitive function.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A4","CH_BA94_SILF:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS7","text":"The decisive recursive transition identifies the function unifying representations in judgment with the function unifying the manifold of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS8","text":"This identity converts the closed table of judgment-functions into a finite inventory of original categories capable of regulated derivative expansion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AS9","text":"Closure is critical rather than absolute: the table restricts metaphysical predicate-making, but its authority remains conditional on an asserted correspondence whose proof is deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Human understanding is non-sensuous, non-intuitive, and therefore discursive.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A1","CH_BA94_SILU:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Receptivity supplies intuitions, while spontaneity supplies the conceptual activity required to think them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:D1","CH_BA94_SILU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] A concept is fundamentally a function that unifies diverse representations under a common representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Concepts become cognitively operative only in judgments, to which all acts of the understanding are reducible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A7","CH_BA94_SILU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Object-directed cognition by the understanding is necessarily mediated through an organized representational order.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SD1","CH_BA94_SILU:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Judgmental form can be abstracted from content and exhaustively ordered through a finite table of functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A1","CH_BA94_SILF:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Formal-logical equivalence does not entail transcendental identity, because cognitively distinct functions may have the same inferential use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A4","CH_BA94_SILF:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Cognition begins with synthesis rather than analysis and requires the ordered cooperation of sensibility, imagination, and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A4","CH_BA94_SIPC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The unifying function exercised in judgment also supplies necessary unity to the manifold given in intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The complete inventory of original categories corresponds exactly to the complete inventory of logical judgment-functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Categories apply to objects only as necessary functions for synthesizing an intuitive manifold into object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD1","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Primitive conceptual closure is compatible with indefinite regulated expansion through derivative predicables.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Cognitive completeness requires articulated exclusions as well as positive connections, while taxonomic exclusion protects the primitive inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:SD4","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Formal conditions of coherent cognition cannot be converted into properties of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX11","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] The table functions as an architectonic operator that inventories primitive concepts, regulates derivation, and organizes rational science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD3","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-01","text":"The exclusion of intellectual intuition retains stable identity throughout and supplies the permanent boundary of finite conceptual cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A2","CH_BA94_SILU:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-02","text":"Function mutates from the arrangement of representations under a common representation into the necessary synthetic unity of an intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-03","text":"Judgment retains its operational identity while expanding from the use of concepts into the source structure for object-directed synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-04","text":"The judgment table changes function from a taxonomy of propositions into a generative table of transcendental concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:SD1","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-05","text":"The fourfold division and its triadic momenta retain formal identity while migrating from judgmental classification to categorical inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-06","text":"Singular and infinite judgment perform local differentiating work but are absorbed into the completed table once its systematic source-function is established.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS4","CH_BA94_SIPC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-07","text":"Negative determination escalates from the limitation of a cognitive field into the critical exclusion of false categories and predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-08","text":"Analysis undergoes reversal: initially available as a logical procedure, it becomes subordinate to a synthesis that must already have unified its material.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX2","CH_BA94_SIPC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-09","text":"Disjunctive reciprocal exclusion is displaced into the category of community, where logical division becomes reciprocal coordination among substances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-10","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection reverse valence from apparent transcendental predicates into formal criteria for the consistency of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A15","CH_BA94_SIPC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-11","text":"Completeness expands from the exhibition of understanding-functions into a condition governing both the category inventory and the architectonic of rational science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A14","CH_BA94_SIPC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RMD-12","text":"Intuition retains immediate object-relation but is displaced from an isolated cognitive mode into the manifold content required by transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between singular and universal judgment remains local machinery for separating cognitive quantity from inferential equivalence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-02","text":"The detailed definitions of infinite, hypothetical, disjunctive, and modal judgments remain local analyses whose higher-level role is to secure the table’s internal differentiation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A6","CH_BA94_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-03","text":"The reduction of conceptual use to judgment scales upward because it makes logical classification a reconstruction of the entire understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SILU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-04","text":"The complete table bridges the units by transforming a promised enumeration of understanding-functions into the source-table for categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS7","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-05","text":"The identity of unification in judgment and intuition is the principal transition catalyst because it converts logical form into transcendental applicability.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-06","text":"The model of analysis as an origin of conceptual content becomes obsolete once synthesis is established as the prior condition of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A3","CH_BA94_SIPC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-07","text":"General logic is recoded from a self-sufficient classifier into a formally indispensable but materially incomplete component of transcendental inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIPC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-08","text":"Community and the primitive status of third categories remain local pressure tests of the broader claim that every category is generated systematically from judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A12","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-09","text":"The programmatic demand for a classification of judgment-functions is fulfilled at this scale by a complete inventory of original categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:CGD-10","text":"Detailed category definitions and the full proof of objective validity remain deliberately deferred and therefore do not belong to the achieved cluster-level closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX12","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-01","text":"The understanding is neither sensuous nor capable of intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:NX1","CH_BA94_SILU:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-02","text":"A concept cannot relate immediately to an object or become operative outside judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:NX4","CH_BA94_SILU:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-03","text":"Analysis cannot generate the conceptual content on which it operates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-04","text":"Receptive intuition and imaginative synthesis are individually insufficient for cognition in the proper sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX3","CH_BA94_SIPC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-05","text":"General logic cannot by itself supply transcendental content or pure concepts applicable to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX1","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-06","text":"The category inventory cannot be empirical, rhapsodic, inherited, or indefinitely revisable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX5","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-07","text":"Inferential equivalence cannot erase distinctions in the cognitive quantity or transcendental function of judgments.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:NX2","CH_BA94_SILF:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-08","text":"Logical affirmation cannot be equated with positive determination of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:DNX2","CH_BA94_SILF:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-09","text":"Modal status cannot be treated as material content or as a property added independently to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:NX8","CH_BA94_SILF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:GNX-10","text":"Logical criteria such as unity, truth, and perfection cannot enlarge the category table or function as properties of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX10","CH_BA94_SIPC:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any model in which objects alone provide the unity required for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:DNX3","CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-02","text":"It excludes a model in which pure concepts acquire determinate cognitive content independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-03","text":"It excludes both the reduction of logical forms to merely subjective classifications and their elevation into self-sufficient sources of content.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX3","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-04","text":"Architectonic completeness presupposes that the primitive functions of cognition are determinate, finite, and systematically enumerable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX6","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-05","text":"Metaphysical vocabulary cannot be authorized by inherited usage, semantic convenience, or empirical accumulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-06","text":"Formal coherence cannot license ontological predication without an account of applicability to synthesized objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:DNX5","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-07","text":"Mere thinkability cannot confer objective standing on a proposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:DBN-08","text":"Inferential utility cannot serve as the final measure of cognitive identity or transcendental significance.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:DNX1","CH_BA94_SILF:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is an intellectual intuition for which concept, synthesis, and object-givenness would coincide immediately.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SNX1","CH_BA94_SILU:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-02","text":"The system denies cognitive entitlement to describe objects wholly outside the conditions through which they become objects for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-03","text":"Pure logical form without sensible content is treated as incapable of crossing into determinate object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIPC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-04","text":"An open-ended primitive ontology is inadmissible because the category inventory must possess a prior ground of completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX4","CH_BA94_SIPC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-05","text":"Things in themselves remain the excluded terminus that prevents conditions of cognition from becoming unrestricted predicates of being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX11","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSA-06","text":"A fully demonstrated passage from judgmental form to objective validity is absent at this stage, and that absence conditions rather than nullifies the achieved architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT2","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-01","text":"The reduction of understanding to judgment allows a complete judgment table to function as a complete functional analysis of the understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-04","CL_BA94_09:GA-06","CH_BA94_SILU:D7","CH_BA94_SILF:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-02","text":"Once the unifying operation of judgment is identified with the unifying operation of intuition, the complete logical table becomes the complete source of original categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-08","CL_BA94_09:GA-09","CL_BA94_09:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-03","text":"Objectivity is neither passively received nor freely produced by understanding; it arises from the rule-governed cooperation of given manifold, synthesis, and conceptual unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-02","CL_BA94_09:GA-08","CL_BA94_09:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIPC:D2","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-04","text":"The distinction between formal validity and transcendental significance prevents the logical table from yielding content by itself even while preserving it as the necessary source structure for categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-06","CL_BA94_09:GA-07","CL_BA94_09:GA-10","CH_BA94_SILF:ME1","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-05","text":"Negative determination becomes architectonically productive because delimitation within judgment prepares the exclusionary regulation of the category inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-13","CL_BA94_09:GA-14","CH_BA94_SILF:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-06","text":"The system can combine a closed primitive vocabulary with open conceptual production because predicables derive from categories without becoming additional primitives.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-12","CL_BA94_09:GA-15","CH_BA94_SIPC:A11","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-07","text":"Necessity is relocated from an object-predicate to the lawful integration of judgment into the understanding’s activity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-07","CL_BA94_09:GA-14","CH_BA94_SILF:A12","CH_BA94_SILF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-08","text":"The migration from disjunctive reciprocity to community generates a model of mutual determination that resists reduction to linear causation, although the correspondence remains unstable.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-09","CL_BA94_09:GA-10","CH_BA94_SILF:A9","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD5","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-09","text":"Because conceptual object-relation is mediated and categories are conditions of synthesis, the cognizable object is structurally inseparable from the representational operations that unify it.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-01","CL_BA94_09:GA-05","CL_BA94_09:GA-11","CH_BA94_SILU:SD4","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:XD-10","text":"The claim of categorical completeness is conditionally inherited from the judgment table rather than independently established at the categorical level.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-06","CL_BA94_09:GA-10","CL_BA94_09:GA-15","CH_BA94_SIPC:D5","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes an achievement of lawful synthesis rather than an immediate relation between an isolated subject and an already unified object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SD1","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-02","text":"The cognitive subject becomes a distributed functional system composed of receptivity, imagination, conceptual spontaneity, and judgmental unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:D2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-03","text":"Spontaneity is productive but not sovereign, because it requires a given manifold and is constrained by finite forms of unification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A5","CH_BA94_SIPC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-04","text":"Transcendental logic converts general logic from a classifier of proposition forms into a controlled generator of conditions for object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-05","text":"Reciprocal coexistence becomes formally distinguishable from causal succession, preventing all relational determination from being temporalized as linear sequence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD5","CH_BA94_SIPC:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-06","text":"Critique operates as regulated admission and exclusion: concepts enter transcendental philosophy only when their place in the generative table can be demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-07","text":"Rational science acquires a finite primitive basis without becoming a static vocabulary, because derivative concepts remain systematically generable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:BSD-08","text":"Possibility, actuality, and necessity become statuses of cognition relative to thought’s lawful organization rather than degrees of independently existing being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A10","CH_BA94_SILF:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-01","text":"The primary loop runs from representations to concepts, from concepts to judgments, from judgments to functions, and from those functions back to the synthesis that makes represented objects cognizable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-02","text":"The system escalates from a negative definition of understanding into a positive architectonic of cognition without abandoning the original prohibition on intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:AF1","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-03","text":"Mediation is the dominant attractor: each apparent relation to objects is redirected through representations, synthesis, conceptual subsumption, and judgmental unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SD4","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-04","text":"Completeness forms a recursive demand because the category table depends on the judgment table, whose authority is itself justified by reducing all understanding to judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SIPC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-05","text":"Unity undergoes phase change from a feature of common representation to the necessary act through which an intuitive manifold becomes an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-06","text":"Repeated negation changes function from excluding false faculty models to delimiting judgments and finally policing the borders of transcendental ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:NX2","CH_BA94_SILF:FGS6","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-07","text":"The table is a conceptual attractor because abstraction, differentiation, synthesis, derivation, and critique all converge on its claim to systematic completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:AS6","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-08","text":"Closure produces renewed expansion: original categories close the primitive field, while their regulated combinations reopen conceptual production at a subordinate level.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A11","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:RLE-09","text":"The system spirals at the identity between judgment and intuition, repeatedly returning to one function at different cognitive altitudes rather than advancing through unrelated stages.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:AS4","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible affection → receptive intuition → given manifold lacking objective unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A4","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B02","text":"Given manifold → imaginative synthesis → connected representation still short of cognition proper.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A5","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B03","text":"Synthetic connection → necessary conceptual unity → object-cognition under the understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A7","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B04","text":"Conceptual unity → use in judgment → subsumption of multiple cognitions under higher representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A7","CH_BA94_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B05","text":"Abstraction from judged content → isolation of intellectual functions → complete fourfold and triadic table.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS1","CH_BA94_SILF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B06","text":"Formal equivalence → transcendental redifferentiation → preservation of cognitively distinct judgment-functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:FGS2","CH_BA94_SILF:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B07","text":"Identity of judgmental and intuitive unification → conversion of logical functions into original categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B08","text":"Original categories → regulated predicables → finite primitive closure with derivative expansion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A11","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FGS-B09","text":"Taxonomic exclusion → rejection of false predicates → critical architectonic of rational science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:AFM-B01","text":"Exclusion of intellectual intuition → discursive mediation → reduction of understanding to judgment → abstraction and classification of judgment-functions → transcendental redifferentiation → priority of synthesis → identity of judgmental and intuitive unification → derivation of categories → finite primitive closure with derivative expansion → restriction of metaphysical predication.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:SNX1","CH_BA94_SILU:A12","CH_BA94_SILF:AS2","CH_BA94_SILF:D1","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF1","CH_BA94_SIPC:A8","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-01","text":"The subject-object model is replaced by a distributed architecture in which object-directedness emerges through coordinated receptive, imaginative, and conceptual operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:ME4","CH_BA94_SIPC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-02","text":"Concepts are reclassified from static representations into functions of unification whose full operation is judgmental and synthetic.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-03","text":"Logic is redrawn from syllogistic classification into the formal source structure of transcendental cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:ME1","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-04","text":"Negative cognition becomes structurally productive by delimiting fields, preserving reciprocal boundaries, and excluding illegitimate primitives.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:SD2","CH_BA94_SILF:DNX4","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-05","text":"The category-predicable distinction makes conceptual expansion compatible with a finite and noninflationary primitive inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ME3","CH_BA94_SIPC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-06","text":"Objects are reclassified as cognizable only under conditions of synthesis rather than as independently accessible bearers of conceptual predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-07","text":"Community becomes visible as reciprocal coordination and therefore cannot be assimilated to unilateral causality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ME6","CH_BA94_SIPC:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-08","text":"Modal vocabulary is relocated from objective being to a proposition’s standing within thought and its laws.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:ME4","CH_BA94_SILF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MME-09","text":"Systematic derivation replaces empirical cataloguing as the legitimate method for identifying primitive transcendental concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIPC:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The system defines understanding, classifies judgments, enumerates categories, and distinguishes original categories from derivative predicables.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:ALT1","CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Cognition is produced by the ordered relay from sensible manifold through imaginative synthesis to conceptual and judgmental unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT2","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system reframes transcendental inquiry as systematic derivation, regulated inclusion, and exclusion rather than introspection or inherited taxonomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Cognizable reality is conditioned by synthesis, while relational form distinguishes causal succession from reciprocal coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The architecture performs its thesis by abstracting, classifying, redifferentiating, mapping, deriving, and excluding through the same formal operations it attributes to understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:AS1","CH_BA94_SIPC:AS6","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-01","text":"Categorical completeness depends on the completeness of the judgment table, which enters as a premise rather than an independently demonstrated result.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:D5","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-02","text":"The identity between unification in judgment and unification in intuition bears the principal derivational load but is asserted more than demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-03","text":"Modality contributes no material content while remaining indispensable to the complete functional architecture of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:MRT5","CH_BA94_SILF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-04","text":"Third categories are generated through combination yet retained as primitive, producing an unresolved tension between derivation and originality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIPC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-05","text":"Objective applicability is assigned to categories as conditions of synthesis, although the full derivation of that validity remains deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD1","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-06","text":"The derivation of community from disjunctive judgment is the weakest correspondence because logical reciprocal exclusion does not transparently entail reciprocal substantial interaction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-07","text":"The understanding is object-directed while never relating immediately to objects; this tension is productive because it necessitates judgmental mediation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:MRT6","CH_BA94_SILU:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:FL-08","text":"General logic is denied transcendental content yet retained as the sole complete source-table for categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A1","CH_BA94_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-01","text":"Function changes from the unity of arranging representations to the act that supplies necessary unity to an intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-02","text":"The judgment table changes from an abstract classification of thought into the generative matrix of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-03","text":"Synthesis changes status from an imaginative connection insufficient for cognition into objective cognition once understanding represents its necessary unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A5","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-04","text":"Negation changes from simple logical opposition into limitative determination and then into architectonic boundary enforcement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A6","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-05","text":"Closure changes from exhaustive classification into bounded generativity when categories produce predicables without multiplying primitives.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD2","CH_BA94_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-06","text":"Alleged transcendental predicates change into formal criteria once unity, truth, and perfection are denied ontological standing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:D10","CH_BA94_SIPC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MP-07","text":"Disjunctive totality changes domains when reciprocal logical exclusion is mapped onto reciprocal coordination among substances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:NX6","CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is implicitly modeled as a relay machine that receives diversity, synthesizes connections, imposes unity, and returns the result as an object available for judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:AS3","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a single observing point but a distributed coordination of receptive, imaginative, conceptual, and judgmental functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:D2","CH_BA94_SILU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is not exhausted by linear succession because the architecture also permits reciprocal coexistence and simultaneous mutual determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:SD5","CH_BA94_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:HSI-04","text":"Navigation occurs through a finite grid of judgmental and categorical forms whose exclusions determine what can count as legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILF:A2","CH_BA94_SIPC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:HSI-05","text":"Escape from arbitrary metaphysics is possible only through deeper formal constraint: primitive concepts must be generated by the same architecture that governs object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:A10","CH_BA94_SIPC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from finite discursive understanding through judgmental classification to synthetic category architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:MRT1","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined at the two decisive joints: the completeness of the logical table and the identity of judgmental with intuitive unification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT3","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if categorical applicability is restricted to objects cognized under synthesis rather than extended to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIPC:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest in its relay from manifold to synthesis, from synthesis to unity, and from unity to systematic conceptual classification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:AF2","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if negative definition, formal abstraction, transcendental redifferentiation, synthetic return, and architectonic exclusion remain sequentially and recursively connected.","refs":["CH_BA94_SILU:AF1","CH_BA94_SILF:FGS2","CH_BA94_SIPC:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_09:MRT-B06","text":"Detailed category definitions, the full proof of objective validity, and the disputed mapping of community remain resistant to reduction within the achieved cluster architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPC:NX12","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT8","CH_BA94_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 25–27","context":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","original_title":"Chapters 25–27","original_context":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6"},"original_title":"Chapters 25–27","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_10","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 28–30","order":10,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIPT","CH_BA94_TTDC","CH_BA94_PCMR"],"revision":"CL_BA94_10@R001","sha256":"39895cd57492b698c4fb53c8ef6a28111a10d65e1a919929ada285b3cf2f08c1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B01","text":"The demand for deduction arises because possession and empirical acquisition do not confer a pure concept’s objective right, while intuitive givenness does not by itself establish categorial authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:AS1","CH_BA94_SIPT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B02","text":"The problem of correspondence is recoded from matching a ready-made object to a representation into determining how representations make objects possible as objects of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:AS2","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B03","text":"Sensibility supplies a manifold but never conjunction; spontaneous synthesis is therefore required before anything can be represented as a unified object.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B04","text":"Experience requires sensible intuition and conceptual determination together, so categories condition objecthood without producing the existence of what is given.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B05","text":"Causality supplies the decisive local test because necessary lawfulness cannot be obtained from observed succession or inductive regularity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B06","text":"Synthesis becomes the universal operation on which represented conjunction, analysis, and categorical unity depend.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B07","text":"The system escalates beyond categorical unity to a higher qualitative unity that grounds judgment and the logical use of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AS-B08","text":"Critical closure consists in one operation that validates categories for possible experience and denies their authorized use beyond that domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity concerns a concept’s right to determine objects, not the historical, psychological, or empirical origin of its possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A1","CH_BA94_SIPT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Objectivity shifts from correspondence with an independently ready-made object to universally necessary conditions under which anything can count as an object of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:SD1","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] An object of experience requires the cooperation of receptive givenness and spontaneous synthesis; neither sensibility nor understanding is sufficient alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Necessity and strict universality cannot be inferred from repetition, expectation, or observed regularity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A12","CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Categories possess objective validity only insofar as they are constitutive conditions of possible experience and of the cognition of objects therein.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The relation between representation and object becomes constitutive without becoming productive of existence: representation determines cognizability rather than creates being.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Every represented conjunction presupposes a unity prior to categorical unity, making higher qualitative unity the regress-point of the architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The proof of categorial validity and the restriction of categories to possible experience are inseparable moments of one critical act.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The distinction between factual acquisition and juridical entitlement remains invariant and governs every later account of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A1","CH_BA94_SIPT:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Space and sensibility recur first as forms of givenness and then as the receptive pole whose manifold requires synthesis; their role shifts from contrastive example to material condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Categories shift from pure conditions of thought whose objective status is uncertain to conditions under which an intuition is cognized as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-04","text":"[Absorption] Causality begins as the exemplary problem of necessary connection and is absorbed into the more general requirement that all objective synthesis carry non-empirical necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:AS5","CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Representation ceases to be merely an effect of objects and becomes a condition under which objects are available as objects of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:AS2","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] Conjunction shifts from a local operation of combining representations into the condition of categorical unity, forcing an ascent to a unity that precedes conjunction itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal transformation] Higher qualitative unity remains functionally indispensable while its content and operation remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX5","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-01","text":"[Local residue] The contrast between observed succession and necessary causation remains a local diagnostic case; at cluster scale it survives as evidence that objectivity cannot be grounded in induction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX5","CH_BA94_SIPT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-02","text":"[Local contrast] The secure applicability of space and time to appearances remains a contrastive model and cannot itself justify the categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","CH_BA94_SIPT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The distinction between empirical origin and transcendental right becomes the governing criterion for all claims concerning necessity, objectivity, and cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A5","CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Sensibility and understanding become complementary conditions of experience: one gives manifold content while the other makes that content objectively thinkable.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-05","text":"[Catalyst] The claim that conjunction is not sensibly given converts the deduction from a problem of applying finished concepts into a problem of the synthesis that first makes objects representable.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:CGD-06","text":"[Recoded] Logical functions of judgment no longer function as a self-sufficient ground; they become downstream expressions of a prior synthetic and qualitative unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-01","text":"A psychological, physiological, or empirical genealogy of concepts cannot establish their objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX1","CH_BA94_SIPT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-02","text":"Necessary validity cannot be derived from recurrent experience, habit, expectation, or subjective compulsion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:NX4","CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-03","text":"Intuitive givenness and sensory manifold cannot supply conjunction, objecthood, or categorial validity by themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-04","text":"Categorial determination does not create objects in existence and cannot be construed as an externally imposed relation between ready-made objects and concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:NX2","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-05","text":"Analysis, logical form, and the category of unity cannot function as the ultimate ground of synthesis or conjunction.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:NX6","CH_BA94_PCMR:NX8","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:GNX-06","text":"Categories are not licenses for cognition beyond possible experience, even when they are valid for every object of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","CH_BA94_TTDC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-01","text":"There is no immediate passage from formal unity in thought to objective validity in what is thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-02","text":"The experienced order of appearances cannot certify itself as necessary order.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:DNX3","CH_BA94_SIPT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-03","text":"Passive reception cannot ground a conjoined object, because the relation of representations is not independently given through objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX1","CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-04","text":"Categories cannot self-ground their own unity, because categorical judgment already presupposes conjunction and its enabling unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-05","text":"Analysis cannot serve as a primitive or symmetrical alternative to synthesis, because its objects must already have been combined.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:DBN-06","text":"Cognition independently of the union of intuition and conceptual determination is structurally unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX4","CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSA-01","text":"A standpoint from which objects are cognized wholly independently of the conditions of possible experience is unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSA-02","text":"Final grounding in either empirical psychology or an independently knowable object-order is foreclosed.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSA-03","text":"A final account of the higher unity that enables synthesis is required by the system but withheld by its present architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX5","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSA-04","text":"Neither a self-sufficient object nor a self-grounding understanding can furnish the ultimate foundation of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX2","CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-01","text":"The juridical demand for categorial validity becomes a constitutive demand to demonstrate that synthesis is necessary for the representation of objects and therefore for possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-01","CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CH_BA94_SIPT:AS1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-02","text":"Possible experience is neither bare sensation nor merely formal judgment but a unified product of receptive givenness and spontaneous determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-03","text":"Categories can govern object cognition only as non-ultimate conditions, because categorical unity itself presupposes conjunction and a prior enabling unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CL_BA94_10:GA-07","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-04","text":"To make an object possible as cognized is to determine the conditions of its objecthood without producing its existence, and synthesis supplies the operative form of that determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CL_BA94_10:GA-06","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD2","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-05","text":"The proof that categories are valid for possible experience is identical with the proof that their use is invalid outside the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CL_BA94_10:GA-08","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:XD-06","text":"A circularity pressures the architecture because experience can validate categories only when experience is already understood as object-cognition structured by categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-02","CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CH_BA94_TTDC:A7","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes an internally structured transcendental status rather than access to an object independently of the conditions of its experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-02","CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD1","CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSD-02","text":"Critique is productive rather than merely prohibitive because the structure that secures scientific necessity also limits transcendent use.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-04","CL_BA94_10:GA-08","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD3","CH_BA94_SIPT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSD-03","text":"The epistemic subject becomes a formal co-author of experienced objecthood without becoming a creator of empirical existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CL_BA94_10:GA-06","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD2","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSD-04","text":"Reflective analysis is demoted from foundational method to a derivative operation whose objects must already have undergone synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-03","CL_BA94_10:GA-07","CH_BA94_PCMR:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:BSD-05","text":"Objective temporal connection cannot be read from succession alone but must be represented as necessary lawful synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-04","CL_BA94_10:GA-05","CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:NX5","CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RLE-01","text":"Manifold givenness generates synthesis; synthesis generates represented objecthood; objecthood grounds possible experience; possible experience then authorizes the categories governing it.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RLE-02","text":"Conjunction represents unity while presupposing unity, and this apparent circle escalates toward a higher qualitative unity beyond the category of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RLE-03","text":"Critical proof forms a feedback loop in which the condition that authorizes categories simultaneously returns them to the experiential field that delimits their use.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:D3","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RLE-04","text":"The term “object” functions as a conceptual attractor because it moves from sensibly given appearance to synthesized unity to categorially determined objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:RLE-05","text":"Necessary unity attracts causality, synthesis, categorical validity, and judgment into one dependency architecture rather than allowing them to remain separate doctrines.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B01","text":"Receptive manifold under an a priori form → absence of given conjunction → requirement for spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A2","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B02","text":"Spontaneous synthesis → representation of synthetical unity → representation of a manifold as conjoined in an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B03","text":"Given intuition plus conceptual determination → object of experience → necessary applicability of categories to experiential objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A4","CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B04","text":"Necessary applicability → juridical entitlement to objective use → rejection of empirical genealogy and habitual necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A2","CH_BA94_SIPT:A5","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B05","text":"Categorical unity → dependence on prior conjunction → ascent to higher qualitative unity as the ground of judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FGS-B06","text":"Grounded validity within possible experience → simultaneous exclusion of transcendent employment → critical closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD3","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B01","text":"Conceptual possession → juridical demand for entitlement → refusal of empirical genealogy as proof of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX3","CH_BA94_SIPT:A2","CH_BA94_SIPT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B02","text":"Intuitive givenness under sensibility → distinction from categorial thought → unresolved problem of pure concepts applying to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A6","CH_BA94_SIPT:A8","CH_BA94_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B03","text":"Sensibly given manifold → absence of sensory conjunction → spontaneous synthesis by understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A1","CH_BA94_PCMR:A3","CH_BA94_PCMR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B04","text":"Synthesis of manifold → representation of an object as conjoined → categories determine the objecthood of what intuition gives.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD1","CH_BA94_TTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B05","text":"Categorical unity → recognition of its dependence on prior unity → higher qualitative unity as the ground of judgment and logical understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A14","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15","CH_BA94_PCMR:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:AFM-B06","text":"Categories as conditions of possible experience → necessary objective validity for experiential objects → restriction of cognition beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:D3","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from empirical correspondence into a transcendental status constituted by necessary conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:ME1","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MME-02","text":"The object is reclassified from a ready-made sensory datum into a unity available only through the cooperation of receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SD1","CH_BA94_PCMR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MME-03","text":"Empiricist overextension and sceptical restriction become structurally linked pathologies produced by the same failure to ground necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:AS5","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MME-04","text":"Logical judgment and categorical unity are displaced from ultimate foundation and become derivative expressions of prior synthesis and qualitative unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SD3","CH_BA94_PCMR:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MME-05","text":"The boundary of legitimate cognition becomes internal to the conditions that make experience possible rather than an external restraint imposed upon reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:ME4","CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] Pure concepts, especially causality, require justification for their necessary application to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:AS5","CH_BA94_TTDC:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Sensibility supplies manifold givenness while understanding supplies synthesis, and their conjunction produces objects of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:AS2","CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_TTDC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Transcendental deduction displaces empirical explanation with an inquiry into enabling conditions and thereby avoids both dogmatic excess and sceptical withdrawal.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:ALT3","CH_BA94_TTDC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Objective temporal connection requires necessary synthesis rather than the mere succession of events, while objecthood remains restricted to phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX5","CH_BA94_TTDC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The argument performs its own regress by repeatedly displacing apparent foundations—empirical origin, sensation, analysis, category—toward a higher unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX1","CH_BA94_PCMR:NX6","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FL-01","text":"Categories are said to ground possible experience, yet their validity is established through possible experience, creating a controlled but unresolved circularity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A8","CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FL-02","text":"Subjective synthesis is required for representing an object as conjoined, but the passage does not yet establish how that subjective activity secures objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A8","CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX6","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FL-03","text":"Categories are constitutive for object-cognition but cannot be ultimate conditions because categorical unity itself presupposes a prior non-categorical unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A6","CH_BA94_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:FL-04","text":"The connection between logical functions of judgment and their objective determination of intuition is foundationally asserted but remains insufficiently demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:A11","CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MP-01","text":"The relation between representation and object mutates from external correspondence into the constitution of an object as cognizable without implying production of its existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:AS2","CH_BA94_TTDC:A3","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MP-02","text":"Unity mutates from the represented unity of conjunction into the prior unity enabling conjunction and finally into higher qualitative unity grounding judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A12","CH_BA94_PCMR:A13","CH_BA94_PCMR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MP-03","text":"Causality mutates from a test case concerning necessary production into a general criterion separating transcendental necessity from empirical generalization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:A11","CH_BA94_SIPT:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as a stratified machine in which receptive manifold, spontaneous synthesis, categorial determination, and higher unity occupy non-interchangeable levels.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:SD2","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither passive recipient nor empirical creator but the locus of spontaneous operations through which objects become representable as objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:A4","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD1","CH_BA94_TTDC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes objectively intelligible only where succession is synthesized as necessary lawful connection rather than registered as repeated sequence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:NX5","CH_BA94_SIPT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in navigating the narrow domain where concepts are both constitutive of experience and barred from transcendent projection.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:D3","CH_BA94_TTDC:NX6","CH_BA94_TTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster-level axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from juridical entitlement through synthesis and objecthood to the validity-and-limit structure of categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT1","CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT1","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined at the point where higher qualitative unity, the completeness of categories, and the transition from subjective synthesis to objective validity require further proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_TTDC:MRT3","CH_BA94_PCMR:SNX5","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if receptive form, synthesis, represented unity, categorical unity, and prior qualitative unity remain rigorously non-identical.","refs":["CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT2","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_10:MRT-B04","text":"The strongest reconstruction preserves the recursive ascent from the failure of empirical justification to the higher unity required by synthesis, rather than treating the cluster as a completed deduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIPT:MRT7","CH_BA94_PCMR:SD7","CH_BA94_PCMR:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 28–30","context":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","original_title":"Chapters 28–30","original_context":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11."},"original_title":"Chapters 28–30","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_11","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 31–33","order":11,"parents":["CH_BA94_OSUA","CH_BA94_PSUA","CH_BA94_WOUS"],"revision":"CL_BA94_11@R001","sha256":"520c77e04cc0ee3c69853328526b7b10df329888ba9082bcd0959808f7845bd3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B01","text":"A manifold becomes attributable to a subject only where it can be synthesized under the possible accompaniment of one identical self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B02","text":"The “I think” functions as spontaneous apperception rather than as an empirical representation or sensory datum.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B03","text":"The identity of consciousness is not primitive but is constituted through the synthesis of intuitively given multiplicity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B04","text":"Object-cognition arises only when a given manifold is united in one consciousness through a determinate act of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:AS2","CH_BA94_PSUA:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B05","text":"Synthetic unity is therefore the common condition of subjective self-relation, conceptual unity, objective validity, and the exercise of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS6","CH_BA94_PSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B06","text":"Empirical association can organize representations contingently but cannot ground the necessity or universality constitutive of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","CH_BA94_WOUS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B07","text":"Human cognition is structurally finite because sensibility supplies its manifold while understanding must synthesize rather than intuit that manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A14","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AS-B08","text":"The movement is recursive: synthesis first makes self-identity possible, then makes objectivity possible, and finally separates objective unity from empirical connectedness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS5","CH_BA94_WOUS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Representations belong to a subject only insofar as they are synthesizable within one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Analytical identity of consciousness presupposes a prior synthetical unity rather than generating that unity independently.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The “I think” begins as the formal condition of subjective ownership and becomes the condition through which manifold intuition can be related to an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:AS5","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Objectivity is constituted by the necessary unification of a manifold in consciousness rather than received from a merely given manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A7","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Understanding is the faculty that actively conjoins representations a priori under the unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Empirical unity is derivative, contingent, and association-governed, whereas transcendental unity is the a priori condition of its possible synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A9","CH_BA94_WOUS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] The highest principle of understanding is supreme only for a discursive human intellect that thinks but does not intuit its objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:AS6","CH_BA94_PSUA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Apperception supplies formal unity but no manifold content; cognition therefore requires irreducible cooperation between receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A13","CH_BA94_PSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis is the generative operation through which selfhood, conceptuality, objecthood, cognition, and objective validity become mutually conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A11","CH_BA94_PSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RMD-01","text":"The unity of consciousness recurs throughout the cluster, but it mutates from the condition of calling representations “mine” into the condition of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ME3","CH_BA94_PSUA:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RMD-02","text":"The “I think” retains formal identity across the units while its function expands from self-ascription to object-reference and universal synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A1","CH_BA94_WOUS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RMD-03","text":"Synthesis recurs as a subjective operation, then expands into the constitutive condition of objects and finally becomes the ground beneath empirical association.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS3","CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS4","CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RMD-04","text":"Empirical consciousness reverses from a candidate source of unity into derivative evidence that unity cannot be grounded in contingent association.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AS4","CH_BA94_WOUS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RMD-05","text":"The contrast with intuitive understanding functions first as a limit on human cognition and is absorbed into the cluster as the definition of human finitude.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:AS6","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between the simple ego and the manifold supplied in intuition remains local machinery until it is recoded as the general finitude of human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:CGD-02","text":"The claim that concepts arise through conjunction scales upward because it establishes synthesis as the common condition of logic, cognition, and objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A9","CH_BA94_OSUA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:CGD-03","text":"Spatial and temporal forms remain receptive conditions local to sensibility, but their insufficiency bridges the transition from intuition to object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A1","CH_BA94_PSUA:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:CGD-04","text":"Association remains an empirical mechanism, but its contingency becomes a cluster-level diagnostic for distinguishing subjective connection from objective unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A6","CH_BA94_WOUS:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:CGD-05","text":"The contrast between human and intuitive understanding does not displace apperception but limits the scope of its supremacy to discursive cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","CH_BA94_PSUA:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GNX-01","text":"Cognition is not passive reception, because a given manifold lacks objective status until it is actively synthesized.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX3","CH_BA94_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GNX-02","text":"The unity of consciousness is not an aggregate, coexistence, succession, or association of empirically connected representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:NX4","CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GNX-03","text":"Objectivity is not supplied by objects as independently unified givens, since the conjunction through which objects are cognizable is an operation of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:NX5","CH_BA94_PSUA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GNX-04","text":"Analytical self-identity is not an autonomous foundation, because it depends upon a prior synthetic relation of representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:GNX-05","text":"The highest principle is not universally transferable to every possible intellect, because it presupposes the human division between intuition and thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:NX5","CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:DBN-01","text":"A subject cannot be conceived as a content-bearing unity existing prior to the synthesis of a manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:DBN-02","text":"An object cannot be objective for finite cognition before the manifold is unified under necessary self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:DNX2","CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:DBN-03","text":"Universal validity cannot emerge from private or variable empirical associations.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:DBN-04","text":"Human cognition cannot collapse sensibility and understanding into one self-intuiting faculty without abolishing the necessity of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SNX6","CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the cluster is an intuition that would generate and know its objects in one act without synthesizing a received manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:A15","CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSA-02","text":"The system excludes an unmediated identity between representation and object for human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSA-03","text":"The system cannot admit objective validity without a necessary relation of representations to one identical self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX6","CH_BA94_WOUS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:XD-01","text":"The same synthetic operation that constitutes subjective identity also constitutes objectivity, so the self-object relation is generated by one formal condition rather than by two independent foundations.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SD1","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD1","CL_BA94_11:GA-01","CL_BA94_11:GA-04","CL_BA94_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:XD-02","text":"Empirical variation is compatible with objective validity because variation occurs in derivative associations whose possibility presupposes a prior transcendental unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:D2","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD3","CL_BA94_11:GA-06","CL_BA94_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:XD-03","text":"The principle of apperception is formally independent of empirical contents while remaining operationally inseparable from sensible material in all human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SD5","CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT5","CL_BA94_11:GA-07","CL_BA94_11:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:XD-04","text":"The exclusion of passive association and object-givenness jointly converts synthesis from a mental episode into the transcendental condition of objectively valid experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:NX2","CH_BA94_PSUA:ME2","CL_BA94_11:GA-04","CL_BA94_11:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSD-01","text":"The cluster generates a non-substantialist subject: the subject is the formal site of unification rather than a self-intuiting reservoir of cognitive content.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:DNX5","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD4","CL_BA94_11:GA-03","CL_BA94_11:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as invariant validity across variable empirical syntheses, not as a property extracted from any one empirical connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:SD1","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD3","CL_BA94_11:GA-04","CL_BA94_11:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSD-03","text":"Human finitude is epistemically productive: the separation of receptivity and spontaneity makes synthesis necessary and thereby makes the conditions of objectivity articulable.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:SD2","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD3","CL_BA94_11:GA-07","CL_BA94_11:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:BSD-04","text":"The a priori is structurally prior rather than temporally earlier, because it names the condition without which empirical unity, object-reference, and cognition cannot occur.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT4","CH_BA94_WOUS:D3","CL_BA94_11:GA-02","CL_BA94_11:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RLE-01","text":"Manifold intuition requires synthesis; synthesis yields unified consciousness; unified consciousness makes the manifold attributable as one’s own; that attribution retroactively defines the manifold as subjectively available.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS1","CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RLE-02","text":"The same synthesis that produces subjective unity escalates into object-conception and objective validity, turning a theory of self-consciousness into a theory of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS5","CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RLE-03","text":"Empirical association repeatedly returns as a derivative imitation of transcendental unity, functioning as the negative foil through which necessity is isolated.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS4","CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:RLE-04","text":"Synthesis is the cluster’s conceptual attractor because every route through selfhood, concept formation, objecthood, and empirical unity converges upon it.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:SD2","CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FGS-B01","text":"Sensibility receives a manifold under space and time without thereby producing cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS1","CH_BA94_PSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FGS-B02","text":"Pure apperception supplies the formal possibility that the manifold can be related to one identical self.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FGS-B03","text":"Understanding synthesizes the received manifold under that unity, converting possible self-ascription into determinate object-relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS3","CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FGS-B04","text":"Object-relation produces cognition and objective validity only where synthesis has necessity beyond contingent association.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS5","CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FGS-B05","text":"Empirical unity appears as the conditioned, variable output of a transcendental synthesis that it cannot itself explain.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:A11","CH_BA94_WOUS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B01","text":"Given manifold → possible accompaniment by the “I think” → formal self-relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B02","text":"Formal self-relation → spontaneous synthesis → synthetical unity of consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B03","text":"Synthetical unity of consciousness → conception of an object → determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS4","CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B04","text":"Determinate cognition → objective validity → foundational condition of human understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B05","text":"Empirical association → contingent subjective unity → demonstration of the insufficiency of empirical consciousness as a ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS4","CH_BA94_WOUS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:AFM-B06","text":"Contrast with intuitive understanding → restriction of the principle’s scope → definition of discursive human finitude.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS7","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MME-01","text":"Self-identity is reclassified from an immediately available inner datum into a formal achievement of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ME2","CH_BA94_OSUA:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MME-02","text":"Objects are reclassified from pre-unified givens into unities constituted through the synthesis of manifold intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:ME2","CH_BA94_PSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MME-03","text":"The field of cognition is divided between receptive conditions of givenness and transcendental conditions of objective relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:ME1","CH_BA94_PSUA:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MME-04","text":"Subjective variation is repositioned as an empirical phenomenon that presupposes, rather than challenges, the universal condition of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:ME3","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, every representation belonging to a subject must be capable of accompaniment by the “I think.”","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, synthesis connects receptive multiplicity to one consciousness and thereby produces the conditions of objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT2","CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry is displaced from empirical contents and associations to the transcendental operation that makes them intelligible as cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:ALT3","CH_BA94_WOUS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the a priori names necessary structure rather than chronological precedence, while time remains a form of givenness rather than a source of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:ALT4","CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the argument performs its thesis by repeatedly separating a necessary constitutive condition from its contingent empirical derivatives.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:AF3","CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FL-01","text":"The principle is described as analytically identical while functioning as the condition of a necessary synthesis, producing a tension between logical form and transcendental operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT4","CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:FL-02","text":"Apperception is independent of empirical contents as a formal condition but cannot yield cognition without sensible content, creating an unresolved dependence between purity and finitude.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT5","CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MP-01","text":"The “I think” mutates from a condition of subjective ownership into the formal point through which objectivity becomes possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:D3","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MP-02","text":"Empirical unity mutates from an apparent form of consciousness into derivative association whose variability establishes its lack of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:AS3","CH_BA94_WOUS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly figured as a field of manifold givenness that becomes cognitively ordered only through operations of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:FGS1","CH_BA94_PSUA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a formally identical integrating function rather than a substantial container of determinate representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:A12","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:HSI-03","text":"Time is the form in which representations appear successively, but temporal succession cannot itself produce their objective unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:ALT1","CH_BA94_WOUS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:HSI-04","text":"Human cognition is a finite navigation between received multiplicity and spontaneous unification, with no access to an intuitive standpoint beyond that division.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:SD2","CH_BA94_PSUA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from manifold intuition through synthesis to self-consciousness, objectivity, and the finite scope of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT1","CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where one synthetic operation is shown to ground both subjective identity and objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_PSUA:SD5","CH_BA94_WOUS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined at the point where the capacity for synthesis and consciousness of synthesis are asserted without derivation from a deeper condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OSUA:MRT6","CH_BA94_PSUA:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_11:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual recursive movement only if empirical association remains subordinate to transcendental unity and human finitude remains a constitutive boundary rather than a removable limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_WOUS:MRT6","CH_BA94_PSUA:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 31–33","context":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","original_title":"Chapters 31–33","original_context":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14"},"original_title":"Chapters 31–33","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_12","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 34–36","order":12,"parents":["CH_BA94_LFAJ","CH_BA94_ASIA","CH_BA94_OBS1"],"revision":"CL_BA94_12@R001","sha256":"e18126d051e018459888cf1292d9c936b2ccac144e57b07a2e36402475867007","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ABS-01","text":"The system converts judgment from an indeterminate relation among conceptions into the synthetic act by which cognitions acquire objective unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS1","CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ABS-02","text":"The unity required for judgment, intuition, and object-cognition is traced to original synthetical apperception rather than to empirical association or sensuous receptivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS4","CH_BA94_ASIA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ABS-03","text":"The logical functions of judgment mutate into categories when they determine an intuitively given manifold, making logical form constitutive of empirical unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ABS-04","text":"The deduction establishes a priori validity by showing that the unity through which an object is represented is identical with category-mediated unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A6","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ABS-05","text":"The argument closes by restricting this constitutive structure to finite discursive cognition: understanding organizes a manifold it does not produce, while the exact architecture of that cognition remains unexplained.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires a synthesis that brings representations under the unity of apperception rather than merely recording their association.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A6","CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity concerns the form of necessary unification, not the empirical necessity or repeated co-occurrence of represented content.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A8","CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Judgment is first the objective unification of cognitions and then the logical operation through which an intuitive manifold is synthetically determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS3","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Categories are logical functions of judgment insofar as those functions organize the manifold required for unified consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Objectual unity is neither contained in sensuous givenness nor produced by subjective imagination; it arises through understanding’s synthesis of a given manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:NX1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Apperception is the common condition through which judgment, categorical determination, represented objects, and empirical consciousness become mutually articulable.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Categories originate independently of sensibility but possess objective applicability only through their necessary operation upon sensibly given manifolds.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Human cognition is finite and discursive because understanding connects and arranges intuition supplied from elsewhere rather than intuiting or generating objects directly.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A8","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] The deduction establishes conditions of validity without deriving why cognition possesses precisely its given inventory of logical functions, categories, and forms of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-01","text":"Objective unity retains stable identity across the cluster, but its function expands from distinguishing judgment from association to constituting intuitive and objectual unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-02","text":"Judgment mutates from a logical relation among cognitions into the operative synthesis that determines manifold intuition under one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A2","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-03","text":"Categories are displaced from an apparently abstract logical register into the necessary conditions under which sensible multiplicity can count as one empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-04","text":"Sensibility reverses from a possible source of objectual unity to a source of pre-given material whose unity requires categorial synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:NX1","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-05","text":"Subjective association persists only as a negative foil: it can describe a subject-conditioned conjunction but disappears once the system turns to the constitutive conditions of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A10","CH_BA94_LFAJ:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RMD-06","text":"The distinction between empirical contingency and transcendental necessity is absorbed into a broader division between received material and the necessary form of its unification.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:D2","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-01","text":"The contrast between “heavy” as object-predicate and weight as subjective impression remains a local diagnostic device, but it scales upward as evidence that objectivity concerns mode of synthesis rather than experiential intensity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX8","CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-02","text":"The plurality of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive forms remains locally corrective, while its cluster-level function is to prevent objective unity from being reduced to predicative form.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX7","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-03","text":"The apperceptive grounding of judgment scales upward because it becomes the bridge connecting logical form, categorical determination, and the unity of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD5","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-04","text":"The claim that categories determine intuitive manifolds is a major transition point because it converts logical functions into conditions of possible object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-05","text":"The independently given manifold becomes structurally indispensable at cluster scale because it limits the constitutive power of understanding without annulling the necessity of categorial organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:CGD-06","text":"The unresolved origin and number of cognitive forms becomes terminal rather than merely local because it marks the point at which transcendental validation cannot become a complete self-explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11","CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects any account in which judgment is exhausted by a relation between conceptions or by categorical predication alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX1","CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects psychologism by separating objective synthesis from reproductive imagination and subject-bound association.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A5","CH_BA94_LFAJ:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects empiricism by denying that repeated conjunction, empirical comparison, or sensuous givenness can independently establish objectual unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX6","CH_BA94_ASIA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects an external relation between understanding and sensibility: neither abstract categories without intuition nor intuition without synthesis suffices for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:SD3","CH_BA94_OBS1:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects an intuitive or self-producing model of human understanding, preserving the dependence of finite cognition on independently supplied manifold material.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:NX3","CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:DBN-01","text":"No transition from subjective occurrence to objective predication is admissible unless a necessary synthetic relation to apperception mediates it.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX2","CH_BA94_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:DBN-02","text":"No empirical object may be treated as pre-unified independently of the categorial synthesis through which its manifold belongs to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:DNX2","CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:DBN-03","text":"No category may be interpreted as an optional classificatory device, since its necessity derives from the conditions of unified empirical consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX4","CH_BA94_OBS1:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:DBN-04","text":"No account of cognition may allow understanding to generate its own sensible material, because the system requires a division between supplied manifold and synthetic rule.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:DBN-05","text":"No complete deduction of the cognitive system’s factual inventory is available, even after the necessity of its operation has been established.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:NX6","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable possibility structuring the system is a cognition in which objects could be given as unified without the mediation of synthesis, judgment, or categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX1","CH_BA94_ASIA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSA-02","text":"The system excludes a fully self-grounding cognitive architecture in which the source of the manifold, the rule of synthesis, and the form of their coordination could all be derived from one principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX2","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSA-03","text":"The conceptually imaginable but inoperative alternative is intuitive understanding, whose exclusion defines the categories as rules of finite discursive rather than universal intellect.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:NX4","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-01","text":"Objectivity is not an additional property attached to judgment after the fact; it is the identity of objective judgment with the categorial unification required for an object to be represented.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-01","CL_BA94_12:GA-03","CL_BA94_12:GA-04","CH_BA94_LFAJ:AS3","CH_BA94_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-02","text":"The category becomes the mediating term that joins the logical unity of judgment to the sensible unity of intuition without allowing either domain to absorb the other.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-03","CL_BA94_12:GA-04","CL_BA94_12:GA-07","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-03","text":"The distinction between objective cognition and subjective association is generalized into a distinction between constitutive synthesis and merely psychological succession.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-01","CL_BA94_12:GA-02","CH_BA94_LFAJ:A5","CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-04","text":"The necessity of categories is established neither by derivation from experience nor by an independent metaphysical assertion, but by their role in the condition that a given manifold belong to one self-consciousness.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-04","CL_BA94_12:GA-06","CH_BA94_ASIA:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-05","text":"The system produces a two-sided constitutivism: understanding does not create the manifold, yet no manifold can become an object of cognition without understanding’s categorial synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-05","CL_BA94_12:GA-08","CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:XD-06","text":"The deduction’s explanatory achievement is bounded by its own success: it can prove the necessity of finite cognitive forms while leaving their particular number and ultimate ground unavailable.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-08","CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CH_BA94_OBS1:D6","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSD-01","text":"The system transforms transcendental deduction into an account of objecthood: an object is not what precedes synthesis as a ready-made unity, but what becomes representable through a synthesis referable to apperception.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-01","CL_BA94_12:GA-04","CL_BA94_12:GA-06","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD1","CH_BA94_OBS1:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSD-02","text":"Logical form gains empirical reach without becoming empirical in origin, because its validity is realized only in organizing the manifold supplied through sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-03","CL_BA94_12:GA-07","CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSD-03","text":"Necessity is relocated from events and sensations to the conditions under which events and sensations can appear as objectively connected for one consciousness.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-02","CL_BA94_12:GA-06","CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD4","CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSD-04","text":"The subject is reconfigured as a finite site of synthesis: it neither invents the manifold nor merely receives a formed world, but unifies given multiplicity under rules it cannot finally ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-05","CL_BA94_12:GA-08","CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:BSD-05","text":"The system’s validity claim is conditional rather than absolute: categories bind every possible object for human discursive cognition, not every conceivable mode of intellect.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:GA-08","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD4","CH_BA94_OBS1:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-01","text":"A recursive loop links apperception to synthesis, synthesis to judgment, judgment to categories, and categories back to the unity of consciousness that apperception requires.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A3","CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-02","text":"The major escalation path runs from subjective association, through objective judgment, to categorial determination, and finally to the constitution of objects of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:D5","CH_BA94_OBS1:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-03","text":"Apperception functions as the cluster’s conceptual attractor because every route to objectivity, intuitive unity, and category validity converges upon relation to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_ASIA:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-04","text":"The category becomes a machine rather than a classificatory term when logical function is repeatedly redeployed as the rule that converts manifold givenness into objectual unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-05","text":"The system spirals between formal abstraction from empirical givenness and reintegration with sensibility, producing validity only by preserving both moments.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A5","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:RLE-06","text":"The terminal attractor is finitude: every constitutive claim returns to the understanding’s dependence on a manifold received from elsewhere and to the incompleteness of its self-grounding.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B01","text":"A merely associative or concept-relational account of judgment is rejected because it cannot explain objective relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A1","CH_BA94_LFAJ:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B02","text":"Representations become objectively related only when synthesis places them under the necessary unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A7","CH_BA94_LFAJ:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B03","text":"The same synthetic operation is identified as the logical function of judgment when it unifies a manifold in one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A4","CH_BA94_ASIA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B04","text":"Logical function becomes category when it determines a manifold given in sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A5","CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B05","text":"Category-mediated synthesis yields represented objecthood and empirical consciousness without making understanding the producer of the manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:D2","CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06","text":"The system validates categories for human experience by identifying sensible unity with categorial unity, then terminates at the underived facticity of finite cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:D5","CH_BA94_OBS1:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:AFM-B01","text":"Indeterminate relation among representations → distinction between subjective association and objective synthesis → apperceptive necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A3","CH_BA94_LFAJ:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:AFM-B02","text":"Apperceptive necessity → judgment as synthetic logical function → determination of intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A2","CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:AFM-B03","text":"Determination of intuitive manifold → categories as operative logical functions → unity in empirical consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_ASIA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:AFM-B04","text":"Unity in empirical consciousness → represented object → a priori validity of categories for objects of sense.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A6","CH_BA94_OBS1:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:AFM-B05","text":"A priori validity for finite discursive cognition → preserved dependence on pre-given sensibility → unresolved ground of the cognitive inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MME-01","text":"Judgment is reclassified as the constitutive operation of objective unity rather than a static relation among already formed concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME1","CH_BA94_ASIA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MME-02","text":"Categories are reclassified as conditions of possible objectual unity rather than as abstract forms externally imposed upon already constituted intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:ME3","CH_BA94_ASIA:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MME-03","text":"The opposition between sensibility and understanding is redrawn as coordinated asymmetry: one supplies the material of cognition while the other supplies its unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:ME4","CH_BA94_OBS1:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MME-04","text":"The opposition between empirical contingency and objective necessity collapses, because contingency concerns given content while necessity concerns the synthesis that makes content objectively cognizable.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME4","CH_BA94_LFAJ:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MME-05","text":"The field of objectivity is relocated from perception’s apparent self-evidence to the transcendental conditions under which perception can be unified as experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:ME2","CH_BA94_LFAJ:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: judgment, category, intuition, manifold, object, and consciousness are organized around the question of how a given multiplicity can be objectively one.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:AS1","CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: apperception, synthesis, logical function, and category form one distributed mechanism that converts given manifold into unified empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT2","CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: the system establishes necessity by regressing from objects and judgments to the conditions under which representations can belong to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT3","CH_BA94_ASIA:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level: objects are constituted neither by temporal repetition nor by pure conceptual production, but by the synthesis of pre-given manifold according to a priori forms.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT4","CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level: the deduction performs its thesis by isolating understanding from empirical givenness and then reattaching that formal unity to sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:SD2","CH_BA94_OBS1:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FL-01","text":"Categories are said to originate independently of sensibility while being necessarily valid only through their application to sensibly given manifolds.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A4","CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FL-02","text":"The system distinguishes understanding from intuition but requires an asserted identity between the unity of sensibility and the unity imposed by categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A10","CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FL-03","text":"The system establishes the necessity of synthesis while withholding a derivation of why the forms and functions of synthesis have their specific finite inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A11","CH_BA94_OBS1:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:FL-04","text":"The system denies that the object is given as already unified, yet it relies on a previously and independently given manifold whose mode of givenness remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A7","CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MP-01","text":"Judgment changes function from the form distinguishing objective predication from subjective association into the synthetic mechanism that unifies intuition itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:A11","CH_BA94_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MP-02","text":"Necessity changes location from the relation asserted in a judgment to the transcendental form under which a manifold can count as one object of consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD4","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MP-03","text":"The category changes status from a logical function to a rule of object-constitution when it is related to intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:A6","CH_BA94_OBS1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MP-04","text":"The apparent separation of sensibility and understanding becomes productive rather than defective because their nonidentity is precisely what makes their necessary coordination a transcendental problem.","refs":["CH_BA94_ASIA:SD3","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of manifold givenness that becomes an object-world only through rule-governed synthesis under self-conscious unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD1","CH_BA94_OBS1:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a finite synthesizer: neither sovereign creator nor passive receiver, but the site at which received multiplicity is rendered objectively thinkable.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:A9","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:HSI-03","text":"Time appears not as repetition sufficient for objectivity but as the domain in which empirical contents remain contingent until ordered by non-empirical conditions of unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:NX5","CH_BA94_LFAJ:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is navigational rather than eliminative: cognition must preserve the distinction and coordination of given material, formal synthesis, and the finite limits of their explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:SD2","CH_BA94_OBS1:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the critique of associative judgment to the deduction of categories as conditions of possible empirical objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT1","CH_BA94_ASIA:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is coherent at this scale because it preserves the distinction between material givenness, synthetic unification, and objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:MRT4","CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where the same apperceptive unity explains objective judgment, intuitive unity, and the applicability of categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_LFAJ:SD2","CH_BA94_ASIA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the transition from formal unity to its asserted coincidence with sensible unity and at the unexplained finitude of the cognitive inventory.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:MRT6","CH_BA94_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_12:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the system’s actual movement only if it retains the recursive return from objectivity to apperception and back through categories to empirical consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_OBS1:AF1","CH_BA94_OBS1:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 34–36","context":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17","original_title":"Chapters 34–36","original_context":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17"},"original_title":"Chapters 34–36","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_13","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 37–39","order":13,"parents":["CH_BA94_CAOE","CH_BA94_ACOS","CH_BA94_TDUP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_13@R001","sha256":"0104d9c0b739a098539190cabc45b2f58f97f54b7f6ddcddeda03d310233bd68","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-01","text":"The system must explain how pure categories can be objectively necessary for experience without becoming cognition of objects independently of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS1","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-02","text":"Objective cognition requires the conjunction of conceptual determination and sensuous object-givenness; neither pole possesses cognitive authority in isolation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS2","CH_BA94_ACOS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-03","text":"Perception is reconstructed as an achieved unity of a sensible manifold, so categories apply to objects because they are conditions of the synthesis through which objects can appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:AS2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-04","text":"Figurative synthesis is the mediating operation through which understanding determines sensibility without converting finite intuition into intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:AS3","CH_BA94_ACOS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-05","text":"The categorial constitution of perception expands into the universal lawfulness of possible experience and of nature in general, while leaving particular empirical laws irreducible to pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:AS4","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-06","text":"The mechanism that produces phenomenal objectivity simultaneously restricts object-cognition and self-cognition to appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD2","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-07","text":"The movement is recursive: empty category requires intuition, intuition requires synthesis, synthesis produces experience, and experience retrospectively establishes the only valid scope of the category.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS1","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ABS-08","text":"The closure is critical rather than ontological: formal necessity is secured for possible experience, while things in themselves and empirical specification remain unavailable to categorical completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:D7","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognition arises only where conceptual determination and intuition jointly determine an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Categories are empty thought-forms apart from intuition but become conditions of objective experience when they govern empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS5","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Productive imagination is the operative mediator that translates the unity of apperception into a categorial determination of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Human intuition is sensuous, spatial-temporal, and therefore incapable of giving either external objects or the self as they are in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A4","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Perception is not primitive givenness but the synthesis of a sensible manifold under the same unity of consciousness expressed through categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Objectivity consists in lawful synthesis of appearances rather than correspondence with independently cognized things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD1","CH_BA94_ACOS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Categories legislate only the universal form of lawful nature, while experience alone determines particular empirical laws.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Formal thought can exceed experience, but legitimate cognition cannot exceed the conditions under which intuition gives an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SD3","CH_BA94_TDUP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The subject is spontaneous as determining intellect but receptive as self-intuited appearance, so self-relation is structurally asymmetric.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD3","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Things in themselves remain limiting correlates of phenomenal cognition rather than objects whose constitution can ground or complete experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A12","CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-01","text":"The category retains its identity as a pure intellectual form but mutates from an empty possibility of thought into the necessary rule of perceptual and experiential synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS5","CH_BA94_TDUP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-02","text":"Intuition recurs as the condition of object-givenness, then expands into the spatial-temporal manifold whose synthesis makes perception possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS3","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-03","text":"Imagination enters as the mediator between understanding and sensibility, then is absorbed into a unified spontaneity differently named according to its sensible or intellectual function.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-04","text":"Experience mutates from category-enabled empirical cognition into the systematic connection of perceptions through which categories gain universal validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A9","CH_BA94_TDUP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-05","text":"Space and time move from formal conditions of possible phenomena to temporal media of self-affection and finally to formal intuitions whose unity implicates synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:D4","CH_BA94_TDUP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-06","text":"The non-sensuous object shifts from a negatively marked limit of thought to the self’s own inaccessible noumenal aspect, reversing the apparent privilege of inwardness.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A13","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-07","text":"Nature emerges only after perceptual synthesis has been generalized, becoming the lawful unity of phenomena rather than an independently available domain that categories merely describe.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:AS6","CH_BA94_TDUP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RMD-08","text":"The thing in itself performs boundary-work throughout but never accumulates positive determination; it is retained only to prevent phenomenal lawfulness from becoming ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX3","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between pure mathematics and the existence of mathematically conforming objects remains locally focused, but scales upward as the prohibition on deriving actuality from formal necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS4","CH_BA94_CAOE:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-02","text":"The faculty distinction between apperception and inner sense remains local as taxonomy, but scales upward as the global asymmetry between spontaneous determination and receptive self-givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A10","CH_BA94_ACOS:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between productive and reproductive imagination remains a local exclusion of psychological association, while the productive mediating function becomes globally indispensable.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A9","CH_BA94_ACOS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-04","text":"Self-affection is the bridge mechanism through which the limit on external object-cognition becomes a limit on self-cognition rather than an external epistemic restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:D4","CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-05","text":"The claim that categories require empirical intuition scales upward into the deduction that every possible perception and experience necessarily falls under categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-06","text":"The synthesis of apprehension catalyzes the transition from a theory of cognitive legitimacy to a theory of phenomenal objectivity and possible nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-07","text":"The distinction between universal categorial lawfulness and particular empirical law becomes terminal because it prevents formal legislation from closing into a complete a priori science of nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:CGD-08","text":"The restriction of self-cognition to appearance scales upward because it demonstrates that reflexive consciousness cannot exempt the subject from the conditions imposed on all cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A11","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-01","text":"Cognition is not identical with thought, because thinkability does not supply an object-giving intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-02","text":"Categories are not independently cognitive representations of determinate objects and cannot acquire objective reference through conceptual form alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-03","text":"Perception is not passive reception, because the manifold must be synthesized under an intellectual unity before it can count as objective.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-04","text":"Inner intuition is not immediate self-disclosure, and the “I think” is not intuition of a substantial self.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:NX7","CH_BA94_ACOS:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-05","text":"Categories do not yield particular empirical laws, even where they supply the universal form of lawful nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:NX6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-06","text":"Nature as appearance is not identical with things in themselves, so phenomenal lawfulness cannot be converted into knowledge of intrinsic being.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:NX7","CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:GNX-07","text":"Negative determination of a non-sensuous object does not generate positive cognition, regardless of how many sensuous predicates are excluded.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:NX8","CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-01","text":"No pre-synthetic object can function as a cognized ground of experience, because objecthood for finite cognition arises only after apprehension, synthesis, and consciousness operate.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-02","text":"No category has autonomous objective reference prior to its relation to an intuition capable of being determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-03","text":"No transition from pure form to actual existence is available without empirical givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-04","text":"No identity of thinker, intuited self, and known self can be achieved within finite cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-05","text":"No deduction of particular scientific laws can proceed from categories, because pure understanding determines only universal lawfulness.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:DBN-06","text":"No claim about things in themselves can be licensed by the demonstrated necessity of phenomenal synthesis and law.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSA-01","text":"A purely intellectual route from concept to object is unavailable to finite human cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSA-02","text":"A nature that is both empirically accessible and wholly independent of the conditions of possible experience is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSA-03","text":"The common ground through which spontaneity affects receptivity remains structurally required yet unavailable as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSA-04","text":"A complete a priori science of materially determinate nature is disallowed because empirical specification cannot be absorbed into formal synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSA-05","text":"The system cannot transform the absence of intuition into positive metaphysical content by accumulating exclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-01","text":"The limitation of categories to experience is not merely an external prohibition, because the same imaginative synthesis that makes categories objectively valid also supplies the only field in which they can operate.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-01","CL_BA94_13:GA-02","CL_BA94_13:GA-03","CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7","CH_BA94_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-02","text":"The system’s constitutive and restrictive functions are identical in origin: the apparatus that produces phenomenal objectivity simultaneously excludes noumenal cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-02","CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CL_BA94_13:GA-08","CH_BA94_ACOS:SD2","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-03","text":"The subject-relativity of phenomenal nature does not entail arbitrariness, because lawful objectivity is generated by universal conditions of synthesis rather than private empirical association.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-05","CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CH_BA94_TDUP:A9","CH_BA94_TDUP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-04","text":"Self-cognition is an internal instance of the same constitutive-limit structure governing outer cognition: the subject can determine itself only in the temporal appearance it receives.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-04","CL_BA94_13:GA-09","CH_BA94_ACOS:AS4","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-05","text":"Empirical inquiry is not a residual supplement to transcendental legislation but the necessary site at which the materially determinate content of nature becomes knowable.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CH_BA94_TDUP:A10","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-06","text":"Formal necessity cannot replace either empirical existence or empirical law-discovery, because neither mathematically possible objects nor particular natural laws follow from pure form alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-02","CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CH_BA94_CAOE:SD5","CH_BA94_TDUP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:XD-07","text":"Noumenal discourse can function only as a boundary-marker for the scope of cognition and cannot furnish a parallel field of categorial objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-08","CL_BA94_13:GA-10","CH_BA94_CAOE:SD6","CH_BA94_TDUP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-01","text":"Transcendental justification becomes immanent: categories are validated not by comparison with independently known objects but by reconstruction of the operations through which objects of experience are possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-02","CL_BA94_13:GA-05","CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD1","CH_BA94_CAOE:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-02","text":"Objective necessity is produced by the coordinated operation of receptivity, imagination, apperception, and understanding rather than inherited from things in themselves.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-03","CL_BA94_13:GA-05","CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD3","CH_BA94_ACOS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-03","text":"Nature is formally necessary but materially open: its universal lawfulness is categorially legislated, while its empirical determinations remain discoverable only through experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD2","CH_BA94_TDUP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-04","text":"The finite subject is internally non-coincident: it is the source of synthetic order while appearing to itself only as an ordered temporal manifold.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-04","CL_BA94_13:GA-09","CH_BA94_ACOS:SD3","CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-05","text":"Time ceases to be a transparent medium of self-presence and becomes the form in which spontaneous activity is received without becoming intellectually intuited.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-04","CL_BA94_13:GA-09","CH_BA94_ACOS:SD4","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:BSD-06","text":"Critical method must investigate conditions of possible objectivity rather than seek unrestricted metaphysical access to objects, selves, or nature beyond those conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CL_BA94_13:GA-08","CL_BA94_13:GA-10","CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT3","CH_BA94_CAOE:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-01","text":"Empty category → demand for intuition → synthesis of manifold → experience → retrospective validation of the category forms the primary recursive circuit.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:FGS1","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-02","text":"Understanding requires sensibility for objectivity, yet sensibility requires understanding for synthetic unity; neither faculty can ground cognition independently.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A5","CH_BA94_ACOS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-03","text":"Self-affection produces a recursive limit: the subject determines inner sense, receives itself temporally, and thereby fails to coincide with the spontaneous activity it knows itself to exercise.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD3","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-04","text":"The system escalates from category-to-object application, through perception and experience, to the formal constitution of nature as a whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A8","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5","CH_BA94_TDUP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-05","text":"Phenomenal objectivity is the conceptual attractor at which receptivity, spontaneity, necessity, and lawful nature converge without yielding access to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD1","CH_BA94_TDUP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:RLE-06","text":"Noumenal limitation is a boundary-feedback mechanism: it prevents the universal necessity of categories from being mistaken for unrestricted ontological sovereignty.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SD6","CH_BA94_TDUP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B01","text":"Formal thought is distinguished from cognition, and sensuous intuition is established as the finite condition under which an object can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A1","CH_BA94_ACOS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B02","text":"A spatial-temporal manifold is given receptively, but receptivity alone lacks the synthetic unity required for perception.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_ACOS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B03","text":"Apperceptive spontaneity determines sensibility through productive imagination, producing figurative synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B04","text":"Figurative synthesis applies categories to the sensible manifold and thereby constitutes determinate phenomenal perception.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:D3","CH_BA94_TDUP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B05","text":"Categorially unified perceptions are connected as experience, within which categories acquire objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5","CH_BA94_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B06","text":"Possible experience becomes formally lawful nature, while empirical inquiry remains necessary for particular laws and determinate content.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:SD2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FGS-B07","text":"The system blocks every conversion of formal synthesis into knowledge of things in themselves, intellectual self-intuition, or positive noumenal objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX1","CH_BA94_TDUP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B01","text":"Formal thought without object-givenness → distinction between conceiving an object and cognizing one.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A1","CH_BA94_CAOE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B02","text":"Sensuous intuition → manifold available only under finite spatial-temporal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS2","CH_BA94_ACOS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B03","text":"Sensible manifold → productive imagination as mediation between spontaneity and receptivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:A6","CH_BA94_ACOS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B04","text":"Figurative synthesis → perception as categorially unified apprehension.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:A2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B05","text":"Unified perception → experience as the field of universally valid categorical application.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:AS5","CH_BA94_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B06","text":"Possible experience → formal nature governed by universal conditions but empirically specified only through inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:D6","CH_BA94_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:AFM-B07","text":"Formal nature and phenomenal self-knowledge → terminal exclusion of noumenal cognition and complete a priori natural science.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX3","CH_BA94_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-01","text":"Categories are reclassified from abstract logical forms into operations necessary for the constitution of empirical objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:ME1","CH_BA94_TDUP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-02","text":"The opposition between subjective conditions and objective validity collapses within the phenomenal domain, because subjective synthesis is the condition of objective appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:ME3","CH_BA94_ACOS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-03","text":"Imagination is reclassified from auxiliary image-production into the constitutive interface through which categories acquire sensible application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ME2","CH_BA94_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-04","text":"Inner experience loses any privileged claim to noumenal access, because self-intuition is as phenomenal and mediated as outer intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ME3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-05","text":"Nature is divided into formally necessary lawfulness and empirically discovered particularity, redistributing rather than abolishing the relation between a priori cognition and inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:ME4","CH_BA94_TDUP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MME-06","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is redrawn as a reconstruction of conditions of application, with the boundary of knowledge determined by the possibility of object-giving synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT3","CH_BA94_CAOE:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: Pure categories can cognize sensible objects only when intuition supplies a manifold for their application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: Apperception, understanding, imagination, and sensibility form a distributed mechanism that synthesizes appearances into perceptions and experiences.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: Objectivity is secured by reconstructing the conditions under which cognition is possible rather than by deriving categories from objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: Finite subjects are spontaneous thinkers but temporally intuited appearances, while nature exists cognitively only as lawful phenomenal connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT4","CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: The analysis performs its thesis by repeatedly converting apparent immediacy—object, perception, nature, self—into mediated synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT3","CH_BA94_TDUP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FL-01","text":"Formal intuition is said to contain an a priori unity while the unity required for perception is located in a synthesis not belonging to sensibility; this tension drives the relocation of form into mediated constitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT5","CH_BA94_TDUP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MP-01","text":"The category mutates from formally empty thought to an operative law of synthesis once imagination supplies its sensible presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:AS5","CH_BA94_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FL-02","text":"The same subject must be both spontaneous determinant and receptively affected appearance, yet the common ground of these functions remains unobjectified.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT5","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MP-02","text":"Objectivity mutates from apparent correspondence with an independent object into lawful validity within the conditions of phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:ME1","CH_BA94_TDUP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:FL-03","text":"Phenomenal laws are conditioned by understanding, while things in themselves are still presumed to possess independent law-conformity; the relation between these claims is not demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT6","CH_BA94_TDUP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MP-03","text":"The scope of categorical application shifts from formal extension to any sensuous intuition toward legitimate human cognition restricted to empirical experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:A11","CH_BA94_CAOE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as a receptivity–spontaneity circuit in which manifold, synthesis, and lawfulness generate the only objects available to cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDUP:SD3","CH_BA94_ACOS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:HSI-02","text":"The subject is imaged as a double-positioned operator: it produces synthetic order while receiving itself only as an ordered temporal appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD3","CH_BA94_ACOS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:HSI-03","text":"Time is imaged not as a transparent container but as the receptive form through which spontaneous activity becomes present to itself without becoming self-identical.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:SD4","CH_BA94_ACOS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:HSI-04","text":"The noumenal is imaged as an exterior boundary that prevents phenomenal constitution from becoming total metaphysical possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:SNX3","CH_BA94_ACOS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:HSI-05","text":"The only internally licensed movement is critical navigation of conditions of cognition, not escape into objects, selves, or nature conceived beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:ALT3","CH_BA94_TDUP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empty category through sensible synthesis to the universal validity and terminal limitation of categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT1","CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where the need for intuition, the mediation of imagination, and the deduction of categorical validity form one continuous mechanism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT4","CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MRT-B03","text":"The ultimate common ground of spontaneity and receptivity, along with the exact status of formal-intuitive unity, remains underdetermined rather than resolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT5","CH_BA94_TDUP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_13:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction remains faithful only if it preserves the recursive sequence by which the conditions enabling cognition repeatedly generate its own limits; a static list of doctrines would lose the system’s operative force.","refs":["CH_BA94_CAOE:MRT7","CH_BA94_ACOS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 37–39","context":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","original_title":"Chapters 37–39","original_context":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22"},"original_title":"Chapters 37–39","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_14","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 40–42","order":14,"parents":["CH_BA94_RTDC","CH_BA94_BIAP","CH_BA94_ITFJ"],"revision":"CL_BA94_14@R001","sha256":"897c9d6363c7f78f80ff6e25de224c062a3458be352edc4a0524afe7503bbc73","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-01","text":"Necessary a priori concepts require a deduction that secures their authority over experience without deriving them from experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-02","text":"The system reframes objectivity as neither a property of bare concepts nor an empirical acquisition, but as valid conceptual application to objects capable of being given.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-03","text":"Categories condition experience because thought requires categories, cognition requires intuition, and human intuition is sensuous.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:AS2","CH_BA94_RTDC:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-04","text":"The central proof proceeds by excluding empirical derivation, subjective preformation, formal self-sufficiency, and transcendent employment as grounds of objective necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A13","CH_BA94_BIAP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-05","text":"Judgment becomes the operative site of validity because it mediates between rules and particular cases under conditions of possible phenomenal givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AS-06","text":"The movement is recursively critical: conditions of valid cognition delimit misuse, delimitations differentiate analytic truth from dialectical illusion, and the remaining task becomes schematized application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires more than conceptual thought: it requires the relation of concepts to intuition and possible object-givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Human cognition is objectively valid only within possible experience because human intuition is sensuous.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:NX2","CH_BA94_RTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Categories are a priori conditions of the possibility of experience rather than empirical products or contingent subjective dispositions.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A7","CH_BA94_RTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Necessity secures objectivity only when it governs the conditions of possible experience rather than merely formal inference or subjective compulsion.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A12","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Validity is an application-relation among pure concept, judgment, and phenomenon, not an intrinsic property of concepts or faculties considered separately.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD6","CH_BA94_BIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Judgment is irreducible in ordinary subsumption but receives restricted transcendental direction through conditions governing pure conceptual employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] The synthetic unity of apperception, together with space and time, provides the formal ground linking categories to experienced objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Reason loses objective authority when it seeks to cognize objects beyond possible experience, becoming dialectical rather than analytic.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:AS4","CH_BA94_BIAP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience functions as the jurisdictional boundary that sorts legitimate a priori cognition from illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:SD2","CH_BA94_BIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Critique secures pure cognition negatively by regulating and preventing misuse rather than positively enlarging a stock of pure knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-01","text":"Categories retain their identity as a priori conceptual conditions, but mutate from grounds of experience into principles whose validity depends on judgmental application to phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A9","CH_BA94_BIAP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-02","text":"Intuition recurs first as the condition distinguishing thought from cognition, then becomes the condition of object-givenness required for the valid use of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-03","text":"Possible experience expands from an epistemic limit on human cognition into a jurisdictional principle dividing truth, application, and dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:D1","CH_BA94_BIAP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-04","text":"Judgment reverses from an unteachable natural talent under general logic into a restrictedly regulable faculty within transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-05","text":"Necessity is displaced from a criterion rejecting empirical and preformation accounts into a criterion that discriminates valid phenomenal law from transcendent rational overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD3","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RMD-06","text":"Reason disappears as a positive source of theoretical object-cognition after performing its structural role as the limit-case that reveals why valid application cannot be generalized.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between thinking and cognizing remains local machinery until it is integrated with judgment, where it becomes the basis for distinguishing conceptual availability from objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:AS2","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-02","text":"The psychology of educational exercise and examples remains local because it illustrates judgment’s irreducibility without itself grounding transcendental validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:D3","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-03","text":"The rejection of empirical origin scales upward because it establishes a general rule that validity cannot be inferred from a causal history of concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:D4","CH_BA94_RTDC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-04","text":"The differentiation of understanding, judgment, and reason bridges the units by converting the deduction’s experiential limit into a faculty-specific architecture of validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:AS3","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-05","text":"Schematism is a catalytic transition rather than a completed doctrine here: it names the required mediation from sensible conditions to synthetic a priori principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A14","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:CGD-06","text":"The thesis that philosophy cannot securely enlarge pure cognition supersedes any residual image of critique as a positive metaphysical construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A9","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects empirical derivation as an explanation of necessary categorical validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects preformation because contingent subjective organization cannot yield objective necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A13","CH_BA94_RTDC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the equation of thought with cognition, since concepts without corresponding intuition do not determine objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:NX1","CH_BA94_RTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects formal logic as a sufficient doctrine of application because rules cannot furnish their own correct subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects the unrestricted transcendental employment of reason as objectively valid cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:NX2","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects positive philosophical expansion as the method of securing pure cognition, assigning critique a primarily regulative and preventative function.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:NX6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which the source, formal possession, and valid application of a concept are equivalent.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:D8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:DBN-02","text":"The architecture disqualifies any account of objectivity that can be secured without a determinate field of possible object-givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:DBN-03","text":"The architecture disqualifies any complete algorithm of judgment, because every rule for application repeats the problem it was introduced to solve.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:DNX1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:DBN-04","text":"The architecture disqualifies any unified theory of a priori cognition that grants understanding, judgment, and reason equal objective jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX2","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:DBN-05","text":"The architecture disqualifies the identification of formal rational necessity with cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:DNX3","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSA-01","text":"Unconditioned cognition is unavailable: no concept, faculty, or form of necessity can provide objects independently of the conditions under which they may be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSA-02","text":"A final formal closure of application is unavailable: objective cognition requires judgment, while judgment cannot be fully replaced by an application-rule.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSA-03","text":"Philosophical sovereignty over the supersensible is inadmissible because critique possesses authority only by delimiting valid use within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSA-04","text":"The system circles an unavailable identity of subject and object: the subject supplies conditions of objectivity but cannot thereby cognize objects beyond experiential givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX3","CH_BA94_RTDC:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:XD-01","text":"Categories attain objective force only when the deduction of their experiential necessity is completed by a doctrine of judgment governing their phenomenal application.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-03","CL_BA94_14:GA-05","CL_BA94_14:GA-09","CH_BA94_RTDC:A9","CH_BA94_BIAP:A11","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:XD-02","text":"Possible experience is not merely a limit on cognition; it positively organizes the division of cognitive labor among categories, judgment, and reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-02","CL_BA94_14:GA-08","CL_BA94_14:GA-09","CH_BA94_RTDC:D1","CH_BA94_BIAP:D6","CH_BA94_BIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:XD-03","text":"The impossibility of a universal rule for subsumption prevents transcendental conditions of application from becoming a mechanical substitute for judgment.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-05","CL_BA94_14:GA-06","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:XD-04","text":"Critique becomes necessary because the same pure concepts that make experience possible can produce illusion when detached from conditions of phenomenal application.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-03","CL_BA94_14:GA-08","CL_BA94_14:GA-10","CH_BA94_BIAP:A8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4","CH_BA94_BIAP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as immanent constraint: it arises from the subject’s a priori conditions only insofar as those conditions legislate the field of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD1","CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSD-02","text":"The system produces an epistemic asymmetry in which thought can exceed experience, but validity cannot exceed the conditions of possible experiential determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD2","CH_BA94_BIAP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSD-03","text":"Critical philosophy becomes a jurisdictional method: it assigns powers to faculties according to their valid domain instead of ranking them by formal generality.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:SD2","CH_BA94_BIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSD-04","text":"The transition from deduction to judgment converts a static theory of conceptual legitimacy into a dynamic theory of controlled application.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:D9","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:BSD-05","text":"The system establishes that the conditions enabling objective law also generate the possibility of transcendental illusion when their jurisdiction is transgressed.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:A7","CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RLE-01","text":"Rules generate the problem of application; attempted rules for application reproduce that problem at a higher level; judgment remains the irreducible operator of subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RLE-02","text":"Categories require intuition for cognition, intuition yields possible experience, and possible experience in turn supplies the only field in which categories receive objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A2","CH_BA94_RTDC:A4","CH_BA94_BIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RLE-03","text":"Formal necessity escalates into illusion whenever it seeks object-reference without phenomenal application, returning critique to the boundary it must police.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:D3","CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RLE-04","text":"Possible experience is the primary attractor because every route to validity, application, limitation, and dialectical exclusion converges upon it.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:ME1","CH_BA94_BIAP:ME6","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:RLE-05","text":"Schematism marks the escalation point where the general demand for object-givenness is transformed into an operational doctrine of temporalized application.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS7","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FGS-B01","text":"Conceptual thought → categories; determinate cognition → corresponding intuition; human sensibility → possible experience as the field of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS1","CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FGS-B02","text":"Necessary conformity between categories and experience → rejection of empirical generation and subjective preformation → categories as transcendental conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS3","CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FGS-B03","text":"Categories as conditions → requirement of phenomenal application → judgment as the operator of valid subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:D5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FGS-B04","text":"General logic cannot formalize subsumption → transcendental logic specifies restricted conditions of pure employment → schematism mediates concepts and sensible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:D1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FGS-B05","text":"Valid application within phenomena → analytic of truth; attempted cognition beyond experience → dialectic of illusion; critique → regulatory closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS6","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B01","text":"Necessary categories → distinction between thinking and cognizing → sensuous restriction of cognition to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:AF1","CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B02","text":"Possible experience → rejection of empirical origin and subjective preformation → categories as objectively necessary conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS3","CH_BA94_RTDC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B03","text":"Categories as valid conditions → faculty differentiation → understanding and judgment assigned phenomenal jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:FGS1","CH_BA94_BIAP:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B04","text":"Phenomenal jurisdiction → application problem → irreducibility of ordinary judgment and restricted transcendental direction.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B05","text":"Conditions of application → schematism → synthetic a priori principles; beyond-experience claims → dialectical exclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF4","CH_BA94_BIAP:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:AFM-B06","text":"Dialectical exclusion → negative critique → regulated rather than expanded pure cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:AS6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is relocated from correspondence with independently cognized supersensible objects to necessity within the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD1","CH_BA94_RTDC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MME-02","text":"The opposition between empiricism and a priori cognition collapses into a distinction between empirical content and transcendental conditions of empirical experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:D2","CH_BA94_RTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MME-03","text":"Cognitive faculties are reclassified as differentiated regimes of jurisdiction rather than uniformly valid sources of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:ME1","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MME-04","text":"Judgment becomes visible as a distinct operation irreducible to possessing rules, inventing rules, or mechanically applying them.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MME-05","text":"Critique becomes a method of boundary-production that protects objective cognition by identifying the conditions under which concepts become invalidly employed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A10","CH_BA94_ITFJ:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — Categories, intuitions, rules, judgment, schematism, principles, and reason are arranged around the problem of valid application.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT1","CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Objective cognition is produced through the coordination of a priori concepts, sensuous givenness, synthetic unity, and judgmental subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The system replaces metaphysical expansion with a critical distribution of cognitive authority and a policing of invalid transcendental use.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:ALT3","CH_BA94_ITFJ:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Objects are cognizable only as phenomena in space and time, while schematism identifies sensible conditions as the route from concepts to determinate experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The architecture performs its thesis by repeatedly generating conceptual ambition and then limiting it through the conditions of its own valid use.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS6","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FL-01","text":"The system requires judgment to be irreducible for ordinary subsumption while claiming that transcendental logic can direct its pure employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FL-02","text":"The system permits thought to exceed intuition while denying cognition beyond possible experience, leaving an asymmetry between rational significance and objective determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD2","CH_BA94_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:FL-03","text":"The system treats the subject’s a priori forms as conditions of objectivity while refusing to permit their use as cognition of objects independently of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX3","CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MP-01","text":"Categories mutate from conditions making experience possible into rules requiring a doctrine of judgment to establish their legitimate concrete employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A14","CH_BA94_BIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MP-02","text":"Judgment mutates from a natural talent beyond formal tuition into a conditionally governable transcendental operation without becoming fully algorithmic.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:A5","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MP-03","text":"Critique mutates from a negative barrier against error into the positive architecture through which objective validity is institutionally distributed among faculties.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:D6","CH_BA94_BIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a constrained field of phenomena whose intelligibility is generated through the coupling of sensuous givenness and categorical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:FGS5","CH_BA94_RTDC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a finite synthesizer: it supplies formal conditions of experience but receives objects only through sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:A14","CH_BA94_RTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as the medium in which pure concepts must become applicable through schematized conditions rather than as a route to supersensible cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:FGS8","CH_BA94_RTDC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in maintaining the difference between lawful orientation within experience and illusory occupation of a transcendent cognitive position.","refs":["CH_BA94_BIAP:D6","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the deduction of categorical validity to the doctrine of their judgmental application and the exclusion of dialectical overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT1","CH_BA94_BIAP:MRT8","CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined regarding the concrete schemata and specific synthetic a priori principles that would execute the announced application doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT3","CH_BA94_ITFJ:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where necessity, object-givenness, and possible experience jointly establish the difference between valid cognition and formal or dialectical semblance.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:SD3","CH_BA94_BIAP:SNX4","CH_BA94_BIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture depends on unresolved but productive tensions between unbounded thought and bounded cognition, and between judgment’s irreducibility and its restricted transcendental regulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_RTDC:MRT7","CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_14:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual recursive movement only if critique is retained as a generative negative operation rather than reduced to a prohibition on metaphysical claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITFJ:SD4","CH_BA94_ITFJ:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 40–42","context":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","original_title":"Chapters 40–42","original_context":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General"},"original_title":"Chapters 40–42","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_15","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 43–45","order":15,"parents":["CH_BA94_CISP","CH_BA94_CISA","CH_BA94_SISP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_15@R001","sha256":"1103b6186277413760d4e687e6859a85504af5b0f4460e3201ada6d0cebdce3f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B01","text":"Pure concepts and formal judgments cannot claim objectivity by themselves because their relation to sensible objects is initially heterogeneous.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B02","text":"Immediate subsumption is replaced by a rule-governed mediation in which time renders categories applicable to appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B03","text":"Schematized categories become a systematic source of principles only through their relation to sensibility and possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B04","text":"Foundational principles receive legitimacy not from a higher premise but from subjective conditions that make cognition of an object possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B05","text":"Noncontradiction eliminates self-nullifying judgments but cannot establish object-relation, empirical truth, or synthetic grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A3","CH_BA94_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B06","text":"Analytical containment permits a restricted positive use of logic, whereas synthetic cognition requires conditions irreducible to logical consistency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A7","CH_BA94_SISP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive: conditions enabling categorical objectivity simultaneously restrict categorical validity to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AS-B08","text":"Closure is finite rather than absolute: the system produces a differentiated order of object-cognition while withholding unconditioned cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective categorical cognition requires rule-governed temporal specification; categories without schemata do not determine objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The principles of pure understanding derive their systematic order from categories as applied to possible experience under sensible conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A1","CH_BA94_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Objective validity is established reflexively through conditions of possible cognition rather than by deduction from a higher object-level proposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A5","CH_BA94_CISA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Noncontradiction is universally necessary for judgment but is insufficient to establish truth, correspondence, or grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:A8","CH_BA94_SISP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The analytical–synthetic distinction distributes logical power unequally: contradiction recognizes analytical truth but only constrains synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Time is the universal medium of categorical applicability but cannot serve as the defining condition of logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD3","CH_BA94_SISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is both the field that realizes categories and the boundary that prevents their objective extension beyond appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS5","CH_BA94_CISA:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Categories retain logical function when abstracted from sensibility, but their objective significance terminates with the loss of sensible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Systematic completeness depends on maintaining functional distinctions among sensibility, intuition, understanding, and formal logic.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SNX2","CH_BA94_SISP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-01","text":"The category retains its identity as a rule of unity but mutates from an apparently self-sufficient object-concept into an organizing grid whose use requires conditions of application.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:ME1","CH_BA94_CISA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-02","text":"Time recurs as the universal form of sensible determination, but its valence reverses when temporal simultaneity is rejected as the basis of logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_SISP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-03","text":"Mediation migrates from a mixed representation between category and phenomenon into a reflexive proof-structure grounded in conditions of object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS2","CH_BA94_CISA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-04","text":"Truth mutates from transcendental enabling conditions into a stratified field of logical admissibility, analytical recognizability, and synthetic determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD5","CH_BA94_SISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-05","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction begins as a classificatory boundary and becomes a mechanism allocating different cognitive powers to contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:AS7","CH_BA94_SISP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-06","text":"Possible experience expands from the terminal restriction on categories into the attractor organizing proof, systematic completeness, and legitimate object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A10","CH_BA94_CISA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RMD-07","text":"Productive imagination is explicit as the source of temporal schemata and then becomes absorbed into the system as a required but incompletely exposed enabling operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD4","CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-01","text":"The particular temporal schemata of quantity, quality, relation, and modality remain local machinery; what scales upward is the general thesis that objective use requires temporal rule-formation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:D3","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-02","text":"The mathematical exception remains locally distinct, but at cluster scale it establishes the noninterchangeability of categorical principles and intuitive synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:NX5","CH_BA94_CISA:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-03","text":"The correction of the temporalized contradiction formula is locally logical, but globally it prevents the temporal conditions of synthesis from being confused with the formal conditions of judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:NX7","CH_BA94_SISP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-04","text":"Schematization and transcendental proof bridge the units by jointly converting conditions of cognition into conditions of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-05","text":"A first principle is recoded from a self-authorizing foundation into a rule whose legitimacy depends on transcendental justification.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A4","CH_BA94_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:CGD-06","text":"The model of immediate thought-object correspondence is superseded by a layered architecture of formal admissibility, temporal application, and restricted object-determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:D1","CH_BA94_SISP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GNX-01","text":"Categories are denied direct objective application to things in themselves or to objects independently of sensible conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:NX4","CH_BA94_CISA:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GNX-02","text":"Logical consistency is denied the status of a universal criterion of truth, correspondence, or epistemic grounding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:NX1","CH_BA94_SISP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GNX-03","text":"A single undifferentiated source of a priori cognition is rejected in favor of differentiated functions of sensibility, intuition, and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GNX-04","text":"Foundational status is denied any exemption from proof, authority, or justificatory burden.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:NX8","CH_BA94_CISA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:GNX-05","text":"Completeness is denied the meaning of universal inclusion and is reconstructed as systematic closure within a delimited faculty-domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SD4","CH_BA94_CISA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:DBN-01","text":"Objecthood cannot be assumed prior to the conjunction of sensible givenness, categorical synthesis, and unified cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A8","CH_BA94_CISP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:DBN-02","text":"No formal logical test can supply the material or synthetic grounds through which cognition determines an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:DNX4","CH_BA94_SISP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:DBN-03","text":"No unconditioned standpoint outside possible experience can provide objectively valid cognition of things as they are independently given.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CISA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:DBN-04","text":"The system collapses if categories, forms of sensibility, mathematical intuition, and logical form are treated as interchangeable sources of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:DNX3","CH_BA94_CISA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:DBN-05","text":"The required productive mediation cannot be rendered wholly transparent, because its source is presupposed as operative before it is fully explicable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3","CH_BA94_SISP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSA-01","text":"The coincidence of pure thought and objecthood without sensible mediation is structurally unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSA-02","text":"Unconditioned cognition of reality independently of appearance is excluded as an admissible endpoint of the system.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSA-03","text":"A single formal rule capable of unifying analytical and synthetic truth is foreclosed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSA-04","text":"A complete account of cognition cannot erase the boundary between the conditions of appearance and the conditions of conceptual synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-01","text":"Temporal schematization supplies the application-condition that transcendental proof treats as a subjective condition of object-cognition; validity is therefore conditioned both by synthesis and by proof.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-01","CL_BA94_15:GA-03","CH_BA94_CISP:A6","CH_BA94_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-02","text":"Formal logic supplies a negative gate for cognition, while schematized categories supply rules of synthetic object-determination; neither function can replace the other.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-01","CL_BA94_15:GA-04","CL_BA94_15:GA-05","CH_BA94_SISP:A2","CH_BA94_CISP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-03","text":"Restriction to possible experience produces systematic completeness rather than merely truncating cognition, because it fixes the domain within which categories count as principles.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-02","CL_BA94_15:GA-07","CL_BA94_15:GA-09","CH_BA94_CISA:SD4","CH_BA94_CISA:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-04","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction aligns with faculty differentiation: conceptual containment licenses logical recognition, while synthetic determination requires a separate sensible route.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-05","CL_BA94_15:GA-09","CH_BA94_SISP:D4","CH_BA94_CISA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-05","text":"Time is constitutive when it specifies categorical application, but it is nonconstitutive of logical contradiction; temporal synthesis and formal consistency therefore operate at distinct levels.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-01","CL_BA94_15:GA-06","CH_BA94_CISP:A10","CH_BA94_SISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:XD-06","text":"The architecture requires productive mediation and synthetic grounds while withholding a fully transparent derivation of either, making opacity an internal condition of transcendental explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-03","CL_BA94_15:GA-08","CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3","CH_BA94_SISP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes a staged validation order: noncontradiction preserves admissibility, analytical containment permits restricted recognition, and schematized synthesis permits objective determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:FGS2","CH_BA94_SISP:FGS4","CH_BA94_CISP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-02","text":"Critical cognition does not derive objects from thought; it determines how appearances may count as objects under conditions that thought itself cannot unilaterally supply.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A9","CH_BA94_SISP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-03","text":"The system converts universality into conditional power: categories and logical principles become authoritative precisely by accepting limits on their valid use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:ME2","CH_BA94_CISP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-04","text":"Truth is reorganized into heterogeneous registers rather than unified under one criterion: logical non-nullity, analytical recognition, transcendental validity, and synthetic object-determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:SD5","CH_BA94_CISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-05","text":"Systematicity is generated through exclusion and differentiation, not through the absorption of every a priori cognition under one faculty.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:DNX6","CH_BA94_CISA:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:BSD-06","text":"The system’s reflexive proof of cognition encounters a constitutive blind spot, because the operation that mediates thought and sensibility remains necessary but incompletely objectified.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD4","CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-01","text":"Heterogeneity produces the demand for a schema, the schema produces categorical applicability, and applicability returns categories to the sensible limits that generated the demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:FGS1","CH_BA94_CISP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-02","text":"Judgment enters through noncontradiction, branches into analytical recognition or synthetic incompletion, and returns synthetic cognition to the demand for conditions of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:FGS1","CH_BA94_SISP:FGS3","CH_BA94_SISP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-03","text":"Categories generate a catalogue of principles, and the proof of those principles returns to the conditions under which categories can relate to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:D1","CH_BA94_CISA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-04","text":"The system repeatedly differentiates faculties in order to generate a structured unity that would collapse if those faculties were merged.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SD3","CH_BA94_CISA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience functions as the dominant attractor because it gathers temporal synthesis, objective validity, categorical restriction, and systematic completeness into one finite field.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:D8","CH_BA94_CISA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:RLE-06","text":"Productive imagination functions as a concealed attractor: the system repeatedly requires mediation while leaving the generative source of mediation partly opaque.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:D2","CH_BA94_CISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B01","text":"Pure categories and formal judgments confront sensible intuition without immediate entitlement to determine an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A2","CH_BA94_SISP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B02","text":"Heterogeneity generates the necessity of a third representation capable of belonging to both categorical rule and sensible appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B03","text":"Universal time and productive imagination generate a schema that translates categorical functions into temporal determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A4","CH_BA94_CISP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B04","text":"Temporal schemata transform categories into principles governing the lawful synthesis of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A10","CH_BA94_CISP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B05","text":"Principles receive transcendental legitimacy through the subjective conditions required for cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:AS5","CH_BA94_CISA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B06","text":"Every judgment passes through noncontradiction and is then divided between analytical truth-recognition and synthetic incompletion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:FGS1","CH_BA94_SISP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FGS-B07","text":"The system closes by restricting objective cognition to possible experience while preserving a residue of formal logical function beyond objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:FGS7","CH_BA94_CISA:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B01","text":"Formal categorical and logical functions → demand for conditions of legitimate object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A12","CH_BA94_SISP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for legitimate object-reference → recognition of heterogeneity between pure concepts and sensible intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B03","text":"Heterogeneity → temporal schema as a mediating rule of productive imagination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B04","text":"Temporal schema → categorical principles governing the synthesis of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B05","text":"Categorical principles → transcendental proof from subjective conditions of object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B06","text":"Transcendental proof → differentiated truth-regimes in which logic constrains, analysis recognizes, and synthesis determines.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:AFM-B07","text":"Differentiated truth-regimes → finite closure within possible experience and exclusion of unconditioned object-cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A10","CH_BA94_CISA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MME-01","text":"The category is reclassified from a self-sufficient object-concept into a conditional rule whose objective use depends on temporal schematization.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:ME1","CH_BA94_CISP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MME-02","text":"A priori principles are reclassified from unsupported foundations into propositions requiring transcendental justification.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MME-03","text":"Truth is reclassified from a unitary criterion into a differentiated field of consistency, conceptual containment, and synthetic grounding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MME-04","text":"Time is split into a condition of synthetic object-determination and a noncondition of purely logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:ME3","CH_BA94_SISP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MME-05","text":"Completeness is reclassified as bounded systematic articulation rather than the inclusion of all forms of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:MRT3","CH_BA94_CISA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit problem is how categories, principles, and the principle of contradiction can legitimately govern judgments about objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:AS1","CH_BA94_SISP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Cognition is organized through temporal schematization, categorical synthesis, faculty differentiation, and graded logical constraint.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:ALT2","CH_BA94_SISP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system replaces appeals to self-evident foundations with an analysis of the conditions under which object-cognition is possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:SD1","CH_BA94_CISA:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Objects are cognizable only as temporally conditioned appearances, while temporal alteration itself does not constitute logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:ALT4","CH_BA94_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its thesis by repeatedly generating validity through differentiation, restriction, and recursive return to conditions of use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:FGS8","CH_BA94_SISP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FL-01","text":"Categories are retained as logically significant beyond sensibility while being denied determinate objective meaning beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:A12","CH_BA94_CISP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FL-02","text":"Objective validity is grounded in subjective conditions, leaving a productive tension between the reflexive form of proof and the objectivity it establishes.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A5","CH_BA94_CISA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:FL-03","text":"The system requires a mediating operation and synthetic grounds that it treats as necessary while declining to derive them transparently.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:MRT5","CH_BA94_SISP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MP-01","text":"The category changes function when it passes from logical form to temporal rule of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD2","CH_BA94_CISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MP-02","text":"A principle changes status when it passes from foundation to a claim requiring proof through conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISA:A3","CH_BA94_CISA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MP-03","text":"Contradiction changes function when it passes from universal negative prohibition to a positive criterion restricted to analytical judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:SD1","CH_BA94_SISP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned synthesis-machine in which sensible temporal material is organized by categorical rules rather than disclosed through immediate intellectual possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:SD1","CH_BA94_CISP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a finite synthesizer whose cognition depends on operations linking receptivity, imagination, understanding, and apperception.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:DNX5","CH_BA94_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as the universal field of objective synthesis but not as an all-purpose explanatory medium for formal logical relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:ME3","CH_BA94_SISP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:HSI-04","text":"Critical navigation consists in routing each claim through its proper register: formal admissibility, analytical containment, sensible intuition, categorical synthesis, or transcendental proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISP:ME6","CH_BA94_CISA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central movement from heterogeneous concepts and intuition to conditioned objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:MRT1","CH_BA94_CISA:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it establishes the reciprocal thesis that sensibility both enables and limits the objective use of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MRT-B03","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive generation of particular schemata and the independent grounds of synthetic truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:MRT5","CH_BA94_SISP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_15:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if its distinctions remain operational: logical function cannot replace objectivity, categories cannot replace intuition, and restriction cannot be detached from realization.","refs":["CH_BA94_CISP:MRT7","CH_BA94_CISA:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 43–45","context":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","original_title":"Chapters 43–45","original_context":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements"},"original_title":"Chapters 43–45","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_16","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 46–48","order":16,"parents":["CH_BA94_SI_2","CH_BA94_SISR","CH_BA94_CIGD"],"revision":"CL_BA94_16@R001","sha256":"65b5b54bf8a22fa3b155a5379d4685b1958479b26ac3e3425de02c3d05e09a5c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B01","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition requires an account of objective validity that neither reduces synthesis to conceptual analysis nor permits unrestricted application beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B02","text":"Heterogeneous representations become objectively combinable only through a mediating architecture joining intuition, synthesis, and unified consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS2","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B03","text":"Possible experience first grounds objective reference and then becomes systematically articulated through the principles generated from pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS4","CH_BA94_SISR:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B04","text":"The transcendental conditions that constitute experiential objects acquire a second function as limits preventing their extension into supersensible cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD2","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B05","text":"Categories govern objects only through schematized employment upon a sensibly given manifold; apart from this conjunction they retain logical form but lose determinate objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A5","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B06","text":"The phenomenal field is determined as doubly quantitative: intuition is extensively composed, while sensible reality varies through intensive degree.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A14","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B07","text":"Transcendental principles determine the invariant form and possibility-space of experience while withholding empirical qualities, actual causes, and intrinsic constitutions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:D14","CH_BA94_CIGD:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AS-B08","text":"Closure is achieved not by acquiring a supersensible domain but by preserving an empty noumenal boundary that limits sensible cognition without yielding positive knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Synthetic a priori principles possess objective validity only by expressing conditions under which possible experience and its objects are possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires sensible givenness together with a priori intuition, synthesis, and conceptual unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS3","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The unification first assigned to imagination and apperception becomes operationally specified as schematic subsumption under concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A6","CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Objectivity consists in necessary conformity to rules through which appearances can be synthesized into cognizable objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SISR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The category table functions as a generator of the complete principial forms through which pure understanding can govern experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS4","CH_BA94_SISR:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Categorical synthesis divides into mathematical determination of intuition and dynamical connection of phenomenal existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A8","CH_BA94_SISR:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Every phenomenon is quantitatively determined through extensive intuition and intensive sensible reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A14","CH_BA94_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] A priori cognition determines invariant experiential form but cannot anticipate the qualitative content supplied by sensation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:SD3","CH_BA94_SISR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] A priori origin does not enlarge legitimate cognitive range; it intensifies the obligation to restrict application to experiential conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:NX1","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the ground of objective validity, the field of principial determination, and the limit of human objective cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Noumenon divides into a negative non-sensible limit-concept and a positive object requiring intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A11","CH_BA94_CIGD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Only the negative noumenon survives critical examination, and it bounds sensible cognition without supplying positive supersensible knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:AS5","CH_BA94_CIGD:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Critical restriction secures empirical validity without converting the conditions of human cognition into conditions of every possible reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:AS6","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-01","text":"Mediation retains its identity throughout the cluster, but the initially generic third domain becomes possible experience and then schematic subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-02","text":"Possible experience changes function from conferring objective reality to defining the exclusive jurisdiction within which categories have determinate use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:D5","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-03","text":"Apperceptive unity persists but is absorbed into the systematic employment of categories as rules governing objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A1","CH_BA94_SISR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-04","text":"A priori intuition migrates from its role as a mediating field into the quantitative determination of appearances in continuous space and time.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A5","CH_BA94_SISR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-05","text":"Imagination disappears from explicit prominence after supplying synthesis, while the schema inherits its mediating function at the point of categorial application.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-06","text":"Objectivity shifts from synthesizability under conditions of possible givenness to the schematized conjunction of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:ME4","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-07","text":"A priori status reverses valence: what initially appears to promise cognitive expansion becomes the basis for strict limitation of application.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-08","text":"The category table expands from classification into a generative machine, but categories contract into indeterminate logical forms when separated from sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:D3","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RMD-09","text":"Noumenon emerges through a semantic split, loses positive cognitive content, and persists only as a negative boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:AS4","CH_BA94_CIGD:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-01","text":"Time and imagination remain lower-layer mechanisms of mediation, while their scalable contribution is the general requirement that objective cognition depend on synthesized givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A5","CH_BA94_SI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-02","text":"The developed principles of extensive and intensive quantity concretize the system locally but do not exhaust the classified architecture of relation and modality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS6","CH_BA94_SISR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-03","text":"The anti-void argument remains an epistemic and modal consequence of intensive quantity rather than a cluster-level ontology of matter.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:MRT9","CH_BA94_SISR:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-04","text":"The requirement of a mediating third term bridges the general theory of synthesis and the later doctrine of schematic subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-05","text":"Possible experience scales upward because it unifies the grounding of objectivity, the application of principles, and the semantic conditions of object-reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD4","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-06","text":"Pure ontology becomes obsolete as a positive project and is recoded as an analytic of the conditions and limits of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:D5","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-07","text":"The noumenal distinction catalyzes terminal closure because its surviving function is to limit sensible cognition rather than populate a second domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A14","CH_BA94_CIGD:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:CGD-08","text":"Completeness of the category table scales upward as a systemic dependency, although its proof remains imported rather than reconstructed within the cluster.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:DNX3","CH_BA94_SISR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-01","text":"Synthetic objective validity cannot be established through formal inspection or logical consistency alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:NX3","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-02","text":"Empirical repetition cannot generate the necessity proper to principles of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:DNX2","CH_BA94_SISR:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-03","text":"Pure thought cannot constitute cognition because it cannot generate the givenness of its object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:NX5","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-04","text":"Neither sensibility nor understanding operating in isolation can produce a determinate object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:D10","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-05","text":"Spatial, temporal, and quantitative determinations cannot be transferred from appearances to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:NX6","CH_BA94_SISR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-06","text":"Logical categories cannot be converted into a synthetic ontology of objects in general.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:NX11","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-07","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot be construed as two symmetrical, positively cognizable worlds.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:NX7","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:GNX-08","text":"Nonperception cannot establish absolute absence because zero is a limit of possible degree rather than a positive experiential datum.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:DNX7","CH_BA94_SISR:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot admit a fully constituted object-domain available independently of the conditions of human givenness and synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX2","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-02","text":"Concepts cannot supply their own objects or establish objective reference while remaining detached from intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-03","text":"Necessity cannot arise from conceptual coherence or inductive accumulation because both lack grounding in the necessary form of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:DNX3","CH_BA94_SISR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-04","text":"The phenomenal field cannot terminate in indivisible simple parts, least positive degrees, or an experientially perceived absolute void.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:SNX4","CH_BA94_SISR:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-05","text":"The system cannot surrender the completeness of its category table without also surrendering the claimed systematic completeness of its principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:DNX3","CH_BA94_SISR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-06","text":"Human sensibility cannot be absolutized into the only possible mode of givenness or transformed into a determination of all being.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:NX8","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:DBN-07","text":"A positive science of supersensible objects is structurally inadmissible because its required mode of intuition is unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSA-01","text":"Intellectual intuition is the constitutive absence around which the entire restriction of positive noumenal cognition is organized.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSA-02","text":"No comparison standpoint exists from which appearance and the same object independently of sensibility could both be cognized.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSA-03","text":"The thing in itself occupies an indispensable but empty place as the unknown remainder produced by the limitation of appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSA-04","text":"Noumena can be neither positively cognized nor eliminated, because positive determination violates the boundary while elimination abolishes its limiting function.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX6","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-01","text":"The supreme principle becomes operational only when the category table unfolds unified experience into determinate classes of objective rules.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-01","CL_BA94_16:GA-05","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CH_BA94_SI_2:D6","CH_BA94_SISR:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-02","text":"The mediating third term required by synthetic judgment is concretized as the conjunction of intuition, schema, and possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-02","CL_BA94_16:GA-03","CH_BA94_SI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-03","text":"Mathematics applies objectively to appearances because the synthesis constituting spatial-temporal intuition also constitutes the form of phenomenal apprehension.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-04","CL_BA94_16:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-08","CH_BA94_SISR:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-04","text":"Intensive continuity prevents nonperception from becoming proof of absolute absence and therefore blocks an illegitimate transition from epistemic limitation to ontological nothingness.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CL_BA94_16:GA-13","CH_BA94_SISR:SD5","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-05","text":"The purity of categories restricts rather than enlarges their range because a priori origin cannot replace sensible conditions of application.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-09","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-06","text":"The same categorical system generates empirical order when schematized and transcendental illusion when detached from sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-05","CL_BA94_16:GA-11","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CH_BA94_SISR:ME3","CH_BA94_CIGD:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-07","text":"Phenomenal continuity establishes the structure of appearances without licensing an inference to the intrinsic constitution of every possible reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-13","CH_BA94_SISR:D10","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:XD-08","text":"The unknown thing in itself is generated as a limiting remainder by the same transcendental conditions that establish empirical objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-01","CL_BA94_16:GA-11","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD2","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is a jurisdictional achievement produced by necessary rule-governance and the schematized cooperation of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-02","CL_BA94_16:GA-04","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CH_BA94_SISR:SD1","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-02","text":"Transcendental conditions possess a dual force: they constitute experiential objects positively and delimit supersensible pretension negatively.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-01","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CH_BA94_SI_2:D5","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-03","text":"The cognitive subject functions as the site of synthetic unity through which a sensible manifold becomes one consciousness of objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-02","CL_BA94_16:GA-03","CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-04","text":"Reality is stratified asymmetrically into a cognizable phenomenal field and an indeterminate remainder, not divided into two coordinate worlds.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-11","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CL_BA94_16:GA-13","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD1","CH_BA94_CIGD:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-05","text":"Mathematics can possess necessary application to phenomena while transcendental analysis remains unable to settle empirical qualities, causes, or physical hypotheses.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-08","CH_BA94_SISR:D6","CH_BA94_SISR:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-06","text":"Continuity converts apparent absence from an ontological conclusion into an epistemically underdetermined relation between zero and imperceptible degree.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-08","CH_BA94_SISR:D11","CH_BA94_SISR:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from the determination of supersensible entities into critique of the conditions, range, and meaning of object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-09","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD4","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:BSD-08","text":"Objective possibility is narrower than logical consistency because only intuition and schema can convert thinkability into determinate reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-02","CL_BA94_16:GA-03","CL_BA94_16:GA-10","CH_BA94_CIGD:A8","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-01","text":"Rule-governed synthesis makes experience possible; possible experience constitutes objects; those objects confirm the objective validity of the rules under which they appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A10","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-02","text":"The category table generates principles, the principles organize objects, and the requirement of object-reference returns to restrict the categories to empirical use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:D3","CH_BA94_SISR:A5","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-03","text":"Possible experience is the dominant attractor because every route to objectivity, necessity, application, and limitation converges upon it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT2","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-04","text":"The recurrence of “conditions” marks a phase change from constitutive grounding, through systematic determination, to jurisdictional limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A11","CH_BA94_SISR:A10","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-05","text":"Quantitative determination escalates from extensive composition through intensive degree to continuity, the exclusion of simples, and restraint concerning void-inferences.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:SD4","CH_BA94_SISR:FGS7","CH_BA94_SISR:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-06","text":"A priori independence from empirical origin recursively produces the illusion of unrestricted application, whose failure reinstates experiential restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-07","text":"Noumenon forms a limit-loop: abstraction from sensibility opens a beyond, lack of intellectual intuition empties it, and the empty place returns as a boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SD2","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:RLE-08","text":"The third term becomes a conceptual machine by recurring successively as mediating intuition, possible experience, and schema.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B01","text":"Conceptual non-containment → failure of purely logical validation → requirement of transcendental mediation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_2:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B02","text":"Heterogeneous representations → a priori intuition → imaginative synthesis → apperceptive unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS2","CH_BA94_SI_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B03","text":"Unified synthesis → possible experience → experiential objecthood → supreme principle of synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_2:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B04","text":"Supreme principle → category table → rules of objective employment → systematic classes of principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS4","CH_BA94_SISR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B05","text":"Intuition-directed synthesis → mathematical determination; existence-directed synthesis → dynamical connection and conditional necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS5","CH_BA94_SISR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B06","text":"Successive composition of intuition → extensive quantity; continuous variation of sensation → intensive degree.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A13","CH_BA94_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B07","text":"Formal determination of experience → exclusion of false empirical necessity → preservation of empirical underdetermination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:D14","CH_BA94_SISR:DNX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FGS-B08","text":"Category without sensible schema → indeterminate logical form → collapse of transcendental ontology → negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:D4","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B01","text":"Synthetic excess over conceptual containment → demand for mediation → coordinated synthesis of representations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B02","text":"Coordinated synthesis → unified possible experience → objective reality and experiential objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:D4","CH_BA94_SI_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B03","text":"Experiential objecthood → supreme principle → category-generated system of necessary rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:D6","CH_BA94_SISR:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B04","text":"Categorical system → mathematical/dynamical division → extensive intuition and intensive reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS5","CH_BA94_SISR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B05","text":"Extensive and intensive continuity → exclusion of phenomenal simples → restraint concerning absolute void.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:FGS7","CH_BA94_SISR:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:AFM-B06","text":"Secured empirical validity → jurisdictional test of categories → failure of transcendental use → retention of the negative noumenon.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:AF1","CH_BA94_CIGD:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence secured by thought alone to lawful synthesis under conditions of possible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-02","text":"The origin of a concept and the legitimate range of its application become distinct questions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:ME1","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-03","text":"Mathematics is repositioned as objectively valid for appearances because its constructions share the forms governing phenomenal apprehension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:SD2","CH_BA94_SISR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-04","text":"Quantity is divided into extensive composition and intensive degree, defeating its reduction to aggregation alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:ME4","CH_BA94_SISR:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-05","text":"Possibility divides into logical consistency and objective possibility grounded in intuition and schema.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A8","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-06","text":"The two-world opposition collapses into an asymmetry between a domain of cognition and an indeterminate boundary-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:ME3","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-07","text":"Ontology is reclassified from synthetic knowledge of things in general into critical analysis of understanding’s experiential contribution.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:D5","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MME-08","text":"Cognitive extension beyond experience is reconceived as negative delimitation rather than acquisition of supersensible territory.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:ME5","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster establishes the supreme principle, classifies pure principles, develops quantitative determinations, and adjudicates the phenomena–noumena division.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT1","CH_BA94_SISR:ALT1","CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, objectivity is generated through sensible givenness, synthesis, apperceptive unity, categorical rules, and schematic subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:ALT2","CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique secures empirical knowledge by distinguishing valid application from transcendental misapplication.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:ALT3","CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, phenomena are constituted through spatial-temporal succession, continuous magnitude, and graduated reality, while reality beyond those conditions remains indeterminate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:ALT4","CH_BA94_CIGD:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the cluster enacts its critical thesis by constructing a system and then recursively restricting that system’s jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS7","CH_BA94_CIGD:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-01","text":"Objective reality depends on an object being given, yet merely possible experience is treated as sufficient to validate a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT2","CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-02","text":"Time is presented as the universal mediating field of representation, while external objectivity also requires space as an irreducible form.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT5","CH_BA94_SISR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-03","text":"Systematic completeness depends on the exhaustive category table, but that completeness is presupposed rather than established within the cluster.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:DNX3","CH_BA94_SISR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-04","text":"Sensation is treated as apprehended in one moment while its intensive degree is explained through a temporal rise from zero.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:MRT6","CH_BA94_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-05","text":"The principle of intensive quantity relies on the continuous diminutability of every sensation without independently demonstrating that universality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:MRT5","CH_BA94_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-06","text":"The thing in itself must be thought to delimit appearance, yet any determination of it as an object is prohibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT6","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:FL-07","text":"Pure categories retain transcendental meaning while their separation from sensibility deprives them of determinate objective use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT7","CH_BA94_CIGD:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MP-01","text":"Mediation mutates from an a priori field of representation into the schema required for categorial subsumption.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:AS2","CH_BA94_CIGD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MP-02","text":"Objectivity mutates from lawful synthesizability into a jurisdiction defined by the conjunction of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD1","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MP-03","text":"A priori independence mutates from apparent authority for unrestricted use into the reason such use must be critically constrained.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:SD3","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MP-04","text":"Noumenon mutates from an ambiguous candidate object into a split concept whose positive sense collapses and whose negative sense survives.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:AS4","CH_BA94_CIGD:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is imaged as a gated synthesis engine in which only sensibly supplied manifolds can pass through schematic and categorial unification into objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:A6","CH_BA94_CIGD:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a functional center of apperceptive unification rather than an independent producer of the manifold it synthesizes.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIGD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:HSI-03","text":"Time is the sequential medium of apprehension through which manifolds become continuous quantities and enter unified consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:A12","CH_BA94_SI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:HSI-04","text":"The phenomenal field is a continuous double register of extensive form and intensive reality bordered by an unknowable noumenal remainder.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:SD4","CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:HSI-05","text":"Legitimate navigation consists in testing claims for intuition, schema, and experiential application rather than attempting escape into supersensible territory.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT4","CH_BA94_CIGD:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from synthetic mediation through systematic principles to the critical boundary of noumenal cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the developed content of relation and modality, the proof of categorical completeness, and the positive status of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:MRT2","CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if logical possibility, objective possibility, transcendental meaning, and valid objective use remain distinct.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT3","CH_BA94_CIGD:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B04","text":"The strongest inferential mechanism is the repeated requirement for a mediating conjunction of concept, intuition, synthesis, and schema.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction must preserve the sequence in which experiential grounding enables systematic determination and then returns as the limit of that system’s use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISR:AS7","CH_BA94_CIGD:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture is most compressed where unity of experience becomes universal object-law and where logical categorial form is declared objectively empty without sensible application.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_2:MRT4","CH_BA94_CIGD:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_16:MRT-B07","text":"The indispensable element resistant to axiomatic reduction is the unknown remainder that must be retained without positive predication or ontological elimination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIGD:SD6","CH_BA94_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 46–48","context":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","original_title":"Chapters 46–48","original_context":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"},"original_title":"Chapters 46–48","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_17","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 49–51","order":17,"parents":["CH_BA94_APPX","CH_BA94_EXPE","CH_BA94_EMPI"],"revision":"CL_BA94_17@R001","sha256":"87ea4b09277157a392e4b69c58574b44194499b0bdf2faa98a8da58c16bd90ac","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B01","text":"The system addresses how judgments can possess objective validity and necessity without deriving either from conceptual completeness or extending cognition to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:AS1","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B02","text":"Objective validity is reframed as a relation among cognitive provenance, sensible givenness, and the lawful procedures through which representations become experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B03","text":"Sensibility and understanding remain irreducible, yet cognition arises only through their coordinated contribution of intuition and synthetic rules.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B04","text":"Contingent perceptions become objects only when apperception synthesizes them through temporally schematized categories.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS1","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B05","text":"Permanence, causal succession, and reciprocal coexistence convert dispersed phenomena into one law-governed nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B06","text":"Possibility, reality, and necessity classify objects by formal experiential conformity, perceptual connection, and causal determination rather than by intrinsic modal properties.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS2","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B07","text":"Temporal self-determination reverses the assumed priority of inward consciousness because the required permanence is presented only through external spatial experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B08","text":"The conditions that authorize objective cognition simultaneously restrict it to phenomena and leave the noumenon only as a negative boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD7","CH_BA94_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AS-B09","text":"The movement is recursively critical: source-confusion is diagnosed, experiential objectivity is reconstructed, modal validity is tested, and every positive result returns as a limitation of scope.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:AS3","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS6","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity requires representations to be assigned to their cognitive sources and connected with corresponding sensible conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_APPX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility and understanding are irreducible faculties whose cooperation, not reduction to either faculty, generates cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is the sole positive domain in which objects, categories, and principles can acquire cognitive validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Objectivity arises neither from perception alone nor from pure conception, but from necessary rule-governed synthesis in one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A1","CH_BA94_EXPE:A2","CH_BA94_EXPE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Space and time condition sensible presentation, while categories apply objectively only through intuition and temporal schemata.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Because time is not perceived, phenomenal existence receives temporal determination only through permanence, succession, and coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Substance, causality, and community cease to determine supersensible being and become rules for organizing phenomenal duration, events, and coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Modality determines a conception’s relation to experiential procedures: possibility through formal conformity, reality through perception, and necessity through causal law.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS2","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Empirically determinate subjectivity depends on external spatial permanence because internal sense supplies no permanent intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] A priori principles determine the form of experiential connection while leaving particular existents, powers, and states empirically open.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS5","CH_BA94_EXPE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] The same experiential conditions that legitimate pure principles prevent their conversion into a transcendent ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4","CH_BA94_EXPE:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The noumenal remainder preserves the non-exhaustiveness of sensibility while withholding every usable criterion of supersensible cognition or modality.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A15","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Nature is one law-governed experiential field whose necessity concerns conditioned phenomenal relations rather than absolute being.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD5","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The distinction between sensibility and understanding retains its identity throughout and remains the non-reducible basis of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] The distinction between logical and objective comparison recurs as the distinction between noncontradictory thought and objective possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:NX2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Reflection expands from assigning comparison-concepts to faculties into a general demand that every principle establish its experiential provenance and scope.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-04","text":"[Migration] Time moves from being a sensible form to becoming the unavailable medium whose determination requires phenomenal relations and external permanence.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EMPI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-05","text":"[Escalation] Necessity moves from what perception lacks, through the rule governing objective succession, to the modal status of a causally determined effect.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A2","CH_BA94_EXPE:A14","CH_BA94_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-06","text":"[Mutation] Relation changes from a limit on cognizing absolute internality into the positive mechanism of reciprocal phenomenal community.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A11","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Externality changes from a merely phenomenal relational order into a constitutive condition of temporally determinate inner experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:NX9","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] The noumenon persists but is absorbed into the broader experiential boundary governing category use and modal judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A15","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal transformation] The fourfold division of nothing ceases to govern the argument directly after differentiating contradiction, privation, empty intuition, and unsupported thought within the modal boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A18","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] The particular critique of Leibnitzian doctrines disappears once intellectualization is generalized into the prohibition against categories authenticating their own objective reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:D3","CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The four reflection-concepts remain a specific diagnostic apparatus; what scales upward is the requirement that objective claims be indexed to their cognitive source.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A5","CH_BA94_APPX:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-02","text":"[Superseded] Indiscernibility, monadology, predetermined harmony, and intellectualized space-time remain local targets absorbed into the higher-order diagnosis of faculty-confusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SD3","CH_BA94_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The three analogies scale from temporal principles into the relational architecture required for one possible experience and one nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-04","text":"[Local] Conservation of the total quantum of phenomenal substance remains a compressed extension of permanence rather than a necessary cluster-level premise.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A11","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Reciprocal temporal determination bridges coexistence and world-unity, while the stronger claim of complete dynamical reciprocity remains locally under-argued.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A15","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] The modal postulates connect the temporal organization of nature with criteria for judging objects as possible, real, or necessary.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] The refutation of problematic idealism catalyzes the transition from category dependence on intuition to the external constitution of empirical subjectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS4","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] The table of nothing survives at cluster level only as a discipline preventing contradiction, non-givenness, privation, and formal emptiness from collapsing into one modal status.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SD7","CH_BA94_EMPI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-01","text":"Logical thinkability and freedom from contradiction do not by themselves confer objective validity or possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:DNX2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-02","text":"An aggregate of perceptions is not experience because perception contains no necessary rule of connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:NX1","CH_BA94_EXPE:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-03","text":"Time is neither a perceivable object nor a self-ordering container independent of phenomenal relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-04","text":"Categories cannot apply directly to phenomena or determine things in themselves independently of intuition and schemata.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:NX10","CH_BA94_EXPE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-05","text":"A priori principles cannot construct particular existence, powers, states, or empirical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:NX4","CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-06","text":"Internal consciousness cannot supply a self-sufficient foundation for temporal determination or external existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:NX6","CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-07","text":"Natural necessity is neither induction from repetition nor logical, absolute, or substantial necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:NX11","CH_BA94_EMPI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:GNX-08","text":"Critical limitation does not authorize the construction of intelligible worlds or a positive non-sensuous ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:NX12","CH_BA94_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot admit a single cognitive continuum in which sensibility is merely a less distinct understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-02","text":"Pure concepts cannot authenticate their own reference, and mere representability cannot establish objective possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:DNX1","CH_BA94_APPX:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-03","text":"No standpoint permits comparison of phenomena with things in themselves or surveys modal domains independently of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:DNX7","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-04","text":"A self-ordering time containing lawless sequences cannot count as the temporal ground of experienced objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX1","CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-05","text":"Completely disconnected coexistents or temporal domains cannot belong to one empirically cognizable world.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX4","CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-06","text":"The subject cannot determine itself through an enduring inward presence independent of externality.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:DBN-07","text":"Conditions required for phenomenal experience cannot be converted into determinations of noumenal reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX9","CH_BA94_APPX:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable possibility of non-sensuous intuition structures the noumenal boundary without becoming a cognitive capacity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSA-02","text":"The nonappearance of time is the constitutive absence around which permanence, causality, and community are organized.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSA-03","text":"The ground coordinating subjective forms of cognition with objective givenness remains inaccessible to the system it enables.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSA-04","text":"The thing in itself remains the inaccessible remainder preventing empirical objectivity and modality from becoming absolute ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX5","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSA-05","text":"No ultimately self-subsisting inward bearer is available: empirical selfhood depends on external permanence, while that externality remains phenomenal.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX2","CH_BA94_APPX:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-01","text":"Objective cognition requires both correct source-assignment and necessary temporal synthesis; either operation without the other leaves representation objectively indeterminate.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-01","CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_17:GA-06","CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-02","text":"Faculty-confusion produces not only false predicates about objects but also false claims that merely coherent conceptions are objectively possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-01","CL_BA94_17:GA-08","CH_BA94_APPX:A13","CH_BA94_EMPI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-03","text":"The modal postulates presuppose the temporal analogies because formal possibility, empirical reality, and causal necessity are intelligible only within an already unified phenomenal nature.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-06","CL_BA94_17:GA-07","CL_BA94_17:GA-08","CL_BA94_17:GA-13","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-04","text":"An unperceived entity can count as real only when lawful connection locates it within the single experiential field shared by perceived phenomena.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_17:GA-08","CL_BA94_17:GA-13","CH_BA94_EMPI:D4","CH_BA94_EXPE:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-05","text":"The permanent substratum required for temporal determination receives its indispensable empirical presentation through matter in external space.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-06","CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10","CH_BA94_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-06","text":"Externality is not an aggregate of isolated objects but a reciprocally connected field through which inner experience acquires temporal determinacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-07","CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_17:GA-13","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-07","text":"Restriction to phenomena is productive rather than merely prohibitive because the same sensible conditions that impose the boundary secure objective cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_17:GA-05","CL_BA94_17:GA-11","CH_BA94_APPX:SD4","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-08","text":"The differentiated modes of nothing prevent mere non-givenness or conceptual emptiness from being mistaken for objective impossibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-08","CL_BA94_17:GA-12","CH_BA94_APPX:SD7","CH_BA94_EMPI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-09","text":"Empirical realism does not require space to characterize things in themselves because externality can constitute experience while remaining a phenomenal form.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-05","CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_17:GA-11","CH_BA94_EMPI:NX5","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:XD-10","text":"The analogies are regulative with respect to constructing existence but constitutive with respect to the objective temporal relations through which existence can be experienced.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_17:GA-07","CL_BA94_17:GA-10","CH_BA94_EXPE:A8","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is an immanent achievement of provenance, sensible presentation, necessary synthesis, and experiential connectability rather than correspondence secured from outside cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:AS2","CH_BA94_EXPE:SD1","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-02","text":"Empirical realism and transcendental restriction become mutually supporting because external objects are required for experience while spatiality is denied application to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A12","CH_BA94_EMPI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-03","text":"The empirical subject becomes a node within the same synthetic and external relations through which objects acquire temporal determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SD3","CH_BA94_EXPE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-04","text":"Nature is not an aggregate discovered first and unified afterward, but the relational totality presupposed whenever objects belong to one experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD5","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-05","text":"Strict formal law and empirical openness are complementary because a priori synthesis determines relations while leaving concrete existence and powers undetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD4","CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-06","text":"Critical boundaries function as positive operators: they secure determinate empirical cognition by preventing categories from expanding into supersensible ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SD5","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-07","text":"Possible experience becomes the system’s attractor because it validates objective claims while rendering the total field of absolute modalities undecidable.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:BSD-08","text":"One objective time is represented through lawful relations among phenomena rather than through perception of an absolute temporal container.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD2","CH_BA94_EXPE:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-01","text":"The provenance loop runs from representation to cognitive-source assignment, from source-assignment to legitimate synthesis, and from synthesis back to a renewed test of scope.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A1","CH_BA94_APPX:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-02","text":"The amphiboly escalation runs from undetermined transcendental place to intellectualized phenomena and then to a coordinated metaphysical system.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A13","CH_BA94_APPX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-03","text":"The temporal spiral begins with perceptual insufficiency, invokes apperceptive synthesis, and returns phenomena to perception as objectively ordered events.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS2","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-04","text":"The three temporal branches converge on one nature: permanence stabilizes duration, causality orders change, and community integrates coexistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-05","text":"The modal circuit progressively tightens experiential relation without adding object-content: formal conformity becomes possibility, perceptual connection reality, and causal determination necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-06","text":"The anti-idealist reversal loops inner experience through external permanence and returns externality as a condition of the subject that initially doubted it.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:D5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-07","text":"The restriction loop converts successful category use into self-limitation: intuition grants objective validity, phenomenal validity marks the boundary, and the boundary generates the negative noumenon.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:SD5","CH_BA94_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:RLE-08","text":"Possible experience is the hidden attractor toward which reflection, temporal synthesis, natural law, modal judgment, and noumenal limitation converge.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B01","text":"Representation or conception → cognitive-source assignment → distinction of sensible and intellectual contributions.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A1","CH_BA94_APPX:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible manifold + a priori form + schematized category → representation eligible for objective synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B03","text":"Contingent apprehension + apperceptive temporal rule → necessary connection → empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A2","CH_BA94_EXPE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B04","text":"Permanence + causal succession + reciprocal coexistence → one temporally unified phenomenal nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B05","text":"Unified nature + direct or lawfully connected perception → empirical reality beyond immediately perceived objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B06","text":"Temporal self-consciousness → requirement of permanence → external spatial matter → dependence of inner experience on outer experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:A11","CH_BA94_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B07","text":"Formal conformity / perceptual connection / causal determination → possibility / reality / necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FGS-B08","text":"Objective validity → restriction to phenomena → negative noumenal boundary → differentiated modes of emptiness and impossibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD7","CH_BA94_APPX:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AFM-B01","text":"Undifferentiated representation → transcendental source-assignment → sensible formalization → temporal synthesis → permanence, causality, and community → unified nature → modal testing → externality reversal → phenomenal restriction → negative boundary and differentiated nothing.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A4","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS3","CH_BA94_APPX:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:AFM-B02","text":"Source-less conceptual comparison → intellectualized phenomenon; apprehension without rule → subjective sequence; conception without experiential conformity → chimera; each failure returns critique to the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:D2","CH_BA94_EXPE:NX10","CH_BA94_EMPI:D2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-01","text":"Logical relations and objective relations become distinct validity-levels connected only through transcendental determination of source and use.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ME2","CH_BA94_APPX:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-02","text":"Experience is reclassified from accumulated perceptions into necessary synthesis under one consciousness.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:ME1","CH_BA94_EXPE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-03","text":"Temporal position is relocated from an absolute container to lawful relations among enduring, successive, and coexistent phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:ME2","CH_BA94_EXPE:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-04","text":"Substance, causality, and community are reclassified from descriptions of supersensible being into relational functions of phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SD3","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-05","text":"Modality is reclassified from intrinsic object-content into the status of a conception under experiential procedures.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:ME1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-06","text":"The priority of inner over outer experience is reversed by making empirical self-determination dependent on external permanence.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:ME4","CH_BA94_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-07","text":"Dogmatic ontology and skeptical idealism cease to be exhaustive alternatives because experiential restriction simultaneously limits and secures objective cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4","CH_BA94_APPX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MME-08","text":"Nonbeing is pluralized into contradiction, privation, empty intuition, and unsupported conception rather than treated as one homogeneous absence.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ME9","CH_BA94_APPX:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system tests comparison-concepts, temporal analogies, modal postulates, and the idealist priority of inner experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:AS4","CH_BA94_EXPE:AS4","CH_BA94_EMPI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, irreducible faculties, schematized categories, apperceptive synthesis, and modal procedures cooperate to generate objective cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ALT2","CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT2","CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, transcendental diagnosis and deduction replace both metaphysical construction and empirical induction by asking what conditions make experience possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ALT3","CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT3","CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality becomes one relational phenomenal field whose laws are necessary conditionally while things in themselves remain indeterminate.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:ALT4","CH_BA94_EMPI:ALT4","CH_BA94_APPX:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly generates a claim, tests the denial of its experiential condition, and then restricts the validated claim to its legitimate domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:FGS8","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-01","text":"The analogies are called regulative while functioning as necessary conditions through which empirical objects acquire objective temporal relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT5","CH_BA94_EXPE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-02","text":"The requirement for some permanent correlate of time does not by itself transparently establish conservation of the total quantum of phenomenal substance.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT6","CH_BA94_EXPE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-03","text":"The epistemic condition for cognizing coexistence is extended into the assertoric claim that all coexistent substances participate in complete dynamical reciprocity.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT7","CH_BA94_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-04","text":"The argument that only external matter supplies permanence compresses its transition from a temporal requirement to external experience and then describes the resulting experience as immediate.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT4","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-05","text":"Modality is described as subjectively synthetic while retaining objective validity, requiring objectivity to mean validity under shared experiential conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT6","CH_BA94_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:FL-06","text":"The transcendental object is described through causal language even though categorial determination beyond phenomena is prohibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT7","CH_BA94_APPX:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MP-01","text":"Reflection mutates from a diagnostic procedure for comparison-concepts into the universal provenance-test governing all principles of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:AS3","CH_BA94_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MP-02","text":"Substance mutates from a presumed noumenal entity into the permanent phenomenal correlate required for duration and change.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:ME3","CH_BA94_EXPE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MP-03","text":"Necessity mutates from absolute or logical status into conditioned dependence among phenomenal states.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:ME5","CH_BA94_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MP-04","text":"Externality reverses from a doubtful object of inference into the constitutive condition of determinate inner experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:ME4","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MP-05","text":"The noumenon mutates from a candidate supersensible object into a negative marker at which every usable criterion of cognition and modality terminates.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ME8","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a two-channel conversion machine in which sensible presentation and intellectual synthesis jointly produce objective experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:A2","CH_BA94_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a self-grounding interior substance but a synthetic node whose temporal self-relation depends on the external experiential field.","refs":["CH_BA94_EMPI:SD3","CH_BA94_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:HSI-03","text":"Time is an absent coordinate that cannot be perceived directly and must be rendered through permanent, successive, and reciprocal phenomenal relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:SNX2","CH_BA94_EXPE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:HSI-04","text":"Nature appears as a mesh of enduring nodes, causally ordered transitions, and reciprocally determining coexistents whose necessity remains conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:A16","CH_BA94_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:HSI-05","text":"Critical navigation remains possible through source-assignment, temporal rule-testing, and modal evaluation, but escape into a positively cognized supersensible domain is disallowed.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:ME1","CH_BA94_EMPI:A5","CH_BA94_APPX:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct source-reflection, temporal objectivity, the modal postulates, external dependence, phenomenal restriction, and the negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT1","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT1","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B02","text":"The relation among thing in itself, transcendental object, and negative noumenon remains underdetermined, as do particular substances, causes, powers, and empirical laws.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT2","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT2","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at this scale if the irreducibility of the faculties, intuition-dependence of categories, and restriction of principles to experience are retained.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT3","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT3","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where source-difference, necessary temporal ordering, and experiential modality independently converge on the same account of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT4","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT4","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when diagnosis, synthesis, modal testing, externality reversal, and boundary-enforcement remain recursively sequenced rather than flattened into static doctrines.","refs":["CH_BA94_APPX:MRT9","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT10","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_17:MRT-B06","text":"The conservation of phenomenal substance, complete dynamical reciprocity, exclusive presentation of permanence through external matter, and integration of the fourfold nothing remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT6","CH_BA94_EXPE:MRT7","CH_BA94_EMPI:MRT4","CH_BA94_APPX:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 49–51","context":"Appendix · Experience · Empicisim","original_title":"Chapters 49–51","original_context":"Appendix · Experience · Empicisim"},"original_title":"Chapters 49–51","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_18","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 52–54","order":18,"parents":["CH_BA94_TRAD","CH_BA94_ITIA","CH_BA94_IPRS"],"revision":"CL_BA94_18@R001","sha256":"6daca5f2bd7747e2897eb62b170dc7d6c66f10bb6b12210c912bebd0be4439d4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B01","text":"The declared dialectical field begins as a preparatory location whose title cannot substitute for an argument.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:AS3","CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B02","text":"Cognition is distributed across sensibility, understanding, and reason, while truth and error arise only where representations enter judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS1","CH_BA94_ITIA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B03","text":"Error begins when subjective sensory influence is unrecognized and is therefore treated as an objective ground of judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS2","CH_BA94_ITIA:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B04","text":"Reason organizes cognition by recursively seeking conditions for conditioned judgements until it demands an unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B05","text":"Transcendental illusion emerges when reason converts its subjective demand for systematic completion into an objective determination of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS5","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B06","text":"Possible experience functions as the boundary separating immanent cognitive employment from transcendent objectification.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B07","text":"Dialectic preserves reason’s regulative demand for unity while suspending its unauthorized constitutive extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AS-B08","text":"The system closes without final closure: criticism neutralizes illusion’s authority while retaining permanent vigilance against its recurrence.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Truth, error, and illusory appearance are assessed at the level of judgement, where subjective grounds can acquire objective status.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS1","CH_BA94_ITIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Reason differs from understanding because it systematizes rules under principles without directly determining sensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS2","CH_BA94_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The recursive search for conditions necessarily produces the demand for an unconditioned ground of cognitive completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Reason’s subjective law of systematic unity becomes transcendental illusion when treated as an objective law of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A13","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the criterion limiting the objective use of principles and excluding transcendent cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Dialectic is the critical separation of regulative systematic unity from constitutive claims about objects beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Criticism cannot abolish transcendental illusion because the rational tendency generating it recurs after its invalidity is disclosed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Sectional designation supplies orientation but cannot provide the predicates, premises, or proof required for doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:D1","CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] The decisive error-form is the conversion of subjective cognitive necessity into objective necessity in things.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS5","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The transition from logical ascent to an objectively given unconditioned totality is synthetic and therefore requires critical suspension.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-01","text":"“Introduction” retains its identity as a textual designation but mutates into a methodological prohibition against deriving doctrine from placement alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:A4","CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-02","text":"Error expands from an unrecognized sensory determination within judgement into a general structure in which rational subjectivity is projected as objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D1","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-03","text":"Logical illusion functions as the correctable contrast-case through which transcendental illusion is identified as natural, structural, and recurrent.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A9","CH_BA94_ITIA:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-04","text":"Reason shifts from a faculty of principles to the higher-order organizer of understanding whose drive toward unity generates its own excess.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A3","CH_BA94_IPRS:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-05","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a demanded terminus of regression into a dialectically prohibited object-claim when asserted as given.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-06","text":"Possible experience retains its identity as a limit but gains juridical force as the criterion of valid theoretical employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A6","CH_BA94_ITIA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RMD-07","text":"Critique changes function from decomposing an erroneous judgement into permanently regulating reason’s tendency to re-objectify its own maxims.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS2","CH_BA94_ITIA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-01","text":"The hierarchy of division, logic, dialectic, and introduction remains local textual machinery and cannot itself establish a substantive doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-02","text":"The local analysis of sensory interference scales upward as a general grammar of error: subjective determination is misrecognized as objective ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D2","CH_BA94_ITIA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-03","text":"The immanent–transcendent distinction scales upward from a classificatory distinction into the system’s universal boundary-condition for valid cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS4","CH_BA94_ITIA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-04","text":"The faculty hierarchy becomes a bridge proposition because reason’s systematic function supplies the genetic explanation of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS1","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-05","text":"The exclusion of empirical illusion remains local, while the resulting reclassification of dialectic as recurrent containment becomes current-level architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:NX5","CH_BA94_ITIA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:CGD-06","text":"The introductory absence becomes a current-level discipline of reconstruction: valid interpretation requires supplied predicates and argument rather than inferred completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT3","CH_BA94_TRAD:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-01","text":"Truth and error cannot be assigned to representations prior to judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-02","text":"Error cannot be attributed to sensibility alone or understanding alone, because it arises from their unrecognized relation in judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-03","text":"Subjective necessity, conceptual purity, and rational inevitability cannot by themselves validate an objective necessity in things.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX1","CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-04","text":"Logical ascent from conditioned cognition cannot directly establish an objectively existing unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:NX6","CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-05","text":"Principles valid only under possible experience cannot be extended into cognition of what lies beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX2","CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-06","text":"Critical exposure cannot remove transcendental illusion by the attentive correction sufficient for logical illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:NX8","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:GNX-07","text":"Sectional titles and classificatory hierarchy cannot count as substantive proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes any coincidence between subjective rational demand and objective ontological necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes a standpoint from which the complete series of conditions could be given, inspected, and empirically verified.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX3","CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes cognitive innocence: no faculty or critical disclosure can guarantee immunity from recurring transcendent projection.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX3","CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:DBN-04","text":"The system excludes a direct transition from inferential form to knowledge of unconditioned being.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:DNX1","CH_BA94_IPRS:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:DBN-05","text":"The system excludes reconstructing a completed dialectical doctrine from an introductory designation that withholds its argument.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX3","CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable coincidence structuring the system is that between reason’s demand for absolute completion and cognition’s capacity for objective verification.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSA-02","text":"The disallowed standpoint is one from which finite cognition could possess the totality of conditions without mediation, limit, or recurrence.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX4","CH_BA94_ITIA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSA-03","text":"The deepest prohibited authorization is the conversion of form, designation, or subjective demand into an objectively demonstrated object.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:SNX1","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSA-04","text":"The system denies final rational self-transparency: reason cannot retain its drive toward the unconditioned while ceasing to generate transcendent illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:SNX1","CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:XD-01","text":"The introductory withholding of doctrine becomes an operative warrant for dialectic: only an analysis of reason’s genesis and limits, rather than a heading, can establish its claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-06","CL_BA94_18:GA-08","CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:XD-02","text":"Sensory error and transcendental illusion share a structure of subjective-to-objective misattribution, but require distinct critical operations because they arise at different cognitive scales.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-01","CL_BA94_18:GA-09","CH_BA94_ITIA:AS2","CH_BA94_ITIA:A5","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:XD-03","text":"The demand for an unconditioned produces illusion only when logical regression crosses the synthetic threshold into objective assertion.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-03","CL_BA94_18:GA-10","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:XD-04","text":"Possible experience functions not merely as an external prohibition but as the rule assigning systematic unity a regulative rather than constitutive status.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-05","CL_BA94_18:GA-06","CH_BA94_ITIA:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:XD-05","text":"Because illusion persists after exposure, critique converts cognition from a project of terminal correction into a practice of recurrent monitoring.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-06","CL_BA94_18:GA-07","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSD-01","text":"Dialectic becomes a discipline governing when rational unity may organize cognition without counting as knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:SD4","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSD-02","text":"Logical necessity loses ontological authority without losing organizational necessity within cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSD-03","text":"Rational time becomes recursive rather than terminal: every achieved condition can reopen the regress, and every exposed illusion can recur.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSD-04","text":"The prohibition against treating a heading as doctrine is structurally homologous to the prohibition against treating logical form as objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX2","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:BSD-05","text":"The unconditioned survives as a necessary horizon of rational organization while remaining inadmissible as a directly determinable object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX3","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned judgement generates a search for condition; each condition is recoded as conditioned; recursion therefore converges on the demand for the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RLE-02","text":"Subjective rational demand becomes objective projection, projection becomes illusion, criticism exposes the projection, and recurrence reactivates it.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS5","CH_BA94_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RLE-03","text":"A local loop of sensory interference, erroneous judgement, and transcendental reflection is nested within the larger rational loop of systematic ascent and objectification.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS1","CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RLE-04","text":"The unconditioned is the central conceptual attractor because it organizes regression while resisting conversion into an available object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A10","CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience does not terminate reason’s search; it recodes the status of the search by blocking constitutive claims at the boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D3","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B01","text":"A textual designation establishes a dialectical site while withholding the predicates needed to infer a completed doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:AS1","CH_BA94_TRAD:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B02","text":"Sensibility supplies intuition, understanding judges under rules, and reason orders rules toward systematic unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A1","CH_BA94_ITIA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B03","text":"Unrecognized sensory influence distorts judgement by converting subjective grounds into objective grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A4","CH_BA94_ITIA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B04","text":"Rational regression through conditions generates the subjective demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B05","text":"Synthetic objectification of that demand crosses the boundary of possible experience and generates transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FGS-B06","text":"Dialectical critique suspends objective extension, preserves regulative unity, and institutes ongoing vigilance rather than final elimination.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B01","text":"Textual threshold → substantive reconstruction is suspended until the missing argument is supplied.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:AS3","CH_BA94_TRAD:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B02","text":"Cognitive hierarchy → sensibility, understanding, and reason are differentiated by distinct functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B03","text":"Composite judgement → error is located in the unrecognized attribution of subjective grounds as objective grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B04","text":"Immanent boundary → possible experience is fixed as the limit of objectively valid principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B05","text":"Rational regression → the demand for an unconditioned completion is generated.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B06","text":"Synthetic objectification → logical completeness is asserted as an objective totality and becomes transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B07","text":"Critical separation → systematic unity is retained as regulative while transcendent cognition is denied constitutive authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:AFM-B08","text":"Recurrent containment → criticism continues because illusion loses authority without losing its appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A11","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MME-01","text":"Illusion is reclassified from an apparent property of objects or sensation into a defective attribution of grounds within judgement.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MME-02","text":"The limit of experience is reclassified from an empirical lack into a constitutive boundary governing valid theoretical principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MME-03","text":"The opposition between rational order and rational error collapses because reason’s lawful drive toward unity is also the source of dialectical excess.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:ME5","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MME-04","text":"Formal inferential validity and objective validity are separated, making logical coherence insufficient for ontological commitment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MME-05","text":"The unconditioned is remapped from an allegedly available object into a problem-horizon produced by rational regression.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system differentiates logical, empirical, and transcendental forms of illusory appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: cognition is a mediated hierarchy in which sensibility supplies intuition, understanding supplies rules, and reason organizes systematic unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: criticism demarcates valid employment, exposes illegitimate extension, and converts cognition into permanent vigilance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:ALT3","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: rational regression projects a totality of conditions whose completed givenness cannot be assumed within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:ALT4","CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the system performs its own critical rule by refusing to infer doctrinal content from headings and by exposing projected authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:ALT3","CH_BA94_ITIA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FL-01","text":"Reason must pursue completion in order to organize cognition, yet that same pursuit continually pressures cognition toward claims prohibited by critical limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FL-02","text":"Critique is effective enough to neutralize the authority of illusion but ineffective as a final cure for illusion’s recurrence.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:A12","CH_BA94_ITIA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:FL-03","text":"The system requires a reliable criterion separating subjective maxim from objective principle while leaving that criterion underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MP-01","text":"Error mutates from a composite distortion within judgement into a structural rational projection beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D1","CH_BA94_IPRS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MP-02","text":"The unconditioned changes status at the point where a logical demand for completion is converted into a synthetic assertion of objective totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:AS4","CH_BA94_IPRS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MP-03","text":"Critique mutates from corrective decomposition into permanent regulation once illusion is recognized as natural to reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:D6","CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:HSI-01","text":"The system images cognition as a stratified relay in which sensibility provides material, understanding rules, and reason organizes the resulting order.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:A1","CH_BA94_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:HSI-02","text":"The system images reason as a recursive engine that continually exceeds each achieved condition in pursuit of a totality it cannot know as given.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:D3","CH_BA94_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:HSI-03","text":"The subject is a finite operator without faculty-level innocence or access to a standpoint from which all conditions are simply present.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:DNX3","CH_BA94_IPRS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in preserving rational orientation toward unity while refusing to convert that orientation into knowledge of transcendent objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:SD4","CH_BA94_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from cognitive hierarchy through rational regression to critical restraint.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the operational criterion that distinguishes subjective rational maxims from objectively valid principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent only insofar as immanent experiential validity remains distinct from reason’s regulative demand for systematic unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IPRS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it reconstructs dialectic as recurrent containment rather than a final removal of false appearance.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITIA:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_18:MRT-B05","text":"An irreducible remainder persists: the introductory designation cannot yield the doctrinal exposition it explicitly withholds.","refs":["CH_BA94_TRAD:DNX1","CH_BA94_TRAD:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 52–54","context":"Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","original_title":"Chapters 52–54","original_context":"Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance"},"original_title":"Chapters 52–54","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_19","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 55–57","order":19,"parents":["CH_BA94_TDBI","CH_BA94_SIIG","CH_BA94_SITI"],"revision":"CL_BA94_19@R001","sha256":"caeb1b5981f3bbf2523f8ab8caaed698c611fee40bfc6607115b74be212c384e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-01","text":"Rational conceptions cannot claim objective validity merely because inference appears to produce them correctly; their use requires critical adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS5","CH_BA94_TDBI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-02","text":"The system divides cognition between understanding, which determines objects within possible experience, and reason, which seeks a comprehension exceeding every empirical attainment.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS3","CH_BA94_TDBI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-03","text":"Reason converts every conditioned cognition into a demand for the totality of conditions and thereby introduces the unconditioned as its necessary horizon.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:AS2","CH_BA94_SITI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-04","text":"Ideas are structurally unpresentable as empirical objects while remaining real as practical standards or regulative orientations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS3","CH_BA94_SITI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-05","text":"Syllogistic relations supply the formal architecture through which reason generates distinct classes of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:AS4","CH_BA94_SITI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-06","text":"Critique transforms reason’s transcendent tendency from a claim to supersensible objecthood into a rule for the systematic use of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A13","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-07","text":"The nonrealization of an idea becomes a task of approximation and judgment rather than evidence against its authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AS-08","text":"The cluster closes through nonpossessive orientation: systematic unity and practical direction are retained while determinate metaphysical possession remains deferred or prohibited.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SD5","CH_BA94_TDBI:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective determination of objects depends upon conditions of possible experience and cannot be extended without qualification beyond that domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_TDBI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Reason necessarily transforms conditioned cognition into a search for totality of grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A4","CH_BA94_SITI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned is necessary as the horizon of totality but unavailable as an object of empirical presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A7","CH_BA94_SITI:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Ideas arise through rational inference, become operative in practical freedom, and regulate the systematic use of understanding without becoming empirical objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A1","CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The absence of a corresponding empirical object does not eliminate an idea’s reality, necessity, or legitimate function.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A5","CH_BA94_SITI:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Systematic unity is the legitimate theoretical surrogate for unavailable knowledge of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SD5","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Validity divides into empirical object-determination, practical normativity, and regulative systematization; none authorizes speculative hypostatization.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A9","CH_BA94_SIIG:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Critique is the tribunal that preserves reason’s necessary excess while denying it unrestricted constitutive use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] The asymmetry between completed regress toward grounds and indefinitely open progress through consequences prevents rational totality from becoming a finished possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A15","CH_BA94_SITI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-01","text":"Understanding retains a stable function across the cluster: it supplies the a priori conditions under which phenomena can count as empirically determinable objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A2","CH_BA94_TDBI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-02","text":"The idea mutates from a terminal taxonomic category into a practical archetype and then into a formally generated rule of systematic orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SITI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-03","text":"The unconditioned shifts from a general terminus of rational inference into the formally articulated totality generated by the three syllogistic relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS4","CH_BA94_SITI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-04","text":"Nonempirical status reverses valence: what initially marks the absence of an object becomes the condition of practical authority and regulative use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS3","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-05","text":"Platonic nomenclature is retained only after mutation into a critically delimited structural insight, while its mystical and hypostatizing implications are discarded.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RMD-06","text":"Nonrealization migrates from apparent inadequacy to a positive mechanism of approximation, reform, and open-ended judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:CGD-01","text":"Stable terminology is locally necessary for preserving distinctions among representations, but scales upward as the condition for preventing category mistakes between empirical, practical, and regulative uses.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A1","CH_BA94_SIIG:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:CGD-02","text":"The derivation of categories from judgement remains local machinery; at cluster scale it functions chiefly as the contrast enabling the derivation of ideas from syllogism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A2","CH_BA94_SITI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:CGD-03","text":"The unconditioned subject, series-ground, and system of division remain local specifications, while their current-level function is to establish the exhaustiveness of reason’s totalizing demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A9","CH_BA94_SITI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:CGD-04","text":"Reciprocal legal freedom is a local exemplary idea, whereas the cluster-level proposition is that practical norms precede and judge empirical institutions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A9","CH_BA94_SIIG:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:CGD-05","text":"The initially deferred distinction between legitimate and sophistical ideas becomes a cluster-wide transition mechanism culminating in the restriction of constitutive transcendence.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:NX7","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects unrestricted application of concepts of understanding beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GNX-02","text":"An idea is excluded from the fields of sensation, intuition, empirical concept, and category.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GNX-03","text":"Empirical examples, customs, and prevailing conduct cannot supply the archetype or ground of ethical and juridical norms.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:NX4","CH_BA94_SIIG:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GNX-04","text":"Neither apparent inferential derivability nor formal logical necessity establishes objective knowledge of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:NX6","CH_BA94_SITI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes both empiricist denial of ideals and dogmatic metaphysical treatment of ideas as independently given beings.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIIG:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:DBN-01","text":"The architecture disqualifies any homogeneous theory in which all a priori conceptions possess the same origin, function, and entitlement to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:DNX1","CH_BA94_SITI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:DBN-02","text":"The architecture excludes the possibility that legitimate cognition is exhausted by sensible presentation while also excluding supersensible object-possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:DNX1","CH_BA94_SITI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:DBN-03","text":"The actuality of institutions and historical conduct cannot function as the measure of normative legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX2","CH_BA94_SIIG:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:DBN-04","text":"Conditioned cognition cannot achieve final intelligibility through a ground it can empirically possess, because its truth-claim invokes unavailable totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SD4","CH_BA94_SITI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSA-01","text":"The system circles an absent adequate presentation of totality: reason must require the unconditioned precisely where no experience can exhibit it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SNX1","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable final reconciliation between rational ideal and complete realization sustains, rather than terminates, practical obligation and approximation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX2","CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSA-03","text":"The deepest prohibition is against a standpoint that could possess the unconditioned while remaining a finite knower of conditioned objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SNX3","CH_BA94_SITI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-01","text":"The excess of ideas beyond experience divides functionally: practical ideas orient freedom and action, while theoretical ideas regulate systematic unity, and neither mode licenses constitutive supersensible cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SITI:A14","CH_BA94_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-02","text":"A nonpresentable standard can direct both the assessment of empirical cognition and the reform of institutions without becoming an empirical item within either domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A8","CH_BA94_SIIG:AS4","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-03","text":"Critical limitation is not simple confinement to experience; it is the allocation of reason’s excess into differentiated practical and regulative uses.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A12","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-04","text":"Empirical noncompletion becomes productive when it is treated as the condition for continued approximation in practice and continued systematization in theory.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5","CH_BA94_SITI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-05","text":"Taxonomic precision and formal derivation jointly recast ideas as necessary rational functions whose misuse, rather than whose existence, is the primary danger.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A2","CH_BA94_SITI:A3","CH_BA94_SITI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:XD-06","text":"The distinction between practical reality and regulative validity remains structurally necessary but incompletely specified, because both modes retain efficacy without corresponding empirical objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT5","CH_BA94_SITI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a three-tier architecture of validity: constitutive cognition for empirical objects, practical authority for freedom, and regulative unity for theoretical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:SD1","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSD-02","text":"Metaphysical excess is recoded as a necessary self-limiting output of reason rather than as either dispensable illusion or attainable supersensible knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SD1","CH_BA94_SITI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSD-03","text":"The absence of completed totality keeps both theoretical systematization and practical freedom temporally open.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SNX1","CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:BSD-04","text":"Inferential truth becomes a task of orientation toward systematic completeness rather than the possession of a final empirically exhibited ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:SD4","CH_BA94_SITI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned cognition generates regress toward totality; the unconditioned cannot be presented; the prohibition on presentation returns reason to the systematic ordering of conditioned cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A7","CH_BA94_SITI:A11","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RLE-02","text":"Terminological differentiation produces functional differentiation; functional differentiation requires critique; critique preserves the terminological distinctions that prevent renewed confusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A1","CH_BA94_SIIG:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RLE-03","text":"Practical ideals produce judgment of imperfect actuality; that judgment produces approximation; approximation renews the ideal’s authority without exhausting it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIG:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RLE-04","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned attracts every series of conditions while permanently withdrawing from empirical capture.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A7","CH_BA94_SITI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:RLE-05","text":"The architecture spirals through completed regress and open progress: grounds are demanded as total, while consequences remain indefinitely extendable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A15","CH_BA94_SITI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B01","text":"A priori cognition differentiates into understanding-based conditions of experience and reason-based demands for totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS1","CH_BA94_SITI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B02","text":"Understanding constitutes empirical object-determination by unifying phenomena under conditions valid only within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS2","CH_BA94_TDBI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B03","text":"Reason subsumes the conditioned under more universal conditions and thereby generates a regress toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A4","CH_BA94_SITI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B04","text":"The unconditioned is required for absolute totality but denied adequate sensible presentation and constitutive objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A8","CH_BA94_SITI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B05","text":"Ideas are redistributed as practical archetypes for freedom and regulative rules for theoretical systematicity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS3","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FGS-B06","text":"Critique blocks hypostatization, preserves differentiated validity, and returns reason’s excess to open-ended orientation rather than final possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS7","CH_BA94_SITI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B01","text":"Possible experience establishes the domain within which understanding can determine objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B02","text":"Conditioned cognition initiates a rational regress toward the totality of its grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B03","text":"Totality of grounds yields the conception of the unconditioned and the formally differentiated classes of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:AS3","CH_BA94_SITI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B04","text":"The lack of adequate sensible presentation blocks constitutive transcendence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B05","text":"Practical freedom retains ideas as archetypal standards for judgment, obligation, and institutional reform.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:AS4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B06","text":"Theoretical reason retains ideas as regulative rules for systematic unity of understanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:AFM-B07","text":"Nonrealization and noncompletion become engines of approximation and further systematization rather than grounds for abandoning rational orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS5","CH_BA94_SITI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MME-01","text":"The conceptual field is reorganized so that nonempirical status no longer entails fiction, while empirical objecthood no longer exhausts cognitive or practical validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A5","CH_BA94_SITI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MME-02","text":"The boundary of experience becomes an allocative criterion distinguishing constitutive, practical, and regulative functions rather than a simple prohibition on rational excess.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:ME2","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MME-03","text":"Metaphysical ideas are reclassified from candidate objects of knowledge into necessary functions of orientation, regulation, and critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:ME1","CH_BA94_SITI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MME-04","text":"The ideal-instance relation is reversed: the rational archetype judges empirical instances, while instances display only partial realization or deviation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:ME2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MME-05","text":"Temporal structure is redrawn as completed regress toward grounds, indefinitely open progress through consequences, and unclosed approximation of freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:ME6","CH_BA94_SIIG:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level differentiates conceptions of understanding, ideas of reason, practical ideals, and the three classes of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS2","CH_BA94_SITI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level distributes cognition across empirical synthesis, inferential totalization, practical causality, and regulative systematization.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:FGS2","CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts critique into a procedure for assigning legitimate uses to reason without abolishing reason’s demand for the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:D7","CH_BA94_SITI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level posits a reality in which conditioned objects are experienceable, complete grounds are unpresentable, and freedom remains open to approximation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIIG:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level enacts its claim by repeatedly generating rational excess, restricting it, and preserving it in transformed use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS7","CH_BA94_SITI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FL-01","text":"[Tension] Objective reality is tied to possible experience, yet cognition without a corresponding empirical object is asserted to possess practical reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A4","CH_BA94_SIIG:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FL-02","text":"[Tension] Practical ideas are said to become operative causes, but the basis on which an unpresentable cognition acquires such efficacy remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FL-03","text":"[Tension] The transition from the logical forms of syllogism to an exhaustive architecture of transcendental ideas is compressed rather than fully demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:AS4","CH_BA94_SITI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:FL-04","text":"[Tension] Regulative unity must guide specific systematization without covertly committing reason to the objective reality of what it regulates toward.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A14","CH_BA94_SITI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MP-01","text":"[Mutation] “Idea” changes function from a taxonomic designation to a practical archetype and then to a theoretically regulative concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:A3","CH_BA94_SIIG:FGS4","CH_BA94_SITI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MP-02","text":"[Mutation] “Validity” changes from empirical objective reality to practical authority and finally to regulative legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:A9","CH_BA94_SIIG:A8","CH_BA94_SITI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:HSI-01","text":"The system imagines reality as a stratified field in which conditioned objects can appear, but their complete grounds cannot be possessed as objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:AS3","CH_BA94_SITI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite rational agent compelled to seek totality, capable of practical orientation, and unable to convert that compulsion into supersensible possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:D1","CH_BA94_SIIG:A6","CH_BA94_SITI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:HSI-03","text":"Time is split between backward demand for completed grounds and forward openness of consequences, reform, and approximation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:A15","CH_BA94_SITI:A16","CH_BA94_SIIG:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:HSI-04","text":"Critique functions as navigational control: it neither abolishes rational totalization nor allows totalization to harden into dogmatic objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:SD5","CH_BA94_SITI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s decisive architecture: experience-bound objecthood, rational totalization, unpresentable ideas, differentiated validity, and critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_TDBI:MRT2","CH_BA94_SITI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive mechanism through which practical ideas are real and regulative ideas prescribe determinate unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT5","CH_BA94_SITI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MRT-B03","text":"The cluster is coherent when ideas are treated neither as empirical objects nor as arbitrary fictions, but as critically delimited practical and regulative functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIG:MRT3","CH_BA94_SITI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_19:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it turns unavailable completion into a source of systematic and practical orientation; it depends on recursive restriction rather than final deductive closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SITI:MRT4","CH_BA94_SITI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 55–57","context":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","original_title":"Chapters 55–57","original_context":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas"},"original_title":"Chapters 55–57","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_20","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 58–60","order":20,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIST","CH_BA94_TD_2","CH_BA94_CIPP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_20@R001","sha256":"0ab12c61339c6c1d7d76542f56a9b3f47c75baebcd92e6b085bee3568809c770","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-01","text":"Reason necessarily seeks the unconditioned but converts that legitimate demand into illegitimate claims of objective reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AS2","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-02","text":"The triad of subject, world, and supreme being is a generative grammar that distributes one demand across three forms of metaphysical totalization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:D1","CH_BA94_TD_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-03","text":"Logical form generates the architecture of transcendental ideas without warranting cognition of their corresponding objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SD2","CH_BA94_SIST:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-04","text":"The classificatory triad mutates into a taxonomy of paralogism, antinomy, and ideal when reason treats its necessary ideas as premises for transcendent inference.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:AS5","CH_BA94_TD_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-05","text":"The paralogism instantiates the first branch by projecting categories onto formal self-consciousness and equivocating between a subject-function and an intuited object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:AS4","CH_BA94_CIPP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-06","text":"Dialectical error is structural because valid logical forms become false metaphysical determinations when used beyond their objective conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:D5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-07","text":"Critique therefore regulates unavoidable illusion, converts failed doctrine into reciprocal dogmatic restraint, and withholds speculative closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:AS6","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AS-08","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: reason cannot stop positing totality, yet each attempted objectification returns it to disciplinary limitation and practical relocation.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX6","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Reason necessarily ascends from conditioned cognition toward unconditioned unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires intuition governed by the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A5","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The triad of self, world, and supreme being becomes the dialectical triad of paralogism, antinomy, and ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A17","CH_BA94_TD_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The subjective necessity of an idea never by itself establishes knowledge of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AS5","CH_BA94_TD_2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Logical form is both the generative grammar of rational ideas and the vehicle of dialectical error when transferred into ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SD2","CH_BA94_TD_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The thinking subject is demanded as an absolute unity but remains unavailable as a determinate object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Reason’s system-producing power becomes a structurally necessary power of overreach once its forms are treated as object-determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SD1","CH_BA94_TD_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental illusion cannot be eliminated, although assent to its conclusions can be disciplined.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Rational psychology survives speculative collapse only as a negative discipline restraining opposed dogmatisms.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:AS6","CH_BA94_CIPP:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Claims about existence beyond present life are removed from theoretical proof and preserved only as practically oriented commitments.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:AS7","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned remains operationally necessary precisely where it remains unavailable within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX4","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-01","text":"The demand for the unconditioned retains stable identity while its products mutate from ideas into dialectical inferences and disciplinary problems.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-02","text":"Subject, world, and supreme being recur with altered function as paralogism, antinomy, and ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AS4","CH_BA94_TD_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-03","text":"Subjective deduction shifts from legitimating the necessary origin of ideas to explaining why their corresponding illusions are unavoidable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A14","CH_BA94_TD_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-04","text":"Syllogistic form shifts from a source of transcendental architecture to the medium of its own transcendental misuse.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:ME4","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-05","text":"The absolute unity of the thinking subject contracts into an indeterminate transcendental subject cognizable only through its acts of thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:A4","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-06","text":"The absence of objective deduction expands into a criterion requiring intuition for every objectively valid categorical determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-07","text":"Reason’s upward search for conditions is displaced into a circular attempt to explain the subject through categories that already presuppose its unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:D5","CH_BA94_CIPP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-08","text":"The closed triadic classification recedes after supplying the architecture for the detailed diagnosis of its first branch.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AF7","CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RMD-09","text":"Speculative suspension becomes practical relocation after the soul’s projected ontology has been dismantled.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SD5","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-01","text":"The exact correspondence between syllogistic forms and transcendental ideas remains a local classificatory mechanism rather than an objective proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A18","CH_BA94_SIST:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-02","text":"The antinomy and ideal remain taxonomic branches at this scale because only the paralogism receives detailed diagnostic execution.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A12","CH_BA94_TD_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-03","text":"The intensive diminution of consciousness remains a local counterexample whose wider function is to defeat an auxiliary proof of permanence.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between rational necessity and objective knowledge scales upward because it governs idea-production, dialectical illusion, and the critique of the soul.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:DNX3","CH_BA94_TD_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-05","text":"The categorical production of absolute subject-unity bridges the general system of ideas to the paralogism and its reduction to “I think.”","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A10","CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-06","text":"The attribution of objective reality catalyzes the transition from general dialectical diagnosis to testing every soul-predicate against the conditions of intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-07","text":"Rational psychology’s status as an objective science is superseded by its recoding as a boundary-discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:NX4","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:CGD-08","text":"Practical relocation scales upward as a terminal consequence, but its positive warrant remains dependent on moral premises external to this cluster.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:D13","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-01","text":"Transcendental ideas are not empirically presentable objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:NX2","CH_BA94_TD_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-02","text":"Subjective rational necessity is not objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:DNX3","CH_BA94_TD_2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-03","text":"No unestablished logical-transcendental homology licenses the transfer from formal subjecthood to substantial existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SNX2","CH_BA94_CIPP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-04","text":"Dialectical illusion is neither an accidental fabrication nor something removable by greater intelligence.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:NX6","CH_BA94_TD_2:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-05","text":"Logical simplicity and analytical identity are not metaphysical simplicity and diachronic personal identity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:NX5","CH_BA94_CIPP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-06","text":"The failure of one speculative account does not establish its contrary or vindicate an opposed dogmatism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX7","CH_BA94_CIPP:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-07","text":"Practical necessity cannot be converted into theoretical cognition of the soul.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX8","CH_BA94_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:GNX-08","text":"A totality of consequences, including a complete future series, is not required as a transcendental condition of present cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:NX5","CH_BA94_SIST:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes a theoretically transparent subject capable of occupying an unconditioned standpoint on its own cognitive activity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-02","text":"The necessity of reason’s demand cannot be treated as evidence that its unconditioned object exists or can enter experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIST:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-03","text":"Abstraction from empirical conditions cannot establish that the represented being exists independently of those conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX4","CH_BA94_CIPP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-04","text":"Logical thinkability cannot exhaust the conditions of real possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-05","text":"Critique cannot abolish the structure producing error because no standpoint wholly outside reason is available.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX8","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:DBN-06","text":"Materialism and spiritualism cannot function as exhaustive alternatives when contrary constructions possess equal speculative standing.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:D7","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an intuitable unconditioned object within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX1","CH_BA94_TD_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSA-02","text":"No position external to reason can permanently terminate reason’s dialectical tendency.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX2","CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSA-03","text":"The subject cannot complete theoretical reason by transforming its indispensable unifying function into a self-grounding object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSA-04","text":"The unavailable completion of reason is the absence that perpetually regenerates its demand for totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX5","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-01","text":"The triadic distribution of unconditioned unity becomes a taxonomy of dialectical error once each idea receives an unwarranted attribution of objective reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-07","CH_BA94_SIST:D1","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-02","text":"Subjective deduction is structurally double: it legitimates the necessary genesis of ideas while simultaneously explaining the inevitability of their illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-04","CL_BA94_20:GA-08","CH_BA94_SIST:A14","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-03","text":"The absence of objective deduction becomes operationally decisive when categories are shown to possess objective reality only through sensuous intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-02","CL_BA94_20:GA-04","CH_BA94_SIST:A13","CH_BA94_CIPP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-04","text":"The paralogism demonstrates how logical form can generate a determinate but unsupported ontology through categorical projection and amplification.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-05","CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIST:SD2","CH_BA94_CIPP:D1","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-05","text":"Because the object is unavailable while the illusion is ineradicable, critique must regulate assent rather than suppress rational totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-02","CL_BA94_20:GA-08","CL_BA94_20:GA-11","CH_BA94_TD_2:A3","CH_BA94_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-06","text":"The collapse of substantial self-knowledge imposes symmetrical restraint on spiritualist affirmations and materialist denials.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CL_BA94_20:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIST:A6","CH_BA94_CIPP:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-07","text":"Theoretical limitation opens a protected practical domain without turning practical commitment into speculative knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIST:SD5","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:XD-08","text":"The ascent toward conditions becomes circular when the subject attempts to derive its own unity through categories that already presuppose that unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-01","CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIST:A15","CH_BA94_CIPP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-01","text":"The system of pure reason becomes a map of reason’s own operations and failures rather than a map of independently cognized metaphysical beings.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-05","CL_BA94_20:GA-07","CH_BA94_SIST:ME5","CH_BA94_TD_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-02","text":"The subject acquires a split status as the indispensable condition of cognition and an indeterminate object of theoretical inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-02","CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD3","CH_BA94_CIPP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-03","text":"Metaphysical predicates are reclassified as hypostatized expansions of logical roles rather than discovered properties of transcendent objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-05","CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CH_BA94_TD_2:SD2","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-04","text":"Inner time presents phenomenal alteration but cannot establish substantial personal persistence or a completed totality of future changes.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-02","CL_BA94_20:GA-06","CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD6","CH_BA94_SIST:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-05","text":"Critical method becomes immanent regulation because it diagnoses illusion as an effect of reason’s own laws.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-07","CL_BA94_20:GA-08","CH_BA94_TD_2:A9","CH_BA94_TD_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-06","text":"Theoretical unknowability protects practical orientation from speculative disproof without supplying theoretical cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:GA-10","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD7","CH_BA94_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:BSD-07","text":"Rational wisdom changes the relation to illusion but cannot change the generative existence of the demand producing it.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-08","CL_BA94_20:GA-11","CH_BA94_TD_2:SD5","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned synthesis generates a demand for the unconditioned; the demand becomes objective attribution; critical failure returns reason to the still-active demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AS2","CH_BA94_TD_2:A7","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-02","text":"The triadic distribution of representations repeatedly branches one totalizing mechanism into subject-unity, series-unity, and totality-of-beings.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS3","CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-03","text":"The self-objectification loop uses categories grounded in apperceptive unity to explain that same unity as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A14","CH_BA94_CIPP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-04","text":"The attribution of substance escalates by combinatory amplification into simplicity, personality, spirituality, permanence, and immortality.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-05","text":"Critical suspension blocks assent, but the conditions generating error persist and reactivate the need for vigilance.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:D6","CH_BA94_TD_2:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-06","text":"The subject-idea undergoes a phase change from rational destination to paralogistic object and finally to a reciprocal prohibition on dogmatism.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:RLE-07","text":"The absent knowable unconditioned functions as the cluster’s conceptual attractor because it remains operative wherever cognition denies it objective availability.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX5","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B01","text":"Logical form is separated from transcendental content so that pure reason can be isolated as the source of non-empirical ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B02","text":"Conditioned synthesis generates an ascent toward unconditioned unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B03","text":"Relations of representation and syllogistic forms distribute that ascent into subject, world, and supreme being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIST:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B04","text":"The absence of empirical objects blocks objective deduction while leaving subjective generation intact.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B05","text":"Non-empirical syllogisms move from something known toward the unconditioned and attribute objective reality to it.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS4","CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B06","text":"The first branch removes empirical predicates and projects the category table onto “I think.”","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS1","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B07","text":"Critical testing separates determining consciousness from the determinable self, demands intuition, and exposes the governing equivocation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS4","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS5","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FGS-B08","text":"The failed ontology is converted into reciprocal restraint and its trans-experiential claims are relocated to practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS8","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned synthesis → demand for unconditioned unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B02","text":"Unconditioned demand → distribution into self, world, and supreme being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B03","text":"Triadic distribution → subjective syllogistic genesis without empirical objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B04","text":"Subjective genesis → non-empirical inference beyond adequate object-knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B05","text":"Non-empirical inference → objective attribution → dialectical sophism.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:AF3","CH_BA94_TD_2:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B06","text":"Common dialectical mechanism → paralogism as the branch of absolute subject-unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:AS5","CH_BA94_TD_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B07","text":"Paralogism → empirical exclusion → categorical projection onto “I think” → exposure of equivocation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS1","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:AFM-B08","text":"Collapse of soul-ontology → reciprocal dogmatic restraint → practical relocation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS8","CH_BA94_CIPP:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-01","text":"The boundary between empirical cognition and rational totalization becomes the primary map of metaphysical legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-02","text":"Psychology, cosmology, and theology are reclassified from object-domains into forms of one totalizing operation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:SD3","CH_BA94_TD_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-03","text":"The opposition between reason and illusion collapses because reason itself generates the illusion critique must regulate.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-04","text":"The Ego is reclassified from a cognized substance into the formal and indeterminate vehicle of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-05","text":"Materialism and spiritualism cease to define an exhaustive theoretical terrain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-06","text":"Critique is redefined as regulation and boundary-discipline rather than eradication of dialectic or production of a rival ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:ME6","CH_BA94_CIPP:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MME-07","text":"Immortality is displaced from demonstrable soul-property into a practical commitment protected from speculative disproof.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system classifies three transcendental ideas and tests the soul-predicates projected from the first.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:ALT1","CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, non-empirical syllogistic reasoning converts unconditioned demand into object-claims through categorical transfer without mediating intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT2","CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique accepts the persistence of illusion while converting failed doctrine into restraint and practical redirection.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:ALT3","CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is knowable only as conditioned appearance, while the temporally presented self remains noumenally indeterminate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:ALT4","CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the cluster generates a totalizing architecture, enters its first branch, and reverses construction into disciplinary closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:FGS6","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-01","text":"The logical-transcendental analogy remains unestablished even though logical form performs indispensable taxonomic and generative work.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIST:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-02","text":"The distinction between legitimate syllogistic origin and sophistical result lacks a fully specified formal criterion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:SD6","CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-03","text":"The system oscillates between denying an adequate conception of the unconditioned and preserving it as problematic thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-04","text":"“I think” shifts among pure apperception, intellectual Ego-representation, and empirically conditioned existential occurrence.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-05","text":"The negative use of apperceptive simplicity against materialist reduction approaches the ontological use of simplicity rejected elsewhere.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-06","text":"Practical relocation exceeds what the cluster can positively ground because its moral and teleological premises remain external.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:FL-07","text":"The operative triad of soul, world, and supreme being remains incompletely integrated with the practical triad of God, freedom, and immortality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIST:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MP-01","text":"The neutral architecture of necessary ideas mutates into a systematic taxonomy of reason’s intrinsic errors.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:D6","CH_BA94_TD_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MP-02","text":"Absolute subject-unity mutates from a rational destination into a paralogistic construction centered on an indeterminate subject.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MP-03","text":"Rational psychology mutates from a purported positive doctrine into a boundary-discipline of speculative reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:NX13","CH_BA94_CIPP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of conditioned presentations recursively referred toward a final condition that cannot itself become an object of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:A2","CH_BA94_TD_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:HSI-02","text":"Reason is a triadic inference engine that projects its unconditioned horizon as an object and thereby manufactures unavoidable illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS1","CH_BA94_TD_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:HSI-03","text":"The subject is an indispensable but blind operational center: a transcendental x that cannot convert self-consciousness into self-intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A4","CH_BA94_CIPP:DNX2","CH_BA94_CIPP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:HSI-04","text":"Time is the medium of phenomenal self-presentation but supplies no permanent Ego; navigation therefore consists in critical restraint and practical orientation rather than speculative self-possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:A9","CH_BA94_CIPP:A11","CH_BA94_CIPP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from necessary idea-production through dialectical illusion to the paralogism’s critical dismantling.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT1","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B02","text":"The logical-transcendental analogy and the undeveloped mechanisms of antinomy and ideal remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:MRT6","CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at this scale because the restriction blocking objective deduction also explains both illusion and its intuition-based diagnosis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIST:MRT3","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it distinguishes determining consciousness from the determinable self and exposes their syllogistic equivocation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction requires the sequence from triadic architecture to dialectical taxonomy, concrete self-objectification, and disciplinary reversal.","refs":["CH_BA94_TD_2:MRT8","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_20:MRT-B06","text":"Full axiomatic closure remains impossible because the ambiguity of “I think” and the external dependence of practical warrant resist reduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT6","CH_BA94_CIPP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 58–60","context":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","original_title":"Chapters 58–60","original_context":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 58–60","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_21","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 61–63","order":21,"parents":["CH_BA94_CIAP","CH_BA94_SISC","CH_BA94_SIAP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_21@R001","sha256":"25a4883ac567298e13d46f550da4abb0b973b883cfc79b0b690c1f23534d35f2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B01","text":"The system must derive cosmological problems systematically without presupposing that the world-whole is an available object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B02","text":"Understanding supplies categorial forms, while reason converts conditioned synthesis into a demand for absolute completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AS2","CH_BA94_SISC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B03","text":"A serial-regressive filter selects the forms of conditioning capable of generating the four admissible cosmological fields.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AS3","CH_BA94_SISC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B04","text":"The unconditioned bifurcates between a complete series and a first condition, supplying the architecture for opposed cosmological determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B05","text":"Antinomy arises from the structural mismatch between reason’s absolute demand and understanding’s restriction to conditioned experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:AS3","CH_BA94_SIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B06","text":"Because transcendent cosmological claims lack experiential adjudication, unrestricted conflict becomes a diagnostic rather than a doctrinal procedure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A10","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B07","text":"The viable response is neither skeptical renunciation nor dogmatic victory, but the diagnosis of the misunderstanding generating both sides.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:AS5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B08","text":"The movement is recursive: category becomes idea, idea generates antinomy, critique restricts the result, and recurrent illusion renews the demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A3","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AS-B09","text":"Closure remains critical rather than doctrinal because cosmological ideas are retained as problematic demands while the positive rule of resolution remains absent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AF6","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Whenever a conditioned phenomenon is given, reason necessarily demands the absolute totality of its antecedent conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Understanding supplies pure categories, but reason transforms eligible conditioned syntheses into transcendental ideas by extending them beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A1","CH_BA94_SISC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Only regressively subordinated serial conditions support cosmological totalization; coordinated aggregates and progressive consequences do not.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SISC:NX4","CH_BA94_SISC:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Composition, division, origination, and dependence are the four modes through which phenomenal conditioning becomes cosmologically totalized.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SISC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned is represented either as the complete totality of conditioned members or as an unconditioned first member, and this bifurcation generates opposed determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A15","CH_BA94_SISC:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Cosmological synthesis must obey the conditions of possible experience while answering a demand for completeness that no merely empirical synthesis can satisfy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A1","CH_BA94_SIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Extending the demand for unconditioned unity beyond possible experience produces natural, recurrent, and internally contradictory transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Cosmological ideas retain phenomenal reference while exceeding every possible empirical completion and therefore cannot become knowledge of noumenal totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A17","CH_BA94_SISC:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Each side of an antinomy can possess internal rational necessity even though neither side admits empirical or intuitive adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","CH_BA94_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Contradiction is evidence that reason has misclassified the scope or status of its principles, not evidence that the world itself bears contradictory predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:SD2","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Critical philosophy must reject both dogmatic selection and skeptical abandonment in favor of a method that diagnoses the source of disagreement.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:AS5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Because transcendental illusion survives its exposure, critique must operate as a recurrent discipline rather than a final eradication of cosmological error.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-01","text":"Categories retain their identity as concepts of understanding but mutate functionally when reason elevates their conditioned syntheses into transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A1","CH_BA94_SISC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-02","text":"Absolute totality shifts from the intended endpoint of explanatory completion to a problematic idea whose defective scope becomes visible through contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A14","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-03","text":"Regress first functions as the privileged procedure for locating antecedent conditions and then becomes the engine of paired cosmological demonstrations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-04","text":"The unconditioned migrates across magnitude, composition, causality, and existence without losing its role as the common demand for completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-05","text":"The two representations of the unconditioned reverse valence across the antinomies: first-condition forms generate terminal claims, while sensible conditions regenerate indefinite series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A15","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-06","text":"Phenomenality persists as the reference domain of cosmological ideas but expands into the boundary that prevents their transcendent adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A17","CH_BA94_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-07","text":"Skepticism reverses function: as surrender it destroys philosophy, but as method it stages conflict to locate misunderstanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:NX5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-08","text":"Contradiction escalates from the result of an apparently plausible cosmological principle into the endogenous self-conflict of pure reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:AS3","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-09","text":"The syllogistic taxonomy performs an initial ordering function and is then absorbed into the more operative categorial and serial derivation of cosmological problems.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A2","CH_BA94_SISC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-10","text":"World and nature separate functionally: world names mathematical phenomenal totality, while nature becomes the law-governed causal demand opposing spontaneous interruption.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A16","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RMD-11","text":"The ideal of pure reason remains distinguished from cosmical totality, preventing the problem of necessary existence from silently becoming an extra-world metaphysical solution.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-01","text":"The classification by syllogistic species and the contrast with pneumatism remain introductory local machinery rather than governing principles of the cluster.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A2","CH_BA94_CIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-02","text":"The separate treatments of time, space, matter, causality, and existence remain local demonstrations, while their common totalizing generator scales upward.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AF4","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-03","text":"The demand for absolute completeness scales upward because it organizes the derivation, conflict, and critical restriction of every cosmological idea.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-04","text":"The serial-subordination criterion bridges the categorial table to the four antinomies by determining which syntheses can become cosmological.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SISC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-05","text":"The bifurcation between complete series and first condition catalyzes the transition from systematic enumeration to paired opposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-06","text":"The mathematical-dynamical division bridges the taxonomy of cosmological ideas with distinct conflicts concerning magnitude and causal existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A18","CH_BA94_SIAP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-07","text":"The distinction between world and nature scales only partially, becoming respectively the terrain of mathematical totality and law-governed causal connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A16","CH_BA94_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-08","text":"Rational cosmology is superseded as doctrine and recoded as a critical object generated by reason’s own totalizing activity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:A14","CH_BA94_CIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:CGD-09","text":"The detailed assumptions of individual proofs do not scale into global axioms; they survive as local compression points that destabilize strict argumentative parity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT5","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-01","text":"Reason is neither an independent source of pure concepts nor licensed to form cosmological ideas detached from categorial synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:NX1","CH_BA94_SISC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-02","text":"Coordinated aggregates and progressive consequences are excluded because they do not provide the serial antecedent conditions required for cosmological regression.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:NX4","CH_BA94_SISC:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-03","text":"Cosmological totality is neither a noumenal inventory nor replaceable by a knowable intelligible world.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:NX9","CH_BA94_SIAP:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-04","text":"Rational necessity does not establish empirical existence, completed synthesis, or objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:DNX6","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-05","text":"The world-whole is not an object of possible experience, and space and time are not empty containers capable of bounding it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIAP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-06","text":"An empirical regress cannot be completed by an unwarranted transition to an intellectual condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-07","text":"Neither one-sided authority nor doctrinal allegiance can decide antinomial conflict.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:NX4","CH_BA94_SIAP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-08","text":"Skeptical surrender is excluded, but diagnostic skeptical method is retained.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:NX5","CH_BA94_SIAP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-09","text":"Antinomy is neither an artificial sophism nor evidence that the world independently possesses contradictory predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:NX1","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:GNX-10","text":"Mathematical construction, experiment, and empirical testing cannot adjudicate claims that exceed possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:NX6","CH_BA94_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot admit that a conditioned phenomenon is fully intelligible from its immediately given state alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-02","text":"Explanation cannot terminate arbitrarily, and antecedents cannot be treated symmetrically with consequences.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:DNX2","CH_BA94_SISC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-03","text":"Formal validity and subjective rational necessity cannot guarantee legitimate ontological reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX2","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-04","text":"The phenomenal series cannot be presupposed as an absolutely completed object available from an empirical standpoint.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX1","CH_BA94_SISC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-05","text":"No single undifferentiated object can simultaneously satisfy absolute rational totality and empirical conditionedness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-06","text":"No transcendent metacriterion can compare thesis and antithesis as independently available objects of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-07","text":"A noumenal redescription cannot substitute for an answer to a question constituted under phenomenal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:NX9","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-08","text":"Critique cannot permanently extinguish reason’s drive toward the unconditioned or abolish transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX4","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:DBN-09","text":"Antinomial contradiction cannot be relocated from reason’s mode of representation into the world as an independent contradictory being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-01","text":"The deepest organizing absence is any possible intuition of the world-whole whose predicates cosmological reason attempts to determine.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an unavailable standpoint from which the complete phenomenal series could be empirically given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-03","text":"No guarantee connects reason’s necessary demand for totality to a structure actually possessed by phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-04","text":"A final metaphysical inventory is unavailable because totality survives only in the problematic form of an idea.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-05","text":"The positive rule capable of assigning opposed propositions to distinct standpoints remains absent, although its necessity structures the conflict.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSA-06","text":"Transparent mastery of the demanded totality and permanent repose from cosmological conflict are both excluded.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:SNX4","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-01","text":"The four antinomies are not an empirical list of disputes but the necessary conflict outputs of the serial categorial selector under absolute totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-03","CL_BA94_21:GA-04","CL_BA94_21:GA-07","CH_BA94_SISC:SD4","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-02","text":"The bifurcation of the unconditioned generates paired outputs: a terminating condition under the completion operator and an indefinite series under reapplied sensible conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-05","CL_BA94_21:GA-06","CH_BA94_SISC:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-03","text":"Because cosmological ideas remain phenomenal while reason drives them beyond experience, antinomy belongs to reason’s representation rather than to the world independently considered.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-07","CL_BA94_21:GA-08","CL_BA94_21:GA-10","CH_BA94_SISC:SD6","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-04","text":"The conjunction of equally grounded oppositions and unavailable external adjudication converts contradiction into a diagnostic of cognitive boundaries.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-09","CL_BA94_21:GA-10","CH_BA94_CIAP:SD2","CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-05","text":"Since antinomy arises naturally and illusion recurs after exposure, critique must remain iterative rather than terminate in a final correction.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-07","CL_BA94_21:GA-12","CH_BA94_CIAP:A10","CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-06","text":"The privilege of antecedent regress prohibits completing an empirical series by importing an intellectual condition without a justified change of standpoint.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-03","CL_BA94_21:GA-06","CL_BA94_21:GA-08","CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-07","text":"Boundaries, simples, spontaneous causality, and necessary existence are differentiated manifestations of one completion operator rather than independent metaphysical discoveries.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-04","CL_BA94_21:GA-05","CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-08","text":"Necessary existence remains cosmological only while treated as a condition within the series; its conversion into an extra-world ideal would collapse the distinction between cosmical conception and the ideal of pure reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-04","CL_BA94_21:GA-08","CH_BA94_CIAP:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-09","text":"Rejecting skepticism and dogmatism as terminal responses makes skeptical method a third position that preserves reason by exposing its illicit totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-10","CL_BA94_21:GA-11","CH_BA94_CIAP:AS5","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:XD-10","text":"The methodological value of paired conflict does not secure strict parity between every local proof, because substantive transcendental commitments can privilege particular arguments.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-09","CL_BA94_21:GA-10","CH_BA94_CIAP:DNX4","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-01","text":"Categories have a dual career: they organize legitimate experience when conditioned and produce dialectical illusion when converted into demands for absolute completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A1","CH_BA94_CIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-02","text":"The world ceases to be a ready-made cosmological object and becomes the problematic form of a regress whose completion reason demands but cannot exhibit.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SD2","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes a self-divided totalizing operation whose demand for unity produces its own internal opposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:SD1","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-04","text":"Cosmological time is asymmetrical: antecedent conditions are required for explaining the present, yet their absolute totality cannot be given as completed experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:D3","CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-05","text":"Finitude, simplicity, freedom, and necessity become terminal figures through which reason attempts to arrest otherwise conditioned series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:D10","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-06","text":"Contradiction becomes a transcendental experiment that forces principles to disclose their illicit scope through maximal opposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:D10","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-07","text":"Objective validity requires more than internal consistency or rational necessity; it requires conformity to the conditions under which an object can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:DNX2","CH_BA94_SISC:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-08","text":"Critical discipline becomes constitutively recurrent because exposure can restrict transcendental illusion without eliminating its rational generator.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:BSD-09","text":"Antinomial deadlock generates the need for a standpoint distinction that the cluster structurally requires but does not yet positively formulate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-01","text":"The primary loop runs from a conditioned phenomenon through antecedent regress and absolute totality to transgression, contradiction, critical restriction, and renewed demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS1","CH_BA94_SISC:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A5","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-02","text":"The escalation path converts category into idea, idea into paired demonstration, and rational deadlock into critical diagnosis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A3","CH_BA94_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-03","text":"The unconditioned is the dominant attractor: reason requires it as explanatory completion while experience withholds it as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SD3","CH_BA94_SISC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-04","text":"The four cosmological domains converge because each is a transformation of the same mismatch between totalization and conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A13","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-05","text":"The system spirals between first-condition closure and indefinitely conditioned regress, with each standpoint regenerating the contrary determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A15","CH_BA94_SIAP:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-06","text":"Phenomenal reference and trans-empirical completion form a recursive capture: the idea cannot leave phenomena, yet its demand cannot be completed within them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A17","CH_BA94_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-07","text":"Dogmatic combat is self-renewing because argumentative reversal can indefinitely substitute sequence and defensive success for objective adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:D9","CH_BA94_SIAP:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-08","text":"Exposure of illusion produces a phase change from deception to vigilance rather than an escape from the illusion’s rational source.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:RLE-09","text":"Conflict becomes a machine when maximal opposition repeatedly converts cosmological propositions into tests of transcendental legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:D10","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned phenomenon + categorial synthesis → selection of a regressively subordinated series of antecedent conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS1","CH_BA94_SISC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B02","text":"Serial regress + rational demand for completeness → transcendental idea of absolute totality rather than an experienced object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS3","CH_BA94_SISC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B03","text":"Serial selection across eligible categories → four cosmological problem-fields concerning magnitude, composition, causality, and existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS6","CH_BA94_SIAP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B04","text":"Absolute totality → bifurcation between unlimited complete whole and first unconditioned condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B05","text":"Completion operator → limits, simples, spontaneous causality, and necessity; reapplied sensible conditions → infinity, divisibility, natural causality, and contingency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B06","text":"Equal rational grounding + unavailable objective criterion → antinomial deadlock.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A8","CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B07","text":"Unrestricted conflict → exposure of standpoint shift → diagnosis of concealed misunderstanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:FGS7","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FGS-B08","text":"Diagnosis → restriction of doctrinal validity + retention of the idea + recurrent critical discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:D8","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned synthesis → demand for completeness → transcendental extension → paired cosmological claims → rational deadlock → recurrent critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AFM-B02","text":"Categorial distinction → serial-subordination filter → privileging of regress → four cosmological ideas → problematic unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:AF2","CH_BA94_SISC:AF3","CH_BA94_SISC:AF6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AFM-B03","text":"Assumption of one opposite → reductive proof → standpoint reversal → proof of the contrary → indefinitely reversible dogmatic sequence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:AF2","CH_BA94_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AFM-B04","text":"Unrestricted conflict → boundary diagnosis → rejection of dogmatic and destructive skeptical closure → structurally required but absent positive resolution.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A11","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:AFM-B05","text":"World magnitude → material elements → cosmical causality → ground of existence → convergence on the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:AF3","CH_BA94_SISC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-01","text":"Metaphysical questions are reclassified as procedural outputs of admissible modes of rational regression rather than independently discovered objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SD4","CH_BA94_SISC:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-02","text":"The world is reclassified from an immediately attributable totality into a problematic construction generated by reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:ME1","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-03","text":"The opposition between finite first condition and infinite regress becomes internal to the representation of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:ME6","CH_BA94_SISC:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-04","text":"Four heterogeneous cosmological disputes become visible as recurrent outputs of one structural mismatch.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-05","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from an obstacle to knowledge into evidence concerning the limits and scope of transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:ME2","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-06","text":"Explanation is redrawn asymmetrically around dependence on antecedent grounds rather than exhaustive extension toward consequences.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:ME2","CH_BA94_SISC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-07","text":"Categories are divided according to their capacity to generate subordinated regress, preventing every categorial relation from becoming cosmological.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:ME3","CH_BA94_SISC:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-08","text":"Space and time cease to be independent cosmical containers and become conditions governing possible phenomenal assertions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:NX8","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-09","text":"The opposition between skepticism and dogmatism collapses once destructive suspension is distinguished from diagnostic skeptical method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:ME6","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MME-10","text":"Boundaries, simples, freedom, and necessity are reclassified as variants of one rational demand for the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ME6","CH_BA94_SISC:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, four paired disputes concern world magnitude, material composition, causal origination, and necessary dependence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT1","CH_BA94_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, every conflict is generated by the interaction of serially subordinated conditions with reason’s demand for absolute totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT2","CH_BA94_SISC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, doctrinal adjudication is replaced by maximal conflict and diagnosis of illicit assumptions, scope extensions, and level shifts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is phenomenal and law-governed, while antecedent time is required regressively without becoming an absolutely completed object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT4","CH_BA94_SISC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the paired demonstrations enact reason’s self-reversal, making the text’s argumentative conflict perform the structure it diagnoses.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:D10","CH_BA94_SIAP:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-01","text":"Reason requires the whole series of conditions while the possibility of completing phenomenal regress remains unestablished.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:MRT5","CH_BA94_SISC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-02","text":"The declaration of equal rational grounding is strained by local arguments that rely on substantive transcendental commitments and thereby privilege some conclusions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SD8","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-03","text":"Cosmological ideas are restricted to phenomena yet necessarily extend beyond every possible empirical completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-04","text":"Space is treated as a nonserial aggregate while its delimitation is made dependent on successive synthesis capable of regression.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A9","CH_BA94_SISC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-05","text":"Skeptical method promises critical certainty concerning boundaries even though the positive rule resolving the antinomies remains absent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:SD3","CH_BA94_SIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-06","text":"The freedom thesis establishes a cosmological demand for a first causal beginning but extends toward intraworld agency without an equivalent proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:FL-07","text":"Necessary existence oscillates between membership in the temporal series and an unsettled relation to an extra-world ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MP-01","text":"A category mutates into a transcendental idea when reason extends its conditioned synthesis toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A3","CH_BA94_SISC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MP-02","text":"Regressive explanation mutates into cosmological ontology when the idea of totality is treated as a determinate world-whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MP-03","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a single explanatory requirement into the opposed forms of infinite completeness and first-condition termination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A15","CH_BA94_SISC:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MP-04","text":"Contradiction mutates from doctrinal deadlock into a diagnostic criterion for defective transcendental legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:SD2","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MP-05","text":"Skepticism mutates from philosophical surrender into a method that suspends dogmatic victory in order to expose misunderstanding.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:NX5","CH_BA94_SIAP:NX4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a network of conditioned series organized under forms of sensible intuition rather than as a completed container of things.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A4","CH_BA94_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a sovereign spectator but a recursive totalizing engine whose demand for unity generates internal conflict.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:SD1","CH_BA94_SIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-03","text":"Time is an asymmetrical regress in which the present requires antecedent conditions without granting cognition a completed past totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:A5","CH_BA94_SISC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-04","text":"The unconditioned functions as a receding horizon required by reason but unavailable as an object of completed experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-05","text":"Navigation consists in staging conflict, detecting illicit level shifts, and refusing to terminate empirical series through unlicensed intellectual substitutions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:HSI-06","text":"Escape from illusion is impossible; the remaining freedom is recurrent critical vigilance over the scope of reason’s own operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:A9","CH_BA94_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the common generator, four cosmological outputs, antinomial deadlock, and skeptical-critical method.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT1","CH_BA94_SISC:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning the positive standpoint rule and subsequent general resolution of the antinomies.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a diagnosis of why reason produces opposed necessities under conditions that prohibit experiential adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIAP:MRT3","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the serial-subordination filter and the diagnosis of illicit transitions jointly derive both the cosmological fields and their conflict.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on preserving staged reversal and rhetorical parity because maximal conflict, rather than linear proof, performs the diagnostic transition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B06","text":"The compressed assumptions of the individual proofs, especially those concerning freedom and necessary existence, remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT5","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_21:MRT-B07","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if cosmological ideas remain procedural products of reason and neither dogmatic side is selected in advance.","refs":["CH_BA94_SISC:MRT8","CH_BA94_SIAP:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 61–63","context":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","original_title":"Chapters 61–63","original_context":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 61–63","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_22","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 64–66","order":22,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIIR","CH_BA94_SINI","CH_BA94_SVSE"],"revision":"CL_BA94_22@R001","sha256":"9543715d613a8ac41c4fff7a58865f94b788dc6abdf72f5f48976ccce1dfab01","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B01","text":"The system confronts a demand for unconditioned cosmological totality that reason necessarily generates although experience cannot furnish its adequate object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","CH_BA94_SINI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B02","text":"Neither partisan interest nor selection between opposed doctrines can resolve the conflict once the object presupposed by both sides proves groundless.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","CH_BA94_SVSE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B03","text":"Inquiry therefore shifts from determining which cosmological description is true to diagnosing how reason mistakes an idea of totality for a determinable object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B04","text":"Possible experience becomes the governing standard of speculative objectivity rather than one contestant within the cosmological dispute.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B05","text":"The common proof-form is a double failure: an infinite regress cannot be completed, while any finite terminus remains conditioned and renews the regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B06","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a reflexive transcendental critique in which sceptical conflict exposes the cognitive ground of its own production.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A10","CH_BA94_SVSE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B07","text":"Its movement is recursive: suspended adjudication redirects inquiry from the alternatives to their source, where the shared presupposition of an absolute object is invalidated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS4","CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AS-B08","text":"Closure consists not in possessing cosmological totality but in preserving its regulative demand through reason’s critical self-limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SD2","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective speculative cognition is authorized only where an object can be related to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A6","CH_BA94_SVSE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Reason necessarily seeks an unconditioned totality for every conditioned empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Cosmological totality changes from a presumed terminal object into an idea governing the required progress of empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A5","CH_BA94_SINI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Neither dogmatic affirmation nor dogmatic negation can claim knowledge beyond the conditions warranted by possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:NX11","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Empirical regress is valid as an indefinitely extensible procedure but invalid as a completed cosmological whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Reason answers internally generated cosmological questions reflexively by determining their cognitive ground and legitimate use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A1","CH_BA94_SINI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Every dogmatic cosmological alternative fails by either exceeding possible synthesis or terminating at something that still requires a condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Antinomy is a symptom of transcendental illusion produced when reason treats its idea as an independently determinable object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:AS6","CH_BA94_SVSE:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Critical self-limitation is reason’s positive form of completion because it resolves the illusion without supplying an impossible total object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SD1","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Practical and architectonic interests explain allegiance to cosmological doctrines but remain categorically distinct from speculative authorization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:NX2","CH_BA94_SIIR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-01","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a supposed terminus grounding the conditioned into a regulative demand that cannot itself appear as an empirical completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A9","CH_BA94_SINI:SD2","CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-02","text":"Possible experience escalates from a boundary on assertion into the criterion of objective reality and finally the diagnostic standard applied to every cosmological alternative.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ME4","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-03","text":"Empiricism changes function from one partisan doctrine into a methodological rule of indefinite inquiry and then into a critical test of objectivity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-04","text":"Scepticism reverses valence: speculative oscillation becomes a critical verdict on question-formation and then a cathartic instrument against dogmatism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A16","CH_BA94_SINI:SD3","CH_BA94_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-05","text":"Thesis and antithesis lose their doctrinal identities and become formal positions defined by excess beyond possible synthesis and deficiency through conditioned termination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-06","text":"“Solution” mutates from a deferred inquiry into reason’s disunion into a transfer from object-determination to exposure of the question’s false presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A17","CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RMD-07","text":"Practical and architectonic interests perform transitional work by explaining adherence, but they recede once the validity of the presupposed object becomes the decisive issue.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:D5","CH_BA94_SVSE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-01","text":"The taxonomy of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests remains a local diagnosis of allegiance rather than a current-level proof of cosmological truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS3","CH_BA94_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-02","text":"The fourfold enumeration of cosmological series remains lower-layer implementation, while their shared excess-or-deficiency mechanism scales upward.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A3","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-03","text":"The absence of an experiential object adequate to absolute totality persists across all units and becomes a global prohibition on any standpoint surveying the completed series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A1","CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between methodological and dogmatic empiricism bridges the units by allowing experience to regulate inquiry without converting its limits into ontological denial.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A8","CH_BA94_SVSE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-05","text":"Reason’s obligation to answer its own questions catalyzes the transition from suspended partisan diagnosis to positive critical resolution.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A11","CH_BA94_SIIR:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:CGD-06","text":"The requirement to choose between thesis and antithesis becomes obsolete when both are recoded as products of the same groundless object-presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX2","CH_BA94_SVSE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes direct speculative possession of an unconditioned cosmological whole and any standpoint from which such a whole could be surveyed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX1","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-02","text":"Practical need and partisan interest are excluded as logical criteria of speculative truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:NX2","CH_BA94_SIIR:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-03","text":"Indifference, suppression, and declarations of necessary ignorance are excluded as final responses to conflicts generated by reason itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:NX12","CH_BA94_SINI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-04","text":"Dogmatic affirmation and dogmatic negation are both excluded because neither can determine the presumed cosmological object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:NX6","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-05","text":"Absolute totality is excluded from empirical accumulation because neither an infinite succession nor a finite terminus supplies an unconditioned whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:NX3","CH_BA94_SVSE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes both inference from unknowability to nonexistence and inference from conceptual necessity to existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX1","CH_BA94_SINI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the world not to be cognizable as a thing whose total conditioned series is given independently of empirical regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX2","CH_BA94_SINI:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:DBN-02","text":"It rejects the assumption that the grammatical form or bivalent alternatives of a question guarantee a valid object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:DNX5","CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies every transition from the subjective necessity of an idea to objective application beyond experiential conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:DNX1","CH_BA94_SINI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:DBN-04","text":"The boundary of experience must be neither an invitation to transcendent speculation nor a warrant for denying what cannot appear.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:NX7","CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:DBN-05","text":"The system rejects the assumption that answering reason requires an object-level determination; its admissible answer is diagnosis of cognitive ground and limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A10","CH_BA94_SINI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is an absolute standpoint capable of occupying and surveying the total series of conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SNX1","CH_BA94_SVSE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around the absence of guaranteed correspondence between reason’s demand for completion and any final metaphysical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX3","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSA-03","text":"No member of the empirical series can simultaneously remain within the order of conditions and function as its unconditioned termination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:NX4","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-01","text":"The inevitability of cosmological ideas and reason’s obligation to answer its own questions jointly make dismissal impossible while forcing the answer to become reflexive rather than dogmatic.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-02","CL_BA94_22:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIIR:D1","CH_BA94_SINI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-02","text":"Because interests explain adherence without proving either side, the later diagnosis of transcendental illusion neutralizes partisan attraction without erasing its motivational role.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-04","CL_BA94_22:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIIR:D5","CH_BA94_SVSE:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-03","text":"The distinction between an idea and an object becomes operational through the excess-or-deficiency test applied to every cosmological alternative.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-03","CL_BA94_22:GA-07","CL_BA94_22:GA-08","CH_BA94_SINI:A5","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-04","text":"Empirical regress becomes both an open-ended rule for extending inquiry and the standard that prevents this extension from being reified as a completed whole.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-01","CL_BA94_22:GA-05","CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-05","text":"Critical limitation occupies a position distinct from both supersensible cognition and dogmatic denial of what cannot be cognized phenomenally.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-01","CL_BA94_22:GA-04","CL_BA94_22:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIIR:NX7","CH_BA94_SINI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:XD-06","text":"Sceptical exposure of mutual failure becomes reason’s positive self-resolution when contradiction is used to dissolve the false object rather than suspend judgment indefinitely.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-06","CL_BA94_22:GA-08","CL_BA94_22:GA-09","CH_BA94_SINI:SD1","CH_BA94_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-01","text":"The legitimacy and objectivity of a cosmological question must be determined before its opposed answers can be assessed as true or false.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:ME1","CH_BA94_SVSE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-02","text":"The world as totality is deobjectified, while empirical objects remain conditionally situated within an indefinitely extensible regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:DNX2","CH_BA94_SVSE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-03","text":"Cosmological time becomes procedural rather than totalized: neither an infinitely elapsed series nor a conditioned beginning can be possessed as an absolute whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-04","text":"The rational subject becomes both the generator of transcendental illusion and the tribunal capable of diagnosing its source.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:AS6","CH_BA94_SINI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-05","text":"Speculative limitation protects practical commitment from theoretical disproof but leaves their non-dogmatic relation incompletely determined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:SD2","CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:BSD-06","text":"Contradiction acquires methodological force as evidence that the alleged object has been falsely constructed rather than as evidence for either doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:SD2","CH_BA94_SVSE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-01","text":"The principal loop runs from conditioned synthesis to the idea of totality, from the idea’s objectification to antinomy, and from antinomy through critique back to regulated empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:FGS1","CH_BA94_SINI:FGS2","CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-02","text":"The inquiry escalates from explaining adherence, through examining the concepts that produce the dispute, to testing the validity of the object presupposed by both parties.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:D5","CH_BA94_SINI:A10","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-03","text":"Possible experience functions as the dominant attractor: first limiting assertion, then defining objectivity, and finally measuring every proposed cosmological totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ME4","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-04","text":"Repetition of opposed answers marks a phase change when sceptical oscillation becomes a cathartic procedure for exposing invalid question-formation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A16","CH_BA94_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-05","text":"The demand for totality becomes a machine by reproducing the same excess-or-deficiency structure across temporal, spatial, divisional, causal, and modal series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A3","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:RLE-06","text":"Architectonic closure reverses into critical closure: reason ceases to complete the world as an object and instead completes its self-assessment through limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:SD3","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned empirical synthesis activates reason’s demand for unconditioned totality and thereby necessarily generates a cosmological idea.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS1","CH_BA94_SINI:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FGS-B02","text":"The cosmological idea is misread as a determinable object, producing opposed claims whose shared object exceeds experiential presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:FGS2","CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FGS-B03","text":"Practical and architectonic interests attach allegiance to the opposed claims but cannot establish either as speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A13","CH_BA94_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FGS-B04","text":"Reason’s obligation to resolve its own conflict transfers inquiry from the supposed object to the cognitive source and legitimate use of the idea.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A11","CH_BA94_SINI:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FGS-B05","text":"The regressive test shows that infinity exceeds possible synthesis and termination remains conditioned, so double failure exposes the false presupposition and yields critical self-limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS2","CH_BA94_SVSE:NX3","CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned synthesis → demand for unconditioned totality → bivalent objectification → interest-laden conflict → suspended adjudication → reflexive transfer → excess-or-deficiency test → failure of the alleged object → critical self-limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS1","CH_BA94_SIIR:AS4","CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:FGS3","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-01","text":"Cosmological propositions are reclassified from candidate descriptions of the world into tests of the objective validity of reason’s ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ME1","CH_BA94_SVSE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-02","text":"The opposition between metaphysical knowledge and sceptical suspension collapses into critical examination of whether the question presents a valid object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SD3","CH_BA94_SVSE:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-03","text":"The boundary of experience acquires three coordinated functions: it licenses empirical inquiry, constrains objective assertion, and blocks completed cosmological totality without authorizing ontological denial.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ME4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-04","text":"Finite and infinite cease to function as exhaustive world-properties and become formal indices of conditional deficiency and synthetic excess.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-05","text":"Philosophical solution is reclassified from possession of an answer-object into reflexive determination of the representation that generated the demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SD1","CH_BA94_SINI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MME-06","text":"Rational completeness moves from architectonic completion of the world to critical completion of reason’s self-limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX3","CH_BA94_SINI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system addresses four pairs of cosmological claims and the practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests attached to them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT1","CH_BA94_SIIR:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, antinomy arises from the collision between conditioned experience, reason’s demand for totality, and the illusion that the resulting idea determines an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT2","CH_BA94_SINI:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, suspended adjudication is replaced by examination of the validity of the shared object-presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS4","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, appearances exist only within conditioned regress, while neither infinite completion nor an unconditioned beginning can be empirically given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, repeated exposition of the same double failure converts scepticism into the critical operation the system advocates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:D7","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FL-01","text":"Reason must answer every genuine question generated by its constitutive concepts, yet some apparent questions receive only a verdict of nullity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:A1","CH_BA94_SINI:A7","CH_BA94_SINI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FL-02","text":"Cosmological ideas are necessarily generated demands for totality even though the totality they demand cannot acquire objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A2","CH_BA94_SINI:A4","CH_BA94_SINI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FL-03","text":"Possible experience is treated as the exclusive criterion of speculative objectivity, while the system refuses to convert phenomenal inaccessibility into denial and preserves a distinct practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIIR:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:FL-04","text":"The unconditioned is assigned a regulative function, but the precise operation of regulation without objective significance remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:SD2","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MP-01","text":"Empiricism mutates from the antithesis’s universal doctrine into a methodological rule and finally into the criterion of objective reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIR:A7","CH_BA94_SVSE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MP-02","text":"Scepticism mutates from oscillation between alternatives into a cathartic criticism that dissolves their shared presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:A16","CH_BA94_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MP-03","text":"Solution mutates from determination of a cosmological object into reflexive inquiry that resolves reason by limiting its objective claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:AS5","CH_BA94_SINI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an indefinitely regressive field of conditioned appearances encircled, but never completed, by reason’s demand for unconditional totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:ALT2","CH_BA94_SVSE:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided engine that generates the illusion of totality and then becomes the tribunal of that illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:AS6","CH_BA94_SINI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:HSI-03","text":"Time is an open procedure of regress rather than a completed object possessing either a surveyed infinity or an empirically unconditioned beginning.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVSE:A4","CH_BA94_SVSE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible through methodological empiricism and cathartic scepticism, which avoid both dogmatic closure and passive suspension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:FGS5","CH_BA94_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from necessary cosmological demand through antinomial conflict to reflexive critical resolution.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT1","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MRT-B02","text":"The detailed proofs of the four antinomies and the exact mechanism of regulative use remain underdetermined at this synthesis scale.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT2","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if critical limitation counts as a positive solution and scepticism functions diagnostically rather than as indefinite refusal.","refs":["CH_BA94_SINI:MRT7","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest in its common excess-or-deficiency schema and its distinction between methodological empiricism and dogmatic empirical denial.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT3","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_22:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the sequence from suspension through recursive diagnosis to dissolution, while leaving the relation between practical commitment and regulative reason unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIR:MRT8","CH_BA94_SVSE:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIIR:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 64–66","context":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","original_title":"Chapters 64–66","original_context":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas"},"original_title":"Chapters 64–66","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_23","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 67–69","order":23,"parents":["CH_BA94_SVTI","CH_BA94_SVCS","CH_BA94_SVRP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_23@R001","sha256":"9b1113450239e82fa456dc7e7cf333bbd02d1e281fd311bd92ac8fd473d24c39","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-01","text":"Cosmological dialectic begins when objects conditioned by possible experience are treated as independently self-subsistent realities.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-02","text":"Critical reconstruction relocates reality from independent subsistence to lawful givenness within perception and its possible extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-03","text":"Conditionedness obliges reason to seek conditions, but this obligation does not supply a completed series of them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-04","text":"Because phenomenal series exist only through successive regress, their totality cannot be given in advance as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-05","text":"Cosmological opposites lose contradictory force when their common assumption of a self-subsistent world-whole is withdrawn.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-06","text":"The idea of totality is retained only by conversion into a rule directing empirical regress rather than a cognition of a cosmological object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-07","text":"Infinity becomes a mode of authorized continuation rather than a predicate establishing an actually infinite world-series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AS-08","text":"The cluster closes by preserving empirical regress while withholding every dogmatic claim to an empirically given unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Empirical objectivity consists in lawful integration within possible experience, not in self-subsistent existence outside it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Spatial-temporal objects are appearances, so their reality cannot be identified with the reality of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A2","CH_BA94_SVTI:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Conditionedness imposes an unbounded demand to seek further conditions, but never entitles reason to posit their completed availability.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A1","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Cosmological totality is legitimate only as an idea of completed possible experience transformed into a regulative rule.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A11","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Phenomenal conditions are available only through temporally successive synthesis and regress rather than as a co-given whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned directs inquiry as an unattainable terminus while remaining unavailable as an empirical discovery.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A3","CH_BA94_SVRP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] No ontological conclusion about the world follows from the logical necessity of continuing explanatory regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:NX1","CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy diagnoses a shared invalid object-model, allowing opposed cosmological judgments to be jointly false.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:D7","CH_BA94_SVCS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] The world as a phenomenal whole cannot be determined as finite, infinite, begun, unbegun, simple, composite, free, or necessitated in itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCS:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] The unknowable ground of appearances functions as a limiting correlate and cannot furnish positive cognition of a supersensible object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:D6","CH_BA94_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Reason is legitimate where it expands empirical inquiry systematically without converting that expansion into transcendent cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:D3","CH_BA94_SVRP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The dissolution of antinomy operates as an indirect confirmation of transcendental ideality rather than as cosmological knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-01","text":"Empirical reality persists across the cluster, but shifts from perceptual givenness to the lawful integration of objects within an open field of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-02","text":"Regress recurs from a logical requirement to seek conditions into a procedural rule governing empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-03","text":"Totality reverses function: initially the source of illicit world-objectification, it becomes the regulative idea that prevents empirical inquiry from becoming arbitrary.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A11","CH_BA94_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-04","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a falsely asserted cosmological object into an unattainable horizon organizing continued regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","CH_BA94_SVRP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-05","text":"The world is displaced from a self-subsistent whole into an open field constituted through empirically executable regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:NX4","CH_BA94_SVCS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-06","text":"Infinity ceases to designate an intrinsic magnitude of the world and becomes a differentiated form of permissible empirical continuation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A11","CH_BA94_SVRP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-07","text":"Contradiction changes valence from a threat to reason into diagnostic evidence of an invalid shared presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A12","CH_BA94_SVCS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RMD-08","text":"Transcendental idealism changes from a doctrinal distinction between appearances and things in themselves into the critical mechanism that dissolves antinomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:NX1","CH_BA94_SVCS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-01","text":"The doctrine that space and time are forms of sensibility remains lower-layer grounding, while its current-level function is to block the conversion of experiential objects into independent realities.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A1","CH_BA94_SVTI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-02","text":"Claims about past, distant, and unperceived objects become a bridge structure because they demonstrate how empirical reality can exceed present perception without becoming independent subsistence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:AS5","CH_BA94_SVTI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between the empirical self and the noumenal subject remains locally anthropological but scales upward as a prohibition on self-transparent transcendental standpoint.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A6","CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between regressus in infinitum and regressus in indefinitum is a lower-layer technical differentiation that becomes a current-level test for separating procedural continuation from ontological infinity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A9","CH_BA94_SVRP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-05","text":"The opposed predicates finite and infinite remain local cosmological exemplars, while their joint invalidity scales upward into a general critique of world-totality predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT1","CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-06","text":"The distinction between an empirically given whole and a totality allegedly given in the object is a cross-unit hinge that governs the legitimacy of every regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A8","CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-07","text":"The diagnosis of an invalid shared premise is the major transition mechanism by which antinomy becomes critical evidence rather than unresolved doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:D7","CH_BA94_SVCS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:CGD-08","text":"The transcendental object remains a local limiting concept, but its exclusionary function is absorbed into the current-level boundary against positive knowledge of non-sensuous grounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:D6","CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes empirical idealism by preserving the empirical reality of external objects while denying their self-subsistent transcendental reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes spatial-temporal things in themselves and treats space and time as conditions of appearance rather than determinations of independent objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes the pre-givenness of the complete condition-series and therefore the co-presence of phenomenal totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes the inference from the requirement to seek conditions to the existence of a completed unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes empirical arrest at an allegedly absolute unconditioned condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes both finitude and actual infinitude as determinations of the world considered as a phenomenal whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:GNX-07","text":"The system excludes the assumption that formal opposition guarantees objective reference for either opposed cosmological judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:DBN-01","text":"The system disqualifies any correspondence model in which cognition could compare appearances with spatial-temporal things as they exist independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:DBN-02","text":"The system disqualifies any standpoint from which appearances could be surveyed as a completed object while remaining appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:DBN-03","text":"The system disqualifies an identity between the logical form of explanation and the ontological structure of the empirically given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:DBN-04","text":"The system disqualifies every passage from lawful phenomenal order to positive cognition of its non-sensuous ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:DBN-05","text":"The system disqualifies a completed explanation whose end could be empirically given rather than merely guide inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded possibility is a standpoint outside possible experience from which the world could appear as a completed cosmological reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around an end that must regulate cognition while remaining structurally unavailable to cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSA-03","text":"No self-transparent subject survives beneath the temporal succession through which the empirical self is known.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSA-04","text":"No rational demand for completion can legislate the ontological constitution of its empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSA-05","text":"No first, final, or terminal explanatory condition can be admitted as an empirically known member of the phenomenal order.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-01","text":"The requirement to seek every condition becomes a compulsory rule of inquiry only when totality is prevented from becoming an object possessed by inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-03","CL_BA94_23:GA-04","CL_BA94_23:GA-11","CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-02","text":"The joint falsity of opposed cosmological claims preserves reason’s demand for explanation by relocating failure from empirical inquiry to an invalid model of its object.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-08","CL_BA94_23:GA-11","CH_BA94_SVCS:A12","CH_BA94_SVRP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-03","text":"Procedural infinity is legitimate only where empirical givenness determines the form of regress, and it never establishes actual infinity in the object.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-05","CL_BA94_23:GA-07","CH_BA94_SVRP:A9","CH_BA94_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-04","text":"Empirical objectivity and the impossibility of a cosmological object are mutually enabling, because objectivity requires lawful regress while cosmological objecthood requires regress to be complete.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-05","CL_BA94_23:GA-09","CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-05","text":"The unconditioned produces permanent explanatory pressure without yielding an empirical condition capable of satisfying that pressure.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-06","CL_BA94_23:GA-11","CH_BA94_SVRP:A3","CH_BA94_SVRP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-06","text":"The failure of cosmological proof becomes indirect confirmation of transcendental ideality because contradiction exposes the illicit importation of thing-in-itself conditions into phenomenal inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-08","CL_BA94_23:GA-12","CH_BA94_SVCS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:XD-07","text":"Empirical limits cannot close inquiry, because every encountered limit must be distinguished from the series of conditions that explains it.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-03","CL_BA94_23:GA-07","CH_BA94_SVRP:A8","CH_BA94_SVRP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes temporal and procedural: objects are real through lawful synthesis, while no total synthesis is available as a completed worldly object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-02","text":"Cosmology is transformed from a doctrine of world-properties into a discipline for governing the conduct and limits of empirical explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:ME1","CH_BA94_SVRP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-03","text":"The unconditioned becomes constitutively unavailable but methodologically productive, structuring inquiry precisely through its non-attainment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:D2","CH_BA94_SVRP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-04","text":"Rational contradiction becomes metacritical evidence: opposed proofs reveal not competing truths but the covert object-model that makes both arguments possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-05","text":"Infinity is stripped of cosmological substance and redistributed as differentiated permissions for continuing empirical operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SD3","CH_BA94_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-06","text":"Transcendental ideality is no longer merely a thesis about appearances but a diagnostic method for dissolving dogmatic uses of totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AS6","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:BSD-07","text":"The world is cognitively available as a task-structured field of regress rather than as a representation whose total content could be possessed at once.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:SD3","CH_BA94_SVRP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned appearance generates regress; every condition found becomes conditioned; the completed condition remains required but unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-02","text":"The principal capture loop runs from the demand for conditions to reification of totality, then from antinomy back to the critical restoration of regulative regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS2","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-03","text":"Antinomy forms a diagnostic loop in which contradiction exposes a shared presupposition, whose removal converts contradiction into joint invalidity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A13","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-04","text":"Absolute totality functions as the dominant conceptual attractor because it repeatedly tempts reason to convert the rule of inquiry into an object of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:A4","CH_BA94_SVRP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-05","text":"The distinction between infinitum and indefinitum bifurcates empirical regress according to how its field is given, while reconverging in suspension of ontological infinity claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS5","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-06","text":"Every empirical boundary triggers renewed regress rather than closure, because a limitative perception cannot coincide with the conditioned series it limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AF3","CH_BA94_SVRP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:RLE-07","text":"The unknowable ground functions as an outer limiting boundary: it prevents appearances from becoming things in themselves without entering the empirical recursive field.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:SD5","CH_BA94_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B01","text":"Forms of sensibility → spatial-temporal appearances rather than things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A1","CH_BA94_SVTI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B02","text":"Lawful unity of possible experience → empirical objects with valid but non-self-subsistent reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A4","CH_BA94_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B03","text":"Given conditioned appearance → reason’s necessary regress toward further conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A1","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B04","text":"Demand for conditions plus thing-in-itself model → illicit attribution of completed totality to the world.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS2","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B05","text":"Successive phenomenal synthesis → prohibition on treating the complete regress as previously given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS3","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B06","text":"Illicit totalization → antinomial oppositions whose predicates are jointly invalid under their shared presupposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AF4","CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B07","text":"Dissolved object-claim → cosmological totality recoded as a regulative rule for empirical continuation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS3","CH_BA94_SVRP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FGS-B08","text":"Regulative continuation → indefinitely extensible inquiry without empirical completion, final condition, or actual-infinite world-object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:SD2","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance → required search for conditions → pressure toward completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B02","text":"Pressure toward completeness → confusion of a logical demand with a completed cosmological totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B03","text":"Completed-totality claim → substitution of thing-in-itself conditions for phenomenal conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B04","text":"Successive empirical synthesis → impossibility of a pre-given world-series.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B05","text":"Invalid shared presupposition → thesis and antithesis become jointly false dialectical predicates.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B06","text":"Dissolved antinomy → indirect confirmation of transcendental ideality and retention of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:AFM-B07","text":"Retained inquiry → totality as regulative direction → regress without dogmatic closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-01","text":"The world is reclassified from a determinate total object into an open regressive order of appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-02","text":"Objectivity is separated from independent subsistence, allowing empirical realism without transcendental realism about appearances.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-03","text":"Infinity is divided into distinct procedural forms and removed from the inventory of intrinsic world-properties.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-04","text":"Antinomy is reclassified from a contest between doctrines into a method for exposing invalid object-models.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-05","text":"Empirical boundaries become either provisional absences of perception or further-conditioned points of regress rather than signs of absolute closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MME-06","text":"The opposition between dogmatism and scepticism is displaced by a critical position that limits transcendent assertion while preserving empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: claims concerning finite and infinite worlds, beginnings and non-beginnings, composition, causality, freedom, and necessity are deprived of world-totality reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: empirical objects and their conditions exist only within temporally successive synthesis and lawful possible regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A6","CH_BA94_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: critical reason replaces settlement of opposed cosmological doctrines with diagnosis of the premise that generates both.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level: phenomenal reality has no knowable totality, first condition, final condition, or actual infinity independently of synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level: the argument repeatedly converts an object-claim into a procedural rule, thereby performing the critical discipline it asserts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AS3","CH_BA94_SVRP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FL-01","text":"Reason demands a complete conception of conditioned reality while the unconditioned required for completion remains structurally unattainable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FL-02","text":"Regress in infinitum is licensed for an empirically given whole while actual infinity in the object is denied, requiring a distinction that remains compression-sensitive.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FL-03","text":"The transition from possible experiential regress to determinate assertions about unperceived actuality remains dependent on a compressed account of empirical warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FL-04","text":"The indirect proof of transcendental ideality risks circularity because it diagnoses antinomy by relying upon the prior representational framework it claims to confirm.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:FL-05","text":"The transcendental object is invoked as an in-itself ground while being denied objectual cognizability, making it an indispensable but unstable limit-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MP-01","text":"Conditionedness mutates from a feature that prompts logical explanation into the procedural engine that compels empirical regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MP-02","text":"Totality mutates from an alleged property of the world into a regulative idea that directs inquiry without determining an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AS2","CH_BA94_SVRP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MP-03","text":"Antinomy mutates from apparent contradiction between world-claims into jointly invalid predication under an illicit shared condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MP-04","text":"Infinity mutates from a numerical magnitude attributed to the world into differentiated modes of continuing empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:D7","CH_BA94_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a lawful machine of appearance, synthesis, and regress rather than as an inventory of self-subsistent cosmological things.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:FGS2","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:HSI-02","text":"The empirical subject is a temporally given appearance, not a self-transparent observer capable of occupying a transcendental exterior.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:A6","CH_BA94_SVTI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:HSI-03","text":"Time is the irreducible medium of successive synthesis that prevents phenomenal totality from being present all at once.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:A7","CH_BA94_SVCS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:HSI-04","text":"The unconditioned functions as a receding horizon: it orients movement through the field without appearing as a destination within it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in converting the compulsive reification of explanatory demands into disciplined navigation of empirical regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:AS3","CH_BA94_SVRP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the conversion of cosmological totality from constitutive object-claim into regulative principle of inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it distinguishes empirical reality from independent existence and thereby dissolves the shared premise of antinomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVTI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined where indefinitely required regress must be positively characterized without covertly restoring a completed totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction requires the sequence of reification, antinomy, diagnosis, and regulative conversion; removing that recursive movement reduces critique to a static prohibition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:AF2","CH_BA94_SVCS:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B05","text":"The system is coherent at cluster scale if logical necessity, empirical availability, and ontological givenness remain strictly non-equivalent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCS:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_23:MRT-B06","text":"The distinction between procedural infinity and actual infinity remains indispensable and resistant to further reduction because it carries the system’s most concentrated tension.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVRP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 67–69","context":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","original_title":"Chapters 67–69","original_context":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas"},"original_title":"Chapters 67–69","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_24","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 70–72","order":24,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIEU","CH_BA94_ISCI","CH_BA94_IS_2"],"revision":"CL_BA94_24@R001","sha256":"22260d647d816fe7c96084be49d60cbd5a692bd403aadaee1d310c3daba5a86d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B01","text":"Cosmological inquiry begins from reason’s demand for an unconditioned limit of a series whose every presented member is conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B02","text":"The demand becomes antinomial only when appearances are converted into things in themselves and totality into a completed object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:AS1","CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B03","text":"Critical analysis replaces determination of a completed world with determination of the legitimate extent and mode of regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:AS3","CH_BA94_SIEU:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B04","text":"Absolute temporal or spatial closure cannot be empirically established, because its alleged boundary would require an experience against contentless void.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A3","CH_BA94_ISCI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B05","text":"The world-whole possesses no pre-regressive magnitude, because it is represented only through possible empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:AS4","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B06","text":"Noncompletion bifurcates according to synthesis: empirical composition requires indefinitely extensible inquiry, whereas continuous division requires regress in infinitum.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B07","text":"Neither indefinite empirical extension nor infinite divisibility licenses an actually given infinite world-whole or aggregate of parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A8","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B08","text":"Mathematical totality claims fail within homogeneous phenomenal series, while a dynamical intelligible condition is conditionally admissible only when it remains non-empirical and preserves empirical regularity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A12","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AS-B09","text":"Cosmological reason closes as a regulative method whose denied constitutive reach becomes the condition for a wider and more corrective use of experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIEU:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Phenomena cannot bear determinations that require treating them as completed things in themselves.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned persists as the demand ordering the search for conditions while remaining unavailable as an empirical object or terminus.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX2","CH_BA94_ISCI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] The legitimate status of totality is determined by the mode through which its conditions are given, constructed, or related to a series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Regress has non-equivalent forms: empirical composition remains indefinitely extensible, while continuous division proceeds in infinitum through jointly given conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] A rule for seeking conditions cannot be converted into a constitutive predicate of a completed cosmological object.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:SD2","CH_BA94_SIEU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Homogeneous mathematical series exclude an unconditioned phenomenal member, while heterogeneous dynamical dependence may conditionally admit a non-sensuous ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A12","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Every attained empirical condition remains subject to further conditioning, so no empirical datum can function as absolute closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D1","CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Finite and actual-infinite cosmological predicates lose objective standing when neither an absolute boundary nor a pre-given total magnitude can be presented.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D3","CH_BA94_ISCI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Empirical lawfulness is the gate that prevents an intelligible condition from becoming a hidden empirical cause or an interruption of explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A14","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Critical limitation transfers reason’s authority from metaphysical inventory to the correction and extension of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:SD1","CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Antinomy is resolved not by selecting a victorious thesis but by diagnosing the illicit totalization that generates opposed theses.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:NX6","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] The relation of conditioned appearance to a further condition persists as the invariant motor of both empirical inquiry and divisive analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:AS1","CH_BA94_IS_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Totality changes from a falsely presumed object of completed magnitude into a horizon or rule governing its own construction through regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:ME1","CH_BA94_ISCI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Infinity splits into regressus in indefinitum for empirical composition and regressus in infinitum for continuous division, blocking their conflation into one ontological thesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:NX4","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Critical restriction reverses apparent epistemic loss into enlarged empirical authority by converting prohibition into a rule of correction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:SD1","CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-05","text":"[Escalation] The unconditioned moves from an excluded phenomenal endpoint in mathematical synthesis to a conditionally admissible intelligible non-member in dynamical synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:NX7","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] Phenomenal substance and infinitely pre-organized wholes disappear as positive candidates for closure after establishing that neither simple residue nor determinate infinite organization can complete analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:NX3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Infinity ceases to signify a number of already existing parts and becomes inexhaustible determinability through an operation of division.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:NX1","CH_BA94_IS_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The void-boundary argument is a specific anti-finitist proof for cosmical composition rather than the universal ground of all cosmological regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A3","CH_BA94_ISCI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The dismantling of phenomenal substance and infinitely pre-given organization belongs specifically to the analysis of division and discrete multiplicity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:AS4","CH_BA94_IS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-03","text":"[Current-Level Scaling] The distinction between appearances and things in themselves scales across every cosmological conflict by withdrawing the object-domain presupposed by completed totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:AS1","CH_BA94_SIEU:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge Structure] Totality becomes legitimate only as a representation generated through regress rather than as a magnitude available before synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-05","text":"[Transition Catalyst] The distinction between homogeneous and heterogeneous synthesis generates the shift from reciprocal rejection in mathematics to conditional admissibility in dynamical explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-06","text":"[Recoding] The finite-versus-infinite binary is superseded by a typology distinguishing indefinite inquiry, infinite divisibility, and prohibited actual completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ME1","CH_BA94_ISCI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:CGD-07","text":"[Retained Subordinate] Relative empirical beginnings and limits remain valid within experience but cannot be elevated into absolute cosmological closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GNX-01","text":"Reason and understanding are denied transcendental object-knowledge beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:NX1","CH_BA94_SIEU:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GNX-02","text":"Phenomena are denied the status of self-subsistent things whose totality could be known independently of their representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:NX3","CH_BA94_ISCI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GNX-03","text":"No absolute empirical boundary, first condition, or final datum can terminate cosmological regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D2","CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GNX-04","text":"Infinite regress and infinite divisibility are denied conversion into an actually completed infinite aggregate.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A4","CH_BA94_IS_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:GNX-05","text":"No unconditioned phenomenal member, and no intelligible ground functioning as a covert phenomenal member, is admissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:NX7","CH_BA94_IS_2:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:DBN-01","text":"No standpoint external to possible experience can supply the world as a completed object of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:DBN-02","text":"No determinate cosmical magnitude may precede the synthesis through which magnitude is represented.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:DBN-03","text":"No inference from inexhaustible inquiry to an actually infinite object is licensed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:DBN-04","text":"No single model of totality can govern both spatial composition and dynamical dependence.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:DBN-05","text":"No completed metaphysical inventory can provide the legitimate endpoint of cosmological reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSA-01","text":"The system excludes any coincidence between reason’s demand for the unconditioned and a completed sensuous presentation of totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX5","CH_BA94_IS_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSA-02","text":"Cosmological closure is structurally unavailable because totality can be represented only through the regress that prevents its completed presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX3","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSA-03","text":"No admissible exterior may become either a survey-position over the world-whole or an exception that interrupts empirical lawfulness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:SNX3","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-01","text":"The word “infinite” has no unitary cosmological function: its legitimate sense changes with the synthesis that generates the regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-03","CL_BA94_24:GA-04","CH_BA94_ISCI:A11","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-02","text":"Finite and actual-infinite cosmological theses are symmetrical premature closures, because each converts an unfinished rule of representation into a property of a completed object.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-05","CL_BA94_24:GA-08","CH_BA94_ISCI:D3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-03","text":"Global nontermination is compatible with local completion, because the regulative form of inquiry governs extension of experience rather than the duration of every empirical sequence.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-04","CL_BA94_24:GA-07","CH_BA94_ISCI:SD4","CH_BA94_ISCI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-04","text":"The validity of a cosmological claim is decided by the mode of synthesis and the relation of its conditions to the series, not by the assertoric force of either opposed thesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-03","CL_BA94_24:GA-11","CH_BA94_IS_2:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-05","text":"An intelligible condition is admissible only as a noncompetitive limit-condition of explanation, never as an empirical rival cause or a suspension of lawful regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-06","CL_BA94_24:GA-09","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:XD-06","text":"Critical prohibition produces positive epistemic force when it converts the impossibility of total possession into a rule for systematic correction and extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-10","CL_BA94_24:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIEU:SD1","CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSD-01","text":"Cosmological predicates acquire legitimacy only as determinations of the conditions under which a world can be represented, not as descriptions of a world surveyed in itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-03","CL_BA94_24:GA-05","CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT5","CH_BA94_ISCI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSD-02","text":"Infinity becomes a differentiated architecture of noncompletion: indefinite continuation of inquiry, infinite divisibility of continua, and exclusion of actual completed multiplicity.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-04","CL_BA94_24:GA-08","CH_BA94_ISCI:D5","CH_BA94_IS_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSD-03","text":"Cognition is recast as situated, correctable progression within experience rather than possession of an external standpoint over its totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-07","CL_BA94_24:GA-10","CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIEU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSD-04","text":"Antinomy becomes a self-corrective diagnostic machine that exposes the illicit transformation of a regulative demand into a constitutive object-claim.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-05","CL_BA94_24:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:BSD-05","text":"A nonphenomenal condition has only derivative admissibility: it is permitted solely where it preserves the autonomy and continuity of empirical explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-06","CL_BA94_24:GA-09","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14","CH_BA94_IS_2:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned appearance generates a demand for a condition, whose attainment generates a further demand because each attained empirical condition remains conditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D1","CH_BA94_ISCI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-02","text":"The principal critical loop runs from totalizing objectification through antinomy to the removal of the illegitimate object-domain and return as regulative inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-03","text":"Cosmical composition forms a representation loop in which regress constitutes world-totality while simultaneously preventing any pre-given total magnitude from appearing.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A6","CH_BA94_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-04","text":"Division forms a decomposition loop in which every attained part remains divisible, producing infinite determinability without producing an infinite stock of parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-05","text":"The unconditioned functions as an attractor that directs inquiry while receding from empirical attainment and conditionally filtering dynamical explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:RLE-06","text":"The distinction between mathematical and dynamical synthesis is a classifier-loop that routes antinomies toward reciprocal rejection or qualified reconciliation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:SD4","CH_BA94_IS_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance or an intuitively given whole generates reason’s demand for an unconditioned condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:AS1","CH_BA94_IS_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B02","text":"The demand becomes dialectical when it is represented as a predicate of an already completed phenomenal totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:AS1","CH_BA94_ISCI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B03","text":"Critical separation of appearances from things in themselves removes the object-domain required for constitutive totality claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIEU:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B04","text":"The remaining problem is classified by the form of synthesis through which conditions and totality are related.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B05","text":"Empirical cosmical composition yields indefinite extension of possible inquiry without an empirically determinable absolute boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A11","CH_BA94_ISCI:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B06","text":"Continuous division yields regress in infinitum while excluding both a completed infinite aggregate and an unconditioned phenomenal member.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A3","CH_BA94_IS_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B07","text":"Dynamical synthesis admits a possible intelligible condition only where empirical regress remains regular, continuous, and non-interrupted.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:A14","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FGS-B08","text":"The converted regulative principle supplies the practical force of systematic cognition without claiming a completed object as its possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS6","CH_BA94_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance and given intuitive whole → demand for an unconditioned condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:AS1","CH_BA94_IS_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for the unconditioned as a completed object → finite/infinite and simple/composite antinomial alternatives.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS2","CH_BA94_IS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B03","text":"Critical distinction between appearance and thing in itself → removal of constitutive cosmological object-claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIEU:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B04","text":"Removal of the completed object → classification of the kind of synthesis and relation of conditions involved.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B05","text":"Classified synthesis → indefinite empirical regress, infinite divisibility, or conditional intelligible grounding.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D5","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:AFM-B06","text":"Regulative nonclosure → widened empirical correction without metaphysical completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIEU:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MME-01","text":"The finite/infinite opposition ceases to be an exhaustive cosmological alternative and becomes a shared error of completed-totality thinking.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ME1","CH_BA94_ISCI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MME-02","text":"The world is reclassified from an object presumed to possess a knowable magnitude into a horizon constituted through possible synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ME2","CH_BA94_SIEU:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MME-03","text":"Infinity is differentiated into indefinite empirical extension, infinite divisibility of continua, and prohibited actual infinite plurality.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:D5","CH_BA94_IS_2:D2","CH_BA94_IS_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MME-04","text":"Antinomies are reorganized by the type of synthesis they presuppose, separating mathematical reciprocal falsity from dynamical conditional admissibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:ME4","CH_BA94_IS_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MME-05","text":"Empirical explanation is protected both from metaphysical closure and from a covert suprasensible interruption of its lawfulness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:ME4","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system addresses whether the world has a temporal beginning, spatial boundary, infinite magnitude, or ultimately simple constituent parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT1","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: cosmological judgment depends on whether conditions are empirically sought, jointly given in intuition, progressively constituted, or heterogeneous to the phenomenal series.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT2","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: reason is recoded from a declarer of cosmical facts into a legislator and diagnostician of the proper conduct of inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT3","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: phenomenal magnitude is generated through temporal-spatial regress and division, while absolute completed magnitude is denied.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:ALT4","CH_BA94_IS_2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the argument performs its thesis by turning apparent defeat in antinomy into the operational method of critical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FL-01","text":"The system makes negative claims about the world as a whole while denying that the world as a whole is an available object of determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT5","CH_BA94_ISCI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FL-02","text":"The distinction between regressus in indefinitum and regressus in infinitum is indispensable but unstable, because both reject completion while arising from different relations between conditions and inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT7","CH_BA94_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FL-03","text":"The regulative principle is denied constitutive object-status yet granted practical force comparable to a constitutive axiom.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT5","CH_BA94_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:FL-04","text":"The positive status of the intelligible condition remains underdetermined precisely where its conditional admissibility is required for the dynamical resolution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT5","CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MP-01","text":"Totality mutates from an absolute object-question into a procedural question concerning the necessary extent of regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:AF2","CH_BA94_SIEU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MP-02","text":"Infinity mutates from an ontological aggregate of parts into the progressive determinability of a continuum through division.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:SD1","CH_BA94_IS_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MP-03","text":"Antinomy mutates from a uniformly reciprocal contradiction into a typed field in which mathematical and dynamical conflicts receive non-identical resolutions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:ME4","CH_BA94_IS_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of recursively conditioned phenomena rather than a completed world-machine available for external inspection.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A1","CH_BA94_ISCI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:HSI-02","text":"The cognizing subject appears as a finite regressor within experience, authorized to correct and extend inquiry but disqualified from surveying its totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIEU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:HSI-03","text":"Space and time appear not as containers with empirically displayed ultimate edges, but as forms within which magnitude is constructed through continuation and division.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:A3","CH_BA94_IS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:HSI-04","text":"The unconditioned appears as an absent attractor and selective gate: it directs the search for conditions while forbidding empirical capture of itself.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:SNX2","CH_BA94_IS_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:HSI-05","text":"Critical navigation consists in preserving empirical continuity against both dogmatic totalization and covert explanatory exception.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:A4","CH_BA94_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if the distinctions between appearance and thing in itself, completed totality and regress, indefinitum and infinitum, and mathematical and dynamical synthesis are retained.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT1","CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT1","CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where the denial of completed totality is converted into differentiated rules for empirical composition, continuous division, and dynamical explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT1","CH_BA94_SIEU:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MRT-B03","text":"The principal underdetermination is the positive character of the intelligible condition, whose admission remains conditional and whose content is not established.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT5","CH_BA94_IS_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_24:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on preserving the sequence from transcendental misclassification through synthesis-specific diagnosis to regulative reconstruction; flattening that sequence would collapse distinct forms of noncompletion into one doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT4","CH_BA94_ISCI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 70–72","context":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","original_title":"Chapters 70–72","original_context":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition"},"original_title":"Chapters 70–72","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_25","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 73–75","order":25,"parents":["CH_BA94_IS_3","CH_BA94_IS_4","CH_BA94_SI_3"],"revision":"CL_BA94_25@R001","sha256":"275978e57a7b1ed5d7f860d9ed04e4537b8970e9db41d2b2fd27ac6d9a22f9e0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-01","text":"The system asks how reason can retain freedom, necessary dependence, and rational perfection when no unconditioned object can be presented within universally conditioned experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS1","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-02","text":"Empirical conditioning is exhaustive within experience: no action escapes natural causation, no phenomenal existence is necessary, and no idea or ideal is concretely presented by a phenomenon.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-03","text":"The contradiction is dissolved by distinguishing empirical membership from intelligible conditioning rather than by inserting an exception into the empirical series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS4","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-04","text":"Intelligible freedom and intelligible necessity are admitted only as noncontradictory possibilities, whereas the ideal converts nonempirical thought into a regulative standard without acquiring objective existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A16","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-05","text":"The unconditioned mutates from spontaneous causality, through an extramundane condition of dependence, into an individualized archetype completely determined by reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS3","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-06","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a critical allocation of jurisdictions among empirical explanation, intelligible thinkability, and practical regulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS3","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-07","text":"Its movement is recursive: empirical saturation generates demand for an unconditioned, critique relocates that demand outside empirical membership, epistemic restriction withholds assertion, and regulative function returns the excluded term to practice.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS1","CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS5","CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AS-08","text":"Closure is therefore modal and regulative rather than ontological: the supersensible remains thinkable as a limiting ground or standard while actuality, determinate cognition, and empirical embodiment remain unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires sensible presentation; what exceeds possible experience may be thought but cannot thereby become an object of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A1","CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every empirical object, event, and existence remains conditioned within an exceptionless and indefinitely extendable series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A1","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned cannot appear as a temporal beginning, highest phenomenal member, or concretely presented ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] A dynamical condition may differ in kind from what it conditions, permitting empirical and intelligible relations to concern the same effect without entering one homogeneous series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:AS2","CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The intelligible register supports distinct functions that must not be conflated: spontaneous causality for action, an extramundane condition for phenomenal dependence, and rational determination for the ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Complete empirical explanation does not establish that empirical reality exhausts every possible ground, relation, or standard.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX2","CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Thinkability exceeds knowability, but noncontradiction never by itself proves actuality, real possibility, or determinate constitution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A16","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Withdrawal of objective presentation preserves or intensifies systematic function: the nonobjective can orient explanation, judgment, imputation, and perfectibility without becoming constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:D1","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Reason persistently seeks complete unity, an unconditioned condition, and exhaustive determination even though experience can supply none of them.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS2","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The unconditioned is legitimate only as a differentiated boundary-structure or regulative archetype, never as an empirically located or theoretically established entity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A14","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11","CH_BA94_IS_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-01","text":"[Functional recurrence] Empirical conditioning retains its restrictive identity across natural action and phenomenal existence, then reappears as the incompleteness of every empirical copy measured against an ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A2","CH_BA94_IS_4:A1","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] The intelligible changes from a timeless causal source, to a nonmember condition of the world-series, to a field of complete rational determination whose force is archetypal rather than causal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-03","text":"[Displacement] The unconditioned is displaced from a supposed gap inside nature to an extramundane ground and then from explanatory grounding to regulative judgment.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_4:ME1","CH_BA94_SI_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-04","text":"[Expansion] Practical normativity begins as evidence motivating the representation of free reason and expands into an archetypal structure for judging and reforming empirical action.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS4","CH_BA94_SI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-05","text":"[Absorption] The unattainable totality of causal conditions and the unlimited regress of dependence are absorbed into a broader structure of indefinite approximation toward rational completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D1","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-06","text":"[Stable identity with altered use] Nonempirical grounds leave empirical inquiry methodologically intact, but the ideal converts this separation from passive limitation into an active procedure of comparison and criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D6","CH_BA94_IS_4:D3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal transformation] The necessary being ceases to function as a candidate member of the cosmos and becomes the projected object of a transcendent inquiry whose general formal category is the ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:AS6","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-layer local] The empirical–intelligible character distinction is specific machinery for reconciling natural explanation and rational imputation in the case of action.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A8","CH_BA94_IS_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-layer local] The nonmembership of a necessary being in the phenomenal series is specific machinery for resolving the antinomy of existential dependence.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-layer local] Selection among contradictory predicates is the specific mechanism by which an ideal receives exhaustive rational determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A7","CH_BA94_SI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-04","text":"[Current-level scaling] The shared architecture is not any local supersensible object but the restriction of empirical validity combined with the preservation of nonempirical thinkability and function.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A16","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Practical normativity connects the freedom analysis to the ideal analysis by turning rational causality into judgment according to a nonempirical archetype.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS4","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst and supersession] The fourth antinomy catalyzes the transition toward transcendent ideas, while the searches for an empirical first cause, highest member, or perfect specimen are jointly superseded.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:SD4","CH_BA94_IS_3:NX5","CH_BA94_SI_3:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-01","text":"No empirical exception, interruption, or completed member may supply the unconditioned demanded by reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX2","CH_BA94_IS_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-02","text":"The intelligible is not an additional phenomenal cause, worldly substance, or empirical explanatory factor.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX8","CH_BA94_IS_4:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-03","text":"Logical compatibility is not proof of freedom, necessary existence, or the objective reality of an ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX11","CH_BA94_IS_4:NX2","CH_BA94_SI_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-04","text":"Observation and determinate predicates cannot convert a supersensible ground into an object of theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D10","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-05","text":"Rational authority does not require empirical causal competition, empirical explanatory use, or object-producing power.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX8","CH_BA94_IS_4:NX5","CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:GNX-06","text":"Neither an empirical exemplar nor an indeterminate image can substitute for complete a priori determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:A13","CH_BA94_SI_3:NX7","CH_BA94_SI_3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:DBN-01","text":"The system collapses if phenomena are things in themselves, because empirical conditioning would then become ontologically exhaustive.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:NX6","CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes both the identity of empirical and absolute dependence and the claim that empirical order exhausts every possible being.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX1","CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:DBN-03","text":"The system requires the falsity of the assumption that every condition must be homogeneous with, and empirically presentable alongside, what it conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:AS2","CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:DBN-04","text":"The system rejects the equivalence of thinkability, sensible presentation, objective reality, and existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A12","CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:DBN-05","text":"Neither lack of empirical explanatory use nor lack of objective reality entails impossibility, arbitrariness, or chimerical status.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A11","CH_BA94_SI_3:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSA-01","text":"The architecture circles an unavailable unconditioned that must be thinkable as ground, completion, or standard but cannot be contained by the empirical world.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX1","CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX1","CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSA-02","text":"No cognizable bridge unifies timeless causality, extramundane necessity, and nonobjective individuality with their empirical manifestations or applications.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX2","CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSA-03","text":"Completed empirical access to beginning, total dependence, perfection, and rational unity is structurally foreclosed.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX3","CH_BA94_IS_4:NX7","CH_BA94_SI_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSA-04","text":"Perfect determination is demanded while perfect knowledge remains unavailable, leaving the supersensible suspended between necessary rational function and underdetermined ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX5","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT4","CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-01","text":"Exceptionless empirical conditioning and heterogeneous dynamical relations jointly imply that a legitimate intelligible condition must supplement no empirical gap and compete with no empirical cause.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-02","CL_BA94_25:GA-04","CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-02","text":"The same critical operation dissolves both causal and existential antinomies by assigning apparently opposed claims to heterogeneous relations rather than rival objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-03","CL_BA94_25:GA-04","CH_BA94_IS_3:D3","CH_BA94_IS_4:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-03","text":"Because intelligible admission never alters empirical method, natural explanation and empirical regress remain complete in jurisdiction even when reason thinks a nonempirical ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-02","CL_BA94_25:GA-06","CH_BA94_IS_3:D6","CH_BA94_IS_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-04","text":"Practical reason transforms an epistemically restricted supersensible possibility into a regulative orientation without converting it into theoretical evidence.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-07","CL_BA94_25:GA-08","CH_BA94_IS_3:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-05","text":"The loss of empirical membership becomes the enabling condition of three nonempirical functions: rational imputation, boundary-thinking, and universal measurement.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-05","CL_BA94_25:GA-08","CH_BA94_IS_3:SD5","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-06","text":"Normative efficacy need not be object-producing efficacy: rational ideas may orient effects and judgment without appearing as empirical causes or objectively given standards.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-04","CL_BA94_25:GA-08","CH_BA94_IS_3:D8","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:XD-07","text":"Relocating the unconditioned outside empirical totality prepares its terminal mutation into an individualized rational form whose completeness lies in determination rather than existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-03","CL_BA94_25:GA-09","CL_BA94_25:GA-10","CH_BA94_IS_4:D6","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-01","text":"The system produces jurisdictional pluralism without constitutive metaphysical pluralism: several explanatory or normative relations are permitted, but only the empirical relation yields knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD7","CH_BA94_IS_4:ME4","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-02","text":"Time divides the architecture into an endlessly conditioned empirical succession, timelessly conceived intelligible grounds, and indefinite practical approximation toward an unattainable ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D5","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic legitimacy becomes negative and functional: reason may preserve what experience cannot disprove or present, provided it does not convert this permission into existence-claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:D4","CH_BA94_IS_3:A16","CH_BA94_SI_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-04","text":"The subject is simultaneously a fully explicable phenomenon, a rationally imputable agent, and an imperfect copy assessable through an unattainable archetype.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-05","text":"Critical method imposes a symmetrical prohibition: incomplete empirical explanation licenses no supersensible assertion, while empirical self-containment licenses no denial of supersensible possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX2","CH_BA94_IS_4:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:BSD-06","text":"The transition from cosmological antinomy to the ideal converts reason’s failed search for a worldly unconditioned into a formal inquiry concerning completely determined objects of pure thought.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:AS6","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-01","text":"Empirical inquiry generates an endless loop: each conditioned event or existence requires another empirical condition, so the regress perpetually reproduces the absence of an empirical unconditioned.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-02","text":"Antinomy recurs whenever empirical validity is inflated into absolute reality; critical differentiation then dissolves the contradiction by redistributing relations across jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD1","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-03","text":"Functional compensation forms a recurrent loop in which objective presentation is withdrawn while rational organization and practical force increase.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:D1","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-04","text":"Normativity loops from rational ideas to empirical action and back: ought motivates rational causality, while the ideal measures and reforms the action that never fully embodies it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_3:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-05","text":"The escalation path runs from a single action, through the dependence of the world-series, to the formal determination of a transcendent individual object.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS1","CH_BA94_IS_4:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-06","text":"Complete determination is the hidden attractor joining causal origination, necessary conditioning, and ideal individuality, although no empirical totality can satisfy it.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A5","CH_BA94_SI_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:RLE-07","text":"Every attempted escape from empirical conditioning is recaptured by critique and returned as a nonempirical relation, limiting concept, or regulative standard.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_4:ME1","CH_BA94_SI_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B01","text":"Empirical presentation → temporal and existential conditioning → exceptionless causal explanation and indefinite regress.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS2","CH_BA94_IS_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B02","text":"Demand for completion → search for first cause or necessary member → contradiction produced by placing the unconditioned inside the conditioned series.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:FGS2","CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B03","text":"Critical diagnosis → phenomena cease to be treated as things in themselves → empirical validity loses ontological exhaustiveness.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS2","CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B04","text":"Dynamical differentiation → heterogeneous conditioning → empirical and intelligible relations coexist without empirical interference.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS4","CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B05","text":"Intelligible relocation → freedom grounds imputation and necessary being limits dependence → neither acquires theoretical actuality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B06","text":"Epistemic withholding → absence of objective reality → preservation of non-chimerical, regulative, and practical function.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:A12","CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FGS-B07","text":"Individuation by reason → exhaustive a priori determination → ideal as archetype → empirical incompleteness becomes measurable and indefinitely reformable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:AS3","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AFM-B01","text":"Universal empirical conditioning → failed search for an empirical unconditioned → transcendental contradiction → differentiation of empirical and intelligible relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AF1","CH_BA94_IS_4:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AFM-B02","text":"Differentiated relations → compatibility of natural necessity with freedom → compatibility of phenomenal contingency with possible intelligible necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D11","CH_BA94_IS_4:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AFM-B03","text":"Compatibility without proof → restriction of supersensible cognition → relocation of the unconditioned from cosmological object to transcendent idea.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AF3","CH_BA94_IS_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:AFM-B04","text":"Transcendent idea → rational individuation → ideal archetype → comparison, criticism, practical regulation, and indefinite perfectibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS3","CH_BA94_SI_3:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-01","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a missing empirical member into a differentiated relation, limiting thought, or rational standard.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ME3","CH_BA94_IS_4:ME1","CH_BA94_SI_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-02","text":"Empirical completeness and ontological completeness become separable, allowing unrestricted science without empirical exclusivism.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ME2","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-03","text":"Nonobjectivity is reclassified from cognitive failure into the condition under which boundary-concepts and ideals preserve their proper functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-04","text":"The human subject becomes simultaneously naturally determined, rationally imputable, and normatively perfectible.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ME4","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-05","text":"Endless regress and unattainable perfection cease to signify mere failure and become the temporal forms of empirical inquiry and practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:ME3","CH_BA94_SI_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MME-06","text":"Reading must distinguish existence, real possibility, logical compatibility, rational determination, and regulative validity rather than treating them as one modal status.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ME7","CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit objects are free action, the necessary ground of phenomenal existence, and the rational ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT1","CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] The system distributes conditioning, explanation, and determination across empirical series, intelligible relations, and regulative archetypes.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT2","CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] The system limits both dogmatic transcendence and empirical exclusivism by converting supersensible claims into differentiated permissions and prohibitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT3","CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality is presented as temporally conditioned appearance underlain by timelessly conceived relations, while rational completion remains asymptotic rather than historically attained.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ALT4","CH_BA94_IS_4:ALT4","CH_BA94_SI_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The argument repeatedly performs its own rule by withdrawing objective assertion, preserving a restricted rational function, and returning that function to empirical inquiry or practice.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:AS7","CH_BA94_IS_4:FGS5","CH_BA94_SI_3:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FL-01","text":"The attribution of intelligible causality exceeds the acknowledged absence of any cognizable mechanism connecting timeless ground and temporal effect.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SNX2","CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FL-02","text":"The architecture repeatedly moves from non-impossibility to positive functional attribution, although its explicit conclusions establish only compatibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT5","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FL-03","text":"Complete empirical determination remains in tension with rational blame, counterfactual ought, and the subject’s representation as capable of acting otherwise.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT7","CH_BA94_IS_3:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FL-04","text":"The necessary being is denied assertion and determinate cognition but immediately becomes the projected object of a new transcendent inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT5","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:FL-05","text":"The ideal must be individual and exhaustively determined while lacking the objective reality ordinarily associated with an individual object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT5","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MP-01","text":"The unconditioned mutates from an originating cause into a nonmember condition and finally into a standard of rational completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A4","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6","CH_BA94_SI_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MP-02","text":"The intelligible changes function from causal ground to ontological boundary and then to noncausal rational determination, so recurrence of the term does not imply stable identity.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:D5","CH_BA94_IS_4:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MP-03","text":"Practical force mutates from reason’s alleged production of phenomenal effects into the ideal’s critical measurement of action.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:A14","CH_BA94_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MP-04","text":"Absence changes valence from epistemic deprivation into the enabling condition of universality, regulative validity, and perfectibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_4:SNX3","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a seamless empirical network suspended from intelligible relations that cannot enter the network as observable members.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a double-aspect node: empirically determined in every appearance, rationally imputable in action, and perpetually deficient when compared with its ideal archetype.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:HSI-03","text":"Time is an endless empirical regress bordered by timelessly conceived grounds and redirected into asymptotic practical improvement.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:DNX3","CH_BA94_IS_4:A9","CH_BA94_SI_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists neither in breaking natural law nor attaining the ideal, but in maintaining jurisdictional distinctions while orienting empirical conduct toward unavailable rational completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the shared movement from empirical conditioning through intelligible differentiation to epistemically restricted regulative function.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT1","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT1","CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MRT-B02","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if empirical validity is restricted to appearances and supersensible admission remains regulative rather than constitutive.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT3","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT2","CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning intelligible efficacy, positive conception of an extramundane condition, and the ontological status of ideal individuality.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT2","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT4","CH_BA94_SI_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_25:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction is strongest where it preserves empirical completeness without ontological exhaustion and weakest where compatibility is compressed into positive attribution.","refs":["CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT4","CH_BA94_IS_4:MRT3","CH_BA94_IS_3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 73–75","context":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General","original_title":"Chapters 73–75","original_context":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General"},"original_title":"Chapters 73–75","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_26","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 76–78","order":26,"parents":["CH_BA94_SI_4","CH_BA94_SIAE","CH_BA94_SIIO"],"revision":"CL_BA94_26@R001","sha256":"e8502b49ebf1c93e4e43a3a1ed77cad50d85b320336b894422bd4116e029e715","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B01","text":"Reason seeks complete determination and an unconditioned ground, yet cannot convert this demand for completion into cognition of an independently existing total object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B02","text":"The totality of reality is generated as an ideal required for thought, not initially as an object whose existence has been established.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B03","text":"Transcendental illusion begins when a condition governing empirical determination is converted into an individual totality, a highest cause, and finally a personified supreme being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A13","CH_BA94_SI_4:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B04","text":"Causal regress converts the demand for an unconditioned ground into a search for a concept suitable to necessary existence, then mistakes comparative conceptual suitability for existential determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B05","text":"The cosmological route cannot identify its necessary being without drawing on the ontological privilege of complete reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:SD2","CH_BA94_SIIO:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B06","text":"The ontological route collapses because existence cannot function as a determining predicate contained in the concept of a most real being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B07","text":"The distinct speculative proof-types converge as variants of one failed operation: transforming a rational need for completion into knowledge of an unconditioned existent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AS-B08","text":"The terminal position preserves the Supreme Being as a regulative or practically significant orientation while foreclosing its theoretical demonstration.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:SD5","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Complete determination requires relation to a total field of possible predicates and therefore exceeds mere exclusion of contradictories.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_4:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Negative determination is structurally derivative of affirmative reality, because limitation is intelligible only against a prior material of reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A6","CH_BA94_SI_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The totality of reality condenses into the ens realissimum as the ideal through which finite determinations are conceived as partial and limited.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The ens realissimum changes function from regulative prototype to preferred candidate for necessity, but never acquires theoretical existence through that transition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SIAE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Reason’s demand for an unconditioned ground arises from conditioned existence and causal regress, but the demand itself supplies no object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIAE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Speculative theology depends on the illicit conversion of non-equivalent operations—regress, conceptual selection, existential assertion, and practical endorsement—into one alleged proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT2","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Necessity belonging to a judgement or predicate-relation cannot establish the necessary existence of the subject or object judged.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Existential cognition requires an object’s givenness within possible experience and cannot be supplied by conceptual completeness alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A11","CH_BA94_SIIO:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] The exhaustion of speculative proof-paths terminates not in atheistic demonstration but in the foreclosure of theoretical theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Practical obligation may sustain subjective rational adherence where speculative cognition fails, but cannot restore objective proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A9","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-01","text":"Determination recurs, but migrates from the logical exclusion of predicates to the transcendental relation of each object to a total material field of reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:ME1","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-02","text":"Total reality retains its identity as a required whole while changing function from the material of possibility to an individualized ideal and then to a merely preferred candidate for necessary existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SIAE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-03","text":"The unconditioned migrates from a condition of systematic thought, through a demanded terminus of causal regress, into a limit-idea that cannot be given as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-04","text":"The ens realissimum reverses from archetype of finite determination into a concept whose alleged uniqueness and existential force are progressively dismantled.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:D4","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-05","text":"Necessity is displaced from logical relation, to alleged ontological status, to a differentiated field of conceptual compatibility, existential assertion, and practical endorsement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:A2","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-06","text":"Possible experience changes function from the unified field within which objects are cognizable to the criterion restricting legitimate existential claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A12","CH_BA94_SIIO:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RMD-07","text":"Theological personification is absorbed into a higher-order diagnosis of reification rather than retained as an independent theological result.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:ME5","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-01","text":"The disjunctive grammar of contradictory predicates remains local machinery; it becomes current-level significant only when it is expanded into the demand for exhaustive determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-02","text":"The priority of affirmative reality over privation is a local derivational ground for the ideal, but its current-level role is to explain why complete reality can become a privileged theological candidate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A7","CH_BA94_SI_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between regulative representation and objectively given existence scales upward as the cluster’s principal critical boundary.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-04","text":"The ens realissimum bridges all units by linking the ideal of complete determination to cosmological selection and ontological invalidation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SD2","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-05","text":"The classification of speculative proofs becomes a current-level closure mechanism because it establishes that no alternative route escapes the same transcendental error.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS6","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:CGD-06","text":"Practical supplementation remains a conditional residue rather than a solution, because it changes the modality of assent without repairing speculative insufficiency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS5","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-01","text":"Complete conceptual determination is not existential determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:NX6","CH_BA94_SIIO:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-02","text":"The empirical unity required for cognition is not an individual object containing all reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:NX7","CH_BA94_SI_4:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-03","text":"Explanatory termination is not evidence of a real, determinate, uniquely necessary terminus.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:DNX2","CH_BA94_SIAE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-04","text":"The necessity of predicates under an assumed subject is not the necessity of the subject’s existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:NX2","CH_BA94_SIIO:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-05","text":"Logical possibility and freedom from contradiction are not evidence of real possibility or actual existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:NX5","CH_BA94_SIIO:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-06","text":"Practical reinforcement is not an objective speculative proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:GNX-07","text":"The failure of theological proofs is not a proof of divine non-existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:DBN-01","text":"Essence cannot generate being through analysis, however complete, because no conceptual totality supplies existential positing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX4","CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:DBN-02","text":"The unity required by cognition cannot be transferred into the independent ontology of a total being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:DNX5","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:DBN-03","text":"No supersensible object can be theoretically cognized without an independent mode of givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX4","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:DBN-04","text":"No finite cognition can possess the totality of possibility that complete determination requires in concreto.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:DBN-05","text":"No speculative proof-route can authorize determinate knowledge of supersensible existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8","CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSA-01","text":"The sought coincidence of rational necessity, conceptual necessity, and real existence is structurally unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSA-02","text":"Reason cannot convert its unavoidable demand for a final ground into theoretical possession of such a ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX1","CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSA-03","text":"No theoretical passage leads from the ideal of complete reality to an unconditioned object available for cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SNX1","CH_BA94_SIIO:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSA-04","text":"Speculative reason is organized around an irreducible nonclosure: it cannot cease demanding the unconditioned and cannot know it as existent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT5","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-01","text":"Theology arises when the ideal required for complete determination is converted into an independent causal object; proof-critique therefore diagnoses reification rather than merely invalid inference.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-03","CL_BA94_26:GA-04","CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CH_BA94_SI_4:A10","CH_BA94_SI_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-02","text":"Complete reality can be selected as compatible with necessary existence only after necessary existence has already been presupposed; the candidate cannot ground the necessity it is chosen to exemplify.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-03","CL_BA94_26:GA-05","CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIAE:D1","CH_BA94_SIAE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-03","text":"The ontological prohibition on deriving existence from conceptual content deprives the cosmological proof of the conceptual mechanism by which it identifies its alleged necessary being.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CL_BA94_26:GA-07","CL_BA94_26:GA-08","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD2","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-04","text":"The conversion of distributive empirical unity into a collective object and the conversion of logical necessity into real existence are structurally homologous forms of transcendental overreach.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-04","CL_BA94_26:GA-07","CH_BA94_SI_4:A14","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-05","text":"Existence cannot complete the totality of reality as a final perfection, because existence posits an object without adding a predicate to its concept.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-02","CL_BA94_26:GA-07","CL_BA94_26:GA-08","CH_BA94_SI_4:A7","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:XD-06","text":"Practical supplementation preserves rational orientation only by changing the status of the Supreme Being from theoretically demonstrable object to subjectively authorized commitment.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-09","CL_BA94_26:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIAE:A9","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-01","text":"Speculative theology becomes a self-diagnostic machine in which reason produces a total object from its own need for completion and then exposes that production as illegitimate.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-04","CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CL_BA94_26:GA-09","CH_BA94_SI_4:SD3","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-02","text":"Necessity is redistributed into distinct registers—logical conditionality, regulative demand, alleged ontological status, existential assertion, and practical authorization—which cannot be collapsed into one claim.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-05","CL_BA94_26:GA-07","CL_BA94_26:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME2","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-03","text":"The unconditioned is necessary as a limit regulating reason’s movement but impossible as a theoretically cognized object.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-05","CL_BA94_26:GA-08","CL_BA94_26:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT5","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-04","text":"Empirical and a priori theology become derivative variants of one transcendental drive toward unconditioned completion rather than independent evidential enterprises.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-05","CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME4","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-05","text":"The decisive critical achievement is a rule of objectivity: existential claims require givenness, not maximal conceptual content or explanatory indispensability.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-07","CL_BA94_26:GA-08","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD1","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:BSD-06","text":"The Supreme Being survives speculative collapse only in a transformed mode: thinkable as an idea, usable as orientation, but unavailable as theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-09","CL_BA94_26:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-01","text":"Complete determination generates a totality of reality, totality generates an individual ideal, and the ideal repeatedly attracts its own reification as an existing highest being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A2","CH_BA94_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-02","text":"Causal regress generates the demand for necessity, presupposed necessity selects complete reality, and the collapse of uniqueness returns reason to an indeterminate demand for a terminus.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-03","text":"Logical predicate-necessity is repeatedly mistaken for necessary existence until subject-suppression dissolves the alleged necessity of the object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:D3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-04","text":"The unconditioned functions as the dominant attractor: it organizes totalization, causal ascent, proof-construction, and practical supplementation without becoming cognitively present.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A1","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-05","text":"The three speculative proof-types form an escalation path whose apparent diversity contracts into one common dependence on transcendental completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:SD3","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:RLE-06","text":"Practical reason interrupts speculative failure without cancelling it, preserving adherence while leaving theoretical nonclosure intact.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A9","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B01","text":"Finite determination is related to an exhaustive field of possibility rather than secured by pairwise logical exclusion alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B02","text":"Privative negation subordinates finite difference to affirmative reality, thereby generating the thought of a total material of possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A6","CH_BA94_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B03","text":"The total material of reality is condensed into an individual ideal that serves as prototype for finite determinations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8","CH_BA94_SI_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B04","text":"Conditioned existence translates the ideal of systematic completion into a demand for an unconditioned and necessary ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B05","text":"Proof selects complete reality as the most adequate candidate for necessity and mistakes this suitability for evidence of existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A6","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B06","text":"Critical analysis separates conditional predicate-necessity from necessary being and strips existence of predicate-status.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:D3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B07","text":"Existential cognition is restricted to possible experience, blocking the conversion of supersensible ideas into objects of theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:AS6","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FGS-B08","text":"The blocked proof leaves the ideal as a regulative orientation and permits only a non-theoretical practical remainder.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SD6","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B01","text":"Logical determinability → demand for complete determination beyond contradiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A1","CH_BA94_SI_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B02","text":"Complete determination → totality of affirmative reality as the material of possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B03","text":"Total reality → transcendental ideal of the ens realissimum.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B04","text":"Ideal of completion → causal demand for an unconditioned necessary ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B05","text":"Presupposed necessity → conceptual selection of complete reality as privileged candidate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:D1","CH_BA94_SIAE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B06","text":"Privileged candidate → ontological assertion → dissolution through the distinction between conceptual content and existential positing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:SD3","CH_BA94_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:AFM-B07","text":"Speculative foreclosure → regulative persistence and conditional practical residue.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:NX8","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-01","text":"Totality is reclassified as a necessary function of reason that becomes illusory when treated as an object independently containing all reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:ME3","CH_BA94_SIAE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-02","text":"The theological field is redrawn into distinct operations of causal regress, conceptual selection, existential assertion, and practical endorsement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-03","text":"The apparent opposition between empirical and a priori proof collapses because both are governed by the same demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-04","text":"Existence is relocated from conceptual inventory to the relation between a concept and an object’s possible givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:ME2","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-05","text":"Theology is reclassified from a body of successful proofs into a record of transformations by which regulative representations become reified objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:ME5","CH_BA94_SI_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MME-06","text":"Critical reading must track transitions between thought, object, necessity, and existence rather than merely assess the final theological conclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system tests whether a Supreme Being can be established through totality, causal regress, necessary existence, or conceptual analysis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAE:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Complete determination, causal regress, conceptual selection, and existential positing operate as non-equivalent mechanisms whose conflation produces transcendental theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT2","CH_BA94_SI_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critique preserves reason’s demand for systematic completion while blocking every conversion of that demand into constitutive cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIIO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Actuality is not an intensified possibility, while the unconditioned persists as a projected terminus of regress rather than an established being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The system performs its critique by reconstructing the production of theological objects before dismantling the transitions that produced them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_4:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FL-01","text":"Reason cannot cease demanding the unconditioned, yet the unconditioned remains unavailable as a theoretically knowable object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:ALT5","CH_BA94_SIIO:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FL-02","text":"The derivation of a totality of reality depends on the contested priority of affirmative reality over negation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FL-03","text":"The restriction of existential cognition to possible experience strains against the retained thinkability of ideas that exceed experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FL-04","text":"Practical necessity can authorize commitment while remaining unable to provide the objective cognition demanded by speculative proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:FL-05","text":"The representation of the totality of reality as an individual ideal remains underdetermined rather than derivatively secured.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MP-01","text":"The material totality of reality mutates into an individual ideal when reason condenses a condition of complete determination into the ens realissimum.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MP-02","text":"The regulative ideal mutates into an alleged necessary being when complete reality is selected as the concept most compatible with unconditioned necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:A6","CH_BA94_SIAE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MP-03","text":"Conditional necessity within judgement mutates illicitly into the claim of an absolutely necessary object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MP-04","text":"Existence mutates from an apparent component of complete reality into a relation of objective positing and experiential givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MP-05","text":"The failure of speculative theology mutates the Supreme Being from alleged object of knowledge into regulative idea and possible practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SD6","CH_BA94_SIAE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of finite determinations whose objects are intelligible only through relations of limitation and possible experiential givenness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:A9","CH_BA94_SIIO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:HSI-02","text":"Reason appears as a totalizing projector that condenses the material of possibility into an ideal and repeatedly projects that ideal as an independently existing source.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:AS3","CH_BA94_SI_4:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:HSI-03","text":"The subject becomes a finite rational agent required to use totalizing ideas for orientation while refusing to treat them as cognized objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:SD4","CH_BA94_SIIO:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:HSI-04","text":"Time appears as recursive regress: every conditioned ground renews the demand for completion, while critique indefinitely delays the conversion of that demand into a final object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:AS1","CH_BA94_SIAE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from complete determination and total reality to the failure of speculative proofs of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT1","CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MRT-B02","text":"Coherence depends on maintaining the distinctions between conceptual non-derivability, real possibility, existential givenness, and practical authorization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT2","CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the individuation of the transcendental ideal and the positive status of supersensible existence outside cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT3","CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it exposes the equivocation between conditional necessity in thought and unconditional existence of an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIO:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_26:MRT-B05","text":"The indispensable irreducible element is the recursive sequence in which reason generates the illusion it must subsequently diagnose, limit, and retain as regulative demand.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_4:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIAE:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 76–78","context":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","original_title":"Chapters 76–78","original_context":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God"},"original_title":"Chapters 76–78","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_27","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 79–81","order":27,"parents":["CH_BA94_SVIC","CH_BA94_SVIP","CH_BA94_SVCA"],"revision":"CL_BA94_27@R001","sha256":"d9777c3402a631954574e9b35280952905a55410a2157a30b59ef12e8805e8f8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-01","text":"No speculative route from concepts, existence, or natural order can establish a Supreme Being without extending cognition beyond the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:AS3","CH_BA94_SVCA:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-02","text":"The apparently independent proofs form a descending dependency chain in which physico-theology imports cosmological necessity and cosmology reinstates ontological existence-by-concept.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:AS5","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-03","text":"Empirical principles cannot reach absolute totality, while conceptual determination cannot independently posit the existence it requires.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A10","CH_BA94_SVCA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-04","text":"The collapse of the proofs becomes a jurisdictional limit on theoretical cognition rather than a collection of corrigible inferential errors.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-05","text":"The same critical restriction that invalidates speculative theism also prevents speculative failure from becoming a proof of atheism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:AS5","CH_BA94_SVCA:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-06","text":"The Supreme Being consequently changes from a purportedly demonstrable object into a regulative ideal subject to conceptual purification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-07","text":"The movement is recursively reductive: every empirical elevation demands a transcendental supplement, and every supplement returns to the failed ontological transition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:AS7","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AS-08","text":"Closure is asymmetrical: speculative possession of the unconditioned is denied, explanatory inquiry remains open, and possible theological warrant is reserved for practical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX4","CH_BA94_SVCA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Principles supplying objective theoretical cognition are valid only within possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Existence cannot be extracted from a concept, however complete or non-contradictory that concept may be.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:NX1","CH_BA94_SVCA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Every empirically accessible cause remains conditioned, whereas a cause outside the empirical series cannot be reached by empirical causality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A3","CH_BA94_SVIP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Natural purposiveness warrants at most an architect of worldly form, not an all-sufficient creator of matter and existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A6","CH_BA94_SVIP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Comparative empirical predicates cannot yield the absolute totality required for a completely determinate Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A8","CH_BA94_SVIP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Every speculative proof of a Supreme Being ultimately depends upon the ontological transition from complete concept to necessary existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D3","CH_BA94_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Speculative reason can establish neither the existence nor the nonexistence of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A13","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Reason necessarily orients explanation toward an unconditioned ground that it cannot possess as an object of speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A7","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Necessity changes from an alleged property of an object into a regulative demand and then into a possible practical requirement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVCA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The Supreme Being is retained as an as-if ground of systematic unity without corresponding theoretical existence-knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A13","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Speculative reason retains a legitimate negative office: it can purify the divine concept and protect the possibility of practical postulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A14","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Theological warrant divides by jurisdiction: theoretical cognition concerns being, while practical reason may posit conditions required by obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A1","CH_BA94_SVCA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-01","text":"Causality retains a stable identity as an empirical principle but changes critical function from explaining appearances to exposing every attempted ascent beyond them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A2","CH_BA94_SVCA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-02","text":"Empirical existence and natural order recur as occasions for theological inference, but neither supplies the objective warrant or absolute proportionality required by the conclusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D1","CH_BA94_SVIP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-03","text":"Absolute necessity migrates from a purported terminus of causal regression into a regulative horizon and finally into the distinct jurisdiction of practical requirement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ME3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-04","text":"The Supreme Being mutates from a speculatively knowable entity into a concept retained without theoretical existence-knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-05","text":"The empirical character of a proof reverses valence: what initially promises independent evidence becomes merely the entrance through which pure conceptual dependence is concealed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:NX3","CH_BA94_SVIP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-06","text":"The local distinction between architect and creator is absorbed into the higher-order rule that observed effects cannot determine an absolutely supreme cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:ME1","CH_BA94_SVCA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-07","text":"The ontological proof disappears as an independent competitor and becomes the hidden attractor into which every other speculative proof collapses.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:NX2","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RMD-08","text":"Speculative failure is displaced rather than reversed: it becomes the negative condition under which practical postulation may remain admissible without becoming knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:D9","CH_BA94_SVCA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-01","text":"The forced termination of causal regress remains local machinery, while the restriction preventing such termination from reaching the supersensible scales into a general jurisdictional limit.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-02","text":"The architect–creator distinction is locally tied to natural design but scales upward as a prohibition against converting phenomenal arrangement into an ontological explanation of existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT4","CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-03","text":"The taxonomy of theological sources is not itself a proof; it becomes current-level machinery for redistributing reason into unequal theoretical, practical, and revelatory jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:AS1","CH_BA94_SVCA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-04","text":"Dependence on ontological inference bridges the individual proof-critiques and supplies the principal mechanism of their current-level unification.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D3","CH_BA94_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-05","text":"The methodological hypothesis of an all-sufficient ground is superseded as an existence-claim and recoded as a regulative ideal.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D8","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-06","text":"The double failure of individual proofs catalyzes the transition from local refutation to a universal restriction on speculative theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SD1","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:CGD-07","text":"Practical postulation marks the terminal boundary of the cluster rather than a completed proof, because its derivation from moral obligation remains deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:AS6","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-01","text":"A concept of a Supreme Being cannot contain or analytically produce the existence of its object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-02","text":"Empirical causality cannot serve as a bridge to a supersensible cause outside possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:NX3","CH_BA94_SVCA:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-03","text":"The inability to complete a causal regress does not establish an objectively existing first cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A2","CH_BA94_SVIC:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-04","text":"Natural purposiveness does not establish either the creation of worldly matter or an all-sufficient world-creator.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:NX4","CH_BA94_SVIP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-05","text":"Finite comparison and magnification cannot yield an absolute predicate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-06","text":"The invalidity of speculative theism cannot be converted into speculative atheism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:NX9","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:GNX-07","text":"A purified concept of God cannot be produced by enlarging worldly intelligence or human predicates without limit.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:NX10","CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes any cognitive standpoint external to the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-02","text":"The totality of empirical conditions cannot itself be treated as an empirical object requiring an external empirical cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-03","text":"Necessity cannot be used univocally across empirical explanation, conceptual determination, regulative orientation, and moral obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-04","text":"The structural place of an ultimate ground cannot be occupied by any object available to speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-05","text":"No empirical theology remains independent once its desired object requires pure concepts of totality and necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:SD2","CH_BA94_SVIP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:DBN-06","text":"The limits of theoretical proof cannot determine the total limits of rational assent, because practical reason constitutes a different jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSA-01","text":"The deepest structuring absence is an object adequate to the idea of an absolutely necessary and all-sufficient being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSA-02","text":"Final explanatory closure remains unavailable even though reason cannot cease demanding an unconditioned ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX4","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSA-03","text":"An external standpoint from which world and ground could be cognized together is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSA-04","text":"No positive speculative mediation connects the demand for necessity with the determinate existence of the ens realissimum.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSA-05","text":"Practical postulation cannot fill the speculative absence without ceasing to be practical and becoming the theoretical cognition the system forbids.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT2","CH_BA94_SVCA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-01","text":"The three speculative routes form a single dependency cascade: natural design requires cosmological necessity, and cosmological necessity requires ontological determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-06","CH_BA94_SVIP:A11","CH_BA94_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-02","text":"Cosmological and physico-theological inference fail at the same boundary because causality has objective validity only for relations within possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-01","CL_BA94_27:GA-03","CH_BA94_SVIC:A1","CH_BA94_SVIP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-03","text":"The more determinate a theological conclusion becomes, the less its predicates can be supplied by the empirical existence or order from which inference began.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-03","CL_BA94_27:GA-05","CH_BA94_SVIC:A5","CH_BA94_SVIP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-04","text":"Causal regress and natural purposiveness can orient inquiry toward unity only if neither is converted into cognition of a transcendent object.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-08","CL_BA94_27:GA-10","CH_BA94_SVIC:D6","CH_BA94_SVIP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-05","text":"The two-stage failure of cosmological proof and the derivative failure of physico-theology jointly produce a universal restriction on speculative theology.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-06","CL_BA94_27:GA-07","CH_BA94_SVIC:SD1","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-06","text":"Speculative cognition of God is unavailable in both affirmative and negative form, producing disciplined suspension rather than a rival metaphysical conclusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-07","CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX1","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-07","text":"Negative critique preserves practical admissibility only by leaving the moral derivation of the postulate unresolved at this scale.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-11","CL_BA94_27:GA-12","CH_BA94_SVCA:D9","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:XD-08","text":"The failure of finite natural predicates to measure divine perfection becomes the basis for purifying the theological concept of empirical contamination.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-05","CL_BA94_27:GA-11","CH_BA94_SVIP:DNX1","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-01","text":"The plurality of speculative proofs is reclassified as a dependency graph whose distinct entrances converge on one unsupported ontological operation.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-06","CH_BA94_SVIC:ME1","CH_BA94_SVIP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-02","text":"Systematic completeness can regulate inquiry only because it is not possessed as an ultimate object that would terminate inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-08","CL_BA94_27:GA-10","CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX4","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-03","text":"Theology is transformed from a project of acquiring a supersensible object into an allocation of conceptual, regulative, and practical functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-11","CL_BA94_27:GA-12","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD3","CH_BA94_SVCA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-04","text":"The knowing subject becomes a finite standpoint unable to step outside the conditions that confer objective validity upon cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-01","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-05","text":"Explanatory time becomes an indefinitely open regress oriented by completeness but prohibited from closing upon an empirical first ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-03","CL_BA94_27:GA-08","CH_BA94_SVIC:SD3","CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-06","text":"The architecture produces symmetric speculative suspension but asymmetric rational possibility, since practical postulation survives where theoretical assertion and denial do not.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-07","CL_BA94_27:GA-11","CL_BA94_27:GA-12","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4","CH_BA94_SVCA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:BSD-07","text":"Critical method becomes a dependency audit that distinguishes empirical occasions for inference from the transcendental warrants secretly performing the conclusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-01","CL_BA94_27:GA-06","CH_BA94_SVIC:D1","CH_BA94_SVIP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-01","text":"Reason demands an unconditioned ground, projects that demand as an object, fails to conceive any determinate object as necessary, and renews the demand as a regulative horizon.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:FGS5","CH_BA94_SVIC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-02","text":"Natural order escalates into an architect, the architect into absolute perfection, perfection into necessary being, and necessary being into concealed ontology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:D3","CH_BA94_SVIP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-03","text":"Ontological inference functions as the principal attractor because every supposedly independent proof acquires determinate theological force only by entering it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D3","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-04","text":"Empirical ascent generates a boundary loop: remaining within experience yields only conditioned causes, while leaving experience destroys the warrant for causal inference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:SD1","CH_BA94_SVIP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-05","text":"The loss of the Supreme Being as a theoretical object returns it as an ideal required for systematic unity but not for possession of an ultimate ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SD4","CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-06","text":"Negative critique escalates into conceptual purification, and purification opens a practical possibility without reversing theoretical failure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:D9","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:RLE-07","text":"Necessity becomes a phase-changing term whose empirical, regulative, and practical functions must remain non-univocal for the system to operate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX3","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVCA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B01","text":"Possible experience supplies conditioned objects and the formal conditions under which objective theoretical principles are valid.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B02","text":"Reason orients the conditioned series toward a complete and necessary ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B03","text":"The demand for completion projects conceptual, causal, and natural-order routes toward a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:AS3","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B04","text":"Each empirical route abandons its empirical warrant when it seeks absolute necessity or complete determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D2","CH_BA94_SVIP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B05","text":"The imported ontological transition fails because conceptual determination cannot posit the existence of its object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B06","text":"Recurrent failure is generalized into a jurisdictional prohibition against speculative cognition or refutation of supersensible existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1","CH_BA94_SVCA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FGS-B07","text":"The prohibited object is retained as a purified regulative ideal, while practical reason receives a conditional postulatory opening.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A14","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned existence and observed natural order → demand for an unconditioned explanatory ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:AS2","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for a ground → causal and design extrapolation → provisional necessary cause or intelligent architect.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:AS2","CH_BA94_SVIP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B03","text":"Provisional cause or architect → demand for complete determination and absolute perfection.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:AS4","CH_BA94_SVIP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B04","text":"Absolute perfection → ontological transition from concept to necessary existence → failure of objective positing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B05","text":"Failed ontological transition → collapse of every speculative proof → reciprocal disqualification of speculative disproof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:D5","CH_BA94_SVCA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:AFM-B06","text":"Collapse of speculative theology → negative tribunal → regulative ideal → conditional practical postulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-01","text":"The proof-landscape is redrawn from independent arguments into different entrances to a single ontological dependency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-02","text":"The opposition between empirical and conceptual proof collapses because empirical beginnings conceal pure conceptual completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:NX3","CH_BA94_SVIP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-03","text":"Causality is reclassified from a universal bridge between world and ground into a rule for phenomenal relations.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:ME3","CH_BA94_SVIC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-04","text":"Absolute necessity is reclassified from an objective predicate into a regulative demand whose practical use belongs to another jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ME3","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-05","text":"Divine predicates are reclassified from magnified empirical features into purified determinations that finite observation cannot supply.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SD5","CH_BA94_SVCA:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-06","text":"Theology becomes a differentiated field of rational functions rather than a unitary speculative science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:ME1","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-07","text":"Theism and atheism cease to exhaust the theoretical terrain because speculative reason authorizes neither existential assertion nor denial.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:ME4","CH_BA94_SVCA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MME-08","text":"Explanatory inquiry is separated from final closure, allowing indefinite empirical derivation under an unavailable horizon of completeness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ME5","CH_BA94_SVIC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system tests conceptual, cosmological, and natural-order routes to the existence and attributes of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:AS1","CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, proof-plurality conceals ontological dependency beneath principles whose objective validity is restricted to experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:A12","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, speculative theology becomes a negative tribunal that invalidates dogmatic assertion and denial while purifying admissible concepts.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT3","CH_BA94_SVCA:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an open series of conditioned appearances from which no theoretical passage to an unconditioned object is available.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT4","CH_BA94_SVCA:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its thesis by recursively reducing apparently independent proofs to their concealed ontological transition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:AS6","CH_BA94_SVCA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:ALT-B06","text":"At the practical level, the axis of being gives way to the axis of obligation, permitting postulation from practical necessity rather than theoretical inference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A1","CH_BA94_SVCA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FL-01","text":"The system requires reason’s demand for an unconditioned ground while denying every object capable of satisfying that demand speculatively.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX5","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FL-02","text":"The divine concept remains indispensable as an ideal even though it is denied objective theoretical reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FL-03","text":"Design analogy retains rational and heuristic legitimacy while remaining demonstratively inadequate for theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT5","CH_BA94_SVIP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:FL-04","text":"Practical postulation is required for the system’s positive theological opening, but its derivation from moral obligation is deferred rather than established here.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MP-01","text":"Necessity mutates from alleged objective property to regulative orientation and then to a possible condition postulated by practical law.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:DNX3","CH_BA94_SVIC:A9","CH_BA94_SVCA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MP-02","text":"Cause changes from a rule connecting empirical events into an illicit explanation of existence as such, and that mutation triggers critical invalidation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:D1","CH_BA94_SVCA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MP-03","text":"The Supreme Being changes status from inferred object to regulative ideal and then to a possible practical postulate without regaining theoretical objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SD2","CH_BA94_SVCA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MP-04","text":"Repeated local proof-failures undergo a scale shift and become a jurisdictional limit on every theoretical route to supersensible existence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned empirical network governed by immanent principles and oriented toward an unoccupiable horizon of totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVCA:A3","CH_BA94_SVCA:A6","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:HSI-02","text":"The subject is finite reason whose demand for completion generates projected objects that its own conditions of cognition prevent it from possessing.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:SD5","CH_BA94_SVCA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:HSI-03","text":"Time is an indefinitely open regress rather than a completed ascent to a first member, while practical obligation introduces a non-theoretical axis of necessity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:ALT4","CH_BA94_SVIC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in pursuing systematic unity, refusing empirical or conceptual hypostasis, and preserving practical possibility without converting it into knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:D6","CH_BA94_SVCA:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the dependency chain among the speculative proofs, their common invalidation, and the reassignment of the Supreme Being to regulative and practical functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT1","CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined at both ends because the prior ontological critique and the later moral derivation are presupposed rather than fully supplied.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT2","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if constitutive, regulative, and practical necessity remain strictly distinct.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT3","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where ontological dependency and the restriction of causal explanation jointly dismantle the apparent independence of the proofs.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT4","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B05","text":"Adequate reconstruction depends on preserving the recursive sequence from empirical elevation through concealed conceptual dependence to negative speculative discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIP:MRT7","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_27:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is reason’s allegedly unavoidable demand for an unconditioned basis, whose natural necessity is asserted more fully than demonstrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SVIC:MRT5","CH_BA94_SVCA:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 79–81","context":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","original_title":"Chapters 79–81","original_context":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 79–81","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_28","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 82–84","order":28,"parents":["CH_BA94_APP2","CH_BA94_ITDM","CH_BA94_CIDP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_28@R001","sha256":"9f16a2d2c43681fa03942de820250af1c57bf3a1fa3e2c88e266246e27af79ae","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-01","text":"Reason must retain systematic direction even though finite cognition cannot legitimately construct objects beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AS1","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-02","text":"The demand for systematic unity is recovered by converting transcendent aspiration into regulative inquiry and materially proportioned construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AS5","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-03","text":"Because transcendental cognition lacks external empirical or intuitive tests, legitimate system-building requires a negative code directed at method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:AS5","CH_BA94_CIDP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-04","text":"The argument becomes a critical architectonic that diagnoses misemployment, recovers legitimate function, and formalizes the conditions of its safe exercise.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AS4","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-05","text":"Its movement is recursive: reason projects unity, exceeds its jurisdiction, undergoes critique, and returns as a regulated organizer of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD1","CH_BA94_CIDP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-06","text":"Reason consequently becomes both the source of systematic principles and the power that must be subjected to those principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-07","text":"Completion divides into an unattainable completion of empirical knowledge and an attainable formal completeness of cognitive organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A16","CH_BA94_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AS-08","text":"The resulting closure is a secure jurisdiction for open-ended terrestrial inquiry rather than a completed transcendent totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS2","CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the jurisdictional boundary of speculative cognition, but that boundary does not abolish reason’s demand for systematicity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Reason properly operates through the understanding by ordering its cognitions rather than by directly cognizing supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A4","CH_BA94_APP2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Transcendental ideas persist as necessary projections whose legitimate force lies in directing inquiry rather than constituting objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The projected systematic whole becomes both a regulative horizon for cognition and an architectonic plan for arranging its parts.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A10","CH_BA94_ITDM:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Cognitive limitation is an enabling condition of secure system-building because it determines which materials, claims, and methods can be integrated.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:SD1","CH_BA94_ITDM:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Legitimate method must be proportioned to available cognitive materials and differentiated according to the cognition or object being organized.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A4","CH_BA94_ITDM:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Pure reason’s lack of external constraint allows its general principles to generate interconnected illusion rather than merely isolated error.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Negative restraint becomes a constructive condition of knowledge wherever unrestricted positive expansion would amplify systematic illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:AS2","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Dialectic arises from the misemployment, hypostatization, or illicit transfer of rational procedures rather than from the mere existence of ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A3","CH_BA94_CIDP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Complete systematic unity must guide inquiry while remaining unavailable as a completed empirical object.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SNX1","CH_BA94_APP2:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Reason is recursively self-governing: it supplies principles of unity while becoming the object of critical discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] A legitimate system combines positive cognitive materials, regulative orientation, architectonic organization, and negative methodological control within finite experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:D4","CH_BA94_CIDP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The boundary of possible experience retains its prohibitive identity while acquiring the additional function of delimiting a secure cognitive jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Systematic unity changes from an asymptotic regulative horizon into the formal architectonic according to which bounded cognition is organized.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A16","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-03","text":"[Displacement] The soul, world-totality, and God cease to function as speculative objects and migrate into analogical procedures for organizing experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A18","CH_BA94_APP2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Reason changes from the presumed sovereign judge of cognition into a power whose authority makes its own distortions require special scrutiny.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:D3","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Negativity changes from apparent obstruction of knowledge into the disciplinary infrastructure that makes reliable positive cognition possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] Hypostatization first produces local contradiction and explanatory closure, then expands into a connected methodological system of fallacies.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AS7","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-07","text":"[Functional recurrence] The distinction between constrained and unconstrained cognition recurs as the demand that methods remain specific to their valid domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:D4","CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal transformation] Cognitive completion bifurcates into permanently unfinished empirical systematization and formally complete regulation of the systematizing process.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD4","CH_BA94_ITDM:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] The architectural image of materials, plan, and dwelling is absorbed into a general account of how finitude, method, and discipline jointly constitute systematic reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:AS1","CH_BA94_ITDM:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Homogeneity, specification, and affinity remain particular regulative maxims rather than the complete architecture of method.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A13","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The psychological, cosmological, and theological schemata remain domain-specific implementations of the wider rule that ideals may regulate without constituting objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A18","CH_BA94_APP2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The experiential boundary scales from a limit on speculative claims into the jurisdictional principle governing the entire method of pure reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Regulative systematicity bridges the recovery of transcendental ideas and the architectonic construction of a bounded cognitive system.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:FGS7","CH_BA94_ITDM:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The doctrine of method supplies the formal location in which the recovered uses of reason can be organized and policed.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:AS4","CH_BA94_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] The absence of empirical or intuitive tests converts ordinary criticism into a general discipline against systematic transcendental error.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIDP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-07","text":"[Recoded] Failed transcendent construction is not simply discarded; it is recoded as the negative condition for proportioned, finite, and secure construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:AF1","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] A generic method derived from logical form alone is superseded by object-sensitive regulation grounded in cognitive conditions and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A9","CH_BA94_ITDM:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-01","text":"Speculative cognition cannot convert the necessity of transcendental ideas into knowledge of corresponding supersensible beings.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:NX1","CH_BA94_APP2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-02","text":"Systematic unity cannot be treated as a completed empirical totality or as proof of an intrinsically known ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:NX4","CH_BA94_APP2:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-03","text":"Formal organization cannot compensate for absent or invalid cognitive materials.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX2","CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-04","text":"Reason’s aspiration, prestige, and internal coherence cannot independently establish the legitimacy of transcendental cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX3","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-05","text":"Methods validated under empirical or mathematical constraint cannot be transferred without qualification into the transcendental domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:D8","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-06","text":"A network of rational fallacies cannot be neutralized by isolated objections or episodic corrections.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:NX10","CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:GNX-07","text":"Secure systematicity cannot arise from private constructions, unrestricted speculative expansion, or a discipline-free autonomy of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:NX7","CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of the assumption that whatever reason necessarily projects must therefore exist as an object.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-02","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of the assumption that greater systematic coherence automatically yields greater ontological knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:D8","CH_BA94_APP2:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-03","text":"The architecture excludes the possibility that a complete formal plan can generate the missing matter of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A6","CH_BA94_ITDM:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-04","text":"The architecture disqualifies the model of reason as a self-certifying authority whose products require no independent restriction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX2","CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-05","text":"Finitude cannot be overcome by methodological sophistication, because valid method remains dependent on finite materials and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX2","CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:DBN-06","text":"Positive doctrinal construction cannot regulate its own excess without a distinct negative operation directed at the methods producing claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:DNX6","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable coincidence of projected systematic unity with a fully given object organizes the entire architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SNX1","CH_BA94_APP2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an absent external standpoint from which reason’s conformity with reality could be conclusively verified.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSA-03","text":"A completely self-transparent and discipline-free reason is ontologically inadmissible because reason necessarily remains vulnerable to its own projections.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:DNX8","CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSA-04","text":"The completed tower of speculative knowledge is the excluded possibility whose failure makes the bounded cognitive dwelling intelligible.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:NX1","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSA-05","text":"Final empirical completion must remain absent so that systematic unity can continue functioning as direction rather than becoming terminal possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SNX3","CH_BA94_APP2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-01","text":"Regulative ideas become operationally legitimate only when their direction of inquiry is embedded in a method proportioned to finite cognitive materials.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-03","CL_BA94_28:GA-05","CL_BA94_28:GA-06","CH_BA94_APP2:AS2","CH_BA94_ITDM:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-02","text":"The contradiction between unattainable systematic unity and a complete system is displaced by locating incompletion in empirical content and completeness in formal organization.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-04","CL_BA94_28:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_APP2:A16","CH_BA94_ITDM:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-03","text":"Discipline protects the regulative use of ideas by preventing their procedural orientation from leaking into constitutive assertions.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-03","CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-09","CH_BA94_APP2:MRT7","CH_BA94_CIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-04","text":"Cognitive limitation becomes productive because the same boundary that blocks transcendent construction supplies the scale for legitimate architectonic design.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-01","CL_BA94_28:GA-05","CL_BA94_28:GA-06","CH_BA94_ITDM:D1","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-05","text":"Reason’s intrinsic pressure to objectify systematic unity, combined with the absence of external tests, necessitates an internal negative code.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-07","CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-11","CH_BA94_APP2:SD1","CH_BA94_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-06","text":"The functions formerly assigned to metaphysical objects are preserved by relocating them within an architectonic of empirical organization.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-03","CL_BA94_28:GA-04","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_APP2:SD5","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-07","text":"Architectonic and discipline are mutually dependent because an undisciplined plan repeats transcendent construction, while unorganized restraint cannot produce a system.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-04","CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS2","CH_BA94_CIDP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:XD-08","text":"Systematic unity and systematic illusion arise from the same rational power, so critique must recursively preserve and restrict the operation it examines.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-07","CL_BA94_28:GA-09","CL_BA94_28:GA-11","CH_BA94_APP2:A6","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is reorganized as a layered status comprising determinate empirical validity, indirect regulative validity, and formal architectonic legitimacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-03","CL_BA94_28:GA-04","CL_BA94_28:GA-06","CH_BA94_APP2:SD2","CH_BA94_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-02","text":"Rational autonomy becomes recursive self-government rather than unrestricted self-extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-11","CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX5","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-03","text":"Cognitive time becomes asymptotic: inquiry remains indefinitely extensible even after its jurisdiction and method have been formally delimited.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-01","CL_BA94_28:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_APP2:SD4","CH_BA94_APP2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-04","text":"The knowing subject is redistributed across faculties and regulatory functions rather than retained as an undivided source of cognitive authority.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-02","CL_BA94_28:GA-11","CH_BA94_APP2:ALT2","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-05","text":"Critique becomes constitutional rather than merely censorial because it establishes the jurisdiction, divisions, and procedures within which cognition can securely operate.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-05","CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-06","text":"Negative judgment acquires positive systemic force by preventing the procedures of construction from destroying the conditions of valid construction.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:GA-09","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS1","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:BSD-07","text":"The system closes formally around rules of legitimate cognition while remaining materially open to indefinite empirical specification and unification.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-04","CL_BA94_28:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:GA-12","CH_BA94_APP2:A14","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-01","text":"Projected unity directs inquiry, inquiry multiplies determinations, and the expanded manifold renews the demand for higher unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:FGS2","CH_BA94_APP2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-02","text":"Reason necessarily generates ideas, converts them into objects, encounters dialectical failure, and recovers them as regulative procedures.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-03","text":"Failed transcendent construction escalates into recognition of finite materials, proportional design, formal method, and disciplinary control.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:AF1","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS3","CH_BA94_CIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-04","text":"The absence of external tests permits overstepping, overstepping generates connected fallacies, and connected fallacies demand a general negative code.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS6","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-05","text":"Complete systematic unity functions as the principal attractor because every local order is judged from a whole that can guide but never be empirically possessed.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:D4","CH_BA94_APP2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-06","text":"Completion repeatedly changes phase from desired object, to impossible totality, to regulative maximum, to formally complete organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:NX4","CH_BA94_ITDM:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:RLE-07","text":"Discipline forms a feedback loop in which reason detects the systemic consequences of its own procedures and legislates restrictions on their reuse.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B01","text":"Finite cognitive materials indexed to possible experience delimit legitimate scope and block transcendent construction.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:A3","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B02","text":"The necessary demand for unity survives this limitation by becoming a projected rule for organizing the understanding’s cognitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B03","text":"The projected rule precedes the empirical whole and directs the search for lawful connection, differentiation, and continuity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A10","CH_BA94_APP2:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B04","text":"Projection generates objectification pressure whenever procedural unity is mistaken for knowledge of an adequate transcendent object.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD1","CH_BA94_APP2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B05","text":"Because transcendental cognition lacks external constraint, objectification propagates into an interconnected system of fallacies.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A10","CH_BA94_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B06","text":"Critique separates valid direction from invalid constitution and formalizes the separation as negative methodological discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AF1","CH_BA94_CIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FGS-B07","text":"Architectonic organization integrates bounded materials, regulative projection, and disciplinary control into a formally complete but empirically unfinished system.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:D4","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME4","CH_BA94_APP2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AFM-B01","text":"Transcendent aspiration → failed object-constitution → recovery of regulative direction → bounded architectonic design → disciplinary self-government → secure but unfinished inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AF1","CH_BA94_ITDM:AF1","CH_BA94_CIDP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AFM-B02","text":"Necessary idea → projected systematic whole → empirical organization → hypostatization risk → critical restriction → renewed regulative use.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A2","CH_BA94_APP2:FGS6","CH_BA94_APP2:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AFM-B03","text":"Finite materials → legitimate scope → proportional plan → differentiated method → complete formal system.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS1","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:AFM-B04","text":"Absence of external tests → transcendental overstepping → connected illusion → general negative code → jurisdictional security.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS7","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-01","text":"Transcendental ideas are reclassified from failed objects into controlled procedures within a bounded cognitive architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:ME1","CH_BA94_ITDM:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-02","text":"Cognitive limits are reclassified from mere barriers into design parameters for legitimate system-building.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:SD1","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-03","text":"Negativity is reclassified from non-knowledge into an institutional condition of epistemic security.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:A1","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-04","text":"Systematic completion divides into empirical totalization, which remains unavailable, and architectonic completeness, which governs organization.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:NX4","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-05","text":"Error is reclassified from a local false judgment into a reproducible network generated by unregulated rational methods.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:ME5","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-06","text":"Method is relocated from generic logical form to the specific relation among cognitive materials, objects, domains, and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS5","CH_BA94_ITDM:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-07","text":"Reason is reclassified from an exempt authority into a recursively governed power that must distinguish legitimate extension from self-generated illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:NX6","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MME-08","text":"The opposition between positive construction and negative restriction collapses because secure construction depends on systematic restraint.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system distinguishes regulative from constitutive uses, bounded construction from transcendent construction, and discipline from positive instruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:ALT1","CH_BA94_CIDP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, sensibility, understanding, reason, architectonic, and discipline form a distributed machine for acquiring, organizing, directing, and restricting cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:FGS1","CH_BA94_APP2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique changes intervention from refuting isolated claims to regulating the procedures and domains that generate claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SD3","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, nature is presupposed as indefinitely systematizable while its complete unity and ultimate ground remain unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD3","CH_BA94_APP2:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its thesis by converting its own speculative failures into divisions, rules, boundaries, and recursive checks.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:AS4","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FL-01","text":"Indirect objective validity remains unstable because systematic unity is treated both as a rational maxim and as a necessary presupposition concerning nature’s intelligibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A12","CH_BA94_APP2:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FL-02","text":"The demand for a complete system strains against the claim that complete systematic unity is permanently asymptotic.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:A16","CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FL-03","text":"Discipline is grounded in reason while regulating reason, leaving the authority of the regulator recursively dependent on the power regulated.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SD2","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FL-04","text":"Method seeks universally valid formal conditions while also requiring specificity to kinds of cognition and objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:SD3","CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:FL-05","text":"The absence of empirical or intuitive tests motivates transcendental discipline but leaves its concrete tests less determinate than the constraints it replaces.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MP-01","text":"Transcendental ideas mutate from represented objects into rules determining the procedure of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:NX1","CH_BA94_APP2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MP-02","text":"Negation mutates from denial of cognitive content into active governance of cognitive production.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:FGS1","CH_BA94_CIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MP-03","text":"Completeness mutates from possession of an exhaustive totality into completeness of the formal conditions governing finite inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:SNX1","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MP-04","text":"The architectural metaphor mutates into a jurisdictional model in which materials, scope, plan, and discipline define what cognition may construct.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:AS1","CH_BA94_CIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a finite experiential terrain that must be treated as indefinitely systematizable without being mistaken for a completed totality.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:ALT4","CH_BA94_APP2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a divided architect whose faculties supply materials, connections, projections, plans, and restraints rather than as a sovereign spectator.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:ALT2","CH_BA94_ITDM:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as recursive approximation in which each achieved order generates further specification and a renewed demand for unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD4","CH_BA94_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:HSI-04","text":"The remaining form of navigation is disciplined construction between the impossible speculative tower and the secure but permanently unfinished dwelling.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:NX1","CH_BA94_ITDM:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the boundary of cognition, the regulative recovery of systematic reason, architectonic organization, and negative methodological discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:MRT1","CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT1","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B02","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns how regulative unity can possess objective force without becoming a constitutive claim about nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:MRT2","CH_BA94_APP2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent insofar as regulative direction, constitutive determination, positive construction, and negative control remain functionally distinct.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:MRT3","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where speculative failure is converted into proportional design and where systematic illusion generates systematic discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_ITDM:MRT4","CH_BA94_CIDP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on preserving the recursive sequence by which projection produces excess, excess produces critique, and critique restores projection under regulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_APP2:SD1","CH_BA94_APP2:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_28:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is the performative authority by which reason disciplines itself despite lacking the external test whose absence made discipline necessary.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 82–84","context":"Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","original_title":"Chapters 82–84","original_context":"Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 82–84","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_29","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 85–87","order":29,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIDP","CH_BA94_SI_5","CH_BA94_SI_6"],"revision":"CL_BA94_29@R001","sha256":"19e9dca7bfb686b2f00fdabca1477c857af86d1e4b0c2c4e46b9702a55273262","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-01","text":"The system asks how pure reason can preserve systematic authority after mathematical transfer, dogmatic polemic, and unrestricted transcendent hypothesis have all been disqualified.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS2","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-02","text":"Objective speculative authority is confined to the forms and conditions of possible experience; beyond that field, construction, proof, and explanatory supposition lose entitlement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-03","text":"Discipline becomes a positive differentiation of rational functions: mathematics constructs, philosophy deduces, critique adjudicates, empirical inquiry explains, and ideas regulate or defend without determining objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-04","text":"The argument becomes a transcendental-juridical machinery of self-discipline rather than an external prohibition imposed upon reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-05","text":"Its movement proceeds from seductive cognitive success through differentiated limits, jurisdictional adjudication, and finally the internalization of opposition as reason’s permanent corrective mechanism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-06","text":"Every blocked route to transcendence returns in restricted form: demonstration becomes mediated certainty, polemical victory becomes counter-refutation, and hypothesis becomes retractable defence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_5:D7","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-07","text":"Closure consists neither in supersensible knowledge nor permanent scepticism, but in bounded cognition joined to indefinitely open empirical and critical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AS-08","text":"Rational security is achieved when every claim remains criticizable and reason stages its strongest objections within its own lawful domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is simultaneously the domain, validity criterion, and operational boundary of objective speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Theoretical cognition of an object requires an admissible mode of presentation, and the categories determine objects only within possible experiential presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] A priori cognition is internally differentiated into intuitive construction, discursive determination, regulative systematization, and critical adjudication rather than governed by one transferable method.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_5:ME8","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Neither a concept, rational idea, nor logically consistent supposition can establish the objective possibility or reality of its corresponding object.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX3","CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Rational entitlement depends upon function-specific mediation: construction for mathematics, deduction for transcendental principles, and empirical-law connection for explanatory hypotheses.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A4","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Reason is autonomous only by legislating and submitting to universal conditions of criticism, validity, and limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Opposition changes from external methodological contrast and doctrinal combat into an internal engine of reason’s self-examination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D6","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_6:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Supersensible claims remain speculatively undecidable while acquiring sharply delimited practical, regulative, or defensive functions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D1","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] The abolition of dogmatic form does not abolish philosophical system; it reconstructs systematicity through deduction, criticism, and lawful differentiation of uses.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:NX13","CH_BA94_SIDP:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Pure reason terminates in bounded authority: necessary cognition within its domain, declared non-cognition beyond it, and no intermediate theoretical opinion concerning transcendendent objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_6:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-01","text":"Possible experience retains its identity while expanding from the ground of transcendental validity into the tribunal of speculative truth and the condition of admissible explanation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-02","text":"Dogmatism mutates from illicit imitation of mathematical evidence into unauthorized object-level warfare and finally into hyperphysical explanatory closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:NX3","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-03","text":"The supersensible idea changes from an inaccessible speculative object into a practically retained commitment, a regulative principle, or a temporary defensive fiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX7","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-04","text":"Opposition reverses valence from a danger to doctrine into the indispensable mechanism by which reason discovers and corrects its own dialectical excess.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-05","text":"Certainty is displaced from immediate intuitive demonstration toward mediated deduction, while probability is excluded from pure speculative judgment beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ME4","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-06","text":"Scepticism appears as a necessary interruption of dogmatism but disappears as an independent destination once its censuring function is absorbed into critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_5:D8","CH_BA94_SI_5:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RMD-07","text":"Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations recede after establishing the general principle that evidential forms cannot be transferred outside the conditions generating them.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-01","text":"Mathematical construction and the detailed tests of definitions, axioms, and demonstrations remain local machinery, while their scalable result is the prohibition on transferring evidential form between cognitive domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS5","CH_BA94_SIDP:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-02","text":"Cosmological antinomy, causal necessity, and critical education function as local tests of jurisdiction rather than independent foundations of the cluster architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D4","CH_BA94_SI_5:D10","CH_BA94_SI_5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-03","text":"Predictive sufficiency and the weakness introduced by auxiliary assumptions remain local standards for physical hypotheses, but they instantiate the broader demand for warranted mediation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:A8","CH_BA94_SI_6:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-04","text":"Possible experience scales upward because it links methodological legitimacy, speculative jurisdiction, object-possibility, and empirical explanation across all constituent units.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A14","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-05","text":"Mathematical temptation, antithetic conflict, and the apparent freedom of hypothesis are transition catalysts expressing one recurrent drive to occupy a domain for which no presentation is available.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:CGD-06","text":"Object-level victory, permanent scepticism, and transcendent explanation are superseded by jurisdictional critique, determinate non-cognition, and functionally restricted supposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:NX3","CH_BA94_SI_5:NX10","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-01","text":"No cognitive method remains valid merely because it succeeded in another domain governed by different conditions of presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:NX3","CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-02","text":"Neither affirmation nor negation of supersensible existence can become objective speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:NX5","CH_BA94_SI_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-03","text":"Conceptual clarity, logical consistency, and bare thinkability do not establish objective possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX3","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-04","text":"Transcendental hypotheses, hyperphysical causes, and probable opinions cannot substitute for empirical explanation or necessary cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:NX4","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX5","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-05","text":"No doctrine, institution, or defensive interest may secure itself by exemption from criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:NX1","CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-06","text":"Practical usefulness, regulative utility, and polemical force cannot be converted into ontological proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:NX8","CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:GNX-07","text":"Rejection of dogmatism does not authorize either the abandonment of system or the termination of inquiry in scepticism or explanatory closure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:NX13","CH_BA94_SI_5:NX10","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of a universal rational method capable of operating unchanged across constructed intuition, discursive cognition, and supersensible speculation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-02","text":"The architecture excludes intellectual intuition as an available human faculty, because such intuition would dissolve the boundary sustaining all three disciplines.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX1","CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-03","text":"Pure reason cannot manufacture material content, objective possibility, or transcendent objects from concepts and suppositions alone.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX4","CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-04","text":"No external tribunal or protected doctrine can adjudicate reason without either surrendering rational autonomy or evading reciprocal criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-05","text":"Neither repeated failure nor empirical habit can establish reason’s necessary limits, because only systematic a priori criticism can determine incapacity as necessary.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX4","CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX5","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:DBN-06","text":"Durable rational peace cannot arise from object-level victory or an ultimate explanatory ground that terminates inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable presentation of supersensible objects is the central absence that makes demonstration, decisive polemic, and positive transcendental hypothesis equally impossible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX1","CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an unconditioned explanatory ground that reason demands but cannot admit as either cognitive material or a terminus of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SNX4","CH_BA94_SI_6:DNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSA-03","text":"Reason has no standpoint outside itself, yet its closure requires critique to occupy a privileged jurisdiction not reducible to another combatant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX5","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSA-04","text":"The practical ground that would preserve supersensible commitments without restoring theoretical metaphysics remains structurally necessary but deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX6","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT3","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-01","text":"Objective possibility requires an authorized mode of presentation and therefore cannot be inferred from constructibility of a concept or absence of logical contradiction alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-02","CL_BA94_29:GA-04","CH_BA94_SIDP:A12","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-02","text":"Because philosophy lacks intuitive demonstration and transcendent disputes lack a common object, critique can establish authority only through deduction and jurisdictional limitation rather than object-level victory.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIDP:NX11","CH_BA94_SI_5:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-03","text":"A philosophical system remains legitimate only when systematic unity coexists with unrestricted criticizability and deliberate exposure to internally generated opposition.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CL_BA94_29:GA-07","CL_BA94_29:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIDP:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-04","text":"Dogmatism is unified across domains as the assignment of a valid cognitive function to objects or tasks for which its enabling conditions are absent.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-03","CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CH_BA94_SIDP:D13","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-05","text":"Negative polemical entitlement permits a supposition only to cancel an opponent’s unwarranted certainty, and the supposition must disappear when that certainty is withdrawn.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-08","CL_BA94_29:GA-10","CH_BA94_SI_5:A7","CH_BA94_SI_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-06","text":"Supersensible ideas can orient action, organize inquiry, or resist dogmatic negation without acquiring explanatory or constitutive status.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-04","CL_BA94_29:GA-08","CH_BA94_SI_5:A10","CH_BA94_SI_6:A6","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-07","text":"The experiential boundary protects inquiry from premature closure because empirically given matter must be investigated through known connections rather than subordinated to an unconditioned cause.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-01","CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:XD-08","text":"Necessary ignorance is not inferred from failed attempts but established through the same systematic criticism that validates reason’s limited positive domain.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CL_BA94_29:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIDP:DNX5","CH_BA94_SI_5:A13","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-01","text":"Discipline is productive differentiation rather than cognitive impoverishment, because it generates distinct legitimate functions from the collapse of a universal speculative method.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-03","CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CL_BA94_29:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_5:ME3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-02","text":"Rational freedom and rational limitation become mutually constitutive: freedom requires exposure to criticism, while legitimate limitation must arise through reason’s own legislation.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-03","text":"Determinate knowledge of necessary ignorance becomes a positive achievement superior to either dogmatic assertion or indefinite sceptical doubt.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-01","CL_BA94_29:GA-10","CH_BA94_SI_5:D9","CH_BA94_SI_6:A10","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-04","text":"Reason’s enduring adversary is its own tendency to convert regulative demand, methodological success, or explanatory desire into unauthorized object-cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-04","CL_BA94_29:GA-07","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_6:SNX2","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-05","text":"The rational subject ceases to be a sovereign producer of reality and becomes a self-legislating tribunal that differentiates construction, cognition, inquiry, commitment, and defence.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-03","CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:BSD-06","text":"Rational maturity has a recursive temporality in which ambition produces conflict, conflict produces sceptical interruption, and criticism converts interruption into renewed but bounded activity.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-07","CL_BA94_29:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS7","CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT5","CH_BA94_SI_6:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-01","text":"Mathematical success generates analogical expansion, expansion fails for lack of construction, and failure returns philosophy to critical boundary-determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_SIDP:NX3","CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-02","text":"Transcendent assertion generates counterassertion, reciprocal impotence, jurisdictional critique, and free testing rather than a final victorious proposition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:AS4","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_5:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-03","text":"Speculative failure opens hypothetical freedom, experiential criteria restrict that freedom, and the residual defensive supposition is withdrawn before it hardens into doctrine.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS2","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-04","text":"Possible experience is the dominant attractor because every dispute over validity, possibility, causality, or explanation is redirected toward its conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-05","text":"Opposition becomes a machine when suppressed external conflict is reintroduced as reason’s deliberate production and intensification of its own objections.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D6","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX9","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-06","text":"Supersensible ideas persist by status mutation, but any drift from practical, regulative, or defensive use back toward object-assertion restarts dogmatism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_6:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:RLE-07","text":"The system contains a reflexive loop because possible experience validates transcendental principles while those principles are also invoked as conditions making experience possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_5:A5","CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B01","text":"A priori ambition encounters domains whose objects cannot be presented through the form that generated the original cognitive success.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS1","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B02","text":"The system partitions pure formal intuition, empirically given appearances, and supersensible ideas according to their distinct modes or absences of presentation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS4","CH_BA94_SI_5:A8","CH_BA94_SI_6:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B03","text":"The partition assigns construction to mathematics, deduction to philosophy, experiential connection to explanation, and regulative orientation to ideas.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A3","CH_BA94_SIDP:A16","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7","CH_BA94_SI_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B04","text":"Unrestricted adversarial testing exposes every attempt to move a function beyond the conditions authorizing it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B05","text":"Failed transcendent proof is converted into determinate limitation, while residual counterclaims receive only conditional and retractable defensive force.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D9","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11","CH_BA94_SI_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FGS-B06","text":"The machine outputs systematic critical reason: theoretically bounded, empirically open, internally adversarial, and practically oriented without speculative possession of the supersensible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AFM-B01","text":"Mathematical success → analogical expectation → attempted methodological transfer → differentiation of cognitive form.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AFM-B02","text":"Differentiation of cognitive form → loss of philosophical construction → elimination of direct dogmata → relocation of authority to possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:FGS6","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AFM-B03","text":"Experiential criterion → symmetry of transcendent opposition → reduction of victory to counter-refutation → jurisdictional critique.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D1","CH_BA94_SI_5:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AFM-B04","text":"Dogmatic conflict → sceptical censure → rejection of merely empirical limitation → systematic determination of necessary bounds.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:FGS5","CH_BA94_SI_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:AFM-B05","text":"Prohibited transcendental explanation → retractable defensive hypothesis → internalized opposition → bounded but permanently active critical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS4","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS6","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-01","text":"The a priori ceases to name a homogeneous method and becomes a differentiated field of construction, experiential conditions, deduction, and regulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ME3","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-02","text":"Definitions, axioms, demonstrations, proofs, and hypotheses are reclassified as domain-dependent operations rather than universal marks of rationality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ME5","CH_BA94_SI_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-03","text":"Metaphysical disputes become questions about cognitive jurisdiction and claim-status rather than contests for possession of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:ME1","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-04","text":"Opposition is reclassified from a threat requiring censorship into a constitutive resource for rational education and self-correction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:ME7","CH_BA94_SI_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-05","text":"Hypothesis divides into empirical explanation, regulative systematization, and negative polemical defence, preventing utility in one function from licensing another.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:SD1","CH_BA94_SI_6:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MME-06","text":"Cognitive limitation becomes enabling rather than merely privative because it preserves systematic philosophy, free criticism, and indefinitely continuing empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ME7","CH_BA94_SI_5:ME4","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, mathematical proof-forms, disputes over supersensible doctrines, and hypotheses are test sites for determining rational entitlement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT1","CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, modes of presentation and relation to possible experience allocate every cognitive operation to a legitimate or illegitimate domain.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A13","CH_BA94_SI_5:A4","CH_BA94_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry is redirected from winning object-level contests toward classifying claims as knowledge, faith, regulation, explanation, defence, or illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ALT3","CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT3","CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality divides between appearances available under sensibility and an intelligible order that remains neither cognizable nor disprovable, while maturity proceeds recursively through failure and criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SI_5:A8","CH_BA94_SI_6:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the sequence enacts its thesis by staging temptation, opposition, sceptical interruption, and disciplined re-entry rather than merely asserting limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:AS6","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS7","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FL-01","text":"Possible experience functions both as the ground validating transcendental principles and as an order constituted by those principles, producing a reflexive dependence on prior deduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT7","CH_BA94_SI_5:A5","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FL-02","text":"The universal requirement that every claim remain criticizable conflicts with critique’s need to occupy a final jurisdiction rather than appear as one more revisable combatant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A1","CH_BA94_SI_5:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FL-03","text":"Speculative symmetry between transcendent affirmation and negation coexists with practical preference for affirmative commitments whose warrant is deferred.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SD6","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FL-04","text":"The permission to use transcendental fictions defensively strains the general prohibition on hypotheses whose objects lack experiential possibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_6:NX4","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:FL-05","text":"Philosophy’s claim to apodeictic certainty remains dependent on an incompletely defended distinction between mediated deduction and intuitive demonstration.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:NX11","CH_BA94_SIDP:SD5","CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MP-01","text":"Dogmatism mutates from a methodological error of evidential transfer into a constitutional illegitimacy concerning who may claim authority over supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:ME8","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MP-02","text":"The opponent mutates from an external doctrinal adversary into an internal function through which reason discovers its own dialectical structure.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SD4","CH_BA94_SI_6:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MP-03","text":"Supersensible propositions mutate from attempted knowledge into practical commitment, regulative orientation, or defensive fiction, but the criteria separating these statuses remain incomplete.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:SD3","CH_BA94_SI_6:SD5","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MP-04","text":"Scepticism mutates from censure of failed cognition into a transitional mechanism whose own generalization must be overcome by critical knowledge of limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:D8","CH_BA94_SI_5:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a layered field of constructible formal relations and empirically given matter, bordered by an intelligible domain unavailable to human intuition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:A8","CH_BA94_SIDP:A9","CH_BA94_SI_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:HSI-02","text":"Reason appears as a self-legislating court joined to an experimental chamber in which it summons, strengthens, and adjudicates its own adversaries.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:A2","CH_BA94_SI_5:A3","CH_BA94_SI_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:HSI-03","text":"Time assumes the form of recursive maturation: assertion, conflict, interruption, boundary-determination, and renewed testing repeatedly replace linear accumulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:ALT5","CH_BA94_SI_6:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible through deduction, empirical investigation, unrestricted criticism, and retractable defence, but not through theoretical escape into supersensible possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:D12","CH_BA94_SI_5:AS5","CH_BA94_SI_6:A7","CH_BA94_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the common architecture of methodological differentiation, experiential jurisdiction, restricted hypothesis, and internal rational self-discipline.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT1","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT1","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B02","text":"The proofs establishing space and time, the categories, possible experience, and practical warrant remain underdetermined because the cluster inherits them from arguments developed elsewhere.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT2","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT2","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent if possible experience remains the exclusive criterion of objective speculative validity and if distinct rational functions are prevented from substituting for one another.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT3","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT4","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where repeated prohibitions generate positive distinctions among construction, deduction, criticism, empirical explanation, regulation, and defence.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT4","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT5","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B05","text":"The juridical image, critique’s claim to finality, and the defensive use of prohibited fictions remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT6","CH_BA94_SI_5:SD7","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_29:MRT-B06","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when temptation, failure, sceptical interruption, and internalized opposition remain recursive phases rather than a linear sequence of discarded errors.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIDP:MRT9","CH_BA94_SI_5:MRT8","CH_BA94_SI_6:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 85–87","context":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","original_title":"Chapters 85–87","original_context":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis"},"original_title":"Chapters 85–87","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_30","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 88–90","order":30,"parents":["CH_BA94_SI_7","CH_BA94_CICP","CH_BA94_SIUE"],"revision":"CL_BA94_30@R001","sha256":"d1830e9aac8250fd14bf522fe9202eb2fefe2688e11275c6b949ed09f4f0a079","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B01","text":"The system converts the proof-problem from derivation within concepts into jurisdiction over the conditions under which concepts can refer objectively.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B02","text":"Pure reason is incapable of securing metaphysical truth through unrestricted speculation and must therefore discipline its own operations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B03","text":"Possible experience supplies the mediating field in which a transcendental proof can establish the necessity of a conceptual connection.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B04","text":"Limitation of speculative reason is not merely subtractive because it protects legitimate cognition by excluding rival usurpations and dialectical extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B05","text":"The supersensible orientation of reason survives speculative restriction by having its significance transferred from theoretical possession to practical interest.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B06","text":"The positive canon of pure reason can arise only where a proper rational use is possible, and this use is supplied by moral law rather than speculative synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive: rational aspiration exceeds experience, generates illusion when treated constitutively, undergoes self-discipline, and reappears as practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AS-B08","text":"The cluster closes by preserving practical freedom while suspending transcendental freedom as a speculative problem, thereby retaining agency without theoretically completing its metaphysical ground.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Theoretical objectivity belongs only to principles whose validity is restricted to objects of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A transcendental synthetic claim is entitled to proof only after the objective validity of its concepts and the possibility of their synthesis have been established.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Subjective necessities of association, completion, or thought cannot be converted into determinations of objects without producing transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Critique transforms proof from an inferential accomplishment into a self-disciplinary tribunal over the source, right, and scope of rational principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A2","CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Restriction of speculative claims functions positively by securing the residual domain of legitimate cognition against dialectical overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Reason remains oriented toward what exceeds experience even though speculative reason cannot satisfy that orientation through cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A7","CH_BA94_CICP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] A canon governs only a faculty with a possible proper employment; the impossibility of speculative synthesis therefore disqualifies a speculative canon.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A9","CH_BA94_CICP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Moral law alone provides the canon of pure practical reason because it is determined a priori and commands unconditionally.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Freedom, immortality, and God cannot explain objects of experience but retain their highest rational significance as practical concerns.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A4","CH_BA94_SIUE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Practical freedom is admissible as rational determination of the will, while transcendental freedom remains theoretically unresolved.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A13","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] The ultimate question of pure reason is transformed from what can be known into what ought to be done.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-01","text":"Possible experience retains stable identity as the condition of objective reference, but its function expands from validating individual proofs to delimiting speculative reason as such.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-02","text":"Proof mutates from a relation among propositions into a determination of the conditions under which any proposition can claim objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-03","text":"Discipline migrates from a local prohibition on invalid proof procedures into reason’s general practice of internal self-government.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-04","text":"Limitation reverses valence from epistemic deprivation into a protective instrument that preserves legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-05","text":"Transcendent ideas reverse function from failed candidates for theoretical explanation into indispensable practical objects of reason’s highest interest.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RMD-06","text":"Freedom undergoes terminal bifurcation into an empirically admissible practical capacity and a metaphysically suspended transcendental condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:CGD-01","text":"The uniqueness of a transcendental proof-ground and the rejection of indirect proof remain local procedural machinery unless absorbed into the wider distinction between subjective necessity and objective entitlement.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A9","CH_BA94_SI_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:CGD-02","text":"The demand for transcendental deduction scales upward because it becomes the general criterion separating legitimate cognition from dialectical speculative use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between discipline and canon bridges the proof-problem and the practical problem by showing why speculative reason can be regulated without being positively prescribed.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:CGD-04","text":"The cardinal transcendent ideas catalyze the decisive transition because their speculative non-immanence compels their practical revaluation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:CGD-05","text":"The possibility of a speculative canon is superseded, while the concept of canon is recoded as the practical legislation of moral law.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:NX4","CH_BA94_SIUE:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GNX-01","text":"Transcendental proof is not an immediate passage from one concept to another because conceptual linkage alone cannot establish objective reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GNX-02","text":"Ideas of pure reason are not objectively valid principles for speculative cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GNX-03","text":"Pure speculative reason is not capable of a valid synthetic canon because it lacks a proper cognitive employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GNX-04","text":"Speculative limitation is not an externally imposed suppression but reason’s own critical discipline over its self-generated deviations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:GNX-05","text":"Transcendent explanation is not permitted to displace natural causal explanation, and prudential regulation is not equivalent to pure moral legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:NX4","CH_BA94_SIUE:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:DBN-01","text":"Logical coherence is insufficient for objective reference, so formally consistent metaphysical thought cannot authorize itself as cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:DBN-02","text":"The subjective necessity with which reason seeks completion does not demonstrate that an unconditioned object is given.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:DBN-03","text":"The possession of rational concepts does not entail a legitimate speculative rule for employing them beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:DBN-04","text":"Contradictory predicates do not necessarily distribute exhaustive alternatives over a real object, especially where the supposed object is itself illicitly constituted.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:DBN-05","text":"Systematic unity cannot be achieved by unrestricted metaphysical explanation of empirical phenomena.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSA-01","text":"No speculative proof can occupy a standpoint outside the subjective conditions whose unnoticed operation generates transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSA-02","text":"No theoretical standpoint is available from which rational causality can be reconciled with the total causal order of nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSA-03","text":"A positive speculative science of the supersensible remains unavailable even though reason’s demand for a non-speculative completion persists.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSA-04","text":"No final speculative system can fulfill reason’s demand for totality without transgressing the limits of possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-01","text":"Critique makes epistemic authority depend on jurisdiction over the conditions of claim-making rather than on the accumulation of inferential victories.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-02","CL_BA94_30:GA-04","CH_BA94_SI_7:AS3","CH_BA94_CICP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-02","text":"The failure of speculative possession does not abolish rational demand; it preserves that demand by converting it from an objective claim into a practical orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-03","CL_BA94_30:GA-06","CL_BA94_30:GA-09","CH_BA94_SI_7:DNX2","CH_BA94_CICP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-03","text":"The absence of a speculative canon becomes the condition for a distinct practical normativity grounded in moral law.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-07","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CH_BA94_CICP:D5","CH_BA94_SIUE:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-04","text":"Natural explanation and moral legislation are preserved as non-competing rational functions because transcendent causality is barred from empirical explanation while practical law legislates what ought to occur.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-01","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CL_BA94_30:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIUE:A5","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-05","text":"Practical agency can be treated as rationally law-governed without a theoretical resolution of the metaphysical origin of rational causality.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIUE:A13","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:XD-06","text":"Critical refutation becomes upstream and structural because it disqualifies the conditions producing unsupported claims instead of defeating each dialectical conclusion separately.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-03","CL_BA94_30:GA-04","CH_BA94_SI_7:A11","CH_BA94_CICP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSD-01","text":"Reason is reconstructed as a constitutional order in which legitimate powers are determined by jurisdiction, scope, and mode of employment rather than by metaphysical ambition.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-01","CL_BA94_30:GA-04","CL_BA94_30:GA-05","CH_BA94_SI_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSD-02","text":"Rational normativity survives the collapse of speculative metaphysics because normativity is relocated from knowledge of supersensible objects to practical law.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-07","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CH_BA94_CICP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes structurally double: phenomenal action remains subject to natural explanation while practical agency remains attributable to rational law.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-01","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CL_BA94_30:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIUE:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSD-04","text":"The ultimate unity sought by reason becomes a moral end rather than a theoretically possessed totality of objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-06","CL_BA94_30:GA-09","CL_BA94_30:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIUE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:BSD-05","text":"Negation becomes productive: the systematic refusal of speculative extension generates the positive space of practical autonomy without resolving supersensible ontology.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-05","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CL_BA94_30:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIUE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RLE-01","text":"Rational aspiration exceeds experience, produces speculative illusion when objectified, undergoes critique, and returns as practical orientation toward what ought to be done.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RLE-02","text":"The proof-loop proceeds from conceptual claim to demand for objective validity, from validity to possible experience, and from failed deduction to suspension of speculative assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A1","CH_BA94_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RLE-03","text":"The boundary-loop converts limitation into protection: restriction removes illegitimate pretension and thereby secures the legitimate domain that restriction appears to diminish.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RLE-04","text":"The canon-loop converts the absence of speculative proper use into the discovery of practical proper use, making failure itself the route to positive legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:AS6","CH_BA94_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience and moral law operate as paired attractors: the first regulates theoretical objectivity, while the second regulates practical obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A5","CH_BA94_SIUE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B01","text":"A claim to transcendental cognition generates a prior demand for proof of objective validity and possible synthesis.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B02","text":"The demand for objective validity directs theoretical cognition to possible experience as the sole field in which concepts can obtain objective reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B03","text":"Failure to derive a principle from the conditions of possible experience classifies its speculative use as dialectical rather than constitutive.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B04","text":"Dialectical failure requires internal self-discipline, which restricts speculative overreach while protecting legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:A2","CH_BA94_CICP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B05","text":"The exclusion of a speculative canon redirects the question of pure reason’s proper employment toward practical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FGS-B06","text":"Pure practical law supplies a canon of unconditional obligation, while transcendental freedom remains suspended as an unresolved speculative condition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:A11","CH_BA94_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B01","text":"Conceptual claim → demand for objective validity → possible experience as the condition of theoretical reference.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B02","text":"Possible experience → restriction of constitutive validity → classification of unsupported supersensible claims as dialectical.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:A5","CH_BA94_SI_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B03","text":"Dialectical exposure → internal discipline → protection of legitimate cognition through bounded speculative use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D1","CH_BA94_CICP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B04","text":"Persistent rational aspiration → failure of speculative satisfaction → redirection toward practical employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B05","text":"Practical employment → distinction between prudence and pure moral legislation → moral law as the canon of pure reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:D2","CH_BA94_SIUE:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:AFM-B06","text":"Practical freedom → suspension of transcendental freedom → ultimate rational interest reorganized around what ought to be done.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:D5","CH_BA94_SIUE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MME-01","text":"Proof is reclassified from a relation among propositions into a demonstration of the conditions making objective reference possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MME-02","text":"The boundary of reason becomes a positive epistemic instrument rather than a mere deprivation of metaphysical content.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MME-03","text":"Discipline and canon are separated: critique regulates speculative misuse, whereas a canon positively governs only a proper faculty-use.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MME-04","text":"Freedom, immortality, and God are reclassified from theoretical explanations into practical objects of reason’s highest interest.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MME-05","text":"The distinction between what happens and what ought to happen redraws the relation between natural law, practical law, and rational agency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system regulates the permissible forms of transcendental proof, speculative inference, canon formation, and practical reasoning.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Objective cognition depends on possible experience, while dialectic arises from the misattribution of subjective rational conditions to objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Philosophical authority is relocated from producing speculative truths to regulating claims, restricting illegitimate extension, and protecting legitimate domains.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Objects are theoretically determinable only within experiential conditions of temporal ordering, whereas practical agency is attributed under rational law without theoretical reconciliation to nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIUE:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The argument performs self-limitation by making every claim about reason answerable to a prior examination of its entitlement.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FL-01","text":"Reason’s unavoidable aspiration beyond experience conflicts with the prohibition against treating its supersensible objects as theoretically knowable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FL-02","text":"The transition from speculative failure to practical fulfillment is structurally necessary for the system but asserted more strongly than demonstrated in the supplied architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:FL-03","text":"Practical freedom is treated as empirically established while its relation to transcendental freedom remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MP-01","text":"Proof changes meaning at the point where inferential support is subordinated to the deduction of objective validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MP-02","text":"Limitation changes meaning when it ceases to be a negative prohibition and becomes protection of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MP-03","text":"Transcendent ideas change function when their speculative non-immanence becomes the basis for their practical significance.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a jurisdictionally stratified order in which theoretical objects, practical ends, and supersensible ideas possess different modes of admissibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a divided being whose actions are explainable as phenomena while remaining practically attributable to rational self-determination.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as the condition under which objects and events can be theoretically ordered, but not as a standpoint from which absolute totality can be possessed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:HSI-04","text":"Rational struggle appears as navigation between dialectical capture and practical legislation: speculative possession is barred, while practical commitment remains possible.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if objective validity, speculative discipline, practical canon, and suspended transcendental freedom remain jointly operative.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it derives theoretical legitimacy from the conditions of possible experience rather than from conceptual association, indirect refutation, or metaphysical assertion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SI_7:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MRT-B03","text":"The relation between reason’s positive supersensible orientation and its practical status remains underdetermined at this scale.","refs":["CH_BA94_CICP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_30:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent only if its distinction between theoretical restriction and practical legislation is preserved without collapsing practical freedom into transcendental knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIUE:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 88–90","context":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","original_title":"Chapters 88–90","original_context":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 88–90","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_31","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 91–92","order":31,"parents":["CH_BA94_SIIS","CH_BA94_SIOK"],"revision":"CL_BA94_31@R001","sha256":"0e341fc413a4ebad5e506e34767c3c819d9fc31b7dcb2fa1981093e3c8ca73f3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-01","text":"The system must explain how moral obligation can authorize orientation toward supersensible ends when theoretical certainty concerning those ends is unavailable.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS1","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-02","text":"Moral law generates the practical postulates of God and future life, while the taxonomy of assent assigns those postulates the status of belief rather than knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-03","text":"The boundary of speculative cognition becomes the enabling condition for a practically legitimate orientation beyond experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-04","text":"Autonomous obligation first constructs a required unity of moral worthiness and happiness, then authorizes belief in the conditions deemed necessary for that unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A4","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-05","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a practical-transcendental deduction coupled to a two-dimensional epistemology of subjective commitment and objective warrant.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS2","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-06","text":"Its movement is recursive: speculative cognition is removed, practical need generates postulates, and the speculative boundary returns to prevent those postulates from becoming theological legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-07","text":"Universality divides into communicable objective validity for cognition and universally accessible moral orientation for action.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A5","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AS-08","text":"Closure consists not in reconciling belief with knowledge but in stabilizing a morally necessary yet non-cognitive commitment under autonomous law.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Speculative reason cannot cognize the supersensible conditions required by reason’s final moral ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Moral law obligates rational agents independently of happiness, reward, theology, and speculative proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The highest good requires happiness to be distributed in exact proportion to moral worthiness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS4","CH_BA94_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Natural causality supplies no cognizable guarantee that virtue and proportionate happiness will coincide.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] God and future life arise first as practical hypotheses of the highest good and become morally necessary objects of belief.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Moral belief combines subjective sufficiency with acknowledged objective insufficiency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Practical necessity may orient reason beyond experience but may never be converted into theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Theology remains legitimate only as a derivative of autonomous moral law and cannot become the source of obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","CH_BA94_SIIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Rational universality changes form across domains: cognition requires objectively communicable grounds, whereas moral orientation requires universally accessible commitment under law.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The limitation of theoretical reason is productive because it establishes the boundary within which practical belief becomes legitimate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:AS6","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The systematic unity of moral ends organizes freedom, happiness, nature, teleology, hope, and assent without becoming an object of speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-01","text":"The speculative inaccessibility of supersensible objects retains stable identity while changing from an initial prohibition into a boundary governing legitimate assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:NX1","CH_BA94_SIOK:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-02","text":"God and future life mutate from hypotheses needed to complete the highest good into practically necessary conditions of moral belief.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ME2","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-03","text":"The proportional union of happiness and worthiness is absorbed into the more general requirement for a practical unity of all ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A5","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-04","text":"Teleology migrates from the moral interpretation of nature into a regulative form of doctrinal belief guiding natural inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A12","CH_BA94_SIOK:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-05","text":"Moral necessity changes function from determining free action to stabilizing belief against the possible dissolution of moral maxims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-06","text":"Universality is displaced from communicable cognitive grounds to the universal accessibility of moral orientation without erasing the difference between conviction and belief.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:AS2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-07","text":"Happiness disappears as an independent epistemic object after supplying the content through which moral end-unity is constructed.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-08","text":"Explicit theology recedes into an epistemology of rational guidance, but its subordination to antecedent moral law persists.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS7","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RMD-09","text":"Theoretical insufficiency reverses valence: what first blocks cognition subsequently enables a distinct mode of practical legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-01","text":"The exact proportional distribution of happiness remains the local mechanism that gives determinate content to the highest good.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS4","CH_BA94_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-02","text":"Stake-sensitive pragmatical belief remains local to the classification of contingent means and does not ground the current-level moral architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A12","CH_BA94_SIOK:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between conviction and persuasion scales upward by preventing moral belief from presenting subjective commitment as objective proof.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A2","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-04","text":"The postulates of God and future life scale upward because the epistemology of belief supplies their required non-cognitive status.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-05","text":"Teleology bridges the units by connecting the moral demand that nature accommodate freedom with the regulative guidance of inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:D6","CH_BA94_SIOK:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-06","text":"Natural insufficiency catalyzes the postulates, while the distinction between subjective and objective sufficiency prevents the postulates from becoming knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-07","text":"Opinion becomes structurally obsolete at the current level because necessity-governed morality excludes merely problematical assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:NX5","CH_BA94_SIOK:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:CGD-08","text":"Doctrinal belief is recoded as a weaker regulative mode, subordinate to the moral belief generated by necessary end-unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A13","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-01","text":"Supersensible conditions cannot be objects of speculative cognition or demonstrative theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:NX1","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-02","text":"Neither nature nor empirical history can guarantee the final coincidence of virtue and happiness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:NX6","CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-03","text":"Reward, punishment, desire, and expected happiness cannot generate moral worthiness or obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:NX4","CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-04","text":"Sincerity, psychological force, and private assurance cannot constitute objective truth.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:NX2","CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-05","text":"Practical need cannot be converted into theoretical evidence for the objects that answer that need.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX3","CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-06","text":"Essential practical orientation cannot depend on specialist philosophical privilege.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX6","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:GNX-07","text":"Divine command cannot replace autonomous legislation or serve as the original ground of binding obligation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:NX8","CH_BA94_SIIS:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-01","text":"The architecture excludes the model according to which only objectively demonstrated propositions can rationally guide action.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX5","CH_BA94_SIOK:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-02","text":"It excludes psychological certainty as a substitute for relation to an object or a necessary rational law.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-03","text":"It excludes any natural harmonism in which empirical causality inherently aligns moral worth with happiness.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX1","CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-04","text":"It excludes theological voluntarism because externally commanded action would destroy the priority of autonomous moral legislation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX2","CH_BA94_SIIS:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-05","text":"It excludes sceptical suspension because moral agency cannot wait for transcendent certainty before acting.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:DBN-06","text":"It excludes the inference that teleological usefulness or moral desirability supplies theoretical determination of supersensible reality.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:DNX3","CH_BA94_SIOK:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an empirically available moral world and the impossibility of theoretically occupying a standpoint from which it could be known as complete.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX1","CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSA-02","text":"No final standpoint reconciles unconditional duty, conditioned happiness, subjective moral certainty, and objective cognitive insufficiency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSA-03","text":"The divine ground remains unavailable as an external source of authority because admitting such a source would annul moral autonomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX3","CH_BA94_SIIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSA-04","text":"The unavailable completion of moral order structures time as orientation toward a future that cannot become present knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX4","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSA-05","text":"Finite reason must act toward a completion it cannot possess, while refusal to act until possession would destroy practical reason’s function.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2","CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-01","text":"The postulates are subjectively sufficient because moral law requires them for its final end, yet objectively insufficient because practical necessity cannot determine their objects theoretically.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-06","CL_BA94_31:GA-07","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-02","text":"The highest-good deduction supplies moral belief with determinate objects, while the taxonomy of assent supplies those objects with a non-cognitive validity status.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-03","CL_BA94_31:GA-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-06","CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-03","text":"Speculative limitation operates twice: it redirects reason toward practical postulation and then blocks the postulates from returning as speculative knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-01","CL_BA94_31:GA-07","CL_BA94_31:GA-10","CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-04","text":"Moral autonomy is secured at both ends of the construction because law generates the postulates and subsequently governs every permissible interpretation of them.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-02","CL_BA94_31:GA-08","CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","CH_BA94_SIIS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-05","text":"Moral belief cannot become conviction through communicability, yet its grounding in universally binding law and common reason prevents it from collapsing into merely private persuasion.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-06","CL_BA94_31:GA-09","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-06","text":"The future acquires a double function as the temporal horizon of unrealized moral completion and the epistemic marker of an order unavailable to experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-07","CL_BA94_31:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-07","text":"Belief in a wise author of nature is doctrinal when it regulates inquiry but moral when it is required by the final unity of ends.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-11","CH_BA94_SIOK:D6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:XD-08","text":"The claim that God and future life are the necessary conditions of moral unity remains unstable because practical need does not prove that no alternative conditions are possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-07","CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT2","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-01","text":"Rational validity becomes topological rather than scalar: objective warrant and subjective commitment can diverge while remaining systematically coordinated.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD1","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-02","text":"Critique becomes a productive technology of modality that replaces the choice between dogmatic knowledge and sceptical abandonment with differentiated forms of assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD2","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes an autonomous legislator who must also believe under conditions that forbid speculative possession of belief’s objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes ethically asymmetrical: the sensible present exhibits disunity, while the future bears the practically required but unexperienced completion of ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-05","text":"Theology becomes a non-sovereign interface for coordinating freedom, nature, happiness, and causality rather than an independent legislative science.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SD3","CH_BA94_SIIS:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-06","text":"The architecture simultaneously blocks coercive imposition of belief and philosophical monopolization of essential practical orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:D3","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:BSD-07","text":"Practical rationality is reconstructed as disciplined action under theoretical undecidability rather than action suspended until certainty arrives.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:D4","CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-01","text":"Speculative limitation generates practical postulation, and practical postulation recursively reactivates speculative limitation to contain its own claims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-02","text":"The autonomy loop runs from moral law to theological representation and back to moral law as the criterion governing that representation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A11","CH_BA94_SIIS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-03","text":"The principal escalation path runs from unconditional norm through worthiness and the highest good to natural insufficiency and a postulated supersensible cause.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS2","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-04","text":"The epistemic escalation path runs from analysis of subjective and objective grounds through degrees of assent to moral belief under necessary action.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-05","text":"Systematic unity of ends functions as the dominant attractor drawing morality, happiness, nature, theology, temporality, and belief into one architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-06","text":"Repetition of God and future life marks a phase change from hypothesis, to condition of moral orientation, to belief explicitly denied the status of knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ME2","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME4","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:RLE-07","text":"Critical philosophy returns practical orientation to common reason after disciplining it through distinctions unavailable to uncritical assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B01","text":"Speculative subtraction removes cognition of supersensible ends while preserving the demand for rational orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B02","text":"Autonomous legislation supplies an unconditional practical norm and a necessary unity of moral ends.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS2","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B03","text":"Moral worthiness combines with happiness to construct the highest good as the final content of practical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS3","CH_BA94_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B04","text":"Natural non-guarantee opens a structural gap between moral action and the final realization of its end.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS4","CH_BA94_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B05","text":"The gap generates practical postulation of a supreme moral cause and future life.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B06","text":"The assent matrix separates subjective sufficiency from objective sufficiency and thereby differentiates opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:AS3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B07","text":"Moral necessity assigns the postulates to belief because they guide necessary action without becoming cognized objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A15","CH_BA94_SIIS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B08","text":"Recursive containment restores the speculative boundary and subordinates theology to the moral law that generated it.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS7","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FGS-B09","text":"The resulting orientation returns to common reason as action-guiding commitment without philosophical privilege or demonstrative possession.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A17","CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AFM-B01","text":"Speculative inaccessibility → autonomous moral law → highest good → natural non-guarantee → practical postulates → subjective/objective sufficiency assay → moral belief → restricted theology.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS1","CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS5","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS3","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AFM-B02","text":"Practical postulation → risk of speculative inflation → classification as belief → renewed theoretical boundary → preservation of moral autonomy.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:FGS7","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:AFM-B03","text":"Duty → hope → teleological orientation → disciplined assent → universally accessible practical reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SD1","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-01","text":"The opposition between transcendent knowledge and sceptical abandonment collapses into a differentiated field of legitimate and illegitimate assent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD2","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-02","text":"Belief becomes a positive rational category rather than arbitrary opinion or defective knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:A7","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-03","text":"The boundary of theoretical reason is redrawn as a condition of practical legitimacy rather than a merely negative cognitive deficit.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-04","text":"God and future life are reclassified from speculative objects into practically necessary hypotheses and conditions of moral orientation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ME2","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-05","text":"Universality divides into object-grounded communicability and law-grounded practical accessibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-06","text":"The future is reclassified from an empty later time into the horizon of an unexperienced moral order.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-07","text":"Freedom and nature become a coordination problem rather than a theoretically solved causal relation.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ME6","CH_BA94_SIIS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MME-08","text":"Philosophical expertise loses exclusive authority over practical commitments whose validity concerns every rational agent.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:ME6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system classifies action, hope, happiness, God, future life, persuasion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT1","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, a matrix of moral end-unity intersects a matrix of subjective and objective sufficiency.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT2","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, failed speculative ambition is redirected into disciplined practical and regulative employment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT3","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, sensible disunity is governed by orientation toward a future intelligible order that cannot be experienced as present.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT4","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system generates supersensible postulates and then performs critique by restricting their use.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:AS7","CH_BA94_SIOK:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:ALT-B06","text":"At the political-epistemic level, practical orientation is universalized while persuasion is denied coercive authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT5","CH_BA94_SIOK:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FL-01","text":"Practical necessity must stabilize belief without becoming evidence, leaving commitment normatively compulsory but objectively insufficient.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A10","CH_BA94_SIOK:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FL-02","text":"The inference to God and future life depends on an unproved exclusivity claim concerning the conditions of moral unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT2","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FL-03","text":"Moral law is independently binding, yet the complete efficacy of its maxims is represented as dependent on postulated supersensible conditions.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT4","CH_BA94_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:FL-04","text":"Cognitive universality depends on communicable grounds, whereas moral certainty depends on subjective moral disposition under a universally binding law.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:AS2","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX8","CH_BA94_SIOK:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MP-01","text":"God mutates from the supreme cause required by the highest good into an object of morally necessary belief without acquiring theoretical objecthood.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A8","CH_BA94_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MP-02","text":"Theoretical insufficiency mutates from an obstacle to cognition into the condition that makes practical belief legitimate.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A1","CH_BA94_SIOK:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MP-03","text":"The moral world mutates from a practical idea for transforming sensible life into a future order unavailable as present knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A7","CH_BA94_SIIS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MP-04","text":"Teleology mutates from the moral totalization of nature into a regulative condition for investigating nature.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:A12","CH_BA94_SIOK:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a coordination machine in which moral law demands a unity that natural causality cannot independently produce.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ME6","CH_BA94_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a finite autonomous legislator who must commit practically without possessing the supersensible conditions of commitment as known objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:ALT5","CH_BA94_SIOK:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes a vector from present moral-natural disunity toward an unexperienced future completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:SD5","CH_BA94_SIOK:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is to sustain action between dogmatic possession and sceptical suspension by repeatedly policing the boundary between belief and knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:SD4","CH_BA94_SIOK:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative limitation through the highest good and practical postulation to the differentiated status of moral belief.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT1","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT1","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the transition from required moral unity to the claim that only God and future life can condition its realization.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT2","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if practical necessity and theoretical cognition remain irreducibly distinct modalities of rational validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT3","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the content of the highest good is coupled to the distinction between cognitive warrant and rationally necessary commitment.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT6","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if the final return to speculative limitation and common practical reason is treated as constitutive rather than supplementary.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIIS:MRT7","CH_BA94_SIOK:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_31:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is the moral disposition through which loss of belief is represented as threatening the subject’s operative maxims.","refs":["CH_BA94_SIOK:NX8","CH_BA94_SIOK:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 91–92","context":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","original_title":"Chapters 91–92","original_context":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief"},"original_title":"Chapters 91–92","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_BA94_32","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 93–94","order":32,"parents":["CH_BA94_CI_2","CH_BA94_CIHP"],"revision":"CL_BA94_32@R001","sha256":"aeaffb5b10b0e1795e826e94aec94ec69f1b245698740be7e2348eb597683a86","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-01","text":"The cluster confronts cognition divided between aggregate organization and recurrent metaphysical conflict, neither of which can secure durable rational unity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-02","text":"Rational unity requires both a reason-given architectonic idea and a transcendental examination of reason’s competence and limits.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS2","CH_BA94_CIHP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes reconstructive, taxonomic, teleological, and judicial: it builds a system by classifying and testing the legitimacy of rational positions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS3","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-04","text":"The system moves recursively because its governing idea precedes cognition as a rational ground but becomes explicit through historical failure and prospective critical reconstruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-05","text":"Scientific and metaphysical legitimacy arise from the coordinated allocation of objects, sources, methods, limits, and positions within a whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-06","text":"Rational autonomy begins as derivation from principles by the subject and expands into a collectively sustainable critical method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-07","text":"Criticism supplies the operative center by converting systematic reason into negative governance over its own claims and the remaining route toward stable satisfaction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AS-08","text":"Closure remains conditional: moral and systematic unity are posited as necessary, but their completion depends on a future collective transformation of rational method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognition becomes scientific only when systematic procedure subordinates its parts to a rationally unified whole.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A2","CH_BA94_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A rational system legislates the ends, limits, membership, and positions of its parts rather than merely arranging them after accumulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Philosophical history is rationally significant only when reconstructed as a transcendental field of epistemic positions rather than retained as chronology.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The idea governing a rational science may remain germinal until historical conflict retrospectively discloses the structure that can correct inherited definitions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIHP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Rational legitimacy depends on the alignment of cognitive object, source, method, and the subject’s autonomous derivation from principles.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Systematicity is necessary but insufficient because dogmatism and scepticism can themselves be systematic; criticism must judge their admissibility.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Criticism is simultaneously an internal organ of metaphysics and the higher-order path by which reason audits every metaphysical procedure.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Philosophy is an unattained legislative practice that judges actual cognition by its relation to the ultimate ends of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A12","CH_BA94_CI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Metaphysics becomes a jurisdiction defined by a priori origin, disciplinary exclusion, and critical regulation rather than unrestricted speculative extension.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A17","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Reason has a dual temporality in which its systematic idea precedes historical articulation while its critical settlement remains future and collective.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] The destination of humanity supplies the ultimate architectonic end under which the historically motivating concerns of theology and morality must be reordered.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Durable rational satisfaction is sought through the prevention of speculative error rather than through unlimited positive acquisition of transcendent knowledge.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D12","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-01","text":"Systematicity retains stable identity as a condition of science but expands from the unity of cognitions into a requirement governing metaphysical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-02","text":"Criticism mutates from a limiting organ within metaphysics into a tribunal that judges the admissibility of competing systematic procedures.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-03","text":"History reverses valence from chronological succession to a diagnostic taxonomy of recurrent rational failure.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-04","text":"Metaphysics shifts from a positive taxonomy of a priori domains toward the reflexive examination and restraint of reason’s own speculative power.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A18","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-05","text":"Autonomous derivation migrates from the individual knower’s possession of cognition into the collective reproduction of criticism as a public method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D5","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-06","text":"Teleology escalates from the internal end organizing a science to the single human destination organizing the entire economy of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-07","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism cease to function as terminal alternatives and are absorbed as objects within criticism’s higher-order jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-08","text":"Technical classification and naturalistic common sense disappear as positive candidates after establishing the boundary between unaudited arrangement and rational science.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A6","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RMD-09","text":"The absence of an explicit original plan recurs as the deferred availability of a common critical road, converting lack into the temporal mechanism of rational development.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-01","text":"The detailed division of metaphysics into ontology and rational sciences remains local machinery unless interpreted as an instance of the broader jurisdictional principle.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-02","text":"Sensualism, intellectualism, empiricism, and noology remain local historical coordinates until their object and source commitments are subjected to critical alignment.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A4","CH_BA94_CIHP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-03","text":"The requirement of systematicity scales upward because it governs both the internal constitution of science and the legitimacy of metaphysical procedure.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-04","text":"Criticism bridges the units by joining architectonic limitation to the historical adjudication of dogmatic and sceptical alternatives.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-05","text":"Misalignment among cognitive source, claimed object, and method catalyzes the transition from static taxonomy to a tribunal of rational legitimacy.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A6","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-06","text":"Autonomous derivation scales from a distinction in subjective possession to the requirement that reason examine its own competence.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-07","text":"Technical unity and naturalism become obsolete as candidates for science because neither supplies an internally warranted rational plan.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:CGD-08","text":"Founders, inherited definitions, and historical persistence are recoded as evidence to be judged rather than authorities capable of determining rational identity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-01","text":"Rational science is neither an aggregate of cognitions nor a chronology of philosophical positions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX1","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-02","text":"Neither authorial definition nor common sense constitutes a final tribunal of metaphysical validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-03","text":"Architectonic legitimacy cannot arise from empirical resemblance, arbitrary use, or naturalistic repudiation of rational examination.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-04","text":"Empirical derivation and mathematical apriority do not authorize incorporation into metaphysics or claims about objects beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX9","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-05","text":"Neither dogmatism nor scepticism can settle metaphysics when the authority and limits of reason remain unaudited.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:NX6","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-06","text":"Philosophy is neither a completed transmissible doctrine nor metaphysics a license for lawless speculative dogmatism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX6","CH_BA94_CI_2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-07","text":"Specialist sciences cannot be self-sufficient or neutral toward the destination that architectonically orders cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX5","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:GNX-08","text":"Historical failure cannot establish the dispensability of metaphysics because recurrent dissatisfaction indicates an unresolved demand of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX12","CH_BA94_CIHP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-01","text":"Bottom-up accumulation and historical succession cannot independently generate either systematic unity or philosophical validity.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX1","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-02","text":"Choosing between empirical and rational derivation cannot settle metaphysics unless reason’s authority and limits are themselves examined.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-03","text":"Rational identity cannot be constituted by authorial intention, disciplinary convention, antiquity, persistence, or cultural authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-04","text":"Correct content, inherited through authority, cannot confer autonomy, just as systematic procedure cannot confer legitimacy without reflexive criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-05","text":"Pure reason cannot secure transcendent knowledge by exceeding the conditions of its cognitive sources; speculative success must therefore recede behind regulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX2","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-06","text":"Ultimate ends cannot remain coordinate, and specialist sciences cannot retain complete independence within the economy of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX6","CH_BA94_CI_2:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-07","text":"Durable critical settlement cannot be reduced to private mastery, even though autonomous derivation by individual subjects remains indispensable.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:DBN-08","text":"The architecture cannot admit that its rational idea is merely a retrospective imposition, although the criterion separating discovery from imposition remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT2","CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-01","text":"Immediate possession of metaphysical truth is unavailable; the absent complete philosophy can function only as a regulative standard.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX1","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-02","text":"The actual philosopher-legislator is absent, leaving rational subjects to approximate a legislative office no individual fully embodies.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX2","CH_BA94_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-03","text":"The explicit original plan and the positive procedure of criticism are both withheld, although each is declared necessary for rational reconstruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-04","text":"The final unity of nature and freedom structures the system as a necessary but unconstructed horizon.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4","CH_BA94_CI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-05","text":"Final satisfaction remains unavailable in the present because the critical path requires collective methodological transformation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSA-06","text":"The missing guarantee is a demonstrated criterion proving that the architectonic idea has been recovered from reason rather than imposed upon history.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT2","CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-01","text":"Architectonic norms convert philosophical history into a diagnostic tribunal by supplying the standards according to which historical positions can be placed and judged.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-03","CH_BA94_CI_2:A3","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-02","text":"Historical conflict supplies the medium through which the germinal architectonic idea becomes explicit, while the idea retrospectively distinguishes development from mere succession.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-04","CL_BA94_32:GA-10","CH_BA94_CI_2:A7","CH_BA94_CIHP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-03","text":"Criticism acquires a dual jurisdiction: it preserves metaphysics internally by limitation and governs externally among rival systematic methods.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-06","CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-04","text":"The historical matrix of object, origin, and method becomes the diagnostic counterpart of architectonic allocations among objects, sources, and disciplinary jurisdictions.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-05","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","CH_BA94_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-05","text":"Systematicity cannot serve as the final criterion of truth because opposed procedures can satisfy formal systematic requirements while remaining critically illegitimate.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-01","CL_BA94_32:GA-06","CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-06","text":"Individual rational autonomy becomes historically effective only when autonomous derivation is reproducible as a collectively sustained method.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-05","CL_BA94_32:GA-10","CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-07","text":"Metaphysics remains unavoidable because constitutive rational interests repeatedly regenerate inquiry after every failed settlement.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CL_BA94_32:GA-12","CH_BA94_CI_2:D13","CH_BA94_CIHP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-08","text":"Negative governance becomes the operative form of rational satisfaction because stable metaphysics depends on examining and restricting cognition rather than multiplying transcendent claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CL_BA94_32:GA-09","CL_BA94_32:GA-12","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-09","text":"The moral destination architectonically reorders theology and morality from historical stimuli of speculation into subordinate moments of a single human end.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-11","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:XD-10","text":"Technical classification and naturalistic common sense are structurally homologous failures because both substitute contingent or unaudited organization for rationally examined necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-01","CL_BA94_32:GA-05","CH_BA94_CI_2:A6","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-01","text":"Reason appears as a self-organizing court-organism whose parts develop internally while its tribunal classifies and judges their claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-03","CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CH_BA94_CI_2:D2","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-02","text":"Epistemic legitimacy becomes relational rather than atomic: no proposition is secure apart from the alignment of its source, object, method, limits, and systemic position.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-05","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-03","text":"Rational time is recursively split between an ideal precedence, retrospective historical disclosure, and future collective completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-04","CL_BA94_32:GA-10","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-04","text":"The rational subject becomes both legislator and accused: it orders cognition toward ultimate ends while submitting its own powers to examination.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CL_BA94_32:GA-08","CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-05","text":"Philosophical history becomes an internal phase of rational self-constitution because mature organization exposes and corrects earlier misdescriptions of reason’s work.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-03","CL_BA94_32:GA-04","CH_BA94_CI_2:D4","CH_BA94_CIHP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-06","text":"Philosophical legislation has an institutional consequence: specialist cognition must be subordinated to universal ends while critical method becomes publicly reproducible.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-08","CL_BA94_32:GA-10","CL_BA94_32:GA-11","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD4","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics survives by transforming its criterion of success from positive speculative expansion into stable regulation and error-prevention.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-09","CL_BA94_32:GA-12","CH_BA94_CI_2:D12","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:BSD-08","text":"The cluster produces prospective rather than completed closure because negative critical orientation is available before criticism’s positive procedure is fully supplied.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-07","CL_BA94_32:GA-10","CL_BA94_32:GA-12","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX6","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-01","text":"Accumulation produces provisional organization; mature organization discloses its germinal idea; that idea then reconstructs and corrects the accumulated field.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D2","CH_BA94_CI_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-02","text":"Constitutive metaphysical interests generate construction, construction generates unresolved conflict, conflict produces criticism, and criticism preserves the inquiry by limiting it.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D13","CH_BA94_CIHP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-03","text":"Claims about objects refer inquiry back to their cognitive sources and methods, while detected misalignment forces a reallocation of disciplinary jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-04","text":"Criticism limits speculative reason, the limitation preserves metaphysics, and preserved metaphysics regenerates the need for criticism whenever speculation returns.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-05","text":"Autonomous derivation generates critical subjects; public method stabilizes their judgments; stabilized method enables further autonomous derivation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-06","text":"The single human destination functions as a conceptual attractor drawing specialist sciences, metaphysical divisions, and practical interests into one moral-teleological hierarchy.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-07","text":"Systematicity undergoes phase change from the defining form of science to an insufficient form shared by opposed procedures and therefore requiring critical adjudication.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:RLE-08","text":"The absent complete philosophy and deferred common critical road act as attractors that organize present inquiry without becoming presently possessed objects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX1","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B01","text":"Aggregate cognition plus recurrent metaphysical construction without settlement generates the initial demand for rational reconstruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B02","text":"Rational reconstruction begins by replacing chronology with a transcendental classification of disputes concerning objects, sources, and methods.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B03","text":"Transcendental classification excludes technical arrangement, naturalistic authority, and claims exceeding their established cognitive basis.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:NX3","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B04","text":"Exclusion clears the field for an architectonic idea that determines a priori the end, limits, membership, and placement of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS2","CH_BA94_CI_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B05","text":"Architectonic construction becomes reflexive when criticism judges whether systematic procedures remain within the legitimate powers of reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B06","text":"Reflexive criticism produces a jurisdictional metaphysics by coordinating cognitive objects, sources, methods, and disciplinary boundaries.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B07","text":"Jurisdictional order is oriented toward a single human end and redefines rational satisfaction as critically regulated completion.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FGS-B08","text":"The machine reopens because the unity of nature and freedom and the collective realization of criticism remain necessary but incomplete.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AFM-B01","text":"Aggregate cognition and metaphysical ruin → transcendental diagnosis → architectonic idea → critical tribunal → regulated metaphysics → conditional rational satisfaction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AFM-B02","text":"Empirical arrangement and inherited authority → exposure of unaudited cognition → autonomous derivation from principles → collectively sustainable critical method.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D3","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AFM-B03","text":"Internal scientific end → cosmical legislation → one human destination → historical self-testing → deferred unity of nature and freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:AFM-B04","text":"Systematic dogmatism and scepticism → higher-order criticism → negative governance → preservation rather than abandonment of metaphysical inquiry.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:A9","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-01","text":"Science and philosophical history become complementary structures: one is a teleologically articulated whole, and the other a field of epistemic positions awaiting placement.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ME1","CH_BA94_CIHP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-02","text":"History becomes an audit of rational architecture rather than an external record of schools, enabling inherited self-definitions to be corrected.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ME3","CH_BA94_CIHP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-03","text":"Rationality shifts from the possession of objectively correct propositions to autonomous acquisition under principles and examination of cognitive authority.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ME4","CH_BA94_CIHP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-04","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from failed transcendent doctrine into the critically restricted organization of reason’s unavoidable questions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ME10","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-05","text":"Specialist sciences are repositioned within a moral-teleological hierarchy whose legitimacy depends on publicly sustainable criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD4","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-06","text":"Rational temporality is redrawn as retrospective and prospective rather than simply progressive: the idea is prior, its articulation historical, and its settlement future.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-07","text":"Method becomes constitutive rather than secondary because disciplinary placement and metaphysical validity depend on how cognition is produced and tested.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD6","CH_BA94_CIHP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MME-08","text":"The binaries of empiricism versus noology and dogmatism versus scepticism cease to be exhaustive once criticism examines the competence presupposed by every side.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:DNX4","CH_BA94_CIHP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system distributes cognition into sciences and schools while distinguishing their objects, origins, methods, and divisions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT1","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, internal ends organize scientific jurisdictions while metaphysical instability appears wherever claimed objects disconnect from cognitive sources and procedures.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT2","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inherited doctrine and chronological history are converted into materials for autonomous critical reconstruction.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT3","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reason is an organic system whose ideal whole precedes its parts but achieves historical articulation through recursive self-limitation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:ALT4","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text’s taxonomies enact the jurisdiction they assert by assigning positions, exposing overreach, and directing the field toward criticism.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:AS3","CH_BA94_CIHP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-01","text":"The a priori authority of the architectonic idea is strained by its dependence on retrospective visibility after empirical and historical accumulation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT5","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-02","text":"Criticism is required as metaphysics’ internal organ and final tribunal, but its positive procedures are announced rather than demonstrated within the supplied architecture.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX2","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-03","text":"The single moral-human end and the final unity of nature and freedom carry terminal architectonic weight without receiving equivalent demonstration.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT7","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-04","text":"The claimed immutability of architectonic division is destabilized by the provisional admission of empirical psychology and by the shifting highest boundary of metaphysics.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT9","CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-05","text":"Rational autonomy belongs to individual derivation from principles, whereas durable critical completion depends on collective future labor.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A9","CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-06","text":"Systematicity functions both as a condition of legitimacy and as a form shared by procedures that criticism ultimately rejects.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:D5","CH_BA94_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:FL-07","text":"The historical field is made architectonically tractable by compressing diverse schools into stable positions, but that compression may suppress internal variation.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT4","CH_BA94_CIHP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MP-01","text":"Criticism mutates from a restrictive function inside metaphysics into a tribunal governing the entire history and architecture of pure reason.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MP-02","text":"Systematicity mutates from the sufficient contrast with aggregation into a merely necessary condition requiring critical authorization.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A1","CH_BA94_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MP-03","text":"History mutates from chronological narration into transcendental diagnosis of recurring configurations of rational overreach.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:AS1","CH_BA94_CIHP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MP-04","text":"Metaphysics mutates from the promise of positive speculative knowledge into an office centered on error-prevention, limitation, and disciplinary order.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D12","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MP-05","text":"Teleology mutates from the internal end of a particular system into the singular moral destination governing the complete economy of cognition.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A5","CH_BA94_CI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly imaged as a court-organism: an internally articulated whole that grows through its parts while judging their admissible positions.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:D2","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a provisional legislator who orders cognition while remaining answerable to an examination of its own cognitive powers.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:A13","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes a recursive circuit in which the ideal whole is prior, historical conflict makes it explicit, and collective criticism projects its completion forward.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD7","CH_BA94_CIHP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:HSI-04","text":"Possible struggle takes the form of navigation through jurisdictional boundaries, critical exclusion of overreach, and public reconstruction under a universal end.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:SD8","CH_BA94_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from aggregate cognition and metaphysical failure through architectonic organization to criticism’s regulatory office.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT1","CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the criterion of the true rational idea, the positive procedure of criticism, and the final synthesis of nature and freedom.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT2","CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT2","CH_BA94_CI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where differences of kind, systemic placement, and object-source-method alignment mutually reinforce one another.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT4","CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing because the governing idea is treated as prior even though it becomes visible through historical accumulation and classificatory compression.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT5","CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B05","text":"Moral teleology and criticism’s positive content remain resistant to full axiomatic reduction because both bear terminal weight beyond their supplied demonstrations.","refs":["CH_BA94_CI_2:MRT7","CH_BA94_CIHP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_BA94_32:MRT-B06","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if criticism remains a tribunal of rational legitimacy rather than becoming another doctrine or historical school.","refs":["CH_BA94_CIHP:MRT7","CH_BA94_CI_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 93–94","context":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","original_title":"Chapters 93–94","original_context":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Chapters 93–94","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 01","order":1,"parents":["CL_BA94_01","CL_BA94_02","CL_BA94_03","CL_BA94_04"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_01@R001","sha256":"62eac2e887585f01822f9d9746fe0f1a0fe9688f6b0eb621d6213f6b3dc0decd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-01","text":"The governing problem is how cognition can make necessary and augmentative claims when it begins through experience and reaches objects only through finite intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:AS-01","CL_BA94_03:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-02","text":"The empirical–a priori distinction is therefore displaced from chronology to a transcendental inquiry into what conditions the possibility of experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:AS-02","CL_BA94_04:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-03","text":"Because analysis cannot augment cognition and empirical synthesis cannot secure necessity, synthetic a priori cognition becomes the system’s central explanatory demand.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-01","CL_BA94_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-04","text":"Mathematics and natural science establish that necessary synthesis occurs, while metaphysics remains an unavoidable but unverified attempt to extend the same power beyond its demonstrated jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-06","CL_BA94_02:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-05","text":"The argument becomes transcendental and regressive: instead of deriving cognition from independently constituted objects, it recovers the conditions under which objects can be given and known.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:AS-B03","CL_BA94_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-06","text":"This regress differentiates sensibility from understanding and identifies pure intuition, spatial externality, and temporal order as necessary components of finite object-cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-07","text":"Objective validity is secured by restricting necessary cognition to appearances, so the transition from conditions of appearance to determinations of things in themselves is prohibited.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:GA-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-08","text":"The movement is recursive: experience demands a priori conditions, those conditions validate experience, and their experiential jurisdiction simultaneously closes off unrestricted metaphysical extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:AS-07","CL_BA94_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognition may begin through experience without being wholly derivable from experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Synthetic a priori cognition is necessary cognitive augmentation whose possibility cannot be explained by conceptual analysis or empirical association.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Analysis supplies necessity without extension, while experience supplies extension without strict necessity; neither can independently ground theoretical science.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:AS-B01","CL_BA94_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is both the field within which a priori structures acquire objective validity and the criterion limiting their legitimate use.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RLE-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Critical examination of cognitive authority must precede every doctrinal extension of pure reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:GA-09","CL_BA94_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental explanation validates cognition by regressing from claims about objects to the conditions that make object-relation possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:AS-B03","CL_BA94_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Intuition is indispensable both to object-directed conceptual use and to synthetic extension beyond what concepts analytically contain.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-02","CL_BA94_04:ABS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility and understanding are irreducible but ordered faculties: objects must be given before they can be thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The a priori expands from independence from experience into the formal structure of sensibility embodied by space and time.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:RMD-02","CL_BA94_04:ABS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The finite subject is neither a sovereign creator nor a passive spectator, but a receptive-formal operator that supplies conditions without supplying empirical content.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:BSD-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Objective validity is domain-indexed to appearances, and no valid inference runs from conditions of appearance to the intrinsic constitution of things.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:GA-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Metaphysics persists as a rational disposition but can become a science only by submitting its claims to critical authorization.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-08","CL_BA94_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Experience retains its identity as cognition’s occasion but mutates into a field whose intelligibility and certainty depend on non-empirical conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RMD-01","CL_BA94_01:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"The a priori moves from classificatory independence, through necessary judgment, into the formal architecture of sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:GA-03","CL_BA94_03:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Necessity changes from a sign identifying non-empirical cognition into the formal invariance of possible experience and a limit on speculative extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RMD-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Synthesis expands from a subject–predicate relation into the principle-form of theoretical science and finally into the mediated constitution of phenomenal order.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:RMD-02","CL_BA94_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Intuition migrates from its local role in mathematical construction to the general medium of object-givenness and then differentiates into spatial and temporal forms.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GA-07","CL_BA94_04:ABS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Critique reverses from apparently negative restraint into the productive method that isolates and validates pure cognitive conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CL_BA94_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Objectivity reverses from apparent independence of the subject into universal validity within the subject-conditioned field of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:RMD-03","CL_BA94_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Metaphysics mutates from attempted a priori enlargement into a natural demand whose possible scientific form is critique of rational jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:RMD-06","CL_BA94_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"The thing in itself never becomes a positive object; it is absorbed into the boundary function that prevents phenomenal conditions from becoming predicates of being.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:RMD-06","CL_BA94_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"The pure–impure distinction and exclusions concerning morality and taste disappear after isolating the relevant field of pure theoretical cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RMD-07","CL_BA94_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"The pure–impure taxonomy remains lower-layer machinery because it cannot determine the objective validity or scope of a priori cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Subject–predicate containment is locally classificatory, but its failure to augment cognition scales upward as the general limit of analytic explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:CGD-01","CL_BA94_02:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Mathematics scales upward as proof that necessary synthesis occurs, but its intuitive method cannot by itself provide a universal warrant for physics or metaphysics.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:CGD-03","CL_BA94_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"The exclusions of practical morality and empirical taste remain local purity operations and recede after the field of sensibility is isolated.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"Geometrical construction remains a spatial proof-case, while its requirement of non-conceptual intuition scales into the general architecture of pure sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"Temporal unity begins as domain-specific machinery but scales upward into the rule that formal unity precedes every determinate magnitude, relation, or interval.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:CGD-03","CL_BA94_04:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"Space provides the completed local demonstration, while time generalizes formal intuition from externality to phenomenal representation as such.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:HSI-04","CL_BA94_04:ABS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"The current-level architecture begins when authorization, synthetic extension, faculty differentiation, and restricted objective validity become phases of one transcendental machine.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:CGD-07","CL_BA94_04:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Temporal priority, empirical accumulation, and observed sequence cannot generate the a priori necessity presupposed by experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:GNX-01","CL_BA94_04:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Logical consistency, conceptual completion, and contradiction-testing cannot produce synthetic knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:GNX-04","CL_BA94_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Concepts cannot reach objects independently of intuition or generate determinate spatial and temporal cognition by themselves.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GNX-02","CL_BA94_04:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Sensibility and understanding cannot be collapsed into a homogeneous faculty because givenness and thought perform non-interchangeable operations.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GNX-07","CL_BA94_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Formal conditions of appearance cannot be converted into properties or relations of things in themselves.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"The subject can be construed neither as a spectator receiving completed objects nor as an unrestricted creator of them.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:DBN-02","CL_BA94_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Neither dogmatic possession of supersensible objects nor sceptical cancellation of metaphysical demand is admissible.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:GNX-06","CL_BA94_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any account in which experience supplies both cognitive contents and the rules that make those contents intelligible.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:DBN-02","CL_BA94_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Necessary order cannot belong to a world already given as spatially and temporally organized independently of cognitive conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Pure reason cannot carry self-authenticating authority into every domain in which empirical resistance is absent.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"No external tribunal can adjudicate supersensible claims, so reason must perform a reflexive authorization whose circularity cannot be eliminated.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:BSA-04","CL_BA94_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"Unrestricted absolute knowledge must remain unavailable because every legitimate object-claim is conditioned by finite intuition and thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:BSA-01","CL_BA94_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"The system cannot admit a transparent common source that would erase the functional difference between sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:BSA-03","CL_BA94_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable position is an unconditioned standpoint from which reality could be known apart from every form of sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"The system circles but refuses the identity of thinkability and knowability; conceptual possibility can never itself disclose the positive constitution of things.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:BSA-02","CL_BA94_04:ABS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"Necessary objectivity cannot bypass the subject–object relation without losing the conditions that make necessity and object-relation intelligible.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"A final common ground and completed system remain unavailable; systematic unity persists only as a regulative horizon assembled through bounded functional differences.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:BSA-03","CL_BA94_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-01","text":"The separation of experiential onset from epistemic origin combines with the paired failures of analysis and experience to force inquiry toward transcendental conditions of necessary synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-06","CL_BA94_01:XD-01","CL_BA94_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Existing a priori sciences establish an explanatory burden rather than a transferable warrant for metaphysics.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-12","CL_BA94_02:XD-03","CL_BA94_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The demand that cognition justify its authority becomes an ordered proof-procedure in which sensibility must be determined before conceptual claims about objects can be assessed.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-08","CL_BA94_01:GA-09","CL_BA94_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Intuition migrates from mathematical mediation into the general architecture of sensibility, but differentiates into spatial and temporal functions rather than becoming a uniform abstraction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-09","CL_BA94_02:GA-07","CL_BA94_04:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-05","text":"The conditions that explain empirical necessity are also the conditions that restrict the valid domain of necessary claims.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-11","CL_BA94_01:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_04:ABS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-06","text":"The interaction of affection with a priori form produces a subject that is constitutive without being creative and receptive without being passive.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-10","CL_BA94_03:GA-05","CL_BA94_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Subjective origin and objective validity cease to be opposites once validity is indexed to the domain constituted by shared conditions of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-11","CL_BA94_04:MME-02","CL_BA94_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Metaphysics survives the invalidation of dogmatism because the inevitability of its questions is separated from the legitimacy of its answers.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-12","CL_BA94_02:XD-05","CL_BA94_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:XD-09","text":"The thing in itself is generated as a necessary boundary-marker even though its positive determination would exceed the cognition that requires the boundary.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-11","CL_BA94_04:RMD-06","CL_BA94_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Epistemic justification becomes a theory of conditioned validity in which authority is obtained by recovering enabling conditions rather than accumulating observations or doctrines.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:BSD-01","CL_BA94_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Reality for cognition becomes a relational phenomenal field rather than either an independently transparent order or a free construction of thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:BSD-02","CL_BA94_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes simultaneously a finite relay of affection and form and the claimant, investigator, and tribunal of rational authority.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:HSI-02","CL_BA94_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Time differentiates into temporal awakening, transcendental antecedence, and the irreversible form of phenomenal differentiation.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:HSI-03","CL_BA94_03:HSI-04","CL_BA94_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Science and metaphysics are no longer divided primarily by objects but by whether necessary claims remain within conditions of possible appearance.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:MME-03","CL_BA94_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Limitation becomes productive because purification and delimitation are the operations by which warranted cognitive extension is first made possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:BSD-05","CL_BA94_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Critique has an irreducibly recursive structure because reason must investigate and delimit its powers by exercising those same powers.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RLE-06","CL_BA94_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Experience awakens cognition → necessity reveals a priori contribution → a priori conditions explain experience → possible experience validates and limits those conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RLE-01","CL_BA94_01:RLE-02","CL_BA94_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Analysis fails to augment cognition while experience fails to necessitate its connections, and their convergent failure escalates into the synthetic a priori problem.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:RLE-01","CL_BA94_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Existing sciences generate inquiry into their possibility, and that inquiry turns critical limitation into the condition of their rational authorization.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:RLE-03","CL_BA94_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Metaphysical systems generate contradiction → contradiction produces sceptical pressure → scepticism reactivates critique → critique conditionally reopens metaphysics.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:RLE-04","CL_BA94_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Claims to pure cognition trigger critique → critique isolates faculties and forms → isolated conditions become renewed criteria for judging pure claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:RLE-01","CL_BA94_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Thought returns to intuition for object-relation, while every assertion of formal necessity returns to the appearance boundary for restriction of ontological scope.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:RLE-02","CL_BA94_04:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Possible experience is the dominant conceptual attractor because epistemic origin, scientific necessity, objectivity, and metaphysical limitation all converge upon it.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RLE-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Experiential awakening → separation of received material from cognitive contribution → recognition that empirical cognition is composite.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:AS-01","CL_BA94_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Analytic non-augmentation + empirical non-necessity → synthetic a priori cognition as the missing form of theoretical grounding.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:XD-01","CL_BA94_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Existing sciences + recurrent metaphysical contradiction → conversion of doctrinal construction into critique of rational competence.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:XD-03","CL_BA94_02:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Critique of competence → transcendental regress from objects to conditions → ordered differentiation of givenness and thought.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:AS-B03","CL_BA94_03:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Affection and structured receptivity → separation of empirical matter from a priori form → reductive isolation of pure intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:AS-B05","CL_BA94_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Pure intuition → space as external form + time as universal phenomenal form → necessary ordering of appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:ABS-05","CL_BA94_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Necessary phenomenal order → domain-indexed objective validity → prohibition on converting conditions of appearance into predicates of being.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:GA-05","CL_BA94_04:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Experiential awakening → separation of temporal beginning from epistemic origin → composite cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Composite cognition → analytic and empirical deficits → problem of necessary cognitive augmentation.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:AS-B01","CL_BA94_02:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Necessary augmentation → scientific proof-cases and metaphysical contradiction → critique of rational jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:CGD-03","CL_BA94_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Critique of jurisdiction → transcendental relocation from objects to conditions → division of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:AS-B03","CL_BA94_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Priority of sensibility → matter/form distinction → pure intuition as the condition of givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:CGD-06","CL_BA94_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Pure intuition → spatial externality and temporal order → law-governed appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:XD-01","CL_BA94_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:AFM-B07","text":"Law-governed appearances → restricted objective validity → thing in itself as boundary → recursive return to possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:RMD-06","CL_BA94_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Experience is reclassified from the total source of cognition into a materially supplied field constituted by non-empirical conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MME-01","CL_BA94_01:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-02","text":"The a priori is reclassified from a detachable stock of pure knowledge into a domain-bound operation that conditions experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:BSD-03","CL_BA94_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from subject-independence into universal validity for objects under shared conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:MP-01","CL_BA94_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Subjectivity is reclassified from private contingency into the transcendental source of empirically binding formal order.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-05","text":"The boundary between science and metaphysics is redrawn around valid grounding and domain rather than disciplinary object-matter.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:BSD-02","CL_BA94_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-06","text":"The opposition between extension and limitation collapses because critical restriction is the enabling condition of warranted extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:MME-05","CL_BA94_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Space and time are reclassified from independent objects or discovered relations into forms organizing possible appearance.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:MME-01","CL_BA94_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Explanation is reorganized as formal-dependence analysis: apparent object-properties must be tested as possible presuppositions of their own representation.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:MME-04","CL_BA94_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, judgments, mathematics, natural science, metaphysics, sensation, geometry, space, time, succession, and external objects are examined as cases of necessary cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, affection, sensibility, pure form, intuition, and understanding form an ordered mediation through which empirical contents become objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, doctrinal comparison is replaced by regressive inquiry into conditions and by jurisdictional tests of cognitive authority.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_04:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality for cognition is phenomenal and temporally conditioned, while independent being remains inaccessible and temporal origin remains distinct from transcendental antecedence.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_01:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly subtracts empirical or conceptual additions and converts residual presuppositions into explanatory conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Reason must act as the tribunal of its own authority, making critical authorization necessarily reflexive and potentially circular.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Affection supplies empirical matter, but the relation between affection and the subject’s a priori formal contribution remains functionally asserted rather than explained.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The actual existence of mathematics and natural science creates an explanatory demand but does not guarantee that a complete transcendental account is available.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-04","text":"The thing in itself is required as the correlate limiting phenomenal cognition, yet its assertion as real exceeds positive determination by the system.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Space and time must be independent of particular phenomena as their conditions, although their operative significance is defined through their role in representing phenomena.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Mathematical intuition is the strongest displayed model of synthetic production, but its transfer to a universal theory of synthesis remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:FL-02","CL_BA94_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from independence from experience into a condition of experience and then into the formal structure of sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MP-01","CL_BA94_03:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Necessity mutates from an identifying criterion into a jurisdictional boundary and finally into the invariance of formal conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:RMD-03","CL_BA94_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Objectivity mutates from apparent independence of the subject into universal phenomenal validity under subjective conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:MP-01","CL_BA94_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Critique mutates from negative limitation into a generative procedure of purification, isolation, and positive validation.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:GA-04","CL_BA94_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Synthesis mutates from a logical relation between terms into the intuition-mediated generative form of theoretical cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:MP-01","CL_BA94_04:ABS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned processing field in which empirical objects become knowable only after affection is organized through formal mediation.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:HSI-01","CL_BA94_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a formally receptive node and finite relay that also serves as claimant, investigator, and tribunal of its own cognitive authority.","refs":["CL_BA94_04:HSI-02","CL_BA94_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is simultaneously the awakening of cognition, the transcendental antecedence of enabling conditions, and the irreversible dimension of phenomenal differentiation.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:HSI-03","CL_BA94_03:HSI-04","CL_BA94_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Struggle becomes navigation between warranted extension and restraint, avoiding empirical reduction, conceptual abstraction, and transcendent projection.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:HSI-04","CL_BA94_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the current-level movement from experiential initiation through synthetic a priori cognition and pure intuition to restricted phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where the grounding of empirical certainty converges with spatial and temporal arguments to produce the same ground–limit structure.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive ground of synthesis, the internal constitution of the a priori faculty, and the positive status of the thing in itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_02:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_01:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is coherent only if necessity, intuition, faculty-difference, and objective validity remain differentiated and indexed to possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return from experiential validity to formal conditions and from formal conditions to ontological restriction.","refs":["CL_BA94_01:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"The relation between affection and form and the limiting status of the thing in itself remain indispensable elements resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_03:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–12 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","original_title":"Ba94 L1 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–12 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L2 01","order":1,"parents":["MC_BA94_L1_01","MC_BA94_L1_02","MC_BA94_L1_03","MC_BA94_L1_04"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L2_01@R001","sha256":"9c11b509288be392cf3f59f7ec6f9c74a9df9e2cb6b0660669d6100d77ad9199","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B01","text":"The governing problem is how finite cognition can authorize synthetic a priori necessity without deriving it from experience or extending it into supersensible knowledge.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B02","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed by regressing from claims about independently constituted objects to the conditions under which sensible multiplicity can become an object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B03","text":"Finite cognition is generated through the coordinated non-identity of receptivity, synthesis, and conceptual determination rather than through reduction to a single faculty.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B04","text":"Synthesis becomes the mediating operation that converts sensible multiplicity into apperceptively unified and objectively determinable experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B05","text":"Judgment and temporal schematization transform pure conceptual forms into an applied system capable of generating principles of experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B06","text":"Possible experience becomes the common ground, field, criterion, and limit of objective validity, making constitution and restriction inseparable.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursively spiral because each recovered condition creates a new mediation problem and each successful validation reinstates the boundary of finite cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-08","MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B08","text":"Closure is critical rather than absolute: lawful phenomenal nature is secured, empirical determination remains open, and positive noumenal cognition is terminated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires irreducibly distinct sensible and conceptual sources whose coordination cannot be replaced by either source alone.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis is prior to analysis and objecthood because sensible multiplicity does not arrive as an already unified cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Original apperception is the higher unity through which subjective self-ascription and objective relation are co-generated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] A logical function becomes an objectively valid category only when it determines sensible multiplicity through synthesis and temporal specification.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Mediation is the system’s operative center, linking intuition, synthesis, apperception, judgment, schema, category, principle, and object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-13","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the ground of objective validity, the domain of application, and the criterion of cognitive limitation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Objectivity is condition-relative without being arbitrary because universally necessary synthesis replaces comparison with independently cognized being.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-06","MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] A priori authority is domain-indexed: its necessity becomes legitimate only by accepting restriction to possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The subject is a constitutive but non-sovereign integrating function that organizes givenness while remaining temporally non-coincident with its own activity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Pure understanding legislates the universal form of lawful nature while leaving its particular empirical determinations open.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Critique is a positive jurisdictional operation through which cognition examines, distributes, and limits its own authority.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Positive noumenal cognition collapses, but an indeterminate noumenal remainder persists because it can be neither positively known nor eliminated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"Experience retains its identity as cognition’s occasion but expands into the constituted outcome, validating field, and limiting criterion of a priori synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"The a priori mutates from independence from experience into formal sensibility, structural dependence, and domain-restricted legislation for experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"Intuition migrates from mathematical construction into the general medium of object-givenness and then into a synthesized spatial-temporal manifold.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"Synthesis escalates from a relation within judgment into the prior operation of object-constitution and finally into schematically regulated application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"Objectivity reverses from apparent subject-independence into necessary synthesis and then into a jurisdiction of valid application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"Subjectivity reverses from a suspected source of distortion into the universal formal condition and non-substantial integrating function of empirical objectivity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"Time expands from a universal form of appearance into the medium of schematization, objective application, and the subject’s incomplete self-presence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-08","text":"Critique reverses from negative restraint into the constructive architecture that distributes and validates bounded cognitive authority.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-09","text":"Productive imagination appears as explicit mediation and then recedes after schema inherits its application-function.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-10","text":"The thing in itself is absorbed into a boundary function, while noumenon undergoes terminal division into an inadmissible positive object and an indispensable negative limit.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-09","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"Purity taxonomies, moral and aesthetic exclusions, and pedagogical examples remain local isolation devices rather than grounds of transcendental validity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"Geometry, motion, and related demonstrations scale upward only insofar as they expose the dependence of empirical determination upon non-empirical sensible form.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"Detailed logical divisions, judgment forms, and temporal schemata remain local inventories or application mechanisms rather than the current-level architecture itself.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"Faculty differentiation scales upward because it governs givenness, unification, application, validity, and limitation across the entire system.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"Synthesis, apperception, imagination, judgment, and schematism bridge lower-layer analyses by specifying successive moments of one mediating operation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-06","text":"The completion of each regional analysis catalyzes a higher problem, so closure of sensibility opens conceptual determination and bounded analysis opens application and critique.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-07","text":"The underived inventory of forms scales upward from a local omission into the terminal limit of transcendental self-explanation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-10","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-08","text":"Ready-made correspondence and positive ontology become obsolete once objectivity is reconstructed through mediated synthesis and jurisdictional analysis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"Empirical sequence, repetition, and association cannot generate the a priori necessity required for objective cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"Concepts cannot independently reach objects because conceptual form cannot generate the givenness it must determine.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"Sensible givenness cannot independently constitute objects because an unsynthesized manifold lacks conjunction and objective unity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"Logical consistency is only a negative gate and cannot establish material truth, objective possibility, or synthetic grounding.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Categories cannot authorize cognition beyond sensible conditions, regardless of their a priori origin or formal persistence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-06","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot become symmetrical domains of positive cognition because the noumenal position has only a limiting function.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-07","MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-07","text":"Neither a passive spectator, a substantial self, nor a sovereign creator can provide an independent foundation for objectivity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GNX-06","MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-08","text":"No complete algorithm can eliminate judgment because each additional application-rule reproduces the problem of subsumption.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of a model in which objects arrive as already unified, spatially ordered, and cognitively self-present.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"A transparent common source cannot erase the difference between receptivity and spontaneity without collapsing the need for synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"No external or unconditioned standpoint is available from which appearances can be compared with independently cognized intrinsic reality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"Pure reason and formal logic cannot authenticate unrestricted object-knowledge merely because empirical resistance is absent.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"Validation of the necessary operation of cognition cannot become a derivation of the exact inventory or ultimate source of its forms.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-06","text":"Universal formal lawfulness cannot determine particular qualities, causes, laws, or existence without empirical inquiry.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"Intellectual intuition is the deepest unavailable possibility because it would eliminate the mediation constitutive of finite cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"The common ground of receptivity and spontaneity remains structurally presupposed but unavailable as an object of cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"No unconditioned foundation exists on either the object-side or the subject-side of cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"The noumenal remainder cannot be positively known or eliminated because either result would destroy its limiting function.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"The system is organized around the unavailable identity of thinkability, knowability, and complete possession of reality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-01","text":"The problem of synthetic a priori cognition becomes a staged authorization procedure running from differentiated faculties through synthesis and apperception to temporal application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-01","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-02","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-02","text":"Pure intuition and apperceptive unity become mutually insufficient conditions whose coordination generates both object-relation and subject-identity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-01","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-02","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Possible experience unifies validation and restriction because the field constituted by a priori synthesis is also the field within which that synthesis has objective authority.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-06","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-04","text":"Time becomes the privileged mediation through which sensible form is converted into schematic application and the active subject appears to itself without immediate self-coincidence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_01:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-05","text":"The logical inventory becomes a generative system of nature only after apperceptive synthesis and temporal schematization establish its objective entitlement.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-04","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Architectonic completeness and empirical openness coexist because pure cognition can delimit all formal functions without exhausting the material determinations supplied through experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-07","text":"Subject-conditioned objectivity is non-arbitrary because the same synthesis that constitutes formal self-identity also imposes universally necessary object-relations.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-07","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-08","text":"The unknown remainder is generated internally when successful phenomenal constitution prohibits conversion of its own conditions into determinations of independent being.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-09","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Epistemic justification becomes conditioned validity: cognition acquires authority by recovering conditions that simultaneously constitute and limit its objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"Subject and object become differentiated effects of distributed synthesis rather than independently completed terms subsequently brought into correspondence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Nature becomes formally legislated but materially underdetermined, so objective temporal order is necessary while particular laws remain empirical.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes a layered operator joining phenomenal succession, transcendental application, incomplete self-presence, and recursive systematic exposition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"Error becomes jurisdictional: valid forms generate illusion when detached from the sensible conditions that alone authorize their use.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from attempted supersensible doctrine into analysis of grounding, application, scope, and object-reference.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-07","text":"Successful phenomenal constitution intensifies rather than removes the unknown because it generates an excluded terminus that cannot become a second object-domain.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-08","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Experience awakens the demand for necessity, necessity triggers a regress to conditions, those conditions constitute experience, and constituted experience validates and limits them.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"Faculty separation enables cooperation, reciprocal insufficiency generates cognition, and successful cooperation reconfirms irreducible differentiation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"Purification secures universality by removing content, but the resulting emptiness forces sensible givenness to return as the condition of objective application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-03","MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"The manifold demands synthesis, synthesis demands apperception, apperception authorizes categories, and category-generated principles return to organize the manifold.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"Rules generate an application regress that higher-order rules reproduce, while judgment and schema interrupt without finally abolishing it.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-06","text":"Closure repeatedly becomes transition because each completed regional account exposes an unresolved condition of mediation, application, or exhibition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-07","MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-07","text":"Possible experience is the jurisdictional attractor, synthesis the operative attractor, finitude the terminal attractor, and negative noumenon the boundary attractor.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:RLE-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Necessary scientific cognition plus recurrent metaphysical contradiction → demand for synthetic a priori authorization → critique of rational competence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-04","MC_BA94_L1_01:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Critique of competence → transcendental regress from objects to enabling conditions → differentiation of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Formal sensibility → spatial externality and temporal order → a priori field of phenomenal givenness.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Sensible manifold plus conceptual spontaneity → requirement for prior synthesis → represented connection.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Represented connection → regress to original apperception → co-generation of self-ascription and object-relation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B05","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Apperceptive judgment → logical function applied to intuition → category → temporal schema → objective application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B07","text":"Schematized categories → system of principles → universal formal lawfulness of nature plus irreducible empirical openness.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B08","text":"Attempted transcendent extension → loss of sensible application → collapse of positive ontology → persistence of a negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B08","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Experiential cognition and established sciences → analytic and empirical deficits → synthetic a priori demand.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-01","MC_BA94_L1_01:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Synthetic a priori demand → transcendental relocation from objects to conditions → pure spatial-temporal givenness.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:AS-02","MC_BA94_L1_01:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Pure givenness → exposure of dual-source insufficiency → requirement for mediated synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B04","text":"Synthesis → original apperception → judgmental unity → categorial entitlement.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B05","MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B05","text":"Categorial entitlement → application problem → imagination, judgment, and temporal schema.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B06","text":"Schematized application → principles of experience → formally lawful but empirically open nature.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:AFM-B07","text":"Secured phenomenal objectivity → jurisdictional test → exclusion of transcendent use → negative noumenal closure.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B07","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence with ready-made objects into universally necessary synthesis within conditions of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-02","text":"The subject–object opposition is replaced by a differentiation internal to one synthetic architecture.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-03","text":"A priori origin is separated from unrestricted range, making limitation constitutive of authority rather than externally imposed upon it.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Truth becomes stratified across logical consistency, transcendental validity, objective possibility, and synthetic object-determination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-05","text":"Nature is divided into invariant formal lawfulness and empirically discoverable material determination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-06","text":"Metaphysics is redrawn from positive cognition of supersensible entities into jurisdictional analysis of object-reference and scope.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-07","text":"Cognitive limits become internal products of object-constitution rather than external barriers imposed upon an otherwise unrestricted reason.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-08","text":"The two-world model is replaced by an asymmetry between a lawful phenomenal interior and an indeterminate limiting remainder.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-08","MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, mathematics, natural science, metaphysics, space, time, categories, schemata, principles, nature, and noumenon are coordinated around the validity of synthetic cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, receptivity, synthesis, imagination, apperception, understanding, and judgment form a distributed object-generating circuit.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry replaces doctrinal assertion with regressive condition-analysis, jurisdictional testing, and principled exclusion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality-for-cognition is phenomenal and law-governed, while time mediates application, objectivity, and incomplete self-presence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system constructs cognitive mechanisms and recursively derives their limits from their enabling conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-01","text":"Reason must authorize itself through the same powers under examination, producing a reflexive circularity that cannot be externally resolved.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-01","MC_BA94_L1_01:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-02","text":"Cognition presupposes affection by objects whose independent constitution remains unknowable, leaving the passage from affection to formal representation underexplained.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:FL-02","MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-03","text":"Faculty cooperation is indispensable, but the determinate mediation between intuition and conception becomes increasingly opaque as imagination recedes behind schema.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-04","text":"Systematic completeness depends upon an exhaustive logical and categorial table whose own completeness remains asserted rather than independently demonstrated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-05","text":"Possible experience is both constituted by categorial synthesis and invoked as the validating field of that synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-06","text":"Judgment must remain irreducible to rules even though schematism claims to regulate pure conceptual application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-07","text":"Formal intuition appears to possess unity, yet perceptual unity is also attributed to synthesis not belonging to sensibility alone.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from independence from experience into the structural condition and jurisdictional legislation of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-02","text":"Synthesis mutates from a logical relation into the prior operation that generates conjunction, objecthood, and application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Objectivity mutates from apparent correspondence into invariant synthesis and finally into a jurisdiction of valid application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-04","text":"The subject mutates from a presumed bearer into an integrating function constituted through the synthesis it performs.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Time mutates from a form of intuition into the transformation medium of schema, application, experience, and incomplete self-presence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-06","text":"Critique mutates from negative limitation into the positive architecture that generates, distributes, and bounds cognitive authority.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MP-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-07","text":"Noumenon mutates from a candidate supersensible object into an empty position that survives only as a recursive limit.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a gated recursive engine that receives multiplicity, synthesizes unity, routes it through conceptual authorization, and returns a phenomenal object-world.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite switching-center and integrating checkpoint rather than a passive container or sovereign creator.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is the transformation medium through which sensible succession becomes schema, application, objective order, and incomplete self-appearance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"Legitimate struggle consists in routing claims through intuition, synthesis, schema, and experiential jurisdiction rather than attempting escape into supersensible possession.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-05","MC_BA94_L1_01:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-05","text":"The resulting world is a lawful but empirically open interior bordered by an unknowable remainder generated by the system’s own success.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-04","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from synthetic a priori demand through formal givenness, synthesis, apperception, categories, schematization, principles, and critical restriction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the affection–form relation, the exact mechanism of mediation, and the proof of categorical completeness.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B02","MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent only if receptive givenness, synthesis, apperception, categorical determination, judgment, and possible experience remain distinct but recursively coordinated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B04","MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the same conditions explain both the generation of phenomenal objectivity and the restriction of cognitive jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive sequencing, concealed mediation, empirical openness, and terminal negative closure.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B06","text":"The facticity of finite cognition, the common ground of its divided faculties, and the limiting status of independent being remain indispensable elements resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B06","MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-02","MC_BA94_L1_01:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L2 01","context":"Chapters 01–48 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","original_title":"Ba94 L2 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–48 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"},"original_title":"Ba94 L2 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 02","order":2,"parents":["CL_BA94_05","CL_BA94_06","CL_BA94_07","CL_BA94_08"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_02@R001","sha256":"fad18b6695755a462095dde6ad76fea89f7e13498e39efc5685c2fce6e500e73","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-01","text":"Necessary objective cognition must be explained from within finite conditions without converting appearances into things in themselves or presupposing an unconditioned cognitive standpoint.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-01","CL_BA94_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-02","text":"A priori intuition supplies sensible form, while pure conception supplies object-directed unity; cognition arises only through their coordinated operation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-03","text":"Faculty cooperation depends on functional non-interchangeability, so cognitive unity must be generated through differentiation rather than reduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-03","CL_BA94_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-04","text":"The conditions that enable cognition also delimit it, making possible experience the field of objective validity rather than an obstacle imposed from outside.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-05","text":"The inquiry moves from source-indifferent formal logic to transcendental assessment of objective use and then to critique of the illusion produced when conditions become object-producing instruments.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:AS-B02","CL_BA94_07:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-06","text":"Objectivity becomes condition-relative without becoming arbitrary because validity within possible experience replaces correspondence with cognitively inaccessible being.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSD-01","CL_BA94_05:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-07","text":"Pure understanding supplies conceptual form and seeks systematic completeness, while experience activates and gives objects to forms it cannot originate.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:AS-03","CL_BA94_08:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-08","text":"The movement is recursive: abstraction secures necessity, insufficiency restores object-reference, illegitimate expansion generates illusion, and critique returns principles to bounded use.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AS-09","text":"Closure remains provisional because pure principles can be restored to legitimate use and pure conceptions can be systematically ordered, yet determinate mediation and concrete completeness remain unresolved.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:AS-B08","CL_BA94_06:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires both receptive sensible givenness and spontaneous conceptual determination; neither faculty constitutes cognition alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Space and time constitute the a priori field of appearance, so their objective validity extends only to objects under sensible conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-02","CL_BA94_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires pure conceptual conditions to be applied to an object supplied through intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Cognitive possibility and cognitive limitation are one structure because the conditions generating validity also determine its boundary at possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-09","CL_BA94_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Subjectivity is not private construction but the universally binding receptive and conceptual organization through which empirical objectivity becomes possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GA-10","CL_BA94_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Purity names differentiated operations: exclusion of sensation from pure intuition, abstraction from content in logic, and removal of empirical conditions from activated conceptions.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-10","CL_BA94_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Formal coherence is a necessary negative condition of cognition but cannot establish objective agreement by itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The canon–organon distinction governs all pure principles: they may regulate cognition already given but may not independently produce objects or material knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Transcendental status depends on the possibility, scope, and valid object-directed use of a representation rather than on a priori origin alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:AS-B03","CL_BA94_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Systematic totality must assign each pure conception its place and reciprocally validate the legitimacy and completeness of the conceptual field.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-05","CL_BA94_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Experience is neither the origin of pure forms nor dispensable: it supplies givenness, activates conceptual development, and conditions objective application.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-06","CL_BA94_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Critique preserves empirical objectivity and pure principles only by restricting their legitimacy, scope, and extension to authorized use.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-09","CL_BA94_05:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Human finitude is the absence of a self-sufficient cognitive faculty and of intellectual intuition capable of producing or immediately knowing its objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Pure conceptual completeness must be architectonically derivable, but its closure remains anticipatory until the governing principle and concrete inventory are exhibited.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GA-01","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"[Expansion] Space and time retain identity as a priori forms while expanding from conditions of appearance into sources of synthetic content and boundaries of judgment.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:RMD-01","CL_BA94_06:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"[Reversal] Subjectivity changes from a suspected source of illusion into the formal condition of empirical objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Purity shifts from isolating non-empirical sensible form, through exclusion of logical content, to active abstraction from empirically activated conceptions.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RMD-07","CL_BA94_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"[Repartition] Content changes from what formal logic excludes into the intuitive matter that must return for pure concepts to acquire objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:RMD-02","CL_BA94_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"[Displacement] Universality migrates from shared sensible constitution to formal logical reach and finally becomes a trigger for restriction to valid objective use.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RMD-06","CL_BA94_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"[Mutation] Autonomy migrates from a static property of understanding into its operation of generating, developing, purifying, and ordering conceptions.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:RMD-02","CL_BA94_08:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Experience changes from an excluded origin of a priori form into an indispensable occasion of conceptual activation and objective fulfillment.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:RMD-03","CL_BA94_08:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Classification ceases to be inventory-making and becomes derivational placement within a necessary systematic order.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:RMD-04","CL_BA94_08:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] Dialectic changes from an apparent technique of knowledge-production into the critique of hyperphysical extension and rule-misuse.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:RMD-04","CL_BA94_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] The thing in itself persists as a limit but is absorbed into the broader boundary separating possible experience from inadmissible objectivity independent of intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:RMD-09","CL_BA94_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Motion’s definition and empirical mechanics remain local; what scales upward is the dependency of empirical determinations on prior sensible forms.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:CGD-01","CL_BA94_05:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Geometry remains a local demonstration whose current-level function is to establish pure intuition as a source of necessity irreducible to concepts or induction.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:CGD-01","CL_BA94_06:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Detailed divisions of logic and of conceptions remain lower-layer specifications; their scalable function is disciplined functional differentiation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:CGD-04","CL_BA94_08:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding scales upward because it governs both the constitution of cognition and the limits of objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:CGD-08","CL_BA94_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Possible experience becomes the principal bridge joining sensible form, conceptual application, empirical objectivity, and critical restriction.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GA-08","CL_BA94_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Completion of the æsthetic becomes a catalytic transition because delimiting intuition generates the unresolved problem of conceptual determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:CGD-06","CL_BA94_06:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"The transition from inspecting given concepts to reconstructing their generative faculty produces the current-level architectonic mechanism of origin, purification, placement, and validation.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:CGD-04","CL_BA94_08:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"Sensibility as confused thought and logic as an unqualified source of truth become obsolete because each collapses distinctions required by the dual-source architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RMD-08","CL_BA94_07:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Spatial and temporal determinations cannot be transferred from appearances into intrinsic predicates of things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:GNX-02","CL_BA94_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Neither receptivity nor spontaneity may become a complete and independent source of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GNX-04","CL_BA94_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Synthetic or objective cognition cannot arise from concepts alone, and pure thought cannot produce the objective matter it requires.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GNX-02","CL_BA94_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Formal accordance with logical laws cannot constitute material truth or serve as an organon for extending knowledge of objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GNX-01","CL_BA94_07:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"A representation does not become transcendental through a priori origin without a valid account of scope and object-directed use.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GNX-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"Empirical aggregation and continued discovery cannot determine the completeness or endpoint of the pure conceptual field.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:GNX-01","CL_BA94_08:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Psychological descriptions of thinking cannot ground logical normativity because contingent mental occurrence cannot establish universal validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GNX-06","CL_BA94_06:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Finite cognition cannot admit an original intellectual intuition or a transparent unconditioned subject.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:GNX-08","CL_BA94_06:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the absence of any unconditioned or subject-independent standpoint from which forms, objects, and things in themselves could be compared.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSA-01","CL_BA94_05:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Direct realism must be false wherever it transfers the conditions of appearance into determinations of being as such.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:DBN-02","CL_BA94_05:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"Self-authorizing logic and rationalism must be false because conceptual analysis cannot generate either object-givenness or synthetic content.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:DBN-01","CL_BA94_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"The domain of pure understanding cannot be fundamentally unbounded or dependent on external material for its completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:BSA-02","CL_BA94_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"No universally positive criterion of material truth can emerge from formal or architectonic completeness alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:GNX-04","CL_BA94_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"The possibility of thinking an object cannot entail the possibility of knowing or encountering it.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:DBN-07","text":"Neither faculty independence nor mediation-free unity can be admitted; cognition requires unity through irreducibly differentiated operations.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:DBN-01","CL_BA94_06:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Original intellectual intuition is the constitutive absence through which receptive, derivative, and finite cognition becomes intelligible.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSA-02","CL_BA94_06:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"The system requires an outside of appearance but denies every direct correspondence through which that outside could become a positively known domain.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:BSA-02","CL_BA94_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"A universally available positive mark of material truth remains unavailable even when the formal conditions of cognition are complete.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:BSA-02","CL_BA94_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"An unassimilated remainder within pure understanding is architectonically inadmissible, although empirical reality cannot be absorbed into pure conceptual origin.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:BSA-01","CL_BA94_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"Mediation-free unity is unavailable, but every valid cognitive unity must be generated without erasing the difference between intuition and conception.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSA-06","text":"The thing in itself is structurally required as the limit of appearance while its relation to appearance remains unavailable for positive determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:DBN-03","CL_BA94_05:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Empirical realism is secured only when universally binding sensible forms are joined to conceptual determination rather than treated as sufficient by themselves.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-05","CL_BA94_05:XD-05","CL_BA94_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Understanding may autonomously supply conceptual form, but its concepts acquire objective validity only through receptive givenness and intuitive application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-11","CL_BA94_06:GA-01","CL_BA94_07:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_08:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Autonomy of conceptual generation cannot become autonomy of objective knowledge, because formal thought cannot extend cognition or produce objective matter by itself.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","CL_BA94_06:GA-11","CL_BA94_08:GA-02","CL_BA94_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Architectonic completeness within pure understanding remains subordinate to the experiential boundary governing objective validity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-14","CL_BA94_06:GA-09","CL_BA94_08:GA-07","CL_BA94_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Purification forms a distributed sequence in which sensible form is isolated, formal thought is emptied of content, and experience-activated conceptions are stripped of empirical conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-11","CL_BA94_06:RMD-07","CL_BA94_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-06","text":"The empirical has three non-originating functions: it confirms phenomenal reality, supplies objects for pure concepts, and activates the development of conceptual forms.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-11","CL_BA94_05:GA-06","CL_BA94_07:RLE-05","CL_BA94_08:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Whole-to-part validation is legitimate only as an architectonic canon for pure conceptual order; converted into an organon, it would falsely claim to authenticate objective truth.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","CL_BA94_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_08:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-08","text":"Each achieved closure reopens the architecture at a higher problem: completed sensibility demands mediation, bounded logic demands critique, and systematic totality demands concrete exhibition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-14","CL_BA94_06:AS-06","CL_BA94_06:XD-08","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Completeness of conceptual form cannot become a universal material truth-criterion because the abstraction securing universality removes the object-relative content truth requires.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","CL_BA94_07:AS-B04","CL_BA94_08:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:XD-10","text":"The noumenal outside cannot enter pure understanding as an unassimilated conceptual member; it functions only as the limit beyond possible intuition and experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_02:GA-10","CL_BA94_05:DBN-03","CL_BA94_08:BSA-01","CL_BA94_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes a staged achievement in which affection is sensibly formatted, conceptually determined, and validated through possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:HSI-01","CL_BA94_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"A priori necessity becomes modal legislation for possible experience and diagnostic evidence of cognitive constitution rather than disclosure of being without qualification.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSD-04","CL_BA94_05:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Finitude becomes productive because the absence of self-sufficient cognition generates the differentiated field in which synthesis, objectivity, and critique are possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSD-02","CL_BA94_06:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"The subject becomes a divided operator that receives, formats, conceptualizes, purifies, and orders without originally creating its objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSD-03","CL_BA94_08:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Error is stratified into contradiction, material discordance, and scope-violation produced by the misuse of otherwise valid principles.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:BSD-02","CL_BA94_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Transcendental method becomes a possibility-and-domain procedure combining formal testing, object-directed delimitation, and assessment of sensible applicability.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:BSD-05","CL_BA94_07:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"The architecture is formally closed but empirically open: its pure sources admit no external supplement, yet empirical givenness continually supplies application and activation.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:XD-06","CL_BA94_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"Its temporality is recursive and anticipatory because a presupposed totality guides exposition while later articulation retrospectively validates that totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:BSD-03","CL_BA94_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned givenness generates empirical objectivity; attempted noumenal conversion generates illusion; delimitation returns cognition to conditioned givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Faculty separation enables cooperation, cooperation demonstrates mutual necessity, and mutual necessity reinforces functional separation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Purification excludes content to secure universality, but the resulting emptiness forces empirical givenness to return as the condition of objective application.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RLE-03","CL_BA94_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Formal law becomes an apparent organon, produces semblance, and is restored by dialectical critique to canon-status.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Empirical occasion activates conceptual germs, purification removes empirical conditions, and the purified product reconfirms understanding as their source.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"The demand for totality organizes the elements, while the organized elements return as evidence authenticating the totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:RLE-01","CL_BA94_08:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Closure repeatedly changes into transition: the æsthetic opens logic, analytic limitation opens dialectic, and architectonic completion opens the demand for exhibition.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-06","CL_BA94_06:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Bounded objectivity and systematic closure form competing attractors: the first restricts objective use, while the second seeks elimination of every internal conceptual remainder.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:RLE-06","CL_BA94_08:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible affection → spatial and temporal formatting → phenomenal presentation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-02","CL_BA94_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Conditioned givenness + conceptual spontaneity → determination of a presented manifold as an object.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-05","CL_BA94_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Synthetic cognition → necessary validity for sensible objects → restriction to possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Abstraction from object-related content → universal formal relations → negative testing without material truth.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Isolation of pure object-directed cognition → assessment of possible use and scope → bounded transcendental analytic.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B03","CL_BA94_07:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Detachment from object-giving conditions → conversion of canon into organon → dialectical illusion → critical restriction.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B05","CL_BA94_07:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Failure of accumulation → regression from conceptual products to their source → derivation of systematic division and completeness.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:FGS-B02","CL_BA94_08:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Completed system → whole-to-part legitimacy test → architectonic closure internally subordinated to possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:FGS-B06","CL_BA94_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"A priori sensible form → spatial-temporal presentation → phenomenon.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Phenomenal givenness → conceptual determination → empirical objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Faculty differentiation → independent purification → exposure of mutual insufficiency.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Formal abstraction → universal negative test → absence of a material truth-criterion.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B01","CL_BA94_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Reintroduction of object-reference → intuition-conditioned application → possible experience → bounded analytic.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Detachment from application → organon-illusion → dialectical critique → return to empirical judgment.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FGS-B05","CL_BA94_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B07","text":"Open-ended accumulation → faculty-analysis → governing unity → systematic derivation → reciprocal validation.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:FGS-B01","CL_BA94_08:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:AFM-B08","text":"Provisional closure → unresolved mediation and concrete completeness → recursive continuation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence with independently constituted objects into condition-relative validity that is nevertheless non-arbitrary.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MME-01","CL_BA94_06:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-02","text":"The a priori is reclassified from unrestricted metaphysical independence into the formal constitution and ordering of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Objects are reclassified as cognitively available only under conditions of intuition, conceptual determination, and possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:BSD-04","CL_BA94_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Logic is redistributed into formal consistency, transcendental validity, empirical object-reference, and dialectical diagnosis.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Experience becomes both a functional boundary of valid cognition and an interface through which pure forms receive objects and developmental activation.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:MME-02","CL_BA94_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Classification becomes derivation and legitimacy-testing, while completeness moves from the quantity of discovered material to the architecture determining possible elements.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:MME-03","CL_BA94_08:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-07","text":"The opposition between rigor and illusion collapses because valid forms generate sophistical appearance when detached from their conditions of use.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MME-08","text":"Time is redrawn both as the medium of finite appearance and as the recursive, anticipatory temporality of systematic exposition.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:HSI-03","CL_BA94_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] Change, motion, space, time, geometry, logic, pure conceptions, and their complete order are the explicit objects through which cognitive validity is tested.","refs":["CL_BA94_05:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_06:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Cognition is a dual-source circuit governed by sensible formatting, conceptual determination, the canon–organon distinction, and possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Inquiry shifts from assertions about independent objects and contingent collections toward conditions, scope, exclusions, and principled derivation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_08:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality for finite knowers is phenomenal, while time structures both finite self-appearance and the recursive anticipation of systematic totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] Successive acts of purification and repeated exclusions perform the system’s claim that validity arises through disciplined delimitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Cognition presupposes affection by objects whose constitution independently of sensibility remains unknown.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Faculty cooperation is indispensable, yet the determinate connection between conceptions and intuitions remains underdescribed.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:FL-04","CL_BA94_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Transcendental analytic is called a logic of truth even though no universal positive criterion of material truth is available.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Architectonic derivation uses totality to construct the conceptual table while using the completed table to validate totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Pure understanding is declared self-sufficient even though empirical occasions are required for the development of its conceptions.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:FL-06","text":"The forms are universal for human cognition, but their scope across all possible finite knowers remains undecidable.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Subjective form mutates from an apparent threat to reality into the condition distinguishing empirical objectivity from illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Purity mutates from exclusion of experience into differentiated operations of isolating form, excluding object-content, and removing empirical conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:MP-01","CL_BA94_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Content mutates from what pure logic excludes into the intuitive matter through which pure concepts acquire objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Experience mutates from a rejected origin into an activating and object-supplying condition that makes pure conceptual germs explicit.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Closure mutates from termination into transition, while completeness mutates from a demanded precondition into an effect of systematic connection.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MP-05","CL_BA94_08:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a gated machine in which a formally organized phenomenal field receives objects only through sensible entry and conceptual authorization.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:HSI-01","CL_BA94_05:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided gate and internal operator that receives appearances, thinks them, purifies conceptions, and orders their relations without creating their objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:HSI-02","CL_BA94_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is both the medium of finite self-appearance and the recursive rhythm through which anticipated totality is retroactively confirmed.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:HSI-03","CL_BA94_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in navigating boundaries while retaining necessary rules and refusing their expansion into unrestricted object-production.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:HSI-04","CL_BA94_07:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:HSI-05","text":"The total machine is self-sealing at the level of pure form but maintains an internal developmental interface through which empirical givenness enters without becoming origin.","refs":["CL_BA94_08:HSI-01","CL_BA94_08:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from sensible form and faculty duality through transcendental restriction to architectonic conceptual order.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the determinate mediation of intuition and conception, the positive object-information required by judgment, and the concrete proof of conceptual completeness.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_07:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if faculty distinction and cooperation remain coordinated and excluded empirical content is distinguished from required empirical givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where cognitive possibility generates its own limitation, logical scope is restricted by its own grounds, and totality recursively orders its elements.","refs":["CL_BA94_06:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_07:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when critical reversal remains dynamic and sequencing, recursive return, and the irreducible limit of the thing in itself are retained.","refs":["CL_BA94_07:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_08:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_05:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 02","context":"Chapters 13–24 · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3","original_title":"Ba94 L1 02","original_context":"Chapters 13–24 · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L2 02","order":2,"parents":["MC_BA94_L1_05","MC_BA94_L1_06","MC_BA94_L1_07","MC_BA94_L1_08"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L2_02@R001","sha256":"295159b0dcb0e25684fec0e59f63fd065506b0e22b793c839d403a4141fb02d6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B01","text":"Finite reason must secure objective cognition while preserving its demands for totality, practical orientation, and systematic unity without converting those demands into supersensible knowledge.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B02","text":"Rational authority survives through a differentiated constitution that assigns theoretical objectivity, regulative direction, practical legislation, and architectonic organization distinct conditions of validity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-02","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B03","text":"The differentiated faculty system becomes a transcendental-juridical architecture in which cognitive functions acquire authority only within their authorized domains.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B02","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B04","text":"Conditioned cognition generates a demand for completion; this demand produces projections, projections exceed their jurisdiction, and criticism returns them as restricted functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B08","MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B05","text":"Contradiction and proof failure become metacritical evidence that reason has constructed an invalid object rather than discovered incompatible properties of supersensible reality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B06","text":"Negation becomes productive by converting failed transcendent cognition into differentiated rules for empirical inquiry, regulative use, practical commitment, and systemic organization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-06","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B07","text":"The subject becomes a divided legislator, projector, accused party, and finite believer, while time becomes recursive correction directed toward unavailable completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B08","text":"Closure is asymmetrical and formal: speculative access closes, but empirical inquiry, practical agency, criticism, and architectonic construction remain constitutively unfinished.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires sensible presentation, lawful synthesis, and restriction to the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Cognitive functions and jurisdictions are irreducibly differentiated, and validity is destroyed when one function substitutes for another.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Every conditioned cognition activates reason’s unavoidable demand for complete determination, systematic unity, and an unconditioned ground.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned changes from a presumed object or terminal condition into a receding horizon, selective gate, regulative principle, practical concern, and architectonic destination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental illusion is the lawful misclassification by which inferential, rational, or systematic necessity is transferred into objective determination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B06","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the positive field of theoretical objectivity and the tribunal filtering every claim to objective reference.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Contradiction has critical force when it discloses the invalid completed object-model shared by apparently exhaustive alternatives.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Conceptual completeness, causal demand, logical compatibility, natural order, and systematic coherence cannot independently establish existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Negative criticism is a constitutive governance mechanism that secures cognition by allocating claims among distinct modes of validity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Limitation changes from deprivation into productive protection of objective cognition, empirical openness, practical commitment, and systematic order.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Speculative reason can establish neither the existence nor the nonexistence of supersensible objects, and practical authorization cannot reverse this theoretical suspension.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Rational autonomy consists in recursive self-legislation, self-criticism, and submission of every rational power to the conditions of its legitimate use.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Closure consists in bounded theoretical authority and formally governed inquiry rather than possession of empirical, cosmological, moral, or architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Rational validity forms a topology of constitutive cognition, regulative direction, practical necessity, architectonic legitimacy, and critical control rather than a single scalar order.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Possible experience remains the condition of theoretical objectivity while expanding into the central jurisdictional pole of the critical constitution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Differentiated faculties recur as differentiated jurisdictions, converting psychological architecture into a constitutional rule against functional substitution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"[Serial mutation] The unconditioned migrates from demanded completion through cosmological and theological objectification into a regulative, practical, and architectonic horizon.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"[Escalation] Categories first constitute experience, then generate dialectical totalization when absolutized, and finally return as constraints on regulated inquiry.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Totality changes from the intended object and endpoint of explanation into an open procedural rule preventing arbitrary empirical arrest.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"[Reversal] Contradiction changes from evidence of world-conflict into a diagnostic instrument exposing the object-construction shared by opposed claims.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"[Valence reversal] Nonpresentation disqualifies constitutive knowledge but simultaneously enables regulative, organizational, and practical functions that require no presented object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"[Mutation] Negation changes from denial of content into the institutional and procedural governance of positive cognitive production.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal transformation] Completion ceases to mean possession of total content and becomes completeness in the formal allocation and regulation of unfinished inquiry.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-06","MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-10","text":"[Subject mutation] Reason changes from a presumed sovereign projector into a divided architect that legislates, generates error, submits to judgment, and acts without total knowledge.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-11","text":"[Escalation] Critique and discipline expand from local correction into the recurrent tribunal governing theoretical claims, practical entitlement, history, and architectonic organization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Reflection-concepts, individual antinomy proofs, mathematical procedures, and detailed hypothesis tests remain local instruments rather than current-level governing principles.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"[Scales upward] Temporal synthesis and synthesis-sensitive regress scale into the general principle that objective experience is constituted successively while total synthesis remains unavailable.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] Possible experience, transcendental deduction, and authorized presentation scale into the universal criterion separating object-cognition from legitimate nonobjective orientation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] The transcendental ideal bridges complete determination, theological reification, proof dependency, and the regulative recovery of systematic unity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Discipline, canon, teleology, and criticism connect speculative restriction to practical legislation and architectonic reconstruction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] Objective attribution, antinomial contradiction, and repeated proof failure catalyze the transition from local refutation to procedural governance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded and recoded] Soul, world-totality, God, object-level victory, and merely problematical assent lose terminal authority and survive only as regulated problems, functions, or positions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Logical consistency, formal validity, and conceptual completeness do not establish objective possibility or existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"Categories and rational concepts cannot determine objects independently of sensible presentation and possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"The world-whole is not an experiential object, and no exterior standpoint can survey it as a completed totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"No completed phenomenal series, empirical first cause, highest member, or final datum can supply the unconditioned.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"Speculative failure authorizes neither theoretical affirmation nor theoretical denial of supersensible beings.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Regulative utility, practical necessity, and moral desirability cannot become evidence for the objective reality of their correlates.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-07","text":"Aggregation, systematic form, rational prestige, and methodological success cannot independently constitute science, truth, or legitimate transcendence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-08","text":"Neither dogmatic closure nor sceptical suspension can eliminate the recurrent rational mechanism that regenerates transcendental illusion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture cannot admit a homogeneous continuum of faculties or validity in which cognition, regulation, practical authority, and architectonic legitimacy differ only by degree.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-01","MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"Finite reason cannot possess intellectual intuition or occupy an exterior position from which the complete system of conditions is given at once.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"The logical form and necessity of explanation cannot be identified with the ontological structure or existence of its object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"No theoretically transparent subject can convert its indispensable synthesizing or legislative function into cognition of itself as a self-subsisting substance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-03","MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"No natural harmonism can guarantee the coincidence of virtue, happiness, freedom, nature, and completed moral order within empirical history.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-06","text":"No authority external to reason can validate criticism, yet reason’s self-validation cannot become an unconditioned sovereignty exempt from further criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"The deepest organizing absence is an intuition adequate to the unconditioned and capable of presenting the complete world and all its conditions at once.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an absent object capable of occupying the place of an all-sufficient ground without becoming another conditioned member.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"The absent empirical moral world and unavailable unity of nature and freedom structure practical hope and future-directed completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"The system lacks both an external tribunal and an actual philosopher-legislator capable of guaranteeing that criticism and architectonic unity possess final authority.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"Final completion must remain absent because regulative inquiry, practical approximation, and recursive criticism derive their force from completion’s nonpossession.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-04","MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-01","text":"Because objectivity depends on lawful synthesis rather than independent subsistence, withdrawing world-totality as an object preserves empirical reality while invalidating transcendent predication.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-02","text":"The experiential boundary is productive in both directions: it secures theoretical objectivity and reroutes rational excess into empirical openness, regulation, practical orientation, or architectonic work.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-09","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and theology become differentiated outputs of one reification mechanism that converts rational functions or projected totalities into independently existing objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-08","MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Withdrawal of objective presentation permits ideas to acquire regulative and practical efficacy, but this functional recovery cannot restore theoretical objecthood.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-11","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-14","MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-05","text":"Because illusion survives diagnosis, criticism must become a recurrent and publicly reproducible institution rather than a completed private correction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-12","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-13","MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-06","text":"Formal completeness and substantive noncompletion become compatible when jurisdictional organization closes while empirical content and systematic realization remain indefinitely open.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-13","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-14","MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-07","text":"Possible experience and moral law form distinct attractors within a topological validity system, preventing theoretical evidence and practical necessity from collapsing into one scale.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-11","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-14","MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-08","text":"Criticism’s claim to final jurisdiction remains unstable because reason must authorize its own restrictions while universal criticizability forbids an unrevisable tribunal.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-09","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Metaphysics becomes a constitutional topology of validity, jurisdiction, recurrent error, and regulated function rather than an inventory of supersensible beings.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes temporal and procedural because objects are constituted through lawful synthesis while totality remains an unpossessed task-horizon.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes an empirical node, transcendental condition, totalizing projector, rationally imputable agent, critic, and finite believer without unification into a knowable substance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes a recursive asymmetry joining successive empirical synthesis, indefinite regress, retrospective criticism, practical approximation, and prospective architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Rational agency becomes disciplined action under noncompletion rather than sovereignty over objects, causes, history, or the totality of conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysical success is redefined as differentiation, dependency audit, error-prevention, and coordination rather than transcendent acquisition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-07","text":"The architecture produces a political-institutional consequence: specialist authority and private belief lose sovereignty while criticism must become publicly reproducible.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-08","text":"Negative criticism becomes an institutional component of cognition because secure positive production requires recurrent exclusion of unauthorized transitions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"The objectivity loop moves from sensible material through synthesis and validity to experience, then returns every determination to a renewed jurisdictional test.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"The conditioning loop converts every attained condition into another conditioned term, thereby regenerating the demand for an unconditioned completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"The capture loop moves from demand through projection and reification to paralogism, antinomy, or theological proof.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"The diagnostic loop converts contradiction or proof collapse into exposure of the shared object-model and a phase change from deception to vigilance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"The boundary loop blocks constitutive transcendence and returns excluded demand as empirical continuation, regulative unity, practical belief, or architectonic ordering.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"The discipline loop makes reason detect effects generated by its own procedures, legislate restrictions, and resubmit those restrictions to criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-06","MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-07","text":"Practical postulation recursively contains itself because speculative limitation enables belief and belief reactivates that limitation against theological inflation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-08","text":"Architectonic accumulation reveals a governing idea, but historical disclosure simultaneously forces criticism to distinguish rational system from organized error.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-09","text":"Possible experience, moral law, and unavailable systematic completion form the coupled attractors that repeatedly redistribute theoretical, practical, and architectonic demands.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible presentation plus lawful synthesis plus authorized concepts generates objectively valid experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"Conditioned experience plus recursive inquiry generates the unavoidable demand for complete conditions and systematic unity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Rational demand plus logical projection plus absent presentation generates the illusory objectification of subject, world, and supreme ground.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B06","MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Antinomial conflict plus proof dependency exposes the shared reification by which procedural unity is converted into existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Withdrawal of the invalid object-model preserves empirical reality while suspending speculative affirmation and denial.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"Critical allocation redistributes surviving demands among constitutive cognition, regulation, practical legislation, differentiated assent, and architectonic placement.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B07","text":"Publicly reproducible criticism and architectonic integration reopen the machine because empirical inquiry, moral realization, and rational unity remain incomplete.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible givenness → necessary synthesis → objective experience → conditioned cognition → recursive regress.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B03","MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for the unconditioned → projected subject, world, and supreme ground → presumed total objects → dialectical illusion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and proof collapse → diagnosis of shared reification → withdrawal of constitutive totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AFM-B04","text":"Speculative restriction → empirical openness → regulative systematization → autonomous moral law → practical belief → architectonic allocation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:AFM-B05","text":"Restricted return → recurrent self-criticism → public reproduction → bounded authority → prospective rather than completed closure.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is separated from independent subsistence, allowing empirical realism to coexist with the deobjectification of world-totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-02","text":"Validity becomes topological rather than scalar, requiring claims to be located by object, presentation, function, scope, and mode of assent.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-03","text":"Contradiction becomes a method for testing whether the alleged object and question are valid before competing answers are assessed.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-06","MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-04","text":"Boundaries become enabling instruments that protect empirical explanation, practical agency, differentiated belief, and continuing criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified as the critical organization of unavoidable questions rather than cognition of transcendent objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-05","MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Universality divides into cognitive communicability, moral accessibility, and public methodological reproducibility instead of naming one evidential standard.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-07","text":"History becomes an internal audit of reason’s recurrent structures and a temporal medium connecting retrospective diagnosis to future moral-systematic construction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The system adjudicates objective synthesis, selfhood, world-totality, freedom, theological proofs, moral postulates, assent, method, and scientific organization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B01","MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Differentiated faculties and jurisdictions constitute objects, generate projected totalities, diagnose excess, and redistribute nonobjective demands.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B02","MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Intervention shifts from selecting doctrines to testing object-models, governing claim-production, policing functional conversion, and institutionalizing criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B03","MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality is a lawfully synthesized field of conditioned appearances, while time is successive, regressive, recursive, and prospectively oriented toward unavailable completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The system performs its thesis by producing rational projections, exposing their inflation, reclassifying their functions, and reopening the criticism it authorizes.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B05","MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-01","text":"Regulative principles lack constitutive object-status yet operate with axiom-like force, leaving their precise authority over inquiry underdetermined.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-02","text":"The passage from speculative failure to practical authority remains indispensable but does not itself establish the positive efficacy or exclusivity of practical postulates.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-03","text":"An intelligible ground and practical freedom must remain compatible with empirical lawfulness, but their positive status and mutual relation remain unresolved.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-04","text":"Criticism must function as reason’s governing tribunal even though universal criticizability denies it an unrevisable external or internal foundation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-05","text":"Possible experience validates transcendental principles while those principles constitute possible experience, producing a reflexive dependence that risks circular confirmation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-06","text":"Formal and architectonic completeness strain against the permanent empirical, regulative, moral, and historical incompleteness they are meant to organize.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-05","MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-01","text":"Possible experience mutates from the positive domain of objects into a juridical boundary and then the theoretical pole of a wider constitutional system.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-02","text":"The unconditioned mutates from originating cause and terminal ground into a receding horizon, selective gate, regulative idea, and practical concern.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from opposed world-predication into metacritical evidence against the validity of the alleged object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Negation mutates from denial of knowledge into active governance that secures the differentiated production of cognition, belief, and system.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-05","text":"The rational subject mutates from sovereign projector into finite architect, accused party, legislator, investigator, and believer.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-06","text":"Closure mutates from possession of substantive totality into formal self-limitation, open inquiry, and prospective moral-architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a stratified court-network of conditioned appearances whose regions possess distinct laws and whose unity depends on adjudicated coordination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite regressor and provisional legislator situated inside the system it judges, generating illusion while lacking sovereignty over its totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a recursive vector of successive synthesis, renewed conditioning, retrospective diagnosis, and future completion that can orient action but never become present possession.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-03","MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Struggle consists in navigating boundaries between dogmatic possession and sceptical suspension through provenance-testing, critical adjudication, practical commitment, and public correction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-04","MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from experiential objectivity through totalizing projection and dialectical collapse to regulative, practical, and architectonic redistribution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"The positive mechanisms of regulative force, practical postulation, intelligible grounding, transcendental deduction, and architectonic identification remain underdetermined.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B02","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if theoretical cognition, regulative direction, practical necessity, and architectonic legitimacy remain distinct but coordinated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B03","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where lawful object-constitution, dialectical diagnosis, proof-dependency analysis, and generative negation converge on one boundary logic.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if projection, conflict, restriction, functional return, and recursive criticism remain dynamically sequenced rather than reduced to static prohibitions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B05","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable remainder resistant to axiomatic reduction is finite reason’s authority to discipline itself and derive moral-teleological orientation without an external standpoint or complete positive demonstration.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B06","MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L2 02","context":"Chapters 49–94 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","original_title":"Ba94 L2 02","original_context":"Chapters 49–94 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Ba94 L2 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 03","order":3,"parents":["CL_BA94_09","CL_BA94_10","CL_BA94_11","CL_BA94_12"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_03@R001","sha256":"54e7bc48b54f9f1b9d5014354350a5bc4a237a3f65158caa7679b8137d18cf0c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B01","text":"The governing problem is how finite discursive understanding can possess pure concepts that legitimately determine objects despite lacking intellectual intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B02","text":"The logical table supplies a finite candidate inventory of categories, but inventory alone cannot establish their objective entitlement.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS8","CL_BA94_10:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B03","text":"Correspondence is reconstructed as the determination of conditions under which a sensible manifold can count as an object of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B04","text":"Because sensibility gives multiplicity without conjunction, synthesis becomes prior to analysis, judgment, categorical unity, and represented objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B03","CL_BA94_09:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B05","text":"The regress from conjunction reaches original apperception, whose synthetic unity jointly conditions self-ascription, judgment, and objective relation.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B05","CL_BA94_11:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B06","text":"The argument returns from apperception to logical form by treating judgment-functions as categories when they determine a sensibly given manifold.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:ABS-03","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B07","text":"Categorial validation and categorial restriction form one critical act: categories necessarily govern possible experience and possess no authorized transcendent use.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B08","CL_BA94_10:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B08","text":"The movement is spiral rather than linear, repeatedly displacing apparent foundations and then reintegrating formal unity with sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_12:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AS-B09","text":"Closure is conditional: the deduction validates the operation and limits of finite cognition without deriving why its forms have precisely their asserted inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CL_BA94_09:AS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Human cognition is discursive because sensibility supplies manifold content while understanding supplies its conceptual unification.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS3","CL_BA94_11:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Synthesis precedes analysis and every represented conjunction because multiplicity is not received as already connected.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RMD-08","CL_BA94_10:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Original apperception is the higher unity under which manifold representations can belong to one consciousness and refer to an object.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Subjective identity and objective unity are co-generated by the same necessary synthesis rather than founded independently.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B03","CL_BA94_11:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Judgment is both the use of concepts and the synthetic function through which manifold cognition acquires objective unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS4","CL_BA94_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] A logical function becomes a category when it operates as the rule for unifying a sensible manifold under apperception.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-09","CL_BA94_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity consists in universally necessary conditions of possible experience, not in empirical origin, repetition, or subjective compulsion.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-01","CL_BA94_10:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Necessity belongs to the lawful form of synthesis rather than to observed succession or the material content of sensations.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B05","CL_BA94_12:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Understanding constitutes objecthood without producing existence because it unifies material that must be given independently through sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B04","CL_BA94_12:ABS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The proof that categories are valid for possible experience simultaneously restricts their legitimate use to that domain.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-08","CL_BA94_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Apperceptive and categorial principles are supreme only for a finite intellect that thinks a manifold it does not intuitively generate.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The deduction establishes the necessary operation of the cognitive architecture without deriving the exact inventory or ultimate source of its forms.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CL_BA94_12:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Mediation organizes the whole architecture: manifold, synthesis, apperception, judgment, category, and object form a recursive dependency system.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-03","CL_BA94_12:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The exclusion of intellectual intuition remains the permanent boundary separating finite cognition from immediate self-grounding knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RMD-01","CL_BA94_11:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Function develops from arranging representations in judgment into the rule that gives necessary unity to sensible multiplicity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-03","CL_BA94_09:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] Unity moves from conceptual commonality through represented conjunction to apperception as the prior condition of both subject and object.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RMD-06","CL_BA94_10:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Judgment changes from a logical relation among cognitions into the operative synthesis that determines intuition under one consciousness.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:RMD-02","CL_BA94_12:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] Categories move from a formally derived inventory to constitutive rules whose validity depends upon their necessary operation in sensible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RMD-04","CL_BA94_12:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Reversal] Objectivity ceases to mean correspondence with a ready-made object and becomes the necessary synthesis through which objecthood is representable.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RMD-05","CL_BA94_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] The initially underdetermined higher qualitative unity is absorbed into the architecture of original apperception, although its ultimate ground remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B07","CL_BA94_12:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Functional recurrence] The “I think” retains formal identity while expanding from possible self-ascription into the condition of universal object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B01","CL_BA94_11:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Displacement] Necessity migrates from relations apparently found among events to the transcendental form under which events can be objectively connected.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSD-05","CL_BA94_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Empirical association first isolates the difference between subjective connection and objective unity, then recedes once constitutive synthesis is established.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:CGD-04","CL_BA94_12:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-11","text":"[Absorption] The judgment table retains its inventory function but becomes subordinate to apperceptive synthesis as the ground of objective applicability.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RMD-04","CL_BA94_10:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RMD-12","text":"[Terminal transformation] Human finitude changes from an initial lack of intuition into the terminal condition defining both categorial validity and its limits.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:RMD-05","CL_BA94_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"Detailed distinctions among singular, infinite, hypothetical, disjunctive, and modal judgments remain local machinery securing the differentiation of the logical table.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Causality remains a diagnostic case, while its scalable function is to show that empirical succession cannot yield objective necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B05","CL_BA94_10:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Empirical association remains a lower-layer psychological mechanism, but its contingency becomes the cross-unit criterion distinguishing subjective connection from objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B06","CL_BA94_11:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"The logical table scales upward as a finite source inventory, but it does not by itself establish objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:CGD-04","CL_BA94_10:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between conceptual possession and juridical entitlement scales upward into the governing demand for a transcendental deduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RMD-01","CL_BA94_10:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"Synthesis bridges all units because it connects manifold givenness, apperception, judgment, categorical determination, and objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B06","CL_BA94_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"The regress from categorical unity to original apperception is the principal transition catalyst from logical derivation to objective deduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"External correspondence, substantial selfhood, and analysis as an originating method become obsolete once objectivity and self-identity are reconstructed through synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RMD-05","CL_BA94_11:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-09","text":"The judgment-category identity becomes current-level closure only when logical function is reattached to sensibility through apperceptive synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:CGD-03","CL_BA94_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:CGD-10","text":"The underived number of forms is no longer a local omission but the terminal boundary separating transcendental validation from complete self-explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:CGD-06","CL_BA94_12:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Human understanding is neither sensuous intuition nor an intellect that immediately generates and knows its objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GNX-01","CL_BA94_11:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Sensible givenness cannot supply conjunction, objecthood, or the unity required for cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Empirical genealogy, repetition, habit, and subjective expectation cannot establish necessary objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GNX-01","CL_BA94_10:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Analysis cannot originate cognition because it presupposes material already unified through synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GNX-03","CL_BA94_10:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Logical form and categorical inventory cannot independently cross into determinate object-cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GNX-05","CL_BA94_09:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Empirical association cannot ground the necessity or universality that distinguishes objective unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:DBN-03","CL_BA94_11:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Understanding neither creates empirical existence nor generates the sensible manifold it organizes.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GNX-04","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Categories cannot authorize cognition beyond the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GNX-06","CL_BA94_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-09","text":"Neither a self-sufficient object nor a self-grounding subject can serve as the independent foundation of objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSA-04","CL_BA94_11:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:GNX-10","text":"The validation of finite cognitive forms cannot become a complete deduction of their number, origin, and ultimate coordination.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:BSA-02","CL_BA94_12:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which objects arrive as pre-unified sources of their own cognitive unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:DBN-01","CL_BA94_12:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"It requires the falsity of a substantial subject whose determinate identity precedes synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:DBN-01","CL_BA94_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"It rules out external correspondence between independently complete representations and objects as the primitive form of objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B02","CL_BA94_11:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"It cannot admit that categorical unity grounds itself, because every categorical act already presupposes synthesis and apperceptive unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:DBN-04","CL_BA94_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"It cannot admit that formal logic supplies determinate content, even though logical functions provide the categorial inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:DBN-03","CL_BA94_09:FL-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"It cannot convert conditions of finite cognition into unrestricted properties of things independently of those conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-14","CL_BA94_09:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-07","text":"It cannot explain cognition through either passive reception or sovereign conceptual production; both poles must remain incomplete and coordinated.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSA-04","CL_BA94_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:DBN-08","text":"It cannot derive the facticity of the cognitive inventory without exceeding the kind of validation the deduction itself provides.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06","CL_BA94_12:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is intellectual intuition, in which manifold, synthesis, self-consciousness, and object would coincide without mediation.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSA-01","CL_BA94_12:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"The architecture is organized around the absence of a standpoint from which objects can be known outside every condition of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSA-02","CL_BA94_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"No unconditioned foundation is available on either side of cognition: neither independently knowable being nor self-grounding understanding can terminate the regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSA-02","CL_BA94_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"The origin of the division between receptivity and spontaneity remains inaccessible even though that division generates the entire deduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B07","CL_BA94_12:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"The particularity of the logical functions, categories, and forms of intuition remains an irreducible absence inside the achieved proof of their necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSA-06","text":"Things independent of cognitive conditions remain an excluded terminus that limits constitutive claims without becoming an available object of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSA-05","CL_BA94_12:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-01","text":"The logical table can become a categorial source only when juridical deduction shows that its functions are necessary for synthesizing objects of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS8","CL_BA94_10:AS-B01","CL_BA94_10:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-02","text":"The initially unspecified unity prior to categorical conjunction is operationally reconstructed as original apperception.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:ABS-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Because one synthesis makes representations attributable to a subject and referable to an object, selfhood and objectivity are differentiated outputs of one operation.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:GA-01","CL_BA94_12:ABS-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Logical form gains empirical reach only by becoming a category that organizes sensible multiplicity without acquiring an empirical origin.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:GA-10","CL_BA94_12:BSD-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-05","text":"The necessary validity of categories and their prohibition beyond experience are generated by the same dependence on sensibly given manifold material.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B08","CL_BA94_12:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-06","text":"Categories cannot be the ultimate ground of objectivity because their operation presupposes synthesis under a unity that is not itself merely categorial.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:DBN-04","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-07","text":"Causal necessity becomes one specification of the more general principle that objective temporal order must be synthesized rather than extracted from succession.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B05","CL_BA94_11:HSI-03","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-08","text":"The subject is constitutive without being sovereign because its synthetic activity organizes givenness but does not originate the manifold or empirical existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSD-03","CL_BA94_12:HSI-02","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-09","text":"The deduction’s recursive dependence on possible experience is productive only if experience is treated as the structured outcome, not independent proof, of categorial synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:FL-01","CL_BA94_10:RLE-01","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:XD-10","text":"Successful validation intensifies rather than removes the explanatory gap between the necessity of cognitive forms and the underived fact of their exact inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-09","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes a lawful achievement of distributed synthesis rather than immediate access to an independently unified object.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSD-01","CL_BA94_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a non-substantial integrating function whose identity is constituted through the same synthesis that produces object-relation.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:BSD-01","CL_BA94_11:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"The empirical world becomes objectual only within a circuit joining receptive givenness, spontaneous synthesis, apperception, judgment, and category.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:HSI-01","CL_BA94_12:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"The a priori denotes structural dependence rather than temporal priority, because its conditions govern every empirical synthesis without occurring earlier as events.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:BSD-04","CL_BA94_11:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Logic acquires empirical reach without becoming empirical because its functions become operative rules for organizing sensible multiplicity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:BSD-04","CL_BA94_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"Critique is simultaneously productive and restrictive because the conditions generating objectivity also establish the boundary of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSD-02","CL_BA94_10:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"Human finitude is generative rather than merely privative: the separation of receptivity and spontaneity necessitates the synthesis whose structure critique can articulate.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:BSD-03","CL_BA94_12:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"Ontological predication is subordinated to an account of cognizability, preventing formal conditions of experience from becoming unrestricted determinations of being.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:DBN-06","CL_BA94_09:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"The primary loop runs from representations to concepts, judgments, logical functions, categories, synthesis, and back to cognizable objects represented through those functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-01","CL_BA94_09:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"A manifold requires synthesis; synthesis requires apperceptive unity; apperception makes both subjective attribution and objective relation possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:RLE-01","CL_BA94_11:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Possible experience authorizes categories only because categories participate in constituting the objecthood that makes experience possible.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RLE-01","CL_BA94_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"The regress from conjunction to categorical unity and then to higher unity terminates provisionally in apperception rather than in a self-grounding category.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"The architecture spirals between abstraction from empirical content and reintegration with sensible material, gaining validity only by preserving both movements.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:RLE-05","CL_BA94_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"Negation escalates from excluding intellectual intuition and empirical necessity to policing transcendent use and unrestricted ontology.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-06","CL_BA94_09:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"Synthesis is the operative attractor, apperception the unifying attractor, and finitude the terminal attractor of the entire system.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:RLE-04","CL_BA94_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-08","text":"Repeated returns to unity mark phase changes from conceptual commonality to conjunction, self-consciousness, objectivity, and critical limitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-05","CL_BA94_10:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:RLE-09","text":"Primitive closure reopens as bounded generativity: a finite category inventory can organize indefinitely variable experience without multiplying its basic forms.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-08","CL_BA94_09:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Finite discursive cognition → separation of receptive manifold and conceptual spontaneity → requirement for mediation.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS3","CL_BA94_11:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible multiplicity without conjunction → spontaneous synthesis → represented connection.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B03","CL_BA94_10:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Represented connection → regress beyond categorical unity → original synthetical apperception.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Apperceptive unity → possible self-ascription and necessary manifold unification → co-generation of subjectivity and objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B01","CL_BA94_11:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Apperceptive synthesis expressed in judgment → logical function applied to intuition → category.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:ABS-03","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Category-mediated synthesis → unified empirical consciousness → represented object of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:DBN-02","CL_BA94_12:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Necessary object-constitution → juridical validity of categories → simultaneous restriction to possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-01","CL_BA94_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Critical closure → finite validity without transcendent extension → residual underivation of the cognitive inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:BSD-05","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Finite understanding → absence of intellectual intuition → need for a mediated inventory of pure concepts.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS1","CL_BA94_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Logical table → candidate categories → juridical demand for objective entitlement.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:AS8","CL_BA94_10:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Sensible manifold → absence of given conjunction → spontaneous synthesis → regress to prior unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B03","CL_BA94_10:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Prior unity → original apperception → judgmental synthesis → categorial determination of intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:ABS-02","CL_BA94_12:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Categorial determination → unified empirical consciousness → represented object → possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:RLE-02","CL_BA94_12:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:AFM-B06","text":"Possible experience → objective validity → internal restriction → finite critical closure with an underived inventory.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B08","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Correspondence is replaced by constitutive mediation: cognition concerns the conditions under which objects become representable rather than a comparison with ready-made unities.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:AS-B02","CL_BA94_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-02","text":"The subject-object opposition is redrawn as a differentiation internal to one synthetic architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B05","CL_BA94_11:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-03","text":"The opposition between logic and experience is recoded as a dependency in which logical functions gain validity only through sensible application.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:CGD-07","CL_BA94_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Self-consciousness is reclassified from an inner datum into the formal unity achieved through manifold synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:AS-B03","CL_BA94_11:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Necessity is relocated from empirical content and observed sequence to the rule-governed form of objective connection.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-04","CL_BA94_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Cognitive limits become internal to object-constitution rather than externally imposed prohibitions.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:MME-05","CL_BA94_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Human finitude becomes a positive structural principle defining the scope, operation, and intelligibility of categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:RMD-05","CL_BA94_12:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MME-08","text":"Transcendental method becomes a recursive regress from empirical objectivity to enabling synthesis followed by a return to the field whose validity is being explained.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit problem concerns the inventory, entitlement, application, and experiential limits of categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-01","CL_BA94_09:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Receptivity, synthesis, apperception, judgment, and category form a distributed mechanism converting multiplicity into objective cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_12:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system replaces empirical explanation and inherited taxonomy with a regress to enabling conditions followed by regulated inclusion and exclusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_09:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Objects are phenomenal unities of synthesis, while objective succession requires non-empirical lawfulness that time alone cannot provide.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:BSD-05","CL_BA94_11:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The deduction performs its thesis by abstracting logical form, regressing to apperception, and reintegrating that unity with sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Categorical completeness remains dependent on a judgment table whose own completeness is asserted rather than independently demonstrated.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:FL-01","CL_BA94_09:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-02","text":"The identity between judgmental unity and the unity imposed upon intuition bears decisive derivational weight without receiving a fully independent proof.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:FL-02","CL_BA94_12:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Categories originate independently of sensibility yet obtain objective validity only through sensible application.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:GA-07","CL_BA94_12:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Possible experience is both the product of categorial synthesis and the field through which categorial validity is established.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:RLE-01","CL_BA94_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-05","text":"Apperception is presented as analytically identical while functioning as the condition of an irreducibly synthetic operation.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:GA-02","CL_BA94_11:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-06","text":"The deduction requires subjective synthesis to secure objective validity, but the transition between subjective activity and universal necessity remains compressed.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:FL-02","CL_BA94_12:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:FL-07","text":"The object is denied pre-given unity while the manifold on which synthesis operates remains independently supplied and insufficiently explained.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:RMD-04","CL_BA94_12:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Function mutates from representational ordering into the necessary rule of object-constituting synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RMD-02","CL_BA94_12:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Objectivity mutates from correspondence into invariant validity produced through necessary unification.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:GA-02","CL_BA94_11:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Unity mutates from a represented category into the prior apperceptive condition enabling categories themselves.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:MP-02","CL_BA94_12:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Judgment mutates from classificatory logical form into the synthetic operation determining intuitive manifold.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:ABS-01","CL_BA94_12:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-05","text":"The subject mutates from a presumed bearer of representations into the formally identical function generated through their synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:DBN-01","CL_BA94_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MP-06","text":"Closure mutates from finite taxonomic completeness into conditional validity bounded by experience and unresolved cognitive facticity.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:RLE-08","CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a recursive relay that receives multiplicity, synthesizes connection, routes it through apperceptive unity, and returns an object-world.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:HSI-01","CL_BA94_12:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an integrating checkpoint rather than a substantial container, passive spectator, or sovereign creator.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:HSI-02","CL_BA94_12:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Time is a receptive field of succession whose contents become objective only through non-empirical rules of synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_11:HSI-03","CL_BA94_12:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Categories form a finite grid through which manifold givenness becomes navigable as an ordered field of possible objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:HSI-04","CL_BA94_12:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Critical navigation proceeds between passive empiricism and sovereign rationalism by preserving the asymmetry and necessary coordination of givenness and unification.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:MME-03","CL_BA94_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the spiral from logical inventory through synthesis and apperception to categorial validity and critical restriction.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:CGD-05","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where one apperceptive synthesis explains self-consciousness, objective judgment, intuitive unity, and categorial applicability.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_11:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined at the completeness of the logical table, the identity of logical and intuitive unity, and the exact inventory of finite forms.","refs":["CL_BA94_09:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if receptive givenness, synthesis, apperception, categorical unity, and objecthood remain non-identical but recursively coordinated.","refs":["CL_BA94_10:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the regress from objectivity to apperception and the return through judgment and categories to experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_12:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_03:MRT-B06","text":"The irreducible remainder is the facticity of finite cognition: the deduction validates its necessary operation but cannot derive why this particular architecture exists.","refs":["CL_BA94_12:FGS-B06","CL_BA94_12:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 03","context":"Chapters 25–36 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17","original_title":"Ba94 L1 03","original_context":"Chapters 25–36 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 04","order":4,"parents":["CL_BA94_13","CL_BA94_14","CL_BA94_15","CL_BA94_16"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_04@R001","sha256":"8d380db97399a6b47879f58c3a1e94562b50aba85a4033a3cad15c94b70b9bb3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-01","text":"The system must secure the objective necessity of synthetic a priori cognition without deriving it from experience or extending it into cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-01","CL_BA94_16:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-02","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as lawful determination within possible experience rather than correspondence established by pure thought alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CL_BA94_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-03","text":"Sensible givenness and conceptual unity become cognitively effective only through a mediating architecture joining receptivity, synthesis, and apperception.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-04","CL_BA94_16:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-04","text":"Judgment and temporal schematization transform categories from formally available concepts into rules applicable to appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:AS-05","CL_BA94_15:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-05","text":"Schematized categories generate a system of principles that determines the universal form and quantitative organization of possible experience without anticipating empirical content.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-05","CL_BA94_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-06","text":"The conditions that constitute phenomenal objectivity also restrict valid cognition to phenomena, making constitution and limitation two functions of one mechanism.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-06","CL_BA94_16:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-07","text":"The movement is recursively spiral: categories demand mediation, mediation produces experience, experience validates categorical employment, and that validation reinstates experiential restriction.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-07","CL_BA94_16:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AS-08","text":"Closure remains critical and finite: empirical objectivity is secured, empirical determination remains open, and the supersensible survives only as a negative boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-08","CL_BA94_16:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires the conjunction of sensible givenness and conceptual determination; neither receptivity nor thought has independent cognitive sufficiency.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-01","CL_BA94_16:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objectivity consists in necessary rule-governed synthesis through which appearances become determinately cognizable objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-06","CL_BA94_16:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the ground of objective validity, the field of legitimate application, and the limit of finite cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-09","CL_BA94_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Categories change from empty logical forms into objectively determining rules only through sensible and temporal specification.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-02","CL_BA94_15:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Productive imagination supplies the mediating synthesis whose function is subsequently inherited by schema and subsumption.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-03","CL_BA94_16:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] A priori necessity establishes objectivity only by expressing necessary conditions of possible experience, not by origin, formal consistency, or subjective compulsion alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-03","CL_BA94_16:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Systematic cognition depends on preserving the functional differences among sensibility, intuition, imagination, understanding, judgment, and reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-09","CL_BA94_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Validity is an application-relation completed through judgment, although no universally mechanical rule can eliminate judgment from subsumption.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-05","CL_BA94_14:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Truth is distributed across heterogeneous registers: logical admissibility, analytical recognition, transcendental validity, and synthetic object-determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-04","CL_BA94_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Pure understanding legislates the universal form of lawful nature but cannot anticipate particular empirical laws, qualities, or causes.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The finite subject is the functional source of synthetic unity while remaining sensibly and temporally non-coincident with its determining activity.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-09","CL_BA94_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Constitution and restriction are inseparable: the conditions producing experiential objects also prohibit their conversion into things as cognized independently of experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:XD-02","CL_BA94_16:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] The category table becomes a generative system whose schematized forms yield the principial architecture of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-05","CL_BA94_16:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Positive noumenal cognition collapses, while the negative noumenon persists as an indispensable but indeterminate limit-concept.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-12","CL_BA94_16:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"The category retains its identity as a pure rule of unity but changes from empty conceptual form into a schematized principle of experiential determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-01","CL_BA94_14:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Intuition persists as object-givenness, expands into the synthesized spatial-temporal manifold, and is absorbed into quantitative phenomenal determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-02","CL_BA94_16:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Possible experience expands from an epistemic restriction into the jurisdictional attractor coordinating validity, application, principles, and limitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:RMD-03","CL_BA94_16:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"Imagination first appears as explicit mediation and then recedes as schema inherits its application-function.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-03","CL_BA94_16:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Time shifts from a form of intuition and self-affection into the universal medium of schematization, while being excluded from the definition of logical contradiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-05","CL_BA94_15:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"Judgment reverses from an irreducible natural capacity into a restrictedly regulable transcendental operation without becoming algorithmic.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:RMD-04","CL_BA94_14:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-07","text":"A priori necessity reverses its apparent expansionary force and becomes authoritative by accepting the limits imposed by possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-04","CL_BA94_16:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-08","text":"Nature emerges from generalized perceptual synthesis as formally necessary lawfulness while remaining materially open to empirical determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-07","CL_BA94_13:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RMD-09","text":"Noumenon changes from a candidate supersensible object into a split concept whose positive sense disappears and whose negative sense remains structurally indispensable.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RMD-09","CL_BA94_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Particular temporal schemata remain lower-layer application machinery; the current-level proposition is that every objective categorial use requires sensible specification.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:CGD-01","CL_BA94_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Pedagogical examples and exercises remain local illustrations of judgment’s irreducibility and do not ground transcendental validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:CGD-02","CL_BA94_14:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"Noncontradiction remains a local formal gate, while the distinction between logical admissibility and synthetic objectivity scales upward into a differentiated theory of truth.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:AS-B05","CL_BA94_15:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"Extensive and intensive quantity remain regional determinations of appearances, but they demonstrate globally that a priori form can govern phenomena without determining things in themselves.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:AS-B06","CL_BA94_16:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"Faculty distinctions scale upward from local taxonomy into a jurisdictional architecture assigning different validity-functions to receptivity, understanding, judgment, and reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:CGD-02","CL_BA94_14:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-06","text":"Imagination, judgment, and schematism bridge the lower-layer units by successively specifying how pure concepts acquire sensible application.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-04","CL_BA94_14:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-07","text":"Positive ontology is superseded by an analytic of understanding’s conditions, powers, and limits.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:CGD-06","CL_BA94_16:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:CGD-08","text":"Categorical completeness becomes a current-level systemic dependency, although its proof remains presupposed rather than reconstructed.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:CGD-08","CL_BA94_16:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"Thought is not cognition because concepts cannot generate the givenness of their objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GNX-01","CL_BA94_16:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Sensibility is not self-sufficient because an unsynthesized manifold does not yet constitute an objective perception.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GNX-03","CL_BA94_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Empirical derivation and subjective preformation cannot account for the objective necessity of categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GNX-01","CL_BA94_14:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Formal consistency is not a sufficient criterion of truth, objective possibility, or synthetic grounding.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GNX-02","CL_BA94_16:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"No complete algorithm of application can replace judgment, because every application-rule reproduces the problem of subsumption.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:DBN-03","CL_BA94_14:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"Categories cannot determine objects beyond sensible conditions, regardless of their a priori origin or logical persistence.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GNX-01","CL_BA94_16:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-07","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot form two symmetrical domains of positive cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GNX-07","CL_BA94_16:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:GNX-08","text":"Universal formal lawfulness cannot yield particular empirical qualities, causes, laws, or existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:DBN-05","CL_BA94_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"The architecture cannot admit a fully constituted cognizable object-domain prior to sensible givenness and synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:DBN-01","CL_BA94_16:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"The source, formal possession, and valid application of a concept cannot be treated as equivalent.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:DBN-01","CL_BA94_16:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"No single faculty or formal principle can absorb the heterogeneous functions required for objective cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GNX-03","CL_BA94_15:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"No standpoint is available from which appearances and the same objects independently of sensibility could be compared.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:BSA-02","CL_BA94_15:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"The mediating operation cannot become wholly transparent without ceasing to perform the bridge-function the system requires from it.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:DBN-05","CL_BA94_15:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-06","text":"The system cannot admit positive transcendental ontology without collapsing the distinction between logical categories and valid object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GNX-06","CL_BA94_16:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:DBN-07","text":"Systematic completeness cannot survive the abandonment of categorical completeness, even though the latter is not independently secured at this scale.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:DBN-05","CL_BA94_16:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"Intellectual intuition is the unavailable possibility that would eliminate the heterogeneity of concept and givenness but is denied to finite cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:BSA-01","CL_BA94_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"The common ground of spontaneity and receptivity is structurally required but cannot itself become an object of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:BSA-03","CL_BA94_13:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"The system is organized around the absence of any external comparison standpoint capable of validating appearance against independently cognized intrinsic reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:BSA-02","CL_BA94_14:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"The noumenal remainder can be neither positively cognized nor eliminated, because either operation would destroy its limiting function.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:BSA-03","CL_BA94_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSA-05","text":"Unconditioned cognitive closure is unavailable: formal necessity cannot become complete possession of reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:BSA-01","CL_BA94_15:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Categorial validity requires both constitution of the phenomenal object through synthesis and controlled application of the category through judgment.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-05","CL_BA94_14:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-02","text":"Schematism regulates transcendental application without eliminating judgment’s irreducibility, so mediation directs rather than mechanizes subsumption.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:FL-01","CL_BA94_15:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-03","text":"The principial system converts formal natural lawfulness into determinate experiential possibility while leaving empirical specification irreducible to pure understanding.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GA-07","CL_BA94_16:AS-B07","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-04","text":"Logical admissibility and objective possibility form distinct thresholds because consistency can preserve a judgment without supplying intuition, schema, or object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-04","CL_BA94_16:MME-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-05","text":"The subject’s role as synthetic center does not yield intellectual self-possession, because its activity becomes cognizable only through receptive temporal appearance.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:CGD-04","CL_BA94_16:HSI-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-06","text":"Productive imagination becomes structurally more indispensable as it becomes textually less explicit, since schema inherits but does not independently generate its mediation.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:RMD-07","CL_BA94_16:RMD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-07","text":"Critical limitation is a positive organizational operation because it distributes cognitive authority and stabilizes the negative noumenon as a boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-10","CL_BA94_16:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:XD-08","text":"Phenomenal continuity can block inferences to simples or absolute void without licensing claims about the intrinsic constitution of all reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:DBN-04","CL_BA94_16:XD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Cognitive authority becomes conditional power: a priori principles acquire necessity precisely by accepting finite conditions of valid use.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:BSD-03","CL_BA94_16:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Constitution and limitation are one recursive event rather than successive independent operations.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:XD-02","CL_BA94_16:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"Phenomenal objectivity is subject-conditioned without being arbitrary because its conditions are universally necessary for possible experience rather than privately associative.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:XD-03","CL_BA94_16:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes both the medium of objective application and the form through which the subject fails to achieve immediate self-coincidence.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:BSD-05","CL_BA94_15:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"Metaphysics becomes jurisdictional analysis of object-reference rather than positive cognition of supersensible entities.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:BSD-03","CL_BA94_16:BSD-07","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-06","text":"Nature is closed with respect to universal experiential form but open with respect to its particular empirical determinations.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:BSD-03","CL_BA94_13:XD-05","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-07","text":"The capacity of thought to exceed intuition is not eliminated but converted into the recurring possibility of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:BSD-02","CL_BA94_16:XD-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:BSD-08","text":"The unknown remainder is generated internally by successful phenomenal constitution rather than imported as a second positive world.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:XD-08","CL_BA94_16:MME-06","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Empty category → demand for intuition → synthesis → experience → categorical validation → renewed restriction to experience forms the primary recursive loop.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RLE-01","CL_BA94_16:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Understanding requires sensibility for objects, while sensibility requires understanding for objective unity; their reciprocal insufficiency generates cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RLE-02","CL_BA94_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Rules produce the application problem, higher-order rules reproduce it, and schematized judgment interrupts but does not abolish the regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:RLE-01","CL_BA94_14:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"Mediation changes phase from imagination to temporal schema to systematic principles without losing its function as the bridge between heterogeneous representations.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RMD-01","CL_BA94_16:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience is the dominant attractor because objectivity, necessity, application, systematicity, and limitation all return to it.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:RLE-05","CL_BA94_16:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-06","text":"A priori independence produces the temptation of unrestricted application, whose failure recursively reinstates the experiential boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RLE-06","CL_BA94_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-07","text":"Noumenon operates as a limit-loop: abstraction opens a supersensible position, absent intellectual intuition empties it, and the empty position returns as a boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RLE-07","CL_BA94_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:RLE-08","text":"The category table generates principles, principles organize experience, and the requirement of object-reference returns to constrain the categories that generated them.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RLE-02","CL_BA94_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"Pure concept and sensible manifold begin as heterogeneous conditions incapable of independently determining an object.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:AS-B01","CL_BA94_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible givenness → spatial-temporal manifold → imaginative synthesis → apperceptive unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:FGS-B02","CL_BA94_13:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Apperceptive unity → lawful perception → possible experience → phenomenal objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-03","CL_BA94_16:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Judgment and temporal schema convert categorial form into rules for subsuming appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:AS-B02","CL_BA94_16:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Schematized categories → category-generated principles → universal formal determination of experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:AS-B03","CL_BA94_16:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Formal determination divides into mathematical construction of intuition and dynamical connection of phenomenal existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:GA-06","CL_BA94_16:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B07","text":"Universal experiential form preserves empirical inquiry because pure principles cannot supply determinate qualities, causes, existence, or particular laws.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:AS-B07","CL_BA94_13:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FGS-B08","text":"Attempted transcendental extension → loss of sensible application → collapse of positive ontology → negative noumenal closure.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:FGS-B08","CL_BA94_16:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Pure conceptual availability → distinction between thought and cognition → demand for object-givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:GNX-01","CL_BA94_14:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Object-givenness → sensible manifold → synthetic unity → phenomenal perception.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ABS-03","CL_BA94_13:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Phenomenal perception → possible experience → objective validity of categories.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:RMD-04","CL_BA94_14:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B04","text":"Categorical validity → application problem → judgment and temporal schematization.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:AS-05","CL_BA94_14:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B05","text":"Schematization → principial system → formal and quantitative organization of nature.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_16:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B06","text":"Principial organization → empirical underdetermination → continued inquiry within formal lawfulness.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:FGS-B07","CL_BA94_13:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:AFM-B07","text":"Secured empirical objectivity → test of jurisdiction → exclusion of transcendent employment → negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:AFM-B06","CL_BA94_16:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-01","text":"The opposition between subjective conditions and objective validity collapses within phenomena because universally necessary subjective synthesis constitutes phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:MME-02","CL_BA94_15:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-02","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence available to pure thought into jurisdictionally valid synthesis under conditions of givenness.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:MME-01","CL_BA94_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-03","text":"Possibility divides into logical noncontradiction and objective possibility grounded in intuition, schema, and possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:MME-05","CL_BA94_16:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Truth becomes a stratified field rather than a single criterion applicable equally to analytical and synthetic cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:BSD-04","CL_BA94_15:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-05","text":"A priori status is separated from unrestricted range, making critical restriction constitutive of authority rather than external to it.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RMD-07","CL_BA94_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-06","text":"Nature divides into invariant formal lawfulness and empirically discoverable material determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:BSD-03","CL_BA94_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-07","text":"Metaphysics is redrawn as analysis of application, jurisdiction, and limit rather than cognition of objects in general.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:MME-06","CL_BA94_16:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MME-08","text":"The two-world model is replaced by an asymmetry between a cognizable phenomenal field and an indeterminate limiting remainder.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:BSD-04","CL_BA94_16:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, categories, intuition, judgment, schemata, principles, quantity, nature, and noumenon are coordinated around the problem of valid object-reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_16:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, cognition is generated through the distributed cooperation of receptivity, imagination, apperception, understanding, and judgment.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique redistributes cognitive authority by distinguishing valid empirical employment from formal semblance and transcendental misuse.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_16:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality-for-cognition is phenomenal, rule-governed, continuous, and temporally mediated, while intrinsic reality remains indeterminate.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_16:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly constructs a valid cognitive mechanism and then derives its limits from that mechanism’s conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_16:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-01","text":"Judgment must remain irreducible to rules while transcendental schematism claims to regulate pure conceptual application.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:FL-01","CL_BA94_14:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-02","text":"Objective reality requires object-givenness, yet merely possible experience is treated as sufficient for validating a priori cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:FL-01","CL_BA94_14:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-03","text":"The unity attributed to formal intuition remains unstable against the claim that perceptual unity derives from synthesis not belonging to sensibility alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-04","text":"Time is treated as the universal medium of schematization even though external objectivity irreducibly requires space.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:GA-06","CL_BA94_16:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-05","text":"The systematic completeness of the principles depends on an exhaustive category table whose completeness is presupposed at this scale.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:CGD-08","CL_BA94_16:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-06","text":"Productive imagination is indispensable to mediation but becomes increasingly opaque as its function is transferred to schema and principle.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:DBN-05","CL_BA94_15:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:FL-07","text":"The thing in itself must be thought as the limit of appearance, although every determinate cognition of it is prohibited.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:FL-06","CL_BA94_13:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-01","text":"The category mutates from logical form into a temporal rule and then into a principle of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:MP-01","CL_BA94_15:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Objectivity mutates from apparent correspondence into lawful synthesizability and finally into a jurisdiction of valid application.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:MP-02","CL_BA94_16:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Necessity mutates from formal or subjective compulsion into determination by conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:GA-04","CL_BA94_16:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-04","text":"A priori independence reverses from an apparent warrant for expansion into the reason unrestricted employment must be constrained.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:RMD-07","CL_BA94_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-05","text":"Mediation mutates from imaginative synthesis into schematic subsumption while preserving its bridge-function.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:MP-01","CL_BA94_16:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-06","text":"Noumenon mutates from an ambiguous candidate object into a split concept whose positive sense collapses and whose negative sense survives.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:MP-04","CL_BA94_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MP-07","text":"Critique mutates from negative prohibition into the positive jurisdictional architecture distributing cognitive validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_14:MP-03","CL_BA94_14:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a gated synthesis engine through which only sensibly given manifolds can become objects under schematic and categorial unification.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:HSI-01","CL_BA94_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite switching-center between receptivity and spontaneity rather than an independent creator of its manifold.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:HSI-02","CL_BA94_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is the transformation medium through which synthesis becomes application, quantity, experience, and incomplete self-presence.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:HSI-03","CL_BA94_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"The phenomenal field is a continuous lawful interior bordered by an unknowable remainder rather than a self-contained totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:HSI-04","CL_BA94_16:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:HSI-05","text":"Legitimate navigation consists in routing claims through intuition, schema, synthesis, and experiential jurisdiction instead of escaping toward supersensible possession.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:HSI-05","CL_BA94_13:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the current-level movement from heterogeneous cognitive conditions through mediation and principial objectivity to critical limitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_16:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning particular schemata, the developed principles of relation and modality, and the proof of categorical completeness.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_16:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if logical possibility, objective possibility, transcendental meaning, empirical validity, and noumenal limitation remain distinct.","refs":["CL_BA94_16:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_15:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the same mediating conditions explain both the generation of experiential objectivity and the restriction of cognitive jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_15:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_16:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the system only by retaining its recursive sequencing, concealed mediation, empirical openness, and terminal negative remainder.","refs":["CL_BA94_13:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_16:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 04","context":"Chapters 37–48 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","original_title":"Ba94 L1 04","original_context":"Chapters 37–48 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 04","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 05","order":5,"parents":["CL_BA94_17","CL_BA94_18","CL_BA94_19","CL_BA94_20"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_05@R001","sha256":"d43c763174fbbc132d11daabb08a834ab4273d5aa3e5bc73d329d196c18edc18","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B01","text":"The system must secure objective cognition while adjudicating reason’s demand for totality without allowing either conceptual coherence or rational inference to become supersensible knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B01","CL_BA94_19:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B02","text":"Cognition is generated by a differentiated faculty architecture in which sensibility gives, understanding synthesizes, and reason systematizes without collapsing into either lower faculty.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B03","CL_BA94_18:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B03","text":"Necessary synthesis first converts perceptions into objects, after which rational recursion converts every conditioned cognition into a demand for further conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_18:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B04","text":"Possible experience operates simultaneously as the positive field of objective validity and the boundary against constitutive transcendence.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_18:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B05","text":"The unconditioned is generated as the necessary horizon of rational completion precisely where it remains unavailable as an experiential object.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B06","text":"Dialectical illusion arises when subjective or logical necessity is transferred into objective determination without the sensible conditions required for cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B07","text":"Critique preserves reason’s necessary excess by reallocating it from speculative ontology into regulative systematization, practical orientation, and reciprocal dogmatic restraint.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:GA-08","CL_BA94_20:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B08","text":"The movement is recursively critical: each positive construction generates a scope-limit, each limit exposes a new rational excess, and each excess returns cognition to critical regulation.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B09","CL_BA94_20:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AS-B09","text":"Closure remains nonterminal because critique can discipline assent but cannot eliminate the rational demand that regenerates transcendental illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:AS-B08","CL_BA94_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity requires sensible givenness, lawful synthesis, and restriction to the conditions under which an object can enter possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B02","CL_BA94_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility, understanding, and reason possess irreducible functions whose coordination generates cognition without licensing their mutual substitution.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B03","CL_BA94_18:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is both the sole positive domain of theoretical object-determination and the criterion limiting the objective use of principles.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_18:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Reason necessarily ascends from conditioned cognition toward an unconditioned completion that experience cannot supply.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned changes from a demanded terminus into a horizon, a triadic architecture of ideas, and finally a taxonomy of dialectical problems.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RMD-05","CL_BA94_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Logical form is simultaneously the grammar through which reason organizes totality and the vehicle through which rational functions are falsely projected into ontology.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-05","CL_BA94_20:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Subjective necessity, inferential inevitability, and rational demand never establish objective knowledge without corresponding conditions of intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GNX-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Validity differentiates into constitutive object-cognition, regulative systematic unity, and practical orientation without permitting speculative hypostatization.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:BSD-01","CL_BA94_18:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The experiential boundary changes from a prohibition against transcendence into a productive operator that secures objectivity and allocates reason’s nonconstitutive uses.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:XD-07","CL_BA94_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Subjectivity divides into an empirically determinable self dependent on external relations and a transcendental subject required for cognition but unavailable as a determinate object.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Critique is recurrent self-limitation rather than final correction because transcendental illusion survives disclosure of its invalidity.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-07","CL_BA94_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Time divides into a phenomenal order constructed through lawful relations and a rational temporality kept open by regress, approximation, and noncompletion.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-06","CL_BA94_19:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Failed speculative doctrine terminates not in an opposed ontology but in boundary-discipline, reciprocal restraint, and practically oriented commitment.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience and the unavailable unconditioned form coupled poles: one determines what cognition can possess, while the other perpetually organizes what reason must seek.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RLE-08","CL_BA94_20:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The distinction between sensibility and understanding persists, while reason is added as a non-object-determining faculty of systematic totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RMD-01","CL_BA94_18:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Possible experience retains its limiting function but expands from a domain of objectivity into a juridical and allocative criterion for reason’s uses.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RMD-06","CL_BA94_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] Reason changes from an organizer of rules into a totalizing engine whose lawful drive toward unity generates systematic overreach.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RMD-04","CL_BA94_20:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] The unconditioned moves from demanded completion through triadic differentiation to the absent attractor of dialectical regulation.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] The idea changes from taxonomic conception into practical archetype, regulative rule, dialectical premise, and finally disciplined orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:RMD-02","CL_BA94_20:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"[Reversal] Logical form changes from the architecture of rational systematization into the medium of its own ontological misuse.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-05","CL_BA94_20:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation] Critique changes from local diagnosis into permanent regulation once illusion is recognized as structurally recurrent.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RMD-07","CL_BA94_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"[Displacement] The empirical self dependent on external permanence is displaced at the dialectical level by an indispensable but indeterminate transcendental subject.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] Nonempirical status changes from apparent absence of objectivity into the enabling condition of regulative and practical efficacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:RMD-04","CL_BA94_19:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] The negative noumenon is absorbed into the general boundary of category-use, while the unconditioned assumes the distinct dynamic role of reason’s unattainable horizon.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RMD-08","CL_BA94_19:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RMD-11","text":"[Domain bifurcation] Time changes from an unavailable medium reconstructed through phenomenal relations into a recursive structure of rational regress and open approximation.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RMD-04","CL_BA94_18:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Reflection-concepts and the differentiated table of nothing remain diagnostic instruments; only the general provenance-test scales into the current architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:CGD-01","CL_BA94_17:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"[Scales upward] The temporal analogies scale from determinate principles of experience into the positive relational ground presupposed by every later rational totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:CGD-03","CL_BA94_17:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"[Bridge] The local account of sensory error scales into a general grammar in which subjective determination is projected as objective ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:CGD-02","CL_BA94_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] The derivation of ideas from syllogistic forms remains classificatory machinery, but it bridges rational totalization and the taxonomy of dialectical error.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:CGD-02","CL_BA94_20:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Reciprocal legal freedom remains a local example, while the priority of practical norms over empirical institutions becomes current-level architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:CGD-04","CL_BA94_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"[Underdetermined] Antinomy and ideal remain taxonomic branches because only paralogism receives detailed diagnostic execution.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:CGD-02","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] The attribution of objective reality catalyzes the transition from a general theory of rational ideas to the concrete diagnosis of self-objectification.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:CGD-06","CL_BA94_20:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] Rational psychology loses its status as positive science and survives only as a negative discipline restraining opposed dogmatisms.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:CGD-07","CL_BA94_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:CGD-09","text":"[Local] Introductory titles and sectional designations remain insufficient to establish doctrine, though their insufficiency reinforces the prohibition against deriving content from form alone.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:AS-B01","CL_BA94_18:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Logical thinkability, noncontradiction, and inferential validity do not establish objective possibility or existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GNX-01","CL_BA94_18:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"Categories and rational concepts cannot determine objects independently of intuition and the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GNX-04","CL_BA94_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"Subjective rational necessity cannot be converted into objective necessity in things.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GNX-03","CL_BA94_20:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Inner consciousness supplies neither a self-sufficient temporal ground nor knowledge of substantial personal persistence.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GNX-06","CL_BA94_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"The unconditioned cannot appear as an empirically presentable object within the field it is invoked to complete.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GNX-01","CL_BA94_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"Transcendental illusion is neither accidental nor eliminable through heightened attention or intelligence.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GNX-06","CL_BA94_20:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-07","text":"The failure of one speculative doctrine cannot establish its contrary or restore an exhaustive opposition between rival metaphysical systems.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GNX-06","CL_BA94_20:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-08","text":"Practical necessity cannot be converted into theoretical cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GNX-07","CL_BA94_20:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:GNX-09","text":"Empirical institutions and prevailing conduct cannot supply the measure by which their own normative legitimacy is judged.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:GNX-03","CL_BA94_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"The architecture cannot admit a homogeneous cognitive continuum in which sensibility, understanding, and reason differ only by degree.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:DBN-01","CL_BA94_19:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"No standpoint is available from which finite cognition can inspect things in themselves or possess the complete series of conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:DBN-03","CL_BA94_18:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes a theoretically transparent subject capable of converting its indispensable cognitive function into self-intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:DBN-01","CL_BA94_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"No critical achievement can guarantee immunity from the recurrence of transcendent projection.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:DBN-03","CL_BA94_20:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-05","text":"The architecture cannot reduce empirical objectivity, practical authority, and regulative legitimacy to one homogeneous form of validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:DBN-01","CL_BA94_19:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-06","text":"Conditions required for experience cannot be converted into determinations of supersensible being by abstraction from their sensible use.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:DBN-07","CL_BA94_20:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-07","text":"The rational demand for completion cannot be abolished without also abolishing reason’s system-producing function.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:GA-07","CL_BA94_20:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:DBN-08","text":"Neither empiricist exhaustion of validity by sensible presentation nor dogmatic possession of supersensible objects is admissible.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:DBN-02","CL_BA94_19:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the unavailable possibility of an intuition adequate to the unconditioned.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:BSA-01","CL_BA94_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"The nonappearance of time is the absence around which phenomenal succession, persistence, and coexistence must be synthetically reconstructed.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:BSA-02","CL_BA94_17:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"Reason’s demand for absolute completion lacks any adequate presentation through which completion could become objectively verified.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:BSA-01","CL_BA94_19:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"No self-grounding inward bearer is available to unify empirical selfhood and transcendental subjectivity into a knowable substance.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:BSA-05","CL_BA94_20:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-05","text":"The absent reconciliation between rational ideal and complete realization sustains practical approximation rather than terminating it.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:BSA-02","CL_BA94_19:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSA-06","text":"The system denies both final rational self-transparency and any position external to reason from which dialectic could be permanently terminated.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:BSA-04","CL_BA94_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-01","text":"The positive construction of empirical objectivity supplies the criterion by which reason’s ideas are denied constitutive object-use without being abolished.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-07","CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_19:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-02","text":"The boundary of possible experience is productive in both directions: it secures phenomenal cognition and reclassifies rational excess as regulative or practical.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-09","CL_BA94_17:XD-07","CL_BA94_18:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-03","text":"Once temporal synthesis constitutes one law-governed nature, reason can escalate that conditioned unity into the idea of a world-totality without thereby cognizing the totality as given.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-06","CL_BA94_17:GA-13","CL_BA94_20:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-04","text":"The provenance-test governing categories expands into a general diagnosis of dialectic: subjective rational grounds become illusory when projected as objective determinations.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-07","CL_BA94_17:RMD-03","CL_BA94_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-05","text":"Because empirical self-determination depends on external permanence while transcendental subjectivity cannot become an intuited object, no self-sufficient inward substance can unify both levels.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-10","CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-06","text":"Necessary synthesis and systematic unity have asymmetrical validity: the former constitutes experiential objects, while the latter regulates the ordering of already constituted cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_19:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-07","text":"Nonpresentation changes valence across the architecture: it disqualifies constitutive object-knowledge but enables ideas to function as practical and regulative orientations.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","CL_BA94_19:RMD-04","CL_BA94_20:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-08","text":"Since illusion survives its critical exposure, successful boundary-setting necessarily becomes a recurrent practice of monitoring rather than a completed correction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-11","CL_BA94_18:GA-07","CL_BA94_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-09","text":"Speculative limitation protects a practical domain from theoretical disproof but does not itself generate the positive warrant of practical efficacy.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-13","CL_BA94_20:BSD-06","CL_BA94_19:FL-02","CL_BA94_20:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:XD-10","text":"Noumenon and unconditioned converge as unavailable limits but remain functionally distinct: the former marks categorial restriction, whereas the latter drives rational totalization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_05:GA-14","CL_BA94_17:GA-12","CL_BA94_19:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"The system of reason becomes a topology of validity, limits, operations, and recurrent failures rather than an inventory of independently cognized metaphysical beings.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:BSD-01","CL_BA94_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Constitutive objectivity and regulative totality are asymmetrical products of cognition: synthesis determines objects, while reason organizes their cognition toward an unavailable completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_19:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes structurally divided between an empirical node within phenomenal relations and an indispensable condition that cannot know itself as substance.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:BSD-03","CL_BA94_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"Time acquires two nonidentical forms: objective phenomenal time reconstructed through relations and rational time recursively opened by regress, reform, and approximation.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:HSI-03","CL_BA94_18:BSD-03","CL_BA94_19:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from cognition of supersensible beings into an immanent diagnosis of how logical functions become hypostatized predicates.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MME-04","CL_BA94_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Critical limits operate positively by securing phenomenal objectivity, differentiating valid uses, and preventing rational orientation from hardening into ontology.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:BSD-06","CL_BA94_19:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-07","text":"Noncompletion becomes generative because unavailable totality drives continued systematization while unrealized ideals sustain continued practical approximation.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:XD-04","CL_BA94_19:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-08","text":"Critical agency becomes navigation among differentiated validity-conditions rather than possession of either a final system or an external standpoint.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:HSI-04","CL_BA94_18:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:BSD-09","text":"Practical judgment gains critical priority over empirical actuality because institutions are assessed by ideals they can approximate but never exhaust.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:DBN-03","CL_BA94_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"The objectivity loop moves from representation through provenance and synthesis to valid experience, then returns every successful determination to a renewed test of scope.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RLE-01","CL_BA94_17:AS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"The rational regress recodes each achieved condition as conditioned, thereby regenerating the demand for an unconditioned completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RLE-01","CL_BA94_20:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"The boundary loop blocks constitutive transcendence and returns reason’s excess as regulative systematization or practical orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RLE-05","CL_BA94_19:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"The illusion loop converts rational demand into objective projection, exposes the projection, disciplines assent, and then restarts because its generative conditions persist.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RLE-02","CL_BA94_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"The self-objectification loop attempts to derive subject-unity through categories that already presuppose that unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:RMD-07","CL_BA94_20:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"The practical approximation loop judges actuality by an unrealized ideal, produces reform, and renews the ideal’s authority through noncompletion.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:RLE-03","CL_BA94_19:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-07","text":"Triadic rational totalization branches into subject, world, and supreme being, while the subject-branch escalates substance into simplicity, personality, permanence, and immortality.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:RLE-02","CL_BA94_20:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-08","text":"Possible experience and the absent unconditioned form a coupled attractor-system: cognition repeatedly returns to the first while reason repeatedly exceeds it toward the second.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RLE-08","CL_BA94_20:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:RLE-09","text":"The idea becomes a machine when its movement from rational horizon to practical archetype, regulative rule, dialectical premise, and disciplinary problem begins generating further transformations.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:RMD-02","CL_BA94_20:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible givenness + schematized synthesis + apperceptive unity → objectively valid experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B02","CL_BA94_17:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"Permanence + causal succession + reciprocal coexistence → one temporally unified phenomenal nature.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:AS-B05","CL_BA94_17:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Conditioned cognition + recursive search for grounds → demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:AS-B04","CL_BA94_19:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Unconditioned demand + syllogistic distribution → ideas of subject, world, and supreme being.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:AS-02","CL_BA94_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Absence of corresponding intuition → failure of objective deduction → prohibition of constitutive transcendence.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:GA-02","CL_BA94_20:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Transfer of logical function into object-predicate → objective attribution → dialectical illusion and paralogistic ontology.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:AS-05","CL_BA94_20:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B07","text":"Critical adjudication → separation of constitutive, regulative, and practical validity → preservation of rational excess without hypostatization.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:XD-04","CL_BA94_19:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FGS-B08","text":"Persistent illusion + blocked speculative closure → recurrent vigilance, systematic approximation, practical orientation, and reciprocal restraint.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:XD-05","CL_BA94_19:XD-01","CL_BA94_20:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible givenness → lawful synthesis → unified nature → possible experience → rational regress → unconditioned horizon → triadic ideas → objective projection → dialectical illusion → critical allocation → recurrent vigilance.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-04","CL_BA94_17:GA-13","CL_BA94_18:GA-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Faculty-confusion → transfer of subjective or logical necessity → self-objectification → paralogistic ontology → intuition-test → speculative collapse → boundary-discipline and practical relocation.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:RMD-03","CL_BA94_20:AS-05","CL_BA94_20:FGS-B07","CL_BA94_20:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-01","text":"Logical validity and objective validity become distinct levels, preventing formal coherence from carrying automatic ontological commitment.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MME-01","CL_BA94_18:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-02","text":"Possible experience is remapped from a mere empirical limit into both the positive domain of objects and an allocative boundary for rational functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-03","CL_BA94_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-03","text":"The opposition between rational order and rational error collapses because reason’s lawful demand for unity also generates dialectical illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:MME-03","CL_BA94_20:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-04","text":"The opposition between empirical objecthood and fiction collapses because nonempirical ideas can possess regulative or practical validity without becoming presentable objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:MME-01","CL_BA94_20:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-05","text":"The subject is remapped from a self-grounding inward substance into an externally conditioned empirical node and a formally indispensable but indeterminate transcendental function.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MME-06","CL_BA94_20:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-06","text":"Time is remapped from an absolute container and terminal progression into phenomenal relational order and recursive rational noncompletion.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MME-03","CL_BA94_18:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-07","text":"Psychology, cosmology, and theology are reclassified from independent object-domains into differentiated outputs of one totalizing operation.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:MME-02","CL_BA94_20:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MME-08","text":"Critique is redefined from rival doctrine or eradication of error into navigational regulation, boundary-discipline, and controlled reassignment of rational excess.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:MME-06","CL_BA94_19:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system tests reflection-concepts, temporal analogies, modality, rational ideas, the metaphysical triad, and the soul-predicates of paralogism.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_20:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, sensible presentation and understanding constitute objects while reason recursively organizes conditioned cognition toward totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_18:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique assigns legitimate uses, exposes unauthorized transfers between validity-levels, and converts final correction into permanent vigilance.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_19:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a relational phenomenal field whose grounds cannot be possessed, while reason remains divided between backward regress and forward approximation.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_19:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly generates valid cognition, produces rational excess, restricts that excess, and preserves it in transformed use.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_19:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-01","text":"Reason must seek completion to organize cognition, yet every constitutive objectification of that completion violates the conditions of objective knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:FL-01","CL_BA94_20:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-02","text":"Logical form performs indispensable generative and taxonomic work even though the logical-transcendental analogy authorizing that work remains incompletely demonstrated.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:FL-01","CL_BA94_19:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-03","text":"The system requires a criterion distinguishing subjective rational maxims from objective principles, but the operational form of that criterion remains underspecified.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:FL-03","CL_BA94_20:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Practical ideas are assigned efficacy after speculative cognition fails, yet the positive mechanism warranting that efficacy remains external or underdetermined.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:FL-02","CL_BA94_20:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-05","text":"The positive theory of experience contains compressed transitions from temporal conditions to complete reciprocity and from required permanence to external matter.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:FL-03","CL_BA94_17:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-06","text":"The triadic architecture claims systematic reach while antinomy and ideal remain undeveloped relative to the detailed treatment of paralogism.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:CGD-02","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:FL-07","text":"Noumenon and unconditioned converge as unavailable limits, but identifying them would collapse a categorial boundary into a dynamically generated rational horizon.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-12","CL_BA94_20:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Reflection mutates from a local diagnostic of comparison-concepts into the universal provenance-test governing objective claims and dialectical projections.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MP-01","CL_BA94_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-02","text":"Possible experience mutates from the positive domain of objectivity into a juridical boundary and then an allocative operator for differentiated validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:RMD-06","CL_BA94_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-03","text":"The idea mutates from taxonomic designation into practical archetype, regulative concept, and systematic taxonomy of reason’s intrinsic errors.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:MP-01","CL_BA94_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-04","text":"The subject mutates from an externally conditioned empirical self into the indeterminate center of a paralogistic construction.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:GA-09","CL_BA94_20:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-05","text":"Critique mutates from corrective decomposition into permanent regulation and boundary-discipline.","refs":["CL_BA94_18:MP-03","CL_BA94_20:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-06","text":"Validity mutates from empirical objective reality into differentiated practical authority and regulative legitimacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:MP-02","CL_BA94_19:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MP-07","text":"Rational psychology mutates from positive doctrine into a boundary-discipline whose trans-experiential claims are relocated rather than theoretically vindicated.","refs":["CL_BA94_20:MP-03","CL_BA94_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a field of conditioned presentations organized into experience while recursively referred toward a final condition that cannot appear within it.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:HSI-01","CL_BA94_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"Cognition is a layered conversion machine whose objective channel produces experience and whose rational engine projects unavailable completion as apparent objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:HSI-01","CL_BA94_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"The subject is both a relational node within phenomenal nature and an indispensable but blind operational center incapable of self-intuition.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:HSI-02","CL_BA94_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Time is an absent phenomenal coordinate reconstructed through relations and a rational asymmetry between demanded grounds and open consequences.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:HSI-03","CL_BA94_19:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:HSI-05","text":"Navigation remains possible through provenance-testing, intuition-testing, boundary allocation, and approximation, while escape into positively cognized supersensible reality remains disallowed.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:HSI-05","CL_BA94_19:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from experiential objectivity through rational totalization to dialectical illusion, critical restriction, and transformed rational use.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"The positive mechanisms of practical efficacy and determinate regulative guidance remain underdetermined, as do the undeveloped operations of antinomy and ideal.","refs":["CL_BA94_19:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent if experiential objectivity and rational orientation remain differentiated while the same restriction explains both the impossibility of transcendence and its recurrent illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where independent analyses of objective synthesis, rational projection, and the determining-versus-determinable subject converge on one boundary logic.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if positive constitution, recursive excess, triadic differentiation, concrete self-objectification, and disciplinary reversal remain sequentially connected.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_05:MRT-B06","text":"Complete axiomatic closure remains unavailable because analogical overextensions, the logical-transcendental homology, practical warrant, and the relation between empirical and transcendental subjectivity resist final reduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_17:MRT-B06","CL_BA94_20:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 05","context":"Chapters 49–60 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","original_title":"Ba94 L1 05","original_context":"Chapters 49–60 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · Transcendental Dialectic. · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 05","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 06","order":6,"parents":["CL_BA94_21","CL_BA94_22","CL_BA94_23","CL_BA94_24"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_06@R001","sha256":"37e3ccf71c140c0fa8841841b1ad7d42b5ae2fd7b5ae5a8b78cdc403f68a527e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B01","text":"The system must reconcile reason’s necessary demand for unconditioned cosmological completion with the impossibility of cognizing the world-whole as a given object.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:AS-B01","CL_BA94_22:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B02","text":"Categorial synthesis becomes cosmological only where regressively ordered conditions can be totalized across composition, division, causality, and dependence.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-03","CL_BA94_21:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B03","text":"Antinomy arises when reason’s demand for completeness converts appearances and their successive conditions into a completed world existing in itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:AS-B05","CL_BA94_24:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B04","text":"Contradiction therefore diagnoses a misclassified scope and a shared invalid object-model rather than contradictory properties of the world.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-10","CL_BA94_23:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B05","text":"Possible experience becomes the criterion of speculative objectivity, while lawful experiential integration preserves empirical reality without independent subsistence.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B06","text":"Cosmological totality is displaced from a terminal object into a regulative rule directing the continued search for conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-03","CL_BA94_23:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B07","text":"Noncompletion is differentiated by modes of synthesis: homogeneous mathematical series prohibit an unconditioned member, while dynamical dependence may conditionally admit a nonempirical ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-03","CL_BA94_24:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B08","text":"The movement is recursive and spiral: category becomes idea, idea is objectified into antinomy, critique dissolves the objectification, and the demand returns as regulated inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:AS-B08","CL_BA94_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AS-B09","text":"Closure is critical rather than cosmological: reason preserves its regulative demand by renouncing total possession and widening empirical correction.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:AS-B08","CL_BA94_24:AS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every conditioned appearance necessarily activates reason’s demand to seek the unconditioned totality of its conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Speculative objectivity is authorized only where an object can be related to lawful possible experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Spatial-temporal objects are appearances whose empirical reality does not entail self-subsistent transcendental reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-02","CL_BA94_23:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Cosmological totality changes from a presumed terminal object into a regulative principle governing inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-03","CL_BA94_23:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Phenomenal conditions are available through successive synthesis, never as a complete regress already given.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-05","CL_BA94_23:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy is generated when recurrent rational totalization converts conditioned appearances into a completed cosmological object.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-07","CL_BA94_24:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Contradiction has critical force because it exposes the false object presupposed by opposed cosmological judgments.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:XD-06","CL_BA94_23:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The phenomenal world-whole cannot legitimately bear finite, infinite, simple, composite, free, necessitated, or absolutely dependent determinations.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-09","CL_BA94_24:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Empirical regress is necessarily nonterminal, but its form varies between indefinite extension and infinite divisibility according to the synthesis involved.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-04","CL_BA94_24:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Homogeneous mathematical series exclude an unconditioned member, whereas dynamical synthesis may admit a heterogeneous ground only if empirical lawfulness remains uninterrupted.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-06","CL_BA94_24:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned remains an unattainable terminus that orders the search for conditions without becoming its empirical endpoint.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-06","CL_BA94_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Critical self-limitation is reason’s positive completion because prohibition is converted into systematic empirical correction and extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-09","CL_BA94_24:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental illusion survives exposure, so critique must recur as vigilance rather than conclude in permanent emancipation from totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-12","CL_BA94_21:HSI-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-14","text":"[Persistent] The logical necessity of seeking conditions cannot legislate the ontological constitution of the world.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GA-07","CL_BA94_23:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"Categories retain their identity as forms of understanding but acquire a second career when reason converts their conditioned use into cosmological totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RMD-01","CL_BA94_21:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"Totality reverses from the intended endpoint of explanation into a regulative idea preventing arbitrary empirical arrest.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RMD-02","CL_BA94_23:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a supposed grounding terminus into an unattainable horizon and finally a selective gate for admissible explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RMD-01","CL_BA94_23:RMD-04","CL_BA94_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"The world is displaced from a completed object into an open field constituted through possible synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSD-02","CL_BA94_23:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Contradiction changes valence from pure reason’s endogenous self-conflict into diagnostic evidence of an invalid object-model.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RMD-08","CL_BA94_23:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Skepticism reverses from surrender or oscillation into a cathartic instrument against dogmatic question-formation.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RMD-07","CL_BA94_22:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-07","text":"Possible experience escalates from a restriction on claims into the criterion of objective reality and the dominant attractor of critical adjudication.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RMD-02","CL_BA94_22:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-08","text":"Infinity ceases to name one cosmological magnitude and splits into operational forms of continuation and inexhaustible determinability.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RMD-03","CL_BA94_24:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-09","text":"Transcendental idealism changes from a distinction between appearances and things in themselves into the active diagnostic mechanism dissolving antinomy.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:RMD-08","CL_BA94_23:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-10","text":"Architectonic closure reverses into critical closure, replacing metaphysical completion with a regulative method of self-limitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RLE-06","CL_BA94_24:AS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-11","text":"An intellectual condition shifts from an illicit substitute terminating empirical regress into a conditionally admissible nonmember of a dynamical series.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GNX-06","CL_BA94_24:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RMD-12","text":"Practical and architectonic interests disappear after explaining allegiance because they cannot survive as criteria of speculative validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"Syllogistic classification and taxonomies of partisan interest remain local diagnostic machinery rather than governing principles of the meta-cluster.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:CGD-01","CL_BA94_22:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"The detailed proofs of individual antinomies remain local demonstrations and compression points whose asymmetries do not determine the global architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:CGD-02","CL_BA94_21:CGD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"The four cosmological domains scale upward only as differentiated manifestations of one totalizing operator.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-04","CL_BA94_21:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"The serial-subordination criterion bridges categories to cosmological fields but is subsequently absorbed into the broader structure of conditioned regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:CGD-04","CL_BA94_21:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between appearances and things in themselves scales globally because it withdraws the completed object-domain presupposed by every antinomy.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:CGD-03","CL_BA94_24:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-06","text":"Finite and infinite remain local exemplary predicates, while their joint invalidity scales into a general critique of world-totality predication.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:CGD-05","CL_BA94_24:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-07","text":"The technical distinction between indefinite and infinite regress scales upward by replacing the finite-infinite binary with a synthesis-sensitive typology.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:CGD-04","CL_BA94_24:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-08","text":"The mathematical-dynamical distinction becomes a transition catalyst because it separates reciprocal rejection from qualified admissibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:CGD-05","CL_BA94_24:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-09","text":"The intelligible condition bridges critique to dynamical reconciliation but remains subordinate to empirical continuity and derivative admissibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:XD-05","CL_BA94_24:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:CGD-10","text":"Practical and architectonic interests become obsolete once inquiry transfers from motivation to objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RMD-07","CL_BA94_22:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"The world-whole is not an experiential object, and no standpoint outside possible experience may survey it.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GNX-05","CL_BA94_23:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"Rational necessity does not establish objective existence or authorize transition beyond experiential conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GNX-04","CL_BA94_22:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"The complete phenomenal condition-series cannot be presupposed as pregiven.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:DBN-04","CL_BA94_23:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Explanation cannot terminate arbitrarily in an absolute empirical boundary, first condition, or final datum.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:DBN-02","CL_BA94_24:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"Dogmatic selection and skeptical surrender are excluded as terminal responses to antinomial conflict.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GA-11","CL_BA94_21:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"Empirical idealism and transcendental realism are jointly rejected: objects remain empirically real without becoming independent spatial-temporal things.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GNX-01","CL_BA94_23:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-07","text":"Inexhaustible regress never establishes actual infinity in the object.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:DBN-03","CL_BA94_23:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-08","text":"An intelligible ground cannot function as a covert phenomenal member or competitive empirical cause.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GNX-05","CL_BA94_24:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-09","text":"Practical need and partisan interest cannot serve as logical criteria of speculative truth.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GNX-02","CL_BA94_22:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:GNX-10","text":"Formal opposition does not guarantee objective reference for either cosmological alternative.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:GNX-07","CL_BA94_22:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"No standpoint external to possible experience can occupy or survey the completed series of conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:DBN-01","CL_BA94_22:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"The logical form of explanation cannot be identified with the ontological structure of its empirical object.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:DBN-03","CL_BA94_23:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"No single model of totality can govern every mode through which conditions are given or synthesized.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:DBN-04","CL_BA94_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"Appearances cannot be compared with or converted into spatial-temporal things existing independently of sensibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:DBN-01","CL_BA94_24:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"No completed explanation or metaphysical inventory can furnish cosmological reason with a final endpoint.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:DBN-05","CL_BA94_24:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-06","text":"The boundary of experience can authorize neither transcendent speculation nor ontological denial of what cannot appear.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:DBN-04","CL_BA94_22:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-07","text":"Critique cannot permanently extinguish the rational generator of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:DBN-08","CL_BA94_21:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:DBN-08","text":"No self-transparent subject can occupy the transcendental exterior denied to cosmological cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSA-03","CL_BA94_23:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"The deepest organizing absence is an intuition in which the world-whole and all its conditions are given at once.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSA-01","CL_BA94_21:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"Reason’s demand for the unconditioned can never coincide with a completed sensuous presentation of totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:BSA-01","CL_BA94_24:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"No admissible exterior can become either a survey-position over the world or an exception interrupting empirical lawfulness.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:BSA-03","CL_BA94_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"The system is organized around an end that must regulate cognition while remaining unavailable to cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSA-02","CL_BA94_23:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-05","text":"No guaranteed correspondence joins reason’s necessary demand for completion to a final metaphysical object.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:BSA-02","CL_BA94_21:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-06","text":"Permanent repose and final metaphysical inventory are structurally excluded.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSA-06","CL_BA94_24:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSA-07","text":"The positive character of an intelligible dynamical condition remains the unnamed absence required by qualified reconciliation.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:FL-04","CL_BA94_24:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-01","text":"The four antinomies are necessary conflict-outputs of serial totalization, yet critique reclassifies them as tests of objective validity rather than descriptions of the world.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:XD-01","CL_BA94_22:MME-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-02","text":"Once the shared assumption of a self-subsistent completed world is withdrawn, thesis and antithesis can become jointly invalid rather than exhaustively contradictory.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:AS-B07","CL_BA94_23:AS-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-03","text":"Deobjectifying cosmological totality preserves rather than destroys empirical objectivity because objectivity depends on lawful synthesis, not independent subsistence.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:BSD-02","CL_BA94_23:MME-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-04","text":"Because illusion recurs while totality survives as a rule, critique must remain iterative rather than culminate in a final correction.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:XD-05","CL_BA94_23:AS-06","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Open-ended regress does not have one cosmological meaning; its legitimate form varies with the synthesis and givenness of conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:XD-04","CL_BA94_24:XD-01","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-06","text":"The prohibition on importing an intellectual terminus into empirical regress is compatible with a noncompetitive intelligible ground only after mathematical and dynamical synthesis are distinguished.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:XD-06","CL_BA94_24:XD-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-07","text":"Antinomial failure can indirectly confirm transcendental ideality, but this metacritical proof risks circularity because it relies on the framework it claims to confirm.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:XD-06","CL_BA94_23:FL-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-08","text":"Critical prohibition acquires positive force when methodological empiricism converts the impossibility of total possession into regulated expansion of experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:XD-06","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-09","text":"Practical interests can survive speculative limitation without authorizing cosmological knowledge, although their non-dogmatic relation to reason remains unsettled.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:GA-10","CL_BA94_22:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-10","text":"The original bifurcation between complete series and first condition is recoded by synthesis-type into mathematical reciprocal invalidity and dynamical conditional admissibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:AS-B04","CL_BA94_24:MME-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:XD-11","text":"Categories acquire a threefold career: they organize legitimate experience, generate dialectical illusion when totalized, and return as constraints on regulative inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSD-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"Cosmology becomes a metadiscipline governing the conduct and limits of explanation rather than a doctrine inventorying world-properties.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-02","CL_BA94_22:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes temporal and procedural because objects are lawfully synthesized while total synthesis remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-01","CL_BA94_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"The world becomes a task-structured field and horizon of regress rather than an object whose total content can be simultaneously possessed.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-07","CL_BA94_24:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"The rational subject becomes both generator and tribunal of illusion, but only as a finite regressor situated within experience.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:BSD-04","CL_BA94_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"Cosmological time becomes an asymmetrical open procedure: antecedent conditions are required, but their total past cannot be given as complete.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSD-04","CL_BA94_22:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-06","text":"Infinity is redistributed from an intrinsic world-property into differentiated permissions for continuation, division, and noncompletion.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-05","CL_BA94_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-07","text":"Contradiction becomes metacritical evidence that the alleged object has been falsely constructed.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-04","CL_BA94_22:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-08","text":"Critical authority shifts from metaphysical possession to the correction and extension of empirical cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-10","CL_BA94_24:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-09","text":"Supersensible grounding receives only derivative admissibility and cannot become a covert interruption of empirical explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:BSD-05","CL_BA94_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:BSD-10","text":"Agency becomes situated correction and navigation within experience rather than sovereignty over its totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:BSD-03","CL_BA94_24:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned appearance generates a demand for a condition, and each attained empirical condition regenerates the demand.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RLE-01","CL_BA94_23:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"The primary escalation converts category into idea, idea into paired demonstration, and demonstration into critical diagnosis.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RLE-02","CL_BA94_21:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"The principal capture loop moves from demand for conditions to reification of totality and antinomial breakdown.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:RLE-02","CL_BA94_24:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"The diagnostic loop begins when contradiction exposes a shared presupposition and converts opposition into joint invalidity.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:RLE-03","CL_BA94_23:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"Critical removal of the completed object-domain returns cosmology as regulative inquiry rather than abolishing its demand.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RLE-02","CL_BA94_23:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"The unconditioned is the dominant attractor because it directs inquiry while receding from every empirical attainment.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RLE-03","CL_BA94_24:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-07","text":"Possible experience and empirical lawfulness form a coupled attractor-gate that both licenses objectivity and filters admissible explanation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RLE-03","CL_BA94_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-08","text":"Cosmical composition forms a representation loop in which regress constitutes world-totality while preventing its pregiven completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RLE-03","CL_BA94_24:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-09","text":"Division forms a decomposition loop producing infinite determinability without an actually completed stock of parts.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RLE-04","CL_BA94_24:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-10","text":"The mathematical-dynamical classifier routes conflict toward reciprocal rejection or qualified reconciliation.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:RLE-06","CL_BA94_24:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:RLE-11","text":"Exposure produces a phase change from deception to vigilance, not escape from the totalizing impulse.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:RLE-08","CL_BA94_21:HSI-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance + categorial organization → selection of a regressively subordinated condition-series.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"Serial regress + rational demand for completeness → transcendental idea of absolute totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Idea misread as determinable object + appearances converted into things in themselves → antinomial opposition.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:FGS-B02","CL_BA94_24:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Unadjudicable conflict → reflexive transcendental critique of the object-presupposition generating both sides.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:AS-B06","CL_BA94_22:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"Withdrawal of the shared self-subsistent world-model → joint invalidity of opposed predicates + preservation of empirical reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:AS-05","CL_BA94_23:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B06","text":"Dissolved constitutive totality → cosmological idea recoded as a regulative principle.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B07","text":"Regulative problem + classification by form of synthesis → differentiated modes of noncompletion.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B08","text":"Empirical composition → indefinite extension; continuous division → regress in infinitum; neither → completed infinite aggregate.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:AS-B06","CL_BA94_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B09","text":"Homogeneous mathematical series → exclusion of an unconditioned member; heterogeneous dynamical series → conditional nonempirical ground preserving lawful regress.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:GA-06","CL_BA94_24:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FGS-B10","text":"Regulative continuation → systematic correction + renewed conditioning + no final cosmological closure.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:FGS-B08","CL_BA94_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned empirical synthesis → demand for unconditioned totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Serial-categorial selection → four cosmological problem-fields.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Objectification of totality → paired antinomial claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B04","text":"Unresolved conflict → diagnosis of how reason mistakes an idea for a valid object.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:AS-B03","CL_BA94_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B05","text":"Transcendental ideality → dissolution of the completed world-object + retention of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:AFM-B06","CL_BA94_23:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B06","text":"Synthesis-sensitive classification → indefinite regress, infinite division, or conditionally admissible nonempirical grounding.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:AFM-B05","CL_BA94_24:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:AFM-B07","text":"Regulative nonclosure → widened empirical correction + recurrent vigilance.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:AFM-B06","CL_BA94_21:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-01","text":"The world is reclassified from a total object into an open regressive order and horizon of possible synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MME-01","CL_BA94_24:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-02","text":"Objectivity is separated from independent subsistence, making empirical realism compatible with transcendental ideality.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MME-02","CL_BA94_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-03","text":"The legitimacy of a cosmological question must be assessed before its bivalent answers can be evaluated.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:BSD-01","CL_BA94_22:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Finite and infinite cease to be exhaustive world-properties and become indices of premature closure.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:MME-04","CL_BA94_23:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-05","text":"Infinity is differentiated into indefinite empirical extension, infinite divisibility, and prohibited actual completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:MME-03","CL_BA94_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-06","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from an obstacle into a method for exposing invalid object-models.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MME-05","CL_BA94_23:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-07","text":"The opposition between dogmatism and skepticism collapses into critical limitation that preserves inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MME-09","CL_BA94_23:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-08","text":"Categories become visible as structurally dual: stable within experience and dialectical under absolute totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:BSD-01","CL_BA94_21:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-09","text":"Explanation is protected simultaneously from metaphysical closure and covert supersensible interruption.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:MME-05","CL_BA94_24:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-10","text":"Philosophical solution is reclassified from possession of an answer-object into reflexive determination of the representation producing the question.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:MME-05","CL_BA94_22:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MME-11","text":"Transcendental ideality becomes a diagnostic method rather than merely a static doctrine about appearances.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:BSD-06","CL_BA94_23:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, disputes over world magnitude, material composition, causality, dependence, temporal beginning, and spatial boundary supply exemplary conflict-material.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_24:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, temporally successive synthesis interacts with reason’s demand for total conditions to produce regress and recurrent totalization.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_21:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, adjudication between doctrines is replaced by diagnosis of the shared premise and validity of the alleged object.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_23:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality consists of conditioned appearances synthesized successively without a knowable first, final, or total condition.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_23:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, paired demonstrations enact reason’s self-reversal and convert apparent defeat into critical method.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_24:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-01","text":"Reason requires a complete conception of conditioned reality while the required regulative end remains structurally unavailable.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:FL-01","CL_BA94_23:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-02","text":"The regulative principle lacks constitutive object-status yet exerts force comparable to an axiom, leaving its precise operation underdetermined.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:FL-03","CL_BA94_22:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-03","text":"Intellectual termination is prohibited in empirical regress, yet a positive intelligible condition is required for dynamical reconciliation.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:XD-06","CL_BA94_24:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-04","text":"Possible experience is the criterion of speculative objectivity, but phenomenal inaccessibility cannot become ontological denial or theoretical disproof of practical orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:FL-03","CL_BA94_22:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-05","text":"Procedural infinity must remain distinct from actual infinity, although the distinction between its forms is indispensable and unstable.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:FL-02","CL_BA94_24:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-06","text":"The system makes negative determinations concerning the world-whole while denying that the whole is an available object of determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_24:FL-01","CL_BA94_21:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-07","text":"The indirect confirmation of transcendental ideality risks circularity because the antinomial diagnosis presupposes the representational framework it confirms.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:FL-04","CL_BA94_23:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:FL-08","text":"Critical limitation must count as positive resolution even though it cannot permanently extinguish the illusion requiring resolution.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:DBN-08","CL_BA94_22:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-01","text":"A category mutates into a transcendental idea when conditioned synthesis is extended toward absolute completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MP-01","CL_BA94_21:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-02","text":"Totality mutates from a world-property into a procedural question and regulative direction that determines no object.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MP-02","CL_BA94_24:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-03","text":"Antinomy mutates from doctrinal deadlock into joint invalidity and then into a typed field with nonidentical resolutions.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MP-03","CL_BA94_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-04","text":"The unconditioned mutates from an alleged terminus into an unattainable horizon and selective gate.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:RMD-04","CL_BA94_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-05","text":"Infinity mutates from intrinsic magnitude into differentiated permissions and progressive determinability.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:RMD-06","CL_BA94_24:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-06","text":"Skepticism mutates from oscillation and surrender into cathartic criticism of invalid question-formation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RMD-04","CL_BA94_22:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-07","text":"Closure mutates from architectonic completion of the world into the positive completion of reason’s self-limitation.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:RLE-06","CL_BA94_22:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MP-08","text":"The intelligible condition mutates from an illicit empirical terminus into a conditionally admissible nonmember of dynamical synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:GNX-06","CL_BA94_24:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a lawful network of conditioned appearances, synthesis, and regress rather than a completed inventory of things.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:HSI-01","CL_BA94_23:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite regressor inside experience that generates illusion and then acts as its tribunal.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:HSI-02","CL_BA94_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is the asymmetrical medium of successive synthesis that requires antecedents while preventing their simultaneous total presence.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:HSI-03","CL_BA94_21:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"The unconditioned is a receding horizon and selective gate that orients movement without becoming an empirical destination.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:HSI-04","CL_BA94_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in converting compulsive reification into navigation that preserves empirical continuity.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:HSI-05","CL_BA94_24:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:HSI-06","text":"Escape from totalizing illusion is unavailable; the remaining agency is recurrent vigilance and situated correction.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:HSI-06","CL_BA94_24:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the derivation of cosmological demand, objectification, antinomy, critical dissolution, synthesis-sensitive differentiation, and regulative return.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_24:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"The exact mechanism of regulative use and the positive character of the intelligible dynamical condition remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_BA94_22:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_24:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if logical necessity, empirical availability, and ontological givenness remain non-equivalent.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_23:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest in the sequence from reification through antinomial diagnosis to regulative reconstruction differentiated by synthesis.","refs":["CL_BA94_23:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_24:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on preserving staged reversal, recursive return, and synthesis-specific sequencing rather than reducing critique to static prohibition.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_24:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B06","text":"Individual proof assumptions, procedural infinity, and the intelligible condition remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MRT-B06","CL_BA94_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_06:MRT-B07","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if neither dogmatic side is selected and empirical inquiry survives the dissolution of cosmological objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_21:MRT-B07","CL_BA94_23:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 06","context":"Chapters 61–72 · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","original_title":"Ba94 L1 06","original_context":"Chapters 61–72 · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 07","order":7,"parents":["CL_BA94_25","CL_BA94_26","CL_BA94_27","CL_BA94_28"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_07@R001","sha256":"2f3d9f70665bce6e957efca386e3384faf20ca5e8d267e693263e0bad2f8cc41","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-01","text":"The system asks how reason can preserve freedom, necessity, and systematic completion when finite cognition cannot present an unconditioned object.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:AS-01","CL_BA94_26:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-02","text":"Reason generates projected totalities from its demand for completion and then repeatedly mistakes those projections for independently existing objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:AS-B03","CL_BA94_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-03","text":"The central resolution restricts objective cognition to possible experience while preserving supersensible ideas as practical, regulative, and organizational functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-01","CL_BA94_28:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-04","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a constitutional critique that assigns jurisdictions, audits illicit transitions, and regulates the procedures generating transcendental claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AS-04","CL_BA94_28:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-05","text":"Its movement is recursive: conditioned experience generates demand, demand produces projection, projection exceeds its jurisdiction, critique intervenes, and the restricted projection returns as legitimate orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:AS-07","CL_BA94_28:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-06","text":"The critique of theology is a dependency reduction in which empirical and cosmological proofs converge upon an unsupported ontological transition.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AS-02","CL_BA94_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-07","text":"Speculative suspension is asymmetrical because it blocks theoretical assertion and denial while leaving distinct practical authorization conditionally available.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AS-08","CL_BA94_27:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AS-08","text":"Closure is formal rather than substantive: cognitive jurisdiction and method become complete while empirical inquiry remains indefinitely open.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:AS-07","CL_BA94_28:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective theoretical cognition requires sensible givenness within possible experience; thought beyond experience does not by itself produce an object.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-01","CL_BA94_26:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every empirically accessible object, event, and cause remains conditioned within an indefinitely extendable series.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-02","CL_BA94_27:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Reason necessarily demands complete determination, systematic unity, and an unconditioned ground that experience cannot present as complete.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-09","CL_BA94_28:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned changes from intelligible causality and extramundane ground into ideal totality, alleged necessary being, and finally regulative horizon.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:AS-05","CL_BA94_26:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Conceptual completeness, causal demand, logical compatibility, and natural order cannot independently establish existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:AS-B06","CL_BA94_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Speculative theology is generated by collapsing regress, conceptual selection, existential positing, and practical endorsement into one purported proof.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-06","CL_BA94_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Speculative reason establishes neither the existence nor the nonexistence of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GA-07","CL_BA94_27:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Transcendental ideas survive loss of constitutive objecthood by becoming regulative projections and architectonic principles of inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-03","CL_BA94_28:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Negative restriction is constitutive of secure cognition because it governs the procedures through which reason would otherwise systematize illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Rational autonomy terminates in recursive self-government rather than unrestricted speculative expansion.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GA-11","CL_BA94_28:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Practical reason may authorize adherence, imputation, or postulation where speculative cognition fails, but it cannot convert this authorization into objective proof.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-10","CL_BA94_27:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Withdrawal of objective presentation enables nonobjective ideas to acquire intensified organizational, critical, and practical functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-08","CL_BA94_28:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Empirical conditioning remains exceptionless, but its function expands from explaining events to exposing the illegitimacy of every empirical ascent toward an absolute ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:RMD-01","CL_BA94_27:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"[Serial mutation] The unconditioned migrates from spontaneous causality through extramundane dependence and ideal completeness into an unoccupiable regulative horizon.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:AS-05","CL_BA94_27:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"[Reversal] The ideal of complete reality changes from a prototype of determination into a claimed necessary being and then reverses into a nonconstitutive orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GA-04","CL_BA94_27:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"[Differentiation] Necessity divides into empirical conditionality, conceptual relation, alleged ontological status, regulative demand, and practical requirement.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:BSD-02","CL_BA94_27:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Negation changes from apparent deprivation into the disciplinary infrastructure governing positive cognitive production.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RMD-05","CL_BA94_28:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"[Terminal transformation] Completion ceases to mean possession of an exhaustive object and becomes formal completeness in the regulation of unfinished inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RLE-06","CL_BA94_28:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"[Subject mutation] Reason changes from a totalizing projector and presumed sovereign judge into a finite architect subjected to its own rules.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:HSI-02","CL_BA94_28:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] The speculative proofs disappear as independent routes and are absorbed into one dependency structure centered on ontological reification.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:RMD-07","CL_BA94_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"The empirical–intelligible character distinction remains local machinery for reconciling natural causation with rational imputation.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"Disjunctive predicate selection and the priority of affirmative reality remain local derivations until they generate the ideal of complete determination.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:CGD-01","CL_BA94_26:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"The architect–creator distinction and regulative maxims remain domain-specific implementations rather than the current-level architecture itself.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:CGD-02","CL_BA94_28:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between regulative representation and objective existence scales upward into the jurisdictional principle governing the entire critique.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:CGD-03","CL_BA94_28:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"The transcendental ideal bridges complete determination, speculative theology, proof dependency, and regulative recovery.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"Repeated proof failure and the absence of external transcendental tests catalyze the shift from local refutation to general methodological discipline.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:CGD-06","CL_BA94_28:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:CGD-07","text":"Soul, world-totality, and God are superseded as speculative objects but recoded as regulated procedures and negative design constraints.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RMD-03","CL_BA94_28:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"No empirical first cause, highest member, or completed regress can supply the unconditioned.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GNX-01","CL_BA94_27:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"No complete concept can contain or analytically produce the existence of its object.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GNX-01","CL_BA94_27:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"Neither empirical causality nor natural purposiveness provides a bridge from conditioned appearances to an all-sufficient supersensible creator.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:GNX-02","CL_BA94_27:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"Logical compatibility and freedom from contradiction do not establish real possibility, actuality, or objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GNX-03","CL_BA94_26:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"The failure of speculative theism cannot be transformed into a speculative proof of atheism.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:GNX-07","CL_BA94_27:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Regulative unity cannot be treated as a completed object, and formal organization cannot replace missing cognitive matter.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GNX-02","CL_BA94_28:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:GNX-07","text":"Rational prestige, coherence, and discipline-free autonomy cannot certify transcendental claims.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:GNX-04","CL_BA94_28:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires that empirical validity not be identical with ontological exhaustiveness; otherwise no intelligible or regulative remainder survives.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:DBN-01","CL_BA94_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"The necessity with which reason projects completion cannot be transferred into the independent existence of a total being.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:DBN-01","CL_BA94_26:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"Necessity cannot be used univocally across empirical explanation, conceptual determination, ontological assertion, regulation, and obligation.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:DBN-03","CL_BA94_26:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"Greater systematic coherence cannot generate ontological knowledge or compensate for absent material conditions of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:DBN-02","CL_BA94_28:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"No external cognitive standpoint or self-certifying rational authority is available to validate the relation between projected unity and reality.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:DBN-01","CL_BA94_28:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:DBN-06","text":"Positive doctrinal construction cannot regulate its own systematic excess without a distinct critique of claim-producing procedures.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:DBN-06","CL_BA94_28:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is the coincidence of complete systematic unity with a fully given object adequate to it.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around an absent object capable of occupying the structural place of an unconditioned and all-sufficient ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:BSA-01","CL_BA94_25:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"No standpoint exists from which conditioned world and unconditioned ground can be cognized together.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:BSA-02","CL_BA94_27:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"Final completion must remain absent because reason cannot cease demanding it and regulative unity functions only while completion remains unpossessed.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:BSA-04","CL_BA94_28:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSA-05","text":"No seamless passage unifies speculative failure with practical postulation without destroying the jurisdictional distinction that preserves both.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:BSA-05","CL_BA94_27:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-01","text":"A legitimate nonempirical function can regulate empirical inquiry only by supplementing no empirical gap and competing with no empirical cause.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-12","CL_BA94_25:XD-01","CL_BA94_28:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-02","text":"Speculative theology begins when the ideal required for complete determination is reified as an independent causal and necessary being.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_26:XD-01","CL_BA94_26:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-03","text":"The apparent plurality of theological proofs contracts into a dependency chain because each acquires determinate force only through concealed ontological reification.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-06","CL_BA94_27:AS-02","CL_BA94_27:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Speculative failure becomes constructive when it preserves regulative orientation while blocking leakage from procedural use into constitutive assertion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","CL_BA94_27:RMD-08","CL_BA94_28:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-05","text":"Because reason generates both systematic unity and systematic illusion, its legitimate autonomy requires an internally legislated negative code.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:XD-05","CL_BA94_28:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-06","text":"Practical authorization preserves freedom, obligation, and theological admissibility without producing theoretical evidence, while its positive derivation remains incomplete at this scale.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-11","CL_BA94_25:XD-04","CL_BA94_27:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-07","text":"The failure of substantive totalization is compensated by a bifurcation between unfinished empirical content and formally complete cognitive organization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:XD-02","CL_BA94_28:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:XD-08","text":"Objectivity becomes layered because withdrawal of constitutive presentation leaves room for empirical validity, regulative validity, and architectonic legitimacy.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-12","CL_BA94_28:BSD-01","CL_BA94_25:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from possession of supersensible objects into a constitutional allocation of conceptual, regulative, practical, and methodological functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","CL_BA94_27:BSD-03","CL_BA94_28:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes simultaneously an empirically explicable phenomenon, a rationally imputable agent, a totalizing projector, and a disciplined architect.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-11","CL_BA94_25:BSD-04","CL_BA94_28:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"Time divides into endless empirical regress, asymptotic practical improvement, and formally delimited but indefinitely extensible inquiry.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","CL_BA94_25:BSD-02","CL_BA94_28:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Nonobjective ideas acquire efficacy by orienting imputation, explanation, comparison, and organization without producing their own objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-12","CL_BA94_25:XD-06","CL_BA94_28:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"Negative critique becomes an institutional component of cognition rather than a merely destructive verdict on failed propositions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","CL_BA94_27:GA-11","CL_BA94_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-06","text":"Closure becomes asymmetrical: speculative access to the unconditioned closes while empirical inquiry, regulative orientation, and practical possibility remain open.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-11","CL_BA94_27:AS-08","CL_BA94_28:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:BSD-07","text":"Rational autonomy is reconstructed as self-limitation because the same power that legislates unity remains vulnerable to its own projections.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_07:GA-10","CL_BA94_28:BSD-02","CL_BA94_28:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"Empirical regress reproduces the absence of completion, and that absence renews reason’s demand for an unconditioned ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:RLE-01","CL_BA94_27:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"The ideal attracts reification, reification produces dialectical failure, and failure returns the ideal as regulative procedure.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:RLE-01","CL_BA94_28:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"Natural order escalates into architect, absolute perfection, necessary being, and finally the concealed ontological inference into which every proof collapses.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:RLE-02","CL_BA94_27:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"Functional compensation forms a loop in which objective presentation is withdrawn while regulative, critical, and organizational force increases.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:RLE-03","CL_BA94_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Regulative inquiry multiplies determinations, the enlarged manifold renews demand for higher unity, and unavailable totality continues directing local order.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RLE-01","CL_BA94_28:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"Discipline is a reflexive feedback loop in which reason detects systemic effects generated by its own procedures and restricts their repetition.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RLE-07","CL_BA94_28:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:RLE-07","text":"Complete determination and systematic unity form the dominant attractor, joining freedom, world-ground, theology, inquiry, and architectonic organization without becoming an object.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:RLE-06","CL_BA94_28:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"Finite cognitive materials indexed to possible experience establish the field of valid objective cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:FGS-B01","CL_BA94_25:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"Empirical conditioning generates an unavoidable demand for complete determination and systematic unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:GA-09","CL_BA94_28:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"The demand projects intelligible grounds, total reality, necessary being, causal completion, and natural purposiveness.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:AS-B03","CL_BA94_27:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Projection becomes illusion when procedural unity is converted into an independently existing and constitutively known object.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:XD-01","CL_BA94_28:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Dependency analysis reduces the plurality of proofs to the failed transition by which conceptual determination allegedly posits existence.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AS-02","CL_BA94_27:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"Critical restriction blocks both speculative assertion and denial while recovering ideas as practical, regulative, and architectonic functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AS-05","CL_BA94_28:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FGS-B07","text":"Architectonic organization and negative discipline produce a formally complete but empirically unfinished system of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:FGS-B07","CL_BA94_28:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned experience → demand for completion → supersensible projection → jurisdictional excess → critical restriction → regulated return.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:AS-07","CL_BA94_28:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Free action → world-dependence → complete determination → ideal totality → alleged necessary being.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:RLE-05","CL_BA94_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Causal and natural-order proofs → dependency cascade → ontological transition → failure of existential positing.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:XD-01","CL_BA94_27:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Speculative failure → reciprocal suspension of theism and atheism → regulative persistence → conditional practical admissibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:AFM-B05","CL_BA94_27:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Failed transcendent construction → finite materials → proportional method → architectonic organization → disciplinary self-government → formally secure open inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:RLE-03","CL_BA94_28:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-01","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a missing empirical member or objective predicate into a differentiated regulative function.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:MME-01","CL_BA94_27:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-02","text":"Theology is redrawn from a body of independent proofs into a record of transformations and a differentiated field of rational functions.","refs":["CL_BA94_26:MME-05","CL_BA94_27:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-03","text":"Cognitive limits become constitutional design parameters rather than merely external barriers to knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MME-02","CL_BA94_28:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Negativity becomes an institutional condition of epistemic security, collapsing the opposition between restraint and construction.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MME-03","CL_BA94_28:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Completion divides into unavailable empirical totalization and attainable completeness of the formal conditions governing inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MME-04","CL_BA94_28:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-06","text":"Critical method becomes a dependency audit and procedural governance rather than assessment of isolated conclusions.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:BSD-07","CL_BA94_28:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MME-07","text":"Objectivity becomes a modal hierarchy requiring distinctions among empirical validity, logical compatibility, regulative force, practical authorization, and architectonic legitimacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:BSD-01","CL_BA94_25:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit field contains free action, phenomenal dependence, complete reality, theological proofs, systematic unity, and methodological discipline.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_27:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Empirical conditioning, conceptual projection, existential positing, regulation, architectonic, and discipline form a distributed cognitive machine.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_26:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Critique shifts intervention from refuting dogmatic claims to governing the procedures and jurisdictions that generate them.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_27:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality appears as an open series of conditioned appearances under indefinitely extensible inquiry and an unavailable horizon of totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_28:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The system performs its thesis by reconstructing its projected objects, exposing their production, and converting failure into rules and recursive checks.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_26:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-01","text":"The architecture repeatedly moves from non-impossibility to positive functional attribution even though noncontradiction proves neither actuality nor determinate efficacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:FL-02","CL_BA94_25:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-02","text":"Practical postulation supplies the system’s positive remainder, but its derivation from moral obligation remains deferred.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:FL-04","CL_BA94_26:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-03","text":"Regulative unity oscillates between a procedural maxim and a necessary presupposition concerning nature, threatening renewed constitutive commitment.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:FL-01","CL_BA94_28:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-04","text":"Reason’s authority to discipline itself remains recursively dependent on the power whose lack of external constraint generated the problem.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:FL-03","CL_BA94_28:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:FL-05","text":"Formal completeness strains against the permanent asymptotic incompleteness of systematic unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:FL-02","CL_BA94_28:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-01","text":"The unconditioned mutates from originating cause and extramundane condition into ideal completeness and regulative horizon.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Necessity mutates from an alleged objective property into regulative orientation and possible practical requirement.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Negation mutates from denial of cognitive content into active governance of cognitive production.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-04","text":"Completeness mutates from possession of exhaustive totality into completeness of formal conditions and jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MP-05","text":"The Supreme Being mutates from inferred object into regulative ideal and possible practical postulate without recovering theoretical objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a conditioned empirical network spread across finite terrain and oriented toward an unoccupiable horizon of systematic totality.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:HSI-01","CL_BA94_28:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a double-aspect node and divided architect: empirically determined, rationally imputable, projective, and recursively restrained.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:HSI-02","CL_BA94_28:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is an endless empirical regress transformed into recursive approximation, where each achieved order renews the demand for unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_25:HSI-03","CL_BA94_28:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in pursuing systematic unity while refusing hypostasis and constructing a secure but permanently unfinished dwelling.","refs":["CL_BA94_27:HSI-04","CL_BA94_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empirical conditioning through supersensible projection and proof failure to regulative recovery and disciplined method.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_27:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"Coherence requires strict functional separation among constitutive cognition, regulative direction, practical authorization, architectonic organization, and negative control.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B03","CL_BA94_27:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning the objective force of regulative unity and the moral derivation of practical postulation.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_27:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"The system is strongest where it reduces proof-plurality to ontological dependency and converts speculative failure into proportional design and discipline.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_27:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if projection, excess, critique, and regulated return remain recursively sequenced rather than flattened into static prohibitions.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_27:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_07:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is the performative authority by which finite reason validates its own discipline while continuing to demand an unconditioned ground.","refs":["CL_BA94_28:MRT-B06","CL_BA94_27:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 07","context":"Chapters 73–84 · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","original_title":"Ba94 L1 07","original_context":"Chapters 73–84 · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 07","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08","layer":"MC","title":"Ba94 L1 08","order":8,"parents":["CL_BA94_29","CL_BA94_30","CL_BA94_31","CL_BA94_32"],"revision":"MC_BA94_L1_08@R001","sha256":"b2014b9b062073f7c27dd03c78cef2c9a175bbc4822cd2ae96838ee8eb36ff45","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-01","text":"The system asks how reason can retain objective authority, practical orientation, and systematic unity after unrestricted speculative metaphysics, methodological transfer, and aggregate organization have failed.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:AS-01","CL_BA94_31:AS-01","CL_BA94_32:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-02","text":"Its central claim is that rational authority survives only through a differentiated constitution assigning theoretical cognition to possible experience, practical legislation to moral law, and systemic organization to architectonic criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:AS-02","CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CL_BA94_32:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a transcendental-juridical architectonic that constructs reason’s unity by allocating powers, exposing usurpations, and regulating their permissible interaction.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:AS-04","CL_BA94_32:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-04","text":"Possible experience and moral law operate as distinct centers of validity, while criticism coordinates them without converting either theoretical limitation or practical necessity into supersensible cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RLE-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-06","CL_BA94_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-05","text":"The movement is recursive and spiral: rational aspiration exceeds its jurisdiction, conflict reveals the excess, critique restricts it, and the restricted demand returns as empirical inquiry, practical belief, or reconstructed system.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:AS-B07","CL_BA94_31:RLE-01","CL_BA94_32:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-06","text":"Negation becomes generative because every exclusion of illegitimate cognition produces a positive differentiation of method, assent, practical employment, or institutional form.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:BSD-01","CL_BA94_30:BSD-05","CL_BA94_32:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-07","text":"The rational subject becomes simultaneously legislator, accused, and finite believer, while rational time divides into retrospective diagnosis, recursive self-correction, and prospective moral-systematic completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:BSD-03","CL_BA94_32:BSD-04","CL_BA94_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AS-08","text":"Closure is conditional rather than final: theoretical cognition is bounded, practical commitment remains non-cognitive, empirical and critical inquiry stay open, and collective architectonic completion is deferred.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:AS-07","CL_BA94_31:AS-08","CL_BA94_32:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the exclusive field of theoretical objectivity and the primary tribunal for claims concerning objects, causes, and explanations.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-01","CL_BA94_30:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires an authorized mode of presentation and a prior deduction of the validity and synthesis of its concepts.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-02","CL_BA94_30:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Rational entitlement is function-specific: construction, deduction, empirical explanation, regulation, practical legislation, and critical adjudication cannot substitute for one another.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-03","CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CL_BA94_32:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Reason is autonomous only when it legislates its own powers while submitting every exercise of those powers to criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-06","CL_BA94_30:GA-04","CL_BA94_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Systematic unity is necessary but never self-validating; architectonic organization requires critical authorization because error can itself assume systematic form.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-09","CL_BA94_32:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Supersensible ideas lose constitutive theoretical status but persist as regulative orientations, practical concerns, postulates, beliefs, or retractable defensive suppositions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-08","CL_BA94_30:GA-09","CL_BA94_31:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Moral law is autonomous from happiness and theology and supplies the only proper canon of pure practical reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:GA-08","CL_BA94_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] The highest good generates practically necessary belief by requiring a unity of virtue and happiness that natural causality cannot guarantee, while objective theoretical sufficiency remains absent.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GA-03","CL_BA94_31:GA-04","CL_BA94_31:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Theoretical objectivity and practical necessity are irreducible but coordinated modalities of reason rather than competing claims to one undifferentiated validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RLE-05","CL_BA94_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Limitation reverses from deprivation into productive protection by securing cognition, belief, inquiry, and systematic order against illegitimate extension.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RMD-04","CL_BA94_31:GA-10","CL_BA94_32:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Architectonic unity orders objects, methods, disciplines, practical interests, and historical positions under a single human destination without supplying speculative possession of that destination.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-11","CL_BA94_31:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] The rational subject becomes a self-legislating and self-accusing office that must act and believe without becoming the sovereign producer of its objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:BSD-02","CL_BA94_31:BSD-03","CL_BA94_32:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Rational development has a recursively divided temporality in which failure discloses prior conditions, criticism reorganizes the present, and moral-systematic completion remains future.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:BSD-06","CL_BA94_31:BSD-04","CL_BA94_32:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] The system terminates in bounded theoretical authority, practically governed commitment, open inquiry, and prospective rather than completed architectonic closure.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-10","CL_BA94_31:BSD-07","CL_BA94_32:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-01","text":"Possible experience retains its identity as the condition of objective cognition while expanding into the theoretical pole of a larger constitutional architecture.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RMD-01","CL_BA94_30:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-02","text":"Proof mutates from inferential support among propositions into a jurisdictional demonstration that concepts possess objective reference and lawful scope.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RMD-02","CL_BA94_30:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-03","text":"Limitation reverses valence twice: it first protects theoretical cognition and then enables practical belief without licensing theoretical recovery of the supersensible.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RMD-04","CL_BA94_31:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-04","text":"The supersensible undergoes terminal status transformation from attempted object-cognition through regulative orientation to practically necessary but non-cognitive belief.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RMD-03","CL_BA94_30:RMD-05","CL_BA94_31:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-05","text":"Discipline escalates from local restriction of method into criticism’s general tribunal over theoretical, practical, historical, and architectonic entitlement.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:RMD-03","CL_BA94_32:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-06","text":"Systematicity retains its scientific necessity but loses sufficiency once opposed dogmatic and sceptical procedures prove equally capable of systematic organization.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:RMD-01","CL_BA94_32:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-07","text":"Opposition migrates from external doctrinal combat into reason’s internal adversarial procedure and then into historical diagnosis of recurrent rational positions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RMD-04","CL_BA94_32:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-08","text":"Universality divides into objectively communicable cognition, universally binding moral law, and publicly reproducible criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:AS-07","CL_BA94_31:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-09","text":"Teleology escalates from regulation of inquiry and moral interpretation of nature into the architectonic destination governing the entire economy of reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:RMD-04","CL_BA94_32:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RMD-10","text":"Scepticism disappears as an independent destination after its interruptive function is absorbed into criticism, while dogmatism survives only as a recurrent object of that criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RMD-06","CL_BA94_32:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-01","text":"Mathematical construction, individual proof restrictions, and detailed hypothesis tests remain lower-layer machinery; their current-level result is the non-transferability of cognitive functions across jurisdictions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:CGD-01","CL_BA94_30:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-02","text":"Exact distribution within the highest good and classifications of historical schools remain local specifications until absorbed into the wider coordination of moral ends and epistemic positions.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:CGD-01","CL_BA94_32:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-03","text":"Possible experience, transcendental deduction, and the non-cognitive status of practical postulates scale upward because they jointly determine the boundary between cognition and legitimate orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:CGD-04","CL_BA94_30:CGD-02","CL_BA94_31:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-04","text":"Discipline, canon, teleology, and criticism bridge the units by connecting jurisdictional restriction, practical legislation, and architectonic reconstruction.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:CGD-03","CL_BA94_31:CGD-05","CL_BA94_32:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-05","text":"Methodological temptation, natural insufficiency, and object-source-method misalignment catalyze transitions because each exposes a demand operating without its enabling conditions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:CGD-05","CL_BA94_31:CGD-06","CL_BA94_32:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:CGD-06","text":"Object-level victory, merely problematical assent, technical unity, and naturalism are superseded by jurisdictional critique, practical necessity, and rationally warranted system.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:CGD-06","CL_BA94_31:CGD-07","CL_BA94_32:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-01","text":"No successful method, formal derivation, or a priori status authorizes transfer into a domain lacking the relevant mode of presentation.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GNX-01","CL_BA94_32:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-02","text":"Neither affirmation nor denial of supersensible conditions can become theoretical cognition merely through conceptual consistency, practical need, or rational demand.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GNX-02","CL_BA94_31:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-03","text":"Regulative utility, practical necessity, and moral desirability cannot be converted into evidence for the objective reality of their supersensible correlates.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GNX-06","CL_BA94_31:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-04","text":"Theology cannot legislate morality from outside because divine command would destroy the autonomy that alone generates legitimate practical orientation.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GNX-07","CL_BA94_31:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-05","text":"Neither aggregation nor systematic form alone constitutes science, truth, or rational legitimacy.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:GNX-01","CL_BA94_32:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-06","text":"Dogmatism, scepticism, and explanatory closure are rejected as terminal positions because each suppresses the critical examination of reason’s authority.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GNX-07","CL_BA94_32:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:GNX-07","text":"Private assurance and coercively imposed belief cannot substitute for objective warrant, autonomous moral law, or publicly sustainable criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:GNX-04","CL_BA94_31:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires that intellectual intuition be unavailable to finite reason, since its availability would collapse the distinction between thought, presentation, belief, and cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-02","text":"It rejects the rival model in which cognition exhausts rational legitimacy, because practical action must remain possible without a speculative science of the supersensible.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:DBN-01","CL_BA94_30:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-03","text":"It excludes any natural harmonism capable of guaranteeing the coincidence of virtue, happiness, and moral completion within empirical history.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:DBN-03","CL_BA94_31:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-04","text":"It disqualifies the assumption that accumulation or historical succession can generate their own rational plan, while also refusing to admit that architectonic unity is merely retrospectively imposed.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:DBN-01","CL_BA94_32:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-05","text":"No standpoint external to reason can validate reason’s powers, practical commitments, or system without sacrificing autonomy or reproducing the unexamined conditions of illusion.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:DBN-04","CL_BA94_30:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:DBN-06","text":"Ultimate ends cannot remain coordinate, yet the final unity of nature and freedom cannot become a theoretically possessed object.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:DBN-06","CL_BA94_32:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable presentation of supersensible objects is the central absence around which theoretical limitation, practical postulation, and differentiated assent are organized.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-02","text":"The absent empirical moral world structures hope and futurity because reason requires a completed moral order that can never be present as theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:BSA-01","CL_BA94_31:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-03","text":"The system lacks both an external standpoint and an actual philosopher-legislator, leaving critique to exercise authority that no embodied subject can finally possess.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:BSA-03","CL_BA94_32:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-04","text":"The missing positive procedure of criticism and the missing guarantee of the true architectonic idea remain concealed conditions of the system’s claim to final jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:BSA-03","CL_BA94_32:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSA-05","text":"The unity of nature and freedom functions as an unavailable horizon that organizes the whole while postponing final satisfaction beyond present critical achievement.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:BSA-04","CL_BA94_32:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-01","text":"Once experiential validity is combined with architectonic allocation, epistemic legitimacy becomes relational: a claim is entitled only when object, source, presentation, method, scope, and systemic position align.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","CL_BA94_29:GA-05","CL_BA94_32:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-02","text":"Speculative restriction preserves supersensible demand by routing it through autonomous moral law and then classifying its objects as practical belief rather than recovered knowledge.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-09","CL_BA94_30:XD-02","CL_BA94_31:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-03","text":"The distinction between discipline and canon yields a constitutional model of reason in which negative regulation is general, while positive legislation belongs only to a demonstrably proper employment.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","CL_BA94_30:XD-03","CL_BA94_30:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-04","text":"Internalized opposition becomes historically and institutionally productive when autonomous self-criticism is reproduced as a collective method rather than confined to private rational mastery.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-12","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-13","CL_BA94_29:RLE-05","CL_BA94_32:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-05","text":"The moral future and the architectonic future converge as distinct expressions of completion demanded in the present but unavailable as present possession.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-13","CL_BA94_31:XD-06","CL_BA94_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-06","text":"Moral belief can be universally accessible without becoming objectively communicable knowledge, while public criticism prevents this accessibility from licensing coercion or philosophical monopoly.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","CL_BA94_31:XD-05","CL_BA94_32:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-07","text":"Metaphysics survives its speculative defeat by becoming the regulated office that organizes unavoidable questions, prevents error, and coordinates rational functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","CL_BA94_32:XD-08","CL_BA94_32:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:XD-08","text":"The same boundary that blocks transcendent explanation protects empirical inquiry from closure and practical agency from suspension pending impossible certainty.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-10","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-14","CL_BA94_29:XD-07","CL_BA94_30:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a jurisdictionally stratified coordination system in which appearances, practical ends, supersensible ideas, and historical positions possess different modes of admissibility.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-01","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-06","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-09","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","CL_BA94_30:HSI-01","CL_BA94_31:HSI-01","CL_BA94_32:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-02","text":"Rational validity becomes topological rather than scalar because claims occupy distinct coordinates of objectivity, subjective commitment, practical necessity, and systemic placement.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-02","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","CL_BA94_31:BSD-01","CL_BA94_32:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-03","text":"The subject is reconstructed as legislator, accused, investigator, and believer whose autonomy consists in disciplined self-government rather than metaphysical sovereignty.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-12","CL_BA94_29:BSD-05","CL_BA94_31:BSD-03","CL_BA94_32:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes ethically and architectonically asymmetric: present disunity is interpreted through prior rational conditions and directed toward an unpossessed future completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-13","CL_BA94_31:BSD-04","CL_BA94_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-05","text":"The architecture has a political-institutional consequence: belief loses coercive authority, specialist reason loses sovereignty, and criticism must become publicly reproducible.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-04","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-11","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-12","CL_BA94_31:BSD-06","CL_BA94_32:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-06","text":"Rational agency is action under disciplined undecidability: finite reason must act from law while refusing both dogmatic possession and sceptical suspension.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-07","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-08","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-14","CL_BA94_31:BSD-07","CL_BA94_31:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics changes its criterion of success from transcendent acquisition to productive differentiation, error-prevention, and durable coordination of rational powers.","refs":["MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-03","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-05","MC_BA94_L1_08:GA-10","CL_BA94_32:BSD-07","CL_BA94_29:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-01","text":"Cognitive success or metaphysical interest generates expansion, expansion generates conflict, conflict generates criticism, and criticism returns the original demand in a restricted function.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RLE-01","CL_BA94_30:RLE-01","CL_BA94_32:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-02","text":"Possible experience and moral law form paired attractors: one repeatedly draws theoretical claims back to objective presentation, while the other draws practical orientation back to autonomous obligation.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RLE-04","CL_BA94_30:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-03","text":"The highest good is an escalation machine carrying unconditional law through worthiness and natural insufficiency toward postulation and disciplined belief.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:RLE-03","CL_BA94_31:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-04","text":"The single human destination and the absent complete philosophy function as architectonic attractors that organize present cognition without becoming presently possessed objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:RLE-06","CL_BA94_32:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-05","text":"Opposition becomes a machine when reason produces its own objections, submits them to public stabilization, and uses the resulting method to regenerate autonomous criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:RLE-05","CL_BA94_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-06","text":"Practical postulation contains itself recursively: speculative limitation generates belief, and belief reactivates the same limitation to prevent theological inflation.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:RLE-01","CL_BA94_31:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:RLE-07","text":"Systematicity undergoes phase change when accumulated organization discloses its governing idea but simultaneously reveals that formal unity requires critical adjudication.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:RLE-01","CL_BA94_32:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B01","text":"Cognitive ambition, moral demand, and accumulated metaphysical construction generate a field requiring rational reconstruction.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:FGS-B01","CL_BA94_29:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B02","text":"Every claim is tested for object, source, mode of presentation, method, scope, and objective validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:GA-02","CL_BA94_32:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B03","text":"Valid functions are allocated to construction, deduction, empirical explanation, regulation, practical legislation, and architectonic adjudication.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:FGS-B03","CL_BA94_30:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B04","text":"Adversarial testing and transcendental classification exclude every operation that exceeds its enabling conditions or substitutes contingent organization for rational necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:FGS-B04","CL_BA94_32:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B05","text":"Excluded speculative demand is preserved as empirical openness, regulative orientation, autonomous obligation, or practical postulation.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:XD-02","CL_BA94_31:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B06","text":"The assent matrix assigns each surviving demand a status determined by subjective sufficiency, objective sufficiency, and practical necessity.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:FGS-B06","CL_BA94_31:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FGS-B07","text":"Architectonic integration orders the resulting jurisdictions under a human end, then reopens the machine because collective criticism and the unity of nature and freedom remain incomplete.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:FGS-B07","CL_BA94_32:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AFM-B01","text":"Cognitive success and aggregate organization → unauthorized expansion → metaphysical conflict → demand for transcendental jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:AS-05","CL_BA94_32:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AFM-B02","text":"Jurisdictional assay → possible experience → differentiated cognitive functions → bounded theoretical authority → open empirical inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:AS-B03","CL_BA94_29:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AFM-B03","text":"Speculative subtraction → autonomous moral law → highest good → natural insufficiency → practical postulates → moral belief → renewed speculative containment.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:FGS-B01","CL_BA94_31:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:AFM-B04","text":"Critical differentiation → architectonic placement → historical self-diagnosis → public reproduction of criticism → prospective moral-systematic closure.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:FGS-B05","CL_BA94_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-01","text":"The a priori becomes a differentiated constitutional field rather than a homogeneous method transferable across mathematics, experience, morality, and metaphysics.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MME-01","CL_BA94_29:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-02","text":"Proof and refutation are relocated from proposition-level combat to examination of the conditions authorizing objective reference.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:MME-01","CL_BA94_30:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-03","text":"Boundaries become enabling instruments that preserve cognition, practical agency, empirical openness, and systematic unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:MME-02","CL_BA94_31:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-04","text":"Belief becomes a positive rational modality coordinated with but irreducible to knowledge, opinion, persuasion, and theological doctrine.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:MME-02","CL_BA94_31:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-05","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from transcendent doctrine into the critically regulated organization of unavoidable rational questions.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:MME-04","CL_BA94_32:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-06","text":"History becomes an internal audit of reason’s architecture rather than a chronological record external to philosophical validity.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:MME-02","CL_BA94_32:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MME-07","text":"Universality divides into cognitive communicability, moral accessibility, and public methodological reproducibility, dissolving the equation of universality with one evidential form.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:MME-05","CL_BA94_32:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, proofs, hypotheses, supersensible ideas, moral postulates, forms of assent, sciences, and philosophical schools are classified and tested.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_31:ALT-B01","CL_BA94_32:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, jurisdictional alignment among objects, sources, presentations, methods, laws, and ends determines every claim’s admissibility.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:ALT-B02","CL_BA94_32:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, struggle shifts from possessing supersensible objects to assigning claim-status, policing conversion between functions, and reconstructing inherited positions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:ALT-B03","CL_BA94_32:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, experiential reality remains theoretically ordered while moral-systematic completion recedes into a future horizon disclosed through recursive failure.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:ALT-B04","CL_BA94_32:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its thesis by generating claims, exposing their inflation, reclassifying their functions, and reopening criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:ALT-B05","CL_BA94_32:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-01","text":"Universal criticizability conflicts with critique’s need to function as the final tribunal rather than one more revisable position.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:FL-02","CL_BA94_32:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-02","text":"Possible experience validates transcendental principles while those principles constitute possible experience, leaving the theoretical criterion reflexively dependent on the deduction it is meant to authorize.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-03","text":"The passage from speculative failure to practical authority is indispensable but does not by itself demonstrate that reason’s supersensible demand has acquired legitimate positive objects.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:FL-02","CL_BA94_31:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-04","text":"The postulation of God and future life depends on an underdemonstrated claim that no alternative conditions could realize the required moral unity.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:XD-08","CL_BA94_31:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-05","text":"Moral law is independently binding, yet the represented efficacy and completion of its maxims depend on postulated conditions that remain objectively insufficient.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:FL-03","CL_BA94_31:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-06","text":"Practical freedom is retained as necessary for agency while transcendental freedom remains theoretically suspended, leaving their relation structurally unresolved.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:FL-03","CL_BA94_30:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:FL-07","text":"The architectonic idea claims a priori authority although it becomes visible through historical accumulation and classificatory compression.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:FL-01","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-01","text":"Proof changes function when inferential support is subordinated to deduction of objective validity and jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:MP-01","CL_BA94_30:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-02","text":"Limitation changes from prohibition into the productive condition of cognition, practical belief, and continuing inquiry.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:MP-02","CL_BA94_31:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-03","text":"Supersensible objects change into regulative ideas and then into objects of practical belief without acquiring theoretical objecthood.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MP-03","CL_BA94_31:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-04","text":"Criticism changes from an internal restrictive organ into the tribunal governing the history, architecture, and future reproduction of reason.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:MP-01","CL_BA94_32:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-05","text":"Systematicity changes from the defining contrast with aggregation into a merely necessary form requiring independent critical authorization.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:MP-02","CL_BA94_32:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MP-06","text":"Teleology changes from local regulation of inquiry into the singular moral destination ordering the complete economy of cognition.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:MP-04","CL_BA94_32:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as a stratified court-organism whose regions possess different laws and whose unity depends on adjudicated coordination rather than homogeneous substance.","refs":["CL_BA94_32:HSI-01","CL_BA94_30:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite provisional legislator who judges cognition, submits its own powers to trial, and commits practically without possessing the supersensible.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:HSI-02","CL_BA94_32:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is a recursive vector in which prior rational conditions are disclosed through conflict and projected toward an unexperienced future completion.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:HSI-03","CL_BA94_32:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:HSI-04","text":"Struggle consists in navigating jurisdictional boundaries between dogmatic possession and sceptical suspension while sustaining empirical inquiry, autonomous action, and public criticism.","refs":["CL_BA94_31:HSI-04","CL_BA94_32:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the current-level engine if experiential objectivity, practical legislation, differentiated assent, architectonic placement, and recursive criticism remain jointly operative.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_31:MRT-B01","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning the transcendental deductions validating possible experience, the exclusivity of the moral postulates, and the criterion identifying the true architectonic idea.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_31:MRT-B02","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if theoretical cognition, practical necessity, and architectonic regulation remain distinct without becoming disconnected.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_31:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where repeated negation produces positive differentiation among cognitive functions, modes of assent, jurisdictions, and systemic positions.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MRT-B04","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when temptation, conflict, speculative subtraction, practical return, and historical reorganization remain recursive rather than successive discarded errors.","refs":["CL_BA94_29:MRT-B06","CL_BA94_31:MRT-B05","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_BA94_L1_08:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable remainder resistant to axiomatic reduction is the transition from critical restriction to moral-teleological orientation, because that transition bears terminal weight beyond its supplied demonstrations.","refs":["CL_BA94_30:FL-02","CL_BA94_32:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Ba94 L1 08","context":"Chapters 85–94 · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","original_title":"Ba94 L1 08","original_context":"Chapters 85–94 · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"Ba94 L1 08","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_BA94","layer":"WB","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","order":1,"parents":["MC_BA94_L2_01","MC_BA94_L2_02"],"revision":"WB_BA94@R002","sha256":"eb14ad0ce697e4f25a03898acc71fc7c336589545b76c07505eb0eed7df0d2a1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-01","text":"Finite reason must authorize necessary objective cognition while preserving its demands for totality and orientation without converting those demands into knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B01","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-02","text":"Rational authority is reconstructed as a differentiated constitution in which possible experience grounds theoretical objectivity and distinct jurisdictions govern reason’s remaining functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B06","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-03","text":"Objective experience arises through the synthesis of sensible multiplicity under concepts whose authority extends only to the conditions under which objects can be presented.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-04","text":"The successful legislation of lawful nature produces conditioned cognition, which necessarily generates a further demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-05","text":"Critique becomes a reflexive tribunal that secures cognition by diagnosing contradiction and proof failure as effects of unauthorized object-construction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-11","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-06","text":"Negation becomes generative when the exclusion of transcendent knowledge redistributes reason’s demands into empirical, regulative, practical, and architectonic functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-07","text":"The movement is spiral: each recovered condition enables cognition, generates a new demand or excess, and returns that excess through another jurisdictional mediation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B07","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BAS-08","text":"Closure is asymmetrical: phenomenal lawfulness and formal jurisdiction close, while empirical inquiry, practical realization, criticism, and systematic construction remain open.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires irreducibly differentiated sensible presentation and conceptual synthesis under the common restriction of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Mediation is the operative center through which cognitive functions acquire authority only by being coordinated within their proper jurisdictions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the constituted field of objective cognition and the tribunal testing every theoretical claim to object-reference.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Objectivity changes from presumed correspondence with independent being into universally necessary, temporally enacted, and jurisdictionally restricted synthesis.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The subject is generated as an apperceptive integrating function and subsequently divided into projector, legislator, accused party, critic, and finite believer.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The field of conditioned experience necessarily activates reason’s demand for complete determination, systematic unity, and an unconditioned ground.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B06","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental illusion is generated when valid conceptual or inferential functions are detached from sensible authorization and transferred to unpresentable total objects.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Contradiction and proof collapse acquire critical force by exposing an invalid completed object-model shared by opposed metaphysical claims.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B05","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Critique is a positive governance mechanism that produces valid cognition by allocating, restricting, and coordinating heterogeneous rational functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-11","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Rational validity is topological because differentiated faculties and functions support irreducibly distinct modes of authorization rather than one homogeneous scale.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Limitation changes from apparent deprivation into the condition protecting objective validity, empirical openness, practical commitment, and systematic orientation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Supersensible objects remain theoretically indeterminate, and their regulative or practical recovery cannot restore theoretical objecthood.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-12","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Formal legislation and jurisdiction can close while particular empirical determinations and the realization of rational ends remain unfinished.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Rational autonomy consists in recursively legislating and criticizing authority through the same finite powers whose legitimacy remains under examination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity with expansion] Experience remains cognition’s field but expands from constituted outcome into the central jurisdictional pole governing theoretical reference.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-02","text":"[Serial mutation] Synthesis escalates from the prior operation of object-constitution into lawful natural legislation and becomes dialectical totalization when extended beyond presentation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Faculty differentiation migrates from an account of cognitive production into a constitutional prohibition against jurisdictional substitution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-04","text":"[Subject mutation] Subjectivity reverses from suspected distortion into a universal formal condition, then becomes a divided architect implicated in its own projections.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-05","text":"[Domain expansion] Time develops from sensible form and schematic medium into the recursive asymmetry of incomplete self-presence, regress, criticism, and prospective completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-06","text":"[Valence reversal] Critique and negation change from restraint into the constructive, institutional, and procedural governance of positive rational production.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Contradiction changes from an apparent collision within reality into a diagnostic instrument for exposing valid forms operating through an invalid object-model.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Noumenal objecthood and the unconditioned are absorbed into a differentiated boundary architecture of negative limit, regulative horizon, practical concern, and architectonic destination.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal transformation] Completion ceases to mean possession of total content and becomes formal completeness in the allocation of permanently open inquiry.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-01","text":"Detailed logical inventories, temporal schemata, reflection-concepts, proofs, examples, and hypothesis tests remain local instruments rather than the whole-book architecture.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-02","text":"Faculty differentiation scales upward because it governs both the production of objects and the constitutional separation of rational jurisdictions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-03","text":"Synthesis, apperception, judgment, and schematism bridge object-constitution to the universal criterion separating objective cognition from nonobjective orientation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-04","text":"The completion of phenomenal constitution catalyzes the transition to dialectic because every constituted object remains conditioned and therefore reactivates the demand for completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-05","text":"The underived inventory of cognitive forms and the underdetermined force of regulative and practical principles scale upward as limits of transcendental self-explanation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:CGD-06","text":"Ready-made correspondence, substantial soul, completed world-totality, and supreme object lose terminal authority and survive only as critically recoded positions or functions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:CGD-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-01","text":"Empirical recurrence and association cannot generate the necessity required for universally valid objective cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-02","text":"Concepts and categories cannot determine objects without sensible givenness and the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-03","text":"Sensible multiplicity cannot constitute an object before synthesis because an unsynthesized manifold lacks objective conjunction and unity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-04","text":"Logical consistency, formal validity, and conceptual completeness cannot establish material truth, objective possibility, or existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-05","text":"Neither the world-whole nor intrinsic reality can be surveyed from an external or unconditioned standpoint.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-06","text":"Theoretical reason can neither affirm nor deny supersensible objects when categorial application lacks sensible authorization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-07","text":"Regulative utility and practical necessity cannot become evidence for theoretical objecthood or existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:GNX-08","text":"Neither an exhaustive application algorithm nor a single successful critique can abolish judgment, recurrent illusion, or the need for renewed criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which independently constituted objects arrive already unified and can be inspected from outside finite cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-02","text":"Receptivity and spontaneity cannot be dissolved into a homogeneous faculty or validity continuum without eliminating the mediations that generate cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-03","text":"Finite reason cannot possess intellectual intuition because immediate presentation of complete conditions would dissolve both synthesis and critical jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-04","text":"No transparent or substantial subject can convert its integrating activity into cognition of itself as an independently subsisting entity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GNX-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-05","text":"Universal formal order cannot determine particular existence or guarantee the empirical coincidence of nature, freedom, virtue, and happiness.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:DBN-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:DBN-06","text":"No external authority can validate critique, yet self-validation cannot confer unconditioned sovereignty on the finite powers being examined.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent capacity is an intellectual intuition that would disclose the common ground of receptivity and spontaneity while presenting complete conditions immediately.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an unavailable all-sufficient ground that would terminate conditioning without becoming another conditioned object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSA-03","text":"The unavailable identity of thinkability, knowability, and complete possession of reality prevents the complete world and all its conditions from becoming one object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSA-04","text":"The absent empirical unity of nature, freedom, virtue, and happiness gives practical hope its orientation while withholding theoretical completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSA-05","text":"Final completion and an external guarantor must remain absent because criticism, inquiry, and practical approximation derive their force from nonpossession.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-01","text":"The conditions constituting objective experience also generate its boundary, so authorization and restriction are two operations of one critical structure.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-02","WB_BA94:GA-01","WB_BA94:GA-03","WB_BA94:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-02","text":"The same categories that generate lawful nature produce dialectical illusion when their constitutive use is extended into complete totalization.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-04","WB_BA94:GA-04","WB_BA94:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-03","text":"Successful phenomenal constitution internally generates an unknown remainder whose withdrawal from objecthood permits regulative and practical use without restoring theoretical cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-04","WB_BA94:GA-03","WB_BA94:GA-11","WB_BA94:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-04","text":"Temporal schematization and recursive noncompletion jointly make finitude appear as both incomplete self-presence and the impossibility of possessing total conditions at once.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:XD-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-04","WB_BA94:GA-05","WB_BA94:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-05","text":"Positive object-constitution and negative criticism form a feedback system in which valid production depends on recurrent exclusion of unauthorized transitions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-08","WB_BA94:GA-02","WB_BA94:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-06","text":"Because nature is formally legislated but materially open, regulative totality can guide empirical inquiry only as a procedure that prevents arbitrary arrest.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-05","WB_BA94:GA-06","WB_BA94:GA-10","WB_BA94:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-07","text":"Domain-indexed theoretical authority and practical necessity form distinct attractors, preventing practical authorization from becoming supersensible evidence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-08","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-07","WB_BA94:GA-10","WB_BA94:GA-11","WB_BA94:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:XD-08","text":"Since reason judges itself through the powers under judgment, criticism must remain recurrent and publicly reproducible rather than privately or finally completed.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-05","WB_BA94:GA-09","WB_BA94:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-01","text":"Epistemic authority becomes a conditioned and procedural achievement established through provenance, application, scope, and jurisdiction rather than direct possession of being.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-06","WB_BA94:GA-02","WB_BA94:GA-03","WB_BA94:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-02","text":"Subject and object become differentiated effects of distributed synthesis, while the subject’s later rational roles remain irreducible to a knowable substance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-03","WB_BA94:GA-04","WB_BA94:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-03","text":"Reality-for-cognition becomes a formally legislated field of conditioned appearances whose material laws remain empirical and whose totality remains an unpossessed task.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-02","WB_BA94:GA-03","WB_BA94:GA-06","WB_BA94:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes a layered recursive operator joining succession, schematic application, regress, retrospective diagnosis, and prospective completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-04","WB_BA94:GA-04","WB_BA94:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-05","text":"Error is an internally recurrent jurisdictional effect because valid rational forms generate illusion when detached from their authorized conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-08","WB_BA94:GA-07","WB_BA94:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysics becomes a constitutional topology of grounding, application, recurrent error, differentiated validity, and regulated function.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-01","WB_BA94:GA-09","WB_BA94:GA-10","WB_BA94:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-07","text":"Critical authority acquires a public and institutional dimension because recurrent illusion cannot be governed by specialist sovereignty or private correction alone.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:XD-05","WB_BA94:GA-09","WB_BA94:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:BSD-08","text":"Rational agency becomes disciplined action under noncompletion rather than sovereign command over objects, history, or the totality of conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-09","WB_BA94:GA-05","WB_BA94:GA-13","WB_BA94:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-01","text":"Experience provokes a demand for necessity, necessity triggers a regress to conditions, those conditions constitute experience, and every determination returns for renewed jurisdictional testing.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-02","text":"Faculty separation enables cooperation, cooperation produces cognition, and reason then resubmits both its operations and restrictions to criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-03","text":"Conditioned cognition generates demand, demand becomes projection, and projection passes through reification into paralogism, antinomy, or theological proof.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B04","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-04","text":"Valid forms generate illusion, but contradiction or proof collapse converts that illusion into a diagnostic phase change from object-dispute to object-critique.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-05","text":"Phenomenal constitution intensifies the unknown, while the resulting boundary returns excluded totalizing demand as empirical, regulative, practical, or architectonic work.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-06","text":"Rules reproduce the application regress, and criticism reproduces the authorization regress, so judgment and public review interrupt but never abolish recursive mediation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-07","text":"Practical belief is recursively contained because speculative limitation enables it, while belief reactivates that same limitation against theological inflation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:RLE-08","text":"Possible experience, synthesis, moral law, and unavailable completion form coupled attractors that repeatedly redistribute reason’s operations without terminal convergence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:RLE-07","MC_BA94_L2_02:RLE-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B01","text":"Necessary cognition plus metaphysical contradiction → demand for synthetic a priori authorization → critique of finite rational competence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B01","MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B02","text":"Receptive presentation plus irreducibly distinct conceptual activity → prior synthesis → objectively unified sensible multiplicity.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B03","text":"Synthesis plus apperception plus judgment → category → temporal schema → authorized objective application.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B05","MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B04","text":"Schematized categories → principles of experience → universal lawful nature → materially open and conditioned cognition.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:GA-10","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B05","text":"Conditioned cognition plus recursive inquiry → demand for total conditions → illusory objectification of subject, world, and supreme ground.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B06","text":"Antinomial contradiction plus proof dependency → exposure of reification → invalidation of procedural unity converted into existence.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B05","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B07","text":"Withdrawal of the invalid object-model → speculative suspension → redistribution into constitutive cognition, regulation, practical legislation, and architectonic placement.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B05","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FGS-B08","text":"Public criticism plus architectonic integration → formally bounded authority → reopening of empirical, practical, and systematic tasks.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B07","MC_BA94_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B01","text":"Scientific necessity and metaphysical conflict → synthetic a priori demand → transcendental regress from objects to enabling conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B01","MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B02","text":"A priori sensible field → manifold requiring synthesis → original apperception → co-generation of self-ascription and object-relation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B03","MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B03","text":"Judgmental function → category → temporal schema → principles of experience → lawful but empirically open nature.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B06","MC_BA94_L2_01:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B04","text":"Conditioned experience → unavoidable demand for completion → projected subject, world, and supreme ground → transcendental illusion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B02","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B05","text":"Paralogism, antinomy, and proof collapse → diagnosis of shared reification → withdrawal of constitutive totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B04","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:AFM-B06","text":"Speculative restriction → empirical continuation → regulative systematization → practical commitment → architectonic allocation → recurrent public criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B06","MC_BA94_L2_02:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence with ready-made objects into necessary synthesis compatible with empirical realism and the deobjectification of totality.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-02","text":"The subject–object opposition becomes an internal differentiation of one synthetic architecture whose subject occupies multiple irreducible roles without becoming a substance.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-03","text":"A priori origin is separated from unrestricted range, making boundaries enabling instruments rather than external restraints on an otherwise unlimited reason.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-04","text":"Truth and validity become stratified and topological, requiring each claim to be located by presentation, function, scope, object, and mode of assent.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-05","text":"Contradiction becomes a metacritical method for testing the validity of the alleged object and question before evaluating competing answers.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-06","text":"Nature divides into invariant formal lawfulness and empirical material determination, while world-totality is withdrawn as an object.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MME-07","text":"Metaphysics and history become recursive audits of reason’s claims, errors, jurisdictions, and future-oriented construction rather than inventories of transcendent being.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MME-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, mathematics, natural science, selfhood, world-totality, freedom, theology, morality, and system are coordinated around the validity and limits of rational claims.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B01","MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, receptivity, synthesis, imagination, apperception, understanding, judgment, and reason form a distributed circuit that constitutes objects and projects totalities.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry shifts from doctrinal assertion to provenance-testing, object-model criticism, jurisdictional allocation, and governance of claim-production.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality-for-cognition is a lawfully synthesized field of conditioned appearances, while time mediates constitution, regress, correction, and unavailable completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system constructs rational mechanisms, generates their excesses, reclassifies their functions, and derives limitations from their enabling conditions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_BA94_L2_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-01","text":"Reason must validate criticism through the powers under examination even though universal criticizability denies the tribunal an unrevisable foundation.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-02","text":"Cognition presupposes affection by independently constituted objects whose passage into formal representation remains underexplained.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-02","MC_BA94_L2_01:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-03","text":"Claims of categorial and architectonic completeness depend on inventories whose completeness is asserted while substantive inquiry remains permanently unfinished.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-04","text":"Possible experience is constituted by transcendental principles while simultaneously serving as their validating field, producing reflexive dependence rather than external proof.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-05","text":"Judgment must remain irreducible to rules, while regulative principles exercise axiom-like force whose precise authority over inquiry remains underdetermined.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:FL-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-06","text":"Speculative limitation enables practical authority but does not itself establish the efficacy, necessity, or exclusivity of particular practical postulates.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:GA-11","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:FL-07","text":"Practical freedom and intelligible grounding must remain compatible with empirical lawfulness despite the absence of any guaranteed harmony or resolved relation between them.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_02:DBN-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from independence from experience into the structural condition, juridical boundary, and differentiated authorization of possible experience.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-02","text":"Synthesis mutates from a logical relation into the operation generating objecthood and then into dialectical totalization when absolutized.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-03","text":"Objectivity mutates from apparent correspondence into invariant synthesis, valid application, and temporally procedural constitution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-04","text":"The subject mutates from presumed bearer into an integrating function and then into a finite architect, accused party, legislator, critic, and believer.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-05","text":"Time mutates from intuitive form into schematic transformation medium and then into a vector of recursive diagnosis and future completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-06","text":"Critique and negation mutate from limitation into the positive governance that generates, secures, and differentiates cognition, belief, and system.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MP-06","MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MP-07","text":"Closure mutates from possession of substantive totality into formal self-limitation, open inquiry, and prospective practical-architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_BA94_L2_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a gated recursive court-engine that receives multiplicity, synthesizes objects, generates totalizing claims, and adjudicates them across stratified jurisdictions.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite switching-center, regressor, legislator, defendant, and critic situated inside the system it attempts to judge.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:HSI-03","text":"Time is the transformation medium and recursive vector through which succession becomes objectivity, renewed conditioning, retrospective correction, and never-present completion.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:HSI-04","text":"Struggle consists in routing claims through presentation and jurisdiction while navigating between dogmatic possession and sceptical suspension.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-04","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:HSI-05","text":"The resulting world is a lawful but empirically open interior whose unity depends on adjudicated coordination and whose boundary generates an unknowable remainder.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:HSI-05","MC_BA94_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from synthetic a priori authorization through phenomenal constitution to dialectical projection, critical restriction, and functional redistribution.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B02","text":"The system is coherent only if receptivity, synthesis, apperception, theoretical cognition, regulation, practical necessity, and architectonic legitimacy remain distinct but recursively coordinated.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where phenomenal object-generation and dialectical diagnosis converge on one boundary logic that simultaneously authorizes and restricts reason.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B04","text":"The affection–form relation, exact mediation mechanism, categorial completeness, regulative force, practical postulation, and intelligible grounding remain underdetermined.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive sequencing, projection, conflict, restriction, functional return, and renewed criticism.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B05","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_BA94:MRT-B06","text":"Finite reason’s capacity to discipline itself without an external standpoint remains the indispensable element resistant to complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["MC_BA94_L2_01:MRT-B06","MC_BA94_L2_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","context":"","original_title":"The Critique of Pure Reason"},"original_title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_BA94_D478443A55A558525B9C","version_work_id":"WORK_TRIAL_84B8A4537CF74654B5E46D40D58586CB","version_label":"Version 3 · Build · Added 7 Oct 2026 · Run 7 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-sol / gpt-5.6-terra","version_revision":"dae58ba6337cb3129d462c3698501bf2061d0c63a98898a26dfbd797744f08cd","original_title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true}},{"slug":"hume","title":"A Treatise of Human Nature","author":"David Hume","edition":"Original English · Gutenberg eText #4705 · retained 2012 transcription","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/4705","year":"1739–1740","description":"How do perceptions become beliefs, a self, and a world held in common?","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-d0138d38bb5ad732-8308e15450bed635.png","id":"WORK_A_TREATISE_OF_HUMAN_NATURE_DAVID_HUME","seal":"SEAL_THN_B3917927880001FF07EB","units":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR","layer":"CH","title":"Introduction","order":1,"parents":["RAW_THN_INTR"],"revision":"CH_THN_INTR@R001","sha256":"dd8ac7fb96e4845069ae1d62bb4f83c69ee978f2a08d3932b23d8bab63f6e4d5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS1","text":"The governing problem is the absence of evidentially secure and internally coherent foundations across the sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS2","text":"Scientific disorder displaces rational adjudication with controversy and rhetorical persuasion, thereby discrediting philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS3","text":"The resulting rejection of abstruse reasoning mistakes previous failure for proof that difficult inquiry is illegitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS4","text":"Human nature is the common epistemic center of the sciences because every science is cognized and judged through human faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS5","text":"The science of human nature must itself be reconstructed through experience and observation rather than speculation about mental essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS6","text":"Moral experimentation must use naturally occurring conduct because deliberate self-experiment alters the operations under investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS7","text":"The argument is diagnostic, polemical, and reconstructive: foundational crisis → anti-metaphysical prejudice → anthropological recentering → experimental method → epistemic limit → observational protocol.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A1","text":"Inherited philosophical systems rest on inadequately warranted and incoherently connected principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A2","text":"Where decisive evidence is absent, eloquence can govern assent more effectively than reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A3","text":"Repeated failure in abstruse inquiry produces indiscriminate aversion to metaphysical reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A4","text":"If truth is within human capacity, its depth makes sustained abstruse inquiry necessary rather than dispensable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A5","text":"Every science is cognized and judged through human powers and faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A6","text":"The most humanly important sciences directly concern reasoning, ideas, sentiments, tastes, and social dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A7","text":"Every important scientific decision depends on prior acquaintance with human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A8","text":"The science of human nature can be securely founded only on experience and observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A9","text":"The essence of mind is no more cognitively available than the essence of external bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A10","text":"Mental powers and qualities are knowable only through their effects under differing circumstances and situations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A11","text":"Observed effects should be reduced to maximally universal principles and the simplest, fewest causes compatible with experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A12","text":"Inquiry into human nature cannot legitimately pass beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A13","text":"Hypotheses claiming access to the ultimate original qualities of human nature are presumptuous and chimerical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A14","text":"The experiential limit is common to every science and art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A15","text":"Recognition that reason has reached its utmost extent dissolves the demand for further explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A16","text":"Premeditated production of moral phenomena alters the natural principles being observed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A17","text":"Ordinary human conduct is the proper evidential field for moral experiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A18","text":"Judicious collection and comparison of ordinary-life observations can establish a science of human nature with non-inferior certainty and superior utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX1","text":"Established credit does not supply secure warrant for philosophical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX2","text":"The proliferation and intensity of disputes do not constitute certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX3","text":"Rhetorical attractiveness is not rational evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX4","text":"Metaphysical reasoning is not confined to a particular subject matter; the prejudice targets abstruse argument generally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX5","text":"Previous failures in difficult inquiry do not justify abandoning it without embracing comprehensive scepticism and indolence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX6","text":"Ease and obviousness are not presumptions in favor of philosophical truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX7","text":"Mental powers cannot be deduced from speculative knowledge of mental essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX8","text":"The pursuit of universal principles does not license passage beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX9","text":"Ultimate qualities of human nature are not legitimate objects of explanatory hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX10","text":"Moral experiments cannot be validly produced by deliberately placing oneself in the condition under examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX11","text":"Ignorance of ultimate principles is not a defect peculiar to the science of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D1","text":"Systemic evidential disorder both discredits philosophy and generates overbroad rejection of difficult reasoning.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A2","CH_THN_INTR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D2","text":"Past failure supplies no warrant for methodological indolence if truth remains humanly attainable.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A3","CH_THN_INTR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D3","text":"Reform of the science of human nature becomes the precondition for reforming the sciences dependent upon human reasoning, sentiment, and sociality.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A6","CH_THN_INTR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D4","text":"Investigation of mind must proceed from circumstance and effect to powers and operations, not from essence to deduced consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D5","text":"Empirical generalization may compress effects into simple causes but may not convert causal economy into an account of ultimate origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D6","text":"Confessed ignorance at the experiential boundary constitutes legitimate closure rather than a unique failure of moral philosophy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A14","CH_THN_INTR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D7","text":"Moral experimentalism must be naturalistic and observational rather than deliberately interventionist.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D8","text":"Comparison across ordinary circumstances supplies the variation from which general principles of human nature may be inferred.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D9","text":"A successful science of human nature acquires exceptional utility because its closest dependent sciences govern reasoning, evaluation, and social life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A6","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD1","text":"The science of human nature becomes a metascience governing the possible security of other sciences while remaining empirically constrained itself.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD2","text":"Foundational security is redefined from access to essence into disciplined knowledge of recurrent effects within experiential limits.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD3","text":"Reflexivity does not defeat moral science; it relocates experimentation from constructed situations to naturally occurring social life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD4","text":"Scepticism receives a selective function: it destroys inherited pretensions and ultimate hypotheses but cannot license abandonment of abstruse inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A4","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_INTR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD5","text":"Scientific certainty and ultimate ignorance can coexist because certainty concerns observed regularities while ignorance concerns their final origin.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A14","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD6","text":"Humanity becomes both the medium through which science operates and the object whose investigation is required to secure that operation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX1","text":"Scientific objects and standards are not intelligible independently of the faculties by which humans apprehend and judge them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","text":"Knowledge of mental operations does not require prior access to an underlying mental substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX3","text":"Human conduct cannot be irreducibly singular or unstable; recurrent relations across circumstances must be available to comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX4","text":"Controlled intervention cannot be a necessary condition of all scientific warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX5","text":"Premeditated introspection cannot be assumed to leave the operations under inspection unchanged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX6","text":"A science need not justify its most general experiential principles through a deeper non-experiential ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX1","text":"Ultimate original qualities remain unavailable, and their absence functions as the terminal boundary of legitimate explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX2","text":"No science can occupy an unmediated standpoint outside human nature from which human cognition could be judged independently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX3","text":"A pure moral experiment separating observer, intervention, and observed operation is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX4","text":"The proposed foundation of the sciences has no further foundation beyond experienced reality and the regularities abstracted from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS1","text":"Insecure principles → defective deductions → unresolved controversy → rhetorical assent → philosophical discredit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS2","text":"Philosophical discredit → indiscriminate anti-metaphysical prejudice → conditional attainability of deep truth → reinstatement of abstruse inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS3","text":"Mediation of every science by human faculties → common dependence on human nature → strategic redirection toward the science of man.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS4","text":"Unavailable mental essence → observation of effects across circumstances → inference to powers and operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS5","text":"Comparative observations → causal simplification → maximally general empirical principles → bounded scientific system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS6","text":"Claim to ultimate qualities → transgression of experiential limits → rejection of hypothesis → confessed ignorance as closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS7","text":"Deliberate moral intervention → reflexive disturbance → ordinary conduct as experimental field → judicious comparison → moral science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AF1","text":"Foundational disorder → rhetorical controversy → anti-metaphysical prejudice → defense of difficult inquiry → human nature as common center → experience as foundation → ultimate explanation excluded → ordinary life converted into the field of moral experiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME1","text":"Disciplinary disorder is reclassified from a plurality of local failures into a common foundational deficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME2","text":"Human nature is repositioned from one scientific object among others to the epistemic center of the sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME3","text":"Metaphysics is divided into legitimate abstruse empirical reasoning and illegitimate speculation about ultimate qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME4","text":"Philosophical foundation is reclassified from knowledge of essences into observed regularities and constrained causal generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME5","text":"Ordinary social life becomes an experimental archive rather than merely the domain to which theory is applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME6","text":"The difference between natural and moral science becomes a difference in experimental access rather than a difference in possible scientific status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME7","text":"The opposition between certainty and confessed ignorance collapses once certainty is assigned to experienced relations and ignorance to ultimate origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT1","text":"Surface — Existing sciences lack secure principles, coherent deductions, and rationally decisive outcomes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT2","text":"Structural — Human faculties mediate every scientific judgment, making human nature the common dependency beneath disciplinary plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Inquiry is redirected from piecemeal disciplinary conquest toward the central investigation of human nature and its downstream sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essences and ultimate origins remain inaccessible; reality enters inquiry as effects under circumstances, while experimental philosophy extends historically from nature to humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from disciplinary crisis through anthropological recentering to observational moral science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT2","text":"The specific taxonomy of human faculties, the rules of observational comparison, and the criterion of adequate certainty remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as inaccessible essence, empirical method, causal compression, and explanatory limitation mutually constrain one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT4","text":"The strongest mechanism is the conversion of reflexive interference from an objection against moral science into a reason for naturalistic observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT5","text":"The transition from human mediation of science to the exclusive foundational priority of the science of man is asserted more strongly than it is demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT6","text":"The promise of non-inferior certainty depends on ordinary-life observations supplying sufficient control, representativeness, and comparability despite the prohibition on constructed experiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT7","text":"The universal prohibition on passing beyond experience risks functioning as a methodological principle whose own experiential warrant is not supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT8","text":"Foundational recursion remains unresolved because the science intended to secure human cognition must already employ cognition, observation, and comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement without the martial, patriotic, and honorific rhetoric, none of which generates the methodological conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"Introduction","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Introduction","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"Introduction","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","order":2,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIOO"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIOO@R001","sha256":"bcf40194b70e4074c589b58c2958589f8b807bb364d3b8645afd1092931c051a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the kinds of mental perception and the direction of origin between them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS2","text":"Core thesis: simple ideas, on first appearance, derive from prior corresponding simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","text":"Argument type: introspective taxonomy → empirical genealogy → anti-innatist reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS4","text":"Initial impression–idea resemblance is restricted through the simple–complex distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","text":"Correspondence plus constant conjunction establishes dependence; temporal priority fixes impression-to-idea causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS6","text":"Deprivation and recursive copying qualify the maxim; the missing-shade anomaly is contained rather than permitted to revise it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A1","text":"Every mental perception is either an impression or an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A2","text":"Impressions are perceptions at first appearance marked generally by greater force and liveliness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A3","text":"Ideas are fainter perceptions operating in thinking and reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A4","text":"Every impression and idea is either simple or complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A5","text":"Simple perceptions admit no distinction or separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A6","text":"Complex perceptions are formed from distinguishable simple parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A7","text":"Every simple perception has a correspondent in the other perception class that matches its content and differs only in force and liveliness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A8","text":"Complex ideas and complex impressions need not correspond as exact wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A9","text":"Constant conjunction between corresponding perceptions establishes dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A10","text":"Within a dependent pair, temporal priority identifies causal direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A11","text":"A simple impression precedes its corresponding idea on first appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A12","text":"Unavailable operation of an impression-producing faculty entails absence of the corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A13","text":"Every simple idea on first appearance derives from its corresponding simple impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A14","text":"Secondary ideas may copy prior ideas while remaining mediately derived from impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A15","text":"A missing shade can be imaginatively supplied as a simple idea without its corresponding prior impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A16","text":"A singular counterinstance is insufficient to alter the generally established derivation maxim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX1","text":"Impression does not name the mechanism by which a lively perception is produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX2","text":"Idea does not name every mental perception indiscriminately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX3","text":"The impression–idea distinction is not infallibly discriminable by vivacity in every exceptional state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX4","text":"Exact whole-to-whole correspondence is not universal among complex perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX5","text":"Merely thinking an idea does not ordinarily produce its corresponding sensation, passion, or emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX6","text":"The conjunction of impressions and ideas is not treated as accidental or causally neutral.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX7","text":"Secondary ideation is not a reversal of the impression-to-idea genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX8","text":"The missing-shade case is not allowed to repeal the general maxim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX9","text":"Ideas preceded by corresponding lively perceptions are not innate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D1","text":"The perception field is organized by two intersecting distinctions: impression versus idea and simple versus complex. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A1 + CH_THN_SIOO:A4 + CH_THN_SIOO:A5 + CH_THN_SIOO:A6)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D2","text":"Failure of exact complex correspondence does not defeat exact correspondence at the simple level. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A6 + CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A8)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D3","text":"Corresponding simple impressions and ideas form dependent rather than accidental pairs. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A9)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D4","text":"Impressions are causes and first ideas are effects. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A9 + CH_THN_SIOO:A10 + CH_THN_SIOO:A11)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D5","text":"Normal acquisition of a simple idea requires operative access to its corresponding impression. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A12 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D6","text":"Complex imagination may exceed experienced wholes without exceeding impression-derived simple content. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A6 + CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A8 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D7","text":"Ideas may generate further ideas without becoming the ultimate source of their simple content. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A13 + CH_THN_SIOO:A14)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D8","text":"The admitted missing-shade case converts the derivation principle logically into an exception-tolerant general maxim. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A13 + CH_THN_SIOO:A15 + CH_THN_SIOO:A16)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D9","text":"The dispute over innate ideas is reconstructed as a question of whether ideas are preceded by livelier perceptions. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A2 + CH_THN_SIOO:A3 + CH_THN_SIOO:A11 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","text":"Empirical restriction at the level of simple content coexists with recombinatorial freedom at the level of complex ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","text":"The origin of an idea becomes a temporal-causal question rather than a question settled by its apparent content alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD3","text":"The missing-shade case reveals that relations among acquired ideas may generate an unexperienced simple candidate, but the system quarantines this generativity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","text":"The proof of the copy principle employs constant conjunction and succession as causal criteria before the promised full examination of causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD5","text":"Where force and liveliness cease to discriminate impressions from ideas, genealogical order must carry the classificatory burden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX1","text":"Simple ideational content cannot generally originate from an autonomous productive intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX2","text":"Complex wholes cannot function as irreducible primitive contents independent of simple perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX3","text":"Causal symmetry between impressions and ideas cannot hold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX4","text":"Ideas cannot normally compensate for the total absence of the faculties required to produce their corresponding impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX5","text":"A singular counterexample cannot be permitted the logical force ordinarily sufficient to defeat an unrestricted universal proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX1","text":"Foreclosed possibility: the mind as an original source of primitive simple representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX2","text":"Quarantined possibility: relations among acquired perceptions constitute a general power for synthesizing genuinely new simple qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX3","text":"Unavailable foundation: an exceptionless demonstration of the copy principle independent of introspective induction and challenge to produce a counterexample.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1","text":"Mental perception → force-and-liveliness partition → impression / idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS2","text":"Each perception class → separability test → simple / complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS3","text":"Initial universal resemblance → complex counterinstances → restriction of exact correspondence to simple perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS4","text":"Simple correspondence + constant conjunction → dependence; first-appearance priority → impressions cause ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS5","text":"Obstructed impression-faculty → absent impression + absent corresponding idea → confirmation of derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS6","text":"Idea → secondary idea → recursive copying without reversal of ultimate impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","text":"Missing-shade counterinstance → concession → singularity filter → maxim retained → innateness dispute translated into perceptual genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","text":"Undifferentiated perceptions → impression–idea distinction → simple–complex distinction → failure of universal complex copying → exact simple correspondence → causal dependence → impression priority → derivation maxim → anomaly containment → anti-innatist reframing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME1","text":"Mental contents become classifiable along two independent axes: vivacity and compositional separability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME2","text":"Feeling and thinking become different intensities of corresponding perceptual content rather than wholly different kinds of content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME3","text":"Novel complex imagination is separated from the production of novel primitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME4","text":"Sensory or affective deprivation becomes a genealogical test for the availability of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME5","text":"Innateness is reclassified from an obscure metaphysical status into a question of perceptual precedence and derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT1","text":"Surface: perceptions are classified as impressions or ideas and as simple or complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT2","text":"Structural: simple correspondence, conjunction, and temporal priority produce a causal-copy model of mental content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the vocabulary of innate ideas is displaced by an empirically testable genealogy of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: mental content is modeled as a succession of perceptions whose causal status depends on order of appearance, while corresponding thought and feeling differ primarily by degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the taxonomy, causal derivation, qualification, and anti-innatist reframing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT2","text":"The criteria for simplicity, exact correspondence, and measurable force or liveliness remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent as a general empirical genealogy but not as an exceptionless universal system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where temporal priority and deprivation converge on the same direction of dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5","text":"The causal inference compresses dependence into constant conjunction and succession before causation itself has been examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6","text":"The missing-shade case directly conflicts with the unrestricted wording of the first principle and is managed by rarity rather than resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if the missing-shade anomaly and the deferred theory of causation remain explicit structural fault lines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 02"},"original_title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","order":3,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIDS"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIDS@R001","sha256":"5ca2887122328300e80be704688f596ced4d0676c1c4c90d6cfea047dcab0d9f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS1","text":"The argument is classificatory, genealogical, and methodological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS2","text":"The problem is to determine the proper order for examining impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS3","text":"The general priority of simple impressions over correspondent ideas initially requires impressions-first inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS4","text":"The division of impressions into sensation and reflection replaces undifferentiated priority with a stratified genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS5","text":"The operative sequence is sensation → idea-copy → reflective impression → reflective idea → possible recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS6","text":"Because sensations are disciplinarily deferred and reflective impressions mostly arise from ideas, the inquiry must begin with ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A1","text":"Simple impressions generally precede their correspondent ideas, notwithstanding rare exceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A2","text":"Impressions divide into impressions of sensation and impressions of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A3","text":"Impressions of sensation arise originally in the soul from unknown causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A4","text":"An idea is a mental copy that remains after its originating impression ceases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A5","text":"Returned ideas of pleasure and pain produce desire, aversion, hope, and fear as impressions of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A6","text":"Memory and imagination copy impressions of reflection into correspondent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A7","text":"Resulting ideas may generate further impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A8","text":"An impression of reflection is posterior to and derived from sensation while antecedent to its own correspondent idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A9","text":"Passions, desires, and emotions are the principal objects of the present inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A10","text":"Impressions of reflection arise mostly from ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A11","text":"Examination of sensations belongs to anatomy and natural philosophy rather than the present moral inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A12","text":"Method requires the operative antecedents of the focal object to be examined before that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX1","text":"Ideas are not ordinarily prior to their correspondent simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX2","text":"The original causes of sensory impressions are not known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX3","text":"Impressions of reflection are not original sensory occurrences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX4","text":"Reflective impressions are not prior to the sensations from which they derive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX5","text":"Ideas are not causally inert copies; returned ideas can produce new impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX6","text":"The present inquiry is not an anatomical or natural-philosophical investigation of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX7","text":"General impression-priority does not require every kind of impression to precede ideas in the order of exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D1","text":"Impressions-first inquiry is the default method when simple impressions and their correspondent ideas are considered without further division.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D2","text":"Priority cannot be assigned uniformly to impressions as an undifferentiated class.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D3","text":"Ideas mediate the derivation of reflective impressions from sensory impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D4","text":"The copy relation can enter a recursive production sequence rather than terminate in a single idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A6","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D5","text":"Sensations can be deferred without abandoning the principal objects of the inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D6","text":"Ideas must be examined before the reflective impressions principally under investigation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","text":"Ideas are derivative relative to sensation but productive relative to reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","text":"Temporal priority is relational: each item may be posterior to one stratum while anterior to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","text":"The methodological reversal changes the relevant reference class rather than contradicting the priority of simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD4","text":"The perception-chain is recursively extensible but asymmetrically grounded in sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD5","text":"Moral inquiry begins from an intermediate representational mechanism while leaving the ultimate sensory origin unexplained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX1","text":"Explanation of reflective life must not require prior resolution of the unknown causes of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX2","text":"The principal reflective impressions cannot be predominantly independent of antecedent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX3","text":"Chronological firstness alone cannot determine method once the object and disciplinary scope are restricted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX4","text":"Rare exceptions must not invalidate the general precedence of simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX5","text":"Recursive production must not be mistaken for a self-originating circle detached from sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1","text":"A complete account of the first cause of perception is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2","text":"A purely immediate and representation-free account of passion is foreclosed as the primary moral model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX3","text":"A single invariant order governing every relation between impressions and ideas is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4","text":"Exceptionless closure is withheld by the qualifiers governing priority, derivation, and recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS1","text":"General precedence: simple impression → correspondent idea → default impressions-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS2","text":"Class division: impression → sensation or reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","text":"Genetic sequence: unknown cause → sensory impression → idea-copy → returned pleasure-or-pain idea → reflective impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4","text":"Recursive extension: reflective impression → memory-or-imagination copy → reflective idea → possible further impression or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS5","text":"Scope filter: sensation → anatomy and natural philosophy; reflection → present moral inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS6","text":"Local methodological output: ideas as antecedents of reflection → ideas-first exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AF1","text":"General impression-priority → division of impressions → stratified genealogy → relative-priority correction → disciplinary exclusion of sensation → concentration on reflection → ideas-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME1","text":"“Impression” is reclassified from a uniform category into kinds distinguished by origin and dependency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME2","text":"“Idea” is reclassified as both a copy of an antecedent impression and a cause of subsequent reflective impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME3","text":"Priority becomes indexed to a specific correspondence and genealogical stratum rather than assigned absolutely to a whole class.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME4","text":"Order of exposition is detached from absolute origin and governed by the dependencies of the selected object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT1","text":"Surface: impressions are divided, and ideas are selected as the starting point of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT2","text":"Structural: copying and return mediate a recursive sequence between sensation, idea, reflection, and further ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: disciplinary jurisdiction converts the natural impressions-first order into an ideas-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: perception has an unknown sensory origin, layered temporal precedence, and recursively productive mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the classification, genealogy, and methodological reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT2","text":"The causes of sensation and the rare exceptions to impression-priority remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3","text":"The mechanism by which an idea “returns” and produces reflection is asserted rather than explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent if genetic order, correspondent order, and expository order remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT5","text":"The strongest inferential joint is the derivation of ideas-first inquiry from the idea-dependence of the focal reflective impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT6","text":"The principal fault line is that sensation supplies the indispensable foundation while being excluded from present analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7","text":"The argument depends on compressed qualifiers—rarely, mostly, and perhaps—that prevent universal closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if the final reversal is treated as local and methodological rather than as a denial of sensory precedence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","original_context":"Chapter 03"},"original_title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","order":4,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIIM"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIIM@R001","sha256":"c77c75966dc93d81fc13f9073b99a4625607eabe71fdfa1d7914927bd14c330f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish memory from imagination when both consist of impressions returning as ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","text":"The argument is empirical-taxonomic and reconstructive: experienced differences among ideas are assigned to distinct faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS3","text":"Both faculties remain subject to a common antecedent-impression constraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS4","text":"The first differential is vivacity: memory retains considerable force, whereas imagination loses it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS5","text":"The second differential is relational fidelity: memory conserves original form and order, whereas imagination can transpose them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS6","text":"Imaginative liberty is generated from copy-dependence combined with distinction, separability, and simple-complex composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS7","text":"The movement is recurrence → common origin → force distinction → order distinction → combinatorial explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A1","text":"A previously present impression can later reappear in the mind as an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A2","text":"Every idea is copied from a corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A3","text":"Memory repeats an impression as an idea retaining a considerable degree of its original vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A4","text":"Imagination repeats an impression as an idea after its original vivacity has been lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A5","text":"Proper memory preserves the original form, order, and position of its objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A6","text":"A departure in recollection from the original arrangement arises from defect or imperfection in memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A7","text":"Imagination is free to transpose and change copied ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A8","text":"No two impressions are perfectly inseparable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A9","text":"Any difference perceived by imagination among ideas permits their separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A10","text":"Ideas are divided into simple and complex forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","text":"Neither memory nor imagination can supply an idea without a prior corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX2","text":"Memory is not free to alter the original relations among remembered objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX3","text":"Imagination is not bound to reproduce the original conjunction or order of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","text":"Imaginative freedom is not the creation of unprecedented simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX5","text":"An altered order of exposition is not an altered remembered order when the original positions remain acknowledged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D1","text":"Memory and imagination differ by mode of recurrence rather than by the origin of their contents. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A1 + CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A3 + CH_THN_SIIM:A4)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D2","text":"Memory ideas are more forceful, lively, distinct, and stable than imagination ideas. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A3 + CH_THN_SIIM:A4)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D3","text":"Memory’s characteristic work is preservation of relational order and position rather than preservation of simple contents as such. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A5 + CH_THN_SIIM:A10)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D4","text":"Deliberate narrative reordering remains compatible with memory when due positions are retained. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A5 + CH_THN_SIIM:A6)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D5","text":"Imagination can form unexperienced complexes by separating and rearranging impression-derived components. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A7 + CH_THN_SIIM:A9 + CH_THN_SIIM:A10)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D6","text":"Perceived difference becomes compositional liberty because distinguishable components are separable rather than necessarily conjoined. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A7 + CH_THN_SIIM:A8 + CH_THN_SIIM:A9)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","text":"Mental recurrence is organized along two axes—vivacity and relational fidelity—rather than by differences in elemental source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","text":"Memory conserves experienced temporal and positional order, while imagination releases copied contents from that order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","text":"Imagination is dependent at the level of elements but free at the level of arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD4","text":"The faculty boundary is maintained asymmetrically: mnemonic disorder counts as failure, while imaginative disorder counts as successful liberty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","text":"Copy-dependence is not relational determinism; derivation from impressions does not require preservation of their conjunctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX2","text":"A component idea’s identity is not constituted by the complex whole in which its impression originally appeared.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX3","text":"Distinguishability cannot coexist with an unbreakable necessary union among ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX4","text":"Mnemonic variation cannot count as an equally proper mode of memory without dissolving the fidelity criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","text":"Absolute mental spontaneity is unavailable: productivity begins only after impression-given materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX2","text":"Necessary continuity between experienced world-order and thinkable world-order is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX3","text":"Mixed cases in which vivacity and order-fidelity point toward different faculties remain unformalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","text":"Independent access to the original arrangement by which mnemonic fidelity is judged is absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS1","text":"Present impression → later recurrence as corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS2","text":"Recurrence with considerable retained vivacity → memory → force, distinctness, and relative stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS3","text":"Recurrence after loss of original vivacity → imagination → faintness and instability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS4","text":"Preservation of original form, order, and position → proper memory; altered remembered order → mnemonic defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS5","text":"Perceived difference + absence of perfect inseparability → separable idea-components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS6","text":"Separable components + simple-complex composition → transposition and changed conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS7","text":"Copy-fixed elements + variable arrangement → imaginative fiction without novel elemental content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AF1","text":"Experienced return of impressions as ideas → bifurcation by vivacity → common copy constraint → bifurcation by fidelity to original order → memory as positional conservation → imagination as transposition → separability as the ground of fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME1","text":"Ideas are reclassified from a homogeneous derivative class into faculties differentiated by force and operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","text":"Common provenance is separated from divergent treatment: identical source materials can support conservation or recombination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME3","text":"Memory is reclassified as an archive of relations and positions rather than merely a repository of simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME4","text":"Imaginative liberty is localized in arrangement rather than in the origination of content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","text":"Unnatural fictional objects become evidence of recombination rather than exceptions to impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT1","text":"Surface: Memory and imagination are distinguished by vivacity and fidelity to original arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT2","text":"Structural: The mind applies conservation or separation-recombination to common impression-derived materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The faculty distinction is secured by coding mnemonic variation as defect and fictional anomaly as lawful recombination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Ideas are posterior copies; memory preserves experienced succession and position, while imagination detaches contents from that succession without escaping derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the two faculties, their dual criteria, and the bounded liberty of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2","text":"The vivacity threshold remains underdetermined; memory is both an idea and intermediate between impression and fully diminished idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT3","text":"The relation between vivacity and order-fidelity is unspecified, leaving mixed cases unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent if copy-dependence governs elements while separability governs arrangements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT5","text":"The tightest inference is the derivation of novel complexes from copied but rearrangeable components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT6","text":"The memory principle depends on compressed appeal to common phenomena rather than an independent derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT7","text":"The move from perceived difference and non-inseparability to easy separation is asserted without specifying limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT8","text":"Mnemonic fidelity lacks an independent standard for recovering original order, while the classification of deviations as defects partially insulates the principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the text’s movement from experiential recurrence through dual differentiation to combinatorial closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","order":5,"parents":["RAW_THN_SICA"],"revision":"CH_THN_SICA@R001","sha256":"d15603460219e0b659086e422a41830bed5d13c1445bdcf3cabe89df7eea21e9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS1","text":"The central problem is how freely separable and recombinable ideas nevertheless form regular sequences and complexes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS2","text":"Uniformity requires universal associative principles that guide imagination without determining it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS3","text":"The argument is empirical, reconstructive, taxonomic, and methodologically anti-speculative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS4","text":"Free combination → inadequacy of chance → non-necessary associative force → three relational operators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS5","text":"Direct association expands into attenuated relational chains, with causation extending from production to power and social influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS6","text":"Inquiry terminates at original qualities of human nature and redirects itself from unknown causes to complex ideational effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A1","text":"Every simple idea is separable and recombinable by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A2","text":"The regular recurrence of simple ideas in complex formations requires a universal bond rather than chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A3","text":"Association is a gentle prevailing tendency that introduces one idea through another without compelling the transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A4","text":"Resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause and effect are the three associative qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A5","text":"Resemblance alone is sufficient to facilitate an imaginative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A6","text":"Custom transfers the regular contiguity of sensory succession into the imagination’s method of transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A7","text":"Cause and effect produce the strongest and most extensive associative relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A8","text":"Association propagates through relational intermediaries while weakening at each remove.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A9","text":"Causal association includes the production of an object’s existence, action, or motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A10","text":"A power to produce action establishes causal relation because its exertion through will remains possible and often probable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A11","text":"Relations of interest, duty, government, and subordination arise from powers by which persons influence one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A12","text":"Associative cohesion supplies within imagination the role played by inseparable connexion within memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A13","text":"The causes of associative attraction are mostly unknown and terminate here in original qualities of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A14","text":"Once a principle is experimentally established, inquiry should examine its effects rather than pursue obscure ultimate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A15","text":"Complex ideas, as the common objects of thought and reasoning, generally arise from associative union among simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A16","text":"Complex ideas divide into relations, modes, and substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX1","text":"Association is not an inseparable or necessary connexion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX2","text":"Association is not a condition without which imagination becomes unable to join ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX3","text":"Regular ideational composition is not attributable to chance alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX4","text":"Associative connexion is not confined to objects standing in an immediate relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX5","text":"Causation is not restricted to actual production or to the production of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX6","text":"The theory does not claim to explain the ultimate causes of associative principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX7","text":"Philosophical inquiry should not continue into obscure and uncertain speculation after experimental sufficiency is reached.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D1","text":"Mental order is compatible with combinatorial freedom because association governs probabilistically rather than necessarily.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D2","text":"Correspondence among languages follows from shared associative tendencies toward similar complex formations.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D3","text":"Relational proximity is inversely proportional to the number of intermediaries, making kinship distance representable as causal-chain length.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D4","text":"Social offices and hierarchies become associatively causal wherever positional power can be converted into another person’s action.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D5","text":"Association is admissible as an experimentally supported original principle even when its underlying mechanism remains unknown.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D6","text":"The investigation must proceed from the associative mechanism to the classes of complex ideas it generates.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SICA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD1","text":"The imagination is neither mechanically determined nor randomly discontinuous; it is free under recurrent natural tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD2","text":"The ideational field forms an attenuating network in which relation type and relational distance jointly regulate recall.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD3","text":"Causation becomes a bridge between natural production, counterfactual capacity, and institutional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD4","text":"A science of mind can map the production of complex thought through observable associative effects without possessing an ultimate metaphysics of mental connexion.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A12","CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX1","text":"Radical cross-contextual idiosyncrasy in the succession of ideas must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX2","text":"The opposition between necessary determination and pure chance must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX3","text":"No independent fourth principle may be required to explain ordinary associative transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX4","text":"Relational force cannot remain unchanged across indefinitely extended mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX5","text":"Social interest and duty cannot require an irreducibly normative bond wholly outside causal power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX6","text":"Adequate empirical explanation cannot depend upon access to the ultimate mechanism of every observed principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absence of necessary mental connexion, whose functional place is occupied by gentle attraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","text":"The origin of associative attraction remains unavailable; “original qualities of human nature” names the terminal explanatory absence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX3","text":"The possibility of further primitive associative sources is excluded by the triadic taxonomy without being independently refuted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX4","text":"The nature of causation remains deferred even while causation carries the greatest explanatory scope within the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS1","text":"Free separability and recombination + recurrent complex formations → demand for a non-random ordering principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS2","text":"Ordering without compulsion → gentle associative force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS3","text":"Associative force → resemblance / contiguity / cause and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS4","text":"Immediate relation + intermediary relations → extended but progressively weakened associative chains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS5","text":"Causal production → action and existence → power to produce → social influence and hierarchy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS6","text":"Observable effects + unknown ultimate causes → methodological arrest at original human qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS7","text":"Associative cohesion → complex ideas → relations / modes / substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AF1","text":"Freedom of combination → recurrent uniformity → insufficiency of chance → gentle universal association → triadic operators → mediated attenuation → causal expansion into power → methodological limit → taxonomy of complex effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME1","text":"Imagination is reclassified from an arbitrary combinatorial faculty into a free faculty regulated by natural tendencies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME2","text":"Necessary connexion and random succession cease to exhaust the possible models of mental order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME3","text":"Ideational connexion becomes graded and transmissible rather than merely present or absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME4","text":"Causation expands from actual event-production to latent power and institutional command.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME5","text":"Complex ideas become generated effects of associative operations rather than primitive mental units.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME6","text":"Philosophical explanation is redirected from inaccessible origins toward experimentally observable effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT1","text":"Surface: One idea regularly recalls another, and simple ideas regularly form complexes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT2","text":"Structural: Three relational operators organize transitions through direct and attenuated chains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The inquiry expands causation into social power while restricting philosophy to experimentally warranted effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Human nature supplies universal but non-necessary tendencies, while sensory succession and custom sediment spatial-temporal order into imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s central account of regularity within a freely combinatorial imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT2","text":"The core structure is coherent insofar as gentle tendency mediates between necessity and chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT3","text":"The exhaustiveness and universality of the three associative relations remain underdetermined rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT4","text":"Causation is assigned maximal explanatory force before its own nature is examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT5","text":"Contiguity is explained through acquired custom, while associative causes are classified as original qualities; the relation between acquisition and originality remains unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT6","text":"The argument is strongest in its graded model of mediated association and its separation of regularity from necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT7","text":"Its extension into social relations depends on compressing interest, duty, authority, and obedience into causal power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from combinatorial freedom through associative regulation to complex ideational effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","order":6,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIOQ"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIOQ@R001","sha256":"e4f6d92f6c618661d4df2f9202bcdcbf3378912b38990881cee3e7ed30dae00e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS1","text":"Problem — Determine the impression-origin and internal composition of the ideas of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS2","text":"Thesis — Space and time are not distinct impressions but ordered manners in which sensible or perceptual contents appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3","text":"Argument type — Empiricist genealogy joined to eliminative, separability, and compositional tests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS4","text":"Spatial movement — Sensible points → disposition → extension → abstraction across variable sensible qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS5","text":"Temporal movement — Successive perceptions → noncoexistent order → duration → exclusion of time without change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS6","text":"Closure — Indivisible spatial and temporal components remain conceivable only when filled by sensible quality or real existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","text":"Every idea is preceded by a correspondent impression that it copies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","text":"Only sight and touch supply impressions from which extension can be derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","text":"An extended sensible impression consists of indivisible colored or tangible points disposed in a determinate order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","text":"Every abstract idea is a particular idea considered under a shared aspect and attached to a general term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","text":"The idea of time originates in the succession of perceptions of every kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","text":"Time is the manner in which successive perceptions appear, not an additional impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","text":"The parts of duration are noncoexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","text":"The parts of extension are coexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A9","text":"Whatever is different is distinguishable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","text":"Whatever is distinguishable is separable in idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","text":"What is not distinguishable cannot be separated in idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","text":"An idea can properly represent only the objects or impressions from which it derives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","text":"The compound ideas of extension and duration resolve into simple indivisible component ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","text":"A real compound idea cannot be composed of nonentities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A15","text":"An indivisible spatial point is conceivable only through retained color or tangibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX1","text":"The idea of extension is not derived from passions, emotions, desires, or aversions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","text":"Extension is not an impression additional to colored or tangible points and their disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX3","text":"An abstract idea is not an indeterminate or contentless universal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX4","text":"Time cannot appear to the mind alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX5","text":"A steady and unchangeable object cannot originate the idea of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","text":"Time is neither an additional sensation nor an additional impression of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX7","text":"Duration cannot properly be predicated of an unchangeable object without fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX8","text":"A simple spatial point is neither itself extended nor a nonentity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX9","text":"A point deprived of every visible or tangible quality is unavailable to thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D1","text":"The idea of extension copies sensible points together with their relative disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","text":"Abstract extension suppresses variable sensible peculiarities only while retaining their shared disposition in a particular sensible representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D3","text":"One abstract idea of extension can represent both visible and tangible configurations because their parts share a disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D4","text":"Abstract time ranges over sensations, reflections, impressions, and ideas while remaining represented by a determinate succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D5","text":"Without successive perceptions there is no notion of time, even where objects undergo real succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D6","text":"Apparent duration varies with the rate and discriminability of successive perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D7","text":"Coexistence and noncoexistence provide the constitutive distinction between extension and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","text":"Time may be abstracted from particular successive objects but cannot be separated from some succession of objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","text":"Applying duration to an unchangeable object exceeds the impression from which duration derives and is therefore fictional.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D10","text":"The indivisible component of extension must be qualitatively sensible rather than extended or nonexistent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","text":"No idea of space or extension remains when all visible and tangible content is removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D12","text":"An indivisible moment must be filled by a real object or existence whose succession makes duration conceivable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1","text":"Space and time are higher-order arrangements of content rather than additional contents: space orders coexistent sensible points, while time orders successive existences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2","text":"Abstract universality is representational rather than imagistic: a determinate particular represents heterogeneous cases through selective regard and general denomination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD3","text":"Experienced temporality is constrained by the mind’s resolution and rate of perception rather than determined immediately by objective succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4","text":"Pure spatial and temporal minima are conceptually unavailable because every minimum must retain sensible quality or existential filling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5","text":"The genealogy of an idea functions as a limit on its legitimate application: predication cannot properly outrun the originating impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX1","text":"Space and time cannot be innate or independently given ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX2","text":"Repeated contemplation cannot generate a new simple idea unless a new original impression occurs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX3","text":"Abstraction cannot remove every particular determination while leaving a positive idea intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4","text":"A manner of appearance cannot be reified as an additional constituent of what appears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX5","text":"Common linguistic application cannot establish conceptual legitimacy independently of impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX6","text":"Objective occurrence alone cannot furnish an idea when the corresponding order is not perceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","text":"Pure empty spatiality is the absent object around which the spatial argument is organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0042","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2","text":"Bare duration without successive existence is excluded from possible conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX3","text":"Absolute time capable of measuring changeless being is foreclosed except as fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX4","text":"Direct cognitive access to space or time independently of perceptual content is unavailable to the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS1","text":"Copy principle → impression audit → elimination of reflective sources → restriction to sight and touch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS2","text":"Sensible minima → relative disposition → compound extension → spatial idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS3","text":"Recurrent disposition across variable qualities → selective omission → particular representative → abstract space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS4","text":"Heterogeneous perceptions → perceivable succession → noncoexistent order → temporal idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS5","text":"Absence of distinct temporal impression → non-distinguishability → non-separability from succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS6","text":"Genetic restriction → absence of temporal source in changeless objects → fictional application of duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS7","text":"Compound decomposition → indivisible component → exclusion of nonentity → sensible or existential filling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AF1","text":"Copy principle → source elimination → spatial derivation → abstraction mechanism → temporal derivation → separability test → exclusion of changeless duration → indivisible-component test → content-filled space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an independent receptacle into the disposition of visible or tangible minima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME2","text":"Time is reclassified from an independent medium into the perceivable succession of changing contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME3","text":"Abstraction is reclassified from production of a contentless universal into selective use of a particular idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME4","text":"Coexistence and noncoexistence replace independent substances as the primary distinction between space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5","text":"Changeless duration and qualityless spatial points are relocated from proper ideas to fiction or nonconception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6","text":"Questions concerning space and time are made answerable through impression-tracing rather than through unrestricted metaphysical postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0045","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate inquiry identifies the impressions and component qualities represented by ideas of extension and duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT2","text":"Structural — Copying, abstraction, separability, and decomposition govern the formation and limits of both ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Conceptual disputes are adjudicated by tracing ideas to impressions and rejecting applications that exceed that genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Spatiality and temporality become orders of existent content: coexistence for sensible points and succession for changing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the parallel genealogies of extension and duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2","text":"The claim that the senses present only sensible points and their disposition is asserted phenomenologically rather than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT3","text":"The terminal argument depends on the prior establishment of indivisible component ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT4","text":"Internal coherence is strongest where the copy principle, absence of a distinct impression, and inseparability test converge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT5","text":"The exact-copy principle strains against abstraction: abstract extension omits determinate color while every spatial representative must retain color or tangibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT6","text":"Temporal abstraction separates time from particular perceptions while denying its separation from some perceptions; the type-token mechanism remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT7","text":"The denial of duration to changeless objects depends on treating genetic origin as a binding semantic limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT8","text":"The argument establishes conditions of conception more securely than conditions of independent existence; its closing language partially compresses that distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0045","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s movement only if spatial disposition and temporal succession remain parallel but nonidentical structures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","original_context":"Chapter 06"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","order":7,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVIE"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVIE@R001","sha256":"59f7a851dd68eb552883d6142b5a1af280ac54a7b6e886514884b370f7544f6f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the origin and content of the ideas of existence and external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS2","text":"Argument type: empiricist genealogy conducted through an exhaustive dilemma, elimination, and conceptual boundary test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS3","text":"The impression-copy principle and the universal conception of perceptions as existent force existence to be either a distinct accompanying perception or identical with the object conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS4","text":"The absence of a distinct impression inseparably accompanying every perception eliminates the first alternative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","text":"Existence is identical with the idea of the object and adds nothing to its conceived content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS6","text":"Because only perceptions are present to the mind, no positive idea can represent an existence specifically different from perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS7","text":"Impression-origin test → exhaustive dilemma → elimination of distinct existence-content → identity thesis → perceptual closure → relative conception of externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A1","text":"Every idea arises from a similar antecedent impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A2","text":"Every impression or idea available to consciousness or memory is conceived as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A3","text":"No distinct impression is inseparably conjoined with every perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A4","text":"The idea of existence is identical with the idea of the object conceived as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A5","text":"A distinction without real difference requires one simple idea to bear different resemblance-relations to other ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A6","text":"Nothing is present to the mind except perceptions: impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A7","text":"External objects are known only through the perceptions they occasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A8","text":"An external object supposed specifically different from perceptions can be conceived only relatively, without comprehension of its intrinsic nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A9","text":"External objects are ordinarily differentiated from perceptions by attributed relations, connections, and durations rather than by a specifically different content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX1","text":"Existence is not derived from a distinct impression attached to every perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX2","text":"Existence is not an additional quality inserted into an object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX3","text":"Reflecting on an object simply is not distinct from reflecting on it as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX4","text":"Existence cannot be rescued as a distinction without difference, because presented objects supply no differential resemblance with respect to existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX5","text":"Loving, hating, thinking, feeling, and seeing are not modes of mental presence beyond perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX6","text":"No specifically extra-perceptual kind of existence can be positively represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX7","text":"Directing attention outward does not move conception beyond the universe of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX8","text":"Relative reference to external objects is not comprehension of those objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D1","text":"Conscious awareness supplies the maximal idea and assurance of being available within the argument.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D2","text":"The idea of existence must arise either from a distinct impression universally conjoined with perception or from the perception itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D3","text":"The distinct-impression alternative fails, leaving identity between existence and the conceived object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D4","text":"Conjoining existence with an object-idea produces no conceptual enlargement.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D5","text":"No contrast class permits existence to function as a distinction of reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D6","text":"Every positive conception remains composed of perceptual content already available within the mind.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D7","text":"Knowledge of external objects is necessarily mediated and cannot become direct presence of those objects to the mind.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVIE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D8","text":"Externality is available either as an uncomprehended relative correlate or as a redistribution of relations, connections, and durations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","text":"Being functions at the conceptual level as the universal status of presentation rather than as discriminating content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD2","text":"Conceivability guarantees existence-as-conceived but supplies no additional content establishing independent external actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD3","text":"Positive ontology is bounded by the inventory of possible perceptions; whatever exceeds that inventory cannot be conceived according to its specific nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","text":"External realism remains thinkable only through mediation, relational attribution, or an intrinsically unfilled correlate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX1","text":"An intellectual idea of being independent of impressions is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX2","text":"Existence cannot be both universally attached to perceptions and positively distinguishable from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX3","text":"Presented objects cannot differ in kind merely through possession or absence of conceived existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX4","text":"Attention and imagination cannot generate access to a domain specifically heterogeneous to perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX5","text":"Merely positing a specifically different external object cannot generate positive content for that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX6","text":"The requirement that relative reference comprehend its relatum is excluded by the permitted conception of external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","text":"The positive essence of mind-independent being is the governing absence: external existence is retained only where intrinsic comprehension is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","text":"No bare existential marker separates an imagined object from an independently existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3","text":"No representational passage connects the perceptual universe to a positively conceived heterogeneous realm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX4","text":"The external source said to occasion perceptions remains named through relation but unavailable as a comprehended kind of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5","text":"The intrinsic nature of external objects remains neither positively affirmable nor eliminable within the chapter’s conceptual resources.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS1","text":"Copy principle + universal existential conception → exhaustive origin dilemma.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS2","text":"Candidate universal existence-impression → test for distinctness and inseparability → failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS3","text":"Elimination of distinct impression → identity of existence with object-conception → zero conceptual addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS4","text":"Proposed distinction without difference → differential-resemblance test → absence of an existence contrast class → rejection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS5","text":"Perceptions alone present + ideas copied from prior perceptions → closure of positive conception within perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS6","text":"Specifically different external object posited → no qualitative comprehension → relative idea only.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS7","text":"Ordinary external attribution → altered relations, connections, and durations → no specifically different object-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AF1","text":"Question of existence-content → impression-origin dilemma → elimination of a distinct existence-impression → identity and non-addition → rejection of distinction by resemblance → perception-only horizon → impossibility of specifically external conception → relative or relational externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME1","text":"Existence is reclassified from a detachable predicate or quality into identity with conceived content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME2","text":"Existential predication loses the power to enlarge an idea or distinguish kinds of conceived objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME3","text":"The internal/external distinction is displaced from specific qualitative difference to mediation and relational organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME4","text":"The universe of imagination becomes the boundary of all positive conceptual content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME5","text":"External-object discourse divides into comprehensible relations and an incomprehensible related term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT1","text":"Surface: the chapter determines what the ideas of existence and external existence contain and from which impressions they derive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT2","text":"Structural: the copy principle, separability test, and perception-only presence rule regulate admissible concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: metaphysical claims are converted into demands for an originating impression, with unsupported positive content eliminated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: being adds no further essence to conceived objects, while external objects are ordinarily differentiated through relations, connections, and durations rather than through a separately conceived ontological kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVIE:A1 through CH_THN_SVIE:A9 are sufficient to reconstruct both the existence argument and its extension to external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT2","text":"The legitimacy of a relative idea whose relatum cannot be positively conceived remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if the copy principle, perceptual presence rule, and separability test are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT4","text":"The strongest operation is the elimination of a distinct existence-impression through the demand for a universal inseparable source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the difference between existence-as-conceived and independent actuality into an unresolved shift in the use of “existent.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT6","text":"The claim that external objects occasion perceptions invokes a causal relation to the very term whose specific nature cannot be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT7","text":"Because existence adds nothing to an idea, the criterion distinguishing fiction from external actuality is unavailable within this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from conceptual genealogy through eliminative identity to the negative boundary of external conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","context":"Chapter 07","original_title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","original_context":"Chapter 07"},"original_title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","order":8,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVNI"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVNI@R001","sha256":"b62fe1e03a05f25dd82d109c2fca7a5cef6a69805504a3bf535369760e46913c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS1","text":"Central problem: belief contains an object-idea yet exceeds mere conception; its surplus must be identified without adding representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS2","text":"Scope: matter-of-fact assent, whose contrary remains conceivable, rather than demonstrative determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS3","text":"Elimination: neither existence nor belief contributes a distinct idea or changes the composition of the object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS4","text":"Argument type: diagnostic elimination → phenomenological reconstruction → taxonomic revision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS5","text":"Core thesis: belief is an idea conceived with superior force, vivacity, firmness, or steadiness through relation to a present impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS6","text":"Direction: conception–belief gap → content invariance → modal difference → intensive definition → collapse of the scholastic taxonomy → experiential confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A1","text":"The idea of an object is necessary but insufficient for belief in that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A2","text":"Conceiving an object as existent adds nothing to the simple conception of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A3","text":"Believing an object to exist adds no idea and changes no part of the object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A4","text":"Both sides of a question concerning matter of fact remain intelligibly conceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A5","text":"Intuition and demonstration determine conception by rendering the contrary unintelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A6","text":"Believer and disbeliever can understand a proposition through the same constituent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A7","text":"Factual opinion requires a principle that fixes one conceivable alternative as the object of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A8","text":"Because the fixing principle adds no idea, it can alter only the manner of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A9","text":"All mental perceptions are impressions or ideas, distinguished only by degrees of force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A10","text":"Ideas copy impressions and represent their constituent parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A11","text":"An idea can vary while representing the same object only through a change in force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A12","text":"Belief is a strong, lively, and steady idea related or associated with a present impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A13","text":"Matter-of-fact inference requires a perception present to sense or memory as its foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A14","text":"The transition from a present perception to a believed idea is determined by custom or association, not reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A15","text":"Superior force gives believed ideas greater presence, weight, persistence, and influence over passion, imagination, and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A16","text":"Conception, judgment, and reasoning resolve into different ways of conceiving objects rather than compositionally distinct acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A17","text":"The distinctive manner of belief is ultimately given as a feeling that philosophical vocabulary can only approximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX1","text":"Belief is not the mere possession or comprehension of an object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX2","text":"Existence is not a separable idea added as a predicate to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX3","text":"Assent does not add ideas to the proposition assented to.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX4","text":"Matter-of-fact belief is not demonstrative necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX5","text":"Disbelief is not an inability to understand or conceive the rejected proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX6","text":"Judgment does not necessarily consist in uniting or separating two distinct ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX7","text":"Reasoning does not necessarily require a third idea mediating between two others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX8","text":"Imaginative recombination and circumstantial completeness cannot by themselves produce belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","text":"Belief and fiction are not distinguished by the nature, number, or order of their represented parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D1","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A2 + CH_THN_SVNI:A3 + CH_THN_SVNI:A6: bare conception, existential conception, and existential belief can preserve identical object-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D2","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A4 + CH_THN_SVNI:A5 + CH_THN_SVNI:A6: factual assent cannot be identified by contradiction, intelligibility, or semantic content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D3","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A7 + CH_THN_SVNI:A8 + CH_THN_SVNI:A11: the principle fixing opinion must operate through scalar intensification rather than representational modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D4","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A2 + CH_THN_SVNI:A16: existential judgments can contain only one object-idea, invalidating the universal model of judgment as the union of two ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D5","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A13 + CH_THN_SVNI:A14 + CH_THN_SVNI:A16: causal inference can pass directly from a present perception to a correlative idea without an interposed third idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D6","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A12 + CH_THN_SVNI:A15: belief distinguishes experienced reality from fiction by cognitive weight and practical influence despite representational identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D7","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A9 + CH_THN_SVNI:A11 + CH_THN_SVNI:A12: belief is not a third species of perception but an idea intensified toward the condition of an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D8","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A12 + CH_THN_SVNI:A17: force, vivacity, solidity, firmness, and steadiness are approximate names for one felt mode rather than separate constituents of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD1","text":"The experienced distinction between fact and fiction is relocated from representational content to the felt intensity and stability of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD2","text":"Conceptual enrichment cannot convert the possible into the actual because actuality contributes no detachable representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD3","text":"The architecture of understanding becomes operationally monistic: conception is the base act, differentiated by necessity, association, and intensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD4","text":"Matter-of-fact commitment depends on present experiential anchoring and associative determination rather than insight into necessary connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD5","text":"The epistemic authority and practical efficacy of belief arise from the same intensification that makes an idea persistent and action-governing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes an independent faculty of judgment that stamps truth onto an otherwise complete idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes any content-internal marker by which an idea announces the actual existence of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX3","text":"The system requires that reason disclose no necessary existential connection between distinct matters of fact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that imaginative detail or coherence alone can generate factual assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes non-intensive changes in the manner of conception that would preserve the identity of the represented object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX6","text":"The system denies irreducible boundaries between conception, judgment, and reasoning as fundamental mental operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX7","text":"The system requires vivid fiction to remain distinguishable from belief when it lacks the steadiness and experiential relation characteristic of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX1","text":"The foreclosed possibility is a purely conceptual passage from the idea of an object to its actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2","text":"No intrinsic representational sign separates the actual from the merely conceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX3","text":"A self-sufficient reason capable of constructing the factual world through necessity is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX4","text":"Complete discursive capture of belief is unavailable; explanation terminates in a commonly recognizable but indefinable feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","text":"The absent criterion is an exact threshold separating exceptionally vivid fiction from genuine belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS1","text":"Conception of object O is required for belief in O, but conception of O does not entail belief in O.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS2","text":"Content of simple conception = content of existential conception = content of existential belief; therefore belief cannot be an added idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS3","text":"Conceivable P + conceivable not-P + non-demonstrative selection of P → difference located in mode of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS4","text":"Same represented object + permissible variation only in force and vivacity → belief as intensified and stabilized conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS5","text":"Present perception → customary or associative transition → intensified related idea → factual assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS6","text":"Intensified idea → greater felt reality → increased influence on passion and imagination → governance of action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS7","text":"Conception as base operation / demonstration as necessary determination / factual judgment as conception plus belief / reasoning as transition among conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AF1","text":"Conception insufficient for belief → existence adds no idea → factual contraries remain conceivable → content cannot explain assent → difference relocated to manner → manner reduced to force and vivacity → belief defined through present-impression relation → judgment and reasoning reduced to modes of conception → definition confirmed by felt and practical effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME1","text":"Existence is reclassified from an added predicate into the object as conceived under a mode of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME2","text":"Belief is reclassified from a content-producing judgment into an intensive modification of an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME3","text":"Belief and disbelief become compatible with identical semantic comprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME4","text":"The boundary between judgment and imagination is redrawn through force, firmness, and influence rather than representational capacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","text":"The scholastic division of conception, judgment, and reasoning collapses into variations of one conceiving operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME6","text":"Demonstrative and factual assent are separated by their determining principles: unintelligibility of the contrary versus associative vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME7","text":"The connection between cognition and action becomes intrinsic to belief’s degree of force rather than dependent on additional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT1","text":"Surface: the immediate object is the difference between merely conceiving a proposition and believing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT2","text":"Structural: the distinction is generated within an impression–idea economy governed by force, vivacity, present perception, and association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the conventional taxonomy of understanding is displaced by a unified theory of conception differentiated by modal intensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: existence has no separable idea, while belief concerning absent objects depends on their present experiential or mnemonic anchoring.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVNI:A1–CH_THN_SVNI:A17 are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from representational invariance to the intensive definition of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT2","text":"The eliminative argument is strongest where identical content is shown to occur under conception, disbelief, and assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT3","text":"The positive reconstruction depends on the exclusivity of the impression–idea taxonomy and the claim that same-object variation is limited to force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT4","text":"The mechanism by which a present impression communicates vivacity through association remains compressed and is deferred beyond the section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5","text":"No exact degree or threshold distinguishing belief from exceptionally vivid imagination is supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT6","text":"The claim that imagination cannot produce belief is asserted through felt difference more than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT7","text":"The vocabulary of force, vivacity, firmness, solidity, and steadiness marks an acknowledged explanatory compression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT8","text":"The structure remains coherent only if intensification preserves representational identity while experiential relation preserves the distinction between belief and vivid fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the section’s real sequence: elimination of added content, relocation to manner, intensive definition, taxonomic revision, and phenomenological confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","context":"Chapter 08","original_title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","original_context":"Chapter 08"},"original_title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","order":9,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXIB"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXIB@R001","sha256":"3dd7c72030f744b31d0a2b07a97fce3a5229049f073f9550db612e6a9f0a2af7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS1","text":"Central problem: explain how custom and habit produce belief-effects extending across reasoning, action, passion, and imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS2","text":"Core thesis: belief is a simple idea intensified toward the force and vivacity of an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS3","text":"Belief mediates between motivationally insufficient present impressions and the intolerable activation of the will by every passing idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS4","text":"Belief, passion, and imagination reciprocally transmit vivacity and thereby reinforce one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","text":"Poetry and madness test the mechanism by producing impression-like vivacity without the customary basis of serious conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS6","text":"Argument type and movement: reconstructive naturalistic psychology; scale objection → motivational dilemma → scalar mechanism → reciprocal confirmation → counterfeit cases → rule-governed correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A1","text":"Pain and pleasure are the chief moving principles of human action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A2","text":"Impressions and ideas differ originally in their degrees of force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A3","text":"Differences in the effects of perceptions arise from differences in their force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A4","text":"Present impressions actuate the mind more strongly than mere ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A5","text":"Belief is almost necessary for an idea to excite the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A6","text":"Ideas of objects believed to exist or to be forthcoming influence action and passion in the same manner as impressions, but ordinarily in a lesser degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A7","text":"Belief is a more vivid and intense conception of an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A8","text":"Serious conviction arises when customary conjunction conveys vivacity from a present impression to a related idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A9","text":"An excited passion transfers its vivacity to the idea of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A10","text":"Forceful and vivid ideas are agreeable to the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A11","text":"Truth and reality affect imaginative enjoyment by securing easy reception and mental acquiescence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A12","text":"Vivacity can diffuse from one idea to other ideas related to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A13","text":"Repetition and familiarity can facilitate imaginative reception without producing judgmental belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A14","text":"A vigorous imagination can vivify ideas sufficiently to procure assent and authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A15","text":"Extraordinary bodily ferment can raise loose ideas to impression-like vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A16","text":"Poetical vivacity arises from present temperament rather than customary connections among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A17","text":"Reflection and general rules regulate assent by discounting vivacity recognized as accidental or disconnected from reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","text":"Education is not a philosophically valid ground of assent, despite its power to produce assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX2","text":"The magnitude of an effect does not require an apparently substantial psychological principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX3","text":"The will is not governed exclusively by present impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX4","text":"The will is not activated by every passing idea of good or evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX5","text":"Belief is not additional representational content attached to an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX6","text":"Literal truth and absolute assurance are not necessary conditions of imaginative satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX7","text":"Historical anchoring within fiction is not necessarily an attempt to deceive judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX8","text":"Vivacity transmitted through accidental poetic relations does not amount to perfect assurance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX9","text":"Poetical enthusiasm is not serious conviction, even when it produces stronger apparent agitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","text":"Apparent mental vehemence is not a reliable measure of genuine cognitive force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX11","text":"Every increase in an idea’s occurrent vivacity does not require a corresponding increase in settled belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D1","text":"Selective vivification permits absent and future goods or evils to motivate action without allowing every fiction to disturb the will.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A1","CH_THN_SXIB:A4","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D2","text":"Because believed ideas imitate the effects of impressions, they must approach impressions in force and vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A2","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D3","text":"A prevailing passion increases assent to representations suited to that passion by transferring its vivacity to them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D4","text":"Belief pleases the imagination by making represented objects enter the mind forcefully and without reluctance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A10","CH_THN_SXIB:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D5","text":"Familiarity can replace literal truth as a mechanism of imaginative admission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D6","text":"A believed or familiar narrative component can vivify connected fictional components without converting the whole fiction into assured belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D7","text":"Eloquence can generate assent by transferring imaginative vivacity into judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D8","text":"When bodily ferment gives every fiction impression-like force, truth and falsehood become operationally indistinguishable and act equally on the passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A2","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D9","text":"Poetically induced passions can entertain even when their real counterparts are painful because counterfeit belief excites the spirits without the weight of reality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A10","CH_THN_SXIB:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D10","text":"Reflection can dissipate poetical illusion by identifying its vivacity as accidental and reality-disconnected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D11","text":"General rules can sustain full conviction despite local reductions in felt force and can resist assent despite local increases in imaginative vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","text":"Belief, causal inference, motivation, passion, aesthetic reception, persuasion, and madness operate within a common economy of distributed mental force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD2","text":"Cognition and affect form a feedback system: belief excites passion, passion vivifies its object, and the vivified object strengthens belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD3","text":"Belief is not psychologically binary; inert fiction, poetic counterfeit belief, probable conviction, and impression-like certainty occupy differentiated positions within one mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","text":"The distinction between genuine and counterfeit conviction must be relocated from apparent intensity to causal provenance, felt character, and correction by general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD5","text":"The mechanism that enables action toward absent realities also exposes judgment to capture by temperament, eloquence, repetition, and pathological vivification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX1","text":"Belief cannot be a sui generis rational act independent of the manner in which an idea is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX2","text":"Representational content alone cannot determine motivational or affective efficacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX3","text":"Judgment, passion, and imagination cannot be causally sealed faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX4","text":"Truth cannot possess an intrinsic psychological mark that automatically guarantees assent or imaginative pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","text":"First-order vividness cannot authenticate its own epistemic legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX6","text":"Fiction and factual conviction cannot be separated by an impermeable boundary at the level of conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1","text":"The foreclosed possibility is an infallible immediate criterion by which consciousness always distinguishes genuine belief from its counterfeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a pure rational agency insulated from custom, bodily disposition, passion, and rhetoric.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","text":"No origin-independent warrant is supplied for the general rules that correct vivid appearances; their authority remains the terminal unformalized condition of epistemic discrimination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS1","text":"Impressions alone → no motivation by foreseen objects; every idea efficacious → perpetual disturbance; selectively vivified ideas → viable motivational medium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS2","text":"Present impression → customary relation → transferred force and vivacity → intensified idea → belief → action and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS3","text":"Affecting object → incipient passion → diffusion into imagination → intensified object-idea → assent → reinforced passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS4","text":"Believed or familiar anchor → relational transmission → vivified fictional network → easy reception → imaginative and affective entertainment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS5","text":"Temperament, eloquence, or bodily ferment → non-customary vivification → counterfeit belief; extreme vivification → collapse of truth-falsehood discrimination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS6","text":"Reflection on source and reality-connection → application of general rules → correction of apparent intensity → restoration of graded conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AF1","text":"Educational resistance → scale objection → motivational extremes → belief as selective medium → definition by vivacity → passion-belief reciprocity → truth reduced to easy reception → poetic substitution → imaginative production of assent → madness as limit case → correction by reflection and general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME1","text":"Belief is reclassified from a distinct judgmental content into a mode of conceiving an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME2","text":"Motivation is extended from present sensation to absent and future objects through selective vivification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME3","text":"Truth in imaginative works is reclassified as one mechanism of easy reception rather than an inviolable aesthetic requirement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME4","text":"Judgment, passion, and imagination become reciprocally permeable rather than hierarchically independent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME5","text":"Causal conviction, poetry, eloquence, and madness are placed on one field of vivification while remaining differentiated by source and regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME6","text":"Epistemic correction shifts from measuring apparent agitation to applying second-order rules about causal origin and connection with reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT1","text":"Surface: belief modifies the effects of ideas on action, passion, and imaginative enjoyment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT2","text":"Structural: mental force is transmitted through custom, affect, familiarity, and relations among ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: phenomena of passion, fiction, eloquence, and madness are converted into tests of the general theory of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: absent and future objects acquire present efficacy through graded modes of perception rather than through direct presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s principal definition, reciprocal mechanisms, limiting cases, and corrective closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT2","text":"The magnitude and qualitative dimensions of force and vivacity remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent at the functional level because the same mechanism explains action, passion, imaginative reception, and counterfeit belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT4","text":"The strongest inference is the reciprocal confirmation obtained by showing belief vivifying passion and passion vivifying belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT5","text":"The inference from similarity of effects to similarity in force and vivacity depends on the compressed premise that force and vivacity exhaust the relevant differences among perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT6","text":"The claim that nature selected a motivational medium supplies a functional rationale rather than an independent causal demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","text":"The scalar reduction is strained by the claim that poetical imagery may appear more vivid than experience while remaining weaker and qualitatively different in feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8","text":"General rules repair this strain, but the chapter does not fully specify their origin, authority, or mode of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if poetry and madness are retained as mechanism-testing cases rather than discarded as illustrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","context":"Chapter 09","original_title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_context":"Chapter 09"},"original_title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","order":10,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXUP"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXUP@R001","sha256":"b314321273c63c891ecc0bc7da77446b5a8448ced2c74901072cd15b2fb19c83","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS1","text":"Problem: philosophically disallowed variations of belief arise from the same principles as sanctioned probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS2","text":"Thesis: every judgment short of knowledge consists in a degree of force and vivacity communicated to a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS3","text":"Mechanism: present impression → customary transition → related idea; diminution of either source force or transitional facility weakens belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS4","text":"Diagnostic movement: temporal fading → inferential attenuation → homogeneous-chain qualification → prejudicial generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS5","text":"Corrective movement: first-order custom mistakes accidental resemblance for efficacy; second-order general rules condemn that operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","text":"Structural contradiction: custom and rule-following generate unphilosophical probability and provide its only correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS7","text":"Argument type: reconstructive psychology joined to skeptical diagnosis and a unified causal account of probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A1","text":"Belief in existence is the force and vivacity of a perception in every judgment that does not amount to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A2","text":"Memory possesses the greatest force and vivacity available to nondemonstrative belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A3","text":"Causal inference borrows vivacity from a present impression and transfers it to a related idea through habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A4","text":"Evidential force varies with the strength of the originating impression and the facility of the associative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A5","text":"Repeated conjunction produces a customary transition of the imagination that precedes reflection and cannot be prevented by it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A6","text":"Custom extends from identical cases to resembling cases, losing force by degrees as resemblance diminishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A7","text":"Temporal remoteness and heterogeneous inferential mediation dissipate vivacity independently of logical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A8","text":"Resemblance among successive inferential links permits cognitive compression and limits the attenuation caused by numerical length.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A9","text":"In contrary experience, each remembered possibility separately affects the imagination, while net belief follows numerical superiority with a deduction for opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A10","text":"Frequent conjunction enables salient accidental circumstances to evoke the effect associated with efficacious circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A11","text":"Reflective general rules derived from experience of the understanding distinguish efficacious circumstances from accidental conjunctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A12","text":"The more extensive and constant inference is attributed to judgment, while the irregular and uncertain exception is attributed to imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A13","text":"Common, universal, direct, and unitary signs communicate vivacity more effectively than rare, oblique, or dispersed signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A14","text":"A lively idea and its corresponding passion can reciprocally amplify one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A15","text":"Philosophical and unphilosophical probabilities differ in normative authorization, not in the psychological principles generating them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX1","text":"Actual conviction is not fixed by philosophy’s recognition of an argument as legitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX2","text":"The unchanged content of an argument does not preserve invariant authority as its originating impression recedes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX3","text":"The infallibility of individual links does not guarantee conviction across a heterogeneous inferential chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX4","text":"The numerical length of historical transmission does not by itself extinguish evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX5","text":"Homogeneous copying is not correctly modeled as an indefinite series of independent probabilistic diminutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX6","text":"Resemblance to a familiar cause does not establish the presence of its efficacious circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX7","text":"Present observation does not automatically defeat a customary association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX8","text":"General rules are not uniformly philosophical; rash generalization is itself a source of prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","text":"Reflection does not prevent the imagination’s initial customary transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX10","text":"Equal assent to the same conclusion does not entail equal imaginative or affective influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX11","text":"The common origin of judgment and imagination in custom does not prevent them from issuing opposed inferences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D1","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7, the same remembered fact loses authority as its impression fades despite retaining the same propositional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D2","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, recent observations receive disproportionate weight because both their impressions and their customary transitions remain intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D3","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7, demonstratively connected reasoning can psychologically degenerate into probability when it requires many distinct transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D4","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8, historical evidence survives long transmission when repeated links are sufficiently homogeneous to be traversed as a compressed general sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D5","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8, a chain composed of genuinely distinct probabilistic links would undergo repeated diminution, whereas homogeneous proof-preserving links need not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D6","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A6 + CH_THN_SXUP:A10, prejudice is ordinary causal habit extended from conspicuous resemblance while material differences are neglected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D7","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A10 + CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12, philosophical correction occurs when a more general rule reclassifies a first-order customary inference as accidental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D8","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, convergence between judgment and a common sign produces stronger affect than an equally certain conclusion assembled from oblique signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D9","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, social rules can make an originally stronger offense indirectly less painful by supplying a vivid adverse inference about the offender.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D10","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A2 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A9, memory, proof, and probability form a descending continuum of vivacity rather than distinct species of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A10 + CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12, philosophical correction redirects custom from first-order resemblance toward more extensive regularity rather than opposing custom with an independent faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13, logical warrant and experienced conviction can vary independently because only the latter tracks the transmission of vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD3","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13, effective inferential distance is determined by cognitive heterogeneity and labor rather than by the number of links alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, modes of presentation can reorganize passion without altering the conclusion accepted by judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD5","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A2 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A9, proof and probability differ by the organization and intensity of one mechanism, not by possessing different psychological foundations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD6","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A12 + CH_THN_SXUP:A15, philosophy remains disposition-sensitive because either the prejudicial rule or its corrective metarule may prevail in a particular mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX1","text":"No independent rational faculty produces belief outside the operations of custom, imagination, and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX2","text":"No proposition carries an intrinsic belief-force invariant under memory, mediation, and presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX3","text":"No causal discontinuity separates sanctioned probability from prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX4","text":"No simple accumulation of inferential links determines evidential loss without regard to their resemblance and cognitive organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5","text":"No reflective correction permanently abolishes the first customary propensity it regulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX6","text":"No affective response remains insulated from the imaginative mode by which its object is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","text":"An extra-customary tribunal of reason is unavailable; philosophical authority can appear only as a more extensive and constant ordering of custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX2","text":"An invariant correspondence between logical validity and felt conviction is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","text":"A final reconciliation between judgment and imagination is absent; corrective judgment competes with rather than extinguishes irregular transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","text":"A temporally and affectively invariant believing subject is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS1","text":"Repeated conjunction → custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS2","text":"Present impression + custom → imaginative transition → vivified related idea → belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS3","text":"Source faintness, temporal remoteness, diminished resemblance, contrary instances, or heterogeneous mediation → reduced vivacity → weakened evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS4","text":"Homogeneous inferential links → compressed traversal → limited attenuation despite numerical length → preservation of historical belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","text":"Resemblance + salient accidental conjunction → first-order general rule → expected effect without efficacious condition → prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","text":"Reflection on prior operations + counterinstances → more general rule → accidental–efficacious distinction → corrective judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","text":"First-order rule ↔ second-order rule → disposition-sensitive conflict between imagination and judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS8","text":"Sign generality and unity → facilitated transition; sign rarity and dispersion → cognitive labor → diminished vivacity and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS9","text":"Opposed remembered possibilities → separate imaginative forces → aggregation and cancellation → graded probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS10","text":"All outputs → single custom–imagination–vivacity architecture → normative division between philosophical and unphilosophical probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AF1","text":"Sanctioned/unphilosophical disparity → common belief mechanism → recency effects → inferential attenuation → ancient-history objection → homogeneous-chain resolution → prejudice as extended custom → corrective metarules → conflict of general rules → presentational and affective consequences → unified theory of probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME1","text":"Philosophical probability and prejudice become differently regulated outputs of one psychological mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME2","text":"Evidential validity and actual conviction become separable dimensions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME3","text":"Inferential distance is redrawn as cognitive heterogeneity rather than numerical length.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME4","text":"Prejudice is reclassified from an alien defect of reason into an excessive operation of ordinary causal habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME5","text":"The opposition between judgment and imagination becomes a conflict between first-order and second-order general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME6","text":"Memory, proof, and probability become positions on a continuum of force rather than psychologically independent kinds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME7","text":"Social and affective responses become functions of sign structure and transitional facility, not merely of what the judgment accepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT1","text":"Surface: belief varies with recency, inferential distance, generalization, and mode of presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT2","text":"Structural: custom transfers vivacity through imagination, while resemblance, mediation, opposition, and sign form regulate that transfer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: philosophy corrects natural bias only by redirecting the same generalizing mechanism that produced it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: belief is a temporally variable event of force-transmission rather than an invariant possession attached to propositional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the four unphilosophical probabilities, the historical-evidence objection, the conflict of general rules, and the final unification of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","text":"The quantitative relation between impression-strength, transitional facility, and resulting conviction remains unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","text":"The authority of the “more general and authentic” corrective rule remains underdetermined, and the complete regulative rules are deferred beyond the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent as a psychological explanation of variations in conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT5","text":"Its strongest operation is the derivation of multiple evidential distortions from a single model of vivacity transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT6","text":"The historical repair depends on treating initial testimony as proof and subsequent transmission as cognitively homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT7","text":"The central fault line is that custom supplies prejudice, philosophical correction, and the standard by which correction is preferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT8","text":"The argument depends on inherited premises that belief is vivacity and causal reasoning is customary transition rather than establishing them anew.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if historical homogeneity is treated as a qualification of attenuation and the social cases as tests of the same mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","context":"Chapter 10","original_title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","original_context":"Chapter 10"},"original_title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","order":11,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXRW"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXRW@R001","sha256":"cc6c291eb4e6d152a70c3056733d45b2b55934f5e0fe38ab76cbda6fcb2d516f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS1","text":"Problem: a priori inspection cannot distinguish actual causal relations from unrestricted causal possibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS2","text":"Core thesis: actual causation is judged through constant conjunction constrained by contiguity, temporal priority, and causal uniformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive and methodological, terminating in an anti-scholastic restriction on formal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS4","text":"Movement: a priori causal indeterminacy → empirical criteria → comparative rules → controlled experimentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS5","text":"Comparative regularities isolate common causes, differentiating causes, compound effects, and incomplete causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS6","text":"Formal rule-production yields to recursive experimental application requiring judgment, perseverance, and widened experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A1","text":"Neither causal capacity nor causal incapacity can be determined by mere inspection independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A2","text":"No objects are properly contrary except existence and non-existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A3","text":"Causation depends wholly on constant conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A4","text":"Cause and effect are contiguous in space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A5","text":"A cause is temporally prior to its effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A6","text":"The same cause always produces the same effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A7","text":"The same effect never arises except from the same cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A8","text":"Every natural phenomenon is compounded and modified by multiple circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A9","text":"Decisive causal inquiry requires experimentally separating superfluous circumstances from essential ones.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A10","text":"Every new experiment remains subject to renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A11","text":"The operative circumstances of moral phenomena are more numerous, implicit, and obscure than those of natural phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A12","text":"Increased security requires enlargement of the experimental sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX1","text":"No object is identifiable as a cause or non-cause through mere conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX2","text":"No intrinsic classification of objects restricts in advance what may cause what.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX3","text":"Mere occurrence without constant conjunction does not constitute causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX4","text":"A non-contiguous or effect-prior relation cannot qualify as causal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX5","text":"A few experiments cannot establish proportional causal decomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX6","text":"Covariation within an observed range does not license indefinite extrapolation beyond that range.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX7","text":"An object fully present before the effect cannot be the sole complete cause of that effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX8","text":"Formal logical precepts neither eliminate experimental judgment nor establish the superiority of scholastic reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D1","text":"Before experience, anything imaginable remains a possible cause or effect of anything else.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:A2","CH_THN_SXRW:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D2","text":"A clear causal experiment licenses projection to phenomena of the same kind without awaiting renewed constant repetition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D3","text":"When different objects produce the same effect, causation must be assigned to a quality common to them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D4","text":"When resembling objects produce different effects, the causal difference must be assigned to the circumstance in which they differ.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D5","text":"Adequately repeated proportional covariation indicates a compound effect composed of effects corresponding to parts of the cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D6","text":"If a putative cause is fully present while its effect is absent, an auxiliary causal principle is required.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D7","text":"Causal investigation proceeds recursively by varying circumstances, testing essentiality, and submitting each result to renewed variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D8","text":"Complex experimental application requires perseverance in inquiry and sagacity in selecting discriminating tests.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D9","text":"Moral inquiry requires broader experimentation because its operative variables are less directly identifiable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","text":"Causal rules regulate inference among experienced regularities rather than disclose intrinsic productive powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD2","text":"Within an adequately specified class, apparent irregularity is reassigned to an unisolated difference, compound contribution, or missing co-cause rather than admitted as brute exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","text":"Experimental correction is structurally open-ended because every control becomes a new object of control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","text":"Causal method cannot be reduced to a self-applying algorithm because no rule independently selects the decisive experimental variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","text":"Moral inquiry employs the same causal architecture as natural inquiry under conditions of greater variable opacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","text":"Causal identity shifts from whole objects to experimentally isolated qualities or circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX1","text":"Objects must not possess causally legible powers available independently of observed conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","text":"Kinds, qualities, and relevant similarities must not be wholly unstable, or comparative causal projection becomes impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX3","text":"Essential circumstances must not be experimentally inseparable in principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4","text":"Causal irregularity must not be brute; it must remain attributable to an undiscovered difference, scope condition, or auxiliary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX5","text":"The obscurity of moral circumstances must not amount to absolute inaccessibility, or enlarged experimentation could provide no increased security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","text":"No observable necessary connection beyond regular conjunction is available to ground causal judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX2","text":"No a priori ontology assigns fixed causal or receptive roles to kinds of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX3","text":"No self-executing logical system can replace experimental discrimination and human judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","text":"No terminal experiment closes causal inquiry against further circumstance-analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS1","text":"Non-contrariety + absence of a priori causal discrimination → unrestricted candidate causal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS2","text":"Candidate relation + spatial-temporal contiguity + cause-priority + constant conjunction → causal attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS3","text":"Clear causal instance + cause-effect uniformity → projection to phenomena of the same kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4","text":"Same effect across differing objects → isolate common circumstance; differing effects across resembling objects → isolate differing circumstance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5","text":"Repeated proportional part-covariation → compound-effect decomposition; full cause without effect → incomplete-cause diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","text":"Compound phenomenon → remove or vary circumstance → test essentiality → reanalyze resulting experiment → repeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS7","text":"Increasing circumstantial complexity → increasing dependence on perseverance, sagacity, and expanded experimental range.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AF1","text":"A priori causal indeterminacy → constant conjunction as empirical ground → spatiotemporal and uniformity rules → comparative isolation → decomposition and completion tests → recursive experimental difficulty → enlargement of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME1","text":"Causal possibility is detached from the conceptual content of objects and left open pending experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME2","text":"Actual causes are distinguished from merely possible causes by observed relational structure rather than intrinsic intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME3","text":"Resemblance and difference become experimental operators for locating causal circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME4","text":"Disappointed expectation becomes evidence of an omitted distinction or condition rather than an immediate defeat of causal uniformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME5","text":"Proportional covariation reclassifies effects as compounds while restricting inference to adequately tested ranges.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME6","text":"Possession of causal rules is separated from competence in their application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME7","text":"Moral phenomena are placed on the same causal map as natural phenomena but assigned a higher degree of circumstantial opacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT1","text":"Surface: eight rules specify the immediate criteria and comparative tests for judging causes and effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT2","text":"Structural: constant conjunction, controlled resemblance, controlled difference, and recursive experimentation isolate operative circumstances within compound phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: formal logic is subordinated to experimental design, persistence, sagacity, and expansion of the evidential field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: any non-contradictory object may enter a causal relation, while actual causation takes the form of contiguous, temporally ordered, uniform succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT1","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the transition from unrestricted causal possibility to empirical rules and recursive application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2","text":"The meanings of same cause, same kind, clear experiment, adequate repetition, full perfection, and essential circumstance remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence depends on stable kinds, causal uniformity, and provisional experimental discriminability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest in its paired comparative rules and its account of recursive experimental control.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT5","text":"The claim that causation depends wholly on constant conjunction remains imperfectly reconciled with contiguity and priority as additional necessary conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT6","text":"The same-effect/same-cause rule conflicts superficially with different objects producing the same effect; the common-quality rule resolves this only by shifting the unit of causal identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT7","text":"Projection from one clear experiment remains in tension with the prohibition on inference from a few experiments because clarity and adequate scope are not formally specified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT8","text":"The anti-scholastic claim that natural understanding can supply the rules is asserted by contrast rather than generated from the causal axioms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement but cannot convert its application into a determinate procedure without suppressing the judgment the text declares indispensable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","context":"Chapter 11","original_title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","original_context":"Chapter 11"},"original_title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","order":12,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIAP"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIAP@R001","sha256":"30e8a1a64abbc5922c4e31762cca6e6720237656d8a92b2891470692cc29633f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain why collections of distinct and variable qualities are represented as simple, identical substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS2","text":"Ancient metaphysical categories are fictions generated by natural operations of imagination rather than discoveries of objective structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS3","text":"The argument is genealogical, diagnostic, epistemological, and polemical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS4","text":"Related qualities → smooth mental transition → imputed identity or simplicity → reflective disclosure of difference → contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS5","text":"Contradiction → fictive substance → homogeneous matter and substantial forms → accidents → occult qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS6","text":"Vulgar custom is first negated by reflection; false philosophy then preserves the demand for objective connection that reflection has made unintelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS7","text":"Moderate scepticism terminates the impossible search, whereas false philosophy terminates it through meaningless words and anthropomorphic projections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A1","text":"Unreasonable philosophical fictions can be generated by natural propensities of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A2","text":"The idea of a body is a mind-formed collection of ideas of distinct sensible qualities found in constant union.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A3","text":"Close relations among ideas produce easy transitions of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A4","text":"The imagination substitutes objects when their modes of affecting thought are similar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A5","text":"Gradual survey of successive qualities suppresses awareness of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A6","text":"Comparison of temporally separated conditions makes their differences salient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A7","text":"Close relation among coexistent qualities suppresses awareness of composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A8","text":"Reflective comparison discloses sensible qualities as distinct, distinguishable, and separable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A9","text":"Contradiction between natural attribution and reflective disclosure prompts imagination to feign a persisting unifier.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A10","text":"Repeated conjunction of qualities with a supposed substance generates an imagined dependence of those qualities upon it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A11","text":"Whatever is distinct and separately conceivable may exist separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A12","text":"Customary conjunction makes the vulgar mistake difficulty in separating ideas for impossibility in separating objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A13","text":"Causal inference rests on observed constant conjunction rather than perceived intrinsic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A14","text":"There is no idea of power or agency as something separate from the mind and inherent in causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A15","text":"False philosophy rejects the vulgar perception of connection while retaining the search for connection in matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A16","text":"Repetition and verbal resemblance can make unintelligible terms appear secretly meaningful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A17","text":"Human imagination tends to transfer inward emotions to external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX1","text":"A body is not given as an intrinsically simple object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX2","text":"Numerical identity is not disclosed by a succession of changing qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","text":"Substance is not an intelligible perception, bearer, or explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX4","text":"Substantial forms are not discovered sources of species-specific qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX5","text":"Sensible qualities are not accidents that require an unknown subject of inhesion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX6","text":"Constant conjunction is not a perceived necessary connection among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","text":"Faculties and occult qualities are not meaningful explanations of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX8","text":"Sympathy, antipathy, and horror of a vacuum are not warranted affections of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D1","text":"Related successive qualities are mistaken for one identical object because their gradual survey resembles the survey of an unchanging object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D2","text":"Related coexistent qualities are mistaken for one simple object because their apprehension resembles a single act of thought.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D3","text":"The same quality-collection appears identical and simple under fluent survey but diverse and composite under reflective comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A7","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D4","text":"Substance is invented to preserve identity through variation and unity through composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D5","text":"Gradual transformations generate the fiction of homogeneous matter, while salient differences generate the fiction of distinct substantial forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D6","text":"The doctrine of accidents converts habitual co-imagination into supposed ontological dependence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D7","text":"The alleged necessary dependence of qualities on substance collapses once qualities are recognized as separately conceivable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D8","text":"Occult-quality theory compounds an unintelligible supporting substance with an obscure supported property.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D9","text":"Vulgar custom produces belief in intrinsic connection; reflection removes that belief; false philosophy continues the external search; true philosophy suspends it.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A12","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:A14","CH_THN_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D10","text":"Terms such as faculty and occult quality end inquiry by simulating the presence of an absent idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D11","text":"Sympathy, antipathy, and horror of a vacuum externalize affective structures belonging to the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","text":"Ancient ontology is a layered repair-system constructed to conceal conflicts produced by incompatible modes of apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD2","text":"Each metaphysical repair increases ontological complexity while decreasing intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD3","text":"False philosophy occupies an unstable middle position: it has enough reflection to destroy vulgar certainty but insufficient scepticism to relinquish the demand that certainty expressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD4","text":"Metaphysical terminology functions as cognitive consolation by converting an acknowledged absence of explanation into apparent explanatory closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD5","text":"Substantiation and anthropomorphism are variants of one operation: features of the mind’s activity are transferred to external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD6","text":"True philosophy and vulgar thought converge in practical indifference while remaining opposed in epistemic basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX1","text":"Similarity between mental operations and object-apprehensions must not entail similarity in the objects apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX2","text":"Psychological difficulty in separating ideas must not establish metaphysical inseparability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","text":"A posit cannot acquire explanatory legitimacy merely by resolving a contradiction in imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX4","text":"Reflection cannot produce a valid positive metaphysics by preserving the object-side agency that it has rendered inconceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5","text":"Technical vocabulary has no reserve of meaning independent of an assignable idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX6","text":"External nature cannot be presumed to reproduce the emotions and intentions most available to the human mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1","text":"No positively conceivable invariant bearer exists within the system to underwrite identity, simplicity, or cohesion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX2","text":"No object-side reconciliation of sameness with change or unity with plurality is available; the demanded reconciliation exists only as fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3","text":"The supposed inner power of causes remains unavailable as an object of thought, leaving the metaphysical interior of causation empty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX4","text":"No philosophical metalanguage is guaranteed to escape the imaginative mechanisms it diagnoses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS1","text":"Distinct sensible qualities + constant union → closely related quality-collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS2","text":"Successive relation + gradual survey + similarity of mental act → fictive identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS3","text":"Coexistent relation + imperceptible transition among parts + similarity of mental act → fictive simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4","text":"Distant or analytic comparison → disclosed variation or composition → contradiction → unknown substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5","text":"Gradual transformation / salient difference → homogeneous matter / substantial form → accident-substrate system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6","text":"Constant conjunction → vulgar necessary connection → reflective dissolution → sceptical cessation or false-philosophical search.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS7","text":"Retained demand for agency + absent idea → faculty or occult-quality label → semantic illusion → termination of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS8","text":"Internal emotion + projective propensity → affective predicates assigned to nature → anthropomorphic physics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF1","text":"Quality-collection → smooth transition → identity and simplicity → comparative disruption → substance fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF2","text":"Substance fiction → homogeneous matter and substantial forms → accidents → occult qualities → total incomprehensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF3","text":"Vulgar conjunction → reflective separation → no known connection → false search or moderate scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF4","text":"Meaningless terminology → simulated explanation → inquiry closure → anthropomorphic projection exposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME1","text":"Identity and simplicity are reclassified from object-properties to effects of a mode of mental survey.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME2","text":"Substance, substantial form, accident, and occult quality are relocated from ontology into a genealogy of imaginative repair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME3","text":"Philosophical sophistication ceases to form a linear ascent: false philosophy becomes an unstable interval between vulgar custom and true scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME4","text":"Explanatory terms are reclassified as possible mechanisms for stopping inquiry rather than advancing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME5","text":"The apparent distance between vulgar thought and true philosophy contracts because both terminate speculation, though for contrary reasons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME6","text":"Ancient physical principles become legible as projections of affect rather than descriptions of external agency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface: Substance, matter, substantial form, accident, faculty, occult quality, sympathy, and antipathy are subjected to genealogical dissolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural: Relation, smooth transition, comparison, habit, contradiction-repair, verbal resemblance, and affective projection generate the ancient system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Philosophical inquiry is divided between vulgar belief, failed metaphysical repair, and moderate sceptical cessation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Unity and identity vary with temporal and analytic perspective, while intrinsic substance and objective causal power remain unavailable to conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the section’s genealogy of ancient metaphysical categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT2","text":"The wider foundations of the separability principle, causal inference, and moderate scepticism remain underdetermined within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as one mechanism—confusion produced by similar effects on thought—generates both identity and simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where alternative modes of survey produce the contradiction that substance is introduced to conceal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT5","text":"The inference from separate conceivability to possible separate existence is load-bearing but asserted rather than established here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT6","text":"The genealogy of a belief explains its production but does not by itself establish its falsity; the refutation also depends on the absence of a corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT7","text":"The ranking of vulgar, false, and true philosophy compresses a normative criterion by treating sceptical cessation as justified and verbal cessation as illusory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from cognitive mechanism through metaphysical proliferation to semantic and anthropomorphic exposure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","context":"Chapter 12","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","original_context":"Chapter 12"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","order":13,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVIS"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVIS@R001","sha256":"8f199b9b4c6cbb525c7d9b36262c1c9dc3b63ca75b74e0cfbb7e81ee586baba3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","text":"The central problem is whether perceptions can intelligibly be said to inhere in a material or immaterial substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS2","text":"The argument is diagnostic, aporetic, empiricist, and polemically retortive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","text":"The concepts of substance and inhesion must pass the impression-origin and separability tests before the dispute can proceed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS4","text":"Neither concept passes; the question concerning the soul’s substance is therefore unintelligible rather than merely unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS5","text":"Perceptions divide into extended and nonspatial kinds, defeating every account that assigns all thought to one uniform spatial or nonspatial subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS6","text":"The simple-soul hypothesis reproduces the structural difficulties charged against Spinoza’s simple universal substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS7","text":"The movement terminates by separating substance from causation: constant conjunction licenses matter–thought causation while leaving a priori claims about duration without foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A1","text":"Every idea is derived from a precedent impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A2","text":"No impression can be identified as the impression of a mental substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A3","text":"Whatever is clearly conceived may exist as conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A4","text":"Everything different is distinguishable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A5","text":"Everything distinguishable is separable by the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A6","text":"Perceptions are different from one another and from everything else.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A7","text":"Nothing appears requisite to support the existence of a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A8","text":"What remains unknown concerning mind must remain undecided rather than be supplied by speculative hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A9","text":"Perceptions are heterogeneous with respect to extension: some are extended and others exist without spatial place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A10","text":"Whatever has a spatial place must be either extended or an indivisible mathematical point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A11","text":"A relation of place requires both relata to possess spatial characteristics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A12","text":"The mind tends to add a new relation to objects already united by other relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A13","text":"Every intelligible quality attributed to an external object must be representable through an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A14","text":"No necessary connection between cause and effect is perceived in the ideas of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A15","text":"Constant conjunction constitutes the operative content of the causal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A16","text":"Any pair of real objects is, a priori, susceptible of constant conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A17","text":"Changes in bodily disposition are constantly conjoined with changes in thought and sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A18","text":"Every conceivable object can be imagined to become inactive or to be annihilated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX1","text":"Ignorance concerning mind does not authorize contradictory hypotheses concerning its substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX2","text":"The materiality or immateriality of the soul is not an intelligible dispute before substance and inhesion are defined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX3","text":"Independent existence does not distinguish substance from perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX4","text":"Perceptions are not intelligibly defined as accidents requiring an unknown support.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX5","text":"Existence does not universally entail spatial location.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX6","text":"A nonspatial perception is not a mathematical point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX7","text":"Causal and temporal conjunction is not literal conjunction in place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX8","text":"Thought is not uniformly extended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX9","text":"Thought is not uniformly simple, indivisible, and unextended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX10","text":"A simple immaterial soul is not established by the incompatibility of some perceptions with extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX11","text":"Renaming perceptions “actions” rather than “modifications” does not explain their dependence upon substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX12","text":"The absence of an apparent conceptual connection between motion and thought does not prove their causal incompatibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX13","text":"Simplicity or immateriality does not yield an a priori proof of immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D1","text":"The idea of mental substance lacks legitimate cognitive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D2","text":"Because perceptions are separably conceivable and possibly separately existent, defining substance by independent existence makes every perception a substance and destroys the intended distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A3","CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D3","text":"Inhesion has no intelligible content where no support for perception can be identified as necessary.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D4","text":"The question whether perceptions inhere in material or immaterial substance is composed of terms for which no adequate ideas exist.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A2","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D5","text":"Nonspatial perceptions must be admitted as existing nowhere, because treating them as points would make aggregates of them extended.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D6","text":"A nonspatial perception cannot be locally conjoined with an extended body.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D7","text":"Where an extended object and a nonspatial perception are causally and temporally associated, the imagination fabricates spatial conjunction to complete their union.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D8","text":"The attribution of place to nonspatial qualities persists through a conflict between associative imagination and spatially discriminating reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A10","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D9","text":"Materialism cannot coherently conjoin all thought with extension because some perceptions exclude place.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D10","text":"The simple-soul doctrine cannot coherently conjoin all perceptions with an indivisible subject because some perceptions are themselves extended and divisible.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D11","text":"Any intelligible repugnance between an extended object and a simple substratum transfers to the relation between an extended impression and a simple soul.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D12","text":"The objections to Spinoza’s simple universal substance apply symmetrically to the theological doctrine of a simple thinking substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D13","text":"If an action is defined as an inseparable abstract mode of substance, distinct and separable perceptions cannot be explained merely by calling them actions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D14","text":"No a priori comparison of motion and thought can exclude their causal conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVIS:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D15","text":"Bodily motion and disposition may be regarded as actual causes of thought insofar as causation consists in experienced constant conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVIS:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D16","text":"Requiring an apparent necessary connection would eliminate every cause; invoking divine agency to repair that absence would make the deity the cause of all volitions and perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D17","text":"Matter and spirit are equally incapable of supporting an a priori inference to necessary continued existence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A3","CH_THN_SVIS:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","text":"The explanatory burden shifts from identifying a bearer of perceptions to accounting for the relations among perceptions themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0040","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD2","text":"The decisive ontological division is not mind against matter but extended perception against nonspatial perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","text":"Associative relation-completion generates pseudo-ontological placement and allows contradictions to persist under familiar forms of speech.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","text":"Materialism and spiritualism become reciprocal overextensions: each universalizes one class of perception into a subject adequate to all perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD5","text":"Agnosticism concerning mental substance is compatible with an empirical account in which bodily processes cause thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD6","text":"Religion is deprived of metaphysical support from substance and simplicity while remaining formally open to moral and analogical arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX1","text":"No impression-transcendent concept may determine the nature of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","text":"Perceptions do not require a numerically identical bearer beneath their differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX3","text":"Spatial location is not a universal condition of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","text":"Theological substance is not exempt from standards applied to materialist or Spinozist substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX5","text":"Causal efficacy does not require resemblance, conceptual containment, or a perceivable necessary nexus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX6","text":"Immaterial simplicity does not contain a principle of necessary persistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","text":"The unavailable object organizing the dispute is the supposed substratum in which perceptions occur but which can never itself appear as a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX2","text":"No common ontological medium is available through which extended and nonspatial perceptions can be rendered locally homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX3","text":"No privileged simple subject survives the reciprocal application of mode–substance objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX4","text":"No a priori bridge remains from the vocabulary of soul to the religious conclusion of immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS1","text":"Proposed mental entity → demand originating impression → no impression identifiable → concept excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS2","text":"Difference → distinguishability → imaginative separability → possible separate existence → self-subsistence definition collapses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS3","text":"Perceptual field → extended/nonspatial partition → spatial relation requires shared spatiality → universal local union fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS4","text":"Causal and temporal association → propensity to complete relation → fictive spatial incorporation → concealed contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS5","text":"Nonspatial perceptions defeat universal extension / extended perceptions defeat universal indivisibility → bilateral closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS6","text":"Object-quality mediated by impression → object-level repugnance transfers to impressions → Spinozist and theological hypotheses become structurally equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS7","text":"Modification renamed action → separability test reapplied → terminological repair fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0020","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS8","text":"No perceived necessary nexus → causal dilemma → constant conjunction selected → matter–thought causation admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS9","text":"Conceivable inactivity or annihilation → equal contingency of matter and spirit → metaphysical immortality proof suspended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF1","text":"Undefined substance and inhesion → impression test → separability test → definitional collapse → substance question condemned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF2","text":"Spatial incompatibility argument → heterogeneous perceptions → associative fiction of place → reciprocal defeat of materialism and immaterialism → Spinozist retortion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF3","text":"Substance question closed → causal question isolated → necessary connection rejected → constant conjunction installed → matter–thought causation admitted → a priori immortality neutralized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME1","text":"A metaphysical dispute is reclassified as an unintelligible question generated by cognitively empty terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME2","text":"The definition of substance by independent existence collapses the substance–accident distinction at the level of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME3","text":"The mind–matter opposition is displaced by a cross-cutting distinction between extended and nonspatial perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME4","text":"“Existing nowhere” becomes an admissible mode of existence rather than a contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME5","text":"The apparent location of nonspatial qualities within bodies is reclassified as an associative construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME6","text":"Theological immaterialism and Spinozist monism become mutually testable by the same objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","text":"Causation is detached from substantial ontology and relocated in experienced conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME8","text":"Immortality is removed from a priori metaphysics without being directly decided by the analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The soul’s materiality or immateriality is unintelligible, while matter and motion may count as causes of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Impression-origin, separability, spatial heterogeneity, associative relation-completion, and constant conjunction govern the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reciprocal application of identical standards blocks theological polemic from condemning rival substance systems while exempting its own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Some beings exist without place; causal possibility exceeds conceptual resemblance; neither matter nor spirit possesses demonstrable necessary duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the semantic, spatial, polemical, causal, and temporal movements of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT2","text":"The positive ontological status of external objects and the precise referent of “matter” remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the copy principle, conceivability principle, separability principle, and constant-conjunction account are jointly retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT4","text":"Its strongest operation is the reciprocal transfer of objections between Spinozist substance and the simple theological soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT5","text":"Its greatest compression lies in the claims that all real objects are causally combinable a priori and that conceivability establishes possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT6","text":"A structural fault line remains between condemning substance discourse as unintelligible and continuing to manipulate substance hypotheses for polemical retortion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","text":"The claim of actual matter–thought causation depends on treating bodily and mental covariation as sufficient constant conjunction without further causal individuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT8","text":"The religious apology claims neutrality although the metaphysical arguments for immortality have been withdrawn; neutrality depends on isolating moral and analogical arguments from that loss.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the actual movement from conceptual invalidation through reciprocal critique to empirical causal admissibility and temporal suspension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","context":"Chapter 13","original_title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","original_context":"Chapter 13"},"original_title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","order":14,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVCT"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVCT@R001","sha256":"de0aa867f594757274e736b419c1b42f2ad33f073321678876dc7944752e086e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","text":"Central problem: whether philosophical inquiry can continue after reflection deprives reason of any independent criterion for assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS2","text":"Core thesis: belief is generated by experience, habit, imaginative vivacity, and natural propensity rather than rational self-grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS3","text":"The imagination is simultaneously indispensable to cognition and productive of error, contradiction, and illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS4","text":"Neither unrestricted fancy, purified understanding, nor the rejection of refined reasoning supplies a coherent epistemic rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS5","text":"Nature practically dissolves radical skepticism without rationally refuting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS6","text":"Philosophy resumes as a natural, pleasurable, comparatively safe inclination governed by skeptical restraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS7","text":"Argument type and movement: diagnostic → skeptical self-subversion → affective crisis → natural restoration → pragmatic reconstruction → science of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A1","text":"Assent to reasoning has no further rational ground beyond a felt propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A2","text":"Experience supplies past conjunctions, and habit projects those conjunctions into the future.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A3","text":"Differential vivacity imparted by imagination is necessary for belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A4","text":"Memory, sensory attribution of existence, and causal understanding depend upon imagination or vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A5","text":"Imagination is an unavoidable but inconstant and fallible cognitive principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A6","text":"Causal inference and belief in continued external existence are equally natural and necessary yet can directly conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A7","text":"The represented connection between cause and effect is a customary determination of mind, not a discoverable external power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A8","text":"Unrestricted assent to imaginative suggestion produces contradiction, obscurity, and error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A9","text":"Understanding acting solely through its most general principles subverts all evidence, including its own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A10","text":"A universal prohibition on refined reasoning is itself refined, self-contradictory, and destructive of science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A11","text":"Embodied sensation, passion, diversion, and social intercourse necessarily restore ordinary belief and action after skeptical crisis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A12","text":"Reason possesses practical authority only when it is lively and conjoined with propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A13","text":"Curiosity, ambition, and pleasure naturally regenerate philosophical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A14","text":"The human mind generally exceeds the sphere of common affairs and therefore must choose among speculative guides.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A15","text":"Philosophy is generally safer than superstition because philosophical error is behaviorally weak while superstition strongly governs conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A16","text":"A stable philosophy must restrain speculative imagination by common practice, experience, and critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A17","text":"Human nature is the only science of man and the privileged domain of attainable assurance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A18","text":"True skepticism extends diffidence to both philosophical conviction and philosophical doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX1","text":"Assent is not grounded in an independently demonstrable rational criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX2","text":"Ultimate causal energy is not known as a property residing in external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX3","text":"Every suggestion of imagination is not entitled to assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","text":"Understanding is not an autonomous faculty separable from imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX5","text":"Refined reasoning cannot be universally prohibited without employing what the prohibition rejects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX6","text":"Total suspension of belief cannot be permanently sustained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX7","text":"Nature’s restoration of belief is not a rational refutation of skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX8","text":"No rational obligation compels philosophical labor against inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX9","text":"Philosophy is not a universal vocation required of every person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX10","text":"Speciousness, imaginative warmth, and agreeableness do not establish a hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX11","text":"Philosophical inquiry does not guarantee truth or certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX12","text":"Positive expressions of certainty do not necessarily imply dogmatism or infallibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D1","text":"Belief is psychologically generated rather than rationally self-founded.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D2","text":"The contradiction among natural beliefs cannot be removed by discarding imagination, because imagination is their necessary common source.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D3","text":"Inquiry into ultimate causal power terminates in an account of customary mental transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D4","text":"The choice between unrestricted fancy and purified reason becomes a choice between false reason and no reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D5","text":"The practical necessity of ordinary belief supplies continuation without epistemic vindication.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D6","text":"Philosophy can continue only through natural inclination rather than purely rational obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D7","text":"Preference for philosophy over superstition is comparative and prudential rather than demonstratively certain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A14","CH_THN_SVCT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D8","text":"Philosophical legitimacy depends upon methodological restraint and redirection toward human nature.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D9","text":"Particular assertions may acquire momentary assurance while remaining globally defeasible under skeptical caveat.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D10","text":"Skepticism must recur as self-correction rather than stabilize as permanent disbelief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD1","text":"The system replaces epistemic foundation with regulated alternation between skeptical reflection and unavoidable natural belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","text":"Philosophical activity forms a recursive cycle: inquiry intensifies doubt, nature attenuates doubt, and restored curiosity renews inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD3","text":"Philosophy is justified by comparative harmlessness, natural pleasure, and critical restraint rather than guaranteed access to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","text":"The science of human nature is reflexive: fallible faculties investigate the operations and limits of those same faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD5","text":"Epistemic normativity is naturalized because reason’s title to assent depends upon psychological vivacity and propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","text":"Human faculties cannot be independently amended or validated by another available cognitive instrument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX2","text":"The will cannot permanently control assent against habit, sensation, passion, and social life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX3","text":"Pure reason cannot motivate or govern belief independently of affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","text":"The natural necessity of a belief cannot by itself establish that belief’s truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX5","text":"Skeptical reasoning cannot coherently exempt its own conclusions from the general fallibility it establishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","text":"No Archimedean standpoint is available from which reason, imagination, and natural belief can be jointly validated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX2","text":"No final reconciliation is supplied between contradictory natural principles; their conflict is managed rather than resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX3","text":"Ultimate external power and absolute certainty remain unavailable as terminal objects of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX4","text":"Continued philosophy lacks a purely epistemic warrant and survives through inclination, comparative safety, and provisional satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS1","text":"Faculty audit: exact reasoning → demand for its ground → discovery of propensity rather than rational warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2","text":"Belief production: past conjunction → habit → imaginative vivacity → expectation, memory, and existence-attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS3","text":"Contradiction production: common imaginative source → equally natural operations → incompatible conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS4","text":"Skeptical dilemma: unrestricted fancy → error; purified understanding → self-subversion; prohibition of refinement → self-contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS5","text":"Affective crisis: sustained reflection → loss of comparative probability → melancholy and cognitive paralysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6","text":"Natural reset: sensation, passion, diversion, and society → attenuation of reflection → restored common belief and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS7","text":"Recursive return: restored disposition → curiosity, ambition, and pleasure → renewed philosophical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS8","text":"Strategic closure: inevitable speculation → philosophy preferred to superstition → hypotheses restrained by practice and experience → inquiry redirected to human nature → local assertion retained under global diffidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AF1","text":"Faculty weakness → search for a criterion of assent → imaginative genealogy of belief → conflict among natural principles → self-subversion of purified reason → skeptical delirium → natural restoration → non-foundational preference for philosophy → science of human nature → recursive skeptical caveat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME1","text":"Reason is reclassified as an operation dependent upon the general and established tendencies of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME2","text":"Contradiction is relocated from accidental error to the interaction of natural and necessary cognitive operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME3","text":"Nature is reclassified from a guarantor of truth to a mechanism that restores practical viability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME4","text":"Skepticism is reclassified from permanent suspension into diffidence toward both belief and doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME5","text":"The philosophy–superstition distinction is redrawn around psychological force, behavioral danger, and comparative moderation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME6","text":"Philosophical certainty is divided into momentary local assurance and prohibited global dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME7","text":"The object of philosophy shifts from ultimate external powers to the principles and limits of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT1","text":"Surface: philosophical crisis culminates in the decision whether inquiry should be abandoned or resumed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT2","text":"Structural: imagination generates belief, contradictory natural operations, skeptical collapse, and practical restoration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: inquiry is reconstructed as a restrained, inclination-driven, comparatively safe practice directed against superstition and speculative excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: past, future, external continuity, and remembered identity are accessible only through present vivacity, customary projection, and recurrent changes of disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the skeptical diagnosis, practical restoration, and qualified return to philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT2","text":"The system is coherent as an oscillatory practical structure, not as a deductive resolution of skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT3","text":"Its strongest mechanism is the dependency chain from experience and habit through vivacity to unavoidable belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT4","text":"The criterion distinguishing just philosophy from extravagant hypothesis remains only partially specified by moderation, practice, experience, and critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5","text":"The transition from reason’s psychological liveliness to its normative title to assent remains under-argued.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT6","text":"The comparative safety of philosophy is a qualified empirical generalization and admits philosophical counterinstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT7","text":"The privileging of human nature remains reflexively unstable because the investigating faculties are those already shown to be fallible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if nature’s cure is retained as practical compulsion rather than epistemic solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","context":"Chapter 14","original_title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","original_context":"Chapter 14"},"original_title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","order":15,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVLT"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVLT@R001","sha256":"4bf93d17c8bfb4d63c77fb2d73a4d14913327e608b84c7b28f4a03335d7c2826","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS1","text":"The unrestricted system overgenerates pride and humility from every pleasurable or painful object related to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS2","text":"Pride and humility arise only when affective valence and self-relation satisfy additional relational, comparative, social, and temporal conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS3","text":"The argument is diagnostic, restrictive, and reconstructive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS4","text":"Universal affective law → distinction between joy and self-directed passion → four restrictive thresholds → customary enlargement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS5","text":"Joy and misery require fewer mediations than pride and humility because self is essential only to the latter pair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS6","text":"Real happiness and misery can diverge from the distribution of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A1","text":"Pride and humility take self as their object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A2","text":"Their causes first produce pleasure or pain distinguishable from the resulting self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A3","text":"Pride and humility require a conjoint association of ideas and impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A4","text":"Relations possess variable degrees of force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A5","text":"Pride requires a closer relation to self than joy requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A6","text":"Pride directs attention to both its valenced cause and self, whereas joy properly takes only the valenced cause as its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A7","text":"A stronger relation of the cause to another person weakens or destroys its relation to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A8","text":"A cause of pride or humility must be peculiar to oneself or shared with only a few persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A9","text":"Frequent presentation and long familiarity reduce an object's perceived value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A10","text":"Human valuation proceeds more through comparison than through intrinsic merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A11","text":"A cause of pride or humility must be discernible and obvious both to oneself and to others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A12","text":"A cause must maintain a relatively constant and durable connection with self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A13","text":"Imagination anticipates the loss of an inconstant cause and thereby weakens both satisfaction and self-satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A14","text":"General rules formed through custom and practice stabilize the valuation and proportional production of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A15","text":"Custom can carry passions beyond the exact bounds warranted by particular circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX1","text":"Not every pleasurable or painful object related to self produces pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX2","text":"Mere relation is insufficient; the relation must be close.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX3","text":"A common or thoroughly familiar good is not ordinarily a sufficient cause of vanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX4","text":"A private or indiscernible quality is not fully eligible to produce pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX5","text":"A casual or short-lived connection cannot ground a stable inference concerning self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX6","text":"Intrinsic merit alone does not determine affective value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX7","text":"Passions are not calibrated anew from each particular object without customary maxims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX8","text":"Joy is not pride, and pain or misery is not humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX9","text":"The proudest person is not necessarily the happiest, and the most humble person is not necessarily the most miserable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX10","text":"The reality of an evil does not depend on its eligibility to diminish pride.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D1","text":"A weak self-relation can produce joy while failing to produce pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D2","text":"Objects too foreign to support vanity can still produce direct pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D3","text":"Common goods can satisfy without distinguishing their possessor and therefore without producing pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D4","text":"Commonness weakens pride twice: the cause lacks singularity, and self gains no comparative distinction through possessing it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D5","text":"Social invisibility blocks one condition by which a valued quality becomes self-apparent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D6","text":"A transient cause gives little joy and still less pride because anticipated loss weakens satisfaction and its connection with the durable self.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D7","text":"Customary maxims convert initially irregular and delicate affective principles into comparatively regular responses.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D8","text":"Established classifications can govern passion despite individual circumstances that remove the expected enjoyment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D9","text":"An evil can cause misery without humility whenever it produces pain but fails the relational, peculiar, public, durable, or rule-governed conditions of self-appraisal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D10","text":"Degrees of pride and humility cannot serve as direct measures of happiness and misery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD1","text":"The general system is a gated transformation of pleasure and pain into self-directed passions, not an unconditional causal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD2","text":"Pride is more selective than joy because its dual-object structure multiplies the conditions under which affect must operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD3","text":"Pride and humility register comparatively legible and temporally stable self-distinction rather than total welfare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD4","text":"Custom supplies macroscopic regularity to affective mechanisms whose uncultivated operation would remain variable and uncertain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD5","text":"Common, hidden, transient, or externally caused evils can dominate misery while remaining weakly represented in self-evaluative passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","text":"The universal mechanism is preserved by restricting its domain of successful operation rather than by abandoning the mechanism itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX1","text":"Relation cannot be binary; a mere connection to self cannot have the same force as a close and uncontested connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","text":"Affective valuation cannot be a transparent registration of intrinsic goodness or badness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX3","text":"Private consciousness cannot independently determine vanity without comparative and social mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX4","text":"Momentary possession cannot constitute durable evidence of personal excellence or defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX5","text":"Untutored passion cannot be presumed to assign stable values correctly on first encounter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX6","text":"Hedonic condition and self-estimation cannot be coextensive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX7","text":"General rules cannot remain merely cognitive; the system requires them to organize passion itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX1","text":"There is no unmediated passage from the reality of good or evil to a corresponding judgment of self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX2","text":"There is no guaranteed recognition of suffering within the economy of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3","text":"There is no self-sufficient measure of personal worth insulated from comparison, publicity, and customary classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX4","text":"There is no invariant intrinsic scale capable of explaining the passions independently of familiarity and contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX5","text":"There is no assured equilibrium between particular affective reality and general rules, because custom both regularizes and exceeds particular grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS1","text":"Valenced object → primary pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2","text":"Primary affect + relation to self → candidate transition toward pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS3","text":"Relational test: closeness sufficient, competing relation subordinate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS4","text":"Comparative test: cause peculiar, unfamiliar enough to retain value, and capable of distinguishing self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS5","text":"Discernibility test: cause apparent to self and others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS6","text":"Temporal test: cause sufficiently constant and proportionate to the duration attributed to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS7","text":"Customary-rule operation → stabilized valuation, facilitated passion, possible extension beyond particular warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS8","text":"Successful tests + association of ideas and impressions → pride or humility; failed tests → pleasure or pain without corresponding self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF1","text":"Unrestricted general system → overgeneration problem → necessity of limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF2","text":"Joy/pride distinction → graded relation → peculiarity → public discernibility → temporal constancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF3","text":"Variable first-order responses → custom and practice → general maxims → regularized passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF4","text":"Qualified causal system → separation of self-estimation from welfare → rejection of any necessary pride–happiness or humility–misery alignment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME1","text":"A universal causal claim is redrawn as a threshold-dependent transformation model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME2","text":"Hedonic affect and self-evaluative passion become distinct classes of response.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME3","text":"Relation is reclassified from simple presence into graded, competitive, and interruptible force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME4","text":"Value is relocated from intrinsic quality alone to familiarity, comparison, rarity, and distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME5","text":"Public appearance becomes a causal condition of self-directed passion rather than an incidental accompaniment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME6","text":"Real evil is separated from the mechanisms by which evil becomes a basis of humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME7","text":"Custom appears simultaneously as an affective regularizer and a source of overextension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT1","text":"Surface — Five qualifications restrict or enlarge the production of pride and humility from agreeable and disagreeable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT2","text":"Structural — A dual-object passion requires a stronger transition than a direct pleasure or pain and is therefore more vulnerable to failed mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT3","text":"Social — Peculiarity, comparison, shared possession, and visibility to others determine whether a quality distinguishes self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT4","text":"Metastrategic — General rules explain how delicate causal principles can operate with sufficient regularity to sustain the larger system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A relatively durable self functions as the comparator against which transient causes lose self-defining force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT6","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The reality of good and evil remains independent of whether representational conditions convert them into pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of the general causal system and its concluding separation of welfare from self-estimation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT2","text":"The exact degree of relational closeness required for pride or humility remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT3","text":"The causal basis of public discernibility is deferred rather than demonstrated within the section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT4","text":"The formation and authority of general rules depend on a prior account of custom that is imported in compressed form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent where it distinguishes direct pleasure and pain from their conversion into self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT6","text":"Its strongest operation is the explanation of cases in which pleasure or misery persists while pride or humility fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT7","text":"A fault line remains between familiarity as a depreciator of value and custom as a stabilizer of value; no priority rule resolves their interaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT8","text":"General rules can exceed particular warrant, but their capacity to override the other limitations is not formally bounded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT9","text":"The claim that the most solid evils often evade humility is structurally licensed but not demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from unrestricted law through limitation and enlargement to affective dissociation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","context":"Chapter 15","original_title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","original_context":"Chapter 15"},"original_title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","order":16,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXLF"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXLF@R001","sha256":"4b63b75d3411137ed732cc527b7684b958f3826519cc14c18324bf9eaf4adcb5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","text":"Problem: explain why reputation governs pride and humility and amplifies their otherwise original causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS2","text":"Thesis: love of fame is secondary; praise and blame affect self through sympathetic communication, while authority governs uptake of the judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS3","text":"Another’s expressed sentiment becomes an idea whose vivacity is increased through its relations to the persistently vivid self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS4","text":"Sufficient vivacity converts the represented sentiment into an impression experienced as one’s own affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS5","text":"Variations in esteem, acquaintance, self-concurrence, proximity, and relational convergence test the mechanism against an undifferentiated instinct for fame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS6","text":"Explanatory-reconstructive movement: reputational effect → sympathy mechanism → application to self-evaluation → differential tests → assimilation of apparent objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A1","text":"Ideas and impressions share representational content and differ in force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A2","text":"Consciousness supplies an invariably intimate and maximally lively impression of self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A3","text":"Relations enable the imagination to transfer vivacity from a present impression to a related idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A4","text":"External signs produce an idea of another person’s affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A5","text":"Causal inference establishes the represented affection as real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A6","text":"General human resemblance relates another person’s mental structure to one’s own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A7","text":"Peculiar resemblance, contiguity, kinship, and acquaintance strengthen the relation between self and another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A8","text":"A sufficiently enlivened idea of an affection becomes the corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A9","text":"Pride and humility require a self-related source of independent pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A10","text":"Praise and blame represent another person’s favorable or unfavorable sentiment toward a quality attributed to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A11","text":"Reasoning treats another person’s judgment as evidence for the attributed quality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A12","text":"Evaluative influence increases with the authority accorded to the evaluator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A13","text":"Praise yields substantial pleasure only when its attribution concurs with, or can alter, the recipient’s own opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A14","text":"Relations amplify sympathy when they converge upon the same evaluator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A15","text":"Sympathetic impressions remain divided when supporting relations are distributed across different evaluators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A16","text":"Passion gives additional force to related ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A17","text":"Conscious self-partiality favors confirming evaluations and resists opposing evaluations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","text":"Love of fame is not an original instinct responding equally to every favorable or unfavorable opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX2","text":"National uniformity of sentiment is not adequately explained by soil or climate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","text":"Sympathy is not immediate access to another’s passion; the passion first appears as an idea inferred from signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","text":"Conversion between idea and impression is not a change in representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX5","text":"Approbation is not equally effective regardless of its source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX6","text":"Abstract esteem for a quality is not sufficient to make praise pleasurable when possession of that quality is disbelieved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX7","text":"Relations do not operate merely by existing; they must facilitate associative transition and vivacity transfer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX8","text":"Relations distributed across different persons do not produce the unified force achieved when they converge upon the same persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX9","text":"Blame from a despised or unserious source is not affectively equivalent to credible blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D1","text":"Sympathy occurs when signs and causal inference form an idea of another’s sentiment and self-related relations enliven it into the corresponding impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D2","text":"Common humanity makes sympathy generally possible, while specific similarity and proximity regulate its intensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D3","text":"Praise generates pride when another’s favorable sentiment is sympathetically reproduced, accepted as evidence, and attached as pleasure to a self-related quality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D4","text":"Preference for the approval of competent judges over indiscriminate approval follows from unequal authority and contradicts an undifferentiated fame instinct.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D5","text":"Intimate acquaintance strengthens approbation by increasing both associative proximity and the evidential weight of judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D6","text":"Praise incongruent with self-attribution cannot ordinarily establish the pleasure required for pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D7","text":"Separating kinship from contiguity across different evaluators prevents their contempt from forming a unified sympathetic impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SXLF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D8","text":"Concealment of prior rank suppresses unfavorable comparison in observers and prevents private misfortune from being affectively doubled through sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D9","text":"Numerical accumulation can compensate for low individual esteem by conferring aggregate authority upon popular opinion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D10","text":"Undeserved praise can please when imagination fabricates a provisional self-attribution and sympathetic agreement stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A13","CH_THN_SXLF:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D11","text":"Contempt can wound without securing assent because the sympathetically received passion conflicts with antecedent pride; the effect disappears when the critic lacks credibility or seriousness.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SXLF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","text":"Reputation is mediated self-affection: an external evaluation becomes causally effective only by being internally reproduced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","text":"The self is both the source of vivacity and the eventual object of the sentiment that vivacity imports, producing a recursive self–other–self circuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","text":"Social evaluation has a relational topology: proximity, authority, convergence, separation, and multiplicity determine affective force more than bare evaluative content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD4","text":"Individual self-esteem and collective uniformity are generated by the same mechanism of communicated sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD5","text":"Social opposition can alter affect without altering belief because sympathetic reception and intellectual assent are separable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD6","text":"The distinction between original and secondary causes of pride is functionally porous because communicated opinion amplifies the qualities nominally capable of producing pride independently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX1","text":"Other minds cannot be affectively sealed from one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX2","text":"Ideas cannot be permanently separated from impressions by an absolute difference of kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX3","text":"Self-evaluation cannot be sovereign or insulated from socially transmitted sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX4","text":"Evaluative influence cannot be reduced to propositional content independently of relational configuration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX5","text":"Relations cannot be inert classifications; they must alter transitions and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX6","text":"Reason cannot remain neutral when its judgments are connected with passion and self-partiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an autonomous self whose passions originate wholly within its private judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX2","text":"Fame has no independent affective substance apart from communicated sentiments and their authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","text":"A neutral tribunal of self-worth is unavailable because both external testimony and internal judgment are modulated by passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","text":"Expression → external sign → causal certification → idea of another’s sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS2","text":"Vivid self-impression → human resemblance plus contingent relations → associative transition → transferred vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS3","text":"Enlivened idea → idea–impression threshold crossed → corresponding affection reproduced → sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4","text":"Evaluative sentiment about self → sympathetic affect plus testimonial evidence → pleasure or pain attached to a self-quality → pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS5","text":"Judge authority plus relational proximity plus self-concurrence plus relational convergence plus numerical accumulation → degree of reputational influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS6","text":"Distance plus disesteem plus nonconcurrence plus divided relations plus perceived jest → attenuation or interruption of reputational influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","text":"Apparent counterexample → identify compensating authority, imaginative supplementation, or passion-conflict → return variation to the sympathy mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AF1","text":"Reputational power → rejection of undifferentiated fame instinct → general sympathy principle → vivacity-transfer mechanism → application to praise and blame → differential phenomena → assimilation of objections → communication-of-sentiments thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","text":"Fame is reclassified from an original appetite into a secondary effect of sympathetic communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","text":"Opinions are reclassified as both propositions carrying evidential authority and sentiments capable of affective transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME3","text":"Resemblance, contiguity, kinship, and acquaintance become conductors of vivacity rather than merely descriptive relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME4","text":"Reputation becomes a topological phenomenon governed by the arrangement of evaluators around the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME5","text":"Affective injury is separated from intellectual assent, making resisted contempt psychologically operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME6","text":"Relocation, anonymity, and concealment become mechanisms for altering shame without altering the underlying material condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT1","text":"Surface: praise, blame, reputation, and infamy produce differential pride and humiliation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT2","text":"Structural: relational networks regulate the conversion of others’ sentiments into self-affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: controlled variations in authority, proximity, concurrence, and relational convergence test sympathy against the rival instinct model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas and impressions occupy a continuum of vivacity, while sympathy unfolds as the compressed sequence sign → idea → enlivening → impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from sympathy’s perceptual mechanism to the secondary production of pride and humility through reputation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","text":"The quantitative threshold for idea–impression conversion and the relative weights of authority and relational proximity remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent across variations in evaluator status, acquaintance, concurrence, and relational distribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT4","text":"Its strongest discriminator is the unequal influence of opinions that an original, undifferentiated fame instinct would treat equally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT5","text":"Reconstruction depends on the prior theories of association and of independent pleasure or pain as causes of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT6","text":"The case of consciously undeserved praise requires imaginative fiction to preserve the concurrence condition, introducing an auxiliary mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT7","text":"The response of pride to rejected contempt leaves an unresolved boundary between communication of sentiment and adoption of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","text":"The claim of lossless conversion under perfected sympathy coexists with graded ordinary influence without a specified threshold or measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s real movement only if these qualifications remain visible rather than being absorbed into an unrestricted sympathy principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","context":"Chapter 16","original_title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","original_context":"Chapter 16"},"original_title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","order":17,"parents":["RAW_THN_SI_2"],"revision":"CH_THN_SI_2@R001","sha256":"983ad9df8120aa176157e93bedb872b4f6863973277be878b4233652a7f4207a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","text":"Problem: reconcile apparent exceptions to the pleasure–pain account of love and hatred and explain their conjunction with further passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS2","text":"Core thesis: affection arises when pleasure or uneasiness is joined to a sufficiently vivid and durable imaginative relation to a person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS3","text":"Argument type: diagnostic, eliminative, and reconstructive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS4","text":"Momentary causation → intention/durability distinction → explanation of transient versus lasting passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS5","text":"Relation, acquaintance, and resemblance → increased vivacity → agreeable agitation → affection, modulated by directional association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS6","text":"Candidate causes of wealth-esteem → elimination of object-pleasure and expected advantage as primary → sympathy → recursive amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS7","text":"Passion-mixture → rejection of identity between love and benevolence → contingent natural conjunction → constructive sympathy as the basis of pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A1","text":"Love and hatred vary in valence and intensity with the pleasure or uneasiness received from another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A2","text":"Love and hatred require both a relation of ideas to a person and a relation of impressions between pleasure or pain and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A3","text":"An enduring personal quality connects its pleasure or uneasiness to the person independently of intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A4","text":"A momentary action requires intention to remain connected to its author after the action disappears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A5","text":"Intention intensifies injury or service by signifying contempt, hatred, kindness, or esteem toward the recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A6","text":"A sufficiently forceful pleasure or pain can activate passion despite only a weak relation of ideas to its personal cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A7","text":"An established passion tends to generate judgments that prevent its diminution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A8","text":"Ideas and impressions differ principally in force and vivacity, permitting a sufficiently lively idea to become an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A9","text":"Lively conceptions are agreeable, and conceptions of passions produce especially strong mental agitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A10","text":"Relation, acquaintance, and resemblance increase the force of conception through association, custom, or dispositional correspondence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A11","text":"A personal relation is strongest when imaginative transition between its terms is easy in both directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A12","text":"Sympathy converts a vivid idea of another’s sentiment into a corresponding impression through resemblance between persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A13","text":"Extension by a general rule requires predominantly uniform experience connecting the relevant condition and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A14","text":"Possession, power, and utility produce actual or represented satisfaction in their possessor or beneficiary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A15","text":"Power and riches elicit esteem even where the observer can expect no benefit from their possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A16","text":"Sentiments communicated between persons can be reciprocally reflected and amplified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A17","text":"Passions can blend into a compound impression, whereas ideas combine without losing their distinctness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A18","text":"Love and hatred stand in an original but contingent conjunction with benevolence and anger rather than in identity with them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A19","text":"Sympathy can construct the passion customarily associated with a condition even when that passion is absent from the person observed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A20","text":"Imaginative proximity and contrast intensify sympathetically constructed emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX1","text":"Mere causal production of pleasure or pain is not sufficient for durable love or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX2","text":"Intention is not universally necessary for affection toward personal qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX3","text":"Accidental and involuntary harm is not incapable of producing immediate anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX4","text":"Acknowledged justice does not necessarily extinguish anger caused by harm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX5","text":"The opinion of iniquity is not always the antecedent cause of hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX6","text":"Affection arising from relation or acquaintance is not proportioned solely to objective merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX7","text":"Logical reciprocity between objects does not guarantee equal psychological transition in both directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","text":"Esteem for riches is not principally caused by contemplation of agreeable possessions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","text":"Esteem for riches is not principally caused by expected personal advantage or a general rule derived from such expectation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX10","text":"Love is not identical with desire for another’s happiness, nor hatred with desire for another’s misery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX11","text":"The conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger is not conceptually necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX12","text":"Pity does not require antecedent friendship or reflection on one’s own vulnerability to misfortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX13","text":"Sympathy does not require the observed person actually to feel the passion communicated to the observer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D1","text":"Enduring qualities produce stable affection without design, whereas transient actions require design to disclose enduring character.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D2","text":"Intention strengthens passion twice: by connecting the action to the person and by adding communicated esteem or contempt to its effect.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D3","text":"Accidental harm can produce violent but short-lived anger because the impression remains strong while the personal relation remains weak.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D4","text":"Recognized justice diminishes but need not erase anger because it opposes the painful impression without annihilating it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D5","text":"Harm can first produce hatred, after which hatred produces an opinion of injury that stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A6","CH_THN_SI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D6","text":"Relation and acquaintance produce affection by making the related or familiar person unusually vivid and therefore agreeable to contemplate.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A9","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D7","text":"Resemblance facilitates affection both by transferring vivacity and by predisposing the observer toward the corresponding emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A10","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D8","text":"A competing relation weakens an existing tie when it redirects imagination and obstructs the return transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D9","text":"Agreeable possessions generate esteem chiefly when imagination passes from the possessions to the satisfaction of their possessor.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A10","CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D10","text":"Expected advantage cannot principally explain wealth-esteem because esteem persists where benefit is impossible and the uniformity required for a general rule is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D11","text":"Sympathy remains the general cause of wealth-esteem because it communicates the proprietor’s satisfaction independently of benefit to the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D12","text":"The perceived beauty of utility is pleasure communicated from the beneficiary rather than pleasure intrinsic to sensible form.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D13","text":"Wealth produces a feedback circuit in which possession causes satisfaction, communicated satisfaction causes esteem, and perceived esteem increases the possessor’s satisfaction.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D14","text":"Love and hatred acquire practical tendencies through their conjunction with benevolence and anger, not through their own conceptual essence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A17","CH_THN_SI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D15","text":"Pity for strangers follows from the general human resemblance that permits their sorrow to become a vivid impression in the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D16","text":"An observed misfortune can produce stronger pity when the sufferer feels less sorrow because general expectation supplies the absent passion and contrast heightens it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:A20"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","text":"Personal attribution is temporally organized: durable passion requires either a durable quality or a sign of durable character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD2","text":"Moral accusation can function as an endogenous preservation-mechanism of prior affect rather than as its independent rational cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","text":"Kinship, acquaintance, resemblance, and sympathy form one economy of transmitted vivacity whose recurrent output is affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","text":"Social attachment depends on the topology of imaginative passage, not merely on the existence of an objective relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","text":"Economic rank and utility-based beauty are affective appropriations of satisfaction located initially in another person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","text":"Wealth reproduces its own desirability through recursive exchanges between possessed power, observer esteem, and proprietary vanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","text":"The benevolent or destructive direction of love and hatred is naturally installed but not logically or morally entailed by those passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","text":"Sympathy is a constructive simulation capable of exceeding, supplementing, or replacing the actual emotion of its apparent source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX1","text":"Rational judgment of guilt cannot be the exclusive ground of anger or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX2","text":"Deliberate intention cannot be the universal gate through which affective attribution passes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX3","text":"Objective merit cannot independently determine affection against the force of familiarity and associative vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","text":"The mind cannot be affectively self-sufficient; its animation depends on lively relations to external objects and other minds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX5","text":"Relations cannot be treated as static, symmetrical properties without regard to the direction and interruption of imaginative movement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX6","text":"Social esteem cannot be reduced to calculated self-interest or probable exchange.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX7","text":"The practical tendencies of passions cannot all be deduced from their conceptual content; some conjunctions remain irreducibly constitutional.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX8","text":"Sympathetic communication cannot be assumed to reproduce another’s inner state with veridical fidelity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","text":"An affectively neutral standpoint from which persons are judged independently of pleasure, pain, association, and prior passion is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX2","text":"Intrinsic social or aesthetic value independent of communicated satisfaction is structurally displaced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","text":"No necessary moral order guarantees that love seeks happiness or hatred seeks misery; the alignment rests on contingent nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4","text":"Transparent correspondence between the actual emotion of one mind and the sympathetic emotion of another is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX5","text":"A closed individual mind capable of sustaining pleasure, passion, and value without reflective dependence on other minds is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS1","text":"Pleasure or uneasiness from another → agreeable or disagreeable impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS2","text":"Enduring quality ∨ intention-as-character-sign → strong person-relation; momentary accident → weak person-relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS3","text":"Impression-relation × person-relation → love or hatred; strong impression × weak relation → acute but unstable passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS4","text":"Intention → communicated esteem or contempt → vanity or mortification → amplified affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5","text":"Pain-generated passion → threat of diminution by innocence or justice → justificatory injury-judgment → partial stabilization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS6","text":"Self-vivacity → relation, custom, or resemblance → lively idea of another → agreeable agitation → affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS7","text":"Easy outward transition + easy return → strong tie; competing transition + obstructed return → weakened tie.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8","text":"Possession or power → proprietor satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → perceived esteem → vanity → renewed satisfaction and esteem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS9","text":"Love or hatred + original constitutional coupling → benevolence or anger → blended compound affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10","text":"Observed condition + human resemblance + customary affect-rule + proximity or contrast → vivid constructed impression → pity despite absent original sorrow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF1","text":"Pleasure–pain rule → intentionality difficulty → quality/action distinction → explanation of passion duration → passion-generated injury judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF2","text":"Love of relations and acquaintance → search for common cause → force and vivacity → reciprocal-transition model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF3","text":"Wealth-esteem → competing causal hypotheses → failure of direct object-pleasure and expected advantage → sympathy → recursive status amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF4","text":"Compound passions → identity hypothesis → experiential rejection → original contingent conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF5","text":"Pity without friendship → sympathy through resemblance → communication of sorrow → passion without an actual original → constructive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME1","text":"Agency is reclassified from immediate causal authorship to durable attribution through character or intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME2","text":"The judgment of injury becomes potentially an effect of hatred rather than its necessary antecedent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME3","text":"Kinship, familiarity, and resemblance become variants of one vivacity-producing mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","text":"Relations become directional pathways of imagination rather than merely reciprocal logical connections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME5","text":"Esteem for wealth is relocated from rational advantage to vicarious participation in proprietary satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME6","text":"Utility-based beauty is relocated from sensible form to sympathetic apprehension of another’s benefit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME7","text":"Love and hatred are separated from the benevolent and destructive desires that ordinarily accompany them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","text":"Sympathy is expanded from emotional transmission to affective construction under association, general rules, and contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface: variations in anger, affection, social esteem, benevolence, and pity are treated as test cases for the passion-system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural: double relations, force and vivacity, directional association, sympathy, and recursive reflection generate the observed passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: apparent counterexamples are converted into discriminating tests that eliminate rival mechanisms and qualify the governing hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas and impressions differ by degree of force; enduring qualities sustain personal attribution, while transient events produce unstable passions unless mediated by intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the principal transitions from personal causation through association and sympathy to compound affection and pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction preserves the operative movement: anomaly → distinction → rival elimination → revised mechanism → extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence depends on accepting force and vivacity as the common currency connecting association, pleasure, impression, and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where rival explanations generate discriminating cases, especially intention, impossible benefit, and love–desire identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","text":"The relative weights of harm, justice, intention, associative strength, and individual disposition remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6","text":"Appeal to an original constitutional conjunction preserves the phenomenon of benevolence and anger at the cost of further causal derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","text":"Sympathy shifts from reproducing actual emotion to constructing absent emotion without a fully specified criterion governing the shift.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT8","text":"Malice is classified as a secondary affection but is not derived with the completeness given to pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT9","text":"Compression removes illustrative cases without removing the distinctions, eliminations, feedback structures, or terminal tensions required to regenerate the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","context":"Chapter 17","original_title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","original_context":"Chapter 17"},"original_title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","order":18,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVME"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVME@R001","sha256":"b75f58586d12fa7dea441030fae19fc49850b4079da82f325f4d255301c78022","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS1","text":"Problem: explain unprovoked pleasure in another’s misery and pain in another’s enjoyment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS2","text":"Core thesis: malice and envy arise when comparison converts another’s condition into an inverse estimate of one’s own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive psychological diagnosis grounded in affective succession and associative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS4","text":"Comparative judgments vary because comparison alters an idea’s attendant emotion, which custom then attributes to the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS5","text":"Comparison becomes affectively operative only where relations permit easy transition between ideas and their impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS6","text":"Movement: comparative distortion → altered self-estimate → reverse affect → malice/envy differentiation → relation-conditioned general law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A1","text":"Human sentiments ordinarily assign value through comparison rather than intrinsic assessment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A2","text":"Every sensed object or imagined idea is accompanied by an emotion proportioned to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A3","text":"A succeeding emotion is magnified or diminished by contrast with the preceding emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A4","text":"Habitual general rules carry the mind from a degree of emotion to the object’s customary corresponding degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A5","text":"Satisfaction with one’s condition varies with its apparent fortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A6","text":"In comparison, one term alters the felt value of the other in a direction contrary to its own direct affective effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A7","text":"Associative relation between ideas enables an easy transition of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A8","text":"Easy transition preserves the first object’s impression during passage to the second.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A9","text":"Preserved impressions can modify one another through comparison or affective contrariety.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A10","text":"Envy is pain produced when another’s present enjoyment comparatively diminishes one’s own apparent condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A11","text":"Malice is the unprovoked desire to produce another’s misery for the pleasure of comparative self-advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A12","text":"A predominant passion assimilates affective additions to its own direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX1","text":"Malice is not hatred caused by antecedent offense or injury.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX2","text":"Affective judgment is not governed chiefly by reason or intrinsic value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX3","text":"Comparative variation is not produced by alteration of the immediate impression, image, or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX4","text":"The direct survey of another’s condition is not affectively equivalent to comparison with that condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX5","text":"Envy is not proportionate to the absolute magnitude of another’s superiority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX6","text":"Proximity in merit alone is not sufficient for envy without supporting relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX7","text":"Unrelated ideas cannot substantially affect one another merely through juxtaposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX8","text":"Contrary passions do not encounter or neutralize one another when their objects remain associatively separate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D1","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A2 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A4) Comparison can change judgment while leaving representation unchanged because contrast alters the attendant emotion and custom attributes that alteration to the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D2","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A5 + CH_THN_SVME:A6) Another’s misery elevates one’s apparent happiness and produces pleasure, while another’s happiness diminishes it and produces pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D3","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A6) The same misery can be painful under direct survey and pleasurable under comparative survey.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D4","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A5) Past pain becomes agreeable when contrasted with present satisfaction, while past pleasure becomes painful when contrasted with present deficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D5","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A12) Self-inflicted suffering can remove a contrast that would otherwise intensify predominant grief or the imagined punishment of remorse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D6","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A5 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) An inferior’s approach converts decreased comparative pleasure into positive pain by comparing the reduced advantage with its former degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D7","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A9 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) Nearby or resembling rivals provoke more envy than remote superiors because proximity sustains the transition required for comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D8","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A9) Separating related objects into distinct conceptual units prevents their attendant passions from influencing or contradicting one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D9","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) The mind seeks relief from related superiority either by breaking the association or by removing the superiority itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD1","text":"Evaluative magnitude is relationally produced even where the perceived object and its representation remain invariant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD2","text":"Malice converts another’s suffering into an instrument for changing one’s own apparent position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD3","text":"Envy follows associative proximity more reliably than absolute inequality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD4","text":"Comparative passions are recursively temporal: a loss of former comparative pleasure can itself become a new source of pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD5","text":"Associative organization governs whether passions interact across personal, social, political, and aesthetic domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD6","text":"Emotional distance can be produced by weakening relations even when objective superiority remains unchanged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX1","text":"Affective self-estimation is not insulated from the fortunes of comparable others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX2","text":"Identity of representation does not entail identity of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX3","text":"Objective inequality cannot by itself generate envy without an associative route of comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX4","text":"Natural contrariety between passions cannot ensure their interaction without continuity between their ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX5","text":"Sensory invariance cannot reliably correct judgments imposed by habitual effect-to-cause association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX6","text":"Evil need not be desired as an independent end for malicious action to arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX1","text":"The system supplies no affectively operative intrinsic baseline capable of stabilizing self-worth against comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX2","text":"No autonomous faculty of malevolence is required; comparative self-augmentation occupies its explanatory place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX3","text":"The boundary of operative relation has no independent metric and appears only where transition succeeds or fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX4","text":"The system does not fully specify what determines whether another’s condition is surveyed directly or comparatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS1","text":"Object O → attendant emotion E(O) proportioned to O.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS2","text":"Relation R(O1,O2) → easy transition O1→O2 → preservation of E(O1).","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS3","text":"Preserved E(O1) + succeeding E(O2) → contrast → altered E′(O2).","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS4","text":"Altered E′(O2) + habitual emotion–magnitude rule → altered judgment of O2 despite invariant representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS5","text":"Another’s misery compared with self → augmented apparent self-condition → pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS6","text":"Another’s enjoyment compared with self → diminished apparent self-condition → pain → envy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS7","text":"Anticipated misery of another → anticipated comparative elevation of self → desire to produce misery → malice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS8","text":"Strong relation → sustained comparison and impression interaction; broken relation → affective separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS9","text":"Former comparative advantage A0 → reduced advantage A1 → comparison A1<A0 → lost pleasure converted into pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AF1","text":"Same object/different estimate → attendant-emotion hypothesis → contrast amplification → habitual projection of altered emotion onto the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AF2","text":"Comparative self-estimation → inverse response to others’ fortunes → envy/malice distinction → proximity and relation as affective gates → general law of impression transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME1","text":"Malice and envy are reclassified from responses to moral injury as products of comparative self-estimation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME2","text":"Evaluative change is relocated from the object or idea to its attendant impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME3","text":"Social hierarchy is redrawn through associative proximity rather than absolute rank alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME4","text":"Distance becomes an affective mechanism capable of disabling comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME5","text":"Direct valence and comparative valence become distinct: what is painful in itself may become pleasurable through contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME6","text":"Personal envy, political rivalry, aesthetic consistency, and passion-conflict become instances of one relation-mediated transition law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT1","text":"Surface: malice and envy are differentiated by whether another’s misery is already present or deliberately sought for comparative pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT2","text":"Structural: comparison, emotional succession, habitual attribution, and associative relation jointly produce altered judgments and passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: explanation shifts from rational or moral appraisal to the formation, severance, and manipulation of comparative relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas carry attendant impressions whose effects depend on sequence, preserved transition, present comparison, and remembered prior condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVME:A1–CH_THN_SVME:A12 are sufficient to reconstruct the perceptual mechanism, passion differentiation, and relational boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","text":"The relative activation of direct survey and comparative survey remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT3","text":"The kinds and degrees of relation required for effective comparison lack a formal threshold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT4","text":"The structure is internally coherent if proportional attendant emotion and habitual effect-to-cause attribution are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT5","text":"The strongest explanatory linkage is the relation-conditioned transfer from stable representation to variable affective judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT6","text":"The extension from magnitude and number to virtue, wealth, happiness, and misery depends on analogical generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT7","text":"The inference from absent relation preventing an effect establishes relation as contributory or necessary, not independently sufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT8","text":"The self-affliction cases require the auxiliary predominance principle rather than following from comparison alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from comparative perception through reverse affect to the general associative law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","context":"Chapter 18","original_title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_context":"Chapter 18"},"original_title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","order":19,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXLH"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXLH@R001","sha256":"3a99be11cec27c64fa436cbce9d14add164015776d4a99b54c0bf9499ae41b70","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS1","text":"Problem: whether the preceding causal system of love and hatred extends beyond humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS2","text":"Thesis: animal affections arise from simple, species-general principles continuous with those governing human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS3","text":"Argument type: comparative, empirical, and confirmatory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS4","text":"Movement: reduced animal reflection → isolation of sensible causes → qualified relational causes → instinct → sympathy → comparative passion prevalence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS5","text":"Cognitive demand determines which affective mechanisms operate strongly in animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","text":"The recurrence of the predicted passions under reduced cognition corroborates the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A1","text":"Love and hatred are common to the whole sensitive creation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A2","text":"The operative causes of love and hatred require no force of reflection or penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A3","text":"The springs governing love and hatred are not peculiar to humanity or any single animal species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A4","text":"Animals are only weakly susceptible to pleasures and pains of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A5","text":"Animals judge affective objects by the sensible good or evil those objects produce.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A6","text":"Animal love is not restricted to members of the animal’s own species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A7","text":"The affective force of relation is limited by the animal’s capacity to trace relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A8","text":"Cognitively obvious associative relations generate affection in animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A9","text":"Parental affection toward offspring arises from a peculiar instinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A10","text":"Sympathy communicates passions among animals without knowledge of the initiating cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A11","text":"Animals possess a sense of one another’s pain and pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A12","text":"Sympathetic conjunction intensifies animal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A13","text":"Envy and malice require less thought and imagination than pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX1","text":"Love and hatred are not exclusively human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX2","text":"Their production does not require reflective penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","text":"Animal love is not bounded by identity of species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX4","text":"Animal affection is not governed primarily by imagined goods and evils.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX5","text":"Relation is not wholly absent from animal affection, though its influence is cognitively restricted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX6","text":"Parental affection is not reduced to acquired benefit or traced relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","text":"Sympathy does not require cognition of the original cause of a passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX8","text":"Restraint toward companions is not explained solely by fear of retaliation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX9","text":"Pity is not assumed to be more primitive or prevalent than envy and malice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D1","text":"Benefits generate animal love and injuries generate animal hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D2","text":"Relation is weaker in animals than in humans but remains effective through acquaintance, resemblance, and other obvious associations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D3","text":"Species identity is neither a necessary nor sufficient boundary for an animal’s objects of affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D4","text":"Parental attachment constitutes an instinctive branch distinct from ordinary sensible and associative causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D5","text":"Passions can organize animal social conduct without shared knowledge of their originating object.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D6","text":"Collective animation exceeds isolated passion when animals act in sympathetic conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D7","text":"Envy and malice may be more common among animals than pity because their cognitive threshold is lower.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A4","CH_THN_SXLH:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D8","text":"The persistence of love, hatred, and sympathy under diminished reflection supports their derivation from simple, species-general springs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A1","CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A4","CH_THN_SXLH:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","text":"Animal psychology functions as a cognitive-subtraction test: mechanisms surviving reduced reflection cannot depend essentially on reflective human faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","text":"Affective universality is compatible with unequal cognitive complexity because common passions can be produced through differently available channels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","text":"The chapter treats lower cognitive cost as a condition of wider animal prevalence among passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","text":"Social intensification of affect precedes and exceeds conscious representation of its causal origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","text":"The unity of the system lies in species-general springs rather than in a single uniform route of affective production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX1","text":"Reflective self-consciousness cannot be a necessary condition of love and hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX2","text":"Species-specific human faculties cannot constitute the ultimate source of the passions shared with animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX3","text":"Abstract relation-tracing cannot be required for every form of associative attachment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","text":"Affective communication cannot depend on propositional knowledge of what caused the communicated passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX5","text":"Animal social restraint cannot be exhausted by retaliation avoidance if it evidences sensitivity to another’s pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX6","text":"A wholly acquired or associative account cannot explain parental affection if peculiar instinct is irreducible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","text":"Greater moral valuation cannot be assumed to entail greater natural prevalence; pity may be rarer because it is cognitively costlier.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX1","text":"An ontological rupture between human and animal affective life is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","text":"Direct animal self-testimony is unavailable; behavioral analogy must bear the attribution of internal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX3","text":"Conscious causal representation is unavailable as the universal ground of social affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","text":"A unitary reduction of affection to sensible reward and injury is foreclosed by relation, instinct, and sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS1","text":"Species-general causes + cognitive simplicity → prediction of animal applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS2","text":"Reduced reflection and imagination → isolation of sensible affective causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS3","text":"Sensible good or benefit → love; sensible evil or injury → hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS4","text":"Limited relation-tracing + obvious association → restricted relational affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS5","text":"Parent–offspring configuration → instinctive affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS6","text":"Perceived passion or suffering → sympathetic communication → coordinated or intensified affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS7","text":"Reduced imaginative demand → greater probable prevalence of envy and malice relative to pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS8","text":"Convergence of animal mechanisms with prior predictions → confirmation of the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AF1","text":"Foregoing causal system → animal test case → cognitive restriction → sensible causation → qualified association and instinct → sympathetic transmission → cognitive-cost comparison → systemic corroboration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME1","text":"The human–animal opposition is redrawn as continuity of affective mechanism with variation in cognitive degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME2","text":"Species membership ceases to define the possible object-range of love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","text":"Animal affection is reclassified into sensible, associative, instinctive, and sympathetic causal channels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME4","text":"Relation becomes cognitively graded rather than simply present or absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME5","text":"Collective animal animation becomes visible as transmitted and amplified passion rather than mere aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME6","text":"Comparative prevalence among passions becomes explicable through differing demands on thought and imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT1","text":"Surface: animals exhibit love, hatred, sympathy, envy, malice, and sensitivity to one another’s suffering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT2","text":"Structural: sensible effects, obvious associations, instinct, and sympathetic transmission form distinct affective mechanisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: animal conduct tests whether the preceding theory depends on specifically human reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT4","text":"Ontological: sensitive life forms a continuous affective domain across species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT5","text":"Epistemic: internal animal passions are inferred from patterned conduct and analogy with human experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from cross-species continuity to confirmation of the prior system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT2","text":"The precise mechanisms converting sensible good, resemblance, and communicated emotion into affection remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent only if patterned animal conduct warrants attribution of passions analogous to human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where reduced animal reflection independently tests the claimed simplicity of affective causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT5","text":"The restriction of animal judgment to sensible good and evil is not explicitly reconciled with acquaintance and resemblance as additional causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6","text":"Peculiar parental instinct introduces a causal exception without integration into the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT7","text":"Cross-species preference and conspecific preference are both asserted, but the conditions governing their relative strength remain unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8","text":"Human analogy explains animal sympathy while animal sympathy confirms the human system, creating a partially reciprocal evidential structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if its universal claims are retained despite their compression from selective observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","original_context":"Chapter 19"},"original_title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","order":20,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVEC"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVEC@R001","sha256":"83c276b42ed267646bdb20b1bde3d71a522ad10255ea47e9df46c24f55b433af","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how custom can intensify or diminish passion and reverse pleasure and pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS2","text":"All effects of custom are reduced to facility and subsequent inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS3","text":"The argument is a causal-psychological reduction through variations in mental motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS4","text":"Unfamiliarity produces difficulty, agitation, novelty, and amplified passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS5","text":"Repetition removes novelty and produces tranquillity before generating pleasurable facility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS6","text":"Moderate facility can reconcile the mind to pain, whereas excessive facility destroys interest and can reverse pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS7","text":"Facility and inclination jointly weaken passive habits while strengthening active habits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A1","text":"Facility and inclination exhaust the original effects of custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A2","text":"Repetition first produces facility in action or conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A3","text":"Repetition subsequently produces inclination toward the repeated action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A4","text":"Unaccustomed action or conception encounters resistance from unpliable faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A5","text":"Mental resistance excites the spirits and generates the emotions of novelty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A6","text":"Moderate enlivening of the mind is pleasurable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A7","text":"An emotion attending a passion is readily converted into and augments that passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A8","text":"Repetition extinguishes novelty, agitation, and their amplification of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A9","text":"Moderate facility produces pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A10","text":"The pleasure of facility consists in orderly motion rather than agitated motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A11","text":"Excessive facility renders mental action faint, languid, and unable to sustain interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A12","text":"Custom-induced aversion ordinarily requires an object naturally attended by emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A13","text":"Extinction of an expected agreeable emotion disposes the mind toward the opposite affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A14","text":"An entirely disagreeable action cannot become an object of inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A15","text":"Facility weakens passive habits by rendering the motion of the spirits faint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A16","text":"Active performance supplies independent support to the spirits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A17","text":"Customary inclination adds force to active performance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX1","text":"Custom does not affect passion monotonically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX2","text":"The pleasure of novelty is not the pleasure of facility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX3","text":"Orderly facility does not augment painful affection as novelty-agitation does.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX4","text":"Frequent repetition does not ordinarily make affectively neutral objects disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX5","text":"Custom cannot create inclination toward what remains entirely disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","text":"Active and passive habits are not strengthened or weakened by the same mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D1","text":"Novel objects produce more pleasure or pain than their intrinsic affective character supplies.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A6","CH_THN_SVEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D2","text":"Early repetition diminishes passion by removing the agitation that had amplified it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SVEC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D3","text":"Moderate facility can convert initial harshness into relish because its pleasure counteracts rather than inflames pain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D4","text":"Excessive repetition can convert pleasure into pain by extinguishing the emotion required to sustain interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D5","text":"Affectively neutral repeated objects normally remain non-aversive because custom has no natural emotion to destroy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D6","text":"Custom produces inclination only where residual disagreeability does not amount to complete repugnance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SVEC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D7","text":"Passive habits decline under repetition, while active habits acquire additional force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A15","CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SVEC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D8","text":"The affective trajectory of repetition is non-linear: amplification gives way to tranquillity, pleasure, languor, and possible reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","text":"Experienced valence depends on the history and degree of encounter, not solely on the object’s natural affective character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","text":"Custom operates through successive regimes rather than through cumulative reinforcement alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","text":"Difficulty and facility are affectively reversible: either can support or weaken the mind according to degree and mode of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","text":"Repetition redistributes affective force between passive receptivity and active tendency rather than merely increasing familiarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","text":"Intrinsic pleasure or pain cannot by itself determine the experienced strength or direction of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX2","text":"Facility cannot be inherently pleasurable, because its excess must be capable of producing languor and aversion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX3","text":"Diminished excitation cannot have a uniform effect across passive reception and active performance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","text":"Repetition cannot explain its own effects without distinctions of degree, affective endowment, and activity type.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX1","text":"A fixed subject-object relation of affect, insulated from temporal repetition, is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2","text":"A single continuous law by which increasing custom always increases or always decreases passion is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX3","text":"Custom is denied unlimited transformative power by the irreducible boundary of complete disagreeability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS1","text":"Custom/repetition → facility + subsequent inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS2","text":"Unfamiliarity → resistance → excitation → novelty-pleasure → conversion into the predominant passion → affective amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","text":"Repetition → novelty-decay → tranquillity → moderate facility → orderly motion → pleasure → possible pain-to-pleasure reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4","text":"Continued repetition → excessive facility → faintness and languor → destruction of sustaining emotion → indifference → possible opposite affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS5","text":"Inclination + complete disagreeability → blocked tendency; inclination + tolerable action → strengthened tendency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS6","text":"Passive mode + facility → weakened habit; active mode + self-supported spirits + inclination → strengthened habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AF1","text":"Opposed effects of custom → reduction to facility and inclination → novelty-agitation mechanism → repetition and tranquillity → moderate facility and relish → excessive facility and affective reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AF2","text":"Inclination effect → disagreeability boundary → active/passive distinction → active strengthening and passive weakening.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME1","text":"Repetition is reclassified from a uniform influence into a threshold-dependent process with contrary phases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","text":"Affective force is divided between the object’s natural valence and the mental motion attending its apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME3","text":"Novelty and familiarity cease to be simple opposites and become distinct regimes of excitation and order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME4","text":"Habit is divided into passive diminution and active reinforcement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME5","text":"Pleasure and pain become reversible outcomes of altered mental operation rather than fixed responses to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT1","text":"Surface: Custom increases, diminishes, and reverses passions while differentiating active from passive habits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","text":"Structural: Resistance, agitation, orderly motion, languor, and tendency mediate custom’s effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Diverse and apparently contradictory phenomena are reduced to two primary operations governed by degree and mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Affect is a path-dependent process whose present force is constituted by successive encounters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s principal causal sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","text":"The boundary between moderate and excessive facility remains quantitatively undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","text":"The language of the spirits supplies a causal vocabulary without a fully specified mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT4","text":"The transition from acquired facility to inclination is asserted more strongly than it is derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT5","text":"The structure is strongest where one graded mechanism explains both affective reconciliation and affective exhaustion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT6","text":"The active/passive divergence depends on the compressed claim that active performance supports its own excitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT7","text":"The scope boundary between pain convertible by facility and complete disagreeability resistant to inclination remains unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT8","text":"The system is internally coherent if attendant-emotion conversion and degree-sensitive facility are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from dual-effect reduction through facility’s phases to the final habit bifurcation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","original_context":"Chapter 20"},"original_title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","order":21,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXCL"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXCL@R001","sha256":"2554e1b2b7fc3c20f3cf49d5b569e4baf3e9c3a396bd015c61ad98987d0dca3e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS1","text":"Problem: explain why truth motivates inquiry when truth and assurance alone do not reliably produce pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS2","text":"Thesis: scientific curiosity is generated by qualified acts of discovery, not by truth considered intrinsically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS3","text":"Argument type: differential psychological genealogy reinforced by cross-domain analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS4","text":"Active inquiry derives its principal pleasure from exercised understanding, while perceived importance fixes attention and success completes the pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS5","text":"Passion originating in pursuit transfers affective concern to the pursued end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS6","text":"Non-investigative curiosity has a distinct source: belief vivifies and stabilizes an engaging idea, relieving the pain of doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS7","text":"Movement: intrinsic-truth hypothesis → differential test → composite pursuit mechanism → analogical validation → bifurcation of curiosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A1","text":"Equal truth and equal assurance can yield unequal pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A2","text":"Exercised genius and understanding in difficult discovery are the principal source of scientific pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A3","text":"Attention is necessary for cognitive exercise to produce satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A4","text":"Perceived importance functions as a condition for fixing attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A5","text":"Perceived importance contributes little direct pleasure independently of its effect on attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A6","text":"Imagined utility can operate through faint sympathy without genuine concern for its beneficiaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A7","text":"Some success in attaining the pursued end is required to complete the pleasure of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A8","text":"Passion originating in action and pursuit naturally produces derivative concern for the related end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A9","text":"The active-inquiry mechanism applies both to abstract relations and to real connections and existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A10","text":"Non-investigative curiosity arises from a principle distinct from exercised inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A11","text":"Belief vivifies an idea in the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A12","text":"Belief fixes an idea against hesitation and uncertainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A13","text":"A vivid idea gives pleasure by affecting the imagination like a moderate passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A14","text":"Doubt produces pain by rapidly varying thought among competing ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A15","text":"Instability generates curiosity when interest, relation, greatness, or novelty makes an idea sufficiently forceful and proximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX1","text":"Truth is not desired merely as truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX2","text":"Demonstrative superiority or increased assurance is not sufficient to increase epistemic pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX3","text":"Objective difficulty is not valuable when knowledge is acquired without cognitive exertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX4","text":"Exercised understanding alone is not sufficient without attention-fixing importance and some success.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX5","text":"A requirement that inquiry appear publicly useful does not establish public spirit in the inquirer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX6","text":"Utility is not the primary direct passion sustaining scientific inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","text":"Concern for an end need not precede the activity directed toward it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX8","text":"Informational curiosity is not reducible to self-interest or active study.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX9","text":"Curiosity attaches neither to every fact nor exclusively to facts of practical interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D1","text":"Variations in epistemic pleasure must be attributed to qualities of discovery rather than to correctness or certainty alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A1","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D2","text":"Scientific enjoyment requires the conjunction of demanding cognitive activity, engaged attention, and partial attainment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D3","text":"A philosopher can require belief in public utility while remaining indifferent to the public good because imagined sympathy supplies attention rather than a terminal motive.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D4","text":"Error and disappointment become painful even when the pursued end was initially indifferent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D5","text":"Philosophy, hunting, and gaming instantiate a common architecture in which a represented stake activates attention and difficult pursuit generates concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D6","text":"The affective structure of active inquiry remains constant across mathematical, moral, political, and natural-philosophical objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D7","text":"Non-investigative curiosity seeks a vivid and settled conception when an already engaging idea remains unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A10","CH_THN_SXCL:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D8","text":"Familiarity increases curiosity by strengthening relation and ideational force, while fading representation extinguishes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD1","text":"Conditions can be motivationally indispensable without being original sources of pleasure: importance engages attention, and success consummates an independently generated pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD2","text":"Epistemic motivation forms a recursive circuit: represented importance fixes attention → cognitive activity becomes pleasurable → concern transfers to the end → success and error acquire affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD3","text":"Apparently disinterested inquiry can arise without benevolence or material interest through the conjunction of imaginative sympathy and exercised understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD4","text":"Truth functions affectively in two distinct ways: as the endpoint of active achievement and as the content of stabilizing belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","text":"Operational uncertainty can intensify active pursuit, while doxastic vacillation produces pain and demands closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD6","text":"Epistemic value within the system depends less on an object considered alone than on its relation to effort, attention, imagination, and temporal stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX1","text":"Epistemic motivation cannot transparently track truth as an intrinsic good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX2","text":"Cognition cannot be affectively self-sufficient or independent of attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX3","text":"Objective utility cannot be identical with subjective concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX4","text":"Every passion cannot originate in the antecedent value of its end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX5","text":"Belief cannot be treated as merely representational; it modifies pleasure, pain, and ideational stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX6","text":"Curiosity cannot be determined by informational content alone, independently of relation, novelty, familiarity, and memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX1","text":"The system excludes an autonomous, purely truth-directed intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX2","text":"The singular name “love of truth” does not correspond to a single causal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX3","text":"No object-side property of truth guarantees attention, pleasure, or pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","text":"The account of informational curiosity contains no explicit truth-sensitive discriminator between true belief and a false belief that equally vivifies and fixes an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5","text":"A normative reason why truth ought to be pursued remains absent; the system supplies an affective genealogy rather than an epistemic justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1","text":"Truth and assurance held constant + pleasure varied by mode of attainment → truth-as-such excluded as sufficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS2","text":"Perceived utility → faint imaginative sympathy → attention fixed; difficulty → exercised understanding; attention × exercise × success → scientific pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS3","text":"Pleasure in pursuit → relational transfer of passion → acquired concern for end → pain at error or failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS4","text":"Philosophy / hunting / gaming → represented stake + demanding pursuit → conjoint effect unavailable from either factor alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS5","text":"Abstract relations or real existence → same active-inquiry architecture → general scientific curiosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS6","text":"Interest, relation, greatness, or novelty → forceful unstable idea → doubt and painful alternation → demand for belief → vivification + fixation → pleasure and relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AF1","text":"Neglected passion → division of truth → intrinsic-truth explanation falsified → mental exercise identified → importance reclassified as attentional support → success added → concern transferred to the end → analogical confirmation → extension across sciences → separate informational curiosity diagnosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AF2","text":"Curiosity → active discovery branch: exertion + attention + success → pleasure; informational branch: ideational force + instability → painful doubt → stabilizing belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","text":"Truth is reclassified from an intrinsically desired object into an endpoint mediated by affective and cognitive operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME2","text":"Utility is reclassified from terminal motive to imaginative support for attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME3","text":"Disinterested inquiry becomes intelligible without attribution of benevolence or material self-interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME4","text":"Philosophy, hunting, and gaming become structurally comparable as stake-mediated pursuits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME5","text":"Curiosity divides into active challenge-seeking and passive uncertainty-relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME6","text":"Belief becomes visible as an affective operation of vivification and stabilization, not merely assent to content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT1","text":"Surface — Variable pleasure in scientific discovery and the desire to know otherwise irrelevant facts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2","text":"Structural — Attention mediates between represented importance and pleasurable cognitive exertion; belief mediates between unstable ideas and affective relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument decomposes motivation into principal source, enabling condition, completion condition, and acquired end-concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The mind is treated as a succession of ideas whose vivacity, fixation, rapid alteration, and relational transfer generate passions over time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct both the active-inquiry mechanism and the distinct informational-curiosity mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction depends on prior theories of belief, sympathy, attention, and the relational transfer of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent where it distinguishes original pleasure from attentional conditions and acquired concern for ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT4","text":"Its strongest operation is the controlled separation of truth and assurance from cognitive exertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5","text":"The role of faint sympathy is compressed: its capacity to support attention without genuine concern is asserted more than generated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT6","text":"The informational branch explains desire for settled belief more directly than desire for truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT7","text":"The opposed valences of pursuit-uncertainty and belief-instability remain differentiated but not fully unified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if the final bifurcation of curiosity is retained rather than reduced to a single truth-drive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","original_context":"Chapter 21"},"original_title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI","layer":"CH","title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","order":22,"parents":["RAW_THN_PIJI"],"revision":"CH_THN_PIJI@R001","sha256":"a67eca46add21f1a06d2888b20d39c1269a2b52733630b716bad8496ffd0e88a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain both the establishment of justice and the subsequent attribution of moral virtue to its observance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS2","text":"Justice is an artificial but necessary and non-arbitrary virtue generated by human convention under determinate social conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS3","text":"The argument is eliminative, genealogical, functional, and sentimentalist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS4","text":"Semantic disambiguation of nature leads to an affective criterion of morality, which leads to the priority of motive over action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS5","text":"The circularity of making regard for justice its own first motive forces the elimination of self-love, public benevolence, and private benevolence as original motives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS6","text":"Human weakness, partial affection, transferable scarcity, and social dependence generate a reciprocal convention stabilizing possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS7","text":"Common interest generates observance; sympathy with public interest generates moral approval; education, reputation, and government reinforce the result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A1","text":"Virtue is marked by satisfaction and vice by uneasiness produced under general contemplation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A2","text":"Moral merit belongs ultimately to the motive signified by an action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A3","text":"The first virtuous motive for an action must precede and differ from regard for that action’s morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A4","text":"Standards of duty track the ordinary force and direction of passions in human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A5","text":"Human affection ordinarily prioritizes self, relations, acquaintances, and strangers in descending order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A6","text":"Society is necessary to compensate human weakness and secure the satisfaction of human wants.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A7","text":"External goods are scarce relative to desire, transferable, and insecure against appropriation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A8","text":"Uncultivated moral judgment conforms to natural partiality rather than correcting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A9","text":"Acquisitive self-interest can be restrained only by its reflective redirection toward the greater advantages of social preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A10","text":"Stability of possession arises through mutually known and conditionally reciprocal resolutions grounded in common interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A11","text":"Convention does not depend upon a prior promise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A12","text":"Convention acquires force gradually through repeated experience and increasing confidence in reciprocal conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A13","text":"Justice, injustice, right, and obligation arise only after possession has been stabilized by convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A14","text":"Property is a moral relation constituted by the laws of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A15","text":"The whole scheme of justice is socially and individually advantageous even when particular just acts are harmful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A16","text":"Interest in preserving the general scheme supplies the natural obligation to justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A17","text":"Sympathy with public interest supplies the moral approbation of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A18","text":"The rules of justice are artificial without being arbitrary because their invention is necessary and obvious under human circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX1","text":"Natural and unnatural do not mark the boundary between virtue and vice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX2","text":"The external performance is not itself the bearer of moral merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX3","text":"Regard for the justice of an action cannot be that action’s first virtuous motive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","text":"Justice is not an innate passion capable of originally producing equitable conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX5","text":"Unrestrained private interest is not a universal motive to honest action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","text":"An affection for humanity merely as humanity is not an operative universal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","text":"Private benevolence cannot ground invariant property rights or obligations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","text":"Direct concern for public interest is not the original ordinary motive of just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX9","text":"Justice is not founded upon eternal and immutable relations of ideas discovered by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX10","text":"Convention is not a promise and cannot be founded upon promises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX11","text":"Property, right, and obligation do not exist naturally before justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX12","text":"Justice is not required under universal benevolence or complete abundance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX13","text":"The solitary state of nature is not an actual historical condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","text":"Artificiality does not entail arbitrariness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX15","text":"Political inculcation is not the sole cause of moral distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX16","text":"The absence of property before convention does not mean that violating another’s property was permissible; the relevant property relation did not yet exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D1","text":"Duty can secondarily motivate an action only because an antecedent natural motive has already made that kind of action morally intelligible.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D2","text":"A natural sense of justice cannot be the first cause of just conduct; justice becomes intelligible only after the convention it evaluates.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:A8","CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D3","text":"Partial generosity both forms close associations and generates conflict between those associations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D4","text":"Social instability arises from the conjunction of partial affection with scarce and transferable goods, not from either condition alone.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D5","text":"Immediate self-interest threatens society, while reflected self-interest preserves society by accepting reciprocal restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D6","text":"Non-promissory convention avoids regress by grounding coordination in reciprocal expectations rather than antecedent obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D7","text":"Justice and property are co-generated; property cannot be invoked to explain the origin of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D8","text":"Removing either confined generosity or scarcity removes the functional conditions that make justice necessary.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A7","CH_THN_PIJI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D9","text":"The justice of a particular act is determined by its place within a beneficial general scheme rather than by its isolated consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D10","text":"Compliance precedes moralization: interest first establishes observance, and sympathy later converts observance into virtue.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D11","text":"As society expands and direct awareness of common interest weakens, sympathy and general rules preserve moral disapproval of injustice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A1","CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D12","text":"Political rhetoric can extend an existing moral sentiment but cannot create moral intelligibility without natural affective materials.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A1","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D13","text":"Education and reputation internalize established conventions as durable sentiments of honor.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A4","CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D14","text":"A pre-conventional condition contains neither justice nor injustice because it contains no moral relation of property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","text":"Normative order emerges from non-moral coordination: interested restraint produces convention, and convention becomes morally charged through sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD2","text":"The universality of justice is rule-general and reciprocal rather than metaphysical or consequence-sensitive at the level of individual acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","text":"Property and right are socially constituted relations sustained by stabilized expectations rather than intrinsic features of persons or objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD4","text":"The same partial interest that generates social conflict also supplies the only passion capable of resolving it when redirected by understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","text":"Justice is conditionally necessary: contingent relative to scarcity and limited generosity, yet effectively inseparable from humanity while those conditions persist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","text":"Artificial rules become second-nature through sympathy, custom, education, public judgment, and reputational interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD7","text":"Justice converts long-range systemic advantage into stable obligations capable of overriding contrary local advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","text":"Moral and institutional emergence are temporally ordered but mutually reinforcing once established: convention enables approval, and approval stabilizes convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","text":"A moral predicate cannot generate the first passion that makes its own application possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX2","text":"Natural impartial benevolence cannot be sufficiently strong or general to stabilize possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX3","text":"Property cannot be a pre-institutional relation waiting merely to be recognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX4","text":"Reason cannot independently create concern, motivation, or moral obligation from relations of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX5","text":"The consequences of isolated acts cannot determine the justice of those acts independently of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6","text":"Neither a sovereign command nor an antecedent promise is necessary for conventions to emerge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX7","text":"The genealogy of society does not require an actual period of solitary human existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX8","text":"Stable restraint cannot persist without confidence that others will regulate their conduct by the same rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX1","text":"Justice occupies the absence of a direct natural route from partial passion to impartial social order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX2","text":"Property occupies the absence of natural stability in scarce and transferable possessions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX3","text":"Moral approbation occupies the gap between interested compliance and disinterested evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX4","text":"The system excludes self-grounding normativity: every moral obligation depends upon antecedent passions, interests, and sentiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","text":"Stable property forecloses case-by-case redistribution according to immediate merit, benevolence, need, or public advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","text":"The system contains no primordial rights-bearing individual prior to convention; the pre-social subject lacks the relations that would make such rights intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS1","text":"Naturalness disambiguated → morality detached from nature/unnature classification → pleasure and uneasiness installed as evaluative criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS2","text":"Action treated as sign → motive identified as moral object → regard for morality rejected as first motive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS3","text":"Candidate natural motives tested → private interest, public benevolence, private benevolence, and universal humanity eliminated → artificial origin required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS4","text":"Human wants and weakness → sexual and familial association → habit-mediated recognition of social advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS5","text":"Partial affection + scarce transferable goods → unstable possession → threat to socially necessary cooperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS6","text":"Reflection on long-range interest → redirection of acquisitive passion → reciprocal rule of abstinence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS7","text":"Mutual expression of conditional restraint → non-promissory convention → repeated experience → stable expectation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","text":"Stable possession → justice and injustice → property, right, and obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS9","text":"Advantage of the whole scheme → interested observance → sympathy with public effects → moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS10","text":"Moral approval → education, political reinforcement, and reputation → internalized probity → increased institutional stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF1","text":"Ambiguity of nature → affective moral criterion → priority of motive → prohibition of motivational circularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF2","text":"Failure of natural motives → anthropological and material diagnosis → reflected self-interest → reciprocal convention → artificial justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF3","text":"Stability of possession → property and obligation → systemic advantage → sympathetic approval → reinforced moral practice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME1","text":"The natural/artificial distinction replaces the natural/unnatural distinction as the relevant classification of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME2","text":"The primary object of moral judgment shifts from external action to motivating passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME3","text":"Justice is reclassified from an innate virtue into a convention-dependent practice subsequently moralized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME4","text":"Self-interest is divided into an impulsive form destructive of society and a reflective form capable of sustaining it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME5","text":"Property shifts from being the presumed ground of justice to being an effect of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME6","text":"Utility shifts from the isolated act to the coordinated system of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME7","text":"Convention becomes visible as decentralized reciprocal coordination rather than promise, command, or explicit contract.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME8","text":"The state of nature shifts from historical origin to analytical fiction produced by separating passions from understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME9","text":"Moral obligation is separated from natural obligation: sympathy explains approval, while interest explains initial observance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT1","text":"Surface: Justice is tested as a natural or artificial virtue, and its rules and moral valuation are assigned distinct origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT2","text":"Structural: Partial affection, scarcity, transferability, reciprocal expectation, general rules, and social reinforcement constitute the mechanism of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Explanation must proceed from motive and convention to property and obligation, prohibiting the use of established moral categories to explain their own genesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Property and right are emergent relations formed gradually through coordinated expectations; their contingency coexists with their practical inseparability from enduring human conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_PIJI:A1–CH_THN_PIJI:A18 are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s semantic, motivational, genealogical, institutional, and sentimental stages.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT2","text":"The structure is coherent only if the establishment of rules, interested observance, and moral approbation remain distinct explanatory moments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT3","text":"The strongest transition is from motivational circularity and failed natural motives to reciprocal convention grounded in reflected interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT4","text":"The shift from rejected private interest to accepted common interest depends upon distinguishing immediate unilateral gain from conditional systemic advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","text":"The co-generation of justice and property risks circularity unless physical possession is kept distinct from morally constituted property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT6","text":"The account of moral obligation remains underdetermined because the full mechanism of sympathy and the natural virtues is explicitly deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT7","text":"General-rule utility explains adherence despite harmful particular acts but leaves the limits, revision, and exception conditions of those rules unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT8","text":"The artificial-versus-natural tension is contained by assigning different senses to nature, but the resolution depends upon controlled semantic variation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from conceptual clearing through genealogy to moral internalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","context":"Chapter 22","original_title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","original_context":"Chapter 22"},"original_title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","order":23,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIRW"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIRW@R001","sha256":"f34752db164d8282a15b9d3c642160af2d3996b26c5d0caa17146eaabcfdcd41","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS1","text":"Problem: the necessary convention of stable possession does not determine which goods belong to which persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS2","text":"Thesis: particular assignments require general, society-wide rules insulated from case-specific judgments of fitness or utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS3","text":"General utility necessitates determinate property, while imagination principally selects the relations through which titles are assigned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS4","text":"Argument type: genealogical reconstruction joined to psychological and jurisprudential diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS5","text":"Instability of possession → convention of stability → initial assignment by present possession → restriction of that founding rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS6","text":"Established society differentiates acquisition into occupation, prescription, accession, and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS7","text":"Gradual relations and conflicting criteria defeat complete natural determination, requiring municipal law to close residual disputes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A1","text":"Stable possession is necessary to human society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A2","text":"The general rule of stable possession cannot by itself assign particular goods to particular persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A3","text":"Particular fitness or utility is too contestable and partial to serve as a peaceful rule of property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A4","text":"Property assignments must be governed by general and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A5","text":"Property is constant possession secured by social rules, not a real quality inhering in objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A6","text":"The imagination tends to complete an existing relation between objects by adding further resembling relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A7","text":"General utility requires determinate settlement, while imagination principally fixes the specific forms of property rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A8","text":"At society’s first formation, property is assigned to the present possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A9","text":"The rule favoring present possession is valid only for initial social settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A10","text":"After society is established, occupation attaches property to first possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A11","text":"Possession consists in a causal power to use, alter, move, or destroy an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A12","text":"Possessory power varies by degrees of probability and therefore lacks universally precise boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A13","text":"Prescription transfers title toward long and uninterrupted present possession as temporal distance weakens the first possessor’s relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A14","text":"Accession extends ownership from a principal object to an intimately connected and inferior object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A15","text":"Municipal law must determine cases in which natural criteria of relation and comparative importance remain balanced or contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A16","text":"Succession directs property toward near relations through presumed consent and public interest, reinforced by associative relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX1","text":"Property is not to be distributed through case-by-case judgments of personal fitness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX2","text":"Justice does not vary title according to the owner’s character, need, or effective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX3","text":"Property is not an intrinsic or perceptible feature of the owned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX4","text":"Present possession does not remain a perpetual source of title after society has been established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX5","text":"Occupation is not grounded in a literal incorporation of labor into an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX6","text":"Possession is not reducible to immediate physical contact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX7","text":"Reason and public interest do not supply exact boundaries for every possessory dispute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX8","text":"Mere connection does not produce accession symmetrically; the principal draws the inferior, not the inferior the principal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX9","text":"Natural associative principles do not constitute a complete code for deciding compound and borderline cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D1","text":"Justice must disregard whether property is useful to its owner because individualized adjustment would restore the contention that stability was instituted to prevent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D2","text":"Present possession becomes the founding title because custom strengthens attachment and because present possession resembles the constant possession constituted as property.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D3","text":"Permanent adherence to present possession would authorize seizure and exclude restitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D4","text":"Occupation supplies the default title to previously unassigned goods because rejecting first possession leaves no non-arbitrary basis for preferring a later possession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SIRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D5","text":"The extent of possession varies with imaginative boundedness, salience, and proportionality between possessor and object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_SIRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D6","text":"Time produces prescription by changing the influence of relations upon sentiment, not by producing a new real feature in the object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D7","text":"Accession can confer title before actual possession because ownership travels through an established chain of relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D8","text":"The apparent ease of transition from a small object to a great one does not make the great object an accession; the compound whole is imaginatively dominated by its most considerable part.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D9","text":"When inseparable properties are combined, the whole tends toward the proprietor of the most considerable part; when components remain distinguishable, distinct or proportional claims persist.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A14","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D10","text":"Succession can follow the line from which goods originated even when neither presumed consent nor public interest specifies that division.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD1","text":"Property is generated by a dual mechanism: utility creates the demand for settled rules, while imagination supplies their concrete relational form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","text":"The property regime is temporally layered: present possession governs founding, occupation governs first acquisition, prescription revises obscured origins, accession extends existing title, and succession resolves death-created vacancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","text":"Legal determinacy is practical closure rather than the discovery of an independently precise natural boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","text":"A title can change without any corresponding real alteration in its object because property depends upon socially stabilized sentiment and relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD5","text":"Juridical attribution follows salience, continuity, proximity, scale, and associative ease rather than distributive optimality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","text":"Municipal rules may be mutually inconsistent at the level of principle while remaining systemically necessary as devices for terminating controversy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","text":"The system requires pre-social property rights independent of convention to be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2","text":"The system requires public utility alone to be incapable of deriving every particular title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX3","text":"The system requires historical facts not to retain unchanged psychological force across temporal distance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX4","text":"The system requires durability, value, extent, visibility, and independence not to form a universally commensurable hierarchy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX5","text":"The usefulness of a natural-seeming property rule does not entail its universal or permanent validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX6","text":"Social peace and continuously revised allocation by individual suitability cannot operate as coequal governing principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX7","text":"“Natural” property rules cannot mean intrinsic rights antecedent to society; they depend upon natural psychological propensities operating within convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","text":"No final adjudicative measure exists beneath convention, imagination, and positive legal stipulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX2","text":"No property bond remains as an objective feature once social recognition, law, and sentiment are removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","text":"Stable justice forecloses continuous correction of ownership according to need, merit, or optimal use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4","text":"Complete recovery of original title is unavailable as a permanent foundation of present ownership.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","text":"The system contains no natural procedure capable of eliminating every borderline dispute without authoritative closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS1","text":"Changeable possession → insecurity and conflict → convention of stable possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","text":"General stability → unresolved allocation problem → demand for modifying rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS3","text":"Case-specific fitness → controversy and partiality → rejection in favor of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS4","text":"Antecedent person-object relation + associative completion + social security → property relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS5","text":"Initial social settlement → present possession + custom + resemblance → founding title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS6","text":"Established society → rejection of perpetual present-possession title → differentiated acquisition rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS7","text":"Unassigned object → first possessory power → occupation → title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS8","text":"Obscured original title + prolonged current possession → weakened first relation + strengthened present relation → prescription.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS9","text":"Owned principal + intimate inferior connection → imaginative union → accession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS10","text":"Death-created vacancy + kinship association + presumed consent and public interest → succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS11","text":"Graded relation or conflicting salience criteria → natural indeterminacy → municipal stipulation → terminated dispute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AF1","text":"Necessary stability → allocation gap → rejection of particular utility → general-rule requirement → imaginative attachment to antecedent relations → founding title by present possession → restriction of that title → occupation, prescription, accession, and succession → municipal closure of residual indeterminacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME1","text":"Property is reclassified from an inherent object-quality into a socially secured moral relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME2","text":"General utility and particular title-generation are separated: utility explains why settlement is needed, while imagination explains how goods become connected to persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME3","text":"The natural/artificial opposition is reorganized: artificial conventions acquire their form through natural habits of association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME4","text":"Spatial proximity, causal power, temporal duration, comparative scale, and kinship become juridically productive relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME5","text":"Occupation, prescription, accession, and succession become variants of one mechanism: the extension or redistribution of property through antecedent relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME6","text":"Municipal law is reclassified from discovery of natural right to authoritative completion of naturally underdetermined judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT1","text":"Surface: the argument determines the recognized sources by which particular goods become assigned to particular persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT2","text":"Structural: conventions transform unstable possession into a rule-governed property regime whose residual ambiguities are closed by municipal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: jurisprudential explanation shifts from personal fitness and abstract reason toward the interaction of public utility, imagination, and authoritative settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: property has no object-inherent reality, and title changes as time alters the force of relations upon sentiment rather than the object itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SIRW:A1 through CH_THN_SIRW:A16 are sufficient to reconstruct the allocation problem, the dual explanatory mechanism, and the four established titles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","text":"The relative causal weight of public utility and imagination remains explicitly underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3","text":"The thresholds of possession, temporal prescription, inferiority, and comparative importance remain naturally indeterminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent once founding rules are distinguished from rules governing an established society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT5","text":"The strongest mechanism is the division between utility’s demand for determinate settlement and imagination’s production of particular relational titles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on analogy and comparative psychological plausibility when identifying imagination as the predominant cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT7","text":"Its principal tension is that inflexible legal rules are generated from fluid, context-sensitive, and sometimes contradictory relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from general stability through differentiated titles to the necessity of municipal closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","context":"Chapter 23","original_title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","original_context":"Chapter 23"},"original_title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","order":24,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVSF"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVSF@R001","sha256":"0d3b432f3c3368878e6bc3631480e3c09917e0003a839422f108dab1858a0ad6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS1","text":"Problem: whether justice and its juridical relations precede convention or are constituted by it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS2","text":"Thesis: justice is an artificial virtue generated by human convention rather than an original natural principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive genealogy joined to conceptual, modal, and motivational reductio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS4","text":"Presumed antecedence of property → analysis of property → dependence of property upon justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS5","text":"Gradable natural morality → non-gradable juridical relations → exclusion of justice from natural virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS6","text":"Variable natural motives → socially destructive particular judgment → institution of universal and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS7","text":"Coordinated interest → conventional observance → social utility → subsequent moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A1","text":"Human well-being requires society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A2","text":"Social peace and security require strict observance of stability of possession, consensual transfer, and performance of promises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A3","text":"Self-love is the originating passion of the laws of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A4","text":"The self-love of different persons is naturally conflictual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A5","text":"Convention adjusts conflicting interests into a common system of conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A6","text":"Property consists in the influence of object-person relations upon minds and actions as duties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A7","text":"Duties of justice constitute property; property does not antecedently constitute justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A8","text":"Rules determining property, right, and obligation display contrivance through multiplicity, legal mutability, and orientation toward civil utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A9","text":"Natural moral qualities pass into one another by imperceptible degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A10","text":"Property, right, and obligation are integral rather than gradable within their stipulated scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A11","text":"Property, right, and obligation depend upon justice and injustice for their variations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A12","text":"Natural moral conduct inherits the particularity and variability of its motivating passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A13","text":"The laws of justice are universal and inflexible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A14","text":"Unregulated particular judgment, under avidity and partiality, produces social disorder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A15","text":"Convention institutes invariant rules to prevent that disorder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A16","text":"The first foundation of justice is recognized interest in the possibility and preservation of society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A17","text":"The second foundation of justice is the moral approval that arises after the social utility of its rules is established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX1","text":"Justice is not a natural virtue grounded in a peculiar original principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX2","text":"“Giving every one his due” cannot define justice without presupposing the property and right that justice constitutes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX3","text":"Property is not independent of moral sentiment and convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX4","text":"Property is not a sensible quality of an object, a relation among external objects, or a merely corporeal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX5","text":"Bare possession, occupation, or transfer does not naturally carry an original sentiment of moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX6","text":"Regard for public good is not the originating motive of the rules of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX7","text":"Imperfect property or dominion is not a rationally grounded intermediate juridical state, but a civil fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX8","text":"Justice is not the flexible application of immediate private or public motives to particular cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D1","text":"Social indispensability does not entail natural origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D2","text":"Property changes without sensible or corporeal alteration because its constitutive change occurs in duties governing conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D3","text":"The vulgar definition of justice reverses the order of dependence and becomes circular.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D4","text":"The adjustment of opposed self-interests can produce public advantage without public advantage being intended.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D5","text":"Even deliberate orientation toward public good would establish artifice rather than naturality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D6","text":"If justice admits degrees, its dependent juridical relations must admit degrees, contradicting their integral form.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D7","text":"If justice does not admit degrees, it lacks the gradual form attributed to natural virtue and vice.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D8","text":"Gradated justificatory pressures underdetermine binary juridical verdicts, forcing adjudication to impose decisive closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D9","text":"Without convention, circumstance-sensitive natural motives could authorize partial conduct because no antecedent property relation would be violated.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D10","text":"Universal justice is a remedial constraint upon ordinary motivation rather than its immediate expression.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D11","text":"Moral obligation is posterior to interest-based conventional stabilization.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD1","text":"Artificiality and natural genesis are compatible: convention is a second-order strategy by which passion secures satisfaction against the destructive effects of its first-order operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD2","text":"Property has no independent object-ontology; it is a normative effect of institutionally stabilized expectations and duties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD3","text":"Juridical impartiality is produced by suppressing the case-sensitive responsiveness characteristic of natural motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD4","text":"Legal determinacy exceeds the determinacy of its gradual utilitarian and imaginative grounds, creating recurrent pressure for arbitrary boundary decisions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD5","text":"Justice has a dual genealogy: interested coordination produces the rule-system, while contemplation of its social effects produces its moral sanction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD6","text":"Necessity attaches to the social function of justice, not to an innate moral source.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX1","text":"What is necessary for human life need not be naturally implanted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2","text":"What is artificial need not be arbitrary, dispensable, or opposed to human passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3","text":"The moral status of justice cannot itself supply the pre-moral motive required to establish just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4","text":"No normative fact of mine and thine survives the subtraction of conventionally organized duty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX5","text":"No immediate natural motive possesses the universality and invariability required by juridical order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX6","text":"Collective benefit does not require collective benevolence or a consciously shared public end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a pre-social normative world in which objects intrinsically belong to persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses spontaneous social stability through unrestricted case-sensitive equity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX3","text":"Nature supplies no impartial legislative motive capable of directly generating the formal structure of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4","text":"No stable middle term reconciles gradated grounds of entitlement with the binary verdicts required by juridical decision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1","text":"Social necessity + opposed self-love → coordination problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS2","text":"Particular motives + avidity + partiality → variable judgment → social confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS3","text":"Recognition of common interest → voluntary convention → universal and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4","text":"Inflexible rules → duties of abstention, restoration, transfer, and performance → property, right, and obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS5","text":"Coordinated private interests → unintended public utility → peace and social security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS6","text":"Perceived social utility → pleasure in observance and uneasiness at violation → moral sanction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS7","text":"Constitutive-priority test + gradation test + motive-rule mismatch test → justice classified as artificial virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF1","text":"Social necessity of the three laws → question of their origin → artificial-virtue thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF2","text":"Vulgar definition → presumed antecedent property → relational analysis → priority of justice over property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF3","text":"Gradable natural morality → integral juridical relations → dichotomous reductio → exclusion of natural justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF4","text":"Particular natural motivation → partial judgment → social disorder → conventional inflexibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF5","text":"Interest-based convention → stable social utility → natural moral approval → dual foundation of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME1","text":"Necessity and naturality are separated: an institution may be indispensable without being innate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME2","text":"Property is reclassified from a quality of objects into an effect of norm-governed relations among persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME3","text":"Justice is reclassified from an original sentiment into a conventional rule-system that later receives moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME4","text":"Self-interest is reclassified from a mere source of disorder into the generative material of social order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME5","text":"Public good is reclassified from an originating intention into an emergent effect of coordinated private interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME6","text":"Adjudication is reclassified as the forced production of binary closure over grounds that often appear continuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME7","text":"Moral motivation divides into a pre-moral motive of interest and a post-institutional sentiment of approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT1","text":"Surface: the three laws of possession, transfer, and promise are necessary but artificial rules of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT2","text":"Structural: convention redirects opposed self-interests into invariant conduct capable of sustaining society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the naturality claim is defeated through inversion of conceptual priority, mismatch of gradation, and mismatch of motivational form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: property exists as a norm-mediated relation, while interest-based institution temporally precedes moral approbation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVSF:A1–CH_THN_SVSF:A17 are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s three proofs and final two-foundation model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2","text":"The exact historical mechanism by which voluntary convention first stabilizes is underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent if natural passion is distinguished from the second-order arrangements generated by passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT4","text":"The strongest support lies in the constitutive priority of justice over property and the mismatch between variable motives and invariant rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT5","text":"The gradation argument depends on the broad and insufficiently demonstrated premise that all natural moral qualities vary continuously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT6","text":"The inference from the multiplicity of juridical rules to their non-natural origin is compressed rather than independently established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT7","text":"The insistence on integral rights remains in tension with the acknowledged graduality of utility, imagination, and ordinary judgments of half-right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT8","text":"The claim that justice is both an offspring of passion and contrary to ordinary passionate conduct is resolved only by an implicit distinction between first-order impulse and second-order strategy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the operative movement: necessity → conceptual inversion → modal reductio → motivational diagnosis → convention → moralization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","context":"Chapter 24","original_title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","original_context":"Chapter 24"},"original_title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","order":25,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIMA"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIMA@R001","sha256":"3cc5d7088dd720bd33ba5ac15637037d713ee7fd9e3967e731e4cf6cad23269b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS1","text":"Problem: determine when allegiance ceases and resistance becomes morally permissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS2","text":"Contract theory reaches the correct anti-tyrannical conclusion through the false premise of an original promise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS3","text":"Promise reduces genealogically to convention and interest and therefore cannot be the ultimate source of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS4","text":"Public interest in security and protection is the original motive and immediate sanction of government and obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS5","text":"Intolerable oppression destroys that interest and thereby terminates the natural obligation of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS6","text":"Moral obligation may outlive its natural cause through general-rule inertia, but recurrent abuses of power establish a general exception permitting resistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS7","text":"Reconstructive-genealogical movement: valid conclusion → false contractual ground → interest-ground → moral persistence objection → generalized resistance exception → common-judgment confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A1","text":"A promise is a human convention invented for the sake of interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A2","text":"Government is a human invention ordered to the interest of society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A3","text":"Political security and protection are unattainable under perfect freedom and independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A4","text":"Public interest is the immediate natural sanction of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A5","text":"The natural obligation of allegiance lasts no longer than the public interest that causes it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A6","text":"The moral obligation of allegiance is founded on the natural obligation of interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A7","text":"Human beings extend general moral rules beyond the reasons that originally established them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A8","text":"An exception to a general moral rule ordinarily requires its own general-rule form, grounded in numerous and common cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A9","text":"The frailties and passions of human nature persist in rulers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A10","text":"Political office gives rulers a more immediate interest in preserving order and executing justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A11","text":"Rulers can be driven by passion to neglect that immediate interest and pursue cruelty or ambition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A12","text":"Human nature, history, and present experience make severe abuses of ruling power recurrent and foreseeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A13","text":"Settled moral practice approves resistance to flagrant tyranny and rejects passive obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A14","text":"The general opinion of mankind is authoritative and, in morals, infallible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A15","text":"Imitation and custom cannot originate submission because both presuppose prior instances of submission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A16","text":"Public interest is the only original motive capable of overcoming natural ambition and producing political submission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX1","text":"The justified exception to allegiance does not depend on an original contract.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX2","text":"Promise is not a natural, preconventional, or self-grounding source of political obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX3","text":"Government need not be derived through reflective institution followed by a promise of obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX4","text":"The cessation of a natural cause does not by itself guarantee the immediate cessation of moral obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX5","text":"A general moral rule is not exceptionless merely because it has become habitual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX6","text":"Political authority does not transform rulers into beings of superior nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX7","text":"A ruler’s situational interest in justice does not guarantee just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX8","text":"Imitation and custom are not original motives of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX9","text":"Passive obedience is not a coherent moral absolute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","text":"Resistance is not authorized by every partial, private, or isolated disadvantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D1","text":"Promise cannot be the ultimate foundation of allegiance because its own authority derives from convention and interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D2","text":"When intolerable oppression removes political security, it removes the natural obligation to obey.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D3","text":"Moral allegiance may persist temporarily after its originating interest has ceased because general rules exceed their original causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D4","text":"Recurrent rulerly abuse gives the anti-tyrannical exception the generality required to correct the rule of obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D5","text":"Once loss of public interest is grave and generalizable, conscience no longer binds submission to violent tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D6","text":"Resistance to flagrant tyranny is neither criminal nor unjust within the prevailing moral judgment of mankind.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A13","CH_THN_SIMA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D7","text":"Custom and imitation can transmit allegiance but cannot explain its first production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A15","CH_THN_SIMA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D8","text":"The operative measure of resistance is conjunctive: public interest must fail in a great degree, and the failure must belong to a considerable class of cases.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","text":"Allegiance is conditionally binding without being contractually founded: its condition is the continued public function of government.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","text":"The same general-rule mechanism that stabilizes ordinary obedience also generates the exception to obedience when abuses become recurrent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","text":"A ruler who systematically destroys security is reclassified from magistrate into tyrant and public enemy because authority is functionally sanctioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","text":"Legitimate resistance is internal to the rule-governed structure of allegiance rather than an arbitrary suspension of political morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD5","text":"Common moral practice can embody the causal logic of political obligation implicitly without articulating its philosophical genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX1","text":"Political obligation cannot possess an independent normative foundation severed from public security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2","text":"Elevation to office cannot eliminate the passions that make institutional failure foreseeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","text":"General-rule inertia cannot be absolute, or moral allegiance would survive the destruction of its own foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX4","text":"A singular private grievance cannot constitute the public-interest failure required for resistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX5","text":"Correct moral judgment cannot require explicit theoretical knowledge of its generating principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX6","text":"Custom cannot be self-originating or explain the first act that later becomes customary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX1","text":"No transcendent, natural, or unconditional residue of allegiance remains once public interest and its moral generalization cease.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX2","text":"No ruler possesses an indefeasible title to obedience independently of the protective function of government.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","text":"No exact decision procedure measures intolerable tyranny; the threshold remains confined to qualitative severity and recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX4","text":"A political order from which justified resistance could be permanently excluded is unavailable because rulerly frailty is inseparable from human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS1","text":"Correct anti-tyrannical conclusion → separation from contractual premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS2","text":"Promise → human convention → interest → collapse of promise as ultimate foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS3","text":"Need for security → invention of government → public-interest sanction → natural allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","text":"Intolerable tyranny → destruction of protection → cessation of interest → cessation of natural allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS5","text":"General-rule propensity → persistence of moral allegiance beyond its immediate cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","text":"Invariant human frailty + rulerly passions + repeated historical abuse → generalizable exception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS7","text":"Generalized exception + common moral judgment → morally permissible resistance to violent supreme power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS8","text":"Regress of imitation and custom → exclusion of rival origin motives → public-interest foundation secured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","text":"Correct resistance conclusion → rejection of original contract → reduction of promise to interest → direct grounding of allegiance in public security → natural obligation canceled by severe tyranny → moral-rule persistence objection → recurrent abuse generalized into an exception → resistance morally authorized → custom and imitation excluded as origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME1","text":"Political conditionality is relocated from contractual nonperformance to the functional cessation of public protection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","text":"Allegiance is reclassified from an absolute duty to a defeasible effect of public interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","text":"Tyranny becomes a reversal of political function capable of converting the governor into a public enemy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME4","text":"General rules acquire a double role: they preserve obedience beyond immediate calculation and authorize resistance through generalized exceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME5","text":"History, common practice, and moral sentiment become evidence for the structure and limits of political obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME6","text":"Custom and imitation are demoted from generative causes to mechanisms that direct or reproduce an allegiance generated by interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT1","text":"Surface: the argument identifies the threshold at which subjects may resist supreme power without crime or injustice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT2","text":"Structural: government converts common insecurity into coordinated submission but remains vulnerable to rulers whose passions override institutional incentives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the argument preserves a general rule of allegiance while making resistance an internal, generalizable exception rather than a contractual escape.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: invariant human frailty persists across political positions, while moral rules outlive their causes until repeated experience produces a corrective counter-rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the replacement of contractual allegiance by interest-grounded allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where the same public interest explains both the institution of government and the limit of obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","text":"The natural-obligation/moral-obligation distinction preserves the actual movement from direct causal cessation to the general-rule objection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","text":"The precise threshold between tolerable misgovernment and resistance-authorizing tyranny remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT5","text":"The exclusivity of public interest as original motive is compressed into a regress against imitation and custom rather than established against every possible rival.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT6","text":"The appeal to universal moral approval depends heavily on the asserted infallibility of general opinion in morals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT7","text":"The central tension lies between moral rules exceeding their causes and moral obligation ultimately ceasing with natural obligation; the general exception mediates but does not quantify this transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement provided qualitative severity, recurrent abuse, and common moral judgment are accepted as sufficient to generalize the exception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","context":"Chapter 25","original_title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","original_context":"Chapter 25"},"original_title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV","layer":"CH","title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","order":26,"parents":["RAW_THN_PIOV"],"revision":"CH_THN_PIOV@R001","sha256":"836759be6feff278934bd248b27bd22686d6d52d6efe070c62faca909509b1f9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how natural qualities become virtues or vices without reducing morality to either rational judgment or political contrivance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS2","text":"Virtue and vice arise from pleasure and uneasiness produced by surveying durable character from a common evaluative standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS3","text":"Sympathy communicates the pleasures and pains attached to a quality’s effects on its possessor or on others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS4","text":"The argument is a sentimental genealogy supported by causal comparison, explanatory parsimony, objection-resolution, and application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS5","text":"Artificial virtue supplies the controlled case from which the sympathetic basis of approval is extended to natural virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS6","text":"Variability of sympathy and accidental failure of useful dispositions are neutralized by the general point of view and evaluation of natural tendency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS7","text":"Pride functions as the decisive test: its moral valence changes with its utility, intrinsic agreeableness, comparative effect, degree, and mode of expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A1","text":"Moral distinctions are constituted by peculiar sentiments of pleasure and uneasiness elicited by contemplating mental qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A2","text":"A pleasing quality produces virtue, pride, or love; an uneasy quality produces vice, humility, or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A3","text":"Actions enter morality only as evidence of durable principles of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A4","text":"Sympathy converts a sufficiently lively idea of another’s inferred affection into a corresponding sentiment in the spectator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A5","text":"An object’s natural fitness for an agreeable end can please the imagination even when external circumstances prevent the end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A6","text":"Moral pleasure has four sources: usefulness to others, usefulness to the possessor, immediate agreeableness to others, and immediate agreeableness to the possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A7","text":"The usefulness of a quality becomes agreeable to a disinterested spectator through sympathy with its beneficiaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A8","text":"Qualities agreeable only to their possessor or associates become agreeable to other spectators through sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A9","text":"Reflection on tendencies determines the principal lines of duty, while immediate taste independently produces lesser moral approvals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A10","text":"General moral judgment adopts the interests of the person evaluated and of those connected with that person as the common standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A11","text":"General evaluation corrects variations caused by distance, situation, personal interest, present disposition, and fortune, even when occurrent passions remain unequal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A12","text":"A disposition’s natural tendency, rather than its accidental actual efficacy, determines its standing as virtue or vice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A13","text":"Imaginative sympathy can determine moral taste without becoming strong enough to motivate sacrifice or restrain private passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A14","text":"Natural and artificial virtues differ in the scale of beneficial operation: natural virtues benefit through particular acts, whereas justice benefits through a general scheme.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A15","text":"A cause demonstrated sufficient for an effect must be extended to analogous effects unless another cause is necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A16","text":"Well-grounded self-esteem derives merit from both its usefulness and its immediate agreeableness to its possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A17","text":"Sympathy and self-comparison respond oppositely to another’s condition: sufficiently vivid and credible representation is shared, while intermediate representation produces a contrary self-feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A18","text":"Moral love and hatred generate benevolence and anger, thereby producing judgments of good and ill desert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX1","text":"Moral good and evil are not discovered by reason or comparison of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX2","text":"An isolated action without a constant originating principle is not a proper object of moral praise or blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX3","text":"Another person’s passion is not perceived immediately; it is inferred from its causes or effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX4","text":"Utility-based beauty and moral approval are not absolute qualities detached from relations to agreeable ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","text":"Moral distinctions are not inventions imposed wholly by politicians, education, honour, or shame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX6","text":"Public utility does not exhaust the sources of moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX7","text":"The spectator’s private advantage cannot serve as the universal standard of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX8","text":"Variation in the intensity of sympathy does not entail variation in general moral esteem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX9","text":"Actual success is not necessary for a disposition to retain virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX10","text":"Extensive moral sympathy does not entail unlimited generosity or practical self-sacrifice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX11","text":"Pride is not intrinsically vicious, and socially required modesty is not identical with sincere inward self-abasement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D1","text":"Moral inquiry must infer enduring character from conduct rather than assign final value to isolated events.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D2","text":"A spectator can approve qualities useful or agreeable to strangers without possessing any private interest in them.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0020","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D3","text":"The sympathetic explanation established for artificial justice extends to socially useful natural virtues, whose particular benefits facilitate rather than obstruct sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D4","text":"Equal esteem for near and remote characters signifies corrected counterfactual judgment, not equal intensity of present affection.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D5","text":"Accidental impotence or bad fortune can weaken affective vividness without reducing the virtue attributed to a disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A5","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D6","text":"Limited generosity is compatible with extensive moral concern because taste requires only imaginative participation, whereas action requires stronger passion.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D7","text":"Moral evaluation is irreducibly fourfold, although considerations of useful tendency possess priority in major duties.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D8","text":"The constancy and universality required for moral discourse exclude each spectator’s shifting private interest from the speculative standard.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D9","text":"Due self-esteem is virtuous because it enables action and supplies immediate satisfaction to its possessor.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D10","text":"Unsupported self-exaltation becomes vicious when sympathy gives its claim enough vivacity for comparison but not enough credibility for shared admiration.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D11","text":"Haughtiness toward remote or historical others remains blameworthy through a double rebound: the spectator sympathizes with associates whose uneasiness already arose from sympathetic comparison with the proud person.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0040","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D12","text":"Good-breeding suppresses direct self-applause while permitting concealed, well-grounded self-esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D13","text":"Heroic pride can remain admirable despite social harm when immediate sympathy with elevation overpowers reflected pain from its public tendency.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D14","text":"Moral approval and blame terminate in desires to benefit the virtuous and punish the vicious.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","text":"Moral universality is produced by intersubjective stabilization of sentiment rather than by escape from sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD2","text":"The authority conventionally attributed to reason is the authority of calm, generalized passion over judgment, not the necessary control of immediate passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","text":"Moral character is a counterfactual-dispositional object: evaluation concerns what a quality is fitted to produce under adequate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD4","text":"Natural and artificial virtues share one mechanism of approval while differing in whether utility belongs to the act or to the coordinated system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD5","text":"The four-source system integrates self-regarding and other-regarding excellence by making sympathetic mobility, rather than direct benefit, the condition of spectator approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","text":"Good-breeding operates as an artificial coordination system for natural pride, preventing reciprocal comparison from making social intercourse intolerable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","text":"Heroic admiration exposes moral judgment as a conflict among affective sources rather than as the output of a single utility measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD8","text":"Moral desert is not an independent rational relation but the practical extension of pleasure into love and benevolence, or uneasiness into hatred and anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX1","text":"Radical affective incommensurability among human beings must be false, because sympathy presupposes similarity of passions and operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX2","text":"Ideas of another’s condition cannot remain permanently affectless representations, because moral transmission requires their conversion into impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX3","text":"First-person interest cannot possess legitimate universal authority, because its variability would dissolve shared moral language.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX4","text":"Actual outcomes cannot exhaust the value of causes, because fortune may obstruct a disposition without altering its internal fitness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX5","text":"Moral approval cannot be required to produce corresponding action, because the system distinguishes imaginative taste from motivational force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6","text":"General judgment cannot be required to coincide with immediate passion, because correction may regulate judgment or language while leaving affection resistant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","text":"An affect-free foundation of moral universality is unavailable; universality remains dependent on shared constitution and corrected sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX2","text":"Perfect impartiality at the level of lived passion is unavailable; only judgment and evaluative language can be regularly stabilized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX3","text":"A single rule for commensurating utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regarding merit, and other-regarding merit is absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX4","text":"Unmediated access to character is unavailable; moral evaluation depends on signs, inferred dispositions, general rules, and counterfactual tendencies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX5","text":"Complete unity between inward virtue and outward presentation is foreclosed by the requirement that legitimate pride conceal itself under conventional modesty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS1","text":"Conduct or expression → inference to durable character → admission into moral evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS2","text":"Character → useful or immediately agreeable relation → actual or imagined pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS3","text":"Useful to others / useful to possessor / agreeable to others / agreeable to possessor → four-source moral classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","text":"Causes or effects of another’s condition → lively idea → sympathy → corresponding spectator sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS5","text":"Pleasure in survey → virtue → pride or love; uneasiness in survey → vice → humility or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS6","text":"Situational sentiment → common standpoint → correction for distance, interest, disposition, and fortune → stable esteem and moral language.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS7","text":"Natural virtue → particular beneficial act → determinate beneficiary → vivid sympathy; artificial virtue → coordinated scheme → general social benefit → diffuse sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS8","text":"Imaginative sympathy below motivational threshold → moral taste without sacrifice; sympathy touching the heart → practical control of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS9","text":"Another’s self-assessment → sympathetic vivacity → credible superiority produces shared elevation and respect / unsupported superiority produces comparison, humiliation, and dislike.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS10","text":"Moral love or hatred → benevolence or anger → attribution of good or ill desert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","text":"Natural-virtue problem → sentimental criterion → character filter → sympathy mechanism → artificial-virtue experiment → extension to natural virtues → variability objection → general-point-of-view correction → inactive-virtue objection → dispositional correction → four-source taxonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AF2","text":"Four-source hypothesis → pride test → sympathy/comparison conflict → condemnation of overweening pride → rehabilitation of due self-esteem → conventional concealment → heroic-source conflict → confirmation with unresolved weighting tension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME1","text":"The primary moral object shifts from isolated action to durable character and disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME2","text":"Natural and artificial virtues cease to require different sources of approval while retaining different structures of beneficial operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","text":"Impartiality is reclassified from rational detachment to correction of sentiment through a common standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME4","text":"Virtue expands beyond public utility to include usefulness and immediate agreeableness to the possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME5","text":"Moral esteem separates from both affective intensity and practical motivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","text":"Moral value becomes counterfactual: natural tendency remains evaluable when fortune suppresses actual effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME7","text":"Pride is reclassified from a unitary vice into a variable quality governed by ground, degree, utility, comparative effect, and expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME8","text":"Good-breeding becomes visible as a coordination technology managing conflicts among self-evaluations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME9","text":"Heroism becomes a conflict-site where immediate elevation can defeat reflected concern for society without ceasing to be morally ambivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies natural virtues and vices, then determines the moral limits of pride, humility, modesty, and greatness of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sympathy, imagination, comparison, general rules, and standpoint correction generate and stabilize moral evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Artificial virtue functions as an experimental baseline, objections isolate corrective mechanisms, and pride serves as a stress test of the four-source system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The person is treated as a durable dispositional character whose tendencies extend across conduct and remain distinct from momentary action, situational feeling, and accidental fortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the sentimental criterion, sympathetic mechanism, common standpoint, four-source taxonomy, and application to pride.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if occurrent sentiment, corrected judgment, moral language, and motivating passion remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT3","text":"The strongest inferential segment is the movement from artificial virtue as a sufficient-cause case to analogous socially useful natural virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","text":"The weighting of the four moral sources remains underdetermined; predominance of useful tendency is asserted without a general aggregation rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT5","text":"The scope of the relevant connected persons and the procedure for resolving conflicts among their interests remain unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","text":"Heroic admiration exposes an unresolved fault line between social tendency and immediate sympathetic elevation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT7","text":"The inference from actions to durable character and the threshold separating comparison from sympathy depend on compressed probabilistic judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement from causal genealogy through objection-correction to fourfold classification and pride-based verification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT9","text":"The system supplies an explanatory architecture of moral judgment but not a complete decision procedure for conflicting virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 26","title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","context":"Chapter 26","original_title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","original_context":"Chapter 26"},"original_title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","order":27,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIGB"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIGB@R001","sha256":"0c06954bc73d358265c3d5d2fb23c83b0fcbfa26c68e1dd509f015c1e2e0764b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the sources and standard of merit attributed to goodness and benevolence under conditions of limited human generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS2","text":"Thesis: benevolent character receives merit through beneficial tendency and the immediate agreeableness communicated by sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive moral psychology joined to a diagnostic correction of situated judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS4","text":"Limited generosity → appraisal within the agent’s relational sphere → conflict among private standpoints → socially generalized standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS5","text":"Variable sympathy → reflective correction of distance → stable calm judgment without equal emotional concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS6","text":"Relational usefulness → benevolence as director of capacities; contagious tenderness → merit independent of utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS7","text":"Qualified anger → condemnation of cruelty and incommodiousness → role-complete relational desirability as the final test of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A1","text":"Ordinary human generosity is limited to friends, family, and at most country.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A2","text":"Character must be judged within the ordinary relational sphere in which its passions operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A3","text":"A passion is approved or blamed according to its tendency to advantage or harm persons connected with its possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A4","text":"Sympathy communicates the pleasures, pains, and sentiments of affected persons to the spectator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A5","text":"Judgment from private interest produces contradictions among differently situated spectators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A6","text":"Social intercourse generates a general and stable standard for judging character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A7","text":"Reflection corrects calm judgment for distance-based variations in sympathetic force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A8","text":"The general standard can regulate discourse without governing actual love and hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A9","text":"Loving and tender passions are immediately agreeable through sympathy independently of their utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A10","text":"Sympathetically reproduced love readily transfers affection from the displayed sentiment to its bearer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A11","text":"Courage, ambition, judgment, and capacity acquire social valence from the passions directing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A12","text":"Angry passions are constitutionally inherent and can be fitting in limited degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A13","text":"Cruelty and inhumanity are vicious through the misery and harm they impose on others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A14","text":"Complete virtue requires desirability in every social relation together with adequate regard for oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX1","text":"Goodness does not require impossible or universally extended generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","text":"Moral judgment is not validly fixed to the spectator’s private interest or position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX3","text":"Raw sympathetic intensity is not the final measure of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX4","text":"Benevolence does not derive all its merit from social utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX5","text":"Courage, ambition, judgment, and capacity are not intrinsically beneficial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX6","text":"Angry passions are not categorically vicious merely because they are disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX7","text":"The naturalness of anger does not excuse its escalation into cruelty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX8","text":"Adequacy toward others alone does not constitute complete perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D1","text":"The object of appraisal remains local to the agent, while the standpoint of appraisal is generalized across spectators.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D2","text":"Distance-based variations in sympathy are treated as corrigible appearances rather than differences in character.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D3","text":"Benefits remote from the spectator can ground stable praise despite producing weaker immediate concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D4","text":"Benevolent direction converts otherwise indifferent capacities into socially useful powers, while hostile direction converts them into instruments of harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D5","text":"Sympathy with displayed love generates love toward the benevolent agent through a transfer of the passion’s object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D6","text":"A tender excess can remain amiable even when its public utility is negligible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D7","text":"The mere presence of anger cannot determine vice; degree, necessity, and consequence must intervene.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D8","text":"Sympathy with sufferers converts pity into intensified hatred of the cruel agent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D9","text":"Any disposition that makes ordinary relations persistently harmful or intolerable constitutes a defect of character.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD1","text":"The system universalizes the standpoint of appraisal without universalizing either benevolent motivation or beneficiary scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD2","text":"Moral stability is a reflective-social achievement imposed upon variable sympathy, not constancy of spontaneous feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD3","text":"Benevolence possesses two irreducible routes to merit: beneficial tendency and affective self-communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD4","text":"Character becomes a relational risk-and-benefit architecture in which capacities receive value from direction and dispositions from their effects across roles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD5","text":"Shared moral discourse can persist without convergence between calm verdicts and personal affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD6","text":"Moral perfection combines interpersonal harmlessness and usefulness with preservation of the agent’s own good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX1","text":"Partial sympathy is not epistemically final or incapable of correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX2","text":"A shared moral standard does not require equal emotional concern for all persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3","text":"Utility cannot become morally salient to a disinterested spectator without sympathetic access to those affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX4","text":"Virtue cannot be identified with maximal public impact, since minute tenderness can possess exceptional merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX5","text":"Powerful capacities do not carry self-validating moral worth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX6","text":"Natural occurrence is not equivalent to moral innocence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX7","text":"Immediate agreeableness and beneficial consequence are not reducible to a single source of approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX1","text":"Universal benevolence is unavailable as an ordinary human motive; generalized judgment substitutes for universal affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX2","text":"A view from nowhere is unavailable; moral generality is constructed through correction among situated spectators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX3","text":"Complete coincidence between moral verdict and actual love or hatred is absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX4","text":"No explicit common metric reconciles utility, immediate tenderness, fitting anger, and self-regard when they conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX5","text":"Character conceived apart from relations is excluded from final evaluation; moral identity appears only through effects on others and self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS1","text":"Experience of human affairs → recognition of limited generosity → restriction of appraisal to feasible relational spheres.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS2","text":"Private interest → contradictory verdicts → conversational pressure → general standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS3","text":"Distance-attenuated sympathy → reflective correction → constant calm judgment → possible divergence from actual affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS4","text":"Utility channel: passion → advantage or harm to connected persons → sympathetic uptake → approval or blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS5","text":"Immediate channel: displayed tenderness → contagious love → agreeable movement → transfer to bearer → affection and merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS6","text":"Regulatory channel: capacity → benevolent direction or hostile direction → usefulness or magnified harm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS7","text":"Anger boundary: constitutional passion → limited degree → excusability or praise; escalation → cruelty → suffering → intensified hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS8","text":"Closure: test every social relation → no relation refused → test self-relation → complete character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AF1","text":"Limited generosity → sphere-bounded appraisal → contradictions among situated judgments → corrected common standpoint → utility-based merit → immediately sympathetic merit → qualified anger → relational test of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME1","text":"Goodness is relocated from universal benevolent reach to beneficial operation within an agent’s feasible relational sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME2","text":"Distance is reclassified from a moral difference into a perspectival distortion correctable by reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME3","text":"Moral constancy is separated from emotional impartiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME4","text":"Benevolence is reclassified as the directing principle that determines the social valence of power and ability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME5","text":"Merit is expanded beyond useful consequences to include the immediate sympathetic pleasure of tender affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME6","text":"Anger is reclassified by degree and relational consequence rather than by a simple agreeable–disagreeable opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME7","text":"Character perfection is transformed into role-complete relational desirability supplemented by adequate self-relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT1","text":"Surface: explanation of the merit of benevolent qualities and the vice of cruelty and inhumanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT2","text":"Structural: social intercourse converts conflicting situated sympathies into a stable standard of character judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: evaluation proceeds through a generalized relational standpoint and two distinct channels of merit rather than through universal motivation or utility alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: human beings possess limited affections, contagious sympathy, and inherent anger, while repeated changes of position and reflection stabilize judgment over time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SIGB:A1–CH_THN_SIGB:A14 are sufficient to reconstruct the dual-source account of benevolent merit and its relational closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","text":"The precise mechanism by which conversation produces an inalterable standard remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3","text":"No weighting rule determines precedence between beneficial tendency and immediate agreeableness when they conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as sympathy mediates both consequence-based and immediately affective approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5","text":"The strongest generative linkage runs from positional contradiction through reflective correction to a common relational standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT6","text":"The optical-distance analogy establishes the need for correction but does not prove the unique content of the corrected moral judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT7","text":"Similarity-based approval reintroduces spectator partiality into the mechanism intended to correct private standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT8","text":"The merit assigned to excessive tenderness can conflict with the requirement of adequate self-relation unless an unstated threshold limits excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9","text":"The qualified praise of anger prevents immediate agreeableness from functioning as a universal criterion and leaves the system evaluatively plural.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from limited affection through generalized judgment and dual merit to relational perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 27","title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","context":"Chapter 27","original_title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","original_context":"Chapter 27"},"original_title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_THN_INTR","CH_THN_SIOO","CH_THN_SIDS"],"revision":"CL_THN_01@R001","sha256":"4cab4837a91c5ae44fcf685f1482d0d7493e68889f490a353872123e71debd89","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-01","text":"Scientific insecurity is traced beneath disciplinary disputes to inadequate knowledge of the human faculties through which all sciences are constituted and judged.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS1","CH_THN_INTR:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-02","text":"Human nature is therefore installed as a metascientific object, but its investigation is denied access to mental essence and restricted to experienced operations and effects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-03","text":"This restriction is operationalized by decomposing mental life into impressions and ideas, then into simple and complex perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D4","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-04","text":"Simple ideational content is genealogically subordinated to prior impressions, while complex imagination remains free to recombine impression-derived elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-05","text":"The linear copy model mutates when impressions divide into sensation and reflection: ideas remain effects of sensation but become causes of passions and further perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-06","text":"Priority consequently becomes relational rather than absolute, distinguishing original, correspondent, causal, and expository order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-07","text":"The inquiry begins from ideas not because ideas are ultimately first, but because they are the operative antecedents of the reflective phenomena selected for moral investigation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-08","text":"The cluster moves by recursive tightening: foundational crisis → empirical restriction → perceptual genealogy → recursive mediation → methodological reversal → bounded closure at unknown sensory origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS7","CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] A secure science of human nature is the strategic center of scientific reconstruction because human faculties mediate every scientific judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Mental inquiry must proceed from experienced operations, effects, and relations rather than from speculative access to mental essence.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Empirical investigation of mind requires both a taxonomy of perceptions and a genealogy specifying their dependency and order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D1","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Simple ideas generally derive on first appearance from corresponding prior simple impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Restriction at the level of simple content permits recombination and recursive productivity at the level of complex ideas and later perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Temporal priority establishes causal direction only within a specified dependency relation; it cannot assign one absolute order to impressions and ideas as total classes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Ideas are derivative copies relative to sensation but productive mediators relative to impressions of reflection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Mental life forms an asymmetrically grounded recursive sequence: sensation → idea → reflection → idea → possible further perception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Proper methodological order follows the operative antecedents of the selected object, not necessarily the chronology of absolute origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A12","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The ultimate causes of sensation and the original qualities of human nature remain unavailable; legitimate explanation terminates at maximally general experienced relations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Scientific warrant at this level belongs to stable empirical generalization rather than exceptionless universality.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The human subject functions as both the medium of inquiry and a relay within the perception-chain being investigated.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-01","text":"Stable identity: experience remains the admissible ground of inquiry from the foundational program through the perceptual genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_SIOO:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-02","text":"Functional recurrence: observation of effects becomes classification of perceptions, comparison of correspondences, and reconstruction of temporal dependencies.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-03","text":"Mutation: general impression-priority is restricted from a class-wide hierarchy to indexed relations between particular perceptions and genealogical strata.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-04","text":"Reversal: impressions-first genetic order yields an ideas-first exposition once reflective impressions become the focal object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-05","text":"Mutation: the idea changes from faint copy and terminal effect into an intermediate cause capable of producing desire, aversion, hope, and fear.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A3","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-06","text":"Escalation: the simple copy relation expands into a recursively extensible production machine without losing its asymmetrical sensory grounding.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-07","text":"Displacement: the rejection of speculative metaphysics becomes an operational rejection of innate simple content and self-originating reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:NX7","CH_THN_SIOO:D9","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-08","text":"Terminal transformation: ignorance changes from a sign of disciplinary failure into the legitimate endpoint of genealogy at unknown sensory causes.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-09","text":"Partial disappearance: ordinary conduct is declared the proper moral archive, but the subsequent machinery relies chiefly on introspective taxonomy, deprivation, and conceptual genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-10","text":"Contained anomaly: the missing shade and repeated qualifiers convert strict universal precedence into an exception-tolerant empirical rule without being allowed to reorganize the system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The initial diagnosis of philosophical discredit and rhetorical controversy motivates the reconstruction but does not itself govern the later perceptual machinery.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A2","CH_THN_INTR:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Force and liveliness provide the initial impression–idea distinction, but exceptional states prevent them from functioning as the complete current-level criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A2","CH_THN_SIOO:A3","CH_THN_SIOO:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The missing-shade case remains a concentrated anomaly rather than an alternative generative principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] The prohibition on essence-first explanation becomes the common boundary governing both the science of human nature and the origin of perception.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The simple–complex distinction links empirical restriction to imaginative productivity: primitive content is constrained while complex formation remains open.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A5","CH_THN_SIOO:A6","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] Division of impressions into sensation and reflection converts the linear copy model into an asymmetrical recursive system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] Undifferentiated impressions-first method is recoded as a default valid only before object, scope, and internal dependency are specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-08","text":"[Scales upward] Disciplinary exclusion of sensory causes becomes a general methodological principle: proximate operations may be investigated securely without resolving absolute origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-01","text":"Mental science is not an inquiry from substance or essence to deduced operations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:NX7","CH_THN_INTR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-02","text":"Simple ideational content is not generally generated by an autonomous productive intellect.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:DNX1","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-03","text":"Complex invention does not establish the production of primitive content independent of prior impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D6","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-04","text":"Impressions and ideas do not stand in a causally symmetrical or uniformly ordered relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-05","text":"Reflective impressions are not predominantly original, immediate occurrences independent of antecedent ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-06","text":"Chronological firstness does not by itself determine the proper order of inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-07","text":"Scientific security does not require access to ultimate causes, essences, or an exceptionless first principle.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-08","text":"Controlled self-intervention is not the exclusive route to empirical warrant in the science of human nature.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which cognition can secure itself from a standpoint outside human faculties and perceptual mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX1","CH_THN_INTR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-02","text":"It excludes a foundationalism for which only knowledge of first causes can warrant knowledge of derivative regularities.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX6","CH_THN_INTR:SNX4","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies the mind as a static receptacle: derivative perceptions must be capable of returning as causes within reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-04","text":"It rejects a single linear chronology as sufficient to organize origin, causation, correspondence, recurrence, and exposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-05","text":"It cannot admit that reflective phenomena are irreducibly singular, because their scientific treatment requires recurrent relations between ideas, circumstances, and passions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-06","text":"It implicitly rejects strict falsification by isolated anomaly, preserving general empirical rules through rarity and qualification.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-01","text":"The system circles an unavailable absolute origin: sensory impressions ground the genealogy, but the cause of their first appearance remains outside the inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-02","text":"No unmediated foundation is available: science depends on human faculties, while those faculties are accessible only through their mediated effects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-03","text":"Purely immediate passion is excluded as the governing model; reflection is structurally dependent on representational return.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-04","text":"Exceptionless closure remains unavailable because the copy principle, impression-priority, and recursive sequence survive only through controlled qualifications.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-05","text":"The absent possibility organizing the cluster is a nonrecursive foundation capable of grounding cognition without already employing cognition, comparison, and perceptual succession.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT8","CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-01","text":"The impression–idea genealogy operationalizes the introductory science of human nature by replacing speculative anthropology with observable dependencies among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:D4","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-02","text":"Foundational security is reconstructed as a bounded genealogy: reliable order among perceptions can be known even when mental essence and sensory origins cannot.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD2","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-03","text":"Because ideas are both impression-derived and reflectively productive, human reasoning is neither self-originating nor causally inert.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-04","text":"The science intended to provide a foundation begins from an intermediate layer—ideas—because absolute origins are both inaccessible and unnecessary for the selected inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-05","text":"Recursive mental productivity does not abolish empiricism: later perceptions may generate one another while the primitive content of the sequence remains asymmetrically grounded in sensation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-06","text":"The naturalistic protocol for moral science and the later reliance on introspective perceptual taxonomy remain methodologically misaligned unless introspection is treated as observation of naturally occurring operations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD3","CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-07","text":"Empirical certainty must mean robust qualified regularity rather than universality, because the cluster retains both the missing-shade anomaly and rare exceptions to impression-priority.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-08","text":"The proposed causal genealogy incurs a methodological debt: it uses conjunction and succession to establish dependence before the promised analysis of causation secures those criteria.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-01","text":"Foundationalism is converted from discovery of an ultimate ground into disciplined reconstruction of dependency relations within an acknowledged horizon of ignorance.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD2","CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-02","text":"The science of human nature is necessarily reflexive: faculties investigate the perceptual operations through which those same faculties know and judge.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-03","text":"The operative subject becomes a relay rather than an origin: it receives impressions, retains copies, returns ideas, and produces reflective effects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-04","text":"Mental temporality is layered: irreversible sensory precedence grounds a downstream field in which derivative perceptions recursively become antecedents.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-05","text":"Ideational creativity is constrained but real: it cannot generally originate simple qualities, yet it can recombine contents and generate new reflective states.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-06","text":"Methodological relevance supersedes absolute origin: an inquiry may begin from the nearest productive condition of its object while leaving the remote ground unexplained.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A12","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-01","text":"Foundational recursion: insecure sciences require a science of human nature, but that science must employ the cognition and comparison it is intended to secure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-02","text":"Perceptual production loop: sensory impression → retained idea → reflective impression → reflective idea → possible further impression or idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-03","text":"Restriction–proliferation loop: simple content is empirically restricted, complex ideas recombine it, and returned ideas produce additional reflective states.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-04","text":"Boundary loop: inquiry reaches unknown origin, withdraws to observable effects, compresses them into general principles, and treats the acknowledged limit as closure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D5","CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-05","text":"Anomaly-containment loop: universal formulation → counterinstance or qualifier → restriction of scope → retention of the governing maxim.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-06","text":"Ideas become the principal conceptual attractor because they connect sensation to reflection, empirical derivation to productivity, and genetic order to expository method.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-07","text":"Sensation functions as the recessed ground-attractor: every recursive sequence depends on it, yet its causes are deferred beyond the operative system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:A8","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B01","text":"Disciplinary insecurity + rhetorical controversy → demand for a common foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B02","text":"Mediation of science by human faculties → human nature installed as the common metascientific object.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B03","text":"Unavailable mental essence → effects, circumstances, and perceptions become the legitimate evidential field.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS4","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B04","text":"Perceptual classification → impression/idea distinction + simple/complex distinction → isolable units of genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B05","text":"Simple correspondence + conjunction + first-appearance priority → general impression-to-idea derivation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS4","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B06","text":"Division of impressions → sensation grounds ideas → ideas generate reflection → recursive downstream production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS2","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B07","text":"Selection of reflective phenomena + exclusion of sensory origins → ideas-first exposition within an asymmetrically sensation-grounded system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS5","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B08","text":"Qualified regularity + terminal ignorance → bounded empirical foundation without essential or absolute closure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS5","CH_THN_INTR:FGS6","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B01","text":"Foundational disorder → human nature identified as the common dependency beneath the sciences.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME1","CH_THN_INTR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B02","text":"Human nature as common dependency → experience and observation replace speculation about essence.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B03","text":"Observational restriction → mental life decomposed into impressions, ideas, simple contents, and complex formations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D1","CH_THN_SIOO:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B04","text":"Perceptual decomposition → simple correspondence and temporal succession establish impression-to-idea genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D3","CH_THN_SIOO:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B05","text":"Linear genealogy → division of impressions → ideas become mediators between sensation and reflection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B06","text":"Ideational mediation → recursive production → absolute priority replaced by indexed priority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B07","text":"Indexed priority + disciplinary scope → ideas-first method → bounded closure at unknown sensory origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-01","text":"Scientific disorder is redrawn as a problem of the human medium through which evidence, judgment, and assent operate.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME1","CH_THN_INTR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-02","text":"Foundation is reclassified from access to essence into a qualified causal genealogy of experienced perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME4","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-03","text":"Mind is mapped as a field of ordered perceptions rather than approached through an antecedently known mental substance.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between receptivity and productivity collapses: ideas are received copies at one stratum and productive causes at another.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-05","text":"The opposition between linear and recursive causality is replaced by asymmetrical recursion: one-way grounding supports downstream feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-06","text":"Priority is redistributed among genetic, correspondent, causal, disciplinary, and expository orders.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-07","text":"Certainty and ignorance cease to be opposites: reliable relations may be established precisely by refusing claims about ultimate origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME7","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: insecure sciences, disputed metaphysics, impressions, ideas, sensations, reflections, passions, and the correct order of inquiry constitute the explicit objects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT1","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT1","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: perception is organized by classification, copying, conjunction, temporal precedence, return, and recursive mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:ALT2","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: inquiry is redirected from disciplinary disputes and innate-content vocabulary toward a bounded empirical genealogy of the operative antecedents of reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT3","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT3","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: reality enters mental science as successive effects whose priority is relational, whose sensory grounding is asymmetrical, and whose ultimate origin is unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT4","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT4","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the argument performs its empiricism by beginning with a general distinction, submitting it to counterinstances and subdivisions, and revising method without abandoning the governing genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS3","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-01","text":"The ordinary-life observational protocol is not fully integrated with the later introspective classification of perceptions and hypothetical deprivation tests.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-02","text":"Constant conjunction and temporal succession perform causal work before the system has supplied its full account of causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-03","text":"The missing shade contradicts the unrestricted copy principle and is contained by rarity rather than incorporated into a revised theory of simple-content production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-04","text":"Sensation is indispensable as the ultimate empirical ground but is simultaneously unexplained and excluded from the present inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-05","text":"The science of human nature remains foundationally recursive because it uses perception, cognition, and comparison to establish the authority of perception, cognition, and comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_INTR:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-06","text":"The mechanism by which an idea returns and generates a reflective impression is structurally indispensable but causally unexplained.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-01","text":"Impression mutates from a uniform class opposed to ideas into a divided category containing both original sensations and idea-dependent reflections.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-02","text":"Idea mutates from derivative effect into causal mediator without becoming the ultimate source of its primitive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-03","text":"Priority mutates from class-wide precedence into an indexed relation varying by correspondence, stratum, and selected object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-04","text":"Methodological order mutates from reproduction of genetic order into selection of the proximate antecedents needed to explain the focal phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as an asymmetrically grounded cascade whose lower sensory entry is one-way but whose upper perceptual strata form recursive feedback circuits.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not operationally an originating substance but a switching relay that receives, copies, returns, recombines, and transmits perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is stratified: first appearance grounds irreversible derivation, while later ideas and reflections repeatedly exchange the positions of cause and effect.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in tracing effects, decomposing complexes, indexing priorities, following operative antecedents, and stopping where experience no longer supplies relations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_SIOO:A6","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from scientific crisis through empirical perceptual genealogy to recursive mediation and ideas-first method.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT1","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B02","text":"The criteria for simplicity, exact correspondence, force, adequate observation, causal dependence, and the productive return of ideas remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT2","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT2","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent if ultimate origin, genetic order, correspondent order, recursive production, and expository order remain strictly distinguished.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT3","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT3","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest joint is the conversion of an empirically grounded linear copy relation into recursive reflective production without granting autonomous origin to ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on sequencing and recursive return: the methodological reversal is intelligible only after sensory precedence, copying, and reflection have been separately established.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B06","text":"The missing shade, rare exceptions, and unexplained return of ideas resist complete axiomatic reduction and prevent exceptionless closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the transition from foundational ambition to bounded genealogy, and from linear derivation to asymmetrical recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS7","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject"},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_THN_SIIM","CH_THN_SICA","CH_THN_SIOQ"],"revision":"CL_THN_02@R001","sha256":"45e4013562a51cdd21b30227b6161661d519e35f7a537ee3768cfbc0d98aebb4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is to explain how impression-derived ideas can preserve experience, depart from it, form regular complexes, and generate spatial and temporal order without acquiring independent content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS1","CH_THN_SICA:AS1","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-02","text":"The governing thesis is that ideational differences arise primarily from force, arrangement, recurrence, and relational operation rather than from heterogeneous elemental origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SICA:SD1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes an empirical genealogy of mental organization: impressions supply materials, while faculties and relations determine how those materials recur, combine, and appear.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","CH_THN_SICA:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-04","text":"Memory conserves force and experienced order; imagination releases copied elements from that order; association then supplies noncompulsory pathways through the liberated field.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-05","text":"Spatiality and temporality complete the architecture by showing that order itself is not an additional impression but the disposition or succession of content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-06","text":"Mental freedom is therefore bounded twice: it cannot originate unprecedented simple contents, and its ordinary transitions remain weighted by natural associative tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: copied order is preserved, dissolved, probabilistically regenerated, and finally exposed as constitutive of the ideas of space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-08","text":"Closure occurs through paired limits: conception terminates in content-filled minima, while explanation terminates at experimentally established principles whose ultimate causes remain unknown.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every legitimate idea must derive its elemental content from antecedent impression-material.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Elemental provenance and relational organization are distinct explanatory levels: identical copied materials can support preservation, recombination, association, or ordered appearance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Order is the common object of the cluster: memory conserves it, imagination alters it, association regenerates it, and space and time consist in its coexistent or successive forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Difference licenses distinguishability and separability, but separation remains possible only where a positive component can be conceived apart.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A8","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A9","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Memory is recurrence under retained vivacity and fidelity to the experienced form, order, and position of its objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Imagination is dependent at the level of simple contents but free to separate, transpose, and recombine those contents at the level of arrangement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Associative tendency mediates between compulsory connexion and random succession, producing regularity without abolishing imaginative liberty.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:D1","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Resemblance, spatiotemporal contiguity, and cause and effect are the primary operators by which one idea introduces another.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Relational efficacy is graded: it propagates through intermediaries, weakens with distance, and expands causally from production to capacity and social influence.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Space and time are ordered manners of appearance rather than independent impressions: extension is coexistent disposition, and duration is noncoexistent succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Abstraction produces general representation by selectively regarding a determinate particular, not by generating a contentless universal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] An idea’s genealogy limits its legitimate application: recombination may alter conjunctions, but predication cannot properly exceed the kind of content or order supplied by the originating impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Empirical explanation may stop at a recurrent principle established through its effects even when the ultimate cause of that principle remains inaccessible.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Conceptual decomposition cannot terminate in nonentity: spatial minima must retain sensible quality, and temporal minima must be filled by successive existence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-01","text":"The copy principle retains stable identity across the cluster, functioning first as a faculty constraint and later as a test of spatial, temporal, and predicative legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-02","text":"Order mutates from a criterion of mnemonic fidelity into an object of imaginative alteration, an associative product, and finally the constitutive form of extension and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-03","text":"Separability expands from the ground of imaginative liberty to a universal compositional premise, then reverses into a limiting test when no distinct spatial or temporal content can be isolated.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-04","text":"Imagination changes function from a faculty of transposition into a regulated field whose transitions are weighted by resemblance, contiguity, and causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-05","text":"Contiguity migrates from the experienced order preserved by memory into a customary associative pathway grounded in spatial and temporal arrangements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-06","text":"Complex ideas divide into three generative regimes: freely rearranged fictions, associatively stabilized objects of thought, and compounds whose order constitutes extension or duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SICA:A16","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-07","text":"Causation migrates from an associative relation between ideas into a model of production, counterfactual capacity, authority, and social subordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-08","text":"Vivacity remains indispensable for establishing the memory–imagination distinction but disappears from the later explanations of associative order and spatiotemporal composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SICA:AS4","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-09","text":"Connexion changes valence: memory approximates inseparable conservation, association supplies nonnecessary cohesion, and space and time remain nonseparable from some positive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A12","CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-10","text":"Fiction reverses from a successful exercise of combinatorial liberty into a mark of illegitimate application when duration or spatiality is detached from every possible originating content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX7","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The precise degree of vivacity distinguishing memory from imagination remains a faculty-specific criterion and does not govern association or spatiotemporal conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The claim that sight and touch alone provide extension’s source is required for spatial genealogy but does not scale into a general theory of all ideational recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The asserted exhaustiveness of resemblance, contiguity, and causation remains local to the associative taxonomy; the current level requires only nonnecessary relational regulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:SNX3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] The copy principle scales upward because it constrains faculty content, abstract representation, spatial and temporal conception, and semantic application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-05","text":"[Scaled] The distinction between content and arrangement becomes the cluster’s primary architecture, subsuming memory, imagination, association, space, and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Separability connects imaginative composition to conceptual analysis, but its cross-unit use requires distinguishing separation from an original complex from separation from every content-bearing instance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D6","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Imaginative freedom generates the problem solved by association: once ideas can combine arbitrarily, recurrent regularity demands a non-random but noncompulsory principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:AS1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] The binary opposition between necessary determination and pure chance is replaced by graded tendency, custom, and attenuating relational force.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Fiction is divided into lawful relational recombination and genealogically unsupported predication; novelty alone no longer determines illegitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-10","text":"[Transition] The expansion of causal association into social power demonstrates domain migration, but it does not itself govern the spatial and temporal ontology of the cluster.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:D4","CH_THN_SICA:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT7","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-01","text":"No faculty, abstraction, or relational operation can originate an unprecedented simple idea independently of prior impression-material.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-02","text":"Copy-dependence does not entail preservation of original conjunctions, positions, or sequences.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-03","text":"Mental regularity is neither necessary determination nor random succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SICA:NX3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-04","text":"Order, extension, and duration are not additional simple impressions superadded to the contents they organize.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-05","text":"Generality cannot consist in an indeterminate or contentless universal representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX3","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-06","text":"Neither empty spatiality nor bare duration can remain positively conceivable after all sensible or successive existence is removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-07","text":"Memory cannot properly vary experienced order as freely as imagination, while imagination cannot escape impression-derived elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIIM:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-08","text":"Empirical adequacy does not require speculative access to the ultimate mechanism producing associative attraction.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:NX6","CH_THN_SICA:NX7","CH_THN_SICA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any model in which the origin of an idea determines all of its possible arrangements; otherwise imagination and association collapse into mere replay.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-02","text":"It excludes both the passive-copy model of mind and the sovereign-creation model: the mind transforms relations without supplying its own simple materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies any account that treats relations as independently given substances rather than operations, dispositions, successions, or tendencies among contents.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-04","text":"It rejects an ontology of empty frameworks in which space and time exist for thought independently of sensible points or successive existences.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:D12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-05","text":"It excludes semantic autonomy from genealogy: common language, objective occurrence, or habitual predication cannot by themselves legitimate an idea’s application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX5","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-06","text":"It requires that stable mental order be explainable without necessary connexion, because necessity would destroy imaginative freedom while pure chance would destroy recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-07","text":"It rejects the demand that every empirically usable principle possess an independently known metaphysical substrate or ultimate cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable object is pure order without ordered content: empty space, bare time, relation without relata, and memory without a prior arrangement are structurally inadmissible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-02","text":"Sovereign mental spontaneity is absent; all available freedom is post-impressional freedom exercised through separation, recombination, and selective regard.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-03","text":"Necessary mental connexion is the missing force around which association is organized: gentle attraction occupies its functional place without acquiring its compulsion.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SICA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-04","text":"Ultimate explanatory grounding remains absent; “original qualities of human nature” marks the point where operational regularity survives without disclosed origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","CH_THN_SICA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-05","text":"No independent tribunal verifies original order, absolute succession, or pure spatiality apart from the perceptual materials whose fidelity or arrangement is being judged.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX3","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-01","text":"Mental novelty consists primarily in relational transformation: copied elements remain constrained while their conjunction, sequence, emphasis, and representative function vary.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-02","text":"Association supplies default trajectories to a freely recombinable imagination, explaining stable complex thought without converting tendency into necessity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-03","text":"Memory and associated imagination partially overlap because customary contiguity can lead imagination to reproduce the experienced sequences that memory preserves by fidelity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-04","text":"Because space and time are arrangements of content, mnemonic fidelity conserves constitutive coexistence and succession rather than merely locating objects inside independent containers.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D3","CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-05","text":"Customary association by contiguity depends on perceived spatial and temporal order: sensory succession supplies the sequence that custom converts into an imaginative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-06","text":"Separability is conditional rather than absolute: components may be detached from original complexes, but extension cannot be detached from all sensible quality and time cannot be detached from every succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-07","text":"Fiction bifurcates into legitimate composition and illegitimate predication: an unexperienced complex can be formed lawfully, whereas duration without change exceeds its genetic basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-08","text":"Abstract space and time demonstrate how generality can arise without spontaneous simple content: imagination varies instances while retaining a recurrent disposition or succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-09","text":"The expansion of causal association into power and government shows that ideational relations can organize social complexes by converting possible action into associative proximity.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-10","text":"Impression-audit and effect-oriented inquiry combine into a restricted science of mind that maps sources, operations, and consequences without postulating inaccessible substrates.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:SD4","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a relational ontology in which many apparent mental objects are reclassified as organized configurations of more elementary content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SICA:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a differential operator rather than an origin: it retains, weakens, separates, recombines, follows tendencies, and selectively generalizes impression-derived materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-03","text":"Temporality is generated through perceptible succession, conserved through memory, routed through customary association, and rearranged through imagination without becoming an empty medium.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-04","text":"Novelty becomes finite and grammatical: new complexes arise through transformations of relation and order rather than through creation of new simple elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-05","text":"Epistemic legitimacy is jointly genetic and operational: an idea must possess an impression-source and must not be applied beyond the structure that source can represent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-06","text":"Causal explanation acquires political reach when powers of possible production are treated as sufficient to associate authority, duty, government, and obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-07","text":"Methodologically, the system substitutes source-tracing, compositional analysis, and observable effects for unrestricted speculation about hidden essences.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:ME6","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-08","text":"Reality is thinkable only as content-bearing order: relations may vary, weaken, or generalize, but no positive spatial, temporal, or ideational structure survives complete removal of its terms.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-01","text":"Impression → idea → genealogical audit → permissible operation → renewed audit forms the basic recursive loop governing both composition and application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-02","text":"Impression → memory recurrence → imaginative release → associative rerouting → stable complex converts experienced order into a reproducible but nonnecessary pattern.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-03","text":"Difference → distinction → separation → recombination → recurrent complex → demand for association transforms a logical test into a generative mental machine.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-04","text":"Sensory succession → custom → associative expectation → repeated transition recursively reinforces temporal contiguity as an imaginative pathway.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-05","text":"Order is the principal attractor: it returns as original position, changed conjunction, associative sequence, spatial disposition, temporal succession, and abstract invariant.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-06","text":"Causation follows an escalation path from ideational transition to actual production, possible exertion, interpersonal influence, and institutional hierarchy.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-07","text":"Particular configuration → selective regard → general term → heterogeneous applications → renewed particular representation forms the abstraction loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-08","text":"Absence of a distinct impression → non-distinguishability → non-separability → reclassification as manner of appearance is the cluster’s principal eliminative loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B01","text":"Impression-field supplies simple sensible and reflective materials; no subsequent operation adds unprecedented elemental content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B02","text":"Impression-material recurs as ideas differentiated by retained force and fidelity: strong ordered recurrence produces memory, diminished recurrence opens imagination.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B03","text":"Distinguishable components become separable; separable components become recombinable; recombination produces unexperienced complexes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B04","text":"Recurrent regularity among free combinations generates the requirement for a bond weaker than necessity and stronger than chance.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B05","text":"Resemblance, contiguity, and causation route ideational transitions through direct and progressively attenuated relational chains.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B06","text":"Associative routing stabilizes simple ideas into complex objects of thought while preserving the possibility of divergent recombination.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B07","text":"Analysis of order reveals spatial coexistence and temporal succession as arrangements of content rather than independent receptacles.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B08","text":"Selective recurrence of disposition or succession permits abstract representation without generating a contentless universal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B09","text":"Genealogical testing excludes empty space, bare duration, changeless time, and other applications that outrun possible originating impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B10","text":"The machine terminates in content-filled minima and experimentally established original tendencies rather than metaphysically disclosed ultimate causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B01","text":"Impression-given content → copied elemental ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A1","CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B02","text":"Copied recurrence → differentiation by vivacity and fidelity → memory/imagination bifurcation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B03","text":"Imaginative release → separation and recombination → novel but derivative complexes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B04","text":"Free combination → recurrent uniformity → rejection of chance → gentle associative regulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B05","text":"Associative regulation → relational chains → causal expansion → stabilized complex thought and social power.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B06","text":"Analysis of relation and order → coexistence/succession distinction → reconstruction of space and time as content-arrangements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B07","text":"Impression genealogy → separability test → exclusion of empty forms and unsupported predications → content-filled conceptual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-01","text":"Ideas cease to form a homogeneous class of copies and become differentiated by force, fidelity, compositional role, associative position, and representative function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME1","CH_THN_SICA:ME3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-02","text":"Provenance and operation become separable dimensions: common impression-derived materials can be archived, recombined, associated, or abstractly deployed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:ME1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-03","text":"Order becomes a productive object rather than a passive background, spanning mnemonic conservation, imaginative transposition, associative transition, and spatiotemporal constitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME3","CH_THN_SICA:ME3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-04","text":"The opposition between freedom and determination collapses into bounded liberty governed by recurrent but defeasible tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-05","text":"Space and time are removed from the category of independent containers and relocated within the ordered appearance of positive contents.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:ME1","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-06","text":"Fiction is reclassified by operation: recombined complexes remain conceivable, while contentless forms and genealogically unsupported applications do not.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-07","text":"Causation becomes a cross-domain operator connecting natural production, possible action, mental transition, and institutional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:ME4","CH_THN_SICA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-08","text":"Philosophical method is redrawn around impression-audit, operational decomposition, and experimentally observable effects rather than unrestricted metaphysical postulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:ME6","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit objects are memory, imagination, ideational association, extension, duration, abstraction, and the formation of complex ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT1","CH_THN_SICA:ALT1","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] The active mechanisms are copying, recurrence, vivacity loss, order preservation, separation, recombination, associative routing, abstraction, and decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT2","CH_THN_SICA:ALT2","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Conceptual disputes are reframed as questions of source, operation, relational distance, and permissible application rather than contests among untested metaphysical entities.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT3","CH_THN_SICA:ALT3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality available to thought consists of content-bearing configurations, while time emerges from perceptible noncoexistence and is preserved or rearranged through mental recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT4","CH_THN_SICA:ALT4","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system repeatedly performs its own claim by decomposing apparent unities, tracing their sources, removing unsupported additions, and reconstructing them as relational operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","CH_THN_SICA:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-01","text":"The exact-copy principle strains against abstraction, which suppresses determinate sensible peculiarities while requiring a particular sensible representative.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-02","text":"Universal separability of simple ideas conflicts with the insistence that spatiality cannot be separated from all sensible quality and temporality cannot be separated from every succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-03","text":"Memory’s fidelity criterion presupposes access to an original arrangement whose independent recovery the system does not explain.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-04","text":"Vivacity and relational fidelity can classify the same recurrence differently, yet mixed cases are not formally resolved.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX3","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-05","text":"Imagination is described as freely combinatorial and universally guided by association, but the strength and defeasibility of that guidance remain unquantified.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-06","text":"Association is treated as an original quality, while contiguity is explained through acquired custom; the relation between innate principle and acquired pathway remains unresolved.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-07","text":"Causation receives maximal associative force before its nature is analyzed, and its extension into duty and authority compresses normative relations into productive power.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:MRT4","CH_THN_SICA:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-08","text":"The inference from an idea’s perceptual genealogy to the impossibility or illegitimacy of independent existence exceeds what a theory of conception alone securely establishes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT7","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-09","text":"Experienced duration varies with perceptual rate, but the relation between variable experienced time and objective succession remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D6","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-01","text":"Order mutates from a standard against which recurrence is judged into a generative operator and then into the constitutive structure of spatial and temporal ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-02","text":"Separability mutates from a positive authorization of imaginative freedom into a negative criterion for denying independent space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from preserved or altered conjunction into a graded force capable of producing recall, complex thought, and social hierarchy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-04","text":"Fiction mutates from lawful recombination into genealogical excess when an idea is predicated where no corresponding perceptual order can occur.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-05","text":"Causation mutates from one associative transition among ideas into a generalized schema of actual production, latent capacity, and institutional command.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-06","text":"Abstraction mutates copying from literal reproduction into selective representation while retaining dependence on determinate particular content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of content-filled points and events whose coexistences, successions, similarities, and productive relations generate the structures ordinarily mistaken for independent forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-02","text":"The mind is an archive-workshop-network: memory preserves routes, imagination cuts and recombines components, and association weights the available transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-03","text":"The subject is not a sovereign producer but a switching point through which impression-derived materials acquire differential force, order, and relational direction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-04","text":"Time is a pulse of distinguishable succession rather than an empty stream; memory binds its experienced sequence, while imagination and association reroute its components.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-05","text":"The remaining freedom is navigational: the system permits movement among copied contents and weighted relations but no escape into contentless form or absolute spontaneity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:SD1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if the distinction between elemental provenance and relational organization remains primary.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT4","CH_THN_SICA:MRT1","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction is strongest where copy-dependence, separability, nonnecessary association, and content-bearing order jointly explain bounded mental productivity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT5","CH_THN_SICA:MRT6","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined at its thresholds and taxonomic closures: mnemonic vivacity, the exhaustiveness of associative relations, and the limits of separation are asserted rather than fully derived.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT7","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on recursive sequencing: imaginative freedom must precede associative regulation, and relational analysis must precede the rejection of independent space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT9","CH_THN_SICA:MRT8","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B05","text":"The architecture depends on productive omission at two terminal points: the unknown origin of associative attraction and the unverified phenomenology of sensible or successive minima.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B06","text":"No indispensable propositional element resists reduction, but the system’s actual movement is preserved only if order is allowed to mutate from remembered relation into associative machine and spatiotemporal form.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AF1","CH_THN_SICA:AF1","CH_THN_SIOQ:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time"},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":3,"parents":["CH_THN_SVIE","CH_THN_SVNI","CH_THN_SXIB"],"revision":"CL_THN_03@R001","sha256":"d872e9c863076b16087dda93ac53003b3f2c1ac192c14c5c7fb28606b6a45add","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-01","text":"The governing problem is how factual assent and practical reality can exceed bare conception when existence contributes no additional idea and positive conception remains bounded by perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","CH_THN_SVIE:AS6","CH_THN_SVNI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-02","text":"The core thesis is that actuality is not represented by added content; belief differentiates an otherwise invariant object-idea through its forceful, steady, impression-related manner of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:AS3","CH_THN_SVNI:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-03","text":"A present impression and customary relation generate this manner by transferring vivacity to an absent, future, or otherwise merely conceived object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-04","text":"The argument begins as an eliminative genealogy of existential content and becomes a reconstructive naturalistic psychology of assent, motivation, and affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS2","CH_THN_SVNI:AS4","CH_THN_SXIB:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-05","text":"Its movement is cumulative and recursive: ontological de-addition produces a modal theory of belief, which expands into a distributed economy of mental force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS7","CH_THN_SVNI:AS6","CH_THN_SXIB:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-06","text":"Poetry, eloquence, passion, and madness then reverse the evidential direction by showing that apparent vivacity can imitate belief without supplying its settled authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS4","CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-07","text":"Closure is operational rather than metaphysical: the system explains how ideas acquire reality-effects while withholding positive comprehension of mind-independent actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD3","CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-08","text":"The terminal correction appeals to causal provenance, steadiness, reflection, and general rules, but leaves their authority and relation to external reality incompletely grounded.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Positive conceptual content is restricted to impressions, ideas, and their copied or recombined perceptual materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Existence is not an additional quality, predicate, or idea but is identical with the object as conceived.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Bare conception, existential conception, and factual belief can preserve identical representational content while producing radically different cognitive and practical effects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Belief begins as a difference in manner, becomes an intensification of force and vivacity, and terminates as a comparatively steady, impression-related, causally regulated mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A8","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Since contrary matters of fact remain conceivable, factual selection cannot arise from demonstrative necessity and must be determined by custom or association.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A5","CH_THN_SVNI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] A present impression functions both as the experiential anchor of inference and as the reservoir from which vivacity is transmitted to a related idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Selective vivification gives absent and future objects present motivational efficacy without granting equivalent efficacy to every passing fiction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A4","CH_THN_SXIB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity expand from a distinction between impressions and ideas into a transmissible causal currency linking belief, passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Judgment, imagination, passion, and volition form a reciprocally permeable system rather than independent faculties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Factuality converges on stable practical presence: an idea counts as real for the mind by persistently affecting inference, passion, and conduct without acquiring new content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] First-order vividness cannot authenticate itself; settled conviction requires discrimination by source, steadiness, reality-connection, reflection, and general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Reality is retained as a regulative relation organizing assent and correction while its intrinsic extra-perceptual nature remains positively unrepresented.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-01","text":"Stable identity: the non-addition of existence persists unchanged from the analysis of being into the analysis of belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-02","text":"Absorption: the original question concerning the idea of existence disappears as an independent inquiry after becoming the premise that forces belief into a modal rather than representational explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D4","CH_THN_SVNI:AS1","CH_THN_SVNI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-03","text":"Functional mutation: present perception changes from the maximal positively available instance of being into the anchor and force-source of factual inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-04","text":"Escalation: force and vivacity move from taxonomic differences between perceptions to the defining mode of belief and then to a trans-domain mechanism of psychological causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-05","text":"Domain migration: custom moves from selecting one conceivable factual alternative to transmitting vivacity and thereby generating motivation, passion, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-06","text":"Displacement: externality shifts from an intrinsically unfilled relative correlate to the “connection with reality” invoked when reflection regulates assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-07","text":"Reversal: vivacity initially explains why belief exceeds fiction, but counterfeit vivacity later demonstrates that intensity alone cannot establish genuine conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-08","text":"Terminal transformation: the fact–fiction distinction moves from presumed content difference to scalar force difference and finally to causal pedigree, steadiness, and second-order correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-09","text":"Disappearance through absorption: conception, judgment, and reasoning cease to function as irreducible faculties once they have been recoded as modes and transitions within one conception-system.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-01","text":"The intrinsic nature of external objects remains a local ontological boundary; at cluster level, its scalable function is to deny any content-internal marker of actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-02","text":"Poetry and madness remain local cases in subject matter but scale upward as indispensable stress tests of the proposed identity between belief and vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-03","text":"The proposition that existence adds no idea scales directly because it organizes both the boundary of ontology and the reconstruction of factual assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","CH_THN_SVNI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-04","text":"The present-impression requirement bridges conceptual genealogy and causal psychology by converting perceptual presence into a mechanism for generating belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-05","text":"The motivational dilemma catalyzes the major transition from defining belief phenomenologically to demonstrating its systemic necessity for conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:AS3","CH_THN_SXIB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-06","text":"The simple equation of belief with occurrent intensity is superseded by a distinction between accidental vehemence and settled, source-regulated conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","CH_THN_SXIB:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-07","text":"The impression–idea taxonomy survives, but its original binary form is recoded into a graded and dynamically transmissible economy of perceptual force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:D7","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-01","text":"Neither existence nor belief is an additional predicate, quality, or representational component attached to an object-idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:NX1","CH_THN_SVIE:NX2","CH_THN_SVNI:NX2","CH_THN_SVNI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-02","text":"No intrinsic semantic mark distinguishes an actual object from the same object merely conceived.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-03","text":"No positive representational passage gives direct comprehension of a specifically extra-perceptual kind of being.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:NX6","CH_THN_SVIE:NX7","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-04","text":"Matter-of-fact assent is not demonstrative necessity, and disbelief is not failure to understand the rejected proposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX4","CH_THN_SVNI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-05","text":"Judgment, reasoning, passion, and imagination are not causally sealed faculties governed by irreducibly different operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX6","CH_THN_SVNI:NX7","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-06","text":"First-order intensity, imaginative completeness, and rhetorical vehemence do not independently authenticate factual belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX8","CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","CH_THN_SXIB:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-07","text":"Fiction and fact cannot be separated by an impermeable boundary at the level of represented content or raw vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-08","text":"Viable motivation can arise neither from present impressions alone nor from the indiscriminate efficacy of every passing idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:NX3","CH_THN_SXIB:NX4","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes any ontology in which actual existence alters the intrinsic representational constitution of an object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:DNX2","CH_THN_SVIE:DNX3","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-02","text":"It excludes direct representational realism: positive knowledge cannot cross from perceptual content to the intrinsic nature of a heterogeneous external realm.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:DNX4","CH_THN_SVIE:DNX5","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-03","text":"It excludes a rationalist construction of the factual world in which necessary conceptual connection determines existential truth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:DNX3","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-04","text":"It excludes faculty psychology as a final explanation: judgment cannot be an independent act that stamps truth onto completed ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:DNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-05","text":"It excludes a sovereign rational subject insulated from custom, bodily condition, affective feedback, repetition, and rhetoric.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:SD5","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-06","text":"It excludes introspective foundationalism because the immediate felt strength of a perception cannot guarantee its own epistemic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-01","text":"The governing absence is the positively knowable essence of mind-independent actuality: it must remain thinkable as a correlate while unavailable as represented content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX4","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility is an intrinsic sign by which an idea could announce that its object exists independently.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-03","text":"No exact threshold separates vivid imagination, counterfeit belief, probable assent, and settled conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-04","text":"The terminal ungrounded condition is the authority of general rules that distinguish legitimate causal vivification from accidental or pathological force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-01","text":"Since existence adds no idea but belief exceeds bare conception, the surplus of factual assent must be modal and intensive rather than representational.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A1","CH_THN_SVNI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-02","text":"Perceptual closure combined with present-impression anchoring makes factual commitment a relation generated within experience rather than direct cognition of an external object’s intrinsic being.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVNI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-03","text":"If identical content produces different degrees of assent and action, force and steadiness must function as causal variables independent of representational composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-04","text":"The same intensification that constitutes factual assent also gives an absent object practical efficacy; epistemic commitment and motivation therefore share one mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-05","text":"The appeal to “connection with reality” regulates belief by reference to a term whose extra-perceptual nature the system denies it can positively comprehend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-06","text":"Poetry, eloquence, and madness force belief to mutate from raw intensity into intensity qualified by causal provenance, steadiness, and resistance to reflective correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-07","text":"Customary transfer from impression to idea and passionate transfer back to the object-idea form a self-amplifying loop capable of strengthening assent beyond its initial basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-08","text":"Because fictional and factual ideas can share content, a familiar or believed component can lend force to an entire fictional network without converting the network into settled belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-09","text":"The reduction of judgment and reasoning to modes of conception, combined with the permeability of passion and imagination, yields a unified dynamics of mental force rather than a hierarchy of faculties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-10","text":"Custom is accepted as the causal generator of factual assent while education is rejected as a valid ground of assent; the system therefore distinguishes psychological genesis from epistemic warrant without fully formalizing that distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-01","text":"The system separates ontological content from actuality-effects: nothing representational is added, yet intensified conception transforms inference, passion, persistence, and conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","CH_THN_SVNI:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-02","text":"Factual warrant becomes second-order and genealogical: assent must be evaluated by how vivacity was produced and regulated, not merely by how forcefully it is felt.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-03","text":"Temporality becomes a force-transfer structure in which a present perception confers present efficacy upon absent and future objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-04","text":"The subject becomes a relay within circulating intensities rather than an autonomous judge: impressions, habits, passions, bodily states, and rhetoric jointly modulate assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-05","text":"Pathological and aesthetic cases are theoretically privileged because they separate apparent intensity from legitimate belief and thereby expose hidden conditions of the ordinary mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-06","text":"The mechanism enabling action toward absent realities simultaneously exposes judgment to capture by repetition, eloquence, temperament, and affective feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-07","text":"The cluster produces practical realism without positive metaphysical realism: ideas can govern life as realities even though independent actuality supplies no distinct conceptual content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-01","text":"Negative recursion: existence adds no content → belief cannot add content → factual difference relocates to manner → manner requires an intensive mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D4","CH_THN_SVNI:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-02","text":"Generative loop: present impression → customary transition → transferred vivacity → intensified related idea → belief → action and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-03","text":"Affective feedback loop: belief excites passion → passion vivifies its object → the vivified object increases assent → strengthened assent reinforces passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-04","text":"Fictional diffusion loop: familiar anchor → relational spread of vivacity → easier imaginative reception → affective engagement without complete judgmental assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-05","text":"Counterfeit escalation: temperament, eloquence, or bodily ferment raises ideas toward impression-like force, culminating in the possible collapse of operative truth–falsehood discrimination.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-06","text":"Corrective loop: excessive vividness generates error → reflection identifies accidental provenance → general rules discount local force → graded conviction is restored.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:D10","CH_THN_SXIB:D11","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-07","text":"Aporetic return: correction invokes reality-connection, but positive comprehension of external reality remains unavailable, returning epistemic regulation to the boundary established by perceptual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-08","text":"The conceptual attractor is regulated vivacity: sufficient force to make ideas cognitively and practically present, sufficient steadiness to resist fluctuation, and sufficient pedigree to distinguish them from counterfeits.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B01","text":"Perceptual admissibility: every positive idea must derive from perceptual materials; no extra-perceptual existential content is available.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B02","text":"Representational invariance: content of object O = content of O-as-existent = content of believed O; therefore factual difference cannot be compositional.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B03","text":"Modal generation: present impression I + customary relation R(I,O) → transfer of vivacity → steady intensified conception of O → belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B04","text":"Operational expansion: belief in O → increased presence and persistence of O → influence on passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B05","text":"Recursive amplification: passion or related vivid ideas → renewed vivacity in O → strengthened assent and affective efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B06","text":"Counterfeit production: non-customary vivification by rhetoric, temperament, or bodily ferment → belief-like force without secure factual pedigree.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B07","text":"Regulatory closure: source-reflection + general rules + reality-connection → correction of apparent intensity, while external actuality remains an intrinsically unfilled correlate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:AFM-B01","text":"Existential-origin test → elimination of added being-content → perceptual closure → belief-surplus problem → content invariance → relocation to manner → force-and-vivacity definition → customary transfer from present impression → practical and affective expansion → counterfeit vivification → correction by provenance and general rules → unresolved appeal to reality beyond positive representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AF1","CH_THN_SVNI:AF1","CH_THN_SXIB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-01","text":"Being is reclassified from a detachable predicate into the nondiscriminating status of conceived presentation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ME1","CH_THN_SVIE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-02","text":"The actual–possible distinction is relocated from represented content to the force, steadiness, provenance, and practical effects of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:ME2","CH_THN_SVNI:ME3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-03","text":"The internal–external distinction is redrawn as a problem of mediation, relational attribution, and causal anchoring rather than qualitative content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ME3","CH_THN_SVIE:ME5","CH_THN_SVNI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-04","text":"Understanding is reclassified from a hierarchy of faculties into a single conception-system differentiated by necessity, association, intensity, and transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SD3","CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-05","text":"Absent and future objects are reclassified as potentially present causes of conduct when belief supplies sufficient force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-06","text":"Fact, fiction, persuasion, and madness become neighboring configurations within one economy rather than categorically separate mental domains.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-07","text":"Epistemic reading must become genealogical and second-order, tracking the source and regulation of vivacity rather than trusting represented content or felt intensity alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:ME6","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system addresses existence, external objects, belief, factual assent, motivation, passion, fiction, eloquence, and madness.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT1","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT1","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: impression-copy constraints, present-perception anchoring, customary association, force transmission, affective feedback, and corrective rules compose one distributed mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT2","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT2","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: metaphysical and epistemic distinctions are converted into tests of perceptual origin, modal difference, causal provenance, and susceptibility to correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT3","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT3","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: being adds no essence, while absent and future objects acquire present causal efficacy through impression-related modes of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT4","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT4","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the system first compresses distinctions into a scalar mechanism and then uses poetry and madness to force its own refinement from simple intensity to regulated intensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-01","text":"The system oscillates between existence as the universal status of every presented perception and existence as the actuality affirmed only in belief; the same term covers conceptual presence and factual commitment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:SD2","CH_THN_SVNI:A1","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-02","text":"Perceptual closure denies positive access to extra-perceptual being, yet external objects are said to occasion perceptions and “reality-connection” regulates assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A7","CH_THN_SVIE:MRT6","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-03","text":"The scalar reduction of belief is strained by vivid fiction and poetry, which may exhibit greater apparent vehemence while remaining experientially and epistemically weaker.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-04","text":"General rules repair the insufficiency of first-order force but lack a specified origin, authority, and mechanism capable of avoiding circular dependence on already accepted beliefs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-05","text":"Habit is necessary to generate factual assent, while education demonstrates that repeated habit can produce epistemically invalid assent; causal production and warrant remain incompletely separated.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","CH_THN_SXIB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-01","text":"Existence mutates from the explicit object of conceptual genealogy into a zero-addition constraint governing every subsequent account of belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","CH_THN_SVNI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-02","text":"Force and vivacity mutate from marks distinguishing impressions and ideas into transferable quantities linking cognition, affect, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-03","text":"Belief mutates from intensified conception into regulated intensification once counterfeit cases show that occurrent vividness is insufficient.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-04","text":"Truth and reality mutate from presumed properties of represented objects into causal and regulatory terms governing easy reception, assent, and reflective correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a perceptually closed transmission machine in which impressions and ideas circulate, receive, lose, and redistribute force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a relay and regulatory threshold within this machine, not its sovereign origin: habit, affect, bodily ferment, and reflection compete to set the force of ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-03","text":"Time is organized around present anchors that draw absent and future objects into current causal efficacy through associative transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is navigational: distinguish customary and regulated reality-effects from rhetorical, passionate, poetic, or pathological counterfeits without access to an intrinsic sign of actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the principal engine: existential non-addition → modal belief → customary vivification → practical expansion → counterfeit stress → regulatory correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:MRT1","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT1","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B02","text":"What remains underdetermined is the exact dimensional structure of force and vivacity, the mechanism of their transfer, and the threshold separating belief from vivid imagination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT4","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at the functional level but not metaphysically closed: it explains reality-effects more successfully than independent actuality or the warrant of correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:MRT2","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest construction is the transition from representational invariance to a unified mechanism explaining assent, motivation, passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT2","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if poetry, eloquence, and madness remain recursive tests that transform the initial scalar account rather than detachable illustrations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B06","text":"The elements resistant to axiomatic reduction are the felt quality of belief, the positive nature of external actuality, and the authority of the general rules that arbitrate genuine and counterfeit force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5","CH_THN_SVNI:A17","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX4","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_title":"Chapters 07–09","original_context":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief"},"original_title":"Chapters 07–09","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":4,"parents":["CH_THN_SXUP","CH_THN_SXRW","CH_THN_SIAP"],"revision":"CL_THN_04@R001","sha256":"bccfc61a8d5600031f2e8ab700a9cd3b02d1bd57b37d21cc2fa1ed9c9a807366","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how the same natural operations can generate causal belief, prejudice, fictive ontology, and the procedures by which philosophy regulates them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS1","CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","CH_THN_SXRW:AS1","CH_THN_SIAP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B02","text":"Repeated conjunction, relation, and facilitated transition form a common engine whose outputs differ less by origin than by the degree to which comparison and experiment control them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a genealogical-methodological reconstruction: intrinsic substance and power are displaced by collections of qualities, observable relations, customary transitions, and regulated causal projections.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B04","text":"Reflection does not transcend natural belief; it exposes discrepancies within it and forces a bifurcation between fictive metaphysical repair and more extensive general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS5","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B05","text":"Resemblance and ease of transition are epistemically neutral: uncontrolled, they produce prejudice and fictive unity; experimentally opposed by difference, they isolate causal circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B06","text":"Philosophical correction therefore consists in recursive circumstance-analysis, widened experience, and semantic restraint rather than access to necessary connection or an independent rational faculty.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B07","text":"The movement is spiral and mutation-driven: custom generates an inference, discrepancy reopens it, a higher-order rule reorganizes it, and the corrective result becomes subject to renewed testing.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B08","text":"Closure is operational but nonterminal: metaphysical inquiry into hidden substance or power ceases, while empirical causal judgment remains corrigible because neither custom nor circumstance-analysis admits final completion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Repeated conjunction and close relation acquire cognitive efficacy by facilitating transitions among perceptions rather than by revealing intrinsic bonds among objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Neither substance nor causal power is available through mere inspection; actual causal judgment begins only from experienced relational structure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ease of mental transition produces belief when it transfers vivacity, but produces fictive identity and simplicity when its own fluency is attributed to objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Natural customary transition precedes reflection and remains active after reflective correction; philosophy can redirect but cannot abolish it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","CH_THN_SIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Resemblance extends inference beyond identical cases, becoming prejudice when material differences are neglected and an experimental operator when resemblance is controlled by contrast.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Reflection works through comparison, counterinstance, and circumstance-variation; it corrects fluent apprehension by making suppressed differences operationally salient.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SXUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Philosophical normativity is immanent to custom: more extensive and constant regularities regulate narrower associations without invoking an extra-customary tribunal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Actual causation is operationally constituted by contiguous, temporally ordered, uniform conjunction among experimentally discriminated circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Phenomena are compound, so causal identity must migrate from whole objects to qualities, differences, and auxiliary circumstances isolated through comparative tests.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Contradiction and disappointed expectation do not determine their own resolution: they can provoke fictive substrates, prejudicial persistence, or renewed experimental decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Evidential warrant and experienced conviction are separable because conviction varies with vivacity, transition, temporal distance, and presentation, while warrant depends on comparative regularity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Corrective inquiry has no self-applying or final form: every metarule competes with prior habit, and every experiment becomes subject to renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Legitimate explanation converges on two constraints: terms must possess assignable ideational content, and causal distinctions must survive discriminating variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Moderate scepticism suspends claims to hidden substance and power while preserving disciplined empirical inquiry into observable regularities.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Constant conjunction retains its function as the empirical condition of causal inference while remaining explicitly distinct from perceived necessary connection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Facilitated transition recurs as belief-production, inferential compression, imputed identity, and imputed simplicity; its function changes with the object onto which fluency is projected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Custom moves from a first-order producer of expectation and prejudice to the material from which second-order philosophical rules are constructed.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:A10","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Resemblance changes from a source of illicit transfer into a controlled comparative instrument once difference is deliberately introduced.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-05","text":"[Migration] Reflective comparison migrates from exposing variation and composition in bodies to isolating causal circumstances within compound phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] The general rule expands from a correction of prejudicial inference into a recursive methodology for causal projection, decomposition, and experimental enlargement.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Qualities cease to require a substantial bearer and become separable elements whose causal relevance must be experimentally determined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Contradiction ceases to be evidence for an unknown substrate and becomes a demand to identify an omitted difference, scope condition, or auxiliary cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] The local theory of vivacity is absorbed into the current-level distinction between psychological uptake and methodological warrant.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance] Substance, substantial form, accident, faculty, and occult quality disappear after supplying negative cases of how unexplained transition is converted into simulated ontology.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SD2","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Temporal fading, historical transmission, sign generality, and passion-amplification remain local specifications of how belief-force varies.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXUP:A8","CH_THN_SXUP:A13","CH_THN_SXUP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The individual comparative, proportional, and incomplete-cause rules remain local instruments; their recursive subjection to renewed variation scales upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D5","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The specific genealogy of matter, substantial forms, accidents, and occult qualities remains a local historical diagnosis.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:D5","CH_THN_SIAP:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] The denial of intrinsic causal legibility scales upward into a general restriction against both a priori causal ontology and metaphysical explanation by hidden powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX2","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The conflict between first-order and second-order general rules bridges the psychology of prejudice and the methodology of experimental causal judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Smooth mental transition connects belief-formation to fictive object-construction, revealing a shared mechanism beneath epistemic and ontological error.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Reflectively disclosed contradiction catalyzes the transition from passive customary association to either metaphysical repair or active experimental differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] “Same cause” is recoded from identity of whole objects to identity of experimentally isolated operative circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-09","text":"[Superseded] Objective substances, intrinsic faculties, and occult agencies are superseded by relational descriptions that remain answerable to assignable ideas and observable variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-10","text":"[Current-Level Emergent] Genuine philosophy becomes the conjunction of psychological self-diagnosis, comparative experiment, and refusal of semantically empty closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-01","text":"Causal roles cannot be assigned by conceptual inspection or an a priori taxonomy of objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-02","text":"Identity, simplicity, cohesion, and causal agency cannot be grounded in an intelligible invariant substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:NX1","CH_THN_SIAP:NX2","CH_THN_SIAP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-03","text":"No proposition possesses a belief-force invariant under temporal distance, mediation, presentation, and disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX2","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-04","text":"Philosophical correction cannot appeal to a rational faculty independent of custom, imagination, comparison, and experience.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX1","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-05","text":"Ease, repetition, resemblance, and customary inseparability do not by themselves establish object identity, metaphysical dependence, or causal efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:NX6","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-06","text":"Technical vocabulary cannot acquire explanatory force through repetition, professional authority, or verbal resemblance alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A16","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-07","text":"Neither formal rules nor a decisive experiment can eliminate the need for renewed judgment and circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:NX8","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-08","text":"Reflection does not permanently suppress the customary propensity whose outputs it corrects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-09","text":"Human affects, intentions, and inward agency cannot be projected into nature as explanations of external phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A17","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-01","text":"The system requires rationalist transparency to be false: causes, substances, and powers must not be intrinsically legible to conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-02","text":"It also excludes absolute irregularity and total kind-instability; without provisionally stable resemblances, causal uniformity and comparative projection would collapse.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-03","text":"Normative correction must not depend on an external tribunal, yet it must not reduce to whichever custom is psychologically strongest; the system survives only through comparative extension and constancy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-04","text":"Object identity and causal identity cannot be primitively given; both must remain decomposable into qualities, relations, and operative circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-05","text":"Explanatory usefulness cannot legitimate an otherwise inconceivable posit; fictive reconciliation and verbal closure must remain distinguishable from explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-06","text":"A temporally invariant, affectively insulated, and uniformly rational subject cannot be admitted because belief is produced as a variable event of force-transmission and habit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-07","text":"Experimental competence cannot be exhausted by possession of explicit rules; decisive variation must remain dependent on judgment that the rules cannot independently generate.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absent metaphysical interior of causation: repeated conjunction is available, but the power supposedly connecting cause and effect is not.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility of a transcendent criterion gives correction its recursive form: philosophy can judge custom only through a more extensive organization of custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-03","text":"No final reconciliation between natural propensity and reflective discipline is available; their conflict is constitutive rather than temporary.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-04","text":"No terminal causal decomposition is admissible because every isolated circumstance can become part of a newly compounded phenomenon requiring further control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-05","text":"The invariant bearer is absent on both sides of cognition: objects dissolve into related qualities, while subjects dissolve into temporally variable operations of habit, comparison, and force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-06","text":"The system circles but cannot fully formalize the exact boundary between warranted generalization and prejudice; that boundary remains dependent on scope, constancy, and discriminating judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-01","text":"Because prejudice, fictive ontology, and causal judgment arise from related customary mechanisms, causal method must be understood as a disciplined mutation of association rather than the intervention of a foreign faculty.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-02","text":"Resemblance has no stable epistemic valence: left unopposed, it exports vivacity and object-unity; paired with controlled difference, it isolates operative causal circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-03","text":"Contradiction is a methodological fork: imagination may conceal it through a substrate, preserve it through prejudice, or convert it into a test for omitted differences and auxiliary causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-04","text":"Causal uniformity functions as a regulative rule for organizing experience, not as evidence that necessary connection or productive power has become perceptible.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-05","text":"A methodologically warranted inference may remain psychologically faint, while an experimentally defective association may remain vivid; epistemic rank and subjective force therefore require separate assessment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:A15","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-06","text":"Correction is necessarily provisional because reflective metarules never erase first-order habit and experimental controls never escape renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-07","text":"Once bodies are treated as collections of separable qualities, causal inquiry can no longer take whole substances as primitive causes; it must identify operative qualities within relational compounds.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-08","text":"Legitimate theoretical distinctions are constrained simultaneously by semantics and experiment: they must correspond to assignable ideas and support discriminating variations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-09","text":"Moral inquiry is especially unstable because obscure variables interact with vivid signs, passions, and anthropomorphic projection, increasing both causal opacity and susceptibility to prejudicial inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A13","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-10","text":"Moderate scepticism does not terminate causal practice; it terminates the conversion of empirically useful rules into claims about hidden ontological powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-01","text":"Normativity is naturalized without being reduced to immediate habit: warranted judgment is the recursive stabilization of custom through broader comparison, counterinstance, and experimental control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-02","text":"The resulting ontology is thin and relational: separable qualities enter recurring spatiotemporal conjunctions, but neither substantial bearers nor intrinsic productive powers are required.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-03","text":"The epistemic subject becomes a temporally variable relay among impressions, habits, comparisons, passions, and reflective rules rather than a stable faculty of rational assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-04","text":"Anomaly becomes productive only when it is prevented from hardening into fiction: disappointed expectation must trigger decomposition rather than occult supplementation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD2","CH_THN_SIAP:D8","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-05","text":"Time is constitutive at two levels: causal order requires priority and succession, while belief-strength varies with recency, mediation, and the temporal mode of survey.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-06","text":"Evidence becomes two-dimensional: comparative regularity determines warrant, whereas vivacity and transitional facility determine experienced conviction and affective efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-07","text":"Method cannot be fully formalized because its rules generate tasks of discrimination whose decisive execution depends on non-algorithmic judgment and expanded experience.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SXRW:A12","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-08","text":"Natural and moral phenomena occupy one causal field, but moral inquiry demands stronger safeguards because its operative circumstances are obscured by affective and imaginative participation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-09","text":"Philosophical success consists not in recovering objective necessity but in keeping inference revisable, terms meaningful, and metaphysical demands from dictating fictive closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3","CH_THN_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-01","text":"Repetition → custom → facilitated transition → vivified expectation → reinforced readiness for the same transition forms the basal belief loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-02","text":"Related qualities → fluent survey → imputed identity or simplicity → reflective contradiction → substance fiction → further metaphysical categories forms the ontological escalation loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:FGS1","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-03","text":"Resemblance → first-order generalization → counterinstance → more general rule → continued competition with the first propensity forms the prejudice-correction loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-04","text":"Compound phenomenon → controlled variation → provisional circumstance-isolation → new compound description → renewed variation forms the experimental recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-05","text":"Contradiction is the principal bifurcation point: one path proliferates fictive entities, while the other intensifies comparison and experimental differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-06","text":"“General rule” becomes a machine when it ceases merely to state regularity and begins selecting, condemning, and reorganizing lower-order transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-07","text":"“Circumstance” becomes a machine when causal identity shifts from whole objects to variables that can be compared, removed, decomposed, or supplied as co-causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-08","text":"Moderate scepticism functions as an attractor that halts metaphysical proliferation without halting empirical recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-09","text":"Apparent repetition marks phase change when customary recurrence is re-entered as an object of reflection: the same mechanism then becomes belief, error, diagnosis, or correction according to its recursive position.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","text":"Distinct qualities + repeated conjunction + close relation → facilitated transition among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","text":"Facilitated transition + present force or fluent survey → causal expectation / imputed identity / imputed simplicity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS2","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS2","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B03","text":"Temporal distance, contrary instance, analytic comparison, or changed circumstance → interrupted fluency → weakened conviction or disclosed contradiction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS3","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B04","text":"Uncontrolled contradiction + retained demand for unity or agency → substance / occult quality / prejudicial persistence → simulated closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B05","text":"Controlled contradiction + counterinstance + broader comparison → second-order general rule → accidental–efficacious distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B06","text":"Compound phenomenon + controlled resemblance and difference → operative circumstance / common quality / missing co-cause → provisional causal attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B07","text":"Provisional attribution + renewed circumstance-analysis + enlarged experimental sphere → recursively corrected causal map.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B08","text":"Absence of intrinsic power + absence of final control → moderate sceptical restriction + continued empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B09","text":"Variable vivacity and presentation → unequal psychological uptake of equally regulated conclusions → persistent gap between method and assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","CH_THN_SXUP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B01","text":"Repeated conjunction and related qualities → fluent customary transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B02","text":"Fluent transition → causal belief, resemblance-extension, and imputed object-unity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B03","text":"Temporal, comparative, or experimental disruption → diminished conviction, disclosed composition, or failed expectation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","text":"Disruption → bifurcation between fictive metaphysical repair and reflective reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B05","text":"Reflective reclassification → more general rules + controlled resemblance and difference → causal circumstance-isolation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B06","text":"Circumstance-isolation → provisional projection → renewed experimental control → widened evidential field.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D2","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B07","text":"Recursive correction → rejection of hidden power and empty terminology → moderate sceptical, nonterminal closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-01","text":"Reason and error cease to belong to independent faculties; both become differently organized outputs of relation, custom, comparison, and rule-governed correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A15","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-02","text":"Ontology is divided into two registers: fictive intrinsic structure is genealogically dissolved, while observable relational structure remains available for causal practice.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-03","text":"Resemblance becomes a truth-neutral operator whose epistemic status depends on whether difference and counterinstance control its extension.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ME4","CH_THN_SXRW:ME3","CH_THN_SIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-04","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from a demand for an unseen bearer into a diagnostic opportunity for causal decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:ME4","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-05","text":"Causation becomes thinkable without intrinsic power: its legitimate content lies in regular succession, controlled comparison, and projection within tested kinds.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:ME2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-06","text":"Evidence divides into logical-methodological warrant and psychological-affective force, dissolving the assumption that valid arguments must command proportionate conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ME2","CH_THN_SXUP:ME7","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-07","text":"Philosophical vocabulary is judged by ideational content and experimental work rather than by systematic position or technical appearance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-08","text":"Natural and moral phenomena enter a unified causal terrain, differentiated by variable opacity rather than by distinct causal architectures.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","CH_THN_SXRW:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-09","text":"The hidden presuppositions of empirical inquiry become visible: stable kinds, discriminable circumstances, and nonbrute irregularity are required even after necessary connection is denied.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system addresses variations in belief, rules for judging causes, and the ancient categories of substance, form, accident, faculty, and occult quality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT1","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT1","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: relation and repetition facilitate transitions; comparison and variation interrupt them; higher-order rules reorganize the resulting expectations and contradictions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT2","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT2","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: philosophy intervenes by redirecting custom, expanding the experimental field, and refusing fictive or verbal closure when objective connection is unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT3","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT3","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality is composed of separable qualities in temporally ordered conjunctions, while identity, conviction, and causal attribution vary with survey, succession, and experimental scope.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT4","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT4","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the architecture performs its thesis by repeatedly turning its own rules, categories, and conclusions into objects of further comparison and correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-01","text":"Custom supplies prejudice, causal belief, corrective metarules, and the criterion of greater regularity; the distinction between bad and good custom therefore remains immanent and incompletely secured.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD6","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-02","text":"The claim that causation depends wholly on constant conjunction remains strained by the additional requirements of contiguity and temporal priority.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-03","text":"Strict causal uniformity conflicts with contrary experience and graded probability unless every apparent exception is reassigned to an undiscovered difference or missing co-cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-04","text":"A priori causal openness coexists with dependence on stable kinds, clear experiments, and discriminable circumstances whose criteria are not fully specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D1","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-05","text":"Denial of observable necessary connection coexists with strong projection from a clear experiment, leaving the practical authority of uniformity stronger than its ontological basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-06","text":"A genealogy of substance explains why it is believed but does not alone establish its falsity; the refutation additionally depends on the absence of an assignable idea and the separability principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-07","text":"Experimentally isolated qualities risk becoming replacements for occult qualities unless their identity remains tied to reproducible comparison rather than verbal designation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-08","text":"The architecture distinguishes warrant from conviction but supplies no quantitative rule connecting impression-strength, transitional facility, and resulting assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-01","text":"Resemblance mutates from spontaneous evidence into a controlled experimental variable when paired with discriminating difference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-02","text":"The general rule mutates from a habitual extension that produces prejudice into a reflective metarule that regulates lower-order inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A10","CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from a stimulus for ontological repair into an experimental demand for further differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-04","text":"Cause mutates from an apparently whole object into a common quality, differentiating circumstance, compound contribution, or auxiliary condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SXRW:D5","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-05","text":"Sceptical cessation mutates from termination of the search for intrinsic power into the enabling boundary of continued empirical causal inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a field of separable qualities entering recurrent, contiguous, temporally ordered configurations without an observable inner power binding them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a relay-system in which impressions, custom, imagination, comparison, passion, and reflective rules transfer, amplify, interrupt, and reorganize force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is not a neutral container: causal priority, recency, gradual survey, distant comparison, and inferential mediation actively constitute both objects and beliefs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is between fluent closure and recursive differentiation; escape is not departure from custom but the controlled reopening of its products through variation, comparison, and sceptical restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if facilitated transition, reflective disruption, and the bifurcation between fictive repair and experimental correction are preserved.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT1","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT1","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the points where it invokes more general rules, same kinds, clear experiments, adequate repetition, and essential circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as an immanent account of naturalized correction: the mechanisms generating error also supply the materials for disciplined judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT4","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT3","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of relation and custom into a branching machine capable of producing belief, prejudice, fictive ontology, and recursive causal method.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT5","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT4","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing: natural transition must precede disruption, and disruption must precede either metaphysical proliferation or methodological control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture is weakest where anti-scholastic valuation, causal uniformity, and the superiority of sceptical cessation are asserted more strongly than their internal criteria determine.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:MRT8","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT7","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B07","text":"Experimental sagacity and the variable force of conviction remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction because neither decisive variation nor psychological intensity is rendered calculable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B08","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if correction is not represented as abolishing imagination and scepticism is not represented as abolishing empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","original_title":"Chapters 10–12","original_context":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy"},"original_title":"Chapters 10–12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":5,"parents":["CH_THN_SVIS","CH_THN_SVCT","CH_THN_SVLT"],"revision":"CL_THN_05@R001","sha256":"668d93c244eed7fd465c965d35e7aecc82504c14c09fd83ae3171f4db122fa84","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how explanatory inquiry can remain operative after unknown substances, self-grounding reason, and unrestricted affective laws cease to provide legitimate foundations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B02","text":"The system relocates explanation from hidden bearers and intrinsic powers to observable differences, experienced conjunctions, associative transitions, affective forces, and domain-specific thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B03","text":"Its argument becomes an immanent audit: concepts, faculties, and general laws are tested by their experiential origin, internal consequences, reciprocal applicability, and tendency to overgenerate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","CH_THN_SVIS:AS6","CH_THN_SVCT:AS4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B04","text":"The directional movement is universal claim → contradiction or excess → restrictive analysis → preservation of a limited mechanism rather than restoration of the original foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B05","text":"Imagination is the common engine: it completes relations, confers vivacity, sustains belief, mediates passion, and produces error whenever its transitions outrun their conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B06","text":"The self survives the destruction of substantial soul-talk not as an appearing bearer but as an operational object, comparator, and relatively durable pole of affective attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B07","text":"Habit, custom, practice, and nature restore regular operation after metaphysical, epistemic, or causal certainty has been withdrawn, but they do not convert practical stability into truth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B08","text":"Closure is recursive and pragmatic: inquiry continues through constrained natural propensity, repeatedly correcting both speculative excess and skeptical paralysis without reaching final foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate explanation begins from experienced perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and their observable transitions rather than from impression-transcendent entities.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Once unknown supports are excluded, the primary explanatory object becomes the network of relations and transformations among perceptions and affects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Imagination is simultaneously the indispensable medium of cognitive and affective synthesis and the source of fictive completion, contradiction, and excess.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Relation is not a uniform binary bond: it changes across separability, spatial compatibility, causal succession, self-reference, comparison, publicity, and temporal durability.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A7","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Repetition becomes operational through habit or custom, which projects conjunctions, stabilizes classifications, and regularizes otherwise delicate transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Cognitive and affective efficacy requires force: vivacity, pleasure, pain, passion, or propensity must animate a relation before it governs assent or self-evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Causal intelligibility does not require perceived necessity; experienced conjunction and the transition it induces provide the operative causal content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Heterogeneous effects cannot be explained by indiscriminately extending one medium, faculty, or response-class over the entire field.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Unrestricted principles reveal their inadequacy by overgeneration: universal extension, pure reason, unrestricted fancy, and mere self-relation each produce contradictions or false positives.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Correction is immanent and restrictive: undecidability, skeptical diffidence, empirical practice, and threshold conditions limit mechanisms without abolishing them.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The self has no established status as a substantial bearer but remains indispensable as an object of passion and a temporally extended standard of comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Natural production and representational success do not guarantee correspondence: belief may lack rational foundation, apparent location may be fictive, and self-estimation may diverge from welfare.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The viable endpoint is a reflexive science of human nature that investigates its own fallible operations under permanent skeptical and empirical restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-01","text":"The demand for origin mutates from a semantic test excluding mental substance into a genealogy of belief and then into a distinction between primary affect and mediated self-directed passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-02","text":"Imagination reverses valence: first exposed as fabricating spatial conjunction, it becomes the necessary source of belief, then the associative medium whose affective operation must be gated rather than removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-03","text":"Relation migrates from logical separability and spatial compatibility into causal projection, comparative distinction, public discernibility, and temporal attachment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-04","text":"Custom expands from projecting past conjunctions into future expectation to regulating philosophical assent and stabilizing passion, while retaining the capacity to exceed particular warrant.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-05","text":"The self is displaced from the role of hidden support into the role of represented object; substantial identity disappears while functional self-reference persists.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-06","text":"Contradiction changes function from a reciprocal weapon against material and immaterial systems into an endogenous conflict among natural faculties and finally into a diagnostic of affective overgeneration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-07","text":"Causation is absorbed into a broader transition-model: constant conjunction licenses bodily influence on thought, habit converts conjunction into expectation, and affective conversion requires additional relational gates.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-08","text":"Limitation escalates from suspension of undecidable ontology, through skeptical restraint of cognition, to explicit restriction of a causal law by comparative, social, and temporal thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-09","text":"Spinozist retortion and immortality cease to be active objects after establishing two higher rules: privileged theories receive no exemption, and no simplicity claim yields necessary persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVIS:D17","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-01","text":"The extended/nonspatial partition remains local as a spatial analysis, but its higher-order effect is the prohibition against explaining heterogeneous phenomena through one uniform medium.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-02","text":"Peculiarity, visibility, and durability remain local gates of pride and humility; what scales upward is the form of a threshold-dependent transformation rather than those particular thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-03","text":"Melancholy, diversion, social intercourse, and revived curiosity remain the local phenomenology of skeptical recovery; their scalable function is non-rational restoration of viable cognition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-04","text":"Separability, contradictory natural beliefs, and the joy–pride distinction scale upward because each differentiates effects that an unrestricted theory falsely homogenizes.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-05","text":"Associative completion, customary projection, and general affective rules bridge the units by showing how repeated relations acquire force without becoming intrinsic necessities.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-06","text":"Definitional collapse, skeptical self-subversion, and affective overgeneration are transition catalysts: each forces a move from foundational or universal explanation to restricted operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-07","text":"The simple soul, autonomous understanding, and unconditional passion-law become obsolete as explanatory centers and are recoded as failed cases of unrestricted unification.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-08","text":"Matter–thought causation, the resumption of philosophy, and welfare’s divergence from self-estimation remain distinct local conclusions but jointly establish operation without metaphysical, epistemic, or affective equivalence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD5","CH_THN_SVCT:SD3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-01","text":"No unknown substratum may be installed merely to support perceptions whose relations can be described without it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:NX4","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-02","text":"No autonomous reason or external cognitive instrument can validate the faculties from outside the natural operations under examination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-03","text":"Relation is neither an intrinsic, uniform, nor merely binary property; its force depends upon domain, competition, repetition, and mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A7","CH_THN_SVCT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-04","text":"Imagination can neither be granted unrestricted authority nor eliminated, because both cognition and error arise through its operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-05","text":"Natural necessity, customary stability, and felt conviction do not establish truth, intrinsic value, or ontological homogeneity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVIS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-06","text":"Neither universal extension, universal simplicity, pure understanding, nor mere self-relation can serve as an unrestricted explanatory principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:NX8","CH_THN_SVIS:NX9","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-07","text":"No necessary alignment joins causal operation to substantial ontology, ordinary belief to certainty, or pride and humility to happiness and misery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","CH_THN_SVCT:NX11","CH_THN_SVLT:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of the model in which appearances are accidents that become intelligible only by inhering in an independently knowable substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:D3","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-02","text":"It requires the falsity of any epistemology in which reason can ground assent without habit, imagination, vivacity, passion, or natural propensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-03","text":"It excludes the model in which causation is intelligible only through resemblance, conceptual containment, or a perceived necessary nexus.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX5","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-04","text":"It excludes the model in which affective value transparently registers intrinsic good and evil independently of comparison, publicity, familiarity, and custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-05","text":"It disqualifies the assumption that failures at the margins can be ignored while a general law remains genuinely universal; edge cases determine the law’s required restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-06","text":"It cannot admit a self that is simultaneously an inaccessible substance, a transparent foundation of cognition, and an invariant measure of personal worth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an unmediated common medium joining spatial and nonspatial perception, belief and truth, or welfare and self-estimation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility is a standpoint from which substance, causation, cognition, and value could be validated together without using the fallible transitions under examination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-03","text":"No perfect alignment is available between what is naturally produced and what is real: fictive location, unavoidable belief, and socially legible pride can each diverge from their objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-04","text":"Final repose is inadmissible: neither metaphysical certainty, permanent skepticism, nor perfectly calibrated customary judgment can terminate recursive correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-05","text":"The deepest absent object is a fully self-present subject that could bear perceptions, authorize reason, measure value, and guarantee its own persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX3","CH_THN_SVIS:SNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-01","text":"Once the bearer of perception and the intrinsic power of causation are withdrawn, the passion-system reveals the positive replacement: explanation becomes a study of gated transformations among experiential states.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-02","text":"Associative completion, habitual projection, and customary passion-rules are three scales of one operation by which relations acquire efficacy through repetition and mental transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-03","text":"Because imagination produces both indispensable cognition and systematic fiction, correction cannot purify thought of imagination; it must discriminate among imaginative operations by their conditions and consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-04","text":"Reciprocal failure of materialism and immaterialism, failure of unrestricted fancy and pure understanding, and overproduction of pride instantiate one rule: explanatory extremes collapse when generalized beyond their proper domain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-05","text":"The destruction of the substantial soul does not eliminate self-reference; it transforms the subject into a represented and temporally weighted node within causal, imaginative, and affective relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-06","text":"Time has no single function: habit projects future conjunction, anticipated loss weakens self-attribution, and conceivability prevents either matter or spirit from claiming necessary persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A13","CH_THN_SVIS:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-07","text":"Felt success cannot measure reality: apparent place may be fabricated, natural belief may remain unjustified, and intense self-evaluation may fail to register actual welfare.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME5","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-08","text":"Once theological privilege is removed and custom is recognized as behaviorally formative, speculative systems must be assessed partly by how their rules organize conduct rather than by claimed access to hidden reality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD6","CH_THN_SVCT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes operationally describable as a heterogeneous field of perceptions and affects linked by contingent, force-bearing transitions rather than unified by a known substratum.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes an effect and target of organization: it is where memories, beliefs, comparisons, publicity, and durable attachments converge, not the substance from which they emanate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-03","text":"Causal law is transformed into differential conversion: conjunction establishes possible transition, while domain-specific relations determine whether that transition becomes belief, apparent location, joy, pride, pain, or humility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-04","text":"Temporality is naturalized as projection, anticipated loss, comparative duration, and conceivability of cessation; it supplies no proof of necessary identity or immortality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-05","text":"Human agency is non-sovereign: assent and passion arise from propensities, habits, relations, and general rules that the will cannot simply suspend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-06","text":"Epistemic normativity becomes regulatory rather than foundational: legitimate inquiry is the constrained use of unavoidable faculties, not their validation by an independent criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-07","text":"Error is endogenous to successful machinery: the same operations that locate, project, classify, and stabilize also fabricate, contradict, and overextend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-08","text":"The science of human nature must therefore operate experimentally and recursively, treating every generalization as a revisable mechanism whose limits are disclosed by its own excesses.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-01","text":"Causal or temporal association → imaginative relation-completion → apparent spatial placement → familiar attribution → renewed reinforcement of the fictive relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVIS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-02","text":"Repeated conjunction → habit → vivacity and expectation → ordinary action → further experienced conjunction forms the basic belief-production loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-03","text":"Valenced cause → candidate self-relation → comparative and social filtering → pride or humility → customary classification → facilitated future passion forms the affective loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-04","text":"Inquiry → skeptical intensification → affective paralysis → natural restoration → curiosity → renewed inquiry is the cluster’s explicit spiral rather than a linear resolution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS5","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-05","text":"Universal mechanism → anomalous output → immanent critique → restrictive thresholds → preserved mechanism → renewed exposure to anomalies is the general escalation path.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-06","text":"Human nature is the principal attractor: disputes about soul, causation, certainty, and passion converge on the operations and limits of perception, imagination, habit, and affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVLT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-07","text":"“Relation” becomes a machine when it ceases to name a static connection and begins to regulate conceivability, spatial compatibility, causal expectation, self-reference, comparison, and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-08","text":"“Custom” marks phase change rather than repetition: projection becomes cognitive stability, cognitive stability becomes methodological restraint, and restraint becomes affective regularization with renewed risk of excess.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B01","text":"Input field: heterogeneous perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and candidate relations are given without an independently appearing support.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B02","text":"Semantic audit: demand experiential origin, distinguishability, and separability; exclude concepts that add no identifiable content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B03","text":"Foundational subtraction: remove unknown substance, external necessary power, autonomous reason, and intrinsic affective scale.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D4","CH_THN_SVCT:NX2","CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","text":"Associative synthesis: conjunction and relation generate transition, while vivacity or valence supplies the force required for belief and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","text":"Productive expansion: imagination completes relations, habit projects expectations, and self-reference converts primary affect into candidate self-evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B06","text":"Excess detection: fictive placement, contradictory beliefs, and indiscriminate pride expose the failure of unrestricted transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D8","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","text":"Restrictive gating: apply spatial compatibility, skeptical diffidence, practice, relational closeness, peculiarity, publicity, and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B08","text":"Natural stabilization: habit, embodied restoration, and general rules produce workable regularity without guaranteeing truth or exact proportionality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","text":"Recursive closure: submit stabilized operations to renewed audit; preserve empirically useful mechanisms while suspending metaphysical completion and global certainty.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B01","text":"Unknown support and unrestricted law → demand for experiential and operational justification.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B02","text":"Justificatory demand → decomposition into heterogeneous perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B03","text":"Decomposition → displacement of substance, intrinsic power, and pure reason by associative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B04","text":"Associative transition → belief, apparent placement, and candidate self-directed passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B05","text":"Unrestricted transition → spatial fiction, contradictory natural belief, and affective overgeneration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B06","text":"Contradiction and excess → reciprocal critique, skeptical restraint, and relational, comparative, social, and temporal gating.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","text":"Gated mechanism → customary and natural stabilization → constrained science of human nature → renewed skeptical audit.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-01","text":"The substance–accident map is replaced by a field of separable perceptions and describable transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-02","text":"The mind–matter opposition is subordinated to cross-cutting differences among extended, nonspatial, cognitive, hedonic, and self-directed states.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:ME2","CH_THN_SVCT:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-03","text":"Reason and imagination cease to be autonomous opposites; reason becomes a regulated operation within the imaginative and affective economy of assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVCT:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-04","text":"Causation is detached from substantial ontology and redrawn as conjunction plus a domain-specific transition whose success may require additional gates.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","CH_THN_SVCT:A7","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-05","text":"The self is reclassified from hidden bearer to operational target of memory, comparison, publicity, and temporally durable attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-06","text":"Value is divided into experienced good or evil, social legibility, self-estimation, and welfare; these distributions need not coincide.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:SD3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-07","text":"Error is relocated from an alien disturbance to the overextension of otherwise necessary mechanisms such as imagination, habit, and custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-08","text":"Skepticism is redrawn from terminal suspension into a recurring control function that restrains both philosophical conviction and philosophical doubt.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-09","text":"General theories become readable as transformation models whose validity depends on specified ranges, thresholds, and failure conditions rather than maximal universality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The explicit objects are the soul’s substance, the grounds of belief and philosophy, and the conditions under which pleasure and pain become pride and humility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Heterogeneous inputs are joined by imagination, habit, causal transition, self-relation, comparison, and custom, then restricted where those operations overgenerate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Inquiry advances by applying identical standards to privileged and rival systems, forcing universal claims to disclose their exclusions and limiting conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level — Reality contains spatially heterogeneous states; future and continuity are habitually projected; durable self-relations are stronger than transient ones; necessary persistence remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX3","CH_THN_SVIS:D17","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level — The system performs its thesis by generating speculative excess, exposing collapse, undergoing skeptical interruption, and returning with narrower, provisional machinery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AF3","CH_THN_SVCT:AF1","CH_THN_SVLT:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","text":"Social/Affective level — Public discernibility, comparison, custom, superstition, and philosophy show that representations acquire force through collective circulation and behavioral consequence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-01","text":"The substantial self is excluded, yet a relatively durable self remains necessary as the object and comparator of passion; the status of this operational continuity is not fully specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-02","text":"No Archimedean criterion validates cognition, yet practice, experience, moderation, and critical examination are assigned regulatory authority without a complete account of their title.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-03","text":"Constant conjunction licenses actual matter–thought causation, but the available covariation does not fully individuate the cause or exclude competing conjunctions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D15","CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-04","text":"Imagination is both the condition of cognition and the source of contradiction; no wholly independent faculty exists to delimit its legitimate operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-05","text":"Familiarity depreciates value while custom stabilizes and may amplify passion; the architecture supplies no general priority rule when these effects conflict.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-06","text":"Nature restores belief and action without vindicating them, leaving practical necessity structurally separated from epistemic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-01","text":"Substance mutates from a proposed bearer into an excluded surplus, transferring explanatory burden to relations among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D1","CH_THN_SVIS:D3","CH_THN_SVIS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-02","text":"Imagination mutates from local fabricator of place into the general medium of belief, memory, causal expectation, and affective association.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from logical and spatial compatibility into a graded force governing social distinction and temporal self-attachment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-04","text":"The self mutates from a metaphysical candidate into a functional object that organizes attention, comparison, and passion without appearing as a separate impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A2","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-05","text":"Skepticism mutates from threatened cessation of inquiry into recurrent diffidence that enables rather than terminates constrained philosophy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-06","text":"Universal law mutates into gated mechanism: failures no longer simply refute causal regularity but determine its range and internal conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-07","text":"Custom mutates from a projector of expectation into a regulator of inquiry and passion, then reverses into a source of renewed overextension.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of unsupported, heterogeneous events linked by associative, habitual, causal, affective, social, and temporal relays.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a mobile junction within that field: a point at which vivacity becomes belief and valenced relations become self-attribution, not a hidden substance beneath them.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is a machine of projection and attenuation: repetition opens the future, anticipated loss weakens attachment, and possible annihilation prevents duration from becoming necessity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A13","CH_THN_SVIS:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-04","text":"No escape outside human faculties is available; viable navigation consists in moving between imaginative overproduction and skeptical paralysis through experience, custom, social practice, and recurrent correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if perception-first analysis, associative mediation, heterogeneous effects, threshold restriction, customary stabilization, and skeptical recursion are retained together.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT1","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT1","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined regarding the external status of matter, causal individuation, the normative authority of lively reason, exact affective thresholds, and the scope of general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT2","CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT2","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a recursive operational architecture, not as a deductive foundation or final reconciliation of its natural conflicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT3","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT2","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of failed ontological foundations into a science of constrained transitions whose errors arise from the excess of their own productive mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if negation is followed by operational return: conceptual invalidation must lead to empirical causation, skepticism to natural restoration, and overgeneration to restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT9","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to complete axiomatic reduction is differential felt force—vivacity, pleasure, pain, propensity, familiarity, and passion—because these intensities make transitions operative but are not derivable from relation alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","original_title":"Chapters 13–15","original_context":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System"},"original_title":"Chapters 13–15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":6,"parents":["CH_THN_SXLF","CH_THN_SI_2","CH_THN_SVME"],"revision":"CL_THN_06@R001","sha256":"093f27ecad33fb692b9e7abefe20ad8e9149a52da0c3f2c8f8f7b2a6191e033f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is how socially directed passions vary while their represented objects, qualities, or conditions remain unchanged.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","CH_THN_SVME:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-02","text":"The governing thesis is that social affect is generated by relation-conditioned movements of vivacity: others affect the self through sympathetic reproduction or construction, and through comparison that alters apparent self-position.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes a distributed causal reconstruction in which anomalies eliminate original instincts, intrinsic values, and rational-interest models in favor of associative mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS5","CH_THN_SI_2:AS3","CH_THN_SI_2:AS6","CH_THN_SVME:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-04","text":"The self functions first as a source of vivacity, then as recipient of communicated sentiment, and finally as the comparative term whose apparent condition is altered by another’s fortune.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS6","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-05","text":"Sympathy carries or constructs another’s satisfaction, esteem, contempt, or sorrow within the observer, producing reputation, affection, wealth-esteem, utility-pleasure, and pity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D3","CH_THN_SI_2:D9","CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-06","text":"Comparison preserves one affect across transition to another object, reverses its contribution to self-estimation, and generates envy or malice without requiring injury or autonomous malevolence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-07","text":"Generated passions recursively modify their own conditions through justificatory judgment, imagined self-attribution, reflected esteem, and attempts to alter or sever comparative relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-08","text":"Closure is conditional rather than quantitative: a common relational architecture is established, but the thresholds governing conversion, comparison, and mode selection remain unspecified.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ideas and impressions differ principally in force and vivacity, permitting associative processes to transform represented affect into operative passion without changing its content.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relations are effective only as directed pathways that facilitate, preserve, intensify, interrupt, or redirect imaginative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7","CH_THN_SVME:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Social passion requires a coupling between affective force and a relation that assigns or compares that force to a person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The self is not a sovereign source of valuation: its initial vivacity enables the very transitions through which external sentiments and comparative conditions alter it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Sympathy begins as conversion of an inferred sentiment into an impression but expands into constructive simulation capable of supplementing or replacing the source’s actual emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Sympathy and comparison are distinct transformations of retained affect: sympathy imports another’s represented condition, whereas comparison converts that condition into an inverse estimate of the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Affective valence belongs neither to representation nor object alone; it depends on whether the object is directly surveyed, sympathetically appropriated, or comparatively opposed.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Personal attribution is regulated by forms of certification—durability, intention, authority, causal inference, and general expectation—that determine whether an affect is attached to a person and sustained.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Relational proximity is valence-neutral amplification: it can intensify affection, praise, pity, rivalry, envy, or humiliation according to the process routed through it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Social affect has a topology in which authority, resemblance, contiguity, acquaintance, convergence, direction, and separation govern force more decisively than bare content or absolute rank.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The recurrent generative circuit is self → related other → transformed affect → altered self, with the altered self producing further judgments, signs, desires, and reflected sentiments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Passions can endogenously produce the beliefs and imagined conditions that preserve or amplify them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SVME:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] No intrinsic affective baseline stabilizes self-worth, social value, or hierarchy independently of transmitted sentiment and active comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Force and vivacity retain their function as the common currency linking ideas, impressions, associative transitions, and passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Relation recurs as a conductor of sympathy, a condition of durable affection, a bidirectional pathway of attachment, and a gate permitting comparative impressions to interact.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7","CH_THN_SVME:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Sympathy shifts from reproducing another’s expressed sentiment to appropriating proprietary satisfaction and finally to constructing sorrow absent from the observed person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-04","text":"[Domain migration] Resemblance moves from general human access to other minds, through affection and pity, into the wider topology determining which persons become affectively comparable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SI_2:D7","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] The self moves from vivid associative origin, to object of socially communicated judgment, to variable magnitude constituted against related others.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SVME:A5","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-06","text":"[Reversal] Pleasure and pain cease to have invariant direction: another’s satisfaction can become esteem through sympathy or pain through comparison, while another’s misery can become pity or malicious pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Proximity changes from a means of sympathetic intimacy into a condition of intensified rivalry without ceasing to perform its basic function of preserving affect across transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] General rules progress from tests of causal regularity to constructive templates that supply absent passions and project altered emotional magnitude back onto objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SVME:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] Intention and evaluator authority lose the status of isolated causes and become forms of personal certification governing durable attribution and evidential uptake.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-10","text":"[Terminal transformation] Esteem becomes recursive social capital: communicated satisfaction generates esteem, perceived esteem increases satisfaction, and renewed satisfaction generates further esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-11","text":"[Disappearance] Original fame, intrinsic wealth-value, calculated advantage, and autonomous malevolence disappear after their explanatory roles are redistributed across sympathy, comparison, and associative topology.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-layer local] The exact weighting of evaluator authority, self-concurrence, jest, and numerical accumulation remains specific to reputational influence rather than defining every social passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SXLF:A13","CH_THN_SXLF:D9","CH_THN_SXLF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-layer local] The distinction between durable qualities and transient intentional actions solves the temporal attribution of love and hatred but scales upward only as a general certification principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-layer local] The contingent conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger remains an irreducible constitutional supplement rather than a global relational mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A18","CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-04","text":"[Lower-layer local] Habitual projection from altered emotion to altered object-magnitude is the specific perceptual machinery of comparative judgment, not the general form of sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:A3","CH_THN_SVME:A4","CH_THN_SVME:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Force, vivacity, associative transition, and retained impressions form the common machinery through which all three units generate social affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Fame and wealth-esteem jointly establish that social rank is produced by circulated satisfaction and reflected judgment rather than by isolated possession or intrinsic excellence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Pity and envy demonstrate that proximity does not determine valence; it intensifies whichever sympathetic or comparative operation controls the transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SI_2:D16","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] Constructed pity, resisted contempt, and direct pain joined to comparative pleasure force the separation of affective reception from belief, source-fidelity, and direct valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-09","text":"[Superseded] Primitive social appetites and static classifications are recoded as outputs of directed transitions, relational weights, and recursive feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-01","text":"Social passions are not independent instincts responding uniformly to praise, wealth, misery, enjoyment, or rank.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-02","text":"Social value is not fixed by intrinsic merit, objective magnitude, sensible form, or absolute inequality.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:NX6","CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SVME:NX2","CH_THN_SVME:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-03","text":"Relations are not inert, symmetric classifications; their force depends on direction, convergence, continuity, and the transitions they enable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX7","CH_THN_SXLF:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX7","CH_THN_SVME:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-04","text":"Invariant representation does not guarantee invariant judgment or affective response.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","CH_THN_SVME:NX3","CH_THN_SVME:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-05","text":"Sympathy is neither immediate access to another mind nor necessarily faithful reproduction of another’s actual passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:NX13","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-06","text":"Rational judgment, calculated advantage, and objective justice are not sufficient sovereign causes of social affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A17","CH_THN_SI_2:NX4","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-07","text":"A passion is not identical with its ordinary practical tendency: love need not conceptually contain benevolence, hatred need not contain anger, and misery need not remain an object of pain under comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:NX10","CH_THN_SI_2:NX11","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-01","text":"The cluster requires the falsity of an autonomous self whose valuation can remain insulated from communicated sentiments and comparable others.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-02","text":"It excludes a neutral tribunal capable of judging worth independently of prior passion, associative vivacity, and reflected social affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies any model in which stable representation fixes stable judgment regardless of affective sequence and route.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-04","text":"It cannot admit transparent one-to-one correspondence between another’s interior state and the observer’s sympathetic impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-05","text":"It rules out purely linear causality because passion can generate its own judgments, esteem can amplify the satisfaction it reflects, and comparative pain can motivate alteration of its relational cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-06","text":"Objective hierarchy cannot be affectively operative without an associative route that makes persons proximate, authoritative, familiar, or comparable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-07","text":"Ideas and impressions cannot be absolutely different kinds, because the entire social-affective architecture requires conversion by altered vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX2","CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-08","text":"No necessary moral order may guarantee that relational intensification produces benevolence rather than rivalry, pity rather than malice, or esteem rather than mortification.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent object is an intrinsic baseline of self-worth against which social communication and comparison could be finally corrected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-02","text":"The system lacks a general criterion determining when another’s condition will be directly surveyed, sympathetically appropriated, or comparatively inverted.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SNX4","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-03","text":"No independent metric fixes the threshold at which relation transfers enough vivacity, sustains comparison, or converts an idea into an impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SVME:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-04","text":"The authentic interior state of the other is unavailable as a final ground because social affect depends on inferred, reflected, supplemented, or fabricated sentiments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-05","text":"A morally guaranteed direction for social relation is inadmissible: the same relational machinery can sustain affection, pity, esteem, envy, humiliation, or malice.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","CH_THN_SVME:SD5","CH_THN_SXLF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-06","text":"There is no affective position wholly outside the relational system from which its generated values could be judged without participating in their production.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX1","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX5","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-01","text":"Sympathy and comparison form dual operators on socially represented affect: the first reproduces or constructs another’s sentiment in the self, while the second preserves that sentiment as a contrasting force against the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-02","text":"Relational topology determines not only affective intensity but whether two personal conditions can interact at all.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-03","text":"Proximity is structurally ambivalent: it intensifies affection and pity when transition supports sympathetic appropriation, but intensifies envy when transition sustains comparative opposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-04","text":"Authority, intention, acquaintance, and general expectation are heterogeneous certification devices that stabilize otherwise transient affective attributions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-05","text":"Fame and wealth operate through the same recursive status circuit: perceived satisfaction or approval generates esteem, and reflected esteem intensifies the state that elicited it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SXLF:SD6","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-06","text":"Once sympathy can construct absent emotion, social effects depend on the observer’s operative model of another’s condition rather than on veridical access to that condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-07","text":"Another’s misery can simultaneously support direct sympathetic pain and comparative pleasure, so interpersonal affect need not resolve into a single valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D3","CH_THN_SVME:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-08","text":"Passion preserves itself through cognitive and imaginative supplementation: hatred generates injury judgments, while praise can generate a provisional self-attribution that makes praise effective.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-09","text":"Relational severance is a general affective strategy: concealment weakens shame, competing transitions weaken attachment, and distance disables envy without changing the underlying condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-10","text":"Fame, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, and malice require no autonomous faculty when their functions can be distributed among vivacity transfer, construction, contrast, and recursive self-estimation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX12","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-01","text":"Social reality becomes a distributed affective computation in which persons continuously transmit, simulate, compare, and reflect one another’s conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD4","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a relay and comparator rather than an enclosed origin: its vivid self-impression enables external affect to return as altered self-affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-03","text":"Value is generated in circulation: qualities, possessions, utility, rank, happiness, and misery acquire force through the paths by which their attendant sentiments reach or oppose the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-04","text":"Social hierarchy is recursively produced rather than merely perceived, because esteem amplifies satisfaction and comparative attention makes relationally proximate differences more affectively real than remote inequalities.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","CH_THN_SVME:SD3","CH_THN_SXLF:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-05","text":"Social time is retentive and recursive: durable character sustains attribution, prior impressions remain active during transition, and lost comparative advantage becomes a new present pain.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","CH_THN_SVME:A8","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-06","text":"Social judgment is epistemically endogenous to passion: testimony can alter self-belief, while prior passion can generate the judgment needed to preserve itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-07","text":"Strategic intervention shifts from changing objects to changing relational routes: authority can be discredited, identities concealed, associations severed, and comparative proximity weakened.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SXLF:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-08","text":"No ethical valence inheres in social connectedness itself; intensified relation can produce solidarity or antagonism according to the transformation governing retained affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-01","text":"Reputation loop: vivid self → inferred external judgment → sympathetic reproduction → pride or humility → increased receptivity to confirming judgments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SXLF:A17","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-02","text":"Wealth loop: possession → proprietary satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → perceived esteem → vanity → renewed satisfaction and esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-03","text":"Injury loop: painful event → hatred → threatened diminution by justice or innocence → generated judgment of injury → stabilized hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-04","text":"Envy escalation: comparative advantage → rival approach → loss of former comparative pleasure → comparison with the former state → new positive pain → pressure to sever relation or remove superiority.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D6","CH_THN_SVME:D9","CH_THN_SVME:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-05","text":"Constructive-sympathy loop: observed condition → customary expectation of passion → construction of absent sentiment → intensified response through proximity or contrast → confirmation of the general expectation.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:A20","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-06","text":"Relational intensification spirals rather than advances linearly: increased acquaintance or resemblance strengthens transition, which strengthens passion, which gives further force to related ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-07","text":"The principal attractor is relation-mediated vivacity: heterogeneous passions repeatedly return to the question of which transition preserves, transfers, constructs, or opposes affective force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-08","text":"The terminal escalation path is sign or condition → lively idea → impression → passion → judgment or desire → altered social condition → new sign or comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS3","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B01","text":"Initialize a persistently vivid self S, represented persons O, attendant affects E(O), and a directed relational network R.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SVME:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B02","text":"Weight R by resemblance, contiguity, acquaintance, kinship, authority, convergence, directionality, durability, and separation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B03","text":"Direct attribution operator: pleasure or pain × durable quality or intention-sign × person-relation → stable love or hatred; strong affect × weak attribution → acute but unstable passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:FGS2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B04","text":"Sympathetic operator Sᵧ: signs or represented conditions → idea of another’s sentiment → relational vivacity transfer → corresponding or constructed impression in the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B05","text":"Comparative operator C: R(O₁,O₂) → preservation of E(O₁) during transition → contrast with E(O₂) → altered apparent magnitude or self-condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:FGS2","CH_THN_SVME:FGS3","CH_THN_SVME:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B06","text":"Route Sᵧ through favorable or unfavorable evaluation to generate pride or humility; route it through satisfaction, utility, or misfortune to generate esteem, beauty, or pity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B07","text":"Route C through another’s enjoyment to generate diminished self-estimation and envy; route it through anticipated misery to generate comparative elevation and malice.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:FGS6","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B08","text":"Feed generated passion back into cognition and relation: passion strengthens associated ideas, constructs supporting judgments, reflects esteem, or seeks to alter the comparative field.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:A16","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B09","text":"Attenuate output by disesteem, nonconcurrence, weak return transition, divided relations, distance, or conceptual separation; intensify it by authority, proximity, convergence, custom, and retained impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS5","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS6","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS7","CH_THN_SVME:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B10","text":"Closure rule: apparent exceptions do not trigger new faculties until variation in routing, certification, retention, construction, or comparison has been exhausted.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B01","text":"Unequal praise, unstable affection, anomalous pity, and unprovoked malevolence expose failures of undifferentiated social-passion models →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","CH_THN_SVME:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B02","text":"Original instinct, intrinsic value, rational advantage, moral injury, and autonomous malevolence are eliminated as sufficient causes →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SVME:NX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B03","text":"Force and vivacity establish a common currency connecting representation, impression, and passion →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B04","text":"Directed relations establish the pathways, weights, continuities, and interruptions of affective transition →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B05","text":"Sympathy imports or constructs another’s sentiment, while comparison preserves and inverts its contribution to self-estimation →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B06","text":"The dual operations differentiate reputation, attachment, esteem, pity, envy, and malice without assigning each an independent faculty →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME5","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B07","text":"Generated passions recursively stabilize themselves through judgments, reflected esteem, imaginative supplementation, and relational intervention →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B08","text":"The system closes on a relational social ontology whose governing mechanisms are reconstructible but whose activation thresholds and mode-selection rules remain open.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-01","text":"Social passions are reclassified from discrete faculties into outputs of a shared relational economy of vivacity, retention, simulation, and contrast.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME3","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-02","text":"Relations become causal infrastructure rather than descriptive predicates: their direction and arrangement determine which affects can interact.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME3","CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","CH_THN_SVME:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-03","text":"Other minds are reclassified as inferred and constructible affective models rather than transparent sources or sealed interiors.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-04","text":"The opposition between direct and social value collapses: social value is generated either by vicarious participation in another’s satisfaction or by inverse comparison with another’s condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-05","text":"Hierarchy is redrawn through affective adjacency: near rivals and credible judges can exert more force than objectively superior but relationally remote persons.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D4","CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SVME:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-06","text":"Agency becomes distributed across author, observer, relation, prior passion, general rule, and reflected response rather than residing solely in intentional action.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-07","text":"Distance, anonymity, concealment, and conceptual separation become active techniques for modifying passion without altering objective conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME6","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-08","text":"Interpretation must distinguish content from force, relation from transition, direct valence from comparative valence, and transmitted emotion from constructed emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SVME:ME2","CH_THN_SVME:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: praise, blame, affection, anger, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, and malice present variable responses to persons and their fortunes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT1","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT1","CH_THN_SVME:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: force and vivacity, directed relation, retained impression, sympathetic conversion, constructive simulation, and comparison generate those variations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT2","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT2","CH_THN_SVME:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: intervention targets authority, proximity, concurrence, associative continuity, and comparative framing rather than objective conditions alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: reality consists affectively of ideas and impressions differentiated by degree, while social time operates through persistence, succession, retention, recurrence, and reflected amplification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT4","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT4","CH_THN_SVME:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the system repeatedly converts anomalies into controlled variations, tests rival causes, and returns apparent exceptions to a progressively enlarged relational mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-01","text":"Sympathy oscillates between reproduction and construction: the system requires causal signs for reputational transmission but permits pity to arise where the source passion is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-02","text":"Proximity explains both agreeable affection and painful envy, but no general principle specifies what selects sympathetic identification rather than comparative opposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7","CH_THN_SVME:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-03","text":"Direct and comparative valence can reverse one another, yet the activation rule deciding between direct survey and comparison remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-04","text":"Relation is repeatedly established as necessary or contributory, but its sufficiency and quantitative threshold are never independently determined.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-05","text":"Reason functions both as evidential certification and as a product distorted or recruited by passion, leaving its independent corrective power unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:DNX6","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-06","text":"The shared relational machinery does not fully derive practical direction because the conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger remains constitutionally posited.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A18","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-01","text":"The idea–impression threshold is the first mutation point: unchanged representational content becomes causally operative passion through increased vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-02","text":"Sympathy mutates from transmission into simulation when a represented condition and general rule generate an emotion not present in the observed person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-03","text":"The self mutates from associative source into socially produced magnitude when another’s fortune becomes an inverse measure of its apparent condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SVME:A5","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-04","text":"Esteem mutates from communicated pleasure into a recursive status mechanism once the possessor perceives and reflects the observer’s response.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-05","text":"Relation mutates from resemblance or contiguity as static categories into a directional topology of outward passage, return, convergence, obstruction, and severance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-06","text":"Direct affect mutates into comparative affect when a preceding impression is preserved and contributes inversely to the succeeding estimate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:A3","CH_THN_SVME:A8","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a directed affective network whose nodes are persons and conditions, whose edges conduct vivacity, and whose feedback alters the nodes that initiated transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a vivid but permeable relay: it receives signs, simulates other minds, compares positions, and returns the resulting passion as judgment, esteem, injury, or desire.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is a circuit of retained affect: past impressions persist into present transitions, durable attribution extends passions, and reflected consequences become new causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-04","text":"Available struggle consists in routing and rerouting affect—strengthening authority or resemblance, contesting attribution, breaking association, concealing identity, or altering comparative distance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster is reconstructible from force and vivacity, directed relation, personal certification, sympathetic transformation, comparative transformation, and recursive feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT1","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT1","CH_THN_SVME:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B02","text":"These axioms preserve the actual movement only if sympathy and comparison remain distinct operations rather than being flattened into generic social influence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where identical objects or conditions produce divergent effects under controlled changes in authority, intention, proximity, and comparative framing.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT4","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT4","CH_THN_SVME:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on accepting force and vivacity as the common currency and relations as causal pathways rather than static properties.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX5","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B05","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns mode selection and measure: the system does not specify when sympathy, direct survey, or comparison dominates, nor how much relation is sufficient.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B06","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing because passion-generated judgment, reflected esteem, and remembered comparative advantage cannot be reduced to a static inventory of causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B07","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to full axiomatic reduction is the constitutional direction of compound passions and the unspecified thresholds governing idea–impression conversion and relational activation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_title":"Chapters 16–18","original_context":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy"},"original_title":"Chapters 16–18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":7,"parents":["CH_THN_SXLH","CH_THN_SVEC","CH_THN_SXCL"],"revision":"CL_THN_07@R001","sha256":"2c51e4fc172a07dbba2544d90ba8a0230385506e22f361acce293727bde2d317","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-01","text":"The central problem is to explain affective continuity and variation when species, cognitive capacity, encounter history, activity type, and epistemic content vary independently.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS1","CH_THN_SVEC:AS1","CH_THN_SXCL:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-02","text":"Affect is generated less by an object’s intrinsic character than by the mode through which a sensitive system encounters, associates, repeats, pursues, transmits, or stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-03","text":"The cluster becomes a differential causal argument: cognitive subtraction, temporal iteration, and controlled variation of truth and assurance isolate operative mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-04","text":"A species-general affective substrate persists across unequal cognitive powers, while cognition governs which channels, associations, and pleasures become available.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-05","text":"The movement is from object-side explanation toward processual mediation through sensible impact, mental motion, attention, relation, instinct, sympathy, activity, and belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-06","text":"Affective force is mobile: attendant emotion converts into passion, pleasure in action transfers concern to an end, and perceived passion communicates itself between organisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-07","text":"The real movement is recursive and phase-dependent: affective outputs become conditions of repetition, inclination, pursuit, amplification, stabilization, exhaustion, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-08","text":"Closure is conditional rather than monistic: common operations unify the field, but instinct, natural salience, cognitive thresholds, and active/passive divergence prevent reduction to one uniform route.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Affective value is constituted through an encounter between an object and an operating sensitive system, not fixed by the object alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Pleasure and pain function both as direct effects and as mobile forces capable of augmenting, redirecting, transmitting, or reversing passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Cognitive capacity is not the universal source of passion; it regulates the accessibility, complexity, and prevalence of particular affective channels.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Affective causes become identifiable through controlled differences: remove reflection, vary repetition, or hold truth and assurance constant while altering the mode of attainment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Difficulty, facility, excitation, and stability have no invariant valence; their effects depend on degree, temporal position, and active or passive mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Affect is transferable across functional relations: from attendant emotion into a predominant passion, from action into its end, and from one sensitive being into another.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Present passion is path-dependent: prior encounters, repeated performances, acquired inclinations, and transferred concerns enter into its current constitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Relation is a generative operator rather than a static resemblance: it supports animal attachment, strengthens ideational force, transfers concern, and fixes attention.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D2","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Activity and passivity divide affective dynamics: active exercise can sustain force and produce concern, while passive reception tends toward transmitted affect, fading excitation, or uncertainty-relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Stability is pleasurable when it resolves resistance or vacillation, but destructive when excessive facility removes the excitation required for interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Concern for an end need not precede the process directed toward it; repetition and pleasurable activity can generate inclination or acquired end-concern retrospectively.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Affective plasticity is bounded by prior endowment: natural good and evil, complete disagreeability, instinct, and pre-existing salience constrain what process can produce.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The field is unified by species-general and recursively transferable operations, not by a single uniform mechanism of affective production.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pleasure and pain remain the basic affective currency, but their source migrates from sensible benefit and injury to mental motion, exercised understanding, belief, and doubt.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Cognition shifts from a capacity whose subtraction tests affective simplicity to an activity whose difficult exercise becomes an original source of pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-03","text":"[Expansion] Relation begins as obvious animal association, then expands into attentional support, ideational force, and the transfer of passion from pursuit to end.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Difficulty is painful resistance in unfamiliar passive apprehension but pleasurable exercise when attention and attainable success organize active inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Familiarity extinguishes novelty and may produce languor under custom, yet can intensify informational curiosity by strengthening relation and ideational force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-06","text":"[Functional recurrence] Stabilization recurs as tranquillity, orderly facility, and fixed belief; it relieves disturbance but ceases to sustain interest once movement becomes excessively faint.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Sympathy moves from direct communication of animal passion to faint imaginative concern whose function is not benevolence but attentional fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] The active/passive distinction moves from habit formation into a higher division between self-sustaining pursuit and receptive uncertainty-relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal transformation] Truth disappears as an intrinsic motive after performing an exclusionary function, then returns as either an acquired endpoint of pursuit or the content stabilized by belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:NX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Peculiar parental instinct does not generalize into the cluster’s main process model, but remains as the exception that blocks exhaustive associative reduction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:D4","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Peculiar parental instinct remains specific to animal affection; at cluster scale it functions chiefly as a limit on causal monism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The motion of the spirits is a chapter-level causal vocabulary for custom whose exact physiology does not scale upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A15","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The division between abstract relations and matters of existence, and the special role of imagined public utility, remain machinery of scientific curiosity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A6","CH_THN_SXCL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] The survival of passion under reduced reflection becomes the general proposition that cognition selects affective routes without constituting sensitive life itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXLH:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Custom’s degree-sensitive phases become a general rule that affective operations change valence according to threshold, sequence, and mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SVEC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Conversion, relational transfer, and sympathy jointly produce the current-level principle of mobile affective force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] The active/passive habit distinction connects custom to curiosity’s division between challenge-seeking inquiry and uncertainty-relieving fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] Animal comparison, repeated encounter, and equal-truth comparison become three implementations of one differential method for separating content from operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Sensible benefit, natural emotion, utility, and truth retain local efficacy but are subordinated to the histories and operations through which they become affectively forceful.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-01","text":"No intrinsic object-side property is sufficient to determine the strength, direction, or persistence of passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-02","text":"Reflection, propositional understanding, and truth-recognition are not universal prerequisites of affection or social coordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX2","CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-03","text":"Increasing repetition, facility, certainty, or cognitive complexity does not produce a monotonic increase in pleasure or passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2","CH_THN_SXCL:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-04","text":"A single name—love, custom, or curiosity—does not guarantee a single causal mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-05","text":"Species identity cannot serve as the ontological or intentional boundary of affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX1","CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","CH_THN_SXLH:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-06","text":"Antecedent concern for an end is not necessary for inclination, pursuit, or subsequent distress at failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-07","text":"Affective communication and imagined utility do not require accurate knowledge of causes, beneficiaries, or terminal purposes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:A6","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-08","text":"Process cannot transform every object without remainder: complete disagreeability, natural affective endowment, and instinct mark limits to acquired modification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:NX5","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires a synchronic and fixed subject–object relation of affect to be false; present valence must remain penetrable by history, activity, and mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-02","text":"The sovereign rational subject must be disqualified as the universal origin of passion, because affect precedes reflection, produces its own ends, and operates through non-propositional transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX1","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-03","text":"Cognitive hierarchy cannot be treated as moral or affective hierarchy: lower-cost passions may be more prevalent, while cognitively demanding passions require special enabling conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-04","text":"Belief, sympathy, and relation cannot be merely representational links; each must alter force, stability, attention, or transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX5","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-05","text":"Linear reinforcement must be false: recurrence can amplify, reconcile, stabilize, weaken, exhaust, or reverse according to its phase.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-06","text":"Affective individualism must be false wherever another’s passion or imagined beneficiaries can reorganize conduct without explicit shared purpose.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-07","text":"The worth of an object cannot be assumed to motivate in proportion to that worth; natural value, public utility, and truth all require operational mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:NX5","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-01","text":"The system circles an unavailable intrinsic ground of affect: it repeatedly subordinates object-value to process while still presupposing natural goods, evils, saliences, and repugnances that it does not derive.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-02","text":"No common metric specifies how much cognition, facility, excitation, relation, or success crosses the threshold between engagement, indifference, and reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT5","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-03","text":"A self-identical subject prior to affective history is unavailable; the subject appears only as the provisional result of habits, transmitted passions, beliefs, and acquired ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-04","text":"A truth-sensitive normative discriminator is absent: the genealogy can explain pursuit, belief, and relief without explaining why true fixation should outrank equally vivid false fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-05","text":"Final causal unification remains unavailable because instinct, sensible causation, mental motion, sympathy, activity, and belief are functionally coordinated without reduction to one mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-01","text":"The common method isolates affective causation by varying capacity, encounter history, or mode of attainment while preventing object-content from carrying the entire explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-02","text":"Cognition is a variable operator rather than a uniformly beneficial power: passion survives its reduction, inquiry can derive pleasure from its exercise, and excessive ease can extinguish its engagement.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-03","text":"Active pursuit requires managed resistance: sufficient difficulty activates understanding, success keeps the pursuit completable, and excessive facility removes the motion sustaining interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-04","text":"Affect behaves as a transferable magnitude whose destination depends on conjunction: it passes into a dominant passion, a pursued end, or a sympathizing group.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-05","text":"Stabilization is pleasurable only relative to a prior disturbance: orderly facility resolves resistance and belief resolves doubt, but prolonged facility can eliminate the contrast required for interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-06","text":"Familiarity has opposed effects because it operates through distinct mediators: it removes novelty under custom while strengthening relation and ideational proximity in informational curiosity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-07","text":"Conduct can precede the motive later attributed to it: repeated action generates inclination, pursuit generates concern for its end, and sympathetic response organizes behavior without knowledge of the initiating object.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-08","text":"The distribution of passions is governed partly by processing cost: low-reflection mechanisms diffuse broadly, difficult inquiry requires concentrated exercise, and passive repetition weakens when independent activation is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-09","text":"Affective construction is powerful but not unlimited: every recursive process requires an initially available sensible effect, natural emotion, engaging idea, tolerable action, or instinctive disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-01","text":"The subject becomes a path-dependent configuration of sensitivities, habits, beliefs, transmitted passions, and acquired concerns rather than a fixed rational origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-02","text":"Affective time is nonlinear: recurrence produces thresholds and phase changes rather than mere accumulation, while later outputs revise the conditions of subsequent encounters.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-03","text":"Ends can be temporally posterior to action: inclination, concern, and the pain of failure may be generated by the process they later appear to motivate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-04","text":"Epistemic motivation is naturalized without being normatively secured: affect explains why inquiry and belief occur but not why truth deserves pursuit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-05","text":"The individual is affectively porous: sympathy, conjunction, and imagined utility allow social force to operate without shared propositions or benevolent intentions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXCL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-06","text":"Effective agency occupies a moving interval between inert facility and unmastered agitation, using enough resistance, attention, and success to sustain active motion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-07","text":"Affective universality is compatible with mechanistic plurality: common passions need not arise through identical cognitive, associative, instinctive, temporal, or epistemic routes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-08","text":"Causal explanation must decompose nominal passions into source, enabling condition, transfer mechanism, temporal phase, and completion or termination condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:AS2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXLH:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-01","text":"Novelty loop: unfamiliarity → resistance → excitation → amplified passion → repetition → novelty-decay → facility → inclination → further repetition → languor or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:FGS2","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-02","text":"Inquiry loop: represented importance → fixed attention → difficult exercise → pleasure → transferred concern for the end → heightened stakes of success and error → renewed pursuit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-03","text":"Informational-curiosity loop: relation or novelty → forceful but unstable idea → painful vacillation → demand for belief → vivification and fixation → relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-04","text":"Sympathetic escalation loop: perceived passion → affective communication → conjunction → intensified collective passion → stronger perceptible passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXLH:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-05","text":"Active-habit loop: performance supplies excitation → repetition produces facility and inclination → inclination increases performance → performance renews its own support.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SVEC:A17","CH_THN_SVEC:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-06","text":"Transfer attractor: affect generated at one site migrates toward the object, end, passion, or group most strongly related to the generating operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-07","text":"Excitatory-band attractor: the system repeatedly seeks sufficient force to secure attention and motion without crossing into overwhelming resistance, painful vacillation, or affect-destroying facility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A6","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-08","text":"Comparative-confirmation loop: a prior human passion theory predicts animal behavior, analogy interprets that behavior as passion, and the interpreted behavior then corroborates the theory.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B01","text":"Initial endowment: sensible valence, instinct, natural emotion, or ideational salience supplies the minimum difference on which affective processing can operate.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B02","text":"Differential input: vary cognitive capacity, repetition, difficulty, relation, assurance, or activity mode while holding other factors comparatively stable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B03","text":"Mediation: input encounters resistance, facility, attention, association, sympathy, exercise, vivacity, or fixation rather than producing passion immediately.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B04","text":"Channel selection: cognitive power, temporal phase, and active/passive mode determine which mediator becomes effective.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B05","text":"Affective conversion: the mediator generates pleasure, pain, agitation, relief, or transmitted passion, which then augments or redirects the operative affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B06","text":"Recursive transfer: generated affect becomes inclination, acquired concern, social amplification, or demand for stabilized belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B07","text":"Iteration: the output alters the next encounter, producing reinforcement, tranquillity, exhaustion, intensified pursuit, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B08","text":"Conditional closure: recurring operations establish a general dynamic architecture, while instinct, irreducible salience, and unspecified thresholds preserve causal plurality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:AFM-B01","text":"Fixed object-value challenged → controlled variation introduced → mediating operation isolated → affect generated or transmitted → force transferred to passion, end, or group → recurrence changes operating conditions → inclination, stabilization, amplification, exhaustion, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:FGS8","CH_THN_SVEC:AF1","CH_THN_SXCL:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:AFM-B02","text":"Species-general sensitivity → cognition-dependent channel selection → active/passive bifurcation → threshold-sensitive temporal iteration → acquired concern and collective force → conditional processual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-01","text":"The human–animal divide is replaced by affective continuity with graded cognitive access to different mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME1","CH_THN_SXLH:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-02","text":"Object-centered affect is replaced by a relational field in which history, mental operation, attention, and transmission determine experienced force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXLH:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-03","text":"Cognition is reclassified from sovereign source to variable mechanism: it may be unnecessary, limiting, enabling, effortful, or affect-generating.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-04","text":"Repetition becomes a threshold-governed phase process rather than a synonym for reinforcement or familiarity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ME1","CH_THN_SVEC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-05","text":"Motivation is divided into original pleasure, enabling condition, transferred concern, and completion condition, allowing ends to be produced by activity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-06","text":"Social coordination becomes intelligible without shared causal knowledge, species identity, retaliation calculation, or genuine benevolence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","CH_THN_SXLH:NX8","CH_THN_SXCL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-07","text":"Truth, utility, and natural valence are reclassified as inputs whose motivational force depends on mediation rather than as self-executing causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-08","text":"Contradictory effects cease to demand separate faculties once degree, sequence, transfer, and active/passive mode become available as explanatory variables.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXLH:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: animal affections, the contrary effects of custom, scientific pursuit, and informational curiosity supply heterogeneous cases of variable passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT1","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT1","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: sensible effects, association, instinct, sympathy, resistance, facility, attention, cognitive exercise, belief, and doubt form interacting affective mediators.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT2","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: apparent contradictions are decomposed by controlled comparison into source, condition, mode, threshold, transfer, and phase.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT3","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: sensitive life is continuous across species but internally historical, with present passions produced by successive encounters and recursive modifications.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT4","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT4","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B05","text":"Epistemic level: animal passion is inferred analogically, while human truth-seeking is explained genealogically; neither procedure yields direct access to an intrinsically truth-governed interior.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT5","CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B06","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the cluster performs its own theory by repeatedly exposing a fixed explanation to variation until hidden mediators, reversals, and bifurcations appear.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS4","CH_THN_SVEC:AS3","CH_THN_SXCL:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-01","text":"Species-general springs coexist with peculiar parental instinct, leaving uncertain whether instinct belongs to the common architecture or marks an irreducible exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-02","text":"The processual account depends on natural goods, emotions, saliences, and repugnances whose origin it does not explain, limiting its apparent constructivism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-03","text":"Familiarity weakens passion by removing novelty but can strengthen curiosity by increasing relation; the architecture distinguishes mediators without specifying their relative dominance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-04","text":"Uncertainty is enabling within active pursuit but painful as doxastic vacillation; the boundary between productive openness and destabilizing alternation remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-05","text":"Belief explains vivid fixation and relief without securing truth, separating the motivational closure of curiosity from epistemic correctness.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-06","text":"Spirits, sympathy, instinct, attention, and belief provide heterogeneous causal vocabularies whose functional coordination exceeds their explicit theoretical integration.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-07","text":"Animal behavior confirms a theory imported through human analogy, producing an evidential loop whose corroborative force is real but not fully independent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-01","text":"Difficulty mutates from resistant agitation in unfamiliar apprehension into pleasurable exertion in active discovery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-02","text":"Cognition mutates from a dispensable condition of elementary passion into a principal source of scientific pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from an obvious association between beings into an operator that fixes attention, vivifies ideas, and transfers concern to ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-04","text":"Stability mutates from pleasurable tranquillity into affect-destroying languor under excess, while belief-stability remains pleasurable because it terminates vacillation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-05","text":"The end mutates from an antecedently valued object into a product of action-generated passion and relational transfer.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-06","text":"Sympathy mutates from direct interanimal communication into a faint imaginative mechanism that supports attention without producing genuine concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-01","text":"Affective reality appears as an excitable network in which objects, ideas, actions, and organisms become nodes for the circulation and conversion of pleasure, pain, attention, and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable and temporally accumulated switching-point rather than a sovereign center: it receives, amplifies, habituates, pursues, transfers, and stabilizes forces.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is a sequence of phase transitions in which repetition and pursuit alter the mechanism that will govern the next encounter.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-04","text":"Viable navigation consists in regulating relation, resistance, attention, repetition, and activity so that affect remains forceful without collapsing into repugnance, vacillation, or languor.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SXCL:D2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-05","text":"Collective life is an amplification circuit in which passion can cross bodies and organize conduct before its origin is consciously represented.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXLH:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if affect is retained as mediated, transferable, path-dependent, degree-sensitive, and cognitively gated rather than intrinsically object-fixed.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B02","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a plural dynamic architecture, but not as a reduction to one physiological or psychological mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where differential variation separates content from operation and where affective transfer explains the production of amplified passions, inclinations, and acquired ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B04","text":"It remains underdetermined at thresholds of cognition, facility, salience, success, and relation, and at the origin of instinctive or naturally affective endowments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT2","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if recursive sequence and functional reversal are retained; flattening difficulty, familiarity, stability, or cognition into fixed causes would destroy the system.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","CH_THN_SXLH:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable remainder resistant to axiomatic reduction is the evidential and causal bridge supplied by analogy, spirits, instinct, and compressed appeals to sympathy or attention.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals · Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom · Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","original_title":"Chapters 19–21","original_context":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals · Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom · Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth"},"original_title":"Chapters 19–21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_08","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 22–24","order":8,"parents":["CH_THN_PIJI","CH_THN_SIRW","CH_THN_SVSF"],"revision":"CL_THN_08@R001","sha256":"1e867b15f0bce30381c4bd24f45a41e613a760f4712b28700e5fca769b475feb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B01","text":"The governing problem is how partial passions, scarce transferable goods, and social dependence can generate determinate and morally authoritative juridical relations without innate justice or antecedent property rights.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS1","CH_THN_PIJI:AS6","CH_THN_SVSF:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B02","text":"Justice is reconstructed as an artificial but necessary rule-system: natural conditions necessitate coordination, but neither natural impulse nor independent reason directly supplies its form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS2","CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B03","text":"The argument becomes a combined motivational genealogy, institutional ontology, psychological jurisprudence, and reductive test of rival natural-right models.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS3","CH_THN_SIRW:AS4","CH_THN_SVSF:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B04","text":"First-order self-interest threatens cooperation; reflective self-interest redirects the same passion into reciprocal restraint and gradually stabilized expectations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_PIJI:D6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B05","text":"Stabilized possession does not recognize pre-existing property but generates property, right, and obligation as duties constituted by justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B06","text":"The establishment of property creates a second-order allocation problem: general utility requires determinate titles but cannot derive their particular content, so imagination and antecedent relations select juridical forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:AS1","CH_THN_SIRW:AS3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B07","text":"Because possessory, temporal, spatial, causal, and associative relations remain gradual or conflicting, municipal law imposes binary closure where natural criteria underdetermine title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B08","text":"Interest first establishes observance; the perceived utility of the resulting system then produces sympathetic approval, education, reputation, and institutional reinforcement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS7","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B09","text":"The movement is recursive and sedimentary rather than linear: passion generates restraint, restraint generates rights, rights require stipulative determination, and moralization feeds the determinate system back into conduct as second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Society is necessary under conditions in which human weakness, partial affection, conflicting self-love, and scarce transferable goods make unregulated possession unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A moral predicate cannot supply the first motive for the conduct whose morality it evaluates; institutional genesis must therefore begin from pre-moral passions and interests.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Justice is artificial without being arbitrary: it is convention-dependent in origin but functionally necessitated by durable human and material conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Convention arises through mutually known, conditionally reciprocal restraint grounded in common interest and repeated experience, not through an antecedent promise or sovereign command.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Self-interest changes function across scales: immediate acquisition destabilizes society, while reflective and coordinated self-interest generates the rules that restrain acquisition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Property is not antecedent to justice but is constituted as a socially enforced relation of duties, expectations, rights, and exclusions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The convention stabilizing possession does not itself assign goods; institutional existence and juridical individuation are distinct generative problems.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Property has a dual determination: general utility necessitates settled allocation, while imagination and antecedent relations principally specify who receives which title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Stable justice requires society-wide general and inflexible rules because case-sensitive judgments of need, merit, fitness, or immediate utility reproduce partiality and contention.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Title is temporally layered: present possession governs founding settlement, while occupation, prescription, accession, and succession govern differentiated acquisition within established society.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Juridical outputs are integral and binary even though their psychological, utilitarian, temporal, and relational grounds vary by degree.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Municipal law supplies practical closure where natural association and public utility fail to determine a unique title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] The justice of an act derives from its position within a socially advantageous rule-system rather than from its isolated consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_SIRW:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-14","text":"[Persistent] Justice has a temporally divided foundation: common interest generates observance before sympathy with public utility generates moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Once established, convention, juridical determination, moral approbation, education, reputation, and government form a reinforcing system that converts artificial rules into durable dispositions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Justice remains an artificial virtue throughout: neither its necessity, its utility, nor its affective approval converts it into an innate moral principle.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:A17","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Stability of possession first solves social conflict, then reappears as an insufficient general rule whose allocation gap generates the detailed property regime.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] Property moves from an effect of stabilized possession, to a differentiated system of relational titles, to evidence that juridical reality is institutionally constituted.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Utility first explains why justice is necessary, then becomes insufficient to specify particular titles, and finally becomes the public effect whose contemplation moralizes the completed system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Self-love changes from the principal source of possessory conflict into the originating material of reciprocal restraint and unintended public advantage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-06","text":"[Displacement] Imagination migrates from a general psychological faculty of associative completion into a juridical mechanism that joins persons to objects, extends titles, and structures inheritance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-07","text":"[Mutation] Generality changes from reciprocal abstention governing possession into differentiated but society-wide rules whose inflexibility suppresses case-specific natural motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal transformation] Present possession produces founding title but disappears as a perpetual criterion once its founding work is complete; occupation and restitution require its restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-09","text":"[Migration] Graduality moves from possessory probability and temporal association into a general challenge to the integral form of property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] Sympathy begins as the explanation of moral approval and is absorbed into a larger institutional feedback loop involving public judgment, education, reputation, and stabilized compliance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:D11","CH_THN_PIJI:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-11","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Universal benevolence, direct public concern, private benevolence, and an innate sense of justice disappear as originating motives after forcing the transition to convention and reflected interest.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-01","text":"[Local-to-scaling] The semantic disambiguation of “nature” remains preparatory machinery, but it scales upward by preventing artificial justice from being classified as unnatural, arbitrary, or morally defective.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX1","CH_THN_PIJI:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The detailed taxonomies of occupation, prescription, accession, and succession remain lower-layer jurisprudential machinery; only their common relational and temporal structure scales upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A10","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A14","CH_THN_SIRW:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The convention of stable possession becomes the cluster’s constitutive threshold between physical control and normative property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Imagination bridges institutional necessity and determinate title by converting natural relations of proximity, causality, duration, scale, and kinship into socially stabilized juridical attachments.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The priority of motive over moral judgment bridges the sentimental theory of virtue and the institutional genealogy by requiring pre-moral interest to precede moral obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] The gradation test converts local observations about fluid possession and natural motivation into a general explanation of why juridical decision requires imposed binary closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] Present possession is indispensable at the founding threshold but becomes invalid as a universal title once an established regime supports restitution and differentiated acquisition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Education, reputation, and political reinforcement do not originate justice; they recode an established convention as honor, duty, and second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX15","CH_THN_PIJI:D12","CH_THN_PIJI:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-09","text":"[Local] The solitary state of nature remains an analytical fiction used to isolate passions from understanding; the cluster does not require it as an actual historical stage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX13","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX7","CH_THN_PIJI:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-10","text":"[Peripheral] Consensual transfer and promise-performance mark the broader perimeter of justice, but the current architecture develops possession and property rather than independently reconstructing those laws.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-01","text":"Justice cannot be explained by property or right antecedent to convention because those juridical relations are effects of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX11","CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-02","text":"Justice is not an innate passion, peculiar natural virtue, or direct expression of ordinary impartial motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_SVSF:NX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-03","text":"Property is not an intrinsic, sensible, corporeal, or independently real quality of an object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:NX3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-04","text":"Neither reason, public utility, personal fitness, nor natural association supplies a complete and exact code of particular titles.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX9","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:NX7","CH_THN_SIRW:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-05","text":"Stable justice cannot remain continuously responsive to individual need, merit, character, benevolence, or optimal use.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:NX1","CH_THN_SIRW:NX2","CH_THN_SVSF:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-06","text":"Convention cannot be founded on a prior promise, and authoritative law cannot be treated as the original source of moral intelligibility.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX10","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-07","text":"Universal humanity, private benevolence, and direct concern for the public good cannot generate the original invariant rules of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-08","text":"Isolated consequences cannot determine justice independently of the general rule-system to which an act belongs.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX5","CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_SIRW:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-09","text":"Social necessity does not prove natural implantation, while artificiality does not entail dispensability or arbitrariness.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","CH_THN_SVSF:D1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-10","text":"Neither present possession nor recoverable original title can remain the permanent and exhaustive foundation of ownership.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:NX4","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX3","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the primordial-rights model to be false: no pre-social subject-object bond can obligate others before convention institutes duties.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-02","text":"Self-grounding normativity is inadmissible: morality cannot generate its own first motive, and justice cannot presuppose the rights it constitutes.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","CH_THN_PIJI:SNX4","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-03","text":"A rationalist jurisprudence capable of deducing every title from immutable relations must be false because relational grounds remain graded, conflicting, and psychologically mediated.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX4","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-04","text":"A continuously optimizing distributive model must be excluded because recurrent reassignment by need, merit, or use would dissolve the stability justice exists to produce.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX6","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-05","text":"Legal determinacy cannot be assumed to mirror an equally determinate natural terrain; binary verdicts exceed their gradual justificatory grounds.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-06","text":"Social order cannot require benevolent collective design: coordinated private interests may generate public utility without intending it as a shared end.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-07","text":"Historical priority cannot confer perpetual juridical authority because temporal distance changes the force of relations and permits prescription to displace obscured origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:D6","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-08","text":"The inference from indispensability to innateness must remain invalid; otherwise justice’s functional necessity would erase the distinction between natural passion and artificial strategy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","CH_THN_SVSF:D1","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-01","text":"Justice occupies the absence of a naturally impartial legislative passion capable of converting partial desire directly into universal conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-02","text":"Property occupies the absence of a natural and stable “mine and thine” persisting independently of reciprocal recognition, duty, and law.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX2","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX2","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-03","text":"Municipal decision occupies the absence of a final adjudicative measure beneath convention, imagination, utility, and positive stipulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-04","text":"No seamless middle term connects gradual grounds of entitlement to the integral rights demanded by social coordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the system is a property order that is simultaneously perfectly stable, continuously equitable, naturally exact, and responsive to every particular case.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-06","text":"The pre-conventional individual is denied the status of a complete rights-bearing subject; juridical individuality emerges only within stabilized relations of reciprocal conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-01","text":"The first convention generates property only in general form; the allocation rules then individuate that constituted relation by attaching particular persons to particular goods.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-02","text":"Utility, imagination, and law perform irreducible functions: utility demands settlement, imagination selects relational titles, and law terminates disputes where those relations conflict.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-03","text":"Artificial order is generated by natural faculties without directly expressing natural impulse: reflection redirects interest, imagination formats title, and sympathy subsequently moralizes the result.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-04","text":"Reciprocal expectation solves the regress of institutional origin, while stabilized rules convert that non-promissory coordination into duties that can later support promises and transfer.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D6","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-05","text":"The system can be non-arbitrary at the level of its social function while remaining stipulative at particular boundaries: necessity governs the existence of settlement, not every detail of its form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-06","text":"Title can change without physical alteration because juridical change occurs in socially organized expectations and duties rather than in the object’s sensible properties.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","CH_THN_SVSF:D2","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-07","text":"Prescription demonstrates that time is constitutive rather than merely chronological: it redistributes title by weakening one relation and strengthening another in sentiment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:D6","CH_THN_SVSF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-08","text":"Public utility changes temporal status from an unintended effect of coordinated self-interest into the contemplated object that elicits moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-09","text":"Moral approval stabilizes a determinate title-system whose particular assignments are not independently derivable from utility; sympathy sanctions the beneficial scheme rather than rationally deducing each title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-10","text":"Juridical impartiality is not the expression of an impartial originating motive but the institutional suppression of variable motives through invariant rules.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-11","text":"The grounds of justice divide into genetic interest, systemic utility, imaginative title-formation, authoritative closure, and sentimental sanction; no single ground performs every legitimating function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-01","text":"Normative order has a distributed sequence unavailable to any single mechanism: pre-moral coordination establishes stability, relational rules individuate property, authoritative decisions close residual ambiguity, and sympathy moralizes the completed system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-02","text":"Institutions do not merely regulate antecedent objects; they constitute property as an object of moral judgment and then produce sentiments that appear to recognize its authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-03","text":"Legal universality is manufactured impartiality: it arises from invariant application across persons, not from universal benevolence, metaphysical equality, or naturally impartial motives.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-04","text":"Stable justice requires rigidity at the level of ordinary application but mutability at the level of legal form, since positive rules must settle changing and previously underdetermined relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-05","text":"Local stipulation is contained by global necessity: particular boundary decisions may lack unique natural derivation while the demand for determinate settlement remains non-arbitrary.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-06","text":"Juridical legitimacy is architecturally plural rather than foundationally unitary: origin, function, form, decision, and moral force are assigned to different mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-07","text":"The property regime is path-dependent: founding possession establishes initial distributions, established titles structure later associative extensions, and prescription allows elapsed time to overwrite recoverable origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-08","text":"Agency is neither sovereign moral choice nor passive determination by appetite; it is the capacity of partial agents to reflect, coordinate, internalize general rules, and act through instituted relations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-09","text":"Justice recursively converts natural instability into normative reality: passions generate convention, convention generates rights, rights reorganize conduct, and reorganized conduct reinforces the convention’s affective authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-01","text":"Immediate interest → possessory conflict → reflection on long-range advantage → reciprocal restraint → social stability → enlarged interest in preserving restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-02","text":"Conditional restraint → repeated reciprocal conduct → confidence in others’ restraint → stronger expectations → convention capable of sustaining further compliance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-03","text":"Stable possession → property relation → differentiated titles → determinate expectations → increased stability of possession.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-04","text":"Systemic advantage → sympathetic approval → education and reputation → internalized probity → stronger adherence → more visible systemic advantage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D11","CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-05","text":"Possession escalates from causal power over an object, to stabilized social expectation, to constituted property, to integral juridical right.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-06","text":"Graded relations → conflicting title pressures → municipal decision → binary settlement → new relational baselines from which subsequent titles are generated.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-07","text":"Determinate settlement is the principal attractor: utility, imagination, general rules, and positive law converge not on perfect allocation but on terminated controversy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-08","text":"Artificiality becomes a generative machine rather than a negative classification when convention converts natural conflict into universal rules and those rules become morally internalized.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B01","text":"Human weakness + social dependence + partial affection + scarce transferable goods → instability of possession and threat to cooperation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B02","text":"Motivational circularity + failure of universal benevolence, direct public concern, and natural justice → requirement for a pre-moral artificial genesis.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B03","text":"Immediate self-interest + reflection on long-range systemic advantage → second-order redirection of acquisitive passion.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B04","text":"Reciprocal expression + conditional restraint + repeated experience → non-promissory convention and stabilized expectations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B05","text":"Stable expectations of abstention and restoration → justice and injustice → property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B06","text":"General property relation → unresolved person-good allocation → rejection of case-specific fitness and immediate utility.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B07","text":"Demand for determinate settlement + associative imagination → title through antecedent person-object relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B08","text":"Founding present possession → restriction within established society → occupation, prescription, accession, and succession as differentiated title operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B09","text":"Graded possessory power + temporal weakening + conflicting salience → natural underdetermination → municipal stipulation → binary juridical closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B10","text":"Socially advantageous rule-system → interested observance → sympathetic moral approval → education, reputation, and political reinforcement → recursive institutional stabilization.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS9","CH_THN_PIJI:FGS10","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B01","text":"Partial passion + scarce transferable goods + social dependence → unstable possession and coordination failure.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B02","text":"Coordination failure + motivational circularity → elimination of innate justice and antecedent moral obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D2","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B03","text":"Immediate self-interest → reflective redirection → reciprocal non-promissory convention.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_PIJI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B04","text":"Reciprocal convention → stable possession → constituted property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS7","CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B05","text":"Constituted property → allocation gap → general-rule requirement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B06","text":"General utility + associative imagination → temporally differentiated title forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B07","text":"Gradual and conflicting relations → municipal closure → integral juridical verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B08","text":"Systemic utility → sympathetic approval → internalized duty → recursive reinforcement of the conventionally constituted order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-01","text":"The natural/artificial opposition is redrawn: natural passions and psychological associations generate artificial institutions whose necessity does not make them innate.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME1","CH_THN_SIRW:ME3","CH_THN_SVSF:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-02","text":"Property shifts from an object-quality or pre-social right into a norm-mediated relation constituted by duties, expectations, and socially secured title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME5","CH_THN_SIRW:ME1","CH_THN_SVSF:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-03","text":"Possession and property become distinguishable: causal control supplies raw relational material, while justice converts selected possession into normative ownership.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-04","text":"Utility is decomposed into systemic necessity and moral sanction, while particular title-generation is reassigned primarily to imagination and positive settlement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME6","CH_THN_SIRW:ME2","CH_THN_SVSF:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-05","text":"Self-interest is split into first-order destabilization and second-order institutional strategy, collapsing the opposition between egoistic passion and social order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME4","CH_THN_SVSF:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-06","text":"Impartiality shifts from an assumed motive to an institutional effect of universal and inflexible application.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:D10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-07","text":"Municipal law is reclassified from discovery of pre-existing natural right into authoritative completion of an underdetermined relational field.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:ME6","CH_THN_SVSF:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-08","text":"Time becomes juridically productive: founding moments, duration, obscured origins, succession, and institutional sedimentation alter title without altering objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-09","text":"Moral obligation is separated from natural obligation and legal determination: interest establishes conduct, law determines relations, and sympathy supplies approbation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME9","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-10","text":"Genealogical reading becomes mandatory: established categories such as property, right, duty, and justice cannot be projected backward to explain the processes that constitute them.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit objects are justice, stable possession, property, acquisition, title, adjudication, and the moral approval attached to their observance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT1","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT1","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Partial passions, scarcity, reciprocal expectations, general rules, associative imagination, municipal closure, and sympathetic reinforcement form the operative institutional system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT2","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT2","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Explanation proceeds by reversing presumed dependencies: motives precede moral predicates, justice precedes property, systemic utility precedes approval, and legal decision completes rather than discovers natural determinacy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT3","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality contains no object-inherent property; juridical relations emerge gradually, change through temporal and institutional mediation, and become integral only through rule-governed recognition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT4","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT4","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its thesis through successive elimination, inversion, differentiation, and forced closure: its method reproduces the passage from fluid natural materials to determinate artificial relations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS3","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS11","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-01","text":"Inflexible juridical rules are generated from fluid, context-sensitive, and sometimes contradictory relations, leaving rigidity dependent upon materials that cannot themselves justify rigidity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:MRT7","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-02","text":"Justice is declared non-arbitrary at the systemic level, yet municipal law must stipulate boundaries for which no unique natural or utilitarian solution exists.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-03","text":"Property is constituted by justice, but the genealogy relies on pre-juridical possession; coherence requires strict separation between physical control and morally secured property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-04","text":"General utility necessitates determinate title but underdetermines particular title, creating a gap between the justification of having a property system and the justification of its distributions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-05","text":"Justice is both an offspring of passion and contrary to ordinary passionate conduct; the reconciliation depends on an implicit distinction between first-order impulse and second-order strategy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-06","text":"Integral rights remain in tension with gradual possession, utility, associative force, and ordinary judgments of partial entitlement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-07","text":"The convention is structurally intelligible as reciprocal expectation, but its exact historical emergence, thresholds of confidence, and transition into authoritative law remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-01","text":"Self-love mutates from destabilizing appetite into reflective coordination once its object expands from immediate acquisition to preservation of the social system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-02","text":"Possession mutates from causal power over an object into constant socially secured possession and then into an integral property relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_SVSF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-03","text":"Utility mutates from the cause of institutional necessity into an insufficient title criterion and finally into the object of moral contemplation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-04","text":"Present possession changes valence from a peace-preserving founding rule into a seizure-authorizing principle that must be rejected in established society.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-05","text":"Public good changes from an absent originating intention into an emergent effect and then into the object that generates moral approbation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-06","text":"Artificiality changes from a possible objection to justice into the positive name for passion’s second-order capacity to generate necessary universal order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as a layered conversion machine: unstable passions become reciprocal expectations, expectations become rules, rules become rights, and rights become morally charged habits.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a partial desiring node capable of reflection, association, sympathy, and internalization rather than a pre-social bearer of complete rights or an impartial moral will.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is sedimentary and transformative: repetition creates confidence, duration alters title, institutional age weakens origins, and reinforcement converts artifice into second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SIRW:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-04","text":"Law is a hard binary shell constructed over a graded field of possessory power, utility, salience, proximity, duration, and sentiment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-05","text":"Navigation remains possible through reflective coordination, general-rule revision, and authoritative settlement, but not through escape to natural property, unrestricted equity, or case-by-case passionate judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central machine: social instability, reflected interest, reciprocal convention, constituted property, relational title, legal closure, and subsequent moralization.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT1","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT1","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B02","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the exact historical emergence of convention, the relative causal weight of utility and imagination, and the thresholds governing possession, prescription, and comparative importance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence is preserved only if physical possession, constituted property, interested observance, legal determination, and moral approbation remain distinct explanatory moments.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT2","CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it reverses conceptual priority from property to justice and distinguishes utility’s demand for settlement from imagination’s production of particular title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT5","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on sequencing and recursive return: convention must precede property, title determination must follow general stabilization, and moral sanction must feed back only after social utility becomes visible.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","CH_THN_SVSF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is authoritative boundary production: the system demonstrates its necessity but cannot derive every decision from a common natural measure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the actual movement from motivational and ontological negation through institutional generation and juridical differentiation to practical closure and recursive moral reinforcement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT9","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT8","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 22–24","context":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice · Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property · Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","original_title":"Chapters 22–24","original_context":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice · Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property · Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice"},"original_title":"Chapters 22–24","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_09","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 25–27","order":9,"parents":["CH_THN_SIMA","CH_THN_PIOV","CH_THN_SIGB"],"revision":"CL_THN_09@R001","sha256":"e354135cb352e3e724355158a0bb39337b414a443d1e6c97af02e45a77e8dec0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is to generate stable moral and political authority from partial passions, variable sympathy, limited benevolence, and fallible rulers without invoking reason, original contract, or unconditional command.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS1","CH_THN_PIOV:AS1","CH_THN_SIGB:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-02","text":"The cluster becomes a recursive naturalistic genealogy: interest and affect generate local valuations; social interaction generalizes them; generalized norms then exceed, correct, and sometimes reverse their originating conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS3","CH_THN_SIMA:AS6","CH_THN_PIOV:AS2","CH_THN_PIOV:AS6","CH_THN_SIGB:AS4","CH_THN_SIGB:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-03","text":"Public security, useful or agreeable character, and benevolent relational effects supply the first-order materials from which allegiance, virtue, and merit arise.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-04","text":"Because first-order sentiment varies with position, distance, interest, and fortune, moral stability requires general rules, common standpoints, and socially corrected judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-05","text":"Evaluation therefore shifts from isolated acts and immediate outcomes to durable dispositions, natural tendencies, relational roles, and institutional functions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-06","text":"Generalization gives normativity temporal reach: allegiance may persist after its immediate interest weakens, and virtue may survive accidental failure or attenuated sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-07","text":"Temporal persistence is not absoluteness: recurrent anti-functional conduct establishes a generalized counter-rule that converts anger into blame, cruelty into vice, and destructive government into resistance-authorizing tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_PIOV:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-08","text":"The movement is spiral rather than linear: natural limitation → social stabilization → normative excess → corrective reclassification; closure is conditional, relational, and non-algorithmic.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","CH_THN_SIGB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Normative authority is generated within ordinary human nature: interest, pleasure, uneasiness, and sympathetically apprehended relations furnish its operative materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Human motivation is partial, variable, and passion-bound; neither rulers nor spectators occupy a naturally impartial or morally transformed position.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Sympathy makes the effects of qualities on others available to spectators who possess no direct private interest in those effects.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Shared moral order is produced by generalizing situated responses into stable rules, common standpoints, and publicly usable evaluative language.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The principal evaluative object is a durable tendency or function, not an isolated act, transient feeling, or accidental outcome.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Interest and utility change scope across the cluster: they uniquely sanction political allegiance but constitute only part of the plural basis of moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Artifice does not create ultimate value; it coordinates natural limitations and interests into promises, government, justice, good-breeding, and stable moral discourse.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Generalized judgment acquires relative autonomy from its origin: it can preserve obligation beyond immediate interest and preserve esteem despite weak affection, distance, or bad fortune.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] A legitimate exception must itself be generalizable: repeated anti-functional conduct, not private injury or isolated failure, authorizes normative reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_PIOV:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Corrected judgment, occurrent affection, moral language, and practical motivation remain distinct layers and need not converge.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Offices, capacities, passions, and dispositions acquire moral valence from their direction and relational effects; systematic reversal of function produces reclassification from virtue or authority into vice or enmity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A13","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The cluster converges on a conditionally ordered relational field: character is judged across its roles and government by its protective function, while no single utility calculus governs every moral source.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:A14","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Sympathy retains its function as affective transmission: another’s pleasures and pains become spectator sentiments capable of grounding approval, affection, blame, and hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Interest moves from the cause of convention and exclusive sanction of allegiance to one evaluative relation among usefulness, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, and regard for others.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] The general point of view, common standard, general moral rule, and general opinion perform homologous work: they convert situated responses into socially stable verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Generalization first stabilizes allegiance and moral language, then becomes the mechanism by which recurrent abuses establish an exception to obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-05","text":"[Domain expansion] Natural tendency migrates from the evaluation of personal dispositions under bad fortune to the evaluation of government under repeated historical performance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:A12","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] The natural/artificial distinction ceases to divide sources of moral authority and instead marks different scales and mechanisms of coordinated benefit.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation] Neutral capacities become morally charged through direction: benevolence makes power useful, while ambition or cruelty converts capacity and office into magnified harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-08","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Promise, custom, and imitation disappear as original grounds once they have exposed the need for a prior interest capable of generating and transmitting allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A15","CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_SIMA:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption after diagnostic use] Pride, tenderness, modesty, and heroic elevation remain local test cases, but their higher-order result is retained as evaluative pluralism and the separation of inward merit from social presentation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:D6","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-10","text":"[Mutation] Negative passion moves from constitutionally fitting anger to moral blame and, under public, recurrent, and severe injury, to resistance against tyrannical power.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The regress from promise through convention to interest is specific machinery for dismantling contractual allegiance; its scalable result is the dependence of artificial norms on prior human interests.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D1","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The detailed mechanics of pride, comparison, heroic elevation, and conventional modesty remain a stress test of the four-source moral system rather than a global account of political authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D10","CH_THN_PIOV:D12","CH_THN_PIOV:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Tenderness and qualified anger establish the dual sources and degree-sensitivity of benevolent merit but do not independently determine the limits of allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D6","CH_THN_SIGB:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] Correction for distance, private position, current disposition, and fortune becomes the cluster-wide mechanism for separating stable judgment from immediate affect.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Evaluation by durable tendency scales from personal character to institutional function and supplies the shared grammar of virtue, vice, legitimacy, and tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:SD4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Artificial justice and government are coordinated schemes whose moral standing depends on general benefit rather than the independent value of every isolated act.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] The distinction between originating cause and persisting moral rule forces the transition from simple causal genealogy to a temporal account of normative inertia and correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] Utility monism, raw sympathetic intensity, isolated outcome evaluation, and absolute allegiance are jointly replaced by plural sources, corrected judgment, dispositional appraisal, and conditional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-01","text":"Moral and political authority is not founded by affect-free reason, a prepolitical promise, or arbitrary political imposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX1","CH_THN_SIMA:NX2","CH_THN_PIOV:NX1","CH_THN_PIOV:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-02","text":"Convention, custom, imitation, and education can transmit or regulate norms but cannot supply their original evaluative force.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX8","CH_THN_SIMA:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-03","text":"Neither the spectator’s private interest nor raw sympathetic intensity can function as a stable universal standard.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX8","CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","CH_THN_SIGB:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-04","text":"Isolated action, accidental success, and momentary institutional performance do not exhaust the value of a durable disposition or function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX2","CH_THN_PIOV:NX9","CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-05","text":"Moral generality does not require universal benevolence, equal emotional concern, or complete practical self-sacrifice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX10","CH_THN_SIGB:NX1","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-06","text":"Public utility does not exhaust virtue, even though public interest remains the specific natural sanction of political allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX6","CH_THN_SIGB:NX4","CH_THN_SIMA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-07","text":"Office, power, capacity, naturalness, and immediate agreeableness do not carry self-validating moral worth.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX6","CH_THN_SIMA:NX7","CH_THN_SIGB:NX5","CH_THN_SIGB:NX7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-08","text":"Passive obedience is not absolute, but resistance is not authorized by every private injury; both obedience and opposition remain answerable to generalized public function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX9","CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-01","text":"The cluster cannot admit a self-grounding norm severed from human affect, interest, social practice, and relational consequence; such a norm would make its genealogies causally idle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:DNX1","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-02","text":"Radical affective incommensurability must be false: without transferable passions and sufficiently shared operations, neither sympathy nor common moral language could arise.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX1","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-03","text":"No positional privilege—first-person interest, spectator proximity, or political office—can confer universal evaluative authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX3","CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-04","text":"General rules cannot be either identical with their immediate causes or wholly independent of them: identity would prevent stability, while independence would make correction and resistance impossible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-05","text":"Accidental failure and stable anti-tendency cannot be morally indistinguishable; without their separation, bad fortune would destroy virtue and recurrent tyranny would remain protected as mere mischance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-06","text":"Shared moral order cannot require convergence among verdict, affection, and action; human partiality would otherwise make stable discourse and generalized political judgment unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX5","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6","CH_THN_SIGB:A8","CH_THN_SIGB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-01","text":"The absent center is an external moral tribunal: no view from nowhere, rational relation, natural sovereign title, or transcendent obligation terminates evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX1","CH_THN_SIMA:SNX2","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-02","text":"Universal affection is unavailable; generalized judgment substitutes for a motivational impartiality that ordinary human constitution cannot sustain.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-03","text":"Unmediated access to moral reality is unavailable: character and authority appear only through signs, inferred tendencies, historical patterns, and relational effects.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SNX4","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX5","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-04","text":"The system lacks a common metric capable of calculating conflicts among utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, fitting anger, and the threshold of intolerable tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX3","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-05","text":"An authority permanently immune to historical reversal is ontologically inadmissible: human frailty makes recurrent anti-function unavoidable and preserves the possibility of a counter-rule.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX4","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-01","text":"Corrected standpoint converts resistance from a response to private injury into a judgment that public protection has failed across a generalizable class of cases.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_PIOV:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-02","text":"Dispositional evaluation distinguishes accidental failure from systematic anti-function: bad fortune need not destroy virtue, but recurrent oppression reveals a settled institutional tendency and cancels authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-03","text":"Government is evaluable as an artificial virtue operating through a coordinated scheme: its justification belongs to the general security it produces, not to the independent benefit of every governmental act.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-04","text":"Political power is a morally indeterminate capacity whose valence is fixed by direction: office can coordinate justice, but ambition or cruelty can turn the same capacity into enlarged public harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-05","text":"Sympathy supplies the missing moral link between public utility and disinterested judgment, allowing allegiance to be morally intelligible without an original promise.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D1","CH_THN_PIOV:D2","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-06","text":"A calm judgment that allegiance has ceased may precede effective resistance because generalized verdict, habitual obedience, private affection, and motivating passion operate at different intensities.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD2","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-07","text":"Fitting anger and legitimate resistance share a proportional structure: negative response becomes justified when harm is severe, attributable to durable agency, and no longer confined to isolated resentment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-08","text":"Convention is a secondary coordination technology: custom transmits allegiance, good-breeding regulates pride, and social discourse stabilizes judgment, but none supplies the ultimate end by which it is assessed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-09","text":"Political obligation remains more narrowly functional than virtue: immediate tenderness or agreeableness may confer merit on character, but cannot preserve allegiance after government systematically destroys public security.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a relational-dispositional ontology: persons and institutions are what their durable powers are fitted to produce for themselves and others under socially adequate conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-02","text":"Moral objectivity becomes endogenous without becoming private: shared human constitution, sympathetic transmission, and standpoint correction stabilize judgments that no individual interest can dictate.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-03","text":"Normative time is layered: tendencies persist beneath accidents, rules persist beyond immediate causes, and repeated historical failure eventually changes the classification of the object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-04","text":"Resistance becomes an internal operation of moral judgment rather than an external suspension of order: the same generalized functionality that authorizes allegiance also identifies its terminal failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_PIOV:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-05","text":"Power has no autonomous moral title; its worth is derivative from the passions directing it and the relational field of benefit or injury it organizes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-06","text":"The subject divides into partial agent, sympathetic spectator, corrected judge, and potential political resister; these positions communicate but do not collapse into one another.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-07","text":"The terminal moral order is plural but bounded: utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, and regard for others coexist, while role-destroying cruelty and protection-destroying tyranny mark outer limits.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-01","text":"Partial sympathy → contradictory situated judgments → conversational correction → common standpoint → stable moral language → renewed correction of situated judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-02","text":"Insecurity → invention of government → public protection → allegiance → general-rule inertia → rulerly abuse → historical generalization → resistance exception → reaffirmation of protection as the criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS3","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS5","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-03","text":"Conduct or expression → inference to character → sympathetic pleasure or pain → approval or blame → love, anger, and social response → new conduct and expression available for judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:FGS1","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS10","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-04","text":"Natural limitation → artificial coordination → stabilized conduct → acquired moral force → functional reassessment of the coordinating institution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-05","text":"Immediate effect → correction for distance or fortune → evaluation of natural tendency → preservation under accidental failure or reversal under recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-06","text":"Uneasiness → anger or blame → attribution to durable harmful character → recurrence and public generalization → phase change from private resentment to legitimate resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-07","text":"The dominant attractor is role-sustaining relational function: dispersed analyses of benevolence, character, justice, government, cruelty, and tyranny converge on whether a disposition makes shared relations supportable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B01","text":"Human constraints: limited generosity + variable sympathy + insecurity + persistent rulerly passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B02","text":"Relational encounter: conduct, passion, or institution produces advantage, harm, pleasure, uneasiness, protection, or exposure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B03","text":"Transmission: direct interest and sympathy convert relational effects into allegiance, approval, affection, blame, or anger.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B04","text":"Destabilization: distance, private interest, fortune, and divergent position generate variable or contradictory verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B05","text":"Generalization: common standpoint + general rule + social discourse correct local variation and stabilize evaluative language.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B06","text":"Object construction: isolated event → inferred durable disposition; individual benefit → coordinated scheme; office-holder → protective or destructive institutional function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B07","text":"Temporal test: accidental failure preserves standing through natural tendency; recurrent anti-function generates vice, tyranny, and a generalized exception.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B08","text":"Terminal output: plural virtue + role-complete relational judgment + conditional allegiance + morally internal resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B01","text":"Partial affection and public insecurity → relational effects → interest and sympathetic uptake → conflict among situated responses → common standpoint and general rules → dispositional-functional evaluation → normative stabilization → recurrent-failure test → conditional moral-political order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","CH_THN_SIGB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B02","text":"Immediate sentiment → correction for distance and fortune → calm judgment → stable moral language, while practical passion remains only partially transformed.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS8","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B03","text":"Sustained beneficial tendency → virtue or authority; accidental obstruction → preserved standing; recurrent relational harm → vice or tyranny → anger, blame, or resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS2","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-01","text":"Morality and politics become continuous in generative grammar without becoming identical in criterion: both arise from human effects and generalized judgment, but allegiance remains specifically tied to public protection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME1","CH_THN_PIOV:ME4","CH_THN_SIGB:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-02","text":"The moral object shifts from event to architecture: durable character, relational disposition, coordinated scheme, and institutional function replace isolated action as the principal unit of evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME1","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-03","text":"Impartiality is reclassified from affective neutrality to socially corrected judgment compatible with unequal concern and limited motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME5","CH_THN_SIGB:ME2","CH_THN_SIGB:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-04","text":"The natural/artificial opposition collapses as a division of moral sources and survives only as a distinction between immediate relational benefit and coordinated systemic benefit.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME6","CH_THN_PIOV:ME2","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-05","text":"Obedience and resistance cease to be moral opposites: resistance becomes the generalized corrective form taken by allegiance when government reverses its constitutive function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","CH_THN_SIMA:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-06","text":"Power and ability are reclassified as directed capacities rather than intrinsic merits; benevolence, ambition, cruelty, and institutional role determine their social valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-07","text":"Time becomes evaluatively constitutive: tendency corrects fortune, habit extends obligation, and recurrent history supplies evidence for terminal reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME5","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-08","text":"Adequate reading now requires separation of cause, standard, verdict, motive, and presentation; treating them as one level would erase the system’s principal corrections and tensions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the cluster classifies benevolence, pride, anger, cruelty, political allegiance, tyranny, and resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT1","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT1","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: sympathy, interest, general rules, corrected standpoints, dispositional inference, and artificial coordination produce stable moral and political judgments.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT2","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT2","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: struggle is reframed as classification of tendencies and functions; intervention becomes legitimate when private grievance is converted into a general judgment of recurrent relational failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT3","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT3","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality consists of durable yet fallible affective agents and institutions whose standing persists through accidents but mutates under accumulated evidence of anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT4","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT4","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: genealogical regress, objection-correction, analogical transfer, and stress cases perform the movement from situated response to generalized architecture.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS7","CH_THN_PIOV:AS4","CH_THN_PIOV:AS7","CH_THN_SIGB:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-01","text":"Public interest is presented as the exclusive original sanction of allegiance, while virtue has four sources and benevolence two; the cluster supplies no rule determining how far moral pluralism enters political evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-02","text":"General moral opinion is declared infallible in the political argument, but elsewhere the common standard is a constructed correction whose mechanism and unique content remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-03","text":"Natural tendency protects character from accidental outcomes, whereas allegiance is canceled by destructive performance; no exact criterion separates temporary failure from evidence of settled anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-04","text":"Calm verdict can diverge from actual affection and motivation, yet resistance requires practical mobilization; the passage from moral authorization to effective political action remains unspecified.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-05","text":"Utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, heroic elevation, tenderness, and fitting anger lack a common weighting rule, leaving conflicts among approved sources irreducibly qualitative.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-06","text":"Goodness is judged within a limited relational sphere, while allegiance appeals to public interest and general opinion; the transition from connected persons to the relevant political public remains compressed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-07","text":"Moral rules must exceed their original causes to secure constancy but remain dependent on those causes to permit correction; the architecture requires this unresolved relative autonomy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-01","text":"Interest mutates from causal origin of convention, to sanction of allegiance, to one sympathetically mediated source of moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-02","text":"General rule mutates from stabilizer of obedience into generator of the exception that terminates obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-03","text":"Character mutates from an inferred personal disposition into a model for institutional identity, permitting the magistrate–tyrant distinction to be read as functional reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-04","text":"Anger mutates from an inherent, degree-sensitive passion into blame and finally into a generalized political response to recurrent public harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-05","text":"Artifice mutates from a suspected source of arbitrary moral invention into the necessary coordination layer through which natural interests and sentiments acquire stable social form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of affective relays and functional arrangements: passions transmit, standpoints correct, conventions stabilize, and recurrent effects reclassify the nodes that produced them.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither sovereign reason nor passive appetite but a divided agent-spectator whose local passions can be generalized in judgment without being extinguished in action.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is a layered filter: habit preserves rules, counterfactual tendency survives accidents, and accumulated history converts repeated failure into a phase change of moral status.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-04","text":"Struggle remains possible as functional diagnosis and generalized correction: one navigates between private resentment and passive obedience by identifying durable, public, and recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster is reconstructible from six indispensable components: affective-interest genealogy, sympathy, corrected standpoint, dispositional tendency, artificial coordination, and generalized exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT1","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT1","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined at three points: the content of the common standard, the weighting of plural moral sources, and the threshold separating misgovernment from resistance-authorizing tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent only if cause and moral rule, immediate affection and corrected judgment, accidental outcome and durable tendency, and private injury and public failure remain distinct.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","CH_THN_SIMA:MRT7","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where one generalizing mechanism explains both normative stability and corrective reversal, and where one dispositional grammar evaluates both character and government.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT2","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT3","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing rather than source order: local sentiment must generate social correction, correction must produce temporal excess, and excess must encounter recurrent failure before conditional closure appears.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT8","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT8","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B06","text":"No indispensable structural component resists reduction, but the exact phenomenological weighting of pride, tenderness, anger, common opinion, and intolerable oppression remains irreducibly qualitative.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","CH_THN_SIMA:MRT6","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT8","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 25–27","context":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance · Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices · Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","original_title":"Chapters 25–27","original_context":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance · Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices · Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence"},"original_title":"Chapters 25–27","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 1","order":1,"parents":["CL_THN_01","CL_THN_02","CL_THN_03"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_01@R001","sha256":"ce989659be4ac9ad1897ddfe90147fabed53e9ef0d89ac9ae7a1a81cc9654342","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-01","text":"The governing problem is how a science confined to perceptual materials can explain mental productivity, ordered experience, factual assent, and practical reality without appealing to inaccessible essence or independently given forms.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BAS-01","CL_THN_02:BAS-01","CL_THN_03:ASP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis is anti-additive: simple content derives from impressions, while order, space, time, existence, and belief contribute no autonomous elemental content beyond the perceptions they organize or intensify.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-03","text":"The architecture differentiates three irreducible explanatory dimensions: provenance determines available content, relation and order determine configuration, and force determines cognitive and practical efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-04","text":"Sensation asymmetrically grounds ideas, but ideas return as productive relays within reflection, memory, imagination, association, belief, passion, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_01:GA-08","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-05","text":"Mental organization proceeds through preservation, release, recombination, associative weighting, and spatiotemporal arrangement rather than through either passive copying or sovereign creation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BAS-04","CL_THN_02:BAS-05","CL_THN_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-06","text":"Because existence adds no representational content, actuality is reconstructed as regulated vivacity transferred from present impressions through customary relations to absent or future ideas.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-07","text":"The subject consequently becomes a relay and regulator within a distributed perception-machine, while reality becomes operationally available through stable effects rather than intrinsic representation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:HSI-03","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-08","text":"Anomalies and counterfeits do not terminate the machine; they recursively force universal claims to mutate into qualified regularities governed by provenance, steadiness, scope, and correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-05","CL_THN_02:FL-05","CL_THN_03:RMD-07","CL_THN_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-09","text":"The movement is spiral: empirical restriction → perceptual decomposition → recursive mediation → relational organization → ontological de-addition → intensive actualization → second-order correction → bounded closure at unknown origins and unrepresented reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BAS-08","CL_THN_02:BAS-07","CL_THN_03:ASP-05","CL_THN_03:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] A science of human nature must reconstruct the perceptual medium through which evidence, inference, belief, and every other science operate.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-01","CL_THN_01:GA-12","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate inquiry proceeds from experienced perceptions, operations, relations, and effects rather than from speculative access to mental or external essence.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-02","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Simple ideational content is asymmetrically dependent on antecedent impression-material, even when later ideas become causally productive.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Provenance, arrangement, and force constitute distinct explanatory coordinates: what an idea contains, how it is ordered, and how efficaciously it is conceived cannot be reduced to one another.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The idea is first a derivative copy, then a recombinable component, an associative node, and finally a force-bearing mediator of belief, passion, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-07","CL_THN_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Mental production is asymmetrically recursive: sensory grounding remains one-way, while downstream perceptions repeatedly exchange the roles of effect, antecedent, and regulator.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-08","CL_THN_01:BSD-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Novelty is relational and intensive rather than elemental: new complexes, abstractions, expectations, and reality-effects arise without unprecedented simple content.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-04","CL_THN_02:XD-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Order changes from a preserved feature of experience into a manipulable arrangement, an associative pathway, and the constitutive form of spatial and temporal appearance.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-03","CL_THN_02:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Stable mental regularity is generated by associative tendency and custom, which constrain transitions without imposing demonstrative necessity or abolishing imaginative variation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-08","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Space and time are content-bearing orders of coexistence and succession, not independent impressions or empty containers.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-14","CL_THN_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Existence contributes no additional represented quality; factuality must therefore be generated through a difference in the manner and force of conception.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03","CL_THN_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity develop from taxonomic marks into transmissible causal quantities, then into regulated quantities whose epistemic status depends on pedigree and steadiness.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:GA-04","CL_THN_03:GA-08","CL_THN_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] A present impression anchors inference, supplies transferable vivacity, and gives absent or future objects current motivational efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:GA-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Practical reality is stable, regulated presence within the perception-system: an idea becomes real for the mind insofar as it persistently governs inference, passion, expectation, and conduct.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-10","CL_THN_03:BSD-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-15","text":"[Emergent] Epistemic legitimacy is genealogical and second-order: it requires admissible content, permissible relational application, and a force-producing history capable of surviving correction.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-12","CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:GA-11","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-16","text":"[Terminal] Explanation terminates at maximally general experienced relations while sensory origins, associative attraction, external actuality, and the final authority of corrective rules remain undisclosed.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:BSA-01","CL_THN_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Experience remains the admissible field from the initial science of human nature through compositional analysis and the genealogy of factual assent.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-01","CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Observation mutates into perceptual classification, source-audit, compositional decomposition, tracking of associative effects, and diagnosis of force-producing pedigrees.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] The copy principle moves from a claim about simple ideas to a general constraint on imagination, abstraction, spatiotemporal predication, and existential representation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_02:RMD-01","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] The idea changes from faint effect into reflective cause, recombinable component, associated transition-node, and practically efficacious object of belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-05","CL_THN_02:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] Order changes from a standard of mnemonic fidelity into the common machinery of recombination, association, extension, duration, and customary expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"[Domain migration] Association moves from a gentle relation among freely recombinable ideas into the customary mechanism that selects factual alternatives and transmits vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation and reversal] Vivacity progresses from perception-classification and mnemonic discrimination to belief-production, then reverses when poetry, passion, and madness show that intensity cannot warrant itself.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Existence disappears as an independent content-question after its non-addition becomes the constraint forcing factual difference into modal and causal explanation.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RMD-01","CL_THN_03:RMD-02","CL_THN_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] Separability first authorizes imaginative liberty, then becomes a negative test excluding empty space, bare time, and other structures detached from every positive content.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-03","CL_THN_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"[Escalation] Causation migrates from temporal dependence and ideational association to productive capacity, social authority, force transmission, and the regulation of belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:RMD-07","CL_THN_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-11","text":"[Terminal transformation] Ignorance changes from a disciplinary deficiency into a constitutive boundary permitting operational explanation without essential, causal, or metaphysical completion.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-08","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-12","text":"[Disappearance through absorption] Separate faculty names lose final explanatory status as memory, imagination, judgment, passion, and volition become differentiated operations within one distributed perception-system.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:RMD-09","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The initial diagnosis of philosophical controversy motivates the metascientific project but does not govern the later machinery of order, association, and belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-01","CL_THN_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The missing shade remains an exception to strict copy-priority rather than an alternative source of the current-level architecture.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-03","CL_THN_01:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Exact mnemonic vivacity, the sensory source of extension, and content-filled minima remain local criteria whose broader function is only to constrain unconstrained separation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:CGD-01","CL_THN_02:CGD-02","CL_THN_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"[Local] The exhaustiveness of the three associative relations and the extension of causation into social subordination are domain-specific claims, not universal axioms of the whole meta-cluster.","refs":["CL_THN_02:CGD-03","CL_THN_02:CGD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] The prohibition on essence-first explanation becomes the common boundary governing perceptions, faculties, space, time, existence, belief, and reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-04","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"[Scales upward] The copy principle becomes a general genealogy of admissible content, while the distinction between content and operation prevents genealogy from collapsing into passive replay.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-05","CL_THN_02:CGD-04","CL_THN_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Ideas connect sensory derivation to reflection, memory, recombination, association, factual belief, and practical action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-06","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"[Bridge] Association and custom connect relational order to intensive actuality by converting recurrent transitions into channels of transferred vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:GA-05","CL_THN_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-09","text":"[Catalyst] Poetry, eloquence, passion, and madness are local cases that scale only as stress tests forcing belief to mutate from raw vividness into regulated intensity.","refs":["CL_THN_03:CGD-02","CL_THN_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-10","text":"[Superseded and recoded] Linear copy chronology, passive receptivity, sealed faculties, empty forms, and intrinsic actuality-markers are replaced by asymmetrical recursion, relational organization, and second-order genealogy.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-08","CL_THN_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Mental science is not deduction from a known substance, essence, or standpoint external to the faculties under investigation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-01","CL_THN_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"No operation of memory, imagination, abstraction, association, or belief can generally originate unprecedented simple content.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-02","CL_THN_02:GNX-01","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Copy-dependence entails neither passive replay nor preservation of every original conjunction, order, intensity, or practical effect.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:GNX-02","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Relations, order, space, and time are not independent substances or additional simple impressions superadded to their terms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GNX-04","CL_THN_02:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Existence and belief are not predicates or representational components attached to an otherwise incomplete object-idea.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-01","CL_THN_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Mental regularity is neither demonstrative necessity nor random succession; factual assent likewise cannot be generated by conceptual contradiction alone.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GNX-03","CL_THN_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"No intrinsic semantic feature distinguishes an actual object from the same object merely conceived, and no positive representation discloses extra-perceptual being as such.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-02","CL_THN_03:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-08","text":"Felt intensity, imaginative completeness, repetition, or rhetorical force cannot independently authenticate factual conviction.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-09","text":"The subject is neither a sovereign creator nor an autonomous judge insulated from custom, association, affect, bodily condition, and recursive correction.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-10","text":"Scientific adequacy does not require access to ultimate sensory causes, the metaphysical basis of association, or the intrinsic nature of external actuality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-07","CL_THN_02:GNX-08","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which cognition can ground itself from outside perceptual mediation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-01","CL_THN_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"It excludes first-cause foundationalism: knowledge of stable derivative relations cannot depend on prior disclosure of their ultimate origin.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-02","CL_THN_02:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"It rejects both the passive receptacle and sovereign producer, requiring a mind that transforms inherited materials without becoming their absolute source.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"It disqualifies independent forms and empty frameworks: no positive order, space, time, relation, or generality survives the removal of all content-bearing terms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:DBN-04","CL_THN_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"It excludes direct representational realism and any intrinsic actuality-sign capable of transporting thought beyond perceptual content into the essence of external being.","refs":["CL_THN_03:DBN-01","CL_THN_03:DBN-02","CL_THN_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"It excludes rationalist factuality: conceivability, contradiction, and necessary connection cannot select among contrary matters of fact.","refs":["CL_THN_02:DBN-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-07","text":"It cannot identify causal genesis with epistemic warrant, because the same associative and intensive mechanisms can produce ordinary belief, education, persuasion, fiction, and pathology.","refs":["CL_THN_03:XD-06","CL_THN_03:XD-10","CL_THN_03:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-08","text":"It cannot admit an independent tribunal of correction without violating perceptual closure, yet it cannot abandon correction without collapsing factual belief into successful vivification.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-11","CL_THN_03:RLE-07","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"The first governing absence is absolute sensory origin: sensation grounds the entire machine while the cause of its first appearance remains outside it.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSA-01","CL_THN_01:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"The second governing absence is pure order without ordered content: relation, empty space, bare time, and wholly indeterminate generality are inadmissible.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-01","CL_THN_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"The third governing absence is a necessary mental bond: association and custom occupy its functional place while remaining defeasible tendencies.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-03","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"The deepest ontological absence is positively knowable mind-independent actuality; reality persists as correlate, anchor, and corrective term without becoming represented essence.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-01","CL_THN_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"The absent subject is a sovereign spontaneous origin capable of creating content, governing transitions, and certifying its own beliefs without inherited impressions or natural tendencies.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSA-05","CL_THN_02:BSA-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-06","text":"The terminal absence is a noncircular authority for general rules: correction must regulate force from within the same customary perception-system whose products it evaluates.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-04","CL_THN_03:FL-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Restriction of primitive content produces rather than suppresses mental productivity: impression-derived elements become recursively recombinable, associatively routable, and intensively variable.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-05","CL_THN_02:BSD-04","CL_THN_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-02","text":"The full architecture requires a three-axis audit: genealogy tests content, relational analysis tests configuration and application, and force-pedigree tests factual assent.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Space, time, existence, and belief obey one de-additive rule: apparent higher-order structures are reconstructed as arrangements or modes of perception rather than additional ingredients.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GNX-04","CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Ideas are the machine’s central switching elements because they preserve sensory derivation while serving as causes of reflection, units of recombination, nodes of association, and bearers of practical reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Association becomes the bridge between formal order and factuality: recurrent relations first stabilize imaginative transitions and then channel vivacity from present impressions into belief.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Time has a dual generated structure: succession produces duration, while present impressions project force across succession so that remembered, absent, and future objects act now.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:HSI-04","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Fiction divides along two independent axes: a complex may be compositionally legitimate because its materials are admissible yet doxastically counterfeit because its vivacity lacks regulated factual pedigree.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-09","CL_THN_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Epistemic reading must become recursively genealogical, tracing not only where an idea’s content originated but also how its arrangement and degree of conviction were produced.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:MME-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-09","text":"The system yields practical realism without positive metaphysical realism: perceptually generated ideas can organize conduct as realities despite lacking an intrinsic representation of independent actuality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:BSD-08","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-10","text":"Power operates through two linked channels: productive capacity associates authority and obedience, while rhetoric, repetition, and affect modulate the vivacity by which such relations become practically compelling.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-11","text":"The system’s principal operator is also its principal debt: causation establishes perceptual dependence, associative priority, social power, force transfer, and corrective pedigree before its own nature is secured.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:FL-07","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-12","text":"Because corrective rules arise within the same associative field they regulate, epistemic security can only be reflexive, comparative, and qualified rather than absolutely foundational.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-02","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"The meta-cluster produces an anti-additive ontology: apparent mental and formal entities are reconstructed as sourced contents, relational configurations, or intensive modes.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-01","CL_THN_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Mind becomes a distributed transformation machine rather than a substance or faculty hierarchy: it receives, copies, preserves, separates, routes, intensifies, and regulates perceptions.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-02","CL_THN_02:HSI-02","CL_THN_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Actuality becomes a system-effect generated when admissible content is placed within a stable associative route and supplied with regulated practical force.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:GA-10","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"The subject is decentered twice: it is neither the source of primitive content nor the sovereign author of the order and force by which its ideas govern it.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Time is neither an empty continuum nor simple chronology; it is generated succession recursively reorganized by memory, expectation, custom, and present force-transfer.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-04","CL_THN_02:BSD-03","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Agency becomes navigational: freedom consists in recombination, selective attention, rerouting, and reflective correction within constraints imposed by content, association, and affect.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-04","CL_THN_02:HSI-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Scientific foundation is converted into recursive self-audit: secure inquiry tracks sources, relations, force-transfers, anomalies, and corrections while refusing claims to absolute origin.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-01","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Political and rhetorical vulnerability is structural rather than accidental because the same relational and intensive machinery that enables authority, expectation, and action also permits capture by repetition, eloquence, and passion.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-09","text":"The machine generates qualified naturalism: explanatory closure belongs to recurrent operations and effects, while metaphysical closure is systematically withheld.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Total production loop: sensation → idea → reflection → memory or imagination → associative routing → belief → passion and action → renewed vivification or reflective correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Restriction–proliferation loop: simple content is constrained → complexes are recombined → relations stabilize transitions → force actualizes selected complexes → new reflective effects return to the system.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-03","CL_THN_02:FGS-B06","CL_THN_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Genealogical audit loop: impression-source → permissible operation → application test → force-pedigree → correction → renewed audit of the resulting perception.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Order loop: experienced arrangement → mnemonic conservation → imaginative release → associative regeneration → spatiotemporal constitution → customary expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Actualization loop: present impression → customary transition → transferred vivacity → factual belief → practical efficacy → passion → amplified object-idea.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Counterfeit-correction loop: nonstandard vivification → belief-like force → error or capture → source-reflection → general-rule discounting → graded restoration of conviction.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Boundary loop: inquiry reaches inaccessible origin or reality → withdraws to observable effects → formulates a qualified regularity → encounters anomaly → restricts scope without abandoning the machine.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-04","CL_THN_01:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"Attractor convergence: ideas organize recursive production, order organizes relational form, regulated vivacity organizes actuality, and inaccessible reality remains the recessed term around which correction circulates.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-06","CL_THN_02:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-08","CL_THN_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Scientific dependence on human faculties → human nature installed as the metascientific field.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B01","CL_THN_01:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Unavailable essence and ultimate origin → experienced perceptions, operations, relations, and effects become the admissible evidence.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B03","CL_THN_01:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Perceptual decomposition → impressions/ideas + simple/complex → isolable units of provenance and operation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B04","CL_THN_01:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Sensory grounding + ideational return → asymmetrically recursive perception-chain.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B06","CL_THN_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Recurrence differentiated by force and fidelity → memory preserves experienced order while imagination releases components for recombination.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B02","CL_THN_02:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Free recombination + recurrent regularity → resemblance, contiguity, and causation weight transitions without necessitating them.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B04","CL_THN_02:FGS-B05","CL_THN_02:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Analysis of organized content → coexistence and succession reconstructed as extension and duration rather than empty forms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B07","CL_THN_02:FGS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Existential non-addition + content invariance → factual difference relocated from representation to manner of conception.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B01","CL_THN_03:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B09","text":"Present impression + customary relation → transferred vivacity → steady intensified idea → belief → passion and action.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B03","CL_THN_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B10","text":"Affective amplification or counterfeit vivification → source-sensitive reflection and general rules → qualified correction without metaphysical closure.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B05","CL_THN_03:FGS-B06","CL_THN_03:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Foundational disorder → human faculties identified as the common medium of scientific judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Human mediation → empirical restriction → mental life decomposed into perceptions and genealogical dependencies.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B02","CL_THN_01:AFM-B03","CL_THN_01:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Linear copy genealogy → division of impressions → ideas become recursively productive mediators.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B05","CL_THN_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Productive ideas → memory/imagination bifurcation → separation and recombination of inherited contents.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B02","CL_THN_02:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Free recombination → associative regulation → stable but defeasible relational pathways.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B04","CL_THN_02:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Relational analysis → space and time reconstructed as content-bearing coexistence and succession.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B06","CL_THN_02:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B07","text":"Existential non-addition → belief-surplus problem → customary force-transfer → practical actualization.","refs":["CL_THN_03:AFM-B01","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B08","text":"Counterfeit vivacity → second-order genealogy → regulated conviction → unresolved return to unrepresented reality.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Foundation is reclassified from possession of first causes into disciplined reconstruction of perceptual provenance, organization, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-02","CL_THN_02:MME-08","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Mind is remapped from substance and faculty hierarchy into a recursive field of perceptions differentiated by source, order, transition, and intensity.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-03","CL_THN_02:MME-01","CL_THN_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Content, relation, and force become separable analytic dimensions, allowing identical materials to support memory, fiction, abstraction, belief, or practical action.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-02","CL_THN_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between receptivity and productivity collapses into asymmetric recursion, while the opposition between freedom and determination collapses into bounded, tendency-weighted navigation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-04","CL_THN_01:MME-05","CL_THN_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Space, time, existence, and belief are removed from the category of independent additions and redistributed among arrangement, succession, manner, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-05","CL_THN_03:MME-01","CL_THN_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Fact and fiction become neighboring configurations differentiated not by elemental content but by composition, associative route, steadiness, provenance, and practical efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-06","CL_THN_03:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Causation becomes a cross-domain operator spanning perceptual genealogy, associative transition, productive capacity, social authority, belief-generation, and affective feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:MME-07","CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Reading becomes operational and second-order: apparent entities must be decomposed into sources, configurations, force-transfers, exclusions, and corrective histories.","refs":["CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit field comprises human nature, impressions, ideas, memory, imagination, association, space, time, existence, belief, passion, fiction, and factual judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B01","CL_THN_02:ALT-B01","CL_THN_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Copying, recurrence, separation, recombination, associative routing, ordered coexistence and succession, force-transfer, affective feedback, and correction form one distributed mechanism.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B02","CL_THN_02:ALT-B02","CL_THN_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Metaphysical disputes are converted into audits of perceptual origin, relational operation, permissible application, causal pedigree, and susceptibility to correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B03","CL_THN_02:ALT-B03","CL_THN_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality available to thought consists of content-bearing events and configurations; time is generated succession recursively reorganized by memory, custom, expectation, and present practical force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B04","CL_THN_02:ALT-B04","CL_THN_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its claims by decomposing apparent unities, denying unsupported additions, reconstructing operational relations, testing them against anomalies, and replacing universality with qualified recursive regulation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B05","CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","CL_THN_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Causal succession, associative efficacy, force-transfer, and causal pedigree do indispensable explanatory work before causation itself has received a secured analysis.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-02","CL_THN_02:FL-07","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-02","text":"The copy principle strains against the missing shade, abstraction’s selective suppression of detail, and the use of separability to deny forms that cannot be detached from every content-bearing instance.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-03","CL_THN_02:FL-01","CL_THN_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The declared observational science of ordinary conduct remains incompletely integrated with introspective taxonomy, deprivation tests, and phenomenological judgments of force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-01","CL_THN_02:CGD-01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Association is both an original tendency and a customary acquisition, leaving unresolved how innate relational susceptibility and learned pathways divide explanatory labor.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FL-06","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Perceptual closure denies positive access to external actuality, yet sensory causation, reality-connection, and correction continue to invoke it as an operative constraint.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-04","CL_THN_03:FL-02","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Force and vivacity classify impressions, memory, imagination, belief, passion, and counterfeit conviction without a stable metric or exact threshold across these functions.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FL-04","CL_THN_03:FL-03","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-07","text":"Custom generates ordinary factual assent, but education, repetition, rhetoric, and pathology can use the same mechanism to generate invalid assent; causal genesis and warrant remain only partially separated.","refs":["CL_THN_03:XD-10","CL_THN_03:FL-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-08","text":"General rules correct first-order force but lack an independently specified origin and authority, risking circular dependence on the beliefs and customs they are meant to regulate.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-04","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-09","text":"The science intended to secure all inquiry remains reflexive because it uses perception, comparison, causal inference, custom, and correction to establish the authority of those same operations.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Idea mutates from derivative copy into the common relay connecting sensation, reflection, recombination, order, association, belief, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MP-02","CL_THN_02:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Order mutates from retained arrangement into a productive relation, spatiotemporal form, customary route, and condition of expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MP-01","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Association mutates from a nonnecessary bond among ideas into the causal channel through which present experience selects factual alternatives and transfers vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_03:RMD-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Force mutates from a mark of perception-type into a transmissible psychological currency and then into a regulated quantity whose source matters more than its raw magnitude.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:MP-02","CL_THN_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Existence mutates from an explicit ontological object into a zero-addition rule, while reality mutates into a causal and corrective relation governing belief.","refs":["CL_THN_03:MP-01","CL_THN_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Fiction mutates from lawful recombination into genealogical or doxastic excess when either its application outruns perceptual order or its vivacity imitates factual authority.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MP-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as an asymmetrically grounded network of content-bearing events whose copied elements circulate through recursive circuits of order, association, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-01","CL_THN_02:HSI-01","CL_THN_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The mind is an archive-workshop-routing system: it preserves sequences, cuts and recombines components, weights pathways, transfers intensity, and applies corrective feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_02:HSI-02","CL_THN_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"The subject is a switching relay and regulatory threshold rather than an originating substance; natural tendencies, habits, passions, bodily states, and reflection compete to direct its ideas.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-02","CL_THN_02:HSI-03","CL_THN_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Time is a pulse of content-bearing succession folded by memory and expectation, with present impressions drawing absent and future objects into current causal efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_02:HSI-04","CL_THN_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"Navigation consists in tracing provenance, decomposing arrangements, following associative routes, measuring force by pedigree, correcting counterfeits, and stopping where perceptual relations no longer support explanation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-04","CL_THN_02:HSI-05","CL_THN_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the engine if provenance, relational order, and intensive force remain distinct but recursively connected dimensions.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B01","CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The strongest architecture is the conversion of restricted perceptual content into relationally productive and practically efficacious ideas without granting autonomous elemental creation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B04","CL_THN_02:MRT-B02","CL_THN_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at the operational level provided genetic priority, relational configuration, intensive manner, causal genesis, and epistemic warrant are not collapsed into one order.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B03","CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the mechanisms of ideational return, associative attraction, vivacity transfer, exact thresholds of belief, and the authority of corrective rules.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B02","CL_THN_02:MRT-B03","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02","CL_THN_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only through sequencing and recursive return: sensory grounding must precede recombination, recombination must generate association, and existential non-addition must precede intensive belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B05","CL_THN_02:MRT-B04","CL_THN_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residues resistant to axiomatic reduction are sensory origin, the lived quality of force, mind-independent actuality, causal necessity, and the noncircular basis of correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B06","CL_THN_02:MRT-B05","CL_THN_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B07","text":"The minimal reconstruction succeeds as a bounded naturalistic machine, not as metaphysical closure: it explains how perceptions generate order and reality-effects while preserving the absences that make such explanation necessary.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B07","CL_THN_02:MRT-B06","CL_THN_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Metacluster 1","context":"Chapters 01–09 · Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject · Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time · Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_title":"Metacluster 1","original_context":"Chapters 01–09 · Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject · Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time · Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief"},"original_title":"Metacluster 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 2","order":2,"parents":["CL_THN_04","CL_THN_05","CL_THN_06"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_02@R001","sha256":"aa5da733b687a1022c82028bf6ea022e40c74e9d6d1eb59fa14aeb787af6561b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how the same natural mechanisms can generate warranted causal judgment, metaphysical fiction, operational selfhood, affective valuation, and social hierarchy without access to intrinsic powers or an external rational tribunal.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B01","CL_THN_05:AS-B01","CL_THN_06:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B02","text":"The governing thesis is that relation-conditioned transitions become effective through force, vivacity, repetition, and retention; their outputs depend on how comparison, custom, circumstance, and social topology route them.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B02","CL_THN_05:AS-B05","CL_THN_06:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a recursive naturalistic reconstruction: substantial bearers, intrinsic powers, autonomous faculties, and primitive passions are displaced by networks of perceptions, affects, transitions, gates, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B03","CL_THN_05:AS-B02","CL_THN_06:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B04","text":"The directional movement is foundational subtraction → operational transition → productive overextension → anomaly or contradiction → restrictive differentiation → socially recursive application.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B04","CL_THN_05:AS-B04","CL_THN_06:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B05","text":"Comparison becomes the principal branching operator: controlled difference isolates causal circumstances, while retained contrast converts another’s condition into an altered estimate of the self.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B06","text":"The social-affective field is not an external application of the cognitive system; it exposes how vivacity, authority, custom, and relation recursively produce the objects, values, and judgments they appear merely to register.","refs":["CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B07","text":"The subject accordingly mutates from a rejected substantial bearer into a temporally variable relay, represented object, comparator, and feedback-node within cognitive and social processes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:BSD-02","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B08","text":"Closure is operational and recursively skeptical: inquiry preserves tested mechanisms while denying final decomposition, intrinsic value, affective neutrality, and any standpoint outside the operations under examination.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B08","CL_THN_05:AS-B08","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate explanation begins from perceptions, affects, experienced conjunctions, and observable transformations rather than from unknown substances or intrinsic productive powers.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-02","CL_THN_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relation is causally effective only through the transitions it facilitates, preserves, interrupts, or redirects; it is not an inert predicate or an intrinsically binding nexus.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity migrate from determinants of conviction into the general currency by which cognitive, affective, and social representations become operative.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Repetition, habit, and custom stabilize transitions without converting psychological regularity, practical necessity, or social circulation into truth.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-04","CL_THN_05:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Imagination is the indispensable medium of synthesis and the endogenous source of fictive completion, overextension, constructed sentiment, and self-confirming error.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:GA-03","CL_THN_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Comparison changes from controlled experimental differentiation into affective opposition, retaining the common operation of preserving difference while reversing its epistemic function.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Causation expands from regular conjunction among discriminated circumstances into gated conversion and recursive feedback in which effects alter subsequent causes, judgments, and relations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-08","CL_THN_05:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Anomalies and contradictions reveal unrestricted principles by their excesses and force their conversion into range-bound, threshold-dependent mechanisms.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-10","CL_THN_05:GA-09","CL_THN_06:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Normative correction is immanent: broader comparison, discriminating variation, skeptical restraint, and recursive audit regulate the same natural operations that generate error.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-07","CL_THN_05:GA-10","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The self has no warranted status as a substantial origin but remains operative as a vivid relay, represented object, temporal comparator, and socially altered magnitude.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-11","CL_THN_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Natural production and felt efficacy do not establish representational truth, epistemic warrant, intrinsic value, or source-fidelity.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GA-12","CL_THN_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Social value is generated in circulation: transmitted satisfaction, reflected judgment, comparative opposition, and recursive uptake constitute rank and self-estimation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Relational proximity and intensity possess no intrinsic valence; they amplify whichever epistemic, sympathetic, or comparative transformation controls the route.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Time is an active operator of projection, attenuation, retention, durable attribution, and feedback rather than a neutral container for stable objects and subjects.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-05","CL_THN_05:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] The viable endpoint is a reflexive science of human nature that preserves empirically useful causal and affective mechanisms under permanent semantic, experimental, skeptical, and relational correction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-14","CL_THN_05:GA-13","CL_THN_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Experienced conjunction remains the minimum causal input, although its output expands from expectation to bodily influence, affective conversion, and social feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-01","CL_THN_05:RMD-07","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"[Escalation] Vivacity moves from explaining unequal conviction to animating belief and passion, then becomes the transmissible currency of a social-affective network.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-09","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Imagination changes from a producer of causal expectation and fictive object-unity into the general medium of cognition, then into a simulator capable of constructing another’s absent emotion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:RMD-02","CL_THN_06:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Custom recurs as first-order projection, second-order regulation, affective stabilization, and constructive expectation, with renewed overextension at every phase.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-03","CL_THN_05:RMD-04","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Comparison changes from a corrective instrument that exposes suppressed differences into a social operator that preserves difference as inverse self-estimation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-05","CL_THN_05:RMD-03","CL_THN_06:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"[Migration] Relation migrates from conjunction, separability, and spatial compatibility into cognitive gating, self-reference, social proximity, directed transmission, and comparative interaction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:RMD-03","CL_THN_06:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] The self moves from an absent invariant bearer to an operational object and finally to a socially constituted relay-comparator whose states are generated through feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:RMD-05","CL_THN_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] Cause shifts from whole-object identity to operative circumstance, then to domain-specific conversion, and finally to nonlinear circuits in which generated passions modify their own conditions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-08","CL_THN_05:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"[Mutation] Contradiction changes from a stimulus for metaphysical repair into a diagnostic of unrestricted transition and then into a controlled anomaly for reconstructing social-affective routing.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-08","CL_THN_05:RMD-06","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance] Substance, autonomous reason, intrinsic value, primitive social instinct, and sovereign selfhood disappear after their explanatory work is redistributed across relation, force, custom, comparison, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-10","CL_THN_05:CGD-07","CL_THN_06:RMD-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Particular rules for isolating causal circumstances remain local experimental instruments; their scalable function is recursive differentiation under counterinstance.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Spatial compatibility, soul-substance, immortality, and the exact gates of pride and humility remain local problems rather than current-level explanatory centers.","refs":["CL_THN_05:CGD-01","CL_THN_05:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Fame, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, malice, and the constitutional conjunctions of love or hatred remain local outputs of the broader relational machine.","refs":["CL_THN_06:CGD-01","CL_THN_06:CGD-03","CL_THN_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] Relation-mediated force scales upward as the common architecture connecting belief, causal inference, affective conversion, and social valuation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Experimental circumstance-analysis and affective threshold-gating jointly establish that causal mechanisms are individuated by controlled differences in routing, not by whole-object classification.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-08","CL_THN_05:MP-06","CL_THN_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] The operational self of the skeptical and affective analyses becomes the relay-comparator required by social transmission and reflected valuation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:CGD-08","CL_THN_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Social anomalies demonstrate that unchanged content or objective condition can generate opposed effects, forcing causation to include sequence, topology, retention, and mode selection.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GA-08","CL_THN_06:CGD-08","CL_THN_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Causal identity is recoded from an intrinsic power or whole object into a reproducible transformation distributed across input, relation, force, gate, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-08","CL_THN_05:MME-04","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-09","text":"[Current-Level Emergent] Philosophy becomes a reflexive regulation of cognitive and social-affective machinery rather than a separate faculty judging nature from outside.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-10","CL_THN_05:GA-13","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"No substantial bearer, intrinsic causal power, or affective essence is required where relations and transformations among perceptions can perform the explanatory work.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-02","CL_THN_05:GNX-01","CL_THN_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"No autonomous reason, sovereign self, or neutral tribunal can validate cognition and value independently of imagination, habit, affect, and social relation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-04","CL_THN_05:GNX-02","CL_THN_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Relation is not static, symmetric, intrinsically efficacious, or valence-bearing; its force depends on direction, continuity, comparison, competition, and mediation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GNX-03","CL_THN_06:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Stable content, representation, or objective condition does not guarantee stable belief, judgment, passion, or value.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-03","CL_THN_05:GNX-05","CL_THN_06:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Imagination and custom can neither be granted unrestricted authority nor eliminated, because both successful operation and systematic error depend on them.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-08","CL_THN_05:GNX-04","CL_THN_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"No unrestricted explanatory principle survives: causal uniformity, pure understanding, universal relation, and primitive passion-laws all require domain, threshold, or circumstance restrictions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-07","CL_THN_05:GNX-06","CL_THN_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Neither felt conviction nor sympathetic uptake guarantees warrant, truth, intrinsic worth, or faithful access to another’s interior state.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GNX-05","CL_THN_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"No final correction abolishes custom, closes causal decomposition, fixes self-worth, or establishes a nonparticipatory position outside the relational system.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-03","CL_THN_05:BSA-04","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model that assigns cognition, passion, and social valuation to autonomous faculties governed by independent laws.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-01","CL_THN_05:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"It requires an external standpoint to be unavailable: correction must occur through reorganized experience, comparison, custom, and relation rather than through an Archimedean criterion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-03","CL_THN_05:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"It excludes purely linear causality because belief reinforces repetition, passion recruits judgment, esteem amplifies satisfaction, and altered relations become new causes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-01","CL_THN_05:RLE-02","CL_THN_06:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"It cannot admit an invariant subject whose identity, rationality, and worth remain insulated from temporal force, remembered relation, testimony, and comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-06","CL_THN_05:DBN-06","CL_THN_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"It excludes universal commensurability: epistemic warrant, conviction, pleasure, welfare, public esteem, and comparative self-value cannot be reduced to one scale.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"It also excludes absolute irregularity, because recursive correction requires provisionally stable kinds, repeatable transitions, retained impressions, and reproducible relational effects.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-02","CL_THN_05:GA-05","CL_THN_06:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-07","text":"It cannot admit an affect-free methodology, because evidential uptake, inquiry, skeptical recovery, authority, and correction remain operations of force within human nature.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:CGD-03","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around an absent metaphysical interior: neither causal power, substantial self, nor another person’s authentic feeling is directly available as an explanatory ground.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-01","CL_THN_05:BSA-05","CL_THN_06:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is a common metric capable of aligning truth, felt conviction, welfare, social esteem, and self-worth.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-06","CL_THN_05:BSA-01","CL_THN_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"No view from outside human nature can determine the legitimacy of custom, imagination, sympathy, or comparison without using those same operations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-02","CL_THN_05:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"No final alignment joins what nature produces to what is true or good; natural belief, practical stability, social value, and affective intensity can all diverge from warrant and welfare.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:BSA-03","CL_THN_06:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"The architecture lacks a general metric for mode selection and activation: it cannot finally specify when custom becomes rule, relation becomes force, or sympathy yields to comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-06","CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","CL_THN_06:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-06","text":"Final repose is inadmissible because every stabilization can become a new habit, fiction, hierarchy, or compound circumstance requiring renewed differentiation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-04","CL_THN_05:BSA-04","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Causal judgment and social passion are two scales of one natural engine: relation organizes transition, force makes it operative, and gating determines whether the result becomes inference, fiction, attachment, or comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-01","CL_THN_05:XD-01","CL_THN_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Comparison bifurcates normatively: controlled difference corrects causal projection, whereas retained contrast can intensify envy or self-elevation without improving truth-tracking.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-02","CL_THN_06:XD-03","CL_THN_06:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-03","text":"The distinction between warrant and conviction extends into the social field: authority, praise, familiarity, and reflected esteem can amplify assent or self-belief independently of evidential regularity.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-05","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Once the substantial self is removed, cognitive and social analyses converge on a subject constituted by incoming force, retained relations, comparative positioning, and recursive self-attribution.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:XD-05","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Constructive sympathy extends the theory of endogenous error: a representation can generate a real affective consequence even when it does not faithfully reproduce its alleged source.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:XD-03","CL_THN_06:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-06","text":"General rules are simultaneously corrective and productive: they can restrain prejudicial inference, stabilize inquiry, or construct expected passions that reinforce the social model from which they arose.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-06","CL_THN_05:RMD-04","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Causal explanation becomes irreducibly recursive in the social domain because outputs such as belief, hatred, esteem, and envy alter the judgments and relations governing subsequent transitions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-06","CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-08","text":"Strategic intervention follows the same logic as experiment: instead of positing new faculties, alter authority, proximity, concurrence, associative continuity, or comparative framing and observe the transformed output.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","CL_THN_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Social topology becomes epistemically relevant because testimony and judgment acquire force through authority and relation; inquiry must therefore distinguish evidential content from its route of affective uptake.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Moderate skepticism cannot require affective neutrality; it must regulate the unavoidable forces by which custom, curiosity, testimony, and social relations sustain inquiry.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-10","CL_THN_05:RLE-04","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Ontology becomes an operational field of heterogeneous events connected by recurrent, force-bearing, and directionally weighted transitions without known substantial support.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-02","CL_THN_05:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a distributed effect of organization: impressions, habits, memories, testimony, comparisons, and reflected sentiments converge temporarily as self-reference.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:BSD-02","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic normativity becomes topological and regulatory: warrant depends on how transitions survive counterinstance, variation, competing routes, and recursive feedback rather than on their subjective force alone.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-01","CL_THN_05:BSD-06","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Social reality is causally productive rather than representationally secondary: circulated affect generates rank, self-estimation, attachment, antagonism, and the evidential force of judgments.","refs":["CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Time becomes recursively constitutive: repetition projects futures, distance modulates conviction, retention enables comparison, and reflected consequences become new present causes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-05","CL_THN_05:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Human agency is non-sovereign but strategically variable: subjects cannot suspend the machinery of transition, yet they can alter routes, circumstances, associations, and comparative fields.","refs":["CL_THN_05:BSD-05","CL_THN_06:BSD-07","CL_THN_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Error is not external noise but productive excess internal to successful machinery; every mechanism capable of stabilizing cognition or society can also fabricate ontology, sentiment, or hierarchy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-04","CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"The science of human nature becomes experimentally recursive: anomalies are treated as variations revealing hidden routing conditions rather than as immediate grounds for inventing new faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-07","CL_THN_05:BSD-08","CL_THN_06:FGS-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-09","text":"No ethical or epistemic virtue inheres in connectedness itself; intensified relation can support disciplined comparison, prejudice, sympathy, rivalry, or self-confirming passion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-03","CL_THN_06:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Repetition → custom → facilitated transition → vivified belief or action → renewed conjunction forms the basal cognitive loop.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-01","CL_THN_05:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Fluent transition → projected unity or power → contradiction → fictive repair or controlled differentiation forms the principal epistemic bifurcation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-02","CL_THN_04:RLE-05","CL_THN_05:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"General mechanism → anomalous output → restrictive gating → provisional stabilization → renewed anomaly forms the meta-cluster’s dominant escalation sequence.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-04","CL_THN_05:RLE-05","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Inquiry → skeptical intensification → affective interruption → natural restoration → constrained inquiry forms the recurrent anti-foundational spiral.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-08","CL_THN_05:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Sign or condition → represented affect → vivacity transfer or construction → passion → judgment or desire → altered condition forms the social feedback loop.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-05","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → reflected esteem → intensified satisfaction forms a status-producing loop in which social value recursively augments its own cause.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-02","CL_THN_06:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Comparison → altered self-position → passion → attention to the comparative relation → intensified or severed comparison forms the recursive rivalry loop.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-04","CL_THN_06:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"“Relation” becomes the principal machine when it expands from observed connection into a directed infrastructure governing inference, attribution, simulation, comparison, and intervention.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-07","CL_THN_05:RLE-07","CL_THN_06:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-09","text":"Human nature is the hidden attractor: causal method, skeptical regulation, selfhood, passion, and social hierarchy converge on the recursive organization of relation-mediated force.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-09","CL_THN_05:RLE-06","CL_THN_06:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Initialize heterogeneous perceptions, qualities, pleasures, pains, persons, and represented conditions without an independently appearing support.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","CL_THN_05:FGS-B01","CL_THN_06:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Organize inputs through conjunction, resemblance, contiguity, succession, self-reference, authority, proximity, durability, and directed relational pathways.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Add force through vivacity, pleasure, pain, passion, retained impression, or customary propensity; relation without force remains explanatorily incomplete.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Repetition and general rules stabilize transitions into expectation, attribution, self-reference, and socially anticipated affect.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B05","CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Route stabilized force through causal projection, imaginative completion, sympathetic appropriation, or comparative opposition to generate belief, object-unity, passion, and social value.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","CL_THN_06:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Detect excess through contrary instance, fictive ontology, contradictory belief, affective overgeneration, reversed valence, or self-confirming judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B03","CL_THN_05:FGS-B06","CL_THN_06:XD-07","CL_THN_06:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Bifurcate excess into simulated closure or controlled restriction through semantic audit, circumstance-analysis, skeptical diffidence, threshold-gating, and relational rerouting.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B04","CL_THN_04:FGS-B06","CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","CL_THN_06:FGS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Feed generated beliefs and passions back into custom, judgment, authority, reflected esteem, and comparative positioning, thereby modifying subsequent inputs and routes.","refs":["CL_THN_05:FGS-B08","CL_THN_06:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B09","text":"Submit every provisional stabilization to renewed variation, including the rules, classifications, and social models produced by earlier correction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B07","CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","CL_THN_06:FGS-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B10","text":"Terminate only metaphysical completion: preserve recursive empirical and social-affective inquiry while withholding final necessity, intrinsic baseline, and external validation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B08","CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Hidden substance, intrinsic power, autonomous reason, and primitive passion → demand for experiential and operational justification.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","CL_THN_05:AFM-B01","CL_THN_06:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Justificatory demand → decomposition into perceptions, qualities, affects, conjunctions, and relations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B03","CL_THN_05:AFM-B02","CL_THN_06:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Decomposition → relation-conditioned transition animated by force, vivacity, repetition, and custom.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B01","CL_THN_05:AFM-B03","CL_THN_06:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Animated transition → belief, projected unity, apparent placement, self-attribution, sympathy, and comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B02","CL_THN_05:AFM-B04","CL_THN_06:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Unrestricted transition → prejudice, metaphysical fiction, contradictory belief, affective overgeneration, and reversed social valence.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","CL_THN_05:AFM-B05","CL_THN_06:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Excess and anomaly → controlled difference, semantic restraint, skeptical restriction, relational gating, and mode differentiation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B05","CL_THN_05:AFM-B06","CL_THN_06:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B07","text":"Restricted mechanism → recursive belief, reflected esteem, passion-generated judgment, and strategic alteration of relational routes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B06","CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","CL_THN_06:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B08","text":"Recursive feedback → nonterminal science of human nature constrained by experiment and skepticism but lacking final thresholds, intrinsic baselines, or external closure.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B07","CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","CL_THN_06:AFM-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The substance–faculty map is replaced by a field of heterogeneous events, directed relations, force-bearing transitions, and recursive outputs.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-02","CL_THN_05:MME-01","CL_THN_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Reason, imagination, passion, and error cease to name autonomous agencies and become differently regulated phases of one natural economy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-01","CL_THN_05:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Causation is redrawn as a transformation architecture: conjunction supplies repeatability, relation supplies routing, force supplies efficacy, and gating supplies differential output.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-05","CL_THN_05:MME-04","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-04","text":"The self is reclassified from hidden bearer to a temporally extended and socially permeable relay-comparator.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-02","CL_THN_05:MME-05","CL_THN_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Evidence becomes multidimensional: comparative regularity determines warrant, vivacity determines uptake, and social topology determines which judgments acquire force.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-06","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Comparison is split into experimental discrimination and affective inversion, dissolving any assumption that heightened contrast is intrinsically rational or irrational.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-07","text":"Social hierarchy is reclassified as recursively generated affective adjacency rather than the passive registration of intrinsic rank.","refs":["CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-08","text":"Error becomes a phase of productive machinery whose limits are disclosed through overgeneration rather than a foreign corruption added to otherwise pure faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-04","CL_THN_05:MME-07","CL_THN_06:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-09","text":"Intervention and interpretation shift from naming hidden entities to varying circumstances, tracing routes, separating force from content, and testing how feedback transforms the field.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-07","CL_THN_05:MME-09","CL_THN_06:MME-07","CL_THN_06:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: causal judgment, substance and soul, skeptical inquiry, pride and humility, reputation, affection, pity, envy, and malice appear as distinct explicit problems.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B01","CL_THN_05:ALT-B01","CL_THN_06:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: relation, repetition, vivacity, imagination, comparison, custom, retention, and feedback generate and differentiate the surface phenomena.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B02","CL_THN_05:ALT-B02","CL_THN_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: inquiry intervenes by widening comparison, varying circumstances, restricting overgeneralization, and rerouting authority or proximity rather than invoking new faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B03","CL_THN_05:ALT-B03","CL_THN_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality is a field of heterogeneous events in ordered and retained relations; identity, causation, selfhood, and value are provisional products of temporal organization.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B04","CL_THN_05:ALT-B04","CL_THN_06:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"Social / Political level: authority, publicity, custom, reflected esteem, proximity, and comparison distribute the force by which judgments and hierarchies become operative.","refs":["CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B06","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the architecture performs its thesis by producing excess, converting anomaly into controlled variation, and recursively subjecting its own rules and stabilizations to renewed audit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B05","CL_THN_05:ALT-B05","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-01","text":"The same custom and imagination generate error and correction, leaving the distinction between legitimate and illegitimate transition immanent and never completely secured.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-01","CL_THN_05:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Necessary connection is denied, yet causal method depends on strong uniformity, stable kinds, and reproducible transitions whose practical authority exceeds their ontological grounding.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-03","CL_THN_04:FL-05","CL_THN_05:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-03","text":"A common transition architecture must explain heterogeneous outputs, but each domain introduces gates and constitutional supplements that resist complete unification.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GA-08","CL_THN_05:FL-05","CL_THN_06:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-04","text":"The substantial self is rejected, yet correction, passion, memory, and comparison require persistent self-reference whose continuity remains operationally necessary but ontologically unspecified.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:FL-01","CL_THN_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Force and relation are indispensable explanatory variables, but no quantitative rule determines sufficient vivacity, adequate repetition, decisive relation, or conversion threshold.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-08","CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","CL_THN_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-06","text":"Sympathy and comparison can derive opposed affects from the same condition, but the architecture lacks a general selector determining which mode dominates.","refs":["CL_THN_06:FL-02","CL_THN_06:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-07","text":"Nature restores belief, action, and affective order without vindicating them, preserving a structural separation between practical efficacy and epistemic or ethical legitimacy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:FL-06","CL_THN_06:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-08","text":"Corrective inquiry is itself socially and affectively routed through authority, curiosity, testimony, and vivacity, threatening any claim that experimental regulation is insulated from the mechanisms it diagnoses.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Relation mutates from observed conjunction into a directed and weighted infrastructure for causation, self-reference, sympathy, comparison, and intervention.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-05","CL_THN_05:MP-03","CL_THN_06:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Vivacity mutates from a measure of conviction into the common activation threshold for belief, passion, social transmission, and self-alteration.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-09","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Imagination mutates from associative completion into a general productive medium and finally into constructive social simulation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:MP-02","CL_THN_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Comparison mutates from experimental control into a social operation that converts retained affect into inverse self-evaluation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-01","CL_THN_06:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-05","text":"The self mutates from excluded substance into operational object and then into a socially generated magnitude within recursive feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:MP-04","CL_THN_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-06","text":"Cause mutates from whole-object identity into discriminated circumstance, gated conversion, and feedback circuit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-04","CL_THN_05:MP-06","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-07","text":"Skepticism mutates from denial of intrinsic power into recurrent regulation of cognitive and social-affective mechanisms without terminating their operation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-05","CL_THN_05:MP-05","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a recursive laboratory-network: heterogeneous events form nodes, relations form weighted pathways, force animates transmission, and feedback continually alters the field being investigated.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-01","CL_THN_05:HSI-01","CL_THN_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable relay-comparator that receives force, projects expectation, simulates others, measures itself through retained differences, and returns the result as judgment or action.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-02","CL_THN_05:HSI-02","CL_THN_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a nested circuit rather than a line: repetition becomes projection, retained affect becomes comparison, durable attribution becomes identity, and reflected consequence becomes new cause.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-03","CL_THN_05:HSI-03","CL_THN_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is over routing rather than transcendence: viable correction alters circumstances, relations, authorities, associations, and comparative distances while remaining inside the machinery of human nature.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-04","CL_THN_05:HSI-04","CL_THN_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the meta-cluster if unsupported heterogeneity, relation-mediated force, customary stabilization, differential gating, recursive feedback, and immanent correction are retained together.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B01","CL_THN_05:MRT-B01","CL_THN_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning stable kinds, adequate repetition, causal individuation, mode selection, relation thresholds, and the continuity of the operational self.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B02","CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","CL_THN_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a recursive operational naturalism, not as a deductive foundation, unified metric, or final reconciliation of cognition and affect.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B03","CL_THN_05:MRT-B03","CL_THN_06:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of failed intrinsic explanations into a distributed machine capable of generating belief, error, selfhood, passion, hierarchy, and their correction from related operations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B04","CL_THN_05:MRT-B04","CL_THN_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if foundational subtraction is followed by operational return, and operational return by anomaly, restriction, feedback, and renewed audit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B05","CL_THN_05:MRT-B05","CL_THN_06:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture is weakest where it assigns regulatory authority to broader custom, experiment, moderation, or social certification without fully deriving their superiority from the machinery they regulate.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B06","CL_THN_05:FL-02","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B07","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to axiomatic reduction is differential force and selection: neither the intensity activating a transition nor the rule choosing among projection, sympathy, direct survey, and comparison is fully calculable.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B07","CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","CL_THN_06:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Metacluster 2","context":"Chapters 10–18 · Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy · Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System · Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_title":"Metacluster 2","original_context":"Chapters 10–18 · Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy · Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System · Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy"},"original_title":"Metacluster 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 3","order":3,"parents":["CL_THN_07","CL_THN_08","CL_THN_09"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_03@R001","sha256":"571aacd38736984af332d422ff1398b4721a7d965edbbebe04b43425a4fc4b76","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-01","text":"The central problem is how mediated, partial, historically variable affect can generate stable property, justice, moral judgment, and political authority without intrinsic value, innate right, or an external normative tribunal.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:AS-B01","CL_THN_09:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-02","text":"The governing thesis is that normative order is an endogenous construction: affective forces are redirected through repetition, reflection, reciprocal expectation, generalization, and institutional determination until they acquire authority over their own conditions of origin.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:BSD-09","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-03","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a recursive naturalistic genealogy joined to a functional test: it reconstructs the genesis of norms and then evaluates them by the relational effects they durably produce.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-03","CL_THN_08:AS-B03","CL_THN_09:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-04","text":"The movement proceeds from object-side value to affective mediation, from mediated passion to convention, from convention to constituted juridical objects, and from institutional utility to generalized moral authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-05","CL_THN_08:AS-B05","CL_THN_09:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-05","text":"Relation becomes the trans-scalar operator: it transfers affect, associates persons with goods, establishes duties, communicates consequences, and supplies the field in which character and authority receive moral classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-08","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-06","text":"Repetition converts outputs into conditions: encountered force becomes inclination, reciprocal restraint becomes convention, convention becomes second nature, and generalized judgment becomes normative inertia.","refs":["CL_THN_07:FGS-B07","CL_THN_08:AS-B09","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-07","text":"Stability is necessary but nonterminal: rules must resist local passion, yet accumulated anti-function can reactivate correction and reverse property claims, moral standing, or political allegiance.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-10","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-08","text":"The real movement is spiral and sedimentary: affect generates artifice, artifice reorganizes affect, moralization stabilizes artifice, and historical failure returns the stabilized system to functional reassessment.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-07","CL_THN_08:RLE-04","CL_THN_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-09","text":"Closure is conditional and distributed: shared operations unify the architecture, but instinctive endowment, stipulative boundaries, plural moral sources, and qualitative thresholds prevent reduction to one mechanism or final algorithm.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-13","CL_THN_08:BSD-06","CL_THN_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Pleasure, pain, interest, uneasiness, sympathy, and acquired concern are the indispensable materials from which social and normative order must be generated.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-02","CL_THN_08:GA-02","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Affective, juridical, and moral value is relationally constituted: objects, goods, persons, dispositions, and offices acquire force or status through mediated relations rather than intrinsic properties alone.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:GA-06","CL_THN_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Present force and authority are path-dependent effects of prior encounters, repeated conduct, stabilized expectations, institutional history, and accumulated evaluations.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:BSD-07","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Partiality is both destabilizing material and generative resource: immediate passion produces conflict, while reflected, coordinated, and generalized partiality produces durable order.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSD-05","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Reflection is not a sovereign source of value; it changes the scale, temporal horizon, and organization of passions already in operation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-03","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] A generated category cannot explain its own genesis: acquired ends cannot presuppose antecedent concern, justice cannot presuppose property, and allegiance cannot presuppose contractual obligation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:GA-02","CL_THN_09:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Artifice is necessary without being arbitrary when durable natural limitations require second-order coordination but do not independently determine the form of that coordination.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-03","CL_THN_09:GA-07","CL_THN_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Convention begins as mutually legible, conditionally reciprocal conduct whose repetition stabilizes expectation before explicit moral obligation or authoritative command.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:GA-04","CL_THN_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Institutions constitute normative objects: stabilized possession becomes property, coordinated benefit becomes justice, and protective function becomes political authority only within rule-governed relations.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-06","CL_THN_08:BSD-02","CL_THN_09:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Integral rights and stable verdicts require imposed closure because their affective, possessory, utilitarian, and relational grounds remain gradual, conflicting, and underdeterminate.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSA-02","CL_THN_08:GA-11","CL_THN_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Utility changes function across the architecture: it motivates coordination, necessitates institutions, sanctions rule-systems, and tests political authority, but does not uniquely determine every title or exhaust moral merit.","refs":["CL_THN_08:RMD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-06","CL_THN_07:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Sympathy expands from affective transmission into institutional sanction and standpoint generalization, while never becoming universal benevolence or complete motivational impartiality.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-10","CL_THN_09:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Impartiality is manufactured by general rules, corrected standpoints, and invariant application rather than discovered in an originally impartial subject.","refs":["CL_THN_08:BSD-03","CL_THN_09:GA-04","CL_THN_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Persistent] Durable tendency, coordinated scheme, and relational function outrank isolated action, immediate consequence, and accidental outcome as objects of normative evaluation.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-13","CL_THN_09:GA-05","CL_THN_07:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-15","text":"[Emergent] Generated norms acquire relative autonomy: they can outlast their immediate motives, redirect later passions, and authorize judgments unavailable at the moment of their origin.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-15","CL_THN_09:GA-08","CL_THN_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-16","text":"[Terminal] Legitimate reversal must itself be generalized: accumulated, public, and recurrent anti-function—not isolated dislike or private injury—can terminate established authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-05","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-17","text":"[Terminal] The resulting order is endogenous, relational, plural, and conditionally stable; no single affective, legal, utilitarian, or moral mechanism completes every stage.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-13","CL_THN_08:GA-15","CL_THN_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pleasure and pain remain the basic currency, but their function expands from direct affective modulation into support for inclination, institutional observance, moral approval, blame, and resistance.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-01","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Domain expansion] Relation moves from association and affective transfer to juridical attachment and finally to the evaluative field in which dispositions and institutions acquire moral identity.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-03","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Repetition successively produces habit, reciprocal confidence, institutional sedimentation, stable moral language, and historical evidence of durable function or anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:RLE-02","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Stability changes from affective relief into possessory security, juridical rigidity, normative inertia, and finally a condition vulnerable to reversal when it protects recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-06","CL_THN_08:GA-09","CL_THN_09:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Escalation] Sympathy begins as inter-organismic communication, becomes approval of public utility, and culminates in corrected moral judgment capable of evaluating strangers, institutions, and political effects.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-10","CL_THN_09:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Mutation] Cognition shifts from an affective channel-selector into reflective interest, genealogical correction, and standpoint adjustment; its function is organizational rather than value-creating.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-02","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Self-interest changes from immediate acquisitive disruption into reciprocal restraint and then into allegiance to a system whose generalized rules can oppose immediate interest.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:RMD-05","CL_THN_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Mutation] Utility moves from an affectively mediated input to the necessity of settlement, then to institutional sanction, and finally to a functional criterion capable of withdrawing political authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:MME-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] General rules first suppress local variability and preserve authority beyond immediate causes; recurrent public failure then produces a counter-rule that authorizes reclassification and resistance.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-09","CL_THN_09:RMD-04","CL_THN_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] The natural/artificial distinction is absorbed into a layered model in which natural affects and limitations supply materials while artificial coordination produces objects, rules, standpoints, and second-order dispositions.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-12","CL_THN_08:MME-01","CL_THN_09:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-11","text":"[Terminal transformation] Normative objects cease to appear antecedent to their relations: affective value, property, moral character, and political authority become stabilized outcomes of mediation, institution, and functional judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:RMD-03","CL_THN_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Species comparison, cognitive thresholds, excitatory bands, and the detailed mechanisms of curiosity remain affective diagnostics; their scalable result is the mediated and phase-sensitive constitution of motivation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:CGD-01","CL_THN_07:CGD-03","CL_THN_07:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Occupation, prescription, accession, succession, and municipal title rules remain juridical implementations; their scalable result is that gradual relations require socially imposed determinacy.","refs":["CL_THN_08:CGD-02","CL_THN_08:CGD-06","CL_THN_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Pride, tenderness, anger, allegiance, and tyranny remain differentiated moral-political cases; their scalable result is evaluation by durable relational tendency and generalizable exception.","refs":["CL_THN_09:CGD-02","CL_THN_09:CGD-03","CL_THN_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] Affective mediation becomes the substrate of institutional genesis: passions can be redirected, transferred, and stabilized because they are not fixed responses to intrinsic object-values.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Path-dependence becomes sedimentary social ontology: repeated encounters create inclinations, repeated restraint creates convention, and repeated evaluation creates stable authority or evidence of anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSD-02","CL_THN_08:HSI-03","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Affective transfer and sympathy connect individual passion to public normativity by allowing generated force and perceived consequences to cross persons, practices, and institutions.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-06","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Relation and imagination connect affective association to juridical and moral object-construction: the same relational field joins ideas, persons, goods, duties, and evaluative roles.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-08","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] The possibility that conduct generates its later motive scales into the claim that observance can precede duty and that institutional authority can be retrospectively moralized.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Instinct, scarcity, limited benevolence, and shared affective constitution are retained not as complete explanations but as boundary conditions constraining what recursive construction can generate.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-12","CL_THN_08:GA-01","CL_THN_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-10","text":"[Superseded] Intrinsic value, pre-social property, naturally impartial motivation, and absolute political title are replaced by mediated force, constituted relation, corrected judgment, and conditional function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-01","CL_THN_09:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"No intrinsic object-quality is sufficient to determine affective force, juridical ownership, moral merit, or political authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-03","CL_THN_09:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Sovereign reason, innate justice, and an affect-free moral standpoint cannot originate the architecture.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-02","CL_THN_08:GNX-02","CL_THN_09:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Pre-social property, natural right, promise, or contract cannot explain institutions that must first constitute rights, duties, and authoritative expectations.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-06","CL_THN_09:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Universal benevolence and motivational impartiality cannot generate or continuously sustain general social rules.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-07","CL_THN_09:GNX-05","CL_THN_07:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Utility cannot serve as a self-executing and exhaustive measure of affect, title, virtue, or every particular institutional act.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-07","CL_THN_08:GNX-04","CL_THN_09:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Stable order cannot remain continuously responsive to immediate feeling, private interest, isolated need, individual merit, or case-specific optimization.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-03","CL_THN_08:GNX-05","CL_THN_09:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Natural relations do not contain the binary boundaries required by property, legal decision, stable moral verdict, or political classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSA-02","CL_THN_08:DBN-05","CL_THN_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Isolated consequences, accidental outcomes, or singular failures cannot determine the status of a rule, disposition, or institution independently of its durable tendency.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-08","CL_THN_09:GNX-04","CL_THN_07:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-09","text":"Neither repetition nor generalization guarantees permanent reinforcement: accumulated stability may terminate affect, conceal injustice, or generate a counter-rule against recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-05","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-10","text":"No office, law, property distribution, moral consensus, or political authority is absolutely immune to historical reclassification.","refs":["CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:BSA-05","CL_THN_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"A fixed subject–object ontology must be false: both subjects and objects must remain penetrable by affective history, social expectation, and relational classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-01","CL_THN_08:BSD-02","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"The pre-social individual cannot be a complete rational,